on, etc.). However, in contrast to professional athletes, recreational\nrunners have limited schedules to train, which often are adapted to other daily living activities and\nduties. Moreover, although some ex-professional athletes exist in the recreational runner category, it\nis expected that most", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "an intensity ≤100% of the velocity associated\nto maximum oxygen consumption [vVO2max]) also found improvements in performance in running\ncompetitions from 1.5 to 10 km [10–16], while short intervals of HIIT (lasting 20–60 s at a higher intensity\nthan"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0002_c0020", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f461fcfc436fe0ac", "text_span_hash": "f461fcfc436fe0ac", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 20, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "0 s at a higher intensity\nthan the vVO2max) also exhibited improvements in performance from 1.5 to 10 km [9,12,13,17,18].\nAll of these previous studies suggested that diﬀerent forms of HIIT, such as SIT, aerobic HIIT, or\nHIIT with short intervals, co", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "health status. However, there are a plethora of anecdotal guidelines without robust scientiﬁc support,\nincluding the use of nonvalidated algorithms (e.g., Trainingpeaks™) for training monitoring, the\n“10% rule” for weekly training load increments, or the use of diﬀerent types of shoes to reduce injury\nrates. In fact, it is common to see recreational runners imitating training practices similar to those\nof professional athletes, including completion of high weekly mileage (e.g., > 70 km), which may be\nbehind the high prevalence of some health-related problems [7]. Further, as current scientiﬁc evidence\non endurance running was developed from studies observing recreational and professional athletes of\nvery diﬀerent levels (from untrained to elite), it is unclear how recreational runners can eﬀectively use\nall the available information. Furthermore, the deﬁnition of a recreational runner is very wide, since\nwe could include in this category any runner who trains and competes regularly during leisure time\nindependently of the performance level (from novice to well-trained athletes) and speciﬁc objectives\n(e.g., enjoyment, health, competition, etc.). However, in contrast to professional athletes, recreational\nrunners have limited schedules to train, which often are adapted to other daily living activities and\nduties. Moreover, although some ex-professional athletes exist in the recreational runner category, it\nis expected that most", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "0 s at a higher intensity\nthan the vVO2max) also exhibited improvements in performance from 1.5 to 10 km [9,12,13,17,18].\nAll of these previous studies suggested that diﬀerent forms of HIIT, such as SIT, aerobic HIIT, or\nHIIT with short intervals, co"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0002_c0021", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "75bc5a7eda826303", "text_span_hash": "75bc5a7eda826303", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 21, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "HIIT with short intervals, could be implemented in order to improve the performance of recreational", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "health status. However, there are a plethora of anecdotal guidelines without robust scientiﬁc support,\nincluding the use of nonvalidated algorithms (e.g., Trainingpeaks™) for training monitoring, the\n“10% rule” for weekly training load increments, or the use of diﬀerent types of shoes to reduce injury\nrates. In fact, it is common to see recreational runners imitating training practices similar to those\nof professional athletes, including completion of high weekly mileage (e.g., > 70 km), which may be\nbehind the high prevalence of some health-related problems [7]. Further, as current scientiﬁc evidence\non endurance running was developed from studies observing recreational and professional athletes of\nvery diﬀerent levels (from untrained to elite), it is unclear how recreational runners can eﬀectively use\nall the available information. Furthermore, the deﬁnition of a recreational runner is very wide, since\nwe could include in this category any runner who trains and competes regularly during leisure time\nindependently of the performance level (from novice to well-trained athletes) and speciﬁc objectives\n(e.g., enjoyment, health, competition, etc.). However, in contrast to professional athletes, recreational\nrunners have limited schedules to train, which often are adapted to other daily living activities and\nduties. Moreover, although some ex-professional athletes exist in the recreational runner category, it\nis expected that most", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "HIIT with short intervals, could be implemented in order to improve the performance of recreational"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0003_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "86aef9ecb1ed49ec", "text_span_hash": "86aef9ecb1ed49ec", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "runners. However, a combination of several methods might be the best option to improve adaptation\nto endurance running training.\nAerobic HIIT [11,12,14,15,19] and short-interval HIIT [9,11,12,16] were deemed as eﬀective exercise\nmodalities to VO2max.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "runners. However, a combination of several methods might be the best option to improve adaptation\nto endurance running training.\nAerobic HIIT [11,12,14,15,19] and short-interval HIIT [9,11,12,16] were deemed as eﬀective exercise\nmodalities to VO2max. However, Denadai et al. [13] did not ﬁnd improvements in VO2max after\naerobic HIIT in well-trained runners, indicating that short-interval HIIT at very high intensity might\nbe necessary to produce any signiﬁcant eﬀect in well-trained athletes. On the other hand, aerobic HIIT\nwas found to improve the velocity associated with VO2max (vVO2max; [11,14,15]), thereby implicating\nimprovements in running performance even without the presence of higher VO2max values. Running\neconomy (RE), or the energetic cost of running at a given running speed [20], was found to improve\nafter aerobic HIIT [13,15,19] and SIT-based programs [10,17]. The lack of RE improvement after a short\nHIIT intervention [12] suggests that improvements in RE are not only related to the type of HIIT used\nbut also to the total volume completed. In this regard, RE improvements were related to improvements\nof muscle oxidative capacity, as suggested by Denadai et al. [13]. Interestingly, studies comparing\ntraining programs with HIIT and CT showed greater improvements in VO2max [18] and vVO2max [19]\nwhen using HIIT, and in RE when using CT [19].\nWell-structured intervention studies utilizing other types of running methods are", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "runners. However, a combination of several methods might be the best option to improve adaptation\nto endurance running training.\nAerobic HIIT [11,12,14,15,19] and short-interval HIIT [9,11,12,16] were deemed as eﬀective exercise\nmodalities to VO2max."}
