{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0003_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5188abe00cdd6253", "text_span_hash": "5188abe00cdd6253", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "re related to improvements\nof muscle oxidative capacity, as suggested by Denadai et al. [13]. Interestingly, studies comparing\ntraining programs with HIIT and CT showed greater improvements in VO2max [18] and vVO2max [19]\nwhen using HIIT, and in RE w", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "runners. However, a combination of several methods might be the best option to improve adaptation\nto endurance running training.\nAerobic HIIT [11,12,14,15,19] and short-interval HIIT [9,11,12,16] were deemed as eﬀective exercise\nmodalities to VO2max. However, Denadai et al. [13] did not ﬁnd improvements in VO2max after\naerobic HIIT in well-trained runners, indicating that short-interval HIIT at very high intensity might\nbe necessary to produce any signiﬁcant eﬀect in well-trained athletes. On the other hand, aerobic HIIT\nwas found to improve the velocity associated with VO2max (vVO2max; [11,14,15]), thereby implicating\nimprovements in running performance even without the presence of higher VO2max values. Running\neconomy (RE), or the energetic cost of running at a given running speed [20], was found to improve\nafter aerobic HIIT [13,15,19] and SIT-based programs [10,17]. The lack of RE improvement after a short\nHIIT intervention [12] suggests that improvements in RE are not only related to the type of HIIT used\nbut also to the total volume completed. In this regard, RE improvements were related to improvements\nof muscle oxidative capacity, as suggested by Denadai et al. [13]. Interestingly, studies comparing\ntraining programs with HIIT and CT showed greater improvements in VO2max [18] and vVO2max [19]\nwhen using HIIT, and in RE when using CT [19].\nWell-structured intervention studies utilizing other types of running methods are", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "re related to improvements\nof muscle oxidative capacity, as suggested by Denadai et al. [13]. Interestingly, studies comparing\ntraining programs with HIIT and CT showed greater improvements in VO2max [18] and vVO2max [19]\nwhen using HIIT, and in RE w"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0003_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3705fcde92f2d5ab", "text_span_hash": "3705fcde92f2d5ab", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "]\nwhen using HIIT, and in RE when using CT [19].\nWell-structured intervention studies utilizing other types of running methods are scarce in\nliterature (e.g., running on sand, fartlek, etc.), therefore comparing these and other methods through\nexamin", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "runners. However, a combination of several methods might be the best option to improve adaptation\nto endurance running training.\nAerobic HIIT [11,12,14,15,19] and short-interval HIIT [9,11,12,16] were deemed as eﬀective exercise\nmodalities to VO2max. However, Denadai et al. [13] did not ﬁnd improvements in VO2max after\naerobic HIIT in well-trained runners, indicating that short-interval HIIT at very high intensity might\nbe necessary to produce any signiﬁcant eﬀect in well-trained athletes. On the other hand, aerobic HIIT\nwas found to improve the velocity associated with VO2max (vVO2max; [11,14,15]), thereby implicating\nimprovements in running performance even without the presence of higher VO2max values. Running\neconomy (RE), or the energetic cost of running at a given running speed [20], was found to improve\nafter aerobic HIIT [13,15,19] and SIT-based programs [10,17]. The lack of RE improvement after a short\nHIIT intervention [12] suggests that improvements in RE are not only related to the type of HIIT used\nbut also to the total volume completed. In this regard, RE improvements were related to improvements\nof muscle oxidative capacity, as suggested by Denadai et al. [13]. Interestingly, studies comparing\ntraining programs with HIIT and CT showed greater improvements in VO2max [18] and vVO2max [19]\nwhen using HIIT, and in RE when using CT [19].\nWell-structured intervention studies utilizing other types of running methods are", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "]\nwhen using HIIT, and in RE when using CT [19].\nWell-structured intervention studies utilizing other types of running methods are scarce in\nliterature (e.g., running on sand, fartlek, etc.), therefore comparing these and other methods through\nexamin"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0003_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ae0ab964cc01ba0d", "text_span_hash": "ae0ab964cc01ba0d", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "with moderate- and low-intensity CT, is generally recommended to improve performance in recreational\nrunners in a healthy manner.\n2.2. Strength Training\nIn addition to running, strength training (ST) was found to improve muscular strength and RE in\nr", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "runners. However, a combination of several methods might be the best option to improve adaptation\nto endurance running training.\nAerobic HIIT [11,12,14,15,19] and short-interval HIIT [9,11,12,16] were deemed as eﬀective exercise\nmodalities to VO2max. However, Denadai et al. [13] did not ﬁnd improvements in VO2max after\naerobic HIIT in well-trained runners, indicating that short-interval HIIT at very high intensity might\nbe necessary to produce any signiﬁcant eﬀect in well-trained athletes. On the other hand, aerobic HIIT\nwas found to improve the velocity associated with VO2max (vVO2max; [11,14,15]), thereby implicating\nimprovements in running performance even without the presence of higher VO2max values. Running\neconomy (RE), or the energetic cost of running at a given running speed [20], was found to improve\nafter aerobic HIIT [13,15,19] and SIT-based programs [10,17]. The lack of RE improvement after a short\nHIIT intervention [12] suggests that improvements in RE are not only related to the type of HIIT used\nbut also to the total volume completed. In this regard, RE improvements were related to improvements\nof muscle oxidative capacity, as suggested by Denadai et al. [13]. Interestingly, studies comparing\ntraining programs with HIIT and CT showed greater improvements in VO2max [18] and vVO2max [19]\nwhen using HIIT, and in RE when using CT [19].\nWell-structured intervention studies utilizing other types of running methods are", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "with moderate- and low-intensity CT, is generally recommended to improve performance in recreational\nrunners in a healthy manner.\n2.2. Strength Training\nIn addition to running, strength training (ST) was found to improve muscular strength and RE in\nr"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0003_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e23fefc0d48a6007", "text_span_hash": "e23fefc0d48a6007", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "muscular strength and RE in\nrunners of very diﬀerent levels [21–23], and performance in previously trained runners [23]. Thus,\nRE improvements were observed following diﬀerent types of ST, such as resistance training [24–27]\nand plyometric training", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "runners. However, a combination of several methods might be the best option to improve adaptation\nto endurance running training.\nAerobic HIIT [11,12,14,15,19] and short-interval HIIT [9,11,12,16] were deemed as eﬀective exercise\nmodalities to VO2max. However, Denadai et al. [13] did not ﬁnd improvements in VO2max after\naerobic HIIT in well-trained runners, indicating that short-interval HIIT at very high intensity might\nbe necessary to produce any signiﬁcant eﬀect in well-trained athletes. On the other hand, aerobic HIIT\nwas found to improve the velocity associated with VO2max (vVO2max; [11,14,15]), thereby implicating\nimprovements in running performance even without the presence of higher VO2max values. Running\neconomy (RE), or the energetic cost of running at a given running speed [20], was found to improve\nafter aerobic HIIT [13,15,19] and SIT-based programs [10,17]. The lack of RE improvement after a short\nHIIT intervention [12] suggests that improvements in RE are not only related to the type of HIIT used\nbut also to the total volume completed. In this regard, RE improvements were related to improvements\nof muscle oxidative capacity, as suggested by Denadai et al. [13]. Interestingly, studies comparing\ntraining programs with HIIT and CT showed greater improvements in VO2max [18] and vVO2max [19]\nwhen using HIIT, and in RE when using CT [19].\nWell-structured intervention studies utilizing other types of running methods are", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "muscular strength and RE in\nrunners of very diﬀerent levels [21–23], and performance in previously trained runners [23]. Thus,\nRE improvements were observed following diﬀerent types of ST, such as resistance training [24–27]\nand plyometric training"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0003_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "00a944bee14f4624", "text_span_hash": "00a944bee14f4624", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "4–27]\nand plyometric training [28]. In addition, ST might be also eﬀective to improve the VO2max of novice\nrecreational runners after 6 to 14 weeks of training [24,28–30], but this ﬁnding was not observed\nin well-trained runners. One study [31] found", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "runners. However, a combination of several methods might be the best option to improve adaptation\nto endurance running training.\nAerobic HIIT [11,12,14,15,19] and short-interval HIIT [9,11,12,16] were deemed as eﬀective exercise\nmodalities to VO2max. However, Denadai et al. [13] did not ﬁnd improvements in VO2max after\naerobic HIIT in well-trained runners, indicating that short-interval HIIT at very high intensity might\nbe necessary to produce any signiﬁcant eﬀect in well-trained athletes. On the other hand, aerobic HIIT\nwas found to improve the velocity associated with VO2max (vVO2max; [11,14,15]), thereby implicating\nimprovements in running performance even without the presence of higher VO2max values. Running\neconomy (RE), or the energetic cost of running at a given running speed [20], was found to improve\nafter aerobic HIIT [13,15,19] and SIT-based programs [10,17]. The lack of RE improvement after a short\nHIIT intervention [12] suggests that improvements in RE are not only related to the type of HIIT used\nbut also to the total volume completed. In this regard, RE improvements were related to improvements\nof muscle oxidative capacity, as suggested by Denadai et al. [13]. Interestingly, studies comparing\ntraining programs with HIIT and CT showed greater improvements in VO2max [18] and vVO2max [19]\nwhen using HIIT, and in RE when using CT [19].\nWell-structured intervention studies utilizing other types of running methods are", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "4–27]\nand plyometric training [28]. In addition, ST might be also eﬀective to improve the VO2max of novice\nrecreational runners after 6 to 14 weeks of training [24,28–30], but this ﬁnding was not observed\nin well-trained runners. One study [31] found"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0003_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "fe394cf0251facc3", "text_span_hash": "fe394cf0251facc3", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "runners. One study [31] found that neither physiological nor performance beneﬁts\nafter the use of ST by recreational marathon runners. In this regard, it was suggested that the\nlack of improvement in RE may be due to the small sample size used and t", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "runners. However, a combination of several methods might be the best option to improve adaptation\nto endurance running training.\nAerobic HIIT [11,12,14,15,19] and short-interval HIIT [9,11,12,16] were deemed as eﬀective exercise\nmodalities to VO2max. However, Denadai et al. [13] did not ﬁnd improvements in VO2max after\naerobic HIIT in well-trained runners, indicating that short-interval HIIT at very high intensity might\nbe necessary to produce any signiﬁcant eﬀect in well-trained athletes. On the other hand, aerobic HIIT\nwas found to improve the velocity associated with VO2max (vVO2max; [11,14,15]), thereby implicating\nimprovements in running performance even without the presence of higher VO2max values. Running\neconomy (RE), or the energetic cost of running at a given running speed [20], was found to improve\nafter aerobic HIIT [13,15,19] and SIT-based programs [10,17]. The lack of RE improvement after a short\nHIIT intervention [12] suggests that improvements in RE are not only related to the type of HIIT used\nbut also to the total volume completed. In this regard, RE improvements were related to improvements\nof muscle oxidative capacity, as suggested by Denadai et al. [13]. Interestingly, studies comparing\ntraining programs with HIIT and CT showed greater improvements in VO2max [18] and vVO2max [19]\nwhen using HIIT, and in RE when using CT [19].\nWell-structured intervention studies utilizing other types of running methods are", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "runners. One study [31] found that neither physiological nor performance beneﬁts\nafter the use of ST by recreational marathon runners. In this regard, it was suggested that the\nlack of improvement in RE may be due to the small sample size used and t"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0003_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "a2f4df1f2267de20", "text_span_hash": "a2f4df1f2267de20", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "e small sample size used and the short intervention\ncompleted [31]. However, the lack of physiological and performance improvements generated by ST\nmay also be explained by the reduction in muscle size that happens after a marathon training period\nin", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "runners. However, a combination of several methods might be the best option to improve adaptation\nto endurance running training.\nAerobic HIIT [11,12,14,15,19] and short-interval HIIT [9,11,12,16] were deemed as eﬀective exercise\nmodalities to VO2max. However, Denadai et al. [13] did not ﬁnd improvements in VO2max after\naerobic HIIT in well-trained runners, indicating that short-interval HIIT at very high intensity might\nbe necessary to produce any signiﬁcant eﬀect in well-trained athletes. On the other hand, aerobic HIIT\nwas found to improve the velocity associated with VO2max (vVO2max; [11,14,15]), thereby implicating\nimprovements in running performance even without the presence of higher VO2max values. Running\neconomy (RE), or the energetic cost of running at a given running speed [20], was found to improve\nafter aerobic HIIT [13,15,19] and SIT-based programs [10,17]. The lack of RE improvement after a short\nHIIT intervention [12] suggests that improvements in RE are not only related to the type of HIIT used\nbut also to the total volume completed. In this regard, RE improvements were related to improvements\nof muscle oxidative capacity, as suggested by Denadai et al. [13]. Interestingly, studies comparing\ntraining programs with HIIT and CT showed greater improvements in VO2max [18] and vVO2max [19]\nwhen using HIIT, and in RE when using CT [19].\nWell-structured intervention studies utilizing other types of running methods are", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "e small sample size used and the short intervention\ncompleted [31]. However, the lack of physiological and performance improvements generated by ST\nmay also be explained by the reduction in muscle size that happens after a marathon training period\nin"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0003_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "d32f598c67b10e77", "text_span_hash": "d32f598c67b10e77", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "a marathon training period\nin recreational runners, which may be indicative of an adaptation to reduce oxygen consumption at\nsubmaximal intensities [32]. Given that ST is expected to be related to some hypertrophic response [33],\nST could impair mar", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "runners. However, a combination of several methods might be the best option to improve adaptation\nto endurance running training.\nAerobic HIIT [11,12,14,15,19] and short-interval HIIT [9,11,12,16] were deemed as eﬀective exercise\nmodalities to VO2max. However, Denadai et al. [13] did not ﬁnd improvements in VO2max after\naerobic HIIT in well-trained runners, indicating that short-interval HIIT at very high intensity might\nbe necessary to produce any signiﬁcant eﬀect in well-trained athletes. On the other hand, aerobic HIIT\nwas found to improve the velocity associated with VO2max (vVO2max; [11,14,15]), thereby implicating\nimprovements in running performance even without the presence of higher VO2max values. Running\neconomy (RE), or the energetic cost of running at a given running speed [20], was found to improve\nafter aerobic HIIT [13,15,19] and SIT-based programs [10,17]. The lack of RE improvement after a short\nHIIT intervention [12] suggests that improvements in RE are not only related to the type of HIIT used\nbut also to the total volume completed. In this regard, RE improvements were related to improvements\nof muscle oxidative capacity, as suggested by Denadai et al. [13]. Interestingly, studies comparing\ntraining programs with HIIT and CT showed greater improvements in VO2max [18] and vVO2max [19]\nwhen using HIIT, and in RE when using CT [19].\nWell-structured intervention studies utilizing other types of running methods are", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "a marathon training period\nin recreational runners, which may be indicative of an adaptation to reduce oxygen consumption at\nsubmaximal intensities [32]. Given that ST is expected to be related to some hypertrophic response [33],\nST could impair mar"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0003_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f8c415457666764b", "text_span_hash": "f8c415457666764b", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "onse [33],\nST could impair marathon performance, despite the fact that the hypertrophic eﬀect is attenuated\nwhen both ST and endurance training are concurrently performed [34]. In any case, Blagrove and\ncolleagues [21] suggested the use of free-weigh", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "runners. However, a combination of several methods might be the best option to improve adaptation\nto endurance running training.\nAerobic HIIT [11,12,14,15,19] and short-interval HIIT [9,11,12,16] were deemed as eﬀective exercise\nmodalities to VO2max. However, Denadai et al. [13] did not ﬁnd improvements in VO2max after\naerobic HIIT in well-trained runners, indicating that short-interval HIIT at very high intensity might\nbe necessary to produce any signiﬁcant eﬀect in well-trained athletes. On the other hand, aerobic HIIT\nwas found to improve the velocity associated with VO2max (vVO2max; [11,14,15]), thereby implicating\nimprovements in running performance even without the presence of higher VO2max values. Running\neconomy (RE), or the energetic cost of running at a given running speed [20], was found to improve\nafter aerobic HIIT [13,15,19] and SIT-based programs [10,17]. The lack of RE improvement after a short\nHIIT intervention [12] suggests that improvements in RE are not only related to the type of HIIT used\nbut also to the total volume completed. In this regard, RE improvements were related to improvements\nof muscle oxidative capacity, as suggested by Denadai et al. [13]. Interestingly, studies comparing\ntraining programs with HIIT and CT showed greater improvements in VO2max [18] and vVO2max [19]\nwhen using HIIT, and in RE when using CT [19].\nWell-structured intervention studies utilizing other types of running methods are", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "onse [33],\nST could impair marathon performance, despite the fact that the hypertrophic eﬀect is attenuated\nwhen both ST and endurance training are concurrently performed [34]. In any case, Blagrove and\ncolleagues [21] suggested the use of free-weigh"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0003_c0016", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e4ca3101fc59b742", "text_span_hash": "e4ca3101fc59b742", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 16, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "uggested the use of free-weight multi-joint exercises to improve running performance.\nAlthough there is a lack of knowledge regarding the optimal volume and intensity of ST for endurance\nrunning performance [21], the inclusion of ST in the training r", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "runners. However, a combination of several methods might be the best option to improve adaptation\nto endurance running training.\nAerobic HIIT [11,12,14,15,19] and short-interval HIIT [9,11,12,16] were deemed as eﬀective exercise\nmodalities to VO2max. However, Denadai et al. [13] did not ﬁnd improvements in VO2max after\naerobic HIIT in well-trained runners, indicating that short-interval HIIT at very high intensity might\nbe necessary to produce any signiﬁcant eﬀect in well-trained athletes. On the other hand, aerobic HIIT\nwas found to improve the velocity associated with VO2max (vVO2max; [11,14,15]), thereby implicating\nimprovements in running performance even without the presence of higher VO2max values. Running\neconomy (RE), or the energetic cost of running at a given running speed [20], was found to improve\nafter aerobic HIIT [13,15,19] and SIT-based programs [10,17]. The lack of RE improvement after a short\nHIIT intervention [12] suggests that improvements in RE are not only related to the type of HIIT used\nbut also to the total volume completed. In this regard, RE improvements were related to improvements\nof muscle oxidative capacity, as suggested by Denadai et al. [13]. Interestingly, studies comparing\ntraining programs with HIIT and CT showed greater improvements in VO2max [18] and vVO2max [19]\nwhen using HIIT, and in RE when using CT [19].\nWell-structured intervention studies utilizing other types of running methods are", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "uggested the use of free-weight multi-joint exercises to improve running performance.\nAlthough there is a lack of knowledge regarding the optimal volume and intensity of ST for endurance\nrunning performance [21], the inclusion of ST in the training r"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0003_c0017", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3c344d7dcb5edddf", "text_span_hash": "3c344d7dcb5edddf", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 17, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "lusion of ST in the training regime of recreational runners might be\nrecommended, even for marathoners. However, ST should be always performed with caution, while\nlooking for the minimum dose to allow signiﬁcant neuromuscular adaptation.\n2.3. Trainin", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "runners. However, a combination of several methods might be the best option to improve adaptation\nto endurance running training.\nAerobic HIIT [11,12,14,15,19] and short-interval HIIT [9,11,12,16] were deemed as eﬀective exercise\nmodalities to VO2max. However, Denadai et al. [13] did not ﬁnd improvements in VO2max after\naerobic HIIT in well-trained runners, indicating that short-interval HIIT at very high intensity might\nbe necessary to produce any signiﬁcant eﬀect in well-trained athletes. On the other hand, aerobic HIIT\nwas found to improve the velocity associated with VO2max (vVO2max; [11,14,15]), thereby implicating\nimprovements in running performance even without the presence of higher VO2max values. Running\neconomy (RE), or the energetic cost of running at a given running speed [20], was found to improve\nafter aerobic HIIT [13,15,19] and SIT-based programs [10,17]. The lack of RE improvement after a short\nHIIT intervention [12] suggests that improvements in RE are not only related to the type of HIIT used\nbut also to the total volume completed. In this regard, RE improvements were related to improvements\nof muscle oxidative capacity, as suggested by Denadai et al. [13]. Interestingly, studies comparing\ntraining programs with HIIT and CT showed greater improvements in VO2max [18] and vVO2max [19]\nwhen using HIIT, and in RE when using CT [19].\nWell-structured intervention studies utilizing other types of running methods are", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "lusion of ST in the training regime of recreational runners might be\nrecommended, even for marathoners. However, ST should be always performed with caution, while\nlooking for the minimum dose to allow signiﬁcant neuromuscular adaptation.\n2.3. Trainin"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0003_c0018", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5aa121583ba5e829", "text_span_hash": "5aa121583ba5e829", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 18, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "cular adaptation.\n2.3. Training Intensity Distribution\nAccording to Seiler and Kjerland, three intensity zones can be delimited by capillary blood\nlactate responses to steady state continuous running: Zone 1, the low lactate metabolic phase; Zone\n2,", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "runners. However, a combination of several methods might be the best option to improve adaptation\nto endurance running training.\nAerobic HIIT [11,12,14,15,19] and short-interval HIIT [9,11,12,16] were deemed as eﬀective exercise\nmodalities to VO2max. However, Denadai et al. [13] did not ﬁnd improvements in VO2max after\naerobic HIIT in well-trained runners, indicating that short-interval HIIT at very high intensity might\nbe necessary to produce any signiﬁcant eﬀect in well-trained athletes. On the other hand, aerobic HIIT\nwas found to improve the velocity associated with VO2max (vVO2max; [11,14,15]), thereby implicating\nimprovements in running performance even without the presence of higher VO2max values. Running\neconomy (RE), or the energetic cost of running at a given running speed [20], was found to improve\nafter aerobic HIIT [13,15,19] and SIT-based programs [10,17]. The lack of RE improvement after a short\nHIIT intervention [12] suggests that improvements in RE are not only related to the type of HIIT used\nbut also to the total volume completed. In this regard, RE improvements were related to improvements\nof muscle oxidative capacity, as suggested by Denadai et al. [13]. Interestingly, studies comparing\ntraining programs with HIIT and CT showed greater improvements in VO2max [18] and vVO2max [19]\nwhen using HIIT, and in RE when using CT [19].\nWell-structured intervention studies utilizing other types of running methods are", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "cular adaptation.\n2.3. Training Intensity Distribution\nAccording to Seiler and Kjerland, three intensity zones can be delimited by capillary blood\nlactate responses to steady state continuous running: Zone 1, the low lactate metabolic phase; Zone\n2,"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0003_c0019", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4d11224f9345707c", "text_span_hash": "4d11224f9345707c", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 19, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "tate metabolic phase; Zone\n2, the lactate accommodation phase, where blood lactate concentration is higher but production and\nremoval rates are in equilibrium; and Zone 3, the lactate accumulation uneven phase, where blood\nlactate production exceeds", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "runners. However, a combination of several methods might be the best option to improve adaptation\nto endurance running training.\nAerobic HIIT [11,12,14,15,19] and short-interval HIIT [9,11,12,16] were deemed as eﬀective exercise\nmodalities to VO2max. However, Denadai et al. [13] did not ﬁnd improvements in VO2max after\naerobic HIIT in well-trained runners, indicating that short-interval HIIT at very high intensity might\nbe necessary to produce any signiﬁcant eﬀect in well-trained athletes. On the other hand, aerobic HIIT\nwas found to improve the velocity associated with VO2max (vVO2max; [11,14,15]), thereby implicating\nimprovements in running performance even without the presence of higher VO2max values. Running\neconomy (RE), or the energetic cost of running at a given running speed [20], was found to improve\nafter aerobic HIIT [13,15,19] and SIT-based programs [10,17]. The lack of RE improvement after a short\nHIIT intervention [12] suggests that improvements in RE are not only related to the type of HIIT used\nbut also to the total volume completed. In this regard, RE improvements were related to improvements\nof muscle oxidative capacity, as suggested by Denadai et al. [13]. Interestingly, studies comparing\ntraining programs with HIIT and CT showed greater improvements in VO2max [18] and vVO2max [19]\nwhen using HIIT, and in RE when using CT [19].\nWell-structured intervention studies utilizing other types of running methods are", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "tate metabolic phase; Zone\n2, the lactate accommodation phase, where blood lactate concentration is higher but production and\nremoval rates are in equilibrium; and Zone 3, the lactate accumulation uneven phase, where blood\nlactate production exceeds"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0003_c0020", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1a9d03603554a57b", "text_span_hash": "1a9d03603554a57b", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 20, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "od\nlactate production exceeds the maximum clearance rate [35]. Polarized training intensity distribution\n(TID) [35–37] is conducted with a signiﬁcant percentage of time in Zone 1 (75%–80%) and Zone", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "runners. However, a combination of several methods might be the best option to improve adaptation\nto endurance running training.\nAerobic HIIT [11,12,14,15,19] and short-interval HIIT [9,11,12,16] were deemed as eﬀective exercise\nmodalities to VO2max. However, Denadai et al. [13] did not ﬁnd improvements in VO2max after\naerobic HIIT in well-trained runners, indicating that short-interval HIIT at very high intensity might\nbe necessary to produce any signiﬁcant eﬀect in well-trained athletes. On the other hand, aerobic HIIT\nwas found to improve the velocity associated with VO2max (vVO2max; [11,14,15]), thereby implicating\nimprovements in running performance even without the presence of higher VO2max values. Running\neconomy (RE), or the energetic cost of running at a given running speed [20], was found to improve\nafter aerobic HIIT [13,15,19] and SIT-based programs [10,17]. The lack of RE improvement after a short\nHIIT intervention [12] suggests that improvements in RE are not only related to the type of HIIT used\nbut also to the total volume completed. In this regard, RE improvements were related to improvements\nof muscle oxidative capacity, as suggested by Denadai et al. [13]. Interestingly, studies comparing\ntraining programs with HIIT and CT showed greater improvements in VO2max [18] and vVO2max [19]\nwhen using HIIT, and in RE when using CT [19].\nWell-structured intervention studies utilizing other types of running methods are", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "od\nlactate production exceeds the maximum clearance rate [35]. Polarized training intensity distribution\n(TID) [35–37] is conducted with a signiﬁcant percentage of time in Zone 1 (75%–80%) and Zone"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0004_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0abc6d40de612d2a", "text_span_hash": "0abc6d40de612d2a", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "proposed the traditional “pyramidal” TID, in which most of the training time is\nconducted in Zone 1 (70%–80%), with the remaining 20%–30% between Zones 2 and 3 [39]. Nonetheless,\nthe literature regarding TID in recreational runners is scarce, theref", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "3 (15%–20%) but with little or no time in Zone 2. TID was suggested to be more eﬃcient than\nother intensity distributions because it is linked to the physical activity pattern of our ancestors [38].\nIn contrast, Holmberg proposed the traditional “pyramidal” TID, in which most of the training time is\nconducted in Zone 1 (70%–80%), with the remaining 20%–30% between Zones 2 and 3 [39]. Nonetheless,\nthe literature regarding TID in recreational runners is scarce, therefore it is diﬃcult to ascertain what\nmodels might be more beneﬁcial for these runners. Muñoz et al. found that, despite the reduced\nvolume conducted by recreational runners, a polarized model improved their 10 km performance\nto a greater extent than a threshold model, in which most of the training volume was conducted in\nZone 2 [40]. More recently, it was observed that mesocycles with diﬀerent TIDs (polarized vs. HIIT\nvs. low intensity) resulted in similar improvements and physiological adaptations [41]. However, the\nlow-intensity group, in which most of the training was conducted in Zone 1, induced improvement\nin RE, which was not observed in the other groups [41]. Finally, another recent study found that\npolarized TID improved both RE and performance in ultra-endurance runners [42]. The pyramidal\nand polarized models might be the most recommended forms of training intensity distribution for\nrecreational runners, with diﬀerent considerations depending on competitive", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "proposed the traditional “pyramidal” TID, in which most of the training time is\nconducted in Zone 1 (70%–80%), with the remaining 20%–30% between Zones 2 and 3 [39]. Nonetheless,\nthe literature regarding TID in recreational runners is scarce, theref"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0004_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "beda768173d81deb", "text_span_hash": "beda768173d81deb", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "onal runners is scarce, therefore it is diﬃcult to ascertain what\nmodels might be more beneﬁcial for these runners. Muñoz et al. found that, despite the reduced\nvolume conducted by recreational runners, a polarized model improved their 10 km performa", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "3 (15%–20%) but with little or no time in Zone 2. TID was suggested to be more eﬃcient than\nother intensity distributions because it is linked to the physical activity pattern of our ancestors [38].\nIn contrast, Holmberg proposed the traditional “pyramidal” TID, in which most of the training time is\nconducted in Zone 1 (70%–80%), with the remaining 20%–30% between Zones 2 and 3 [39]. Nonetheless,\nthe literature regarding TID in recreational runners is scarce, therefore it is diﬃcult to ascertain what\nmodels might be more beneﬁcial for these runners. Muñoz et al. found that, despite the reduced\nvolume conducted by recreational runners, a polarized model improved their 10 km performance\nto a greater extent than a threshold model, in which most of the training volume was conducted in\nZone 2 [40]. More recently, it was observed that mesocycles with diﬀerent TIDs (polarized vs. HIIT\nvs. low intensity) resulted in similar improvements and physiological adaptations [41]. However, the\nlow-intensity group, in which most of the training was conducted in Zone 1, induced improvement\nin RE, which was not observed in the other groups [41]. Finally, another recent study found that\npolarized TID improved both RE and performance in ultra-endurance runners [42]. The pyramidal\nand polarized models might be the most recommended forms of training intensity distribution for\nrecreational runners, with diﬀerent considerations depending on competitive", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "onal runners is scarce, therefore it is diﬃcult to ascertain what\nmodels might be more beneﬁcial for these runners. Muñoz et al. found that, despite the reduced\nvolume conducted by recreational runners, a polarized model improved their 10 km performa"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0004_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "fbc06717cacb61e2", "text_span_hash": "fbc06717cacb61e2", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "improved their 10 km performance\nto a greater extent than a threshold model, in which most of the training volume was conducted in\nZone 2 [40]. More recently, it was observed that mesocycles with diﬀerent TIDs (polarized vs. HIIT\nvs. low intensity)", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "3 (15%–20%) but with little or no time in Zone 2. TID was suggested to be more eﬃcient than\nother intensity distributions because it is linked to the physical activity pattern of our ancestors [38].\nIn contrast, Holmberg proposed the traditional “pyramidal” TID, in which most of the training time is\nconducted in Zone 1 (70%–80%), with the remaining 20%–30% between Zones 2 and 3 [39]. Nonetheless,\nthe literature regarding TID in recreational runners is scarce, therefore it is diﬃcult to ascertain what\nmodels might be more beneﬁcial for these runners. Muñoz et al. found that, despite the reduced\nvolume conducted by recreational runners, a polarized model improved their 10 km performance\nto a greater extent than a threshold model, in which most of the training volume was conducted in\nZone 2 [40]. More recently, it was observed that mesocycles with diﬀerent TIDs (polarized vs. HIIT\nvs. low intensity) resulted in similar improvements and physiological adaptations [41]. However, the\nlow-intensity group, in which most of the training was conducted in Zone 1, induced improvement\nin RE, which was not observed in the other groups [41]. Finally, another recent study found that\npolarized TID improved both RE and performance in ultra-endurance runners [42]. The pyramidal\nand polarized models might be the most recommended forms of training intensity distribution for\nrecreational runners, with diﬀerent considerations depending on competitive", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "improved their 10 km performance\nto a greater extent than a threshold model, in which most of the training volume was conducted in\nZone 2 [40]. More recently, it was observed that mesocycles with diﬀerent TIDs (polarized vs. HIIT\nvs. low intensity)"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0004_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f2b9e93a5626d5a1", "text_span_hash": "f2b9e93a5626d5a1", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "d vs. HIIT\nvs. low intensity) resulted in similar improvements and physiological adaptations [41]. However, the\nlow-intensity group, in which most of the training was conducted in Zone 1, induced improvement\nin RE, which was not observed in the other", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "3 (15%–20%) but with little or no time in Zone 2. TID was suggested to be more eﬃcient than\nother intensity distributions because it is linked to the physical activity pattern of our ancestors [38].\nIn contrast, Holmberg proposed the traditional “pyramidal” TID, in which most of the training time is\nconducted in Zone 1 (70%–80%), with the remaining 20%–30% between Zones 2 and 3 [39]. Nonetheless,\nthe literature regarding TID in recreational runners is scarce, therefore it is diﬃcult to ascertain what\nmodels might be more beneﬁcial for these runners. Muñoz et al. found that, despite the reduced\nvolume conducted by recreational runners, a polarized model improved their 10 km performance\nto a greater extent than a threshold model, in which most of the training volume was conducted in\nZone 2 [40]. More recently, it was observed that mesocycles with diﬀerent TIDs (polarized vs. HIIT\nvs. low intensity) resulted in similar improvements and physiological adaptations [41]. However, the\nlow-intensity group, in which most of the training was conducted in Zone 1, induced improvement\nin RE, which was not observed in the other groups [41]. Finally, another recent study found that\npolarized TID improved both RE and performance in ultra-endurance runners [42]. The pyramidal\nand polarized models might be the most recommended forms of training intensity distribution for\nrecreational runners, with diﬀerent considerations depending on competitive", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "d vs. HIIT\nvs. low intensity) resulted in similar improvements and physiological adaptations [41]. However, the\nlow-intensity group, in which most of the training was conducted in Zone 1, induced improvement\nin RE, which was not observed in the other"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0004_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "6e455e5b8014c1e9", "text_span_hash": "6e455e5b8014c1e9", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "was not observed in the other groups [41]. Finally, another recent study found that\npolarized TID improved both RE and performance in ultra-endurance runners [42]. The pyramidal\nand polarized models might be the most recommended forms of training in", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "3 (15%–20%) but with little or no time in Zone 2. TID was suggested to be more eﬃcient than\nother intensity distributions because it is linked to the physical activity pattern of our ancestors [38].\nIn contrast, Holmberg proposed the traditional “pyramidal” TID, in which most of the training time is\nconducted in Zone 1 (70%–80%), with the remaining 20%–30% between Zones 2 and 3 [39]. Nonetheless,\nthe literature regarding TID in recreational runners is scarce, therefore it is diﬃcult to ascertain what\nmodels might be more beneﬁcial for these runners. Muñoz et al. found that, despite the reduced\nvolume conducted by recreational runners, a polarized model improved their 10 km performance\nto a greater extent than a threshold model, in which most of the training volume was conducted in\nZone 2 [40]. More recently, it was observed that mesocycles with diﬀerent TIDs (polarized vs. HIIT\nvs. low intensity) resulted in similar improvements and physiological adaptations [41]. However, the\nlow-intensity group, in which most of the training was conducted in Zone 1, induced improvement\nin RE, which was not observed in the other groups [41]. Finally, another recent study found that\npolarized TID improved both RE and performance in ultra-endurance runners [42]. The pyramidal\nand polarized models might be the most recommended forms of training intensity distribution for\nrecreational runners, with diﬀerent considerations depending on competitive", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "was not observed in the other groups [41]. Finally, another recent study found that\npolarized TID improved both RE and performance in ultra-endurance runners [42]. The pyramidal\nand polarized models might be the most recommended forms of training in"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0004_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "08b137a74d16c347", "text_span_hash": "08b137a74d16c347", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "commended forms of training intensity distribution for\nrecreational runners, with diﬀerent considerations depending on competitive distance, time available\nto train, and time of the season. Meanwhile, “high volume” or “high intensity” approaches seem", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "3 (15%–20%) but with little or no time in Zone 2. TID was suggested to be more eﬃcient than\nother intensity distributions because it is linked to the physical activity pattern of our ancestors [38].\nIn contrast, Holmberg proposed the traditional “pyramidal” TID, in which most of the training time is\nconducted in Zone 1 (70%–80%), with the remaining 20%–30% between Zones 2 and 3 [39]. Nonetheless,\nthe literature regarding TID in recreational runners is scarce, therefore it is diﬃcult to ascertain what\nmodels might be more beneﬁcial for these runners. Muñoz et al. found that, despite the reduced\nvolume conducted by recreational runners, a polarized model improved their 10 km performance\nto a greater extent than a threshold model, in which most of the training volume was conducted in\nZone 2 [40]. More recently, it was observed that mesocycles with diﬀerent TIDs (polarized vs. HIIT\nvs. low intensity) resulted in similar improvements and physiological adaptations [41]. However, the\nlow-intensity group, in which most of the training was conducted in Zone 1, induced improvement\nin RE, which was not observed in the other groups [41]. Finally, another recent study found that\npolarized TID improved both RE and performance in ultra-endurance runners [42]. The pyramidal\nand polarized models might be the most recommended forms of training intensity distribution for\nrecreational runners, with diﬀerent considerations depending on competitive", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "commended forms of training intensity distribution for\nrecreational runners, with diﬀerent considerations depending on competitive distance, time available\nto train, and time of the season. Meanwhile, “high volume” or “high intensity” approaches seem"}
