other recent study found that\npolarized TID improved both RE and performance in ultra-endurance runners [42]. The pyramidal\nand polarized models might be the most recommended forms of training intensity distribution for\nrecreational runners, with diﬀerent considerations depending on competitive", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "high and the volume is low in the initial stages of training with the intensity subsequently\ndecreasing and the volume increasing until the end of the training cycle. Research on training\nperiodization in recreational runners is very limited. Bradbu"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0004_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "d04e35de7dcc5bf9", "text_span_hash": "d04e35de7dcc5bf9", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "unners is very limited. Bradbury et al. found that linear and reverse\nlinear periodization generated greater improvements in endurance performance, RE, and VO2max in\nrecreational runners than nonperiodized progression of training load [44]. Further,", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "3 (15%–20%) but with little or no time in Zone 2. TID was suggested to be more eﬃcient than\nother intensity distributions because it is linked to the physical activity pattern of our ancestors [38].\nIn contrast, Holmberg proposed the traditional “pyramidal” TID, in which most of the training time is\nconducted in Zone 1 (70%–80%), with the remaining 20%–30% between Zones 2 and 3 [39]. Nonetheless,\nthe literature regarding TID in recreational runners is scarce, therefore it is diﬃcult to ascertain what\nmodels might be more beneﬁcial for these runners. Muñoz et al. found that, despite the reduced\nvolume conducted by recreational runners, a polarized model improved their 10 km performance\nto a greater extent than a threshold model, in which most of the training volume was conducted in\nZone 2 [40]. More recently, it was observed that mesocycles with diﬀerent TIDs (polarized vs. HIIT\nvs. low intensity) resulted in similar improvements and physiological adaptations [41]. However, the\nlow-intensity group, in which most of the training was conducted in Zone 1, induced improvement\nin RE, which was not observed in the other groups [41]. Finally, another recent study found that\npolarized TID improved both RE and performance in ultra-endurance runners [42]. The pyramidal\nand polarized models might be the most recommended forms of training intensity distribution for\nrecreational runners, with diﬀerent considerations depending on competitive", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "unners is very limited. Bradbury et al. found that linear and reverse\nlinear periodization generated greater improvements in endurance performance, RE, and VO2max in\nrecreational runners than nonperiodized progression of training load [44]. Further,"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0004_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "da6b2faac0d191df", "text_span_hash": "da6b2faac0d191df", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "training load [44]. Further, when considering\nprevious studies including the use of HIIT and SIT in recreational runners, it may be suggested that,\nduring the training period, recreational runners should adopt a linear periodization when using HIIT", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "3 (15%–20%) but with little or no time in Zone 2. TID was suggested to be more eﬃcient than\nother intensity distributions because it is linked to the physical activity pattern of our ancestors [38].\nIn contrast, Holmberg proposed the traditional “pyramidal” TID, in which most of the training time is\nconducted in Zone 1 (70%–80%), with the remaining 20%–30% between Zones 2 and 3 [39]. Nonetheless,\nthe literature regarding TID in recreational runners is scarce, therefore it is diﬃcult to ascertain what\nmodels might be more beneﬁcial for these runners. Muñoz et al. found that, despite the reduced\nvolume conducted by recreational runners, a polarized model improved their 10 km performance\nto a greater extent than a threshold model, in which most of the training volume was conducted in\nZone 2 [40]. More recently, it was observed that mesocycles with diﬀerent TIDs (polarized vs. HIIT\nvs. low intensity) resulted in similar improvements and physiological adaptations [41]. However, the\nlow-intensity group, in which most of the training was conducted in Zone 1, induced improvement\nin RE, which was not observed in the other groups [41]. Finally, another recent study found that\npolarized TID improved both RE and performance in ultra-endurance runners [42]. The pyramidal\nand polarized models might be the most recommended forms of training intensity distribution for\nrecreational runners, with diﬀerent considerations depending on competitive", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "training load [44]. Further, when considering\nprevious studies including the use of HIIT and SIT in recreational runners, it may be suggested that,\nduring the training period, recreational runners should adopt a linear periodization when using HIIT"}
