{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0004_c0017", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "13ef2fc5df0ac641", "text_span_hash": "13ef2fc5df0ac641", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 17, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "in marathons and longer events would probably beneﬁt from reverse periodization.\nNovice, less experienced runners would also beneﬁt from reverse periodization for 21 km or even\n10 km events, in order to be exposed more gradually to peak volume. Furt", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "3 (15%–20%) but with little or no time in Zone 2. TID was suggested to be more eﬃcient than\nother intensity distributions because it is linked to the physical activity pattern of our ancestors [38].\nIn contrast, Holmberg proposed the traditional “pyramidal” TID, in which most of the training time is\nconducted in Zone 1 (70%–80%), with the remaining 20%–30% between Zones 2 and 3 [39]. Nonetheless,\nthe literature regarding TID in recreational runners is scarce, therefore it is diﬃcult to ascertain what\nmodels might be more beneﬁcial for these runners. Muñoz et al. found that, despite the reduced\nvolume conducted by recreational runners, a polarized model improved their 10 km performance\nto a greater extent than a threshold model, in which most of the training volume was conducted in\nZone 2 [40]. More recently, it was observed that mesocycles with diﬀerent TIDs (polarized vs. HIIT\nvs. low intensity) resulted in similar improvements and physiological adaptations [41]. However, the\nlow-intensity group, in which most of the training was conducted in Zone 1, induced improvement\nin RE, which was not observed in the other groups [41]. Finally, another recent study found that\npolarized TID improved both RE and performance in ultra-endurance runners [42]. The pyramidal\nand polarized models might be the most recommended forms of training intensity distribution for\nrecreational runners, with diﬀerent considerations depending on competitive", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "in marathons and longer events would probably beneﬁt from reverse periodization.\nNovice, less experienced runners would also beneﬁt from reverse periodization for 21 km or even\n10 km events, in order to be exposed more gradually to peak volume. Furt"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0004_c0018", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "400c881516b4f64a", "text_span_hash": "400c881516b4f64a", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 18, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "gradually to peak volume. Further studies are needed to\nappropriately test the validity of these suggestions.\nAll these ﬁndings suggest that both HIIT and ST should be implemented in the training regime\nof recreational runners in order to improve per", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "3 (15%–20%) but with little or no time in Zone 2. TID was suggested to be more eﬃcient than\nother intensity distributions because it is linked to the physical activity pattern of our ancestors [38].\nIn contrast, Holmberg proposed the traditional “pyramidal” TID, in which most of the training time is\nconducted in Zone 1 (70%–80%), with the remaining 20%–30% between Zones 2 and 3 [39]. Nonetheless,\nthe literature regarding TID in recreational runners is scarce, therefore it is diﬃcult to ascertain what\nmodels might be more beneﬁcial for these runners. Muñoz et al. found that, despite the reduced\nvolume conducted by recreational runners, a polarized model improved their 10 km performance\nto a greater extent than a threshold model, in which most of the training volume was conducted in\nZone 2 [40]. More recently, it was observed that mesocycles with diﬀerent TIDs (polarized vs. HIIT\nvs. low intensity) resulted in similar improvements and physiological adaptations [41]. However, the\nlow-intensity group, in which most of the training was conducted in Zone 1, induced improvement\nin RE, which was not observed in the other groups [41]. Finally, another recent study found that\npolarized TID improved both RE and performance in ultra-endurance runners [42]. The pyramidal\nand polarized models might be the most recommended forms of training intensity distribution for\nrecreational runners, with diﬀerent considerations depending on competitive", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "gradually to peak volume. Further studies are needed to\nappropriately test the validity of these suggestions.\nAll these ﬁndings suggest that both HIIT and ST should be implemented in the training regime\nof recreational runners in order to improve per"}
