{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0005_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c5bb3b6f160e828e", "text_span_hash": "c5bb3b6f160e828e", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "t accessible to all runners in\nterms of cost or availability. Thus, other low-cost and simple evaluations could be used, especially\nin the ﬁeld. From these tests, incremental ﬁeld-testing for identiﬁcation of maximum aerobic speed\n(MAS) could be cons", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "understanding of the underpinning mechanisms involved in the responses of recreational runners to\ntraining. In this regard, the time completed in Z1 could be more important than intensity polarization\nper se, and both testing sessions and competitions should be also included in TID calculations [46].\nIn addition, the minimum running volume required to improve running performance in recreational\nrunners [47], including the interactions between TID, running, and ST methods [48] and the signiﬁcant\ninﬂuence of incidental physical activity on running training adaptations [49] still require identiﬁcation.\n3. Training Monitoring\nRecreational runners can monitor their progress through several traditional and modern parameters\nobtained from training and testing sessions, or competitions. Namely, traditional laboratory evaluations\naiming to identify changes in gold standard physiological measures, such as VO2max, anaerobic\nthreshold (AT), and RE, can be monitored in recreational runners alongside professional runners [50].\nHowever, the assessment of these maximal and submaximal variables is not accessible to all runners in\nterms of cost or availability. Thus, other low-cost and simple evaluations could be used, especially\nin the ﬁeld. From these tests, incremental ﬁeld-testing for identiﬁcation of maximum aerobic speed\n(MAS) could be considered the most practical, as MAS integrates both VO2max and RE into a single\nparameter and was used", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "t accessible to all runners in\nterms of cost or availability. Thus, other low-cost and simple evaluations could be used, especially\nin the ﬁeld. From these tests, incremental ﬁeld-testing for identiﬁcation of maximum aerobic speed\n(MAS) could be cons"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0005_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "483833d57367f155", "text_span_hash": "483833d57367f155", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "obic speed\n(MAS) could be considered the most practical, as MAS integrates both VO2max and RE into a single\nparameter and was used regarding endurance running performance from middle- to long-distance\nraces [51]. In addition, incremental-intensity ﬁe", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "understanding of the underpinning mechanisms involved in the responses of recreational runners to\ntraining. In this regard, the time completed in Z1 could be more important than intensity polarization\nper se, and both testing sessions and competitions should be also included in TID calculations [46].\nIn addition, the minimum running volume required to improve running performance in recreational\nrunners [47], including the interactions between TID, running, and ST methods [48] and the signiﬁcant\ninﬂuence of incidental physical activity on running training adaptations [49] still require identiﬁcation.\n3. Training Monitoring\nRecreational runners can monitor their progress through several traditional and modern parameters\nobtained from training and testing sessions, or competitions. Namely, traditional laboratory evaluations\naiming to identify changes in gold standard physiological measures, such as VO2max, anaerobic\nthreshold (AT), and RE, can be monitored in recreational runners alongside professional runners [50].\nHowever, the assessment of these maximal and submaximal variables is not accessible to all runners in\nterms of cost or availability. Thus, other low-cost and simple evaluations could be used, especially\nin the ﬁeld. From these tests, incremental ﬁeld-testing for identiﬁcation of maximum aerobic speed\n(MAS) could be considered the most practical, as MAS integrates both VO2max and RE into a single\nparameter and was used", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "obic speed\n(MAS) could be considered the most practical, as MAS integrates both VO2max and RE into a single\nparameter and was used regarding endurance running performance from middle- to long-distance\nraces [51]. In addition, incremental-intensity ﬁe"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0005_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "a4e0208cd4463fdf", "text_span_hash": "a4e0208cd4463fdf", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "tion, incremental-intensity ﬁeld-testing could serve to identify the true maximum\nheart rate (HRmax) [52] and the AT based on HR measures [53], thereby allowing both monitoring of\nrunning capacity and training prescription based on these parameters.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "understanding of the underpinning mechanisms involved in the responses of recreational runners to\ntraining. In this regard, the time completed in Z1 could be more important than intensity polarization\nper se, and both testing sessions and competitions should be also included in TID calculations [46].\nIn addition, the minimum running volume required to improve running performance in recreational\nrunners [47], including the interactions between TID, running, and ST methods [48] and the signiﬁcant\ninﬂuence of incidental physical activity on running training adaptations [49] still require identiﬁcation.\n3. Training Monitoring\nRecreational runners can monitor their progress through several traditional and modern parameters\nobtained from training and testing sessions, or competitions. Namely, traditional laboratory evaluations\naiming to identify changes in gold standard physiological measures, such as VO2max, anaerobic\nthreshold (AT), and RE, can be monitored in recreational runners alongside professional runners [50].\nHowever, the assessment of these maximal and submaximal variables is not accessible to all runners in\nterms of cost or availability. Thus, other low-cost and simple evaluations could be used, especially\nin the ﬁeld. From these tests, incremental ﬁeld-testing for identiﬁcation of maximum aerobic speed\n(MAS) could be considered the most practical, as MAS integrates both VO2max and RE into a single\nparameter and was used", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "tion, incremental-intensity ﬁeld-testing could serve to identify the true maximum\nheart rate (HRmax) [52] and the AT based on HR measures [53], thereby allowing both monitoring of\nrunning capacity and training prescription based on these parameters."}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0005_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "a0b05022e3633994", "text_span_hash": "a0b05022e3633994", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "on based on these parameters. In this regard, the ﬁve minute\nrunning test (T5) could be recommended as a simple and valid alternative for MAS determination in\nthe ﬁeld [54], although MAS identiﬁed using T5 presents greater error and lower reproducibi", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "understanding of the underpinning mechanisms involved in the responses of recreational runners to\ntraining. In this regard, the time completed in Z1 could be more important than intensity polarization\nper se, and both testing sessions and competitions should be also included in TID calculations [46].\nIn addition, the minimum running volume required to improve running performance in recreational\nrunners [47], including the interactions between TID, running, and ST methods [48] and the signiﬁcant\ninﬂuence of incidental physical activity on running training adaptations [49] still require identiﬁcation.\n3. Training Monitoring\nRecreational runners can monitor their progress through several traditional and modern parameters\nobtained from training and testing sessions, or competitions. Namely, traditional laboratory evaluations\naiming to identify changes in gold standard physiological measures, such as VO2max, anaerobic\nthreshold (AT), and RE, can be monitored in recreational runners alongside professional runners [50].\nHowever, the assessment of these maximal and submaximal variables is not accessible to all runners in\nterms of cost or availability. Thus, other low-cost and simple evaluations could be used, especially\nin the ﬁeld. From these tests, incremental ﬁeld-testing for identiﬁcation of maximum aerobic speed\n(MAS) could be considered the most practical, as MAS integrates both VO2max and RE into a single\nparameter and was used", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "on based on these parameters. In this regard, the ﬁve minute\nrunning test (T5) could be recommended as a simple and valid alternative for MAS determination in\nthe ﬁeld [54], although MAS identiﬁed using T5 presents greater error and lower reproducibi"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0005_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e5de88c47778d7e1", "text_span_hash": "e5de88c47778d7e1", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "e., the highest\nsustainable running speed that can be maintained without a continual rise in VO2) from a number\nof maximum time trials [56], the identiﬁcation of HR-running velocity associations at submaximal\nintensities [57], and AT determination wi", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "understanding of the underpinning mechanisms involved in the responses of recreational runners to\ntraining. In this regard, the time completed in Z1 could be more important than intensity polarization\nper se, and both testing sessions and competitions should be also included in TID calculations [46].\nIn addition, the minimum running volume required to improve running performance in recreational\nrunners [47], including the interactions between TID, running, and ST methods [48] and the signiﬁcant\ninﬂuence of incidental physical activity on running training adaptations [49] still require identiﬁcation.\n3. Training Monitoring\nRecreational runners can monitor their progress through several traditional and modern parameters\nobtained from training and testing sessions, or competitions. Namely, traditional laboratory evaluations\naiming to identify changes in gold standard physiological measures, such as VO2max, anaerobic\nthreshold (AT), and RE, can be monitored in recreational runners alongside professional runners [50].\nHowever, the assessment of these maximal and submaximal variables is not accessible to all runners in\nterms of cost or availability. Thus, other low-cost and simple evaluations could be used, especially\nin the ﬁeld. From these tests, incremental ﬁeld-testing for identiﬁcation of maximum aerobic speed\n(MAS) could be considered the most practical, as MAS integrates both VO2max and RE into a single\nparameter and was used", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "e., the highest\nsustainable running speed that can be maintained without a continual rise in VO2) from a number\nof maximum time trials [56], the identiﬁcation of HR-running velocity associations at submaximal\nintensities [57], and AT determination wi"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0005_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c2e66d38b005cb00", "text_span_hash": "c2e66d38b005cb00", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "[57], and AT determination with the changes produced in blood lactate concentration with\nintensity [58,59]. However, CS ideally requires maximal testing on diﬀerent days, therefore it does\nnot represent an appealing option for some recreational runn", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "understanding of the underpinning mechanisms involved in the responses of recreational runners to\ntraining. In this regard, the time completed in Z1 could be more important than intensity polarization\nper se, and both testing sessions and competitions should be also included in TID calculations [46].\nIn addition, the minimum running volume required to improve running performance in recreational\nrunners [47], including the interactions between TID, running, and ST methods [48] and the signiﬁcant\ninﬂuence of incidental physical activity on running training adaptations [49] still require identiﬁcation.\n3. Training Monitoring\nRecreational runners can monitor their progress through several traditional and modern parameters\nobtained from training and testing sessions, or competitions. Namely, traditional laboratory evaluations\naiming to identify changes in gold standard physiological measures, such as VO2max, anaerobic\nthreshold (AT), and RE, can be monitored in recreational runners alongside professional runners [50].\nHowever, the assessment of these maximal and submaximal variables is not accessible to all runners in\nterms of cost or availability. Thus, other low-cost and simple evaluations could be used, especially\nin the ﬁeld. From these tests, incremental ﬁeld-testing for identiﬁcation of maximum aerobic speed\n(MAS) could be considered the most practical, as MAS integrates both VO2max and RE into a single\nparameter and was used", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "[57], and AT determination with the changes produced in blood lactate concentration with\nintensity [58,59]. However, CS ideally requires maximal testing on diﬀerent days, therefore it does\nnot represent an appealing option for some recreational runn"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0005_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "25f3901d5cbfda31", "text_span_hash": "25f3901d5cbfda31", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ion for some recreational runners, while the blood lactate measures\nrequired for AT testing may also represent a barrier in some cases. In contrast, methods identifying\nthe relationship between submaximal constant velocity bouts and HR responses coul", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "understanding of the underpinning mechanisms involved in the responses of recreational runners to\ntraining. In this regard, the time completed in Z1 could be more important than intensity polarization\nper se, and both testing sessions and competitions should be also included in TID calculations [46].\nIn addition, the minimum running volume required to improve running performance in recreational\nrunners [47], including the interactions between TID, running, and ST methods [48] and the signiﬁcant\ninﬂuence of incidental physical activity on running training adaptations [49] still require identiﬁcation.\n3. Training Monitoring\nRecreational runners can monitor their progress through several traditional and modern parameters\nobtained from training and testing sessions, or competitions. Namely, traditional laboratory evaluations\naiming to identify changes in gold standard physiological measures, such as VO2max, anaerobic\nthreshold (AT), and RE, can be monitored in recreational runners alongside professional runners [50].\nHowever, the assessment of these maximal and submaximal variables is not accessible to all runners in\nterms of cost or availability. Thus, other low-cost and simple evaluations could be used, especially\nin the ﬁeld. From these tests, incremental ﬁeld-testing for identiﬁcation of maximum aerobic speed\n(MAS) could be considered the most practical, as MAS integrates both VO2max and RE into a single\nparameter and was used", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ion for some recreational runners, while the blood lactate measures\nrequired for AT testing may also represent a barrier in some cases. In contrast, methods identifying\nthe relationship between submaximal constant velocity bouts and HR responses coul"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0005_c0016", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "269e07650fc76885", "text_span_hash": "269e07650fc76885", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 16, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "r conditions.\nHowever, ﬁeld testing may present greater validity for training prescriptions when compared to\nlaboratory-based testing on a treadmill [63]. Therefore, runners and coaches should identify the\ntests that better suit the needs and possibi", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "understanding of the underpinning mechanisms involved in the responses of recreational runners to\ntraining. In this regard, the time completed in Z1 could be more important than intensity polarization\nper se, and both testing sessions and competitions should be also included in TID calculations [46].\nIn addition, the minimum running volume required to improve running performance in recreational\nrunners [47], including the interactions between TID, running, and ST methods [48] and the signiﬁcant\ninﬂuence of incidental physical activity on running training adaptations [49] still require identiﬁcation.\n3. Training Monitoring\nRecreational runners can monitor their progress through several traditional and modern parameters\nobtained from training and testing sessions, or competitions. Namely, traditional laboratory evaluations\naiming to identify changes in gold standard physiological measures, such as VO2max, anaerobic\nthreshold (AT), and RE, can be monitored in recreational runners alongside professional runners [50].\nHowever, the assessment of these maximal and submaximal variables is not accessible to all runners in\nterms of cost or availability. Thus, other low-cost and simple evaluations could be used, especially\nin the ﬁeld. From these tests, incremental ﬁeld-testing for identiﬁcation of maximum aerobic speed\n(MAS) could be considered the most practical, as MAS integrates both VO2max and RE into a single\nparameter and was used", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "r conditions.\nHowever, ﬁeld testing may present greater validity for training prescriptions when compared to\nlaboratory-based testing on a treadmill [63]. Therefore, runners and coaches should identify the\ntests that better suit the needs and possibi"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0005_c0017", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "afed1359c920ff84", "text_span_hash": "afed1359c920ff84", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 17, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ter suit the needs and possibilities for monitoring targeted physiological adaptations to\ntraining over the season for speciﬁc objectives. Furthermore, although the best performance test is to\ncompete in the same distance to compare performances and", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "understanding of the underpinning mechanisms involved in the responses of recreational runners to\ntraining. In this regard, the time completed in Z1 could be more important than intensity polarization\nper se, and both testing sessions and competitions should be also included in TID calculations [46].\nIn addition, the minimum running volume required to improve running performance in recreational\nrunners [47], including the interactions between TID, running, and ST methods [48] and the signiﬁcant\ninﬂuence of incidental physical activity on running training adaptations [49] still require identiﬁcation.\n3. Training Monitoring\nRecreational runners can monitor their progress through several traditional and modern parameters\nobtained from training and testing sessions, or competitions. Namely, traditional laboratory evaluations\naiming to identify changes in gold standard physiological measures, such as VO2max, anaerobic\nthreshold (AT), and RE, can be monitored in recreational runners alongside professional runners [50].\nHowever, the assessment of these maximal and submaximal variables is not accessible to all runners in\nterms of cost or availability. Thus, other low-cost and simple evaluations could be used, especially\nin the ﬁeld. From these tests, incremental ﬁeld-testing for identiﬁcation of maximum aerobic speed\n(MAS) could be considered the most practical, as MAS integrates both VO2max and RE into a single\nparameter and was used", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ter suit the needs and possibilities for monitoring targeted physiological adaptations to\ntraining over the season for speciﬁc objectives. Furthermore, although the best performance test is to\ncompete in the same distance to compare performances and"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0005_c0019", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "cddf5e20d575901d", "text_span_hash": "cddf5e20d575901d", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 19, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "iate diagnoses of competitive outcomes. Meanwhile,\nthese limitations should be also considered for daily quantiﬁcation of TID via methods based on HR or\nspeeds associated with physiological thresholds, which are not interchangeable. Alternatively, th", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "understanding of the underpinning mechanisms involved in the responses of recreational runners to\ntraining. In this regard, the time completed in Z1 could be more important than intensity polarization\nper se, and both testing sessions and competitions should be also included in TID calculations [46].\nIn addition, the minimum running volume required to improve running performance in recreational\nrunners [47], including the interactions between TID, running, and ST methods [48] and the signiﬁcant\ninﬂuence of incidental physical activity on running training adaptations [49] still require identiﬁcation.\n3. Training Monitoring\nRecreational runners can monitor their progress through several traditional and modern parameters\nobtained from training and testing sessions, or competitions. Namely, traditional laboratory evaluations\naiming to identify changes in gold standard physiological measures, such as VO2max, anaerobic\nthreshold (AT), and RE, can be monitored in recreational runners alongside professional runners [50].\nHowever, the assessment of these maximal and submaximal variables is not accessible to all runners in\nterms of cost or availability. Thus, other low-cost and simple evaluations could be used, especially\nin the ﬁeld. From these tests, incremental ﬁeld-testing for identiﬁcation of maximum aerobic speed\n(MAS) could be considered the most practical, as MAS integrates both VO2max and RE into a single\nparameter and was used", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "iate diagnoses of competitive outcomes. Meanwhile,\nthese limitations should be also considered for daily quantiﬁcation of TID via methods based on HR or\nspeeds associated with physiological thresholds, which are not interchangeable. Alternatively, th"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0005_c0020", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7eda466a4b3009d2", "text_span_hash": "7eda466a4b3009d2", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 20, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "rchangeable. Alternatively, the use\nof a percentage of goal race pace could be also recommended for TID monitoring [64].", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "understanding of the underpinning mechanisms involved in the responses of recreational runners to\ntraining. In this regard, the time completed in Z1 could be more important than intensity polarization\nper se, and both testing sessions and competitions should be also included in TID calculations [46].\nIn addition, the minimum running volume required to improve running performance in recreational\nrunners [47], including the interactions between TID, running, and ST methods [48] and the signiﬁcant\ninﬂuence of incidental physical activity on running training adaptations [49] still require identiﬁcation.\n3. Training Monitoring\nRecreational runners can monitor their progress through several traditional and modern parameters\nobtained from training and testing sessions, or competitions. Namely, traditional laboratory evaluations\naiming to identify changes in gold standard physiological measures, such as VO2max, anaerobic\nthreshold (AT), and RE, can be monitored in recreational runners alongside professional runners [50].\nHowever, the assessment of these maximal and submaximal variables is not accessible to all runners in\nterms of cost or availability. Thus, other low-cost and simple evaluations could be used, especially\nin the ﬁeld. From these tests, incremental ﬁeld-testing for identiﬁcation of maximum aerobic speed\n(MAS) could be considered the most practical, as MAS integrates both VO2max and RE into a single\nparameter and was used", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "rchangeable. Alternatively, the use\nof a percentage of goal race pace could be also recommended for TID monitoring [64]."}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0006_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9635901ba978a3f5", "text_span_hash": "9635901ba978a3f5", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Another valid approach may include frequent monitoring of a typical training session with\ncomparison of both external (e.g., running velocity) and internal (e.g., HR) load parameters [65].\nThus, if the athlete ran faster a given distance with the sam", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Another valid approach may include frequent monitoring of a typical training session with\ncomparison of both external (e.g., running velocity) and internal (e.g., HR) load parameters [65].\nThus, if the athlete ran faster a given distance with the same HR, or reduced their HR when running\nat the same velocity, this would show a positive adaptation with a lower internal load for a given\nexternal load [66]. Comparing internal and external load parameters in real-time is currently very\neasy with the use of wearable technology that simultaneously records various parameters, including\nHR, velocity with GPS technology, kinematics (i.e., stride rate and length) via accelerometry, altitude,\nand temperature, among others [67]. However, there is no evidence demonstrating whether wearable\ntechnology is more valid and eﬃcient than simple monitoring tools, such as session rating of perceived\nexertion (sRPE) and training impulse (TRIMP = sRPE × session time), which were demonstrated\nto be valid for discriminating between short- and long-term adaptations [65]. This is an important\nconsideration, as load indices such as monotony (i.e., mean weekly sRPE/standard deviation) and\nstrain (i.e., weekly sRPE × monotony) could be very helpful to avoid overreaching and overtraining,\nrequiring only the recording of this simple information on a daily basis [68].\nFinally, over the last decade, a number of studies pointed out the validity and practicality ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Another valid approach may include frequent monitoring of a typical training session with\ncomparison of both external (e.g., running velocity) and internal (e.g., HR) load parameters [65].\nThus, if the athlete ran faster a given distance with the sam"}
