standard deviation) and\nstrain (i.e., weekly sRPE × monotony) could be very helpful to avoid overreaching and overtraining,\nrequiring only the recording of this simple information on a daily basis [68].\nFinally, over the last decade, a number of studies pointed out the validity and practicality ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "accessibility of runners and their coaches to these methods\nmay be the main criteria for selecting the most appropriate parameters in every scenario. In this regard,\nwe encourage the use of several methods to simultaneously monitor the external and i"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0006_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9dcd9fe5b97f4a49", "text_span_hash": "9dcd9fe5b97f4a49", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "sly monitor the external and internal load\nparameters (e.g., sRPE + HRV + kinematics) to better assist with training decisions on a daily basis,\nwhile programming testing sessions regularly to objectively identify the targeted improvements of\nphysiol", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Another valid approach may include frequent monitoring of a typical training session with\ncomparison of both external (e.g., running velocity) and internal (e.g., HR) load parameters [65].\nThus, if the athlete ran faster a given distance with the same HR, or reduced their HR when running\nat the same velocity, this would show a positive adaptation with a lower internal load for a given\nexternal load [66]. Comparing internal and external load parameters in real-time is currently very\neasy with the use of wearable technology that simultaneously records various parameters, including\nHR, velocity with GPS technology, kinematics (i.e., stride rate and length) via accelerometry, altitude,\nand temperature, among others [67]. However, there is no evidence demonstrating whether wearable\ntechnology is more valid and eﬃcient than simple monitoring tools, such as session rating of perceived\nexertion (sRPE) and training impulse (TRIMP = sRPE × session time), which were demonstrated\nto be valid for discriminating between short- and long-term adaptations [65]. This is an important\nconsideration, as load indices such as monotony (i.e., mean weekly sRPE/standard deviation) and\nstrain (i.e., weekly sRPE × monotony) could be very helpful to avoid overreaching and overtraining,\nrequiring only the recording of this simple information on a daily basis [68].\nFinally, over the last decade, a number of studies pointed out the validity and practicality ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "sly monitor the external and internal load\nparameters (e.g., sRPE + HRV + kinematics) to better assist with training decisions on a daily basis,\nwhile programming testing sessions regularly to objectively identify the targeted improvements of\nphysiol"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0006_c0016", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "88e60b2617a4edd9", "text_span_hash": "88e60b2617a4edd9", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 16, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "rgeted improvements of\nphysiological and performance parameters in well-standardized conditions.\n4. Performance Predictions\nSince many runners aim to improve their personal best times, it is of interest to predict endurance\nperformance during the tra", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Another valid approach may include frequent monitoring of a typical training session with\ncomparison of both external (e.g., running velocity) and internal (e.g., HR) load parameters [65].\nThus, if the athlete ran faster a given distance with the same HR, or reduced their HR when running\nat the same velocity, this would show a positive adaptation with a lower internal load for a given\nexternal load [66]. Comparing internal and external load parameters in real-time is currently very\neasy with the use of wearable technology that simultaneously records various parameters, including\nHR, velocity with GPS technology, kinematics (i.e., stride rate and length) via accelerometry, altitude,\nand temperature, among others [67]. However, there is no evidence demonstrating whether wearable\ntechnology is more valid and eﬃcient than simple monitoring tools, such as session rating of perceived\nexertion (sRPE) and training impulse (TRIMP = sRPE × session time), which were demonstrated\nto be valid for discriminating between short- and long-term adaptations [65]. This is an important\nconsideration, as load indices such as monotony (i.e., mean weekly sRPE/standard deviation) and\nstrain (i.e., weekly sRPE × monotony) could be very helpful to avoid overreaching and overtraining,\nrequiring only the recording of this simple information on a daily basis [68].\nFinally, over the last decade, a number of studies pointed out the validity and practicality ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "rgeted improvements of\nphysiological and performance parameters in well-standardized conditions.\n4. Performance Predictions\nSince many runners aim to improve their personal best times, it is of interest to predict endurance\nperformance during the tra"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0006_c0017", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0e1274398b9859d1", "text_span_hash": "0e1274398b9859d1", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 17, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "nce\nperformance during the training process. While both procedures are directly linked, performance\nprediction should not be confounded with training monitoring, as the latter focuses on the factors\nassociated with improving the former. The most usua", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Another valid approach may include frequent monitoring of a typical training session with\ncomparison of both external (e.g., running velocity) and internal (e.g., HR) load parameters [65].\nThus, if the athlete ran faster a given distance with the same HR, or reduced their HR when running\nat the same velocity, this would show a positive adaptation with a lower internal load for a given\nexternal load [66]. Comparing internal and external load parameters in real-time is currently very\neasy with the use of wearable technology that simultaneously records various parameters, including\nHR, velocity with GPS technology, kinematics (i.e., stride rate and length) via accelerometry, altitude,\nand temperature, among others [67]. However, there is no evidence demonstrating whether wearable\ntechnology is more valid and eﬃcient than simple monitoring tools, such as session rating of perceived\nexertion (sRPE) and training impulse (TRIMP = sRPE × session time), which were demonstrated\nto be valid for discriminating between short- and long-term adaptations [65]. This is an important\nconsideration, as load indices such as monotony (i.e., mean weekly sRPE/standard deviation) and\nstrain (i.e., weekly sRPE × monotony) could be very helpful to avoid overreaching and overtraining,\nrequiring only the recording of this simple information on a daily basis [68].\nFinally, over the last decade, a number of studies pointed out the validity and practicality ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "nce\nperformance during the training process. While both procedures are directly linked, performance\nprediction should not be confounded with training monitoring, as the latter focuses on the factors\nassociated with improving the former. The most usua"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0006_c0019", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "eb550ce955ce6733", "text_span_hash": "eb550ce955ce6733", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 19, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ween times over diﬀerent distances [75]. As an example, these\nformulas multiply (or divide) the time employed to cover a given distance to obtain the prediction of\nthe desired distance (i.e., marathon time = 10 km time × 4.76). Following this method,", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Another valid approach may include frequent monitoring of a typical training session with\ncomparison of both external (e.g., running velocity) and internal (e.g., HR) load parameters [65].\nThus, if the athlete ran faster a given distance with the same HR, or reduced their HR when running\nat the same velocity, this would show a positive adaptation with a lower internal load for a given\nexternal load [66]. Comparing internal and external load parameters in real-time is currently very\neasy with the use of wearable technology that simultaneously records various parameters, including\nHR, velocity with GPS technology, kinematics (i.e., stride rate and length) via accelerometry, altitude,\nand temperature, among others [67]. However, there is no evidence demonstrating whether wearable\ntechnology is more valid and eﬃcient than simple monitoring tools, such as session rating of perceived\nexertion (sRPE) and training impulse (TRIMP = sRPE × session time), which were demonstrated\nto be valid for discriminating between short- and long-term adaptations [65]. This is an important\nconsideration, as load indices such as monotony (i.e., mean weekly sRPE/standard deviation) and\nstrain (i.e., weekly sRPE × monotony) could be very helpful to avoid overreaching and overtraining,\nrequiring only the recording of this simple information on a daily basis [68].\nFinally, over the last decade, a number of studies pointed out the validity and practicality ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ween times over diﬀerent distances [75]. As an example, these\nformulas multiply (or divide) the time employed to cover a given distance to obtain the prediction of\nthe desired distance (i.e., marathon time = 10 km time × 4.76). Following this method,"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0007_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "84ebaef6ac91c47f", "text_span_hash": "84ebaef6ac91c47f", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "dictions do not provide solutions for the training process, as they do not reveal\nwhat physiological parameters are involved in achieving that performance. Through this approach, it\nwould be only intuitive to consider whether the runner is “too slow", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Apart from many limitations (state of preparedness along time, individual characteristics over some\ndistances, etc.), this method is considered the most accurate to predict running performance [77–80].\nHowever, these predictions do not provide solutions for the training process, as they do not reveal\nwhat physiological parameters are involved in achieving that performance. Through this approach, it\nwould be only intuitive to consider whether the runner is “too slow or too resistant”. In addition, the\ncompletion of one maximal eﬀort to estimate another distance performance may be inconvenient for\nmost recreational runners.\nTo approach the necessity of some physiological information, Mercier et al.\nnomogram-related parameters, such as VO2max and metabolic thresholds, in order to predict times over\na whole range of distances [81]. This approach was validated over diﬀerent distances according to sex\nin several studies from Bosquet’s research group [75,82,83]. This nomogram represents a great tool that\nintegrates diﬀerent predictions that can be individually determined. Although diﬀerent physiological\nparameters were included in diﬀerent prediction models, the most important performance-related\nparameter is the peak velocity achieved at the end of a treadmill-graded test [78,84–86], followed\nby the vVO2max [87–90] and the velocity associated with the second metabolic threshold [91–95].\nAlthough the factors determining endurance run", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "dictions do not provide solutions for the training process, as they do not reveal\nwhat physiological parameters are involved in achieving that performance. Through this approach, it\nwould be only intuitive to consider whether the runner is “too slow"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0007_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "efa259641d03ce5c", "text_span_hash": "efa259641d03ce5c", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ether the runner is “too slow or too resistant”. In addition, the\ncompletion of one maximal eﬀort to estimate another distance performance may be inconvenient for\nmost recreational runners.\nTo approach the necessity of some physiological information,", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Apart from many limitations (state of preparedness along time, individual characteristics over some\ndistances, etc.), this method is considered the most accurate to predict running performance [77–80].\nHowever, these predictions do not provide solutions for the training process, as they do not reveal\nwhat physiological parameters are involved in achieving that performance. Through this approach, it\nwould be only intuitive to consider whether the runner is “too slow or too resistant”. In addition, the\ncompletion of one maximal eﬀort to estimate another distance performance may be inconvenient for\nmost recreational runners.\nTo approach the necessity of some physiological information, Mercier et al.\nnomogram-related parameters, such as VO2max and metabolic thresholds, in order to predict times over\na whole range of distances [81]. This approach was validated over diﬀerent distances according to sex\nin several studies from Bosquet’s research group [75,82,83]. This nomogram represents a great tool that\nintegrates diﬀerent predictions that can be individually determined. Although diﬀerent physiological\nparameters were included in diﬀerent prediction models, the most important performance-related\nparameter is the peak velocity achieved at the end of a treadmill-graded test [78,84–86], followed\nby the vVO2max [87–90] and the velocity associated with the second metabolic threshold [91–95].\nAlthough the factors determining endurance run", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ether the runner is “too slow or too resistant”. In addition, the\ncompletion of one maximal eﬀort to estimate another distance performance may be inconvenient for\nmost recreational runners.\nTo approach the necessity of some physiological information,"}
