ediction models, the most important performance-related\nparameter is the peak velocity achieved at the end of a treadmill-graded test [78,84–86], followed\nby the vVO2max [87–90] and the velocity associated with the second metabolic threshold [91–95].\nAlthough the factors determining endurance run", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "llary blood lactate measurements) could be enough to predict running performance.\nFrom these predictions, coaches and runners should select a given method and apply it systematically,\nincluding not only repeating procedures and ambient conditions, bu"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0007_c0017", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "309ea1c77684ed20", "text_span_hash": "309ea1c77684ed20", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 17, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "res and ambient conditions, but also considering the training\nstage of the individual at the moment of evaluation. These considerations, which are important for\nmore accurate individual predictions, are not considered when developing predictive model", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Apart from many limitations (state of preparedness along time, individual characteristics over some\ndistances, etc.), this method is considered the most accurate to predict running performance [77–80].\nHowever, these predictions do not provide solutions for the training process, as they do not reveal\nwhat physiological parameters are involved in achieving that performance. Through this approach, it\nwould be only intuitive to consider whether the runner is “too slow or too resistant”. In addition, the\ncompletion of one maximal eﬀort to estimate another distance performance may be inconvenient for\nmost recreational runners.\nTo approach the necessity of some physiological information, Mercier et al.\nnomogram-related parameters, such as VO2max and metabolic thresholds, in order to predict times over\na whole range of distances [81]. This approach was validated over diﬀerent distances according to sex\nin several studies from Bosquet’s research group [75,82,83]. This nomogram represents a great tool that\nintegrates diﬀerent predictions that can be individually determined. Although diﬀerent physiological\nparameters were included in diﬀerent prediction models, the most important performance-related\nparameter is the peak velocity achieved at the end of a treadmill-graded test [78,84–86], followed\nby the vVO2max [87–90] and the velocity associated with the second metabolic threshold [91–95].\nAlthough the factors determining endurance run", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "res and ambient conditions, but also considering the training\nstage of the individual at the moment of evaluation. These considerations, which are important for\nmore accurate individual predictions, are not considered when developing predictive model"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0008_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4265fa357eb34df5", "text_span_hash": "4265fa357eb34df5", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ptual approaches, such as verbal and visual feedback [111,112], monitoring\nof tension in arms and shoulders [113], athletic skills [114–116], and habituation to diﬀerent running\npatterns [117,118]; and indirect interventions are those designed to imp", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Since RT is a determining factor of RE, the modiﬁcation of RT is one of the most important objectives\nfor recreational runners [108], as it is expected that changes in RT induce changes in performance [109],\nor even lower the risk of injury [110]. The interventions available for RT improvement may be divided\ninto direct and indirect interventions, where direct interventions involve the enhancement of speciﬁc\nrunning patterns using perceptual approaches, such as verbal and visual feedback [111,112], monitoring\nof tension in arms and shoulders [113], athletic skills [114–116], and habituation to diﬀerent running\npatterns [117,118]; and indirect interventions are those designed to improve conditioning capacities,\nsuch as core stability, strength, and balance, that ultimately may inﬂuence the RT but without any\ndirect intervention from factors associated with running patterns.\nContrary to common belief, the search for a “one-ﬁts-all” RT is not accompanied by improvement\nin performance or RE. In this regard, the recommendation of a global RT is not eﬀective nor advisable.\nThis is the case of the Pose®method, which has become a popular intervention for the modiﬁcation\nof RT. Brieﬂy, this method proposes running with a forward trunk lean, a more ﬂexed position at\ntouch-down, a mid-foot striking style, and a low range of arm motion [119]. A previous study\ndemonstrated that adopting this particular RT did not result in changes in speci", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ptual approaches, such as verbal and visual feedback [111,112], monitoring\nof tension in arms and shoulders [113], athletic skills [114–116], and habituation to diﬀerent running\npatterns [117,118]; and indirect interventions are those designed to imp"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0008_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "57422e3a7a1f5a3d", "text_span_hash": "57422e3a7a1f5a3d", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ions are those designed to improve conditioning capacities,\nsuch as core stability, strength, and balance, that ultimately may inﬂuence the RT but without any\ndirect intervention from factors associated with running patterns.\nContrary to common belie", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Since RT is a determining factor of RE, the modiﬁcation of RT is one of the most important objectives\nfor recreational runners [108], as it is expected that changes in RT induce changes in performance [109],\nor even lower the risk of injury [110]. The interventions available for RT improvement may be divided\ninto direct and indirect interventions, where direct interventions involve the enhancement of speciﬁc\nrunning patterns using perceptual approaches, such as verbal and visual feedback [111,112], monitoring\nof tension in arms and shoulders [113], athletic skills [114–116], and habituation to diﬀerent running\npatterns [117,118]; and indirect interventions are those designed to improve conditioning capacities,\nsuch as core stability, strength, and balance, that ultimately may inﬂuence the RT but without any\ndirect intervention from factors associated with running patterns.\nContrary to common belief, the search for a “one-ﬁts-all” RT is not accompanied by improvement\nin performance or RE. In this regard, the recommendation of a global RT is not eﬀective nor advisable.\nThis is the case of the Pose®method, which has become a popular intervention for the modiﬁcation\nof RT. Brieﬂy, this method proposes running with a forward trunk lean, a more ﬂexed position at\ntouch-down, a mid-foot striking style, and a low range of arm motion [119]. A previous study\ndemonstrated that adopting this particular RT did not result in changes in speci", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ions are those designed to improve conditioning capacities,\nsuch as core stability, strength, and balance, that ultimately may inﬂuence the RT but without any\ndirect intervention from factors associated with running patterns.\nContrary to common belie"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0008_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "44578d6b1908bdce", "text_span_hash": "44578d6b1908bdce", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "of the Pose®method, which has become a popular intervention for the modiﬁcation\nof RT. Brieﬂy, this method proposes running with a forward trunk lean, a more ﬂexed position at\ntouch-down, a mid-foot striking style, and a low range of arm motion [119", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Since RT is a determining factor of RE, the modiﬁcation of RT is one of the most important objectives\nfor recreational runners [108], as it is expected that changes in RT induce changes in performance [109],\nor even lower the risk of injury [110]. The interventions available for RT improvement may be divided\ninto direct and indirect interventions, where direct interventions involve the enhancement of speciﬁc\nrunning patterns using perceptual approaches, such as verbal and visual feedback [111,112], monitoring\nof tension in arms and shoulders [113], athletic skills [114–116], and habituation to diﬀerent running\npatterns [117,118]; and indirect interventions are those designed to improve conditioning capacities,\nsuch as core stability, strength, and balance, that ultimately may inﬂuence the RT but without any\ndirect intervention from factors associated with running patterns.\nContrary to common belief, the search for a “one-ﬁts-all” RT is not accompanied by improvement\nin performance or RE. In this regard, the recommendation of a global RT is not eﬀective nor advisable.\nThis is the case of the Pose®method, which has become a popular intervention for the modiﬁcation\nof RT. Brieﬂy, this method proposes running with a forward trunk lean, a more ﬂexed position at\ntouch-down, a mid-foot striking style, and a low range of arm motion [119]. A previous study\ndemonstrated that adopting this particular RT did not result in changes in speci", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "of the Pose®method, which has become a popular intervention for the modiﬁcation\nof RT. Brieﬂy, this method proposes running with a forward trunk lean, a more ﬂexed position at\ntouch-down, a mid-foot striking style, and a low range of arm motion [119"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0008_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4de830576165e455", "text_span_hash": "4de830576165e455", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "performance variables in runners.\nOn the other hand, many studies demonstrated that strength and explosive training programs\nare capable of improving RE and performance mediated by RT improvement, such as contact time\nreduction [123–125]. Maximum str", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Since RT is a determining factor of RE, the modiﬁcation of RT is one of the most important objectives\nfor recreational runners [108], as it is expected that changes in RT induce changes in performance [109],\nor even lower the risk of injury [110]. The interventions available for RT improvement may be divided\ninto direct and indirect interventions, where direct interventions involve the enhancement of speciﬁc\nrunning patterns using perceptual approaches, such as verbal and visual feedback [111,112], monitoring\nof tension in arms and shoulders [113], athletic skills [114–116], and habituation to diﬀerent running\npatterns [117,118]; and indirect interventions are those designed to improve conditioning capacities,\nsuch as core stability, strength, and balance, that ultimately may inﬂuence the RT but without any\ndirect intervention from factors associated with running patterns.\nContrary to common belief, the search for a “one-ﬁts-all” RT is not accompanied by improvement\nin performance or RE. In this regard, the recommendation of a global RT is not eﬀective nor advisable.\nThis is the case of the Pose®method, which has become a popular intervention for the modiﬁcation\nof RT. Brieﬂy, this method proposes running with a forward trunk lean, a more ﬂexed position at\ntouch-down, a mid-foot striking style, and a low range of arm motion [119]. A previous study\ndemonstrated that adopting this particular RT did not result in changes in speci", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "performance variables in runners.\nOn the other hand, many studies demonstrated that strength and explosive training programs\nare capable of improving RE and performance mediated by RT improvement, such as contact time\nreduction [123–125]. Maximum str"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0008_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4d5e9971938e29b2", "text_span_hash": "4d5e9971938e29b2", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "duction [123–125]. Maximum strength and explosive training programs may increase the net\nexcitation of motoneurons, thus resulting in better RE [123]. While the long-term adjustments of\nRT are still unclear, recent evidence also suggested that isomet", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Since RT is a determining factor of RE, the modiﬁcation of RT is one of the most important objectives\nfor recreational runners [108], as it is expected that changes in RT induce changes in performance [109],\nor even lower the risk of injury [110]. The interventions available for RT improvement may be divided\ninto direct and indirect interventions, where direct interventions involve the enhancement of speciﬁc\nrunning patterns using perceptual approaches, such as verbal and visual feedback [111,112], monitoring\nof tension in arms and shoulders [113], athletic skills [114–116], and habituation to diﬀerent running\npatterns [117,118]; and indirect interventions are those designed to improve conditioning capacities,\nsuch as core stability, strength, and balance, that ultimately may inﬂuence the RT but without any\ndirect intervention from factors associated with running patterns.\nContrary to common belief, the search for a “one-ﬁts-all” RT is not accompanied by improvement\nin performance or RE. In this regard, the recommendation of a global RT is not eﬀective nor advisable.\nThis is the case of the Pose®method, which has become a popular intervention for the modiﬁcation\nof RT. Brieﬂy, this method proposes running with a forward trunk lean, a more ﬂexed position at\ntouch-down, a mid-foot striking style, and a low range of arm motion [119]. A previous study\ndemonstrated that adopting this particular RT did not result in changes in speci", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "duction [123–125]. Maximum strength and explosive training programs may increase the net\nexcitation of motoneurons, thus resulting in better RE [123]. While the long-term adjustments of\nRT are still unclear, recent evidence also suggested that isomet"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0008_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "190b1d0e0bae2eb6", "text_span_hash": "190b1d0e0bae2eb6", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "nners [126]. These modiﬁcations may be\nassociated with enhanced muscle activation patterns during running.\nPrevious observational studies tested the hypothesis of an ideal RT related to RE and performance.\nAlthough there are inconsistent ﬁndings and", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Since RT is a determining factor of RE, the modiﬁcation of RT is one of the most important objectives\nfor recreational runners [108], as it is expected that changes in RT induce changes in performance [109],\nor even lower the risk of injury [110]. The interventions available for RT improvement may be divided\ninto direct and indirect interventions, where direct interventions involve the enhancement of speciﬁc\nrunning patterns using perceptual approaches, such as verbal and visual feedback [111,112], monitoring\nof tension in arms and shoulders [113], athletic skills [114–116], and habituation to diﬀerent running\npatterns [117,118]; and indirect interventions are those designed to improve conditioning capacities,\nsuch as core stability, strength, and balance, that ultimately may inﬂuence the RT but without any\ndirect intervention from factors associated with running patterns.\nContrary to common belief, the search for a “one-ﬁts-all” RT is not accompanied by improvement\nin performance or RE. In this regard, the recommendation of a global RT is not eﬀective nor advisable.\nThis is the case of the Pose®method, which has become a popular intervention for the modiﬁcation\nof RT. Brieﬂy, this method proposes running with a forward trunk lean, a more ﬂexed position at\ntouch-down, a mid-foot striking style, and a low range of arm motion [119]. A previous study\ndemonstrated that adopting this particular RT did not result in changes in speci", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "nners [126]. These modiﬁcations may be\nassociated with enhanced muscle activation patterns during running.\nPrevious observational studies tested the hypothesis of an ideal RT related to RE and performance.\nAlthough there are inconsistent ﬁndings and"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0008_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f767d8c700889c87", "text_span_hash": "f767d8c700889c87", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ulsion [107]. It was suggested that the runner’s experience is associated\nwith RT [108], however the runner’s experience does not appear to signiﬁcantly inﬂuence running\nmechanics [127]. In contrast, using the spring-mass model to understand the dete", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Since RT is a determining factor of RE, the modiﬁcation of RT is one of the most important objectives\nfor recreational runners [108], as it is expected that changes in RT induce changes in performance [109],\nor even lower the risk of injury [110]. The interventions available for RT improvement may be divided\ninto direct and indirect interventions, where direct interventions involve the enhancement of speciﬁc\nrunning patterns using perceptual approaches, such as verbal and visual feedback [111,112], monitoring\nof tension in arms and shoulders [113], athletic skills [114–116], and habituation to diﬀerent running\npatterns [117,118]; and indirect interventions are those designed to improve conditioning capacities,\nsuch as core stability, strength, and balance, that ultimately may inﬂuence the RT but without any\ndirect intervention from factors associated with running patterns.\nContrary to common belief, the search for a “one-ﬁts-all” RT is not accompanied by improvement\nin performance or RE. In this regard, the recommendation of a global RT is not eﬀective nor advisable.\nThis is the case of the Pose®method, which has become a popular intervention for the modiﬁcation\nof RT. Brieﬂy, this method proposes running with a forward trunk lean, a more ﬂexed position at\ntouch-down, a mid-foot striking style, and a low range of arm motion [119]. A previous study\ndemonstrated that adopting this particular RT did not result in changes in speci", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ulsion [107]. It was suggested that the runner’s experience is associated\nwith RT [108], however the runner’s experience does not appear to signiﬁcantly inﬂuence running\nmechanics [127]. In contrast, using the spring-mass model to understand the dete"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0008_c0016", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "079b0879c6f8842f", "text_span_hash": "079b0879c6f8842f", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 16, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "s model to understand the determining factors of\nRT, a recent study observed that faster recreational runners unveiled more “elastic” responses than\nslower runners [128]. The eﬀective aerial time, the elastic system frequency, and the vertical stiﬀne", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Since RT is a determining factor of RE, the modiﬁcation of RT is one of the most important objectives\nfor recreational runners [108], as it is expected that changes in RT induce changes in performance [109],\nor even lower the risk of injury [110]. The interventions available for RT improvement may be divided\ninto direct and indirect interventions, where direct interventions involve the enhancement of speciﬁc\nrunning patterns using perceptual approaches, such as verbal and visual feedback [111,112], monitoring\nof tension in arms and shoulders [113], athletic skills [114–116], and habituation to diﬀerent running\npatterns [117,118]; and indirect interventions are those designed to improve conditioning capacities,\nsuch as core stability, strength, and balance, that ultimately may inﬂuence the RT but without any\ndirect intervention from factors associated with running patterns.\nContrary to common belief, the search for a “one-ﬁts-all” RT is not accompanied by improvement\nin performance or RE. In this regard, the recommendation of a global RT is not eﬀective nor advisable.\nThis is the case of the Pose®method, which has become a popular intervention for the modiﬁcation\nof RT. Brieﬂy, this method proposes running with a forward trunk lean, a more ﬂexed position at\ntouch-down, a mid-foot striking style, and a low range of arm motion [119]. A previous study\ndemonstrated that adopting this particular RT did not result in changes in speci", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "s model to understand the determining factors of\nRT, a recent study observed that faster recreational runners unveiled more “elastic” responses than\nslower runners [128]. The eﬀective aerial time, the elastic system frequency, and the vertical stiﬀne"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0008_c0017", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "32453da8b9439feb", "text_span_hash": "32453da8b9439feb", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 17, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "uency, and the vertical stiﬀness\nwere greater and the eﬀective contact time was shorter in faster runners. These key parameters for\nRT optimization were also associated with greater running economy [128]. This evidence is linked to\nevidence demonstra", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Since RT is a determining factor of RE, the modiﬁcation of RT is one of the most important objectives\nfor recreational runners [108], as it is expected that changes in RT induce changes in performance [109],\nor even lower the risk of injury [110]. The interventions available for RT improvement may be divided\ninto direct and indirect interventions, where direct interventions involve the enhancement of speciﬁc\nrunning patterns using perceptual approaches, such as verbal and visual feedback [111,112], monitoring\nof tension in arms and shoulders [113], athletic skills [114–116], and habituation to diﬀerent running\npatterns [117,118]; and indirect interventions are those designed to improve conditioning capacities,\nsuch as core stability, strength, and balance, that ultimately may inﬂuence the RT but without any\ndirect intervention from factors associated with running patterns.\nContrary to common belief, the search for a “one-ﬁts-all” RT is not accompanied by improvement\nin performance or RE. In this regard, the recommendation of a global RT is not eﬀective nor advisable.\nThis is the case of the Pose®method, which has become a popular intervention for the modiﬁcation\nof RT. Brieﬂy, this method proposes running with a forward trunk lean, a more ﬂexed position at\ntouch-down, a mid-foot striking style, and a low range of arm motion [119]. A previous study\ndemonstrated that adopting this particular RT did not result in changes in speci", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "uency, and the vertical stiﬀness\nwere greater and the eﬀective contact time was shorter in faster runners. These key parameters for\nRT optimization were also associated with greater running economy [128]. This evidence is linked to\nevidence demonstra"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0008_c0019", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e3bc99b0a95c1fa0", "text_span_hash": "e3bc99b0a95c1fa0", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 19, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "so reducing stride length and increasing stride frequency in comparison with young adults.\nSome simple RT changes can be induced using sound or visual feedback methods to modify\nstride frequency and length. Thus, a protocol for increasing 10% the pre", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Since RT is a determining factor of RE, the modiﬁcation of RT is one of the most important objectives\nfor recreational runners [108], as it is expected that changes in RT induce changes in performance [109],\nor even lower the risk of injury [110]. The interventions available for RT improvement may be divided\ninto direct and indirect interventions, where direct interventions involve the enhancement of speciﬁc\nrunning patterns using perceptual approaches, such as verbal and visual feedback [111,112], monitoring\nof tension in arms and shoulders [113], athletic skills [114–116], and habituation to diﬀerent running\npatterns [117,118]; and indirect interventions are those designed to improve conditioning capacities,\nsuch as core stability, strength, and balance, that ultimately may inﬂuence the RT but without any\ndirect intervention from factors associated with running patterns.\nContrary to common belief, the search for a “one-ﬁts-all” RT is not accompanied by improvement\nin performance or RE. In this regard, the recommendation of a global RT is not eﬀective nor advisable.\nThis is the case of the Pose®method, which has become a popular intervention for the modiﬁcation\nof RT. Brieﬂy, this method proposes running with a forward trunk lean, a more ﬂexed position at\ntouch-down, a mid-foot striking style, and a low range of arm motion [119]. A previous study\ndemonstrated that adopting this particular RT did not result in changes in speci", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "so reducing stride length and increasing stride frequency in comparison with young adults.\nSome simple RT changes can be induced using sound or visual feedback methods to modify\nstride frequency and length. Thus, a protocol for increasing 10% the pre"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0009_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b615499acd81447d", "text_span_hash": "b615499acd81447d", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "acute alterations in RT are controversial because their eﬀectiveness to produce beneﬁts are not well\nsupported by current evidence.\nAnother interesting topic that was recently investigated following the concerns of both coaches\nand runners is the eﬀe", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "acute alterations in RT are controversial because their eﬀectiveness to produce beneﬁts are not well\nsupported by current evidence.\nAnother interesting topic that was recently investigated following the concerns of both coaches\nand runners is the eﬀect of forefoot (FFS) and rearfoot strike (RFS) changes on RT, injury risk, and\nperformance. Thus, when evaluating a group of runners using both strike patterns, no mechanical\nadvantages of FFS vs. RFS runners were evident [137]. RFS runners switching to FFS may reduce their\ninjury risk due to the altered distribution of loading between joints, however this should be weighed\nagainst possible performance decrements [137,138]. In another recent study, the retraining from RFS\nto FFS did not aﬀect running economy, but did reduce running-related patellofemoral pain after one\nmonth of training [139]. Therefore, the objectives and the costs of altering the strike pattern in terms\nof injury risk and performance changes must be considered before any intervention. Also, expert\nopinion [110] and meta-analysis [140] conﬁrmed the limited evidence that supported the eﬃcacy of the\ntransition from the RFS to the FFS technique.\nIn summary, a speciﬁc foot-strike pattern does not reduce joint contact forces and the\nrecommendations of changing RT should only be based on individual analysis by experts. Evaluation\nmethods based on optimality are interesting for RT analysis; parameters that assess RT at a", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "acute alterations in RT are controversial because their eﬀectiveness to produce beneﬁts are not well\nsupported by current evidence.\nAnother interesting topic that was recently investigated following the concerns of both coaches\nand runners is the eﬀe"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0009_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2cb5ba9b4e5a6fac", "text_span_hash": "2cb5ba9b4e5a6fac", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ements [137,138]. In another recent study, the retraining from RFS\nto FFS did not aﬀect running economy, but did reduce running-related patellofemoral pain after one\nmonth of training [139]. Therefore, the objectives and the costs of altering the str", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "acute alterations in RT are controversial because their eﬀectiveness to produce beneﬁts are not well\nsupported by current evidence.\nAnother interesting topic that was recently investigated following the concerns of both coaches\nand runners is the eﬀect of forefoot (FFS) and rearfoot strike (RFS) changes on RT, injury risk, and\nperformance. Thus, when evaluating a group of runners using both strike patterns, no mechanical\nadvantages of FFS vs. RFS runners were evident [137]. RFS runners switching to FFS may reduce their\ninjury risk due to the altered distribution of loading between joints, however this should be weighed\nagainst possible performance decrements [137,138]. In another recent study, the retraining from RFS\nto FFS did not aﬀect running economy, but did reduce running-related patellofemoral pain after one\nmonth of training [139]. Therefore, the objectives and the costs of altering the strike pattern in terms\nof injury risk and performance changes must be considered before any intervention. Also, expert\nopinion [110] and meta-analysis [140] conﬁrmed the limited evidence that supported the eﬃcacy of the\ntransition from the RFS to the FFS technique.\nIn summary, a speciﬁc foot-strike pattern does not reduce joint contact forces and the\nrecommendations of changing RT should only be based on individual analysis by experts. Evaluation\nmethods based on optimality are interesting for RT analysis; parameters that assess RT at a", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ements [137,138]. In another recent study, the retraining from RFS\nto FFS did not aﬀect running economy, but did reduce running-related patellofemoral pain after one\nmonth of training [139]. Therefore, the objectives and the costs of altering the str"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0009_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5380f89417e7f9e7", "text_span_hash": "5380f89417e7f9e7", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "vidual analysis by experts. Evaluation\nmethods based on optimality are interesting for RT analysis; parameters that assess RT at an organismal\nlevel may show a clearer mechanistic model to explain the role of RT on performance in recreational\nrunners", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "acute alterations in RT are controversial because their eﬀectiveness to produce beneﬁts are not well\nsupported by current evidence.\nAnother interesting topic that was recently investigated following the concerns of both coaches\nand runners is the eﬀect of forefoot (FFS) and rearfoot strike (RFS) changes on RT, injury risk, and\nperformance. Thus, when evaluating a group of runners using both strike patterns, no mechanical\nadvantages of FFS vs. RFS runners were evident [137]. RFS runners switching to FFS may reduce their\ninjury risk due to the altered distribution of loading between joints, however this should be weighed\nagainst possible performance decrements [137,138]. In another recent study, the retraining from RFS\nto FFS did not aﬀect running economy, but did reduce running-related patellofemoral pain after one\nmonth of training [139]. Therefore, the objectives and the costs of altering the strike pattern in terms\nof injury risk and performance changes must be considered before any intervention. Also, expert\nopinion [110] and meta-analysis [140] conﬁrmed the limited evidence that supported the eﬃcacy of the\ntransition from the RFS to the FFS technique.\nIn summary, a speciﬁc foot-strike pattern does not reduce joint contact forces and the\nrecommendations of changing RT should only be based on individual analysis by experts. Evaluation\nmethods based on optimality are interesting for RT analysis; parameters that assess RT at a", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "vidual analysis by experts. Evaluation\nmethods based on optimality are interesting for RT analysis; parameters that assess RT at an organismal\nlevel may show a clearer mechanistic model to explain the role of RT on performance in recreational\nrunners"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0009_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c0414dbea94370fe", "text_span_hash": "c0414dbea94370fe", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "rmance in recreational\nrunners. Models including integrative parameters, such as elastic bouncing and metabolic economy,\nhave great potential for this purpose. Of note, optimal stride frequency and length (stride frequency and\nlength where the metabo", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "acute alterations in RT are controversial because their eﬀectiveness to produce beneﬁts are not well\nsupported by current evidence.\nAnother interesting topic that was recently investigated following the concerns of both coaches\nand runners is the eﬀect of forefoot (FFS) and rearfoot strike (RFS) changes on RT, injury risk, and\nperformance. Thus, when evaluating a group of runners using both strike patterns, no mechanical\nadvantages of FFS vs. RFS runners were evident [137]. RFS runners switching to FFS may reduce their\ninjury risk due to the altered distribution of loading between joints, however this should be weighed\nagainst possible performance decrements [137,138]. In another recent study, the retraining from RFS\nto FFS did not aﬀect running economy, but did reduce running-related patellofemoral pain after one\nmonth of training [139]. Therefore, the objectives and the costs of altering the strike pattern in terms\nof injury risk and performance changes must be considered before any intervention. Also, expert\nopinion [110] and meta-analysis [140] conﬁrmed the limited evidence that supported the eﬃcacy of the\ntransition from the RFS to the FFS technique.\nIn summary, a speciﬁc foot-strike pattern does not reduce joint contact forces and the\nrecommendations of changing RT should only be based on individual analysis by experts. Evaluation\nmethods based on optimality are interesting for RT analysis; parameters that assess RT at a", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "rmance in recreational\nrunners. Models including integrative parameters, such as elastic bouncing and metabolic economy,\nhave great potential for this purpose. Of note, optimal stride frequency and length (stride frequency and\nlength where the metabo"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0009_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ea895dc263ac0db6", "text_span_hash": "ea895dc263ac0db6", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "cy and\nlength where the metabolic cost is lowest) are normally found at freely chosen conditions [135,141,142].\nDetermining these optimal parameters at the individual level seems to be the better way to analyze RT\nin recreational runners.\n6. Factors", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "acute alterations in RT are controversial because their eﬀectiveness to produce beneﬁts are not well\nsupported by current evidence.\nAnother interesting topic that was recently investigated following the concerns of both coaches\nand runners is the eﬀect of forefoot (FFS) and rearfoot strike (RFS) changes on RT, injury risk, and\nperformance. Thus, when evaluating a group of runners using both strike patterns, no mechanical\nadvantages of FFS vs. RFS runners were evident [137]. RFS runners switching to FFS may reduce their\ninjury risk due to the altered distribution of loading between joints, however this should be weighed\nagainst possible performance decrements [137,138]. In another recent study, the retraining from RFS\nto FFS did not aﬀect running economy, but did reduce running-related patellofemoral pain after one\nmonth of training [139]. Therefore, the objectives and the costs of altering the strike pattern in terms\nof injury risk and performance changes must be considered before any intervention. Also, expert\nopinion [110] and meta-analysis [140] conﬁrmed the limited evidence that supported the eﬃcacy of the\ntransition from the RFS to the FFS technique.\nIn summary, a speciﬁc foot-strike pattern does not reduce joint contact forces and the\nrecommendations of changing RT should only be based on individual analysis by experts. Evaluation\nmethods based on optimality are interesting for RT analysis; parameters that assess RT at a", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "cy and\nlength where the metabolic cost is lowest) are normally found at freely chosen conditions [135,141,142].\nDetermining these optimal parameters at the individual level seems to be the better way to analyze RT\nin recreational runners.\n6. Factors"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0009_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2e8519b2276aacfc", "text_span_hash": "2e8519b2276aacfc", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "reational runners.\n6. Factors Associated with Running-Related Injuries\nEndurance running is very challenging for muscle and connective tissues because it is a\nweight-bearing activity involving stretch–shortening cycles repeated over time [143]. Longe", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "acute alterations in RT are controversial because their eﬀectiveness to produce beneﬁts are not well\nsupported by current evidence.\nAnother interesting topic that was recently investigated following the concerns of both coaches\nand runners is the eﬀect of forefoot (FFS) and rearfoot strike (RFS) changes on RT, injury risk, and\nperformance. Thus, when evaluating a group of runners using both strike patterns, no mechanical\nadvantages of FFS vs. RFS runners were evident [137]. RFS runners switching to FFS may reduce their\ninjury risk due to the altered distribution of loading between joints, however this should be weighed\nagainst possible performance decrements [137,138]. In another recent study, the retraining from RFS\nto FFS did not aﬀect running economy, but did reduce running-related patellofemoral pain after one\nmonth of training [139]. Therefore, the objectives and the costs of altering the strike pattern in terms\nof injury risk and performance changes must be considered before any intervention. Also, expert\nopinion [110] and meta-analysis [140] conﬁrmed the limited evidence that supported the eﬃcacy of the\ntransition from the RFS to the FFS technique.\nIn summary, a speciﬁc foot-strike pattern does not reduce joint contact forces and the\nrecommendations of changing RT should only be based on individual analysis by experts. Evaluation\nmethods based on optimality are interesting for RT analysis; parameters that assess RT at a", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "reational runners.\n6. Factors Associated with Running-Related Injuries\nEndurance running is very challenging for muscle and connective tissues because it is a\nweight-bearing activity involving stretch–shortening cycles repeated over time [143]. Longe"}
{"chunk_id": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020_p0009_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_5e577a90926d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8c5c05695c9af0a0", "text_span_hash": "8c5c05695c9af0a0", "source_file": "1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\1_recreational_runners_factors_2020.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ture consistently revealed that running-related injuries aﬀect most runners.\nHowever, the exact prevalence of running-related injuries in recreational endurance runners is\ndiﬃcult to ascertain, because studies have been inconsistent when reporting re", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "acute alterations in RT are controversial because their eﬀectiveness to produce beneﬁts are not well\nsupported by current evidence.\nAnother interesting topic that was recently investigated following the concerns of both coaches\nand runners is the eﬀect of forefoot (FFS) and rearfoot strike (RFS) changes on RT, injury risk, and\nperformance. Thus, when evaluating a group of runners using both strike patterns, no mechanical\nadvantages of FFS vs. RFS runners were evident [137]. RFS runners switching to FFS may reduce their\ninjury risk due to the altered distribution of loading between joints, however this should be weighed\nagainst possible performance decrements [137,138]. In another recent study, the retraining from RFS\nto FFS did not aﬀect running economy, but did reduce running-related patellofemoral pain after one\nmonth of training [139]. Therefore, the objectives and the costs of altering the strike pattern in terms\nof injury risk and performance changes must be considered before any intervention. Also, expert\nopinion [110] and meta-analysis [140] conﬁrmed the limited evidence that supported the eﬃcacy of the\ntransition from the RFS to the FFS technique.\nIn summary, a speciﬁc foot-strike pattern does not reduce joint contact forces and the\nrecommendations of changing RT should only be based on individual analysis by experts. Evaluation\nmethods based on optimality are interesting for RT analysis; parameters that assess RT at a", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ture consistently revealed that running-related injuries aﬀect most runners.\nHowever, the exact prevalence of running-related injuries in recreational endurance runners is\ndiﬃcult to ascertain, because studies have been inconsistent when reporting re"}
