ron deﬁciency anemia, which presents the\nmost severe form of iron status abnormalities characterized by hemoglobin con-\ncentrations below the clinical range of 130 g/L in males and 120 g/L in female, is\nsimilar among athletes and general population, the prevalence of iron depletion,\ncharacterized by low i", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "es with adequate iron status, as indicated by serum ferritin above >35 µg/L.\nA study among participants of the Zurich marathon revealed that 15 % of male\nrunners demonstrated serum ferritin concentrations that were indicative of iron\noverload (Mettle"}
{"chunk_id": "2016+-+Nutrition+for+Marathon+Running_p0008_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_093dfe9f5873", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "58f340e14b9e0c99", "text_span_hash": "58f340e14b9e0c99", "source_file": "2016+-+Nutrition+for+Marathon+Running.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\2016+-+Nutrition+for+Marathon+Running.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "runners and other\nendurance athletes. However, consumption of antioxidants in the range of the\nrecommended daily allowance from food sources should provide health beneﬁts\nwithout impairing training responses, and should be highly encouraged in athle", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "The role of dietary antioxidants for marathon runners and other endurance athletes\nremains only poorly understood. It is well documented that due to their increased\noxygen turnover, endurance athletes are exposed to greater levels of oxidative\nstress, and that supplementation of dietary antioxidants, primarily vitamin C and\nvitamin E, can attenuate measures of oxidative stress (Gomez-Cabrera et al. 2015).\nHowever, recent evidence suggests that the generation of oxidative stress per se is\nnot critical and in fact may trigger cellular mechanism that are required for optimal\ntraining responses to aerobic exercise training. Because supplementation of high\ndoses of dietary antioxidants may have the capacity to attenuate the beneﬁcial\neffects of chronic exercise training (Gomez-Cabrera et al. 2015; Braakhuis 2012),\nantioxidant supplementation is not recommended for marathon runners and other\nendurance athletes. However, consumption of antioxidants in the range of the\nrecommended daily allowance from food sources should provide health beneﬁts\nwithout impairing training responses, and should be highly encouraged in athletes\n(Pingitore et al. 2015; Braakhuis 2012).\nTable 3.1 Stages of Iron status abnormalities, their effects on marathon-related performance, and\ntheir prevalence in marathon runners\nPerformance and health effectsc\nUnclear, but supplementation\nImpaired marathon performance,\nimmunosuppression, reduced bone\nstrength; revers", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "runners and other\nendurance athletes. However, consumption of antioxidants in the range of the\nrecommended daily allowance from food sources should provide health beneﬁts\nwithout impairing training responses, and should be highly encouraged in athle"}
{"chunk_id": "2016+-+Nutrition+for+Marathon+Running_p0008_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_093dfe9f5873", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4725a400612518af", "text_span_hash": "4725a400612518af", "source_file": "2016+-+Nutrition+for+Marathon+Running.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\2016+-+Nutrition+for+Marathon+Running.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "be highly encouraged in athletes\n(Pingitore et al. 2015; Braakhuis 2012).\nTable 3.1 Stages of Iron status abnormalities, their effects on marathon-related performance, and\ntheir prevalence in marathon runners\nPerformance and health effectsc\nUnclear,", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "The role of dietary antioxidants for marathon runners and other endurance athletes\nremains only poorly understood. It is well documented that due to their increased\noxygen turnover, endurance athletes are exposed to greater levels of oxidative\nstress, and that supplementation of dietary antioxidants, primarily vitamin C and\nvitamin E, can attenuate measures of oxidative stress (Gomez-Cabrera et al. 2015).\nHowever, recent evidence suggests that the generation of oxidative stress per se is\nnot critical and in fact may trigger cellular mechanism that are required for optimal\ntraining responses to aerobic exercise training. Because supplementation of high\ndoses of dietary antioxidants may have the capacity to attenuate the beneﬁcial\neffects of chronic exercise training (Gomez-Cabrera et al. 2015; Braakhuis 2012),\nantioxidant supplementation is not recommended for marathon runners and other\nendurance athletes. However, consumption of antioxidants in the range of the\nrecommended daily allowance from food sources should provide health beneﬁts\nwithout impairing training responses, and should be highly encouraged in athletes\n(Pingitore et al. 2015; Braakhuis 2012).\nTable 3.1 Stages of Iron status abnormalities, their effects on marathon-related performance, and\ntheir prevalence in marathon runners\nPerformance and health effectsc\nUnclear, but supplementation\nImpaired marathon performance,\nimmunosuppression, reduced bone\nstrength; revers", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "be highly encouraged in athletes\n(Pingitore et al. 2015; Braakhuis 2012).\nTable 3.1 Stages of Iron status abnormalities, their effects on marathon-related performance, and\ntheir prevalence in marathon runners\nPerformance and health effectsc\nUnclear,"}
{"chunk_id": "2016+-+Nutrition+for+Marathon+Running_p0009_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_093dfe9f5873", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2dd4025887d20c02", "text_span_hash": "2dd4025887d20c02", "source_file": "2016+-+Nutrition+for+Marathon+Running.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\2016+-+Nutrition+for+Marathon+Running.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Training of Race Strategies\nParticularly during later stages of marathon training, athletes are encouraged to\nincorporate race-speciﬁc strategies into their training regimen. Strategies that may\nwarrant familiarization include CHO loading strategies", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training of Race Strategies\nParticularly during later stages of marathon training, athletes are encouraged to\nincorporate race-speciﬁc strategies into their training regimen. Strategies that may\nwarrant familiarization include CHO loading strategies as well as the consumption\nof CHO and ﬂuid under race-like conditions. These strategies will be discussed in\ndetail in the next section (Table 3.2).\nTable 3.2 Recommended nutritional strategies during marathon training\nWeight stability: meet\nnegative metabolic and\nEnergy expenditure of\nrunning: *1 kcal/kg/km\nMeet CHO requirements\nElevated during periods\nPrevent iron depletion\nHighly bioavailable iron\n(heme iron); facilitate\niron absorption (vitamin\nMonitor iron status 1–2\nSingle training sessions in\n1. Overnight fasted,\nreduce frequency closer\naLoucks et al. (2011)\ndFood and Nutrition Board (2015)\neStellingwerff (2012)\nfEnergy Availability = Dietary Energy Intake −Exercise Energy Expenditure\nNutrition for Marathon Running", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Training of Race Strategies\nParticularly during later stages of marathon training, athletes are encouraged to\nincorporate race-speciﬁc strategies into their training regimen. Strategies that may\nwarrant familiarization include CHO loading strategies"}
