ng volume from\nzone 1 (at or below speed at ﬁrst ventilatory/lactate threshold [LT]) to zone 2 (between speeds associated with either both\nventilatory thresholds or 2 and 4 mmol·L−1 LTs [vLT1 and vLT2, respectively]) and zone 3 (speed above vVT2/vLT2).\nContinuous-tempo runs or interval t", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "a pyramidal TID approach, characterized by a decreasing training volume from\nzone 1 (at or below speed at ﬁrst ventilatory/lactate threshold [LT]) to zone 2 (between speeds associated with either both\nventilatory thresholds or 2 and 4 mmol·L−1 LTs [v"}
{"chunk_id": "2_elite_periodization_casado_2022_p0001_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_df9ead131193", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7ae912f299f8f656", "text_span_hash": "7ae912f299f8f656", "source_file": "2_elite_periodization_casado_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\2_elite_periodization_casado_2022.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "lds or 2 and 4 mmol·L−1 LTs [vLT1 and vLT2, respectively]) and zone 3 (speed above vVT2/vLT2).\nContinuous-tempo runs or interval training sessions at vLT2 in zone 2 (ie, medium and long aerobic intervals) and those in zone 3\n(ie, anaerobic or short-i", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training Periodization, Methods, Intensity Distribution, and Volume\nin Highly Trained and Elite Distance Runners: A Systematic Review\nArturo Casado,1 Fernando González-Mohíno,2,3 José María González-Ravé,2 and Carl Foster4\n1Center for Sport Studies, Rey Juan Carlos University, Madrid, Spain; 2Sport Training Lab, University of Castilla-La Mancha, Toledo, Toledo, Spain;\n3Facultad de Ciencias de la Vida y de la Naturaleza, Universidad Nebrija, Madrid, Spain; 4Department of Exercise and Sport Science,\nUniversity of Wisconsin, La Crosse, WI, USA\nPurpose: This review aimed to determine (1) performance and training characteristics such as training intensity distribution\n(TID), volume, periodization, and methods in highly trained/elite distance runners and (2) differences in training volume and TID\nbetween event distances in highly trained/elite distance runners. Methods: A systematic review of the literature was carried out\nusing the PubMed/MEDLINE, Scopus, and Web of Science databases. Results: Ten articles met the inclusion criteria. Highly\ntrained/elite distance runners typically follow a pyramidal TID approach, characterized by a decreasing training volume from\nzone 1 (at or below speed at ﬁrst ventilatory/lactate threshold [LT]) to zone 2 (between speeds associated with either both\nventilatory thresholds or 2 and 4 mmol·L−1 LTs [vLT1 and vLT2, respectively]) and zone 3 (speed above vVT2/vLT2).\nContinuous-tempo runs or interval t", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "lds or 2 and 4 mmol·L−1 LTs [vLT1 and vLT2, respectively]) and zone 3 (speed above vVT2/vLT2).\nContinuous-tempo runs or interval training sessions at vLT2 in zone 2 (ie, medium and long aerobic intervals) and those in zone 3\n(ie, anaerobic or short-i"}
{"chunk_id": "2_elite_periodization_casado_2022_p0001_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_df9ead131193", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3c00e02c843f1d67", "text_span_hash": "3c00e02c843f1d67", "source_file": "2_elite_periodization_casado_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\2_elite_periodization_casado_2022.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ne 3\n(ie, anaerobic or short-interval training) were both used at least once per week each in elite runners, and they were used to increase\nthe number of either vLT2 or z3 sessions to adopt either a pyramidal or a polarized approach, respectively. Mo", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training Periodization, Methods, Intensity Distribution, and Volume\nin Highly Trained and Elite Distance Runners: A Systematic Review\nArturo Casado,1 Fernando González-Mohíno,2,3 José María González-Ravé,2 and Carl Foster4\n1Center for Sport Studies, Rey Juan Carlos University, Madrid, Spain; 2Sport Training Lab, University of Castilla-La Mancha, Toledo, Toledo, Spain;\n3Facultad de Ciencias de la Vida y de la Naturaleza, Universidad Nebrija, Madrid, Spain; 4Department of Exercise and Sport Science,\nUniversity of Wisconsin, La Crosse, WI, USA\nPurpose: This review aimed to determine (1) performance and training characteristics such as training intensity distribution\n(TID), volume, periodization, and methods in highly trained/elite distance runners and (2) differences in training volume and TID\nbetween event distances in highly trained/elite distance runners. Methods: A systematic review of the literature was carried out\nusing the PubMed/MEDLINE, Scopus, and Web of Science databases. Results: Ten articles met the inclusion criteria. Highly\ntrained/elite distance runners typically follow a pyramidal TID approach, characterized by a decreasing training volume from\nzone 1 (at or below speed at ﬁrst ventilatory/lactate threshold [LT]) to zone 2 (between speeds associated with either both\nventilatory thresholds or 2 and 4 mmol·L−1 LTs [vLT1 and vLT2, respectively]) and zone 3 (speed above vVT2/vLT2).\nContinuous-tempo runs or interval t", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ne 3\n(ie, anaerobic or short-interval training) were both used at least once per week each in elite runners, and they were used to increase\nthe number of either vLT2 or z3 sessions to adopt either a pyramidal or a polarized approach, respectively. Mo"}
{"chunk_id": "2_elite_periodization_casado_2022_p0001_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_df9ead131193", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2bb85ae426f7a912", "text_span_hash": "2bb85ae426f7a912", "source_file": "2_elite_periodization_casado_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\2_elite_periodization_casado_2022.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "zed approach, respectively. More pyramidal- and\npolarized-oriented approaches were used by marathoners and 1500-m runners, respectively. Conclusions: Highly trained and\nelite middle- and long-distance runners are encouraged to adopt a traditional per", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training Periodization, Methods, Intensity Distribution, and Volume\nin Highly Trained and Elite Distance Runners: A Systematic Review\nArturo Casado,1 Fernando González-Mohíno,2,3 José María González-Ravé,2 and Carl Foster4\n1Center for Sport Studies, Rey Juan Carlos University, Madrid, Spain; 2Sport Training Lab, University of Castilla-La Mancha, Toledo, Toledo, Spain;\n3Facultad de Ciencias de la Vida y de la Naturaleza, Universidad Nebrija, Madrid, Spain; 4Department of Exercise and Sport Science,\nUniversity of Wisconsin, La Crosse, WI, USA\nPurpose: This review aimed to determine (1) performance and training characteristics such as training intensity distribution\n(TID), volume, periodization, and methods in highly trained/elite distance runners and (2) differences in training volume and TID\nbetween event distances in highly trained/elite distance runners. Methods: A systematic review of the literature was carried out\nusing the PubMed/MEDLINE, Scopus, and Web of Science databases. Results: Ten articles met the inclusion criteria. Highly\ntrained/elite distance runners typically follow a pyramidal TID approach, characterized by a decreasing training volume from\nzone 1 (at or below speed at ﬁrst ventilatory/lactate threshold [LT]) to zone 2 (between speeds associated with either both\nventilatory thresholds or 2 and 4 mmol·L−1 LTs [vLT1 and vLT2, respectively]) and zone 3 (speed above vVT2/vLT2).\nContinuous-tempo runs or interval t", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "zed approach, respectively. More pyramidal- and\npolarized-oriented approaches were used by marathoners and 1500-m runners, respectively. Conclusions: Highly trained and\nelite middle- and long-distance runners are encouraged to adopt a traditional per"}
