training has been found to be a very effective\nTID approach to improve performance in well-trained and elite\nendurance athletes,11 and in middle- and long-distance runners,13 it\nseems that pyramidal TID is a more commonly used approach in\nhighly trained and elite middle- and long-distance runners. ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "The main ﬁnding of the present review is that highly trained\nmiddle- and long-distance runners typically follow a pyramidal\nTID characterized by conducting much of the training within z2 at\nor just below vLT2. This kind of TID was related to high lev"}
{"chunk_id": "2_elite_periodization_casado_2022_p0010_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_df9ead131193", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "14d744d70263d7cf", "text_span_hash": "14d744d70263d7cf", "source_file": "2_elite_periodization_casado_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\2_elite_periodization_casado_2022.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "of TID was related to high levels of\nperformance and a signiﬁcant development of physiological\ndeterminants. Furthermore, only linear traditional periodization\nmodels have been observed in the limited number of studies\nconducted in this population. I", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "6.43% (0.47%), respectively, for 1500-m runners. Mean (SD) of\npercentages of training time (minutes) at z2 + z3 were 24.15%\nMean (SD) of training distance per week were 195.38 (6.69)\nkm·wk−1 for marathoners and 154.63 (4.37) km·wk−1 for 1500-\nm runners. Effect size were always nearly perfect (>4.42) between\ngroups in running distance, and training zones (Figure 2C and 2D).\nMost z2 training conducted by all the runners was just below or\nThe main ﬁnding of the present review is that highly trained\nmiddle- and long-distance runners typically follow a pyramidal\nTID characterized by conducting much of the training within z2 at\nor just below vLT2. This kind of TID was related to high levels of\nperformance and a signiﬁcant development of physiological\ndeterminants. Furthermore, only linear traditional periodization\nmodels have been observed in the limited number of studies\nconducted in this population. In addition, regardless of the racing\ndistance being prepared for, a strong majority of training (76%–\n87%) was conducted in z1, and most athletes used a clearly hard-\nday, easy-day approach.\nTraining Intensity Distribution\nWhereas polarized training has been found to be a very effective\nTID approach to improve performance in well-trained and elite\nendurance athletes,11 and in middle- and long-distance runners,13 it\nseems that pyramidal TID is a more commonly used approach in\nhighly trained and elite middle- and long-distance runners. ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "of TID was related to high levels of\nperformance and a signiﬁcant development of physiological\ndeterminants. Furthermore, only linear traditional periodization\nmodels have been observed in the limited number of studies\nconducted in this population. I"}
{"chunk_id": "2_elite_periodization_casado_2022_p0010_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_df9ead131193", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4225f5abac5d0774", "text_span_hash": "4225f5abac5d0774", "source_file": "2_elite_periodization_casado_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\2_elite_periodization_casado_2022.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Training Intensity Distribution\nWhereas polarized training has been found to be a very effective\nTID approach to improve performance in well-trained and elite\nendurance athletes,11 and in middle- and long-distance runners,13 it\nseems that pyramidal", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "6.43% (0.47%), respectively, for 1500-m runners. Mean (SD) of\npercentages of training time (minutes) at z2 + z3 were 24.15%\nMean (SD) of training distance per week were 195.38 (6.69)\nkm·wk−1 for marathoners and 154.63 (4.37) km·wk−1 for 1500-\nm runners. Effect size were always nearly perfect (>4.42) between\ngroups in running distance, and training zones (Figure 2C and 2D).\nMost z2 training conducted by all the runners was just below or\nThe main ﬁnding of the present review is that highly trained\nmiddle- and long-distance runners typically follow a pyramidal\nTID characterized by conducting much of the training within z2 at\nor just below vLT2. This kind of TID was related to high levels of\nperformance and a signiﬁcant development of physiological\ndeterminants. Furthermore, only linear traditional periodization\nmodels have been observed in the limited number of studies\nconducted in this population. In addition, regardless of the racing\ndistance being prepared for, a strong majority of training (76%–\n87%) was conducted in z1, and most athletes used a clearly hard-\nday, easy-day approach.\nTraining Intensity Distribution\nWhereas polarized training has been found to be a very effective\nTID approach to improve performance in well-trained and elite\nendurance athletes,11 and in middle- and long-distance runners,13 it\nseems that pyramidal TID is a more commonly used approach in\nhighly trained and elite middle- and long-distance runners. ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Training Intensity Distribution\nWhereas polarized training has been found to be a very effective\nTID approach to improve performance in well-trained and elite\nendurance athletes,11 and in middle- and long-distance runners,13 it\nseems that pyramidal"}
{"chunk_id": "2_elite_periodization_casado_2022_p0010_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_df9ead131193", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1ea109622c9d13f9", "text_span_hash": "1ea109622c9d13f9", "source_file": "2_elite_periodization_casado_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\2_elite_periodization_casado_2022.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "rs,13 it\nseems that pyramidal TID is a more commonly used approach in\nhighly trained and elite middle- and long-distance runners. This\npyramidal approach has also been observed in the training used by\nother highly trained and elite athletes such as a", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "6.43% (0.47%), respectively, for 1500-m runners. Mean (SD) of\npercentages of training time (minutes) at z2 + z3 were 24.15%\nMean (SD) of training distance per week were 195.38 (6.69)\nkm·wk−1 for marathoners and 154.63 (4.37) km·wk−1 for 1500-\nm runners. Effect size were always nearly perfect (>4.42) between\ngroups in running distance, and training zones (Figure 2C and 2D).\nMost z2 training conducted by all the runners was just below or\nThe main ﬁnding of the present review is that highly trained\nmiddle- and long-distance runners typically follow a pyramidal\nTID characterized by conducting much of the training within z2 at\nor just below vLT2. This kind of TID was related to high levels of\nperformance and a signiﬁcant development of physiological\ndeterminants. Furthermore, only linear traditional periodization\nmodels have been observed in the limited number of studies\nconducted in this population. In addition, regardless of the racing\ndistance being prepared for, a strong majority of training (76%–\n87%) was conducted in z1, and most athletes used a clearly hard-\nday, easy-day approach.\nTraining Intensity Distribution\nWhereas polarized training has been found to be a very effective\nTID approach to improve performance in well-trained and elite\nendurance athletes,11 and in middle- and long-distance runners,13 it\nseems that pyramidal TID is a more commonly used approach in\nhighly trained and elite middle- and long-distance runners. ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "rs,13 it\nseems that pyramidal TID is a more commonly used approach in\nhighly trained and elite middle- and long-distance runners. This\npyramidal approach has also been observed in the training used by\nother highly trained and elite athletes such as a"}
{"chunk_id": "2_elite_periodization_casado_2022_p0010_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_df9ead131193", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5404328b1bdd84f1", "text_span_hash": "5404328b1bdd84f1", "source_file": "2_elite_periodization_casado_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\2_elite_periodization_casado_2022.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "d and elite athletes such as a group of nationally\nrank New Zealand distance runners,35 the Norwegian marathoner\nFigure 2 — Training intensity distribution during a training week in 4 world-class 1500-m runners (A) and 4 world-class marathoners (B),", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "6.43% (0.47%), respectively, for 1500-m runners. Mean (SD) of\npercentages of training time (minutes) at z2 + z3 were 24.15%\nMean (SD) of training distance per week were 195.38 (6.69)\nkm·wk−1 for marathoners and 154.63 (4.37) km·wk−1 for 1500-\nm runners. Effect size were always nearly perfect (>4.42) between\ngroups in running distance, and training zones (Figure 2C and 2D).\nMost z2 training conducted by all the runners was just below or\nThe main ﬁnding of the present review is that highly trained\nmiddle- and long-distance runners typically follow a pyramidal\nTID characterized by conducting much of the training within z2 at\nor just below vLT2. This kind of TID was related to high levels of\nperformance and a signiﬁcant development of physiological\ndeterminants. Furthermore, only linear traditional periodization\nmodels have been observed in the limited number of studies\nconducted in this population. In addition, regardless of the racing\ndistance being prepared for, a strong majority of training (76%–\n87%) was conducted in z1, and most athletes used a clearly hard-\nday, easy-day approach.\nTraining Intensity Distribution\nWhereas polarized training has been found to be a very effective\nTID approach to improve performance in well-trained and elite\nendurance athletes,11 and in middle- and long-distance runners,13 it\nseems that pyramidal TID is a more commonly used approach in\nhighly trained and elite middle- and long-distance runners. ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "d and elite athletes such as a group of nationally\nrank New Zealand distance runners,35 the Norwegian marathoner\nFigure 2 — Training intensity distribution during a training week in 4 world-class 1500-m runners (A) and 4 world-class marathoners (B),"}
{"chunk_id": "2_elite_periodization_casado_2022_p0010_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_df9ead131193", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1e4cce95fb118a8f", "text_span_hash": "1e4cce95fb118a8f", "source_file": "2_elite_periodization_casado_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\2_elite_periodization_casado_2022.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "world-class marathoners (B), comparison\nbetween world-class 1500-m runners and marathoners in training intensity distribution based on a 3-zone model (C), and a 2-zone (z1 and z2 + z3) model\n(D) and hypothetical periodization characteristics in a hi", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "6.43% (0.47%), respectively, for 1500-m runners. Mean (SD) of\npercentages of training time (minutes) at z2 + z3 were 24.15%\nMean (SD) of training distance per week were 195.38 (6.69)\nkm·wk−1 for marathoners and 154.63 (4.37) km·wk−1 for 1500-\nm runners. Effect size were always nearly perfect (>4.42) between\ngroups in running distance, and training zones (Figure 2C and 2D).\nMost z2 training conducted by all the runners was just below or\nThe main ﬁnding of the present review is that highly trained\nmiddle- and long-distance runners typically follow a pyramidal\nTID characterized by conducting much of the training within z2 at\nor just below vLT2. This kind of TID was related to high levels of\nperformance and a signiﬁcant development of physiological\ndeterminants. Furthermore, only linear traditional periodization\nmodels have been observed in the limited number of studies\nconducted in this population. In addition, regardless of the racing\ndistance being prepared for, a strong majority of training (76%–\n87%) was conducted in z1, and most athletes used a clearly hard-\nday, easy-day approach.\nTraining Intensity Distribution\nWhereas polarized training has been found to be a very effective\nTID approach to improve performance in well-trained and elite\nendurance athletes,11 and in middle- and long-distance runners,13 it\nseems that pyramidal TID is a more commonly used approach in\nhighly trained and elite middle- and long-distance runners. ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "world-class marathoners (B), comparison\nbetween world-class 1500-m runners and marathoners in training intensity distribution based on a 3-zone model (C), and a 2-zone (z1 and z2 + z3) model\n(D) and hypothetical periodization characteristics in a hi"}
{"chunk_id": "2_elite_periodization_casado_2022_p0010_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_df9ead131193", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "bf2ebea51c940669", "text_span_hash": "bf2ebea51c940669", "source_file": "2_elite_periodization_casado_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\2_elite_periodization_casado_2022.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "zation characteristics in a highly trained/elite distance runner (E). z1 indicates zone 1; z2, zone 2; z3, zone 3. *Cohen d effect\nsizes at least nearly perfect (>4); data were provided by Kenneally et al23 with permission and personal communication", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "6.43% (0.47%), respectively, for 1500-m runners. Mean (SD) of\npercentages of training time (minutes) at z2 + z3 were 24.15%\nMean (SD) of training distance per week were 195.38 (6.69)\nkm·wk−1 for marathoners and 154.63 (4.37) km·wk−1 for 1500-\nm runners. Effect size were always nearly perfect (>4.42) between\ngroups in running distance, and training zones (Figure 2C and 2D).\nMost z2 training conducted by all the runners was just below or\nThe main ﬁnding of the present review is that highly trained\nmiddle- and long-distance runners typically follow a pyramidal\nTID characterized by conducting much of the training within z2 at\nor just below vLT2. This kind of TID was related to high levels of\nperformance and a signiﬁcant development of physiological\ndeterminants. Furthermore, only linear traditional periodization\nmodels have been observed in the limited number of studies\nconducted in this population. In addition, regardless of the racing\ndistance being prepared for, a strong majority of training (76%–\n87%) was conducted in z1, and most athletes used a clearly hard-\nday, easy-day approach.\nTraining Intensity Distribution\nWhereas polarized training has been found to be a very effective\nTID approach to improve performance in well-trained and elite\nendurance athletes,11 and in middle- and long-distance runners,13 it\nseems that pyramidal TID is a more commonly used approach in\nhighly trained and elite middle- and long-distance runners. ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "zation characteristics in a highly trained/elite distance runner (E). z1 indicates zone 1; z2, zone 2; z3, zone 3. *Cohen d effect\nsizes at least nearly perfect (>4); data were provided by Kenneally et al23 with permission and personal communication"}
{"chunk_id": "2_elite_periodization_casado_2022_p0010_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_df9ead131193", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "84de3aed198e117d", "text_span_hash": "84de3aed198e117d", "source_file": "2_elite_periodization_casado_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\2_elite_periodization_casado_2022.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "on and personal communication with the coach of this training\nTraining Characteristics and Performance in Highly Trained Runners\nUnauthenticated | Downloaded 06/08/26 09:35 PM UTC", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "6.43% (0.47%), respectively, for 1500-m runners. Mean (SD) of\npercentages of training time (minutes) at z2 + z3 were 24.15%\nMean (SD) of training distance per week were 195.38 (6.69)\nkm·wk−1 for marathoners and 154.63 (4.37) km·wk−1 for 1500-\nm runners. Effect size were always nearly perfect (>4.42) between\ngroups in running distance, and training zones (Figure 2C and 2D).\nMost z2 training conducted by all the runners was just below or\nThe main ﬁnding of the present review is that highly trained\nmiddle- and long-distance runners typically follow a pyramidal\nTID characterized by conducting much of the training within z2 at\nor just below vLT2. This kind of TID was related to high levels of\nperformance and a signiﬁcant development of physiological\ndeterminants. Furthermore, only linear traditional periodization\nmodels have been observed in the limited number of studies\nconducted in this population. In addition, regardless of the racing\ndistance being prepared for, a strong majority of training (76%–\n87%) was conducted in z1, and most athletes used a clearly hard-\nday, easy-day approach.\nTraining Intensity Distribution\nWhereas polarized training has been found to be a very effective\nTID approach to improve performance in well-trained and elite\nendurance athletes,11 and in middle- and long-distance runners,13 it\nseems that pyramidal TID is a more commonly used approach in\nhighly trained and elite middle- and long-distance runners. ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "on and personal communication with the coach of this training\nTraining Characteristics and Performance in Highly Trained Runners\nUnauthenticated | Downloaded 06/08/26 09:35 PM UTC"}
{"chunk_id": "2_elite_periodization_casado_2022_p0011_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_df9ead131193", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "aed99cfd9d925530", "text_span_hash": "aed99cfd9d925530", "source_file": "2_elite_periodization_casado_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\2_elite_periodization_casado_2022.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Grete Waitz,36 top-class Portuguese and French marathoners,37 or\nhighly trained subelite middle-distance Spanish runners.38 Addi-\ntionally, improvements in performance have been reported after\n5 months of pyramidal TID in highly trained subelite midd", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Grete Waitz,36 top-class Portuguese and French marathoners,37 or\nhighly trained subelite middle-distance Spanish runners.38 Addi-\ntionally, improvements in performance have been reported after\n5 months of pyramidal TID in highly trained subelite middle- and\nlong-distance runners on a cross-country time trial.2 Accordingly,\ndifferent studies have found that training conducted at vLT2\nintensity is associated with improvement in either performance\nphysiological determinants such as VO2max and maximum anaer-\nobic power in highly trained middle- and long-distance runners,39\nor performance in world-class long-distance runners.40,41 While\nthe mechanistic explanation for the relationship between training\nconducted near vLT2 and the improvement in performance and its\nphysiological determinants cannot be elucidated yet, it has been\nproposed that exercising at this speciﬁc intensity improves mus-\ncle-speciﬁc clearing of lactate, as opposed to reducing lactate\nproduction mechanisms.42 Since only recruited motor units are\nlikely to experience increases in mitochondrial and capillary\ndensity, it may be speculated that training near vLT2 optimizes\nthe number of motor units recruited without the consequences of\nelevated levels of catecholamines likely to be experienced with z3\nFurthermore, speciﬁc training characteristics in highly trained\nand elite distance runners appear to inﬂuence performance and\nphysiological determinants globally. For e", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Grete Waitz,36 top-class Portuguese and French marathoners,37 or\nhighly trained subelite middle-distance Spanish runners.38 Addi-\ntionally, improvements in performance have been reported after\n5 months of pyramidal TID in highly trained subelite midd"}
{"chunk_id": "2_elite_periodization_casado_2022_p0011_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_df9ead131193", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9ad83f8d4a0537ca", "text_span_hash": "9ad83f8d4a0537ca", "source_file": "2_elite_periodization_casado_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\2_elite_periodization_casado_2022.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "n highly trained subelite middle- and\nlong-distance runners on a cross-country time trial.2 Accordingly,\ndifferent studies have found that training conducted at vLT2\nintensity is associated with improvement in either performance\nphysiological determi", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Grete Waitz,36 top-class Portuguese and French marathoners,37 or\nhighly trained subelite middle-distance Spanish runners.38 Addi-\ntionally, improvements in performance have been reported after\n5 months of pyramidal TID in highly trained subelite middle- and\nlong-distance runners on a cross-country time trial.2 Accordingly,\ndifferent studies have found that training conducted at vLT2\nintensity is associated with improvement in either performance\nphysiological determinants such as VO2max and maximum anaer-\nobic power in highly trained middle- and long-distance runners,39\nor performance in world-class long-distance runners.40,41 While\nthe mechanistic explanation for the relationship between training\nconducted near vLT2 and the improvement in performance and its\nphysiological determinants cannot be elucidated yet, it has been\nproposed that exercising at this speciﬁc intensity improves mus-\ncle-speciﬁc clearing of lactate, as opposed to reducing lactate\nproduction mechanisms.42 Since only recruited motor units are\nlikely to experience increases in mitochondrial and capillary\ndensity, it may be speculated that training near vLT2 optimizes\nthe number of motor units recruited without the consequences of\nelevated levels of catecholamines likely to be experienced with z3\nFurthermore, speciﬁc training characteristics in highly trained\nand elite distance runners appear to inﬂuence performance and\nphysiological determinants globally. For e", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "n highly trained subelite middle- and\nlong-distance runners on a cross-country time trial.2 Accordingly,\ndifferent studies have found that training conducted at vLT2\nintensity is associated with improvement in either performance\nphysiological determi"}
{"chunk_id": "2_elite_periodization_casado_2022_p0011_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_df9ead131193", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "6efa30a6ed101f86", "text_span_hash": "6efa30a6ed101f86", "source_file": "2_elite_periodization_casado_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\2_elite_periodization_casado_2022.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "formance\nphysiological determinants such as VO2max and maximum anaer-\nobic power in highly trained middle- and long-distance runners,39\nor performance in world-class long-distance runners.40,41 While\nthe mechanistic explanation for the relationship b", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Grete Waitz,36 top-class Portuguese and French marathoners,37 or\nhighly trained subelite middle-distance Spanish runners.38 Addi-\ntionally, improvements in performance have been reported after\n5 months of pyramidal TID in highly trained subelite middle- and\nlong-distance runners on a cross-country time trial.2 Accordingly,\ndifferent studies have found that training conducted at vLT2\nintensity is associated with improvement in either performance\nphysiological determinants such as VO2max and maximum anaer-\nobic power in highly trained middle- and long-distance runners,39\nor performance in world-class long-distance runners.40,41 While\nthe mechanistic explanation for the relationship between training\nconducted near vLT2 and the improvement in performance and its\nphysiological determinants cannot be elucidated yet, it has been\nproposed that exercising at this speciﬁc intensity improves mus-\ncle-speciﬁc clearing of lactate, as opposed to reducing lactate\nproduction mechanisms.42 Since only recruited motor units are\nlikely to experience increases in mitochondrial and capillary\ndensity, it may be speculated that training near vLT2 optimizes\n