erfor-\nmance and its physiological determinants according to the\nspeciﬁc athlete and competitive goal. Within these different\ncombinations observed, interval training methods typically\nwere conducted at z3 and ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "and more speciﬁcally that conducted at vLT2\nTraining Characteristics and Performance in Highly Trained Runners\nUnauthenticated | Downloaded 06/08/26 09:35 PM UTC"}
{"chunk_id": "2_elite_periodization_casado_2022_p0013_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_df9ead131193", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "627f2c8c6fd22884", "text_span_hash": "627f2c8c6fd22884", "source_file": "2_elite_periodization_casado_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\2_elite_periodization_casado_2022.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "seems to be associated with both performance and the enhancement\nof physiological determinants in highly trained and elite distance\nrunners. Continuous tempo runs, and long and medium aerobic\ninterval training with short recovery periods, are the met", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "seems to be associated with both performance and the enhancement\nof physiological determinants in highly trained and elite distance\nrunners. Continuous tempo runs, and long and medium aerobic\ninterval training with short recovery periods, are the methods which\nthese runners use to train at vLT2.\nPractical Applications\nHighly trained/elite middle- and long-distance runners are encour-\naged to accumulate >100 km·wk−1 while following a pyramidal\nTID approach on a hard day–easy day basis. A polarized pattern\nmight be also effective. Linear periodization is generally recom-\nmended for this population although further research is needed to\nunderstand whether other periodization models are effective in\nimproving performance and physiological determinants. Runners\nshould decrease the amount of training at vLT2 (z2), as well as\nincrease the amount of training in z3 (race pace) during the\nAuthors gratefully thank the training details provided by coach Nic Bideau\nand his athletes for the completion of the present study. They also\nappreciate the valuable feedback provided by Dr Trent Stellingwerff,\nwhich helped to improve the quality of the present article. Author\ncontributions: Casado and González-Mohíno collected the data, analyzed\nthe data, and wrote the manuscript; González-Ravé collected the data and\nwrote the manuscript; Foster wrote the manuscript and reviewed a draft of\n1. Billat LV, Koralsztein JP. Signiﬁcance of the velocity at ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "seems to be associated with both performance and the enhancement\nof physiological determinants in highly trained and elite distance\nrunners. Continuous tempo runs, and long and medium aerobic\ninterval training with short recovery periods, are the met"}
