{"chunk_id": "2_elite_periodization_casado_2022_p0013_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_df9ead131193", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9648b6e1f99b4525", "text_span_hash": "9648b6e1f99b4525", "source_file": "2_elite_periodization_casado_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\2_elite_periodization_casado_2022.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "the manuscript; Foster wrote the manuscript and reviewed a draft of\n1. Billat LV, Koralsztein JP. Signiﬁcance of the velocity at ˙VO2max and\ntime to exhaustion at this velocity. Sports Med. 1996;22(2):90–108.\n2. Esteve-Lanao J, Foster C, Seiler S, L", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "seems to be associated with both performance and the enhancement\nof physiological determinants in highly trained and elite distance\nrunners. Continuous tempo runs, and long and medium aerobic\ninterval training with short recovery periods, are the methods which\nthese runners use to train at vLT2.\nPractical Applications\nHighly trained/elite middle- and long-distance runners are encour-\naged to accumulate >100 km·wk−1 while following a pyramidal\nTID approach on a hard day–easy day basis. A polarized pattern\nmight be also effective. Linear periodization is generally recom-\nmended for this population although further research is needed to\nunderstand whether other periodization models are effective in\nimproving performance and physiological determinants. Runners\nshould decrease the amount of training at vLT2 (z2), as well as\nincrease the amount of training in z3 (race pace) during the\nAuthors gratefully thank the training details provided by coach Nic Bideau\nand his athletes for the completion of the present study. They also\nappreciate the valuable feedback provided by Dr Trent Stellingwerff,\nwhich helped to improve the quality of the present article. Author\ncontributions: Casado and González-Mohíno collected the data, analyzed\nthe data, and wrote the manuscript; González-Ravé collected the data and\nwrote the manuscript; Foster wrote the manuscript and reviewed a draft of\n1. Billat LV, Koralsztein JP. Signiﬁcance of the velocity at ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "the manuscript; Foster wrote the manuscript and reviewed a draft of\n1. Billat LV, Koralsztein JP. Signiﬁcance of the velocity at ˙VO2max and\ntime to exhaustion at this velocity. Sports Med. 1996;22(2):90–108.\n2. Esteve-Lanao J, Foster C, Seiler S, L"}
{"chunk_id": "2_elite_periodization_casado_2022_p0013_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_df9ead131193", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b0106d938e0531a0", "text_span_hash": "b0106d938e0531a0", "source_file": "2_elite_periodization_casado_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\2_elite_periodization_casado_2022.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Lanao J, Foster C, Seiler S, Lucia A. Impact of training\nintensity distribution on performance in endurance athletes. J\n3. Billat LV. Use of blood lactate measurements for prediction of\nexercise performance and for control of training. Recommendation", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "seems to be associated with both performance and the enhancement\nof physiological determinants in highly trained and elite distance\nrunners. Continuous tempo runs, and long and medium aerobic\ninterval training with short recovery periods, are the methods which\nthese runners use to train at vLT2.\nPractical Applications\nHighly trained/elite middle- and long-distance runners are encour-\naged to accumulate >100 km·wk−1 while following a pyramidal\nTID approach on a hard day–easy day basis. A polarized pattern\nmight be also effective. Linear periodization is generally recom-\nmended for this population although further research is needed to\nunderstand whether other periodization models are effective in\nimproving performance and physiological determinants. Runners\nshould decrease the amount of training at vLT2 (z2), as well as\nincrease the amount of training in z3 (race pace) during the\nAuthors gratefully thank the training details provided by coach Nic Bideau\nand his athletes for the completion of the present study. They also\nappreciate the valuable feedback provided by Dr Trent Stellingwerff,\nwhich helped to improve the quality of the present article. Author\ncontributions: Casado and González-Mohíno collected the data, analyzed\nthe data, and wrote the manuscript; González-Ravé collected the data and\nwrote the manuscript; Foster wrote the manuscript and reviewed a draft of\n1. Billat LV, Koralsztein JP. Signiﬁcance of the velocity at ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Lanao J, Foster C, Seiler S, Lucia A. Impact of training\nintensity distribution on performance in endurance athletes. J\n3. Billat LV. Use of blood lactate measurements for prediction of\nexercise performance and for control of training. Recommendation"}
{"chunk_id": "2_elite_periodization_casado_2022_p0013_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_df9ead131193", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "03243969d0977548", "text_span_hash": "03243969d0977548", "source_file": "2_elite_periodization_casado_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\2_elite_periodization_casado_2022.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "02(3):215–219.\n5. Péronnet F, Thibault G, Rhodes EC, McKenzie DC. Correlation\nbetween ventilatory threshold and endurance capability in marathon\nrunners. Med Sci Sports Exerc. 1987;19(6):610–615. PubMed ID:\n6. Joyner MJ, Coyle EF. Endurance exercise", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "seems to be associated with both performance and the enhancement\nof physiological determinants in highly trained and elite distance\nrunners. Continuous tempo runs, and long and medium aerobic\ninterval training with short recovery periods, are the methods which\nthese runners use to train at vLT2.\nPractical Applications\nHighly trained/elite middle- and long-distance runners are encour-\naged to accumulate >100 km·wk−1 while following a pyramidal\nTID approach on a hard day–easy day basis. A polarized pattern\nmight be also effective. Linear periodization is generally recom-\nmended for this population although further research is needed to\nunderstand whether other periodization models are effective in\nimproving performance and physiological determinants. Runners\nshould decrease the amount of training at vLT2 (z2), as well as\nincrease the amount of training in z3 (race pace) during the\nAuthors gratefully thank the training details provided by coach Nic Bideau\nand his athletes for the completion of the present study. They also\nappreciate the valuable feedback provided by Dr Trent Stellingwerff,\nwhich helped to improve the quality of the present article. Author\ncontributions: Casado and González-Mohíno collected the data, analyzed\nthe data, and wrote the manuscript; González-Ravé collected the data and\nwrote the manuscript; Foster wrote the manuscript and reviewed a draft of\n1. Billat LV, Koralsztein JP. Signiﬁcance of the velocity at ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "02(3):215–219.\n5. Péronnet F, Thibault G, Rhodes EC, McKenzie DC. Correlation\nbetween ventilatory threshold and endurance capability in marathon\nrunners. Med Sci Sports Exerc. 1987;19(6):610–615. PubMed ID:\n6. Joyner MJ, Coyle EF. Endurance exercise"}
{"chunk_id": "2_elite_periodization_casado_2022_p0013_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_df9ead131193", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8eac1665a811e437", "text_span_hash": "8eac1665a811e437", "source_file": "2_elite_periodization_casado_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\2_elite_periodization_casado_2022.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Coyle EF. Endurance exercise performance: the physiol-\n7. Londeree BR. Effect of training on lactate/ventilatory thresholds: a\nmeta-analysis. Med Sci Sports Exerc. 1997;29(6):837–843. PubMed\n8. Caputo F, Mello MT, Denadai BS. Oxygen uptake kinetics", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "seems to be associated with both performance and the enhancement\nof physiological determinants in highly trained and elite distance\nrunners. Continuous tempo runs, and long and medium aerobic\ninterval training with short recovery periods, are the methods which\nthese runners use to train at vLT2.\nPractical Applications\nHighly trained/elite middle- and long-distance runners are encour-\naged to accumulate >100 km·wk−1 while following a pyramidal\nTID approach on a hard day–easy day basis. A polarized pattern\nmight be also effective. Linear periodization is generally recom-\nmended for this population although further research is needed to\nunderstand whether other periodization models are effective in\nimproving performance and physiological determinants. Runners\nshould decrease the amount of training at vLT2 (z2), as well as\nincrease the amount of training in z3 (race pace) during the\nAuthors gratefully thank the training details provided by coach Nic Bideau\nand his athletes for the completion of the present study. They also\nappreciate the valuable feedback provided by Dr Trent Stellingwerff,\nwhich helped to improve the quality of the present article. Author\ncontributions: Casado and González-Mohíno collected the data, analyzed\nthe data, and wrote the manuscript; González-Ravé collected the data and\nwrote the manuscript; Foster wrote the manuscript and reviewed a draft of\n1. Billat LV, Koralsztein JP. Signiﬁcance of the velocity at ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Coyle EF. Endurance exercise performance: the physiol-\n7. Londeree BR. Effect of training on lactate/ventilatory thresholds: a\nmeta-analysis. Med Sci Sports Exerc. 1997;29(6):837–843. PubMed\n8. Caputo F, Mello MT, Denadai BS. Oxygen uptake kinetics"}
{"chunk_id": "2_elite_periodization_casado_2022_p0013_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_df9ead131193", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ebd89ebf84bbc995", "text_span_hash": "ebd89ebf84bbc995", "source_file": "2_elite_periodization_casado_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\2_elite_periodization_casado_2022.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "The correlation\nbetween running economy and maximal oxygen uptake: cross-sec-\ntional and longitudinal relationships in highly trained distance runners.\n10. Seiler S, Tønnessen E. Intervals, thresholds, and long slow distance:\nthe Role of intensity an", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "seems to be associated with both performance and the enhancement\nof physiological determinants in highly trained and elite distance\nrunners. Continuous tempo runs, and long and medium aerobic\ninterval training with short recovery periods, are the methods which\nthese runners use to train at vLT2.\nPractical Applications\nHighly trained/elite middle- and long-distance runners are encour-\naged to accumulate >100 km·wk−1 while following a pyramidal\nTID approach on a hard day–easy day basis. A polarized pattern\nmight be also effective. Linear periodization is generally recom-\nmended for this population although further research is needed to\nunderstand whether other periodization models are effective in\nimproving performance and physiological determinants. Runners\nshould decrease the amount of training at vLT2 (z2), as well as\nincrease the amount of training in z3 (race pace) during the\nAuthors gratefully thank the training details provided by coach Nic Bideau\nand his athletes for the completion of the present study. They also\nappreciate the valuable feedback provided by Dr Trent Stellingwerff,\nwhich helped to improve the quality of the present article. Author\ncontributions: Casado and González-Mohíno collected the data, analyzed\nthe data, and wrote the manuscript; González-Ravé collected the data and\nwrote the manuscript; Foster wrote the manuscript and reviewed a draft of\n1. Billat LV, Koralsztein JP. Signiﬁcance of the velocity at ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "The correlation\nbetween running economy and maximal oxygen uptake: cross-sec-\ntional and longitudinal relationships in highly trained distance runners.\n10. Seiler S, Tønnessen E. Intervals, thresholds, and long slow distance:\nthe Role of intensity an"}
