{"chunk_id": "2_elite_periodization_casado_2022_p0014_c0022", "source_registry_id": "src_df9ead131193", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9fd62e9d0254b4d2", "text_span_hash": "9fd62e9d0254b4d2", "source_file": "2_elite_periodization_casado_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\2_elite_periodization_casado_2022.pdf", "page": 14, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 22, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "s and Performance in Highly Trained Runners\nUnauthenticated | Downloaded 06/08/26 09:35 PM UTC", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "26. Ingham SA, Fudge BW, Pringle JS. Training distribution, physiolog-\nical proﬁle, and performance for a male international 1500-m runner.\nInt J Sports Physiol Perform. 2012;7(2):193–195. PubMed ID:\n27. Billat VL, Flechet B, Petit B, Muriaux G, Koralsztein JP. Interval\ntraining at ˙VO2max: effects on aerobic performance and overtraining\nmarkers. Med Sci Sports Exerc. 1999;31(1):156–163. PubMed ID:\n28. Enoksen E, Shalfawi SAI, Tønnessen E. The effect of high- vs. low-\nintensity training on aerobic capacity in well-trained male middle-\ndistance runners. J Strength Cond Res. 2011;25(3):812–818. PubMed\n29. Filipas L, Bonato M, Gallo G, Codella R. Effects of 16 weeks of\npyramidal and polarized training intensity distributions in well‐\ntrained endurance runners. Scand J Med Sci Sports. 2021;32(3):\n30. Billat VL, Lepretre PM, Heugas AM, Laurence MH, Salim D,\nKoralsztein JP. Training and bioenergetic characteristics in elite\nmale and female Kenyan runners. Med Sci Sports Exerc. 2003;35(2):\n31. Tjelta LI. A longitudinal case study of the training of the 2012\nEuropean 1500 m track champion. Int J Appl Sports Sci. 2013;25(1):\n32. Tjelta LI, Enoksen E. Training characteristics of male junior cross\ncountry and track runners on European top level. Int J Sports Sci\n33. Galbraith A, Hopker J, Cardinale M, Cunniffe B, Passﬁeld L. A 1-\nyear study of endurance runners: training, laboratory tests, and ﬁeld\ntests. Int J Sports Physiol Perform. 20", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "s and Performance in Highly Trained Runners\nUnauthenticated | Downloaded 06/08/26 09:35 PM UTC"}
{"chunk_id": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898_p0001_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_547f1419e9ba", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8332040b8e563eb4", "text_span_hash": "8332040b8e563eb4", "source_file": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "rt, Qinghai Normal University, Xining, China.\nHigh‐Intensity Interval Training and Moderate‐Intensity\nContinuous Training Affect Running Economy in Endurance\nRunners: A Systematic Review and Meta‐Analysis\nof Randomized Controlled Trials\nYangya Feng 1", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Journal of Human Kinetics volume xx/xxxx, x–x DOI: 10.5114/jhk/205427\nSection II – Exercise Physiology and Sports Medicine\n1 College of P.E. and Sport, Beijing Normal University, Beijing, China.\n2 College of P.E. and Sport, Qinghai Normal University, Xining, China.\nHigh‐Intensity Interval Training and Moderate‐Intensity\nContinuous Training Affect Running Economy in Endurance\nRunners: A Systematic Review and Meta‐Analysis\nof Randomized Controlled Trials\nYangya Feng 1,2, Daxin Li 1, Yangli Liu 1, Donghui Tang 1,*\nThis systematic review evaluated the effects of high‐intensity interval training (HIIT) and moderate‐intensity\ncontinuous training (MICT) on running economy (RE) in endurance runners. The search was completed in March 2024\nbased on research databases, and the language of publication was restricted to English. The primary outcome measure\nwas RE, and it was categorized into three subgroups: Zone 1 (Z1), Zone 2 (Z2), and Zone 3 (Z3). The secondary outcomes\nassessed were maximal oxygen uptake (VO2max) and blood lactate concentration. HIIT significantly improved RE\ncompared to MICT (SMD = 0.44, 95% CI [0.15, 0.72], Z = 3.01, p < 0.05). MICT showed a greater effect on VO2max (MD\n= 2.48, 95% CI [1.61, 3.34], Z = 5.60, p < 0.05). HIIT was more effective at reducing blood lactate levels (MD = −0.15,\n95% CI [−0.28, −0.02], Z = 2.20, p < 0.05). The results indicate that HIIT was more effective than MICT in enhancing\nRE and delayin", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "rt, Qinghai Normal University, Xining, China.\nHigh‐Intensity Interval Training and Moderate‐Intensity\nContinuous Training Affect Running Economy in Endurance\nRunners: A Systematic Review and Meta‐Analysis\nof Randomized Controlled Trials\nYangya Feng 1"}
{"chunk_id": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898_p0001_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_547f1419e9ba", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "84fabde54b3c66dd", "text_span_hash": "84fabde54b3c66dd", "source_file": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ontrolled Trials\nYangya Feng 1,2, Daxin Li 1, Yangli Liu 1, Donghui Tang 1,*\nThis systematic review evaluated the effects of high‐intensity interval training (HIIT) and moderate‐intensity\ncontinuous training (MICT) on running economy (RE) in enduranc", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Journal of Human Kinetics volume xx/xxxx, x–x DOI: 10.5114/jhk/205427\nSection II – Exercise Physiology and Sports Medicine\n1 College of P.E. and Sport, Beijing Normal University, Beijing, China.\n2 College of P.E. and Sport, Qinghai Normal University, Xining, China.\nHigh‐Intensity Interval Training and Moderate‐Intensity\nContinuous Training Affect Running Economy in Endurance\nRunners: A Systematic Review and Meta‐Analysis\nof Randomized Controlled Trials\nYangya Feng 1,2, Daxin Li 1, Yangli Liu 1, Donghui Tang 1,*\nThis systematic review evaluated the effects of high‐intensity interval training (HIIT) and moderate‐intensity\ncontinuous training (MICT) on running economy (RE) in endurance runners. The search was completed in March 2024\nbased on research databases, and the language of publication was restricted to English. The primary outcome measure\nwas RE, and it was categorized into three subgroups: Zone 1 (Z1), Zone 2 (Z2), and Zone 3 (Z3). The secondary outcomes\nassessed were maximal oxygen uptake (VO2max) and blood lactate concentration. HIIT significantly improved RE\ncompared to MICT (SMD = 0.44, 95% CI [0.15, 0.72], Z = 3.01, p < 0.05). MICT showed a greater effect on VO2max (MD\n= 2.48, 95% CI [1.61, 3.34], Z = 5.60, p < 0.05). HIIT was more effective at reducing blood lactate levels (MD = −0.15,\n95% CI [−0.28, −0.02], Z = 2.20, p < 0.05). The results indicate that HIIT was more effective than MICT in enhancing\nRE and delayin", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ontrolled Trials\nYangya Feng 1,2, Daxin Li 1, Yangli Liu 1, Donghui Tang 1,*\nThis systematic review evaluated the effects of high‐intensity interval training (HIIT) and moderate‐intensity\ncontinuous training (MICT) on running economy (RE) in enduranc"}
