{"chunk_id": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898_p0001_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_547f1419e9ba", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "cff542fc8d3a157d", "text_span_hash": "cff542fc8d3a157d", "source_file": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "n when\nperformed at or below the lactate threshold (≤ Z2). VO2max was more pronounced with MICT. These findings suggest that\nendurance runners and coaches should choose appropriate methods to optimize physiological adaptations.\nKeywords: athletes; en", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Journal of Human Kinetics volume xx/xxxx, x–x DOI: 10.5114/jhk/205427\nSection II – Exercise Physiology and Sports Medicine\n1 College of P.E. and Sport, Beijing Normal University, Beijing, China.\n2 College of P.E. and Sport, Qinghai Normal University, Xining, China.\nHigh‐Intensity Interval Training and Moderate‐Intensity\nContinuous Training Affect Running Economy in Endurance\nRunners: A Systematic Review and Meta‐Analysis\nof Randomized Controlled Trials\nYangya Feng 1,2, Daxin Li 1, Yangli Liu 1, Donghui Tang 1,*\nThis systematic review evaluated the effects of high‐intensity interval training (HIIT) and moderate‐intensity\ncontinuous training (MICT) on running economy (RE) in endurance runners. The search was completed in March 2024\nbased on research databases, and the language of publication was restricted to English. The primary outcome measure\nwas RE, and it was categorized into three subgroups: Zone 1 (Z1), Zone 2 (Z2), and Zone 3 (Z3). The secondary outcomes\nassessed were maximal oxygen uptake (VO2max) and blood lactate concentration. HIIT significantly improved RE\ncompared to MICT (SMD = 0.44, 95% CI [0.15, 0.72], Z = 3.01, p < 0.05). MICT showed a greater effect on VO2max (MD\n= 2.48, 95% CI [1.61, 3.34], Z = 5.60, p < 0.05). HIIT was more effective at reducing blood lactate levels (MD = −0.15,\n95% CI [−0.28, −0.02], Z = 2.20, p < 0.05). The results indicate that HIIT was more effective than MICT in enhancing\nRE and delayin", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "n when\nperformed at or below the lactate threshold (≤ Z2). VO2max was more pronounced with MICT. These findings suggest that\nendurance runners and coaches should choose appropriate methods to optimize physiological adaptations.\nKeywords: athletes; en"}
{"chunk_id": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898_p0001_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_547f1419e9ba", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "badd53b897324a80", "text_span_hash": "badd53b897324a80", "source_file": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ations.\nKeywords: athletes; endurance; training methods; aerobic metabolism\nIn endurance running, the efficiency and\nperformance. This efficiency can be quantified\nthrough running economy (RE). RE is defined as\nthe energy expenditure associated with", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Journal of Human Kinetics volume xx/xxxx, x–x DOI: 10.5114/jhk/205427\nSection II – Exercise Physiology and Sports Medicine\n1 College of P.E. and Sport, Beijing Normal University, Beijing, China.\n2 College of P.E. and Sport, Qinghai Normal University, Xining, China.\nHigh‐Intensity Interval Training and Moderate‐Intensity\nContinuous Training Affect Running Economy in Endurance\nRunners: A Systematic Review and Meta‐Analysis\nof Randomized Controlled Trials\nYangya Feng 1,2, Daxin Li 1, Yangli Liu 1, Donghui Tang 1,*\nThis systematic review evaluated the effects of high‐intensity interval training (HIIT) and moderate‐intensity\ncontinuous training (MICT) on running economy (RE) in endurance runners. The search was completed in March 2024\nbased on research databases, and the language of publication was restricted to English. The primary outcome measure\nwas RE, and it was categorized into three subgroups: Zone 1 (Z1), Zone 2 (Z2), and Zone 3 (Z3). The secondary outcomes\nassessed were maximal oxygen uptake (VO2max) and blood lactate concentration. HIIT significantly improved RE\ncompared to MICT (SMD = 0.44, 95% CI [0.15, 0.72], Z = 3.01, p 