. The language of publication\nwas restricted to English, and the publication type\nwas restricted to randomized controlled trials\nInclusion and Study Selection\naccording to PICOS principles. Studies that\nsatisfied the following criteria: (1) participants\nwere endurance runners (P), (2) the intervention\nwas interval", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "w articles.\nOutcome Measurements\nThe test results included running economy\n(RE) as the primary outcome, which was calculated\nincluded at least one of the following: VO2max\nand/or blood lactic acid levels.\nData Collection Process\nThe included studies"}
{"chunk_id": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898_p0004_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_547f1419e9ba", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "eabd1df0cf790ea4", "text_span_hash": "eabd1df0cf790ea4", "source_file": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Training Intensity and Running Economy in Endurance Runners\nJournal of Human Kinetics, volume xxx, xxxxx\nRisk of Bias Assessment\nTwo reviewers independently assessed the\nrisk of bias, and any disagreements were resolved\nthrough discussion with a thir", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training Intensity and Running Economy in Endurance Runners\nJournal of Human Kinetics, volume xxx, xxxxx\nRisk of Bias Assessment\nTwo reviewers independently assessed the\nrisk of bias, and any disagreements were resolved\nthrough discussion with a third author. The bias in\nthe included randomized controlled trials was\nevaluated using the Cochrane Risk of Bias Tool,\nwhich categorized each item as of low risk, high\nrisk, or unclear risk of bias concerning random\nblinding of participants and personnel, blinding of\noutcome assessment, incomplete outcome data,\nselective reporting, and other biases (Higgins and\nThe performance bias item was eliminated\nbecause it was not practicable to blind the\nparticipants. A technique was established to assess\nthe potential for bias in selection (random sequence\ngeneration and allocation concealment), detection,\nattrition, and reporting biases. The study was\nclassified as of “high risk” or “low risk”, and if the\ninformation was omitted, it was graded as\n“unclear”. We addressed the impact of bias levels\non the weights in our analyses by conducting\nsensitivity analyses that excluded studies with\nhigh risk of bias, and we found that the overall\nresults remained robust. When a significant result\n(p < 0.05) was observed, a funnel plot test was also\nperformed to assess publication bias. The effects of\nboth HIIT and MICT on running economy\nperformance were studied through the funnel plot.\nStatistical Analy", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Training Intensity and Running Economy in Endurance Runners\nJournal of Human Kinetics, volume xxx, xxxxx\nRisk of Bias Assessment\nTwo reviewers independently assessed the\nrisk of bias, and any disagreements were resolved\nthrough discussion with a thir"}
