{"chunk_id": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898_p0005_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_547f1419e9ba", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "034404b839020ff2", "text_span_hash": "034404b839020ff2", "source_file": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "evaluated RE at multiple speeds. Additionally,\nmaximal oxygen uptake and blood lactate\nmeasures to assess other factors influencing RE.\nAssessment of Quality\nillustrating the proportion of projects evaluated\nusing different methods. Among the nine a", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "by Yangya Feng et al.\nArticles published in the Journal of Human Kinetics are licensed under an open access Creative Commons CC BY 4.0\nparticipants (110 males and 48 females) were\nincluded in the analysis, with age ranging from\n21.9 to 46.9 years. Maximal oxygen uptake data\nwere recorded, with values ranging from 34.0 to\n71.4 ml∙kg⁻¹∙min⁻¹. Based on the weighted mean\nvalues from the studies reporting participants’\ncharacteristics for each group, minimal differences\nat baseline were observed for age (38.2 vs. 39.3\nyears), body mass (81.6 vs. 82.2 kg), body height\nmL∙kg⁻¹∙min⁻¹) between the control and HIIT\ngroups. Seven studies included moderately trained\nor recreational runners, one study featured well‐\ntrained participants, and another examined highly\ntrained runners. Participants were involved in a\nvariety of activities, including triathlons and\nendurance or recreational distance running.\nTable 2 provides an overview of the\nrunning economy characteristics associated with\nthe outcomes of concurrent HIIT and MICT\ninterventions. Running economy was evaluated by\nmeasuring the oxygen cost associated with each\ncondition at a specific running speed. Statistically\nsignificant improvements in RE were observed in\nat least one of the nine studies. Five studies\nassessed RE at a single speed, while four others\nevaluated RE at multiple speeds. Additionally,\nmaximal oxygen uptake and blood lactate\nmeasures to assess other factors influenci", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "evaluated RE at multiple speeds. Additionally,\nmaximal oxygen uptake and blood lactate\nmeasures to assess other factors influencing RE.\nAssessment of Quality\nillustrating the proportion of projects evaluated\nusing different methods. Among the nine a"}
{"chunk_id": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898_p0005_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_547f1419e9ba", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c93cc5ea04a29460", "text_span_hash": "c93cc5ea04a29460", "source_file": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ee studies\nexhibited low risk of bias and high quality, while\nthe remaining six studies had moderate risk of bias.\nThis systematic review included nine\nrandomized controlled trials (RCTs) that assessed\nrunning economy (RE) at velocities ranging from", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "by Yangya Feng et al.\nArticles published in the Journal of Human Kinetics are licensed under an open access Creative Commons CC BY 4.0\nparticipants (110 males and 48 females) were\nincluded in the analysis, with age ranging from\n21.9 to 46.9 years. Maximal oxygen uptake data\nwere recorded, with values ranging from 34.0 to\n71.4 ml∙kg⁻¹∙min⁻¹. Based on the weighted mean\nvalues from the studies reporting participants’\ncharacteristics for each group, minimal differences\nat baseline were observed for age (38.2 vs. 39.3\nyears), body mass (81.6 vs. 82.2 kg), body height\nmL∙kg⁻¹∙min⁻¹) between the control and HIIT\ngroups. Seven studies included moderately trained\nor recreational runners, one study featured well‐\ntrained participants, and another examined highly\ntrained runners. Participants were involved in a\nvariety of activities, including triathlons and\nendurance or recreational distance running.\nTable 2 provides an overview of the\nrunning economy characteristics associated with\nthe outcomes of concurrent HIIT and MICT\ninterventions. Running economy was evaluated by\nmeasuring the oxygen cost associated with each\ncondition at a specific running speed. Statistically\nsignificant improvements in RE were observed in\nat least one of the nine studies. Five studies\nassessed RE at a single speed, while four others\nevaluated RE at multiple speeds. Additionally,\nmaximal oxygen uptake and blood lactate\nmeasures to assess other factors influenci", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ee studies\nexhibited low risk of bias and high quality, while\nthe remaining six studies had moderate risk of bias.\nThis systematic review included nine\nrandomized controlled trials (RCTs) that assessed\nrunning economy (RE) at velocities ranging from"}
