 a specific running speed. Statistically\nsignificant improvements in RE were observed in\nat least one of the nine studies. Five studies\nassessed RE at a single speed, while four others\nevaluated RE at multiple speeds. Additionally,\nmaximal oxygen uptake and blood lactate\nmeasures to assess other factors influenci", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "speeds within Z2. In Z3, where speeds exceeded\nthe lactate threshold, there was no intra‐group\nheterogeneity (I² = 40%, p > 0.1). The standardized\nmean difference (SMD) was 0.14, with a 95%\nconfidence interval of [−0.60, 0.88]. There was no\nstatistic"}
{"chunk_id": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898_p0005_c0021", "source_registry_id": "src_547f1419e9ba", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b1e4a1daee549c79", "text_span_hash": "b1e4a1daee549c79", "source_file": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 21, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "0.88]. There was no\nstatistical significance in training results within Z3\n(Z = 0.37, p > 0.05), which indicates that there was\nno significant difference in subjects’ running\neconomy between HIIT and MICT at speeds", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "by Yangya Feng et al.\nArticles published in the Journal of Human Kinetics are licensed under an open access Creative Commons CC BY 4.0\nparticipants (110 males and 48 females) were\nincluded in the analysis, with age ranging from\n21.9 to 46.9 years. Maximal oxygen uptake data\nwere recorded, with values ranging from 34.0 to\n71.4 ml∙kg⁻¹∙min⁻¹. Based on the weighted mean\nvalues from the studies reporting participants’\ncharacteristics for each group, minimal differences\nat baseline were observed for age (38.2 vs. 39.3\nyears), body mass (81.6 vs. 82.2 kg), body height\nmL∙kg⁻¹∙min⁻¹) between the control and HIIT\ngroups. Seven studies included moderately trained\nor recreational runners, one study featured well‐\ntrained participants, and another examined highly\ntrained runners. Participants were involved in a\nvariety of activities, including triathlons and\nendurance or recreational distance running.\nTable 2 provides an overview of the\nrunning economy characteristics associated with\nthe outcomes of concurrent HIIT and MICT\ninterventions. Running economy was evaluated by\nmeasuring the oxygen cost associated with each\ncondition at a specific running speed. Statistically\nsignificant improvements in RE were observed in\nat least one of the nine studies. Five studies\nassessed RE at a single speed, while four others\nevaluated RE at multiple speeds. Additionally,\nmaximal oxygen uptake and blood lactate\nmeasures to assess other factors influenci", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "0.88]. There was no\nstatistical significance in training results within Z3\n(Z = 0.37, p > 0.05), which indicates that there was\nno significant difference in subjects’ running\neconomy between HIIT and MICT at speeds"}
{"chunk_id": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898_p0006_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_547f1419e9ba", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "d8f45e766d695aa8", "text_span_hash": "d8f45e766d695aa8", "source_file": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Training Intensity and Running Economy in Endurance Runners\nJournal of Human Kinetics, volume xxx, xxxxx\nexceeding the lactate threshold.\nThe heterogeneity between groups was\nlow (I² = 24%, p > 0.1). The standardized mean\ndifference (SMD) between gro", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training Intensity and Running Economy in Endurance Runners\nJournal of Human Kinetics, volume xxx, xxxxx\nexceeding the lactate threshold.\nThe heterogeneity between groups was\nlow (I² = 24%, p > 0.1). The standardized mean\ndifference (SMD) between groups was 0.44, with a\n95% confidence interval of [0.15, 0.72], indicating\nstatistical significance (Z = 3.01, p < 0.05). This\nindicates that both HIIT and MICT had a greater\neffect on RE, with the control group showing a\nlarger total effect size than the experimental group.\nThis also suggests that the control group consumed\nmore oxygen under similar conditions, while the\nexperimental group consumed less oxygen. Thus,\nit can be stated that HIIT was more effective in\nMaximal Oxygen Uptake\nChanges in maximal oxygen uptake\n(VO2max) were compared between HIIT and MICT,\nbefore and after the experiment. The heterogeneity\namong the groups was low (I² = 38%, p = 0.12),\nwhich justified the use of a fixed‐effects model. In\ngeneral, when the I² value is below 50% (or the\ncommonly used threshold is 25%–50%), the\nheterogeneity is small, the results tend to be\nconsistent, and the use of fixed‐effect models is a\nsuitable choice. The mean difference (MD) was\n2.48, with a 95% confidence interval of [1.61, 3.34],\nindicating statistical significance (Z = 5.60, p < 0.05).\nThe findings revealed significant differences in\nVO2max between the two training regimens, with\nMICT showing a greater improvement co", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Training Intensity and Running Economy in Endurance Runners\nJournal of Human Kinetics, volume xxx, xxxxx\nexceeding the lactate threshold.\nThe heterogeneity between groups was\nlow (I² = 24%, p > 0.1). The standardized mean\ndifference (SMD) between gro"}
{"chunk_id": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898_p0006_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_547f1419e9ba", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "508c20cdedc0bf03", "text_span_hash": "508c20cdedc0bf03", "source_file": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "n\ndifference (SMD) between groups was 0.44, with a\n95% confidence interval of [0.15, 0.72], indicating\nstatistical significance (Z = 3.01, p < 0.05). This\nindicates that both HIIT and MICT had a greater\neffect on RE, with the control group showing a", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training Intensity and Running Economy in Endurance Runners\nJournal of Human Kinetics, volume xxx, xxxxx\nexceeding the lactate threshold.\nThe heterogeneity between groups was\nlow (I² = 24%, p > 0.1). The standardized mean\ndifference (SMD) between groups was 0.44, with a\n95% confidence interval of [0.15, 0.72], indicating\nstatistical significance (Z = 3.01, p < 0.05). This\nindicates that both HIIT and MICT had a greater\neffect on RE, with the control group showing a\nlarger total effect size than the experimental group.\nThis also suggests that the control group consumed\nmore oxygen under similar conditions, while the\nexperimental group consumed less oxygen. Thus,\nit can be stated that HIIT was more effective in\nMaximal Oxygen Uptake\nChanges in maximal oxygen uptake\n(VO2max) were compared between HIIT and MICT,\nbefore and after the experiment. The heterogeneity\namong the groups was low (I² = 38%, p = 0.12),\nwhich justified the use of a fixed‐effects model. In\ngeneral, when the I² value is below 50% (or the\ncommonly used threshold is 25%–50%), the\nheterogeneity is small, the results tend to be\nconsistent, and the use of fix