{"chunk_id": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898_p0010_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_547f1419e9ba", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0b452082a94c4b00", "text_span_hash": "0b452082a94c4b00", "source_file": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "by Yangya Feng et al.\nArticles published in the Journal of Human Kinetics are licensed under an open access Creative Commons CC BY 4.0\nFigure 3. Forest plot for running economy.\nFigure 4. Forest plot for VO2max.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "by Yangya Feng et al.\nArticles published in the Journal of Human Kinetics are licensed under an open access Creative Commons CC BY 4.0\nFigure 3. Forest plot for running economy.\nFigure 4. Forest plot for VO2max.", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "by Yangya Feng et al.\nArticles published in the Journal of Human Kinetics are licensed under an open access Creative Commons CC BY 4.0\nFigure 3. Forest plot for running economy.\nFigure 4. Forest plot for VO2max."}
{"chunk_id": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898_p0011_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_547f1419e9ba", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7e4821731a4bfc3f", "text_span_hash": "7e4821731a4bfc3f", "source_file": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Training Intensity and Running Economy in Endurance Runners\nJournal of Human Kinetics, volume xxx, xxxxx\nFigure 5. Forest plot for blood lactate concentration.\nFigure 6. The risk of bias funnel plot.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training Intensity and Running Economy in Endurance Runners\nJournal of Human Kinetics, volume xxx, xxxxx\nFigure 5. Forest plot for blood lactate concentration.\nFigure 6. The risk of bias funnel plot.", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Training Intensity and Running Economy in Endurance Runners\nJournal of Human Kinetics, volume xxx, xxxxx\nFigure 5. Forest plot for blood lactate concentration.\nFigure 6. The risk of bias funnel plot."}
{"chunk_id": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898_p0012_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_547f1419e9ba", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "53ebbce2bbafabb4", "text_span_hash": "53ebbce2bbafabb4", "source_file": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ature to\ndetermine the effects of HIIT and MICT on running\neconomy (RE). The initial findings revealed that\nHIIT significantly reduced the oxygen cost of\nrunning compared to MICT in endurance runners.\nFurthermore, the effects of different training\nin", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "by Yangya Feng et al.\nArticles published in the Journal of Human Kinetics are licensed under an open access Creative Commons CC BY 4.0\nThe objective of this systematic review was\nto critically evaluate the existing literature to\ndetermine the effects of HIIT and MICT on running\neconomy (RE). The initial findings revealed that\nHIIT significantly reduced the oxygen cost of\nrunning compared to MICT in endurance runners.\nFurthermore, the effects of different training\ninterventions on maximal oxygen uptake indicated\nthat MICT was more effective in improving VO2max.\nFinally, training had a significant impact on blood\nlactate concentration. HIIT was particularly\neffective as it enabled the body to adapt to lactate\nmore efficiently than most of other forms of\nexercise. Individuals who engaged in HIIT\nproduced less lactate at similar exercise intensities\nand demonstrated higher tolerance to lactate.\nOur initial findings showed that, among\nendurance runners, HIIT led to significantly\nreduced oxygen cost of running compared to MICT\n(Wang et al., 2012). Previous studies have indicated\nthat different training interventions can have\ndistinct effects on running economy (Barnes and\nKilding, 2015; Faelli et al., 2021; Franch et al., 1998).\nCompared to MICT interventions, HIIT intervals of\n1 min or less did not demonstrate a significant\ndifference in oxygen costs. However, intervals\nlonger than one minute led to a significant\nimprovement in max", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ature to\ndetermine the effects of HIIT and MICT on running\neconomy (RE). The initial findings revealed that\nHIIT significantly reduced the oxygen cost of\nrunning compared to MICT in endurance runners.\nFurthermore, the effects of different training\nin"}
{"chunk_id": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898_p0012_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_547f1419e9ba", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4ec249ba309e90c0", "text_span_hash": "4ec249ba309e90c0", "source_file": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "fects of different training\ninterventions on maximal oxygen uptake indicated\nthat MICT was more effective in improving VO2max.\nFinally, training had a significant impact on blood\nlactate concentration. HIIT was particularly\neffective as it enabled th", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "by Yangya Feng et al.\nArticles published in the Journal of Human Kinetics are licensed under an open access Creative Commons CC BY 4.0\nThe objective of this systematic review was\nto critically evaluate the existing literature to\ndetermine the effects of HIIT and MICT on running\neconomy (RE). The initial findings revealed that\nHIIT significantly reduced the oxygen cost of\nrunning compared to MICT in endurance runners.\nFurthermore, the effects of different training\ninterventions on maximal oxygen uptake indicated\nthat MICT was more effective in improving VO2max.\nFinally, training had a significant impact on blood\nlactate concentration. HIIT was particularly\neffective as it enabled the body to adapt to lactate\nmore efficiently than most of other forms of\nexercise. Individuals who engaged in HIIT\nproduced less lactate at similar exercise intensities\nand demonstrated higher tolerance to lactate.\nOur initial findings showed that, among\nendurance runners, HIIT led to significantly\nreduced oxygen cost of running compared to MICT\n(Wang et al., 2012). Previous studies have indicated\nthat different training interventions can have\ndistinct effects on running economy (Barnes and\nKilding, 2015; Faelli et al., 2021; Franch et al., 1998).\nCompared to MICT interventions, HIIT intervals of\n1 min or less did not demonstrate a significant\ndifference in oxygen costs. However, intervals\nlonger than one minute led to a significant\nimprovement in max", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "fects of different training\ninterventions on maximal oxygen uptake indicated\nthat MICT was more effective in improving VO2max.\nFinally, training had a significant impact on blood\nlactate concentration. HIIT was particularly\neffective as it enabled th"}
{"chunk_id": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898_p0012_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_547f1419e9ba", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "fdd9a5dbe0c493f2", "text_span_hash": "fdd9a5dbe0c493f2", "source_file": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "rly\neffective as it enabled the body to adapt to lactate\nmore efficiently than most of other forms of\nexercise. Individuals who engaged in HIIT\nproduced less lactate at similar exercise intensities\nand demonstrated higher tolerance to lactate.\nOur in", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "by Yangya Feng et al.\nArticles published in the Journal of Human Kinetics are licensed under an open access Creative Commons CC BY 4.0\nThe objective of this systematic review was\nto critically evaluate the existing literature to\ndetermine the effects of HIIT and MICT on running\neconomy (RE). The initial findings revealed that\nHIIT significantly reduced the oxygen cost of\nrunning compared to MICT in endurance runners.\nFurthermore, the effects of different training\ninterventions on maximal oxygen uptake indicated\nthat MICT was more effective in improving VO2max.\nFinally, training had a significant impact on blood\nlactate concentration. HIIT was particularly\neffective as it enabled the body to adapt to lactate\nmore efficiently than most of other forms of\nexercise. Individuals who engaged in HIIT\nproduced less lactate at similar exercise intensities\nand demonstrated higher tolerance to lactate.\nOur initial findings showed that, among\nendurance runners, HIIT led to significantly\nreduced oxygen cost of running compared to MICT\n(Wang et al., 2012). Previous studies have indicated\nthat different training interventions can have\ndistinct effects on running economy (Barnes and\nKilding, 2015; Faelli et al., 2021; Franch et al., 1998).\nCompared to MICT interventions, HIIT intervals of\n1 min or less did not demonstrate a significant\ndifference in oxygen costs. However, intervals\nlonger than one minute led to a significant\nimprovement in max", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "rly\neffective as it enabled the body to adapt to lactate\nmore efficiently than most of other forms of\nexercise. Individuals who engaged in HIIT\nproduced less lactate at similar exercise intensities\nand demonstrated higher tolerance to lactate.\nOur in"}
{"chunk_id": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898_p0012_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_547f1419e9ba", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "267efd1388a4df5e", "text_span_hash": "267efd1388a4df5e", "source_file": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "r tolerance to lactate.\nOur initial findings showed that, among\nendurance runners, HIIT led to significantly\nreduced oxygen cost of running compared to MICT\n(Wang et al., 2012). Previous studies have indicated\nthat different training interventions ca", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "by Yangya Feng et al.\nArticles published in the Journal of Human Kinetics are licensed under an open access Creative Commons CC BY 4.0\nThe objective of this systematic review was\nto critically evaluate the existing literature to\ndetermine the effects of HIIT and MICT on running\neconomy (RE). The initial findings revealed that\nHIIT significantly reduced the oxygen cost of\nrunning compared to MICT in endurance runners.\nFurthermore, the effects of different training\ninterventions on maximal oxygen uptake indicated\nthat MICT was more effective in improving VO2max.\nFinally, training had a significant impact on blood\nlactate concentration. HIIT was particularly\neffective as it enabled the body to adapt to lactate\nmore efficiently than most of other forms of\nexercise. Individuals who engaged in HIIT\nproduced less lactate at similar exercise intensities\nand demonstrated higher tolerance to lactate.\nOur initial findings showed that, among\nendurance runners, HIIT led to significantly\nreduced oxygen cost of running compared to MICT\n(Wang et al., 2012). Previous studies have indicated\nthat different training interventions can have\ndistinct effects on running economy (Barnes and\nKilding, 2015; Faelli et al., 2021; Franch et al., 1998).\nCompared to MICT interventions, HIIT intervals of\n1 min or less did not demonstrate a significant\ndifference in oxygen costs. However, intervals\nlonger than one minute led to a significant\nimprovement in max", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "r tolerance to lactate.\nOur initial findings showed that, among\nendurance runners, HIIT led to significantly\nreduced oxygen cost of running compared to MICT\n(Wang et al., 2012). Previous studies have indicated\nthat different training interventions ca"}
{"chunk_id": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898_p0012_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_547f1419e9ba", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7b3647dee078dd9c", "text_span_hash": "7b3647dee078dd9c", "source_file": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "rent training interventions can have\ndistinct effects on running economy (Barnes and\nKilding, 2015; Faelli et al., 2021; Franch et al., 1998).\nCompared to MICT interventions, HIIT intervals of\n1 min or less did not demonstrate a significant\ndifferenc", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "by Yangya Feng et al.\nArticles published in the Journal of Human Kinetics are licensed under an open access Creative Commons CC BY 4.0\nThe objective of this systematic review was\nto critically evaluate the existing literature to\ndetermine the effects of HIIT and MICT on running\neconomy (RE). The initial findings revealed that\nHIIT significantly reduced the oxygen cost of\nrunning compared to MICT in endurance runners.\nFurthermore, the effects of different training\ninterventions on maximal oxygen uptake indicated\nthat MICT was more effective in improving VO2max.\nFinally, training had a significant impact on blood\nlactate concentration. HIIT was particularly\neffective as it enabled the body to adapt to lactate\nmore efficiently than most of other forms of\nexercise. Individuals who engaged in HIIT\nproduced less lactate at similar exercise intensities\nand demonstrated higher tolerance to lactate.\nOur initial findings showed that, among\nendurance runners, HIIT led to significantly\nreduced oxygen cost of running compared to MICT\n(Wang et al., 2012). Previous studies have indicated\nthat different training interventions can have\ndistinct effects on running economy (Barnes and\nKilding, 2015; Faelli et al., 2021; Franch et al., 1998).\nCompared to MICT interventions, HIIT intervals of\n1 min or less did not demonstrate a significant\ndifference in oxygen costs. However, intervals\nlonger than one minute led to a significant\nimprovement in max", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "rent training interventions can have\ndistinct effects on running economy (Barnes and\nKilding, 2015; Faelli et al., 2021; Franch et al., 1998).\nCompared to MICT interventions, HIIT intervals of\n1 min or less did not demonstrate a significant\ndifferenc"}
{"chunk_id": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898_p0012_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_547f1419e9ba", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8090956834daf865", "text_span_hash": "8090956834daf865", "source_file": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "strate a significant\ndifference in oxygen costs. However, intervals\nlonger than one minute led to a significant\nimprovement in maximal oxygen uptake (Hov et\nal., 2023). Increasing the work interval from 30 to\n60 s led to an increase in maximal oxygen", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "by Yangya Feng et al.\nArticles published in the Journal of Human Kinetics are licensed under an open access Creative Commons CC BY 4.0\nThe objective of this systematic review was\nto critically evaluate the existing literature to\ndetermine the effects of HIIT and MICT on running\neconomy (RE). The initial findings revealed that\nHIIT significantly reduced the oxygen cost of\nrunning compared to MICT in endurance runners.\nFurthermore, the effects of different training\ninterventions on maximal oxygen uptake indicated\nthat MICT was more effective in improving VO2max.\nFinally, training had a significant impact on blood\nlactate concentration. HIIT was particularly\neffective as it enabled the body to adapt to lactate\nmore efficiently than most of other forms of\nexercise. Individuals who engaged in HIIT\nproduced less lactate at similar exercise intensities\nand demonstrated higher tolerance to lactate.\nOur initial findings showed that, among\nendurance runners, HIIT led to significantly\nreduced oxygen cost of running compared to MICT\n(Wang et al., 2012). Previous studies have indicated\nthat different training interventions can have\ndistinct effects on running economy (Barnes and\nKilding, 2015; Faelli et al., 2021; Franch et al., 1998).\nCompared to MICT interventions, HIIT intervals of\n1 min or less did not demonstrate a significant\ndifference in oxygen costs. However, intervals\nlonger than one minute led to a significant\nimprovement in max", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "strate a significant\ndifference in oxygen costs. However, intervals\nlonger than one minute led to a significant\nimprovement in maximal oxygen uptake (Hov et\nal., 2023). Increasing the work interval from 30 to\n60 s led to an increase in maximal oxygen"}
{"chunk_id": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898_p0012_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_547f1419e9ba", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c4098c10b92994fe", "text_span_hash": "c4098c10b92994fe", "source_file": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "an increase in maximal oxygen uptake,\ndespite shorter total training duration (Wen et al.,\n2019). Doubling the interval duration (2 min versus\n1 min) resulted in a substantial increase in\nanaerobic glycolytic energy release, along with an\neven great", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "by Yangya Feng et al.\nArticles published in the Journal of Human Kinetics are licensed under an open access Creative Commons CC BY 4.0\nThe objective of this systematic review was\nto critically evaluate the existing literature to\ndetermine the effects of HIIT and MICT on running\neconomy (RE). The initial findings revealed that\nHIIT significantly reduced the oxygen cost of\nrunning compared to MICT in endurance runners.\nFurthermore, the effects of different training\ninterventions on maximal oxygen uptake indicated\nthat MICT was more effective in improving VO2max.\nFinally, training had a significant impact on blood\nlactate concentration. HIIT was particularly\neffective as it enabled the body to adapt to lactate\nmore efficiently than most of other forms of\nexercise. Individuals who engaged in HIIT\nproduced less lactate at similar exercise intensities\nand demonstrated higher tolerance to lactate.\nOur initial findings showed that, among\nendurance runners, HIIT led to significantly\nreduced oxygen cost of running compared to MICT\n(Wang et al., 2012). Previous studies have indicated\nthat different training interventions can have\ndistinct effects on running economy (Barnes and\nKilding, 2015; Faelli et al., 2021; Franch et al., 1998).\nCompared to MICT interventions, HIIT intervals of\n1 min or less did not demonstrate a significant\ndifference in oxygen costs. However, intervals\nlonger than one minute led to a significant\nimprovement in max", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "an increase in maximal oxygen uptake,\ndespite shorter total training duration (Wen et al.,\n2019). Doubling the interval duration (2 min versus\n1 min) resulted in a substantial increase in\nanaerobic glycolytic energy release, along with an\neven great"}
{"chunk_id": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898_p0012_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_547f1419e9ba", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "72691f9f7837b4bc", "text_span_hash": "72691f9f7837b4bc", "source_file": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ease, along with an\neven greater enhancement in oxygen availability\n(Vuorimaa et al., 2000). HIIT primarily acts on fast‐\ntwitch muscle fibers through the arrangement of\ninterval training duration and recovery time\n(interval training with duration of", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "by Yangya Feng et al.\nArticles published in the Journal of Human Kinetics are licensed under an open access Creative Commons CC BY 4.0\nThe objective of this systematic review was\nto critically evaluate the existing literature to\ndetermine the effects of HIIT and MICT on running\neconomy (RE). The initial findings revealed that\nHIIT significantly reduced the oxygen cost of\nrunning compared to MICT in endurance runners.\nFurthermore, the effects of different training\ninterventions on maximal oxygen uptake indicated\nthat MICT was more effective in improving VO2max.\nFinally, training had a significant impact on blood\nlactate concentration. HIIT was particularly\neffective as it enabled the body to adapt to lactate\nmore efficiently than most of other forms of\nexercise. Individuals who engaged in HIIT\nproduced less lactate at similar exercise intensities\nand demonstrated higher tolerance to lactate.\nOur initial findings showed that, among\nendurance runners, HIIT led to significantly\nreduced oxygen cost of running compared to MICT\n(Wang et al., 2012). Previous studies have indicated\nthat different training interventions can have\ndistinct effects on running economy (Barnes and\nKilding, 2015; Faelli et al., 2021; Franch et al., 1998).\nCompared to MICT interventions, HIIT intervals of\n1 min or less did not demonstrate a significant\ndifference in oxygen costs. However, intervals\nlonger than one minute led to a significant\nimprovement in max", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ease, along with an\neven greater enhancement in oxygen availability\n(Vuorimaa et al., 2000). HIIT primarily acts on fast‐\ntwitch muscle fibers through the arrangement of\ninterval training duration and recovery time\n(interval training with duration of"}
{"chunk_id": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898_p0012_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_547f1419e9ba", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "46cbde4127cbf736", "text_span_hash": "46cbde4127cbf736", "source_file": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "rval training with duration of ≥ 2 min being\nparticularly effective for aerobic stimulation);\nMICT primarily targets slow muscle fibers,\nthrough continuous training, it increases the\nnumber of mitochondria and the expression of\nrelated proteases in s", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "by Yangya Feng et al.\nArticles published in the Journal of Human Kinetics are licensed under an open access Creative Commons CC BY 4.0\nThe objective of this systematic review was\nto critically evaluate the existing literature to\ndetermine the effects of HIIT and MICT on running\neconomy (RE). The initial findings revealed that\nHIIT significantly reduced the oxygen cost of\nrunning compared to MICT in endurance runners.\nFurthermore, the effects of different training\ninterventions on maximal oxygen uptake indicated\nthat MICT was more effective in improving VO2max.\nFinally, training had a significant impact on blood\nlactate concentration. HIIT was particularly\neffective as it enabled the body to adapt to lactate\nmore efficiently than most of other forms of\nexercise. Individuals who engaged in HIIT\nproduced less lactate at similar exercise intensities\nand demonstrated higher tolerance to lactate.\nOur initial findings showed that, among\nendurance runners, HIIT led to significantly\nreduced oxygen cost of running compared to MICT\n(Wang et al., 2012). Previous studies have indicated\nthat different training interventions can have\ndistinct effects on running economy (Barnes and\nKilding, 2015; Faelli et al., 2021; Franch et al., 1998).\nCompared to MICT interventions, HIIT intervals of\n1 min or less did not demonstrate a significant\ndifference in oxygen costs. However, intervals\nlonger than one minute led to a significant\nimprovement in max", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "rval training with duration of ≥ 2 min being\nparticularly effective for aerobic stimulation);\nMICT primarily targets slow muscle fibers,\nthrough continuous training, it increases the\nnumber of mitochondria and the expression of\nrelated proteases in s"}
{"chunk_id": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898_p0012_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_547f1419e9ba", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "61ee902cd0db7c53", "text_span_hash": "61ee902cd0db7c53", "source_file": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "r et\nal., 2023), increases hemoglobin levels, and\n(Mandić et al., 2022; Sawyer et al., 2016). These\nadaptations contribute to the overall efficiency of\nboth aerobic and anaerobic energy systems\n(Bogdanis et al., 2013; Buchheit and Laursen, 2013).\nMIC", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "by Yangya Feng et al.\nArticles published in the Journal of Human Kinetics are licensed under an open access Creative Commons CC BY 4.0\nThe objective of this systematic review was\nto critically evaluate the existing literature to\ndetermine the effects of HIIT and MICT on running\neconomy (RE). The initial findings revealed that\nHIIT significantly reduced the oxygen cost of\nrunning compared to MICT in endurance runners.\nFurthermore, the effects of different training\ninterventions on maximal oxygen uptake indicated\nthat MICT was more effective in improving VO2max.\nFinally, training had a significant impact on blood\nlactate concentration. HIIT was particularly\neffective as it enabled the body to adapt to lactate\nmore efficiently than most of other forms of\nexercise. Individuals who engaged in HIIT\nproduced less lactate at similar exercise intensities\nand demonstrated higher tolerance to lactate.\nOur initial findings showed that, among\nendurance runners, HIIT led to significantly\nreduced oxygen cost of running compared to MICT\n(Wang et al., 2012). Previous studies have indicated\nthat different training interventions can have\ndistinct effects on running economy (Barnes and\nKilding, 2015; Faelli et al., 2021; Franch et al., 1998).\nCompared to MICT interventions, HIIT intervals of\n1 min or less did not demonstrate a significant\ndifference in oxygen costs. However, intervals\nlonger than one minute led to a significant\nimprovement in max", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "r et\nal., 2023), increases hemoglobin levels, and\n(Mandić et al., 2022; Sawyer et al., 2016). These\nadaptations contribute to the overall efficiency of\nboth aerobic and anaerobic energy systems\n(Bogdanis et al., 2013; Buchheit and Laursen, 2013).\nMIC"}
{"chunk_id": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898_p0012_c0017", "source_registry_id": "src_547f1419e9ba", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "974af58874e82466", "text_span_hash": "974af58874e82466", "source_file": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 17, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "MICT, the faster the speed, and the higher the\nstiffness in the lower limb muscles. The greater the\nstiffness, the more beneficial it is for runners to\nconsume less oxygen (Liu et al., 2022). Compared\nimprovements and adaptive changes over a short\np", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "by Yangya Feng et al.\nArticles published in the Journal of Human Kinetics are licensed under an open access Creative Commons CC BY 4.0\nThe objective of this systematic review was\nto critically evaluate the existing literature to\ndetermine the effects of HIIT and MICT on running\neconomy (RE). The initial findings revealed that\nHIIT significantly reduced the oxygen cost of\nrunning compared to MICT in endurance runners.\nFurthermore, the effects of different training\ninterventions on maximal oxygen uptake indicated\nthat MICT was more effective in improving VO2max.\nFinally, training had a significant impact on blood\nlactate concentration. HIIT was particularly\neffective as it enabled the body to adapt to lactate\nmore efficiently than most of other forms of\nexercise. Individuals who engaged in HIIT\nproduced less lactate at similar exercise intensities\nand demonstrated higher tolerance to lactate.\nOur initial findings showed that, among\nendurance runners, HIIT led to significantly\nreduced oxygen cost of running compared to MICT\n(Wang et al., 2012). Previous studies have indicated\nthat different training interventions can have\ndistinct effects on running economy (Barnes and\nKilding, 2015; Faelli et al., 2021; Franch et al., 1998).\nCompared to MICT interventions, HIIT intervals of\n1 min or less did not demonstrate a significant\ndifference in oxygen costs. However, intervals\nlonger than one minute led to a significant\nimprovement in max", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "MICT, the faster the speed, and the higher the\nstiffness in the lower limb muscles. The greater the\nstiffness, the more beneficial it is for runners to\nconsume less oxygen (Liu et al., 2022). Compared\nimprovements and adaptive changes over a short\np"}
{"chunk_id": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898_p0012_c0018", "source_registry_id": "src_547f1419e9ba", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9dcee7d20583374c", "text_span_hash": "9dcee7d20583374c", "source_file": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 18, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "daptive changes over a short\nperiod of time, thereby enhancing RE of runners\n(Skovgaard et al., 2018a). According to our meta‐\nanalysis, HIIT has more beneficial effects on RE at\nintensities near Z2 compared to those near Z3.\nWhen the velocity exceed", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "by Yangya Feng et al.\nArticles published in the Journal of Human Kinetics are licensed under an open access Creative Commons CC BY 4.0\nThe objective of this systematic review was\nto critically evaluate the existing literature to\ndetermine the effects of HIIT and MICT on running\neconomy (RE). The initial findings revealed that\nHIIT significantly reduced the oxygen cost of\nrunning compared to MICT in endurance runners.\nFurthermore, the effects of different training\ninterventions on maximal oxygen uptake indicated\nthat MICT was more effective in improving VO2max.\nFinally, training had a significant impact on blood\nlactate concentration. HIIT was particularly\neffective as it enabled the body to adapt to lactate\nmore efficiently than most of other forms of\nexercise. Individuals who engaged in HIIT\nproduced less lactate at similar exercise intensities\nand demonstrated higher tolerance to lactate.\nOur initial findings showed that, among\nendurance runners, HIIT led to significantly\nreduced oxygen cost of running compared to MICT\n(Wang et al., 2012). Previous studies have indicated\nthat different training interventions can have\ndistinct effects on running economy (Barnes and\nKilding, 2015; Faelli et al., 2021; Franch et al., 1998).\nCompared to MICT interventions, HIIT intervals of\n1 min or less did not demonstrate a significant\ndifference in oxygen costs. However, intervals\nlonger than one minute led to a significant\nimprovement in max", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "daptive changes over a short\nperiod of time, thereby enhancing RE of runners\n(Skovgaard et al., 2018a). According to our meta‐\nanalysis, HIIT has more beneficial effects on RE at\nintensities near Z2 compared to those near Z3.\nWhen the velocity exceed"}
{"chunk_id": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898_p0012_c0019", "source_registry_id": "src_547f1419e9ba", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "6be6a8e654af2b8d", "text_span_hash": "6be6a8e654af2b8d", "source_file": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 19, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "r Z3.\nWhen the velocity exceeds Z2, the effect size\nbecomes non‐significant (Figure 3). Training at an\nintensity close to Z2 may elicit a combination of\nmolecular signals generated by both high‐volume\nand high‐intensity training. This combination of", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "by Yangya Feng et al.\nArticles published in the Journal of Human Kinetics are licensed under an open access Creative Commons CC BY 4.0\nThe objective of this systematic review was\nto critically evaluate the existing literature to\ndetermine the effects of HIIT and MICT on running\neconomy (RE). The initial findings revealed that\nHIIT significantly reduced the oxygen cost of\nrunning compared to MICT in endurance runners.\nFurthermore, the effects of different training\ninterventions on maximal oxygen uptake indicated\nthat MICT was more effective in improving VO2max.\nFinally, training had a significant impact on blood\nlactate concentration. HIIT was particularly\neffective as it enabled the body to adapt to lactate\nmore efficiently than most of other forms of\nexercise. Individuals who engaged in HIIT\nproduced less lactate at similar exercise intensities\nand demonstrated higher tolerance to lactate.\nOur initial findings showed that, among\nendurance runners, HIIT led to significantly\nreduced oxygen cost of running compared to MICT\n(Wang et al., 2012). Previous studies have indicated\nthat different training interventions can have\ndistinct effects on running economy (Barnes and\nKilding, 2015; Faelli et al., 2021; Franch et al., 1998).\nCompared to MICT interventions, HIIT intervals of\n1 min or less did not demonstrate a significant\ndifference in oxygen costs. However, intervals\nlonger than one minute led to a significant\nimprovement in max", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "r Z3.\nWhen the velocity exceeds Z2, the effect size\nbecomes non‐significant (Figure 3). Training at an\nintensity close to Z2 may elicit a combination of\nmolecular signals generated by both high‐volume\nand high‐intensity training. This combination of"}
{"chunk_id": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898_p0012_c0020", "source_registry_id": "src_547f1419e9ba", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e7eb0e2dd9b00fa4", "text_span_hash": "e7eb0e2dd9b00fa4", "source_file": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 20, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "training. This combination of\nsignals provides greater stimulation and has a\nmore significant impact on the body’s adaptation,\nwhich is more conducive to improving RE\n(Skovgaard et al., 2018b). Training in Z3 primarily\nstimulates the body anaerobical", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "by Yangya Feng et al.\nArticles published in the Journal of Human Kinetics are licensed under an open access Creative Commons CC BY 4.0\nThe objective of this systematic review was\nto critically evaluate the existing literature to\ndetermine the effects of HIIT and MICT on running\neconomy (RE). The initial findings revealed that\nHIIT significantly reduced the oxygen cost of\nrunning compared to MICT in endurance runners.\nFurthermore, the effects of different training\ninterventions on maximal oxygen uptake indicated\nthat MICT was more effective in improving VO2max.\nFinally, training had a significant impact on blood\nlactate concentration. HIIT was particularly\neffective as it enabled the body to adapt to lactate\nmore efficiently than most of other forms of\nexercise. Individuals who engaged in HIIT\nproduced less lactate at similar exercise intensities\nand demonstrated higher tolerance to lactate.\nOur initial findings showed that, among\nendurance runners, HIIT led to significantly\nreduced oxygen cost of running compared to MICT\n(Wang et al., 2012). Previous studies have indicated\nthat different training interventions can have\ndistinct effects on running economy (Barnes and\nKilding, 2015; Faelli et al., 2021; Franch et al., 1998).\nCompared to MICT interventions, HIIT intervals of\n1 min or less did not demonstrate a significant\ndifference in oxygen costs. However, intervals\nlonger than one minute led to a significant\nimprovement in max", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "training. This combination of\nsignals provides greater stimulation and has a\nmore significant impact on the body’s adaptation,\nwhich is more conducive to improving RE\n(Skovgaard et al., 2018b). Training in Z3 primarily\nstimulates the body anaerobical"}
