ndistinct effects on running economy (Barnes and\nKilding, 2015; Faelli et al., 2021; Franch et al., 1998).\nCompared to MICT interventions, HIIT intervals of\n1 min or less did not demonstrate a significant\ndifference in oxygen costs. However, intervals\nlonger than one minute led to a significant\nimprovement in max", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "timulates the body anaerobically, and although\nthis intensity can also stimulate the body’s aerobic\nmetabolism to a certain extent, the effect is inferior\nto the former. This finding is consistent with\nresearch indicating that when lactate concentrat"}
{"chunk_id": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898_p0012_c0022", "source_registry_id": "src_547f1419e9ba", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "81c4e46868bb9fe7", "text_span_hash": "81c4e46868bb9fe7", "source_file": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 22, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "g that when lactate concentration\nis greater than 4 mmol/L, the enhancement in\nrunning economy diminishes (Zinner et al., 2018).\nFurthermore, the impact on running economy\ncontinues to decline as exercise intensity increases\n(Munoz et al., 2015). Amo", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "by Yangya Feng et al.\nArticles published in the Journal of Human Kinetics are licensed under an open access Creative Commons CC BY 4.0\nThe objective of this systematic review was\nto critically evaluate the existing literature to\ndetermine the effects of HIIT and MICT on running\neconomy (RE). The initial findings revealed that\nHIIT significantly reduced the oxygen cost of\nrunning compared to MICT in endurance runners.\nFurthermore, the effects of different training\ninterventions on maximal oxygen uptake indicated\nthat MICT was more effective in improving VO2max.\nFinally, training had a significant impact on blood\nlactate concentration. HIIT was particularly\neffective as it enabled the body to adapt to lactate\nmore efficiently than most of other forms of\nexercise. Individuals who engaged in HIIT\nproduced less lactate at similar exercise intensities\nand demonstrated higher tolerance to lactate.\nOur initial findings showed that, among\nendurance runners, HIIT led to significantly\nreduced oxygen cost of running compared to MICT\n(Wang et al., 2012). Previous studies have indicated\nthat different training interventions can have\ndistinct effects on running economy (Barnes and\nKilding, 2015; Faelli et al., 2021; Franch et al., 1998).\nCompared to MICT interventions, HIIT intervals of\n1 min or less did not demonstrate a significant\ndifference in oxygen costs. However, intervals\nlonger than one minute led to a significant\nimprovement in max", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "g that when lactate concentration\nis greater than 4 mmol/L, the enhancement in\nrunning economy diminishes (Zinner et al., 2018).\nFurthermore, the impact on running economy\ncontinues to decline as exercise intensity increases\n(Munoz et al., 2015). Amo"}
{"chunk_id": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898_p0012_c0023", "source_registry_id": "src_547f1419e9ba", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "32a95fb2b820ec75", "text_span_hash": "32a95fb2b820ec75", "source_file": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 23, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ases\n(Munoz et al., 2015). Among the different runners,", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "by Yangya Feng et al.\nArticles published in the Journal of Human Kinetics are licensed under an open access Creative Commons CC BY 4.0\nThe objective of this systematic review was\nto critically evaluate the existing literature to\ndetermine the effects of HIIT and MICT on running\neconomy (RE). The initial findings revealed that\nHIIT significantly reduced the oxygen cost of\nrunning compared to MICT in endurance runners.\nFurthermore, the effects of different training\ninterventions on maximal oxygen uptake indicated\nthat MICT was more effective in improving VO2max.\nFinally, training had a significant impact on blood\nlactate concentration. HIIT was particularly\neffective as it enabled the body to adapt to lactate\nmore efficiently than most of other forms of\nexercise. Individuals who engaged in HIIT\nproduced less lactate at similar exercise intensities\nand demonstrated higher tolerance to lactate.\nOur initial findings showed that, among\nendurance runners, HIIT led to significantly\nreduced oxygen cost of running compared to MICT\n(Wang et al., 2012). Previous studies have indicated\nthat different training interventions can have\ndistinct effects on running economy (Barnes and\nKilding, 2015; Faelli et al., 2021; Franch et al., 1998).\nCompared to MICT interventions, HIIT intervals of\n1 min or less did not demonstrate a significant\ndifference in oxygen costs. However, intervals\nlonger than one minute led to a significant\nimprovement in max", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ases\n(Munoz et al., 2015). Among the different runners,"}
{"chunk_id": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898_p0013_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_547f1419e9ba", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e721f352279a1dc5", "text_span_hash": "e721f352279a1dc5", "source_file": "36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\36b5639a13f6f78e00f1c2ca46b86e854898.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Training Intensity and Running Economy in Endurance Runners\nJournal of Human Kinetics, volume xxx, xxxxx\nwe found that the improvement in running\neconomy within the Z3 was reduced for well‐\ntrained and recreational runners, with the effect\nbeing part", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training Intensity and Running Economy in Endurance Runners\nJournal of Human Kinetics, volume xxx, xxxxx\nwe found that the improvement in running\neconomy within the Z3 was reduced for well‐\ntrained and recreational runners, with the effect\nbeing particularly diminished for recreational\nrunners. Repeated training in Z3 may result in\nparasympathetic nerve activity (Kingsley et al.,\n2005; Nuuttila et al., 2022). For runners, training\nintensity should be kept close to Z2 to optimize\nrunning economy, especially for low‐ and\nmedium‐level athletes. Overall, our findings\nindicate that HIIT at or below the lactate threshold\nduring the Z2 phase is essential for enhancing\nIt is widely acknowledged that maximal\noxygen uptake is one of the most critical factors for\nsuccessful distance running (Parmar et al., 2021).\nCurrent studies have shown that both HIIT and\nMICT interventions can enhance maximal oxygen\nuptake (Mølmen et al., 2025). Our meta‐analysis\nfound that MICT had a greater effect on peak\noxygen uptake than HIIT, which is consistent with\na recent review of related studies (Guo et al., 2022;\nLiang et al., 2024). However, other scholars have\nresearchers pointed out that HIIT was particularly\neffective in improving VO2max during the initial\nstages of training for individuals with lower fitness\nimprovements in ventilatory thresholds above the\nsecond ventilatory improvements were only\nobserved in well‐trained endurance runners (Seiler\ne", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Training Intensity and Running Economy in Endurance Runners\nJournal of Human Kinetics, volume xxx, xxxxx\nwe found that the improvement in running\neconomy within the Z3 was reduced for well‐\ntrained and recreational runners, with the effect\nbeing part"}
