{"chunk_id": "4_programming_interval_training_tt_2021_p0035_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_b34f4bef25be", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "61e10f7446b00d89", "text_span_hash": "61e10f7446b00d89", "source_file": "4_programming_interval_training_tt_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\4_programming_interval_training_tt_2021.pdf", "page": 35, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Rosenblat, M.A., Lin, E., da Costa, B.R. et al. Programming Interval\nTraining to Optimize Time-Trial Performance: A Systematic Review and\n• Accepted: 19 March 2021\n• Published: 07 April 2021\n• Version of record: 07 April 2021\n• Issue date: August 202", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Rosenblat, M.A., Lin, E., da Costa, B.R. et al. Programming Interval\nTraining to Optimize Time-Trial Performance: A Systematic Review and\n• Accepted: 19 March 2021\n• Published: 07 April 2021\n• Version of record: 07 April 2021\n• Issue date: August 2021\n1. Bruno R. da Costa\nLog in via an institution", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Rosenblat, M.A., Lin, E., da Costa, B.R. et al. Programming Interval\nTraining to Optimize Time-Trial Performance: A Systematic Review and\n• Accepted: 19 March 2021\n• Published: 07 April 2021\n• Version of record: 07 April 2021\n• Issue date: August 202"}
{"chunk_id": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022_p0001_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_1e7a7634c026", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5f74d0900ae0f74e", "text_span_hash": "5f74d0900ae0f74e", "source_file": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nReverse Periodization for Improving Sports\nPerformance: A Systematic Review\nJosé M. González‑Ravé1  , Fernando González‑Mohino1,2*, Víctor Rodrigo‑Carranza1  and David B. Pyne3\nBackground:  Reve", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nReverse Periodization for Improving Sports\nPerformance: A Systematic Review\nJosé M. González‑Ravé1  , Fernando González‑Mohino1,2*, Víctor Rodrigo‑Carranza1  and David B. Pyne3\nBackground:  Reverse periodization is commonly touted as a salient planning strategy to improve sport performance\nin athletes, but benefits have not been clearly described.\nObjectives:  We sought to identify the main characteristics of reverse periodization, and the influence of training\nvolume and periodization models on enhancing physiological measures and sports performance.\nDesign:  Systematic review.\nMethods:  The electronic databases Scopus, PubMed and Web of Science were searched using a comprehensive list\nResults:  A total of 925 studies were identified, and after removal of duplicates and studies based on title and abstract\nscreening, 17 studies remained, and 11 finally included in the systematic review. There was a total of 200 athletes in\nthe included studies. Reverse periodization does not provide superior performance improvements in swimming, run‑\nning, muscular endurance, maximum strength, or maximal oxygen uptake, compared to traditional or block periodi‑\nzation. The quality of evidence levels for the reverse periodization studies was 1b (individual randomized controlled\ntrial) for two investigations, 2b (individual cohort study) for the remaining studies and a mean of 4.9 points in ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nReverse Periodization for Improving Sports\nPerformance: A Systematic Review\nJosé M. González‑Ravé1  , Fernando González‑Mohino1,2*, Víctor Rodrigo‑Carranza1  and David B. Pyne3\nBackground:  Reve"}
{"chunk_id": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022_p0001_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_1e7a7634c026", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e224283178a2a30a", "text_span_hash": "e224283178a2a30a", "source_file": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "vid B. Pyne3\nBackground:  Reverse periodization is commonly touted as a salient planning strategy to improve sport performance\nin athletes, but benefits have not been clearly described.\nObjectives:  We sought to identify the main characteristics of r", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nReverse Periodization for Improving Sports\nPerformance: A Systematic Review\nJosé M. González‑Ravé1  , Fernando González‑Mohino1,2*, Víctor Rodrigo‑Carranza1  and David B. Pyne3\nBackground:  Reverse periodization is commonly touted as a salient planning strategy to improve sport performance\nin athletes, but benefits have not been clearly described.\nObjectives:  We sought to identify the main characteristics of reverse periodization, and the influence of training\nvolume and periodization models on enhancing physiological measures and sports performance.\nDesign:  Systematic review.\nMethods:  The electronic databases Scopus, PubMed and Web of Science were searched using a comprehensive list\nResults:  A total of 925 studies were identified, and after removal of duplicates and studies based on title and abstract\nscreening, 17 studies remained, and 11 finally included in the systematic review. There was a total of 200 athletes in\nthe included studies. Reverse periodization does not provide superior performance improvements in swimming, run‑\nning, muscular endurance, maximum strength, or maximal oxygen uptake, compared to traditional or block periodi‑\nzation. The quality of evidence levels for the reverse periodization studies was 1b (individual randomized controlled\ntrial) for two investigations, 2b (individual cohort study) for the remaining studies and a mean of 4.9 points in ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "vid B. Pyne3\nBackground:  Reverse periodization is commonly touted as a salient planning strategy to improve sport performance\nin athletes, but benefits have not been clearly described.\nObjectives:  We sought to identify the main characteristics of r"}
