{"chunk_id": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022_p0001_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_1e7a7634c026", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "038b8cd67b75460e", "text_span_hash": "038b8cd67b75460e", "source_file": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "iodization mod-\nels in endurance sports require careful planning of\nexperimental design, longer study periods, and where\nappropriate matching of training volumes and inten-\nPeriodization is a process that serves as the macroman-\nagement of an athlete", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nReverse Periodization for Improving Sports\nPerformance: A Systematic Review\nJosé M. González‑Ravé1  , Fernando González‑Mohino1,2*, Víctor Rodrigo‑Carranza1  and David B. Pyne3\nBackground:  Reverse periodization is commonly touted as a salient planning strategy to improve sport performance\nin athletes, but benefits have not been clearly described.\nObjectives:  We sought to identify the main characteristics of reverse periodization, and the influence of training\nvolume and periodization models on enhancing physiological measures and sports performance.\nDesign:  Systematic review.\nMethods:  The electronic databases Scopus, PubMed and Web of Science were searched using a comprehensive list\nResults:  A total of 925 studies were identified, and after removal of duplicates and studies based on title and abstract\nscreening, 17 studies remained, and 11 finally included in the systematic review. There was a total of 200 athletes in\nthe included studies. Reverse periodization does not provide superior performance improvements in swimming, run‑\nning, muscular endurance, maximum strength, or maximal oxygen uptake, compared to traditional or block periodi‑\nzation. The quality of evidence levels for the reverse periodization studies was 1b (individual randomized controlled\ntrial) for two investigations, 2b (individual cohort study) for the remaining studies and a mean of 4.9 points in ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "iodization mod-\nels in endurance sports require careful planning of\nexperimental design, longer study periods, and where\nappropriate matching of training volumes and inten-\nPeriodization is a process that serves as the macroman-\nagement of an athlete"}
{"chunk_id": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022_p0001_c0017", "source_registry_id": "src_1e7a7634c026", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9bdf7457da649c4f", "text_span_hash": "9bdf7457da649c4f", "source_file": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 17, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "lym-\npic Games [3]. Periodization continues to be a valid\n*Correspondence: fernando.GMayoralas@uclm.es\n1 Sports Training Laboratory, Faculty of Sports Sciences, University\nof Castilla-La Mancha, Carlos III Avenue, 45008 Toledo, Spain\nFull list of aut", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nReverse Periodization for Improving Sports\nPerformance: A Systematic Review\nJosé M. González‑Ravé1  , Fernando González‑Mohino1,2*, Víctor Rodrigo‑Carranza1  and David B. Pyne3\nBackground:  Reverse periodization is commonly touted as a salient planning strategy to improve sport performance\nin athletes, but benefits have not been clearly described.\nObjectives:  We sought to identify the main characteristics of reverse periodization, and the influence of training\nvolume and periodization models on enhancing physiological measures and sports performance.\nDesign:  Systematic review.\nMethods:  The electronic databases Scopus, PubMed and Web of Science were searched using a comprehensive list\nResults:  A total of 925 studies were identified, and after removal of duplicates and studies based on title and abstract\nscreening, 17 studies remained, and 11 finally included in the systematic review. There was a total of 200 athletes in\nthe included studies. Reverse periodization does not provide superior performance improvements in swimming, run‑\nning, muscular endurance, maximum strength, or maximal oxygen uptake, compared to traditional or block periodi‑\nzation. The quality of evidence levels for the reverse periodization studies was 1b (individual randomized controlled\ntrial) for two investigations, 2b (individual cohort study) for the remaining studies and a mean of 4.9 points in ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "lym-\npic Games [3]. Periodization continues to be a valid\n*Correspondence: fernando.GMayoralas@uclm.es\n1 Sports Training Laboratory, Faculty of Sports Sciences, University\nof Castilla-La Mancha, Carlos III Avenue, 45008 Toledo, Spain\nFull list of aut"}
{"chunk_id": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022_p0002_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_1e7a7634c026", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b9eeaa8f6e279f92", "text_span_hash": "b9eeaa8f6e279f92", "source_file": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nand reliable model for athletes and is the predominant\ntraining methodology used in individual sports such as\nswimming [4–6]. However, prior to Matveyev’s seminal\ncontribution to the topic, ther", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nand reliable model for athletes and is the predominant\ntraining methodology used in individual sports such as\nswimming [4–6]. However, prior to Matveyev’s seminal\ncontribution to the topic, there was foundational work\nthat underpins the theory of periodization [3, 7–9]. A\nlarge number of authors have conceptualized periodized\ntraining in various models, with different variations of\nthe underlying training process, planning, progressions\nin training volume and intensity, and recovery [10–12].\nThe original concept of periodization was proposed ini-\ntially by Boris Kotov in his book “Olympic Sport” in 1916;\nlater, Pihkala [13] postulated a number of principles such\nas dividing the annual cycle into preparatory, spring and\nsummer phases, and active rest ending the season [14].\nThese authors have conceptualized various approaches\nwithout an accepted formal definition of periodization\nas promulgated by Kataoka et al. [1]. The term Periodiza-\ntion was originally employed to describe programs taking\nthe form of predetermined sequential chains of specifi-\ncally focused training periods. Periodization is a cycli-\ncal method of training, where the removal of linearity,\nand appropriate variation in the form of repeating load\noscillations, can provide a superior method of training\nas Stone et al. identify in their recent (and provocative)\nnarrative review [11]. Kiely [12] asserts the ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nand reliable model for athletes and is the predominant\ntraining methodology used in individual sports such as\nswimming [4–6]. However, prior to Matveyev’s seminal\ncontribution to the topic, ther"}
{"chunk_id": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022_p0002_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_1e7a7634c026", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c646be949b32fd91", "text_span_hash": "c646be949b32fd91", "source_file": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ontribution to the topic, there was foundational work\nthat underpins the theory of periodization [3, 7–9]. A\nlarge number of authors have conceptualized periodized\ntraining in various models, with different variations of\nthe underlying training proce", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nand reliable model for athletes and is the predominant\ntraining methodology used in individual sports such as\nswimming [4–6]. However, prior to Matveyev’s seminal\ncontribution to the topic, there was foundational work\nthat underpins the theory of periodization [3, 7–9]. A\nlarge number of authors have conceptualized periodized\ntraining in various models, with different variations of\nthe underlying training process, planning, progressions\nin training volume and intensity, and recovery [10–12].\nThe original concept of periodization was proposed ini-\ntially by Boris Kotov in his book “Olympic Sport” in 1916;\nlater, Pihkala [13] postulated a number of principles such\nas dividing the annual cycle into preparatory, spring and\nsummer phases, and active rest ending the season [14].\nThese authors have conceptualized various approaches\nwithout an accepted formal definition of periodization\nas promulgated by Kataoka et al. [1]. The term Periodiza-\ntion was originally employed to describe programs taking\nthe form of predetermined sequential chains of specifi-\ncally focused training periods. Periodization is a cycli-\ncal method of training, where the removal of linearity,\nand appropriate variation in the form of repeating load\noscillations, can provide a superior method of training\nas Stone et al. identify in their recent (and provocative)\nnarrative review [11]. Kiely [12] asserts the ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ontribution to the topic, there was foundational work\nthat underpins the theory of periodization [3, 7–9]. A\nlarge number of authors have conceptualized periodized\ntraining in various models, with different variations of\nthe underlying training proce"}
{"chunk_id": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022_p0002_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_1e7a7634c026", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7797dacb42a69bb5", "text_span_hash": "7797dacb42a69bb5", "source_file": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "the underlying training process, planning, progressions\nin training volume and intensity, and recovery [10–12].\nThe original concept of periodization was proposed ini-\ntially by Boris Kotov in his book “Olympic Sport” in 1916;\nlater, Pihkala [13] po", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nand reliable model for athletes and is the predominant\ntraining methodology used in individual sports such as\nswimming [4–6]. However, prior to Matveyev’s seminal\ncontribution to the topic, there was foundational work\nthat underpins the theory of periodization [3, 7–9]. A\nlarge number of authors have conceptualized periodized\ntraining in various models, with different variations of\nthe underlying training process, planning, progressions\nin training volume and intensity, and recovery [10–12].\nThe original concept of periodization was proposed ini-\ntially by Boris Kotov in his book “Olympic Sport” in 1916;\nlater, Pihkala [13] postulated a number of principles such\nas dividing the annual cycle into preparatory, spring and\nsummer phases, and active rest ending the season [14].\nThese authors have conceptualized various approaches\nwithout an accepted formal definition of periodization\nas promulgated by Kataoka et al. [1]. The term Periodiza-\ntion was originally employed to describe programs taking\nthe form of predetermined sequential chains of specifi-\ncally focused training periods. Periodization is a cycli-\ncal method of training, where the removal of linearity,\nand appropriate variation in the form of repeating load\noscillations, can provide a superior method of training\nas Stone et al. identify in their recent (and provocative)\nnarrative review [11]. Kiely [12] asserts the ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "the underlying training process, planning, progressions\nin training volume and intensity, and recovery [10–12].\nThe original concept of periodization was proposed ini-\ntially by Boris Kotov in his book “Olympic Sport” in 1916;\nlater, Pihkala [13] po"}
{"chunk_id": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022_p0002_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_1e7a7634c026", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "27a763fdfb5b80ce", "text_span_hash": "27a763fdfb5b80ce", "source_file": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "s approaches\nwithout an accepted formal definition of periodization\nas promulgated by Kataoka et al. [1]. The term Periodiza-\ntion was originally employed to describe programs taking\nthe form of predetermined sequential chains of specifi-\ncally focus", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nand reliable model for athletes and is the predominant\ntraining methodology used in individual sports such as\nswimming [4–6]. However, prior to Matveyev’s seminal\ncontribution to the topic, there was foundational work\nthat underpins the theory of periodization [3, 7–9]. A\nlarge number of authors have conceptualized periodized\ntraining in various models, with different variations of\nthe underlying training process, planning, progressions\nin training volume and intensity, and recovery [10–12].\nThe original concept of periodization was proposed ini-\ntially by Boris Kotov in his book “Olympic Sport” in 1916;\nlater, Pihkala [13] postulated a number of principles such\nas dividing the annual cycle into preparatory, spring and\nsummer phases, and active rest ending the season [14].\nThese authors have conceptualized various approaches\nwithout an accepted formal definition of periodization\nas promulgated by Kataoka et al. [1]. The term Periodiza-\ntion was originally employed to describe programs taking\nthe form of predetermined sequential chains of specifi-\ncally focused training periods. Periodization is a cycli-\ncal method of training, where the removal of linearity,\nand appropriate variation in the form of repeating load\noscillations, can provide a superior method of training\nas Stone et al. identify in their recent (and provocative)\nnarrative review [11]. Kiely [12] asserts the ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "s approaches\nwithout an accepted formal definition of periodization\nas promulgated by Kataoka et al. [1]. The term Periodiza-\ntion was originally employed to describe programs taking\nthe form of predetermined sequential chains of specifi-\ncally focus"}
{"chunk_id": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022_p0002_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_1e7a7634c026", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "585de8557bf2e204", "text_span_hash": "585de8557bf2e204", "source_file": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "chains of specifi-\ncally focused training periods. Periodization is a cycli-\ncal method of training, where the removal of linearity,\nand appropriate variation in the form of repeating load\noscillations, can provide a superior method of training\nas St", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nand reliable model for athletes and is the predominant\ntraining methodology used in individual sports such as\nswimming [4–6]. However, prior to Matveyev’s seminal\ncontribution to the topic, there was foundational work\nthat underpins the theory of periodization [3, 7–9]. A\nlarge number of authors have conceptualized periodized\ntraining in various models, with different variations of\nthe underlying training process, planning, progressions\nin training volume and intensity, and recovery [10–12].\nThe original concept of periodization was proposed ini-\ntially by Boris Kotov in his book “Olympic Sport” in 1916;\nlater, Pihkala [13] postulated a number of principles such\nas dividing the annual cycle into preparatory, spring and\nsummer phases, and active rest ending the season [14].\nThese authors have conceptualized various approaches\nwithout an accepted formal definition of periodization\nas promulgated by Kataoka et al. [1]. The term Periodiza-\ntion was originally employed to describe programs taking\nthe form of predetermined sequential chains of specifi-\ncally focused training periods. Periodization is a cycli-\ncal method of training, where the removal of linearity,\nand appropriate variation in the form of repeating load\noscillations, can provide a superior method of training\nas Stone et al. identify in their recent (and provocative)\nnarrative review [11]. Kiely [12] asserts the ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "chains of specifi-\ncally focused training periods. Periodization is a cycli-\ncal method of training, where the removal of linearity,\nand appropriate variation in the form of repeating load\noscillations, can provide a superior method of training\nas St"}
{"chunk_id": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022_p0002_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_1e7a7634c026", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "585b029cb203c076", "text_span_hash": "585b029cb203c076", "source_file": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "erior method of training\nas Stone et al. identify in their recent (and provocative)\nnarrative review [11]. Kiely [12] asserts the term perio-\ndization is frequently engaged to describe any form of\ntraining plan, regardless of structure. The challenge", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nand reliable model for athletes and is the predominant\ntraining methodology used in individual sports such as\nswimming [4–6]. However, prior to Matveyev’s seminal\ncontribution to the topic, there was foundational work\nthat underpins the theory of periodization [3, 7–9]. A\nlarge number of authors have conceptualized periodized\ntraining in various models, with different variations of\nthe underlying training process, planning, progressions\nin training volume and intensity, and recovery [10–12].\nThe original concept of periodization was proposed ini-\ntially by Boris Kotov in his book “Olympic Sport” in 1916;\nlater, Pihkala [13] postulated a number of principles such\nas dividing the annual cycle into preparatory, spring and\nsummer phases, and active rest ending the season [14].\nThese authors have conceptualized various approaches\nwithout an accepted formal definition of periodization\nas promulgated by Kataoka et al. [1]. The term Periodiza-\ntion was originally employed to describe programs taking\nthe form of predetermined sequential chains of specifi-\ncally focused training periods. Periodization is a cycli-\ncal method of training, where the removal of linearity,\nand appropriate variation in the form of repeating load\noscillations, can provide a superior method of training\nas Stone et al. identify in their recent (and provocative)\nnarrative review [11]. Kiely [12] asserts the ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "erior method of training\nas Stone et al. identify in their recent (and provocative)\nnarrative review [11]. Kiely [12] asserts the term perio-\ndization is frequently engaged to describe any form of\ntraining plan, regardless of structure. The challenge"}
{"chunk_id": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022_p0002_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_1e7a7634c026", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9e913aba8e97046a", "text_span_hash": "9e913aba8e97046a", "source_file": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ss of structure. The challenge is to\nprovide evidence-based guidelines on periodization that\nmeet the conceptual and practical requirements of a wide\nvariety of sports and events.\nThe rationale of periodized models of strength and\npower training in a", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nand reliable model for athletes and is the predominant\ntraining methodology used in individual sports such as\nswimming [4–6]. However, prior to Matveyev’s seminal\ncontribution to the topic, there was foundational work\nthat underpins the theory of periodization [3, 7–9]. A\nlarge number of authors have conceptualized periodized\ntraining in various models, with different variations of\nthe underlying training process, planning, progressions\nin training volume and intensity, and recovery [10–12].\nThe original concept of periodization was proposed ini-\ntially by Boris Kotov in his book “Olympic Sport” in 1916;\nlater, Pihkala [13] postulated a number of principles such\nas dividing the annual cycle into preparatory, spring and\nsummer phases, and active rest ending the season [14].\nThese authors have conceptualized various approaches\nwithout an accepted formal definition of periodization\nas promulgated by Kataoka et al. [1]. The term Periodiza-\ntion was originally employed to describe programs taking\nthe form of predetermined sequential chains of specifi-\ncally focused training periods. Periodization is a cycli-\ncal method of training, where the removal of linearity,\nand appropriate variation in the form of repeating load\noscillations, can provide a superior method of training\nas Stone et al. identify in their recent (and provocative)\nnarrative review [11]. Kiely [12] asserts the ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ss of structure. The challenge is to\nprovide evidence-based guidelines on periodization that\nmeet the conceptual and practical requirements of a wide\nvariety of sports and events.\nThe rationale of periodized models of strength and\npower training in a"}
