{"chunk_id": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022_p0011_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_1e7a7634c026", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "597425cf38e32fe4", "text_span_hash": "597425cf38e32fe4", "source_file": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nFig. 2  Example of mesocycle distribution of traditional periodization, block periodization and reverse linear periodization. A Intensity distribution of\nthe different periodization models. B Vo", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nFig. 2  Example of mesocycle distribution of traditional periodization, block periodization and reverse linear periodization. A Intensity distribution of\nthe different periodization models. B Volume distribution of the different periodization models", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nFig. 2  Example of mesocycle distribution of traditional periodization, block periodization and reverse linear periodization. A Intensity distribution of\nthe different periodization models. B Vo"}
{"chunk_id": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022_p0011_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_1e7a7634c026", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2f3946a367002c28", "text_span_hash": "2f3946a367002c28", "source_file": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ent periodization models. B Volume distribution of the different periodization models", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nFig. 2  Example of mesocycle distribution of traditional periodization, block periodization and reverse linear periodization. A Intensity distribution of\nthe different periodization models. B Volume distribution of the different periodization models", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ent periodization models. B Volume distribution of the different periodization models"}
{"chunk_id": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022_p0012_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_1e7a7634c026", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c12e75c9dd636c42", "text_span_hash": "c12e75c9dd636c42", "source_file": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ging from 12 to 15 weeks. The short duration of\nthe interventions in periodization studies makes it dif-\nficult to draw firm conclusions regarding longer-term\nchanges in exercise and/or sports performance of any\nparticular periodization model.\nIn rel", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nwere predominantly short-term interventions [26, 27,\n29, 31, 41, 43] lasting ~ 10 weeks, and only four stud-\nies had a duration longer than 12 weeks [28, 34–36]\nranging from 12 to 15 weeks. The short duration of\nthe interventions in periodization studies makes it dif-\nficult to draw firm conclusions regarding longer-term\nchanges in exercise and/or sports performance of any\nparticular periodization model.\nIn relation to competitive (sports) performance, 5 of\nthe 11 studies included in this review were in swim-\nming. A systematic review on swimming periodiza-\ntion identified that the traditional periodization was\nthe most common form used in well-trained swim-\nmers, but only four studies compared traditional ver-\nsus reverse periodization [5]. Our results suggest\nthat reverse periodization improved swimming per-\nformance [26] more than block periodization, while\nClemente-Suárez and Ramos-Campo [29], reported a\nsimilar improvement in swimming technical ability and\nswimming performance with reverse periodization and\ntraditional periodization. However, neither traditional\n(characterized by pyramidal TID) nor reverse perio-\ndization (characterized by polarized TID) yielded sig-\nnificant improvements in 50-m swimming performance\n[27, 31]. Only two studies [26, 34] reported significant\nimprovements in 100-m swimming performance fol-\nlowing reverse periodization and block periodiz", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ging from 12 to 15 weeks. The short duration of\nthe interventions in periodization studies makes it dif-\nficult to draw firm conclusions regarding longer-term\nchanges in exercise and/or sports performance of any\nparticular periodization model.\nIn rel"}
{"chunk_id": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022_p0012_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_1e7a7634c026", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "66622249fdb53c2d", "text_span_hash": "66622249fdb53c2d", "source_file": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ar periodization model.\nIn relation to competitive (sports) performance, 5 of\nthe 11 studies included in this review were in swim-\nming. A systematic review on swimming periodiza-\ntion identified that the traditional periodization was\nthe most common", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nwere predominantly short-term interventions [26, 27,\n29, 31, 41, 43] lasting ~ 10 weeks, and only four stud-\nies had a duration longer than 12 weeks [28, 34–36]\nranging from 12 to 15 weeks. The short duration of\nthe interventions in periodization studies makes it dif-\nficult to draw firm conclusions regarding longer-term\nchanges in exercise and/or sports performance of any\nparticular periodization model.\nIn relation to competitive (sports) performance, 5 of\nthe 11 studies included in this review were in swim-\nming. A systematic review on swimming periodiza-\ntion identified that the traditional periodization was\nthe most common form used in well-trained swim-\nmers, but only four studies compared traditional ver-\nsus reverse periodization [5]. Our results suggest\nthat reverse periodization improved swimming per-\nformance [26] more than block periodization, while\nClemente-Suárez and Ramos-Campo [29], reported a\nsimilar improvement in swimming technical ability and\nswimming performance with reverse periodization and\ntraditional periodization. However, neither traditional\n(characterized by pyramidal TID) nor reverse perio-\ndization (characterized by polarized TID) yielded sig-\nnificant improvements in 50-m swimming performance\n[27, 31]. Only two studies [26, 34] reported significant\nimprovements in 100-m swimming performance fol-\nlowing reverse periodization and block periodiz", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ar periodization model.\nIn relation to competitive (sports) performance, 5 of\nthe 11 studies included in this review were in swim-\nming. A systematic review on swimming periodiza-\ntion identified that the traditional periodization was\nthe most common"}
{"chunk_id": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022_p0012_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_1e7a7634c026", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "bce946d9d46810e4", "text_span_hash": "bce946d9d46810e4", "source_file": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "iodization was\nthe most common form used in well-trained swim-\nmers, but only four studies compared traditional ver-\nsus reverse periodization [5]. Our results suggest\nthat reverse periodization improved swimming per-\nformance [26] more than block pe", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nwere predominantly short-term interventions [26, 27,\n29, 31, 41, 43] lasting ~ 10 weeks, and only four stud-\nies had a duration longer than 12 weeks [28, 34–36]\nranging from 12 to 15 weeks. The short duration of\nthe interventions in periodization studies makes it dif-\nficult to draw firm conclusions regarding longer-term\nchanges in exercise and/or sports performance of any\nparticular periodization model.\nIn relation to competitive (sports) performance, 5 of\nthe 11 studies included in this review were in swim-\nming. A systematic review on swimming periodiza-\ntion identified that the traditional periodization was\nthe most common form used in well-trained swim-\nmers, but only four studies compared traditional ver-\nsus reverse periodization [5]. Our results suggest\nthat reverse periodization improved swimming per-\nformance [26] more than block periodization, while\nClemente-Suárez and Ramos-Campo [29], reported a\nsimilar improvement in swimming technical ability and\nswimming performance with reverse periodization and\ntraditional periodization. However, neither traditional\n(characterized by pyramidal TID) nor reverse perio-\ndization (characterized by polarized TID) yielded sig-\nnificant improvements in 50-m swimming performance\n[27, 31]. Only two studies [26, 34] reported significant\nimprovements in 100-m swimming performance fol-\nlowing reverse periodization and block periodiz", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "iodization was\nthe most common form used in well-trained swim-\nmers, but only four studies compared traditional ver-\nsus reverse periodization [5]. Our results suggest\nthat reverse periodization improved swimming per-\nformance [26] more than block pe"}
{"chunk_id": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022_p0012_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_1e7a7634c026", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ffff81ae129da847", "text_span_hash": "ffff81ae129da847", "source_file": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "rmance [26] more than block periodization, while\nClemente-Suárez and Ramos-Campo [29], reported a\nsimilar improvement in swimming technical ability and\nswimming performance with reverse periodization and\ntraditional periodization. However, neither tr", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nwere predominantly short-term interventions [26, 27,\n29, 31, 41, 43] lasting ~ 10 weeks, and only four stud-\nies had a duration longer than 12 weeks [28, 34–36]\nranging from 12 to 15 weeks. The short duration of\nthe interventions in periodization studies makes it dif-\nficult to draw firm conclusions regarding longer-term\nchanges in exercise and/or sports performance of any\nparticular periodization model.\nIn relation to competitive (sports) performance, 5 of\nthe 11 studies included in this review were in swim-\nming. A systematic review on swimming periodiza-\ntion identified that the traditional periodization was\nthe most common form used in well-trained swim-\nmers, but only four studies compared traditional ver-\nsus reverse periodization [5]. Our results suggest\nthat reverse periodization improved swimming per-\nformance [26] more than block periodization, while\nClemente-Suárez and Ramos-Campo [29], reported a\nsimilar improvement in swimming technical ability and\nswimming performance with reverse periodization and\ntraditional periodization. However, neither traditional\n(characterized by pyramidal TID) nor reverse perio-\ndization (characterized by polarized TID) yielded sig-\nnificant improvements in 50-m swimming performance\n[27, 31]. Only two studies [26, 34] reported significant\nimprovements in 100-m swimming performance fol-\nlowing reverse periodization and block periodiz", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "rmance [26] more than block periodization, while\nClemente-Suárez and Ramos-Campo [29], reported a\nsimilar improvement in swimming technical ability and\nswimming performance with reverse periodization and\ntraditional periodization. However, neither tr"}
{"chunk_id": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022_p0012_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_1e7a7634c026", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ca76979dd6aa9958", "text_span_hash": "ca76979dd6aa9958", "source_file": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "dies [26, 34] reported significant\nimprovements in 100-m swimming performance fol-\nlowing reverse periodization and block periodization.\nThe greater improvements for the reverse periodiza-\ntion group (5%) could be explained by the low perfor-\nmance l", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nwere predominantly short-term interventions [26, 27,\n29, 31, 41, 43] lasting ~ 10 weeks, and only four stud-\nies had a duration longer than 12 weeks [28, 34–36]\nranging from 12 to 15 weeks. The short duration of\nthe interventions in periodization studies makes it dif-\nficult to draw firm conclusions regarding longer-term\nchanges in exercise and/or sports performance of any\nparticular periodization model.\nIn relation to competitive (sports) performance, 5 of\nthe 11 studies included in this review were in swim-\nming. A systematic review on swimming periodiza-\ntion identified that the traditional periodization was\nthe most common form used in well-trained swim-\nmers, but only four studies compared traditional ver-\nsus reverse periodization [5]. Our results suggest\nthat reverse periodization improved swimming per-\nformance [26] more than block periodization, while\nClemente-Suárez and Ramos-Campo [29], reported a\nsimilar improvement in swimming technical ability and\nswimming performance with reverse periodization and\ntraditional periodization. However, neither traditional\n(characterized by pyramidal TID) nor reverse perio-\ndization (characterized by polarized TID) yielded sig-\nnificant improvements in 50-m swimming performance\n[27, 31]. Only two studies [26, 34] reported significant\nimprovements in 100-m swimming performance fol-\nlowing reverse periodization and block periodiz", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "dies [26, 34] reported significant\nimprovements in 100-m swimming performance fol-\nlowing reverse periodization and block periodization.\nThe greater improvements for the reverse periodiza-\ntion group (5%) could be explained by the low perfor-\nmance l"}
{"chunk_id": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022_p0012_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_1e7a7634c026", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7a3efd46ae253dd2", "text_span_hash": "7a3efd46ae253dd2", "source_file": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ned by the low perfor-\nmance level of swimmers used in these studies (~ 65 s\nin 100-m), or a greater specificity of stimulus in the first\nweeks of training (high-intensity training). In addition,\nit appears that traditional periodization can improve", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nwere predominantly short-term interventions [26, 27,\n29, 31, 41, 43] lasting ~ 10 weeks, and only four stud-\nies had a duration longer than 12 weeks [28, 34–36]\nranging from 12 to 15 weeks. The short duration of\nthe interventions in periodization studies makes it dif-\nficult to draw firm conclusions regarding longer-term\nchanges in exercise and/or sports performance of any\nparticular periodization model.\nIn relation to competitive (sports) performance, 5 of\nthe 11 studies included in this review were in swim-\nming. A systematic review on swimming periodiza-\ntion identified that the traditional periodization was\nthe most common form used in well-trained swim-\nmers, but only four studies compared traditional ver-\nsus reverse periodization [5]. Our results suggest\nthat reverse periodization improved swimming per-\nformance [26] more than block periodization, while\nClemente-Suárez and Ramos-Campo [29], reported a\nsimilar improvement in swimming technical ability and\nswimming performance with reverse periodization and\ntraditional periodization. However, neither traditional\n(characterized by pyramidal TID) nor reverse perio-\ndization (characterized by polarized TID) yielded sig-\nnificant improvements in 50-m swimming performance\n[27, 31]. Only two studies [26, 34] reported significant\nimprovements in 100-m swimming performance fol-\nlowing reverse periodization and block periodiz", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ned by the low perfor-\nmance level of swimmers used in these studies (~ 65 s\nin 100-m), or a greater specificity of stimulus in the first\nweeks of training (high-intensity training). In addition,\nit appears that traditional periodization can improve"}
{"chunk_id": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022_p0012_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_1e7a7634c026", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "bc9d203b0bbf8426", "text_span_hash": "bc9d203b0bbf8426", "source_file": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "nal periodization can improve\nthe swimming efficiency by ~ 2% most likely related to\nthe higher volume of technical work performed dur-\ning the training program, while reverse periodization\ncan increase the ­VO2max by 6.4% in trained swimmers\n[27]. R", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nwere predominantly short-term interventions [26, 27,\n29, 31, 41, 43] lasting ~ 10 weeks, and only four stud-\nies had a duration longer than 12 weeks [28, 34–36]\nranging from 12 to 15 weeks. The short duration of\nthe interventions in periodization studies makes it dif-\nficult to draw firm conclusions regarding longer-term\nchanges in exercise and/or sports performance of any\nparticular periodization model.\nIn relation to competitive (sports) performance, 5 of\nthe 11 studies included in this review were in swim-\nming. A systematic review on swimming periodiza-\ntion identified that the traditional periodization was\nthe most common form used in well-trained swim-\nmers, but only four studies compared traditional ver-\nsus reverse periodization [5]. Our results suggest\nthat reverse periodization improved swimming per-\nformance [26] more than block periodization, while\nClemente-Suárez and Ramos-Campo [29], reported a\nsimilar improvement in swimming technical ability and\nswimming performance with reverse periodization and\ntraditional periodization. However, neither traditional\n(characterized by pyramidal TID) nor reverse perio-\ndization (characterized by polarized TID) yielded sig-\nnificant improvements in 50-m swimming performance\n[27, 31]. Only two studies [26, 34] reported significant\nimprovements in 100-m swimming performance fol-\nlowing reverse periodization and block periodiz", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "nal periodization can improve\nthe swimming efficiency by ~ 2% most likely related to\nthe higher volume of technical work performed dur-\ning the training program, while reverse periodization\ncan increase the ­VO2max by 6.4% in trained swimmers\n[27]. R"}
{"chunk_id": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022_p0012_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_1e7a7634c026", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b3b271f1b5a706da", "text_span_hash": "b3b271f1b5a706da", "source_file": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "4% in trained swimmers\n[27]. Reverse periodization has been used in combina-\ntion with a polarized TID for improving performance\nin sprint events. On the other hand, both reverse\nperiodization and traditional periodization improved\n2000 m and 5000 m", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nwere predominantly short-term interventions [26, 27,\n29, 31, 41, 43] lasting ~ 10 weeks, and only four stud-\nies had a duration longer than 12 weeks [28, 34–36]\nranging from 12 to 15 weeks. The short duration of\nthe interventions in periodization studies makes it dif-\nficult to draw firm conclusions regarding longer-term\nchanges in exercise and/or sports performance of any\nparticular periodization model.\nIn relation to competitive (sports) performance, 5 of\nthe 11 studies included in this review were in swim-\nming. A systematic review on swimming periodiza-\ntion identified that the traditional periodization was\nthe most common form used in well-trained swim-\nmers, but only four studies compared traditional ver-\nsus reverse periodization [5]. Our results suggest\nthat reverse periodization improved swimming per-\nformance [26] more than block periodization, while\nClemente-Suárez and Ramos-Campo [29], reported a\nsimilar improvement in swimming technical ability and\nswimming performance with reverse periodization and\ntraditional periodization. However, neither traditional\n(characterized by pyramidal TID) nor reverse perio-\ndization (characterized by polarized TID) yielded sig-\nnificant improvements in 50-m swimming performance\n[27, 31]. Only two studies [26, 34] reported significant\nimprovements in 100-m swimming performance fol-\nlowing reverse periodization and block periodiz", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "4% in trained swimmers\n[27]. Reverse periodization has been used in combina-\ntion with a polarized TID for improving performance\nin sprint events. On the other hand, both reverse\nperiodization and traditional periodization improved\n2000 m and 5000 m"}
{"chunk_id": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022_p0012_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_1e7a7634c026", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4586e441a47f3b8d", "text_span_hash": "4586e441a47f3b8d", "source_file": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "on improved\n2000 m and 5000 m running time trials [28, 43], with-\nout a substantial difference between periodization\nmodels, and anaerobic running performance improved\nin reverse periodization compared to block periodiza-\ntion; although the sample wa", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nwere predominantly short-term interventions [26, 27,\n29, 31, 41, 43] lasting ~ 10 weeks, and only four stud-\nies had a duration longer than 12 weeks [28, 34–36]\nranging from 12 to 15 weeks. The short duration of\nthe interventions in periodization studies makes it dif-\nficult to draw firm conclusions regarding longer-term\nchanges in exercise and/or sports performance of any\nparticular periodization model.\nIn relation to competitive (sports) performance, 5 of\nthe 11 studies included in this review were in swim-\nming. A systematic review on swimming periodiza-\ntion identified that the traditional periodization was\nthe most common form used in well-trained swim-\nmers, but only four studies compared traditional ver-\nsus reverse periodization [5]. Our results suggest\nthat reverse periodization improved swimming per-\nformance [26] more than block periodization, while\nClemente-Suárez and Ramos-Campo [29], reported a\nsimilar improvement in swimming technical ability and\nswimming performance with reverse periodization and\ntraditional periodization. However, neither traditional\n(characterized by pyramidal TID) nor reverse perio-\ndization (characterized by polarized TID) yielded sig-\nnificant improvements in 50-m swimming performance\n[27, 31]. Only two studies [26, 34] reported significant\nimprovements in 100-m swimming performance fol-\nlowing reverse periodization and block periodiz", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "on improved\n2000 m and 5000 m running time trials [28, 43], with-\nout a substantial difference between periodization\nmodels, and anaerobic running performance improved\nin reverse periodization compared to block periodiza-\ntion; although the sample wa"}
{"chunk_id": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022_p0012_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_1e7a7634c026", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "bace7105bf708048", "text_span_hash": "bace7105bf708048", "source_file": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "-\ntion; although the sample was recreational runners,\nthe study supports the proposition that both periodi-\nzation models are better than non-planned training\n[35]. However, the study of Clemente-Suárez et al. [43]\ndid not show improvements in 1000 m", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nwere predominantly short-term interventions [26, 27,\n29, 31, 41, 43] lasting ~ 10 weeks, and only four stud-\nies had a duration longer than 12 weeks [28, 34–36]\nranging from 12 to 15 weeks. The short duration of\nthe interventions in periodization studies makes it dif-\nficult to draw firm conclusions regarding longer-term\nchanges in exercise and/or sports performance of any\nparticular periodization model.\nIn relation to competitive (sports) performance, 5 of\nthe 11 studies included in this review were in swim-\nming. A systematic review on swimming periodiza-\ntion identified that the traditional periodization was\nthe most common form used in well-trained swim-\nmers, but only four studies compared traditional ver-\nsus reverse periodization [5]. Our results suggest\nthat reverse periodization improved swimming per-\nformance [26] more than block periodization, while\nClemente-Suárez and Ramos-Campo [29], reported a\nsimilar improvement in swimming technical ability and\nswimming performance with reverse periodization and\ntraditional periodization. However, neither traditional\n(characterized by pyramidal TID) nor reverse perio-\ndization (characterized by polarized TID) yielded sig-\nnificant improvements in 50-m swimming performance\n[27, 31]. Only two studies [26, 34] reported significant\nimprovements in 100-m swimming performance fol-\nlowing reverse periodization and block periodiz", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "-\ntion; although the sample was recreational runners,\nthe study supports the proposition that both periodi-\nzation models are better than non-planned training\n[35]. However, the study of Clemente-Suárez et al. [43]\ndid not show improvements in 1000 m"}
{"chunk_id": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022_p0012_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_1e7a7634c026", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "229f894f755339ed", "text_span_hash": "229f894f755339ed", "source_file": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ot show improvements in 1000 m performance\nregarding the use of traditional or reverse periodiza-\ntion. These results indicate that reverse periodization\ncould be a viable alternative for improving performance\nin short distance events (primarily anae", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nwere predominantly short-term interventions [26, 27,\n29, 31, 41, 43] lasting ~ 10 weeks, and only four stud-\nies had a duration longer than 12 weeks [28, 34–36]\nranging from 12 to 15 weeks. The short duration of\nthe interventions in periodization studies makes it dif-\nficult to draw firm conclusions regarding longer-term\nchanges in exercise and/or sports performance of any\nparticular periodization model.\nIn relation to competitive (sports) performance, 5 of\nthe 11 studies included in this review were in swim-\nming. A systematic review on swimming periodiza-\ntion identified that the traditional periodization was\nthe most common form used in well-trained swim-\nmers, but only four studies compared traditional ver-\nsus reverse periodization [5]. Our results suggest\nthat reverse periodization improved swimming per-\nformance [26] more than block periodization, while\nClemente-Suárez and Ramos-Campo [29], reported a\nsimilar improvement in swimming technical ability and\nswimming performance with reverse periodization and\ntraditional periodization. However, neither traditional\n(characterized by pyramidal TID) nor reverse perio-\ndization (characterized by polarized TID) yielded sig-\nnificant improvements in 50-m swimming performance\n[27, 31]. Only two studies [26, 34] reported significant\nimprovements in 100-m swimming performance fol-\nlowing reverse periodization and block periodiz", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ot show improvements in 1000 m performance\nregarding the use of traditional or reverse periodiza-\ntion. These results indicate that reverse periodization\ncould be a viable alternative for improving performance\nin short distance events (primarily anae"}
{"chunk_id": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022_p0012_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_1e7a7634c026", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "d8df45fb83a32dbd", "text_span_hash": "d8df45fb83a32dbd", "source_file": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "istance events (primarily anaerobic in nature)\nsuch as the 100-m swim event, while traditional perio-\ndization seems to be the best choice for long distance\n(swimming) events, without a clear effect on short\nsprint events such as the 50-m swim or mid", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nwere predominantly short-term interventions [26, 27,\n29, 31, 41, 43] lasting ~ 10 weeks, and only four stud-\nies had a duration longer than 12 weeks [28, 34–36]\nranging from 12 to 15 weeks. The short duration of\nthe interventions in periodization studies makes it dif-\nficult to draw firm conclusions regarding longer-term\nchanges in exercise and/or sports performance of any\nparticular periodization model.\nIn relation to competitive (sports) performance, 5 of\nthe 11 studies included in this review were in swim-\nming. A systematic review on swimming periodiza-\ntion identified that the traditional periodization was\nthe most common form used in well-trained swim-\nmers, but only four studies compared traditional ver-\nsus reverse periodization [5]. Our results suggest\nthat reverse periodization improved swimming per-\nformance [26] more than block periodization, while\nClemente-Suárez and Ramos-Campo [29], reported a\nsimilar improvement in swimming technical ability and\nswimming performance with reverse periodization and\ntraditional periodization. However, neither traditional\n(characterized by pyramidal TID) nor reverse perio-\ndization (characterized by polarized TID) yielded sig-\nnificant improvements in 50-m swimming performance\n[27, 31]. Only two studies [26, 34] reported significant\nimprovements in 100-m swimming performance fol-\nlowing reverse periodization and block periodiz", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "istance events (primarily anaerobic in nature)\nsuch as the 100-m swim event, while traditional perio-\ndization seems to be the best choice for long distance\n(swimming) events, without a clear effect on short\nsprint events such as the 50-m swim or mid"}
{"chunk_id": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022_p0012_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_1e7a7634c026", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1a3f91134e4bfbdc", "text_span_hash": "1a3f91134e4bfbdc", "source_file": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "s such as the 50-m swim or middle and\nlong-distance running events. The lack of effects on\nswim performance could relate to training a variety of\nfitness adaptations rather than emphasizing the pri-\nmary fitness characteristic [11].\nTo our knowledge,", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nwere predominantly short-term interventions [26, 27,\n29, 31, 41, 43] lasting ~ 10 weeks, and only four stud-\nies had a duration longer than 12 weeks [28, 34–36]\nranging from 12 to 15 weeks. The short duration of\nthe interventions in periodization studies makes it dif-\nficult to draw firm conclusions regarding longer-term\nchanges in exercise and/or sports performance of any\nparticular periodization model.\nIn relation to competitive (sports) performance, 5 of\nthe 11 studies included in this review were in swim-\nming. A systematic review on swimming periodiza-\ntion identified that the traditional periodization was\nthe most common form used in well-trained swim-\nmers, but only four studies compared traditional ver-\nsus reverse periodization [5]. Our results suggest\nthat reverse periodization improved swimming per-\nformance [26] more than block periodization, while\nClemente-Suárez and Ramos-Campo [29], reported a\nsimilar improvement in swimming technical ability and\nswimming performance with reverse periodization and\ntraditional periodization. However, neither traditional\n(characterized by pyramidal TID) nor reverse perio-\ndization (characterized by polarized TID) yielded sig-\nnificant improvements in 50-m swimming performance\n[27, 31]. Only two studies [26, 34] reported significant\nimprovements in 100-m swimming performance fol-\nlowing reverse periodization and block periodiz", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "s such as the 50-m swim or middle and\nlong-distance running events. The lack of effects on\nswim performance could relate to training a variety of\nfitness adaptations rather than emphasizing the pri-\nmary fitness characteristic [11].\nTo our knowledge,"}
{"chunk_id": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022_p0012_c0016", "source_registry_id": "src_1e7a7634c026", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "765c510dd69b8d15", "text_span_hash": "765c510dd69b8d15", "source_file": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 16, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ristic [11].\nTo our knowledge, only two studies have reported\ngreater gains in 1RM strength in traditional periodiza-\ntion/programming as opposed to reverse periodization/\nprogramming [36, 42]. Regarding the effects of periodi-\nzation on muscular str", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nwere predominantly short-term interventions [26, 27,\n29, 31, 41, 43] lasting ~ 10 weeks, and only four stud-\nies had a duration longer than 12 weeks [28, 34–36]\nranging from 12 to 15 weeks. The short duration of\nthe interventions in periodization studies makes it dif-\nficult to draw firm conclusions regarding longer-term\nchanges in exercise and/or sports performance of any\nparticular periodization model.\nIn relation to competitive (sports) performance, 5 of\nthe 11 studies included in this review were in swim-\nming. A systematic review on swimming periodiza-\ntion identified that the traditional periodization was\nthe most common form used in well-trained swim-\nmers, but only four studies compared traditional ver-\nsus reverse periodization [5]. Our results suggest\nthat reverse periodization improved swimming per-\nformance [26] more than block periodization, while\nClemente-Suárez and Ramos-Campo [29], reported a\nsimilar improvement in swimming technical ability and\nswimming performance with reverse periodization and\ntraditional periodization. However, neither traditional\n(characterized by pyramidal TID) nor reverse perio-\ndization (characterized by polarized TID) yielded sig-\nnificant improvements in 50-m swimming performance\n[27, 31]. Only two studies [26, 34] reported significant\nimprovements in 100-m swimming performance fol-\nlowing reverse periodization and block periodiz", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ristic [11].\nTo our knowledge, only two studies have reported\ngreater gains in 1RM strength in traditional periodiza-\ntion/programming as opposed to reverse periodization/\nprogramming [36, 42]. Regarding the effects of periodi-\nzation on muscular str"}
{"chunk_id": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022_p0012_c0017", "source_registry_id": "src_1e7a7634c026", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "add889dd5d63a833", "text_span_hash": "add889dd5d63a833", "source_file": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 17, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "eriodi-\nzation on muscular strength, Prestes et al. [36], reported\nincreases in muscular strength for both forms of perio-\ndization (traditional periodization vs. reverse periodiza-\ntion) in bench press (17% and 16%), lat pull-down (30%\nand 22%), arm", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nwere predominantly short-term interventions [26, 27,\n29, 31, 41, 43] lasting ~ 10 weeks, and only four stud-\nies had a duration longer than 12 weeks [28, 34–36]\nranging from 12 to 15 weeks. The short duration of\nthe interventions in periodization studies makes it dif-\nficult to draw firm conclusions regarding longer-term\nchanges in exercise and/or sports performance of any\nparticular periodization model.\nIn relation to competitive (sports) performance, 5 of\nthe 11 studies included in this review were in swim-\nming. A systematic review on swimming periodiza-\ntion identified that the traditional periodization was\nthe most common form used in well-trained swim-\nmers, but only four studies compared traditional ver-\nsus reverse periodization [5]. Our results suggest\nthat reverse periodization improved swimming per-\nformance [26] more than block periodization, while\nClemente-Suárez and Ramos-Campo [29], reported a\nsimilar improvement in swimming technical ability and\nswimming performance with reverse periodization and\ntraditional periodization. However, neither traditional\n(characterized by pyramidal TID) nor reverse perio-\ndization (characterized by polarized TID) yielded sig-\nnificant improvements in 50-m swimming performance\n[27, 31]. Only two studies [26, 34] reported significant\nimprovements in 100-m swimming performance fol-\nlowing reverse periodization and block periodiz", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "eriodi-\nzation on muscular strength, Prestes et al. [36], reported\nincreases in muscular strength for both forms of perio-\ndization (traditional periodization vs. reverse periodiza-\ntion) in bench press (17% and 16%), lat pull-down (30%\nand 22%), arm"}
{"chunk_id": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022_p0012_c0018", "source_registry_id": "src_1e7a7634c026", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2cca987a3ed359e5", "text_span_hash": "2cca987a3ed359e5", "source_file": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 18, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "t pull-down (30%\nand 22%), arm curl (20% and 16%) and leg extension (37%\nand 32%). However, Prestes et al. [36], asserted that tra-\nditional periodization rather than reverse periodization\nis more effective for strength and hypertrophy. There is a\npo", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nwere predominantly short-term interventions [26, 27,\n29, 31, 41, 43] lasting ~ 10 weeks, and only four stud-\nies had a duration longer than 12 weeks [28, 34–36]\nranging from 12 to 15 weeks. The short duration of\nthe interventions in periodization studies makes it dif-\nficult to draw firm conclusions regarding longer-term\nchanges in exercise and/or sports performance of any\nparticular periodization model.\nIn relation to competitive (sports) performance, 5 of\nthe 11 studies included in this review were in swim-\nming. A systematic review on swimming periodiza-\ntion identified that the traditional periodization was\nthe most common form used in well-trained swim-\nmers, but only four studies compared traditional ver-\nsus reverse periodization [5]. Our results suggest\nthat reverse periodization improved swimming per-\nformance [26] more than block periodization, while\nClemente-Suárez and Ramos-Campo [29], reported a\nsimilar improvement in swimming technical ability and\nswimming performance with reverse periodization and\ntraditional periodization. However, neither traditional\n(characterized by pyramidal TID) nor reverse perio-\ndization (characterized by polarized TID) yielded sig-\nnificant improvements in 50-m swimming performance\n[27, 31]. Only two studies [26, 34] reported significant\nimprovements in 100-m swimming performance fol-\nlowing reverse periodization and block periodiz", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "t pull-down (30%\nand 22%), arm curl (20% and 16%) and leg extension (37%\nand 32%). However, Prestes et al. [36], asserted that tra-\nditional periodization rather than reverse periodization\nis more effective for strength and hypertrophy. There is a\npo"}
{"chunk_id": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022_p0012_c0019", "source_registry_id": "src_1e7a7634c026", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0b5744fc0fe73a9d", "text_span_hash": "0b5744fc0fe73a9d", "source_file": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 19, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "and hypertrophy. There is a\npossibility for traditional periodization to be more effec-\ntive as it allows for more quality training with heavier\nweights at the end of the program [36]. A similar com-\nparison also showed a greater increase in strength", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nwere predominantly short-term interventions [26, 27,\n29, 31, 41, 43] lasting ~ 10 weeks, and only four stud-\nies had a duration longer than 12 weeks [28, 34–36]\nranging from 12 to 15 weeks. The short duration of\nthe interventions in periodization studies makes it dif-\nficult to draw firm conclusions regarding longer-term\nchanges in exercise and/or sports performance of any\nparticular periodization model.\nIn relation to competitive (sports) performance, 5 of\nthe 11 studies included in this review were in swim-\nming. A systematic review on swimming periodiza-\ntion identified that the traditional periodization was\nthe most common form used in well-trained swim-\nmers, but only four studies compared traditional ver-\nsus reverse periodization [5]. Our results suggest\nthat reverse periodization improved swimming per-\nformance [26] more than block periodization, while\nClemente-Suárez and Ramos-Campo [29], reported a\nsimilar improvement in swimming technical ability and\nswimming performance with reverse periodization and\ntraditional periodization. However, neither traditional\n(characterized by pyramidal TID) nor reverse perio-\ndization (characterized by polarized TID) yielded sig-\nnificant improvements in 50-m swimming performance\n[27, 31]. Only two studies [26, 34] reported significant\nimprovements in 100-m swimming performance fol-\nlowing reverse periodization and block periodiz", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "and hypertrophy. There is a\npossibility for traditional periodization to be more effec-\ntive as it allows for more quality training with heavier\nweights at the end of the program [36]. A similar com-\nparison also showed a greater increase in strength"}
{"chunk_id": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022_p0012_c0020", "source_registry_id": "src_1e7a7634c026", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e3931ae5b108408f", "text_span_hash": "e3931ae5b108408f", "source_file": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 20, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "a greater increase in strength after\ntraditional periodization in the study of Rhea et al. [42].\nHowever, both reverse periodization (16%) and tradi-\ntional periodization (15%) showed a similar increase in\nmuscular endurance [42]. Analysis of the eff", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nwere predominantly short-term interventions [26, 27,\n29, 31, 41, 43] lasting ~ 10 weeks, and only four stud-\nies had a duration longer than 12 weeks [28, 34–36]\nranging from 12 to 15 weeks. The short duration of\nthe interventions in periodization studies makes it dif-\nficult to draw firm conclusions regarding longer-term\nchanges in exercise and/or sports performance of any\nparticular periodization model.\nIn relation to competitive (sports) performance, 5 of\nthe 11 studies included in this review were in swim-\nming. A systematic review on swimming periodiza-\ntion identified that the traditional periodization was\nthe most common form used in well-trained swim-\nmers, but only four studies compared traditional ver-\nsus reverse periodization [5]. Our results suggest\nthat reverse periodization improved swimming per-\nformance [26] more than block periodization, while\nClemente-Suárez and Ramos-Campo [29], reported a\nsimilar improvement in swimming technical ability and\nswimming performance with reverse periodization and\ntraditional periodization. However, neither traditional\n(characterized by pyramidal TID) nor reverse perio-\ndization (characterized by polarized TID) yielded sig-\nnificant improvements in 50-m swimming performance\n[27, 31]. Only two studies [26, 34] reported significant\nimprovements in 100-m swimming performance fol-\nlowing reverse periodization and block periodiz", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "a greater increase in strength after\ntraditional periodization in the study of Rhea et al. [42].\nHowever, both reverse periodization (16%) and tradi-\ntional periodization (15%) showed a similar increase in\nmuscular endurance [42]. Analysis of the eff"}
{"chunk_id": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022_p0012_c0021", "source_registry_id": "src_1e7a7634c026", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8903c415dccc124d", "text_span_hash": "8903c415dccc124d", "source_file": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 21, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ance [42]. Analysis of the effect size (ES)\nindicates that traditional periodization was more effec-\ntive at eliciting strength than reverse periodization [42]\n(ES = − 0.31). Both studies matched the intensity and\nvolume of training, with the only di", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nwere predominantly short-term interventions [26, 27,\n29, 31, 41, 43] lasting ~ 10 weeks, and only four stud-\nies had a duration longer than 12 weeks [28, 34–36]\nranging from 12 to 15 weeks. The short duration of\nthe interventions in periodization studies makes it dif-\nficult to draw firm conclusions regarding longer-term\nchanges in exercise and/or sports performance of any\nparticular periodization model.\nIn relation to competitive (sports) performance, 5 of\nthe 11 studies included in this review were in swim-\nming. A systematic review on swimming periodiza-\ntion identified that the traditional periodization was\nthe most common form used in well-trained swim-\nmers, but only four studies compared traditional ver-\nsus reverse periodization [5]. Our results suggest\nthat reverse periodization improved swimming per-\nformance [26] more than block periodization, while\nClemente-Suárez and Ramos-Campo [29], reported a\nsimilar improvement in swimming technical ability and\nswimming performance with reverse periodization and\ntraditional periodization. However, neither traditional\n(characterized by pyramidal TID) nor reverse perio-\ndization (characterized by polarized TID) yielded sig-\nnificant improvements in 50-m swimming performance\n[27, 31]. Only two studies [26, 34] reported significant\nimprovements in 100-m swimming performance fol-\nlowing reverse periodization and block periodiz", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ance [42]. Analysis of the effect size (ES)\nindicates that traditional periodization was more effec-\ntive at eliciting strength than reverse periodization [42]\n(ES = − 0.31). Both studies matched the intensity and\nvolume of training, with the only di"}
{"chunk_id": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022_p0012_c0022", "source_registry_id": "src_1e7a7634c026", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "143235582ddf4358", "text_span_hash": "143235582ddf4358", "source_file": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 22, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "of training, with the only difference being the dis-\ntribution of training over the weeks. The similar increase\nin muscular strength for both periodization approaches\nlikely relates to the training stimulus involving matched\nloads, and a similar pat", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nwere predominantly short-term interventions [26, 27,\n29, 31, 41, 43] lasting ~ 10 weeks, and only four stud-\nies had a duration longer than 12 weeks [28, 34–36]\nranging from 12 to 15 weeks. The short duration of\nthe interventions in periodization studies makes it dif-\nficult to draw firm conclusions regarding longer-term\nchanges in exercise and/or sports performance of any\nparticular periodization model.\nIn relation to competitive (sports) performance, 5 of\nthe 11 studies included in this review were in swim-\nming. A systematic review on swimming periodiza-\ntion identified that the traditional periodization was\nthe most common form used in well-trained swim-\nmers, but only four studies compared traditional ver-\nsus reverse periodization [5]. Our results suggest\nthat reverse periodization improved swimming per-\nformance [26] more than block periodization, while\nClemente-Suárez and Ramos-Campo [29], reported a\nsimilar improvement in swimming technical ability and\nswimming performance with reverse periodization and\ntraditional periodization. However, neither traditional\n(characterized by pyramidal TID) nor reverse perio-\ndization (characterized by polarized TID) yielded sig-\nnificant improvements in 50-m swimming performance\n[27, 31]. Only two studies [26, 34] reported significant\nimprovements in 100-m swimming performance fol-\nlowing reverse periodization and block periodiz", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "of training, with the only difference being the dis-\ntribution of training over the weeks. The similar increase\nin muscular strength for both periodization approaches\nlikely relates to the training stimulus involving matched\nloads, and a similar pat"}
{"chunk_id": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022_p0012_c0023", "source_registry_id": "src_1e7a7634c026", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b149b1239f374f4f", "text_span_hash": "b149b1239f374f4f", "source_file": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 23, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "tched\nloads, and a similar pattern of the functional responses\nto training stress. With respect to improvements in mus-\ncular endurance, reverse periodization was characterized\nby decreased intensity and increased volume toward the\nlast few weeks of", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nwere predominantly short-term interventions [26, 27,\n29, 31, 41, 43] lasting ~ 10 weeks, and only four stud-\nies had a duration longer than 12 weeks [28, 34–36]\nranging from 12 to 15 weeks. The short duration of\nthe interventions in periodization studies makes it dif-\nficult to draw firm conclusions regarding longer-term\nchanges in exercise and/or sports performance of any\nparticular periodization model.\nIn relation to competitive (sports) performance, 5 of\nthe 11 studies included in this review were in swim-\nming. A systematic review on swimming periodiza-\ntion identified that the traditional periodization was\nthe most common form used in well-trained swim-\nmers, but only four studies compared traditional ver-\nsus reverse periodization [5]. Our results suggest\nthat reverse periodization improved swimming per-\nformance [26] more than block periodization, while\nClemente-Suárez and Ramos-Campo [29], reported a\nsimilar improvement in swimming technical ability and\nswimming performance with reverse periodization and\ntraditional periodization. However, neither traditional\n(characterized by pyramidal TID) nor reverse perio-\ndization (characterized by polarized TID) yielded sig-\nnificant improvements in 50-m swimming performance\n[27, 31]. Only two studies [26, 34] reported significant\nimprovements in 100-m swimming performance fol-\nlowing reverse periodization and block periodiz", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "tched\nloads, and a similar pattern of the functional responses\nto training stress. With respect to improvements in mus-\ncular endurance, reverse periodization was characterized\nby decreased intensity and increased volume toward the\nlast few weeks of"}
{"chunk_id": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022_p0012_c0024", "source_registry_id": "src_1e7a7634c026", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "dbaf17a7a82ff8a4", "text_span_hash": "dbaf17a7a82ff8a4", "source_file": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 24, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "toward the\nlast few weeks of training in these studies, which is more\nlike a strength-endurance training stimulus. It seems rea-\nsonable to improve the muscular endurance with train-\ning more specific to this strength attribute before the\npost-test", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nwere predominantly short-term interventions [26, 27,\n29, 31, 41, 43] lasting ~ 10 weeks, and only four stud-\nies had a duration longer than 12 weeks [28, 34–36]\nranging from 12 to 15 weeks. The short duration of\nthe interventions in periodization studies makes it dif-\nficult to draw firm conclusions regarding longer-term\nchanges in exercise and/or sports performance of any\nparticular periodization model.\nIn relation to competitive (sports) performance, 5 of\nthe 11 studies included in this review were in swim-\nming. A systematic review on swimming periodiza-\ntion identified that the traditional periodization was\nthe most common form used in well-trained swim-\nmers, but only four studies compared traditional ver-\nsus reverse periodization [5]. Our results suggest\nthat reverse periodization improved swimming per-\nformance [26] more than block periodization, while\nClemente-Suárez and Ramos-Campo [29], reported a\nsimilar improvement in swimming technical ability and\nswimming performance with reverse periodization and\ntraditional periodization. However, neither traditional\n(characterized by pyramidal TID) nor reverse perio-\ndization (characterized by polarized TID) yielded sig-\nnificant improvements in 50-m swimming performance\n[27, 31]. Only two studies [26, 34] reported significant\nimprovements in 100-m swimming performance fol-\nlowing reverse periodization and block periodiz", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "toward the\nlast few weeks of training in these studies, which is more\nlike a strength-endurance training stimulus. It seems rea-\nsonable to improve the muscular endurance with train-\ning more specific to this strength attribute before the\npost-test"}
