re was\nsubstantial inter-individual variability regarding the\nparticipants in the different studies (which included\nteenage swimmers, local/regional swimmers, experi-\nenced runners, etc.) across all performance variables\nthat may have impaired the ability to establish conclu-\nsive outcomes in this systematic", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "g 10 weeks of training in one study\nwas more than 337,000 m, while for the reverse perio-\ndization the volume was only ~ 160,000 m [27, 31]. The\nabsence of a control group did not reflect the improve-\nments in periodized models vs. control group. Mor"}
{"chunk_id": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022_p0013_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_1e7a7634c026", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e3e6ccda169f6554", "text_span_hash": "e3e6ccda169f6554", "source_file": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "acteristics associated\nwith the different models of periodization, and which\nevents and sports might benefit substantially from\nreverse periodization training.\nIt is not clear if reverse periodization is more effective\nin improving sports performance", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nprograms for at least 12 months, but without specifying\nthe underlying training and periodization model.\nIn addition to effects on performance and physi-\nological parameters, different types of periodization\nmay have variable effects on body composition. Arroyo\nToledo et al. [26] reported that block periodization can\nelicit more favorable improvements in body composi-\ntion than reverse periodization in moderately trained\nfemale swimmers. The primary premise of block peri-\nodization is employing highly concentrated training\nworkload phases (periodization blocks) to stimulate\nadaptation and residual effects [26]. The blocks must be\nsequenced in a logical order to benefit from the resid-\nual effects [26]. Reductions in fat mass can be achieved\nduring a period of high-intensity training [46], and\nincluding a specific phase of training for this purpose\nmaybe useful in sports where body composition is\nimportant for performance.\nThere were some limitations to this review given the\nheterogeneity of sports, training and methodologi-\ncal approaches of the underlying studies. There was\nsubstantial inter-individual variability regarding the\nparticipants in the different studies (which included\nteenage swimmers, local/regional swimmers, experi-\nenced runners, etc.) across all performance variables\nthat may have impaired the ability to establish conclu-\nsive outcomes in this systematic", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "acteristics associated\nwith the different models of periodization, and which\nevents and sports might benefit substantially from\nreverse periodization training.\nIt is not clear if reverse periodization is more effective\nin improving sports performance"}
{"chunk_id": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022_p0013_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_1e7a7634c026", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1355366d9c0f372f", "text_span_hash": "1355366d9c0f372f", "source_file": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "n improving sports performance than other periodized\nmodels. Use of reverse periodization likely induces simi-\nlar improvements to a traditional model in shorter events\nsuch as the 100-m swimming event. Comparative stud-\nies of periodization models i", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nprograms for at least 12 months, but without specifying\nthe underlying training and periodization model.\nIn addition to effects on performance and physi-\nological parameters, different types of periodization\nmay have variable effects on body composition. Arroyo\nToledo et al. [26] reported that block periodization can\nelicit more favorable improvements in body composi-\ntion than reverse periodization in moderately trained\nfemale swimmers. The primary premise of block peri-\nodization is employing highly concentrated training\nworkload phases (periodization blocks) to stimulate\nadaptation and residual effects [26]. The blocks must be\nsequenced in a logical order to benefit from the resid-\nual effects [26]. Reductions in fat mass can be achieved\nduring a period of high-intensity training [46], and\nincluding a specific phase of training for this purpose\nmaybe useful in sports where body composition is\nimportant for performance.\nThere were some limitations to this review given the\nheterogeneity of sports, training and methodologi-\ncal approaches of the underlying studies. There was\nsubstantial inter-individual variability regarding the\nparticipants in the different studies (which included\nteenage swimmers, local/regional swimmers, experi-\nenced runners, etc.) across all performance variables\nthat may have impaired the ability to establish conclu-\nsive outcomes in this systematic", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "n improving sports performance than other periodized\nmodels. Use of reverse periodization likely induces simi-\nlar improvements to a traditional model in shorter events\nsuch as the 100-m swimming event. Comparative stud-\nies of periodization models i"}
{"chunk_id": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022_p0013_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_1e7a7634c026", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0c4046baac5fd465", "text_span_hash": "0c4046baac5fd465", "source_file": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ies of periodization models in endurance sports require\ncareful planning of experimental design, longer study\nperiods, and where appropriate careful matching of train-\ning volumes and intensities.\n[La-]b: Blood lactate; TRIMP: Training impulse; TID:", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nprograms for at least 12 months, but without specifying\nthe underlying training and periodization model.\nIn addition to effects on performance and physi-\nological parameters, different types of periodization\nmay have variable effects on body composition. Arroyo\nToledo et al. [26] reported that block periodization can\nelicit more favorable improvements in body composi-\ntion than reverse periodization in moderately trained\nfemale swimmers. The primary premise of block peri-\nodization is employing highly concentrated training\nworkload phases (periodization blocks) to stimulate\nadaptation and residual effects [26]. The blocks must be\nsequenced in a logical order to benefit from the resid-\nual effects [26]. Reductions in fat mass can be achieved\nduring a period of high-intensity training [46], and\nincluding a specific phase of training for this purpose\nmaybe useful in sports where body composition is\nimportant for performance.\nThere were some limitations to this review given the\nheterogeneity of sports, training and methodologi-\ncal approaches of the underlying studies. There was\nsubstantial inter-individual variability regarding the\nparticipants in the different studies (which included\nteenage swimmers, local/regional swimmers, experi-\nenced runners, etc.) across all performance variables\nthat may have impaired the ability to establish conclu-\nsive outcomes in this systematic", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ies of periodization models in endurance sports require\ncareful planning of experimental design, longer study\nperiods, and where appropriate careful matching of train-\ning volumes and intensities.\n[La-]b: Blood lactate; TRIMP: Training impulse; TID:"}
{"chunk_id": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022_p0013_c0016", "source_registry_id": "src_1e7a7634c026", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "562976ab57d6253a", "text_span_hash": "562976ab57d6253a", "source_file": "5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\5_reverse_periodization_review_2022.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 16, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "TRIMP: Training impulse; TID: Training intensity distribu‑\ntion; RP: Reverse periodization; RM: Repetition maximum; V̇O2max: Maximal\noxygen uptake; V̇O2peak: Peak oxygen uptake; ES: Effect size.\nAuthor contributions\nJMG-R, FG-M and VR-C conceptualiz", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "González‑Ravé et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2022) 8:56\nprograms for at least 12 months, but without specifying\nthe underlying training and periodization model.\nIn addition to effects on performance and physi-\nological parameters, different types of periodization\nmay have variable effects on body composition. Arroyo\nToledo et al. [26] reported that block periodization can\nelicit more favorable improvements in body composi-\ntion than reverse periodization in moderately trained\nfemale swimmers. The primary premise of block peri-\nodization is employing highly concentrated training\nworkload phases (periodization blocks) to stimulate\nadaptation and residual effects [26]. The blocks must be\nsequenced in a logical order to benefit from the resid-\nual effects [26]. Reductions in fat mass can be achieved\nduring a period of high-intensity training [46], and\nincluding a specific phase of training for this purpose\nmaybe useful in sports where body composition is\nimportant for performance.\nThere were some limitations to this review given the\nheterogeneity of sports, training and methodologi-\ncal approaches of the underlying studies. There was\nsubstantial inter-individual variability regarding the\nparticipants in the different studies (which included\nteenage swimmers, local/regional swimmers, experi-\nenced runners, etc.) across all performance variables\nthat may have impaired the ability to establish conclu-\nsive outcomes in this systematic", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "TRIMP: Training impulse; TID: Training intensity distribu‑\ntion; RP: Reverse periodization; RM: Repetition maximum; V̇O2max: Maximal\noxygen uptake; V̇O2peak: Peak oxygen uptake; ES: Effect size.\nAuthor contributions\nJMG-R, FG-M and VR-C conceptualiz"}
