{"chunk_id": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022_p0006_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_77e9bb13a037", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "36369c675b34473d", "text_span_hash": "36369c675b34473d", "source_file": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "tudies focusing on functional and\nacute analgesic effects [36]. Evidence suggests that abnormal structure (i.e., hypoechoic\nareas and irregular structure) may normalize in some individuals following a 12‐week\neccentric exercise protocol [125,127], th", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "3.2. Pathologic Tissue Remodeling\nThe pathogenesis of tendinopathy appears multifaceted, which has given rise to var‐\nious pathophysiological theories [36]. Current rhetoric suggests that initial cyclic over‐\nloading of the tendon leads to degeneration and disorganization of healthy collagen,\nwhich triggers an acute inflammatory response [36,87,101]. If the cyclic overloading is\ncontinued without intervention, the tendon pathology worsens through a positive feed‐\nback loop of injury to both the original and poor‐quality repair tissue, inflammation, and\nfailed repair. Macroscopically, evidence suggests that AT increases tendon CSA [116–118]\nand longitudinal strain [116–118], and decreases modulus [116,119], transverse strain\n[120], longitudinal stiffness [116,118,119], and transverse stiffness [121] in vivo. Taken to‐\ngether, these changes lead to functional deficits across the strength spectrum potentially\nincreasing risk of AT recurrence [122–124].\nTherapeutic exercise remains one of if not the most effective non‐surgical approach\nfor managing AT [1,2]. The suggested mechanism of action is generally considered to be\nrestoration of tendon material, mechanical, and morphological properties similarly to\nhealthy tendon remodeling [36,37,41], thereby improving functional strength [33]. Macro‐\nscopically, evidence suggests that targeted mechanical loading decreases tendon thick‐\nness [125] and volume [126]; however, there is a pauc", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "tudies focusing on functional and\nacute analgesic effects [36]. Evidence suggests that abnormal structure (i.e., hypoechoic\nareas and irregular structure) may normalize in some individuals following a 12‐week\neccentric exercise protocol [125,127], th"}
{"chunk_id": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022_p0006_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_77e9bb13a037", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3ff236f99a6b0c2c", "text_span_hash": "3ff236f99a6b0c2c", "source_file": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "at training should focus on increasing stiffness\nof the triceps surae MTU, increasing strength, and shifting the length‐tension curve of the\ntriceps surae muscles through sarcomerogenesis. Although still an area of exploration, it\nappears that therap", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "3.2. Pathologic Tissue Remodeling\nThe pathogenesis of tendinopathy appears multifaceted, which has given rise to var‐\nious pathophysiological theories [36]. Current rhetoric suggests that initial cyclic over‐\nloading of the tendon leads to degeneration and disorganization of healthy collagen,\nwhich triggers an acute inflammatory response [36,87,101]. If the cyclic overloading is\ncontinued without intervention, the tendon pathology worsens through a positive feed‐\nback loop of injury to both the original and poor‐quality repair tissue, inflammation, and\nfailed repair. Macroscopically, evidence suggests that AT increases tendon CSA [116–118]\nand longitudinal strain [116–118], and decreases modulus [116,119], transverse strain\n[120], longitudinal stiffness [116,118,119], and transverse stiffness [121] in vivo. Taken to‐\ngether, these changes lead to functional deficits across the strength spectrum potentially\nincreasing risk of AT recurrence [122–124].\nTherapeutic exercise remains one of if not the most effective non‐surgical approach\nfor managing AT [1,2]. The suggested mechanism of action is generally considered to be\nrestoration of tendon material, mechanical, and morphological properties similarly to\nhealthy tendon remodeling [36,37,41], thereby improving functional strength [33]. Macro‐\nscopically, evidence suggests that targeted mechanical loading decreases tendon thick‐\nness [125] and volume [126]; however, there is a pauc", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "at training should focus on increasing stiffness\nof the triceps surae MTU, increasing strength, and shifting the length‐tension curve of the\ntriceps surae muscles through sarcomerogenesis. Although still an area of exploration, it\nappears that therap"}
{"chunk_id": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022_p0006_c0018", "source_registry_id": "src_77e9bb13a037", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9b6210686d3e2357", "text_span_hash": "9b6210686d3e2357", "source_file": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 18, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "contrac‐\ntion) or shortens (i.e., concentric contraction). Seminal therapeutic exercise protocols for\ntendinopathy have popularized the use of eccentric training [134,135], although several\nmixed exercise protocols (i.e., those incorporating both the", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "3.2. Pathologic Tissue Remodeling\nThe pathogenesis of tendinopathy appears multifaceted, which has given rise to var‐\nious pathophysiological theories [36]. Current rhetoric suggests that initial cyclic over‐\nloading of the tendon leads to degeneration and disorganization of healthy collagen,\nwhich triggers an acute inflammatory response [36,87,101]. If the cyclic overloading is\ncontinued without intervention, the tendon pathology worsens through a positive feed‐\nback loop of injury to both the original and poor‐quality repair tissue, inflammation, and\nfailed repair. Macroscopically, evidence suggests that AT increases tendon CSA [116–118]\nand longitudinal strain [116–118], and decreases modulus [116,119], transverse strain\n[120], longitudinal stiffness [116,118,119], and transverse stiffness [121] in vivo. Taken to‐\ngether, these changes lead to functional deficits across the strength spectrum potentially\nincreasing risk of AT recurrence [122–124].\nTherapeutic exercise remains one of if not the most effective non‐surgical approach\nfor managing AT [1,2]. The suggested mechanism of action is generally considered to be\nrestoration of tendon material, mechanical, and morphological properties similarly to\nhealthy tendon remodeling [36,37,41], thereby improving functional strength [33]. Macro‐\nscopically, evidence suggests that targeted mechanical loading decreases tendon thick‐\nness [125] and volume [126]; however, there is a pauc", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "contrac‐\ntion) or shortens (i.e., concentric contraction). Seminal therapeutic exercise protocols for\ntendinopathy have popularized the use of eccentric training [134,135], although several\nmixed exercise protocols (i.e., those incorporating both the"}
{"chunk_id": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022_p0007_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_77e9bb13a037", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3b5314e886f7cfb4", "text_span_hash": "3b5314e886f7cfb4", "source_file": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ative studies are needed to confirm this asser‐\nLoad intensity describes the magnitude of the training stimulus, or put differently, the\namount of resistance applied. In healthy persons, load intensities of greater than 70% of\nMVC significantly induc", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "exercise protocols may be a viable option for treating tendinopathies [138], though con‐\nflicting evidence exists specifically for their use in managing AT [139,140]. With the mech‐\nanism of therapeutic action of mechanical loading on AT still unresolved [36–39] and\ngiven a lack of evidence favoring one contraction type over another [141–143], it does not\nseem justified to exclusively prescribe eccentric exercises for AT.\nAs opposed to universally prescribing eccentric exercises for tendinopathy, Millar et\nal. [36] suggest that clinicians should instead focus on conveying the principles of tendon\nloading and individually tailor the program to promote patient engagement with and con‐\nsequently the success of the program. Importantly, individuals with AT have highlighted\nthe burden of therapeutic exercise, particularly those prescriptions incorporating daily or\ntwice daily exercises [144]. With the biopsychosocial impact being a core tenet of AT [144–\n146], and coupled with patient perceptions that passive treatments (e.g., massage, dry\nneedling, ultrasound) are more efficacious than therapeutic exercises for managing AT\n[145], specifying exercises based on muscle contraction type may be less important than\npatient education about mechanical loading and the prescription of exercises according to\nwhat the patient is most likely to perform [36]. Adherence rates and patient satisfaction\nwith mixed protocols appear to be at least as", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ative studies are needed to confirm this asser‐\nLoad intensity describes the magnitude of the training stimulus, or put differently, the\namount of resistance applied. In healthy persons, load intensities of greater than 70% of\nMVC significantly induc"}
{"chunk_id": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022_p0007_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_77e9bb13a037", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2f568bbed6786b67", "text_span_hash": "2f568bbed6786b67", "source_file": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "rtici‐\npant tolerability when exercising consistently at 90% of MVC [103]. A recent systematic\nreview and meta‐analysis by Lazarczuk et al. [32] found that resistance training protocols\ncompleted by healthy individuals at high‐intensities (greater th", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "exercise protocols may be a viable option for treating tendinopathies [138], though con‐\nflicting evidence exists specifically for their use in managing AT [139,140]. With the mech‐\nanism of therapeutic action of mechanical loading on AT still unresolved [36–39] and\ngiven a lack of evidence favoring one contraction type over another [141–143], it does not\nseem justified to exclusively prescribe eccentric exercises for AT.\nAs opposed to universally prescribing eccentric exercises for tendinopathy, Millar et\nal. [36] suggest that clinicians should instead focus on conveying the principles of tendon\nloading and individually tailor the program to promote patient engagement with and con‐\nsequently the success of the program. Importantly, individuals with AT have highlighted\nthe burden of therapeutic exercise, particularly those prescriptions incorporating daily or\ntwice daily exercises [144]. With the biopsychosocial impact being a core tenet of AT [144–\n146], and coupled with patient perceptions that passive treatments (e.g., massage, dry\nneedling, ultrasound) are more efficacious than therapeutic exercises for managing AT\n[145], specifying exercises based on muscle contraction type may be less important than\npatient education about mechanical loading and the prescription of exercises according to\nwhat the patient is most likely to perform [36]. Adherence rates and patient satisfaction\nwith mixed protocols appear to be at least as", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "rtici‐\npant tolerability when exercising consistently at 90% of MVC [103]. A recent systematic\nreview and meta‐analysis by Lazarczuk et al. [32] found that resistance training protocols\ncompleted by healthy individuals at high‐intensities (greater th"}
{"chunk_id": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022_p0008_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_77e9bb13a037", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3570e33fbd3adc85", "text_span_hash": "3570e33fbd3adc85", "source_file": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "according to\nMVC are non‐existent. A close comparative methodology currently being tested is heavy\nslow resistance training (HSR), which functions off of a ‘repetitions maximum’ (RM) pre‐\nscription approach [137]. HSR, which maximally reaches six RM", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Figure 2. Relationship between tendon force (i.e., % MVC) and strain in 20 healthy individuals. The\ntransparent gray area indicates the strain range proposed to be optimal for positive tendon adapta‐\ntion (4.5% to 6.5% strain [42–44]). Data points represent means and error bars denote standard de‐\nviation. Data in this graph was obtained from [147].\nTranslating such evidence to individuals with AT is challenging for several reasons.\nFirstly, given that AT decreases longitudinal strain [116–118] and plantar flexor strength\n[122], it is unclear if those with AT can: (1) consistently reach the optimal strain range; (2)\nconsistently tolerate loads of 70–90% of MVC; and (3) if the optimal strain range causing\nadaptation applies for tendinopathic tissue. Secondly, MVC testing is not always practical\nclinically, and home‐based approaches for monitoring rehabilitative loading according to\nMVC are non‐existent. A close comparative methodology currently being tested is heavy\nslow resistance training (HSR), which functions off of a ‘repetitions maximum’ (RM) pre‐\nscription approach [137]. HSR, which maximally reaches six RM, has been adapted for AT\nand has demonstrated clinical benefits while also being well received by patients [35].\nHowever, more research for HSR is required [142], and adherence and fidelity to exercise\ndose during HSR in individuals with AT may pose an issue [148]. Additionally, the im‐\nportance of load progression fo", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "according to\nMVC are non‐existent. A close comparative methodology currently being tested is heavy\nslow resistance training (HSR), which functions off of a ‘repetitions maximum’ (RM) pre‐\nscription approach [137]. HSR, which maximally reaches six RM"}
{"chunk_id": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022_p0008_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_77e9bb13a037", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "53ac22a8cc2e008b", "text_span_hash": "53ac22a8cc2e008b", "source_file": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "tion\nLoad frequency describes the number of complete repetitions (loading and relaxation)\nthat can be completed over a specific time. Load frequency is calculated as the inverse of\nthe total time needed to complete a single repetition, and is measure", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Figure 2. Relationship between tendon force (i.e., % MVC) and strain in 20 healthy individuals. The\ntransparent gray area indicates the strain range proposed to be optimal for positive tendon adapta‐\ntion (4.5% to 6.5% strain [42–44]). Data points represent means and error bars denote standard de‐\nviation. Data in this graph was obtained from [147].\nTranslating such evidence to individuals with AT is challenging for several reasons.\nFirstly, given that AT decreases longitudinal strain [116–118] and plantar flexor strength\n[122], it is unclear if those with AT can: (1) consistently reach the optimal strain range; (2)\nconsistently tolerate loads of 70–90% of MVC; and (3) if the optimal strain range causing\nadaptation applies for tendinopathic tissue. Secondly, MVC testing is not always practical\nclinically, and home‐based approaches for monitoring rehabilitative loading according to\nMVC are non‐existent. A close comparative methodology currently being tested is heavy\nslow resistance training (HSR), which functions off of a ‘repetitions maximum’ (RM) pre‐\nscription approach [137]. HSR, which maximally reaches six RM, has been adapted for AT\nand has demonstrated clinical benefits while also being well received by patients [35].\nHowever, more research for HSR is required [142], and adherence and fidelity to exercise\ndose during HSR in individuals with AT may pose an issue [148]. Additionally, the im‐\nportance of load progression fo", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "tion\nLoad frequency describes the number of complete repetitions (loading and relaxation)\nthat can be completed over a specific time. Load frequency is calculated as the inverse of\nthe total time needed to complete a single repetition, and is measure"}
{"chunk_id": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022_p0008_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_77e9bb13a037", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8010e79c772ab7a7", "text_span_hash": "8010e79c772ab7a7", "source_file": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "gle repetition, and is measured in hertz (Hz). Load‐\ning rate describes the change in loading intensity with respect to time (e.g., time taken to\nreach target load from no‐load condition). Load duration describes the amount of time a\ntraining stimulu", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Figure 2. Relationship between tendon force (i.e., % MVC) and strain in 20 healthy individuals. The\ntransparent gray area indicates the strain range proposed to be optimal for positive tendon adapta‐\ntion (4.5% to 6.5% strain [42–44]). Data points represent means and error bars denote standard de‐\nviation. Data in this graph was obtained from [147].\nTranslating such evidence to individuals with AT is challenging for several reasons.\nFirstly, given that AT decreases longitudinal strain [116–118] and plantar flexor strength\n[122], it is unclear if those with AT can: (1) consistently reach the optimal strain range; (2)\nconsistently tolerate loads of 70–90% of MVC; and (3) if the optimal strain range causing\nadaptation applies for tendinopathic tissue. Secondly, MVC testing is not always practical\nclinically, and home‐based approaches for monitoring rehabilitative loading according to\nMVC are non‐existent. A close comparative methodology currently being tested is heavy\nslow resistance training (HSR), which functions off of a ‘repetitions maximum’ (RM) pre‐\nscription approach [137]. HSR, which maximally reaches six RM, has been adapted for AT\nand has demonstrated clinical benefits while also being well received by patients [35].\nHowever, more research for HSR is required [142], and adherence and fidelity to exercise\ndose during HSR in individuals with AT may pose an issue [148]. Additionally, the im‐\nportance of load progression fo", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "gle repetition, and is measured in hertz (Hz). Load‐\ning rate describes the change in loading intensity with respect to time (e.g., time taken to\nreach target load from no‐load condition). Load duration describes the amount of time a\ntraining stimulu"}
{"chunk_id": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022_p0008_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_77e9bb13a037", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1881c6d75369a734", "text_span_hash": "1881c6d75369a734", "source_file": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "unt of time a\ntraining stimulus is applied for.\nIn healthy individuals, Arampatzis, Bohm, and colleagues demonstrated that when\na high load‐intensity is used (i.e., 90% of MVC), a low loading frequency (i.e., 0.17 Hz, 3 s\nloading/3 s relaxation) was", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Figure 2. Relationship between tendon force (i.e., % MVC) and strain in 20 healthy individuals. The\ntransparent gray area indicates the strain range proposed to be optimal for positive tendon adapta‐\ntion (4.5% to 6.5% strain [42–44]). Data points represent means and error bars denote standard de‐\nviation. Data in this graph was obtained from [147].\nTranslating such evidence to individuals with AT is challenging for several reasons.\nFirstly, given that AT decreases longitudinal strain [116–118] and plantar flexor strength\n[122], it is unclear if those with AT can: (1) consistently reach the optimal strain range; (2)\nconsistently tolerate loads of 70–90% of MVC; and (3) if the optimal strain range causing\nadaptation applies for tendinopathic tissue. Secondly, MVC testing is not always practical\nclinically, and home‐based approaches for monitoring rehabilitative loading according to\nMVC are non‐existent. A close comparative methodology currently being tested is heavy\nslow resistance training (HSR), which functions off of a ‘repetitions maximum’ (RM) pre‐\nscription approach [137]. HSR, which maximally reaches six RM, has been adapted for AT\nand has demonstrated clinical benefits while also being well received by patients [35].\nHowever, more research for HSR is required [142], and adherence and fidelity to exercise\ndose during HSR in individuals with AT may pose an issue [148]. Additionally, the im‐\nportance of load progression fo", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "unt of time a\ntraining stimulus is applied for.\nIn healthy individuals, Arampatzis, Bohm, and colleagues demonstrated that when\na high load‐intensity is used (i.e., 90% of MVC), a low loading frequency (i.e., 0.17 Hz, 3 s\nloading/3 s relaxation) was"}
{"chunk_id": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022_p0009_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_77e9bb13a037", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "eb05b2fb8129e4a3", "text_span_hash": "eb05b2fb8129e4a3", "source_file": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "37], Alfredson’s eccentric\n[135], Silbernagel’s combined [136], and Stanish and Curwin’s eccentric‐concentric proto‐\ncol [134]. Of the original published protocols, HSR was the only protocol to explicitly state\nload frequency, citing 6 s/repetition.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "contractions at 90% of MVC) [44]. Beyond the Achilles, it appears that conflicting evidence\nexists as to the effect of loading rate [150] and duration [151,152] on tendon adaptation.\nGiven the impact of rest duration on collagen organization [103], more research into this\nfactor may be warranted.\nThere exist many therapeutic exercise protocols for AT; however, there are four\nwhich are consistently cited within the literature [33]—HSR [137], Alfredson’s eccentric\n[135], Silbernagel’s combined [136], and Stanish and Curwin’s eccentric‐concentric proto‐\ncol [134]. Of the original published protocols, HSR was the only protocol to explicitly state\nload frequency, citing 6 s/repetition. The others opted to instead use adjectives (e.g.,\n‘slow’, ‘moderate’, ‘fast’) to specify load frequency as these are practical for home‐based\nrehabilitation. Consequently, much of the AT rehabilitation evidence to date largely ig‐\nnores the effects of loading frequency, rate, and duration generally prioritizing clinical\noutcomes (e.g., pain, function) [40].\n4.4. Exercise Positioning\nExercise positioning describes the orientation of the body in space when completing a\ntherapeutic exercise protocol. Based on current understanding, optimal positioning for\ntherapeutic exercise of the Achilles tendon must: (1) facilitate the generation of controlled\nhigh‐magnitude loading through the triceps surae MTU; (2) be tolerable and practical to\nthe client within t", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "37], Alfredson’s eccentric\n[135], Silbernagel’s combined [136], and Stanish and Curwin’s eccentric‐concentric proto‐\ncol [134]. Of the original published protocols, HSR was the only protocol to explicitly state\nload frequency, citing 6 s/repetition."}
{"chunk_id": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022_p0009_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_77e9bb13a037", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9a7692e3c417c031", "text_span_hash": "9a7692e3c417c031", "source_file": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "uency, citing 6 s/repetition. The others opted to instead use adjectives (e.g.,\n‘slow’, ‘moderate’, ‘fast’) to specify load frequency as these are practical for home‐based\nrehabilitation. Consequently, much of the AT rehabilitation evidence to date l", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "contractions at 90% of MVC) [44]. Beyond the Achilles, it appears that conflicting evidence\nexists as to the effect of loading rate [150] and duration [151,152] on tendon adaptation.\nGiven the impact of rest duration on collagen organization [103], more research into this\nfactor may be warranted.\nThere exist many therapeutic exercise protocols for AT; however, there are four\nwhich are consistently cited within the literature [33]—HSR [137], Alfredson’s eccentric\n[135], Silbernagel’s combined [136], and Stanish and Curwin’s eccentric‐concentric proto‐\ncol [134]. Of the original published protocols, HSR was the only protocol to explicitly state\nload frequency, citing 6 s/repetition. The others opted to instead use adjectives (e.g.,\n‘slow’, ‘moderate’, ‘fast’) to specify load frequency as these are practical for home‐based\nrehabilitation. Consequently, much of the AT rehabilitation evidence to date largely ig‐\nnores the effects of loading frequency, rate, and duration generally prioritizing clinical\noutcomes (e.g., pain, function) [40].\n4.4. Exercise Positioning\nExercise positioning describes the orientation of the body in space when completing a\ntherapeutic exercise protocol. Based on current understanding, optimal positioning for\ntherapeutic exercise of the Achilles tendon must: (1) facilitate the generation of controlled\nhigh‐magnitude loading through the triceps surae MTU; (2) be tolerable and practical to\nthe client within t", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "uency, citing 6 s/repetition. The others opted to instead use adjectives (e.g.,\n‘slow’, ‘moderate’, ‘fast’) to specify load frequency as these are practical for home‐based\nrehabilitation. Consequently, much of the AT rehabilitation evidence to date l"}
{"chunk_id": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022_p0011_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_77e9bb13a037", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1f62e7cd4c981315", "text_span_hash": "1f62e7cd4c981315", "source_file": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "4.5. Exercise Schedule\nThe exercise schedule describes the frequency of exercise in terms of the intervention\nduration, number of therapeutic exercise sessions per week or per day, and number of\nsets/repetitions to be completed per session. A recent", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "4.5. Exercise Schedule\nThe exercise schedule describes the frequency of exercise in terms of the intervention\nduration, number of therapeutic exercise sessions per week or per day, and number of\nsets/repetitions to be completed per session. A recent systematic review by Burton and\nMcCormack [40] synthesized the resistance training protocols amongst exercise interven‐\ntions treating lower limb tendinopathies; Achilles tendinopathy was the most commonly\ninvestigated within this review (26/52 randomized controlled trials [RCTs] included), fol‐\nlowed by patellar tendinopathy (16/52 studies included). Therapeutic exercise interven‐\ntions ranged in duration from 4 to 26 weeks, with 85% of included studies using a 12‐week\nintervention. Exercise session frequency ranged from two to seven days per week, and\ntwo to 14 exercise sessions per week. Exercise sets ranged from 1 to 12, and repetitions\nranged from 3 to 30. The most commonly used therapeutic exercise program was the orig‐\ninal (or slightly modified) ‘Alfredson’ protocol [135], which was cited in 48% of included\nstudies. The Alfredson protocol prescription consists of 12 weeks of exercise sessions com‐\npleted twice a day, seven days per week. Each session consists of two exercises (straight‐\nleg and bent‐knee eccentric heel drops), completed in 3 sets of 15 repetitions totaling 180\nrepetitions per day. Despite the frequent use of the Alfredson protocol, the need for the\nhigh vol", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "4.5. Exercise Schedule\nThe exercise schedule describes the frequency of exercise in terms of the intervention\nduration, number of therapeutic exercise sessions per week or per day, and number of\nsets/repetitions to be completed per session. A recent"}
{"chunk_id": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022_p0011_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_77e9bb13a037", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f2f6900084387f28", "text_span_hash": "f2f6900084387f28", "source_file": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "mpleted per session. A recent systematic review by Burton and\nMcCormack [40] synthesized the resistance training protocols amongst exercise interven‐\ntions treating lower limb tendinopathies; Achilles tendinopathy was the most commonly\ninvestigated w", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "4.5. Exercise Schedule\nThe exercise schedule describes the frequency of exercise in terms of the intervention\nduration, number of therapeutic exercise sessions per week or per day, and number of\nsets/repetitions to be completed per session. A recent systematic review by Burton and\nMcCormack [40] synthesized the resistance training protocols amongst exercise interven‐\ntions treating lower limb tendinopathies; Achilles tendinopathy was the most commonly\ninvestigated within this review (26/52 randomized controlled trials [RCTs] included), fol‐\nlowed by patellar tendinopathy (16/52 studies included). Therapeutic exercise interven‐\ntions ranged in duration from 4 to 26 weeks, with 85% of included studies using a 12‐week\nintervention. Exercise session frequency ranged from two to seven days per week, and\ntwo to 14 exercise sessions per week. Exercise sets ranged from 1 to 12, and repetitions\nranged from 3 to 30. The most commonly used therapeutic exercise program was the orig‐\ninal (or slightly modified) ‘Alfredson’ protocol [135], which was cited in 48% of included\nstudies. The Alfredson protocol prescription consists of 12 weeks of exercise sessions com‐\npleted twice a day, seven days per week. Each session consists of two exercises (straight‐\nleg and bent‐knee eccentric heel drops), completed in 3 sets of 15 repetitions totaling 180\nrepetitions per day. Despite the frequent use of the Alfredson protocol, the need for the\nhigh vol", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "mpleted per session. A recent systematic review by Burton and\nMcCormack [40] synthesized the resistance training protocols amongst exercise interven‐\ntions treating lower limb tendinopathies; Achilles tendinopathy was the most commonly\ninvestigated w"}
{"chunk_id": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022_p0011_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_77e9bb13a037", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b4612d87a9b47724", "text_span_hash": "b4612d87a9b47724", "source_file": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "from 4 to 26 weeks, with 85% of included studies using a 12‐week\nintervention. Exercise session frequency ranged from two to seven days per week, and\ntwo to 14 exercise sessions per week. Exercise sets ranged from 1 to 12, and repetitions\nranged from", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "4.5. Exercise Schedule\nThe exercise schedule describes the frequency of exercise in terms of the intervention\nduration, number of therapeutic exercise sessions per week or per day, and number of\nsets/repetitions to be completed per session. A recent systematic review by Burton and\nMcCormack [40] synthesized the resistance training protocols amongst exercise interven‐\ntions treating lower limb tendinopathies; Achilles tendinopathy was the most commonly\ninvestigated within this review (26/52 randomized controlled trials [RCTs] included), fol‐\nlowed by patellar tendinopathy (16/52 studies included). Therapeutic exercise interven‐\ntions ranged in duration from 4 to 26 weeks, with 85% of included studies using a 12‐week\nintervention. Exercise session frequency ranged from two to seven days per week, and\ntwo to 14 exercise sessions per week. Exercise sets ranged from 1 to 12, and repetitions\nranged from 3 to 30. The most commonly used therapeutic exercise program was the orig‐\ninal (or slightly modified) ‘Alfredson’ protocol [135], which was cited in 48% of included\nstudies. The Alfredson protocol prescription consists of 12 weeks of exercise sessions com‐\npleted twice a day, seven days per week. Each session consists of two exercises (straight‐\nleg and bent‐knee eccentric heel drops), completed in 3 sets of 15 repetitions totaling 180\nrepetitions per day. Despite the frequent use of the Alfredson protocol, the need for the\nhigh vol", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "from 4 to 26 weeks, with 85% of included studies using a 12‐week\nintervention. Exercise session frequency ranged from two to seven days per week, and\ntwo to 14 exercise sessions per week. Exercise sets ranged from 1 to 12, and repetitions\nranged from"}
{"chunk_id": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022_p0011_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_77e9bb13a037", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ac2d01485ace5e45", "text_span_hash": "ac2d01485ace5e45", "source_file": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "2, and repetitions\nranged from 3 to 30. The most commonly used therapeutic exercise program was the orig‐\ninal (or slightly modified) ‘Alfredson’ protocol [135], which was cited in 48% of included\nstudies. The Alfredson protocol prescription consists", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "4.5. Exercise Schedule\nThe exercise schedule describes the frequency of exercise in terms of the intervention\nduration, number of therapeutic exercise sessions per week or per day, and number of\nsets/repetitions to be completed per session. A recent systematic review by Burton and\nMcCormack [40] synthesized the resistance training protocols amongst exercise interven‐\ntions treating lower limb tendinopathies; Achilles tendinopathy was the most commonly\ninvestigated within this review (26/52 randomized controlled trials [RCTs] included), fol‐\nlowed by patellar tendinopathy (16/52 studies included). Therapeutic exercise interven‐\ntions ranged in duration from 4 to 26 weeks, with 85% of included studies using a 12‐week\nintervention. Exercise session frequency ranged from two to seven days per week, and\ntwo to 14 exercise sessions per week. Exercise sets ranged from 1 to 12, and repetitions\nranged from 3 to 30. The most commonly used therapeutic exercise program was the orig‐\ninal (or slightly modified) ‘Alfredson’ protocol [135], which was cited in 48% of included\nstudies. The Alfredson protocol prescription consists of 12 weeks of exercise sessions com‐\npleted twice a day, seven days per week. Each session consists of two exercises (straight‐\nleg and bent‐knee eccentric heel drops), completed in 3 sets of 15 repetitions totaling 180\nrepetitions per day. Despite the frequent use of the Alfredson protocol, the need for the\nhigh vol", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "2, and repetitions\nranged from 3 to 30. The most commonly used therapeutic exercise program was the orig‐\ninal (or slightly modified) ‘Alfredson’ protocol [135], which was cited in 48% of included\nstudies. The Alfredson protocol prescription consists"}
{"chunk_id": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022_p0011_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_77e9bb13a037", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "61a5ab79d01ec72e", "text_span_hash": "61a5ab79d01ec72e", "source_file": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "of 15 repetitions totaling 180\nrepetitions per day. Despite the frequent use of the Alfredson protocol, the need for the\nhigh volume of exercises suggested by this protocol has been drawn into question, with\none study finding equal improvement at si", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "4.5. Exercise Schedule\nThe exercise schedule describes the frequency of exercise in terms of the intervention\nduration, number of therapeutic exercise sessions per week or per day, and number of\nsets/repetitions to be completed per session. A recent systematic review by Burton and\nMcCormack [40] synthesized the resistance training protocols amongst exercise interven‐\ntions treating lower limb tendinopathies; Achilles tendinopathy was the most commonly\ninvestigated within this review (26/52 randomized controlled trials [RCTs] included), fol‐\nlowed by patellar tendinopathy (16/52 studies included). Therapeutic exercise interven‐\ntions ranged in duration from 4 to 26 weeks, with 85% of included studies using a 12‐week\nintervention. Exercise session frequency ranged from two to seven days per week, and\ntwo to 14 exercise sessions per week. Exercise sets ranged from 1 to 12, and repetitions\nranged from 3 to 30. The most commonly used therapeutic exercise program was the orig‐\ninal (or slightly modified) ‘Alfredson’ protocol [135], which was cited in 48% of included\nstudies. The Alfredson protocol prescription consists of 12 weeks of exercise sessions com‐\npleted twice a day, seven days per week. Each session consists of two exercises (straight‐\nleg and bent‐knee eccentric heel drops), completed in 3 sets of 15 repetitions totaling 180\nrepetitions per day. Despite the frequent use of the Alfredson protocol, the need for the\nhigh vol", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "of 15 repetitions totaling 180\nrepetitions per day. Despite the frequent use of the Alfredson protocol, the need for the\nhigh volume of exercises suggested by this protocol has been drawn into question, with\none study finding equal improvement at si"}
{"chunk_id": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022_p0011_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_77e9bb13a037", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "08ca9dabf5699207", "text_span_hash": "08ca9dabf5699207", "source_file": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "inding equal improvement at six weeks between a ‘standard’ Alfredson pre‐\nscription and a ‘do‐as‐tolerated’ prescription, which completed the same exercise inter‐\nvention excluding the repetition volume [173]. Within the study, the do‐as‐tolerated\ngr", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "4.5. Exercise Schedule\nThe exercise schedule describes the frequency of exercise in terms of the intervention\nduration, number of therapeutic exercise sessions per week or per day, and number of\nsets/repetitions to be completed per session. A recent systematic review by Burton and\nMcCormack [40] synthesized the resistance training protocols amongst exercise interven‐\ntions treating lower limb tendinopathies; Achilles tendinopathy was the most commonly\ninvestigated within this review (26/52 randomized controlled trials [RCTs] included), fol‐\nlowed by patellar tendinopathy (16/52 studies included). Therapeutic exercise interven‐\ntions ranged in duration from 4 to 26 weeks, with 85% of included studies using a 12‐week\nintervention. Exercise session frequency ranged from two to seven days per week, and\ntwo to 14 exercise sessions per week. Exercise sets ranged from 1 to 12, and repetitions\nranged from 3 to 30. The most commonly used therapeutic exercise program was the orig‐\ninal (or slightly modified) ‘Alfredson’ protocol [135], which was cited in 48% of included\nstudies. The Alfredson protocol prescription consists of 12 weeks of exercise sessions com‐\npleted twice a day, seven days per week. Each session consists of two exercises (straight‐\nleg and bent‐knee eccentric heel drops), completed in 3 sets of 15 repetitions totaling 180\nrepetitions per day. Despite the frequent use of the Alfredson protocol, the need for the\nhigh vol", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "inding equal improvement at six weeks between a ‘standard’ Alfredson pre‐\nscription and a ‘do‐as‐tolerated’ prescription, which completed the same exercise inter‐\nvention excluding the repetition volume [173]. Within the study, the do‐as‐tolerated\ngr"}
{"chunk_id": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022_p0011_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_77e9bb13a037", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "6a101480c51c4b39", "text_span_hash": "6a101480c51c4b39", "source_file": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "study, the do‐as‐tolerated\ngroup averaged 112 repetitions per day, whereas the standard Alfredson group averaged\n166 repetitions per day. On the whole, Burton and McCormack’s review suggests that\nsubstantial variability exists in exercise interventi", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "4.5. Exercise Schedule\nThe exercise schedule describes the frequency of exercise in terms of the intervention\nduration, number of therapeutic exercise sessions per week or per day, and number of\nsets/repetitions to be completed per session. A recent systematic review by Burton and\nMcCormack [40] synthesized the resistance training protocols amongst exercise interven‐\ntions treating lower limb tendinopathies; Achilles tendinopathy was the most commonly\ninvestigated within this review (26/52 randomized controlled trials [RCTs] included), fol‐\nlowed by patellar tendinopathy (16/52 studies included). Therapeutic exercise interven‐\ntions ranged in duration from 4 to 26 weeks, with 85% of included studies using a 12‐week\nintervention. Exercise session frequency ranged from two to seven days per week, and\ntwo to 14 exercise sessions per week. Exercise sets ranged from 1 to 12, and repetitions\nranged from 3 to 30. The most commonly used therapeutic exercise program was the orig‐\ninal (or slightly modified) ‘Alfredson’ protocol [135], which was cited in 48% of included\nstudies. The Alfredson protocol prescription consists of 12 weeks of exercise sessions com‐\npleted twice a day, seven days per week. Each session consists of two exercises (straight‐\nleg and bent‐knee eccentric heel drops), completed in 3 sets of 15 repetitions totaling 180\nrepetitions per day. Despite the frequent use of the Alfredson protocol, the need for the\nhigh vol", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "study, the do‐as‐tolerated\ngroup averaged 112 repetitions per day, whereas the standard Alfredson group averaged\n166 repetitions per day. On the whole, Burton and McCormack’s review suggests that\nsubstantial variability exists in exercise interventi"}
{"chunk_id": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022_p0012_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_77e9bb13a037", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8c9a972d8926de8b", "text_span_hash": "8c9a972d8926de8b", "source_file": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": ", though positive results have\nbeen found at six weeks [40]\nOf 52 RCTs e, session frequency ranged\nfrom two to seven days per week, and two\nto 14 exercise sessions per week [40]\nOf 52 RCTs, sets ranged from 1 to 12, and\nrepetitions ranged from three", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Table 1. Clinical takeaways for resistance‐exercise based Achilles tendinopathy management.\nClinical Recommendation\nWith a lack of evidence favoring one con‐\ntraction type [141–143], it does not seem\njustified to exclusively prescribe eccentric\nDifferent contraction types can be used\nFocus on conveying the principles of\nLoad Intensity Section 4.2\nHigh‐magnitude loading (>70% of MVC b)\ninduces greater tendon adaptation in\nhealthy individuals [29,32]\nMany AT exercise programs favor body‐\nweight loading and increase resistance as\ntolerated (e.g., 5 kg increments in a back‐\nIncreasing load intensity appears to\nstimulate greater tendon adaptation in\nPrioritize high‐magnitude loading (as\ntolerated) and load progression over\nEvidence pertaining to these factors is\nSeminal AT rehabilitation programs pri‐\noritize ‘slow’ loading [134–136]\nNot enough existing evidence, though\nmost programs use ‘slow’ loading fre‐\nOf the lower limb joint angles, ankle angle\nappears to most impact Achilles tendon\nloading as it largely dictates the force\nthrough the Achilles tendon [161] and ten‐\ndon elongation [168]\nWB c enhances ankle dorsiflexion com‐\npared to NWB d across knee angles in\nhealthy individuals [164]\nSoleus activity is independent of knee an‐\ngle [153–155]; gastrocnemius is less active\nAnkle dorsiflexion, knee/hip exten‐\nsion may be most appropriate for AT\ntherapeutic exercise\nExcessive dorsiflexion may be irritable\nto those with insertional", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": ", though positive results have\nbeen found at six weeks [40]\nOf 52 RCTs e, session frequency ranged\nfrom two to seven days per week, and two\nto 14 exercise sessions per week [40]\nOf 52 RCTs, sets ranged from 1 to 12, and\nrepetitions ranged from three"}
{"chunk_id": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022_p0012_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_77e9bb13a037", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3d163441271503fb", "text_span_hash": "3d163441271503fb", "source_file": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "repetitions ranged from three to 30 [40]\nA 12‐week exercise program duration\nappears most appropriate\nExercise session frequency can vary\nconsiderably, and it largely depends\non the loading intensity, volume, and\nSets/repetitions can vary considerabl", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Table 1. Clinical takeaways for resistance‐exercise based Achilles tendinopathy management.\nClinical Recommendation\nWith a lack of evidence favoring one con‐\ntraction type [141–143], it does not seem\njustified to exclusively prescribe eccentric\nDifferent contraction types can be used\nFocus on conveying the principles of\nLoad Intensity Section 4.2\nHigh‐magnitude loading (>70% of MVC b)\ninduces greater tendon adaptation in\nhealthy individuals [29,32]\nMany AT exercise programs favor body‐\nweight loading and increase resistance as\ntolerated (e.g., 5 kg increments in a back‐\nIncreasing load intensity appears to\nstimulate greater tendon adaptation in\nPrioritize high‐magnitude loading (as\ntolerated) and load progression over\nEvidence pertaining to these factors is\nSeminal AT rehabilitation programs pri‐\noritize ‘slow’ loading [134–136]\nNot enough existing evidence, though\nmost programs use ‘slow’ loading fre‐\nOf the lower limb joint angles, ankle angle\nappears to most impact Achilles tendon\nloading as it largely dictates the force\nthrough the Achilles tendon [161] and ten‐\ndon elongation [168]\nWB c enhances ankle dorsiflexion com‐\npared to NWB d across knee angles in\nhealthy individuals [164]\nSoleus activity is independent of knee an‐\ngle [153–155]; gastrocnemius is less active\nAnkle dorsiflexion, knee/hip exten‐\nsion may be most appropriate for AT\ntherapeutic exercise\nExcessive dorsiflexion may be irritable\nto those with insertional", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "repetitions ranged from three to 30 [40]\nA 12‐week exercise program duration\nappears most appropriate\nExercise session frequency can vary\nconsiderably, and it largely depends\non the loading intensity, volume, and\nSets/repetitions can vary considerabl"}
{"chunk_id": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022_p0013_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_77e9bb13a037", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "56a5ba556e1cf0e9", "text_span_hash": "56a5ba556e1cf0e9", "source_file": "Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Achilles_Tendon_Adaptation_Resistance_Exercise_2022.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "linicians may prescribe more targeted programs thereby reducing client burden and im‐\nproving rehabilitation outcomes.\nResistance‐based exercise therapy is one of the most prevalent management strate‐\ngies for chronic AT. High‐magnitude loading of th", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "alleviation, and psychosocial and contextual effects were also outside the scope of this\ncommentary but are critical features of AT rehabilitation [41]. Additionally, individual\nclinical presentation, response to rehabilitation, and patient values must be considered at\nthe forefront of AT rehabilitation. Evidence has consistently demonstrated that therapeu‐\ntic exercise is a robust treatment strategy for AT, but further research is needed before\noptimal programming can be recommended.\nAlong with replicating some of the work done in healthy persons in those with AT,\nseveral other areas of exploration may progress therapeutic exercise for AT. Further ex‐\nploration of the role of the Achilles subtendons, subtendon interfaces, and subtendon bio‐\nmechanics is warranted [106]. Study of neuromuscular changes, pain processing, and psy‐\nchological effects during rehabilitation may help illuminate the mechanism of action of\ntherapeutic exercise [37]. Measuring Achilles tendon elongation during plantarflexion\ncould promote individualization of exercise programming, particularly if paired with a\npractical system for quantifying MVC [96]. As further evidence emerges pertaining to the\npathophysiology and adaptation of tendinopathic tissues, computational models may en‐\nable load‐based testing with tendinopathic tissues without placing already compromised\nindividuals at further risk [106,174]. By better understanding tendinopathic tissue ada", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "linicians may prescribe more targeted programs thereby reducing client burden and im‐\nproving rehabilitation outcomes.\nResistance‐based exercise therapy is one of the most prevalent management strate‐\ngies for chronic AT. High‐magnitude loading of th"}
