nning is deﬁned as participa-\ntion below age 18. Jumping/multidi-\nrectional loading sports (soccer,\nbasketball) may optimize bone health.\nPsychological development requires\nmonitoring to reduce risk of injuries\nand burnout. Adequate energy avail-\nability is essential for health; screening\nfor disordered eating and optimizing\nnutrition is important. Injuries during\ngrowth spu", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ne does not provide\npredictable gains in bone health. Sports\nthat do not include impact loading\n(such as swimming) may result in\nweaker bones compared with nonath-\nYoung runners who participate in\nsports have crossover beneﬁts for\nAddress corresponde"}
{"chunk_id": "Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J._p0002_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_fa5a70b54e46", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1b72fd6786cdc532", "text_span_hash": "1b72fd6786cdc532", "source_file": "Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J..pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J..pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ral content and bone geom-\nRecommendations. All young ath-\nletes should participate in high or mul-\nchildhood for at least 2 years. Young\nrunners who do not participate in\nsports should be in a structured jump-\nWHAT STRATEGIES CAN BE USED\nFOR YOUTH R", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "bone health. Adolescent male run-\nners who also participated in basket-\nball had an 82% reduction in stress\nfracture risk (57). Older elite runners\nages 18–44 years who participated in\nball sports during their youth had\na 50% lower risk of stress fracture\nduring running compared with peers\nwho had not (16). However, women\nwith menstrual irregularities did not\nattain the same bone beneﬁts from\nball sports (16), supporting a model\nthat accounts for multiple contribu-\ntors inﬂuencing the response to skel-\nGround reaction forces .3.5 fold\nbody weight with peak force ,0.1 sec-\nonds may be the most effective stimuli\nfor bone development (18). If young\nathletes are not exposed to these\nforces in their sports activity, they\ncan cross-train with a jumping pro-\ngram that has high ground reaction\nforces delivered at a rapid rate. For\nswimmers participating in a 9-month\nbone mineral content and bone geom-\nRecommendations. All young ath-\nletes should participate in high or mul-\nchildhood for at least 2 years. Young\nrunners who do not participate in\nsports should be in a structured jump-\nWHAT STRATEGIES CAN BE USED\nFOR YOUTH RUNNERS TO ASSESS\nPSYCHOLOGICAL DEVELOPMENT\nFOR SAFE PARTICIPATION AND\nCreating a safe and supportive envi-\nronment for youth runners may pro-\nmote longevity in the sport, increase\noverall physical activity, and con-\ntribute to psychological well-being.\nYouth runners are developing physi-\ncally and psychologically while", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ral content and bone geom-\nRecommendations. All young ath-\nletes should participate in high or mul-\nchildhood for at least 2 years. Young\nrunners who do not participate in\nsports should be in a structured jump-\nWHAT STRATEGIES CAN BE USED\nFOR YOUTH R"}
{"chunk_id": "Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J._p0002_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_fa5a70b54e46", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "d134ef5d406f4fb1", "text_span_hash": "d134ef5d406f4fb1", "source_file": "Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J..pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J..pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "TEGIES CAN BE USED\nFOR YOUTH RUNNERS TO ASSESS\nPSYCHOLOGICAL DEVELOPMENT\nFOR SAFE PARTICIPATION AND\nCreating a safe and supportive envi-\nronment for youth runners may pro-\nmote longevity in the sport, increase\noverall physical activity, and con-\ntrib", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "bone health. Adolescent male run-\nners who also participated in basket-\nball had an 82% reduction in stress\nfracture risk (57). Older elite runners\nages 18–44 years who participated in\nball sports during their youth had\na 50% lower risk of stress fracture\nduring running compared with peers\nwho had not (16). However, women\nwith menstrual irregularities did not\nattain the same bone beneﬁts from\nball sports (16), supporting a model\nthat accounts for multiple contribu-\ntors inﬂuencing the response to skel-\nGround reaction forces .3.5 fold\nbody weight with peak force ,0.1 sec-\nonds may be the most effective stimuli\nfor bone development (18). If young\nathletes are not exposed to these\nforces in their sports activity, they\ncan cross-train with a jumping pro-\ngram that has high ground reaction\nforces delivered at a rapid rate. For\nswimmers participating in a 9-month\nbone mineral content and bone geom-\nRecommendations. All young ath-\nletes should participate in high or mul-\nchildhood for at least 2 years. Young\nrunners who do not participate in\nsports should be in a structured jump-\nWHAT STRATEGIES CAN BE USED\nFOR YOUTH RUNNERS TO ASSESS\nPSYCHOLOGICAL DEVELOPMENT\nFOR SAFE PARTICIPATION AND\nCreating a safe and supportive envi-\nronment for youth runners may pro-\nmote longevity in the sport, increase\noverall physical activity, and con-\ntribute to psychological well-being.\nYouth runners are developing physi-\ncally and psychologically while", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "TEGIES CAN BE USED\nFOR YOUTH RUNNERS TO ASSESS\nPSYCHOLOGICAL DEVELOPMENT\nFOR SAFE PARTICIPATION AND\nCreating a safe and supportive envi-\nronment for youth runners may pro-\nmote longevity in the sport, increase\noverall physical activity, and con-\ntrib"}
{"chunk_id": "Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J._p0002_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_fa5a70b54e46", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2c99e3f054bd5764", "text_span_hash": "2c99e3f054bd5764", "source_file": "Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J..pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J..pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ysical activity, and con-\ntribute to psychological well-being.\nYouth runners are developing physi-\ncally and psychologically while par-\nticipating in the sport. Beyond age,\nother factors must be considered\nstatus of a youth runner. The source\nof moti", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "bone health. Adolescent male run-\nners who also participated in basket-\nball had an 82% reduction in stress\nfracture risk (57). Older elite runners\nages 18–44 years who participated in\nball sports during their youth had\na 50% lower risk of stress fracture\nduring running compared with peers\nwho had not (16). However, women\nwith menstrual irregularities did not\nattain the same bone beneﬁts from\nball sports (16), supporting a model\nthat accounts for multiple contribu-\ntors inﬂuencing the response to skel-\nGround reaction forces .3.5 fold\nbody weight with peak force ,0.1 sec-\nonds may be the most effective stimuli\nfor bone development (18). If young\nathletes are not exposed to these\nforces in their sports activity, they\ncan cross-train with a jumping pro-\ngram that has high ground reaction\nforces delivered at a rapid rate. For\nswimmers participating in a 9-month\nbone mineral content and bone geom-\nRecommendations. All young ath-\nletes should participate in high or mul-\nchildhood for at least 2 years. Young\nrunners who do not participate in\nsports should be in a structured jump-\nWHAT STRATEGIES CAN BE USED\nFOR YOUTH RUNNERS TO ASSESS\nPSYCHOLOGICAL DEVELOPMENT\nFOR SAFE PARTICIPATION AND\nCreating a safe and supportive envi-\nronment for youth runners may pro-\nmote longevity in the sport, increase\noverall physical activity, and con-\ntribute to psychological well-being.\nYouth runners are developing physi-\ncally and psychologically while", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ysical activity, and con-\ntribute to psychological well-being.\nYouth runners are developing physi-\ncally and psychologically while par-\nticipating in the sport. Beyond age,\nother factors must be considered\nstatus of a youth runner. The source\nof moti"}
