 should be in a structured jump-\nWHAT STRATEGIES CAN BE USED\nFOR YOUTH RUNNERS TO ASSESS\nPSYCHOLOGICAL DEVELOPMENT\nFOR SAFE PARTICIPATION AND\nCreating a safe and supportive envi-\nronment for youth runners may pro-\nmote longevity in the sport, increase\noverall physical activity, and con-\ntribute to psychological well-being.\nYouth runners are developing physi-\ncally and psychologically while", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "letes, who also have energy require-\nments for growth and development\n(2). A speciﬁc cutoff has not been deter-\nmined in male runners. Appropriate EA\nthresholds likely vary among individu-\nals. Low EA can have signiﬁcant health\nand performance conse"}
{"chunk_id": "Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J._p0002_c0019", "source_registry_id": "src_fa5a70b54e46", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8ed0dd8b6a35f8bd", "text_span_hash": "8ed0dd8b6a35f8bd", "source_file": "Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J..pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J..pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 19, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "t health\nand performance consequences (36).\nHowever, determining appropriate EI\nand EA in athletes can be difﬁcult. Die-\ntary and training recall can be inaccu-\nrate, and direct measurement can be\nTherefore, surrogate markers of low\nEA are important", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "bone health. Adolescent male run-\nners who also participated in basket-\nball had an 82% reduction in stress\nfracture risk (57). Older elite runners\nages 18–44 years who participated in\nball sports during their youth had\na 50% lower risk of stress fracture\nduring running compared with peers\nwho had not (16). However, women\nwith menstrual irregularities did not\nattain the same bone beneﬁts from\nball sports (16), supporting a model\nthat accounts for multiple contribu-\ntors inﬂuencing the response to skel-\nGround reaction forces .3.5 fold\nbody weight with peak force ,0.1 sec-\nonds may be the most effective stimuli\nfor bone development (18). If young\nathletes are not exposed to these\nforces in their sports activity, they\ncan cross-train with a jumping pro-\ngram that has high ground reaction\nforces delivered at a rapid rate. For\nswimmers participating in a 9-month\nbone mineral content and bone geom-\nRecommendations. All young ath-\nletes should participate in high or mul-\nchildhood for at least 2 years. Young\nrunners who do not participate in\nsports should be in a structured jump-\nWHAT STRATEGIES CAN BE USED\nFOR YOUTH RUNNERS TO ASSESS\nPSYCHOLOGICAL DEVELOPMENT\nFOR SAFE PARTICIPATION AND\nCreating a safe and supportive envi-\nronment for youth runners may pro-\nmote longevity in the sport, increase\noverall physical activity, and con-\ntribute to psychological well-being.\nYouth runners are developing physi-\ncally and psychologically while", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "t health\nand performance consequences (36).\nHowever, determining appropriate EI\nand EA in athletes can be difﬁcult. Die-\ntary and training recall can be inaccu-\nrate, and direct measurement can be\nTherefore, surrogate markers of low\nEA are important"}
{"chunk_id": "Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J._p0003_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_fa5a70b54e46", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e0f490ae8e80ce7b", "text_span_hash": "e0f490ae8e80ce7b", "source_file": "Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J..pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J..pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "nt between the 2 screening\ntools when assigning participants to\nmoderate-/high-risk categories (21).\nRecommendations. Youth runners\nof both sexes should be screened for\nlow EA and health markers of Triad/\nRED‐S using screening tools such as\nTriad‐CRA", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "and development as being negatively\nimpacted by low EA (34,36), so moni-\ntoring growth curves is important to\nensure both needs for growth and sports\nparticipation are being addressed.\nLow EA may occur with or without\ndisorders/disordered\nScreening for behaviors associated with\neating disorders/disordered eating is\nrecommended at the time of the Pre-\nparticipation Physical Evaluation (4,22),\nand appropriate nutrition should be\nmonitored by coaches, strength and\nconditioning professionals, and others\nwho interact with the youth athlete.\nWhile validated in older female athletes,\nthe Low Energy Availability in Females\nQuestionnaire was developed speciﬁ-\ncally to determine low EA and Triad\nrisk in athletes and focuses largely on\ninjuries and gastrointestinal and repro-\nductive function (32). A Triad cumula-\ntive risk assessment (Triad-CRA) (12)\nand RED-S screening tool (RED-S\nCAT) (35) have both been developed.\nAlthough studies in youth runners are\nlacking, in college runners designated\nmoderate- and high-risk Triad-CRA,\n50% and 71% of runners sustained\na bone stress injury (BSI) within a year,\nrespectively (55). These ﬁndings are\nconsistent with a previous report that\nsuggested cumulative Triad risk factors\nincrease the risk of stress fractures in\nadolescent female runners (57). In a sim-\nilar study of male collegiate runners,\n(excluding menstrual history) predicted\nincreased rates of BSI (25). The RED-S\nCAT has been less tested ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "nt between the 2 screening\ntools when assigning participants to\nmoderate-/high-risk categories (21).\nRecommendations. Youth runners\nof both sexes should be screened for\nlow EA and health markers of Triad/\nRED‐S using screening tools such as\nTriad‐CRA"}
{"chunk_id": "Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J._p0003_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_fa5a70b54e46", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "a9e568779f66e59a", "text_span_hash": "a9e568779f66e59a", "source_file": "Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J..pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J..pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "eening tools such as\nTriad‐CRA and RED‐S CAT. Their body\nmass index (BMI) and weight (percent\nmedian BMI and expected weight per-\ncentile) should be calculated, with careful\ncurves. Detailed menstrual history should\nbe obtained in all female runners,", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "and development as being negatively\nimpacted by low EA (34,36), so moni-\ntoring growth curves is important to\nensure both needs for growth and sports\nparticipation are being addressed.\nLow EA may occur with or without\ndisorders/disordered\nScreening for behaviors associated with\neating disorders/disordered eating is\nrecommended at the time of the Pre-\nparticipation Physical Evaluation (4,22),\nand appropriate nutrition should be\nmonitored by coaches, strength and\nconditioning professionals, and others\nwho interact with the youth athlete.\nWhile validated in older female athletes,\nthe Low Energy Availability in Females\nQuestionnaire was developed speciﬁ-\ncally to determine low EA and Triad\nrisk in athletes and focuses largely on\ninjuries and gastrointestinal and repro-\nductive function (32). A Triad cumula-\ntive risk assessment (Triad-CRA) (12)\nand RED-S screening tool (RED-S\nCAT) (35) have both been developed.\nAlthough studies in youth runners are\nlacking, in college runners designated\nmoderate- and high-risk Triad-CRA,\n50% and 71% of runners sustained\na bone stress injury (BSI) within a year,\nrespectively (55). These ﬁndings are\nconsistent with a previous report that\nsuggested cumulative Triad risk factors\nincrease the risk of stress fractures in\nadolescent female runners (57). In a sim-\nilar study of male collegiate runners,\n(excluding menstrual history) predicted\nincreased rates of BSI (25). The RED-S\nCAT has been less tested ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "eening tools such as\nTriad‐CRA and RED‐S CAT. Their body\nmass index (BMI) and weight (percent\nmedian BMI and expected weight per-\ncentile) should be calculated, with careful\ncurves. Detailed menstrual history should\nbe obtained in all female runners,"}
{"chunk_id": "Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J._p0003_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_fa5a70b54e46", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "905e737d3a0b4791", "text_span_hash": "905e737d3a0b4791", "source_file": "Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J..pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J..pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "btained in all female runners, and\nmale runners should be screened for\nmarkers of low testosterone (such as loss\nof morning erections). Youth runners in\nelevated risk categories should have\na medical workup for menstrual dysfunc-\ntion and impaired bo", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "and development as being negatively\nimpacted by low EA (34,36), so moni-\ntoring growth curves is important to\nensure both needs for growth and sports\nparticipation are being addressed.\nLow EA may occur with or without\ndisorders/disordered\nScreening for behaviors associated with\neating disorders/disordered eating is\nrecommended at the time of the Pre-\nparticipation Physical Evaluation (4,22),\nand appropriate nutrition should be\nmonitored by coaches, strength and\nconditioning professionals, and others\nwho interact with the youth athlete.\nWhile validated in older female athletes,\nthe Low Energy Availability in Females\nQuestionnaire was developed speciﬁ-\ncally to determine low EA and Triad\nrisk in athletes and focuses largely on\ninjuries and gastrointestinal and repro-\nductive function (32). A Triad cumula-\ntive risk assessment (Triad-CRA) (12)\nand RED-S screening tool (RED-S\nCAT) (35) have both been developed.\nAlthough studies in youth runners are\nlacking, in college runners designated\nmoderate- and high-risk Triad-CRA,\n50% and 71% of runners sustained\na bone stress injury (BSI) within a year,\nrespectively (55). These ﬁndings are\nconsistent with a previous report that\nsuggested cumulative Triad risk factors\nincrease the risk of stress fractures in\nadolescent female runners (57). In a sim-\nilar study of male collegiate runners,\n(excluding menstrual history) predicted\nincreased rates of BSI (25). The RED-S\nCAT has been less tested ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "btained in all female runners, and\nmale runners should be screened for\nmarkers of low testosterone (such as loss\nof morning erections). Youth runners in\nelevated risk categories should have\na medical workup for menstrual dysfunc-\ntion and impaired bo"}
{"chunk_id": "Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J._p0003_c0018", "source_registry_id": "src_fa5a70b54e46", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "44aa18e0455950b2", "text_span_hash": "44aa18e0455950b2", "source_file": "Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J..pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J..pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 18, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "tition\nprogram should take into account an\nindividual’s growth and development\nrather than chronological age.\nWHEN SHOULD A YOUTH RUNNER\nSTART COMPETING AND WHAT\nDISTANCES ARE APPROPRIATE?\nEvidence-based guidelines on appropri-\nate race distances by", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "and development as being negatively\nimpacted by low EA (34,36), so moni-\ntoring growth curves is important to\nensure both needs for growth and sports\nparticipation are being addressed.\nLow EA may occur with or without\ndisorders/disordered\nScreening for behaviors associated with\neating disorders/disordered eating is\nrecommended at the time of the Pre-\nparticipation Physical Evaluation (4,22),\nand appropriate nutrition should be\nmonitored by coaches, strength and\nconditioning professionals, and others\nwho interact with the youth athlete.\nWhile validated in older female athletes,\nthe Low Energy Availability in Females\nQuestionnaire was developed speciﬁ-\ncally to determine low EA and Triad\nrisk in athletes and focuses largely on\ninjuries and gastrointestinal and repro-\nductive function (32). A Triad cumula-\ntive risk assessment (Triad-CRA) (12)\nand RED-S screening tool (RED-S\nCAT) (35) have both been developed.\nAlthough studies in youth runners are\nlacking, in college runners designated\nmoderate- and high-risk Triad-CRA,\n50% and 71% of runners sustained\na bone stress injury (BSI) within a year,\nrespectively (55). These ﬁndings are\nconsistent with a previous report that\nsuggested cumulative Triad risk factors\nincrease the risk of stress fractures in\nadolescent female runners (57). In a sim-\nilar study of male collegiate runners,\n(excluding menstrual history) predicted\nincreased rates of BSI (25). The RED-S\nCAT has been less tested ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "tition\nprogram should take into account an\nindividual’s growth and development\nrather than chronological age.\nWHEN SHOULD A YOUTH RUNNER\nSTART COMPETING AND WHAT\nDISTANCES ARE APPROPRIATE?\nEvidence-based guidelines on appropri-\nate race distances by"}
