stained\na bone stress injury (BSI) within a year,\nrespectively (55). These ﬁndings are\nconsistent with a previous report that\nsuggested cumulative Triad risk factors\nincrease the risk of stress fractures in\nadolescent female runners (57). In a sim-\nilar study of male collegiate runners,\n(excluding menstrual history) predicted\nincreased rates of BSI (25). The RED-S\nCAT has been less tested ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "propri-\nate race distances by age are not available\nfor youth runners. Eligibility status for\nyouth runners competing in endurance\nevents, including half and full marathon\ndistances, is controversial. Counseling\nyoung runners and parents is currently"}
{"chunk_id": "Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J._p0003_c0020", "source_registry_id": "src_fa5a70b54e46", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f27cbd7bc6321a00", "text_span_hash": "f27cbd7bc6321a00", "source_file": "Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J..pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J..pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 20, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "nners and parents is currently\nbased on expert opinion and personal\nbias. Normal growth and development\nalong with psychological status must be\nconsidered in the decision for safe race\nLimited available reports suggest youth\nrunners can safely comple", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "and development as being negatively\nimpacted by low EA (34,36), so moni-\ntoring growth curves is important to\nensure both needs for growth and sports\nparticipation are being addressed.\nLow EA may occur with or without\ndisorders/disordered\nScreening for behaviors associated with\neating disorders/disordered eating is\nrecommended at the time of the Pre-\nparticipation Physical Evaluation (4,22),\nand appropriate nutrition should be\nmonitored by coaches, strength and\nconditioning professionals, and others\nwho interact with the youth athlete.\nWhile validated in older female athletes,\nthe Low Energy Availability in Females\nQuestionnaire was developed speciﬁ-\ncally to determine low EA and Triad\nrisk in athletes and focuses largely on\ninjuries and gastrointestinal and repro-\nductive function (32). A Triad cumula-\ntive risk assessment (Triad-CRA) (12)\nand RED-S screening tool (RED-S\nCAT) (35) have both been developed.\nAlthough studies in youth runners are\nlacking, in college runners designated\nmoderate- and high-risk Triad-CRA,\n50% and 71% of runners sustained\na bone stress injury (BSI) within a year,\nrespectively (55). These ﬁndings are\nconsistent with a previous report that\nsuggested cumulative Triad risk factors\nincrease the risk of stress fractures in\nadolescent female runners (57). In a sim-\nilar study of male collegiate runners,\n(excluding menstrual history) predicted\nincreased rates of BSI (25). The RED-S\nCAT has been less tested ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "nners and parents is currently\nbased on expert opinion and personal\nbias. Normal growth and development\nalong with psychological status must be\nconsidered in the decision for safe race\nLimited available reports suggest youth\nrunners can safely comple"}
{"chunk_id": "Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J._p0003_c0021", "source_registry_id": "src_fa5a70b54e46", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ad52cfb212651048", "text_span_hash": "ad52cfb212651048", "source_file": "Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J..pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J..pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 21, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "outh\nrunners can safely complete longer dis-\ntance races. Among 310 youth runners\nages 7–17 years who completed the Twin\nCities Marathon over 26 years, only 4\nrequired ﬁnish area medical assistance\nand all were for minor medical issues\n(49). Even in", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "and development as being negatively\nimpacted by low EA (34,36), so moni-\ntoring growth curves is important to\nensure both needs for growth and sports\nparticipation are being addressed.\nLow EA may occur with or without\ndisorders/disordered\nScreening for behaviors associated with\neating disorders/disordered eating is\nrecommended at the time of the Pre-\nparticipation Physical Evaluation (4,22),\nand appropriate nutrition should be\nmonitored by coaches, strength and\nconditioning professionals, and others\nwho interact with the youth athlete.\nWhile validated in older female athletes,\nthe Low Energy Availability in Females\nQuestionnaire was developed speciﬁ-\ncally to determine low EA and Triad\nrisk in athletes and focuses largely on\ninjuries and gastrointestinal and repro-\nductive function (32). A Triad cumula-\ntive risk assessment (Triad-CRA) (12)\nand RED-S screening tool (RED-S\nCAT) (35) have both been developed.\nAlthough studies in youth runners are\nlacking, in college runners designated\nmoderate- and high-risk Triad-CRA,\n50% and 71% of runners sustained\na bone stress injury (BSI) within a year,\nrespectively (55). These ﬁndings are\nconsistent with a previous report that\nsuggested cumulative Triad risk factors\nincrease the risk of stress fractures in\nadolescent female runners (57). In a sim-\nilar study of male collegiate runners,\n(excluding menstrual history) predicted\nincreased rates of BSI (25). The RED-S\nCAT has been less tested ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "outh\nrunners can safely complete longer dis-\ntance races. Among 310 youth runners\nages 7–17 years who completed the Twin\nCities Marathon over 26 years, only 4\nrequired ﬁnish area medical assistance\nand all were for minor medical issues\n(49). Even in"}
{"chunk_id": "Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J._p0003_c0022", "source_registry_id": "src_fa5a70b54e46", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "03b7657767a57dbe", "text_span_hash": "03b7657767a57dbe", "source_file": "Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J..pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J..pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 22, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "medical issues\n(49). Even in the heat of the 2007 Twin\nCities Marathon, none of the 20 runners\nyounger than 18 years required medical\nassistance, while a record number of adult\nrunners dropped out of the race or\nrequired medical care (48). From 2011", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "and development as being negatively\nimpacted by low EA (34,36), so moni-\ntoring growth curves is important to\nensure both needs for growth and sports\nparticipation are being addressed.\nLow EA may occur with or without\ndisorders/disordered\nScreening for behaviors associated with\neating disorders/disordered eating is\nrecommended at the time of the Pre-\nparticipation Physical Evaluation (4,22),\nand appropriate nutrition should be\nmonitored by coaches, strength and\nconditioning professionals, and others\nwho interact with the youth athlete.\nWhile validated in older female athletes,\nthe Low Energy Availability in Females\nQuestionnaire was developed speciﬁ-\ncally to determine low EA and Triad\nrisk in athletes and focuses largely on\ninjuries and gastrointestinal and repro-\nductive function (32). A Triad cumula-\ntive risk assessment (Triad-CRA) (12)\nand RED-S screening tool (RED-S\nCAT) (35) have both been developed.\nAlthough studies in youth runners are\nlacking, in college runners designated\nmoderate- and high-risk Triad-CRA,\n50% and 71% of runners sustained\na bone stress injury (BSI) within a year,\nrespectively (55). These ﬁndings are\nconsistent with a previous report that\nsuggested cumulative Triad risk factors\nincrease the risk of stress fractures in\nadolescent female runners (57). In a sim-\nilar study of male collegiate runners,\n(excluding menstrual history) predicted\nincreased rates of BSI (25). The RED-S\nCAT has been less tested ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "medical issues\n(49). Even in the heat of the 2007 Twin\nCities Marathon, none of the 20 runners\nyounger than 18 years required medical\nassistance, while a record number of adult\nrunners dropped out of the race or\nrequired medical care (48). From 2011"}
