ustained\na bone stress injury (BSI) within a year,\nrespectively (55). These ﬁndings are\nconsistent with a previous report that\nsuggested cumulative Triad risk factors\nincrease the risk of stress fractures in\nadolescent female runners (57). In a sim-\nilar study of male collegiate runners,\n(excluding menstrual history) predicted\nincreased rates of BSI (25). The RED-S\nCAT has been less tested ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ision with-\nout visible harm (7). The Students Run\nLA program (1989–2018) has had more\nthan 63,000 youth runners complete the\nmarathon distance with no reports of\nadverse outcomes (10).\nRecommendations. Self-motivated\nyouth runners may complete a giv"}
{"chunk_id": "Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J._p0003_c0027", "source_registry_id": "src_fa5a70b54e46", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "00fd051d029eee73", "text_span_hash": "00fd051d029eee73", "source_file": "Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J..pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J..pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 27, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "half and full marathon)\nshould not keep youth age group time\nrecords below age 18 years and should\nOptimizing the Health of Youth Runners\nVOLUME 42 | NUMBER 1 | FEBRUARY 2020\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditioning Association. Unauthorized r", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "and development as being negatively\nimpacted by low EA (34,36), so moni-\ntoring growth curves is important to\nensure both needs for growth and sports\nparticipation are being addressed.\nLow EA may occur with or without\ndisorders/disordered\nScreening for behaviors associated with\neating disorders/disordered eating is\nrecommended at the time of the Pre-\nparticipation Physical Evaluation (4,22),\nand appropriate nutrition should be\nmonitored by coaches, strength and\nconditioning professionals, and others\nwho interact with the youth athlete.\nWhile validated in older female athletes,\nthe Low Energy Availability in Females\nQuestionnaire was developed speciﬁ-\ncally to determine low EA and Triad\nrisk in athletes and focuses largely on\ninjuries and gastrointestinal and repro-\nductive function (32). A Triad cumula-\ntive risk assessment (Triad-CRA) (12)\nand RED-S screening tool (RED-S\nCAT) (35) have both been developed.\nAlthough studies in youth runners are\nlacking, in college runners designated\nmoderate- and high-risk Triad-CRA,\n50% and 71% of runners sustained\na bone stress injury (BSI) within a year,\nrespectively (55). These ﬁndings are\nconsistent with a previous report that\nsuggested cumulative Triad risk factors\nincrease the risk of stress fractures in\nadolescent female runners (57). In a sim-\nilar study of male collegiate runners,\n(excluding menstrual history) predicted\nincreased rates of BSI (25). The RED-S\nCAT has been less tested ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "half and full marathon)\nshould not keep youth age group time\nrecords below age 18 years and should\nOptimizing the Health of Youth Runners\nVOLUME 42 | NUMBER 1 | FEBRUARY 2020\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditioning Association. Unauthorized r"}
{"chunk_id": "Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J._p0004_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_fa5a70b54e46", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "d69da1c1ad7e0302", "text_span_hash": "d69da1c1ad7e0302", "source_file": "Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J..pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J..pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "dration are important to mon-\nitor in youth athletes during participation\nin long‐distance races, and race starts\nshortly after sunrise as advised to avoid\nthe heat of the day (47).\nRECOMMENDED FOR YOUTH\nHumans have been running for approx-\nimately 2", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "not publicize the participation of child or\nadolescent runners. Longer events should\nbe completed with an adult, and youth\nrunners should avoid large events where\nit is easy to “get lost in the crowd.” Nutri-\ntion and hydration are important to mon-\nitor in youth athletes during participation\nin long‐distance races, and race starts\nshortly after sunrise as advised to avoid\nthe heat of the day (47).\nRECOMMENDED FOR YOUTH\nHumans have been running for approx-\nimately 2 million years, with the ﬁrst\nevidence of footwear emerging 10,000\nyears ago (11). These early shoes were\nfabricated from sagebrush bark and com-\nprised a ﬂat sole for plantar protection\nwith straps to keep it on the foot. The\nhuman foot is a complex structure with\n26 bones and 33 articulations, each with\n6 degrees of freedom of motion. The foot\nhas more than 20 muscles, 10 of which\nare in the arch and provide support to\nthe foot during landing. This complex\nﬂexible structure allows the foot to be\nhighly adaptable, acting as a ﬂexible\nstructure to help attenuate load during\nweight acceptance, as well as a rigid lever\nfor push-off. Therefore, the foot is natu-\nrally mobile and very well adapted to\nwalk and run without footwear.\nModern shoes have become increasingly\nsupportive, reducing the demand on the\nfoot and reducing foot strength. Evidence\nto support this comes from populations\nthat do not use modern footwear. A\nstudy of 2,300 Indian children revealed\nthat habit", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "dration are important to mon-\nitor in youth athletes during participation\nin long‐distance races, and race starts\nshortly after sunrise as advised to avoid\nthe heat of the day (47).\nRECOMMENDED FOR YOUTH\nHumans have been running for approx-\nimately 2"}
{"chunk_id": "Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J._p0004_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_fa5a70b54e46", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "abbb42db8b4207be", "text_span_hash": "abbb42db8b4207be", "source_file": "Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J..pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Adam S. Tenforde, MD,1 William O. Roberts, MD, MS,2 Brian J..pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "wear changes the landing\nshown that running in cushioned shoes\nencourages a heel strike landing as\nopposed to landing on the ball of the foot\nas habitual barefoot runners do (26). The\nheel strike landing results in an abrupt\nincrease in forces transm", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "not publicize the participation of child or\nadolescent runners. Longer events should\nbe completed with an adult, and youth\nrunners should avoid large events where\nit is easy to “get lost in the crowd.” Nutri-\ntion and hydration are important to mon-\nitor in youth athletes during participation\nin long‐distance races, and race starts\nshortly after sunrise as advised to avoid\nthe heat of the day (47).\nRECOMMENDED FOR YOUTH\nHumans have been running for approx-\nimately 2 million years, with the ﬁrst\nevidence of footwear emerging 10,000\nyears ago (11). These early shoes were\nfabricated from sagebrush bark and com-\nprised a ﬂat sole for plantar protection\nwith straps to keep it on the foot. The\nhuman foot is a complex structure with\n26 bones and 33 articulations, each with\n6 degrees of freedom of motion. The foot\nhas more than 20 muscles, 10 of which\nare in the arch and provide support to\nthe foot during landing. This complex\nﬂexible structure allows the foot to be\nhighly adaptable, acting as a ﬂexible\nstructure to help attenuate load during\nweight acceptance, as well as a rigid lever\nfor push-off. Therefore, the foot is natu-\nrally mobile and very well adapted to\nwalk and run without footwear.\nModern shoes have become increasingly\nsupportive, reducing the demand on the\nfoot and reducing foot strength. Evidence\nto support this comes from populations\nthat do not use modern footwear. A\nstudy of 2,300 Indian children revealed\nthat habit", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "wear changes the landing\nshown that running in cushioned shoes\nencourages a heel strike landing as\nopposed to landing on the ball of the foot\nas habitual barefoot runners do (26). The\nheel strike landing results in an abrupt\nincrease in forces transm"}
