ceptance, as well as a rigid lever\nfor push-off. Therefore, the foot is natu-\nrally mobile and very well adapted to\nwalk and run without footwear.\nModern shoes have become increasingly\nsupportive, reducing the demand on the\nfoot and reducing foot strength. Evidence\nto support this comes from populations\nthat do not use modern footwear. A\nstudy of 2,300 Indian children revealed\nthat habit", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "hioning. Studies of adults\ntransitioning to minimal shoes in both\nwalking and running have demonstrated\nincreases in the size and strength of their\narch muscles (9,46). In addition, those\nhabituated to minimal shoes for running\nmay be less likely to"}
