f Youth Runners\nVOLUME 42 | NUMBER 1 | FEBRUARY 2020\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditioning Association. Unauthorized reproduction of this article is prohibited.", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ptimizing the Health of Youth Runners\nVOLUME 42 | NUMBER 1 | FEBRUARY 2020\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditioning Association. Unauthorized reproduction of this article is prohibited."}
{"chunk_id": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol_p0001_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_2e498eb97362", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8e68a9e1f64497c4", "text_span_hash": "8e68a9e1f64497c4", "source_file": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Memorial\nUniversity of Newfoundland, 230\nElizabeth Avenue, St. John's, NL A1C\nEmail: fbasset@mun.ca\nSelf-­talk is an effective mental training technique that has been shown to facili-\ntate or debilitate an athlete's performance, depending on its val", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "wileyonlinelibrary.com/journal/psyp\n© 2021 Society for Psychophysiological Research\nRevised: 9 November 2021  |\nO R I G I N A L A R T I C L E\nType of self-­talk matters: Its effects on perceived exertion,\ncardiorespiratory, and cortisol responses during an\niso-­metabolic endurance exercise\n1School of Human Kinetics and\nRecreation, Memorial University of\nNewfoundland, St. John's, Newfoundland,\n2Department of Physiology, Institute of\nBiological Sciences, Federal University of\nJuiz de Fora (UFJF), Juiz de Fora, Brazil\n3School of Health and Human Sciences,\nIndiana University, Indianapolis, Indiana,\nFabien A. Basset, School of Human\nKinetics and Recreation, Memorial\nUniversity of Newfoundland, 230\nElizabeth Avenue, St. John's, NL A1C\nEmail: fbasset@mun.ca\nSelf-­talk is an effective mental training technique that has been shown to facili-\ntate or debilitate an athlete's performance, depending on its valence. Although\nthe effects of self-­talk have been supported by observing change in sport per-\nformance, little is known about how self-­talk can induce physiological changes.\nSpecifically, it is important to understand if the type of self-­talk (positive, neutral,\nor negative) and can influence stress-­related parameters, such as perceived exer-\ntion, cardiorespiratory, and cortisol responses. The study's objective was there-\nfore to investigate the top-­down effect of positive and negative self-­talk compared\nto a dissociative activ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Memorial\nUniversity of Newfoundland, 230\nElizabeth Avenue, St. John's, NL A1C\nEmail: fbasset@mun.ca\nSelf-­talk is an effective mental training technique that has been shown to facili-\ntate or debilitate an athlete's performance, depending on its val"}
{"chunk_id": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol_p0001_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_2e498eb97362", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2c0b978f44c93e86", "text_span_hash": "2c0b978f44c93e86", "source_file": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "objective was there-\nfore to investigate the top-­down effect of positive and negative self-­talk compared\nto a dissociative activity during an iso-­metabolic running exercise on autonomic\nregulation of cardiorespiratory function. Twenty-­nine well-­", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "wileyonlinelibrary.com/journal/psyp\n© 2021 Society for Psychophysiological Research\nRevised: 9 November 2021  |\nO R I G I N A L A R T I C L E\nType of self-­talk matters: Its effects on perceived exertion,\ncardiorespiratory, and cortisol responses during an\niso-­metabolic endurance exercise\n1School of Human Kinetics and\nRecreation, Memorial University of\nNewfoundland, St. John's, Newfoundland,\n2Department of Physiology, Institute of\nBiological Sciences, Federal University of\nJuiz de Fora (UFJF), Juiz de Fora, Brazil\n3School of Health and Human Sciences,\nIndiana University, Indianapolis, Indiana,\nFabien A. Basset, School of Human\nKinetics and Recreation, Memorial\nUniversity of Newfoundland, 230\nElizabeth Avenue, St. John's, NL A1C\nEmail: fbasset@mun.ca\nSelf-­talk is an effective mental training technique that has been shown to facili-\ntate or debilitate an athlete's performance, depending on its valence. Although\nthe effects of self-­talk have been supported by observing change in sport per-\nformance, little is known about how self-­talk can induce physiological changes.\nSpecifically, it is important to understand if the type of self-­talk (positive, neutral,\nor negative) and can influence stress-­related parameters, such as perceived exer-\ntion, cardiorespiratory, and cortisol responses. The study's objective was there-\nfore to investigate the top-­down effect of positive and negative self-­talk compared\nto a dissociative activ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "objective was there-\nfore to investigate the top-­down effect of positive and negative self-­talk compared\nto a dissociative activity during an iso-­metabolic running exercise on autonomic\nregulation of cardiorespiratory function. Twenty-­nine well-­"}
