to understand if the type of self-­talk (positive, neutral,\nor negative) and can influence stress-­related parameters, such as perceived exer-\ntion, cardiorespiratory, and cortisol responses. The study's objective was there-\nfore to investigate the top-­down effect of positive and negative self-­talk compared\nto a dissociative activ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "elf-­talk and\ncreated three positive and three negative self-­talk statements. Finally, partici-\npants underwent a 60-­min steady-­state running exercise on a treadmill at 70% of\nV̇O2max, during which they were cued at 20-­, 35-­, and 50-­min with th"}
{"chunk_id": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol_p0001_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_2e498eb97362", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3444b7511b90d6b0", "text_span_hash": "3444b7511b90d6b0", "source_file": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "overtime during the 60-­min steady-­state running exercise, no significant main\ntreatment effect was found. However, RPE scores, minute ventilation, breathing\nfrequency, and salivary cortisol were significantly higher in the NST group com-\npared to t", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "wileyonlinelibrary.com/journal/psyp\n© 2021 Society for Psychophysiological Research\nRevised: 9 November 2021  |\nO R I G I N A L A R T I C L E\nType of self-­talk matters: Its effects on perceived exertion,\ncardiorespiratory, and cortisol responses during an\niso-­metabolic endurance exercise\n1School of Human Kinetics and\nRecreation, Memorial University of\nNewfoundland, St. John's, Newfoundland,\n2Department of Physiology, Institute of\nBiological Sciences, Federal University of\nJuiz de Fora (UFJF), Juiz de Fora, Brazil\n3School of Health and Human Sciences,\nIndiana University, Indianapolis, Indiana,\nFabien A. Basset, School of Human\nKinetics and Recreation, Memorial\nUniversity of Newfoundland, 230\nElizabeth Avenue, St. John's, NL A1C\nEmail: fbasset@mun.ca\nSelf-­talk is an effective mental training technique that has been shown to facili-\ntate or debilitate an athlete's performance, depending on its valence. Although\nthe effects of self-­talk have been supported by observing change in sport per-\nformance, little is known about how self-­talk can induce physiological changes.\nSpecifically, it is important to understand if the type of self-­talk (positive, neutral,\nor negative) and can influence stress-­related parameters, such as perceived exer-\ntion, cardiorespiratory, and cortisol responses. The study's objective was there-\nfore to investigate the top-­down effect of positive and negative self-­talk compared\nto a dissociative activ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "overtime during the 60-­min steady-­state running exercise, no significant main\ntreatment effect was found. However, RPE scores, minute ventilation, breathing\nfrequency, and salivary cortisol were significantly higher in the NST group com-\npared to t"}
{"chunk_id": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol_p0002_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_2e498eb97362", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0cf2a740681305e8", "text_span_hash": "0cf2a740681305e8", "source_file": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "tensity or duration and\nthe increased rate of perceived exertion (RPE). It includes\nthoughts about feelings and affects body monitoring, pain,\ncommand, instruction, and pace monitoring (Hardy, 2001;\nJohnson & Siegel, 1992; Masters & Ogles, 1998; St C", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Anyone who performed prolonged physical exercise has\nexperienced the inner conversation that encourages them\nto keep going or challenges their motives to cope with\nincreasing discomfort (Gammage et al., 2001). Self-­talk\nrefers to a syntactically recognizable articulation of an\ninternal position that can be expressed internally or out\nloud where the sender of the message is also the receiver\n(Conroy & Coatsworth, 2007; Raalte & Vincent, 2017).\nDuring a physical effort, self-­talk arises from a conscious\nperception of emotional and physiological cues relevant\nenough to create awareness about exercise-­induced stress\n(St Clair Gibson & Foster, 2007).\nSelf-­talk, a mental construct, contains various concep-\ntual elements (Raalte & Vincent, 2017) classified into five\nthemes: nature, structure, person, task instructions, and\nmiscellaneous (Hardy, 2001). The nature dimension re-\nfers to positive and negative self-­talk (e.g., “I can do this”\nand “No way I can do this,” respectively), while structure\nconcerns single words, phrases, or complete sentences; the\nperson dimension applies to self-­talk said in the first or sec-\nond person, the task instruction ascribes categories as skill-­\nspecific (e.g., “Shoot the ball”) or general (e.g., “Go faster”)\nand miscellaneous may include thoughts about work, ca-\nreer, personal problem-­solving or unintelligible inner chat-\nter (Aitchison et al., 2013; Hardy, 2001; Hardy et al., 2005).\nPrevious", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "tensity or duration and\nthe increased rate of perceived exertion (RPE). It includes\nthoughts about feelings and affects body monitoring, pain,\ncommand, instruction, and pace monitoring (Hardy, 2001;\nJohnson & Siegel, 1992; Masters & Ogles, 1998; St C"}
{"chunk_id": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol_p0002_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_2e498eb97362", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "356ec85748df7526", "text_span_hash": "356ec85748df7526", "source_file": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "2; Masters & Ogles, 1998; St Clair\nGibson & Foster, 2007). Associative thoughts occur during\nendurance exercises above 70% of maximal running speed\n(MAS) with RPE scores within 16–­20 using the 6–­20\nBorg's scale (Aitchison et al., 2013; Borg, 1982;", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Anyone who performed prolonged physical exercise has\nexperienced the inner conversation that encourages them\nto keep going or challenges their motives to cope with\nincreasing discomfort (Gammage et al., 2001). Self-­talk\nrefers to a syntactically recognizable articulation of an\ninternal position that can be expressed internally or out\nloud where the sender of the message is also the receiver\n(Conroy & Coatsworth, 2007; Raalte & Vincent, 2017).\nDuring a physical effort, self-­talk arises from a conscious\nperception of emotional and physiological cues relevant\nenough to create awareness about exercise-­induced stress\n(St Clair Gibson & Foster, 2007).\nSelf-­talk, a mental construct, contains various concep-\ntual elements (Raalte & Vincent, 2017) classified into five\nthemes: nature, structure, person, task instructions, and\nmiscellaneous (Hardy, 2001). The nature dimension re-\nfers to positive and negative self-­talk (e.g., “I can do this”\nand “No way I can do this,” respectively), while structure\nconcerns single words, phrases, or complete sentences; the\nperson dimension applies to self-­talk said in the first or sec-\nond person, the task instruction ascribes categories as skill-­\nspecific (e.g., “Shoot the ball”) or general (e.g., “Go faster”)\nand miscellaneous may include thoughts about work, ca-\nreer, personal problem-­solving or unintelligible inner chat-\nter (Aitchison et al., 2013; Hardy, 2001; Hardy et al., 2005).\nPrevious", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "2; Masters & Ogles, 1998; St Clair\nGibson & Foster, 2007). Associative thoughts occur during\nendurance exercises above 70% of maximal running speed\n(MAS) with RPE scores within 16–­20 using the 6–­20\nBorg's scale (Aitchison et al., 2013; Borg, 1982;"}
{"chunk_id": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol_p0002_c0022", "source_registry_id": "src_2e498eb97362", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "545e33ae7a850cad", "text_span_hash": "545e33ae7a850cad", "source_file": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 22, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "decrease\nvelocity (i.e., self-­paced) or tolerate or terminate the exercise\nsession (i.e., exhaustion) is controlled by the prefrontal cor-\ntex (PFC) through a bi-­directional mind/body integration\n(Robertson & Marino, 2020). That is, top-­down neura", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Anyone who performed prolonged physical exercise has\nexperienced the inner conversation that encourages them\nto keep going or challenges their motives to cope with\nincreasing discomfort (Gammage et al., 2001). Self-­talk\nrefers to a syntactically recognizable articulation of an\ninternal position that can be expressed internally or out\nloud where the sender of the message is also the receiver\n(Conroy & Coatsworth, 2007; Raalte & Vincent, 2017).\nDuring a physical effort, self-­talk arises from a conscious\nperception of emotional and physiological cues relevant\nenough to create awareness about exercise-­induced stress\n(St Clair Gibson & Foster, 2007).\nSelf-­talk, a mental construct, contains various concep-\ntual elements (Raalte & Vincent, 2017) classified into five\nthemes: nature, structure, person, task instructions, and\nmiscellaneous (Hardy, 2001). The nature dimension re-\nfers to positive and negative self-­talk (e.g., “I can do this”\nand “No way I can do this,” respectively), while structure\nconcerns single words, phrases, or complete sentences; the\nperson dimension applies to self-­talk said in the first or sec-\nond person, the task instruction ascribes categories as skill-­\nspecific (e.g., “Shoot the ball”) or general (e.g., “Go faster”)\nand miscellaneous may include thoughts about work, ca-\nreer, personal problem-­solving or unintelligible inner chat-\nter (Aitchison et al., 2013; Hardy, 2001; Hardy et al., 2005).\nPrevious", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "decrease\nvelocity (i.e., self-­paced) or tolerate or terminate the exercise\nsession (i.e., exhaustion) is controlled by the prefrontal cor-\ntex (PFC) through a bi-­directional mind/body integration\n(Robertson & Marino, 2020). That is, top-­down neura"}
{"chunk_id": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol_p0002_c0025", "source_registry_id": "src_2e498eb97362", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "96fe718003bb10b5", "text_span_hash": "96fe718003bb10b5", "source_file": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 25, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "nal brain/body\nframework, the interpretation of associative motivational\nself-­talk would increase the top-­down control of action,\nK E Y W O R D S\ncardiorespiratory response, cortisol, endurance running exercise, perceived exertion, self-­talk\n14698", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Anyone who performed prolonged physical exercise has\nexperienced the inner conversation that encourages them\nto keep going or challenges their motives to cope with\nincreasing discomfort (Gammage et al., 2001). Self-­talk\nrefers to a syntactically recognizable articulation of an\ninternal position that can be expressed internally or out\nloud where the sender of the message is also the receiver\n(Conroy & Coatsworth, 2007; Raalte & Vincent, 2017).\nDuring a physical effort, self-­talk arises from a conscious\nperception of emotional and physiological cues relevant\nenough to create awareness about exercise-­induced stress\n(St Clair Gibson & Foster, 2007).\nSelf-­talk, a mental construct, contains various concep-\ntual elements (Raalte & Vincent, 2017) classified into five\nthemes: nature, structure, person, task instructions, and\nmiscellaneous (Hardy, 2001). The nature dimension re-\nfers to positive and negative self-­talk (e.g., “I can do this”\nand “No way I can do this,” respectively), while structure\nconcerns single words, phrases, or complete sentences; the\nperson dimension applies to self-­talk said in the first or sec-\nond person, the task instruction ascribes categories as skill-­\nspecific (e.g., “Shoot the ball”) or general (e.g., “Go faster”)\nand miscellaneous may include thoughts about work, ca-\nreer, personal problem-­solving or unintelligible inner chat-\nter (Aitchison et al., 2013; Hardy, 2001; Hardy et al., 2005).\nPrevious", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "nal brain/body\nframework, the interpretation of associative motivational\nself-­talk would increase the top-­down control of action,\nK E Y W O R D S\ncardiorespiratory response, cortisol, endurance running exercise, perceived exertion, self-­talk\n14698"}
{"chunk_id": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol_p0003_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_2e498eb97362", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "05ddb8c1bff2d990", "text_span_hash": "05ddb8c1bff2d990", "source_file": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "fecting RPE, the\nself-­talk interpretation, cardiorespiratory response and self-­\npace (St Clair Gibson & Foster, 2007; St Clair Gibson et al.,\n2006; Williamson, 2010). In addition, induced dissociation\ncan alter the interplay between central motor d", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "physical effort, and the sympathetic drive (Bellomo et al.,\n2020; Hatzigeorgiadis et al., 2011) as physiological changes\ndue to the exercise stress convey information from the pe-\nriphery to the central nervous system affecting RPE, the\nself-­talk interpretation, cardiorespiratory response and self-­\npace (St Clair Gibson & Foster, 2007; St Clair Gibson et al.,\n2006; Williamson, 2010). In addition, induced dissociation\ncan alter the interplay between central motor drive, central\ncardiovascular command, and perceived exertion due to the\nlimited PFC capacity to process both the peripheral feed-\nback and the induced external stimulus during moderate-­\nto-­high exercise intensities (Fontes et al., 2020; Maddigan\nPrevious studies have investigated the interplay be-\ntween self-­talk and RPE during self-­paced time trials\n(Aitchison et al., 2013; Baden et al., 2004; Blanchfield\net al., 2014; Schomer & Connolly, 2002; St Clair Gibson\n& Foster, 2007). Self-­pace, however, is modulated through\nthe continuous processing of feedforward and feedback\ninformation (Baden et al., 2004; St Clair Gibson et al.,\n2006). In this context, the direction and weight of cause-­\nand-­effect events are difficult to unravel. Accordingly,\nthe current study applied a design intended to match the\nrelative exercise workload (iso-­metabolic/physiological\nstress) among experimental groups to control for the so-\nmatosensory feedback and enhance the top-­down effe", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "fecting RPE, the\nself-­talk interpretation, cardiorespiratory response and self-­\npace (St Clair Gibson & Foster, 2007; St Clair Gibson et al.,\n2006; Williamson, 2010). In addition, induced dissociation\ncan alter the interplay between central motor d"}
{"chunk_id": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol_p0003_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_2e498eb97362", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "6e9217292fca6089", "text_span_hash": "6e9217292fca6089", "source_file": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "­high exercise intensities (Fontes et al., 2020; Maddigan\nPrevious studies have investigated the interplay be-\ntween self-­talk and RPE during self-­paced time trials\n(Aitchison et al., 2013; Baden et al., 2004; Blanchfield\net al., 2014; Schomer & Co", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "physical effort, and the sympathetic drive (Bellomo et al.,\n2020; Hatzigeorgiadis et al., 2011) as physiological changes\ndue to the exercise stress convey information from the pe-\nriphery to the central nervous system affecting RPE, the\nself-­talk interpretation, cardiorespiratory response and self-­\npace (St Clair Gibson & Foster, 2007; St Clair Gibson et al.,\n2006; Williamson, 2010). In addition, induced dissociation\ncan alter the interplay between central motor drive, central\ncardiovascular command, and perceived exertion due to the\nlimited PFC capacity to process both the peripheral feed-\nback and the induced external stimulus during moderate-­\nto-­high exercise intensities (Fontes et al., 2020; Maddigan\nPrevious studies have investigated the interplay be-\ntween self-­talk and RPE during self-­paced time trials\n(Aitchison et al., 2013; Baden et al., 2004; Blanchfield\net al., 2014; Schomer & Connolly, 2002; St Clair Gibson\n& Foster, 2007). Self-­pace, however, is modulated through\nthe continuous processing of feedforward and feedback\ninformation (Baden et al., 2004; St Clair Gibson et al.,\n2006). In this context, the direction and weight of cause-­\nand-­effect events are difficult to unravel. Accordingly,\nthe current study applied a design intended to match the\nrelative exercise workload (iso-­metabolic/physiological\nstress) among experimental groups to control for the so-\nmatosensory feedback and enhance the top-­down effe", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "­high exercise intensities (Fontes et al., 2020; Maddigan\nPrevious studies have investigated the interplay be-\ntween self-­talk and RPE during self-­paced time trials\n(Aitchison et al., 2013; Baden et al., 2004; Blanchfield\net al., 2014; Schomer & Co"}
{"chunk_id": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol_p0003_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_2e498eb97362", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "90b9fad8e9bdb562", "text_span_hash": "90b9fad8e9bdb562", "source_file": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "eld\net al., 2014; Schomer & Connolly, 2002; St Clair Gibson\n& Foster, 2007). Self-­pace, however, is modulated through\nthe continuous processing of feedforward and feedback\ninformation (Baden et al., 2004; St Clair Gibson et al.,\n2006). In this conte", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "physical effort, and the sympathetic drive (Bellomo et al.,\n2020; Hatzigeorgiadis et al., 2011) as physiological changes\ndue to the exercise stress convey information from the pe-\nriphery to the central nervous system affecting RPE, the\nself-­talk interpretation, cardiorespiratory response and self-­\npace (St Clair Gibson & Foster, 2007; St Clair Gibson et al.,\n2006; Williamson, 2010). In addition, induced dissociation\ncan alter the interplay between central motor drive, central\ncardiovascular command, and perceived exertion due to the\nlimited PFC capacity to process both the peripheral feed-\nback and the induced external stimulus during moderate-­\nto-­high exercise intensities (Fontes et al., 2020; Maddigan\nPrevious studies have investigated the interplay be-\ntween self-­talk and RPE during self-­paced time trials\n(Aitchison et al., 2013; Baden et al., 2004; Blanchfield\net al., 2014; Schomer & Connolly, 2002; St Clair Gibson\n& Foster, 2007). Self-­pace, however, is modulated through\nthe continuous processing of feedforward and feedback\ninformation (Baden et al., 2004; St Clair Gibson et al.,\n2006). In this context, the direction and weight of cause-­\nand-­effect events are difficult to unravel. Accordingly,\nthe current study applied a design intended to match the\nrelative exercise workload (iso-­metabolic/physiological\nstress) among experimental groups to control for the so-\nmatosensory feedback and enhance the top-­down effe", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "eld\net al., 2014; Schomer & Connolly, 2002; St Clair Gibson\n& Foster, 2007). Self-­pace, however, is modulated through\nthe continuous processing of feedforward and feedback\ninformation (Baden et al., 2004; St Clair Gibson et al.,\n2006). In this conte"}
{"chunk_id": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol_p0003_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_2e498eb97362", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4d2f21fe3edb0bb0", "text_span_hash": "4d2f21fe3edb0bb0", "source_file": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "n et al.,\n2006). In this context, the direction and weight of cause-­\nand-­effect events are difficult to unravel. Accordingly,\nthe current study applied a design intended to match the\nrelative exercise workload (iso-­metabolic/physiological\nstress)", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "physical effort, and the sympathetic drive (Bellomo et al.,\n2020; Hatzigeorgiadis et al., 2011) as physiological changes\ndue to the exercise stress convey information from the pe-\nriphery to the central nervous system affecting RPE, the\nself-­talk interpretation, cardiorespiratory response and self-­\npace (St Clair Gibson & Foster, 2007; St Clair Gibson et al.,\n2006; Williamson, 2010). In addition, induced dissociation\ncan alter the interplay between central motor drive, central\ncardiovascular command, and perceived exertion due to the\nlimited PFC capacity to process both the peripheral feed-\nback and the induced external stimulus during moderate-­\nto-­high exercise intensities (Fontes et al., 2020; Maddigan\nPrevious studies have investigated the interplay be-\ntween self-­talk and RPE during self-­paced time trials\n(Aitchison et al., 2013; Baden et al., 2004; Blanchfield\net al., 2014; Schomer & Connolly, 2002; St Clair Gibson\n& Foster, 2007). Self-­pace, however, is modulated through\nthe continuous processing of feedforward and feedback\ninformation (Baden et al., 2004; St Clair Gibson et al.,\n2006). In this context, the direction and weight of cause-­\nand-­effect events are difficult to unravel. Accordingly,\nthe current study applied a design intended to match the\nrelative exercise workload (iso-­metabolic/physiological\nstress) among experimental groups to control for the so-\nmatosensory feedback and enhance the top-­down effe", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "n et al.,\n2006). In this context, the direction and weight of cause-­\nand-­effect events are difficult to unravel. Accordingly,\nthe current study applied a design intended to match the\nrelative exercise workload (iso-­metabolic/physiological\nstress)"}
{"chunk_id": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol_p0003_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_2e498eb97362", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e1ab765ec393c184", "text_span_hash": "e1ab765ec393c184", "source_file": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "The study's objective was to\ninvestigate the effect of associative positive and negative\nself-­talk compared to an induced dissociative activity\nduring a one-­hour steady-­state running exercise at 70%\nMAS. The exercise intensity was chosen as a “gr", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "physical effort, and the sympathetic drive (Bellomo et al.,\n2020; Hatzigeorgiadis et al., 2011) as physiological changes\ndue to the exercise stress convey information from the pe-\nriphery to the central nervous system affecting RPE, the\nself-­talk interpretation, cardiorespiratory response and self-­\npace (St Clair Gibson & Foster, 2007; St Clair Gibson et al.,\n2006; Williamson, 2010). In addition, induced dissociation\ncan alter the interplay between central motor drive, central\ncardiovascular command, and perceived exertion due to the\nlimited PFC capacity to process both the peripheral feed-\nback and the induced external stimulus during moderate-­\nto-­high exercise intensities (Fontes et al., 2020; Maddigan\nPrevious studies have investigated the interplay be-\ntween self-­talk and RPE during self-­paced time trials\n(Aitchison et al., 2013; Baden et al., 2004; Blanchfield\net al., 2014; Schomer & Connolly, 2002; St Clair Gibson\n& Foster, 2007). Self-­pace, however, is modulated through\nthe continuous processing of feedforward and feedback\ninformation (Baden et al., 2004; St Clair Gibson et al.,\n2006). In this context, the direction and weight of cause-­\nand-­effect events are difficult to unravel. Accordingly,\nthe current study applied a design intended to match the\nrelative exercise workload (iso-­metabolic/physiological\nstress) among experimental groups to control for the so-\nmatosensory feedback and enhance the top-­down effe", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "The study's objective was to\ninvestigate the effect of associative positive and negative\nself-­talk compared to an induced dissociative activity\nduring a one-­hour steady-­state running exercise at 70%\nMAS. The exercise intensity was chosen as a “gr"}
{"chunk_id": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol_p0003_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_2e498eb97362", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "00205e10ffd708a5", "text_span_hash": "00205e10ffd708a5", "source_file": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "udy was conducted to determine whether the associative\nnegative/positive self-­talk during a one-­hour steady-­state\nrunning exercise would impact the acute cardiorespiratory\nresponse, alter the perception of exertion and the HPA\nactivity compared to", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "physical effort, and the sympathetic drive (Bellomo et al.,\n2020; Hatzigeorgiadis et al., 2011) as physiological changes\ndue to the exercise stress convey information from the pe-\nriphery to the central nervous system affecting RPE, the\nself-­talk interpretation, cardiorespiratory response and self-­\npace (St Clair Gibson & Foster, 2007; St Clair Gibson et al.,\n2006; Williamson, 2010). In addition, induced dissociation\ncan alter the interplay between central motor drive, central\ncardiovascular command, and perceived exertion due to the\nlimited PFC capacity to process both the peripheral feed-\nback and the induced external stimulus during moderate-­\nto-­high exercise intensities (Fontes et al., 2020; Maddigan\nPrevious studies have investigated the interplay be-\ntween self-­talk and RPE during self-­paced time trials\n(Aitchison et al., 2013; Baden et al., 2004; Blanchfield\net al., 2014; Schomer & Connolly, 2002; St Clair Gibson\n& Foster, 2007). Self-­pace, however, is modulated through\nthe continuous processing of feedforward and feedback\ninformation (Baden et al., 2004; St Clair Gibson et al.,\n2006). In this context, the direction and weight of cause-­\nand-­effect events are difficult to unravel. Accordingly,\nthe current study applied a design intended to match the\nrelative exercise workload (iso-­metabolic/physiological\nstress) among experimental groups to control for the so-\nmatosensory feedback and enhance the top-­down effe", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "udy was conducted to determine whether the associative\nnegative/positive self-­talk during a one-­hour steady-­state\nrunning exercise would impact the acute cardiorespiratory\nresponse, alter the perception of exertion and the HPA\nactivity compared to"}
{"chunk_id": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol_p0003_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_2e498eb97362", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2acf219a6e774395", "text_span_hash": "2acf219a6e774395", "source_file": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "d the HPA\nactivity compared to the dissociative activity. Theoretical\nand experimental perspectives are discussed based on the\nexploratory analysis of the outcomes.\nTwenty-­nine well-­trained male runners volunteered to\nparticipate in the study. Part", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "physical effort, and the sympathetic drive (Bellomo et al.,\n2020; Hatzigeorgiadis et al., 2011) as physiological changes\ndue to the exercise stress convey information from the pe-\nriphery to the central nervous system affecting RPE, the\nself-­talk interpretation, cardiorespiratory response and self-­\npace (St Clair Gibson & Foster, 2007; St Clair Gibson et al.,\n2006; Williamson, 2010). In addition, induced dissociation\ncan alter the interplay between central motor drive, central\ncardiovascular command, and perceived exertion due to the\nlimited PFC capacity to process both the peripheral feed-\nback and the induced external stimulus during moderate-­\nto-­high exercise intensities (Fontes et al., 2020; Maddigan\nPrevious studies have investigated the interplay be-\ntween self-­talk and RPE during self-­paced time trials\n(Aitchison et al., 2013; Baden et al., 2004; Blanchfield\net al., 2014; Schomer & Connolly, 2002; St Clair Gibson\n& Foster, 2007). Self-­pace, however, is modulated through\nthe continuous processing of feedforward and feedback\ninformation (Baden et al., 2004; St Clair Gibson et al.,\n2006). In this context, the direction and weight of cause-­\nand-­effect events are difficult to unravel. Accordingly,\nthe current study applied a design intended to match the\nrelative exercise workload (iso-­metabolic/physiological\nstress) among experimental groups to control for the so-\nmatosensory feedback and enhance the top-­down effe", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "d the HPA\nactivity compared to the dissociative activity. Theoretical\nand experimental perspectives are discussed based on the\nexploratory analysis of the outcomes.\nTwenty-­nine well-­trained male runners volunteered to\nparticipate in the study. Part"}
{"chunk_id": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol_p0003_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_2e498eb97362", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1ae6d7cc8da361be", "text_span_hash": "1ae6d7cc8da361be", "source_file": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "participate in the study. Participants were recruited from a\nUniversity Cross-­Country running team and local running\nclubs. The mean age, height, and mass were 38 ± 13 years,\n76 ± 10 kg; and 36 ± 13 years, 178 ± 8 cm, and 69 ± 6 kg for\nthe negative", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "physical effort, and the sympathetic drive (Bellomo et al.,\n2020; Hatzigeorgiadis et al., 2011) as physiological changes\ndue to the exercise stress convey information from the pe-\nriphery to the central nervous system affecting RPE, the\nself-­talk interpretation, cardiorespiratory response and self-­\npace (St Clair Gibson & Foster, 2007; St Clair Gibson et al.,\n2006; Williamson, 2010). In addition, induced dissociation\ncan alter the interplay between central motor drive, central\ncardiovascular command, and perceived exertion due to the\nlimited PFC capacity to process both the peripheral feed-\nback and the induced external stimulus during moderate-­\nto-­high exercise intensities (Fontes et al., 2020; Maddigan\nPrevious studies have investigated the interplay be-\ntween self-­talk and RPE during self-­paced time trials\n(Aitchison et al., 2013; Baden et al., 2004; Blanchfield\net al., 2014; Schomer & Connolly, 2002; St Clair Gibson\n& Foster, 2007). Self-­pace, however, is modulated through\nthe continuous processing of feedforward and feedback\ninformation (Baden et al., 2004; St Clair Gibson et al.,\n2006). In this context, the direction and weight of cause-­\nand-­effect events are difficult to unravel. Accordingly,\nthe current study applied a design intended to match the\nrelative exercise workload (iso-­metabolic/physiological\nstress) among experimental groups to control for the so-\nmatosensory feedback and enhance the top-­down effe", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "participate in the study. Participants were recruited from a\nUniversity Cross-­Country running team and local running\nclubs. The mean age, height, and mass were 38 ± 13 years,\n76 ± 10 kg; and 36 ± 13 years, 178 ± 8 cm, and 69 ± 6 kg for\nthe negative"}
{"chunk_id": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol_p0003_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_2e498eb97362", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "fb2cff24e48e2932", "text_span_hash": "fb2cff24e48e2932", "source_file": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ts' characteristics and training profiles\nare shown in Table 1. Participants were fully informed of\nthe study procedures and provided consent before partici-\npating in accordance with Memorial University's Human\nInvestigation Committee (HIC) regulati", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "physical effort, and the sympathetic drive (Bellomo et al.,\n2020; Hatzigeorgiadis et al., 2011) as physiological changes\ndue to the exercise stress convey information from the pe-\nriphery to the central nervous system affecting RPE, the\nself-­talk interpretation, cardiorespiratory response and self-­\npace (St Clair Gibson & Foster, 2007; St Clair Gibson et al.,\n2006; Williamson, 2010). In addition, induced dissociation\ncan alter the interplay between central motor drive, central\ncardiovascular command, and perceived exertion due to the\nlimited PFC capacity to process both the peripheral feed-\nback and the induced external stimulus during moderate-­\nto-­high exercise intensities (Fontes et al., 2020; Maddigan\nPrevious studies have investigated the interplay be-\ntween self-­talk and RPE during self-­paced time trials\n(Aitchison et al., 2013; Baden et al., 2004; Blanchfield\net al., 2014; Schomer & Connolly, 2002; St Clair Gibson\n& Foster, 2007). Self-­pace, however, is modulated through\nthe continuous processing of feedforward and feedback\ninformation (Baden et al., 2004; St Clair Gibson et al.,\n2006). In this context, the direction and weight of cause-­\nand-­effect events are difficult to unravel. Accordingly,\nthe current study applied a design intended to match the\nrelative exercise workload (iso-­metabolic/physiological\nstress) among experimental groups to control for the so-\nmatosensory feedback and enhance the top-­down effe", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ts' characteristics and training profiles\nare shown in Table 1. Participants were fully informed of\nthe study procedures and provided consent before partici-\npating in accordance with Memorial University's Human\nInvestigation Committee (HIC) regulati"}
