his context, the direction and weight of cause-­\nand-­effect events are difficult to unravel. Accordingly,\nthe current study applied a design intended to match the\nrelative exercise workload (iso-­metabolic/physiological\nstress) among experimental groups to control for the so-\nmatosensory feedback and enhance the top-­down effe", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ey were\nalso screened for the following conditions: prescribed\nAthletes' characteristics and training profile\n14698986, 2022, 3, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/psyp.13980 by Egyptian National Sti. Network (Enstinet), Wile"}
{"chunk_id": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol_p0004_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_2e498eb97362", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "865298b609a5904a", "text_span_hash": "865298b609a5904a", "source_file": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "eived a mental training session on self-­talk delivered by\nthe investigator. The session overviewed research findings\non how the psychological effects of positive and negative\nself-­talk can affect performance across various sports, in-\ncluding runni", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "medication, exercise-­induced fatigue, blood pressure\nabove 140 mmHg, and injury. The presence of screened\nconditions led to testing postponement or dismissal from\nDuring session three, participants were randomly as-\nreceived a mental training session on self-­talk delivered by\nthe investigator. The session overviewed research findings\non how the psychological effects of positive and negative\nself-­talk can affect performance across various sports, in-\ncluding running. In addition, this session discussed how\nthe magnitude effect on performance could depend on\nthe intensity of the self-­talk (e.g., slightly encouraging/\ndiscouraging, moderately encouraging/discouraging, and\nhighly encouraging/discouraging). At the end of the 40-­\nmin session, the participants were requested to create\ntheir self-­talk statements in three categories: (i) slightly\nencouraging self-­talk (level 1), (ii) moderately encourag-\ning self-­talk (level 2), and (iii) highly encouraging self-­talk\n(level 3). To create the “slightly encouraging self-­talk”,\nparticipants were asked to write helpful statements that\nwould give them a slight boost at the start of a race. To cre-\nate “moderately encouraging self-­talk”, participants were\nasked to write helpful statements they would use half-\nway through a race when feeling moderately exhausted.\nFinally, to create “highly encouraging self-­talk”, the partic-\nipants were asked to create helpful statements they woul", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "eived a mental training session on self-­talk delivered by\nthe investigator. The session overviewed research findings\non how the psychological effects of positive and negative\nself-­talk can affect performance across various sports, in-\ncluding runni"}
{"chunk_id": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol_p0004_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_2e498eb97362", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c237d25389609190", "text_span_hash": "c237d25389609190", "source_file": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ious sports, in-\ncluding running. In addition, this session discussed how\nthe magnitude effect on performance could depend on\nthe intensity of the self-­talk (e.g., slightly encouraging/\ndiscouraging, moderately encouraging/discouraging, and\nhighly e", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "medication, exercise-­induced fatigue, blood pressure\nabove 140 mmHg, and injury. The presence of screened\nconditions led to testing postponement or dismissal from\nDuring session three, participants were randomly as-\nreceived a mental training session on self-­talk delivered by\nthe investigator. The session overviewed research findings\non how the psychological effects of positive and negative\nself-­talk can affect performance across various sports, in-\ncluding running. In addition, this session discussed how\nthe magnitude effect on performance could depend on\nthe intensity of the self-­talk (e.g., slightly encouraging/\ndiscouraging, moderately encouraging/discouraging, and\nhighly encouraging/discouraging). At the end of the 40-­\nmin session, the participants were requested to create\ntheir self-­talk statements in three categories: (i) slightly\nencouraging self-­talk (level 1), (ii) moderately encourag-\ning self-­talk (level 2), and (iii) highly encouraging self-­talk\n(level 3). To create the “slightly encouraging self-­talk”,\nparticipants were asked to write helpful statements that\nwould give them a slight boost at the start of a race. To cre-\nate “moderately encouraging self-­talk”, participants were\nasked to write helpful statements they would use half-\nway through a race when feeling moderately exhausted.\nFinally, to create “highly encouraging self-­talk”, the partic-\nipants were asked to create helpful statements they woul", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ious sports, in-\ncluding running. In addition, this session discussed how\nthe magnitude effect on performance could depend on\nthe intensity of the self-­talk (e.g., slightly encouraging/\ndiscouraging, moderately encouraging/discouraging, and\nhighly e"}
{"chunk_id": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol_p0004_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_2e498eb97362", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f5fc801a631914f6", "text_span_hash": "f5fc801a631914f6", "source_file": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "atements (see Supporting Information for examples).\nThese statements were later cued to participants during\nthe steady-­state running exercise at 70% MAS.\nDuring sessions four and five, all participants were re-\nquested to run for one-­hour at 70% MA", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "medication, exercise-­induced fatigue, blood pressure\nabove 140 mmHg, and injury. The presence of screened\nconditions led to testing postponement or dismissal from\nDuring session three, participants were randomly as-\nreceived a mental training session on self-­talk delivered by\nthe investigator. The session overviewed research findings\non how the psychological effects of positive and negative\nself-­talk can affect performance across various sports, in-\ncluding running. In addition, this session discussed how\nthe magnitude effect on performance could depend on\nthe intensity of the self-­talk (e.g., slightly encouraging/\ndiscouraging, moderately encouraging/discouraging, and\nhighly encouraging/discouraging). At the end of the 40-­\nmin session, the participants were requested to create\ntheir self-­talk statements in three categories: (i) slightly\nencouraging self-­talk (level 1), (ii) moderately encourag-\ning self-­talk (level 2), and (iii) highly encouraging self-­talk\n(level 3). To create the “slightly encouraging self-­talk”,\nparticipants were asked to write helpful statements that\nwould give them a slight boost at the start of a race. To cre-\nate “moderately encouraging self-­talk”, participants were\nasked to write helpful statements they would use half-\nway through a race when feeling moderately exhausted.\nFinally, to create “highly encouraging self-­talk”, the partic-\nipants were asked to create helpful statements they woul", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "atements (see Supporting Information for examples).\nThese statements were later cued to participants during\nthe steady-­state running exercise at 70% MAS.\nDuring sessions four and five, all participants were re-\nquested to run for one-­hour at 70% MA"}
{"chunk_id": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol_p0004_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_2e498eb97362", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5385d9ccc357591b", "text_span_hash": "5385d9ccc357591b", "source_file": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "to run for one-­hour at 70% MAS determined from\nthe participant's incremental test results and monitored by\na GPS-­enabled sports watch (Model Forerunner 205/305,\nGarmin Ltd, Kansas City, TX) along with practicing positive\nself-­talk statements. Part", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "medication, exercise-­induced fatigue, blood pressure\nabove 140 mmHg, and injury. The presence of screened\nconditions led to testing postponement or dismissal from\nDuring session three, participants were randomly as-\nreceived a mental training session on self-­talk delivered by\nthe investigator. The session overviewed research findings\non how the psychological effects of positive and negative\nself-­talk can affect performance across various sports, in-\ncluding running. In addition, this session discussed how\nthe magnitude effect on performance could depend on\nthe intensity of the self-­talk (e.g., slightly encouraging/\ndiscouraging, moderately encouraging/discouraging, and\nhighly encouraging/discouraging). At the end of the 40-­\nmin session, the participants were requested to create\ntheir self-­talk statements in three categories: (i) slightly\nencouraging self-­talk (level 1), (ii) moderately encourag-\ning self-­talk (level 2), and (iii) highly encouraging self-­talk\n(level 3). To create the “slightly encouraging self-­talk”,\nparticipants were asked to write helpful statements that\nwould give them a slight boost at the start of a race. To cre-\nate “moderately encouraging self-­talk”, participants were\nasked to write helpful statements they would use half-\nway through a race when feeling moderately exhausted.\nFinally, to create “highly encouraging self-­talk”, the partic-\nipants were asked to create helpful statements they woul", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "to run for one-­hour at 70% MAS determined from\nthe participant's incremental test results and monitored by\na GPS-­enabled sports watch (Model Forerunner 205/305,\nGarmin Ltd, Kansas City, TX) along with practicing positive\nself-­talk statements. Part"}
{"chunk_id": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol_p0004_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_2e498eb97362", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b4d21c4e3a24f34c", "text_span_hash": "b4d21c4e3a24f34c", "source_file": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ve\nself-­talk statements. Participants assigned to the negative\ngroup were also asked to practice associative thoughts with\npositive self-­talk statements because it represents a state of\nmind coherent with moderate steady-­state running exercise\nwit", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "medication, exercise-­induced fatigue, blood pressure\nabove 140 mmHg, and injury. The presence of screened\nconditions led to testing postponement or dismissal from\nDuring session three, participants were randomly as-\nreceived a mental training session on self-­talk delivered by\nthe investigator. The session overviewed research findings\non how the psychological effects of positive and negative\nself-­talk can affect performance across various sports, in-\ncluding running. In addition, this session discussed how\nthe magnitude effect on performance could depend on\nthe intensity of the self-­talk (e.g., slightly encouraging/\ndiscouraging, moderately encouraging/discouraging, and\nhighly encouraging/discouraging). At the end of the 40-­\nmin session, the participants were requested to create\ntheir self-­talk statements in three categories: (i) slightly\nencouraging self-­talk (level 1), (ii) moderately encourag-\ning self-­talk (level 2), and (iii) highly encouraging self-­talk\n(level 3). To create the “slightly encouraging self-­talk”,\nparticipants were asked to write helpful statements that\nwould give them a slight boost at the start of a race. To cre-\nate “moderately encouraging self-­talk”, participants were\nasked to write helpful statements they would use half-\nway through a race when feeling moderately exhausted.\nFinally, to create “highly encouraging self-­talk”, the partic-\nipants were asked to create helpful statements they woul", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ve\nself-­talk statements. Participants assigned to the negative\ngroup were also asked to practice associative thoughts with\npositive self-­talk statements because it represents a state of\nmind coherent with moderate steady-­state running exercise\nwit"}
{"chunk_id": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol_p0004_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_2e498eb97362", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7e3c89f779535731", "text_span_hash": "7e3c89f779535731", "source_file": "Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Basset_2022_SelfTalk_Cortisol_Psychophysiol.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "dy-­state running exercise\nwithin RPE scores of 11–­15 with no pressure for perfor-\nmance enhancement (Aitchison et al., 2013). The aim was\nto practice associative self-­talk per se during a mild exercise\nintensity and volume performed by experienced", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "medication, exercise-­induced fatigue, blood pressure\nabove 140 mmHg, and injury. The presence of screened\nconditions led to testing postponement or dismissal from\nDuring session three, participants were randomly as-\nreceived a mental training session on self-­talk delivered by\nthe investigator. The session overviewed research findings\non how the psychological effects of positive and negative\nself-­talk can affect performance across various sports, in-\ncluding running. In addition, this session discussed how\nthe magnitude effect on performance could depend on\nthe intensity of the self-­talk (e.g., slightly encouraging/\ndiscouraging, moderately encouraging/discouraging, and\nhighly encouraging/discouraging). At the end of the 40-­\nmin session, the participants were requested to create\ntheir self-­talk statements in three categories: (i) slightly\nencouraging self-­talk (level 1), (ii) moderately encourag-\ning self-­talk (level 2), and (iii) highly encouraging self-­talk\n(level 3). To create the “slightly encouraging self-­talk”,\nparticipants were asked to write helpful statements that\nwould give them a slight boost at the start of a race. To cre-\nate “moderately encouraging self-­talk”, participants were\nasked to write helpful statements they would use half-\nway through a race when feeling moderately exhausted.\nFinally, to create “highly encouraging self-­talk”, the partic-\nipants were asked to create helpful statements they woul", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "dy-­state running exercise\nwithin RPE scores of 11–­15 with no pressure for perfor-\nmance enhancement (Aitchison et al., 2013). The aim was\nto practice associative self-­talk per se during a mild exercise\nintensity and volume performed by experienced"}
