effects on perceived exertion,\ncardiorespiratory, and cortisol responses during an\niso-­metabolic endurance exercise. Psychophysiology,\n14698986, 2022, 3, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/psyp.13980 by Egyptian National Sti. Network (Enstinet), Wiley Online Library on [05/11/2022]. See the Terms and Co", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "-­talk\nmatters: Its effects on perceived exertion,\ncardiorespiratory, and cortisol responses during an\niso-­metabolic endurance exercise. Psychophysiology,\n14698986, 2022, 3, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/psyp.13980 by E"}
{"chunk_id": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2_p0001_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_bcbdb149e4c9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9a99f0aa4da150a0", "text_span_hash": "9a99f0aa4da150a0", "source_file": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "and can have a profound impact on\nperformance. This review deﬁnes hot\nweather in terms of running perfor-\nmance, details the physiology\nunderlying heat-related performance\ndecrements, and discusses strate-\ngies that may mitigate the negative\nimpact", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Running in the Heat:\nStrategies to Prepare for\nBrett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2\n1Department of Sport and Movement Science, Salem State University, Salem, Massachusetts; and 2Department of\nHuman Physiology, University of Oregon, Eugene, Oregon\nA B S T R A C T\nCompetitive runners seek to control\nas many performance variables as\npossible when preparing for peak\ncompetition. However, race day\nweather is out of an athlete’s control\nand can have a profound impact on\nperformance. This review deﬁnes hot\nweather in terms of running perfor-\nmance, details the physiology\nunderlying heat-related performance\ndecrements, and discusses strate-\ngies that may mitigate the negative\nimpact of heat on cardiovascular\nstrain and attenuate reductions in\nheat-related running performance.\nlassic laboratory-based studies\noften subject athletes to envi-\nbetween 35 and 458C with varying\nhumidity to examine the impact of\nsponses and exercise performance.\nThe wet bulb globe temperature\n(WBGT; an index that accounts for\nair temperature, humidity, solar radi-\nation, and wind speed) of these lab-\noratory conditions is often at or\nabove 32.28C. However, The Event\nAlert System (Road Runners Club\nof America) recommends road race\ncancellation in “hot” environmental\nconditions (WBGT . 32.28C), and\na recent analysis of medical events\nat the Twin Cities marathon sug-\ngested that running races should be\ncanceled when starting weather is\nwarm (WBGT . 228C) t", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "and can have a profound impact on\nperformance. This review deﬁnes hot\nweather in terms of running perfor-\nmance, details the physiology\nunderlying heat-related performance\ndecrements, and discusses strate-\ngies that may mitigate the negative\nimpact"}
{"chunk_id": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2_p0001_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_bcbdb149e4c9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f568184b0db43591", "text_span_hash": "f568184b0db43591", "source_file": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "onditions is often at or\nabove 32.28C. However, The Event\nAlert System (Road Runners Club\nof America) recommends road race\ncancellation in “hot” environmental\nconditions (WBGT . 32.28C), and\na recent analysis of medical events\nat the Twin Cities mara", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Running in the Heat:\nStrategies to Prepare for\nBrett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2\n1Department of Sport and Movement Science, Salem State University, Salem, Massachusetts; and 2Department of\nHuman Physiology, University of Oregon, Eugene, Oregon\nA B S T R A C T\nCompetitive runners seek to control\nas many performance variables as\npossible when preparing for peak\ncompetition. However, race day\nweather is out of an athlete’s control\nand can have a profound impact on\nperformance. This review deﬁnes hot\nweather in terms of running perfor-\nmance, details the physiology\nunderlying heat-related performance\ndecrements, and discusses strate-\ngies that may mitigate the negative\nimpact of heat on cardiovascular\nstrain and attenuate reductions in\nheat-related running performance.\nlassic laboratory-based studies\noften subject athletes to envi-\nbetween 35 and 458C with varying\nhumidity to examine the impact of\nsponses and exercise performance.\nThe wet bulb globe temperature\n(WBGT; an index that accounts for\nair temperature, humidity, solar radi-\nation, and wind speed) of these lab-\noratory conditions is often at or\nabove 32.28C. However, The Event\nAlert System (Road Runners Club\nof America) recommends road race\ncancellation in “hot” environmental\nconditions (WBGT . 32.28C), and\na recent analysis of medical events\nat the Twin Cities marathon sug-\ngested that running races should be\ncanceled when starting weather is\nwarm (WBGT . 228C) t", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "onditions is often at or\nabove 32.28C. However, The Event\nAlert System (Road Runners Club\nof America) recommends road race\ncancellation in “hot” environmental\nconditions (WBGT . 32.28C), and\na recent analysis of medical events\nat the Twin Cities mara"}
