\nthat optimal performance conditions\nfor distance running are compara-\nA common weather parameter of fast\nrunning events, including marathons,\nis a relatively cool environmental air\ntemperature of approximately ;128C\ncreases in air temperature beyond\n;128C lead to a progressive slowing\nof performance in elite, competitive,\nand casual ru", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "not match\nreal-world performance due to lower\nrates of convective cooling on a sta-\ntionary cycle ergometer or treadmill\nas compared to over-ground running,\na series of analyses in marathon per-\nformance further supports the notion\nthat optimal perf"}
{"chunk_id": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2_p0002_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_bcbdb149e4c9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "926d397df86bf458", "text_span_hash": "926d397df86bf458", "source_file": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "s the notion\nthat optimal performance conditions\nfor distance running are compara-\nA common weather parameter of fast\nrunning events, including marathons,\nis a relatively cool environmental air\ntemperature of approximately ;128C\ncreases in air temper", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "because of the metabolic heat produc-\ntion of high-intensity running, it is not\nuncommon for core temperature to\nrise 28C or more during half marathon\nor marathon competitions (5,9,38,52),\neven in relatively cool conditions. For\nexample, the running pace of mara-\nthon world record (2:01:39 by Eliud\nKipchoge) results in a metabolic heat\nwould require almost 5 L of sweat\nevaporation over the course of the\nmarathon to maintain thermal equilib-\nrium (assuming no heat is lost through\nR and C) (21). Given this substantial\ncore temperature rise and evaporative\nrequirement during running competi-\ntion, it is unsurprising that perfor-\nwould not generally be considered\nheat stress. Laboratory studies exam-\na reduction in endurance at environ-\nmental temperatures as low as ;208C,\nwell below the conditions traditionally\nused in heat stress literature. While\nlaboratory studies may not match\nreal-world performance due to lower\nrates of convective cooling on a sta-\ntionary cycle ergometer or treadmill\nas compared to over-ground running,\na series of analyses in marathon per-\nformance further supports the notion\nthat optimal performance conditions\nfor distance running are compara-\nA common weather parameter of fast\nrunning events, including marathons,\nis a relatively cool environmental air\ntemperature of approximately ;128C\ncreases in air temperature beyond\n;128C lead to a progressive slowing\nof performance in elite, competitive,\nand casual ru", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "s the notion\nthat optimal performance conditions\nfor distance running are compara-\nA common weather parameter of fast\nrunning events, including marathons,\nis a relatively cool environmental air\ntemperature of approximately ;128C\ncreases in air temper"}
{"chunk_id": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2_p0002_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_bcbdb149e4c9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "cdae9f77f033f7d8", "text_span_hash": "cdae9f77f033f7d8", "source_file": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ly ;128C\ncreases in air temperature beyond\n;128C lead to a progressive slowing\nof performance in elite, competitive,\nand casual runners with the slowest\nrunners experiencing a greater reduc-\ntion in performance (19,30,62). Outside\nof performance vari", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "because of the metabolic heat produc-\ntion of high-intensity running, it is not\nuncommon for core temperature to\nrise 28C or more during half marathon\nor marathon competitions (5,9,38,52),\neven in relatively cool conditions. For\nexample, the running pace of mara-\nthon world record (2:01:39 by Eliud\nKipchoge) results in a metabolic heat\nwould require almost 5 L of sweat\nevaporation over the course of the\nmarathon to maintain thermal equilib-\nrium (assuming no heat is lost through\nR and C) (21). Given this substantial\ncore temperature rise and evaporative\nrequirement during running competi-\ntion, it is unsurprising that perfor-\nwould not generally be considered\nheat stress. Laboratory studies exam-\na reduction in endurance at environ-\nmental temperatures as low as ;208C,\nwell below the conditions traditionally\nused in heat stress literature. While\nlaboratory studies may not match\nreal-world performance due to lower\nrates of convective cooling on a sta-\ntionary cycle ergometer or treadmill\nas compared to over-ground running,\na series of analyses in marathon per-\nformance further supports the notion\nthat optimal performance conditions\nfor distance running are compara-\nA common weather parameter of fast\nrunning events, including marathons,\nis a relatively cool environmental air\ntemperature of approximately ;128C\ncreases in air temperature beyond\n;128C lead to a progressive slowing\nof performance in elite, competitive,\nand casual ru", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ly ;128C\ncreases in air temperature beyond\n;128C lead to a progressive slowing\nof performance in elite, competitive,\nand casual runners with the slowest\nrunners experiencing a greater reduc-\ntion in performance (19,30,62). Outside\nof performance vari"}
{"chunk_id": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2_p0002_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_bcbdb149e4c9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b9c8e499fb8e9551", "text_span_hash": "b9c8e499fb8e9551", "source_file": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "f diminished endurance\nor work capacity in the heat as a con-\nsequence of elevated core temperature\nwas ﬁrmly established after several\nFigure 1. Nomogram presenting potential performance decrements (y axis) based on\nprojected marathon ﬁnishing time", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "because of the metabolic heat produc-\ntion of high-intensity running, it is not\nuncommon for core temperature to\nrise 28C or more during half marathon\nor marathon competitions (5,9,38,52),\neven in relatively cool conditions. For\nexample, the running pace of mara-\nthon world record (2:01:39 by Eliud\nKipchoge) results in a metabolic heat\nwould require almost 5 L of sweat\nevaporation over the course of the\nmarathon to maintain thermal equilib-\nrium (assuming no heat is lost through\nR and C) (21). Given this substantial\ncore temperature rise and evaporative\nrequirement during running competi-\ntion, it is unsurprising that perfor-\nwould not generally be considered\nheat stress. Laboratory studies exam-\na reduction in endurance at environ-\nmental temperatures as low as ;208C,\nwell below the conditions traditionally\nused in heat stress literature. While\nlaboratory studies may not match\nreal-world performance due to lower\nrates of convective cooling on a sta-\ntionary cycle ergometer or treadmill\nas compared to over-ground running,\na series of analyses in marathon per-\nformance further supports the notion\nthat optimal performance conditions\nfor distance running are compara-\nA common weather parameter of fast\nrunning events, including marathons,\nis a relatively cool environmental air\ntemperature of approximately ;128C\ncreases in air temperature beyond\n;128C lead to a progressive slowing\nof performance in elite, competitive,\nand casual ru", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "f diminished endurance\nor work capacity in the heat as a con-\nsequence of elevated core temperature\nwas ﬁrmly established after several\nFigure 1. Nomogram presenting potential performance decrements (y axis) based on\nprojected marathon ﬁnishing time"}
{"chunk_id": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2_p0002_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_bcbdb149e4c9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b77095b7c2103cbb", "text_span_hash": "b77095b7c2103cbb", "source_file": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "jected marathon ﬁnishing time (2:10–3:00 hours) with increasing WBGT\ntemperature. A WBGT of 5–108C represents the prevailing environmental\nconditions for the fastest performances for runners of all abilities. As the\nenvironmental conditions warm, per", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "because of the metabolic heat produc-\ntion of high-intensity running, it is not\nuncommon for core temperature to\nrise 28C or more during half marathon\nor marathon competitions (5,9,38,52),\neven in relatively cool conditions. For\nexample, the running pace of mara-\nthon world record (2:01:39 by Eliud\nKipchoge) results in a metabolic heat\nwould require almost 5 L of sweat\nevaporation over the course of the\nmarathon to maintain thermal equilib-\nrium (assuming no heat is lost through\nR and C) (21). Given this substantial\ncore temperature rise and evaporative\nrequirement during running competi-\ntion, it is unsurprising that perfor-\nwould not generally be considered\nheat stress. Laboratory studies exam-\na reduction in endurance at environ-\nmental temperatures as low as ;208C,\nwell below the conditions traditionally\nused in heat stress literature. While\nlaboratory studies may not match\nreal-world performance due to lower\nrates of convective cooling on a sta-\ntionary cycle ergometer or treadmill\nas compared to over-ground running,\na series of analyses in marathon per-\nformance further supports the notion\nthat optimal performance conditions\nfor distance running are compara-\nA common weather parameter of fast\nrunning events, including marathons,\nis a relatively cool environmental air\ntemperature of approximately ;128C\ncreases in air temperature beyond\n;128C lead to a progressive slowing\nof performance in elite, competitive,\nand casual ru", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "jected marathon ﬁnishing time (2:10–3:00 hours) with increasing WBGT\ntemperature. A WBGT of 5–108C represents the prevailing environmental\nconditions for the fastest performances for runners of all abilities. As the\nenvironmental conditions warm, per"}
{"chunk_id": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2_p0002_c0016", "source_registry_id": "src_bcbdb149e4c9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9bc059183697bffe", "text_span_hash": "9bc059183697bffe", "source_file": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 16, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ronmental conditions warm, performance slows in a quantiﬁable\nmanner with the effects greater for the slower runners. For example, the\nqualifying standards for the 2020 Men’s and Women’s US Marathon trials\nare 2:19:00 and 2:45:00, respectively. Runne", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "because of the metabolic heat produc-\ntion of high-intensity running, it is not\nuncommon for core temperature to\nrise 28C or more during half marathon\nor marathon competitions (5,9,38,52),\neven in relatively cool conditions. For\nexample, the running pace of mara-\nthon world record (2:01:39 by Eliud\nKipchoge) results in a metabolic heat\nwould require almost 5 L of sweat\nevaporation over the course of the\nmarathon to maintain thermal equilib-\nrium (assuming no heat is lost through\nR and C) (21). Given this substantial\ncore temperature rise and evaporative\nrequirement during running competi-\ntion, it is unsurprising that perfor-\nwould not generally be considered\nheat stress. Laboratory studies exam-\na reduction in endurance at environ-\nmental temperatures as low as ;208C,\nwell below the conditions traditionally\nused in heat stress literature. While\nlaboratory studies may not match\nreal-world performance due to lower\nrates of convective cooling on a sta-\ntionary cycle ergometer or treadmill\nas compared to over-ground running,\na series of analyses in marathon per-\nformance further supports the notion\nthat optimal performance conditions\nfor distance running are compara-\nA common weather parameter of fast\nrunning events, including marathons,\nis a relatively cool environmental air\ntemperature of approximately ;128C\ncreases in air temperature beyond\n;128C lead to a progressive slowing\nof performance in elite, competitive,\nand casual ru", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ronmental conditions warm, performance slows in a quantiﬁable\nmanner with the effects greater for the slower runners. For example, the\nqualifying standards for the 2020 Men’s and Women’s US Marathon trials\nare 2:19:00 and 2:45:00, respectively. Runne"}
{"chunk_id": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2_p0002_c0017", "source_registry_id": "src_bcbdb149e4c9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "6a139381ddc51dbe", "text_span_hash": "6a139381ddc51dbe", "source_file": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 17, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "d 2:45:00, respectively. Runners with the ﬁtness to run these\nqualifying standards in near-ideal weather conditions (;58C WBGT) would\nbe expected to slow 1–2% for every 58C increase in WBGT. At a WBGT of\n158C, this would equate to a 2% slowing in men", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "because of the metabolic heat produc-\ntion of high-intensity running, it is not\nuncommon for core temperature to\nrise 28C or more during half marathon\nor marathon competitions (5,9,38,52),\neven in relatively cool conditions. For\nexample, the running pace of mara-\nthon world record (2:01:39 by Eliud\nKipchoge) results in a metabolic heat\nwould require almost 5 L of sweat\nevaporation over the course of the\nmarathon to maintain thermal equilib-\nrium (assuming no heat is lost through\nR and C) (21). Given this substantial\ncore temperature rise and evaporative\nrequirement during running competi-\ntion, it is unsurprising that perfor-\nwould not generally be considered\nheat stress. Laboratory studies exam-\na reduction in endurance at environ-\nmental temperatures as low as ;208C,\nwell below the conditions traditionally\nused in heat stress literature. While\nlaboratory studies may not match\nreal-world performance due to lower\nrates of convective cooling on a sta-\ntionary cycle ergometer or treadmill\nas compared to over-ground running,\na series of analyses in marathon per-\nformance further supports the notion\nthat optimal performance conditions\nfor distance running are compara-\nA common weather parameter of fast\nrunning events, including marathons,\nis a relatively cool environmental air\ntemperature of approximately ;128C\ncreases in air temperature beyond\n;128C lead to a progressive slowing\nof performance in elite, competitive,\nand casual ru", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "d 2:45:00, respectively. Runners with the ﬁtness to run these\nqualifying standards in near-ideal weather conditions (;58C WBGT) would\nbe expected to slow 1–2% for every 58C increase in WBGT. At a WBGT of\n158C, this would equate to a 2% slowing in men"}
{"chunk_id": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2_p0002_c0019", "source_registry_id": "src_bcbdb149e4c9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "193f01b52a5e35b7", "text_span_hash": "193f01b52a5e35b7", "source_file": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 19, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "s and would therefore similarly apply to 2:19\nfemale runners and 2:45 male runners. Reprinted with permission from Ely\nStrength and Conditioning Journal | www.nsca-scj.com\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditioning Association. Unauthorized repro", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "because of the metabolic heat produc-\ntion of high-intensity running, it is not\nuncommon for core temperature to\nrise 28C or more during half marathon\nor marathon competitions (5,9,38,52),\neven in relatively cool conditions. For\nexample, the running pace of mara-\nthon world record (2:01:39 by Eliud\nKipchoge) results in a metabolic heat\nwould require almost 5 L of sweat\nevaporation over the course of the\nmarathon to maintain thermal equilib-\nrium (assuming no heat is lost through\nR and C) (21). Given this substantial\ncore temperature rise and evaporative\nrequirement during running competi-\ntion, it is unsurprising that perfor-\nwould not generally be considered\nheat stress. Laboratory studies exam-\na reduction in endurance at environ-\nmental temperatures as low as ;208C,\nwell below the conditions traditionally\nused in heat stress literature. While\nlaboratory studies may not match\nreal-world performance due to lower\nrates of convective cooling on a sta-\ntionary cycle ergometer or treadmill\nas compared to over-ground running,\na series of analyses in marathon per-\nformance further supports the notion\nthat optimal performance conditions\nfor distance running are compara-\nA common weather parameter of fast\nrunning events, including marathons,\nis a relatively cool environmental air\ntemperature of approximately ;128C\ncreases in air temperature beyond\n;128C lead to a progressive slowing\nof performance in elite, competitive,\nand casual ru", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "s and would therefore similarly apply to 2:19\nfemale runners and 2:45 male runners. Reprinted with permission from Ely\nStrength and Conditioning Journal | www.nsca-scj.com\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditioning Association. Unauthorized repro"}
{"chunk_id": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2_p0003_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_bcbdb149e4c9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "31160da1b47929ac", "text_span_hash": "31160da1b47929ac", "source_file": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "erformance precipice at a core\ntemperature of 408C is countered by\nnumerous reports in the literature of\n.408C while apparently maintaining\na high level of performance. This idea\nof a critical core temperature was\ninvestigated in highly ﬁt runners pe", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "fatigue at a “critical” core temperature\nof 408C. This temperature is also asso-\nciated with exertional heat stroke (2),\nas well as brain wave and motor neural\noutput changes (43,46), consistent with\nan interpretation that a core tempera-\nture of ;408C may be a safety brake to\nprevent catastrophic hyperthermia or\nat least mark the precipice of progres-\nmance is diminished with an elevated\nbrain temperature ($408C), the idea of\na sharp performance precipice at a core\ntemperature of 408C is countered by\nnumerous reports in the literature of\n.408C while apparently maintaining\na high level of performance. This idea\nof a critical core temperature was\ninvestigated in highly ﬁt runners per-\nforming an 8k track time trial where\nrectal temperature, skin temperature,\nand heart rate were continuously mon-\nitored in COOL (WBGT ;138C) and\nWARM (WBGT ;278C) conditions\n(17). Core temperature at completion\nof the 8k time trial was slightly higher\nin WARM (0.58C higher than COOL)\nwhile skin temperature was substan-\ntially higher in WARM (48C higher\nwas substantially reduced in WARM\nslower than their personal best for 8k,\nwhile runners in WARM conditions\naveraged 7.9% slower than their per-\nsonal best for 8k). However, mean run-\nning velocity when core temperature\nwas under or over 408C core did not\ndiffer for individuals in either WARM\nor COOL conditions. Furthermore,\nthere was no evidence of runners ad-\njusting pace based on the rate of core\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "erformance precipice at a core\ntemperature of 408C is countered by\nnumerous reports in the literature of\n.408C while apparently maintaining\na high level of performance. This idea\nof a critical core temperature was\ninvestigated in highly ﬁt runners pe"}
