st for 8k,\nwhile runners in WARM conditions\naveraged 7.9% slower than their per-\nsonal best for 8k). However, mean run-\nning velocity when core temperature\nwas under or over 408C core did not\ndiffer for individuals in either WARM\nor COOL conditions. Furthermore,\nthere was no evidence of runners ad-\njusting pace based on the rate of core\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "igated in highly ﬁt runners per-\nforming an 8k track time trial where\nrectal temperature, skin temperature,\nand heart rate were continuously mon-\nitored in COOL (WBGT ;138C) and\nWARM (WBGT ;278C) conditions\n(17). Core temperature at completion\nof the"}
{"chunk_id": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2_p0003_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_bcbdb149e4c9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5106e5f76971b062", "text_span_hash": "5106e5f76971b062", "source_file": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "personal best for 8k,\nwhile runners in WARM conditions\naveraged 7.9% slower than their per-\nsonal best for 8k). However, mean run-\nning velocity when core temperature\nwas under or over 408C core did not\ndiffer for individuals in either WARM\nor COOL c", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "fatigue at a “critical” core temperature\nof 408C. This temperature is also asso-\nciated with exertional heat stroke (2),\nas well as brain wave and motor neural\noutput changes (43,46), consistent with\nan interpretation that a core tempera-\nture of ;408C may be a safety brake to\nprevent catastrophic hyperthermia or\nat least mark the precipice of progres-\nmance is diminished with an elevated\nbrain temperature ($408C), the idea of\na sharp performance precipice at a core\ntemperature of 408C is countered by\nnumerous reports in the literature of\n.408C while apparently maintaining\na high level of performance. This idea\nof a critical core temperature was\ninvestigated in highly ﬁt runners per-\nforming an 8k track time trial where\nrectal temperature, skin temperature,\nand heart rate were continuously mon-\nitored in COOL (WBGT ;138C) and\nWARM (WBGT ;278C) conditions\n(17). Core temperature at completion\nof the 8k time trial was slightly higher\nin WARM (0.58C higher than COOL)\nwhile skin temperature was substan-\ntially higher in WARM (48C higher\nwas substantially reduced in WARM\nslower than their personal best for 8k,\nwhile runners in WARM conditions\naveraged 7.9% slower than their per-\nsonal best for 8k). However, mean run-\nning velocity when core temperature\nwas under or over 408C core did not\ndiffer for individuals in either WARM\nor COOL conditions. Furthermore,\nthere was no evidence of runners ad-\njusting pace based on the rate of core\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "personal best for 8k,\nwhile runners in WARM conditions\naveraged 7.9% slower than their per-\nsonal best for 8k). However, mean run-\nning velocity when core temperature\nwas under or over 408C core did not\ndiffer for individuals in either WARM\nor COOL c"}
{"chunk_id": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2_p0003_c0020", "source_registry_id": "src_bcbdb149e4c9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "66d67e2e7c3143f0", "text_span_hash": "66d67e2e7c3143f0", "source_file": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 20, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "n delivery\nto muscle. This, in turn, leads to a reduc-\ntion in maximal oxygen uptake (V̇O2m-\nThe competition for blood ﬂow during\nrunning in the heat while simulta-\nneously perfusing active skeletal mus-\ncle and warm skin described above is\nfurther e", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "fatigue at a “critical” core temperature\nof 408C. This temperature is also asso-\nciated with exertional heat stroke (2),\nas well as brain wave and motor neural\noutput changes (43,46), consistent with\nan interpretation that a core tempera-\nture of ;408C may be a safety brake to\nprevent catastrophic hyperthermia or\nat least mark the precipice of progres-\nmance is diminished with an elevated\nbrain temperature ($408C), the idea of\na sharp performance precipice at a core\ntemperature of 408C is countered by\nnumerous reports in the literature of\n.408C while apparently maintaining\na high level of performance. This idea\nof a critical core temperature was\ninvestigated in highly ﬁt runners per-\nforming an 8k track time trial where\nrectal temperature, skin temperature,\nand heart rate were continuously mon-\nitored in COOL (WBGT ;138C) and\nWARM (WBGT ;278C) conditions\n(17). Core temperature at completion\nof the 8k time trial was slightly higher\nin WARM (0.58C higher than COOL)\nwhile skin temperature was substan-\ntially higher in WARM (48C higher\nwas substantially reduced in WARM\nslower than their personal best for 8k,\nwhile runners in WARM conditions\naveraged 7.9% slower than their per-\nsonal best for 8k). However, mean run-\nning velocity when core temperature\nwas under or over 408C core did not\ndiffer for individuals in either WARM\nor COOL conditions. Furthermore,\nthere was no evidence of runners ad-\njusting pace based on the rate of core\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "n delivery\nto muscle. This, in turn, leads to a reduc-\ntion in maximal oxygen uptake (V̇O2m-\nThe competition for blood ﬂow during\nrunning in the heat while simulta-\nneously perfusing active skeletal mus-\ncle and warm skin described above is\nfurther e"}
{"chunk_id": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2_p0004_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_bcbdb149e4c9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "6b1b9db3b65a53fd", "text_span_hash": "6b1b9db3b65a53fd", "source_file": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "environment), over\nthe course of 5–14 days, resulted in\na reduced resting core temperature\nand slower rise during exercise, reduced\nheart rate at ﬁxed workloads, increased\nsweating capacity, and increased plasma\nvolume (59). The improved ability to", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "(11), indicating that fully matching sweat\nlosses with ﬂuid replacement during run-\nning competition in the heat may be\ndifﬁcult. However, adequate precompe-\ntition hydration and in-competition ad\nlibitum drinking can help minimize per-\nformance decrements associated with\nreduced central volume and hyperther-\nmia in hot environments (60).\nHEAT ACCLIMATION TO IMPROVE\nHeat acclimation, or repeated exposure\nto exercise-heat stress, was developed\nin occupational settings to prolong the\nwork capacity of individuals subjected\nto hot work conditions (31,53). These\nrepeated exercise-heat exposures (60–\n100 minutes of moderate-intensity exer-\ncise in a warm/hot environment), over\nthe course of 5–14 days, resulted in\na reduced resting core temperature\nand slower rise during exercise, reduced\nheart rate at ﬁxed workloads, increased\nsweating capacity, and increased plasma\nvolume (59). The improved ability to\ndissipate heat mitigates heat storage,\nand the slower rise in core temperature\nprolongs time to exhaustion during\nﬁxed-intensity exercise (42). Similarly,\nthe improved ability to dissipate heat\nwill allow for an elevated exercise inten-\nsity and metabolic heat production dur-\ning self-paced exercise (49). In concert,\nthese adaptations reduce thermoregula-\ntory strain and improve physical perfor-\nHeat acclimation procedures devel-\noped in occupational settings trans-\nlated easily to athletes preparing to\ncompete in hot-weather races, a", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "environment), over\nthe course of 5–14 days, resulted in\na reduced resting core temperature\nand slower rise during exercise, reduced\nheart rate at ﬁxed workloads, increased\nsweating capacity, and increased plasma\nvolume (59). The improved ability to"}
{"chunk_id": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2_p0004_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_bcbdb149e4c9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c91431d86f17f56b", "text_span_hash": "c91431d86f17f56b", "source_file": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Heat acclimation can be\nadministered through use of an envi-\nronmental chamber set to hot/dry or\nwarm/humid conditions, through nat-\nural acclimatization by running out-\npotentially through the addition of in-\nsulative clothing during exercise in\nhea", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "(11), indicating that fully matching sweat\nlosses with ﬂuid replacement during run-\nning competition in the heat may be\ndifﬁcult. However, adequate precompe-\ntition hydration and in-competition ad\nlibitum drinking can help minimize per-\nformance decrements associated with\nreduced central volume and hyperther-\nmia in hot environments (60).\nHEAT ACCLIMATION TO IMPROVE\nHeat acclimation, or repeated exposure\nto exercise-heat stress, was developed\nin occupational settings to prolong the\nwork capacity of individuals subjected\nto hot work conditions (31,53). These\nrepeated exercise-heat exposures (60–\n100 minutes of moderate-intensity exer-\ncise in a warm/hot environment), over\nthe course of 5–14 days, resulted in\na reduced resting core temperature\nand slower rise during exercise, reduced\nheart rate at ﬁxed workloads, increased\nsweating capacity, and increased plasma\nvolume (59). The improved ability to\ndissipate heat mitigates heat storage,\nand the slower rise in core temperature\nprolongs time to exhaustion during\nﬁxed-intensity exercise (42). Similarly,\nthe improved ability to dissipate heat\nwill allow for an elevated exercise inten-\nsity and metabolic heat production dur-\ning self-paced exercise (49). In concert,\nthese adaptations reduce thermoregula-\ntory strain and improve physical perfor-\nHeat acclimation procedures devel-\noped in occupational settings trans-\nlated easily to athletes preparing to\ncompete in hot-weather races, a", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Heat acclimation can be\nadministered through use of an envi-\nronmental chamber set to hot/dry or\nwarm/humid conditions, through nat-\nural acclimatization by running out-\npotentially through the addition of in-\nsulative clothing during exercise in\nhea"}
{"chunk_id": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2_p0004_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_bcbdb149e4c9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2f12803bd8957c98", "text_span_hash": "2f12803bd8957c98", "source_file": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "production on the\nskin surface (4,27). Because athletes\ntraining at a high intensity in rela-\ntively cool environments likely expe-\na regular basis, runners are believed\nto be “partially heat acclimated” as\ncompared to sedentary counterparts\n(25). T", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "(11), indicating that fully matching sweat\nlosses with ﬂuid replacement during run-\nning competition in the heat may be\ndifﬁcult. However, adequate precompe-\ntition hydration and in-competition ad\nlibitum drinking can help minimize per-\nformance decrements associated with\nreduced central volume and hyperther-\nmia in hot environments (60).\nHEAT ACCLIMATION TO IMPROVE\nHeat acclimation, or repeated exposure\nto exercise-heat stress, was developed\nin occupational settings to prolong the\nwork capacity of individuals subjected\nto hot work conditions (31,53). These\nrepeated exercise-heat exposures (60–\n100 minutes of moderate-intensity exer-\ncise in a warm/hot environment), over\nthe course of 5–14 days, resulted in\na reduced resting core temperature\nand slower rise during exercise, reduced\nheart rate at ﬁxed workloads, increased\nsweating capacity, and increased plasma\nvolume (59). The improved ability to\ndissipate heat mitigates heat storage,\nand the slower rise in core temperature\nprolongs time to exhaustion during\nﬁxed-intensity exercise (42). Similarly,\nthe improved ability to dissipate heat\nwill allow for an elevated exercise inten-\nsity and metabolic heat production dur-\ning self-paced exercise (49). In concert,\nthese adaptations reduce thermoregula-\ntory strain and improve physical perfor-\nHeat acclimation procedures devel-\noped in occupational settings trans-\nlated easily to athletes preparing to\ncompete in hot-weather races, a", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "production on the\nskin surface (4,27). Because athletes\ntraining at a high intensity in rela-\ntively cool environments likely expe-\na regular basis, runners are believed\nto be “partially heat acclimated” as\ncompared to sedentary counterparts\n(25). T"}
{"chunk_id": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2_p0004_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_bcbdb149e4c9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7ade6314ebed96be", "text_span_hash": "7ade6314ebed96be", "source_file": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "days) period of accli-\nmation. Because exercise-heat expo-\nsure constitutes a greater training\nstress than running in cool condi-\ntions, the minimal exposure for ben-\neﬁt is an important consideration,\nparticularly because heat acclimation\nis usually", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "(11), indicating that fully matching sweat\nlosses with ﬂuid replacement during run-\nning competition in the heat may be\ndifﬁcult. However, adequate precompe-\ntition hydration and in-competition ad\nlibitum drinking can help minimize per-\nformance decrements associated with\nreduced central volume and hyperther-\nmia in hot environments (60).\nHEAT ACCLIMATION TO IMPROVE\nHeat acclimation, or repeated exposure\nto exercise-heat stress, was developed\nin occupational settings to prolong the\nwork capacity of individuals subjected\nto hot work conditions (31,53). These\nrepeated exercise-heat exposures (60–\n100 minutes of moderate-intensity exer-\ncise in a warm/hot environment), over\nthe course of 5–14 days, resulted in\na reduced resting core temperature\nand slower rise during exercise, reduced\nheart rate at ﬁxed workloads, increased\nsweating capacity, and increased plasma\nvolume (59). The improved ability to\ndissipate heat mitigates heat storage,\nand the slower rise in core temperature\nprolongs time to exhaustion during\nﬁxed-intensity exercise (42). Similarly,\nthe improved ability to dissipate heat\nwill allow for an elevated exercise inten-\nsity and metabolic heat production dur-\ning self-paced exercise (49). In concert,\nthese adaptations reduce thermoregula-\ntory strain and improve physical perfor-\nHeat acclimation procedures devel-\noped in occupational settings trans-\nlated easily to athletes preparing to\ncompete in hot-weather races, a", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "days) period of accli-\nmation. Because exercise-heat expo-\nsure constitutes a greater training\nstress than running in cool condi-\ntions, the minimal exposure for ben-\neﬁt is an important consideration,\nparticularly because heat acclimation\nis usually"}
{"chunk_id": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2_p0004_c0016", "source_registry_id": "src_bcbdb149e4c9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "308bb77609001d1b", "text_span_hash": "308bb77609001d1b", "source_file": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 16, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "se heat acclimation\nis usually used before competition\nduring a taper period (49).\nThe concern of overstressing a tapering\nathlete with the addition of exercise-\nheat acclimation has resulted in the\ninnovation of passive heat acclimation\nFigure 2. To", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "(11), indicating that fully matching sweat\nlosses with ﬂuid replacement during run-\nning competition in the heat may be\ndifﬁcult. However, adequate precompe-\ntition hydration and in-competition ad\nlibitum drinking can help minimize per-\nformance decrements associated with\nreduced central volume and hyperther-\nmia in hot environments (60).\nHEAT ACCLIMATION TO IMPROVE\nHeat acclimation, or repeated exposure\nto exercise-heat stress, was developed\nin occupational settings to prolong the\nwork capacity of individuals subjected\nto hot work conditions (31,53). These\nrepeated exercise-heat exposures (60–\n100 minutes of moderate-intensity exer-\ncise in a warm/hot environment), over\nthe course of 5–14 days, resulted in\na reduced resting core temperature\nand slower rise during exercise, reduced\nheart rate at ﬁxed workloads, increased\nsweating capacity, and increased plasma\nvolume (59). The improved ability to\ndissipate heat mitigates heat storage,\nand the slower rise in core temperature\nprolongs time to exhaustion during\nﬁxed-intensity exercise (42). Similarly,\nthe improved ability to dissipate heat\nwill allow for an elevated exercise inten-\nsity and metabolic heat production dur-\ning self-paced exercise (49). In concert,\nthese adaptations reduce thermoregula-\ntory strain and improve physical perfor-\nHeat acclimation procedures devel-\noped in occupational settings trans-\nlated easily to athletes preparing to\ncompete in hot-weather races, a", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "se heat acclimation\nis usually used before competition\nduring a taper period (49).\nThe concern of overstressing a tapering\nathlete with the addition of exercise-\nheat acclimation has resulted in the\ninnovation of passive heat acclimation\nFigure 2. To"}
{"chunk_id": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2_p0005_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_bcbdb149e4c9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "fb26550646fc1f58", "text_span_hash": "fb26550646fc1f58", "source_file": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ays of hot water immersion after 40 mi-\nnutes of treadmill running in temperate\nconditions reliably enhanced heat toler-\nance and expanded plasma volume in\nboth highly ﬁt (65) and modestly\ntrained (64) male runners. Although\nperformance in the heat w", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "strategies (29). It is recommended that\nathletes incorporate passive heat accli-\nmation bouts, including sauna or hot\nimmersion, immediately\nalready elevated (29). Recent research\nhas shown that as few as 6 consecutive\ndays of hot water immersion after 40 mi-\nnutes of treadmill running in temperate\nconditions reliably enhanced heat toler-\nance and expanded plasma volume in\nboth highly ﬁt (65) and modestly\ntrained (64) male runners. Although\nperformance in the heat was not specif-\nically investigated, 3 weeks of post-\ntrained male runners increased plasma\nvolume and enhanced performance in\na simulated 5,000-m run in temperate\nconditions (56). The increased plasma\nvolume, in particular, may contribute to\nimproved maximal performance in cool\nconditions as well (36,56), although this\nis not universally noted (40,45).\nThus, the physiological underpin-\nnings of performance decrements in\nthe heat can all be mitigated by heat\nacclimation. The reduced basal tem-\nperature and slower rise during exer-\nincreased sweating response facili-\ntates heat exchange through a wid-\ngradient, and the increased plasma\nvolume enables a greater skin and\nmuscle blood ﬂow and increases the\nsweating response (Figure 3). In mild\nheat stress, this means that perfor-\nmance can be likely maintained as\ncompared to cooler conditions with\nmore severe heat stress, heat acclima-\ntion mitigates but does not abolish\nheat-associated performance decre-\nThe profound me", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ays of hot water immersion after 40 mi-\nnutes of treadmill running in temperate\nconditions reliably enhanced heat toler-\nance and expanded plasma volume in\nboth highly ﬁt (65) and modestly\ntrained (64) male runners. Although\nperformance in the heat w"}
{"chunk_id": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2_p0005_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_bcbdb149e4c9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e149075876242af4", "text_span_hash": "e149075876242af4", "source_file": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ough\nperformance in the heat was not specif-\nically investigated, 3 weeks of post-\ntrained male runners increased plasma\nvolume and enhanced performance in\na simulated 5,000-m run in temperate\nconditions (56). The increased plasma\nvolume, in particul", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "strategies (29). It is recommended that\nathletes incorporate passive heat accli-\nmation bouts, including sauna or hot\nimmersion, immediately\nalready elevated (29). Recent research\nhas shown that as few as 6 consecutive\ndays of hot water immersion after 40 mi-\nnutes of treadmill running in temperate\nconditions reliably enhanced heat toler-\nance and expanded plasma volume in\nboth highly ﬁt (65) and modestly\ntrained (64) male runners. Although\nperformance in the heat was not specif-\nically investigated, 3 weeks of post-\ntrained male runners increased plasma\nvolume and enhanced performance in\na simulated 5,000-m run in temperate\nconditions (56). The increased plasma\nvolume, in particular, may contribute to\nimproved maximal performance in cool\nconditions as well (36,56), although this\nis not universally noted (40,45).\nThus, the physiological underpin-\nnings of performance decrements in\nthe heat can all be mitigated by heat\nacclimation. The reduced basal tem-\nperature and slower rise during exer-\nincreased sweating response facili-\ntates heat exchange through a wid-\ngradient, and the increased plasma\nvolume enables a greater skin and\nmuscle blood ﬂow and increases the\nsweating response (Figure 3). In mild\nheat stress, this means that perfor-\nmance can be likely maintained as\ncompared to cooler conditions with\nmore severe heat stress, heat acclima-\ntion mitigates but does not abolish\nheat-associated performance decre-\nThe profound me", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ough\nperformance in the heat was not specif-\nically investigated, 3 weeks of post-\ntrained male runners increased plasma\nvolume and enhanced performance in\na simulated 5,000-m run in temperate\nconditions (56). The increased plasma\nvolume, in particul"}
{"chunk_id": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2_p0005_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_bcbdb149e4c9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9a36e778606ed507", "text_span_hash": "9a36e778606ed507", "source_file": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "s to the environment. As the gra-\nand the environment narrows, the\nability to dissipate heat is attenuated\nand more metabolic heat is stored,\nand body temperature rises. This\nmeans that the environment can\ntemperatures, and running perfor-\nmance cont", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "strategies (29). It is recommended that\nathletes incorporate passive heat accli-\nmation bouts, including sauna or hot\nimmersion, immediately\nalready elevated (29). Recent research\nhas shown that as few as 6 consecutive\ndays of hot water immersion after 40 mi-\nnutes of treadmill running in temperate\nconditions reliably enhanced heat toler-\nance and expanded plasma volume in\nboth highly ﬁt (65) and modestly\ntrained (64) male runners. Although\nperformance in the heat was not specif-\nically investigated, 3 weeks of post-\ntrained male runners increased plasma\nvolume and enhanced performance in\na simulated 5,000-m run in temperate\nconditions (56). The increased plasma\nvolume, in particular, may contribute to\nimproved maximal performance in cool\nconditions as well (36,56), although this\nis not universally noted (40,45).\nThus, the physiological underpin-\nnings of performance decrements in\nthe heat can all be mitigated by heat\nacclimation. The reduced basal tem-\nperature and slower rise during exer-\nincreased sweating response facili-\ntates heat exchange through a wid-\ngradient, and the increased plasma\nvolume enables a greater skin and\nmuscle blood ﬂow and increases the\nsweating response (Figure 3). In mild\nheat stress, this means that perfor-\nmance can be likely maintained as\ncompared to cooler conditions with\nmore severe heat stress, heat acclima-\ntion mitigates but does not abolish\nheat-associated performance decre-\nThe profound me", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "s to the environment. As the gra-\nand the environment narrows, the\nability to dissipate heat is attenuated\nand more metabolic heat is stored,\nand body temperature rises. This\nmeans that the environment can\ntemperatures, and running perfor-\nmance cont"}
{"chunk_id": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2_p0005_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_bcbdb149e4c9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7be741a68fdd1d38", "text_span_hash": "7be741a68fdd1d38", "source_file": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "and running perfor-\nmance continues to slow as ambient\ntemperature increases. The primary\ncausative factors involve elevated\nblood ﬂow, and progressive ﬂuid los-\nsweating. Heat acclimation, through\nrepeated active or passive exposures,\ncan help minim", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "strategies (29). It is recommended that\nathletes incorporate passive heat accli-\nmation bouts, including sauna or hot\nimmersion, immediately\nalready elevated (29). Recent research\nhas shown that as few as 6 consecutive\ndays of hot water immersion after 40 mi-\nnutes of treadmill running in temperate\nconditions reliably enhanced heat toler-\nance and expanded plasma volume in\nboth highly ﬁt (65) and modestly\ntrained (64) male runners. Although\nperformance in the heat was not specif-\nically investigated, 3 weeks of post-\ntrained male runners increased plasma\nvolume and enhanced performance in\na simulated 5,000-m run in temperate\nconditions (56). The increased plasma\nvolume, in particular, may contribute to\nimproved maximal performance in cool\nconditions as well (36,56), although this\nis not universally noted (40,45).\nThus, the physiological underpin-\nnings of performance decrements in\nthe heat can all be mitigated by heat\nacclimation. The reduced basal tem-\nperature and slower rise during exer-\nincreased sweating response facili-\ntates heat exchange through a wid-\ngradient, and the increased plasma\nvolume enables a greater skin and\nmuscle blood ﬂow and increases the\nsweating response (Figure 3). In mild\nheat stress, this means that perfor-\nmance can be likely maintained as\ncompared to cooler conditions with\nmore severe heat stress, heat acclima-\ntion mitigates but does not abolish\nheat-associated performance decre-\nThe profound me", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "and running perfor-\nmance continues to slow as ambient\ntemperature increases. The primary\ncausative factors involve elevated\nblood ﬂow, and progressive ﬂuid los-\nsweating. Heat acclimation, through\nrepeated active or passive exposures,\ncan help minim"}
{"chunk_id": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2_p0005_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_bcbdb149e4c9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "644d797f03e8dcea", "text_span_hash": "644d797f03e8dcea", "source_file": "Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Brett R. Ely, PhD1 and Matthew R. Ely, MS2.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "rain to improve\nhot-weather running performance.\nVOLUME 42 | NUMBER 1 | FEBRUARY 2020\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditioning Association. Unauthorized reproduction of this article is prohibited.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "strategies (29). It is recommended that\nathletes incorporate passive heat accli-\nmation bouts, including sauna or hot\nimmersion, immediately\nalready elevated (29). Recent research\nhas shown that as few as 6 consecutive\ndays of hot water immersion after 40 mi-\nnutes of treadmill running in temperate\nconditions reliably enhanced heat toler-\nance and expanded plasma volume in\nboth highly ﬁt (65) and modestly\ntrained (64) male runners. Although\nperformance in the heat was not specif-\nically investigated, 3 weeks of post-\ntrained male runners increased plasma\nvolume and enhanced performance in\na simulated 5,000-m run in temperate\nconditions (56). The increased plasma\nvolume, in particular, may contribute to\nimproved maximal performance in cool\nconditions as well (36,56), although this\nis not universally noted (40,45).\nThus, the physiological underpin-\nnings of performance decrements in\nthe heat can all be mitigated by heat\nacclimation. The reduced basal tem-\nperature and slower rise during exer-\nincreased sweating response facili-\ntates heat exchange through a wid-\ngradient, and the increased plasma\nvolume enables a greater skin and\nmuscle blood ﬂow and increases the\nsweating response (Figure 3). In mild\nheat stress, this means that perfor-\nmance can be likely maintained as\ncompared to cooler conditions with\nmore severe heat stress, heat acclima-\ntion mitigates but does not abolish\nheat-associated performance decre-\nThe profound me", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "rain to improve\nhot-weather running performance.\nVOLUME 42 | NUMBER 1 | FEBRUARY 2020\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditioning Association. Unauthorized reproduction of this article is prohibited."}
