{"chunk_id": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan_p0002_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_d0fd178b5458", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c77d1421853fdc0a", "text_span_hash": "c77d1421853fdc0a", "source_file": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Deciding to run your first marathon comes with so many emotions. You may be feeling a\nbit nervous about it, but laying a strong foundation and slowly building your endurance\nwill help you feel confident and capable as you head into your next stage of", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Deciding to run your first marathon comes with so many emotions. You may be feeling a\nbit nervous about it, but laying a strong foundation and slowly building your endurance\nwill help you feel confident and capable as you head into your next stage of training.\nAthletes capable of running three miles should start this plan 28 weeks out from Every\nWoman’s Marathon (EWM). Once this plan is completed, runners can start the Beginners\nMarathon Plan 16 weeks out from EWM.\nIn partnership with Milk, Coach Alysha Flynn of What Runs You and Kristy Baumann, RD, LD, Running Dietitian, created this plan to support\nfirst-timers and beginner marathoners. Over the coming weeks, you’ll prepare for EWM using a combination of base mileage, endurance and\nstrength-training, along with weekly nutrition tips and helpful insights from our team captains.\nThis plan provides a relaxed build-up and emphasizes strength training to help you reach your full potential. You’ll run four days a week (on\nboth hard and soft surfaces) with plenty of walking for recovery. Each week, your expertly designed workouts and nutrition information will\nE gradually increase your weekly mileage with easy-effort runy\nE incorporate strength training and mobility to prevent injurj\nE facilitate recovery with planned rest days and step-back weeksN\nE build race-ready confidence with increased enduranceN\nE establish the importance of nutrition in your training journey\nLay your found", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Deciding to run your first marathon comes with so many emotions. You may be feeling a\nbit nervous about it, but laying a strong foundation and slowly building your endurance\nwill help you feel confident and capable as you head into your next stage of"}
{"chunk_id": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan_p0002_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_d0fd178b5458", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5aba0a120a39256c", "text_span_hash": "5aba0a120a39256c", "source_file": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "u head into your next stage of training.\nAthletes capable of running three miles should start this plan 28 weeks out from Every\nWoman’s Marathon (EWM). Once this plan is completed, runners can start the Beginners\nMarathon Plan 16 weeks out from EWM.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Deciding to run your first marathon comes with so many emotions. You may be feeling a\nbit nervous about it, but laying a strong foundation and slowly building your endurance\nwill help you feel confident and capable as you head into your next stage of training.\nAthletes capable of running three miles should start this plan 28 weeks out from Every\nWoman’s Marathon (EWM). Once this plan is completed, runners can start the Beginners\nMarathon Plan 16 weeks out from EWM.\nIn partnership with Milk, Coach Alysha Flynn of What Runs You and Kristy Baumann, RD, LD, Running Dietitian, created this plan to support\nfirst-timers and beginner marathoners. Over the coming weeks, you’ll prepare for EWM using a combination of base mileage, endurance and\nstrength-training, along with weekly nutrition tips and helpful insights from our team captains.\nThis plan provides a relaxed build-up and emphasizes strength training to help you reach your full potential. You’ll run four days a week (on\nboth hard and soft surfaces) with plenty of walking for recovery. Each week, your expertly designed workouts and nutrition information will\nE gradually increase your weekly mileage with easy-effort runy\nE incorporate strength training and mobility to prevent injurj\nE facilitate recovery with planned rest days and step-back weeksN\nE build race-ready confidence with increased enduranceN\nE establish the importance of nutrition in your training journey\nLay your found", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "u head into your next stage of training.\nAthletes capable of running three miles should start this plan 28 weeks out from Every\nWoman’s Marathon (EWM). Once this plan is completed, runners can start the Beginners\nMarathon Plan 16 weeks out from EWM."}
{"chunk_id": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan_p0002_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_d0fd178b5458", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "68ebc732d6607786", "text_span_hash": "68ebc732d6607786", "source_file": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ition information will\nE gradually increase your weekly mileage with easy-effort runy\nE incorporate strength training and mobility to prevent injurj\nE facilitate recovery with planned rest days and step-back weeksN\nE build race-ready confidence with", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Deciding to run your first marathon comes with so many emotions. You may be feeling a\nbit nervous about it, but laying a strong foundation and slowly building your endurance\nwill help you feel confident and capable as you head into your next stage of training.\nAthletes capable of running three miles should start this plan 28 weeks out from Every\nWoman’s Marathon (EWM). Once this plan is completed, runners can start the Beginners\nMarathon Plan 16 weeks out from EWM.\nIn partnership with Milk, Coach Alysha Flynn of What Runs You and Kristy Baumann, RD, LD, Running Dietitian, created this plan to support\nfirst-timers and beginner marathoners. Over the coming weeks, you’ll prepare for EWM using a combination of base mileage, endurance and\nstrength-training, along with weekly nutrition tips and helpful insights from our team captains.\nThis plan provides a relaxed build-up and emphasizes strength training to help you reach your full potential. You’ll run four days a week (on\nboth hard and soft surfaces) with plenty of walking for recovery. Each week, your expertly designed workouts and nutrition information will\nE gradually increase your weekly mileage with easy-effort runy\nE incorporate strength training and mobility to prevent injurj\nE facilitate recovery with planned rest days and step-back weeksN\nE build race-ready confidence with increased enduranceN\nE establish the importance of nutrition in your training journey\nLay your found", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ition information will\nE gradually increase your weekly mileage with easy-effort runy\nE incorporate strength training and mobility to prevent injurj\nE facilitate recovery with planned rest days and step-back weeksN\nE build race-ready confidence with"}
{"chunk_id": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan_p0002_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_d0fd178b5458", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8e7ae23566a9f94c", "text_span_hash": "8e7ae23566a9f94c", "source_file": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ng Experts:\nCoach Alysha Flynn is a Road Marathoner / Trail Ultra Runner and\nhas been running for over 25 years. She uses this expertise to pursue\nher true passion of coaching through her business What Runs You,\nLLC. Her coaching philosophy makes run", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Deciding to run your first marathon comes with so many emotions. You may be feeling a\nbit nervous about it, but laying a strong foundation and slowly building your endurance\nwill help you feel confident and capable as you head into your next stage of training.\nAthletes capable of running three miles should start this plan 28 weeks out from Every\nWoman’s Marathon (EWM). Once this plan is completed, runners can start the Beginners\nMarathon Plan 16 weeks out from EWM.\nIn partnership with Milk, Coach Alysha Flynn of What Runs You and Kristy Baumann, RD, LD, Running Dietitian, created this plan to support\nfirst-timers and beginner marathoners. Over the coming weeks, you’ll prepare for EWM using a combination of base mileage, endurance and\nstrength-training, along with weekly nutrition tips and helpful insights from our team captains.\nThis plan provides a relaxed build-up and emphasizes strength training to help you reach your full potential. You’ll run four days a week (on\nboth hard and soft surfaces) with plenty of walking for recovery. Each week, your expertly designed workouts and nutrition information will\nE gradually increase your weekly mileage with easy-effort runy\nE incorporate strength training and mobility to prevent injurj\nE facilitate recovery with planned rest days and step-back weeksN\nE build race-ready confidence with increased enduranceN\nE establish the importance of nutrition in your training journey\nLay your found", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ng Experts:\nCoach Alysha Flynn is a Road Marathoner / Trail Ultra Runner and\nhas been running for over 25 years. She uses this expertise to pursue\nher true passion of coaching through her business What Runs You,\nLLC. Her coaching philosophy makes run"}
{"chunk_id": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan_p0002_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_d0fd178b5458", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0e7fb407eb19961e", "text_span_hash": "0e7fb407eb19961e", "source_file": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ir bodies effectively to excel in both their training and their\neveryday-life pursuits.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Deciding to run your first marathon comes with so many emotions. You may be feeling a\nbit nervous about it, but laying a strong foundation and slowly building your endurance\nwill help you feel confident and capable as you head into your next stage of training.\nAthletes capable of running three miles should start this plan 28 weeks out from Every\nWoman’s Marathon (EWM). Once this plan is completed, runners can start the Beginners\nMarathon Plan 16 weeks out from EWM.\nIn partnership with Milk, Coach Alysha Flynn of What Runs You and Kristy Baumann, RD, LD, Running Dietitian, created this plan to support\nfirst-timers and beginner marathoners. Over the coming weeks, you’ll prepare for EWM using a combination of base mileage, endurance and\nstrength-training, along with weekly nutrition tips and helpful insights from our team captains.\nThis plan provides a relaxed build-up and emphasizes strength training to help you reach your full potential. You’ll run four days a week (on\nboth hard and soft surfaces) with plenty of walking for recovery. Each week, your expertly designed workouts and nutrition information will\nE gradually increase your weekly mileage with easy-effort runy\nE incorporate strength training and mobility to prevent injurj\nE facilitate recovery with planned rest days and step-back weeksN\nE build race-ready confidence with increased enduranceN\nE establish the importance of nutrition in your training journey\nLay your found", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ir bodies effectively to excel in both their training and their\neveryday-life pursuits."}
{"chunk_id": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan_p0003_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_d0fd178b5458", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e3002b11ba234116", "text_span_hash": "e3002b11ba234116", "source_file": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "king sure you get enough to drink during and immediately after your runs. Make sure you’re drinking throughout the day—\naim to get 8–10 cups of water to meet your fluid needs so that you go into your training hydrated. Other non-alcoholic and non-caf", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Fuel your training with performance plates\nNutrition is one of the most powerful tools you have to support your training. Just like gasoline powers a car,\nfood fuels the body. The right nutrients in the right amounts give your body the energy it needs to improve\nendurance, recover faster and reduce the risk of injury. The wrong kind of fuel can hold you back, making you\nfeel sluggish or cause digestive problems.\nIndividual nutritional needs will vary depending on your training. Performance plates are a visual tool to help ensure you’re getting the right\nratio of macronutrients (carbs, protein and fats) to fuel your training. The ratios will change as your training progresses, and making nutritious\nchoices within each category will ensure you also get the variety of micronutrients (vitamins and minerals) your body needs.\nPerformance plates are divided into three sections:\nFound in whole grains like brown rice,\nquinoa, oats and farro; legumes and\nbeans like lentils, black beans and\nchickpeas and root vegetables like\nsweet potatoes, green peas and corn,\nstarches and grains provide\ncarbohydrates that are your body’s\npreferred source of energy during\nendurance activities like running. Be\nintentional about incorporating\ncarbohydrate foods in your meals and\nsnacks, especially before your long\nProtein is essential for muscle repair\nand recovery. Lean protein sources\ninclude lean meats, poultry, fish, eggs,\ntofu, tempeh, beans and lent", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "king sure you get enough to drink during and immediately after your runs. Make sure you’re drinking throughout the day—\naim to get 8–10 cups of water to meet your fluid needs so that you go into your training hydrated. Other non-alcoholic and non-caf"}
{"chunk_id": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan_p0004_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_d0fd178b5458", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c0712e91674c952d", "text_span_hash": "c0712e91674c952d", "source_file": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "sed on running first thing in the morning.\nWithin 1 hour of waking up or finishing run\n3-4 hours after breakfast\nTurkey and avocado wrap\nWhole grain tortilla + sliced turkey breast\n+ cheese + sliced red bell pepper +\nspinach + mashed avocado\nCarrot s", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "While following this Base Training Plan, use the performance plate visual tool to help create three balanced meals each day to help keep you satisfied.\nCraving sweets or savory snacks? Go ahead, add in your favorite foods as a part of these balanced meals.\nModerate Training Plate\nBase training: Sample meal plan\nUse this sample meal plan to help guide your meal ideas and timing of meals and snacks throughout the day.\nThis meal plan is based on running first thing in the morning.\nWithin 1 hour of waking up or finishing run\n3-4 hours after breakfast\nTurkey and avocado wrap\nWhole grain tortilla + sliced turkey breast\n+ cheese + sliced red bell pepper +\nspinach + mashed avocado\nCarrot sticks and hummus for dipping\n3-4 hours after lunch\n3-4 hours after afternoon snack\nBaked salmon + quinoa + mixed green\nsalad with cherry tomatoes, cucumber,\nand balsamic vinaigrette dressing\nAfter eating your lunch, allow 2-3 hours\nbefore you start your run. Consider eating\na pre-run snack 30-60 minutes prior to\nstarting your mid-day run if you need an\nRunning in the evening?\n1-2 hours before your run, consider eating a\nlarger afternoon snack like a banana with\npeanut butter and a glass of milk. Or, think\nabout eating an early dinner and allowing at\nleast 2-3 hours to pass before you start to run.\nStay focused on the habit of running\nconsistently. Putting on your running gear\nand going for a run is super important!\nThat's the goal. Nothing more, noth", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "sed on running first thing in the morning.\nWithin 1 hour of waking up or finishing run\n3-4 hours after breakfast\nTurkey and avocado wrap\nWhole grain tortilla + sliced turkey breast\n+ cheese + sliced red bell pepper +\nspinach + mashed avocado\nCarrot s"}
{"chunk_id": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan_p0004_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_d0fd178b5458", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4d7973044dfeb624", "text_span_hash": "4d7973044dfeb624", "source_file": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "After eating your lunch, allow 2-3 hours\nbefore you start your run. Consider eating\na pre-run snack 30-60 minutes prior to\nstarting your mid-day run if you need an\nRunning in the evening?\n1-2 hours before your run, consider eating a\nlarger afternoon", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "While following this Base Training Plan, use the performance plate visual tool to help create three balanced meals each day to help keep you satisfied.\nCraving sweets or savory snacks? Go ahead, add in your favorite foods as a part of these balanced meals.\nModerate Training Plate\nBase training: Sample meal plan\nUse this sample meal plan to help guide your meal ideas and timing of meals and snacks throughout the day.\nThis meal plan is based on running first thing in the morning.\nWithin 1 hour of waking up or finishing run\n3-4 hours after breakfast\nTurkey and avocado wrap\nWhole grain tortilla + sliced turkey breast\n+ cheese + sliced red bell pepper +\nspinach + mashed avocado\nCarrot sticks and hummus for dipping\n3-4 hours after lunch\n3-4 hours after afternoon snack\nBaked salmon + quinoa + mixed green\nsalad with cherry tomatoes, cucumber,\nand balsamic vinaigrette dressing\nAfter eating your lunch, allow 2-3 hours\nbefore you start your run. Consider eating\na pre-run snack 30-60 minutes prior to\nstarting your mid-day run if you need an\nRunning in the evening?\n1-2 hours before your run, consider eating a\nlarger afternoon snack like a banana with\npeanut butter and a glass of milk. Or, think\nabout eating an early dinner and allowing at\nleast 2-3 hours to pass before you start to run.\nStay focused on the habit of running\nconsistently. Putting on your running gear\nand going for a run is super important!\nThat's the goal. Nothing more, noth", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "After eating your lunch, allow 2-3 hours\nbefore you start your run. Consider eating\na pre-run snack 30-60 minutes prior to\nstarting your mid-day run if you need an\nRunning in the evening?\n1-2 hours before your run, consider eating a\nlarger afternoon"}
{"chunk_id": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan_p0004_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_d0fd178b5458", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1919518956755741", "text_span_hash": "1919518956755741", "source_file": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "of running\nconsistently. Putting on your running gear\nand going for a run is super important!\nThat's the goal. Nothing more, nothing less.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "While following this Base Training Plan, use the performance plate visual tool to help create three balanced meals each day to help keep you satisfied.\nCraving sweets or savory snacks? Go ahead, add in your favorite foods as a part of these balanced meals.\nModerate Training Plate\nBase training: Sample meal plan\nUse this sample meal plan to help guide your meal ideas and timing of meals and snacks throughout the day.\nThis meal plan is based on running first thing in the morning.\nWithin 1 hour of waking up or finishing run\n3-4 hours after breakfast\nTurkey and avocado wrap\nWhole grain tortilla + sliced turkey breast\n+ cheese + sliced red bell pepper +\nspinach + mashed avocado\nCarrot sticks and hummus for dipping\n3-4 hours after lunch\n3-4 hours after afternoon snack\nBaked salmon + quinoa + mixed green\nsalad with cherry tomatoes, cucumber,\nand balsamic vinaigrette dressing\nAfter eating your lunch, allow 2-3 hours\nbefore you start your run. Consider eating\na pre-run snack 30-60 minutes prior to\nstarting your mid-day run if you need an\nRunning in the evening?\n1-2 hours before your run, consider eating a\nlarger afternoon snack like a banana with\npeanut butter and a glass of milk. Or, think\nabout eating an early dinner and allowing at\nleast 2-3 hours to pass before you start to run.\nStay focused on the habit of running\nconsistently. Putting on your running gear\nand going for a run is super important!\nThat's the goal. Nothing more, noth", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "of running\nconsistently. Putting on your running gear\nand going for a run is super important!\nThat's the goal. Nothing more, nothing less."}
{"chunk_id": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan_p0005_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_d0fd178b5458", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c161f79bcfad8ad5", "text_span_hash": "c161f79bcfad8ad5", "source_file": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "This calendar outlines a day-by-day schedule to build your base mileage and strength over the next 12 weeks. Keep in mind, this program can\nbe adapted for those who want to use walk/run intervals. Simply follow the guide and use your preferred walk/r", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "This calendar outlines a day-by-day schedule to build your base mileage and strength over the next 12 weeks. Keep in mind, this program can\nbe adapted for those who want to use walk/run intervals. Simply follow the guide and use your preferred walk/run interval (30:30, 1:1, 9:1, etc.).\nAs you progress through the plan, you'll see the calendar segmented into weeks, accompanied by additional details and nutritional", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "This calendar outlines a day-by-day schedule to build your base mileage and strength over the next 12 weeks. Keep in mind, this program can\nbe adapted for those who want to use walk/run intervals. Simply follow the guide and use your preferred walk/r"}
{"chunk_id": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan_p0006_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_d0fd178b5458", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "af9f822300f799ef", "text_span_hash": "af9f822300f799ef", "source_file": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Tune into your body and run by effort\nwhile building your aerobic base. Aim\nfor an intensity where you can\ncomfortably maintain a conversation.\nThis effort level will be used for the\nmajority of your training runs to\nimprove your endurance.\nCoaching", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Tune into your body and run by effort\nwhile building your aerobic base. Aim\nfor an intensity where you can\ncomfortably maintain a conversation.\nThis effort level will be used for the\nmajority of your training runs to\nimprove your endurance.\nCoaching cue: Training volume will\ninfluence your perceived effort from\nweek to week. Feel free to make\nadjustments so these runs always feel\nlike a 3/4 on a scale of 1-10.\nRunning step-back week\nTaking a step-back week in running\nallows you to “step back” from\ntraining intensity, reflect on what\nyou’ve accomplished (both mentally\nand physically) and prepare for\ntraining intensity to come. These\nweeks also reduce your risk of injury.\nCoaching cue: Think of these weeks as\na nice mental reset button.\nStrides consist of brief intervals of\nfaster running followed by recovery\nperiods. Strides are important in\ntraining because they create an\nopportunity to work on running\nmechanics and form. Strides are also\nversatile and can be completed on\nroad, track, turf or grass. Start slowly,\nthen build your speed over 10-30\nseconds to your max controlled speed\n(not all-out sprinting) then decelerate\nin the last few steps. Take full recovery\nbetween each one (90 seconds to 2\nminutes), so that lactic acid doesn't\nbuild up. Let your heart rate come\nback down by walking or slowly\njogging before starting again.\nCoaching cue: Try doing your strides\non a slight incline to improve form\nand stride efficiency while", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Tune into your body and run by effort\nwhile building your aerobic base. Aim\nfor an intensity where you can\ncomfortably maintain a conversation.\nThis effort level will be used for the\nmajority of your training runs to\nimprove your endurance.\nCoaching"}
{"chunk_id": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan_p0006_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_d0fd178b5458", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "92ea85677a8052f9", "text_span_hash": "92ea85677a8052f9", "source_file": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "rove your endurance.\nCoaching cue: Training volume will\ninfluence your perceived effort from\nweek to week. Feel free to make\nadjustments so these runs always feel\nlike a 3/4 on a scale of 1-10.\nRunning step-back week\nTaking a step-back week in runnin", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Tune into your body and run by effort\nwhile building your aerobic base. Aim\nfor an intensity where you can\ncomfortably maintain a conversation.\nThis effort level will be used for the\nmajority of your training runs to\nimprove your endurance.\nCoaching cue: Training volume will\ninfluence your perceived effort from\nweek to week. Feel free to make\nadjustments so these runs always feel\nlike a 3/4 on a scale of 1-10.\nRunning step-back week\nTaking a step-back week in running\nallows you to “step back” from\ntraining intensity, reflect on what\nyou’ve accomplished (both mentally\nand physically) and prepare for\ntraining intensity to come. These\nweeks also reduce your risk of injury.\nCoaching cue: Think of these weeks as\na nice mental reset button.\nStrides consist of brief intervals of\nfaster running followed by recovery\nperiods. Strides are important in\ntraining because they create an\nopportunity to work on running\nmechanics and form. Strides are also\nversatile and can be completed on\nroad, track, turf or grass. Start slowly,\nthen build your speed over 10-30\nseconds to your max controlled speed\n(not all-out sprinting) then decelerate\nin the last few steps. Take full recovery\nbetween each one (90 seconds to 2\nminutes), so that lactic acid doesn't\nbuild up. Let your heart rate come\nback down by walking or slowly\njogging before starting again.\nCoaching cue: Try doing your strides\non a slight incline to improve form\nand stride efficiency while", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "rove your endurance.\nCoaching cue: Training volume will\ninfluence your perceived effort from\nweek to week. Feel free to make\nadjustments so these runs always feel\nlike a 3/4 on a scale of 1-10.\nRunning step-back week\nTaking a step-back week in runnin"}
