last few steps. Take full recovery\nbetween each one (90 seconds to 2\nminutes), so that lactic acid doesn't\nbuild up. Let your heart rate come\nback down by walking or slowly\njogging before starting again.\nCoaching cue: Try doing your strides\non a slight incline to improve form\nand stride efficiency while", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ogging before starting again.\nCoaching cue: Try doing your strides\non a slight incline to improve form\nand stride efficiency while increasing\nstrength with less impact.\nIf possible, complete these runs on a\nsofter surface like groomed grass,\ntrack, t"}
{"chunk_id": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan_p0006_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_d0fd178b5458", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "07c2c220c98a2ef0", "text_span_hash": "07c2c220c98a2ef0", "source_file": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "you train at a lower\nintensity so your body can recover\nfrom the demands of higher-intensity\ntraining. You can do this by reducing\nthe volume of your training.\nCoaching cue: Just like the step-back\nweek, think of this lower intensity\nstrength-trainin", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Tune into your body and run by effort\nwhile building your aerobic base. Aim\nfor an intensity where you can\ncomfortably maintain a conversation.\nThis effort level will be used for the\nmajority of your training runs to\nimprove your endurance.\nCoaching cue: Training volume will\ninfluence your perceived effort from\nweek to week. Feel free to make\nadjustments so these runs always feel\nlike a 3/4 on a scale of 1-10.\nRunning step-back week\nTaking a step-back week in running\nallows you to “step back” from\ntraining intensity, reflect on what\nyou’ve accomplished (both mentally\nand physically) and prepare for\ntraining intensity to come. These\nweeks also reduce your risk of injury.\nCoaching cue: Think of these weeks as\na nice mental reset button.\nStrides consist of brief intervals of\nfaster running followed by recovery\nperiods. Strides are important in\ntraining because they create an\nopportunity to work on running\nmechanics and form. Strides are also\nversatile and can be completed on\nroad, track, turf or grass. Start slowly,\nthen build your speed over 10-30\nseconds to your max controlled speed\n(not all-out sprinting) then decelerate\nin the last few steps. Take full recovery\nbetween each one (90 seconds to 2\nminutes), so that lactic acid doesn't\nbuild up. Let your heart rate come\nback down by walking or slowly\njogging before starting again.\nCoaching cue: Try doing your strides\non a slight incline to improve form\nand stride efficiency while", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "you train at a lower\nintensity so your body can recover\nfrom the demands of higher-intensity\ntraining. You can do this by reducing\nthe volume of your training.\nCoaching cue: Just like the step-back\nweek, think of this lower intensity\nstrength-trainin"}
{"chunk_id": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan_p0006_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_d0fd178b5458", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "a91086752727a928", "text_span_hash": "a91086752727a928", "source_file": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "wer intensity\nstrength-training as a nice mental\nStrengthening the muscles that\nsupport your lower leg, foot and ankle\nand will help keep your ankle joints\nstable when running. You will see\nthese exercises alternate with mobility\nCoaching cue: A litt", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Tune into your body and run by effort\nwhile building your aerobic base. Aim\nfor an intensity where you can\ncomfortably maintain a conversation.\nThis effort level will be used for the\nmajority of your training runs to\nimprove your endurance.\nCoaching cue: Training volume will\ninfluence your perceived effort from\nweek to week. Feel free to make\nadjustments so these runs always feel\nlike a 3/4 on a scale of 1-10.\nRunning step-back week\nTaking a step-back week in running\nallows you to “step back” from\ntraining intensity, reflect on what\nyou’ve accomplished (both mentally\nand physically) and prepare for\ntraining intensity to come. These\nweeks also reduce your risk of injury.\nCoaching cue: Think of these weeks as\na nice mental reset button.\nStrides consist of brief intervals of\nfaster running followed by recovery\nperiods. Strides are important in\ntraining because they create an\nopportunity to work on running\nmechanics and form. Strides are also\nversatile and can be completed on\nroad, track, turf or grass. Start slowly,\nthen build your speed over 10-30\nseconds to your max controlled speed\n(not all-out sprinting) then decelerate\nin the last few steps. Take full recovery\nbetween each one (90 seconds to 2\nminutes), so that lactic acid doesn't\nbuild up. Let your heart rate come\nback down by walking or slowly\njogging before starting again.\nCoaching cue: Try doing your strides\non a slight incline to improve form\nand stride efficiency while", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "wer intensity\nstrength-training as a nice mental\nStrengthening the muscles that\nsupport your lower leg, foot and ankle\nand will help keep your ankle joints\nstable when running. You will see\nthese exercises alternate with mobility\nCoaching cue: A litt"}
{"chunk_id": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan_p0006_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_d0fd178b5458", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4b8adbf359c0df26", "text_span_hash": "4b8adbf359c0df26", "source_file": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "mobility\nCoaching cue: A little bit of time on\nthis goes a long way. Running is done\nwith two legs, but it’s a single-leg\nsport, so you need your feet and\nankles to be strong.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Tune into your body and run by effort\nwhile building your aerobic base. Aim\nfor an intensity where you can\ncomfortably maintain a conversation.\nThis effort level will be used for the\nmajority of your training runs to\nimprove your endurance.\nCoaching cue: Training volume will\ninfluence your perceived effort from\nweek to week. Feel free to make\nadjustments so these runs always feel\nlike a 3/4 on a scale of 1-10.\nRunning step-back week\nTaking a step-back week in running\nallows you to “step back” from\ntraining intensity, reflect on what\nyou’ve accomplished (both mentally\nand physically) and prepare for\ntraining intensity to come. These\nweeks also reduce your risk of injury.\nCoaching cue: Think of these weeks as\na nice mental reset button.\nStrides consist of brief intervals of\nfaster running followed by recovery\nperiods. Strides are important in\ntraining because they create an\nopportunity to work on running\nmechanics and form. Strides are also\nversatile and can be completed on\nroad, track, turf or grass. Start slowly,\nthen build your speed over 10-30\nseconds to your max controlled speed\n(not all-out sprinting) then decelerate\nin the last few steps. Take full recovery\nbetween each one (90 seconds to 2\nminutes), so that lactic acid doesn't\nbuild up. Let your heart rate come\nback down by walking or slowly\njogging before starting again.\nCoaching cue: Try doing your strides\non a slight incline to improve form\nand stride efficiency while", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "mobility\nCoaching cue: A little bit of time on\nthis goes a long way. Running is done\nwith two legs, but it’s a single-leg\nsport, so you need your feet and\nankles to be strong."}
{"chunk_id": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan_p0007_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_d0fd178b5458", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "d668796f192222db", "text_span_hash": "d668796f192222db", "source_file": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ide of your\nbody. Engaging your back, glutes, hamstrings, calves, etc.,\nincreases power critical for running.\nCancer survivor, amputee and world champion athlete who ran the NYC Marathon. Danielle is also an All Star on the\nTeam CAF softball team.\n“R", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Long runs are done over an extended distance or duration at a\nconstant pace and kept to an easy-effort intensity. It’s all\nabout time on your feet. Try to minimize long stops, and\npractice fueling and hydration during this run the way you\nCoaching cue: Towards the end of the run you might feel more\nchallenged due to fatigue—remember, that’s okay!\nMobility allows a joint to experience full, unrestricted range of\nmotion. It’s important because it reduces your risk of injury and\nallows your glutes to fire more effectively when running.\nCoaching cue: Mobility can be found on the Monday schedule,\nbut feel free to move it around, as needed, before a run or\nThese strength workouts are done on Tuesdays. They focus on\nthe anterior chain, which runs along the front side of your\nbody. By engaging your abdominals, hip flexors, quadriceps,\ntibialis, etc., these workouts build core stability, control knee\nposition and improve stability at the hip, which is critical for\nTotal body posterior\nThese strength workouts are done on Thursdays. They focus on\nthe posterior chain, which runs along the back side of your\nbody. Engaging your back, glutes, hamstrings, calves, etc.,\nincreases power critical for running.\nCancer survivor, amputee and world champion athlete who ran the NYC Marathon. Danielle is also an All Star on the\nTeam CAF softball team.\n“Running a marathon is all about your mental game. From Day 1, start sharpening it. I mean\nmentally co", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ide of your\nbody. Engaging your back, glutes, hamstrings, calves, etc.,\nincreases power critical for running.\nCancer survivor, amputee and world champion athlete who ran the NYC Marathon. Danielle is also an All Star on the\nTeam CAF softball team.\n“R"}
