], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "osterior workouts in weeks 1-5. Then, in weeks 6-12 you’ll follow Block 2. Block 2 also has weekly total\nbody anterior and posterior workouts, but it advances all of the exercises from Block 1 to challenge your body and allow your musculoskeletal sys"}
{"chunk_id": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan_p0014_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_d0fd178b5458", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5e7c361ec3e89bdf", "text_span_hash": "5e7c361ec3e89bdf", "source_file": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "page": 14, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "lunch will help to\nreplenish energy stores after a morning workout or busy morning\nroutine and provide sustained energy levels throughout the rest of\nPlanning ahead and making mindful choices will set you up for\nsuccess during your training journey.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Weekly Nutrition Tip:\nNavigating Lunchtime\nAt lunchtime, you may find yourself unsure of what to eat or may\ninadvertently skip a meal due to your schedule and time constraints.\nPrioritizing a balanced nourishing meal at lunch will help to\nreplenish energy stores after a morning workout or busy morning\nroutine and provide sustained energy levels throughout the rest of\nPlanning ahead and making mindful choices will set you up for\nsuccess during your training journey. Refer to the Base Training\nPerformance Plate (Page 2) for how to create balanced meals.\nIf you’re short on time, here is a quick idea:\nEggs & Greens Lunch with Smoothie\nCreate a balanced snack plate. Choose one or two\nitems from each category:\n Grains/Starches: Whole grain crackers, pita bread, pretzels,\n Protein: Hard-boiled egg, canned tuna, edamame, turkey deli\nmeat, smoked salmon, pumpkin seed\n Fruit/Vegetable: Sugar snap peas, mini cucumber, cherry\ntomatoes, blackberries, apple, orange, kiw\n Dairy: Glass of milk, Greek yogurt, string cheese, cream\n Fat: Hummus cup, almonds, cashews, pistachios, guacamole\nALISON MARIELLA DÉSIR\nAuthor of ‘Running While Black,’ activist, co-founder and\nformer co-chair of the Running Industry Diversity\nCoalition and founder of Harlem Run\n“1. Stretch! 2. Strength train. 3. Do not pile on\nworkouts. If you miss a workout, do not try to make\nup for it by doing more.”", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "lunch will help to\nreplenish energy stores after a morning workout or busy morning\nroutine and provide sustained energy levels throughout the rest of\nPlanning ahead and making mindful choices will set you up for\nsuccess during your training journey."}
{"chunk_id": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan_p0014_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_d0fd178b5458", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "08a2158f2394d0a3", "text_span_hash": "08a2158f2394d0a3", "source_file": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "page": 14, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "MARIELLA DÉSIR\nAuthor of ‘Running While Black,’ activist, co-founder and\nformer co-chair of the Running Industry Diversity\nCoalition and founder of Harlem Run\n“1. Stretch! 2. Strength train. 3. Do not pile on\nworkouts. If you miss a workout, do not t", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Weekly Nutrition Tip:\nNavigating Lunchtime\nAt lunchtime, you may find yourself unsure of what to eat or may\ninadvertently skip a meal due to your schedule and time constraints.\nPrioritizing a balanced nourishing meal at lunch will help to\nreplenish energy stores after a morning workout or busy morning\nroutine and provide sustained energy levels throughout the rest of\nPlanning ahead and making mindful choices will set you up for\nsuccess during your training journey. Refer to the Base Training\nPerformance Plate (Page 2) for how to create balanced meals.\nIf you’re short on time, here is a quick idea:\nEggs & Greens Lunch with Smoothie\nCreate a balanced snack plate. Choose one or two\nitems from each category:\n Grains/Starches: Whole grain crackers, pita bread, pretzels,\n Protein: Hard-boiled egg, canned tuna, edamame, turkey deli\nmeat, smoked salmon, pumpkin seed\n Fruit/Vegetable: Sugar snap peas, mini cucumber, cherry\ntomatoes, blackberries, apple, orange, kiw\n Dairy: Glass of milk, Greek yogurt, string cheese, cream\n Fat: Hummus cup, almonds, cashews, pistachios, guacamole\nALISON MARIELLA DÉSIR\nAuthor of ‘Running While Black,’ activist, co-founder and\nformer co-chair of the Running Industry Diversity\nCoalition and founder of Harlem Run\n“1. Stretch! 2. Strength train. 3. Do not pile on\nworkouts. If you miss a workout, do not try to make\nup for it by doing more.”", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "MARIELLA DÉSIR\nAuthor of ‘Running While Black,’ activist, co-founder and\nformer co-chair of the Running Industry Diversity\nCoalition and founder of Harlem Run\n“1. Stretch! 2. Strength train. 3. Do not pile on\nworkouts. If you miss a workout, do not t"}
{"chunk_id": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan_p0014_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_d0fd178b5458", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0919cf9ad6526d6a", "text_span_hash": "0919cf9ad6526d6a", "source_file": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "page": 14, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "f you miss a workout, do not try to make\nup for it by doing more.”", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Weekly Nutrition Tip:\nNavigating Lunchtime\nAt lunchtime, you may find yourself unsure of what to eat or may\ninadvertently skip a meal due to your schedule and time constraints.\nPrioritizing a balanced nourishing meal at lunch will help to\nreplenish energy stores after a morning workout or busy morning\nroutine and provide sustained energy levels throughout the rest of\nPlanning ahead and making mindful choices will set you up for\nsuccess during your training journey. Refer to the Base Training\nPerformance Plate (Page 2) for how to create balanced meals.\nIf you’re short on time, here is a quick idea:\nEggs & Greens Lunch with Smoothie\nCreate a balanced snack plate. Choose one or two\nitems from each category:\n Grains/Starches: Whole grain crackers, pita bread, pretzels,\n Protein: Hard-boiled egg, canned tuna, edamame, turkey deli\nmeat, smoked salmon, pumpkin seed\n Fruit/Vegetable: Sugar snap peas, mini cucumber, cherry\ntomatoes, blackberries, apple, orange, kiw\n Dairy: Glass of milk, Greek yogurt, string cheese, cream\n Fat: Hummus cup, almonds, cashews, pistachios, guacamole\nALISON MARIELLA DÉSIR\nAuthor of ‘Running While Black,’ activist, co-founder and\nformer co-chair of the Running Industry Diversity\nCoalition and founder of Harlem Run\n“1. Stretch! 2. Strength train. 3. Do not pile on\nworkouts. If you miss a workout, do not try to make\nup for it by doing more.”", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "f you miss a workout, do not try to make\nup for it by doing more.”"}
{"chunk_id": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan_p0015_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_d0fd178b5458", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c292a736888742a9", "text_span_hash": "c292a736888742a9", "source_file": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "page": 15, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "to eat your pre-run fuel of choice at\nleast 30 to 60 minutes before you start running.\nFor example, eat half of an applesauce pouch or one graham\ncracker square 30 to 60 minutes before your run (see visual).\nContinue with this portion for 3 to 5 days", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "strides at the end of\nsoft surface 2 miles\nWeekly Nutrition Tip:\nto eat before a run\nAs mileage increases, it'll be important to start fueling before your\nlong runs for an extra energy boost. You’ll want to train your\nstomach to digest simple carbohydrate foods to avoid stomach\nproblems. Foods like applesauce or graham crackers are typically\nwell tolerated. Start with a small portion and work your way up as\nyour mileage increases. Plan to eat your pre-run fuel of choice at\nleast 30 to 60 minutes before you start running.\nFor example, eat half of an applesauce pouch or one graham\ncracker square 30 to 60 minutes before your run (see visual).\nContinue with this portion for 3 to 5 days or once you can tolerate\nand feel comfortable with it. Then, increase the portion to a full\ngraham cracker or one applesauce pouch. Continue increasing the\ncarbohydrates you eat pre-run until you reach about 30 grams.\nHOW TO TRAIN YOUR STOMACH\nIncrease carbs every 3 to 5 days\nor once you tolerate/feel comfortable", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "to eat your pre-run fuel of choice at\nleast 30 to 60 minutes before you start running.\nFor example, eat half of an applesauce pouch or one graham\ncracker square 30 to 60 minutes before your run (see visual).\nContinue with this portion for 3 to 5 days"}
{"chunk_id": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan_p0018_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_d0fd178b5458", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "77004df8bfb3afac", "text_span_hash": "77004df8bfb3afac", "source_file": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "page": 18, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "on\n“Like all runners, success of completion is\nbased not on talent but on consistency of\ndaily training. Magic only happens when\nyou put in the training, so record your\ntraining; it keeps you honest.”\nConsider keeping a food diary or journal.\nIn your", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "strides at the end of\nWeekly Nutrition Tip:\nStart to pay attention to how different foods make you feel during\nyour runs. The foods you eat throughout the day will lay the\nfoundation in fueling your body for your next run or workout. For\nexample, eating a high-fat meal the night before your run may\nmake you feel sluggish the next morning. Eating a more balanced\nmeal (as shown in the performance plates) with whole grains,\nprotein, healthy fats and fruits and vegetables can help you feel\nmore energized during your run.\nSome runners may find they perform better when eating only\ncarbohydrate foods (i.e., a banana) before a run, while others may\nwith peanut butter). Everyone's nutritional needs are unique, so\nexperiment with different foods to see what works best for you.\nAvoid trying new foods right before a long run.\nFirst woman to officially compete in the Boston Marathon\n“Like all runners, success of completion is\nbased not on talent but on consistency of\ndaily training. Magic only happens when\nyou put in the training, so record your\ntraining; it keeps you honest.”\nConsider keeping a food diary or journal.\nIn your training log or a separate food diary, keep track of how different foods make you feel and how they impact your running\nperformance. Note what you eat before and after runs, as well as any symptoms or changes in energy levels that you experience. Over\ntime, you'll start to identify patterns and make informed decisions", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "on\n“Like all runners, success of completion is\nbased not on talent but on consistency of\ndaily training. Magic only happens when\nyou put in the training, so record your\ntraining; it keeps you honest.”\nConsider keeping a food diary or journal.\nIn your"}
{"chunk_id": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan_p0019_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_d0fd178b5458", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "cc588b7167cf0ab4", "text_span_hash": "cc588b7167cf0ab4", "source_file": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "page": 19, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ews or food) with you to keep your\nenergy level up and avoid feeling exhausted by the end of your\nALISON MARIELLA DÉSIR\nAuthor of ‘Running While Black,’ activist, co-founder and\nformer co-chair of the Running Industry Diversity\nCoalition and founder", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "strides at the end of\nWeekly Nutrition Tip:\nfueling during my long\nOnce your weekly long run becomes longer than 60-70 minutes at\nonce, you’ll want to start bringing water, electrolytes and\ncarbohydrates (energy gels, chews or food) with you to keep your\nenergy level up and avoid feeling exhausted by the end of your\nALISON MARIELLA DÉSIR\nAuthor of ‘Running While Black,’ activist, co-founder and\nformer co-chair of the Running Industry Diversity\nCoalition and founder of Harlem Run\n“Use every long run as preparation for race\nday. Try to run your long runs at the same\ntime as the race. Eat the same food before\nyour long run. That way race day feels like\njust another long run you've rehearsed.”\nFollow these steps to get started:\nâõ Step 1: Choose one of the following:æ\nß Food (jelly beans, sugary candy, applesauce pouch, datesÝ\nÊõ Step 2: 30-45 minutes into your long run, take half of an energy gel or four-five jelly beans (aim for about 8-15 g of carbohydrates)\nand drink with water. (Note: You can drink a carb/electrolyte drink with your gels instead of plain water, but you’ll have to train your\nstomach to do so first.Ý\n»õ Step 3: Aim to drink 6-20+ oz fluids within 1 hour.", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ews or food) with you to keep your\nenergy level up and avoid feeling exhausted by the end of your\nALISON MARIELLA DÉSIR\nAuthor of ‘Running While Black,’ activist, co-founder and\nformer co-chair of the Running Industry Diversity\nCoalition and founder"}
{"chunk_id": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan_p0019_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_d0fd178b5458", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "d3bc06ff7bbd7c51", "text_span_hash": "d3bc06ff7bbd7c51", "source_file": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "page": 19, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "st another long run you've rehearsed.”\nFollow these steps to get started:\nâõ Step 1: Choose one of the following:æ\nß Food (jelly beans, sugary candy, applesauce pouch, datesÝ\nÊõ Step 2: 30-45 minutes into your long run, take half of an energy gel or", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "strides at the end of\nWeekly Nutrition Tip:\nfueling during my long\nOnce your weekly long run becomes longer than 60-70 minutes at\nonce, you’ll want to start bringing water, electrolytes and\ncarbohydrates (energy gels, chews or food) with you to keep your\nenergy level up and avoid feeling exhausted by the end of your\nALISON MARIELLA DÉSIR\nAuthor of ‘Running While Black,’ activist, co-founder and\nformer co-chair of the Running Industry Diversity\nCoalition and founder of Harlem Run\n“Use every long run as preparation for race\nday. Try to run your long runs at the same\ntime as the race. Eat the same food before\nyour long run. That way race day feels like\njust another long run you've rehearsed.”\nFollow these steps to get started:\nâõ Step 1: Choose one of the following:æ\nß Food (jelly beans, sugary candy, applesauce pouch, datesÝ\nÊõ Step 2: 30-45 minutes into your long run, take half of an energy gel or four-five jelly beans (aim for about 8-15 g of carbohydrates)\nand drink with water. (Note: You can drink a carb/electrolyte drink with your gels instead of plain water, but you’ll have to train your\nstomach to do so first.Ý\n»õ Step 3: Aim to drink 6-20+ oz fluids within 1 hour.", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "st another long run you've rehearsed.”\nFollow these steps to get started:\nâõ Step 1: Choose one of the following:æ\nß Food (jelly beans, sugary candy, applesauce pouch, datesÝ\nÊõ Step 2: 30-45 minutes into your long run, take half of an energy gel or"}
{"chunk_id": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan_p0022_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_d0fd178b5458", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "dbcfe3413a609e8e", "text_span_hash": "dbcfe3413a609e8e", "source_file": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "page": 22, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "erican in 33 years to\nwin the Boston Marathon (women’s category)\n“1. Prioritize sleep. 2. Splurge on a massage\nor two. 3. Easy days are easy—hard days\nare hard. If an easy day is scheduled, take\nadvantage of it and make sure you're\nWithin 30-60 minut", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "strides at the end of\nWeekly Nutrition Tip:\nOn rest or recovery days, especially after a long run, you may find\nyourself hungrier. This is completely normal as your body is\nreplenishing energy stores and supporting muscle repair from the\nday before. Focus on nourishing your body with nutrient-dense\nfoods, including whole grains, lean proteins, fruits, vegetables,\nhealthy fats and dairy foods like milk.\nTwo-time Olympian and the first American in 33 years to\nwin the Boston Marathon (women’s category)\n“1. Prioritize sleep. 2. Splurge on a massage\nor two. 3. Easy days are easy—hard days\nare hard. If an easy day is scheduled, take\nadvantage of it and make sure you're\nWithin 30-60 minutes after a long run, drink a glass of chocolate milk. It contains the right balance of carbs and protein, plus fluids\nand electrolytes, which are scientifically proven to help refuel exhausted muscles and prevent next-day muscle soreness.\nNote from Expert Alysha Flynn:\n“Avoid running too hard as you follow this plan. Avoiding this will help you steer clear of\nbreakdown from fatigue as your body adapts to the mileage and strength-training routine.”", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "erican in 33 years to\nwin the Boston Marathon (women’s category)\n“1. Prioritize sleep. 2. Splurge on a massage\nor two. 3. Easy days are easy—hard days\nare hard. If an easy day is scheduled, take\nadvantage of it and make sure you're\nWithin 30-60 minut"}
{"chunk_id": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan_p0022_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_d0fd178b5458", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b92c560a958ece9c", "text_span_hash": "b92c560a958ece9c", "source_file": "EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\EWM-Training-Guides-Base-Plan.pdf", "page": 22, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "e mileage and strength-training routine.”", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "strides at the end of\nWeekly Nutrition Tip:\nOn rest or recovery days, especially after a long run, you may find\nyourself hungrier. This is completely normal as your body is\nreplenishing energy stores and supporting muscle repair from the\nday before. Focus on nourishing your body with nutrient-dense\nfoods, including whole grains, lean proteins, fruits, vegetables,\nhealthy fats and dairy foods like milk.\nTwo-time Olympian and the first American in 33 years to\nwin the Boston Marathon (women’s category)\n“1. Prioritize sleep. 2. Splurge on a massage\nor two. 3. Easy days are easy—hard days\nare hard. If an easy day is scheduled, take\nadvantage of it and make sure you're\nWithin 30-60 minutes after a long run, drink a glass of chocolate milk. It contains the right balance of carbs and protein, plus fluids\nand electrolytes, which are scientifically proven to help refuel exhausted muscles and prevent next-day muscle soreness.\nNote from Expert Alysha Flynn:\n“Avoid running too hard as you follow this plan. Avoiding this will help you steer clear of\nbreakdown from fatigue as your body adapts to the mileage and strength-training routine.”", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "e mileage and strength-training routine.”"}
{"chunk_id": "Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022_p0001_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_5d8ac1378426", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "eb27da3703a7b1d8", "text_span_hash": "eb27da3703a7b1d8", "source_file": "Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "developed by chronic exposure to habitual me-\nchanical loading from daily exercise, which is associated with greater energy storage, release and overall\nAbstract: The morphological and mechanical properties (e.g., stiffness, stress, and force) of the", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "www.mdpi.com/journal/biology\nExercise Effects on the Biomechanical Properties of the Achilles\nTendon—A Narrative Review\nChangxiao Yu 1, Liqin Deng 1, Li Li 2, Xini Zhang 1,* and Weijie Fu 1,3,*\n1 Key Laboratory of Exercise and Health Sciences of Ministry of Education, School of Kinesiology, Shanghai\nUniversity of Sport, Shanghai 200438, China; yuchangxiao1014@163.com (C.Y.);\n2 Department of Health Sciences and Kinesiology, Georgia Southern University, Statesboro, GA 30460, USA;\nlili@georgiasouthern.edu\n3 Shanghai Frontiers Science Research Base of Exercise and Metabolic Health, School of Kinesiology,\nShanghai University of Sport, Shanghai 200438, China\nSimple Summary: The Achilles tendon influences the running economy because of its ability to store\nand release strain energy, and it remains one of the most vulnerable tendons among athletes and recrea-\ntional runners. Exercised-related mechanical loading appears to induce changes in the Achilles tendon\nmorphology and mechanical material properties. Both acute and relatively long-term exercise induces\ntendon adaptation, although biomechanical changes, e.g., cross-sectional area, plantarflexion moment,\nYoung’s modulus, and stiffness, in response to exercise duration, type, and loading-regimes differ\nwidely. Furthermore, a strong Achilles tendon can be developed by chronic exposure to habitual me-\nchanical loading from daily exercise, which is associated with greater energy storag", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "developed by chronic exposure to habitual me-\nchanical loading from daily exercise, which is associated with greater energy storage, release and overall\nAbstract: The morphological and mechanical properties (e.g., stiffness, stress, and force) of the"}
{"chunk_id": "Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022_p0001_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_5d8ac1378426", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b71fa4bf412e2d63", "text_span_hash": "b71fa4bf412e2d63", "source_file": "Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "e showing the possibility to influence the mechanical properties of the AT from the perspec-\ntive of acute exercise and long-term training interventions, and we discuss the reasons for incon-\nsistent results. Finally, we review the role of the habitu", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "www.mdpi.com/journal/biology\nExercise Effects on the Biomechanical Properties of the Achilles\nTendon—A Narrative Review\nChangxiao Yu 1, Liqin Deng 1, Li Li 2, Xini Zhang 1,* and Weijie Fu 1,3,*\n1 Key Laboratory of Exercise and Health Sciences of Ministry of Education, School of Kinesiology, Shanghai\nUniversity of Sport, Shanghai 200438, China; yuchangxiao1014@163.com (C.Y.);\n2 Department of Health Sciences and Kinesiology, Georgia Southern University, Statesboro, GA 30460, USA;\nlili@georgiasouthern.edu\n3 Shanghai Frontiers Science Research Base of Exercise and Metabolic Health, School of Kinesiology,\nShanghai University of Sport, Shanghai 200438, China\nSimple Summary: The Achilles tendon influences the running economy because of its ability to store\nand release strain energy, and it remains one of the most vulnerable tendons among athletes and recrea-\ntional runners. Exercised-related mechanical loading appears to induce changes in the Achilles tendon\nmorphology and mechanical material properties. Both acute and relatively long-term exercise induces\ntendon adaptation, although biomechanical changes, e.g., cross-sectional area, plantarflexion moment,\nYoung’s modulus, and stiffness, in response to exercise duration, type, and loading-regimes differ\nwidely. Furthermore, a strong Achilles tendon can be developed by chronic exposure to habitual me-\nchanical loading from daily exercise, which is associated with greater energy storag", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "e showing the possibility to influence the mechanical properties of the AT from the perspec-\ntive of acute exercise and long-term training interventions, and we discuss the reasons for incon-\nsistent results. Finally, we review the role of the habitu"}
{"chunk_id": "Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022_p0002_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_5d8ac1378426", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f16dae6cb2a3b5b0", "text_span_hash": "f16dae6cb2a3b5b0", "source_file": "Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "es [11,12] identified\nseveral factors related to Achilles tendinopathy in an active, athletic population. These\nincluded previous tendinopathy or injuries, advancing age, sex, and antibiotic treatment.\nMoreover, training errors, hard running surfaces", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "internal plantar flexor moment that occurs at the ankle during the stance phase of running\n[5]. Previous studies have shown that the AT can load up to four times the body’s weight\nwhen walking, and 12.5 times the body’s weight when running and jumping [6–8].\nAs one of the most common sports injuries, Achilles tendinopathy accounts for 8–\n15% of all running injuries [9], with a high lifetime risk of 52% among elite male runners\n[10]. This injury will compromise sports performance, may prevent active, athletic indi-\nviduals from participating in physical activities, can interfere with daily living, and may\npossibly lead to tendon rupture [11,12]. Unfortunately, conservative treatment is not al-\nways successful and can lead to surgical intervention (25–45% of patients) [13], with a cost\nranging from $10,000 to as much as $31,000 (https://www.howmuchisit.org/achilles-ten-\ndon-surgery-cost/) (accessed on 1 December 2021). In 3–5% of cases, it can be career-end-\ning for athletes, resulting in a health and personal cost both immediately and in the future\n[7]. From recreational to elite athletes, the reduction in physical activity can result in over-\nall physical deconditioning and can increase the risk of cardiovascular problems in time\nAchilles tendinopathy causes pain, swelling, and thickening in the AT, and ulti-\nmately impairs health-promoting activities [17]. It most often occurs in the mid-substance\nof the tendon (55–65%) and l", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "es [11,12] identified\nseveral factors related to Achilles tendinopathy in an active, athletic population. These\nincluded previous tendinopathy or injuries, advancing age, sex, and antibiotic treatment.\nMoreover, training errors, hard running surfaces"}
