{"chunk_id": "Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022_p0007_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_5d8ac1378426", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "97c5f6c1f59b2e9a", "text_span_hash": "97c5f6c1f59b2e9a", "source_file": "Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "hin 3 months. However, one study [49] showed that AT stiffness\nincreased significantly by 18.8 ± 10.4% for 8 weeks (4 days/w) con-ecc weight (reps) train-\ning with 70% RM. These results suggested that resistance training can effectively improve\nthe s", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "(30–50 min habitual running for 2–3 times per week) in previously untrained subjects did\nanother study [57] was to determine the effect of transition of running style on the AT\nmechanical properties over a 12-week intervention, its results seem to demonstrate an in-\ncrease in AT properties (stiffness, module, CSA) in men after a 12-week minimalist run-\nning transition program and 6-month follow-up. The viscoelastic property of the AT plays\na crucial role in improving stiffness and running economy while transitioning to the fore-\nfoot strike pattern given the shortened contact time and contracting velocity of plantar\nflexors [57]. In agreement with the recent findings, Zhang et al. [51] investigated the effect\nof 12-week transition training on the AT loading; they found the peak AT force could\nsignificantly increase for habitual rearfoot strikers when they were switching to the fore-\nfoot strike pattern with minimalist shoes compared with the control group who could\nchoose the strike pattern after 12-week transition training. In addition, a study reported that\nafter 6 months of repeated 2 km runs, as one type of rigorous endurance training program,\nthe new elite infantries had a significant increase in the AT CSA [58] (see Table 2).\nLastly, regarding the duration of the exercise intervention, most of the above-men-\ntioned studies featuring an exercise duration of 12–14 weeks found significant adaptations\nof AT properties [30,52", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "hin 3 months. However, one study [49] showed that AT stiffness\nincreased significantly by 18.8 ± 10.4% for 8 weeks (4 days/w) con-ecc weight (reps) train-\ning with 70% RM. These results suggested that resistance training can effectively improve\nthe s"}
{"chunk_id": "Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022_p0008_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_5d8ac1378426", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e38a25c08c7bb83b", "text_span_hash": "e38a25c08c7bb83b", "source_file": "Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Table 3. The differences in the AT stiffness, Young’s modulus, CSA and strength between runners\nNote: M/W, men/women. CSA, the cross-sectional area. N/D, not described. MRI, magnetic reso-\nnance imaging. *, statistically significant differences betwe", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Table 3. The differences in the AT stiffness, Young’s modulus, CSA and strength between runners\nNote: M/W, men/women. CSA, the cross-sectional area. N/D, not described. MRI, magnetic reso-\nnance imaging. *, statistically significant differences between runners or sprinters and other\nThe influence of mechanical loading associated with exercise on the AT properties\nhas been assessed by comparing the AT between cohorts of individuals habitually ex-\nposed to different activities. Male runners were shown to have a significantly larger CSA\n[75], especially at the most distal part of the tendon (+36.0%) [74]; while the sprinters also\nhad higher AT stiffness and maximal tendon forces than the endurance runners and non-\nactive participants [54]. Furthermore, male athletes who frequently performed weight-\nbearing exercises (e.g., running, jumping) were found to have a larger AT CSA than ath-\nletes in non-weight-bearing sports (e.g., kayakers) [76]. This study reported that the in-\ncreased AT CSA would be subjected to intermittent high-tension tendon loading. How-\never, the effect of different training methods on tendon characteristics and injury risk\nneeds further study. Another study found that the AT in the jump leg of male collegiate-\nlevel jumpers was much stiffer (17.8% and 24.4% greater stiffness and Young’s modulus,\nrespectively) compared to the non-jump leg [77]. From the perspective of pathology, the\nincreased AT stiffness can ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Table 3. The differences in the AT stiffness, Young’s modulus, CSA and strength between runners\nNote: M/W, men/women. CSA, the cross-sectional area. N/D, not described. MRI, magnetic reso-\nnance imaging. *, statistically significant differences betwe"}
{"chunk_id": "Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022_p0008_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_5d8ac1378426", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "30d3bfccf6606b4f", "text_span_hash": "30d3bfccf6606b4f", "source_file": "Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "significant differences between runners or sprinters and other\nThe influence of mechanical loading associated with exercise on the AT properties\nhas been assessed by comparing the AT between cohorts of individuals habitually ex-\nposed to different a", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Table 3. The differences in the AT stiffness, Young’s modulus, CSA and strength between runners\nNote: M/W, men/women. CSA, the cross-sectional area. N/D, not described. MRI, magnetic reso-\nnance imaging. *, statistically significant differences between runners or sprinters and other\nThe influence of mechanical loading associated with exercise on the AT properties\nhas been assessed by comparing the AT between cohorts of individuals habitually ex-\nposed to different activities. Male runners were shown to have a significantly larger CSA\n[75], especially at the most distal part of the tendon (+36.0%) [74]; while the sprinters also\nhad higher AT stiffness and maximal tendon forces than the endurance runners and non-\nactive participants [54]. Furthermore, male athletes who frequently performed weight-\nbearing exercises (e.g., running, jumping) were found to have a larger AT CSA than ath-\nletes in non-weight-bearing sports (e.g., kayakers) [76]. This study reported that the in-\ncreased AT CSA would be subjected to intermittent high-tension tendon loading. How-\never, the effect of different training methods on tendon characteristics and injury risk\nneeds further study. Another study found that the AT in the jump leg of male collegiate-\nlevel jumpers was much stiffer (17.8% and 24.4% greater stiffness and Young’s modulus,\nrespectively) compared to the non-jump leg [77]. From the perspective of pathology, the\nincreased AT stiffness can ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "significant differences between runners or sprinters and other\nThe influence of mechanical loading associated with exercise on the AT properties\nhas been assessed by comparing the AT between cohorts of individuals habitually ex-\nposed to different a"}
{"chunk_id": "Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022_p0008_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_5d8ac1378426", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "dcc72b070fad2156", "text_span_hash": "dcc72b070fad2156", "source_file": "Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "id-\nuals in the running, jumping, and sprinting events represent a favorable adaptation in\nresponse to the habitual loading of running. Regarding running, among the runners with\ndifferent foot strike patterns (FSP: forefoot—FFS, midfoot—MFS, and rear", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Table 3. The differences in the AT stiffness, Young’s modulus, CSA and strength between runners\nNote: M/W, men/women. CSA, the cross-sectional area. N/D, not described. MRI, magnetic reso-\nnance imaging. *, statistically significant differences between runners or sprinters and other\nThe influence of mechanical loading associated with exercise on the AT properties\nhas been assessed by comparing the AT between cohorts of individuals habitually ex-\nposed to different activities. Male runners were shown to have a significantly larger CSA\n[75], especially at the most distal part of the tendon (+36.0%) [74]; while the sprinters also\nhad higher AT stiffness and maximal tendon forces than the endurance runners and non-\nactive participants [54]. Furthermore, male athletes who frequently performed weight-\nbearing exercises (e.g., running, jumping) were found to have a larger AT CSA than ath-\nletes in non-weight-bearing sports (e.g., kayakers) [76]. This study reported that the in-\ncreased AT CSA would be subjected to intermittent high-tension tendon loading. How-\never, the effect of different training methods on tendon characteristics and injury risk\nneeds further study. Another study found that the AT in the jump leg of male collegiate-\nlevel jumpers was much stiffer (17.8% and 24.4% greater stiffness and Young’s modulus,\nrespectively) compared to the non-jump leg [77]. From the perspective of pathology, the\nincreased AT stiffness can ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "id-\nuals in the running, jumping, and sprinting events represent a favorable adaptation in\nresponse to the habitual loading of running. Regarding running, among the runners with\ndifferent foot strike patterns (FSP: forefoot—FFS, midfoot—MFS, and rear"}
{"chunk_id": "Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022_p0008_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_5d8ac1378426", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "a1a047e6c358fd1e", "text_span_hash": "a1a047e6c358fd1e", "source_file": "Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "oot—FFS, midfoot—MFS, and rearfoot—RFS),\nKernozek et al. [79] found habitual FFS/MFS runners did not have greater CSA despite\nhigher AT loading according to a cross-sectional study that was conducted by recruiting\nfemale runners. In addition, Kubo et", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Table 3. The differences in the AT stiffness, Young’s modulus, CSA and strength between runners\nNote: M/W, men/women. CSA, the cross-sectional area. N/D, not described. MRI, magnetic reso-\nnance imaging. *, statistically significant differences between runners or sprinters and other\nThe influence of mechanical loading associated with exercise on the AT properties\nhas been assessed by comparing the AT between cohorts of individuals habitually ex-\nposed to different activities. Male runners were shown to have a significantly larger CSA\n[75], especially at the most distal part of the tendon (+36.0%) [74]; while the sprinters also\nhad higher AT stiffness and maximal tendon forces than the endurance runners and non-\nactive participants [54]. Furthermore, male athletes who frequently performed weight-\nbearing exercises (e.g., running, jumping) were found to have a larger AT CSA than ath-\nletes in non-weight-bearing sports (e.g., kayakers) [76]. This study reported that the in-\ncreased AT CSA would be subjected to intermittent high-tension tendon loading. How-\never, the effect of different training methods on tendon characteristics and injury risk\nneeds further study. Another study found that the AT in the jump leg of male collegiate-\nlevel jumpers was much stiffer (17.8% and 24.4% greater stiffness and Young’s modulus,\nrespectively) compared to the non-jump leg [77]. From the perspective of pathology, the\nincreased AT stiffness can ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "oot—FFS, midfoot—MFS, and rearfoot—RFS),\nKernozek et al. [79] found habitual FFS/MFS runners did not have greater CSA despite\nhigher AT loading according to a cross-sectional study that was conducted by recruiting\nfemale runners. In addition, Kubo et"}
{"chunk_id": "Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022_p0008_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_5d8ac1378426", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5bfae7f72fef50e7", "text_span_hash": "5bfae7f72fef50e7", "source_file": "Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "runners. In addition, Kubo et al. [47] reported no significant differences in the CSA\nand stiffness of AT by recruiting trained male long-distance participants. It is, however,\nnoteworthy that the FSP during shod running was determined at a velocity", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Table 3. The differences in the AT stiffness, Young’s modulus, CSA and strength between runners\nNote: M/W, men/women. CSA, the cross-sectional area. N/D, not described. MRI, magnetic reso-\nnance imaging. *, statistically significant differences between runners or sprinters and other\nThe influence of mechanical loading associated with exercise on the AT properties\nhas been assessed by comparing the AT between cohorts of individuals habitually ex-\nposed to different activities. Male runners were shown to have a significantly larger CSA\n[75], especially at the most distal part of the tendon (+36.0%) [74]; while the sprinters also\nhad higher AT stiffness and maximal tendon forces than the endurance runners and non-\nactive participants [54]. Furthermore, male athletes who frequently performed weight-\nbearing exercises (e.g., running, jumping) were found to have a larger AT CSA than ath-\nletes in non-weight-bearing sports (e.g., kayakers) [76]. This study reported that the in-\ncreased AT CSA would be subjected to intermittent high-tension tendon loading. How-\never, the effect of different training methods on tendon characteristics and injury risk\nneeds further study. Another study found that the AT in the jump leg of male collegiate-\nlevel jumpers was much stiffer (17.8% and 24.4% greater stiffness and Young’s modulus,\nrespectively) compared to the non-jump leg [77]. From the perspective of pathology, the\nincreased AT stiffness can ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "runners. In addition, Kubo et al. [47] reported no significant differences in the CSA\nand stiffness of AT by recruiting trained male long-distance participants. It is, however,\nnoteworthy that the FSP during shod running was determined at a velocity"}
{"chunk_id": "Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022_p0008_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_5d8ac1378426", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "50ac608745dc3020", "text_span_hash": "50ac608745dc3020", "source_file": "Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "g was determined at a velocity of 18 km/h\nwhich was much higher than most of the other running studies. Being inconsistent with\nKubo et al., a previous study [80] showed the AT in minimalist shod runners (MFS/FFS)\nadapted by increasing +9.2% CSA, +90", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Table 3. The differences in the AT stiffness, Young’s modulus, CSA and strength between runners\nNote: M/W, men/women. CSA, the cross-sectional area. N/D, not described. MRI, magnetic reso-\nnance imaging. *, statistically significant differences between runners or sprinters and other\nThe influence of mechanical loading associated with exercise on the AT properties\nhas been assessed by comparing the AT between cohorts of individuals habitually ex-\nposed to different activities. Male runners were shown to have a significantly larger CSA\n[75], especially at the most distal part of the tendon (+36.0%) [74]; while the sprinters also\nhad higher AT stiffness and maximal tendon forces than the endurance runners and non-\nactive participants [54]. Furthermore, male athletes who frequently performed weight-\nbearing exercises (e.g., running, jumping) were found to have a larger AT CSA than ath-\nletes in non-weight-bearing sports (e.g., kayakers) [76]. This study reported that the in-\ncreased AT CSA would be subjected to intermittent high-tension tendon loading. How-\never, the effect of different training methods on tendon characteristics and injury risk\nneeds further study. Another study found that the AT in the jump leg of male collegiate-\nlevel jumpers was much stiffer (17.8% and 24.4% greater stiffness and Young’s modulus,\nrespectively) compared to the non-jump leg [77]. From the perspective of pathology, the\nincreased AT stiffness can ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "g was determined at a velocity of 18 km/h\nwhich was much higher than most of the other running studies. Being inconsistent with\nKubo et al., a previous study [80] showed the AT in minimalist shod runners (MFS/FFS)\nadapted by increasing +9.2% CSA, +90"}
{"chunk_id": "Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022_p0009_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_5d8ac1378426", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5e22a7b4b3384352", "text_span_hash": "5e22a7b4b3384352", "source_file": "Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "r the acute exercise intervention.\nMoreover, there are gender-specific effects in response to the acute loading. Specifically,\nthe stiffness and Young’s modulus of the AT show significantly lower in women com-\npared to men, which may be an important", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "pared to traditionally shod runners (RFS), which seems more in accordance with the un-\nderstanding of mechanical adaptation of the tendon to different AT loading patterns\namong runners with different FSP [81]. This inspired us that multiple exercise and training\nmodalities combined with a step-by-step approach could lead to AT biomechanical adap-\ntations, so may do minimalist running.\nFirstly, high compliance of the AT was observed after the acute exercise intervention.\nMoreover, there are gender-specific effects in response to the acute loading. Specifically,\nthe stiffness and Young’s modulus of the AT show significantly lower in women com-\npared to men, which may be an important cause of fewer AT injuries in women. Secondly,\nperiodic plyometric training can improve the morphological adaptability of the AT, but\nthe exercise type, intensity, and duration are the three key factors that restrict the effec-\ntiveness of training. Finally, habitual running can effectively improve the mechanical\nproperties of the AT (e.g., greater stiffness, CSA, and Young’s modulus). In particular, the\nhigh stiffness of the AT is conducive to enhancing the transmission of muscle strength,\nthereby improving the running economy. Future research is required to explore more\ntraining methods to improve the biomechanical adaptability of the AT from the perspec-\ntive of mechanism and multiple influencing factors and reduce the risk of AT-related in-\nAutho", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "r the acute exercise intervention.\nMoreover, there are gender-specific effects in response to the acute loading. Specifically,\nthe stiffness and Young’s modulus of the AT show significantly lower in women com-\npared to men, which may be an important"}
{"chunk_id": "Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022_p0009_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_5d8ac1378426", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f4031a79659957b7", "text_span_hash": "f4031a79659957b7", "source_file": "Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "en, which may be an important cause of fewer AT injuries in women. Secondly,\nperiodic plyometric training can improve the morphological adaptability of the AT, but\nthe exercise type, intensity, and duration are the three key factors that restrict the", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "pared to traditionally shod runners (RFS), which seems more in accordance with the un-\nderstanding of mechanical adaptation of the tendon to different AT loading patterns\namong runners with different FSP [81]. This inspired us that multiple exercise and training\nmodalities combined with a step-by-step approach could lead to AT biomechanical adap-\ntations, so may do minimalist running.\nFirstly, high compliance of the AT was observed after the acute exercise intervention.\nMoreover, there are gender-specific effects in response to the acute loading. Specifically,\nthe stiffness and Young’s modulus of the AT show significantly lower in women com-\npared to men, which may be an important cause of fewer AT injuries in women. Secondly,\nperiodic plyometric training can improve the morphological adaptability of the AT, but\nthe exercise type, intensity, and duration are the three key factors that restrict the effec-\ntiveness of training. Finally, habitual running can effectively improve the mechanical\nproperties of the AT (e.g., greater stiffness, CSA, and Young’s modulus). In particular, the\nhigh stiffness of the AT is conducive to enhancing the transmission of muscle strength,\nthereby improving the running economy. Future research is required to explore more\ntraining methods to improve the biomechanical adaptability of the AT from the perspec-\ntive of mechanism and multiple influencing factors and reduce the risk of AT-related in-\nAutho", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "en, which may be an important cause of fewer AT injuries in women. Secondly,\nperiodic plyometric training can improve the morphological adaptability of the AT, but\nthe exercise type, intensity, and duration are the three key factors that restrict the"}
{"chunk_id": "Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022_p0009_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_5d8ac1378426", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "cdee921319c4ebd2", "text_span_hash": "cdee921319c4ebd2", "source_file": "Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "key factors that restrict the effec-\ntiveness of training. Finally, habitual running can effectively improve the mechanical\nproperties of the AT (e.g., greater stiffness, CSA, and Young’s modulus). In particular, the\nhigh stiffness of the AT is cond", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "pared to traditionally shod runners (RFS), which seems more in accordance with the un-\nderstanding of mechanical adaptation of the tendon to different AT loading patterns\namong runners with different FSP [81]. This inspired us that multiple exercise and training\nmodalities combined with a step-by-step approach could lead to AT biomechanical adap-\ntations, so may do minimalist running.\nFirstly, high compliance of the AT was observed after the acute exercise intervention.\nMoreover, there are gender-specific effects in response to the acute loading. Specifically,\nthe stiffness and Young’s modulus of the AT show significantly lower in women com-\npared to men, which may be an important cause of fewer AT injuries in women. Secondly,\nperiodic plyometric training can improve the morphological adaptability of the AT, but\nthe exercise type, intensity, and duration are the three key factors that restrict the effec-\ntiveness of training. Finally, habitual running can effectively improve the mechanical\nproperties of the AT (e.g., greater stiffness, CSA, and Young’s modulus). In particular, the\nhigh stiffness of the AT is conducive to enhancing the transmission of muscle strength,\nthereby improving the running economy. Future research is required to explore more\ntraining methods to improve the biomechanical adaptability of the AT from the perspec-\ntive of mechanism and multiple influencing factors and reduce the risk of AT-related in-\nAutho", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "key factors that restrict the effec-\ntiveness of training. Finally, habitual running can effectively improve the mechanical\nproperties of the AT (e.g., greater stiffness, CSA, and Young’s modulus). In particular, the\nhigh stiffness of the AT is cond"}
{"chunk_id": "Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022_p0009_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_5d8ac1378426", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "001e0862c290c082", "text_span_hash": "001e0862c290c082", "source_file": "Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "to thank Irene Davis, professor at Harvard Medical School, for her\nhelp in writing the manuscript.\nBramble, D.M.; Lieberman, D.E. Endurance running and the evolution of Homo. Nature 2004, 432, 345–352, doi:10.1038/na-\nLorimer, A.V.; Hume, P.A. Stiff", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "pared to traditionally shod runners (RFS), which seems more in accordance with the un-\nderstanding of mechanical adaptation of the tendon to different AT loading patterns\namong runners with different FSP [81]. This inspired us that multiple exercise and training\nmodalities combined with a step-by-step approach could lead to AT biomechanical adap-\ntations, so may do minimalist running.\nFirstly, high compliance of the AT was observed after the acute exercise intervention.\nMoreover, there are gender-specific effects in response to the acute loading. Specifically,\nthe stiffness and Young’s modulus of the AT show significantly lower in women com-\npared to men, which may be an important cause of fewer AT injuries in women. Secondly,\nperiodic plyometric training can improve the morphological adaptability of the AT, but\nthe exercise type, intensity, and duration are the three key factors that restrict the effec-\ntiveness of training. Finally, habitual running can effectively improve the mechanical\nproperties of the AT (e.g., greater stiffness, CSA, and Young’s modulus). In particular, the\nhigh stiffness of the AT is conducive to enhancing the transmission of muscle strength,\nthereby improving the running economy. Future research is required to explore more\ntraining methods to improve the biomechanical adaptability of the AT from the perspec-\ntive of mechanism and multiple influencing factors and reduce the risk of AT-related in-\nAutho", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "to thank Irene Davis, professor at Harvard Medical School, for her\nhelp in writing the manuscript.\nBramble, D.M.; Lieberman, D.E. Endurance running and the evolution of Homo. Nature 2004, 432, 345–352, doi:10.1038/na-\nLorimer, A.V.; Hume, P.A. Stiff"}
