{"chunk_id": "Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022_p0011_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_5d8ac1378426", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1bf5d0aae0228ebf", "text_span_hash": "1bf5d0aae0228ebf", "source_file": "Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Exercise_Effects_Achilles_Tendon_Biomechanics_2022.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "i:10.1038/s41598-020-62464-3.\nLieberman, D.E.; Venkadesan, M.; Werbel, W.A.; Daoud, A.I.; D'Andrea, S.; Davis, I.S.; Mang'eni, R.O.; Pitsiladis, Y. Foot strike\npatterns and collision forces in habitually barefoot versus shod runners. Nature 2010, 463", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Zhang, X.; Deng, L.; Xiao, S.; Li, L.; Fu, W. Sex differences in the morphological and mechanical properties of the Achilles\nZhou, J.; Liu, C.; Zhang, Z. Non-uniform stiffness within Gastrocnemius-Achilles tendon complex observed after static stretch-\nFukutani, A.; Sawatsky, A.; Leonard, T.; Herzog, W. Contribution of the Achilles tendon to force potentiation in a stretch-short-\nLieber, R.L.; Leonard, M.E.; Brown-Maupin, C.G. Effects of Muscle Contraction on the Load-Strain Properties of Frog Aponeu-\nKjaer, M.; Magnusson, P.; Krogsgaard, M.; Boysen Møller, J.; Olesen, J.; Heinemeier, K.; Hansen, M.; Haraldsson, B.; Koskinen,\nS.; Esmarck, B.; et al. Extracellular matrix adaptation of tendon and skeletal muscle to exercise. J. Anat. 2006, 208, 445–450,\nKulmala, J.P.; Avela, J.; Pasanen, K.; Parkkari, J. Forefoot strikers exhibit lower running-induced knee loading than rearfoot\nYong, J.R.; Dembia, C.L.; Silder, A.; Jackson, R.W.; Fredericson, M.; Delp, S.L. Foot strike pattern during running alters muscle-\ntendon dynamics of the gastrocnemius and the soleus. Sci. Rep. 2020, 10, 5872, doi:10.1038/s41598-020-62464-3.\nLieberman, D.E.; Venkadesan, M.; Werbel, W.A.; Daoud, A.I.; D'Andrea, S.; Davis, I.S.; Mang'eni, R.O.; Pitsiladis, Y. Foot strike\npatterns and collision forces in habitually barefoot versus shod runners. Nature 2010, 463, 531–535, doi:10.1038/nature08723.\nKubo, K.; Miyazaki, D.; Tanaka, S.; Shimoju, S.; Tsunoda, N. Rel", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "i:10.1038/s41598-020-62464-3.\nLieberman, D.E.; Venkadesan, M.; Werbel, W.A.; Daoud, A.I.; D'Andrea, S.; Davis, I.S.; Mang'eni, R.O.; Pitsiladis, Y. Foot strike\npatterns and collision forces in habitually barefoot versus shod runners. Nature 2010, 463"}
{"chunk_id": "Gabbett_2016_ACWR_Paradox_p0001_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_f95b062d735b", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8be3f5392d5a81e1", "text_span_hash": "8be3f5392d5a81e1", "source_file": "Gabbett_2016_ACWR_Paradox.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Gabbett_2016_ACWR_Paradox.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "to high training loads have fewer\ninjuries than athletes training at lower workloads. The\nModel is based on evidence that non-contact injuries are\nnot caused by training per se, but more likely by an\ninappropriate training programme. Excessive and ra", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "The training-injury prevention paradox: should\nathletes be training smarter and harder?\n1School of Exercise Science,\nAustralian Catholic University,\nBrisbane, Queensland,\n2School of Human Movement\nStudies, University of\nQueensland, Brisbane,\nQueensland, Australia\nDr Tim J Gabbett, School of\nExercise Science, Australian\nCatholic University, 1100\nNudgee Road, Brisbane,\nQLD 4014, Australia;\ntim_gabbett@yahoo.com.au\nAccepted 16 November 2015\nTo cite: Gabbett TJ. Br J\nSports Med Published Online\nFirst: [please include Day\nBackground There is dogma that higher training load\ncauses higher injury rates. However, there is also\nevidence that training has a protective effect against\ninjury. For example, team sport athletes who performed\nmore than 18 weeks of training before sustaining their\ninitial injuries were at reduced risk of sustaining a\nsubsequent injury, while high chronic workloads have\nbeen shown to decrease the risk of injury. Second,\nacross a wide range of sports, well-developed physical\nqualities are associated with a reduced risk of injury.\nClearly, for athletes to develop the physical capacities\nrequired to provide a protective effect against injury, they\nmust be prepared to train hard. Finally, there is also\nevidence that under-training may increase injury risk.\nCollectively, these results emphasise that reductions in\nworkloads may not always be the best approach to\nprotect against injury.\nMain thesis This paper describes", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "to high training loads have fewer\ninjuries than athletes training at lower workloads. The\nModel is based on evidence that non-contact injuries are\nnot caused by training per se, but more likely by an\ninappropriate training programme. Excessive and ra"}
