d perceptual\nresponses to an 800 m effort than a trained runner. Although\nthe external training load is identical, the internal training load\nwill be much higher in the older, unﬁt individual! As the dose–\nresponse to training varies between individuals, training should\nbe ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "range of subjective questionnaires\nare used with many of them employing a simple 5, 7 or\nsurveys are also employed.24 25\nHypothetical relationship between training loads, ﬁtness,\ninjuries and performance. Redrawn from Orchard.1\nRelationship between"}
{"chunk_id": "Gabbett_2016_ACWR_Paradox_p0003_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_f95b062d735b", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9d6fb8038393c32e", "text_span_hash": "9d6fb8038393c32e", "source_file": "Gabbett_2016_ACWR_Paradox.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Gabbett_2016_ACWR_Paradox.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": ", quadriceps, hamstring, groin and\ncalf. The sum of the questions indicates the athlete’s well-being.\nPractitioners can then adapt the training prescription for players\non an individual basis (eg, continue regular training, investigate\ntraining loads", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "These questionnaires are used to determine the readiness of\nteam sport athletes to train. Typically players report their mood,\nstress level, energy, sleep and diet, along with their feelings of\nsoreness in the upper-body, quadriceps, hamstring, groin and\ncalf. The sum of the questions indicates the athlete’s well-being.\nPractitioners can then adapt the training prescription for players\non an individual basis (eg, continue regular training, investigate\ntraining loads or modify training programme).\nRELATIONSHIP BETWEEN TRAINING LOADS AND INJURY\nTraining load monitoring is increasingly popular in high per-\nformance sport to ensure athletes achieve an adequate training\nstimulus and to minimise the negative consequences of training\n(injury risk, overtraining). In the following section, I discuss the\nrelationship between training loads (both internal and external\nloads) and injury in team sport athletes.\nEXTERNAL WORKLOADS AND INJURY\nIn elite rugby league, players who performed greater amounts\n(>9 m) of very high-speed (>7 m/s) running per session were\n2.7 times more likely to sustain a non-contact, soft-tissue injury\nthan players who performed less very high-speed running per\nsession (table 1).26 This ‘threshold’ of 9 m of very high-speed\nrunning is lower than what would typically be performed in\nother team sport training sessions (eg, soccer and Australian\nrepeated-effort demands, and lower running demands of rugby\nleague.27 For e", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": ", quadriceps, hamstring, groin and\ncalf. The sum of the questions indicates the athlete’s well-being.\nPractitioners can then adapt the training prescription for players\non an individual basis (eg, continue regular training, investigate\ntraining loads"}
{"chunk_id": "Gabbett_2016_ACWR_Paradox_p0003_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_f95b062d735b", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "d44832eec0fd9ad6", "text_span_hash": "d44832eec0fd9ad6", "source_file": "Gabbett_2016_ACWR_Paradox.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Gabbett_2016_ACWR_Paradox.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "eam sport training sessions (eg, soccer and Australian\nrepeated-effort demands, and lower running demands of rugby\nleague.27 For example, in studies of Australian football players,\nhigher 3-weekly total distance (73 721–86 662 m, OR=5.5) and\n3-weekly", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "These questionnaires are used to determine the readiness of\nteam sport athletes to train. Typically players report their mood,\nstress level, energy, sleep and diet, along with their feelings of\nsoreness in the upper-body, quadriceps, hamstring, groin and\ncalf. The sum of the questions indicates the athlete’s well-being.\nPractitioners can then adapt the training prescription for players\non an individual basis (eg, continue regular training, investigate\ntraining loads or modify training programme).\nRELATIONSHIP BETWEEN TRAINING LOADS AND INJURY\nTraining load monitoring is increasingly popular in high per-\nformance sport to ensure athletes achieve an adequate training\nstimulus and to minimise the negative consequences of training\n(injury risk, overtraining). In the following section, I discuss the\nrelationship between training loads (both internal and external\nloads) and injury in team sport athletes.\nEXTERNAL WORKLOADS AND INJURY\nIn elite rugby league, players who performed greater amounts\n(>9 m) of very high-speed (>7 m/s) running per session were\n2.7 times more likely to sustain a non-contact, soft-tissue injury\nthan players who performed less very high-speed running per\nsession (table 1).26 This ‘threshold’ of 9 m of very high-speed\nrunning is lower than what would typically be performed in\nother team sport training sessions (eg, soccer and Australian\nrepeated-effort demands, and lower running demands of rugby\nleague.27 For e", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "eam sport training sessions (eg, soccer and Australian\nrepeated-effort demands, and lower running demands of rugby\nleague.27 For example, in studies of Australian football players,\nhigher 3-weekly total distance (73 721–86 662 m, OR=5.5) and\n3-weekly"}
{"chunk_id": "Gabbett_2016_ACWR_Paradox_p0003_c0020", "source_registry_id": "src_f95b062d735b", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8f7caa3e2845a03c", "text_span_hash": "8f7caa3e2845a03c", "source_file": "Gabbett_2016_ACWR_Paradox.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Gabbett_2016_ACWR_Paradox.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 20, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "d power training\nloads were signiﬁcantly related to the incidence of strength and\npower injuries (r=0.63). There was no signiﬁcant relationship\nbetween ﬁeld training loads and the incidence of strength and\npower injuries. However, strength and power", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "These questionnaires are used to determine the readiness of\nteam sport athletes to train. Typically players report their mood,\nstress level, energy, sleep and diet, along with their feelings of\nsoreness in the upper-body, quadriceps, hamstring, groin and\ncalf. The sum of the questions indicates the athlete’s well-being.\nPractitioners can then adapt the training prescription for players\non an individual basis (eg, continue regular training, investigate\ntraining loads or modify training programme).\nRELATIONSHIP BETWEEN TRAINING LOADS AND INJURY\nTraining load monitoring is increasingly popular in high per-\nformance sport to ensure athletes achieve an adequate training\nstimulus and to minimise the negative consequences of training\n(injury risk, overtraining). In the following section, I discuss the\nrelationship between training loads (both internal and external\nloads) and injury in team sport athletes.\nEXTERNAL WORKLOADS AND INJURY\nIn elite rugby league, players who performed greater amounts\n(>9 m) of very high-speed (>7 m/s) running per session were\n2.7 times more likely to sustain a non-contact, soft-tissue injury\nthan players who performed less very high-speed running per\nsession (table 1).26 This ‘threshold’ of 9 m of very high-speed\nrunning is lower than what would typically be performed in\nother team sport training sessions (eg, soccer and Australian\nrepeated-effort demands, and lower running demands of rugby\nleague.27 For e", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "d power training\nloads were signiﬁcantly related to the incidence of strength and\npower injuries (r=0.63). There was no signiﬁcant relationship\nbetween ﬁeld training loads and the incidence of strength and\npower injuries. However, strength and power"}
{"chunk_id": "Gabbett_2016_ACWR_Paradox_p0003_c0021", "source_registry_id": "src_f95b062d735b", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "dceaccc7acc76a2f", "text_span_hash": "dceaccc7acc76a2f", "source_file": "Gabbett_2016_ACWR_Paradox.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Gabbett_2016_ACWR_Paradox.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 21, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": ". However, strength and power training loads\nwere signiﬁcantly associated with the incidence of contact\n(r=0.75) and non-contact\n(r=0.87) ﬁeld training injuries.\nCollectively, these ﬁndings suggest that (1) the harder rugby\nleague players train, the", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "These questionnaires are used to determine the readiness of\nteam sport athletes to train. Typically players report their mood,\nstress level, energy, sleep and diet, along with their feelings of\nsoreness in the upper-body, quadriceps, hamstring, groin and\ncalf. The sum of the questions indicates the athlete’s well-being.\nPractitioners can then adapt the training prescription for players\non an individual basis (eg, continue regular training, investigate\ntraining loads or modify training programme).\nRELATIONSHIP BETWEEN TRAINING LOADS AND INJURY\nTraining load monitoring is increasingly popular in high per-\nformance sport to ensure athletes achieve an adequate training\nstimulus and to minimise the negative consequences of training\n(injury risk, overtraining). In the following section, I discuss the\nrelationship between training loads (both internal and external\nloads) and injury in team sport athletes.\nEXTERNAL WORKLOADS AND INJURY\nIn elite rugby league, players who performed greater amounts\n(>9 m) of very high-speed (>7 m/s) running per session were\n2.7 times more likely to sustain a non-contact, soft-tissue injury\nthan players who performed less very high-speed running per\nsession (table 1).26 This ‘threshold’ of 9 m of very high-speed\nrunning is lower than what would typically be performed in\nother team sport training sessions (eg, soccer and Australian\nrepeated-effort demands, and lower running demands of rugby\nleague.27 For e", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": ". However, strength and power training loads\nwere signiﬁcantly associated with the incidence of contact\n(r=0.75) and non-contact\n(r=0.87) ﬁeld training injuries.\nCollectively, these ﬁndings suggest that (1) the harder rugby\nleague players train, the"}
{"chunk_id": "Gabbett_2016_ACWR_Paradox_p0003_c0022", "source_registry_id": "src_f95b062d735b", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "12b5f9272ac49c75", "text_span_hash": "12b5f9272ac49c75", "source_file": "Gabbett_2016_ACWR_Paradox.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Gabbett_2016_ACWR_Paradox.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 22, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "gby\nleague players train, the more injuries they will sustain and (2)\nhigh strength and power training loads may contribute indirectly\nto ﬁeld injuries. Monitoring of training loads and careful sched-\nuling of ﬁeld and gymnasium sessions to avoid res", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "These questionnaires are used to determine the readiness of\nteam sport athletes to train. Typically players report their mood,\nstress level, energy, sleep and diet, along with their feelings of\nsoreness in the upper-body, quadriceps, hamstring, groin and\ncalf. The sum of the questions indicates the athlete’s well-being.\nPractitioners can then adapt the training prescription for players\non an individual basis (eg, continue regular training, investigate\ntraining loads or modify training programme).\nRELATIONSHIP BETWEEN TRAINING LOADS AND INJURY\nTraining load monitoring is increasingly popular in high per-\nformance sport to ensure athletes achieve an adequate training\nstimulus and to minimise the negative consequences of training\n(injury risk, overtraining). In the following section, I discuss the\nrelationship between training loads (both internal and external\nloads) and injury in team sport athletes.\nEXTERNAL WORKLOADS AND INJURY\nIn elite rugby league, players who performed greater amounts\n(>9 m) of very high-speed (>7 m/s) running per session were\n2.7 times more likely to sustain a non-contact, soft-tissue injury\nthan players who performed less very high-speed running per\nsession (table 1).26 This ‘threshold’ of 9 m of very high-speed\nrunning is lower than what would typically be performed in\nother team sport training sessions (eg, soccer and Australian\nrepeated-effort demands, and lower running demands of rugby\nleague.27 For e", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "gby\nleague players train, the more injuries they will sustain and (2)\nhigh strength and power training loads may contribute indirectly\nto ﬁeld injuries. Monitoring of training loads and careful sched-\nuling of ﬁeld and gymnasium sessions to avoid res"}
{"chunk_id": "Gabbett_2016_ACWR_Paradox_p0003_c0025", "source_registry_id": "src_f95b062d735b", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0949cebac5a861fc", "text_span_hash": "0949cebac5a861fc", "source_file": "Gabbett_2016_ACWR_Paradox.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Gabbett_2016_ACWR_Paradox.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 25, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "athletes. Training loads\nwere measured using the\nsession-rating of perceived exertion\nmethod. Redrawn from Gabbett.37\non January 12, 2016 - Published by\nhttp://bjsm.bmj.com/", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "These questionnaires are used to determine the readiness of\nteam sport athletes to train. Typically players report their mood,\nstress level, energy, sleep and diet, along with their feelings of\nsoreness in the upper-body, quadriceps, hamstring, groin and\ncalf. The sum of the questions indicates the athlete’s well-being.\nPractitioners can then adapt the training prescription for players\non an individual basis (eg, continue regular training, investigate\ntraining loads or modify training programme).\nRELATIONSHIP BETWEEN TRAINING LOADS AND INJURY\nTraining load monitoring is increasingly popular in high per-\nformance sport to ensure athletes achieve an adequate training\nstimulus and to minimise the negative consequences of training\n(injury risk, overtraining). In the following section, I discuss the\nrelationship between training loads (both internal and external\nloads) and injury in team sport athletes.\nEXTERNAL WORKLOADS AND INJURY\nIn elite rugby league, players who performed greater amounts\n(>9 m) of very high-speed (>7 m/s) running per session were\n2.7 times more likely to sustain a non-contact, soft-tissue injury\nthan players who performed less very high-speed running per\nsession (table 1).26 This ‘threshold’ of 9 m of very high-speed\nrunning is lower than what would typically be performed in\nother team sport training sessions (eg, soccer and Australian\nrepeated-effort demands, and lower running demands of rugby\nleague.27 For e", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "athletes. Training loads\nwere measured using the\nsession-rating of perceived exertion\nmethod. Redrawn from Gabbett.37\non January 12, 2016 - Published by\nhttp://bjsm.bmj.com/"}
{"chunk_id": "Gabbett_2016_ACWR_Paradox_p0004_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_f95b062d735b", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e6e369765b7953cc", "text_span_hash": "e6e369765b7953cc", "source_file": "Gabbett_2016_ACWR_Paradox.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Gabbett_2016_ACWR_Paradox.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ssociated with a 14-week ﬁeld condition-\ning programme in junior (approximately 17 years) and senior\nimproved muscular power and maximal aerobic power in the\njunior and senior players, however the improvement in muscular\npower and maximal aerobic pow", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "DIFFERENCES IN TRAINING ADAPTATIONS BETWEEN\nYOUNGER AND OLDER ATHLETES\nThe age of the athlete inﬂuences adaptations to training.38 39\nGabbett38 investigated training loads, injury rates and physical\nperformance changes associated with a 14-week ﬁeld condition-\ning programme in junior (approximately 17 years) and senior\nimproved muscular power and maximal aerobic power in the\njunior and senior players, however the improvement in muscular\npower and maximal aerobic power were greatest in the junior\nplayers. Training loads and injury rates were higher in the senior\nplayers. Thus, junior and senior rugby league players may adapt\ndifferently to a given training stimulus, suggesting that training\nprogrammes should be modiﬁed to accommodate differences in\nRogalski et al39 also showed that at a given training load,\nolder and more experienced (7+ years’ experience in the\nAustralian Football League competition) players were at greater\nrisk of injury than less experienced, younger (1–3 years’ experi-\nence in the Australian Football League competition) players. It is\nlikely that the higher training injury risk in the more experi-\nenced players is confounded by previous injury which is a major\nrisk factor for a new injury.40 Older players likely had experi-\nenced a greater number of injuries across the course of their\ncareers than the less experienced ﬁrst to third year players.\nClearly, further research investigating the dose–response rela", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ssociated with a 14-week ﬁeld condition-\ning programme in junior (approximately 17 years) and senior\nimproved muscular power and maximal aerobic power in the\njunior and senior players, however the improvement in muscular\npower and maximal aerobic pow"}
