f\ntraining-related injuries.\nWhat are the ﬁndings?\n▸Dogma exists around the effects of high (and low) training\n▸This review highlights the positive and negative effects of\nhigh training loads on injury risk, ﬁtness and thus,\n▸There is a relationship between high training lo", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "e train-\ning that is being prescribed. Excessive and rapid increases in\ntraining loads are likely responsible for a large proportion of\nnon-contact, soft-tissue injuries. However, physically hard (and\nappropriate) training develops physical qualities"}
{"chunk_id": "Gabbett_2016_ACWR_Paradox_p0008_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_f95b062d735b", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "24c59aee487a8929", "text_span_hash": "24c59aee487a8929", "source_file": "Gabbett_2016_ACWR_Paradox.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Gabbett_2016_ACWR_Paradox.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ic workload ratio),\nas a best practice approach to the long-term reduction of\ntraining-related injuries.\nWhat are the ﬁndings?\n▸Dogma exists around the effects of high (and low) training\n▸This review highlights the positive and negative effects of\nhi", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "breaks down repeatedly (potentially with different injuries)\nbecause his or her training load is not high enough to adapt to\nmatch demands. The data presented suggest that prescribing\nhigh training loads can lead to improved levels of ﬁtness, which\nin turn offers a protective effect against injury, ultimately\nleading to (1) greater physical outputs and resilience in competi-\ntion, and (2) a greater proportion of the squad available for\nselection each week (ﬁgure 7).\nIn conclusion, while there is a relationship between high train-\ning loads and injury, this paper demonstrates that the problem is\nnot with training per se, but more likely the inappropriate train-\ning that is being prescribed. Excessive and rapid increases in\ntraining loads are likely responsible for a large proportion of\nnon-contact, soft-tissue injuries. However, physically hard (and\nappropriate) training develops physical qualities, which in turn\nprotects against injuries. This paper highlights the importance of\nmonitoring training load, including the load that athletes are\nprepared for (by calculating the acute:chronic workload ratio),\nas a best practice approach to the long-term reduction of\ntraining-related injuries.\nWhat are the ﬁndings?\n▸Dogma exists around the effects of high (and low) training\n▸This review highlights the positive and negative effects of\nhigh training loads on injury risk, ﬁtness and thus,\n▸There is a relationship between high training lo", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ic workload ratio),\nas a best practice approach to the long-term reduction of\ntraining-related injuries.\nWhat are the ﬁndings?\n▸Dogma exists around the effects of high (and low) training\n▸This review highlights the positive and negative effects of\nhi"}
{"chunk_id": "Gabbett_2016_ACWR_Paradox_p0008_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_f95b062d735b", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "03e8c2c6f3298a41", "text_span_hash": "03e8c2c6f3298a41", "source_file": "Gabbett_2016_ACWR_Paradox.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Gabbett_2016_ACWR_Paradox.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Med 2012; June 20,\nguest blog. http://blogs.bmj.com/bjsm/2012/06/20/who-is-to-blame-for-all-the-\nBanister EW, Calvert TW, Savage MV, et al. A systems model of training for athletic\nperformance. Aust J Sports Med 1975;7:57–61.\nMorton RH. Modeling trai", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "breaks down repeatedly (potentially with different injuries)\nbecause his or her training load is not high enough to adapt to\nmatch demands. The data presented suggest that prescribing\nhigh training loads can lead to improved levels of ﬁtness, which\nin turn offers a protective effect against injury, ultimately\nleading to (1) greater physical outputs and resilience in competi-\ntion, and (2) a greater proportion of the squad available for\nselection each week (ﬁgure 7).\nIn conclusion, while there is a relationship between high train-\ning loads and injury, this paper demonstrates that the problem is\nnot with training per se, but more likely the inappropriate train-\ning that is being prescribed. Excessive and rapid increases in\ntraining loads are likely responsible for a large proportion of\nnon-contact, soft-tissue injuries. However, physically hard (and\nappropriate) training develops physical qualities, which in turn\nprotects against injuries. This paper highlights the importance of\nmonitoring training load, including the load that athletes are\nprepared for (by calculating the acute:chronic workload ratio),\nas a best practice approach to the long-term reduction of\ntraining-related injuries.\nWhat are the ﬁndings?\n▸Dogma exists around the effects of high (and low) training\n▸This review highlights the positive and negative effects of\nhigh training loads on injury risk, ﬁtness and thus,\n▸There is a relationship between high training lo", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Med 2012; June 20,\nguest blog. http://blogs.bmj.com/bjsm/2012/06/20/who-is-to-blame-for-all-the-\nBanister EW, Calvert TW, Savage MV, et al. A systems model of training for athletic\nperformance. Aust J Sports Med 1975;7:57–61.\nMorton RH. Modeling trai"}
{"chunk_id": "Gabbett_2016_ACWR_Paradox_p0008_c0017", "source_registry_id": "src_f95b062d735b", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8cfa20e1bbd6f287", "text_span_hash": "8cfa20e1bbd6f287", "source_file": "Gabbett_2016_ACWR_Paradox.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Gabbett_2016_ACWR_Paradox.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 17, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Krebs PS, Zinkgraf S, Virgilio SJ. Predicting competitive bicycling performance with\ntraining and physiological variables. J Sports Med Phys Fit 1986;26:323–30.\nMujika I, Chatard JC, Busso T, et al. Effects of training on performance in\ncompetitive", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "breaks down repeatedly (potentially with different injuries)\nbecause his or her training load is not high enough to adapt to\nmatch demands. The data presented suggest that prescribing\nhigh training loads can lead to improved levels of ﬁtness, which\nin turn offers a protective effect against injury, ultimately\nleading to (1) greater physical outputs and resilience in competi-\ntion, and (2) a greater proportion of the squad available for\nselection each week (ﬁgure 7).\nIn conclusion, while there is a relationship between high train-\ning loads and injury, this paper demonstrates that the problem is\nnot with training per se, but more likely the inappropriate train-\ning that is being prescribed. Excessive and rapid increases in\ntraining loads are likely responsible for a large proportion of\nnon-contact, soft-tissue injuries. However, physically hard (and\nappropriate) training develops physical qualities, which in turn\nprotects against injuries. This paper highlights the importance of\nmonitoring training load, including the load that athletes are\nprepared for (by calculating the acute:chronic workload ratio),\nas a best practice approach to the long-term reduction of\ntraining-related injuries.\nWhat are the ﬁndings?\n▸Dogma exists around the effects of high (and low) training\n▸This review highlights the positive and negative effects of\nhigh training loads on injury risk, ﬁtness and thus,\n▸There is a relationship between high training lo", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Krebs PS, Zinkgraf S, Virgilio SJ. Predicting competitive bicycling performance with\ntraining and physiological variables. J Sports Med Phys Fit 1986;26:323–30.\nMujika I, Chatard JC, Busso T, et al. Effects of training on performance in\ncompetitive"}
{"chunk_id": "Gabbett_2016_ACWR_Paradox_p0008_c0018", "source_registry_id": "src_f95b062d735b", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "146587ae9de87fc3", "text_span_hash": "146587ae9de87fc3", "source_file": "Gabbett_2016_ACWR_Paradox.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Gabbett_2016_ACWR_Paradox.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 18, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "on performance in\ncompetitive swimming. Can J Appl Physiol 1995;20:395–406.\nMujika I, Busso T, LaCoste L, et al. Modeled responses to training and taper in\ncompetitive swimmers. Med Sci Sports Exerc 1996;28:251–8.\nScrimgeour AG, Noakes TD, Adams B, e", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "breaks down repeatedly (potentially with different injuries)\nbecause his or her training load is not high enough to adapt to\nmatch demands. The data presented suggest that prescribing\nhigh training loads can lead to improved levels of ﬁtness, which\nin turn offers a protective effect against injury, ultimately\nleading to (1) greater physical outputs and resilience in competi-\ntion, and (2) a greater proportion of the squad available for\nselection each week (ﬁgure 7).\nIn conclusion, while there is a relationship between high train-\ning loads and injury, this paper demonstrates that the problem is\nnot with training per se, but more likely the inappropriate train-\ning that is being prescribed. Excessive and rapid increases in\ntraining loads are likely responsible for a large proportion of\nnon-contact, soft-tissue injuries. However, physically hard (and\nappropriate) training develops physical qualities, which in turn\nprotects against injuries. This paper highlights the importance of\nmonitoring training load, including the load that athletes are\nprepared for (by calculating the acute:chronic workload ratio),\nas a best practice approach to the long-term reduction of\ntraining-related injuries.\nWhat are the ﬁndings?\n▸Dogma exists around the effects of high (and low) training\n▸This review highlights the positive and negative effects of\nhigh training loads on injury risk, ﬁtness and thus,\n▸There is a relationship between high training lo", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "on performance in\ncompetitive swimming. Can J Appl Physiol 1995;20:395–406.\nMujika I, Busso T, LaCoste L, et al. Modeled responses to training and taper in\ncompetitive swimmers. Med Sci Sports Exerc 1996;28:251–8.\nScrimgeour AG, Noakes TD, Adams B, e"}
