f\ntraining-related injuries.\nWhat are the ﬁndings?\n▸Dogma exists around the effects of high (and low) training\n▸This review highlights the positive and negative effects of\nhigh training loads on injury risk, ﬁtness and thus,\n▸There is a relationship between high training lo", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "s to monitor adaptive\nresponses to training and competition in elite Australian football. J Strength Cond\nWehbe GM, Gabbett TJ, Hartwig TB, et al. Reliability of a cycle ergometer peak\npower test in running-based team sport athletes: a technical repo"}
{"chunk_id": "Gluteal-strengthening exercises are_p0001_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_b09a10ec3f70", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e67bf1d772f64d97", "text_span_hash": "e67bf1d772f64d97", "source_file": "Gluteal-strengthening exercises are.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Gluteal-strengthening exercises are.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Exploring the Role of the\nLateral Gluteal Muscles in\nRunning: Implications for\nK. Aleisha Fetters, MS, CSCS\nA B S T R A C T\nGluteal-strengthening exercises are\nwidely used for improving running\nperformance. However, as running\noccurs primarily in the", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Exploring the Role of the\nLateral Gluteal Muscles in\nRunning: Implications for\nK. Aleisha Fetters, MS, CSCS\nA B S T R A C T\nGluteal-strengthening exercises are\nwidely used for improving running\nperformance. However, as running\noccurs primarily in the sagittal plane,\nsuch strength exercises have\nfocused on the gluteus maximus and\nits role in producing ground reaction\nforces as a hip extensor. Meanwhile,\nstrength deﬁcits in the lateral gluteal\nmuscles, the medius and minimus,\nare common among runners. This\narticle will explore the role of the\nlateral gluteal muscles in running\nkinematics, performance, and\ninjury, with implications for strength\nand conditioning professionals,\nsports medicine specialists, and\nphysical therapists. Recommenda-\ntions for strengthening the gluteus\nmedius and minimus in runners will\ninking the lower extremities to\nthe axial skeleton, the hips are\nintegral to running mechanics.\nOf the more than 20 muscles that act\non the ball-and-socket joint (3), the\ngluteals are arguably the most critical\nto running performance. Comprising\nand minimus, they form the largest\nmuscle group of the hips. Together,\nthey allow the femur to act in all 3\ntransverse—performing\nsion, ﬂexion, abduction, adduction,\ninternal rotation, and external rota-\nRunning is primarily a sagittal plane\nactivity, with hip extension serving\nas a fundamental movement pattern.\nHip extension is largely responsible\nforces necessary to move the body\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Exploring the Role of the\nLateral Gluteal Muscles in\nRunning: Implications for\nK. Aleisha Fetters, MS, CSCS\nA B S T R A C T\nGluteal-strengthening exercises are\nwidely used for improving running\nperformance. However, as running\noccurs primarily in the"}
{"chunk_id": "Gluteal-strengthening exercises are_p0001_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_b09a10ec3f70", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3bc4ec086aeb6fe4", "text_span_hash": "3bc4ec086aeb6fe4", "source_file": "Gluteal-strengthening exercises are.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Gluteal-strengthening exercises are.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ioning professionals,\nsports medicine specialists, and\nphysical therapists. Recommenda-\ntions for strengthening the gluteus\nmedius and minimus in runners will\ninking the lower extremities to\nthe axial skeleton, the hips are\nintegral to running mechan", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Exploring the Role of the\nLateral Gluteal Muscles in\nRunning: Implications for\nK. Aleisha Fetters, MS, CSCS\nA B S T R A C T\nGluteal-strengthening exercises are\nwidely used for improving running\nperformance. However, as running\noccurs primarily in the sagittal plane,\nsuch strength exercises have\nfocused on the gluteus maximus and\nits role in producing ground reaction\nforces as a hip extensor. Meanwhile,\nstrength deﬁcits in the lateral gluteal\nmuscles, the medius and minimus,\nare common among runners. This\narticle will explore the role of the\nlateral gluteal muscles in running\nkinematics, performance, and\ninjury, with implications for strength\nand conditioning professionals,\nsports medicine specialists, and\nphysical therapists. Recommenda-\ntions for strengthening the gluteus\nmedius and minimus in runners will\ninking the lower extremities to\nthe axial skeleton, the hips are\nintegral to running mechanics.\nOf the more than 20 muscles that act\non the ball-and-socket joint (3), the\ngluteals are arguably the most critical\nto running performance. Comprising\nand minimus, they form the largest\nmuscle group of the hips. Together,\nthey allow the femur to act in all 3\ntransverse—performing\nsion, ﬂexion, abduction, adduction,\ninternal rotation, and external rota-\nRunning is primarily a sagittal plane\nactivity, with hip extension serving\nas a fundamental movement pattern.\nHip extension is largely responsible\nforces necessary to move the body\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ioning professionals,\nsports medicine specialists, and\nphysical therapists. Recommenda-\ntions for strengthening the gluteus\nmedius and minimus in runners will\ninking the lower extremities to\nthe axial skeleton, the hips are\nintegral to running mechan"}
{"chunk_id": "Gluteal-strengthening exercises are_p0001_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_b09a10ec3f70", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0ebc04153c25e6f6", "text_span_hash": "0ebc04153c25e6f6", "source_file": "Gluteal-strengthening exercises are.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Gluteal-strengthening exercises are.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "are\nintegral to running mechanics.\nOf the more than 20 muscles that act\non the ball-and-socket joint (3), the\ngluteals are arguably the most critical\nto running performance. Comprising\nand minimus, they form the largest\nmuscle group of the hips. Toge", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Exploring the Role of the\nLateral Gluteal Muscles in\nRunning: Implications for\nK. Aleisha Fetters, MS, CSCS\nA B S T R A C T\nGluteal-strengthening exercises are\nwidely used for improving running\nperformance. However, as running\noccurs primarily in the sagittal plane,\nsuch strength exercises have\nfocused on the gluteus maximus and\nits role in producing ground reaction\nforces as a hip extensor. Meanwhile,\nstrength deﬁcits in the lateral gluteal\nmuscles, the medius and minimus,\nare common among runners. This\narticle will explore the role of the\nlateral gluteal muscles in running\nkinematics, performance, and\ninjury, with implications for strength\nand conditioning professionals,\nsports medicine specialists, and\nphysical therapists. Recommenda-\ntions for strengthening the gluteus\nmedius and minimus in runners will\ninking the lower extremities to\nthe axial skeleton, the hips are\nintegral to running mechanics.\nOf the more than 20 muscles that act\non the ball-and-socket joint (3), the\ngluteals are arguably the most critical\nto running performance. Comprising\nand minimus, they form the largest\nmuscle group of the hips. Together,\nthey allow the femur to act in all 3\ntransverse—performing\nsion, ﬂexion, abduction, adduction,\ninternal rotation, and external rota-\nRunning is primarily a sagittal plane\nactivity, with hip extension serving\nas a fundamental movement pattern.\nHip extension is largely responsible\nforces necessary to move the body\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "are\nintegral to running mechanics.\nOf the more than 20 muscles that act\non the ball-and-socket joint (3), the\ngluteals are arguably the most critical\nto running performance. Comprising\nand minimus, they form the largest\nmuscle group of the hips. Toge"}
{"chunk_id": "Gluteal-strengthening exercises are_p0001_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_b09a10ec3f70", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e024071e7686e880", "text_span_hash": "e024071e7686e880", "source_file": "Gluteal-strengthening exercises are.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Gluteal-strengthening exercises are.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "muscle group of the hips. Together,\nthey allow the femur to act in all 3\ntransverse—performing\nsion, ﬂexion, abduction, adduction,\ninternal rotation, and external rota-\nRunning is primarily a sagittal plane\nactivity, with hip extension serving\nas a f", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Exploring the Role of the\nLateral Gluteal Muscles in\nRunning: Implications for\nK. Aleisha Fetters, MS, CSCS\nA B S T R A C T\nGluteal-strengthening exercises are\nwidely used for improving running\nperformance. However, as running\noccurs primarily in the sagittal plane,\nsuch strength exercises have\nfocused on the gluteus maximus and\nits role in producing ground reaction\nforces as a hip extensor. Meanwhile,\nstrength deﬁcits in the lateral gluteal\nmuscles, the medius and minimus,\nare common among runners. This\narticle will explore the role of the\nlateral gluteal muscles in running\nkinematics, performance, and\ninjury, with implications for strength\nand conditioning professionals,\nsports medicine specialists, and\nphysical therapists. Recommenda-\ntions for strengthening the gluteus\nmedius and minimus in runners will\ninking the lower extremities to\nthe axial skeleton, the hips are\nintegral to running mechanics.\nOf the more than 20 muscles that act\non the ball-and-socket joint (3), the\ngluteals are arguably the most critical\nto running performance. Comprising\nand minimus, they form the largest\nmuscle group of the hips. Together,\nthey allow the femur to act in all 3\ntransverse—performing\nsion, ﬂexion, abduction, adduction,\ninternal rotation, and external rota-\nRunning is primarily a sagittal plane\nactivity, with hip extension serving\nas a fundamental movement pattern.\nHip extension is largely responsible\nforces necessary to move the body\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "muscle group of the hips. Together,\nthey allow the femur to act in all 3\ntransverse—performing\nsion, ﬂexion, abduction, adduction,\ninternal rotation, and external rota-\nRunning is primarily a sagittal plane\nactivity, with hip extension serving\nas a f"}
{"chunk_id": "Gluteal-strengthening exercises are_p0001_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_b09a10ec3f70", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "05c6d4605d1fb0c2", "text_span_hash": "05c6d4605d1fb0c2", "source_file": "Gluteal-strengthening exercises are.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Gluteal-strengthening exercises are.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "orces that drive\ndownward into the ground and act\nreciprocally on the body to propel\nthe opposite leg’s swing phase. Ver-\ntical forces applied to the ground are\na primary determiner of running\nThe gluteus maximus connects the\nlateral aspect of the do", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Exploring the Role of the\nLateral Gluteal Muscles in\nRunning: Implications for\nK. Aleisha Fetters, MS, CSCS\nA B S T R A C T\nGluteal-strengthening exercises are\nwidely used for improving running\nperformance. However, as running\noccurs primarily in the sagittal plane,\nsuch strength exercises have\nfocused on the gluteus maximus and\nits role in producing ground reaction\nforces as a hip extensor. Meanwhile,\nstrength deﬁcits in the lateral gluteal\nmuscles, the medius and minimus,\nare common among runners. This\narticle will explore the role of the\nlateral gluteal muscles in running\nkinematics, performance, and\ninjury, with implications for strength\nand conditioning professionals,\nsports medicine specialists, and\nphysical therapists. Recommenda-\ntions for strengthening the gluteus\nmedius and minimus in runners will\ninking the lower extremities to\nthe axial skeleton, the hips are\nintegral to running mechanics.\nOf the more than 20 muscles that act\non the ball-and-socket joint (3), the\ngluteals are arguably the most critical\nto running performance. Comprising\nand minimus, they form the largest\nmuscle group of the hips. Together,\nthey allow the femur to act in all 3\ntransverse—performing\nsion, ﬂexion, abduction, adduction,\ninternal rotation, and external rota-\nRunning is primarily a sagittal plane\nactivity, with hip extension serving\nas a fundamental movement pattern.\nHip extension is largely responsible\nforces necessary to move the body\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "orces that drive\ndownward into the ground and act\nreciprocally on the body to propel\nthe opposite leg’s swing phase. Ver-\ntical forces applied to the ground are\na primary determiner of running\nThe gluteus maximus connects the\nlateral aspect of the do"}
