{"chunk_id": "Gluteal-strengthening exercises are_p0001_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_b09a10ec3f70", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "d55e2d6e8efdbaef", "text_span_hash": "d55e2d6e8efdbaef", "source_file": "Gluteal-strengthening exercises are.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Gluteal-strengthening exercises are.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ail.com.\nK E Y W O R D S :\nrunning; running mechanics; gluteus med-\nius; gluteus minimus; hip abductors;\nVOLUME 42 | NUMBER 1 | FEBRUARY 2020\nCopyright \u0001 National Strength and Conditioning Association\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditioning As", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Exploring the Role of the\nLateral Gluteal Muscles in\nRunning: Implications for\nK. Aleisha Fetters, MS, CSCS\nA B S T R A C T\nGluteal-strengthening exercises are\nwidely used for improving running\nperformance. However, as running\noccurs primarily in the sagittal plane,\nsuch strength exercises have\nfocused on the gluteus maximus and\nits role in producing ground reaction\nforces as a hip extensor. Meanwhile,\nstrength deﬁcits in the lateral gluteal\nmuscles, the medius and minimus,\nare common among runners. This\narticle will explore the role of the\nlateral gluteal muscles in running\nkinematics, performance, and\ninjury, with implications for strength\nand conditioning professionals,\nsports medicine specialists, and\nphysical therapists. Recommenda-\ntions for strengthening the gluteus\nmedius and minimus in runners will\ninking the lower extremities to\nthe axial skeleton, the hips are\nintegral to running mechanics.\nOf the more than 20 muscles that act\non the ball-and-socket joint (3), the\ngluteals are arguably the most critical\nto running performance. Comprising\nand minimus, they form the largest\nmuscle group of the hips. Together,\nthey allow the femur to act in all 3\ntransverse—performing\nsion, ﬂexion, abduction, adduction,\ninternal rotation, and external rota-\nRunning is primarily a sagittal plane\nactivity, with hip extension serving\nas a fundamental movement pattern.\nHip extension is largely responsible\nforces necessary to move the body\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ail.com.\nK E Y W O R D S :\nrunning; running mechanics; gluteus med-\nius; gluteus minimus; hip abductors;\nVOLUME 42 | NUMBER 1 | FEBRUARY 2020\nCopyright \u0001 National Strength and Conditioning Association\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditioning As"}
