tion of\nthe lateral gluteal muscles is to stabi-\nlize the hip joint throughout the\nElectromyography suggests that glu-\nteus medius activity peaks in the begin-\nning of the gait cycle; (23,25) at foot\nstrike, the gluteus medius acts eccentri-\ncally to control hip adduction and,\nthen, from the support phase into pro-\npul", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "to abduction\nas well as internal and external rota-\ntion (24). The gluteus minimus ante-\nrotation, most notably when the\nhip is ﬂexed. The posterior ﬁbers of\nand minimus also contribute to hip\nCONTRIBUTIONS OF THE LATERAL\nIn running, the primary func"}
