ion of\nthe lateral gluteal muscles is to stabi-\nlize the hip joint throughout the\nElectromyography suggests that glu-\nteus medius activity peaks in the begin-\nning of the gait cycle; (23,25) at foot\nstrike, the gluteus medius acts eccentri-\ncally to control hip adduction and,\nthen, from the support phase into pro-\npul", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "on have been observed\nwith increased running speeds (23).\nDuring the midphase and late phases\nof the gait cycle, the gluteus minimus\nassists in stabilizing the hips (9). Elec-\ntromyography shows that the ﬁbers of\nthe posterior segment are largely re-"}
{"chunk_id": "Gluteal-strengthening exercises are_p0003_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_b09a10ec3f70", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4058bd8f4e078564", "text_span_hash": "4058bd8f4e078564", "source_file": "Gluteal-strengthening exercises are.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Gluteal-strengthening exercises are.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "nefﬁciency, and\ncould therefore adversely affect running\nspeed and performance (5).\nThe instability created by weaknesses\nin the lateral gluteals has been shown\nto simultaneously increase the peak\nimpact vertical ground reaction forces\nin running and", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "extension in the stance phase of run-\nThe gluteus medius and minimus pro-\nduce peak forces that, in sum, are 3.5\ntimes that of the gluteus maximus.\nThis is likely due to the lateral gluteals’\ncontribution to hip joint movements in\nall 3 planes of motion during run-\nWeaknesses in the lateral gluteals can\ncontribute to an increase in thigh\nadduction and frontal plane pelvic drop,\nthe latter of which has been shown to\nresult in metabolic inefﬁciency, and\ncould therefore adversely affect running\nspeed and performance (5).\nThe instability created by weaknesses\nin the lateral gluteals has been shown\nto simultaneously increase the peak\nimpact vertical ground reaction forces\nin running and impair the body’s ability\nto properly absorb those forces. This\ncan potentially increase the risk of\nlower extremity injury (2).\nAlthough the role of hip abductor\nweaknesses in iliotibial band syndrome\nis currently debated (1,6), weaknesses\nin the gluteus medius speciﬁcally have\nbeen shown to increase knee valgus, or\ndynamic Q-angle. theoretically creat-\ning excess tension on the iliotibial band\nand increasing its risk of impingement\nagainst the femur. Dynamic Q-angle\nrefers to the changing line of force of\nthe quadriceps, illustrated by a line\nconnecting the anterior superior iliac\nspine to the midpoint of the patella.\ngreatest in the early stance phase of\nthe gait cycle when, to absorb ground\nreaction forces, maximal deceleration\nAn increased dyna", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "nefﬁciency, and\ncould therefore adversely affect running\nspeed and performance (5).\nThe instability created by weaknesses\nin the lateral gluteals has been shown\nto simultaneously increase the peak\nimpact vertical ground reaction forces\nin running and"}
{"chunk_id": "Gluteal-strengthening exercises are_p0003_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_b09a10ec3f70", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "799fde7638bd6c05", "text_span_hash": "799fde7638bd6c05", "source_file": "Gluteal-strengthening exercises are.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Gluteal-strengthening exercises are.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "igher than\nthe planted one. Maintain\nfull hip extension in the\nplanted leg throughout. Per-\nform all repetitions and then\nrepeat on the opposite side.\nFigure 5. Hip hitch with toe tap. (A) Stand\ntall with a short object (about\n6–8 in) in front of fee", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "extension in the stance phase of run-\nThe gluteus medius and minimus pro-\nduce peak forces that, in sum, are 3.5\ntimes that of the gluteus maximus.\nThis is likely due to the lateral gluteals’\ncontribution to hip joint movements in\nall 3 planes of motion during run-\nWeaknesses in the lateral gluteals can\ncontribute to an increase in thigh\nadduction and frontal plane pelvic drop,\nthe latter of which has been shown to\nresult in metabolic inefﬁciency, and\ncould therefore adversely affect running\nspeed and performance (5).\nThe instability created by weaknesses\nin the lateral gluteals has been shown\nto simultaneously increase the peak\nimpact vertical ground reaction forces\nin running and impair the body’s ability\nto properly absorb those forces. This\ncan potentially increase the risk of\nlower extremity injury (2).\nAlthough the role of hip abductor\nweaknesses in iliotibial band syndrome\nis currently debated (1,6), weaknesses\nin the gluteus medius speciﬁcally have\nbeen shown to increase knee valgus, or\ndynamic Q-angle. theoretically creat-\ning excess tension on the iliotibial band\nand increasing its risk of impingement\nagainst the femur. Dynamic Q-angle\nrefers to the changing line of force of\nthe quadriceps, illustrated by a line\nconnecting the anterior superior iliac\nspine to the midpoint of the patella.\ngreatest in the early stance phase of\nthe gait cycle when, to absorb ground\nreaction forces, maximal deceleration\nAn increased dyna", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "igher than\nthe planted one. Maintain\nfull hip extension in the\nplanted leg throughout. Per-\nform all repetitions and then\nrepeat on the opposite side.\nFigure 5. Hip hitch with toe tap. (A) Stand\ntall with a short object (about\n6–8 in) in front of fee"}
{"chunk_id": "Gluteal-strengthening exercises are_p0003_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_b09a10ec3f70", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "446d9d61c49fa156", "text_span_hash": "446d9d61c49fa156", "source_file": "Gluteal-strengthening exercises are.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Gluteal-strengthening exercises are.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "h the toes to the object.\nPause and then lower the hip.\nMaintain full hip extension in\nthe planted leg throughout to\nincrease gluteus medius acti-\nvation. Perform all repetitions\nand then repeat on the\nFigure 6. Hip hitch with leg swing. (A)\nStand ta", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "extension in the stance phase of run-\nThe gluteus medius and minimus pro-\nduce peak forces that, in sum, are 3.5\ntimes that of the gluteus maximus.\nThis is likely due to the lateral gluteals’\ncontribution to hip joint movements in\nall 3 planes of motion during run-\nWeaknesses in the lateral gluteals can\ncontribute to an increase in thigh\nadduction and frontal plane pelvic drop,\nthe latter of which has been shown to\nresult in metabolic inefﬁciency, and\ncould therefore adversely affect running\nspeed and performance (5).\nThe instability created by weaknesses\nin the lateral gluteals has been shown\nto simultaneously increase the peak\nimpact vertical ground reaction forces\nin running and impair the body’s ability\nto properly absorb those forces. This\ncan potentially increase the risk of\nlower extremity injury (2).\nAlthough the role of hip abductor\nweaknesses in iliotibial band syndrome\nis currently debated (1,6), weaknesses\nin the gluteus medius speciﬁcally have\nbeen shown to increase knee valgus, or\ndynamic Q-angle. theoretically creat-\ning excess tension on the iliotibial band\nand increasing its risk of impingement\nagainst the femur. Dynamic Q-angle\nrefers to the changing line of force of\nthe quadriceps, illustrated by a line\nconnecting the anterior superior iliac\nspine to the midpoint of the patella.\ngreatest in the early stance phase of\nthe gait cycle when, to absorb ground\nreaction forces, maximal deceleration\nAn increased dyna", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "h the toes to the object.\nPause and then lower the hip.\nMaintain full hip extension in\nthe planted leg throughout to\nincrease gluteus medius acti-\nvation. Perform all repetitions\nand then repeat on the\nFigure 6. Hip hitch with leg swing. (A)\nStand ta"}
{"chunk_id": "Gluteal-strengthening exercises are_p0003_c0016", "source_registry_id": "src_b09a10ec3f70", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "03120cdbc495087d", "text_span_hash": "03120cdbc495087d", "source_file": "Gluteal-strengthening exercises are.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Gluteal-strengthening exercises are.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 16, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "he Lateral Gluteal Muscles in Running\nVOLUME 42 | NUMBER 1 | FEBRUARY 2020\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditioning Association. Unauthorized reproduction of this article is prohibited.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "extension in the stance phase of run-\nThe gluteus medius and minimus pro-\nduce peak forces that, in sum, are 3.5\ntimes that of the gluteus maximus.\nThis is likely due to the lateral gluteals’\ncontribution to hip joint movements in\nall 3 planes of motion during run-\nWeaknesses in the lateral gluteals can\ncontribute to an increase in thigh\nadduction and frontal plane pelvic drop,\nthe latter of which has been shown to\nresult in metabolic inefﬁciency, and\ncould therefore adversely affect running\nspeed and performance (5).\nThe instability created by weaknesses\nin the lateral gluteals has been shown\nto simultaneously increase the peak\nimpact vertical ground reaction forces\nin running and impair the body’s ability\nto properly absorb those forces. This\ncan potentially increase the risk of\nlower extremity injury (2).\nAlthough the role of hip abductor\nweaknesses in iliotibial band syndrome\nis currently debated (1,6), weaknesses\nin the gluteus medius speciﬁcally have\nbeen shown to increase knee valgus, or\ndynamic Q-angle. theoretically creat-\ning excess tension on the iliotibial band\nand increasing its risk of impingement\nagainst the femur. Dynamic Q-angle\nrefers to the changing line of force of\nthe quadriceps, illustrated by a line\nconnecting the anterior superior iliac\nspine to the midpoint of the patella.\ngreatest in the early stance phase of\nthe gait cycle when, to absorb ground\nreaction forces, maximal deceleration\nAn increased dyna", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "he Lateral Gluteal Muscles in Running\nVOLUME 42 | NUMBER 1 | FEBRUARY 2020\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditioning Association. Unauthorized reproduction of this article is prohibited."}
{"chunk_id": "Gluteal-strengthening exercises are_p0004_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_b09a10ec3f70", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "dd534058df772c93", "text_span_hash": "dd534058df772c93", "source_file": "Gluteal-strengthening exercises are.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Gluteal-strengthening exercises are.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "-\nterior segment of the gluteus minimus\nHowever, because running is a dynamic\nactivity involving multijoint movement,\nit is also important to integrate com-\npound resistance training exercises to\nstrengthen the lateral gluteal muscles in\nrunners. Uni", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "showed evidence that the strengthen-\ning of the hip abductor muscles can\nreduce pain and improve function in\nHowever, authors note that research\nin highly trained athletes with patello-\nfemoral pain is currently lacking (20).\nmuscles have also been linked with\npain (4) as well as greater trochanteric\npain syndrome (8), and strengthening\nthe hip abductors and external rotators,\nwhich would include both the gluteus\nmedius and minimus, has been shown\nto be effective in helping reduce pain in\nplantar fasciitis (13).\nSTRENGTHENING THE LATERAL\nGLUTEALS IN RUNNERS:\nGiven the functions of the lateral gluteal\nmuscles and their role in running\nmechanics and injury risk, it is clear that\nexternal rotation, and, to some degree,\ninternal rotation can help recruit the\ngluteus medius and minimus to address\nstrength deﬁcits in those muscles.\nThe hip hitch (Figure 4), hip hitch with\ntoe tap (Figure 5), and hip hitch with\nleg swing (Figure 6) have been shown\nto produce high levels of muscle activ-\nity in all sections of the gluteus medius\n(Figure 7) has similarly been shown\nto elicit high levels of activation of\nthe posterior ﬁbers of the gluteus med-\nius (17). This may be due to the lateral\ngluteals’ primary role of stabilizing the\nplanted leg in single stance.\nHigh levels of gluteus medius activa-\ntion occur in the single-leg bridge (Fig-\nure 8), clamshell (Figure 9), side plank\nwith hip abduction (Figure 10), and\nside-lying hip abduction (Fi", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "-\nterior segment of the gluteus minimus\nHowever, because running is a dynamic\nactivity involving multijoint movement,\nit is also important to integrate com-\npound resistance training exercises to\nstrengthen the lateral gluteal muscles in\nrunners. Uni"}
