s of the gluteus med-\nius (17). This may be due to the lateral\ngluteals’ primary role of stabilizing the\nplanted leg in single stance.\nHigh levels of gluteus medius activa-\ntion occur in the single-leg bridge (Fig-\nure 8), clamshell (Figure 9), side plank\nwith hip abduction (Figure 10), and\nside-lying hip abduction (Fi", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "increase glu-\nteus medius activation. Main-\ntain a level lumbo-pelvic\nposition (not letting the pelvis\ndrop at all) throughout the\nexercise. Hold. Perform all\nrepetitions on 1 side, then\nrepeat on the opposite side, or\nperform alternating sides with"}
