ers of the gluteus med-\nius (17). This may be due to the lateral\ngluteals’ primary role of stabilizing the\nplanted leg in single stance.\nHigh levels of gluteus medius activa-\ntion occur in the single-leg bridge (Fig-\nure 8), clamshell (Figure 9), side plank\nwith hip abduction (Figure 10), and\nside-lying hip abduction (Fi", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "to the ﬂoor on full hip\nextension. However, some\nachieve heightened activa-\ntion with a foot setup that\nallows the shin to form an\nacute or obtuse angle with\nthe ﬂoor on full extension).\nResist the tendency for the\nhip of the raised leg to drop.\nPerf"}
