ises fol-\nAt the onset of gluteal-speciﬁc train-\ning, medius and minimus exercises\nshould be performed according to\nthe rate of perceived exertion with\n1–2 minutes of rest between sets.\nThe target rate of perceived exertion\nshould be based on the athlete’s cur-\nrent strength and injury status. Over\nloads can be pro", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ciﬁc training follows a traditional\nresistance training model consisting\nof 3–5 sets of an exercise, depending\non the performance level. At the begin-\nning of a training session, more com-\nplex and heavily loaded exercises may\nloaded and bodyweight e"}
{"chunk_id": "Gluteal-strengthening exercises are_p0005_c0016", "source_registry_id": "src_b09a10ec3f70", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "17ea245312e61f81", "text_span_hash": "17ea245312e61f81", "source_file": "Gluteal-strengthening exercises are.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Gluteal-strengthening exercises are.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 16, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "toward ﬂoor without putting\nany weight into the foot.\nPerform all repetitions and\nthen repeat on the opposite\nside. Bench height can be\nincreased and external load\nadded to increase resistance.\nThe Role of the Lateral Gluteal Muscles in Running\nVOLU", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "sports-speciﬁc to runners. These exer-\ncises functionally train the lateral gluteals\nto stabilize the hips, just as they are\nrequired to do during the single-leg\nstance phase of running. Unilateral com-\npound exercises that have been shown\nto produce high levels of gluteus medius\nactivation include the lateral step-up\n(Figure 12), single-leg squat (Figure 13),\nand skater squat (Figure 14) (26).\nSingle-leg squats and skater squats are\nboth advanced movements and also\nquadriceps. It is advised that athletes\nsquats and use a small range of motion\nbefore increasing unilateral squat depth.\nIn the gluteus minimus, high activity lev-\nels have been generated in the anterior\nsegment, by the resisted hip abduction–\nextension, and in the posterior segment,\nby the single-leg bridge, side-lying hip\nabduction, and single-leg squat (15).\nIt has been advised that lateral gluteal-\nspeciﬁc training follows a traditional\nresistance training model consisting\nof 3–5 sets of an exercise, depending\non the performance level. At the begin-\nning of a training session, more com-\nplex and heavily loaded exercises may\nloaded and bodyweight exercises fol-\nAt the onset of gluteal-speciﬁc train-\ning, medius and minimus exercises\nshould be performed according to\nthe rate of perceived exertion with\n1–2 minutes of rest between sets.\nThe target rate of perceived exertion\nshould be based on the athlete’s cur-\nrent strength and injury status. Over\nloads can be pro", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "toward ﬂoor without putting\nany weight into the foot.\nPerform all repetitions and\nthen repeat on the opposite\nside. Bench height can be\nincreased and external load\nadded to increase resistance.\nThe Role of the Lateral Gluteal Muscles in Running\nVOLU"}
{"chunk_id": "Gluteal-strengthening exercises are_p0005_c0017", "source_registry_id": "src_b09a10ec3f70", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1a9e925e0170ccac", "text_span_hash": "1a9e925e0170ccac", "source_file": "Gluteal-strengthening exercises are.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Gluteal-strengthening exercises are.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 17, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "luteal Muscles in Running\nVOLUME 42 | NUMBER 1 | FEBRUARY 2020\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditioning Association. Unauthorized reproduction of this article is prohibited.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "sports-speciﬁc to runners. These exer-\ncises functionally train the lateral gluteals\nto stabilize the hips, just as they are\nrequired to do during the single-leg\nstance phase of running. Unilateral com-\npound exercises that have been shown\nto produce high levels of gluteus medius\nactivation include the lateral step-up\n(Figure 12), single-leg squat (Figure 13),\nand skater squat (Figure 14) (26).\nSingle-leg squats and skater squats are\nboth advanced movements and also\nquadriceps. It is advised that athletes\nsquats and use a small range of motion\nbefore increasing unilateral squat depth.\nIn the gluteus minimus, high activity lev-\nels have been generated in the anterior\nsegment, by the resisted hip abduction–\nextension, and in the posterior segment,\nby the single-leg bridge, side-lying hip\nabduction, and single-leg squat (15).\nIt has been advised that lateral gluteal-\nspeciﬁc training follows a traditional\nresistance training model consisting\nof 3–5 sets of an exercise, depending\non the performance level. At the begin-\nning of a training session, more com-\nplex and heavily loaded exercises may\nloaded and bodyweight exercises fol-\nAt the onset of gluteal-speciﬁc train-\ning, medius and minimus exercises\nshould be performed according to\nthe rate of perceived exertion with\n1–2 minutes of rest between sets.\nThe target rate of perceived exertion\nshould be based on the athlete’s cur-\nrent strength and injury status. Over\nloads can be pro", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "luteal Muscles in Running\nVOLUME 42 | NUMBER 1 | FEBRUARY 2020\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditioning Association. Unauthorized reproduction of this article is prohibited."}
{"chunk_id": "Gluteal-strengthening exercises are_p0006_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_b09a10ec3f70", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "858bc9398ebc7790", "text_span_hash": "858bc9398ebc7790", "source_file": "Gluteal-strengthening exercises are.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Gluteal-strengthening exercises are.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "h imbalance, the\nweaker side should determine all pro-\ngramming—including training volume,\nrepetitions. The athlete\nshould also train the weaker side\nbefore the stronger one. Unilateral ex-\nercises may be advised over bilateral\nFinally, although the", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "In cases of injury or severe unilateral\nhip abductor strength imbalance, run-\nners may need to avoid heavily loaded\nexercise in favor of protocols more\ngeared to muscular endurance, with\nlight sets of 15–30 reps (18). Professio-\nnals without a strong background in\nrehabilitation should consult with or\nrefer to a specialized physical therapist\nfor recommendations individualized\nto that athlete. In cases of unilateral\nhip abductor strength imbalance, the\nweaker side should determine all pro-\ngramming—including training volume,\nrepetitions. The athlete\nshould also train the weaker side\nbefore the stronger one. Unilateral ex-\nercises may be advised over bilateral\nFinally, although the lateral gluteals\nfunction to stabilize the pelvis and knee,\nelectromyography shows that perform-\ning on an unstable surface does not\nrecruitment during squatting. The use\nof unstable surfaces in lateral gluteal\ntraining is likely unnecessary (26).\nPRACTICAL APPLICATIONS AND\nDeﬁcits in lateral gluteal strength,\nrunning performance and injury risk,\nare common among recreational and\nelite runners. Therefore, it is impor-\ntant for strength and conditioning\nprofessionals, sports medicine spe-\ncialists, and physical therapists to\nhelp their athletes optimize gluteus\nmedius and minimus strength and\nthe roles of both muscles in running\nmechanics, efﬁciency, and overuse\ninjuries such as IT band syndrome\nand low-back pain. Although more\nresearch is needed, som", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "h imbalance, the\nweaker side should determine all pro-\ngramming—including training volume,\nrepetitions. The athlete\nshould also train the weaker side\nbefore the stronger one. Unilateral ex-\nercises may be advised over bilateral\nFinally, although the"}
{"chunk_id": "Gluteal-strengthening exercises are_p0006_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_b09a10ec3f70", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "fdc39a89e556abd5", "text_span_hash": "fdc39a89e556abd5", "source_file": "Gluteal-strengthening exercises are.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Gluteal-strengthening exercises are.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "t is impor-\ntant for strength and conditioning\nprofessionals, sports medicine spe-\ncialists, and physical therapists to\nhelp their athletes optimize gluteus\nmedius and minimus strength and\nthe roles of both muscles in running\nmechanics, efﬁciency, an", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "In cases of injury or severe unilateral\nhip abductor strength imbalance, run-\nners may need to avoid heavily loaded\nexercise in favor of protocols more\ngeared to muscular endurance, with\nlight sets of 15–30 reps (18). Professio-\nnals without a strong background in\nrehabilitation should consult with or\nrefer to a specialized physical therapist\nfor recommendations individualized\nto that athlete. In cases of unilateral\nhip abductor strength imbalance, the\nweaker side should determine all pro-\ngramming—including training volume,\nrepetitions. The athlete\nshould also train the weaker side\nbefore the stronger one. Unilateral ex-\nercises may be advised over bilateral\nFinally, although the lateral gluteals\nfunction to stabilize the pelvis and knee,\nelectromyography shows that perform-\ning on an unstable surface does not\nrecruitment during squatting. The use\nof unstable surfaces in lateral gluteal\ntraining is likely unnecessary (26).\nPRACTICAL APPLICATIONS AND\nDeﬁcits in lateral gluteal strength,\nrunning performance and injury risk,\nare common among recreational and\nelite runners. Therefore, it is impor-\ntant for strength and conditioning\nprofessionals, sports medicine spe-\ncialists, and physical therapists to\nhelp their athletes optimize gluteus\nmedius and minimus strength and\nthe roles of both muscles in running\nmechanics, efﬁciency, and overuse\ninjuries such as IT band syndrome\nand low-back pain. Although more\nresearch is needed, som", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "t is impor-\ntant for strength and conditioning\nprofessionals, sports medicine spe-\ncialists, and physical therapists to\nhelp their athletes optimize gluteus\nmedius and minimus strength and\nthe roles of both muscles in running\nmechanics, efﬁciency, an"}
{"chunk_id": "Gluteal-strengthening exercises are_p0006_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_b09a10ec3f70", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "28433ee644ffa129", "text_span_hash": "28433ee644ffa129", "source_file": "Gluteal-strengthening exercises are.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Gluteal-strengthening exercises are.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "tion, should\nformed, with training load and vol-\nume contingent on current strength\nlevels and rehabilitation status.\nConﬂicts of Interest and Source of Funding:\nThe author reports no conﬂicts of interest\nand no source of funding.\nK. Aleisha Fetters", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "In cases of injury or severe unilateral\nhip abductor strength imbalance, run-\nners may need to avoid heavily loaded\nexercise in favor of protocols more\ngeared to muscular endurance, with\nlight sets of 15–30 reps (18). Professio-\nnals without a strong background in\nrehabilitation should consult with or\nrefer to a specialized physical therapist\nfor recommendations individualized\nto that athlete. In cases of unilateral\nhip abductor strength imbalance, the\nweaker side should determine all pro-\ngramming—including training volume,\nrepetitions. The athlete\nshould also train the weaker side\nbefore the stronger one. Unilateral ex-\nercises may be advised over bilateral\nFinally, although the lateral gluteals\nfunction to stabilize the pelvis and knee,\nelectromyography shows that perform-\ning on an unstable surface does not\nrecruitment during squatting. The use\nof unstable surfaces in lateral gluteal\ntraining is likely unnecessary (26).\nPRACTICAL APPLICATIONS AND\nDeﬁcits in lateral gluteal strength,\nrunning performance and injury risk,\nare common among recreational and\nelite runners. Therefore, it is impor-\ntant for strength and conditioning\nprofessionals, sports medicine spe-\ncialists, and physical therapists to\nhelp their athletes optimize gluteus\nmedius and minimus strength and\nthe roles of both muscles in running\nmechanics, efﬁciency, and overuse\ninjuries such as IT band syndrome\nand low-back pain. Although more\nresearch is needed, som", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "tion, should\nformed, with training load and vol-\nume contingent on current strength\nlevels and rehabilitation status.\nConﬂicts of Interest and Source of Funding:\nThe author reports no conﬂicts of interest\nand no source of funding.\nK. Aleisha Fetters"}
{"chunk_id": "Gluteal-strengthening exercises are_p0006_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_b09a10ec3f70", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2d3801c9eb8e1e4d", "text_span_hash": "2d3801c9eb8e1e4d", "source_file": "Gluteal-strengthening exercises are.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Gluteal-strengthening exercises are.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "orts Med 4: 51–57, 2010.\nCooper NA, Scavo KM, Strickland KJ, et al.\nPrevalence of gluteus medius weakness in\npeople with chronic low back pain\ncompared to healthy controls. Eur Spine J\nEstivalet M and Brisson P. Relationship\nbetween gluteus medius mu", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "In cases of injury or severe unilateral\nhip abductor strength imbalance, run-\nners may need to avoid heavily loaded\nexercise in favor of protocols more\ngeared to muscular endurance, with\nlight sets of 15–30 reps (18). Professio-\nnals without a strong background in\nrehabilitation should consult with or\nrefer to a specialized physical therapist\nfor recommendations individualized\nto that athlete. In cases of unilateral\nhip abductor strength imbalance, the\nweaker side should determine all pro-\ngramming—including training volume,\nrepetitions. The athlete\nshould also train the weaker side\nbefore the stronger one. Unilateral ex-\nercises may be advised over bilateral\nFinally, although the lateral gluteals\nfunction to stabilize the pelvis and knee,\nelectromyography shows that perform-\ning on an unstable surface does not\nrecruitment during squatting. The use\nof unstable surfaces in lateral gluteal\ntraining is likely unnecessary (26).\nPRACTICAL APPLICATIONS AND\nDeﬁcits in lateral gluteal strength,\nrunning performance and injury risk,\nare common among recreational and\nelite runners. Therefore, it is impor-\ntant for strength and conditioning\nprofessionals, sports medicine spe-\ncialists, and physical therapists to\nhelp their athletes optimize gluteus\nmedius and minimus strength and\nthe roles of both muscles in running\nmechanics, efﬁciency, and overuse\ninjuries such as IT band syndrome\nand low-back pain. Although more\nresearch is needed, som", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "orts Med 4: 51–57, 2010.\nCooper NA, Scavo KM, Strickland KJ, et al.\nPrevalence of gluteus medius weakness in\npeople with chronic low back pain\ncompared to healthy controls. Eur Spine J\nEstivalet M and Brisson P. Relationship\nbetween gluteus medius mu"}
{"chunk_id": "Gluteal-strengthening exercises are_p0006_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_b09a10ec3f70", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "a7d0fcf3f2ded4a1", "text_span_hash": "a7d0fcf3f2ded4a1", "source_file": "Gluteal-strengthening exercises are.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Gluteal-strengthening exercises are.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ship\nbetween gluteus medius muscle activity,\npelvic motion, and metabolic energy in running\nFigure 13. Single-leg squat. (A) Stand tall\nwith feet together, transfer\nbody weight to 1 leg and\nextend the opposite leg and\nboth arms in front of the\nbody.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "In cases of injury or severe unilateral\nhip abductor strength imbalance, run-\nners may need to avoid heavily loaded\nexercise in favor of protocols more\ngeared to muscular endurance, with\nlight sets of 15–30 reps (18). Professio-\nnals without a strong background in\nrehabilitation should consult with or\nrefer to a specialized physical therapist\nfor recommendations individualized\nto that athlete. In cases of unilateral\nhip abductor strength imbalance, the\nweaker side should determine all pro-\ngramming—including training volume,\nrepetitions. The athlete\nshould also train the weaker side\nbefore the stronger one. Unilateral ex-\nercises may be advised over bilateral\nFinally, although the lateral gluteals\nfunction to stabilize the pelvis and knee,\nelectromyography shows that perform-\ning on an unstable surface does not\nrecruitment during squatting. The use\nof unstable surfaces in lateral gluteal\ntraining is likely unnecessary (26).\nPRACTICAL APPLICATIONS AND\nDeﬁcits in lateral gluteal strength,\nrunning performance and injury risk,\nare common among recreational and\nelite runners. Therefore, it is impor-\ntant for strength and conditioning\nprofessionals, sports medicine spe-\ncialists, and physical therapists to\nhelp their athletes optimize gluteus\nmedius and minimus strength and\nthe roles of both muscles in running\nmechanics, efﬁciency, and overuse\ninjuries such as IT band syndrome\nand low-back pain. Although more\nresearch is needed, som", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ship\nbetween gluteus medius muscle activity,\npelvic motion, and metabolic energy in running\nFigure 13. Single-leg squat. (A) Stand tall\nwith feet together, transfer\nbody weight to 1 leg and\nextend the opposite leg and\nboth arms in front of the\nbody."}
{"chunk_id": "Gluteal-strengthening exercises are_p0006_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_b09a10ec3f70", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3bced3d4e0fe0293", "text_span_hash": "3bced3d4e0fe0293", "source_file": "Gluteal-strengthening exercises are.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Gluteal-strengthening exercises are.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "rm all\nrepetitions and then repeat\non the opposite side. Squat\ndepth will depend on not\nonly strength but also\nmobility as deﬁned by joint\nstructure. Perform sitting to\na tall bench or step before\nprogressing below parallel.\nHolding a weight in the\no", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "In cases of injury or severe unilateral\nhip abductor strength imbalance, run-\nners may need to avoid heavily loaded\nexercise in favor of protocols more\ngeared to muscular endurance, with\nlight sets of 15–30 reps (18). Professio-\nnals without a strong background in\nrehabilitation should consult with or\nrefer to a specialized physical therapist\nfor recommendations individualized\nto that athlete. In cases of unilateral\nhip abductor strength imbalance, the\nweaker side should determine all pro-\ngramming—including training volume,\nrepetitions. The athlete\nshould also train the weaker side\nbefore the stronger one. Unilateral ex-\nercises may be advised over bilateral\nFinally, although the lateral gluteals\nfunction to stabilize the pelvis and knee,\nelectromyography shows that perform-\ning on an unstable surface does not\nrecruitment during squatting. The use\nof unstable surfaces in lateral gluteal\ntraining is likely unnecessary (26).\nPRACTICAL APPLICATIONS AND\nDeﬁcits in lateral gluteal strength,\nrunning performance and injury risk,\nare common among recreational and\nelite runners. Therefore, it is impor-\ntant for strength and conditioning\nprofessionals, sports medicine spe-\ncialists, and physical therapists to\nhelp their athletes optimize gluteus\nmedius and minimus strength and\nthe roles of both muscles in running\nmechanics, efﬁciency, and overuse\ninjuries such as IT band syndrome\nand low-back pain. Although more\nresearch is needed, som", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "rm all\nrepetitions and then repeat\non the opposite side. Squat\ndepth will depend on not\nonly strength but also\nmobility as deﬁned by joint\nstructure. Perform sitting to\na tall bench or step before\nprogressing below parallel.\nHolding a weight in the\no"}
