g and translate key ﬁnd-\nings from research into useable\nevidence-based recommendations for\npacing strategy preparation and\nadherence during competition.\nacing is the purposeful distribu-\ntion of speed, power, or energy\nprocess behind pacing (i.e., strategy)\nis recognized as an important element\nin endurance performance optimiza-\ntion. The challenge posed to runners\nis to avoid prem", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "performance. Further discus-\nsion of mechanisms underpinning pac-\npractitioner-focused review. Rather, this\nreview aims to translate key ﬁndings\nfrom research into useable evidence-\nbased pacing strategy recommenda-\ntions for runners and practitioner"}
{"chunk_id": "J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla_p0001_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_cdf0bb142189", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "85322169c13058bf", "text_span_hash": "85322169c13058bf", "source_file": "J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "nce.\nPACING STRATEGY OVERVIEW\nThere is not unanimous agreement on\nthe “best” pacing strategy for all runners\nand race types, in part, because pacing is\na complex process inﬂuenced by a num-\nber of factors (Figure 1). Figure 2\nillustrates the 5 genera", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Practitioner-Focused\nStrategies in Running\nJ. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hillary A. Yoder, MS,3 and David P. Looney, PhD, CSCS4\n1Department of Exercise and Nutrition Sciences, Center for Research and Education in Special Environments,\nUniversity at Buffalo, Buffalo, New York; 2Division of Kinesiology & Health, University of Wyoming, Laramie, Wyoming;\n3Department of Kinesiology, University of Alabama, Tuscaloosa, Alabama; and 4United States Army Research Institute\nof Environmental Medicine (USARIEM), Natick, Massachusetts\nA B S T R A C T\nThe purposeful distribution of speed,\npower, or energy is termed as the\npacing or pacing strategy and is rec-\nognized as a key determinant in opti-\nmal run performance. There is no\nagreement on the best pacing strategy\nfor all runners and race types. Thus, the\nchallenge posed to runners and prac-\ntitioners is pacing strategy selection\nand in-race adherence. This review\nbrieﬂy discusses pacing strategy types\nand selection considerations. More\nimportantly, we overview factors inﬂu-\nencing pacing and translate key ﬁnd-\nings from research into useable\nevidence-based recommendations for\npacing strategy preparation and\nadherence during competition.\nacing is the purposeful distribu-\ntion of speed, power, or energy\nprocess behind pacing (i.e., strategy)\nis recognized as an important element\nin endurance performance optimiza-\ntion. The challenge posed to runners\nis to avoid prem", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "nce.\nPACING STRATEGY OVERVIEW\nThere is not unanimous agreement on\nthe “best” pacing strategy for all runners\nand race types, in part, because pacing is\na complex process inﬂuenced by a num-\nber of factors (Figure 1). Figure 2\nillustrates the 5 genera"}
{"chunk_id": "J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla_p0001_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_cdf0bb142189", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2283bf9f1440c495", "text_span_hash": "2283bf9f1440c495", "source_file": "J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "o be optimal in events of 2 mi-\nnutes to 24 hours based on mathemat-\nical models (76,87), men’s track world-\nHowever, most real-life competitions\nare not characterized by time-trial type\ncompetitions (28). Runners must bal-\nance human-environment int", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Practitioner-Focused\nStrategies in Running\nJ. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hillary A. Yoder, MS,3 and David P. Looney, PhD, CSCS4\n1Department of Exercise and Nutrition Sciences, Center for Research and Education in Special Environments,\nUniversity at Buffalo, Buffalo, New York; 2Division of Kinesiology & Health, University of Wyoming, Laramie, Wyoming;\n3Department of Kinesiology, University of Alabama, Tuscaloosa, Alabama; and 4United States Army Research Institute\nof Environmental Medicine (USARIEM), Natick, Massachusetts\nA B S T R A C T\nThe purposeful distribution of speed,\npower, or energy is termed as the\npacing or pacing strategy and is rec-\nognized as a key determinant in opti-\nmal run performance. There is no\nagreement on the best pacing strategy\nfor all runners and race types. Thus, the\nchallenge posed to runners and prac-\ntitioners is pacing strategy selection\nand in-race adherence. This review\nbrieﬂy discusses pacing strategy types\nand selection considerations. More\nimportantly, we overview factors inﬂu-\nencing pacing and translate key ﬁnd-\nings from research into useable\nevidence-based recommendations for\npacing strategy preparation and\nadherence during competition.\nacing is the purposeful distribu-\ntion of speed, power, or energy\nprocess behind pacing (i.e., strategy)\nis recognized as an important element\nin endurance performance optimiza-\ntion. The challenge posed to runners\nis to avoid prem", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "o be optimal in events of 2 mi-\nnutes to 24 hours based on mathemat-\nical models (76,87), men’s track world-\nHowever, most real-life competitions\nare not characterized by time-trial type\ncompetitions (28). Runners must bal-\nance human-environment int"}
{"chunk_id": "J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla_p0001_c0017", "source_registry_id": "src_cdf0bb142189", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9f6df6d2c5c6ebe0", "text_span_hash": "9f6df6d2c5c6ebe0", "source_file": "J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 17, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": ":\nenergy expenditure; even;\nnegative; positive; endurance; time-\nCopyright \u0001 National Strength and Conditioning Association\nStrength and Conditioning Journal | www.nsca-scj.com\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditioning Association. Unauthorized", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Practitioner-Focused\nStrategies in Running\nJ. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hillary A. Yoder, MS,3 and David P. Looney, PhD, CSCS4\n1Department of Exercise and Nutrition Sciences, Center for Research and Education in Special Environments,\nUniversity at Buffalo, Buffalo, New York; 2Division of Kinesiology & Health, University of Wyoming, Laramie, Wyoming;\n3Department of Kinesiology, University of Alabama, Tuscaloosa, Alabama; and 4United States Army Research Institute\nof Environmental Medicine (USARIEM), Natick, Massachusetts\nA B S T R A C T\nThe purposeful distribution of speed,\npower, or energy is termed as the\npacing or pacing strategy and is rec-\nognized as a key determinant in opti-\nmal run performance. There is no\nagreement on the best pacing strategy\nfor all runners and race types. Thus, the\nchallenge posed to runners and prac-\ntitioners is pacing strategy selection\nand in-race adherence. This review\nbrieﬂy discusses pacing strategy types\nand selection considerations. More\nimportantly, we overview factors inﬂu-\nencing pacing and translate key ﬁnd-\nings from research into useable\nevidence-based recommendations for\npacing strategy preparation and\nadherence during competition.\nacing is the purposeful distribu-\ntion of speed, power, or energy\nprocess behind pacing (i.e., strategy)\nis recognized as an important element\nin endurance performance optimiza-\ntion. The challenge posed to runners\nis to avoid prem", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": ":\nenergy expenditure; even;\nnegative; positive; endurance; time-\nCopyright \u0001 National Strength and Conditioning Association\nStrength and Conditioning Journal | www.nsca-scj.com\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditioning Association. Unauthorized"}
{"chunk_id": "J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla_p0002_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_cdf0bb142189", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "bf0582229542a690", "text_span_hash": "bf0582229542a690", "source_file": "J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "optimal pacing requires additional con-\nsiderations beyond distance/duration.\nEvidence from laboratory studies indi-\ncate distance runners adopt an even-\npacing strategy that minimizes physio-\nlogical, perceptual, and metabolic dis-\nexercise likabili", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "optimal pacing requires additional con-\nsiderations beyond distance/duration.\nEvidence from laboratory studies indi-\ncate distance runners adopt an even-\npacing strategy that minimizes physio-\nlogical, perceptual, and metabolic dis-\nexercise likability and maximizing per-\nformance (1,4,78,79). On the other\nhand, endurance athletes tend to adopt\na positive or variable-pacing strategy in\ncompetition (2,39,89). These ﬁndings\nled some practitioners to suggest a neg-\native pacing strategy to avoid late-race\nfatigue (23). Finally, a terrain-dependent\npacing strategy may be best for race\ncourses with undulating topography\namong practitioners, pacing models,\nstudies underscore the fact that there\nis not one “optimal” pacing strategy for\nall runners and events. Rather, this\nshould be taken as a beginning for\nexploring pacing strategies in various\nindividuals and events. Leveraging pac-\ning in competition requires proper\nstrategy selection with an understand-\ning of factors that could inﬂuence in-\nrace pacing decision-making and over-\nFACTORS INFLUENCING PACING\nA pacing strategy should be identiﬁed\nand integrated early into training pro-\ngramming. Pacing is a self-regulation\nskill that, like most athletic skills, must\nbe practiced and crafted (19). Previous\nrace experience and/or competition\nemulation is essential for developing\nskills that optimize pacing (6,51,83).\nFor example, experienced endurance\nathletes show effective information", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "optimal pacing requires additional con-\nsiderations beyond distance/duration.\nEvidence from laboratory studies indi-\ncate distance runners adopt an even-\npacing strategy that minimizes physio-\nlogical, perceptual, and metabolic dis-\nexercise likabili"}
