G PACING\nA pacing strategy should be identiﬁed\nand integrated early into training pro-\ngramming. Pacing is a self-regulation\nskill that, like most athletic skills, must\nbe practiced and crafted (19). Previous\nrace experience and/or competition\nemulation is essential for developing\nskills that optimize pacing (6,51,83).\nFor example, experienced endurance\nathletes show effective information", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ntial for developing\nskills that optimize pacing (6,51,83).\nFor example, experienced endurance\nathletes show effective information\nseeking behavior (6) and attentional\nfocus on task-related sensory informa-\ntion (e.g., physical and mental exertion\nan"}
{"chunk_id": "J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla_p0002_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_cdf0bb142189", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "43de256beb74576d", "text_span_hash": "43de256beb74576d", "source_file": "J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "mfort in anticipation of\nPacing Strategy in Running\nVOLUME 42 | NUMBER 1 | FEBRUARY 2020\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditioning Association. Unauthorized reproduction of this article is prohibited.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "optimal pacing requires additional con-\nsiderations beyond distance/duration.\nEvidence from laboratory studies indi-\ncate distance runners adopt an even-\npacing strategy that minimizes physio-\nlogical, perceptual, and metabolic dis-\nexercise likability and maximizing per-\nformance (1,4,78,79). On the other\nhand, endurance athletes tend to adopt\na positive or variable-pacing strategy in\ncompetition (2,39,89). These ﬁndings\nled some practitioners to suggest a neg-\native pacing strategy to avoid late-race\nfatigue (23). Finally, a terrain-dependent\npacing strategy may be best for race\ncourses with undulating topography\namong practitioners, pacing models,\nstudies underscore the fact that there\nis not one “optimal” pacing strategy for\nall runners and events. Rather, this\nshould be taken as a beginning for\nexploring pacing strategies in various\nindividuals and events. Leveraging pac-\ning in competition requires proper\nstrategy selection with an understand-\ning of factors that could inﬂuence in-\nrace pacing decision-making and over-\nFACTORS INFLUENCING PACING\nA pacing strategy should be identiﬁed\nand integrated early into training pro-\ngramming. Pacing is a self-regulation\nskill that, like most athletic skills, must\nbe practiced and crafted (19). Previous\nrace experience and/or competition\nemulation is essential for developing\nskills that optimize pacing (6,51,83).\nFor example, experienced endurance\nathletes show effective information", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "mfort in anticipation of\nPacing Strategy in Running\nVOLUME 42 | NUMBER 1 | FEBRUARY 2020\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditioning Association. Unauthorized reproduction of this article is prohibited."}
{"chunk_id": "J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla_p0003_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_cdf0bb142189", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "eb56157bb9aa2622", "text_span_hash": "eb56157bb9aa2622", "source_file": "J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "decision-making. Ideally, coaches and\nrunners should practice foreseeable in-\nrace situations and determine appro-\npriate decision-making options based\non available information congruent\nwith the predetermined pacing strat-\negy. Such actionable infor", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "decision-making. Ideally, coaches and\nrunners should practice foreseeable in-\nrace situations and determine appro-\npriate decision-making options based\non available information congruent\nwith the predetermined pacing strat-\negy. Such actionable information might\ninclude distance covered or remaining,\nmajor terrain changes, feelings of effort\nperformance and pacing with growing\ninﬂuence as race duration/distance in-\n(12,65) result in an earlier and more\nrapid decline in run velocity or power\noutput, with the potential absence of\nan end spurt (41). In addition, greater\ncardiovascular strain occurs as dehy-\ndration progresses (cardiac drift) (54).\nCardiovascular strain seems to inﬂu-\nence effort perception and peak oxygen\nuptake, which is important as exercise\nintensity selection is partly dependent\non perceived effort (22,70,83).\nThe ability to sustain in-race glycogen\nstores and hydration status can vary\ndrastically based on intrinsic (i.e., ath-\nlete choice) and extrinsic factors (e.g.,\nevent provision and distance between\naid stations) factors. Minimizing dehy-\ndration and glycogen depletion is par-\nticularly vital in hot/humid weather\nthese conditions are most likely to\noccur (21,71). Practicing prerace and\nin-race nutritional strategies is recom-\nCHO loading, and high in-race CHO\nintake rates (30–90 g CHO/hr) are\ninversely related to ﬁnish times in lon-\nger events (59,68) despite potential gas-\ntrointestinal complaints (59)", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "decision-making. Ideally, coaches and\nrunners should practice foreseeable in-\nrace situations and determine appro-\npriate decision-making options based\non available information congruent\nwith the predetermined pacing strat-\negy. Such actionable infor"}
{"chunk_id": "J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla_p0003_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_cdf0bb142189", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "71232043ab0363b1", "text_span_hash": "71232043ab0363b1", "source_file": "J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "For example, runners can pre-\nmix a CHO beverage containing 90 g\nof CHO/L and attempt to consume 1\nL over the course of an hour. In events\nwith aid stations, the runner may\ninstead choose to take in large quanti-\nties of CHO in a single feeding. Long", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "decision-making. Ideally, coaches and\nrunners should practice foreseeable in-\nrace situations and determine appro-\npriate decision-making options based\non available information congruent\nwith the predetermined pacing strat-\negy. Such actionable information might\ninclude distance covered or remaining,\nmajor terrain changes, feelings of effort\nperformance and pacing with growing\ninﬂuence as race duration/distance in-\n(12,65) result in an earlier and more\nrapid decline in run velocity or power\noutput, with the potential absence of\nan end spurt (41). In addition, greater\ncardiovascular strain occurs as dehy-\ndration progresses (cardiac drift) (54).\nCardiovascular strain seems to inﬂu-\nence effort perception and peak oxygen\nuptake, which is important as exercise\nintensity selection is partly dependent\non perceived effort (22,70,83).\nThe ability to sustain in-race glycogen\nstores and hydration status can vary\ndrastically based on intrinsic (i.e., ath-\nlete choice) and extrinsic factors (e.g.,\nevent provision and distance between\naid stations) factors. Minimizing dehy-\ndration and glycogen depletion is par-\nticularly vital in hot/humid weather\nthese conditions are most likely to\noccur (21,71). Practicing prerace and\nin-race nutritional strategies is recom-\nCHO loading, and high in-race CHO\nintake rates (30–90 g CHO/hr) are\ninversely related to ﬁnish times in lon-\nger events (59,68) despite potential gas-\ntrointestinal complaints (59)", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "For example, runners can pre-\nmix a CHO beverage containing 90 g\nof CHO/L and attempt to consume 1\nL over the course of an hour. In events\nwith aid stations, the runner may\ninstead choose to take in large quanti-\nties of CHO in a single feeding. Long"}
{"chunk_id": "J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla_p0003_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_cdf0bb142189", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b72c9bb97054b912", "text_span_hash": "b72c9bb97054b912", "source_file": "J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "CHO in a single feeding. Longer\nﬁnish times, larger elevation changes,\nand runners with higher oxygen con-\nsumption rates or body mass require\ngreater CHO consumption rates. The\nmetering of CHO is partially depen-\ndent on the athlete’s goal ﬁnish ti", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "decision-making. Ideally, coaches and\nrunners should practice foreseeable in-\nrace situations and determine appro-\npriate decision-making options based\non available information congruent\nwith the predetermined pacing strat-\negy. Such actionable information might\ninclude distance covered or remaining,\nmajor terrain changes, feelings of effort\nperformance and pacing with growing\ninﬂuence as race duration/distance in-\n(12,65) result in an earlier and more\nrapid decline in run velocity or power\noutput, with the potential absence of\nan end spurt (41). In addition, greater\ncardiovascular strain occurs as dehy-\ndration progresses (cardiac drift) (54).\nCardiovascular strain seems to inﬂu-\nence effort perception and peak oxygen\nuptake, which is important as exercise\nintensity selection is partly dependent\non perceived effort (22,70,83).\nThe ability to sustain in-race glycogen\nstores and hydration status can vary\ndrastically based on intrinsic (i.e., ath-\nlete choice) and extrinsic factors (e.g.,\nevent provision and distance between\naid stations) factors. Minimizing dehy-\ndration and glycogen depletion is par-\nticularly vital in hot/humid weather\nthese conditions are most likely to\noccur (21,71). Practicing prerace and\nin-race nutritional strategies is recom-\nCHO loading, and high in-race CHO\nintake rates (30–90 g CHO/hr) are\ninversely related to ﬁnish times in lon-\nger events (59,68) despite potential gas-\ntrointestinal complaints (59)", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "CHO in a single feeding. Longer\nﬁnish times, larger elevation changes,\nand runners with higher oxygen con-\nsumption rates or body mass require\ngreater CHO consumption rates. The\nmetering of CHO is partially depen-\ndent on the athlete’s goal ﬁnish ti"}
