Minimizing dehy-\ndration and glycogen depletion is par-\nticularly vital in hot/humid weather\nthese conditions are most likely to\noccur (21,71). Practicing prerace and\nin-race nutritional strategies is recom-\nCHO loading, and high in-race CHO\nintake rates (30–90 g CHO/hr) are\ninversely related to ﬁnish times in lon-\nger events (59,68) despite potential gas-\ntrointestinal complaints (59)", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "to sweat rate (48). Caf-\nfeine consumption (3–6 mg/kg) reduces\nFigure 2. General types of pacing strategies. Energy distribution is commonly\ndescribed in practical terms such as run velocity (mph or kph) or pace\n(min/mile) as is it difﬁcult to quant"}
{"chunk_id": "J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla_p0003_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_cdf0bb142189", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b57ad39f5947db46", "text_span_hash": "b57ad39f5947db46", "source_file": "J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "le) as is it difﬁcult to quantify the total energy reserves of a runner\nand energy expenditure (e.g., oxygen consumption) outside of a labora-\nStrength and Conditioning Journal | www.nsca-scj.com\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditioning Associa", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "decision-making. Ideally, coaches and\nrunners should practice foreseeable in-\nrace situations and determine appro-\npriate decision-making options based\non available information congruent\nwith the predetermined pacing strat-\negy. Such actionable information might\ninclude distance covered or remaining,\nmajor terrain changes, feelings of effort\nperformance and pacing with growing\ninﬂuence as race duration/distance in-\n(12,65) result in an earlier and more\nrapid decline in run velocity or power\noutput, with the potential absence of\nan end spurt (41). In addition, greater\ncardiovascular strain occurs as dehy-\ndration progresses (cardiac drift) (54).\nCardiovascular strain seems to inﬂu-\nence effort perception and peak oxygen\nuptake, which is important as exercise\nintensity selection is partly dependent\non perceived effort (22,70,83).\nThe ability to sustain in-race glycogen\nstores and hydration status can vary\ndrastically based on intrinsic (i.e., ath-\nlete choice) and extrinsic factors (e.g.,\nevent provision and distance between\naid stations) factors. Minimizing dehy-\ndration and glycogen depletion is par-\nticularly vital in hot/humid weather\nthese conditions are most likely to\noccur (21,71). Practicing prerace and\nin-race nutritional strategies is recom-\nCHO loading, and high in-race CHO\nintake rates (30–90 g CHO/hr) are\ninversely related to ﬁnish times in lon-\nger events (59,68) despite potential gas-\ntrointestinal complaints (59)", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "le) as is it difﬁcult to quantify the total energy reserves of a runner\nand energy expenditure (e.g., oxygen consumption) outside of a labora-\nStrength and Conditioning Journal | www.nsca-scj.com\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditioning Associa"}
{"chunk_id": "J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla_p0004_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_cdf0bb142189", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4c802fd5bd82bb96", "text_span_hash": "4c802fd5bd82bb96", "source_file": "J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ermine energy\ncosts of running on uphill and downhill\nslopes (53). Body water losses during\nrunning can be estimated from energy\nexpenditure and pre–post change in\nbody mass with a simple equation:\n0.20/kcal (10). More accurate predic-\ntions of sweat", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "effort perception and enhances triglyc-\neride oxidation sparing glycogen which,\nin part, enhances aerobic performance\n(17,69) and potentially pacing ﬁdelity.\nequations are useful for determining\ngeneral nutritional and ﬂuid require-\nments to delay premature fatigue. For\nexample, level running energy expen-\nditure can be roughly estimated as 1\nkcal$kg body mass21$km21 regardless\nof speed (46). A more elaborate equa-\ntion is needed to determine energy\ncosts of running on uphill and downhill\nslopes (53). Body water losses during\nrunning can be estimated from energy\nexpenditure and pre–post change in\nbody mass with a simple equation:\n0.20/kcal (10). More accurate predic-\ntions of sweat loss require additional\ninformation (clothing, equipment, and\nenvironmental conditions) (60).\nENVIRONMENTAL CONDITIONS\nconditions increase the physiological\nand psychological strain incurred dur-\ning running, challenging pacing strat-\negy ﬁdelity. The consequence of added\nenvironment stress on pacing may be\nmanifest by earlier and greater decline\nin run velocity with or without marked\nvariation. This section overviews the\nimpact of heat, humidity, altitude,\nHeat and humidity. Hot and/or\nhumid conditions evoke earlier and\nlarger declines in exercise intensity, as\nwell as greater variation in pacing versus\nEarly pace modiﬁcations in the heat are\nlikely due to increased thermal sensations\nrelated to elevated skin temperatures and\nthen later cardiovascu", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ermine energy\ncosts of running on uphill and downhill\nslopes (53). Body water losses during\nrunning can be estimated from energy\nexpenditure and pre–post change in\nbody mass with a simple equation:\n0.20/kcal (10). More accurate predic-\ntions of sweat"}
