h or without marked\nvariation. This section overviews the\nimpact of heat, humidity, altitude,\nHeat and humidity. Hot and/or\nhumid conditions evoke earlier and\nlarger declines in exercise intensity, as\nwell as greater variation in pacing versus\nEarly pace modiﬁcations in the heat are\nlikely due to increased thermal sensations\nrelated to elevated skin temperatures and\nthen later cardiovascu", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "n pace uphill is greater than\nﬂat ground running. Therefore, runners\nmust reduce pace to maintain a physi-\nological steady state; alternatively, sus-\ntaining pace is at the cost of elevated\nphysiological and psychological distur-\nbances. Conversely,"}
