{"chunk_id": "J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla_p0004_c0026", "source_registry_id": "src_cdf0bb142189", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9bdc10a5bc94b7f0", "text_span_hash": "9bdc10a5bc94b7f0", "source_file": "J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 26, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "acing differs from above\nground running (26) likely because of\nthe conscious requirement to adjust\nPacing Strategy in Running\nVOLUME 42 | NUMBER 1 | FEBRUARY 2020\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditioning Association. Unauthorized reproduction o", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "effort perception and enhances triglyc-\neride oxidation sparing glycogen which,\nin part, enhances aerobic performance\n(17,69) and potentially pacing ﬁdelity.\nequations are useful for determining\ngeneral nutritional and ﬂuid require-\nments to delay premature fatigue. For\nexample, level running energy expen-\nditure can be roughly estimated as 1\nkcal$kg body mass21$km21 regardless\nof speed (46). A more elaborate equa-\ntion is needed to determine energy\ncosts of running on uphill and downhill\nslopes (53). Body water losses during\nrunning can be estimated from energy\nexpenditure and pre–post change in\nbody mass with a simple equation:\n0.20/kcal (10). More accurate predic-\ntions of sweat loss require additional\ninformation (clothing, equipment, and\nenvironmental conditions) (60).\nENVIRONMENTAL CONDITIONS\nconditions increase the physiological\nand psychological strain incurred dur-\ning running, challenging pacing strat-\negy ﬁdelity. The consequence of added\nenvironment stress on pacing may be\nmanifest by earlier and greater decline\nin run velocity with or without marked\nvariation. This section overviews the\nimpact of heat, humidity, altitude,\nHeat and humidity. Hot and/or\nhumid conditions evoke earlier and\nlarger declines in exercise intensity, as\nwell as greater variation in pacing versus\nEarly pace modiﬁcations in the heat are\nlikely due to increased thermal sensations\nrelated to elevated skin temperatures and\nthen later cardiovascu", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "acing differs from above\nground running (26) likely because of\nthe conscious requirement to adjust\nPacing Strategy in Running\nVOLUME 42 | NUMBER 1 | FEBRUARY 2020\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditioning Association. Unauthorized reproduction o"}
{"chunk_id": "J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla_p0005_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_cdf0bb142189", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ec0e563c9ac9cbd8", "text_span_hash": "ec0e563c9ac9cbd8", "source_file": "J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "erception (37,92).\nHowever, opponents can also inﬂuence\npacing decision-making (49,80). In sev-\neral middle- and long-distance events,\nthe best endurance athletes adopted\npositive pacing whereby the fast initial\npace cannot be sustained by most sub-", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "speed or elevation. At minimum, emu-\nlating major elevation changes and dis-\ntances of a race on alternate above-\nground routes is practical. Terrain-\ndependent pacing seems worthwhile\nin longer events (31,61), but its utility\nin shorter events is unknown.\nWhen exploring the pacing strategy\nand behavior, it is beneﬁcial to incorpo-\nrate and practice the inﬂuence of oppo-\npositioning and expectations (36). An\naudience or competitor(s) usually re-\nsults in better performance, a phenom-\nena known as social facilitation (94).\nRacing against opponents enhances\nphysiological and psychological aspects\nyielding faster run velocities without\ninﬂuencing effort perception (37,92).\nHowever, opponents can also inﬂuence\npacing decision-making (49,80). In sev-\neral middle- and long-distance events,\nthe best endurance athletes adopted\npositive pacing whereby the fast initial\npace cannot be sustained by most sub-\nelite athletes (25,39,67,77). Subelite run-\nners must decide to adopt the group\npace or not. The perception of ap-\nproaching or getting behind an oppo-\nnent is usually (49) but not always (61)\nactionable information and highly moti-\nvational (49,61). Often, runners con-\nsciously and/or subconsciously deviate\nfrom their pacing strategy to remain\nwithin the group (80), consequentially\nadopting an unsustainable pace. Such\na decision may not be perceived as haz-\nardous due to large untapped energy\nresources (52) and negligible changes\nin ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "erception (37,92).\nHowever, opponents can also inﬂuence\npacing decision-making (49,80). In sev-\neral middle- and long-distance events,\nthe best endurance athletes adopted\npositive pacing whereby the fast initial\npace cannot be sustained by most sub-"}
