{"chunk_id": "J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla_p0006_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_cdf0bb142189", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c00d3b1a0317e964", "text_span_hash": "c00d3b1a0317e964", "source_file": "J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "with the thought\nthat pacing is a skill to be practiced and\ncrafted over years of experience (19).\nThere is not a single “optimal” pacing\nstrategy for all runners and events.\nRather, consideration of factors known\nto inﬂuence pacing is required to\nd", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "linked with topographical databases to\ndetermine elevation changes and run-\nning surface gradients (35). Heart rate\nmonitoring is an inexpensive, time-\nefﬁcient, and noninvasive approach to\naddition, minute-by-minute heart rate\nmeasurements can be used to calculate\nreal-time estimations of core body tem-\nperature (42). Electronic foot contact\nmonitors provide further insight on\nforce generation per running stride\n(30). Consequently, runners have many\noptions for real-time tracking of exercise\nintensity and distance at their disposal.\nTRAINING-INDUCED FATIGUE\nTraining has been shown to result in\nadopting a more theoretically optimal\n“even”-pacing strategy (34), in line with\npacing proﬁles of elite long-distance\nrunners (67,84). However, 2 studies\nshow that 1–4 weeks of intensiﬁed\n(74,93). In both studies, after 2–4 days\nof recovery, pacing proﬁles returned to\npreintensiﬁed training patterning. A\nproper taper before major endurance\nevents can optimize training adapta-\ntions, recovery, and pacing strategy\nBecause male runners tend to reduce run\nvelocity (negative pacing) throughout\nmarathon (16) and 10-k races (15) more\nthan women, coaches and male runners\nshould make concerted efforts to address\nthis trend. Older and likely more experi-\nenced runners exhibit less slowing over\na race (15,16) consistent with the thought\nthat pacing is a skill to be practiced and\ncrafted over years of experience (19).\nThere is not a single “optimal", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "with the thought\nthat pacing is a skill to be practiced and\ncrafted over years of experience (19).\nThere is not a single “optimal” pacing\nstrategy for all runners and events.\nRather, consideration of factors known\nto inﬂuence pacing is required to\nd"}
{"chunk_id": "J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla_p0006_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_cdf0bb142189", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "00307a7254da104e", "text_span_hash": "00307a7254da104e", "source_file": "J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\J. Luke Pryor, PhD, ATC, CSCS,1 Evan C. Johnson, PhD,2 Hilla.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ﬂuence pacing is required to\ndevelop and use strategies to leverage\nthese factors in training and practicing\na planned pacing strategy is recom-\na planned pacing strategy in response\nto new information is important for\noverall adherence and successfu", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "linked with topographical databases to\ndetermine elevation changes and run-\nning surface gradients (35). Heart rate\nmonitoring is an inexpensive, time-\nefﬁcient, and noninvasive approach to\naddition, minute-by-minute heart rate\nmeasurements can be used to calculate\nreal-time estimations of core body tem-\nperature (42). Electronic foot contact\nmonitors provide further insight on\nforce generation per running stride\n(30). Consequently, runners have many\noptions for real-time tracking of exercise\nintensity and distance at their disposal.\nTRAINING-INDUCED FATIGUE\nTraining has been shown to result in\nadopting a more theoretically optimal\n“even”-pacing strategy (34), in line with\npacing proﬁles of elite long-distance\nrunners (67,84). However, 2 studies\nshow that 1–4 weeks of intensiﬁed\n(74,93). In both studies, after 2–4 days\nof recovery, pacing proﬁles returned to\npreintensiﬁed training patterning. A\nproper taper before major endurance\nevents can optimize training adapta-\ntions, recovery, and pacing strategy\nBecause male runners tend to reduce run\nvelocity (negative pacing) throughout\nmarathon (16) and 10-k races (15) more\nthan women, coaches and male runners\nshould make concerted efforts to address\nthis trend. Older and likely more experi-\nenced runners exhibit less slowing over\na race (15,16) consistent with the thought\nthat pacing is a skill to be practiced and\ncrafted over years of experience (19).\nThere is not a single “optimal", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ﬂuence pacing is required to\ndevelop and use strategies to leverage\nthese factors in training and practicing\na planned pacing strategy is recom-\na planned pacing strategy in response\nto new information is important for\noverall adherence and successfu"}
