{"chunk_id": "jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366_p0001_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_a01904e03507", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3f59d294a3ab85af", "text_span_hash": "3f59d294a3ab85af", "source_file": "jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "iodization methodology including the appropriate\nprocedure for designing sessions, microcycles and mesocycles is of paramount importance. Key words:\nTRAINING PLAN, PROGRAM DESIGN, MACROCYCLE, MESOCYCLE, MICROCYCLE, SESSION.\n1 Corresponding author. Pr", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "VOLUME 8 | ISSUE 2 | 2013 | 350\nApplied periodization: a methodological\nFERNANDO NACLERIO1 , JEREMY MOODY2, MARK CHAPMAN3\n1Principal Lecturer in Strength and Conditioning and Sports Nutrition. Programme Leader MSc in Strength and\nConditioning Centre of Sports Sciences and Human Performance, School of Sciences, University of Greenwich\nMedway Campus Central Avenue Chatham Maritime\n2 Programme Director MSc Strength and Conditioning; Cardiff School of Sport, Cardiff Metropolitan University, Cardiff\n3 Programme Leader of Sport Science with Professional Football Coaching and Sport Science with Coaching\nCentre of Sports Sciences and Human Performance, School of Sciences, University of Greenwich Medway Campus\nCentral Avenue Chatham Maritime\nNaclerio F, Moody J, Chapman M. Applied Periodization: A Methodological Approach. J. Hum. Sport Exerc.\nVol. 8, No. 2, pp. 350-366, 2013. Periodization represents an optimal modality for organizing training\nprograms in athletes, recreational and rehabilitative practitioners. The selected procedure, however, should\nbe based on the athlete’s age, level of performance, specific goals or competition characteristics. A\ncommon theme throughout all periodization paradigms is the requirement to manipulate the entire program\nvariables (intensity, volume, frequency, recovery periods and exercise selection) in order to progress from\ngeneral to a more sport-specific training, dissipate fatigue and reduce the ri", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "iodization methodology including the appropriate\nprocedure for designing sessions, microcycles and mesocycles is of paramount importance. Key words:\nTRAINING PLAN, PROGRAM DESIGN, MACROCYCLE, MESOCYCLE, MICROCYCLE, SESSION.\n1 Corresponding author. Pr"}
{"chunk_id": "jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366_p0002_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_a01904e03507", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "387da30c00fb5ad7", "text_span_hash": "387da30c00fb5ad7", "source_file": "jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Naclerio et al. / Applied periodization: a methodological approach JOURNAL OF HUMAN SPORT & EXERCISE\n| 2013 | ISSUE 2 | VOLUME 8 © 2013 University of Alicante\nThe progression of increased knowledge within the sports sciences has resulted in different", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Naclerio et al. / Applied periodization: a methodological approach JOURNAL OF HUMAN SPORT & EXERCISE\n| 2013 | ISSUE 2 | VOLUME 8 © 2013 University of Alicante\nThe progression of increased knowledge within the sports sciences has resulted in different criteria for\norganizing training programs which is often dominated by the trend to design workouts based on the\nspecific adaptation and outcomes achieved throughout the training process. Therefore athletes can pursue\nperformance progressions with more complex or effective training modalities only once they have achieved\nprogressive basic adaptations (Verkhoshansky, 1998).\nThe foundations of modern periodization were developed in the Soviet Union around the time of the\nRussian revolution, Kotov (1917) divided training into general, preparatory, and specific stages while Ozolin\n(1949) stressed the importance of considering the competitive calendar and climatic factors in periodization\n(Graham, 2002). Later, Matveyev summarized the modern concept of periodization dividing the training\nyear into distinct phases, each with different characteristics and special application to train endurance or\nstrength and power athletes (Siff, 2004). The fundamental concepts presented by Matveyev were applied to\nthe preparation of many sports such as track and field, swimming, rowing, cycling, skiing, weightlifting or\nskating (Issurin, 2010; Siff, 2004). However, when the competitive practices were ex", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Naclerio et al. / Applied periodization: a methodological approach JOURNAL OF HUMAN SPORT & EXERCISE\n| 2013 | ISSUE 2 | VOLUME 8 © 2013 University of Alicante\nThe progression of increased knowledge within the sports sciences has resulted in different"}
{"chunk_id": "jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366_p0002_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_a01904e03507", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3e4a7627216af38b", "text_span_hash": "3e4a7627216af38b", "source_file": "jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "nces has resulted in different criteria for\norganizing training programs which is often dominated by the trend to design workouts based on the\nspecific adaptation and outcomes achieved throughout the training process. Therefore athletes can pursue\npe", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Naclerio et al. / Applied periodization: a methodological approach JOURNAL OF HUMAN SPORT & EXERCISE\n| 2013 | ISSUE 2 | VOLUME 8 © 2013 University of Alicante\nThe progression of increased knowledge within the sports sciences has resulted in different criteria for\norganizing training programs which is often dominated by the trend to design workouts based on the\nspecific adaptation and outcomes achieved throughout the training process. Therefore athletes can pursue\nperformance progressions with more complex or effective training modalities only once they have achieved\nprogressive basic adaptations (Verkhoshansky, 1998).\nThe foundations of modern periodization were developed in the Soviet Union around the time of the\nRussian revolution, Kotov (1917) divided training into general, preparatory, and specific stages while Ozolin\n(1949) stressed the importance of considering the competitive calendar and climatic factors in periodization\n(Graham, 2002). Later, Matveyev summarized the modern concept of periodization dividing the training\nyear into distinct phases, each with different characteristics and special application to train endurance or\nstrength and power athletes (Siff, 2004). The fundamental concepts presented by Matveyev were applied to\nthe preparation of many sports such as track and field, swimming, rowing, cycling, skiing, weightlifting or\nskating (Issurin, 2010; Siff, 2004). However, when the competitive practices were ex", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "nces has resulted in different criteria for\norganizing training programs which is often dominated by the trend to design workouts based on the\nspecific adaptation and outcomes achieved throughout the training process. Therefore athletes can pursue\npe"}
{"chunk_id": "jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366_p0002_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_a01904e03507", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c978957bc7fed48a", "text_span_hash": "c978957bc7fed48a", "source_file": "jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "erefore athletes can pursue\nperformance progressions with more complex or effective training modalities only once they have achieved\nprogressive basic adaptations (Verkhoshansky, 1998).\nThe foundations of modern periodization were developed in the So", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Naclerio et al. / Applied periodization: a methodological approach JOURNAL OF HUMAN SPORT & EXERCISE\n| 2013 | ISSUE 2 | VOLUME 8 © 2013 University of Alicante\nThe progression of increased knowledge within the sports sciences has resulted in different criteria for\norganizing training programs which is often dominated by the trend to design workouts based on the\nspecific adaptation and outcomes achieved throughout the training process. Therefore athletes can pursue\nperformance progressions with more complex or effective training modalities only once they have achieved\nprogressive basic adaptations (Verkhoshansky, 1998).\nThe foundations of modern periodization were developed in the Soviet Union around the time of the\nRussian revolution, Kotov (1917) divided training into general, preparatory, and specific stages while Ozolin\n(1949) stressed the importance of considering the competitive calendar and climatic factors in periodization\n(Graham, 2002). Later, Matveyev summarized the modern concept of periodization dividing the training\nyear into distinct phases, each with different characteristics and special application to train endurance or\nstrength and power athletes (Siff, 2004). The fundamental concepts presented by Matveyev were applied to\nthe preparation of many sports such as track and field, swimming, rowing, cycling, skiing, weightlifting or\nskating (Issurin, 2010; Siff, 2004). However, when the competitive practices were ex", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "erefore athletes can pursue\nperformance progressions with more complex or effective training modalities only once they have achieved\nprogressive basic adaptations (Verkhoshansky, 1998).\nThe foundations of modern periodization were developed in the So"}
