004). The fundamental concepts presented by Matveyev were applied to\nthe preparation of many sports such as track and field, swimming, rowing, cycling, skiing, weightlifting or\nskating (Issurin, 2010; Siff, 2004). However, when the competitive practices were ex", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "IZATION AND BASIC PROCEDURES\nPeriodization has been defined as the methodical planning and structuring of training process that involve a\nlogical and systematic sequencing of multiple training variables (intensity, volume, frequency, recovery\nperiod"}
{"chunk_id": "jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366_p0002_c0016", "source_registry_id": "src_a01904e03507", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f517742cdd3743f9", "text_span_hash": "f517742cdd3743f9", "source_file": "jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 16, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "formance (Bondarchuk, 1988; Plisk & Stone, 2003; Tschiene, 2000; Verkhoshansky, 1998), the\nprimary characteristics of such approaches are the systematic variations in training content and/or the\nintensity and volume of the workload throughout the ove", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Naclerio et al. / Applied periodization: a methodological approach JOURNAL OF HUMAN SPORT & EXERCISE\n| 2013 | ISSUE 2 | VOLUME 8 © 2013 University of Alicante\nThe progression of increased knowledge within the sports sciences has resulted in different criteria for\norganizing training programs which is often dominated by the trend to design workouts based on the\nspecific adaptation and outcomes achieved throughout the training process. Therefore athletes can pursue\nperformance progressions with more complex or effective training modalities only once they have achieved\nprogressive basic adaptations (Verkhoshansky, 1998).\nThe foundations of modern periodization were developed in the Soviet Union around the time of the\nRussian revolution, Kotov (1917) divided training into general, preparatory, and specific stages while Ozolin\n(1949) stressed the importance of considering the competitive calendar and climatic factors in periodization\n(Graham, 2002). Later, Matveyev summarized the modern concept of periodization dividing the training\nyear into distinct phases, each with different characteristics and special application to train endurance or\nstrength and power athletes (Siff, 2004). The fundamental concepts presented by Matveyev were applied to\nthe preparation of many sports such as track and field, swimming, rowing, cycling, skiing, weightlifting or\nskating (Issurin, 2010; Siff, 2004). However, when the competitive practices were ex", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "formance (Bondarchuk, 1988; Plisk & Stone, 2003; Tschiene, 2000; Verkhoshansky, 1998), the\nprimary characteristics of such approaches are the systematic variations in training content and/or the\nintensity and volume of the workload throughout the ove"}
{"chunk_id": "jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366_p0002_c0017", "source_registry_id": "src_a01904e03507", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ea1084692ebb617f", "text_span_hash": "ea1084692ebb617f", "source_file": "jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 17, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "he workload throughout the overall program (Plisk & Stone, 2003; Wathen et al.,\n2008). However, periodization should not be considered a simply training variation strategy but an\nappropriate sequencing and integration of different training variables", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Naclerio et al. / Applied periodization: a methodological approach JOURNAL OF HUMAN SPORT & EXERCISE\n| 2013 | ISSUE 2 | VOLUME 8 © 2013 University of Alicante\nThe progression of increased knowledge within the sports sciences has resulted in different criteria for\norganizing training programs which is often dominated by the trend to design workouts based on the\nspecific adaptation and outcomes achieved throughout the training process. Therefore athletes can pursue\nperformance progressions with more complex or effective training modalities only once they have achieved\nprogressive basic adaptations (Verkhoshansky, 1998).\nThe foundations of modern periodization were developed in the Soviet Union around the time of the\nRussian revolution, Kotov (1917) divided training into general, preparatory, and specific stages while Ozolin\n(1949) stressed the importance of considering the competitive calendar and climatic factors in periodization\n(Graham, 2002). Later, Matveyev summarized the modern concept of periodization dividing the training\nyear into distinct phases, each with different characteristics and special application to train endurance or\nstrength and power athletes (Siff, 2004). The fundamental concepts presented by Matveyev were applied to\nthe preparation of many sports such as track and field, swimming, rowing, cycling, skiing, weightlifting or\nskating (Issurin, 2010; Siff, 2004). However, when the competitive practices were ex", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "he workload throughout the overall program (Plisk & Stone, 2003; Wathen et al.,\n2008). However, periodization should not be considered a simply training variation strategy but an\nappropriate sequencing and integration of different training variables"}
{"chunk_id": "jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366_p0002_c0018", "source_registry_id": "src_a01904e03507", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "49a1ad141c065167", "text_span_hash": "49a1ad141c065167", "source_file": "jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 18, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "different training variables involving not only volume and intensity\nbut also frequency, recovery periods or density and exercises selection. Thus the main objective of\nperiodization will be to achieve the desired outcomes and training gains at the", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Naclerio et al. / Applied periodization: a methodological approach JOURNAL OF HUMAN SPORT & EXERCISE\n| 2013 | ISSUE 2 | VOLUME 8 © 2013 University of Alicante\nThe progression of increased knowledge within the sports sciences has resulted in different criteria for\norganizing training programs which is often dominated by the trend to design workouts based on the\nspecific adaptation and outcomes achieved throughout the training process. Therefore athletes can pursue\nperformance progressions with more complex or effective training modalities only once they have achieved\nprogressive basic adaptations (Verkhoshansky, 1998).\nThe foundations of modern periodization were developed in the Soviet Union around the time of the\nRussian revolution, Kotov (1917) divided training into general, preparatory, and specific stages while Ozolin\n(1949) stressed the importance of considering the competitive calendar and climatic factors in periodization\n(Graham, 2002). Later, Matveyev summarized the modern concept of periodization dividing the training\nyear into distinct phases, each with different characteristics and special application to train endurance or\nstrength and power athletes (Siff, 2004). The fundamental concepts presented by Matveyev were applied to\nthe preparation of many sports such as track and field, swimming, rowing, cycling, skiing, weightlifting or\nskating (Issurin, 2010; Siff, 2004). However, when the competitive practices were ex", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "different training variables involving not only volume and intensity\nbut also frequency, recovery periods or density and exercises selection. Thus the main objective of\nperiodization will be to achieve the desired outcomes and training gains at the"}
{"chunk_id": "jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366_p0002_c0019", "source_registry_id": "src_a01904e03507", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "d279a29d7433e7eb", "text_span_hash": "d279a29d7433e7eb", "source_file": "jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 19, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "mes and training gains at the predetermined time point\nwithin the training process (Haff, 2013).", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Naclerio et al. / Applied periodization: a methodological approach JOURNAL OF HUMAN SPORT & EXERCISE\n| 2013 | ISSUE 2 | VOLUME 8 © 2013 University of Alicante\nThe progression of increased knowledge within the sports sciences has resulted in different criteria for\norganizing training programs which is often dominated by the trend to design workouts based on the\nspecific adaptation and outcomes achieved throughout the training process. Therefore athletes can pursue\nperformance progressions with more complex or effective training modalities only once they have achieved\nprogressive basic adaptations (Verkhoshansky, 1998).\nThe foundations of modern periodization were developed in the Soviet Union around the time of the\nRussian revolution, Kotov (1917) divided training into general, preparatory, and specific stages while Ozolin\n(1949) stressed the importance of considering the competitive calendar and climatic factors in periodization\n(Graham, 2002). Later, Matveyev summarized the modern concept of periodization dividing the training\nyear into distinct phases, each with different characteristics and special application to train endurance or\nstrength and power athletes (Siff, 2004). The fundamental concepts presented by Matveyev were applied to\nthe preparation of many sports such as track and field, swimming, rowing, cycling, skiing, weightlifting or\nskating (Issurin, 2010; Siff, 2004). However, when the competitive practices were ex", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "mes and training gains at the predetermined time point\nwithin the training process (Haff, 2013)."}
{"chunk_id": "jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366_p0003_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_a01904e03507", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "972e60cb4a4ac4a3", "text_span_hash": "972e60cb4a4ac4a3", "source_file": "jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Naclerio et al. / Applied periodization: a methodological approach JOURNAL OF HUMAN SPORT & EXERCISE\nVOLUME 8 | ISSUE 2 | 2013 | 352\nAppropriate manipulation of all training variables should be considered at all levels within the training\nprocess (wo", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Naclerio et al. / Applied periodization: a methodological approach JOURNAL OF HUMAN SPORT & EXERCISE\nVOLUME 8 | ISSUE 2 | 2013 | 352\nAppropriate manipulation of all training variables should be considered at all levels within the training\nprocess (workout, day by day, microcycle, mesocycle and macrocycle, year and multi-year training plan).\nVariation should never be excessive or randomly applied rather it has to be introduced considering the\ninterrelation and sequencing of each training variable (Haff, 2013).\nThe periodization approach is based on breaking the training plan into specific interrelated periods of time\nwhich are structured to meet specific goals (Haff, 2013). This procedure provides the opportunity for a\nsystematic, organized method to all training in terms of several basic structural units, namely the training\nsessions which are the fundamental unit, the microcycles, mesocycles and macrocycles (figure 1). In\naddition to this, there are more extended cycles such as Olympic or quadrennial cycles that consider long\ntime preparation for athletes (Siff, 2004).\nFigure 1. Basic component of training plan. T: transition period.\nANNUAL PLAN, MACROCYCLES, AND PHASES\nDepending on the sports and athletes characteristics, there are several variants for developing annual\ntraining plans. In general, the number of competitive period serves as a foundation to determine the\nnumber of macrocycles within an annual training plan.\nTh", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Naclerio et al. / Applied periodization: a methodological approach JOURNAL OF HUMAN SPORT & EXERCISE\nVOLUME 8 | ISSUE 2 | 2013 | 352\nAppropriate manipulation of all training variables should be considered at all levels within the training\nprocess (wo"}
{"chunk_id": "jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366_p0003_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_a01904e03507", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "77baa0e02ac70180", "text_span_hash": "77baa0e02ac70180", "source_file": "jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ithin the training\nprocess (workout, day by day, microcycle, mesocycle and macrocycle, year and multi-year training plan).\nVariation should never be excessive or randomly applied rather it has to be introduced considering the\ninterrelation and sequen", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Naclerio et al. / Applied periodization: a methodological approach JOURNAL OF HUMAN SPORT & EXERCISE\nVOLUME 8 | ISSUE 2 | 2013 | 352\nAppropriate manipulation of all training variables should be considered at all levels within the training\nprocess (workout, day by day, microcycle, mesocycle and macrocycle, year and multi-year training plan).\nVariation should never be excessive or randomly applied rather it has to be introduced considering the\ninterrelation and sequencing of each training variable (Haff, 2013).\nThe periodization approach is based on breaking the training plan into specific interrelated periods of time\nwhich are structured to meet specific goals (Haff, 2013). This procedure provides the opportunity for a\nsystematic, organized method to all training in terms of several basic structural units, namely the training\nsessions which are the fundamental unit, the microcycles, mesocycles and macrocycles (figure 1). In\naddition to this, there are more extended cycles such as Olympic or quadrennial cycles that consider long\ntime preparation for athletes (Siff, 2004).\nFigure 1. Basic component of training plan. T: transition period.\nANNUAL PLAN, MACROCYCLES, AND PHASES\nDepending on the sports and athletes characteristics, there are several variants for developing annual\ntraining plans. In general, the number of competitive period serves as a foundation to determine the\nnumber of macrocycles within an annual training plan.\nTh", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ithin the training\nprocess (workout, day by day, microcycle, mesocycle and macrocycle, year and multi-year training plan).\nVariation should never be excessive or randomly applied rather it has to be introduced considering the\ninterrelation and sequen"}
