\nDepending on the sports and athletes characteristics, there are several variants for developing annual\ntraining plans. In general, the number of competitive period serves as a foundation to determine the\nnumber of macrocycles within an annual training plan.\nTh", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "the number of competitive period serves as a foundation to determine the\nnumber of macrocycles within an annual training plan.\nThe Macrocycle is generally referred to a single competitive season. One macrocycle involves a number of\nmesocycles that i"}
{"chunk_id": "jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366_p0004_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_a01904e03507", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "86882b856c1fb4b4", "text_span_hash": "86882b856c1fb4b4", "source_file": "jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Naclerio et al. / Applied periodization: a methodological approach JOURNAL OF HUMAN SPORT & EXERCISE\n| 2013 | ISSUE 2 | VOLUME 8 © 2013 University of Alicante\nmacrocycles can run over a 4 years cycle (Turner, 2011). Macrocycles are structured to cont", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Naclerio et al. / Applied periodization: a methodological approach JOURNAL OF HUMAN SPORT & EXERCISE\n| 2013 | ISSUE 2 | VOLUME 8 © 2013 University of Alicante\nmacrocycles can run over a 4 years cycle (Turner, 2011). Macrocycles are structured to contain the entire\nseason of training. Thus, is not an annual training plan, for example, some sports such as swimming or\nboxing can contain multiple seasons or macrocycles over the annual calendar (Haff, 2013).\nPreparatory phase: Depending upon the length of the macrocycle, type of sport and athletes level of\nperformance, this phase can last for more than 2 until 6 month. Even if this phase is usually broken down\ninto a general and specific preparation, both general and specific sub phases should be always considered\ninterconnected unit (Siff, 2004). The General preparatory phase is aimed to provide fundamentals physical\nand technical conditioning (basic strength, endurance, flexibility and basic motor skills) in order to support\nthe further development of the specific capacities and motor sport skills (Siff, 2004; Verchoshansky, 1996).\nIn general more advanced athletes will depend less on this phase compared to less prepared or novice\nathletes (Haff, 2013).\nConversely the specific preparatory phase is aimed to translate the previously established fitness gains into\nvery specific performance characteristics. This subphase is focused to develop specific sports capacities\nwhile maintain", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Naclerio et al. / Applied periodization: a methodological approach JOURNAL OF HUMAN SPORT & EXERCISE\n| 2013 | ISSUE 2 | VOLUME 8 © 2013 University of Alicante\nmacrocycles can run over a 4 years cycle (Turner, 2011). Macrocycles are structured to cont"}
{"chunk_id": "jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366_p0004_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_a01904e03507", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "fa8e5d927d63df65", "text_span_hash": "fa8e5d927d63df65", "source_file": "jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "technical conditioning (basic strength, endurance, flexibility and basic motor skills) in order to support\nthe further development of the specific capacities and motor sport skills (Siff, 2004; Verchoshansky, 1996).\nIn general more advanced athletes", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Naclerio et al. / Applied periodization: a methodological approach JOURNAL OF HUMAN SPORT & EXERCISE\n| 2013 | ISSUE 2 | VOLUME 8 © 2013 University of Alicante\nmacrocycles can run over a 4 years cycle (Turner, 2011). Macrocycles are structured to contain the entire\nseason of training. Thus, is not an annual training plan, for example, some sports such as swimming or\nboxing can contain multiple seasons or macrocycles over the annual calendar (Haff, 2013).\nPreparatory phase: Depending upon the length of the macrocycle, type of sport and athletes level of\nperformance, this phase can last for more than 2 until 6 month. Even if this phase is usually broken down\ninto a general and specific preparation, both general and specific sub phases should be always considered\ninterconnected unit (Siff, 2004). The General preparatory phase is aimed to provide fundamentals physical\nand technical conditioning (basic strength, endurance, flexibility and basic motor skills) in order to support\nthe further development of the specific capacities and motor sport skills (Siff, 2004; Verchoshansky, 1996).\nIn general more advanced athletes will depend less on this phase compared to less prepared or novice\nathletes (Haff, 2013).\nConversely the specific preparatory phase is aimed to translate the previously established fitness gains into\nvery specific performance characteristics. This subphase is focused to develop specific sports capacities\nwhile maintain", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "technical conditioning (basic strength, endurance, flexibility and basic motor skills) in order to support\nthe further development of the specific capacities and motor sport skills (Siff, 2004; Verchoshansky, 1996).\nIn general more advanced athletes"}
{"chunk_id": "jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366_p0004_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_a01904e03507", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8509cd515a9fc68f", "text_span_hash": "8509cd515a9fc68f", "source_file": "jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Transition Phase: Crucial linking structure used to bridge macrocycles or annual training plans in which\nathletes have the opportunity to recover from the previous training cycle (Haff, 2013). Athletes should not\ncompletely stop training but reduce", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Naclerio et al. / Applied periodization: a methodological approach JOURNAL OF HUMAN SPORT & EXERCISE\n| 2013 | ISSUE 2 | VOLUME 8 © 2013 University of Alicante\nmacrocycles can run over a 4 years cycle (Turner, 2011). Macrocycles are structured to contain the entire\nseason of training. Thus, is not an annual training plan, for example, some sports such as swimming or\nboxing can contain multiple seasons or macrocycles over the annual calendar (Haff, 2013).\nPreparatory phase: Depending upon the length of the macrocycle, type of sport and athletes level of\nperformance, this phase can last for more than 2 until 6 month. Even if this phase is usually broken down\ninto a general and specific preparation, both general and specific sub phases should be always considered\ninterconnected unit (Siff, 2004). The General preparatory phase is aimed to provide fundamentals physical\nand technical conditioning (basic strength, endurance, flexibility and basic motor skills) in order to support\nthe further development of the specific capacities and motor sport skills (Siff, 2004; Verchoshansky, 1996).\nIn general more advanced athletes will depend less on this phase compared to less prepared or novice\nathletes (Haff, 2013).\nConversely the specific preparatory phase is aimed to translate the previously established fitness gains into\nvery specific performance characteristics. This subphase is focused to develop specific sports capacities\nwhile maintain", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Transition Phase: Crucial linking structure used to bridge macrocycles or annual training plans in which\nathletes have the opportunity to recover from the previous training cycle (Haff, 2013). Athletes should not\ncompletely stop training but reduce"}
