{"chunk_id": "jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366_p0008_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_a01904e03507", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2fb2426406f6be84", "text_span_hash": "2fb2426406f6be84", "source_file": "jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Naclerio et al. / Applied periodization: a methodological approach JOURNAL OF HUMAN SPORT & EXERCISE\n| 2013 | ISSUE 2 | VOLUME 8 © 2013 University of Alicante\ndesign a session can include several small numbers of training units (Platonov, 2001). Trai", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Naclerio et al. / Applied periodization: a methodological approach JOURNAL OF HUMAN SPORT & EXERCISE\n| 2013 | ISSUE 2 | VOLUME 8 © 2013 University of Alicante\ndesign a session can include several small numbers of training units (Platonov, 2001). Training sessions\nand their composition are the primary requirement for the configuration of microcycles, providing them with\ntheir final appearance and detailed characteristics (Siff, 2004). However, it is important to highlight that the\ndefinitive design of both sessions and microcycle have to be based on the mesocycle main objectives.\nDepending on the dominant factor and the dynamic correspondence, each session can be allocated into\nthe following three main groups:\n• Physical conditioning: These sessions are specifically oriented to develop physical capacities such\nas strength, explosiveness, speed, flexibility, and aerobic or anaerobic endurance. In addition\nthese can be aimed to assess or control the athletes’ level of performance.\n• Technical: These sessions are focused on learning and technique and therefore should not be\nperformed in fatigue state. Fatigue not only has the potential to hinder the learning process but can\nalso induce negative outcomes related to the assimilation of incorrect motor patterns (Bompa &\n• Tactical: Involves specific sports actions with correct technique, motor control and knowledge of\nthe dynamic roles and basic structure of the sports. These session", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Naclerio et al. / Applied periodization: a methodological approach JOURNAL OF HUMAN SPORT & EXERCISE\n| 2013 | ISSUE 2 | VOLUME 8 © 2013 University of Alicante\ndesign a session can include several small numbers of training units (Platonov, 2001). Trai"}
{"chunk_id": "jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366_p0008_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_a01904e03507", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c2db717635ce1cd6", "text_span_hash": "c2db717635ce1cd6", "source_file": "jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "g units (Platonov, 2001). Training sessions\nand their composition are the primary requirement for the configuration of microcycles, providing them with\ntheir final appearance and detailed characteristics (Siff, 2004). However, it is important to high", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Naclerio et al. / Applied periodization: a methodological approach JOURNAL OF HUMAN SPORT & EXERCISE\n| 2013 | ISSUE 2 | VOLUME 8 © 2013 University of Alicante\ndesign a session can include several small numbers of training units (Platonov, 2001). Training sessions\nand their composition are the primary requirement for the configuration of microcycles, providing them with\ntheir final appearance and detailed characteristics (Siff, 2004). However, it is important to highlight that the\ndefinitive design of both sessions and microcycle have to be based on the mesocycle main objectives.\nDepending on the dominant factor and the dynamic correspondence, each session can be allocated into\nthe following three main groups:\n• Physical conditioning: These sessions are specifically oriented to develop physical capacities such\nas strength, explosiveness, speed, flexibility, and aerobic or anaerobic endurance. In addition\nthese can be aimed to assess or control the athletes’ level of performance.\n• Technical: These sessions are focused on learning and technique and therefore should not be\nperformed in fatigue state. Fatigue not only has the potential to hinder the learning process but can\nalso induce negative outcomes related to the assimilation of incorrect motor patterns (Bompa &\n• Tactical: Involves specific sports actions with correct technique, motor control and knowledge of\nthe dynamic roles and basic structure of the sports. These session", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "g units (Platonov, 2001). Training sessions\nand their composition are the primary requirement for the configuration of microcycles, providing them with\ntheir final appearance and detailed characteristics (Siff, 2004). However, it is important to high"}
