ue with more endurance oriented exercises is\nnearly immediate, as opposed to maximal intensit", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "s can easily determine high or maximum volume of training, which may, as a result,"}
{"chunk_id": "jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366_p0011_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_a01904e03507", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c8794f2cf776e120", "text_span_hash": "c8794f2cf776e120", "source_file": "jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Naclerio et al. / Applied periodization: a methodological approach JOURNAL OF HUMAN SPORT & EXERCISE\nVOLUME 8 | ISSUE 2 | 2013 | 360\nrequire longer recovery periods. Such an example can occur in team sports with one or two competition per\nweek. Compl", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Naclerio et al. / Applied periodization: a methodological approach JOURNAL OF HUMAN SPORT & EXERCISE\nVOLUME 8 | ISSUE 2 | 2013 | 360\nrequire longer recovery periods. Such an example can occur in team sports with one or two competition per\nweek. Complex sessions involving more than three or four capacities should be trained toward the middle\nof microcycle when there is enough time for appropriate recovery before the match.\nTypes of Microcycles\nAs the specific microcycle design will determine their global effects and outcomes achieved at the end of\nthe planned mesocycles, their structure should be considered in the context of mesocycles and based on\nthe sports demand and individual athletes’ requirements (Platonov, 2001).\nIn general it is possible to distinguish between five types of microcycles: 1) introductory or applied; 2)\nstandard or ordinary; 3) shock; 4) restorative and 5) competitive or activation (which include the session\nallocated before competition or test) (Siff, 2004). In order to facilitate the achievement of some specific\ngoals (learning technique, maintenance or increases in performance) these microcycles should be\nappropriately integrated within the mesocycles.\nIntroductory or applied:\nThese microcycles do not include maximum volume session and are not recommended to involve high\nvolume ones. Introductory microcycles could be applied to teach new technical skills, correct mistakes or\nadjust training loads. In a", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Naclerio et al. / Applied periodization: a methodological approach JOURNAL OF HUMAN SPORT & EXERCISE\nVOLUME 8 | ISSUE 2 | 2013 | 360\nrequire longer recovery periods. Such an example can occur in team sports with one or two competition per\nweek. Compl"}
{"chunk_id": "jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366_p0011_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_a01904e03507", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "69f4710b21a04bc0", "text_span_hash": "69f4710b21a04bc0", "source_file": "jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ume ones. Introductory microcycles could be applied to teach new technical skills, correct mistakes or\nadjust training loads. In addition they are used as a form of ‘training barometer’ of the programmed training\ntasks in order to assess if the presc", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Naclerio et al. / Applied periodization: a methodological approach JOURNAL OF HUMAN SPORT & EXERCISE\nVOLUME 8 | ISSUE 2 | 2013 | 360\nrequire longer recovery periods. Such an example can occur in team sports with one or two competition per\nweek. Complex sessions involving more than three or four capacities should be trained toward the middle\nof microcycle when there is enough time for appropriate recovery before the match.\nTypes of Microcycles\nAs the specific microcycle design will determine their global effects and outcomes achieved at the end of\nthe planned mesocycles, their structure should be considered in the context of mesocycles and based on\nthe sports demand and individual athletes’ requirements (Platonov, 2001).\nIn general it is possible to distinguish between five types of microcycles: 1) introductory or applied; 2)\nstandard or ordinary; 3) shock; 4) restorative and 5) competitive or activation (which include the session\nallocated before competition or test) (Siff, 2004). In order to facilitate the achievement of some specific\ngoals (learning technique, maintenance or increases in performance) these microcycles should be\nappropriately integrated within the mesocycles.\nIntroductory or applied:\nThese microcycles do not include maximum volume session and are not recommended to involve high\nvolume ones. Introductory microcycles could be applied to teach new technical skills, correct mistakes or\nadjust training loads. In a", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ume ones. Introductory microcycles could be applied to teach new technical skills, correct mistakes or\nadjust training loads. In addition they are used as a form of ‘training barometer’ of the programmed training\ntasks in order to assess if the presc"}
{"chunk_id": "jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366_p0011_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_a01904e03507", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "18390c700b2e350e", "text_span_hash": "18390c700b2e350e", "source_file": "jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "n order to assess if the prescribed training activities are appropriate for the athlete’s age or biological\nmaturation as well as the level of performance (Smith, 2003). In some sports, appropriately scheduled and\nstructured low to moderate volume se", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Naclerio et al. / Applied periodization: a methodological approach JOURNAL OF HUMAN SPORT & EXERCISE\nVOLUME 8 | ISSUE 2 | 2013 | 360\nrequire longer recovery periods. Such an example can occur in team sports with one or two competition per\nweek. Complex sessions involving more than three or four capacities should be trained toward the middle\nof microcycle when there is enough time for appropriate recovery before the match.\nTypes of Microcycles\nAs the specific microcycle design will determine their global effects and outcomes achieved at the end of\nthe planned mesocycles, their structure should be considered in the context of mesocycles and based on\nthe sports demand and individual athletes’ requirements (Platonov, 2001).\nIn general it is possible to distinguish between five types of microcycles: 1) introductory or applied; 2)\nstandard or ordinary; 3) shock; 4) restorative and 5) competitive or activation (which include the session\nallocated before competition or test) (Siff, 2004). In order to facilitate the achievement of some specific\ngoals (learning technique, maintenance or increases in performance) these microcycles should be\nappropriately integrated within the mesocycles.\nIntroductory or applied:\nThese microcycles do not include maximum volume session and are not recommended to involve high\nvolume ones. Introductory microcycles could be applied to teach new technical skills, correct mistakes or\nadjust training loads. In a", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "n order to assess if the prescribed training activities are appropriate for the athlete’s age or biological\nmaturation as well as the level of performance (Smith, 2003). In some sports, appropriately scheduled and\nstructured low to moderate volume se"}
{"chunk_id": "jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366_p0011_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_a01904e03507", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "6748756d2410aaf9", "text_span_hash": "6748756d2410aaf9", "source_file": "jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "son when there is a need to stimulate profound\nadaptation in specific phases of the training cycle (Verchoshansky, 1996). Shock microcycles include more\nthan one maximum (two to three) load volume sessions. However, when more than one session a day i", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Naclerio et al. / Applied periodization: a methodological approach JOURNAL OF HUMAN SPORT & EXERCISE\nVOLUME 8 | ISSUE 2 | 2013 | 360\nrequire longer recovery periods. Such an example can occur in team sports with one or two competition per\nweek. Complex sessions involving more than three or four capacities should be trained toward the middle\nof microcycle when there is enough time for appropriate recovery before the match.\nTypes of Microcycles\nAs the specific microcycle design will determine their global effects and outcomes achieved at the end of\nthe planned mesocycles, their structure should be considered in the context of mesocycles and based on\nthe sports demand and individual athletes’ requirements (Platonov, 2001).\nIn general it is possible to distinguish between five types of microcycles: 1) introductory or applied; 2)\nstandard or ordinary; 3) shock; 4) restorative and 5) competitive or activation (which include the session\nallocated before competition or test) (Siff, 2004). In order to facilitate the achievement of some specific\ngoals (learning technique, maintenance or increases in performance) these microcycles should be\nappropriately integrated within the mesocycles.\nIntroductory or applied:\nThese microcycles do not include maximum volume session and are not recommended to involve high\nvolume ones. Introductory microcycles could be applied to teach new technical skills, correct mistakes or\nadjust training loads. In a", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "son when there is a need to stimulate profound\nadaptation in specific phases of the training cycle (Verchoshansky, 1996). Shock microcycles include more\nthan one maximum (two to three) load volume sessions. However, when more than one session a day i"}
{"chunk_id": "jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366_p0012_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_a01904e03507", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "d6681b5d4ae37e29", "text_span_hash": "d6681b5d4ae37e29", "source_file": "jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\jhse_Vol_8_N_II_350-366.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Naclerio et al. / Applied periodization: a methodological approach JOURNAL OF HUMAN SPORT & EXERCISE\n| 2013 | ISSUE 2 | VOLUME 8 © 2013 University of Alicante\nAs general rule shock microcycles should be followed by regenerative microcycles. It is not", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Naclerio et al. / Applied periodization: a methodological approach JOURNAL OF HUMAN SPORT & EXERCISE\n| 2013 | ISSUE 2 | VOLUME 8 © 2013 University of Alicante\nAs general rule shock microcycles should be followed by regenerative microcycles. It is not recommended\nto assign shock microcycles during a period with frequent competitions, which is the case of many team\nsports that have at least one competition per week. The high level of fatigue determined by such a hard\nmicrocycle, in addition to impaired performance can contribute to an increased risk of injury or unplanned\noverreaching. Technical and strength and conditioning coaches must work closely together to carefully\ncontrol and monitor athletes’ performances during these extremely challenging microcycles (Smith, 2003).\nAs a final recommendation, due its high levels of physical demand it is recommended not to use shock\nmicrocycles when training biologically young athletes (Martin et al., 2001).\nRegenerative or restorative:\nThese microcycles are aimed to assist the body in recovery from a previous period of programmed shock\ntraining or several standard microcycles. The principal goal of these microcycles is to lead athletes to the\nlevel of performance required to continue with the next training phase. Restorative microcycles will always\nstart with a regenerative session (low volume and low intensity exercises such as light aerobic swimming or\nlight dynamic flexibility exerci", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Naclerio et al. / Applied periodization: a methodological approach JOURNAL OF HUMAN SPORT & EXERCISE\n| 2013 | ISSUE 2 | VOLUME 8 © 2013 University of Alicante\nAs general rule shock microcycles should be followed by regenerative microcycles. It is not"}
