to arginine [542]. Due to this and its\nnon-competitive uptake for cell transport [542], oral\ncitrulline supplementation has been shown to be more\neffective in increasing arginine [543, 544] and activation of\nnitric oxide synthase (NOS) [544] as well as various\nbiomarkers of nitric oxide [545]. Multiple s", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "tiple-bout resistance exercise performance.\nAlternatively, Cultrufello and colleagues [552] reported\nthat a 6 g dose of L-citrulline failed to impact both aerobic\nand anaerobic indicators of exercise performance. The role\nof malate in combination wit"}
{"chunk_id": "Kreider_2018_ISSN_Sports_Nutrition_p0034_c0017", "source_registry_id": "src_1668a67fe73d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4a3ed82febff317c", "text_span_hash": "4a3ed82febff317c", "source_file": "Kreider_2018_ISSN_Sports_Nutrition.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Kreider_2018_ISSN_Sports_Nutrition.pdf", "page": 34, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 17, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": ", it is pres-\nunclear whether these benefits can\nattributed to citrulline, as well as what role citrulline may\nplay in aerobic and anaerobic performance.\nEssential amino acids (EAA) Research exploring the\nimpact of essential amino acids with various", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "data exist to support an ergogenic outcome, mixed out-\ncomes currently prevail as other studies have failed to\nreport an ergogenic impact of BCAAs [247, 537]. Conse-\nquently, more research is needed to fully determine the\nergogenic impact, if any, of BCAAs. An important point\nto highlight surrounding BCAAs is the growing body of\nliterature supporting their ability to mitigate outcomes\nsurrounding muscle damage. In this respect, multiple\nstudies have investigated and offered support for BCAA’s\nability to promote recovery, mitigate soreness and at-\ntenuate losses in force production [249, 250, 537, 538].\nCitrulline Citrulline (2-Amino-5-(carbamoylamino)pen-\ntanoic acid or L-Carnitine) is endogenously produced\nfrom ornithine and carbamoyl phosphate in the urea cycle.\nIn the body, citrulline is efficiently recycled into arginine\nfor subsequent nitric oxide production through the\ncitrulline-nitric oxide cycle [539]. Unlike arginine, citrul-\nline catabolism is limited in the intestines [540] as well as\nits extraction from hepatic tissue [541] resulting in the\nmajority of citrulline passing into systemic circulation\nbefore conversion to arginine [542]. Due to this and its\nnon-competitive uptake for cell transport [542], oral\ncitrulline supplementation has been shown to be more\neffective in increasing arginine [543, 544] and activation of\nnitric oxide synthase (NOS) [544] as well as various\nbiomarkers of nitric oxide [545]. Multiple s", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": ", it is pres-\nunclear whether these benefits can\nattributed to citrulline, as well as what role citrulline may\nplay in aerobic and anaerobic performance.\nEssential amino acids (EAA) Research exploring the\nimpact of essential amino acids with various"}
{"chunk_id": "Kreider_2018_ISSN_Sports_Nutrition_p0034_c0025", "source_registry_id": "src_1668a67fe73d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "55a1785a10793bbf", "text_span_hash": "55a1785a10793bbf", "source_file": "Kreider_2018_ISSN_Sports_Nutrition.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Kreider_2018_ISSN_Sports_Nutrition.pdf", "page": 34, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 25, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ts are mixed on\nwhether it can improve exercise capacity [166, 559–564].\nRegarding endurance performance Coutts and investiga-\ntors [565] had ten trained endurance athletes complete an\nOlympic distance triathlon under both placebo and gly-\ncerol hype", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "data exist to support an ergogenic outcome, mixed out-\ncomes currently prevail as other studies have failed to\nreport an ergogenic impact of BCAAs [247, 537]. Conse-\nquently, more research is needed to fully determine the\nergogenic impact, if any, of BCAAs. An important point\nto highlight surrounding BCAAs is the growing body of\nliterature supporting their ability to mitigate outcomes\nsurrounding muscle damage. In this respect, multiple\nstudies have investigated and offered support for BCAA’s\nability to promote recovery, mitigate soreness and at-\ntenuate losses in force production [249, 250, 537, 538].\nCitrulline Citrulline (2-Amino-5-(carbamoylamino)pen-\ntanoic acid or L-Carnitine) is endogenously produced\nfrom ornithine and carbamoyl phosphate in the urea cycle.\nIn the body, citrulline is efficiently recycled into arginine\nfor subsequent nitric oxide production through the\ncitrulline-nitric oxide cycle [539]. Unlike arginine, citrul-\nline catabolism is limited in the intestines [540] as well as\nits extraction from hepatic tissue [541] resulting in the\nmajority of citrulline passing into systemic circulation\nbefore conversion to arginine [542]. Due to this and its\nnon-competitive uptake for cell transport [542], oral\ncitrulline supplementation has been shown to be more\neffective in increasing arginine [543, 544] and activation of\nnitric oxide synthase (NOS) [544] as well as various\nbiomarkers of nitric oxide [545]. Multiple s", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ts are mixed on\nwhether it can improve exercise capacity [166, 559–564].\nRegarding endurance performance Coutts and investiga-\ntors [565] had ten trained endurance athletes complete an\nOlympic distance triathlon under both placebo and gly-\ncerol hype"}
{"chunk_id": "Kreider_2018_ISSN_Sports_Nutrition_p0035_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_1668a67fe73d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "420bb0218ba91b2c", "text_span_hash": "420bb0218ba91b2c", "source_file": "Kreider_2018_ISSN_Sports_Nutrition.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Kreider_2018_ISSN_Sports_Nutrition.pdf", "page": 35, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "a-methyl-butyrate (HMB) has received\ninterest for its ability to enhance training adaptations and\nperformance while also delaying or preventing muscle\ndamage [15, 168, 571]. Initial work by Nissen and\ncolleagues [171] showed significant increases in", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "VO2max in hot conditions (26-27 °C) followed two-minute\nintervals at 80% VO2max and concluded that glycerol\nhyperhydration significantly improved performance. In con-\ntrast, Marino et al. [567] reported that a similar glycerol\nhyperhydration protocol did not improve the total distance\ncovered when moderately trained cyclists completed a\nvariable-intensity cycling protocol. Additionally, Goulet et\nal. [568] combined a hyperhydration strategy (1.2 g/kg gly-\ncerol + 26 mL/kg water) 2 h before commencing a\ntwo-hour cycling bout at 66% VO2max and 25 °C with con-\nsuming (500 mL/hour) a sports drink and reported that gly-\ncerol hyperhydration failed to impact cardiovascular or\nthermoregulatory functions as well as endurance perform-\nance. McKenna and investigators [569] were one of the\nonly research groups to examine glycerol’s potential to im-\npact anaerobic power after glycerol hyperhydration. After\nfollowing a double-blind hyperhydration protocol, male col-\nlegiate wrestlers lost 3% of their body mass from fluid and\ncompleted an anaerobic test where no impact on perform-\nance was found. Variable outcomes surrounding glycerol\ncontinue to undermine its potential and the ability to offer\na recommendation for its use. Consequently, as pointed\nout by Goulet et al. [556], it is concluded that more re-\nsearch needs to be completed to work through the nuance\nsurrounding glycerol’s potential efficacy, a key point previ-\nously summarized by", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "a-methyl-butyrate (HMB) has received\ninterest for its ability to enhance training adaptations and\nperformance while also delaying or preventing muscle\ndamage [15, 168, 571]. Initial work by Nissen and\ncolleagues [171] showed significant increases in"}
{"chunk_id": "Kreider_2018_ISSN_Sports_Nutrition_p0035_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_1668a67fe73d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5d1963d9ceba4d89", "text_span_hash": "5d1963d9ceba4d89", "source_file": "Kreider_2018_ISSN_Sports_Nutrition.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Kreider_2018_ISSN_Sports_Nutrition.pdf", "page": 35, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "conclusion which aligns with other previous studies [172,\nrandomization, and supervision varied in the initial studies\nand may have contributed to the mixed results. HMB\nappears to have the greatest effects on performance when\ntraining intensity is m", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "VO2max in hot conditions (26-27 °C) followed two-minute\nintervals at 80% VO2max and concluded that glycerol\nhyperhydration significantly improved performance. In con-\ntrast, Marino et al. [567] reported that a similar glycerol\nhyperhydration protocol did not improve the total distance\ncovered when moderately trained cyclists completed a\nvariable-intensity cycling protocol. Additionally, Goulet et\nal. [568] combined a hyperhydration strategy (1.2 g/kg gly-\ncerol + 26 mL/kg water) 2 h before commencing a\ntwo-hour cycling bout at 66% VO2max and 25 °C with con-\nsuming (500 mL/hour) a sports drink and reported that gly-\ncerol hyperhydration failed to impact cardiovascular or\nthermoregulatory functions as well as endurance perform-\nance. McKenna and investigators [569] were one of the\nonly research groups to examine glycerol’s potential to im-\npact anaerobic power after glycerol hyperhydration. After\nfollowing a double-blind hyperhydration protocol, male col-\nlegiate wrestlers lost 3% of their body mass from fluid and\ncompleted an anaerobic test where no impact on perform-\nance was found. Variable outcomes surrounding glycerol\ncontinue to undermine its potential and the ability to offer\na recommendation for its use. Consequently, as pointed\nout by Goulet et al. [556], it is concluded that more re-\nsearch needs to be completed to work through the nuance\nsurrounding glycerol’s potential efficacy, a key point previ-\nously summarized by", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "conclusion which aligns with other previous studies [172,\nrandomization, and supervision varied in the initial studies\nand may have contributed to the mixed results. HMB\nappears to have the greatest effects on performance when\ntraining intensity is m"}
{"chunk_id": "Kreider_2018_ISSN_Sports_Nutrition_p0035_c0017", "source_registry_id": "src_1668a67fe73d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "d2da8b4574468f89", "text_span_hash": "d2da8b4574468f89", "source_file": "Kreider_2018_ISSN_Sports_Nutrition.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Kreider_2018_ISSN_Sports_Nutrition.pdf", "page": 35, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 17, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "rowers over a 12-week period significantly\nimproved aerobic (VO2max, time to reach ventilatory\nthreshold) performance markers and decreased fat mass\nwhen compared to changes seen with placebo [178]. Later,\nDurkalec-Michalski and Jeszka [179] require", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "VO2max in hot conditions (26-27 °C) followed two-minute\nintervals at 80% VO2max and concluded that glycerol\nhyperhydration significantly improved performance. In con-\ntrast, Marino et al. [567] reported that a similar glycerol\nhyperhydration protocol did not improve the total distance\ncovered when moderately trained cyclists completed a\nvariable-intensity cycling protocol. Additionally, Goulet et\nal. [568] combined a hyperhydration strategy (1.2 g/kg gly-\ncerol + 26 mL/kg water) 2 h before commencing a\ntwo-hour cycling bout at 66% VO2max and 25 °C with con-\nsuming (500 mL/hour) a sports drink and reported that gly-\ncerol hyperhydration failed to impact cardiovascular or\nthermoregulatory functions as well as endurance perform-\nance. McKenna and investigators [569] were one of the\nonly research groups to examine glycerol’s potential to im-\npact anaerobic power after glycerol hyperhydration. After\nfollowing a double-blind hyperhydration protocol, male col-\nlegiate wrestlers lost 3% of their body mass from fluid and\ncompleted an anaerobic test where no impact on perform-\nance was found. Variable outcomes surrounding glycerol\ncontinue to undermine its potential and the ability to offer\na recommendation for its use. Consequently, as pointed\nout by Goulet et al. [556], it is concluded that more re-\nsearch needs to be completed to work through the nuance\nsurrounding glycerol’s potential efficacy, a key point previ-\nously summarized by", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "rowers over a 12-week period significantly\nimproved aerobic (VO2max, time to reach ventilatory\nthreshold) performance markers and decreased fat mass\nwhen compared to changes seen with placebo [178]. Later,\nDurkalec-Michalski and Jeszka [179] require"}
{"chunk_id": "Kreider_2018_ISSN_Sports_Nutrition_p0035_c0019", "source_registry_id": "src_1668a67fe73d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "01ca7ab7bd2ca00b", "text_span_hash": "01ca7ab7bd2ca00b", "source_file": "Kreider_2018_ISSN_Sports_Nutrition.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Kreider_2018_ISSN_Sports_Nutrition.pdf", "page": 35, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 19, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "when HMB was provided in comparison to a\n12 weeks in highly-trained combat sport athletes signifi-\ncantly increased (in comparison to placebo) several indica-\ntors of aerobic and anaerobic exercise performance [180].\nThe recent studies by Durkalec-M", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "VO2max in hot conditions (26-27 °C) followed two-minute\nintervals at 80% VO2max and concluded that glycerol\nhyperhydration significantly improved performance. In con-\ntrast, Marino et al. [567] reported that a similar glycerol\nhyperhydration protocol did not improve the total distance\ncovered when moderately trained cyclists completed a\nvariable-intensity cycling protocol. Additionally, Goulet et\nal. [568] combined a hyperhydration strategy (1.2 g/kg gly-\ncerol + 26 mL/kg water) 2 h before commencing a\ntwo-hour cycling bout at 66% VO2max and 25 °C with con-\nsuming (500 mL/hour) a sports drink and reported that gly-\ncerol hyperhydration failed to impact cardiovascular or\nthermoregulatory functions as well as endurance perform-\nance. McKenna and investigators [569] were one of the\nonly research groups to examine glycerol’s potential to im-\npact anaerobic power after glycerol hyperhydration. After\nfollowing a double-blind hyperhydration protocol, male col-\nlegiate wrestlers lost 3% of their body mass from fluid and\ncompleted an anaerobic test where no impact on perform-\nance was found. Variable outcomes surrounding glycerol\ncontinue to undermine its potential and the ability to offer\na recommendation for its use. Consequently, as pointed\nout by Goulet et al. [556], it is concluded that more re-\nsearch needs to be completed to work through the nuance\nsurrounding glycerol’s potential efficacy, a key point previ-\nously summarized by", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "when HMB was provided in comparison to a\n12 weeks in highly-trained combat sport athletes signifi-\ncantly increased (in comparison to placebo) several indica-\ntors of aerobic and anaerobic exercise performance [180].\nThe recent studies by Durkalec-M"}
{"chunk_id": "Kreider_2018_ISSN_Sports_Nutrition_p0037_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_1668a67fe73d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2c217dffc04191b7", "text_span_hash": "2c217dffc04191b7", "source_file": "Kreider_2018_ISSN_Sports_Nutrition.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Kreider_2018_ISSN_Sports_Nutrition.pdf", "page": 37, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ce while Davis et al.\nquercetin and placebo and found that quercetin may im-\nprove VO2max and endurance capacity. A meta-analysis\nwas completed by Pelletier and researchers [617] to\nsummarize the potential impact of quercetin supplemen-\ntation on end", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "some health benefits with therapeutic use. In addition,\nquercetin has been purported in both animal and human\nmodels to improve endurance performance. In this\nrespect, Cureton and colleagues [610] supplemented 30\nrecreationally active, but not highly trained men in a\ndouble-blind fashion to ingest either quercetin (1 g/day)\nor placebo. No changes in total work performed, substrate\nutilization, or perception of effort were found after supple-\nmentation. Similarly, Bigelman and investigators [611]\nsupplemented ROTC cadets with either 1 g of quercetin\nor a placebo and concluded that VO2max was unchanged\nas a result. These results correspond with the outcomes of\nother studies that failed to document ergogenic potential\nfor quercetin [612, 613]. In contrast, Nieman et al. [614]\nsupplemented untrained adult males with 1 g of quercetin\nin a double-blind fashion for 2 weeks and reported that\ntreadmill performance and markers of mitochondrial bio-\ngenesis were improved. Similarly, Patrizio et al. [615] used\na resistance exercise model and reported quercetin may\nimprove neuromuscular performance while Davis et al.\nquercetin and placebo and found that quercetin may im-\nprove VO2max and endurance capacity. A meta-analysis\nwas completed by Pelletier and researchers [617] to\nsummarize the potential impact of quercetin supplemen-\ntation on endurance performance. This analysis involved\nseven published studies representing 288 research partici", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ce while Davis et al.\nquercetin and placebo and found that quercetin may im-\nprove VO2max and endurance capacity. A meta-analysis\nwas completed by Pelletier and researchers [617] to\nsummarize the potential impact of quercetin supplemen-\ntation on end"}
{"chunk_id": "Kreider_2018_ISSN_Sports_Nutrition_p0037_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_1668a67fe73d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ccb824f993f58f7a", "text_span_hash": "ccb824f993f58f7a", "source_file": "Kreider_2018_ISSN_Sports_Nutrition.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Kreider_2018_ISSN_Sports_Nutrition.pdf", "page": 37, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "cetin supplemen-\ntation on endurance performance. This analysis involved\nseven published studies representing 288 research partici-\npants. Only in untrained participants was quercetin found\nto significantly increase endurance performance. A 2011\nmeta", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "some health benefits with therapeutic use. In addition,\nquercetin has been purported in both animal and human\nmodels to improve endurance performance. In this\nrespect, Cureton and colleagues [610] supplemented 30\nrecreationally active, but not highly trained men in a\ndouble-blind fashion to ingest either quercetin (1 g/day)\nor placebo. No changes in total work performed, substrate\nutilization, or perception of effort were found after supple-\nmentation. Similarly, Bigelman and investigators [611]\nsupplemented ROTC cadets with either 1 g of quercetin\nor a placebo and concluded that VO2max was unchanged\nas a result. These results correspond with the outcomes of\nother studies that failed to document ergogenic potential\nfor quercetin [612, 613]. In contrast, Nieman et al. [614]\nsupplemented untrained adult males with 1 g of quercetin\nin a double-blind fashion for 2 weeks and reported that\ntreadmill performance and markers of mitochondrial bio-\ngenesis were improved. Similarly, Patrizio et al. [615] used\na resistance exercise model and reported quercetin may\nimprove neuromuscular performance while Davis et al.\nquercetin and placebo and found that quercetin may im-\nprove VO2max and endurance capacity. A meta-analysis\nwas completed by Pelletier and researchers [617] to\nsummarize the potential impact of quercetin supplemen-\ntation on endurance performance. This analysis involved\nseven published studies representing 288 research partici", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "cetin supplemen-\ntation on endurance performance. This analysis involved\nseven published studies representing 288 research partici-\npants. Only in untrained participants was quercetin found\nto significantly increase endurance performance. A 2011\nmeta"}
{"chunk_id": "Kreider_2018_ISSN_Sports_Nutrition_p0037_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_1668a67fe73d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c90fd78fa3c1cba3", "text_span_hash": "c90fd78fa3c1cba3", "source_file": "Kreider_2018_ISSN_Sports_Nutrition.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Kreider_2018_ISSN_Sports_Nutrition.pdf", "page": 37, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "[619, 620] derived from\ncysteine metabolism that plays a role in a wide variety of\nphysiological functions [621–623]. Studies have indi-\ncated that training status (higher in trained vs. untrained\nmuscle, reviewed in [624]) and fiber type (higher in", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "some health benefits with therapeutic use. In addition,\nquercetin has been purported in both animal and human\nmodels to improve endurance performance. In this\nrespect, Cureton and colleagues [610] supplemented 30\nrecreationally active, but not highly trained men in a\ndouble-blind fashion to ingest either quercetin (1 g/day)\nor placebo. No changes in total work performed, substrate\nutilization, or perception of effort were found after supple-\nmentation. Similarly, Bigelman and investigators [611]\nsupplemented ROTC cadets with either 1 g of quercetin\nor a placebo and concluded that VO2max was unchanged\nas a result. These results correspond with the outcomes of\nother studies that failed to document ergogenic potential\nfor quercetin [612, 613]. In contrast, Nieman et al. [614]\nsupplemented untrained adult males with 1 g of quercetin\nin a double-blind fashion for 2 weeks and reported that\ntreadmill performance and markers of mitochondrial bio-\ngenesis were improved. Similarly, Patrizio et al. [615] used\na resistance exercise model and reported quercetin may\nimprove neuromuscular performance while Davis et al.\nquercetin and placebo and found that quercetin may im-\nprove VO2max and endurance capacity. A meta-analysis\nwas completed by Pelletier and researchers [617] to\nsummarize the potential impact of quercetin supplemen-\ntation on endurance performance. This analysis involved\nseven published studies representing 288 research partici", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "[619, 620] derived from\ncysteine metabolism that plays a role in a wide variety of\nphysiological functions [621–623]. Studies have indi-\ncated that training status (higher in trained vs. untrained\nmuscle, reviewed in [624]) and fiber type (higher in"}
{"chunk_id": "Kreider_2018_ISSN_Sports_Nutrition_p0037_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_1668a67fe73d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0546de2a86b60bf8", "text_span_hash": "0546de2a86b60bf8", "source_file": "Kreider_2018_ISSN_Sports_Nutrition.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Kreider_2018_ISSN_Sports_Nutrition.pdf", "page": 37, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ious work, ergogenic outcomes re-\nlated to taurine administration continue to be mixed.\nMilioni and investigators [628] failed to show an improve-\nment in performance with a 6 g dose of taurine while\ncompleting high-intensity treadmill running. Simil", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "some health benefits with therapeutic use. In addition,\nquercetin has been purported in both animal and human\nmodels to improve endurance performance. In this\nrespect, Cureton and colleagues [610] supplemented 30\nrecreationally active, but not highly trained men in a\ndouble-blind fashion to ingest either quercetin (1 g/day)\nor placebo. No changes in total work performed, substrate\nutilization, or perception of effort were found after supple-\nmentation. Similarly, Bigelman and investigators [611]\nsupplemented ROTC cadets with either 1 g of quercetin\nor a placebo and concluded that VO2max was unchanged\nas a result. These results correspond with the outcomes of\nother studies that failed to document ergogenic potential\nfor quercetin [612, 613]. In contrast, Nieman et al. [614]\nsupplemented untrained adult males with 1 g of quercetin\nin a double-blind fashion for 2 weeks and reported that\ntreadmill performance and markers of mitochondrial bio-\ngenesis were improved. Similarly, Patrizio et al. [615] used\na resistance exercise model and reported quercetin may\nimprove neuromuscular performance while Davis et al.\nquercetin and placebo and found that quercetin may im-\nprove VO2max and endurance capacity. A meta-analysis\nwas completed by Pelletier and researchers [617] to\nsummarize the potential impact of quercetin supplemen-\ntation on endurance performance. This analysis involved\nseven published studies representing 288 research partici", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ious work, ergogenic outcomes re-\nlated to taurine administration continue to be mixed.\nMilioni and investigators [628] failed to show an improve-\nment in performance with a 6 g dose of taurine while\ncompleting high-intensity treadmill running. Simil"}
{"chunk_id": "Kreider_2018_ISSN_Sports_Nutrition_p0037_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_1668a67fe73d", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0d543339481a8b25", "text_span_hash": "0d543339481a8b25", "source_file": "Kreider_2018_ISSN_Sports_Nutrition.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Kreider_2018_ISSN_Sports_Nutrition.pdf", "page": 37, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "nsity treadmill running. Similarly,\nBalshaw et al. [625] indicated that taurine failed to posi-\ntively impact 3-km running performance in trained run-\nners. In contrast, a 2017 study by Warnock et al. [630]\nreported that a 50 mg/kg dose of taurine ou", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "some health benefits with therapeutic use. In addition,\nquercetin has been purported in both animal and human\nmodels to improve endurance performance. In this\nrespect, Cureton and colleagues [610] supplemented 30\nrecreationally active, but not highly trained men in a\ndouble-blind fashion to ingest either quercetin (1 g/day)\nor placebo. No changes in total work performed, substrate\nutilization, or perception of effort were found after supple-\nmentation. Similarly, Bigelman and investigators [611]\nsupplemented ROTC cadets with either 1 g of quercetin\nor a placebo and concluded that VO2max was unchanged\nas a result. These results correspond with the outcomes of\nother studies that failed to document ergogenic potential\nfor quercetin [612, 613]. In contrast, Nieman et al. [614]\nsupplemented untrained adult males with 1 g of quercetin\nin a double-blind fashion for 2 weeks and reported that\ntreadmill performance and markers of mitochondrial bio-\ngenesis were improved. Similarly, Patrizio et al. [615] used\na resistance exercise model and reported quercetin may\nimprove neuromuscular performance while Davis et al.\nquercetin and placebo and found that quercetin may im-\nprove VO2max and endurance capacity. A meta-analysis\nwas completed by Pelletier and researchers [617] to\nsummarize the potential impact of quercetin supplemen-\ntation on endurance performance. This analysis involved\nseven published studies representing 288 research partici", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "nsity treadmill running. Similarly,\nBalshaw et al. [625] indicated that taurine failed to posi-\ntively impact 3-km running performance in trained run-\nners. In contrast, a 2017 study by Warnock et al. [630]\nreported that a 50 mg/kg dose of taurine ou"}
