er improvement can be expected unless an increased training\nload is used to force the body to a further adaptation and, therefore, to a further\nimprovement of physical performance.\nEssentially, the training programme must provide an adequate amount of work to\ncause the athlete to become fatigued. After the athlete has had an opportunity\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "objectives.\nProgressiveness of training\nThe improvement of physical performance comes with the adaptation of the human\nbody to a certain quality and quantity of work. After the athlete’s body has adapted to\nthe given work, no further improvement can"}
{"chunk_id": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English_p0003_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_18cfc6bbe1e5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3c03aa87b7646f0d", "text_span_hash": "3c03aa87b7646f0d", "source_file": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "k, no further improvement can be expected unless an increased training\nload is used to force the body to a further adaptation and, therefore, to a further\nimprovement of physical performance.\nEssentially, the training programme must provide an adequa", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II 49\nThe utilisation of a variety of physical activities provides two benefits. One is physical;\nthe other is psychological.\nA variety of physical activities, particularly during the early part of the training season,\nincreases the all-round physical development of the athlete and, thereby, improves the\npeak performance capabilities that may be achieved with rowing specific training.\nThe increasing demands of rowing specific training, which necessitates a high volume\nof training and the utilisation of repetitive special exercises, may result in the athlete\nbecoming stale and suffering mental fatigue.\nTherefore, it is important for the coach to be creative by drawing upon a repertoire of\nvariations in training in order to maintain the athlete’s interest and motivation to achieve\nshort and long term performance objectives.\nProgressiveness of training\nThe improvement of physical performance comes with the adaptation of the human\nbody to a certain quality and quantity of work. After the athlete’s body has adapted to\nthe given work, no further improvement can be expected unless an increased training\nload is used to force the body to a further adaptation and, therefore, to a further\nimprovement of physical performance.\nEssentially, the training programme must provide an adequate amount of work to\ncause the athlete to become fatigued. After the athlete has had an opportunity\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "k, no further improvement can be expected unless an increased training\nload is used to force the body to a further adaptation and, therefore, to a further\nimprovement of physical performance.\nEssentially, the training programme must provide an adequa"}
{"chunk_id": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English_p0003_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_18cfc6bbe1e5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "727bda23f71f4bd1", "text_span_hash": "727bda23f71f4bd1", "source_file": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "tic\nplan. This plan should be based on scientific and training principles and be arranged\nmethodically in a form that organises the training of the athlete and ensures the proper\nregularity of training.\nThe development of a systematic plan will impro", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II 49\nThe utilisation of a variety of physical activities provides two benefits. One is physical;\nthe other is psychological.\nA variety of physical activities, particularly during the early part of the training season,\nincreases the all-round physical development of the athlete and, thereby, improves the\npeak performance capabilities that may be achieved with rowing specific training.\nThe increasing demands of rowing specific training, which necessitates a high volume\nof training and the utilisation of repetitive special exercises, may result in the athlete\nbecoming stale and suffering mental fatigue.\nTherefore, it is important for the coach to be creative by drawing upon a repertoire of\nvariations in training in order to maintain the athlete’s interest and motivation to achieve\nshort and long term performance objectives.\nProgressiveness of training\nThe improvement of physical performance comes with the adaptation of the human\nbody to a certain quality and quantity of work. After the athlete’s body has adapted to\nthe given work, no further improvement can be expected unless an increased training\nload is used to force the body to a further adaptation and, therefore, to a further\nimprovement of physical performance.\nEssentially, the training programme must provide an adequate amount of work to\ncause the athlete to become fatigued. After the athlete has had an opportunity\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "tic\nplan. This plan should be based on scientific and training principles and be arranged\nmethodically in a form that organises the training of the athlete and ensures the proper\nregularity of training.\nThe development of a systematic plan will impro"}
{"chunk_id": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English_p0003_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_18cfc6bbe1e5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ea93b3c6cc2196d6", "text_span_hash": "ea93b3c6cc2196d6", "source_file": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "f a systematic plan will improve the quality of training because the\nplan would provide a format in which the athlete and coach could test, monitor and\ncontrol performance capabilities. A systematic plan will also provide a model to be\nreviewed and r", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II 49\nThe utilisation of a variety of physical activities provides two benefits. One is physical;\nthe other is psychological.\nA variety of physical activities, particularly during the early part of the training season,\nincreases the all-round physical development of the athlete and, thereby, improves the\npeak performance capabilities that may be achieved with rowing specific training.\nThe increasing demands of rowing specific training, which necessitates a high volume\nof training and the utilisation of repetitive special exercises, may result in the athlete\nbecoming stale and suffering mental fatigue.\nTherefore, it is important for the coach to be creative by drawing upon a repertoire of\nvariations in training in order to maintain the athlete’s interest and motivation to achieve\nshort and long term performance objectives.\nProgressiveness of training\nThe improvement of physical performance comes with the adaptation of the human\nbody to a certain quality and quantity of work. After the athlete’s body has adapted to\nthe given work, no further improvement can be expected unless an increased training\nload is used to force the body to a further adaptation and, therefore, to a further\nimprovement of physical performance.\nEssentially, the training programme must provide an adequate amount of work to\ncause the athlete to become fatigued. After the athlete has had an opportunity\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "f a systematic plan will improve the quality of training because the\nplan would provide a format in which the athlete and coach could test, monitor and\ncontrol performance capabilities. A systematic plan will also provide a model to be\nreviewed and r"}
{"chunk_id": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English_p0003_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_18cfc6bbe1e5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "fb251ebf2a686fee", "text_span_hash": "fb251ebf2a686fee", "source_file": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "e a model to be\nreviewed and revised for the next training season and throughout the athlete’s career.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II 49\nThe utilisation of a variety of physical activities provides two benefits. One is physical;\nthe other is psychological.\nA variety of physical activities, particularly during the early part of the training season,\nincreases the all-round physical development of the athlete and, thereby, improves the\npeak performance capabilities that may be achieved with rowing specific training.\nThe increasing demands of rowing specific training, which necessitates a high volume\nof training and the utilisation of repetitive special exercises, may result in the athlete\nbecoming stale and suffering mental fatigue.\nTherefore, it is important for the coach to be creative by drawing upon a repertoire of\nvariations in training in order to maintain the athlete’s interest and motivation to achieve\nshort and long term performance objectives.\nProgressiveness of training\nThe improvement of physical performance comes with the adaptation of the human\nbody to a certain quality and quantity of work. After the athlete’s body has adapted to\nthe given work, no further improvement can be expected unless an increased training\nload is used to force the body to a further adaptation and, therefore, to a further\nimprovement of physical performance.\nEssentially, the training programme must provide an adequate amount of work to\ncause the athlete to become fatigued. After the athlete has had an opportunity\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "e a model to be\nreviewed and revised for the next training season and throughout the athlete’s career."}
{"chunk_id": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English_p0004_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_18cfc6bbe1e5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4878bafad22319e3", "text_span_hash": "4878bafad22319e3", "source_file": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "50 The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II\nPeriodisation is the process of dividing an annual training programme into periods of\ntraining to allow the programme to be set into manageable segments and to ensure a\ncorrect peaking f", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "50 The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II\nPeriodisation is the process of dividing an annual training programme into periods of\ntraining to allow the programme to be set into manageable segments and to ensure a\ncorrect peaking for the main competition or training objective of the year.\nThe process of periodisation depends on the use of the concepts of training loads\nand the wave principle of training. This information will enable better understanding of\nthe principal components of periodisation: training period, training cycle and training\nThe training load consists of the quantity and quality of work. Quantity is represented\nby distance of work, time of work, or number of repetitions, etc. Thus, a rowing training\nsession may be described as, for example, 16km in distance during which three\nrepetitions of ten minutes of work will be performed.\nQuality is the effort exerted in the training session. It may be represented as the speed of\nrunning, the amount of weight lifted, heart rate maintained, or, in the boat, a combination\nof pressure applied on the blade and the stroke rating, etc. Thus, the example rowing\ntraining session above may be further described as three repetitions of ten minutes of\nwork at a rating of 26 strokes per minute while maintaining a heart rate of 140 to 170.\nIn a systematic plan of training, the training load goes from quantity to quality. This\nmeans that, after a period of adjustin", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "50 The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II\nPeriodisation is the process of dividing an annual training programme into periods of\ntraining to allow the programme to be set into manageable segments and to ensure a\ncorrect peaking f"}
{"chunk_id": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English_p0004_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_18cfc6bbe1e5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "25678a35893b349d", "text_span_hash": "25678a35893b349d", "source_file": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "to ensure a\ncorrect peaking for the main competition or training objective of the year.\nThe process of periodisation depends on the use of the concepts of training loads\nand the wave principle of training. This information will enable better underst", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "50 The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II\nPeriodisation is the process of dividing an annual training programme into periods of\ntraining to allow the programme to be set into manageable segments and to ensure a\ncorrect peaking for the main competition or training objective of the year.\nThe process of periodisation depends on the use of the concepts of training loads\nand the wave principle of training. This information will enable better understanding of\nthe principal components of periodisation: training period, training cycle and training\nThe training load consists of the quantity and quality of work. Quantity is represented\nby distance of work, time of work, or number of repetitions, etc. Thus, a rowing training\nsession may be described as, for example, 16km in distance during which three\nrepetitions of ten minutes of work will be performed.\nQuality is the effort exerted in the training session. It may be represented as the speed of\nrunning, the amount of weight lifted, heart rate maintained, or, in the boat, a combination\nof pressure applied on the blade and the stroke rating, etc. Thus, the example rowing\ntraining session above may be further described as three repetitions of ten minutes of\nwork at a rating of 26 strokes per minute while maintaining a heart rate of 140 to 170.\nIn a systematic plan of training, the training load goes from quantity to quality. This\nmeans that, after a period of adjustin", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "to ensure a\ncorrect peaking for the main competition or training objective of the year.\nThe process of periodisation depends on the use of the concepts of training loads\nand the wave principle of training. This information will enable better underst"}
