ting, etc. Thus, the example rowing\ntraining session above may be further described as three repetitions of ten minutes of\nwork at a rating of 26 strokes per minute while maintaining a heart rate of 140 to 170.\nIn a systematic plan of training, the training load goes from quantity to quality. This\nmeans that, after a period of adjustin", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "70.\nIn a systematic plan of training, the training load goes from quantity to quality. This\nmeans that, after a period of adjusting to a new training season, the training starts\nwith a large quantity of a relatively low or medium quality of work to p"}
{"chunk_id": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English_p0004_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_18cfc6bbe1e5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "96f2d0fa672c6c4a", "text_span_hash": "96f2d0fa672c6c4a", "source_file": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "or medium quality of work to provide all-round\ndevelopment and improvement in endurance capabilities.\nAs the training season progresses, there is a gradual increase in quality and a\ncorresponding decrease in quantity. This enables the development of", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "50 The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II\nPeriodisation is the process of dividing an annual training programme into periods of\ntraining to allow the programme to be set into manageable segments and to ensure a\ncorrect peaking for the main competition or training objective of the year.\nThe process of periodisation depends on the use of the concepts of training loads\nand the wave principle of training. This information will enable better understanding of\nthe principal components of periodisation: training period, training cycle and training\nThe training load consists of the quantity and quality of work. Quantity is represented\nby distance of work, time of work, or number of repetitions, etc. Thus, a rowing training\nsession may be described as, for example, 16km in distance during which three\nrepetitions of ten minutes of work will be performed.\nQuality is the effort exerted in the training session. It may be represented as the speed of\nrunning, the amount of weight lifted, heart rate maintained, or, in the boat, a combination\nof pressure applied on the blade and the stroke rating, etc. Thus, the example rowing\ntraining session above may be further described as three repetitions of ten minutes of\nwork at a rating of 26 strokes per minute while maintaining a heart rate of 140 to 170.\nIn a systematic plan of training, the training load goes from quantity to quality. This\nmeans that, after a period of adjustin", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "or medium quality of work to provide all-round\ndevelopment and improvement in endurance capabilities.\nAs the training season progresses, there is a gradual increase in quality and a\ncorresponding decrease in quantity. This enables the development of"}
