ating, etc. Thus, the example rowing\ntraining session above may be further described as three repetitions of ten minutes of\nwork at a rating of 26 strokes per minute while maintaining a heart rate of 140 to 170.\nIn a systematic plan of training, the training load goes from quantity to quality. This\nmeans that, after a period of adjustin", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "of training, usually between four and eight weeks,\nduring which the programme is directed toward a certain training objective. Training\ncycles follow the wave principle of applying alternating phases of increasing and\ndecreasing training load.\nIt ha"}
{"chunk_id": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English_p0004_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_18cfc6bbe1e5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "567dc15edb18fed0", "text_span_hash": "567dc15edb18fed0", "source_file": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ecreasing training load.\nIt has been demonstrated that this step or wave approach is more efficient than the\nlinear or continuous method of loading. As opposed to the continuous method, the\nwave principle requires that a training load increase must b", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "50 The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II\nPeriodisation is the process of dividing an annual training programme into periods of\ntraining to allow the programme to be set into manageable segments and to ensure a\ncorrect peaking for the main competition or training objective of the year.\nThe process of periodisation depends on the use of the concepts of training loads\nand the wave principle of training. This information will enable better understanding of\nthe principal components of periodisation: training period, training cycle and training\nThe training load consists of the quantity and quality of work. Quantity is represented\nby distance of work, time of work, or number of repetitions, etc. Thus, a rowing training\nsession may be described as, for example, 16km in distance during which three\nrepetitions of ten minutes of work will be performed.\nQuality is the effort exerted in the training session. It may be represented as the speed of\nrunning, the amount of weight lifted, heart rate maintained, or, in the boat, a combination\nof pressure applied on the blade and the stroke rating, etc. Thus, the example rowing\ntraining session above may be further described as three repetitions of ten minutes of\nwork at a rating of 26 strokes per minute while maintaining a heart rate of 140 to 170.\nIn a systematic plan of training, the training load goes from quantity to quality. This\nmeans that, after a period of adjustin", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ecreasing training load.\nIt has been demonstrated that this step or wave approach is more efficient than the\nlinear or continuous method of loading. As opposed to the continuous method, the\nwave principle requires that a training load increase must b"}
{"chunk_id": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English_p0004_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_18cfc6bbe1e5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "13fee72630de664c", "text_span_hash": "13fee72630de664c", "source_file": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "training load increase must be followed by a decrease in\ntraining load during which the athlete’s body is able to recover and adapt to the training\nload. This enables the athlete to be subjected to progressively increasing training loads.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "50 The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II\nPeriodisation is the process of dividing an annual training programme into periods of\ntraining to allow the programme to be set into manageable segments and to ensure a\ncorrect peaking for the main competition or training objective of the year.\nThe process of periodisation depends on the use of the concepts of training loads\nand the wave principle of training. This information will enable better understanding of\nthe principal components of periodisation: training period, training cycle and training\nThe training load consists of the quantity and quality of work. Quantity is represented\nby distance of work, time of work, or number of repetitions, etc. Thus, a rowing training\nsession may be described as, for example, 16km in distance during which three\nrepetitions of ten minutes of work will be performed.\nQuality is the effort exerted in the training session. It may be represented as the speed of\nrunning, the amount of weight lifted, heart rate maintained, or, in the boat, a combination\nof pressure applied on the blade and the stroke rating, etc. Thus, the example rowing\ntraining session above may be further described as three repetitions of ten minutes of\nwork at a rating of 26 strokes per minute while maintaining a heart rate of 140 to 170.\nIn a systematic plan of training, the training load goes from quantity to quality. This\nmeans that, after a period of adjustin", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "training load increase must be followed by a decrease in\ntraining load during which the athlete’s body is able to recover and adapt to the training\nload. This enables the athlete to be subjected to progressively increasing training loads."}
{"chunk_id": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English_p0005_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_18cfc6bbe1e5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "76bbcae99d379b68", "text_span_hash": "76bbcae99d379b68", "source_file": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II 51\nIt has also been demonstrated that the best results in improvement of performance can\nbe achieved if the training load is gradually increased during three successive training\nsessions up", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II 51\nIt has also been demonstrated that the best results in improvement of performance can\nbe achieved if the training load is gradually increased during three successive training\nsessions up to the athlete’s maximum load capability (for example 50%, 75%, 100%)\nand followed by a very light training session or a complete rest. This applies to training\nprogrammes of five or more sessions per week. If the frequency of training sessions is\nless, the programme can be designed with one day off after every outing and with the\ntraining load being gradually increased to its maximum on the weekend.\nThe wave principle of changing the training loads will apply throughout the training cycle,\nwhich means that the maximum load will vary from week to week creating the wave\napproach to training. Appendix A provides an example of this principle.\nPlanning each training period\nA systematic training programme based on scientific and training principles is\nfundamental to the successful pursuit of high level athletic performance. The systematic\ntraining programme is developed by working in reverse chronological order from the\ndate of the main competition or training objective and dividing the training season into\nthe appropriate number of training periods.\nEach period has different aims and, to some extent, will continue the application of the\nconcepts of training load and the wave principle", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II 51\nIt has also been demonstrated that the best results in improvement of performance can\nbe achieved if the training load is gradually increased during three successive training\nsessions up"}
