me is developed by working in reverse chronological order from the\ndate of the main competition or training objective and dividing the training season into\nthe appropriate number of training periods.\nEach period has different aims and, to some extent, will continue the application of the\nconcepts of training load and the wave principle", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "essions per week. If the frequency of training sessions is\nless, the programme can be designed with one day off after every outing and with the\ntraining load being gradually increased to its maximum on the weekend.\nThe wave principle of changing the"}
{"chunk_id": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English_p0005_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_18cfc6bbe1e5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0a550b9524718f04", "text_span_hash": "0a550b9524718f04", "source_file": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ave principle of changing the training loads will apply throughout the training cycle,\nwhich means that the maximum load will vary from week to week creating the wave\napproach to training. Appendix A provides an example of this principle.\nPlanning ea", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II 51\nIt has also been demonstrated that the best results in improvement of performance can\nbe achieved if the training load is gradually increased during three successive training\nsessions up to the athlete’s maximum load capability (for example 50%, 75%, 100%)\nand followed by a very light training session or a complete rest. This applies to training\nprogrammes of five or more sessions per week. If the frequency of training sessions is\nless, the programme can be designed with one day off after every outing and with the\ntraining load being gradually increased to its maximum on the weekend.\nThe wave principle of changing the training loads will apply throughout the training cycle,\nwhich means that the maximum load will vary from week to week creating the wave\napproach to training. Appendix A provides an example of this principle.\nPlanning each training period\nA systematic training programme based on scientific and training principles is\nfundamental to the successful pursuit of high level athletic performance. The systematic\ntraining programme is developed by working in reverse chronological order from the\ndate of the main competition or training objective and dividing the training season into\nthe appropriate number of training periods.\nEach period has different aims and, to some extent, will continue the application of the\nconcepts of training load and the wave principle", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ave principle of changing the training loads will apply throughout the training cycle,\nwhich means that the maximum load will vary from week to week creating the wave\napproach to training. Appendix A provides an example of this principle.\nPlanning ea"}
{"chunk_id": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English_p0005_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_18cfc6bbe1e5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "207a1558d7ff81cd", "text_span_hash": "207a1558d7ff81cd", "source_file": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "of this principle.\nPlanning each training period\nA systematic training programme based on scientific and training principles is\nfundamental to the successful pursuit of high level athletic performance. The systematic\ntraining programme is developed b", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II 51\nIt has also been demonstrated that the best results in improvement of performance can\nbe achieved if the training load is gradually increased during three successive training\nsessions up to the athlete’s maximum load capability (for example 50%, 75%, 100%)\nand followed by a very light training session or a complete rest. This applies to training\nprogrammes of five or more sessions per week. If the frequency of training sessions is\nless, the programme can be designed with one day off after every outing and with the\ntraining load being gradually increased to its maximum on the weekend.\nThe wave principle of changing the training loads will apply throughout the training cycle,\nwhich means that the maximum load will vary from week to week creating the wave\napproach to training. Appendix A provides an example of this principle.\nPlanning each training period\nA systematic training programme based on scientific and training principles is\nfundamental to the successful pursuit of high level athletic performance. The systematic\ntraining programme is developed by working in reverse chronological order from the\ndate of the main competition or training objective and dividing the training season into\nthe appropriate number of training periods.\nEach period has different aims and, to some extent, will continue the application of the\nconcepts of training load and the wave principle", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "of this principle.\nPlanning each training period\nA systematic training programme based on scientific and training principles is\nfundamental to the successful pursuit of high level athletic performance. The systematic\ntraining programme is developed b"}
