mme is developed by working in reverse chronological order from the\ndate of the main competition or training objective and dividing the training season into\nthe appropriate number of training periods.\nEach period has different aims and, to some extent, will continue the application of the\nconcepts of training load and the wave principle", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ining programme is developed by working in reverse chronological order from the\ndate of the main competition or training objective and dividing the training season into\nthe appropriate number of training periods.\nEach period has different aims and, t"}
{"chunk_id": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English_p0005_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_18cfc6bbe1e5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "062311a2918339ee", "text_span_hash": "062311a2918339ee", "source_file": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "raining in the boat.\n2 To develop and stabilise competition performance.\n1 Physical and mental relaxation\n2 Relief from the pattern of systematic training", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II 51\nIt has also been demonstrated that the best results in improvement of performance can\nbe achieved if the training load is gradually increased during three successive training\nsessions up to the athlete’s maximum load capability (for example 50%, 75%, 100%)\nand followed by a very light training session or a complete rest. This applies to training\nprogrammes of five or more sessions per week. If the frequency of training sessions is\nless, the programme can be designed with one day off after every outing and with the\ntraining load being gradually increased to its maximum on the weekend.\nThe wave principle of changing the training loads will apply throughout the training cycle,\nwhich means that the maximum load will vary from week to week creating the wave\napproach to training. Appendix A provides an example of this principle.\nPlanning each training period\nA systematic training programme based on scientific and training principles is\nfundamental to the successful pursuit of high level athletic performance. The systematic\ntraining programme is developed by working in reverse chronological order from the\ndate of the main competition or training objective and dividing the training season into\nthe appropriate number of training periods.\nEach period has different aims and, to some extent, will continue the application of the\nconcepts of training load and the wave principle", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "raining in the boat.\n2 To develop and stabilise competition performance.\n1 Physical and mental relaxation\n2 Relief from the pattern of systematic training"}
{"chunk_id": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English_p0006_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_18cfc6bbe1e5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5ce6dcccf7e3d35c", "text_span_hash": "5ce6dcccf7e3d35c", "source_file": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "52 The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II\nPlanning each training cycle\nEach period is divided into one or more training cycles of four to eight weeks in length.\nThe plan for each cycle gives the athlete an outline of the particu", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "52 The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II\nPlanning each training cycle\nEach period is divided into one or more training cycles of four to eight weeks in length.\nThe plan for each cycle gives the athlete an outline of the particular activities on the\nland and in the boat. It shows the kinds of exercises, the quantity and quality of the\nwork, and a detailed programme for each training session in the cycle. The plan for\neach cycle takes into consideration the different degrees of training load and rest\nintervals within the week and within the whole cycle (see Appendices A & B).\nPlanning each training session\nThe aim of the period and cycle and the purpose of each session should be carefully\nexplained to the athlete. Every training session must begin with a good warm-up on\nland (five to ten minutes of light jogging and five minutes of mobility exercises) and also\nin the boat (systems of warm-up in the boat vary from crew to crew).\nAfter the warm-up in the boat, there is a time for technical exercises because the\nteaching of technique or correction of technical faults requires mental concentration,\nwhich deteriorates with the increasing fatigue of the athlete during the training session.\nAfter the technical exercises have been completed, the main part of the session is\ndevoted to the principal aim of the training period and the training cycle. The last part\nof the training session is for relaxation and a warm-", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "52 The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II\nPlanning each training cycle\nEach period is divided into one or more training cycles of four to eight weeks in length.\nThe plan for each cycle gives the athlete an outline of the particu"}
{"chunk_id": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English_p0006_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_18cfc6bbe1e5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4f563a7fa27ab11d", "text_span_hash": "4f563a7fa27ab11d", "source_file": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "lete an outline of the particular activities on the\nland and in the boat. It shows the kinds of exercises, the quantity and quality of the\nwork, and a detailed programme for each training session in the cycle. The plan for\neach cycle takes into consi", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "52 The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II\nPlanning each training cycle\nEach period is divided into one or more training cycles of four to eight weeks in length.\nThe plan for each cycle gives the athlete an outline of the particular activities on the\nland and in the boat. It shows the kinds of exercises, the quantity and quality of the\nwork, and a detailed programme for each training session in the cycle. The plan for\neach cycle takes into consideration the different degrees of training load and rest\nintervals within the week and within the whole cycle (see Appendices A & B).\nPlanning each training session\nThe aim of the period and cycle and the purpose of each session should be carefully\nexplained to the athlete. Every training session must begin with a good warm-up on\nland (five to ten minutes of light jogging and five minutes of mobility exercises) and also\nin the boat (systems of warm-up in the boat vary from crew to crew).\nAfter the warm-up in the boat, there is a time for technical exercises because the\nteaching of technique or correction of technical faults requires mental concentration,\nwhich deteriorates with the increasing fatigue of the athlete during the training session.\nAfter the technical exercises have been completed, the main part of the session is\ndevoted to the principal aim of the training period and the training cycle. The last part\nof the training session is for relaxation and a warm-", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "lete an outline of the particular activities on the\nland and in the boat. It shows the kinds of exercises, the quantity and quality of the\nwork, and a detailed programme for each training session in the cycle. The plan for\neach cycle takes into consi"}
