nformation on the athlete’s\ndeveloping capabilities and on the effectiveness of the plan in directing the athlete\ntowards the training objective.\nAs well, it is important that both the coach and athlete maintain a training journal\nto document their observations and comments about the training. This information\nis invaluable in the proces", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "valuable information on the athlete’s\ndeveloping capabilities and on the effectiveness of the plan in directing the athlete\ntowards the training objective.\nAs well, it is important that both the coach and athlete maintain a training journal\nto docum"}
{"chunk_id": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English_p0008_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_18cfc6bbe1e5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "62cacf333b2bc777", "text_span_hash": "62cacf333b2bc777", "source_file": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "in a training journal\nto document their observations and comments about the training. This information\nis invaluable in the process of reviewing the plan both during and after the training\nseason. This review will allow modification to be made in the", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "54 The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II\nDiagram 1 – Periodisation plan\nIt is important to realise that a systematic plan must be developed in conjunction with\nan understanding of how to implement the plan. This is probably the most difficult\naspect of designing and monitoring a training programme. It requires an understanding\nof the basic principles of training (see section 2.0), the concepts of training loads\nand training cycles (see sections 3.1 and 3.2), the energy systems, proper rowing\ntechnique and the methodology of learning.\nThe coach and athlete must make a conscious decision to proceed with the plan\nand become active participants in the training process. This ensures that the athlete\nmaintains the proper motivation in the pursuit of the training objective and that the\nathlete trains regularly and conscientiously.\nMonitor and review the plan\nIt is important that the plan includes various tests and standards that are to be\nattempted and successfully completed, both on and off the water, during the training\nseason. These tests and standards will provide valuable information on the athlete’s\ndeveloping capabilities and on the effectiveness of the plan in directing the athlete\ntowards the training objective.\nAs well, it is important that both the coach and athlete maintain a training journal\nto document their observations and comments about the training. This information\nis invaluable in the proces", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "in a training journal\nto document their observations and comments about the training. This information\nis invaluable in the process of reviewing the plan both during and after the training\nseason. This review will allow modification to be made in the"}
{"chunk_id": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English_p0008_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_18cfc6bbe1e5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7564593314479ab3", "text_span_hash": "7564593314479ab3", "source_file": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "modification to be made in the programme during the\nseason and from season to season to enhance the athlete’s opportunity to achieve his\nor her athletic potential.\nThis procedure for the planning of a training programme should be repeated for each\nne", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "54 The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II\nDiagram 1 – Periodisation plan\nIt is important to realise that a systematic plan must be developed in conjunction with\nan understanding of how to implement the plan. This is probably the most difficult\naspect of designing and monitoring a training programme. It requires an understanding\nof the basic principles of training (see section 2.0), the concepts of training loads\nand training cycles (see sections 3.1 and 3.2), the energy systems, proper rowing\ntechnique and the methodology of learning.\nThe coach and athlete must make a conscious decision to proceed with the plan\nand become active participants in the training process. This ensures that the athlete\nmaintains the proper motivation in the pursuit of the training objective and that the\nathlete trains regularly and conscientiously.\nMonitor and review the plan\nIt is important that the plan includes various tests and standards that are to be\nattempted and successfully completed, both on and off the water, during the training\nseason. These tests and standards will provide valuable information on the athlete’s\ndeveloping capabilities and on the effectiveness of the plan in directing the athlete\ntowards the training objective.\nAs well, it is important that both the coach and athlete maintain a training journal\nto document their observations and comments about the training. This information\nis invaluable in the proces", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "modification to be made in the programme during the\nseason and from season to season to enhance the athlete’s opportunity to achieve his\nor her athletic potential.\nThis procedure for the planning of a training programme should be repeated for each\nne"}
{"chunk_id": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English_p0009_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_18cfc6bbe1e5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "901a1171dbb98a64", "text_span_hash": "901a1171dbb98a64", "source_file": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II 55\nAppendix A – Wave principle of training", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II 55\nAppendix A – Wave principle of training", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II 55\nAppendix A – Wave principle of training"}
{"chunk_id": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English_p0010_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_18cfc6bbe1e5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1b9f72a5d11c46db", "text_span_hash": "1b9f72a5d11c46db", "source_file": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "56 The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II\nAppendix B – Author: Thor S. Nilsen, NOR\nThis “Training Programme for Clubs” is based on The FISA Development Club\nProgramme distributed in 1993. Many coaches have asked for an update, b", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "56 The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II\nAppendix B – Author: Thor S. Nilsen, NOR\nThis “Training Programme for Clubs” is based on The FISA Development Club\nProgramme distributed in 1993. Many coaches have asked for an update, but in\ntraining methodology and rowing technique not much has changed, and the basics\nstay the same. Anyhow, some training models have been improved and distribution of\ntime looks a bit different.\nThe difference from 1993 is the higher number of hours invested in training by the\ninternational elite. With more than 40 hours a week in training a lot of injuries in form of\nstress fractures and lower back problems occurred.\nAmong the lightweight rowers it looks as if the immune defense got strongly reduced\nand common colds, influenza and other fever-related illnesses caused many breaks in\nthe training system. This is probably a result of high training load and reduced intake\nof food to keep the weight down. A problem we must address strongly in the time to\nIn this programme we have two new appendices presenting a better programme for\nflexibility and series of additional exercises to give the clubs the possibility to create a\nmore “all-round” training programme and avoid “disharmony” between the different\ngroups of muscles. Many injuries could be related to underdevelopment in non-specific\nrowing muscles (see chapter 5 – General Fitness Training).\nThe aim of the programme will follow the", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "56 The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II\nAppendix B – Author: Thor S. Nilsen, NOR\nThis “Training Programme for Clubs” is based on The FISA Development Club\nProgramme distributed in 1993. Many coaches have asked for an update, b"}
{"chunk_id": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English_p0010_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_18cfc6bbe1e5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "80b358beed528fe6", "text_span_hash": "80b358beed528fe6", "source_file": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "es have asked for an update, but in\ntraining methodology and rowing technique not much has changed, and the basics\nstay the same. Anyhow, some training models have been improved and distribution of\ntime looks a bit different.\nThe difference from 1993", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "56 The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II\nAppendix B – Author: Thor S. Nilsen, NOR\nThis “Training Programme for Clubs” is based on The FISA Development Club\nProgramme distributed in 1993. Many coaches have asked for an update, but in\ntraining methodology and rowing technique not much has changed, and the basics\nstay the same. Anyhow, some training models have been improved and distribution of\ntime looks a bit different.\nThe difference from 1993 is the higher number of hours invested in training by the\ninternational elite. With more than 40 hours a week in training a lot of injuries in form of\nstress fractures and lower back problems occurred.\nAmong the lightweight rowers it looks as if the immune defense got strongly reduced\nand common colds, influenza and other fever-related illnesses caused many breaks in\nthe training system. This is probably a result of high training load and reduced intake\nof food to keep the weight down. A problem we must address strongly in the time to\nIn this programme we have two new appendices presenting a better programme for\nflexibility and series of additional exercises to give the clubs the possibility to create a\nmore “all-round” training programme and avoid “disharmony” between the different\ngroups of muscles. Many injuries could be related to underdevelopment in non-specific\nrowing muscles (see chapter 5 – General Fitness Training).\nThe aim of the programme will follow the", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "es have asked for an update, but in\ntraining methodology and rowing technique not much has changed, and the basics\nstay the same. Anyhow, some training models have been improved and distribution of\ntime looks a bit different.\nThe difference from 1993"}
{"chunk_id": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English_p0010_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_18cfc6bbe1e5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "556af668a913076a", "text_span_hash": "556af668a913076a", "source_file": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ty and series of additional exercises to give the clubs the possibility to create a\nmore “all-round” training programme and avoid “disharmony” between the different\ngroups of muscles. Many injuries could be related to underdevelopment in non-specific", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "56 The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II\nAppendix B – Author: Thor S. Nilsen, NOR\nThis “Training Programme for Clubs” is based on The FISA Development Club\nProgramme distributed in 1993. Many coaches have asked for an update, but in\ntraining methodology and rowing technique not much has changed, and the basics\nstay the same. Anyhow, some training models have been improved and distribution of\ntime looks a bit different.\nThe difference from 1993 is the higher number of hours invested in training by the\ninternational elite. With more than 40 hours a week in training a lot of injuries in form of\nstress fractures and lower back problems occurred.\nAmong the lightweight rowers it looks as if the immune defense got strongly reduced\nand common colds, influenza and other fever-related illnesses caused many breaks in\nthe training system. This is probably a result of high training load and reduced intake\nof food to keep the weight down. A problem we must address strongly in the time to\nIn this programme we have two new appendices presenting a better programme for\nflexibility and series of additional exercises to give the clubs the possibility to create a\nmore “all-round” training programme and avoid “disharmony” between the different\ngroups of muscles. Many injuries could be related to underdevelopment in non-specific\nrowing muscles (see chapter 5 – General Fitness Training).\nThe aim of the programme will follow the", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ty and series of additional exercises to give the clubs the possibility to create a\nmore “all-round” training programme and avoid “disharmony” between the different\ngroups of muscles. Many injuries could be related to underdevelopment in non-specific"}
{"chunk_id": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English_p0010_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_18cfc6bbe1e5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b7e8d7fbd533239c", "text_span_hash": "b7e8d7fbd533239c", "source_file": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "derdevelopment in non-specific\nrowing muscles (see chapter 5 – General Fitness Training).\nThe aim of the programme will follow the same lines as the last edition:\n• Increase maximum VO2\n• Increase strength endurance\n• Increase maximum strength\n• High", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "56 The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II\nAppendix B – Author: Thor S. Nilsen, NOR\nThis “Training Programme for Clubs” is based on The FISA Development Club\nProgramme distributed in 1993. Many coaches have asked for an update, but in\ntraining methodology and rowing technique not much has changed, and the basics\nstay the same. Anyhow, some training models have been improved and distribution of\ntime looks a bit different.\nThe difference from 1993 is the higher number of hours invested in training by the\ninternational elite. With more than 40 hours a week in training a lot of injuries in form of\nstress fractures and lower back problems occurred.\nAmong the lightweight rowers it looks as if the immune defense got strongly reduced\nand common colds, influenza and other fever-related illnesses caused many breaks in\nthe training system. This is probably a result of high training load and reduced intake\nof food to keep the weight down. A problem we must address strongly in the time to\nIn this programme we have two new appendices presenting a better programme for\nflexibility and series of additional exercises to give the clubs the possibility to create a\nmore “all-round” training programme and avoid “disharmony” between the different\ngroups of muscles. Many injuries could be related to underdevelopment in non-specific\nrowing muscles (see chapter 5 – General Fitness Training).\nThe aim of the programme will follow the", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "derdevelopment in non-specific\nrowing muscles (see chapter 5 – General Fitness Training).\nThe aim of the programme will follow the same lines as the last edition:\n• Increase maximum VO2\n• Increase strength endurance\n• Increase maximum strength\n• High"}
{"chunk_id": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English_p0012_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_18cfc6bbe1e5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7f443f0216b50a17", "text_span_hash": "7f443f0216b50a17", "source_file": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "heart rate is estimated as 220 minus\nage. In the programmes 200 HR is used as maximum and 180 HR as minimum.\nIndividual variations will occur frequently.\nAll training models where HR is indicated show the physiological effect expected, and\nrefer to t", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "58 The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II\nHow to use the programmes?\nThe intensity is expressed in “heart rate” with an indicated “target zone”, based on\npercentage of “maximum heart rate”. Maximum heart rate is estimated as 220 minus\nage. In the programmes 200 HR is used as maximum and 180 HR as minimum.\nIndividual variations will occur frequently.\nAll training models where HR is indicated show the physiological effect expected, and\nrefer to the oxygen transport system.\nIt is not necessary to stay strictly within the “target zone”, but to obtain maximum\ntraining effect these rules should be respected:\nTraining time in ” “target zone”:\nAnaerobic threshold:\nIndicated stroke rate\nThe stroke rate is closely connected to the heart rate, but has its own technical effect.\nClose to the regatta season, and inside the regatta season, it is important to train in\nthe “stroke rate area” where we are supposed to compete.\nThe single sculler and the eight will use different “stroke rate areas”, and in the\nprogramme the lowest number is an indication for the slow boats and the highest\nnumber for the fast boats. Weather conditions must be taken into consideration with\nslower rate in headwind and upstream.\nLightweight rowers should not use the “volume” or “maximum strength training”,\nbecause it will increase bodyweight and muscle volume. With the “top pyramid”,\nmaximum strength can be improved without weight gain.", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "heart rate is estimated as 220 minus\nage. In the programmes 200 HR is used as maximum and 180 HR as minimum.\nIndividual variations will occur frequently.\nAll training models where HR is indicated show the physiological effect expected, and\nrefer to t"}
{"chunk_id": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English_p0012_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_18cfc6bbe1e5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e449a0ac41b3a7f7", "text_span_hash": "e449a0ac41b3a7f7", "source_file": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ffect expected, and\nrefer to the oxygen transport system.\nIt is not necessary to stay strictly within the “target zone”, but to obtain maximum\ntraining effect these rules should be respected:\nTraining time in ” “target zone”:\nAnaerobic threshold:\nInd", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "58 The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II\nHow to use the programmes?\nThe intensity is expressed in “heart rate” with an indicated “target zone”, based on\npercentage of “maximum heart rate”. Maximum heart rate is estimated as 220 minus\nage. In the programmes 200 HR is used as maximum and 180 HR as minimum.\nIndividual variations will occur frequently.\nAll training models where HR is indicated show the physiological effect expected, and\nrefer to the oxygen transport system.\nIt is not necessary to stay strictly within the “target zone”, but to obtain maximum\ntraining effect these rules should be respected:\nTraining time in ” “target zone”:\nAnaerobic threshold:\nIndicated stroke rate\nThe stroke rate is closely connected to the heart rate, but has its own technical effect.\nClose to the regatta season, and inside the regatta season, it is important to train in\nthe “stroke rate area” where we are supposed to compete.\nThe single sculler and the eight will use different “stroke rate areas”, and in the\nprogramme the lowest number is an indication for the slow boats and the highest\nnumber for the fast boats. Weather conditions must be taken into consideration with\nslower rate in headwind and upstream.\nLightweight rowers should not use the “volume” or “maximum strength training”,\nbecause it will increase bodyweight and muscle volume. With the “top pyramid”,\nmaximum strength can be improved without weight gain.", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ffect expected, and\nrefer to the oxygen transport system.\nIt is not necessary to stay strictly within the “target zone”, but to obtain maximum\ntraining effect these rules should be respected:\nTraining time in ” “target zone”:\nAnaerobic threshold:\nInd"}
{"chunk_id": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English_p0012_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_18cfc6bbe1e5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f494e7f68e20cd40", "text_span_hash": "f494e7f68e20cd40", "source_file": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "one”:\nAnaerobic threshold:\nIndicated stroke rate\nThe stroke rate is closely connected to the heart rate, but has its own technical effect.\nClose to the regatta season, and inside the regatta season, it is important to train in\nthe “stroke rate area”", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "58 The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II\nHow to use the programmes?\nThe intensity is expressed in “heart rate” with an indicated “target zone”, based on\npercentage of “maximum heart rate”. Maximum heart rate is estimated as 220 minus\nage. In the programmes 200 HR is used as maximum and 180 HR as minimum.\nIndividual variations will occur frequently.\nAll training models where HR is indicated show the physiological effect expected, and\nrefer to the oxygen transport system.\nIt is not necessary to stay strictly within the “target zone”, but to obtain maximum\ntraining effect these rules should be respected:\nTraining time in ” “target zone”:\nAnaerobic threshold:\nIndicated stroke rate\nThe stroke rate is closely connected to the heart rate, but has its own technical effect.\nClose to the regatta season, and inside the regatta season, it is important to train in\nthe “stroke rate area” where we are supposed to compete.\nThe single sculler and the eight will use different “stroke rate areas”, and in the\nprogramme the lowest number is an indication for the slow boats and the highest\nnumber for the fast boats. Weather conditions must be taken into consideration with\nslower rate in headwind and upstream.\nLightweight rowers should not use the “volume” or “maximum strength training”,\nbecause it will increase bodyweight and muscle volume. With the “top pyramid”,\nmaximum strength can be improved without weight gain.", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "one”:\nAnaerobic threshold:\nIndicated stroke rate\nThe stroke rate is closely connected to the heart rate, but has its own technical effect.\nClose to the regatta season, and inside the regatta season, it is important to train in\nthe “stroke rate area”"}
{"chunk_id": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English_p0012_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_18cfc6bbe1e5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "55d6e7f99cbe9f28", "text_span_hash": "55d6e7f99cbe9f28", "source_file": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "highest\nnumber for the fast boats. Weather conditions must be taken into consideration with\nslower rate in headwind and upstream.\nLightweight rowers should not use the “volume” or “maximum strength training”,\nbecause it will increase bodyweight and m", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "58 The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II\nHow to use the programmes?\nThe intensity is expressed in “heart rate” with an indicated “target zone”, based on\npercentage of “maximum heart rate”. Maximum heart rate is estimated as 220 minus\nage. In the programmes 200 HR is used as maximum and 180 HR as minimum.\nIndividual variations will occur frequently.\nAll training models where HR is indicated show the physiological effect expected, and\nrefer to the oxygen transport system.\nIt is not necessary to stay strictly within the “target zone”, but to obtain maximum\ntraining effect these rules should be respected:\nTraining time in ” “target zone”:\nAnaerobic threshold:\nIndicated stroke rate\nThe stroke rate is closely connected to the heart rate, but has its own technical effect.\nClose to the regatta season, and inside the regatta season, it is important to train in\nthe “stroke rate area” where we are supposed to compete.\nThe single sculler and the eight will use different “stroke rate areas”, and in the\nprogramme the lowest number is an indication for the slow boats and the highest\nnumber for the fast boats. Weather conditions must be taken into consideration with\nslower rate in headwind and upstream.\nLightweight rowers should not use the “volume” or “maximum strength training”,\nbecause it will increase bodyweight and muscle volume. With the “top pyramid”,\nmaximum strength can be improved without weight gain.", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "highest\nnumber for the fast boats. Weather conditions must be taken into consideration with\nslower rate in headwind and upstream.\nLightweight rowers should not use the “volume” or “maximum strength training”,\nbecause it will increase bodyweight and m"}
{"chunk_id": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English_p0013_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_18cfc6bbe1e5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5f0b32f450093a1b", "text_span_hash": "5f0b32f450093a1b", "source_file": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II 59\nJunior rowers should have passed puberty and have a settled body before they start\nwith heavy weight training. The best period to improve muscle volume and strength\nseems to be between 1", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II 59\nJunior rowers should have passed puberty and have a settled body before they start\nwith heavy weight training. The best period to improve muscle volume and strength\nseems to be between 18 and 23 years. For younger rowers their own body weight can\nbe used as load. Circuit training and endurance training are to be preferred.\nWomen can follow the same training principles as men. Their maximum strength is\nlower and muscle volume smaller, but their adaptation to endurance is as high as for\nmen. Some scientists insist that women recover faster from heavy endurance load\nBe careful with weight training, and take time to learn a good lifting technique.\nTo follow the programme completely from October to the end of August, you will need\napproximately 650 hours of effective training. Total numbers of kilometres on the water\nare of approximately 4,000.\nAn international elite rower will use up to 1,500 hours/ year and row between 7 and\n9,000km. Remember: less quantity needs more quality.\nReduction of the programme\nSchool and work problems might reduce the possibility to follow the programme and\nreduction will be needed. With reduction try to keep the endurance part, and give\npriority to the boat training.", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II 59\nJunior rowers should have passed puberty and have a settled body before they start\nwith heavy weight training. The best period to improve muscle volume and strength\nseems to be between 1"}
