{"chunk_id": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English_p0013_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_18cfc6bbe1e5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ad61ef1e66089cb2", "text_span_hash": "ad61ef1e66089cb2", "source_file": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "lems might reduce the possibility to follow the programme and\nreduction will be needed. With reduction try to keep the endurance part, and give\npriority to the boat training.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II 59\nJunior rowers should have passed puberty and have a settled body before they start\nwith heavy weight training. The best period to improve muscle volume and strength\nseems to be between 18 and 23 years. For younger rowers their own body weight can\nbe used as load. Circuit training and endurance training are to be preferred.\nWomen can follow the same training principles as men. Their maximum strength is\nlower and muscle volume smaller, but their adaptation to endurance is as high as for\nmen. Some scientists insist that women recover faster from heavy endurance load\nBe careful with weight training, and take time to learn a good lifting technique.\nTo follow the programme completely from October to the end of August, you will need\napproximately 650 hours of effective training. Total numbers of kilometres on the water\nare of approximately 4,000.\nAn international elite rower will use up to 1,500 hours/ year and row between 7 and\n9,000km. Remember: less quantity needs more quality.\nReduction of the programme\nSchool and work problems might reduce the possibility to follow the programme and\nreduction will be needed. With reduction try to keep the endurance part, and give\npriority to the boat training.", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "lems might reduce the possibility to follow the programme and\nreduction will be needed. With reduction try to keep the endurance part, and give\npriority to the boat training."}
{"chunk_id": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English_p0014_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_18cfc6bbe1e5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "bab1c36820fdccd9", "text_span_hash": "bab1c36820fdccd9", "source_file": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "page": 14, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "60 The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II\nTo avoid misunderstanding we will explain the following “terms” used in the\nAerobic training with metabolic balance. Energy covered 100% aerobic or with small\namount of anaerobic capacit", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "60 The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II\nTo avoid misunderstanding we will explain the following “terms” used in the\nAerobic training with metabolic balance. Energy covered 100% aerobic or with small\namount of anaerobic capacity involved, but without accumulated production of acid\n”LSD” Long Slow Distance\nTraining after the continuity principle to increase or maintain oxygen utilisation in the\nmuscle fibres recruited. Energycovered 100% aerobic.\nInterval principle: Training with periodic changes between exertion and rest, or between\nhigh and low workload (interval work).\nThe various types of interval training can be divided into two categories: short interval\ntraining and long interval training.\nShort interval involves work periods of up to two minutes and rests that are so short\nthat oxygen uptake and the pulse (in the rest) does not decrease appreciably before\nthe start of the next work period.\nLong interval involves work periods from two minutes and up to between 10 and 15\nminutes, and rest lengths such that work intensity can be maintained approximately\nconstant during each work period.\nThe short interval is very important during the regatta season to keep a good quantity\nof training in the right area of race velocity, and use of stroke rate valid for competition.\nTraining after the interval principle will increase or maintain the heart’s stroke volume\n(increased aerobic capacity).\nTraining with main", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "60 The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II\nTo avoid misunderstanding we will explain the following “terms” used in the\nAerobic training with metabolic balance. Energy covered 100% aerobic or with small\namount of anaerobic capacit"}
{"chunk_id": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English_p0014_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_18cfc6bbe1e5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f0918269f8d35ca3", "text_span_hash": "f0918269f8d35ca3", "source_file": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "page": 14, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ll\namount of anaerobic capacity involved, but without accumulated production of acid\n”LSD” Long Slow Distance\nTraining after the continuity principle to increase or maintain oxygen utilisation in the\nmuscle fibres recruited. Energycovered 100% aerobi", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "60 The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II\nTo avoid misunderstanding we will explain the following “terms” used in the\nAerobic training with metabolic balance. Energy covered 100% aerobic or with small\namount of anaerobic capacity involved, but without accumulated production of acid\n”LSD” Long Slow Distance\nTraining after the continuity principle to increase or maintain oxygen utilisation in the\nmuscle fibres recruited. Energycovered 100% aerobic.\nInterval principle: Training with periodic changes between exertion and rest, or between\nhigh and low workload (interval work).\nThe various types of interval training can be divided into two categories: short interval\ntraining and long interval training.\nShort interval involves work periods of up to two minutes and rests that are so short\nthat oxygen uptake and the pulse (in the rest) does not decrease appreciably before\nthe start of the next work period.\nLong interval involves work periods from two minutes and up to between 10 and 15\nminutes, and rest lengths such that work intensity can be maintained approximately\nconstant during each work period.\nThe short interval is very important during the regatta season to keep a good quantity\nof training in the right area of race velocity, and use of stroke rate valid for competition.\nTraining after the interval principle will increase or maintain the heart’s stroke volume\n(increased aerobic capacity).\nTraining with main", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ll\namount of anaerobic capacity involved, but without accumulated production of acid\n”LSD” Long Slow Distance\nTraining after the continuity principle to increase or maintain oxygen utilisation in the\nmuscle fibres recruited. Energycovered 100% aerobi"}
