ing each work period.\nThe short interval is very important during the regatta season to keep a good quantity\nof training in the right area of race velocity, and use of stroke rate valid for competition.\nTraining after the interval principle will increase or maintain the heart’s stroke volume\n(increased aerobic capacity).\nTraining with main", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ted. Energycovered 100% aerobic.\nInterval principle: Training with periodic changes between exertion and rest, or between\nhigh and low workload (interval work).\nThe various types of interval training can be divided into two categories: short interval"}
{"chunk_id": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English_p0014_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_18cfc6bbe1e5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "eb6b3c08fccc4c9e", "text_span_hash": "eb6b3c08fccc4c9e", "source_file": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "page": 14, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "two categories: short interval\ntraining and long interval training.\nShort interval involves work periods of up to two minutes and rests that are so short\nthat oxygen uptake and the pulse (in the rest) does not decrease appreciably before\nthe start of", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "60 The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II\nTo avoid misunderstanding we will explain the following “terms” used in the\nAerobic training with metabolic balance. Energy covered 100% aerobic or with small\namount of anaerobic capacity involved, but without accumulated production of acid\n”LSD” Long Slow Distance\nTraining after the continuity principle to increase or maintain oxygen utilisation in the\nmuscle fibres recruited. Energycovered 100% aerobic.\nInterval principle: Training with periodic changes between exertion and rest, or between\nhigh and low workload (interval work).\nThe various types of interval training can be divided into two categories: short interval\ntraining and long interval training.\nShort interval involves work periods of up to two minutes and rests that are so short\nthat oxygen uptake and the pulse (in the rest) does not decrease appreciably before\nthe start of the next work period.\nLong interval involves work periods from two minutes and up to between 10 and 15\nminutes, and rest lengths such that work intensity can be maintained approximately\nconstant during each work period.\nThe short interval is very important during the regatta season to keep a good quantity\nof training in the right area of race velocity, and use of stroke rate valid for competition.\nTraining after the interval principle will increase or maintain the heart’s stroke volume\n(increased aerobic capacity).\nTraining with main", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "two categories: short interval\ntraining and long interval training.\nShort interval involves work periods of up to two minutes and rests that are so short\nthat oxygen uptake and the pulse (in the rest) does not decrease appreciably before\nthe start of"}
{"chunk_id": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English_p0014_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_18cfc6bbe1e5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5073806fd685740b", "text_span_hash": "5073806fd685740b", "source_file": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "page": 14, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ppreciably before\nthe start of the next work period.\nLong interval involves work periods from two minutes and up to between 10 and 15\nminutes, and rest lengths such that work intensity can be maintained approximately\nconstant during each work period.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "60 The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II\nTo avoid misunderstanding we will explain the following “terms” used in the\nAerobic training with metabolic balance. Energy covered 100% aerobic or with small\namount of anaerobic capacity involved, but without accumulated production of acid\n”LSD” Long Slow Distance\nTraining after the continuity principle to increase or maintain oxygen utilisation in the\nmuscle fibres recruited. Energycovered 100% aerobic.\nInterval principle: Training with periodic changes between exertion and rest, or between\nhigh and low workload (interval work).\nThe various types of interval training can be divided into two categories: short interval\ntraining and long interval training.\nShort interval involves work periods of up to two minutes and rests that are so short\nthat oxygen uptake and the pulse (in the rest) does not decrease appreciably before\nthe start of the next work period.\nLong interval involves work periods from two minutes and up to between 10 and 15\nminutes, and rest lengths such that work intensity can be maintained approximately\nconstant during each work period.\nThe short interval is very important during the regatta season to keep a good quantity\nof training in the right area of race velocity, and use of stroke rate valid for competition.\nTraining after the interval principle will increase or maintain the heart’s stroke volume\n(increased aerobic capacity).\nTraining with main", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ppreciably before\nthe start of the next work period.\nLong interval involves work periods from two minutes and up to between 10 and 15\nminutes, and rest lengths such that work intensity can be maintained approximately\nconstant during each work period."}
