ng each work period.\nThe short interval is very important during the regatta season to keep a good quantity\nof training in the right area of race velocity, and use of stroke rate valid for competition.\nTraining after the interval principle will increase or maintain the heart’s stroke volume\n(increased aerobic capacity).\nTraining with main", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "opportunity to control and train the technique at different levels of\nTraining according to the interval principle, of relatively long duration (8-12km), with\nimprovised alteration between high and low intensity, and with the main purpose of\nincreas"}
{"chunk_id": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English_p0014_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_18cfc6bbe1e5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "6b04041ebac17c1e", "text_span_hash": "6b04041ebac17c1e", "source_file": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "page": 14, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "th the main purpose of\nincreasing or maintaining aerobic endurance. Gives a good opportunity to control the\ntechnique at different levels of intensity.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "60 The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II\nTo avoid misunderstanding we will explain the following “terms” used in the\nAerobic training with metabolic balance. Energy covered 100% aerobic or with small\namount of anaerobic capacity involved, but without accumulated production of acid\n”LSD” Long Slow Distance\nTraining after the continuity principle to increase or maintain oxygen utilisation in the\nmuscle fibres recruited. Energycovered 100% aerobic.\nInterval principle: Training with periodic changes between exertion and rest, or between\nhigh and low workload (interval work).\nThe various types of interval training can be divided into two categories: short interval\ntraining and long interval training.\nShort interval involves work periods of up to two minutes and rests that are so short\nthat oxygen uptake and the pulse (in the rest) does not decrease appreciably before\nthe start of the next work period.\nLong interval involves work periods from two minutes and up to between 10 and 15\nminutes, and rest lengths such that work intensity can be maintained approximately\nconstant during each work period.\nThe short interval is very important during the regatta season to keep a good quantity\nof training in the right area of race velocity, and use of stroke rate valid for competition.\nTraining after the interval principle will increase or maintain the heart’s stroke volume\n(increased aerobic capacity).\nTraining with main", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "th the main purpose of\nincreasing or maintaining aerobic endurance. Gives a good opportunity to control the\ntechnique at different levels of intensity."}
{"chunk_id": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English_p0015_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_18cfc6bbe1e5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "64677028b476d7e2", "text_span_hash": "64677028b476d7e2", "source_file": "Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Level2꞉Chapter4꞉BasicTrainingMethodology_English.pdf", "page": 15, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II 61\nTraining that simulates the race condition including warming up, start proceeding\nand tactic. Should be organised with other crews and made as close up to regatta\nconditions as possible.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II 61\nTraining that simulates the race condition including warming up, start proceeding\nand tactic. Should be organised with other crews and made as close up to regatta\nconditions as possible. (Used in connection with “supercompensation”.)\nThis term is used as a description of high intensity training in preparation for regattas\n(supercompensation principle.) It means mainly overproduction of speed (speed higher\nthan race speed.). This is the only specific anaerobic training in the programme. It also\nhas a technical element and overstimulates the muscular contraction velocity.\nTraining that stimulates race conditions, physiologically and mentally, about 60%\naerobic and 40% anaerobic with increased load. The model has also a good\nTraining with specific exercises to improve the rowing technique. The rowing stroke\ndivided into sections and trained separately followed by combined sections – up to", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "The FISA Coaching Development Programme: Handbook – Level II 61\nTraining that simulates the race condition including warming up, start proceeding\nand tactic. Should be organised with other crews and made as close up to regatta\nconditions as possible."}
