{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0001_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9620a78e51fe7a7e", "text_span_hash": "9620a78e51fe7a7e", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "icut; and\n2Pioneer Performance Center, Sacred Heart University, Fairﬁeld, Connecticut\nA B S T R A C T\nRunning gait retraining has been\nreported to reduce lower-extremity\nloads and the risk for some running-\nrelated injuries. This has provided\nevidenc", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Retraining on Running\nEconomy: A Review and\nPotential Applications\nMatthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2\n1Department of Physical Therapy & Human Movement Science, Sacred Heart University, Fairﬁeld, Connecticut; and\n2Pioneer Performance Center, Sacred Heart University, Fairﬁeld, Connecticut\nA B S T R A C T\nRunning gait retraining has been\nreported to reduce lower-extremity\nloads and the risk for some running-\nrelated injuries. This has provided\nevidence that distance running gait\ncan safely be modiﬁed in runners.\nOptimal running gait at a given\nspeed is the product of anthropo-\nmetric, neuromuscular, fatigue,\nand external factors. Because run-\nning biomechanics are integrally\nlinked with economy, a major\ndeterminant of endurance running\nperformance, it is conceivable that\ngait retraining could improve per-\nformance. The evidence investigat-\ning the inﬂuence of gait retraining on\nrunning economy will be evaluated\nand potential applications dis-\nver the past several years, gait\nretraining for the treatment of\nrunning-related injuries (RRI)\nhas become more widely accepted.\nSpeciﬁcally, gait retraining seems to\nbe a viable strategy for runners deal-\ning with RRI including patellofe-\nmoral pain (PFP) syndrome (94)\nmodes vary from simple (e.g., step\nrate manipulation) to complex (e.g.,\nwith strategies that can be clinic- or\nﬁeld-based (93). Numerous publica-\ntions have critically reviewed these\nseems to be mounting cli", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "icut; and\n2Pioneer Performance Center, Sacred Heart University, Fairﬁeld, Connecticut\nA B S T R A C T\nRunning gait retraining has been\nreported to reduce lower-extremity\nloads and the risk for some running-\nrelated injuries. This has provided\nevidenc"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0001_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2e839b3280842b34", "text_span_hash": "2e839b3280842b34", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "nce of gait retraining on\nrunning economy will be evaluated\nand potential applications dis-\nver the past several years, gait\nretraining for the treatment of\nrunning-related injuries (RRI)\nhas become more widely accepted.\nSpeciﬁcally, gait retraining", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Retraining on Running\nEconomy: A Review and\nPotential Applications\nMatthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2\n1Department of Physical Therapy & Human Movement Science, Sacred Heart University, Fairﬁeld, Connecticut; and\n2Pioneer Performance Center, Sacred Heart University, Fairﬁeld, Connecticut\nA B S T R A C T\nRunning gait retraining has been\nreported to reduce lower-extremity\nloads and the risk for some running-\nrelated injuries. This has provided\nevidence that distance running gait\ncan safely be modiﬁed in runners.\nOptimal running gait at a given\nspeed is the product of anthropo-\nmetric, neuromuscular, fatigue,\nand external factors. Because run-\nning biomechanics are integrally\nlinked with economy, a major\ndeterminant of endurance running\nperformance, it is conceivable that\ngait retraining could improve per-\nformance. The evidence investigat-\ning the inﬂuence of gait retraining on\nrunning economy will be evaluated\nand potential applications dis-\nver the past several years, gait\nretraining for the treatment of\nrunning-related injuries (RRI)\nhas become more widely accepted.\nSpeciﬁcally, gait retraining seems to\nbe a viable strategy for runners deal-\ning with RRI including patellofe-\nmoral pain (PFP) syndrome (94)\nmodes vary from simple (e.g., step\nrate manipulation) to complex (e.g.,\nwith strategies that can be clinic- or\nﬁeld-based (93). Numerous publica-\ntions have critically reviewed these\nseems to be mounting cli", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "nce of gait retraining on\nrunning economy will be evaluated\nand potential applications dis-\nver the past several years, gait\nretraining for the treatment of\nrunning-related injuries (RRI)\nhas become more widely accepted.\nSpeciﬁcally, gait retraining"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0001_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b855ae8385be0197", "text_span_hash": "b855ae8385be0197", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Speciﬁcally, gait retraining seems to\nbe a viable strategy for runners deal-\ning with RRI including patellofe-\nmoral pain (PFP) syndrome (94)\nmodes vary from simple (e.g., step\nrate manipulation) to complex (e.g.,\nwith strategies that can be clinic-", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Retraining on Running\nEconomy: A Review and\nPotential Applications\nMatthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2\n1Department of Physical Therapy & Human Movement Science, Sacred Heart University, Fairﬁeld, Connecticut; and\n2Pioneer Performance Center, Sacred Heart University, Fairﬁeld, Connecticut\nA B S T R A C T\nRunning gait retraining has been\nreported to reduce lower-extremity\nloads and the risk for some running-\nrelated injuries. This has provided\nevidence that distance running gait\ncan safely be modiﬁed in runners.\nOptimal running gait at a given\nspeed is the product of anthropo-\nmetric, neuromuscular, fatigue,\nand external factors. Because run-\nning biomechanics are integrally\nlinked with economy, a major\ndeterminant of endurance running\nperformance, it is conceivable that\ngait retraining could improve per-\nformance. The evidence investigat-\ning the inﬂuence of gait retraining on\nrunning economy will be evaluated\nand potential applications dis-\nver the past several years, gait\nretraining for the treatment of\nrunning-related injuries (RRI)\nhas become more widely accepted.\nSpeciﬁcally, gait retraining seems to\nbe a viable strategy for runners deal-\ning with RRI including patellofe-\nmoral pain (PFP) syndrome (94)\nmodes vary from simple (e.g., step\nrate manipulation) to complex (e.g.,\nwith strategies that can be clinic- or\nﬁeld-based (93). Numerous publica-\ntions have critically reviewed these\nseems to be mounting cli", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Speciﬁcally, gait retraining seems to\nbe a viable strategy for runners deal-\ning with RRI including patellofe-\nmoral pain (PFP) syndrome (94)\nmodes vary from simple (e.g., step\nrate manipulation) to complex (e.g.,\nwith strategies that can be clinic-"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0001_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e938922495273d07", "text_span_hash": "e938922495273d07", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "strategies that can be clinic- or\nﬁeld-based (93). Numerous publica-\ntions have critically reviewed these\nseems to be mounting clinical accep-\ntance that neuromuscular patterns\ncan be changed in a short amount\nthe long-term adoption of running\ngait c", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Retraining on Running\nEconomy: A Review and\nPotential Applications\nMatthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2\n1Department of Physical Therapy & Human Movement Science, Sacred Heart University, Fairﬁeld, Connecticut; and\n2Pioneer Performance Center, Sacred Heart University, Fairﬁeld, Connecticut\nA B S T R A C T\nRunning gait retraining has been\nreported to reduce lower-extremity\nloads and the risk for some running-\nrelated injuries. This has provided\nevidence that distance running gait\ncan safely be modiﬁed in runners.\nOptimal running gait at a given\nspeed is the product of anthropo-\nmetric, neuromuscular, fatigue,\nand external factors. Because run-\nning biomechanics are integrally\nlinked with economy, a major\ndeterminant of endurance running\nperformance, it is conceivable that\ngait retraining could improve per-\nformance. The evidence investigat-\ning the inﬂuence of gait retraining on\nrunning economy will be evaluated\nand potential applications dis-\nver the past several years, gait\nretraining for the treatment of\nrunning-related injuries (RRI)\nhas become more widely accepted.\nSpeciﬁcally, gait retraining seems to\nbe a viable strategy for runners deal-\ning with RRI including patellofe-\nmoral pain (PFP) syndrome (94)\nmodes vary from simple (e.g., step\nrate manipulation) to complex (e.g.,\nwith strategies that can be clinic- or\nﬁeld-based (93). Numerous publica-\ntions have critically reviewed these\nseems to be mounting cli", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "strategies that can be clinic- or\nﬁeld-based (93). Numerous publica-\ntions have critically reviewed these\nseems to be mounting clinical accep-\ntance that neuromuscular patterns\ncan be changed in a short amount\nthe long-term adoption of running\ngait c"}
