re widely accepted.\nSpeciﬁcally, gait retraining seems to\nbe a viable strategy for runners deal-\ning with RRI including patellofe-\nmoral pain (PFP) syndrome (94)\nmodes vary from simple (e.g., step\nrate manipulation) to complex (e.g.,\nwith strategies that can be clinic- or\nﬁeld-based (93). Numerous publica-\ntions have critically reviewed these\nseems to be mounting cli", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "indicating that it is a key\ncomponent in predicting endurance\nrunning performance (24). RE is\ndeﬁned as the energy required to\nsubmaximally run a given distance\n(77) and is assessed by measuring\nthe steady-state oxygen consump-\ntion at a given runni"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0001_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "398793f8cc3cc16a", "text_span_hash": "398793f8cc3cc16a", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "consump-\ntion at a given running velocity.\nImproving RE would likely lead to\nimprovements in race performance\n(26,77). Runners can be more or less\neconomical depending on velocity,\nwith both physiological and biome-\nchanical factors contributing to R", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Retraining on Running\nEconomy: A Review and\nPotential Applications\nMatthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2\n1Department of Physical Therapy & Human Movement Science, Sacred Heart University, Fairﬁeld, Connecticut; and\n2Pioneer Performance Center, Sacred Heart University, Fairﬁeld, Connecticut\nA B S T R A C T\nRunning gait retraining has been\nreported to reduce lower-extremity\nloads and the risk for some running-\nrelated injuries. This has provided\nevidence that distance running gait\ncan safely be modiﬁed in runners.\nOptimal running gait at a given\nspeed is the product of anthropo-\nmetric, neuromuscular, fatigue,\nand external factors. Because run-\nning biomechanics are integrally\nlinked with economy, a major\ndeterminant of endurance running\nperformance, it is conceivable that\ngait retraining could improve per-\nformance. The evidence investigat-\ning the inﬂuence of gait retraining on\nrunning economy will be evaluated\nand potential applications dis-\nver the past several years, gait\nretraining for the treatment of\nrunning-related injuries (RRI)\nhas become more widely accepted.\nSpeciﬁcally, gait retraining seems to\nbe a viable strategy for runners deal-\ning with RRI including patellofe-\nmoral pain (PFP) syndrome (94)\nmodes vary from simple (e.g., step\nrate manipulation) to complex (e.g.,\nwith strategies that can be clinic- or\nﬁeld-based (93). Numerous publica-\ntions have critically reviewed these\nseems to be mounting cli", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "consump-\ntion at a given running velocity.\nImproving RE would likely lead to\nimprovements in race performance\n(26,77). Runners can be more or less\neconomical depending on velocity,\nwith both physiological and biome-\nchanical factors contributing to R"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0001_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8ee2326716d0f589", "text_span_hash": "8ee2326716d0f589", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "gait\nAddress correspondence to Dr. Matthew F.\nMoran, moranm@sacredheart.edu.\nK E Y W O R D S :\nrunning form; technique; performance; foot\nstrike; stride rate; form manipulation; form\nretraining; vertical oscillation; ground con-\nVOLUME 42 | NUMBER 1", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Retraining on Running\nEconomy: A Review and\nPotential Applications\nMatthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2\n1Department of Physical Therapy & Human Movement Science, Sacred Heart University, Fairﬁeld, Connecticut; and\n2Pioneer Performance Center, Sacred Heart University, Fairﬁeld, Connecticut\nA B S T R A C T\nRunning gait retraining has been\nreported to reduce lower-extremity\nloads and the risk for some running-\nrelated injuries. This has provided\nevidence that distance running gait\ncan safely be modiﬁed in runners.\nOptimal running gait at a given\nspeed is the product of anthropo-\nmetric, neuromuscular, fatigue,\nand external factors. Because run-\nning biomechanics are integrally\nlinked with economy, a major\ndeterminant of endurance running\nperformance, it is conceivable that\ngait retraining could improve per-\nformance. The evidence investigat-\ning the inﬂuence of gait retraining on\nrunning economy will be evaluated\nand potential applications dis-\nver the past several years, gait\nretraining for the treatment of\nrunning-related injuries (RRI)\nhas become more widely accepted.\nSpeciﬁcally, gait retraining seems to\nbe a viable strategy for runners deal-\ning with RRI including patellofe-\nmoral pain (PFP) syndrome (94)\nmodes vary from simple (e.g., step\nrate manipulation) to complex (e.g.,\nwith strategies that can be clinic- or\nﬁeld-based (93). Numerous publica-\ntions have critically reviewed these\nseems to be mounting cli", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "gait\nAddress correspondence to Dr. Matthew F.\nMoran, moranm@sacredheart.edu.\nK E Y W O R D S :\nrunning form; technique; performance; foot\nstrike; stride rate; form manipulation; form\nretraining; vertical oscillation; ground con-\nVOLUME 42 | NUMBER 1"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0002_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8c25891a65f87435", "text_span_hash": "8c25891a65f87435", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "the runner, factors\nsuch as environmental effects (e.g.,\nground surface) can inﬂuence a runner’s\ngait and RE. Finally, as a runner pro-\ngresses through a range of speeds, their\nrunning gait will adjust (68,71) and\ntheir RE can change (71,73). In a re", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "DETERMINANTS OF RUNNING\nA runner’s gait is biomechanically mea-\nsured in terms of its timing (spatiotempo-\n(kinetics) (Table 1). Ultimately, the adop-\nted gait pattern is the product of the\nrunner’s (a) anthropometry, (b) neuro-\nmuscular control, (c) fatigue state, (d)\nexternal factors, and (e) speed (Figure 1).\nAnthropometrically, the lower-extremity\nsegments have intrinsic properties (e.g.,\nlength, mass, mass distribution, and\nmusculotendon unit stiffness), which\ndeﬁne the passive dynamic character-\nistics and inﬂuence the preferred run-\nning pattern (11). In addition, soft\ntissue ﬂexibility and strength will inﬂu-\nence joint motion throughout the run-\nning cycle. As runners fatigue, their gait\nadjusts with subsequent changes in\nspatiotemporal parameters, kinematics,\nand kinetics (28,51,59). As a result, RE\nmay change throughout a fatiguing run\n(35,48). External to the runner, factors\nsuch as environmental effects (e.g.,\nground surface) can inﬂuence a runner’s\ngait and RE. Finally, as a runner pro-\ngresses through a range of speeds, their\nrunning gait will adjust (68,71) and\ntheir RE can change (71,73). In a recent\nstudy of 20 male recreational runners\ntested across 3 speeds (65, 75, and 85%\nvelocity of maximal aerobic capacity),\n25% were most economical at the\nslowest speed, 20% at the middle\nspeed, and 55% at the fastest speed\ntested (71). This indicates that runners\nwill be more or less economical de-\npending on what pace", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "the runner, factors\nsuch as environmental effects (e.g.,\nground surface) can inﬂuence a runner’s\ngait and RE. Finally, as a runner pro-\ngresses through a range of speeds, their\nrunning gait will adjust (68,71) and\ntheir RE can change (71,73). In a re"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0002_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7144c83fc0c29c4d", "text_span_hash": "7144c83fc0c29c4d", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "RE can change (71,73). In a recent\nstudy of 20 male recreational runners\ntested across 3 speeds (65, 75, and 85%\nvelocity of maximal aerobic capacity),\n25% were most economical at the\nslowest speed, 20% at the middle\nspeed, and 55% at the fastest spe", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "DETERMINANTS OF RUNNING\nA runner’s gait is biomechanically mea-\nsured in terms of its timing (spatiotempo-\n(kinetics) (Table 1). Ultimately, the adop-\nted gait pattern is the product of the\nrunner’s (a) anthropometry, (b) neuro-\nmuscular control, (c) fatigue state, (d)\nexternal factors, and (e) speed (Figure 1).\nAnthropometrically, the lower-extremity\nsegments have intrinsic properties (e.g.,\nlength, mass, mass distribution, and\nmusculotendon unit stiffness), which\ndeﬁne the passive dynamic character-\nistics and inﬂuence the preferred run-\nning pattern (11). In addition, soft\ntissue ﬂexibility and strength will inﬂu-\nence joint motion throughout the run-\nning cycle. As runners fatigue, their gait\nadjusts with subsequent changes in\nspatiotemporal parameters, kinematics,\nand kinetics (28,51,59). As a result, RE\nmay change throughout a fatiguing run\n(35,48). External to the runner, factors\nsuch as environmental effects (e.g.,\nground surface) can inﬂuence a runner’s\ngait and RE. Finally, as a runner pro-\ngresses through a range of speeds, their\nrunning gait will adjust (68,71) and\ntheir RE can change (71,73). In a recent\nstudy of 20 male recreational runners\ntested across 3 speeds (65, 75, and 85%\nvelocity of maximal aerobic capacity),\n25% were most economical at the\nslowest speed, 20% at the middle\nspeed, and 55% at the fastest speed\ntested (71). This indicates that runners\nwill be more or less economical de-\npending on what pace", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "RE can change (71,73). In a recent\nstudy of 20 male recreational runners\ntested across 3 speeds (65, 75, and 85%\nvelocity of maximal aerobic capacity),\n25% were most economical at the\nslowest speed, 20% at the middle\nspeed, and 55% at the fastest spe"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0002_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "25c8f40303b2fd24", "text_span_hash": "25c8f40303b2fd24", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "s likely\nstrength, ﬂexibility, and other factors\n(e.g., running experience) between indi-\nviduals. In addition, multiple gait patterns\ncan produce a similar RE, indicating that\nthere is not a universal most economical\ngait at submaximal speeds (53).", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "DETERMINANTS OF RUNNING\nA runner’s gait is biomechanically mea-\nsured in terms of its timing (spatiotempo-\n(kinetics) (Table 1). Ultimately, the adop-\nted gait pattern is the product of the\nrunner’s (a) anthropometry, (b) neuro-\nmuscular control, (c) fatigue state, (d)\nexternal factors, and (e) speed (Figure 1).\nAnthropometrically, the lower-extremity\nsegments have intrinsic properties (e.g.,\nlength, mass, mass distribution, and\nmusculotendon unit stiffness), which\ndeﬁne the passive dynamic character-\nistics and inﬂuence the preferred run-\nning pattern (11). In addition, soft\ntissue ﬂexibility and strength will inﬂu-\nence joint motion throughout the run-\nning cycle. As runners fatigue, their gait\nadjusts with subsequent changes in\nspatiotemporal parameters, kinematics,\nand kinetics (28,51,59). As a result, RE\nmay change throughout a fatiguing run\n(35,48). External to the runner, factors\nsuch as environmental effects (e.g.,\nground surface) can inﬂuence a runner’s\ngait and RE. Finally, as a runner pro-\ngresses through a range of speeds, their\nrunning gait will adjust (68,71) and\ntheir RE can change (71,73). In a recent\nstudy of 20 male recreational runners\ntested across 3 speeds (65, 75, and 85%\nvelocity of maximal aerobic capacity),\n25% were most economical at the\nslowest speed, 20% at the middle\nspeed, and 55% at the fastest speed\ntested (71). This indicates that runners\nwill be more or less economical de-\npending on what pace", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "s likely\nstrength, ﬂexibility, and other factors\n(e.g., running experience) between indi-\nviduals. In addition, multiple gait patterns\ncan produce a similar RE, indicating that\nthere is not a universal most economical\ngait at submaximal speeds (53)."}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0002_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5b45d55ae2a3ea9b", "text_span_hash": "5b45d55ae2a3ea9b", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "it at submaximal speeds (53). In a study\nof 58 runners, Lussiana et al. (53) found\nsimilar RE values across 2 groups, with\nsome runners using a greater reliance on\nelastic energy return (shorter ground con-\ntact time [GCT]), and other runners\nlimitin", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "DETERMINANTS OF RUNNING\nA runner’s gait is biomechanically mea-\nsured in terms of its timing (spatiotempo-\n(kinetics) (Table 1). Ultimately, the adop-\nted gait pattern is the product of the\nrunner’s (a) anthropometry, (b) neuro-\nmuscular control, (c) fatigue state, (d)\nexternal factors, and (e) speed (Figure 1).\nAnthropometrically, the lower-extremity\nsegments have intrinsic properties (e.g.,\nlength, mass, mass distribution, and\nmusculotendon unit stiffness), which\ndeﬁne the passive dynamic character-\nistics and inﬂuence the preferred run-\nning pattern (11). In addition, soft\ntissue ﬂexibility and strength will inﬂu-\nence joint motion throughout the run-\nning cycle. As runners fatigue, their gait\nadjusts with subsequent changes in\nspatiotemporal parameters, kinematics,\nand kinetics (28,51,59). As a result, RE\nmay change throughout a fatiguing run\n(35,48). External to the runner, factors\nsuch as environmental effects (e.g.,\nground surface) can inﬂuence a runner’s\ngait and RE. Finally, as a runner pro-\ngresses through a range of speeds, their\nrunning gait will adjust (68,71) and\ntheir RE can change (71,73). In a recent\nstudy of 20 male recreational runners\ntested across 3 speeds (65, 75, and 85%\nvelocity of maximal aerobic capacity),\n25% were most economical at the\nslowest speed, 20% at the middle\nspeed, and 55% at the fastest speed\ntested (71). This indicates that runners\nwill be more or less economical de-\npending on what pace", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "it at submaximal speeds (53). In a study\nof 58 runners, Lussiana et al. (53) found\nsimilar RE values across 2 groups, with\nsome runners using a greater reliance on\nelastic energy return (shorter ground con-\ntact time [GCT]), and other runners\nlimitin"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0002_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "649f5bb1ac8a6b09", "text_span_hash": "649f5bb1ac8a6b09", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "T]), and other runners\nlimiting the upward translation of the cen-\nter of mass (COM) (longer GCT). This\ninvestigation highlights the individuality\nRUNNING GAIT RETRAINING\nRunning gait retraining seeks to create\na new neuromuscular “solution” for an\ns", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "DETERMINANTS OF RUNNING\nA runner’s gait is biomechanically mea-\nsured in terms of its timing (spatiotempo-\n(kinetics) (Table 1). Ultimately, the adop-\nted gait pattern is the product of the\nrunner’s (a) anthropometry, (b) neuro-\nmuscular control, (c) fatigue state, (d)\nexternal factors, and (e) speed (Figure 1).\nAnthropometrically, the lower-extremity\nsegments have intrinsic properties (e.g.,\nlength, mass, mass distribution, and\nmusculotendon unit stiffness), which\ndeﬁne the passive dynamic character-\nistics and inﬂuence the preferred run-\nning pattern (11). In addition, soft\ntissue ﬂexibility and strength will inﬂu-\nence joint motion throughout the run-\nning cycle. As runners fatigue, their gait\nadjusts with subsequent changes in\nspatiotemporal parameters, kinematics,\nand kinetics (28,51,59). As a result, RE\nmay change throughout a fatiguing run\n(35,48). External to the runner, factors\nsuch as environmental effects (e.g.,\nground surface) can inﬂuence a runner’s\ngait and RE. Finally, as a runner pro-\ngresses through a range of speeds, their\nrunning gait will adjust (68,71) and\ntheir RE can change (71,73). In a recent\nstudy of 20 male recreational runners\ntested across 3 speeds (65, 75, and 85%\nvelocity of maximal aerobic capacity),\n25% were most economical at the\nslowest speed, 20% at the middle\nspeed, and 55% at the fastest speed\ntested (71). This indicates that runners\nwill be more or less economical de-\npending on what pace", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "T]), and other runners\nlimiting the upward translation of the cen-\nter of mass (COM) (longer GCT). This\ninvestigation highlights the individuality\nRUNNING GAIT RETRAINING\nRunning gait retraining seeks to create\na new neuromuscular “solution” for an\ns"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0002_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b4f483eab6902926", "text_span_hash": "b4f483eab6902926", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "romuscular “solution” for an\nstate, external factors (e.g., footwear),\nAlthough most of the running gait re-\ntraining interventions target RRI (8),\nseveral have evaluated its effect on\nRE in healthy runners through pre-\npost intervention RE measureme", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "DETERMINANTS OF RUNNING\nA runner’s gait is biomechanically mea-\nsured in terms of its timing (spatiotempo-\n(kinetics) (Table 1). Ultimately, the adop-\nted gait pattern is the product of the\nrunner’s (a) anthropometry, (b) neuro-\nmuscular control, (c) fatigue state, (d)\nexternal factors, and (e) speed (Figure 1).\nAnthropometrically, the lower-extremity\nsegments have intrinsic properties (e.g.,\nlength, mass, mass distribution, and\nmusculotendon unit stiffness), which\ndeﬁne the passive dynamic character-\nistics and inﬂuence the preferred run-\nning pattern (11). In addition, soft\ntissue ﬂexibility and strength will inﬂu-\nence joint motion throughout the run-\nning cycle. As runners fatigue, their gait\nadjusts with subsequent changes in\nspatiotemporal parameters, kinematics,\nand kinetics (28,51,59). As a result, RE\nmay change throughout a fatiguing run\n(35,48). External to the runner, factors\nsuch as environmental effects (e.g.,\nground surface) can inﬂuence a runner’s\ngait and RE. Finally, as a runner pro-\ngresses through a range of speeds, their\nrunning gait will adjust (68,71) and\ntheir RE can change (71,73). In a recent\nstudy of 20 male recreational runners\ntested across 3 speeds (65, 75, and 85%\nvelocity of maximal aerobic capacity),\n25% were most economical at the\nslowest speed, 20% at the middle\nspeed, and 55% at the fastest speed\ntested (71). This indicates that runners\nwill be more or less economical de-\npending on what pace", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "romuscular “solution” for an\nstate, external factors (e.g., footwear),\nAlthough most of the running gait re-\ntraining interventions target RRI (8),\nseveral have evaluated its effect on\nRE in healthy runners through pre-\npost intervention RE measureme"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0002_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ee154173c18608e4", "text_span_hash": "ee154173c18608e4", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "post intervention RE measurements\n(Tables 3 and 4). In general, these stud-\nies had small sample sizes (n 5 6–28)\nand varied both the implementation\n(e.g., verbal and visual) and duration\nINFLUENCE ON RUNNING\nEvidence to support the efﬁcacy of run-\nn", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "DETERMINANTS OF RUNNING\nA runner’s gait is biomechanically mea-\nsured in terms of its timing (spatiotempo-\n(kinetics) (Table 1). Ultimately, the adop-\nted gait pattern is the product of the\nrunner’s (a) anthropometry, (b) neuro-\nmuscular control, (c) fatigue state, (d)\nexternal factors, and (e) speed (Figure 1).\nAnthropometrically, the lower-extremity\nsegments have intrinsic properties (e.g.,\nlength, mass, mass distribution, and\nmusculotendon unit stiffness), which\ndeﬁne the passive dynamic character-\nistics and inﬂuence the preferred run-\nning pattern (11). In addition, soft\ntissue ﬂexibility and strength will inﬂu-\nence joint motion throughout the run-\nning cycle. As runners fatigue, their gait\nadjusts with subsequent changes in\nspatiotemporal parameters, kinematics,\nand kinetics (28,51,59). As a result, RE\nmay change throughout a fatiguing run\n(35,48). External to the runner, factors\nsuch as environmental effects (e.g.,\nground surface) can inﬂuence a runner’s\ngait and RE. Finally, as a runner pro-\ngresses through a range of speeds, their\nrunning gait will adjust (68,71) and\ntheir RE can change (71,73). In a recent\nstudy of 20 male recreational runners\ntested across 3 speeds (65, 75, and 85%\nvelocity of maximal aerobic capacity),\n25% were most economical at the\nslowest speed, 20% at the middle\nspeed, and 55% at the fastest speed\ntested (71). This indicates that runners\nwill be more or less economical de-\npending on what pace", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "post intervention RE measurements\n(Tables 3 and 4). In general, these stud-\nies had small sample sizes (n 5 6–28)\nand varied both the implementation\n(e.g., verbal and visual) and duration\nINFLUENCE ON RUNNING\nEvidence to support the efﬁcacy of run-\nn"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0002_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "fd1024b08eda2421", "text_span_hash": "fd1024b08eda2421", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "support the efﬁcacy of run-\nning gait retraining to improve RE is\nlacking. Of the running gait retraining\ninvestigations that have measured RE\nMorgan et al. (63) demonstrated that\nstep length could be decreased over 3\nweeks to improve RE, the study", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "DETERMINANTS OF RUNNING\nA runner’s gait is biomechanically mea-\nsured in terms of its timing (spatiotempo-\n(kinetics) (Table 1). Ultimately, the adop-\nted gait pattern is the product of the\nrunner’s (a) anthropometry, (b) neuro-\nmuscular control, (c) fatigue state, (d)\nexternal factors, and (e) speed (Figure 1).\nAnthropometrically, the lower-extremity\nsegments have intrinsic properties (e.g.,\nlength, mass, mass distribution, and\nmusculotendon unit stiffness), which\ndeﬁne the passive dynamic character-\nistics and inﬂuence the preferred run-\nning pattern (11). In addition, soft\ntissue ﬂexibility and strength will inﬂu-\nence joint motion throughout the run-\nning cycle. As runners fatigue, their gait\nadjusts with subsequent changes in\nspatiotemporal parameters, kinematics,\nand kinetics (28,51,59). As a result, RE\nmay change throughout a fatiguing run\n(35,48). External to the runner, factors\nsuch as environmental effects (e.g.,\nground surface) can inﬂuence a runner’s\ngait and RE. Finally, as a runner pro-\ngresses through a range of speeds, their\nrunning gait will adjust (68,71) and\ntheir RE can change (71,73). In a recent\nstudy of 20 male recreational runners\ntested across 3 speeds (65, 75, and 85%\nvelocity of maximal aerobic capacity),\n25% were most economical at the\nslowest speed, 20% at the middle\nspeed, and 55% at the fastest speed\ntested (71). This indicates that runners\nwill be more or less economical de-\npending on what pace", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "support the efﬁcacy of run-\nning gait retraining to improve RE is\nlacking. Of the running gait retraining\ninvestigations that have measured RE\nMorgan et al. (63) demonstrated that\nstep length could be decreased over 3\nweeks to improve RE, the study"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0002_c0017", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "cb7e39bec2030c62", "text_span_hash": "cb7e39bec2030c62", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 17, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "t retraining speciﬁcally seeks to change running form from the\npreferred/habitual running form by altering the neuromuscular component. Red arrows indicate factors that are not\nmanipulated during a gait retraining program.\nStrength and Conditioning J", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "DETERMINANTS OF RUNNING\nA runner’s gait is biomechanically mea-\nsured in terms of its timing (spatiotempo-\n(kinetics) (Table 1). Ultimately, the adop-\nted gait pattern is the product of the\nrunner’s (a) anthropometry, (b) neuro-\nmuscular control, (c) fatigue state, (d)\nexternal factors, and (e) speed (Figure 1).\nAnthropometrically, the lower-extremity\nsegments have intrinsic properties (e.g.,\nlength, mass, mass distribution, and\nmusculotendon unit stiffness), which\ndeﬁne the passive dynamic character-\nistics and inﬂuence the preferred run-\nning pattern (11). In addition, soft\ntissue ﬂexibility and strength will inﬂu-\nence joint motion throughout the run-\nning cycle. As runners fatigue, their gait\nadjusts with subsequent changes in\nspatiotemporal parameters, kinematics,\nand kinetics (28,51,59). As a result, RE\nmay change throughout a fatiguing run\n(35,48). External to the runner, factors\nsuch as environmental effects (e.g.,\nground surface) can inﬂuence a runner’s\ngait and RE. Finally, as a runner pro-\ngresses through a range of speeds, their\nrunning gait will adjust (68,71) and\ntheir RE can change (71,73). In a recent\nstudy of 20 male recreational runners\ntested across 3 speeds (65, 75, and 85%\nvelocity of maximal aerobic capacity),\n25% were most economical at the\nslowest speed, 20% at the middle\nspeed, and 55% at the fastest speed\ntested (71). This indicates that runners\nwill be more or less economical de-\npending on what pace", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "t retraining speciﬁcally seeks to change running form from the\npreferred/habitual running form by altering the neuromuscular component. Red arrows indicate factors that are not\nmanipulated during a gait retraining program.\nStrength and Conditioning J"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0003_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "60c1881a7b5cb4f8", "text_span_hash": "60c1881a7b5cb4f8", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "limited to 9 runners who initially dem-\nlengths (overstriding). No other inter-\nventions were effective at improving\nRE and no studies have reported a long\nterm follow-up. The longest interven-\ntion was 12 weeks and it resulted in\na signiﬁcantly impa", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "limited to 9 runners who initially dem-\nlengths (overstriding). No other inter-\nventions were effective at improving\nRE and no studies have reported a long\nterm follow-up. The longest interven-\ntion was 12 weeks and it resulted in\na signiﬁcantly impaired RE in 8 triath-\nletes trained with the Pose method (23).\nAlthough many reports highlight the\nefﬁcacy of running gait retraining in\nthe treatment and prevention of RRIs,\nrelatively few have also measured RE\npre-post intervention (16,74,85). A gait\ninjury risk without a deleterious effect\non RE would be beneﬁcial to perfor-\nmance by mitigating training interrup-\ntions. In a group of 22 male runners,\nClansey et al. (16) retrained 12 recrea-\ntional runners to minimize TA and\nreported a reduction of impact load\nwithout a signiﬁcant negative effect\non RE. Similarly, Roper et al. (74) re-\nported that retraining recreational run-\nners with PFP to use a forefoot strike\ndid not signiﬁcantly reduce RE. By\ncontrast, Townsend et al. (85) reported\nthat RE was signiﬁcantly impaired\nwhen 12 healthy runners performed\na single gait retraining session to lower\npeak TA. From these reports, RE may\nbe unchanged or compromised when\ninitially altering running gait. When RE\nis compromised initially, continued\npractice could return RE to pretraining\nlevels, but long term follow-up reports\nare needed to evaluate this.\nIt could be argued that a runner self-\noptimizes gait and becomes more eco-\nnomical th", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "limited to 9 runners who initially dem-\nlengths (overstriding). No other inter-\nventions were effective at improving\nRE and no studies have reported a long\nterm follow-up. The longest interven-\ntion was 12 weeks and it resulted in\na signiﬁcantly impa"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0003_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "49deb9f781734b2c", "text_span_hash": "49deb9f781734b2c", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "esulted in\na signiﬁcantly impaired RE in 8 triath-\nletes trained with the Pose method (23).\nAlthough many reports highlight the\nefﬁcacy of running gait retraining in\nthe treatment and prevention of RRIs,\nrelatively few have also measured RE\npre-post", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "limited to 9 runners who initially dem-\nlengths (overstriding). No other inter-\nventions were effective at improving\nRE and no studies have reported a long\nterm follow-up. The longest interven-\ntion was 12 weeks and it resulted in\na signiﬁcantly impaired RE in 8 triath-\nletes trained with the Pose method (23).\nAlthough many reports highlight the\nefﬁcacy of running gait retraining in\nthe treatment and prevention of RRIs,\nrelatively few have also measured RE\npre-post intervention (16,74,85). A gait\ninjury risk without a deleterious effect\non RE would be beneﬁcial to perfor-\nmance by mitigating training interrup-\ntions. In a group of 22 male runners,\nClansey et al. (16) retrained 12 recrea-\ntional runners to minimize TA and\nreported a reduction of impact load\nwithout a signiﬁcant negative effect\non RE. Similarly, Roper et al. (74) re-\nported that retraining recreational run-\nners with PFP to use a forefoot strike\ndid not signiﬁcantly reduce RE. By\ncontrast, Townsend et al. (85) reported\nthat RE was signiﬁcantly impaired\nwhen 12 healthy runners performed\na single gait retraining session to lower\npeak TA. From these reports, RE may\nbe unchanged or compromised when\ninitially altering running gait. When RE\nis compromised initially, continued\npractice could return RE to pretraining\nlevels, but long term follow-up reports\nare needed to evaluate this.\nIt could be argued that a runner self-\noptimizes gait and becomes more eco-\nnomical th", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "esulted in\na signiﬁcantly impaired RE in 8 triath-\nletes trained with the Pose method (23).\nAlthough many reports highlight the\nefﬁcacy of running gait retraining in\nthe treatment and prevention of RRIs,\nrelatively few have also measured RE\npre-post"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0003_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "eb72580efe625ace", "text_span_hash": "eb72580efe625ace", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "sey et al. (16) retrained 12 recrea-\ntional runners to minimize TA and\nreported a reduction of impact load\nwithout a signiﬁcant negative effect\non RE. Similarly, Roper et al. (74) re-\nported that retraining recreational run-\nners with PFP to use a fo", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "limited to 9 runners who initially dem-\nlengths (overstriding). No other inter-\nventions were effective at improving\nRE and no studies have reported a long\nterm follow-up. The longest interven-\ntion was 12 weeks and it resulted in\na signiﬁcantly impaired RE in 8 triath-\nletes trained with the Pose method (23).\nAlthough many reports highlight the\nefﬁcacy of running gait retraining in\nthe treatment and prevention of RRIs,\nrelatively few have also measured RE\npre-post intervention (16,74,85). A gait\ninjury risk without a deleterious effect\non RE would be beneﬁcial to perfor-\nmance by mitigating training interrup-\ntions. In a group of 22 male runners,\nClansey et al. (16) retrained 12 recrea-\ntional runners to minimize TA and\nreported a reduction of impact load\nwithout a signiﬁcant negative effect\non RE. Similarly, Roper et al. (74) re-\nported that retraining recreational run-\nners with PFP to use a forefoot strike\ndid not signiﬁcantly reduce RE. By\ncontrast, Townsend et al. (85) reported\nthat RE was signiﬁcantly impaired\nwhen 12 healthy runners performed\na single gait retraining session to lower\npeak TA. From these reports, RE may\nbe unchanged or compromised when\ninitially altering running gait. When RE\nis compromised initially, continued\npractice could return RE to pretraining\nlevels, but long term follow-up reports\nare needed to evaluate this.\nIt could be argued that a runner self-\noptimizes gait and becomes more eco-\nnomical th", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "sey et al. (16) retrained 12 recrea-\ntional runners to minimize TA and\nreported a reduction of impact load\nwithout a signiﬁcant negative effect\non RE. Similarly, Roper et al. (74) re-\nported that retraining recreational run-\nners with PFP to use a fo"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0003_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "586d715458db918b", "text_span_hash": "586d715458db918b", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "run-\nners with PFP to use a forefoot strike\ndid not signiﬁcantly reduce RE. By\ncontrast, Townsend et al. (85) reported\nthat RE was signiﬁcantly impaired\nwhen 12 healthy runners performed\na single gait retraining session to lower\npeak TA. From these r", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "limited to 9 runners who initially dem-\nlengths (overstriding). No other inter-\nventions were effective at improving\nRE and no studies have reported a long\nterm follow-up. The longest interven-\ntion was 12 weeks and it resulted in\na signiﬁcantly impaired RE in 8 triath-\nletes trained with the Pose method (23).\nAlthough many reports highlight the\nefﬁcacy of running gait retraining in\nthe treatment and prevention of RRIs,\nrelatively few have also measured RE\npre-post intervention (16,74,85). A gait\ninjury risk without a deleterious effect\non RE would be beneﬁcial to perfor-\nmance by mitigating training interrup-\ntions. In a group of 22 male runners,\nClansey et al. (16) retrained 12 recrea-\ntional runners to minimize TA and\nreported a reduction of impact load\nwithout a signiﬁcant negative effect\non RE. Similarly, Roper et al. (74) re-\nported that retraining recreational run-\nners with PFP to use a forefoot strike\ndid not signiﬁcantly reduce RE. By\ncontrast, Townsend et al. (85) reported\nthat RE was signiﬁcantly impaired\nwhen 12 healthy runners performed\na single gait retraining session to lower\npeak TA. From these reports, RE may\nbe unchanged or compromised when\ninitially altering running gait. When RE\nis compromised initially, continued\npractice could return RE to pretraining\nlevels, but long term follow-up reports\nare needed to evaluate this.\nIt could be argued that a runner self-\noptimizes gait and becomes more eco-\nnomical th", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "run-\nners with PFP to use a forefoot strike\ndid not signiﬁcantly reduce RE. By\ncontrast, Townsend et al. (85) reported\nthat RE was signiﬁcantly impaired\nwhen 12 healthy runners performed\na single gait retraining session to lower\npeak TA. From these r"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0003_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5dfa56fc11572bd5", "text_span_hash": "5dfa56fc11572bd5", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "to lower\npeak TA. From these reports, RE may\nbe unchanged or compromised when\ninitially altering running gait. When RE\nis compromised initially, continued\npractice could return RE to pretraining\nlevels, but long term follow-up reports\nare needed to e", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "limited to 9 runners who initially dem-\nlengths (overstriding). No other inter-\nventions were effective at improving\nRE and no studies have reported a long\nterm follow-up. The longest interven-\ntion was 12 weeks and it resulted in\na signiﬁcantly impaired RE in 8 triath-\nletes trained with the Pose method (23).\nAlthough many reports highlight the\nefﬁcacy of running gait retraining in\nthe treatment and prevention of RRIs,\nrelatively few have also measured RE\npre-post intervention (16,74,85). A gait\ninjury risk without a deleterious effect\non RE would be beneﬁcial to perfor-\nmance by mitigating training interrup-\ntions. In a group of 22 male runners,\nClansey et al. (16) retrained 12 recrea-\ntional runners to minimize TA and\nreported a reduction of impact load\nwithout a signiﬁcant negative effect\non RE. Similarly, Roper et al. (74) re-\nported that retraining recreational run-\nners with PFP to use a forefoot strike\ndid not signiﬁcantly reduce RE. By\ncontrast, Townsend et al. (85) reported\nthat RE was signiﬁcantly impaired\nwhen 12 healthy runners performed\na single gait retraining session to lower\npeak TA. From these reports, RE may\nbe unchanged or compromised when\ninitially altering running gait. When RE\nis compromised initially, continued\npractice could return RE to pretraining\nlevels, but long term follow-up reports\nare needed to evaluate this.\nIt could be argued that a runner self-\noptimizes gait and becomes more eco-\nnomical th", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "to lower\npeak TA. From these reports, RE may\nbe unchanged or compromised when\ninitially altering running gait. When RE\nis compromised initially, continued\npractice could return RE to pretraining\nlevels, but long term follow-up reports\nare needed to e"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0003_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "afa7ede7bd54543b", "text_span_hash": "afa7ede7bd54543b", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "low-up reports\nare needed to evaluate this.\nIt could be argued that a runner self-\noptimizes gait and becomes more eco-\nnomical through training. Moore et al.\n(61) assessed RE in 10 female beginner\nrunners pre-post a 10-week training\nprogram. Althoug", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "limited to 9 runners who initially dem-\nlengths (overstriding). No other inter-\nventions were effective at improving\nRE and no studies have reported a long\nterm follow-up. The longest interven-\ntion was 12 weeks and it resulted in\na signiﬁcantly impaired RE in 8 triath-\nletes trained with the Pose method (23).\nAlthough many reports highlight the\nefﬁcacy of running gait retraining in\nthe treatment and prevention of RRIs,\nrelatively few have also measured RE\npre-post intervention (16,74,85). A gait\ninjury risk without a deleterious effect\non RE would be beneﬁcial to perfor-\nmance by mitigating training interrup-\ntions. In a group of 22 male runners,\nClansey et al. (16) retrained 12 recrea-\ntional runners to minimize TA and\nreported a reduction of impact load\nwithout a signiﬁcant negative effect\non RE. Similarly, Roper et al. (74) re-\nported that retraining recreational run-\nners with PFP to use a forefoot strike\ndid not signiﬁcantly reduce RE. By\ncontrast, Townsend et al. (85) reported\nthat RE was signiﬁcantly impaired\nwhen 12 healthy runners performed\na single gait retraining session to lower\npeak TA. From these reports, RE may\nbe unchanged or compromised when\ninitially altering running gait. When RE\nis compromised initially, continued\npractice could return RE to pretraining\nlevels, but long term follow-up reports\nare needed to evaluate this.\nIt could be argued that a runner self-\noptimizes gait and becomes more eco-\nnomical th", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "low-up reports\nare needed to evaluate this.\nIt could be argued that a runner self-\noptimizes gait and becomes more eco-\nnomical through training. Moore et al.\n(61) assessed RE in 10 female beginner\nrunners pre-post a 10-week training\nprogram. Althoug"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0003_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "478f2ae811a5c378", "text_span_hash": "478f2ae811a5c378", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "week training\nprogram. Although maximal aerobic\ncapacity did not change, RE and tread-\nmill time to exhaustion both improved,\nwhich demonstrated that beginner run-\nners improved RE simply through train-\ning. Of the variables collected, Moore\net al. (", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "limited to 9 runners who initially dem-\nlengths (overstriding). No other inter-\nventions were effective at improving\nRE and no studies have reported a long\nterm follow-up. The longest interven-\ntion was 12 weeks and it resulted in\na signiﬁcantly impaired RE in 8 triath-\nletes trained with the Pose method (23).\nAlthough many reports highlight the\nefﬁcacy of running gait retraining in\nthe treatment and prevention of RRIs,\nrelatively few have also measured RE\npre-post intervention (16,74,85). A gait\ninjury risk without a deleterious effect\non RE would be beneﬁcial to perfor-\nmance by mitigating training interrup-\ntions. In a group of 22 male runners,\nClansey et al. (16) retrained 12 recrea-\ntional runners to minimize TA and\nreported a reduction of impact load\nwithout a signiﬁcant negative effect\non RE. Similarly, Roper et al. (74) re-\nported that retraining recreational run-\nners with PFP to use a forefoot strike\ndid not signiﬁcantly reduce RE. By\ncontrast, Townsend et al. (85) reported\nthat RE was signiﬁcantly impaired\nwhen 12 healthy runners performed\na single gait retraining session to lower\npeak TA. From these reports, RE may\nbe unchanged or compromised when\ninitially altering running gait. When RE\nis compromised initially, continued\npractice could return RE to pretraining\nlevels, but long term follow-up reports\nare needed to evaluate this.\nIt could be argued that a runner self-\noptimizes gait and becomes more eco-\nnomical th", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "week training\nprogram. Although maximal aerobic\ncapacity did not change, RE and tread-\nmill time to exhaustion both improved,\nwhich demonstrated that beginner run-\nners improved RE simply through train-\ning. Of the variables collected, Moore\net al. ("}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0003_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5413c2432dc274c5", "text_span_hash": "5413c2432dc274c5", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "and\nGregor (65) studied 10 competitive col-\nlegiate distance runners over a two-year\nperiod and found that they all decreased\nstride length, increased stride rate, and\ndecreased GCT in the absence of any\ngait retraining intervention. Because all\nthes", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "limited to 9 runners who initially dem-\nlengths (overstriding). No other inter-\nventions were effective at improving\nRE and no studies have reported a long\nterm follow-up. The longest interven-\ntion was 12 weeks and it resulted in\na signiﬁcantly impaired RE in 8 triath-\nletes trained with the Pose method (23).\nAlthough many reports highlight the\nefﬁcacy of running gait retraining in\nthe treatment and prevention of RRIs,\nrelatively few have also measured RE\npre-post intervention (16,74,85). A gait\ninjury risk without a deleterious effect\non RE would be beneﬁcial to perfor-\nmance by mitigating training interrup-\ntions. In a group of 22 male runners,\nClansey et al. (16) retrained 12 recrea-\ntional runners to minimize TA and\nreported a reduction of impact load\nwithout a signiﬁcant negative effect\non RE. Similarly, Roper et al. (74) re-\nported that retraining recreational run-\nners with PFP to use a forefoot strike\ndid not signiﬁcantly reduce RE. By\ncontrast, Townsend et al. (85) reported\nthat RE was signiﬁcantly impaired\nwhen 12 healthy runners performed\na single gait retraining session to lower\npeak TA. From these reports, RE may\nbe unchanged or compromised when\ninitially altering running gait. When RE\nis compromised initially, continued\npractice could return RE to pretraining\nlevels, but long term follow-up reports\nare needed to evaluate this.\nIt could be argued that a runner self-\noptimizes gait and becomes more eco-\nnomical th", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "and\nGregor (65) studied 10 competitive col-\nlegiate distance runners over a two-year\nperiod and found that they all decreased\nstride length, increased stride rate, and\ndecreased GCT in the absence of any\ngait retraining intervention. Because all\nthes"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0003_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "177afe6b23ca0951", "text_span_hash": "177afe6b23ca0951", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "nd stride\nlength (see below) is equal to running velocity.\nStride rate or step ratea The number of steps or strides (equivalent to 2 steps, deﬁned as successive foot-ground contacts of the\nsame foot) a runner takes in a given amount of time, typicall", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "limited to 9 runners who initially dem-\nlengths (overstriding). No other inter-\nventions were effective at improving\nRE and no studies have reported a long\nterm follow-up. The longest interven-\ntion was 12 weeks and it resulted in\na signiﬁcantly impaired RE in 8 triath-\nletes trained with the Pose method (23).\nAlthough many reports highlight the\nefﬁcacy of running gait retraining in\nthe treatment and prevention of RRIs,\nrelatively few have also measured RE\npre-post intervention (16,74,85). A gait\ninjury risk without a deleterious effect\non RE would be beneﬁcial to perfor-\nmance by mitigating training interrup-\ntions. In a group of 22 male runners,\nClansey et al. (16) retrained 12 recrea-\ntional runners to minimize TA and\nreported a reduction of impact load\nwithout a signiﬁcant negative effect\non RE. Similarly, Roper et al. (74) re-\nported that retraining recreational run-\nners with PFP to use a forefoot strike\ndid not signiﬁcantly reduce RE. By\ncontrast, Townsend et al. (85) reported\nthat RE was signiﬁcantly impaired\nwhen 12 healthy runners performed\na single gait retraining session to lower\npeak TA. From these reports, RE may\nbe unchanged or compromised when\ninitially altering running gait. When RE\nis compromised initially, continued\npractice could return RE to pretraining\nlevels, but long term follow-up reports\nare needed to evaluate this.\nIt could be argued that a runner self-\noptimizes gait and becomes more eco-\nnomical th", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "nd stride\nlength (see below) is equal to running velocity.\nStride rate or step ratea The number of steps or strides (equivalent to 2 steps, deﬁned as successive foot-ground contacts of the\nsame foot) a runner takes in a given amount of time, typicall"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0003_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3b82e0b0350772c1", "text_span_hash": "3b82e0b0350772c1", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "given amount of time, typically 1 min (e.g., 180 steps per minute or 90\nstrides per minute). Step rate is sometimes referred to as cadence.\nStep length or stride\nThe distance covered during a step or stride. Step length is the distance from foot stri", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "limited to 9 runners who initially dem-\nlengths (overstriding). No other inter-\nventions were effective at improving\nRE and no studies have reported a long\nterm follow-up. The longest interven-\ntion was 12 weeks and it resulted in\na signiﬁcantly impaired RE in 8 triath-\nletes trained with the Pose method (23).\nAlthough many reports highlight the\nefﬁcacy of running gait retraining in\nthe treatment and prevention of RRIs,\nrelatively few have also measured RE\npre-post intervention (16,74,85). A gait\ninjury risk without a deleterious effect\non RE would be beneﬁcial to perfor-\nmance by mitigating training interrup-\ntions. In a group of 22 male runners,\nClansey et al. (16) retrained 12 recrea-\ntional runners to minimize TA and\nreported a reduction of impact load\nwithout a signiﬁcant negative effect\non RE. Similarly, Roper et al. (74) re-\nported that retraining recreational run-\nners with PFP to use a forefoot strike\ndid not signiﬁcantly reduce RE. By\ncontrast, Townsend et al. (85) reported\nthat RE was signiﬁcantly impaired\nwhen 12 healthy runners performed\na single gait retraining session to lower\npeak TA. From these reports, RE may\nbe unchanged or compromised when\ninitially altering running gait. When RE\nis compromised initially, continued\npractice could return RE to pretraining\nlevels, but long term follow-up reports\nare needed to evaluate this.\nIt could be argued that a runner self-\noptimizes gait and becomes more eco-\nnomical th", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "given amount of time, typically 1 min (e.g., 180 steps per minute or 90\nstrides per minute). Step rate is sometimes referred to as cadence.\nStep length or stride\nThe distance covered during a step or stride. Step length is the distance from foot stri"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0003_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5e1c3e3f3beb133c", "text_span_hash": "5e1c3e3f3beb133c", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "is the distance from foot strike of one foot to\nfoot strike of the opposite foot. Stride length is the distance between 2 consecutive foot strikes of the\nGround contact timea The amount of time the foot is on the ground during a single foot strike (i", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "limited to 9 runners who initially dem-\nlengths (overstriding). No other inter-\nventions were effective at improving\nRE and no studies have reported a long\nterm follow-up. The longest interven-\ntion was 12 weeks and it resulted in\na signiﬁcantly impaired RE in 8 triath-\nletes trained with the Pose method (23).\nAlthough many reports highlight the\nefﬁcacy of running gait retraining in\nthe treatment and prevention of RRIs,\nrelatively few have also measured RE\npre-post intervention (16,74,85). A gait\ninjury risk without a deleterious effect\non RE would be beneﬁcial to perfor-\nmance by mitigating training interrup-\ntions. In a group of 22 male runners,\nClansey et al. (16) retrained 12 recrea-\ntional runners to minimize TA and\nreported a reduction of impact load\nwithout a signiﬁcant negative effect\non RE. Similarly, Roper et al. (74) re-\nported that retraining recreational run-\nners with PFP to use a forefoot strike\ndid not signiﬁcantly reduce RE. By\ncontrast, Townsend et al. (85) reported\nthat RE was signiﬁcantly impaired\nwhen 12 healthy runners performed\na single gait retraining session to lower\npeak TA. From these reports, RE may\nbe unchanged or compromised when\ninitially altering running gait. When RE\nis compromised initially, continued\npractice could return RE to pretraining\nlevels, but long term follow-up reports\nare needed to evaluate this.\nIt could be argued that a runner self-\noptimizes gait and becomes more eco-\nnomical th", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "is the distance from foot strike of one foot to\nfoot strike of the opposite foot. Stride length is the distance between 2 consecutive foot strikes of the\nGround contact timea The amount of time the foot is on the ground during a single foot strike (i"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0003_c0019", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4e4280d2b111f519", "text_span_hash": "4e4280d2b111f519", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 19, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ferred to as the braking and propulsive forces.\nThe motion of the segments and body during the gait cycle. Typically, joint angles or segment (e.g.,\nthigh) angles are reported.\nVertical oscillationa\nThe change in height of a runner’s center of mass d", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "limited to 9 runners who initially dem-\nlengths (overstriding). No other inter-\nventions were effective at improving\nRE and no studies have reported a long\nterm follow-up. The longest interven-\ntion was 12 weeks and it resulted in\na signiﬁcantly impaired RE in 8 triath-\nletes trained with the Pose method (23).\nAlthough many reports highlight the\nefﬁcacy of running gait retraining in\nthe treatment and prevention of RRIs,\nrelatively few have also measured RE\npre-post intervention (16,74,85). A gait\ninjury risk without a deleterious effect\non RE would be beneﬁcial to perfor-\nmance by mitigating training interrup-\ntions. In a group of 22 male runners,\nClansey et al. (16) retrained 12 recrea-\ntional runners to minimize TA and\nreported a reduction of impact load\nwithout a signiﬁcant negative effect\non RE. Similarly, Roper et al. (74) re-\nported that retraining recreational run-\nners with PFP to use a forefoot strike\ndid not signiﬁcantly reduce RE. By\ncontrast, Townsend et al. (85) reported\nthat RE was signiﬁcantly impaired\nwhen 12 healthy runners performed\na single gait retraining session to lower\npeak TA. From these reports, RE may\nbe unchanged or compromised when\ninitially altering running gait. When RE\nis compromised initially, continued\npractice could return RE to pretraining\nlevels, but long term follow-up reports\nare needed to evaluate this.\nIt could be argued that a runner self-\noptimizes gait and becomes more eco-\nnomical th", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ferred to as the braking and propulsive forces.\nThe motion of the segments and body during the gait cycle. Typically, joint angles or segment (e.g.,\nthigh) angles are reported.\nVertical oscillationa\nThe change in height of a runner’s center of mass d"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0003_c0020", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1e49e20248117cb2", "text_span_hash": "1e49e20248117cb2", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 20, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "of a runner’s center of mass during the gait cycle. Most commonly assessed\nthrough motion capture systems or estimated from an accelerometer.\nThe change in velocity of the tibia bone. This is typically estimated using a skin-mounted accelerometer\nor", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "limited to 9 runners who initially dem-\nlengths (overstriding). No other inter-\nventions were effective at improving\nRE and no studies have reported a long\nterm follow-up. The longest interven-\ntion was 12 weeks and it resulted in\na signiﬁcantly impaired RE in 8 triath-\nletes trained with the Pose method (23).\nAlthough many reports highlight the\nefﬁcacy of running gait retraining in\nthe treatment and prevention of RRIs,\nrelatively few have also measured RE\npre-post intervention (16,74,85). A gait\ninjury risk without a deleterious effect\non RE would be beneﬁcial to perfor-\nmance by mitigating training interrup-\ntions. In a group of 22 male runners,\nClansey et al. (16) retrained 12 recrea-\ntional runners to minimize TA and\nreported a reduction of impact load\nwithout a signiﬁcant negative effect\non RE. Similarly, Roper et al. (74) re-\nported that retraining recreational run-\nners with PFP to use a forefoot strike\ndid not signiﬁcantly reduce RE. By\ncontrast, Townsend et al. (85) reported\nthat RE was signiﬁcantly impaired\nwhen 12 healthy runners performed\na single gait retraining session to lower\npeak TA. From these reports, RE may\nbe unchanged or compromised when\ninitially altering running gait. When RE\nis compromised initially, continued\npractice could return RE to pretraining\nlevels, but long term follow-up reports\nare needed to evaluate this.\nIt could be argued that a runner self-\noptimizes gait and becomes more eco-\nnomical th", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "of a runner’s center of mass during the gait cycle. Most commonly assessed\nthrough motion capture systems or estimated from an accelerometer.\nThe change in velocity of the tibia bone. This is typically estimated using a skin-mounted accelerometer\nor"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0003_c0022", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f05d57ba0114d5a8", "text_span_hash": "f05d57ba0114d5a8", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 22, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "onsumer wearable technologies.\nRunning Gait Adjustments\nVOLUME 42 | NUMBER 1 | FEBRUARY 2020\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditioning Association. Unauthorized reproduction of this article is prohibited.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "limited to 9 runners who initially dem-\nlengths (overstriding). No other inter-\nventions were effective at improving\nRE and no studies have reported a long\nterm follow-up. The longest interven-\ntion was 12 weeks and it resulted in\na signiﬁcantly impaired RE in 8 triath-\nletes trained with the Pose method (23).\nAlthough many reports highlight the\nefﬁcacy of running gait retraining in\nthe treatment and prevention of RRIs,\nrelatively few have also measured RE\npre-post intervention (16,74,85). A gait\ninjury risk without a deleterious effect\non RE would be beneﬁcial to perfor-\nmance by mitigating training interrup-\ntions. In a group of 22 male runners,\nClansey et al. (16) retrained 12 recrea-\ntional runners to minimize TA and\nreported a reduction of impact load\nwithout a signiﬁcant negative effect\non RE. Similarly, Roper et al. (74) re-\nported that retraining recreational run-\nners with PFP to use a forefoot strike\ndid not signiﬁcantly reduce RE. By\ncontrast, Townsend et al. (85) reported\nthat RE was signiﬁcantly impaired\nwhen 12 healthy runners performed\na single gait retraining session to lower\npeak TA. From these reports, RE may\nbe unchanged or compromised when\ninitially altering running gait. When RE\nis compromised initially, continued\npractice could return RE to pretraining\nlevels, but long term follow-up reports\nare needed to evaluate this.\nIt could be argued that a runner self-\noptimizes gait and becomes more eco-\nnomical th", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "onsumer wearable technologies.\nRunning Gait Adjustments\nVOLUME 42 | NUMBER 1 | FEBRUARY 2020\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditioning Association. Unauthorized reproduction of this article is prohibited."}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0004_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8d2d509897c50349", "text_span_hash": "8d2d509897c50349", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "red because the inter-\nvention was not long enough. However,\nFranch et al. (34) reported RE improve-\nments of 3.1, 3, and 0.9%, respectively, in\nengaged in distance, long-interval, or\nshort-interval training over a 6-week\nperiod. No changes were foun", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "were not reported in two 6-week\n(34,50) and one 7-week retraining study\n(6). Lake and Cavanagh (50) reported\nno RE improvements or biomechanical\nchanges after a 6-week run training pro-\ngram in 15 untrained college males.\nStudy inclusion required that partici-\npants complete a 30-minute run, which\nresulted in a substantially more trained\nsubject pool than in Moore et al. (61). It\nis possible that no RE improvements or\ngait changes occurred because the inter-\nvention was not long enough. However,\nFranch et al. (34) reported RE improve-\nments of 3.1, 3, and 0.9%, respectively, in\nengaged in distance, long-interval, or\nshort-interval training over a 6-week\nperiod. No changes were found in spa-\ntiotemporal mechanics.\nglobal and/or local feedback. With\nglobal feedback, the runner is provided\nan overarching goal, but is not in-\nstructed on how to alter gait to achieve\nthe stated goal. For instance, a global\nstrategy may provide biofeedback on\nTA and instruct runners to reduce it\n(16). The runner is not given a speciﬁc\nsolution and can adjust gait in any man-\nner to accomplish the goal. Conversely,\na local feedback strategy targets a spe-\nciﬁc gait feature and explicitly attempts\nto adjust it. For instance, runners with\nPFP have been provided visual feedback\non knee position with instructions to\nlimit its valgus movement during the\nAlthough generally accepted ﬂaws such\nas extraneous upper-extremity motion\nare thought to lead to poor ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "red because the inter-\nvention was not long enough. However,\nFranch et al. (34) reported RE improve-\nments of 3.1, 3, and 0.9%, respectively, in\nengaged in distance, long-interval, or\nshort-interval training over a 6-week\nperiod. No changes were foun"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0004_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0b461b1e5e5a12bd", "text_span_hash": "0b461b1e5e5a12bd", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "k\nperiod. No changes were found in spa-\ntiotemporal mechanics.\nglobal and/or local feedback. With\nglobal feedback, the runner is provided\nan overarching goal, but is not in-\nstructed on how to alter gait to achieve\nthe stated goal. For instance, a gl", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "were not reported in two 6-week\n(34,50) and one 7-week retraining study\n(6). Lake and Cavanagh (50) reported\nno RE improvements or biomechanical\nchanges after a 6-week run training pro-\ngram in 15 untrained college males.\nStudy inclusion required that partici-\npants complete a 30-minute run, which\nresulted in a substantially more trained\nsubject pool than in Moore et al. (61). It\nis possible that no RE improvements or\ngait changes occurred because the inter-\nvention was not long enough. However,\nFranch et al. (34) reported RE improve-\nments of 3.1, 3, and 0.9%, respectively, in\nengaged in distance, long-interval, or\nshort-interval training over a 6-week\nperiod. No changes were found in spa-\ntiotemporal mechanics.\nglobal and/or local feedback. With\nglobal feedback, the runner is provided\nan overarching goal, but is not in-\nstructed on how to alter gait to achieve\nthe stated goal. For instance, a global\nstrategy may provide biofeedback on\nTA and instruct runners to reduce it\n(16). The runner is not given a speciﬁc\nsolution and can adjust gait in any man-\nner to accomplish the goal. Conversely,\na local feedback strategy targets a spe-\nciﬁc gait feature and explicitly attempts\nto adjust it. For instance, runners with\nPFP have been provided visual feedback\non knee position with instructions to\nlimit its valgus movement during the\nAlthough generally accepted ﬂaws such\nas extraneous upper-extremity motion\nare thought to lead to poor ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "k\nperiod. No changes were found in spa-\ntiotemporal mechanics.\nglobal and/or local feedback. With\nglobal feedback, the runner is provided\nan overarching goal, but is not in-\nstructed on how to alter gait to achieve\nthe stated goal. For instance, a gl"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0004_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "36bd3ae8f53cb85b", "text_span_hash": "36bd3ae8f53cb85b", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "tated goal. For instance, a global\nstrategy may provide biofeedback on\nTA and instruct runners to reduce it\n(16). The runner is not given a speciﬁc\nsolution and can adjust gait in any man-\nner to accomplish the goal. Conversely,\na local feedback stra", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "were not reported in two 6-week\n(34,50) and one 7-week retraining study\n(6). Lake and Cavanagh (50) reported\nno RE improvements or biomechanical\nchanges after a 6-week run training pro-\ngram in 15 untrained college males.\nStudy inclusion required that partici-\npants complete a 30-minute run, which\nresulted in a substantially more trained\nsubject pool than in Moore et al. (61). It\nis possible that no RE improvements or\ngait changes occurred because the inter-\nvention was not long enough. However,\nFranch et al. (34) reported RE improve-\nments of 3.1, 3, and 0.9%, respectively, in\nengaged in distance, long-interval, or\nshort-interval training over a 6-week\nperiod. No changes were found in spa-\ntiotemporal mechanics.\nglobal and/or local feedback. With\nglobal feedback, the runner is provided\nan overarching goal, but is not in-\nstructed on how to alter gait to achieve\nthe stated goal. For instance, a global\nstrategy may provide biofeedback on\nTA and instruct runners to reduce it\n(16). The runner is not given a speciﬁc\nsolution and can adjust gait in any man-\nner to accomplish the goal. Conversely,\na local feedback strategy targets a spe-\nciﬁc gait feature and explicitly attempts\nto adjust it. For instance, runners with\nPFP have been provided visual feedback\non knee position with instructions to\nlimit its valgus movement during the\nAlthough generally accepted ﬂaws such\nas extraneous upper-extremity motion\nare thought to lead to poor ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "tated goal. For instance, a global\nstrategy may provide biofeedback on\nTA and instruct runners to reduce it\n(16). The runner is not given a speciﬁc\nsolution and can adjust gait in any man-\nner to accomplish the goal. Conversely,\na local feedback stra"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0004_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5adbb39f1ae8fbaf", "text_span_hash": "5adbb39f1ae8fbaf", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "lead to improved RE. Based strictly\non the available evidence, it seems that\nrunning gait retraining to improve RE is\ndifﬁcult. Although it seems plausible\nthat a runner can safely adopt a new\ngait, the best outcome would seem to\nbe that RE was not", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "were not reported in two 6-week\n(34,50) and one 7-week retraining study\n(6). Lake and Cavanagh (50) reported\nno RE improvements or biomechanical\nchanges after a 6-week run training pro-\ngram in 15 untrained college males.\nStudy inclusion required that partici-\npants complete a 30-minute run, which\nresulted in a substantially more trained\nsubject pool than in Moore et al. (61). It\nis possible that no RE improvements or\ngait changes occurred because the inter-\nvention was not long enough. However,\nFranch et al. (34) reported RE improve-\nments of 3.1, 3, and 0.9%, respectively, in\nengaged in distance, long-interval, or\nshort-interval training over a 6-week\nperiod. No changes were found in spa-\ntiotemporal mechanics.\nglobal and/or local feedback. With\nglobal feedback, the runner is provided\nan overarching goal, but is not in-\nstructed on how to alter gait to achieve\nthe stated goal. For instance, a global\nstrategy may provide biofeedback on\nTA and instruct runners to reduce it\n(16). The runner is not given a speciﬁc\nsolution and can adjust gait in any man-\nner to accomplish the goal. Conversely,\na local feedback strategy targets a spe-\nciﬁc gait feature and explicitly attempts\nto adjust it. For instance, runners with\nPFP have been provided visual feedback\non knee position with instructions to\nlimit its valgus movement during the\nAlthough generally accepted ﬂaws such\nas extraneous upper-extremity motion\nare thought to lead to poor ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "lead to improved RE. Based strictly\non the available evidence, it seems that\nrunning gait retraining to improve RE is\ndifﬁcult. Although it seems plausible\nthat a runner can safely adopt a new\ngait, the best outcome would seem to\nbe that RE was not"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0004_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "bdffd311f086764a", "text_span_hash": "bdffd311f086764a", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ld seem to\nbe that RE was not degraded. Because\nthere does not exist a universal “opti-\nmal” running gait, perhaps the lack of\npositive ﬁndings could be attributed to\nthe need for individualized retraining\nprograms as opposed to a “one size ﬁts\nall”", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "were not reported in two 6-week\n(34,50) and one 7-week retraining study\n(6). Lake and Cavanagh (50) reported\nno RE improvements or biomechanical\nchanges after a 6-week run training pro-\ngram in 15 untrained college males.\nStudy inclusion required that partici-\npants complete a 30-minute run, which\nresulted in a substantially more trained\nsubject pool than in Moore et al. (61). It\nis possible that no RE improvements or\ngait changes occurred because the inter-\nvention was not long enough. However,\nFranch et al. (34) reported RE improve-\nments of 3.1, 3, and 0.9%, respectively, in\nengaged in distance, long-interval, or\nshort-interval training over a 6-week\nperiod. No changes were found in spa-\ntiotemporal mechanics.\nglobal and/or local feedback. With\nglobal feedback, the runner is provided\nan overarching goal, but is not in-\nstructed on how to alter gait to achieve\nthe stated goal. For instance, a global\nstrategy may provide biofeedback on\nTA and instruct runners to reduce it\n(16). The runner is not given a speciﬁc\nsolution and can adjust gait in any man-\nner to accomplish the goal. Conversely,\na local feedback strategy targets a spe-\nciﬁc gait feature and explicitly attempts\nto adjust it. For instance, runners with\nPFP have been provided visual feedback\non knee position with instructions to\nlimit its valgus movement during the\nAlthough generally accepted ﬂaws such\nas extraneous upper-extremity motion\nare thought to lead to poor ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ld seem to\nbe that RE was not degraded. Because\nthere does not exist a universal “opti-\nmal” running gait, perhaps the lack of\npositive ﬁndings could be attributed to\nthe need for individualized retraining\nprograms as opposed to a “one size ﬁts\nall”"}
