ish the goal. Conversely,\na local feedback strategy targets a spe-\nciﬁc gait feature and explicitly attempts\nto adjust it. For instance, runners with\nPFP have been provided visual feedback\non knee position with instructions to\nlimit its valgus movement during the\nAlthough generally accepted ﬂaws such\nas extraneous upper-extremity motion\nare thought to lead to poor ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "titive collegiate middle\ndistance runners (male & female)\nNo kinematic predictors; reduced braking GRF and mediolateral GRF\n16 male recreational runners\nReduced total vertical impulse and reduced net vertical impulse\nHeise et al. (43) 16 male recreat"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0004_c0019", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1fdae201e6186aad", "text_span_hash": "1fdae201e6186aad", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 19, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "nd reaction force; RFS 5 rearfoot strike; ROM 5 range of motion; SL 5 stride length; SR 5 stride rate.\nStrength and Conditioning Journal | www.nsca-scj.com\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditioning Association. Unauthorized reproduction of this", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "were not reported in two 6-week\n(34,50) and one 7-week retraining study\n(6). Lake and Cavanagh (50) reported\nno RE improvements or biomechanical\nchanges after a 6-week run training pro-\ngram in 15 untrained college males.\nStudy inclusion required that partici-\npants complete a 30-minute run, which\nresulted in a substantially more trained\nsubject pool than in Moore et al. (61). It\nis possible that no RE improvements or\ngait changes occurred because the inter-\nvention was not long enough. However,\nFranch et al. (34) reported RE improve-\nments of 3.1, 3, and 0.9%, respectively, in\nengaged in distance, long-interval, or\nshort-interval training over a 6-week\nperiod. No changes were found in spa-\ntiotemporal mechanics.\nglobal and/or local feedback. With\nglobal feedback, the runner is provided\nan overarching goal, but is not in-\nstructed on how to alter gait to achieve\nthe stated goal. For instance, a global\nstrategy may provide biofeedback on\nTA and instruct runners to reduce it\n(16). The runner is not given a speciﬁc\nsolution and can adjust gait in any man-\nner to accomplish the goal. Conversely,\na local feedback strategy targets a spe-\nciﬁc gait feature and explicitly attempts\nto adjust it. For instance, runners with\nPFP have been provided visual feedback\non knee position with instructions to\nlimit its valgus movement during the\nAlthough generally accepted ﬂaws such\nas extraneous upper-extremity motion\nare thought to lead to poor ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "nd reaction force; RFS 5 rearfoot strike; ROM 5 range of motion; SL 5 stride length; SR 5 stride rate.\nStrength and Conditioning Journal | www.nsca-scj.com\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditioning Association. Unauthorized reproduction of this"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0005_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3086b4b04f624b3d", "text_span_hash": "3086b4b04f624b3d", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "verbal instructions over an 11-week\nintervention to 10 year olds. Partici-\npants were instructed to reduce VOSC\nand were given general information\nstride rate. After the intervention, they\nreported no changes in RE, VOSC,\nstride rate, and stride leng", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "verbal instructions over an 11-week\nintervention to 10 year olds. Partici-\npants were instructed to reduce VOSC\nand were given general information\nstride rate. After the intervention, they\nreported no changes in RE, VOSC,\nstride rate, and stride length. Messier\nand Cirillo (57) carefully selected 11\nfemale novice runners who demon-\nstrated at least one gait “ﬂaw.” Retrain-\ning consisted of comparing a video of\neach runner to an experienced runner.\nFlaws were explained and pointed out\nto each runner. The runners then per-\nformed 3 treadmill runs per week for 5\nweeks and received both verbal and\nvisual cueing. A control group per-\nformed the same treadmill runs, but\nwas not provided feedback. Similar to\nthe ﬁndings of Lake and Cavanagh\n(50), the RE and biomechanics of the\ncontrol group were unchanged after\nthe 5 weeks of treadmill runs. How-\never, the retraining group exhibited sig-\nniﬁcantly longer stride lengths, greater\nknee ﬂexion during the stance and\nswing phases, greater dorsiﬂexion dur-\ning the stance phase, and increased\n0.43 cm). All these biomechanical\nexception of VOSC. This highlights\nthe interdependence of biomechanical\ngait parameters as a change in one may\nanother. In addition, 2 members of\nthe experimental group were able to\nadjust to a rearfoot strike (RFS) as\nLong-term (chronic) running gait retraining interventions where running economy was measured pre- and post-\nY VOSC, information on posture,\nY VOSC, Y exc", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "verbal instructions over an 11-week\nintervention to 10 year olds. Partici-\npants were instructed to reduce VOSC\nand were given general information\nstride rate. After the intervention, they\nreported no changes in RE, VOSC,\nstride rate, and stride leng"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0005_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "a54c4cd9a04e473d", "text_span_hash": "a54c4cd9a04e473d", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": ",\nstride rate, and stride length. Messier\nand Cirillo (57) carefully selected 11\nfemale novice runners who demon-\nstrated at least one gait “ﬂaw.” Retrain-\ning consisted of comparing a video of\neach runner to an experienced runner.\nFlaws were explain", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "verbal instructions over an 11-week\nintervention to 10 year olds. Partici-\npants were instructed to reduce VOSC\nand were given general information\nstride rate. After the intervention, they\nreported no changes in RE, VOSC,\nstride rate, and stride length. Messier\nand Cirillo (57) carefully selected 11\nfemale novice runners who demon-\nstrated at least one gait “ﬂaw.” Retrain-\ning consisted of comparing a video of\neach runner to an experienced runner.\nFlaws were explained and pointed out\nto each runner. The runners then per-\nformed 3 treadmill runs per week for 5\nweeks and received both verbal and\nvisual cueing. A control group per-\nformed the same treadmill runs, but\nwas not provided feedback. Similar to\nthe ﬁndings of Lake and Cavanagh\n(50), the RE and biomechanics of the\ncontrol group were unchanged after\nthe 5 weeks of treadmill runs. How-\never, the retraining group exhibited sig-\nniﬁcantly longer stride lengths, greater\nknee ﬂexion during the stance and\nswing phases, greater dorsiﬂexion dur-\ning the stance phase, and increased\n0.43 cm). All these biomechanical\nexception of VOSC. This highlights\nthe interdependence of biomechanical\ngait parameters as a change in one may\nanother. In addition, 2 members of\nthe experimental group were able to\nadjust to a rearfoot strike (RFS) as\nLong-term (chronic) running gait retraining interventions where running economy was measured pre- and post-\nY VOSC, information on posture,\nY VOSC, Y exc", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": ",\nstride rate, and stride length. Messier\nand Cirillo (57) carefully selected 11\nfemale novice runners who demon-\nstrated at least one gait “ﬂaw.” Retrain-\ning consisted of comparing a video of\neach runner to an experienced runner.\nFlaws were explain"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0005_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c7e02f4212f32a22", "text_span_hash": "c7e02f4212f32a22", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ced runner.\nFlaws were explained and pointed out\nto each runner. The runners then per-\nformed 3 treadmill runs per week for 5\nweeks and received both verbal and\nvisual cueing. A control group per-\nformed the same treadmill runs, but\nwas not provided", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "verbal instructions over an 11-week\nintervention to 10 year olds. Partici-\npants were instructed to reduce VOSC\nand were given general information\nstride rate. After the intervention, they\nreported no changes in RE, VOSC,\nstride rate, and stride length. Messier\nand Cirillo (57) carefully selected 11\nfemale novice runners who demon-\nstrated at least one gait “ﬂaw.” Retrain-\ning consisted of comparing a video of\neach runner to an experienced runner.\nFlaws were explained and pointed out\nto each runner. The runners then per-\nformed 3 treadmill runs per week for 5\nweeks and received both verbal and\nvisual cueing. A control group per-\nformed the same treadmill runs, but\nwas not provided feedback. Similar to\nthe ﬁndings of Lake and Cavanagh\n(50), the RE and biomechanics of the\ncontrol group were unchanged after\nthe 5 weeks of treadmill runs. How-\never, the retraining group exhibited sig-\nniﬁcantly longer stride lengths, greater\nknee ﬂexion during the stance and\nswing phases, greater dorsiﬂexion dur-\ning the stance phase, and increased\n0.43 cm). All these biomechanical\nexception of VOSC. This highlights\nthe interdependence of biomechanical\ngait parameters as a change in one may\nanother. In addition, 2 members of\nthe experimental group were able to\nadjust to a rearfoot strike (RFS) as\nLong-term (chronic) running gait retraining interventions where running economy was measured pre- and post-\nY VOSC, information on posture,\nY VOSC, Y exc", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ced runner.\nFlaws were explained and pointed out\nto each runner. The runners then per-\nformed 3 treadmill runs per week for 5\nweeks and received both verbal and\nvisual cueing. A control group per-\nformed the same treadmill runs, but\nwas not provided"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0006_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b269369a2469add2", "text_span_hash": "b269369a2469add2", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "instructed. Despite all these biome-\nchanical changes, there were no signif-\nicant changes to RE or ratings of\nperceived effort (RPE).\nMore recently, 3 gait retraining inter-\nventions sought to teach runners the\nmidstance-to-midstance running pro-\ngr", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "instructed. Despite all these biome-\nchanical changes, there were no signif-\nicant changes to RE or ratings of\nperceived effort (RPE).\nMore recently, 3 gait retraining inter-\nventions sought to teach runners the\nmidstance-to-midstance running pro-\ngram (20). Both Pose method interven-\ntions were effective at adjusting gait;\nhowever, the adjustments either re-\nsulted in no RE change or a signiﬁ-\ncantly worse RE. Dallam et al. (23)\nmethod with 1 hour of instruction\nper week, whereas Fletcher et al. (31)\nused drills over 7 days. Both interven-\ntions reduced stride length, increased\nstride rate, and reduced VOSC. Craig-\nhead et al. (20) exposed 9 recreational\nrunners to ﬁve 1-hour instructional ses-\nsions on the midstance-to-midstance\naims to promote midfoot ground con-\ntact. The 8-week intervention was\neffective at reducing stride length,\nincreasing stride rate, and reducing\nmaximum knee ﬂexion in stance, but\ndid not change foot contact position\nor VOSC. More importantly, the inter-\nvention did not signiﬁcantly change\nA combined approach was used by\nWarne et al. (90) in an 8-week inter-\nvention in 12 male moderately trained\nrunners. Runners were instructed over\na 30-minute session to increase step\nrate by 10%, aim for a nonrearfoot\nground contact, keep the trunk upright,\nand land softer. Runners were also in-\nstructed to run in minimalist style foot-\nconventional midsole footwear. Run-\nners signiﬁcantly increased step rate\nin min", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "instructed. Despite all these biome-\nchanical changes, there were no signif-\nicant changes to RE or ratings of\nperceived effort (RPE).\nMore recently, 3 gait retraining inter-\nventions sought to teach runners the\nmidstance-to-midstance running pro-\ngr"}
