 an 8-week inter-\nvention in 12 male moderately trained\nrunners. Runners were instructed over\na 30-minute session to increase step\nrate by 10%, aim for a nonrearfoot\nground contact, keep the trunk upright,\nand land softer. Runners were also in-\nstructed to run in minimalist style foot-\nconventional midsole footwear. Run-\nners signiﬁcantly increased step rate\nin min", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "istance runners and determined\nShort-term (acute) running gait retraining interventions where running economy was measured pre- and post-\n1 session Preferred SL had best RE\n19 RFS and 18 NRFS Adjusted\n1 session Worse when switching from RFS to FFS at"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0007_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9261a4256d3d8a64", "text_span_hash": "9261a4256d3d8a64", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "that stride rate and stride length had\naccounting for 28% and 23% of its var-\niance, respectively. It is possible that\nspatiotemporal factors had the greatest\nrelationship to RE because adjusting\nstride rate may change the leg’s posi-\ntion at foot st", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "that stride rate and stride length had\naccounting for 28% and 23% of its var-\niance, respectively. It is possible that\nspatiotemporal factors had the greatest\nrelationship to RE because adjusting\nstride rate may change the leg’s posi-\ntion at foot strike, which could inﬂu-\nence both elastic energy return and\nConversely, Pizzuto et al. (71) reported\nthat stride rate and stride length did not\nhave a strong relationship to RE in\ntheir study of 20 recreational runners.\nimportance of stride rate on RE, retrain-\ning a runner’s stride rate remains an\nenticing intervention due to the ease of\nimplementation. Stride rate manipula-\ntion can be achieved by using a metro-\nnome (e.g., 89), music (selecting songs\nwith the desired beats per minute) (38),\nor wearable technology (93). Willy et al.\nincreased stride rate by monitoring out-\nput from wearable technology in 8 over-\nground runs. Increasing stride rate by\n10% has been reported to effectively\nreduce joint loads (41,52,92), which\nwould theoretically reduce a runner’s\ninherent RRI risk (78). However, none\nof these studies assessed RE. Evidence\nsuggest that both experienced and inex-\nperienced runners may self-optimize to\nthe most economical pairing of stride\nlength and stride rate (14,47). In addition,\nrunners demonstrate the ability to main-\ntain the self-optimization of spatiotem-\nporal mechanics even when fatigued\n(87). To date, a one size ﬁts all “optimal”\nstride rate/length for impr", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "that stride rate and stride length had\naccounting for 28% and 23% of its var-\niance, respectively. It is possible that\nspatiotemporal factors had the greatest\nrelationship to RE because adjusting\nstride rate may change the leg’s posi-\ntion at foot st"}
