{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0007_c0018", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "719e4f0c9a11fe88", "text_span_hash": "719e4f0c9a11fe88", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 18, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "idence, there is\nnot a “gold standard” stride rate that all\nrunners should target to optimize RE.\nAlthough it seems that runners with\nshow the potential to retrain gait to\nimprove RE, identifying which runners\nto retrain may be challenging in prac-\nt", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "that stride rate and stride length had\naccounting for 28% and 23% of its var-\niance, respectively. It is possible that\nspatiotemporal factors had the greatest\nrelationship to RE because adjusting\nstride rate may change the leg’s posi-\ntion at foot strike, which could inﬂu-\nence both elastic energy return and\nConversely, Pizzuto et al. (71) reported\nthat stride rate and stride length did not\nhave a strong relationship to RE in\ntheir study of 20 recreational runners.\nimportance of stride rate on RE, retrain-\ning a runner’s stride rate remains an\nenticing intervention due to the ease of\nimplementation. Stride rate manipula-\ntion can be achieved by using a metro-\nnome (e.g., 89), music (selecting songs\nwith the desired beats per minute) (38),\nor wearable technology (93). Willy et al.\nincreased stride rate by monitoring out-\nput from wearable technology in 8 over-\nground runs. Increasing stride rate by\n10% has been reported to effectively\nreduce joint loads (41,52,92), which\nwould theoretically reduce a runner’s\ninherent RRI risk (78). However, none\nof these studies assessed RE. Evidence\nsuggest that both experienced and inex-\nperienced runners may self-optimize to\nthe most economical pairing of stride\nlength and stride rate (14,47). In addition,\nrunners demonstrate the ability to main-\ntain the self-optimization of spatiotem-\nporal mechanics even when fatigued\n(87). To date, a one size ﬁts all “optimal”\nstride rate/length for impr", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "idence, there is\nnot a “gold standard” stride rate that all\nrunners should target to optimize RE.\nAlthough it seems that runners with\nshow the potential to retrain gait to\nimprove RE, identifying which runners\nto retrain may be challenging in prac-\nt"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0007_c0019", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3378feb595db3907", "text_span_hash": "3378feb595db3907", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 19, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "may be challenging in prac-\ntice. Runners show the capacity to\nincrease stride rate in both treadmill\nand in-ﬁeld retraining, which increases\nthe clinical application. Because it does\nnot seem that small increases in stride\nrate negatively impact RE", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "that stride rate and stride length had\naccounting for 28% and 23% of its var-\niance, respectively. It is possible that\nspatiotemporal factors had the greatest\nrelationship to RE because adjusting\nstride rate may change the leg’s posi-\ntion at foot strike, which could inﬂu-\nence both elastic energy return and\nConversely, Pizzuto et al. (71) reported\nthat stride rate and stride length did not\nhave a strong relationship to RE in\ntheir study of 20 recreational runners.\nimportance of stride rate on RE, retrain-\ning a runner’s stride rate remains an\nenticing intervention due to the ease of\nimplementation. Stride rate manipula-\ntion can be achieved by using a metro-\nnome (e.g., 89), music (selecting songs\nwith the desired beats per minute) (38),\nor wearable technology (93). Willy et al.\nincreased stride rate by monitoring out-\nput from wearable technology in 8 over-\nground runs. Increasing stride rate by\n10% has been reported to effectively\nreduce joint loads (41,52,92), which\nwould theoretically reduce a runner’s\ninherent RRI risk (78). However, none\nof these studies assessed RE. Evidence\nsuggest that both experienced and inex-\nperienced runners may self-optimize to\nthe most economical pairing of stride\nlength and stride rate (14,47). In addition,\nrunners demonstrate the ability to main-\ntain the self-optimization of spatiotem-\nporal mechanics even when fatigued\n(87). To date, a one size ﬁts all “optimal”\nstride rate/length for impr", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "may be challenging in prac-\ntice. Runners show the capacity to\nincrease stride rate in both treadmill\nand in-ﬁeld retraining, which increases\nthe clinical application. Because it does\nnot seem that small increases in stride\nrate negatively impact RE"}
