ssed RE. Evidence\nsuggest that both experienced and inex-\nperienced runners may self-optimize to\nthe most economical pairing of stride\nlength and stride rate (14,47). In addition,\nrunners demonstrate the ability to main-\ntain the self-optimization of spatiotem-\nporal mechanics even when fatigued\n(87). To date, a one size ﬁts all “optimal”\nstride rate/length for impr", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ride\nrate negatively impact RE, this inter-\nvention warrants consideration from\na coach as it may reduce a runner’s risk\nthought to inﬂuence RE, which has\nled some to suggest that it should be\na focus of gait retraining. Although\nWilliams and Cavanag"}
