ssed RE. Evidence\nsuggest that both experienced and inex-\nperienced runners may self-optimize to\nthe most economical pairing of stride\nlength and stride rate (14,47). In addition,\nrunners demonstrate the ability to main-\ntain the self-optimization of spatiotem-\nporal mechanics even when fatigued\n(87). To date, a one size ﬁts all “optimal”\nstride rate/length for impr", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "s may be\nspeed dependent, with some runners\nusing a more anterior foot strike at fast-\ner speeds (10). Regardless, most of the\nelite marathon runners at the 2017\nIAAF World Championships used an\nRFS and there was no difference in the\nincidence of NRF"}
