ssed RE. Evidence\nsuggest that both experienced and inex-\nperienced runners may self-optimize to\nthe most economical pairing of stride\nlength and stride rate (14,47). In addition,\nrunners demonstrate the ability to main-\ntain the self-optimization of spatiotem-\nporal mechanics even when fatigued\n(87). To date, a one size ﬁts all “optimal”\nstride rate/length for impr", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ase of running\nincreased oxygen costs, which sup-\nports the link between arch deforma-\ndeformation and elastic energy storage\nmay not differ between NRFS and RFS\npatterns (55,88), suggesting there may\nbe little difference in RE between the 2\nfoot str"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0007_c0027", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7222974fe119bd25", "text_span_hash": "7222974fe119bd25", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 27, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "e in RE between the 2\nfoot strike patterns.\nRunning Gait Adjustments\nVOLUME 42 | NUMBER 1 | FEBRUARY 2020\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditioning Association. Unauthorized reproduction of this article is prohibited.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "that stride rate and stride length had\naccounting for 28% and 23% of its var-\niance, respectively. It is possible that\nspatiotemporal factors had the greatest\nrelationship to RE because adjusting\nstride rate may change the leg’s posi-\ntion at foot strike, which could inﬂu-\nence both elastic energy return and\nConversely, Pizzuto et al. (71) reported\nthat stride rate and stride length did not\nhave a strong relationship to RE in\ntheir study of 20 recreational runners.\nimportance of stride rate on RE, retrain-\ning a runner’s stride rate remains an\nenticing intervention due to the ease of\nimplementation. Stride rate manipula-\ntion can be achieved by using a metro-\nnome (e.g., 89), music (selecting songs\nwith the desired beats per minute) (38),\nor wearable technology (93). Willy et al.\nincreased stride rate by monitoring out-\nput from wearable technology in 8 over-\nground runs. Increasing stride rate by\n10% has been reported to effectively\nreduce joint loads (41,52,92), which\nwould theoretically reduce a runner’s\ninherent RRI risk (78). However, none\nof these studies assessed RE. Evidence\nsuggest that both experienced and inex-\nperienced runners may self-optimize to\nthe most economical pairing of stride\nlength and stride rate (14,47). In addition,\nrunners demonstrate the ability to main-\ntain the self-optimization of spatiotem-\nporal mechanics even when fatigued\n(87). To date, a one size ﬁts all “optimal”\nstride rate/length for impr", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "e in RE between the 2\nfoot strike patterns.\nRunning Gait Adjustments\nVOLUME 42 | NUMBER 1 | FEBRUARY 2020\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditioning Association. Unauthorized reproduction of this article is prohibited."}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0008_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "309c44c4240cd34f", "text_span_hash": "309c44c4240cd34f", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Several investigations have successfully\nmanipulated foot strike in either an\nacute (1 session) (62) or chronic inter-\nvention (12 weeks) (23). Runners dem-\nonstrate the capacity to switch foot\nstrike on instruction; however, there is\nno evidence tha", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Several investigations have successfully\nmanipulated foot strike in either an\nacute (1 session) (62) or chronic inter-\nvention (12 weeks) (23). Runners dem-\nonstrate the capacity to switch foot\nstrike on instruction; however, there is\nno evidence that switching from RFS to\nNRFS or vice versa will improve RE.\nDirect measurements of RE with run-\nners switching between RFS and NRFS\nsuggest that an RFS may be more eco-\nnomical in habitual RFS runners and\nthat the 2 foot strike patterns are equally\neconomical in habitual NRFS runners\n(37). This is echoed by Melcher et al.\n(56), who reported that runners (all\nhabitual RFS) were 5.6% more econom-\nical when using an RFS at both the\nbeginning and end of a long treadmill\nrun. Additional studies report that RE\ndoes not change between foot strike\npatterns (4,22,69). Based on the avail-\nable gait retraining evidence, a coach\nshould not expect an improvement in\nRE if they instruct a runner to switch\nhabitual foot strike pattern.\nVERTICAL OSCILLATION\nThe body’s COM goes through a vertical\ntranslation throughout the gait cycle. It\npeaks during the aerial phase and rea-\nches its lowest point during maximum\nleg compression in stance phase. This\noverall vertical displacement is termed\nVOSC. When VOSC is increased during\nthe running cycle, RE is compromised\n(19,86). In fact, VOSC has been reported\nto explain 27.7% of RE variation (32)\nwith smaller oscillation being character-\nistic of better perf", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Several investigations have successfully\nmanipulated foot strike in either an\nacute (1 session) (62) or chronic inter-\nvention (12 weeks) (23). Runners dem-\nonstrate the capacity to switch foot\nstrike on instruction; however, there is\nno evidence tha"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0008_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "09e5afd5bc570040", "text_span_hash": "09e5afd5bc570040", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ever, there is\nno evidence that switching from RFS to\nNRFS or vice versa will improve RE.\nDirect measurements of RE with run-\nners switching between RFS and NRFS\nsuggest that an RFS may be more eco-\nnomical in habitual RFS runners and\nthat the 2 foot", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Several investigations have successfully\nmanipulated foot strike in either an\nacute (1 session) (62) or chronic inter-\nvention (12 weeks) (23). Runners dem-\nonstrate the capacity to switch foot\nstrike on instruction; however, there is\nno evidence that switching from RFS to\nNRFS or vice versa will improve RE.\nDirect measurements of RE with run-\nners switching between RFS and NRFS\nsuggest that an RFS may be more eco-\nnomical in habitual RFS runners and\nthat the 2 foot strike patterns are equally\neconomical in habitual NRFS runners\n(37). This is echoed by Melcher et al.\n(56), who reported that runners (all\nhabitual RFS) were 5.6% more econom-\nical when using an RFS at both the\nbeginning and end of a long treadmill\nrun. Additional studies report that RE\ndoes not change between foot strike\npatterns (4,22,69). Based on the avail-\nable gait retraining evidence, a coach\nshould not expect an improvement in\nRE if they instruct a runner to switch\nhabitual foot strike pattern.\nVERTICAL OSCILLATION\nThe body’s COM goes through a vertical\ntranslation throughout the gait cycle. It\npeaks during the aerial phase and rea-\nches its lowest point during maximum\nleg compression in stance phase. This\noverall vertical displacement is termed\nVOSC. When VOSC is increased during\nthe running cycle, RE is compromised\n(19,86). In fact, VOSC has been reported\nto explain 27.7% of RE variation (32)\nwith smaller oscillation being character-\nistic of better perf", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ever, there is\nno evidence that switching from RFS to\nNRFS or vice versa will improve RE.\nDirect measurements of RE with run-\nners switching between RFS and NRFS\nsuggest that an RFS may be more eco-\nnomical in habitual RFS runners and\nthat the 2 foot"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0008_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "425bf2d0a10c1a20", "text_span_hash": "425bf2d0a10c1a20", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "FS runners and\nthat the 2 foot strike patterns are equally\neconomical in habitual NRFS runners\n(37). This is echoed by Melcher et al.\n(56), who reported that runners (all\nhabitual RFS) were 5.6% more econom-\nical when using an RFS at both the\nbeginni", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Several investigations have successfully\nmanipulated foot strike in either an\nacute (1 session) (62) or chronic inter-\nvention (12 weeks) (23). Runners dem-\nonstrate the capacity to switch foot\nstrike on instruction; however, there is\nno evidence that switching from RFS to\nNRFS or vice versa will improve RE.\nDirect measurements of RE with run-\nners switching between RFS and NRFS\nsuggest that an RFS may be more eco-\nnomical in habitual RFS runners and\nthat the 2 foot strike patterns are equally\neconomical in habitual NRFS runners\n(37). This is echoed by Melcher et al.\n(56), who reported that runners (all\nhabitual RFS) were 5.6% more econom-\nical when using an RFS at both the\nbeginning and end of a long treadmill\nrun. Additional studies report that RE\ndoes not change between foot strike\npatterns (4,22,69). Based on the avail-\nable gait retraining evidence, a coach\nshould not expect an improvement in\nRE if they instruct a runner to switch\nhabitual foot strike pattern.\nVERTICAL OSCILLATION\nThe body’s COM goes through a vertical\ntranslation throughout the gait cycle. It\npeaks during the aerial phase and rea-\nches its lowest point during maximum\nleg compression in stance phase. This\noverall vertical displacement is termed\nVOSC. When VOSC is increased during\nthe running cycle, RE is compromised\n(19,86). In fact, VOSC has been reported\nto explain 27.7% of RE variation (32)\nwith smaller oscillation being character-\nistic of better perf", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "FS runners and\nthat the 2 foot strike patterns are equally\neconomical in habitual NRFS runners\n(37). This is echoed by Melcher et al.\n(56), who reported that runners (all\nhabitual RFS) were 5.6% more econom-\nical when using an RFS at both the\nbeginni"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0008_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8aad0a08912752f2", "text_span_hash": "8aad0a08912752f2", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ing an RFS at both the\nbeginning and end of a long treadmill\nrun. Additional studies report that RE\ndoes not change between foot strike\npatterns (4,22,69). Based on the avail-\nable gait retraining evidence, a coach\nshould not expect an improvement in", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Several investigations have successfully\nmanipulated foot strike in either an\nacute (1 session) (62) or chronic inter-\nvention (12 weeks) (23). Runners dem-\nonstrate the capacity to switch foot\nstrike on instruction; however, there is\nno evidence that switching from RFS to\nNRFS or vice versa will improve RE.\nDirect measurements of RE with run-\nners switching between RFS and NRFS\nsuggest that an RFS may be more eco-\nnomical in habitual RFS runners and\nthat the 2 foot strike patterns are equally\neconomical in habitual NRFS runners\n(37). This is echoed by Melcher et al.\n(56), who reported that runners (all\nhabitual RFS) were 5.6% more econom-\nical when using an RFS at both the\nbeginning and end of a long treadmill\nrun. Additional studies report that RE\ndoes not change between foot strike\npatterns (4,22,69). Based on the avail-\nable gait retraining evidence, a coach\nshould not expect an improvement in\nRE if they instruct a runner to switch\nhabitual foot strike pattern.\nVERTICAL OSCILLATION\nThe body’s COM goes through a vertical\ntranslation throughout the gait cycle. It\npeaks during the aerial phase and rea-\nches its lowest point during maximum\nleg compression in stance phase. This\noverall vertical displacement is termed\nVOSC. When VOSC is increased during\nthe running cycle, RE is compromised\n(19,86). In fact, VOSC has been reported\nto explain 27.7% of RE variation (32)\nwith smaller oscillation being character-\nistic of better perf", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ing an RFS at both the\nbeginning and end of a long treadmill\nrun. Additional studies report that RE\ndoes not change between foot strike\npatterns (4,22,69). Based on the avail-\nable gait retraining evidence, a coach\nshould not expect an improvement in"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0008_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "74dcb0cdaf4e05e4", "text_span_hash": "74dcb0cdaf4e05e4", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "d not expect an improvement in\nRE if they instruct a runner to switch\nhabitual foot strike pattern.\nVERTICAL OSCILLATION\nThe body’s COM goes through a vertical\ntranslation throughout the gait cycle. It\npeaks during the aerial phase and rea-\nches its", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Several investigations have successfully\nmanipulated foot strike in either an\nacute (1 session) (62) or chronic inter-\nvention (12 weeks) (23). Runners dem-\nonstrate the capacity to switch foot\nstrike on instruction; however, there is\nno evidence that switching from RFS to\nNRFS or vice versa will improve RE.\nDirect measurements of RE with run-\nners switching between RFS and NRFS\nsuggest that an RFS may be more eco-\nnomical in habitual RFS runners and\nthat the 2 foot strike patterns are equally\neconomical in habitual NRFS runners\n(37). This is echoed by Melcher et al.\n(56), who reported that runners (all\nhabitual RFS) were 5.6% more econom-\nical when using an RFS at both the\nbeginning and end of a long treadmill\nrun. Additional studies report that RE\ndoes not change between foot strike\npatterns (4,22,69). Based on the avail-\nable gait retraining evidence, a coach\nshould not expect an improvement in\nRE if they instruct a runner to switch\nhabitual foot strike pattern.\nVERTICAL OSCILLATION\nThe body’s COM goes through a vertical\ntranslation throughout the gait cycle. It\npeaks during the aerial phase and rea-\nches its lowest point during maximum\nleg compression in stance phase. This\noverall vertical displacement is termed\nVOSC. When VOSC is increased during\nthe running cycle, RE is compromised\n(19,86). In fact, VOSC has been reported\nto explain 27.7% of RE variation (32)\nwith smaller oscillation being character-\nistic of better perf", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "d not expect an improvement in\nRE if they instruct a runner to switch\nhabitual foot strike pattern.\nVERTICAL OSCILLATION\nThe body’s COM goes through a vertical\ntranslation throughout the gait cycle. It\npeaks during the aerial phase and rea-\nches its"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0008_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c125167414724d8b", "text_span_hash": "c125167414724d8b", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "erial phase and rea-\nches its lowest point during maximum\nleg compression in stance phase. This\noverall vertical displacement is termed\nVOSC. When VOSC is increased during\nthe running cycle, RE is compromised\n(19,86). In fact, VOSC has been reported", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Several investigations have successfully\nmanipulated foot strike in either an\nacute (1 session) (62) or chronic inter-\nvention (12 weeks) (23). Runners dem-\nonstrate the capacity to switch foot\nstrike on instruction; however, there is\nno evidence that switching from RFS to\nNRFS or vice versa will improve RE.\nDirect measurements of RE with run-\nners switching between RFS and NRFS\nsuggest that an RFS may be more eco-\nnomical in habitual RFS runners and\nthat the 2 foot strike patterns are equally\neconomical in habitual NRFS runners\n(37). This is echoed by Melcher et al.\n(56), who reported that runners (all\nhabitual RFS) were 5.6% more econom-\nical when using an RFS at both the\nbeginning and end of a long treadmill\nrun. Additional studies report that RE\ndoes not change between foot strike\npatterns (4,22,69). Based on the avail-\nable gait retraining evidence, a coach\nshould not expect an improvement in\nRE if they instruct a runner to switch\nhabitual foot strike pattern.\nVERTICAL OSCILLATION\nThe body’s COM goes through a vertical\ntranslation throughout the gait cycle. It\npeaks during the aerial phase and rea-\nches its lowest point during maximum\nleg compression in stance phase. This\noverall vertical displacement is termed\nVOSC. When VOSC is increased during\nthe running cycle, RE is compromised\n(19,86). In fact, VOSC has been reported\nto explain 27.7% of RE variation (32)\nwith smaller oscillation being character-\nistic of better perf", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "erial phase and rea-\nches its lowest point during maximum\nleg compression in stance phase. This\noverall vertical displacement is termed\nVOSC. When VOSC is increased during\nthe running cycle, RE is compromised\n(19,86). In fact, VOSC has been reported"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0008_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "496089a0c5348d3b", "text_span_hash": "496089a0c5348d3b", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "fact, VOSC has been reported\nto explain 27.7% of RE variation (32)\nwith smaller oscillation being character-\nistic of better performing runners (91).\nVOSC has also been reported to increase\n17.3% during an exhaustive treadmill run\n(33). It seems tha", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Several investigations have successfully\nmanipulated foot strike in either an\nacute (1 session) (62) or chronic inter-\nvention (12 weeks) (23). Runners dem-\nonstrate the capacity to switch foot\nstrike on instruction; however, there is\nno evidence that switching from RFS to\nNRFS or vice versa will improve RE.\nDirect measurements of RE with run-\nners switching between RFS and NRFS\nsuggest that an RFS may be more eco-\nnomical in habitual RFS runners and\nthat the 2 foot strike patterns are equally\neconomical in habitual NRFS runners\n(37). This is echoed by Melcher et al.\n(56), who reported that runners (all\nhabitual RFS) were 5.6% more econom-\nical when using an RFS at both the\nbeginning and end of a long treadmill\nrun. Additional studies report that RE\ndoes not change between foot strike\npatterns (4,22,69). Based on the avail-\nable gait retraining evidence, a coach\nshould not expect an improvement in\nRE if they instruct a runner to switch\nhabitual foot strike pattern.\nVERTICAL OSCILLATION\nThe body’s COM goes through a vertical\ntranslation throughout the gait cycle. It\npeaks during the aerial phase and rea-\nches its lowest point during maximum\nleg compression in stance phase. This\noverall vertical displacement is termed\nVOSC. When VOSC is increased during\nthe running cycle, RE is compromised\n(19,86). In fact, VOSC has been reported\nto explain 27.7% of RE variation (32)\nwith smaller oscillation being character-\nistic of better perf", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "fact, VOSC has been reported\nto explain 27.7% of RE variation (32)\nwith smaller oscillation being character-\nistic of better performing runners (91).\nVOSC has also been reported to increase\n17.3% during an exhaustive treadmill run\n(33). It seems tha"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0008_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "d37ae4edf231aa63", "text_span_hash": "d37ae4edf231aa63", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "eadmill run\n(33). It seems that a reduced VOSC may\nbe beneﬁcial for RE; however, a norma-\ntive set of VOSC across a wide popula-\ntion of runners and speeds is currently\nlacking for informed decision-making.\nEvidence supports that runners can\nretrain", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Several investigations have successfully\nmanipulated foot strike in either an\nacute (1 session) (62) or chronic inter-\nvention (12 weeks) (23). Runners dem-\nonstrate the capacity to switch foot\nstrike on instruction; however, there is\nno evidence that switching from RFS to\nNRFS or vice versa will improve RE.\nDirect measurements of RE with run-\nners switching between RFS and NRFS\nsuggest that an RFS may be more eco-\nnomical in habitual RFS runners and\nthat the 2 foot strike patterns are equally\neconomical in habitual NRFS runners\n(37). This is echoed by Melcher et al.\n(56), who reported that runners (all\nhabitual RFS) were 5.6% more econom-\nical when using an RFS at both the\nbeginning and end of a long treadmill\nrun. Additional studies report that RE\ndoes not change between foot strike\npatterns (4,22,69). Based on the avail-\nable gait retraining evidence, a coach\nshould not expect an improvement in\nRE if they instruct a runner to switch\nhabitual foot strike pattern.\nVERTICAL OSCILLATION\nThe body’s COM goes through a vertical\ntranslation throughout the gait cycle. It\npeaks during the aerial phase and rea-\nches its lowest point during maximum\nleg compression in stance phase. This\noverall vertical displacement is termed\nVOSC. When VOSC is increased during\nthe running cycle, RE is compromised\n(19,86). In fact, VOSC has been reported\nto explain 27.7% of RE variation (32)\nwith smaller oscillation being character-\nistic of better perf", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "eadmill run\n(33). It seems that a reduced VOSC may\nbe beneﬁcial for RE; however, a norma-\ntive set of VOSC across a wide popula-\ntion of runners and speeds is currently\nlacking for informed decision-making.\nEvidence supports that runners can\nretrain"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0008_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7a062fa03058a728", "text_span_hash": "7a062fa03058a728", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "orts that runners can\nretrain VOSC through either verbal\n(2,86), visual (19), or visual plus auditory\ncueing (29). This raises the prospect of\nretraining runners to limit VOSC and\nimprove RE. Copriviza (19) provided\nvisual VOSC feedback to 12 recreat", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Several investigations have successfully\nmanipulated foot strike in either an\nacute (1 session) (62) or chronic inter-\nvention (12 weeks) (23). Runners dem-\nonstrate the capacity to switch foot\nstrike on instruction; however, there is\nno evidence that switching from RFS to\nNRFS or vice versa will improve RE.\nDirect measurements of RE with run-\nners switching between RFS and NRFS\nsuggest that an RFS may be more eco-\nnomical in habitual RFS runners and\nthat the 2 foot strike patterns are equally\neconomical in habitual NRFS runners\n(37). This is echoed by Melcher et al.\n(56), who reported that runners (all\nhabitual RFS) were 5.6% more econom-\nical when using an RFS at both the\nbeginning and end of a long treadmill\nrun. Additional studies report that RE\ndoes not change between foot strike\npatterns (4,22,69). Based on the avail-\nable gait retraining evidence, a coach\nshould not expect an improvement in\nRE if they instruct a runner to switch\nhabitual foot strike pattern.\nVERTICAL OSCILLATION\nThe body’s COM goes through a vertical\ntranslation throughout the gait cycle. It\npeaks during the aerial phase and rea-\nches its lowest point during maximum\nleg compression in stance phase. This\noverall vertical displacement is termed\nVOSC. When VOSC is increased during\nthe running cycle, RE is compromised\n(19,86). In fact, VOSC has been reported\nto explain 27.7% of RE variation (32)\nwith smaller oscillation being character-\nistic of better perf", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "orts that runners can\nretrain VOSC through either verbal\n(2,86), visual (19), or visual plus auditory\ncueing (29). This raises the prospect of\nretraining runners to limit VOSC and\nimprove RE. Copriviza (19) provided\nvisual VOSC feedback to 12 recreat"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0008_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "fc6e7179638bc4bf", "text_span_hash": "fc6e7179638bc4bf", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "al VOSC feedback to 12 recreational\nrunners under 5 conditions. Runners\nwere most economical at their preferred\nVOSC and signiﬁcantly less economical\nwhen VOSC was increased. When run-\nners reduced VOSC to 50% and 75% of\nthe preferred VOSC, RE was no", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Several investigations have successfully\nmanipulated foot strike in either an\nacute (1 session) (62) or chronic inter-\nvention (12 weeks) (23). Runners dem-\nonstrate the capacity to switch foot\nstrike on instruction; however, there is\nno evidence that switching from RFS to\nNRFS or vice versa will improve RE.\nDirect measurements of RE with run-\nners switching between RFS and NRFS\nsuggest that an RFS may be more eco-\nnomical in habitual RFS runners and\nthat the 2 foot strike patterns are equally\neconomical in habitual NRFS runners\n(37). This is echoed by Melcher et al.\n(56), who reported that runners (all\nhabitual RFS) were 5.6% more econom-\nical when using an RFS at both the\nbeginning and end of a long treadmill\nrun. Additional studies report that RE\ndoes not change between foot strike\npatterns (4,22,69). Based on the avail-\nable gait retraining evidence, a coach\nshould not expect an improvement in\nRE if they instruct a runner to switch\nhabitual foot strike pattern.\nVERTICAL OSCILLATION\nThe body’s COM goes through a vertical\ntranslation throughout the gait cycle. It\npeaks during the aerial phase and rea-\nches its lowest point during maximum\nleg compression in stance phase. This\noverall vertical displacement is termed\nVOSC. When VOSC is increased during\nthe running cycle, RE is compromised\n(19,86). In fact, VOSC has been reported\nto explain 27.7% of RE variation (32)\nwith smaller oscillation being character-\nistic of better perf", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "al VOSC feedback to 12 recreational\nrunners under 5 conditions. Runners\nwere most economical at their preferred\nVOSC and signiﬁcantly less economical\nwhen VOSC was increased. When run-\nners reduced VOSC to 50% and 75% of\nthe preferred VOSC, RE was no"}
