peaks during the aerial phase and rea-\nches its lowest point during maximum\nleg compression in stance phase. This\noverall vertical displacement is termed\nVOSC. When VOSC is increased during\nthe running cycle, RE is compromised\n(19,86). In fact, VOSC has been reported\nto explain 27.7% of RE variation (32)\nwith smaller oscillation being character-\nistic of better perf", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "the preferred VOSC, RE was not signif-\nicantly different from the preferred con-\ndition. Not surprisingly, when VOSC\nwas decreased, there was a concomitant\nincrease in stride rate. Adams et al. (2)\nrecently showed in 20 recreational run-\nners that V"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0008_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4b8bdb19f4928007", "text_span_hash": "4b8bdb19f4928007", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "recreational run-\nners that VOSC could be reduced by\neither having the runner increase stride\nrate or by instructing them to limit\nVOSC. In both interventions, average\nVOSC was reduced by 17.1 and 21.7%,\nrespectively. Although both retraining\nmethod", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Several investigations have successfully\nmanipulated foot strike in either an\nacute (1 session) (62) or chronic inter-\nvention (12 weeks) (23). Runners dem-\nonstrate the capacity to switch foot\nstrike on instruction; however, there is\nno evidence that switching from RFS to\nNRFS or vice versa will improve RE.\nDirect measurements of RE with run-\nners switching between RFS and NRFS\nsuggest that an RFS may be more eco-\nnomical in habitual RFS runners and\nthat the 2 foot strike patterns are equally\neconomical in habitual NRFS runners\n(37). This is echoed by Melcher et al.\n(56), who reported that runners (all\nhabitual RFS) were 5.6% more econom-\nical when using an RFS at both the\nbeginning and end of a long treadmill\nrun. Additional studies report that RE\ndoes not change between foot strike\npatterns (4,22,69). Based on the avail-\nable gait retraining evidence, a coach\nshould not expect an improvement in\nRE if they instruct a runner to switch\nhabitual foot strike pattern.\nVERTICAL OSCILLATION\nThe body’s COM goes through a vertical\ntranslation throughout the gait cycle. It\npeaks during the aerial phase and rea-\nches its lowest point during maximum\nleg compression in stance phase. This\noverall vertical displacement is termed\nVOSC. When VOSC is increased during\nthe running cycle, RE is compromised\n(19,86). In fact, VOSC has been reported\nto explain 27.7% of RE variation (32)\nwith smaller oscillation being character-\nistic of better perf", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "recreational run-\nners that VOSC could be reduced by\neither having the runner increase stride\nrate or by instructing them to limit\nVOSC. In both interventions, average\nVOSC was reduced by 17.1 and 21.7%,\nrespectively. Although both retraining\nmethod"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0008_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "af0d2d373a0e8e2f", "text_span_hash": "af0d2d373a0e8e2f", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "lthough both retraining\nmethods signiﬁcantly increased stride\nrate and reduced VOSC, they had dif-\nferent effects on GCT. When runners\nreduce VOSC and GCT. When runners\nfocus on reducing VOSC, they increase\ncadence and increase GCT. Because it\nincrea", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Several investigations have successfully\nmanipulated foot strike in either an\nacute (1 session) (62) or chronic inter-\nvention (12 weeks) (23). Runners dem-\nonstrate the capacity to switch foot\nstrike on instruction; however, there is\nno evidence that switching from RFS to\nNRFS or vice versa will improve RE.\nDirect measurements of RE with run-\nners switching between RFS and NRFS\nsuggest that an RFS may be more eco-\nnomical in habitual RFS runners and\nthat the 2 foot strike patterns are equally\neconomical in habitual NRFS runners\n(37). This is echoed by Melcher et al.\n(56), who reported that runners (all\nhabitual RFS) were 5.6% more econom-\nical when using an RFS at both the\nbeginning and end of a long treadmill\nrun. Additional studies report that RE\ndoes not change between foot strike\npatterns (4,22,69). Based on the avail-\nable gait retraining evidence, a coach\nshould not expect an improvement in\nRE if they instruct a runner to switch\nhabitual foot strike pattern.\nVERTICAL OSCILLATION\nThe body’s COM goes through a vertical\ntranslation throughout the gait cycle. It\npeaks during the aerial phase and rea-\nches its lowest point during maximum\nleg compression in stance phase. This\noverall vertical displacement is termed\nVOSC. When VOSC is increased during\nthe running cycle, RE is compromised\n(19,86). In fact, VOSC has been reported\nto explain 27.7% of RE variation (32)\nwith smaller oscillation being character-\nistic of better perf", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "lthough both retraining\nmethods signiﬁcantly increased stride\nrate and reduced VOSC, they had dif-\nferent effects on GCT. When runners\nreduce VOSC and GCT. When runners\nfocus on reducing VOSC, they increase\ncadence and increase GCT. Because it\nincrea"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0008_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c97ee72113823286", "text_span_hash": "c97ee72113823286", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ncrease GCT. Because it\nincreased leg stiffness has been shown\nto increase stride rate while limiting\nboth GCTand VOSC (30), it is probable\nthat the interventions had different ef-\nfects on leg stiffness.\nThese results raise the possibility of gait\nr", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Several investigations have successfully\nmanipulated foot strike in either an\nacute (1 session) (62) or chronic inter-\nvention (12 weeks) (23). Runners dem-\nonstrate the capacity to switch foot\nstrike on instruction; however, there is\nno evidence that switching from RFS to\nNRFS or vice versa will improve RE.\nDirect measurements of RE with run-\nners switching between RFS and NRFS\nsuggest that an RFS may be more eco-\nnomical in habitual RFS runners and\nthat the 2 foot strike patterns are equally\neconomical in habitual NRFS runners\n(37). This is echoed by Melcher et al.\n(56), who reported that runners (all\nhabitual RFS) were 5.6% more econom-\nical when using an RFS at both the\nbeginning and end of a long treadmill\nrun. Additional studies report that RE\ndoes not change between foot strike\npatterns (4,22,69). Based on the avail-\nable gait retraining evidence, a coach\nshould not expect an improvement in\nRE if they instruct a runner to switch\nhabitual foot strike pattern.\nVERTICAL OSCILLATION\nThe body’s COM goes through a vertical\ntranslation throughout the gait cycle. It\npeaks during the aerial phase and rea-\nches its lowest point during maximum\nleg compression in stance phase. This\noverall vertical displacement is termed\nVOSC. When VOSC is increased during\nthe running cycle, RE is compromised\n(19,86). In fact, VOSC has been reported\nto explain 27.7% of RE variation (32)\nwith smaller oscillation being character-\nistic of better perf", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ncrease GCT. Because it\nincreased leg stiffness has been shown\nto increase stride rate while limiting\nboth GCTand VOSC (30), it is probable\nthat the interventions had different ef-\nfects on leg stiffness.\nThese results raise the possibility of gait\nr"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0008_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4193b5bfd44b72ee", "text_span_hash": "4193b5bfd44b72ee", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "aise the possibility of gait\nretraining to improve RE in a runner\nwith a high VOSC. However, identify-\ning a suitable runner is challenging\nbecause no target value for VOSC is\ncurrently known. Wearable technology\nmay provide valid and reliable measur", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Several investigations have successfully\nmanipulated foot strike in either an\nacute (1 session) (62) or chronic inter-\nvention (12 weeks) (23). Runners dem-\nonstrate the capacity to switch foot\nstrike on instruction; however, there is\nno evidence that switching from RFS to\nNRFS or vice versa will improve RE.\nDirect measurements of RE with run-\nners switching between RFS and NRFS\nsuggest that an RFS may be more eco-\nnomical in habitual RFS runners and\nthat the 2 foot strike patterns are equally\neconomical in habitual NRFS runners\n(37). This is echoed by Melcher et al.\n(56), who reported that runners (all\nhabitual RFS) were 5.6% more econom-\nical when using an RFS at both the\nbeginning and end of a long treadmill\nrun. Additional studies report that RE\ndoes not change between foot strike\npatterns (4,22,69). Based on the avail-\nable gait retraining evidence, a coach\nshould not expect an improvement in\nRE if they instruct a runner to switch\nhabitual foot strike pattern.\nVERTICAL OSCILLATION\nThe body’s COM goes through a vertical\ntranslation throughout the gait cycle. It\npeaks during the aerial phase and rea-\nches its lowest point during maximum\nleg compression in stance phase. This\noverall vertical displacement is termed\nVOSC. When VOSC is increased during\nthe running cycle, RE is compromised\n(19,86). In fact, VOSC has been reported\nto explain 27.7% of RE variation (32)\nwith smaller oscillation being character-\nistic of better perf", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "aise the possibility of gait\nretraining to improve RE in a runner\nwith a high VOSC. However, identify-\ning a suitable runner is challenging\nbecause no target value for VOSC is\ncurrently known. Wearable technology\nmay provide valid and reliable measur"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0008_c0020", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "701d2cda1104c8cb", "text_span_hash": "701d2cda1104c8cb", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 20, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "iana\net al. (54) recently reported that both\nshorter GCT (greater reliance on elastic\nenergy, increased VOSC) and longer\nGCT (limiting VOSC) were effective\nstrategies for economical gait. No re-\nported gait retraining intervention has\nAdams et al. (2", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Several investigations have successfully\nmanipulated foot strike in either an\nacute (1 session) (62) or chronic inter-\nvention (12 weeks) (23). Runners dem-\nonstrate the capacity to switch foot\nstrike on instruction; however, there is\nno evidence that switching from RFS to\nNRFS or vice versa will improve RE.\nDirect measurements of RE with run-\nners switching between RFS and NRFS\nsuggest that an RFS may be more eco-\nnomical in habitual RFS runners and\nthat the 2 foot strike patterns are equally\neconomical in habitual NRFS runners\n(37). This is echoed by Melcher et al.\n(56), who reported that runners (all\nhabitual RFS) were 5.6% more econom-\nical when using an RFS at both the\nbeginning and end of a long treadmill\nrun. Additional studies report that RE\ndoes not change between foot strike\npatterns (4,22,69). Based on the avail-\nable gait retraining evidence, a coach\nshould not expect an improvement in\nRE if they instruct a runner to switch\nhabitual foot strike pattern.\nVERTICAL OSCILLATION\nThe body’s COM goes through a vertical\ntranslation throughout the gait cycle. It\npeaks during the aerial phase and rea-\nches its lowest point during maximum\nleg compression in stance phase. This\noverall vertical displacement is termed\nVOSC. When VOSC is increased during\nthe running cycle, RE is compromised\n(19,86). In fact, VOSC has been reported\nto explain 27.7% of RE variation (32)\nwith smaller oscillation being character-\nistic of better perf", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "iana\net al. (54) recently reported that both\nshorter GCT (greater reliance on elastic\nenergy, increased VOSC) and longer\nGCT (limiting VOSC) were effective\nstrategies for economical gait. No re-\nported gait retraining intervention has\nAdams et al. (2"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0008_c0021", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e92a6771b13bbc72", "text_span_hash": "e92a6771b13bbc72", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 21, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "tervention has\nAdams et al. (2) recently demonstrated\nthat interventions targeting either stride\nrate or VOSC will signiﬁcantly change\nGCT. Based on the available evidence,\na coach should not explicitly instruct\na runner to decrease or increase GCT\nw", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Several investigations have successfully\nmanipulated foot strike in either an\nacute (1 session) (62) or chronic inter-\nvention (12 weeks) (23). Runners dem-\nonstrate the capacity to switch foot\nstrike on instruction; however, there is\nno evidence that switching from RFS to\nNRFS or vice versa will improve RE.\nDirect measurements of RE with run-\nners switching between RFS and NRFS\nsuggest that an RFS may be more eco-\nnomical in habitual RFS runners and\nthat the 2 foot strike patterns are equally\neconomical in habitual NRFS runners\n(37). This is echoed by Melcher et al.\n(56), who reported that runners (all\nhabitual RFS) were 5.6% more econom-\nical when using an RFS at both the\nbeginning and end of a long treadmill\nrun. Additional studies report that RE\ndoes not change between foot strike\npatterns (4,22,69). Based on the avail-\nable gait retraining evidence, a coach\nshould not expect an improvement in\nRE if they instruct a runner to switch\nhabitual foot strike pattern.\nVERTICAL OSCILLATION\nThe body’s COM goes through a vertical\ntranslation throughout the gait cycle. It\npeaks during the aerial phase and rea-\nches its lowest point during maximum\nleg compression in stance phase. This\noverall vertical displacement is termed\nVOSC. When VOSC is increased during\nthe running cycle, RE is compromised\n(19,86). In fact, VOSC has been reported\nto explain 27.7% of RE variation (32)\nwith smaller oscillation being character-\nistic of better perf", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "tervention has\nAdams et al. (2) recently demonstrated\nthat interventions targeting either stride\nrate or VOSC will signiﬁcantly change\nGCT. Based on the available evidence,\na coach should not explicitly instruct\na runner to decrease or increase GCT\nw"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0008_c0022", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "31ca52f10ce74cc5", "text_span_hash": "31ca52f10ce74cc5", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 22, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "to decrease or increase GCT\nwith the goal of improving RE.\nArm swing during running is critical to\ncounterbalance the angular momen-\ntum produced by the legs (44) and con-\ntributes 5–10% of the body’s lift off the\nground (46). The arms work in an\nop", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Several investigations have successfully\nmanipulated foot strike in either an\nacute (1 session) (62) or chronic inter-\nvention (12 weeks) (23). Runners dem-\nonstrate the capacity to switch foot\nstrike on instruction; however, there is\nno evidence that switching from RFS to\nNRFS or vice versa will improve RE.\nDirect measurements of RE with run-\nners switching between RFS and NRFS\nsuggest that an RFS may be more eco-\nnomical in habitual RFS runners and\nthat the 2 foot strike patterns are equally\neconomical in habitual NRFS runners\n(37). This is echoed by Melcher et al.\n(56), who reported that runners (all\nhabitual RFS) were 5.6% more econom-\nical when using an RFS at both the\nbeginning and end of a long treadmill\nrun. Additional studies report that RE\ndoes not change between foot strike\npatterns (4,22,69). Based on the avail-\nable gait retraining evidence, a coach\nshould not expect an improvement in\nRE if they instruct a runner to switch\nhabitual foot strike pattern.\nVERTICAL OSCILLATION\nThe body’s COM goes through a vertical\ntranslation throughout the gait cycle. It\npeaks during the aerial phase and rea-\nches its lowest point during maximum\nleg compression in stance phase. This\noverall vertical displacement is termed\nVOSC. When VOSC is increased during\nthe running cycle, RE is compromised\n(19,86). In fact, VOSC has been reported\nto explain 27.7% of RE variation (32)\nwith smaller oscillation being character-\nistic of better perf", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "to decrease or increase GCT\nwith the goal of improving RE.\nArm swing during running is critical to\ncounterbalance the angular momen-\ntum produced by the legs (44) and con-\ntributes 5–10% of the body’s lift off the\nground (46). The arms work in an\nop"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0008_c0023", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "dd871438a30f696d", "text_span_hash": "dd871438a30f696d", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 23, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "d (46). The arms work in an\nopposite direction to leg motion and\ntheir movement helps to limit rota-\ntional contributions from the head\nand trunk (5). When the arms are\nintentionally restricted during running\nby placing them on the head or behind\nthe", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Several investigations have successfully\nmanipulated foot strike in either an\nacute (1 session) (62) or chronic inter-\nvention (12 weeks) (23). Runners dem-\nonstrate the capacity to switch foot\nstrike on instruction; however, there is\nno evidence that switching from RFS to\nNRFS or vice versa will improve RE.\nDirect measurements of RE with run-\nners switching between RFS and NRFS\nsuggest that an RFS may be more eco-\nnomical in habitual RFS runners and\nthat the 2 foot strike patterns are equally\neconomical in habitual NRFS runners\n(37). This is echoed by Melcher et al.\n(56), who reported that runners (all\nhabitual RFS) were 5.6% more econom-\nical when using an RFS at both the\nbeginning and end of a long treadmill\nrun. Additional studies report that RE\ndoes not change between foot strike\npatterns (4,22,69). Based on the avail-\nable gait retraining evidence, a coach\nshould not expect an improvement in\nRE if they instruct a runner to switch\nhabitual foot strike pattern.\nVERTICAL OSCILLATION\nThe body’s COM goes through a vertical\ntranslation throughout the gait cycle. It\npeaks during the aerial phase and rea-\nches its lowest point during maximum\nleg compression in stance phase. This\noverall vertical displacement is termed\nVOSC. When VOSC is increased during\nthe running cycle, RE is compromised\n(19,86). In fact, VOSC has been reported\nto explain 27.7% of RE variation (32)\nwith smaller oscillation being character-\nistic of better perf", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "d (46). The arms work in an\nopposite direction to leg motion and\ntheir movement helps to limit rota-\ntional contributions from the head\nand trunk (5). When the arms are\nintentionally restricted during running\nby placing them on the head or behind\nthe"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0008_c0027", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2aebdc29e3672cff", "text_span_hash": "2aebdc29e3672cff", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 27, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "plane arm\nmotion in the 10 recreational runners\nthey tested. They concluded that this\nto minimize the side-to-side COM\nmovement. Hinrichs (45) found that 2\nStrength and Conditioning Journal | www.nsca-scj.com\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditi", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Several investigations have successfully\nmanipulated foot strike in either an\nacute (1 session) (62) or chronic inter-\nvention (12 weeks) (23). Runners dem-\nonstrate the capacity to switch foot\nstrike on instruction; however, there is\nno evidence that switching from RFS to\nNRFS or vice versa will improve RE.\nDirect measurements of RE with run-\nners switching between RFS and NRFS\nsuggest that an RFS may be more eco-\nnomical in habitual RFS runners and\nthat the 2 foot strike patterns are equally\neconomical in habitual NRFS runners\n(37). This is echoed by Melcher et al.\n(56), who reported that runners (all\nhabitual RFS) were 5.6% more econom-\nical when using an RFS at both the\nbeginning and end of a long treadmill\nrun. Additional studies report that RE\ndoes not change between foot strike\npatterns (4,22,69). Based on the avail-\nable gait retraining evidence, a coach\nshould not expect an improvement in\nRE if they instruct a runner to switch\nhabitual foot strike pattern.\nVERTICAL OSCILLATION\nThe body’s COM goes through a vertical\ntranslation throughout the gait cycle. It\npeaks during the aerial phase and rea-\nches its lowest point during maximum\nleg compression in stance phase. This\noverall vertical displacement is termed\nVOSC. When VOSC is increased during\nthe running cycle, RE is compromised\n(19,86). In fact, VOSC has been reported\nto explain 27.7% of RE variation (32)\nwith smaller oscillation being character-\nistic of better perf", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "plane arm\nmotion in the 10 recreational runners\nthey tested. They concluded that this\nto minimize the side-to-side COM\nmovement. Hinrichs (45) found that 2\nStrength and Conditioning Journal | www.nsca-scj.com\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditi"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0009_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "281c9dd0e34a45c5", "text_span_hash": "281c9dd0e34a45c5", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "of 10 subjects demonstrated asymmet-\nrical arm motion that potentially com-\npensated for asymmetries in the rest of\nthe body. Clinically, visual observation\nof asymmetrical arm action may be\na clue for the coach that a runner\nmay have other imbalance", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "of 10 subjects demonstrated asymmet-\nrical arm motion that potentially com-\npensated for asymmetries in the rest of\nthe body. Clinically, visual observation\nof asymmetrical arm action may be\na clue for the coach that a runner\nmay have other imbalances that the\narms may be compensating for. This is\nan important consideration for a runner\nreturning from injury or during fatigued\nrunning. During a fatiguing run, changes\nto arm swing would most likely be due\nto a compensatory strategy as opposed\nto local arm muscle fatigue (79). It is\nclear that arm movement is dependent\non the motion of other body segments\nand that a retraining strategy to adjust\narm swing would have both kinematic\nand kinetic consequences. However, the\nevidence currently does not support ad-\njusting arm swing to improve RE.\nBecause hill running requires increased\nforces (36) and arm motion has been\n(58), the “lifting” motion of the arms\nmay be beneﬁcial during hill running\nand could be something that coaches\nINDIVIDUALIZED APPROACH\nAlthough it is quite clear that there is no\nbasis for a coach implementing a global\ngait retraining for an entire team, this\ndoes not imply that biomechanical inter-\nventions cannot improve RE for individ-\nual runners. Morgan et al. (63) provided\nevidence that this is indeed possible. Of\nall the reviewed gait interventions, this is\nthe only study that used a combined bio-\nmechanical and physiological approach\nto screen runners. They d", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "of 10 subjects demonstrated asymmet-\nrical arm motion that potentially com-\npensated for asymmetries in the rest of\nthe body. Clinically, visual observation\nof asymmetrical arm action may be\na clue for the coach that a runner\nmay have other imbalance"}
