ng a global\ngait retraining for an entire team, this\ndoes not imply that biomechanical inter-\nventions cannot improve RE for individ-\nual runners. Morgan et al. (63) provided\nevidence that this is indeed possible. Of\nall the reviewed gait interventions, this is\nthe only study that used a combined bio-\nmechanical and physiological approach\nto screen runners. They d", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "g would have both kinematic\nand kinetic consequences. However, the\nevidence currently does not support ad-\njusting arm swing to improve RE.\nBecause hill running requires increased\nforces (36) and arm motion has been\n(58), the “lifting” motion of the"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0009_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4ab334d287efebd7", "text_span_hash": "4ab334d287efebd7", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": ", the “lifting” motion of the arms\nmay be beneﬁcial during hill running\nand could be something that coaches\nINDIVIDUALIZED APPROACH\nAlthough it is quite clear that there is no\nbasis for a coach implementing a global\ngait retraining for an entire team", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "of 10 subjects demonstrated asymmet-\nrical arm motion that potentially com-\npensated for asymmetries in the rest of\nthe body. Clinically, visual observation\nof asymmetrical arm action may be\na clue for the coach that a runner\nmay have other imbalances that the\narms may be compensating for. This is\nan important consideration for a runner\nreturning from injury or during fatigued\nrunning. During a fatiguing run, changes\nto arm swing would most likely be due\nto a compensatory strategy as opposed\nto local arm muscle fatigue (79). It is\nclear that arm movement is dependent\non the motion of other body segments\nand that a retraining strategy to adjust\narm swing would have both kinematic\nand kinetic consequences. However, the\nevidence currently does not support ad-\njusting arm swing to improve RE.\nBecause hill running requires increased\nforces (36) and arm motion has been\n(58), the “lifting” motion of the arms\nmay be beneﬁcial during hill running\nand could be something that coaches\nINDIVIDUALIZED APPROACH\nAlthough it is quite clear that there is no\nbasis for a coach implementing a global\ngait retraining for an entire team, this\ndoes not imply that biomechanical inter-\nventions cannot improve RE for individ-\nual runners. Morgan et al. (63) provided\nevidence that this is indeed possible. Of\nall the reviewed gait interventions, this is\nthe only study that used a combined bio-\nmechanical and physiological approach\nto screen runners. They d", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": ", the “lifting” motion of the arms\nmay be beneﬁcial during hill running\nand could be something that coaches\nINDIVIDUALIZED APPROACH\nAlthough it is quite clear that there is no\nbasis for a coach implementing a global\ngait retraining for an entire team"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0009_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "745206ec8f90d685", "text_span_hash": "745206ec8f90d685", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "it interventions, this is\nthe only study that used a combined bio-\nmechanical and physiological approach\nto screen runners. They determined the\nrunner’s optimal stride length from an\ninitial metabolic screening and then re-\ntrained the runner to adop", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "of 10 subjects demonstrated asymmet-\nrical arm motion that potentially com-\npensated for asymmetries in the rest of\nthe body. Clinically, visual observation\nof asymmetrical arm action may be\na clue for the coach that a runner\nmay have other imbalances that the\narms may be compensating for. This is\nan important consideration for a runner\nreturning from injury or during fatigued\nrunning. During a fatiguing run, changes\nto arm swing would most likely be due\nto a compensatory strategy as opposed\nto local arm muscle fatigue (79). It is\nclear that arm movement is dependent\non the motion of other body segments\nand that a retraining strategy to adjust\narm swing would have both kinematic\nand kinetic consequences. However, the\nevidence currently does not support ad-\njusting arm swing to improve RE.\nBecause hill running requires increased\nforces (36) and arm motion has been\n(58), the “lifting” motion of the arms\nmay be beneﬁcial during hill running\nand could be something that coaches\nINDIVIDUALIZED APPROACH\nAlthough it is quite clear that there is no\nbasis for a coach implementing a global\ngait retraining for an entire team, this\ndoes not imply that biomechanical inter-\nventions cannot improve RE for individ-\nual runners. Morgan et al. (63) provided\nevidence that this is indeed possible. Of\nall the reviewed gait interventions, this is\nthe only study that used a combined bio-\nmechanical and physiological approach\nto screen runners. They d", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "it interventions, this is\nthe only study that used a combined bio-\nmechanical and physiological approach\nto screen runners. They determined the\nrunner’s optimal stride length from an\ninitial metabolic screening and then re-\ntrained the runner to adop"}
{"chunk_id": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2_p0009_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_6e63889b32c5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "fe415e281d3022bf", "text_span_hash": "fe415e281d3022bf", "source_file": "Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Matthew F. Moran, PhD1,2 and Justin C. Wager, PhD1,2.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "re-\ntrained the runner to adopt a more eco-\nnomical stride pattern. Therefore, it is\nnot surprising that all but one (23) of\nthe other studies reviewed here reported\nno change to RE after gait retraining. It is\nquite possible, and likely, that some o", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "of 10 subjects demonstrated asymmet-\nrical arm motion that potentially com-\npensated for asymmetries in the rest of\nthe body. Clinically, visual observation\nof asymmetrical arm action may be\na clue for the coach that a runner\nmay have other imbalances that the\narms may be compensating for. This is\nan important consideration for a runner\nreturning from injury or during fatigued\nrunning. During a fatiguing run, changes\nto arm swing would most likely be due\nto a compensatory strategy as opposed\nto local arm muscle fatigue (79). It is\nclear that arm movement is dependent\non the motion of other body segments\nand that a retraining strategy to adjust\narm swing would have both kinematic\nand kinetic consequences. However, the\nevidence currently does not support ad-\njusting arm swing to improve RE.\nBecause hill running requires increased\nforces (36) and arm motion has been\n(58), the “lifting” motion of the arms\nmay be beneﬁcial during hill running\nand could be something that coaches\nINDIVIDUALIZED APPROACH\nAlthough it is quite clear that there is no\nbasis for a coach implementing a global\ngait retraining for an entire team, this\ndoes not imply that biomechanical inter-\nventions cannot improve RE for individ-\nual runners. Morgan et al. (63) provided\nevidence that this is indeed possible. Of\nall the reviewed gait interventions, this is\nthe only study that used a combined bio-\nmechanical and physiological approach\nto screen runners. They d", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "re-\ntrained the runner to adopt a more eco-\nnomical stride pattern. Therefore, it is\nnot surprising that all but one (23) of\nthe other studies reviewed here reported\nno change to RE after gait retraining. It is\nquite possible, and likely, that some o"}
