se factors include sampling methods, di-\nurnal and seasonal variations of catecholamine excretion,\nand sex difference effects. Because the relationship between\n24 h or nocturnal catecholamine urinary excretion and per-\nformance or training monitoring is inconclusive, it is thu", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "-6 and tumor necro-\nsis factor-alpha, have been recently investigated as possi-\nbilities for the monitoring of training (67). The authors\nconcluded that although some of these parameters measured\nin the fasting state or postexercise may provide infor"}
{"chunk_id": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining_p0006_c0026", "source_registry_id": "src_da12131b8c41", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4166f94456816c7f", "text_span_hash": "4166f94456816c7f", "source_file": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 26, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "postexercise may provide informa-\ntion about energetic regulatory mechanisms and may change\nafter heavy training or inadequate recovery, there are no\nstudies supporting the possible suitability of these variables\nas markers of training stress or for", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "cortisol concentration and 24-h urinary free cortisol excre-\ntion in resting endurance-trained men are similar to those of\nage-matched sedentary subjects (29,53,74). Because urinary\nfree cortisol represents an integrated measure of the 24-h\ncortisol secretion, this is in accordance with the previously\nreported normal diurnal HPA axis rhythm in endurance-\ntrained men (28,29). Finally, endurance-trained men main-\ntain the seasonal rhythmicity of cortisol excretion; as in\nsedentary men, the highest concentrations of urinary corti-\nsol, morning plasma cortisol, and saliva cortisol are ob-\nserved during autumn and winter compared with spring and\nsummer (53). Therefore, it can be concluded that resting\ncortisol is not a useful measurement.\nThere is no consensus about plasma, 24-h, or overnight\nurinary excretion of catecholamines for monitoring the ef-\nfect of the training load and/or an overload. Some studies\nreport an increase, a decrease, or no change of urinary cat-\necholamine excretion (for a review see Duclos [25]) with\nsuccessful training, OR or OTS. Factors other than training\nload influence secretion and could result in variations be-\ntween studies; these factors include sampling methods, di-\nurnal and seasonal variations of catecholamine excretion,\nand sex difference effects. Because the relationship between\n24 h or nocturnal catecholamine urinary excretion and per-\nformance or training monitoring is inconclusive, it is thu", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "postexercise may provide informa-\ntion about energetic regulatory mechanisms and may change\nafter heavy training or inadequate recovery, there are no\nstudies supporting the possible suitability of these variables\nas markers of training stress or for"}
{"chunk_id": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining_p0006_c0027", "source_registry_id": "src_da12131b8c41", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "6481a0ed3f9c4e3d", "text_span_hash": "6481a0ed3f9c4e3d", "source_file": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 27, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ers of training stress or for the prevention or diag-\nnosis of OR or OTS.\nIn conclusion, the endocrine system is one of the major\nsystems involved in the responses to acute stress and adap-\ntation to chronic stress. A great diversity of mechanisms is", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "cortisol concentration and 24-h urinary free cortisol excre-\ntion in resting endurance-trained men are similar to those of\nage-matched sedentary subjects (29,53,74). Because urinary\nfree cortisol represents an integrated measure of the 24-h\ncortisol secretion, this is in accordance with the previously\nreported normal diurnal HPA axis rhythm in endurance-\ntrained men (28,29). Finally, endurance-trained men main-\ntain the seasonal rhythmicity of cortisol excretion; as in\nsedentary men, the highest concentrations of urinary corti-\nsol, morning plasma cortisol, and saliva cortisol are ob-\nserved during autumn and winter compared with spring and\nsummer (53). Therefore, it can be concluded that resting\ncortisol is not a useful measurement.\nThere is no consensus about plasma, 24-h, or overnight\nurinary excretion of catecholamines for monitoring the ef-\nfect of the training load and/or an overload. Some studies\nreport an increase, a decrease, or no change of urinary cat-\necholamine excretion (for a review see Duclos [25]) with\nsuccessful training, OR or OTS. Factors other than training\nload influence secretion and could result in variations be-\ntween studies; these factors include sampling methods, di-\nurnal and seasonal variations of catecholamine excretion,\nand sex difference effects. Because the relationship between\n24 h or nocturnal catecholamine urinary excretion and per-\nformance or training monitoring is inconclusive, it is thu", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ers of training stress or for the prevention or diag-\nnosis of OR or OTS.\nIn conclusion, the endocrine system is one of the major\nsystems involved in the responses to acute stress and adap-\ntation to chronic stress. A great diversity of mechanisms is"}
{"chunk_id": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining_p0007_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_da12131b8c41", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b704f1ce69f51e5e", "text_span_hash": "b704f1ce69f51e5e", "source_file": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "s, the hormonal response will depend on\nthe phase of the menstrual cycle;\n\u0001 Aerobic and resistance protocols typically elicit different\nendocrine responses;\n\u0001 Hormone concentrations at rest and after stimulation (ex-\nercise = acute stimulus) respond", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "and develop symptoms of OTS. Further studies using mul-\ntiple exercise tests and/or multiple hormone analyses will\nbe necessary for evaluating the possibility of a hormonal\ndiagnostic test for OR/OTS.\nThe problems with hormonal data are as follows:\n\u0001 Many factors affect blood hormone concentrations, and\nthese include factors linked to sampling conditions and/or\nconservation of the sampling: stress of the sampling, intra-\nand interassay coefficient of variability;\n\u0001 Food intake (nutrient composition and/or pre- vs postmeal\nsampling) can modify significantly either the basal concen-\ntration of some hormones (cortisol, Dehydroepiandrosteron-\nsulphate, and total testosterone) or their concentration change\nin response to exercise (cortisol and GH);\n\u0001 Pulsatility of the secretion of some hormones, which\nmodulates the tissue sensitivity to these hormones;\n\u0001 In female athletes, the hormonal response will depend on\nthe phase of the menstrual cycle;\n\u0001 Aerobic and resistance protocols typically elicit different\nendocrine responses;\n\u0001 Hormone concentrations at rest and after stimulation (ex-\nercise = acute stimulus) respond differently;\n\u0001 Diurnal and seasonal variations of the hormones;\n\u0001 Stress-induced measures (exercise, pro-hormones, etc) need\nto be compared with baseline measures from the same\n\u0001 Poor reproducibility and feasibility of some techniques\nused to measure some hormones (e.g., free testosterone by\nradio immunoassay instead o", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "s, the hormonal response will depend on\nthe phase of the menstrual cycle;\n\u0001 Aerobic and resistance protocols typically elicit different\nendocrine responses;\n\u0001 Hormone concentrations at rest and after stimulation (ex-\nercise = acute stimulus) respond"}
{"chunk_id": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining_p0007_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_da12131b8c41", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3a2f6ba532310c9c", "text_span_hash": "3a2f6ba532310c9c", "source_file": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ise = acute stimulus) respond differently;\n\u0001 Diurnal and seasonal variations of the hormones;\n\u0001 Stress-induced measures (exercise, pro-hormones, etc) need\nto be compared with baseline measures from the same\n\u0001 Poor reproducibility and feasibility of s", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "and develop symptoms of OTS. Further studies using mul-\ntiple exercise tests and/or multiple hormone analyses will\nbe necessary for evaluating the possibility of a hormonal\ndiagnostic test for OR/OTS.\nThe problems with hormonal data are as follows:\n\u0001 Many factors affect blood hormone concentrations, and\nthese include factors linked to sampling conditions and/or\nconservation of the sampling: stress of the sampling, intra-\nand interassay coefficient of variability;\n\u0001 Food intake (nutrient composition and/or pre- vs postmeal\nsampling) can modify significantly either the basal concen-\ntration of some hormones (cortisol, Dehydroepiandrosteron-\nsulphate, and total testosterone) or their concentration change\nin response to exercise (cortisol and GH);\n\u0001 Pulsatility of the secretion of some hormones, which\nmodulates the tissue sensitivity to these hormones;\n\u0001 In female athletes, the hormonal response will depend on\nthe phase of the menstrual cycle;\n\u0001 Aerobic and resistance protocols typically elicit different\nendocrine responses;\n\u0001 Hormone concentrations at rest and after stimulation (ex-\nercise = acute stimulus) respond differently;\n\u0001 Diurnal and seasonal variations of the hormones;\n\u0001 Stress-induced measures (exercise, pro-hormones, etc) need\nto be compared with baseline measures from the same\n\u0001 Poor reproducibility and feasibility of some techniques\nused to measure some hormones (e.g., free testosterone by\nradio immunoassay instead o", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ise = acute stimulus) respond differently;\n\u0001 Diurnal and seasonal variations of the hormones;\n\u0001 Stress-induced measures (exercise, pro-hormones, etc) need\nto be compared with baseline measures from the same\n\u0001 Poor reproducibility and feasibility of s"}
{"chunk_id": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining_p0007_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_da12131b8c41", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8b0adc1ecb28febf", "text_span_hash": "8b0adc1ecb28febf", "source_file": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "decreased sport-specific performance capacity\nwhen the training load is maintained or even increased\n(96,136). Athletes experiencing OTS are usually able to start\na normal training sequence or a race at their normal train-\ning pace but are not able t", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "and develop symptoms of OTS. Further studies using mul-\ntiple exercise tests and/or multiple hormone analyses will\nbe necessary for evaluating the possibility of a hormonal\ndiagnostic test for OR/OTS.\nThe problems with hormonal data are as follows:\n\u0001 Many factors affect blood hormone concentrations, and\nthese include factors linked to sampling conditions and/or\nconservation of the sampling: stress of the sampling, intra-\nand interassay coefficient of variability;\n\u0001 Food intake (nutrient composition and/or pre- vs postmeal\nsampling) can modify significantly either the basal concen-\ntration of some hormones (cortisol, Dehydroepiandrosteron-\nsulphate, and total testosterone) or their concentration change\nin response to exercise (cortisol and GH);\n\u0001 Pulsatility of the secretion of some hormones, which\nmodulates the tissue sensitivity to these hormones;\n\u0001 In female athletes, the hormonal response will depend on\nthe phase of the menstrual cycle;\n\u0001 Aerobic and resistance protocols typically elicit different\nendocrine responses;\n\u0001 Hormone concentrations at rest and after stimulation (ex-\nercise = acute stimulus) respond differently;\n\u0001 Diurnal and seasonal variations of the hormones;\n\u0001 Stress-induced measures (exercise, pro-hormones, etc) need\nto be compared with baseline measures from the same\n\u0001 Poor reproducibility and feasibility of some techniques\nused to measure some hormones (e.g., free testosterone by\nradio immunoassay instead o", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "decreased sport-specific performance capacity\nwhen the training load is maintained or even increased\n(96,136). Athletes experiencing OTS are usually able to start\na normal training sequence or a race at their normal train-\ning pace but are not able t"}
{"chunk_id": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining_p0007_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_da12131b8c41", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b141f67abad7bc29", "text_span_hash": "b141f67abad7bc29", "source_file": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "n-\ning pace but are not able to complete the training load they\nare given, or race as usual. The key indicator of OTS can\nbe considered an unexplainable decrease in performance.\nTherefore, an exercise/performance test is considered to be\nessential fo", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "and develop symptoms of OTS. Further studies using mul-\ntiple exercise tests and/or multiple hormone analyses will\nbe necessary for evaluating the possibility of a hormonal\ndiagnostic test for OR/OTS.\nThe problems with hormonal data are as follows:\n\u0001 Many factors affect blood hormone concentrations, and\nthese include factors linked to sampling conditions and/or\nconservation of the sampling: stress of the sampling, intra-\nand interassay coefficient of variability;\n\u0001 Food intake (nutrient composition and/or pre- vs postmeal\nsampling) can modify significantly either the basal concen-\ntration of some hormones (cortisol, Dehydroepiandrosteron-\nsulphate, and total testosterone) or their concentration change\nin response to exercise (cortisol and GH);\n\u0001 Pulsatility of the secretion of some hormones, which\nmodulates the tissue sensitivity to these hormones;\n\u0001 In female athletes, the hormonal response will depend on\nthe phase of the menstrual cycle;\n\u0001 Aerobic and resistance protocols typically elicit different\nendocrine responses;\n\u0001 Hormone concentrations at rest and after stimulation (ex-\nercise = acute stimulus) respond differently;\n\u0001 Diurnal and seasonal variations of the hormones;\n\u0001 Stress-induced measures (exercise, pro-hormones, etc) need\nto be compared with baseline measures from the same\n\u0001 Poor reproducibility and feasibility of some techniques\nused to measure some hormones (e.g., free testosterone by\nradio immunoassay instead o", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "n-\ning pace but are not able to complete the training load they\nare given, or race as usual. The key indicator of OTS can\nbe considered an unexplainable decrease in performance.\nTherefore, an exercise/performance test is considered to be\nessential fo"}
{"chunk_id": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining_p0007_c0017", "source_registry_id": "src_da12131b8c41", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0a68ba67b89626c5", "text_span_hash": "0a68ba67b89626c5", "source_file": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 17, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "eached rugby players (20) but\nincreased after 1 wk of taper.\nThe problems with performance testing are as follows:\n\u0001 Baseline measures are often not available, and therefore,\nthe degree of performance limitation may not be exactly\ndetermined. Individ", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "and develop symptoms of OTS. Further studies using mul-\ntiple exercise tests and/or multiple hormone analyses will\nbe necessary for evaluating the possibility of a hormonal\ndiagnostic test for OR/OTS.\nThe problems with hormonal data are as follows:\n\u0001 Many factors affect blood hormone concentrations, and\nthese include factors linked to sampling conditions and/or\nconservation of the sampling: stress of the sampling, intra-\nand interassay coefficient of variability;\n\u0001 Food intake (nutrient composition and/or pre- vs postmeal\nsampling) can modify significantly either the basal concen-\ntration of some hormones (cortisol, Dehydroepiandrosteron-\nsulphate, and total testosterone) or their concentration change\nin response to exercise (cortisol and GH);\n\u0001 Pulsatility of the secretion of some hormones, which\nmodulates the tissue sensitivity to these hormones;\n\u0001 In female athletes, the hormonal response will depend on\nthe phase of the menstrual cycle;\n\u0001 Aerobic and resistance protocols typically elicit different\nendocrine responses;\n\u0001 Hormone concentrations at rest and after stimulation (ex-\nercise = acute stimulus) respond differently;\n\u0001 Diurnal and seasonal variations of the hormones;\n\u0001 Stress-induced measures (exercise, pro-hormones, etc) need\nto be compared with baseline measures from the same\n\u0001 Poor reproducibility and feasibility of some techniques\nused to measure some hormones (e.g., free testosterone by\nradio immunoassay instead o", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "eached rugby players (20) but\nincreased after 1 wk of taper.\nThe problems with performance testing are as follows:\n\u0001 Baseline measures are often not available, and therefore,\nthe degree of performance limitation may not be exactly\ndetermined. Individ"}
{"chunk_id": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining_p0007_c0020", "source_registry_id": "src_da12131b8c41", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "a1a269a101c81fe8", "text_span_hash": "a1a269a101c81fe8", "source_file": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 20, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ated maximal tests,\nrequired for assessment of an individual baseline measure,\nare difficult to obtain in athletes.\n\u0001 In this regard, because adequate standardization of labo-\nratory tests is problematic, it may be that index training\nsessions record", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "and develop symptoms of OTS. Further studies using mul-\ntiple exercise tests and/or multiple hormone analyses will\nbe necessary for evaluating the possibility of a hormonal\ndiagnostic test for OR/OTS.\nThe problems with hormonal data are as follows:\n\u0001 Many factors affect blood hormone concentrations, and\nthese include factors linked to sampling conditions and/or\nconservation of the sampling: stress of the sampling, intra-\nand interassay coefficient of variability;\n\u0001 Food intake (nutrient composition and/or pre- vs postmeal\nsampling) can modify significantly either the basal concen-\ntration of some hormones (cortisol, Dehydroepiandrosteron-\nsulphate, and total testosterone) or their concentration change\nin response to exercise (cortisol and GH);\n\u0001 Pulsatility of the secretion of some hormones, which\nmodulates the tissue sensitivity to these hormones;\n\u0001 In female athletes, the hormonal response will depend on\nthe phase of the menstrual cycle;\n\u0001 Aerobic and resistance protocols typically elicit different\nendocrine responses;\n\u0001 Hormone concentrations at rest and after stimulation (ex-\nercise = acute stimulus) respond differently;\n\u0001 Diurnal and seasonal variations of the hormones;\n\u0001 Stress-induced measures (exercise, pro-hormones, etc) need\nto be compared with baseline measures from the same\n\u0001 Poor reproducibility and feasibility of some techniques\nused to measure some hormones (e.g., free testosterone by\nradio immunoassay instead o", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ated maximal tests,\nrequired for assessment of an individual baseline measure,\nare difficult to obtain in athletes.\n\u0001 In this regard, because adequate standardization of labo-\nratory tests is problematic, it may be that index training\nsessions record"}
{"chunk_id": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining_p0007_c0024", "source_registry_id": "src_da12131b8c41", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e431c02ebdd1ac25", "text_span_hash": "e431c02ebdd1ac25", "source_file": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 24, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "More frequent assessments indicated that mood state\nexhibits a predictable dose–response relationship with train-\ning whereby disturbances increase in a stepwise fashion as\ntraining loads rise in volume or intensity, with the peak of\ntraining and moo", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "and develop symptoms of OTS. Further studies using mul-\ntiple exercise tests and/or multiple hormone analyses will\nbe necessary for evaluating the possibility of a hormonal\ndiagnostic test for OR/OTS.\nThe problems with hormonal data are as follows:\n\u0001 Many factors affect blood hormone concentrations, and\nthese include factors linked to sampling conditions and/or\nconservation of the sampling: stress of the sampling, intra-\nand interassay coefficient of variability;\n\u0001 Food intake (nutrient composition and/or pre- vs postmeal\nsampling) can modify significantly either the basal concen-\ntration of some hormones (cortisol, Dehydroepiandrosteron-\nsulphate, and total testosterone) or their concentration change\nin response to exercise (cortisol and GH);\n\u0001 Pulsatility of the secretion of some hormones, which\nmodulates the tissue sensitivity to these hormones;\n\u0001 In female athletes, the hormonal response will depend on\nthe phase of the menstrual cycle;\n\u0001 Aerobic and resistance protocols typically elicit different\nendocrine responses;\n\u0001 Hormone concentrations at rest and after stimulation (ex-\nercise = acute stimulus) respond differently;\n\u0001 Diurnal and seasonal variations of the hormones;\n\u0001 Stress-induced measures (exercise, pro-hormones, etc) need\nto be compared with baseline measures from the same\n\u0001 Poor reproducibility and feasibility of some techniques\nused to measure some hormones (e.g., free testosterone by\nradio immunoassay instead o", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "More frequent assessments indicated that mood state\nexhibits a predictable dose–response relationship with train-\ning whereby disturbances increase in a stepwise fashion as\ntraining loads rise in volume or intensity, with the peak of\ntraining and moo"}
{"chunk_id": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining_p0007_c0025", "source_registry_id": "src_da12131b8c41", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "691cf9c68b228b09", "text_span_hash": "691cf9c68b228b09", "source_file": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 25, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "h the peak of\ntraining and mood disturbance coinciding. Conversely, train-\ning tapers usually result in a reduction in negative moods\nand an increase in vigor such that at the end of a taper,\nthe mood scores return to the positive pattern typically o", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "and develop symptoms of OTS. Further studies using mul-\ntiple exercise tests and/or multiple hormone analyses will\nbe necessary for evaluating the possibility of a hormonal\ndiagnostic test for OR/OTS.\nThe problems with hormonal data are as follows:\n\u0001 Many factors affect blood hormone concentrations, and\nthese include factors linked to sampling conditions and/or\nconservation of the sampling: stress of the sampling, intra-\nand interassay coefficient of variability;\n\u0001 Food intake (nutrient composition and/or pre- vs postmeal\nsampling) can modify significantly either the basal concen-\ntration of some hormones (cortisol, Dehydroepiandrosteron-\nsulphate, and total testosterone) or their concentration change\nin response to exercise (cortisol and GH);\n\u0001 Pulsatility of the secretion of some hormones, which\nmodulates the tissue sensitivity to these hormones;\n\u0001 In female athletes, the hormonal response will depend on\nthe phase of the menstrual cycle;\n\u0001 Aerobic and resistance protocols typically elicit different\nendocrine responses;\n\u0001 Hormone concentrations at rest and after stimulation (ex-\nercise = acute stimulus) respond differently;\n\u0001 Diurnal and seasonal variations of the hormones;\n\u0001 Stress-induced measures (exercise, pro-hormones, etc) need\nto be compared with baseline measures from the same\n\u0001 Poor reproducibility and feasibility of some techniques\nused to measure some hormones (e.g., free testosterone by\nradio immunoassay instead o", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "h the peak of\ntraining and mood disturbance coinciding. Conversely, train-\ning tapers usually result in a reduction in negative moods\nand an increase in vigor such that at the end of a taper,\nthe mood scores return to the positive pattern typically o"}
{"chunk_id": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining_p0007_c0026", "source_registry_id": "src_da12131b8c41", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "a1f1388d0142d4de", "text_span_hash": "a1f1388d0142d4de", "source_file": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 26, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "e positive pattern typically ob-\nserved at the outset of the season, called the iceberg profile\n(100,103,116). Dose–response relationships between training\nhttp://www.acsm-msse.org\nOfficial Journal of the American College of Sports Medicine\nSPECIAL C", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "and develop symptoms of OTS. Further studies using mul-\ntiple exercise tests and/or multiple hormone analyses will\nbe necessary for evaluating the possibility of a hormonal\ndiagnostic test for OR/OTS.\nThe problems with hormonal data are as follows:\n\u0001 Many factors affect blood hormone concentrations, and\nthese include factors linked to sampling conditions and/or\nconservation of the sampling: stress of the sampling, intra-\nand interassay coefficient of variability;\n\u0001 Food intake (nutrient composition and/or pre- vs postmeal\nsampling) can modify significantly either the basal concen-\ntration of some hormones (cortisol, Dehydroepiandrosteron-\nsulphate, and total testosterone) or their concentration change\nin response to exercise (cortisol and GH);\n\u0001 Pulsatility of the secretion of some hormones, which\nmodulates the tissue sensitivity to these hormones;\n\u0001 In female athletes, the hormonal response will depend on\nthe phase of the menstrual cycle;\n\u0001 Aerobic and resistance protocols typically elicit different\nendocrine responses;\n\u0001 Hormone concentrations at rest and after stimulation (ex-\nercise = acute stimulus) respond differently;\n\u0001 Diurnal and seasonal variations of the hormones;\n\u0001 Stress-induced measures (exercise, pro-hormones, etc) need\nto be compared with baseline measures from the same\n\u0001 Poor reproducibility and feasibility of some techniques\nused to measure some hormones (e.g., free testosterone by\nradio immunoassay instead o", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "e positive pattern typically ob-\nserved at the outset of the season, called the iceberg profile\n(100,103,116). Dose–response relationships between training\nhttp://www.acsm-msse.org\nOfficial Journal of the American College of Sports Medicine\nSPECIAL C"}
{"chunk_id": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining_p0008_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_da12131b8c41", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5972f2d9eef588b5", "text_span_hash": "5972f2d9eef588b5", "source_file": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "load and mood state have since been observed in studies in-\nvolving more than 1000 athletes in a variety of endurance and\nnonendurance sports requiring rigorous training regimens\nResearch also indicates that mood responses of male and\nfemale athletes", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "load and mood state have since been observed in studies in-\nvolving more than 1000 athletes in a variety of endurance and\nnonendurance sports requiring rigorous training regimens\nResearch also indicates that mood responses of male and\nfemale athletes do not differ except when they are exposed\nto significantly different training regimens (103,115). Simi-\nlar dose–response patterns have also been observed using\nsimple self-report measures of muscle soreness, appetite,\nsleep disturbances, ‘‘heaviness,’’ and perception of effort\n(72,101,110,118), indicating perceptual responses to in-\ncreased training are global and systemic in nature, although\nthe magnitude of change differs across measures (102,118).\nWhen conditioning programs involve rapid increases in\ntraining load over a course of days, the instructions to com-\nplete psychological measures should, if possible, be modified\nto yield a more transient state measure of mood by having\nsubjects respond according to how they feel ‘‘today’’ or\n‘‘right now.’’ Research reveals that as few as 2 d of inten-\nsified training can result in significant increases in POMS\nmeasures (110) and scores on other psychological scales,\nwhich precede changes in commonly used biochemical\nmarkers of training stress such as cortisol (19,111). More\nimportant for the standpoint of monitoring, athletes with\nsigns of OTS typically exhibit both a greater increase in total\nmood disturbance and a different patter", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "load and mood state have since been observed in studies in-\nvolving more than 1000 athletes in a variety of endurance and\nnonendurance sports requiring rigorous training regimens\nResearch also indicates that mood responses of male and\nfemale athletes"}
