les,\nwhich precede changes in commonly used biochemical\nmarkers of training stress such as cortisol (19,111). More\nimportant for the standpoint of monitoring, athletes with\nsigns of OTS typically exhibit both a greater increase in total\nmood disturbance and a different patter", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "responsive to non–training-related sport stressors\n(116). For example, POMS tension scores often remain el-\nevated or even increase during training tapers, most likely\nbecause this factor is particularly sensitive to the impending\nstress of major com"}
{"chunk_id": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining_p0008_c0025", "source_registry_id": "src_da12131b8c41", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "a5bfc2ce80dce2b1", "text_span_hash": "a5bfc2ce80dce2b1", "source_file": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 25, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "been developed, including several for\nNFOR/OTS. In the case of OTS, the decision to use a gen-\neral or sport-specific measure depends not only on published\nFIGURE 2—Magnitude of changes in POMS mood states from easy to\nmaximal overload training in c", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "load and mood state have since been observed in studies in-\nvolving more than 1000 athletes in a variety of endurance and\nnonendurance sports requiring rigorous training regimens\nResearch also indicates that mood responses of male and\nfemale athletes do not differ except when they are exposed\nto significantly different training regimens (103,115). Simi-\nlar dose–response patterns have also been observed using\nsimple self-report measures of muscle soreness, appetite,\nsleep disturbances, ‘‘heaviness,’’ and perception of effort\n(72,101,110,118), indicating perceptual responses to in-\ncreased training are global and systemic in nature, although\nthe magnitude of change differs across measures (102,118).\nWhen conditioning programs involve rapid increases in\ntraining load over a course of days, the instructions to com-\nplete psychological measures should, if possible, be modified\nto yield a more transient state measure of mood by having\nsubjects respond according to how they feel ‘‘today’’ or\n‘‘right now.’’ Research reveals that as few as 2 d of inten-\nsified training can result in significant increases in POMS\nmeasures (110) and scores on other psychological scales,\nwhich precede changes in commonly used biochemical\nmarkers of training stress such as cortisol (19,111). More\nimportant for the standpoint of monitoring, athletes with\nsigns of OTS typically exhibit both a greater increase in total\nmood disturbance and a different patter", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "been developed, including several for\nNFOR/OTS. In the case of OTS, the decision to use a gen-\neral or sport-specific measure depends not only on published\nFIGURE 2—Magnitude of changes in POMS mood states from easy to\nmaximal overload training in c"}
{"chunk_id": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining_p0009_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_da12131b8c41", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "df8fb942d1d762f7", "text_span_hash": "df8fb942d1d762f7", "source_file": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "evidence of its predictive efficacy and construct validity but\nalso the theoretical orientation of the researcher. If it is be-\nlieved the risk of OTS is a function of the sum total of\nstressors an athlete is exposed to—be they training related or\nno", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "evidence of its predictive efficacy and construct validity but\nalso the theoretical orientation of the researcher. If it is be-\nlieved the risk of OTS is a function of the sum total of\nstressors an athlete is exposed to—be they training related or\nnot—then a nonspecific questionnaire that captures broad\nmoods, feelings, or perceptions would be most appropri-\nate. Conversely, if non–sport-related stressors (e.g., psy-\nchosocial stressors and time zone travel) are viewed as\ninconsequential or only minor contributors to the OTS, then\nquestionnaires delimited to items particular to the context\nof training should be used.\nFor these and other reasons, researchers have devel-\noped POMS-based OT scales in the attempt to enhance its\nsensitivity. Raglin and Morgan (114) created a Training\nDistress Scale (TDS) based on discriminant function analy-\nses of POMS data from 186 healthy and overtrained col-\nlege swimmers. A TDS spreadsheet may be accessed at\nhttp://champ.usuhs.mil/choptimize.html. The seven-item (five\ndepression and two anger items) TDS was more accurate\nin identifying OT athletes compared with predictions using\nPOMS total mood disturbance scores or depression scores,\nand subsequent research (72,118) using translations of the\nscale in several languages found TDS scores to be elevated\nin young swimmers reporting OTS. Kentta¨ et al. (73) created\na POMS energy index measure by subtracting fatigue from\nvigor scores to study 11 eli", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "evidence of its predictive efficacy and construct validity but\nalso the theoretical orientation of the researcher. If it is be-\nlieved the risk of OTS is a function of the sum total of\nstressors an athlete is exposed to—be they training related or\nno"}
{"chunk_id": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining_p0009_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_da12131b8c41", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f729fd8c6046b178", "text_span_hash": "f729fd8c6046b178", "source_file": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "be they training related or\nnot—then a nonspecific questionnaire that captures broad\nmoods, feelings, or perceptions would be most appropri-\nate. Conversely, if non–sport-related stressors (e.g., psy-\nchosocial stressors and time zone travel) are vie", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "evidence of its predictive efficacy and construct validity but\nalso the theoretical orientation of the researcher. If it is be-\nlieved the risk of OTS is a function of the sum total of\nstressors an athlete is exposed to—be they training related or\nnot—then a nonspecific questionnaire that captures broad\nmoods, feelings, or perceptions would be most appropri-\nate. Conversely, if non–sport-related stressors (e.g., psy-\nchosocial stressors and time zone travel) are viewed as\ninconsequential or only minor contributors to the OTS, then\nquestionnaires delimited to items particular to the context\nof training should be used.\nFor these and other reasons, researchers have devel-\noped POMS-based OT scales in the attempt to enhance its\nsensitivity. Raglin and Morgan (114) created a Training\nDistress Scale (TDS) based on discriminant function analy-\nses of POMS data from 186 healthy and overtrained col-\nlege swimmers. A TDS spreadsheet may be accessed at\nhttp://champ.usuhs.mil/choptimize.html. The seven-item (five\ndepression and two anger items) TDS was more accurate\nin identifying OT athletes compared with predictions using\nPOMS total mood disturbance scores or depression scores,\nand subsequent research (72,118) using translations of the\nscale in several languages found TDS scores to be elevated\nin young swimmers reporting OTS. Kentta¨ et al. (73) created\na POMS energy index measure by subtracting fatigue from\nvigor scores to study 11 eli", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "be they training related or\nnot—then a nonspecific questionnaire that captures broad\nmoods, feelings, or perceptions would be most appropri-\nate. Conversely, if non–sport-related stressors (e.g., psy-\nchosocial stressors and time zone travel) are vie"}
{"chunk_id": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining_p0009_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_da12131b8c41", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1500851e1fcdc0c8", "text_span_hash": "1500851e1fcdc0c8", "source_file": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "and time zone travel) are viewed as\ninconsequential or only minor contributors to the OTS, then\nquestionnaires delimited to items particular to the context\nof training should be used.\nFor these and other reasons, researchers have devel-\noped POMS-ba", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "evidence of its predictive efficacy and construct validity but\nalso the theoretical orientation of the researcher. If it is be-\nlieved the risk of OTS is a function of the sum total of\nstressors an athlete is exposed to—be they training related or\nnot—then a nonspecific questionnaire that captures broad\nmoods, feelings, or perceptions would be most appropri-\nate. Conversely, if non–sport-related stressors (e.g., psy-\nchosocial stressors and time zone travel) are viewed as\ninconsequential or only minor contributors to the OTS, then\nquestionnaires delimited to items particular to the context\nof training should be used.\nFor these and other reasons, researchers have devel-\noped POMS-based OT scales in the attempt to enhance its\nsensitivity. Raglin and Morgan (114) created a Training\nDistress Scale (TDS) based on discriminant function analy-\nses of POMS data from 186 healthy and overtrained col-\nlege swimmers. A TDS spreadsheet may be accessed at\nhttp://champ.usuhs.mil/choptimize.html. The seven-item (five\ndepression and two anger items) TDS was more accurate\nin identifying OT athletes compared with predictions using\nPOMS total mood disturbance scores or depression scores,\nand subsequent research (72,118) using translations of the\nscale in several languages found TDS scores to be elevated\nin young swimmers reporting OTS. Kentta¨ et al. (73) created\na POMS energy index measure by subtracting fatigue from\nvigor scores to study 11 eli", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "and time zone travel) are viewed as\ninconsequential or only minor contributors to the OTS, then\nquestionnaires delimited to items particular to the context\nof training should be used.\nFor these and other reasons, researchers have devel-\noped POMS-ba"}
{"chunk_id": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining_p0009_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_da12131b8c41", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3008d4aab89d39ac", "text_span_hash": "3008d4aab89d39ac", "source_file": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "chers have devel-\noped POMS-based OT scales in the attempt to enhance its\nsensitivity. Raglin and Morgan (114) created a Training\nDistress Scale (TDS) based on discriminant function analy-\nses of POMS data from 186 healthy and overtrained col-\nlege s", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "evidence of its predictive efficacy and construct validity but\nalso the theoretical orientation of the researcher. If it is be-\nlieved the risk of OTS is a function of the sum total of\nstressors an athlete is exposed to—be they training related or\nnot—then a nonspecific questionnaire that captures broad\nmoods, feelings, or perceptions would be most appropri-\nate. Conversely, if non–sport-related stressors (e.g., psy-\nchosocial stressors and time zone travel) are viewed as\ninconsequential or only minor contributors to the OTS, then\nquestionnaires delimited to items particular to the context\nof training should be used.\nFor these and other reasons, researchers have devel-\noped POMS-based OT scales in the attempt to enhance its\nsensitivity. Raglin and Morgan (114) created a Training\nDistress Scale (TDS) based on discriminant function analy-\nses of POMS data from 186 healthy and overtrained col-\nlege swimmers. A TDS spreadsheet may be accessed at\nhttp://champ.usuhs.mil/choptimize.html. The seven-item (five\ndepression and two anger items) TDS was more accurate\nin identifying OT athletes compared with predictions using\nPOMS total mood disturbance scores or depression scores,\nand subsequent research (72,118) using translations of the\nscale in several languages found TDS scores to be elevated\nin young swimmers reporting OTS. Kentta¨ et al. (73) created\na POMS energy index measure by subtracting fatigue from\nvigor scores to study 11 eli", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "chers have devel-\noped POMS-based OT scales in the attempt to enhance its\nsensitivity. Raglin and Morgan (114) created a Training\nDistress Scale (TDS) based on discriminant function analy-\nses of POMS data from 186 healthy and overtrained col-\nlege s"}
{"chunk_id": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining_p0009_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_da12131b8c41", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3559102dfbfc1d5f", "text_span_hash": "3559102dfbfc1d5f", "source_file": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "the entire POMS after practice each day and in the morning\nbefore practice to assess mood state after training and re-\ncovery. POMS energy index scores were responsive to both\ntraining stress and recovery, whereas depression scores were\nunchanged, su", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "evidence of its predictive efficacy and construct validity but\nalso the theoretical orientation of the researcher. If it is be-\nlieved the risk of OTS is a function of the sum total of\nstressors an athlete is exposed to—be they training related or\nnot—then a nonspecific questionnaire that captures broad\nmoods, feelings, or perceptions would be most appropri-\nate. Conversely, if non–sport-related stressors (e.g., psy-\nchosocial stressors and time zone travel) are viewed as\ninconsequential or only minor contributors to the OTS, then\nquestionnaires delimited to items particular to the context\nof training should be used.\nFor these and other reasons, researchers have devel-\noped POMS-based OT scales in the attempt to enhance its\nsensitivity. Raglin and Morgan (114) created a Training\nDistress Scale (TDS) based on discriminant function analy-\nses of POMS data from 186 healthy and overtrained col-\nlege swimmers. A TDS spreadsheet may be accessed at\nhttp://champ.usuhs.mil/choptimize.html. The seven-item (five\ndepression and two anger items) TDS was more accurate\nin identifying OT athletes compared with predictions using\nPOMS total mood disturbance scores or depression scores,\nand subsequent research (72,118) using translations of the\nscale in several languages found TDS scores to be elevated\nin young swimmers reporting OTS. Kentta¨ et al. (73) created\na POMS energy index measure by subtracting fatigue from\nvigor scores to study 11 eli", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "the entire POMS after practice each day and in the morning\nbefore practice to assess mood state after training and re-\ncovery. POMS energy index scores were responsive to both\ntraining stress and recovery, whereas depression scores were\nunchanged, su"}
{"chunk_id": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining_p0009_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_da12131b8c41", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "17abe47e8315adad", "text_span_hash": "17abe47e8315adad", "source_file": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ture have been\nexpressed by other researchers (e.g., Ref. [23]), research in-\ndicates the RESTQ is responsive to changes in training load,\nparticularly in athletes with signs of OTS (69). Other\nless well-documented OTS scales include the questionnair", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "evidence of its predictive efficacy and construct validity but\nalso the theoretical orientation of the researcher. If it is be-\nlieved the risk of OTS is a function of the sum total of\nstressors an athlete is exposed to—be they training related or\nnot—then a nonspecific questionnaire that captures broad\nmoods, feelings, or perceptions would be most appropri-\nate. Conversely, if non–sport-related stressors (e.g., psy-\nchosocial stressors and time zone travel) are viewed as\ninconsequential or only minor contributors to the OTS, then\nquestionnaires delimited to items particular to the context\nof training should be used.\nFor these and other reasons, researchers have devel-\noped POMS-based OT scales in the attempt to enhance its\nsensitivity. Raglin and Morgan (114) created a Training\nDistress Scale (TDS) based on discriminant function analy-\nses of POMS data from 186 healthy and overtrained col-\nlege swimmers. A TDS spreadsheet may be accessed at\nhttp://champ.usuhs.mil/choptimize.html. The seven-item (five\ndepression and two anger items) TDS was more accurate\nin identifying OT athletes compared with predictions using\nPOMS total mood disturbance scores or depression scores,\nand subsequent research (72,118) using translations of the\nscale in several languages found TDS scores to be elevated\nin young swimmers reporting OTS. Kentta¨ et al. (73) created\na POMS energy index measure by subtracting fatigue from\nvigor scores to study 11 eli", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ture have been\nexpressed by other researchers (e.g., Ref. [23]), research in-\ndicates the RESTQ is responsive to changes in training load,\nparticularly in athletes with signs of OTS (69). Other\nless well-documented OTS scales include the questionnair"}
{"chunk_id": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining_p0009_c0016", "source_registry_id": "src_da12131b8c41", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "09f2a19d410c2547", "text_span_hash": "09f2a19d410c2547", "source_file": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 16, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "past\nday using a three-point Likert format.\nIn summary, research has provided general support for\nthe efficacy of psychological assessments in both basic\nand applied research involving athletes undergoing overload\ntraining. There remains, however, a", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "evidence of its predictive efficacy and construct validity but\nalso the theoretical orientation of the researcher. If it is be-\nlieved the risk of OTS is a function of the sum total of\nstressors an athlete is exposed to—be they training related or\nnot—then a nonspecific questionnaire that captures broad\nmoods, feelings, or perceptions would be most appropri-\nate. Conversely, if non–sport-related stressors (e.g., psy-\nchosocial stressors and time zone travel) are viewed as\ninconsequential or only minor contributors to the OTS, then\nquestionnaires delimited to items particular to the context\nof training should be used.\nFor these and other reasons, researchers have devel-\noped POMS-based OT scales in the attempt to enhance its\nsensitivity. Raglin and Morgan (114) created a Training\nDistress Scale (TDS) based on discriminant function analy-\nses of POMS data from 186 healthy and overtrained col-\nlege swimmers. A TDS spreadsheet may be accessed at\nhttp://champ.usuhs.mil/choptimize.html. The seven-item (five\ndepression and two anger items) TDS was more accurate\nin identifying OT athletes compared with predictions using\nPOMS total mood disturbance scores or depression scores,\nand subsequent research (72,118) using translations of the\nscale in several languages found TDS scores to be elevated\nin young swimmers reporting OTS. Kentta¨ et al. (73) created\na POMS energy index measure by subtracting fatigue from\nvigor scores to study 11 eli", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "past\nday using a three-point Likert format.\nIn summary, research has provided general support for\nthe efficacy of psychological assessments in both basic\nand applied research involving athletes undergoing overload\ntraining. There remains, however, a"}
