onsiveness to catecholamines, and of\nchanges in adrenergic receptor activity or may simply be the\nresult of a reduced power output achieved with maximal\neffort. Several other reductions in maximal physiological\nmeasures (oxygen uptake, heart rate, blood lactate) might be\na consequence of a reducti", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ny study inducing OR or OTS.\nHowever, it should be mentioned that an infectious disease—\nmaybe facilitated by the intermittently depressed immuno-\nlogical state—occurring in an athlete engaged in heavy\ntraining may expose the individual to a higher r"}
{"chunk_id": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining_p0011_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_da12131b8c41", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "529eab86b12cde77", "text_span_hash": "529eab86b12cde77", "source_file": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "\u0001 The present data do not allow to distinguish between\nchanges in physiological measures resulting from FOR,\nThere are many reports on URTI due to increased train-\ning, and also in OR and OTS athletes. It seems feasible that\nintensified training (lea", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "\u0001 The present data do not allow to distinguish between\nchanges in physiological measures resulting from FOR,\nThere are many reports on URTI due to increased train-\ning, and also in OR and OTS athletes. It seems feasible that\nintensified training (leading to OR or OTS) may increase\nboth the duration of the so-called ‘‘open window’’ and the\ndegree of the resultant immunodepression. However, the\namount of scientific information to substantiate these ar-\nguments is limited. More data are available that each bout\nof prolonged and intensive exercise has transient but sig-\nnificant, wide ranging effects on the immune system (49,108).\nHeavy exertion leads to alterations in immunity and host\npathogen defense and elevations in stress hormones, pro-\nand anti-inflammatory cytokines, and reactive oxygen species.\nThe exercise-induced immune perturbations and physiologic\nstress are associated with an elevated risk of URTI, espe-\ncially during the 1- 2-wk period after competitive marathon\nand ultramarathon race events (109). These data imply that\nchronic immune dysfunction and increased URTI symp-\ntomatology may result when exercise training is intensified,\nleading to OR and OTS, but few well-designed studies have\nbeen conducted to verify this hypothesis.\nSeveral studies that have investigated the effects of short\nperiods (typically 1–3 wk) of intensified training on rest-\ning immune function and on immunoendocrine responses to\nendurance exer", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "\u0001 The present data do not allow to distinguish between\nchanges in physiological measures resulting from FOR,\nThere are many reports on URTI due to increased train-\ning, and also in OR and OTS athletes. It seems feasible that\nintensified training (lea"}
{"chunk_id": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining_p0011_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_da12131b8c41", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ccf2b3109b9eb4e7", "text_span_hash": "ccf2b3109b9eb4e7", "source_file": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "that\nintensified training (leading to OR or OTS) may increase\nboth the duration of the so-called ‘‘open window’’ and the\ndegree of the resultant immunodepression. However, the\namount of scientific information to substantiate these ar-\nguments is limi", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "\u0001 The present data do not allow to distinguish between\nchanges in physiological measures resulting from FOR,\nThere are many reports on URTI due to increased train-\ning, and also in OR and OTS athletes. It seems feasible that\nintensified training (leading to OR or OTS) may increase\nboth the duration of the so-called ‘‘open window’’ and the\ndegree of the resultant immunodepression. However, the\namount of scientific information to substantiate these ar-\nguments is limited. More data are available that each bout\nof prolonged and intensive exercise has transient but sig-\nnificant, wide ranging effects on the immune system (49,108).\nHeavy exertion leads to alterations in immunity and host\npathogen defense and elevations in stress hormones, pro-\nand anti-inflammatory cytokines, and reactive oxygen species.\nThe exercise-induced immune perturbations and physiologic\nstress are associated with an elevated risk of URTI, espe-\ncially during the 1- 2-wk period after competitive marathon\nand ultramarathon race events (109). These data imply that\nchronic immune dysfunction and increased URTI symp-\ntomatology may result when exercise training is intensified,\nleading to OR and OTS, but few well-designed studies have\nbeen conducted to verify this hypothesis.\nSeveral studies that have investigated the effects of short\nperiods (typically 1–3 wk) of intensified training on rest-\ning immune function and on immunoendocrine responses to\nendurance exer", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "that\nintensified training (leading to OR or OTS) may increase\nboth the duration of the so-called ‘‘open window’’ and the\ndegree of the resultant immunodepression. However, the\namount of scientific information to substantiate these ar-\nguments is limi"}
{"chunk_id": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining_p0011_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_da12131b8c41", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e8a879ea4cfd14ba", "text_span_hash": "e8a879ea4cfd14ba", "source_file": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "associated with an elevated risk of URTI, espe-\ncially during the 1- 2-wk period after competitive marathon\nand ultramarathon race events (109). These data imply that\nchronic immune dysfunction and increased URTI symp-\ntomatology may result when exer", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "\u0001 The present data do not allow to distinguish between\nchanges in physiological measures resulting from FOR,\nThere are many reports on URTI due to increased train-\ning, and also in OR and OTS athletes. It seems feasible that\nintensified training (leading to OR or OTS) may increase\nboth the duration of the so-called ‘‘open window’’ and the\ndegree of the resultant immunodepression. However, the\namount of scientific information to substantiate these ar-\nguments is limited. More data are available that each bout\nof prolonged and intensive exercise has transient but sig-\nnificant, wide ranging effects on the immune system (49,108).\nHeavy exertion leads to alterations in immunity and host\npathogen defense and elevations in stress hormones, pro-\nand anti-inflammatory cytokines, and reactive oxygen species.\nThe exercise-induced immune perturbations and physiologic\nstress are associated with an elevated risk of URTI, espe-\ncially during the 1- 2-wk period after competitive marathon\nand ultramarathon race events (109). These data imply that\nchronic immune dysfunction and increased URTI symp-\ntomatology may result when exercise training is intensified,\nleading to OR and OTS, but few well-designed studies have\nbeen conducted to verify this hypothesis.\nSeveral studies that have investigated the effects of short\nperiods (typically 1–3 wk) of intensified training on rest-\ning immune function and on immunoendocrine responses to\nendurance exer", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "associated with an elevated risk of URTI, espe-\ncially during the 1- 2-wk period after competitive marathon\nand ultramarathon race events (109). These data imply that\nchronic immune dysfunction and increased URTI symp-\ntomatology may result when exer"}
