{"chunk_id": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining_p0011_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_da12131b8c41", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c613baccdaf8013d", "text_span_hash": "c613baccdaf8013d", "source_file": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "multiple stressors are likely to in-\nduce a pattern of immunoendocrine responses that amplify\nthe exercise-induced alterations.\nStudies that have examined athletes exposed to a long-\nterm training periods (e.g., over the course of a 5- to\n10-month c", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "\u0001 The present data do not allow to distinguish between\nchanges in physiological measures resulting from FOR,\nThere are many reports on URTI due to increased train-\ning, and also in OR and OTS athletes. It seems feasible that\nintensified training (leading to OR or OTS) may increase\nboth the duration of the so-called ‘‘open window’’ and the\ndegree of the resultant immunodepression. However, the\namount of scientific information to substantiate these ar-\nguments is limited. More data are available that each bout\nof prolonged and intensive exercise has transient but sig-\nnificant, wide ranging effects on the immune system (49,108).\nHeavy exertion leads to alterations in immunity and host\npathogen defense and elevations in stress hormones, pro-\nand anti-inflammatory cytokines, and reactive oxygen species.\nThe exercise-induced immune perturbations and physiologic\nstress are associated with an elevated risk of URTI, espe-\ncially during the 1- 2-wk period after competitive marathon\nand ultramarathon race events (109). These data imply that\nchronic immune dysfunction and increased URTI symp-\ntomatology may result when exercise training is intensified,\nleading to OR and OTS, but few well-designed studies have\nbeen conducted to verify this hypothesis.\nSeveral studies that have investigated the effects of short\nperiods (typically 1–3 wk) of intensified training on rest-\ning immune function and on immunoendocrine responses to\nendurance exer", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "multiple stressors are likely to in-\nduce a pattern of immunoendocrine responses that amplify\nthe exercise-induced alterations.\nStudies that have examined athletes exposed to a long-\nterm training periods (e.g., over the course of a 5- to\n10-month c"}
{"chunk_id": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining_p0011_c0016", "source_registry_id": "src_da12131b8c41", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2d8fb5f504da329b", "text_span_hash": "2d8fb5f504da329b", "source_file": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 16, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "e course of a 5- to\n10-month competitive season) have shown a general trend\nof depression of both systemic and mucosal immunity\nnity was most commonly observed either at the end of the\nseason or after the most intensive periods of training and/or\ncom", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "\u0001 The present data do not allow to distinguish between\nchanges in physiological measures resulting from FOR,\nThere are many reports on URTI due to increased train-\ning, and also in OR and OTS athletes. It seems feasible that\nintensified training (leading to OR or OTS) may increase\nboth the duration of the so-called ‘‘open window’’ and the\ndegree of the resultant immunodepression. However, the\namount of scientific information to substantiate these ar-\nguments is limited. More data are available that each bout\nof prolonged and intensive exercise has transient but sig-\nnificant, wide ranging effects on the immune system (49,108).\nHeavy exertion leads to alterations in immunity and host\npathogen defense and elevations in stress hormones, pro-\nand anti-inflammatory cytokines, and reactive oxygen species.\nThe exercise-induced immune perturbations and physiologic\nstress are associated with an elevated risk of URTI, espe-\ncially during the 1- 2-wk period after competitive marathon\nand ultramarathon race events (109). These data imply that\nchronic immune dysfunction and increased URTI symp-\ntomatology may result when exercise training is intensified,\nleading to OR and OTS, but few well-designed studies have\nbeen conducted to verify this hypothesis.\nSeveral studies that have investigated the effects of short\nperiods (typically 1–3 wk) of intensified training on rest-\ning immune function and on immunoendocrine responses to\nendurance exer", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "e course of a 5- to\n10-month competitive season) have shown a general trend\nof depression of both systemic and mucosal immunity\nnity was most commonly observed either at the end of the\nseason or after the most intensive periods of training and/or\ncom"}
{"chunk_id": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining_p0011_c0028", "source_registry_id": "src_da12131b8c41", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "343dd7d714bc3518", "text_span_hash": "343dd7d714bc3518", "source_file": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 28, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "change\nwith exercise and training. With heavy training, the T-lymphocyte\nCD4+/CD8+ (helper/suppressor) ratio falls. However, this\nhas not been shown to be different in athletes diagnosed as ex-\nperiencing OTS compared with healthy well-trained athlet", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "\u0001 The present data do not allow to distinguish between\nchanges in physiological measures resulting from FOR,\nThere are many reports on URTI due to increased train-\ning, and also in OR and OTS athletes. It seems feasible that\nintensified training (leading to OR or OTS) may increase\nboth the duration of the so-called ‘‘open window’’ and the\ndegree of the resultant immunodepression. However, the\namount of scientific information to substantiate these ar-\nguments is limited. More data are available that each bout\nof prolonged and intensive exercise has transient but sig-\nnificant, wide ranging effects on the immune system (49,108).\nHeavy exertion leads to alterations in immunity and host\npathogen defense and elevations in stress hormones, pro-\nand anti-inflammatory cytokines, and reactive oxygen species.\nThe exercise-induced immune perturbations and physiologic\nstress are associated with an elevated risk of URTI, espe-\ncially during the 1- 2-wk period after competitive marathon\nand ultramarathon race events (109). These data imply that\nchronic immune dysfunction and increased URTI symp-\ntomatology may result when exercise training is intensified,\nleading to OR and OTS, but few well-designed studies have\nbeen conducted to verify this hypothesis.\nSeveral studies that have investigated the effects of short\nperiods (typically 1–3 wk) of intensified training on rest-\ning immune function and on immunoendocrine responses to\nendurance exer", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "change\nwith exercise and training. With heavy training, the T-lymphocyte\nCD4+/CD8+ (helper/suppressor) ratio falls. However, this\nhas not been shown to be different in athletes diagnosed as ex-\nperiencing OTS compared with healthy well-trained athlet"}
{"chunk_id": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining_p0012_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_da12131b8c41", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b3b9f576acb36d92", "text_span_hash": "b3b9f576acb36d92", "source_file": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "tivity. However, CD45RO is a marker of\nT-memory cells and activated T-cells. Thus, higher expres-\nsion of CD45RO on T-cells may merely be indicative of the\npresence of acute infection, which is, of course, a possible\ncause of the underperformance. Fr", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "on the cell surface of T-lymphocytes does seem to be sensitive\nenough to distinguish between the majority of ‘‘overtrained’’\nathletes and healthy athletes. The expression of CD45RO on\nT-helper CD4+ cells (but not the circulating numbers of\nCD45RO+ T-cells) was significantly higher in athletes ex-\nperiencing OTS compared with healthy well-trained con-\ntrols. Using this indicator, ‘‘OT’’ could be classified with high\nspecificity and sensitivity. However, CD45RO is a marker of\nT-memory cells and activated T-cells. Thus, higher expres-\nsion of CD45RO on T-cells may merely be indicative of the\npresence of acute infection, which is, of course, a possible\ncause of the underperformance. Fry et al. (44) reported a\nsignificant increase in activation markers [CD25, human\nleucocyte antigen receptor test (HLA-DR)] in blood lym-\nphocytes of ‘‘overtrained’’ athletes. Unresolved viral infec-\ntions are not routinely assessed in elite athletes, but it may be\nworth investigating this in individuals experiencing fatigue\nand underperformance in training and competition. Thus,\ninfection might be one of the ‘‘triggering’’ factors that can\nlead to the induction of OTS, or in some cases, the diagnosis\nof OTS cannot be differentiated from a state of postviral\nfatigue such as that observed with episodes of glandular\nfever. In the OTS diagnostic flowchart (Fig. 3), it is rec-\nommended to evaluate for ‘‘primary’’ viral and bacterial\ninfections and systemi", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "tivity. However, CD45RO is a marker of\nT-memory cells and activated T-cells. Thus, higher expres-\nsion of CD45RO on T-cells may merely be indicative of the\npresence of acute infection, which is, of course, a possible\ncause of the underperformance. Fr"}
{"chunk_id": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining_p0012_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_da12131b8c41", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "34b14bd5077eb5ec", "text_span_hash": "34b14bd5077eb5ec", "source_file": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "r that the immune system is sen-\nsitive to stress—both physiological and psychological—and\nthus, potentially, immune variables could be used as an in-\ndex of stress in relation to exercise training. The current\ninformation regarding the immune system", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "on the cell surface of T-lymphocytes does seem to be sensitive\nenough to distinguish between the majority of ‘‘overtrained’’\nathletes and healthy athletes. The expression of CD45RO on\nT-helper CD4+ cells (but not the circulating numbers of\nCD45RO+ T-cells) was significantly higher in athletes ex-\nperiencing OTS compared with healthy well-trained con-\ntrols. Using this indicator, ‘‘OT’’ could be classified with high\nspecificity and sensitivity. However, CD45RO is a marker of\nT-memory cells and activated T-cells. Thus, higher expres-\nsion of CD45RO on T-cells may merely be indicative of the\npresence of acute infection, which is, of course, a possible\ncause of the underperformance. Fry et al. (44) reported a\nsignificant increase in activation markers [CD25, human\nleucocyte antigen receptor test (HLA-DR)] in blood lym-\nphocytes of ‘‘overtrained’’ athletes. Unresolved viral infec-\ntions are not routinely assessed in elite athletes, but it may be\nworth investigating this in individuals experiencing fatigue\nand underperformance in training and competition. Thus,\ninfection might be one of the ‘‘triggering’’ factors that can\nlead to the induction of OTS, or in some cases, the diagnosis\nof OTS cannot be differentiated from a state of postviral\nfatigue such as that observed with episodes of glandular\nfever. In the OTS diagnostic flowchart (Fig. 3), it is rec-\nommended to evaluate for ‘‘primary’’ viral and bacterial\ninfections and systemi", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "r that the immune system is sen-\nsitive to stress—both physiological and psychological—and\nthus, potentially, immune variables could be used as an in-\ndex of stress in relation to exercise training. The current\ninformation regarding the immune system"}
{"chunk_id": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining_p0012_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_da12131b8c41", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "a1bb36690a784380", "text_span_hash": "a1bb36690a784380", "source_file": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "on regarding the immune system and OR confirms\nthat periods of intensified training result in depressed im-\nmune cell functions with little or no alteration in circulating\ncell numbers. However, although immune parameters change\nin response to increa", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "on the cell surface of T-lymphocytes does seem to be sensitive\nenough to distinguish between the majority of ‘‘overtrained’’\nathletes and healthy athletes. The expression of CD45RO on\nT-helper CD4+ cells (but not the circulating numbers of\nCD45RO+ T-cells) was significantly higher in athletes ex-\nperiencing OTS compared with healthy well-trained con-\ntrols. Using this indicator, ‘‘OT’’ could be classified with high\nspecificity and sensitivity. However, CD45RO is a marker of\nT-memory cells and activated T-cells. Thus, higher expres-\nsion of CD45RO on T-cells may merely be indicative of the\npresence of acute infection, which is, of course, a possible\ncause of the underperformance. Fry et al. (44) reported a\nsignificant increase in activation markers [CD25, human\nleucocyte antigen receptor test (HLA-DR)] in blood lym-\nphocytes of ‘‘overtrained’’ athletes. Unresolved viral infec-\ntions are not routinely assessed in elite athletes, but it may be\nworth investigating this in individuals experiencing fatigue\nand underperformance in training and competition. Thus,\ninfection might be one of the ‘‘triggering’’ factors that can\nlead to the induction of OTS, or in some cases, the diagnosis\nof OTS cannot be differentiated from a state of postviral\nfatigue such as that observed with episodes of glandular\nfever. In the OTS diagnostic flowchart (Fig. 3), it is rec-\nommended to evaluate for ‘‘primary’’ viral and bacterial\ninfections and systemi", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "on regarding the immune system and OR confirms\nthat periods of intensified training result in depressed im-\nmune cell functions with little or no alteration in circulating\ncell numbers. However, although immune parameters change\nin response to increa"}
{"chunk_id": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining_p0012_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_da12131b8c41", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "02a7c8440aec53f9", "text_span_hash": "02a7c8440aec53f9", "source_file": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "e last\n\u0001 lack of consistency of the data in literature,\n\u0001 being time consuming and very expensive (for func-\nResistance exercise. Although most research on OT\nand OR has focused on endurance activities, some research\nhas shed light on stressful train", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "on the cell surface of T-lymphocytes does seem to be sensitive\nenough to distinguish between the majority of ‘‘overtrained’’\nathletes and healthy athletes. The expression of CD45RO on\nT-helper CD4+ cells (but not the circulating numbers of\nCD45RO+ T-cells) was significantly higher in athletes ex-\nperiencing OTS compared with healthy well-trained con-\ntrols. Using this indicator, ‘‘OT’’ could be classified with high\nspecificity and sensitivity. However, CD45RO is a marker of\nT-memory cells and activated T-cells. Thus, higher expres-\nsion of CD45RO on T-cells may merely be indicative of the\npresence of acute infection, which is, of course, a possible\ncause of the underperformance. Fry et al. (44) reported a\nsignificant increase in activation markers [CD25, human\nleucocyte antigen receptor test (HLA-DR)] in blood lym-\nphocytes of ‘‘overtrained’’ athletes. Unresolved viral infec-\ntions are not routinely assessed in elite athletes, but it may be\nworth investigating this in individuals experiencing fatigue\nand underperformance in training and competition. Thus,\ninfection might be one of the ‘‘triggering’’ factors that can\nlead to the induction of OTS, or in some cases, the diagnosis\nof OTS cannot be differentiated from a state of postviral\nfatigue such as that observed with episodes of glandular\nfever. In the OTS diagnostic flowchart (Fig. 3), it is rec-\nommended to evaluate for ‘‘primary’’ viral and bacterial\ninfections and systemi", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "e last\n\u0001 lack of consistency of the data in literature,\n\u0001 being time consuming and very expensive (for func-\nResistance exercise. Although most research on OT\nand OR has focused on endurance activities, some research\nhas shed light on stressful train"}
{"chunk_id": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining_p0012_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_da12131b8c41", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ed0019d843f7791f", "text_span_hash": "ed0019d843f7791f", "source_file": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "shed light on stressful training when using heavy resis-\ntance exercise, and it is summarized in several reviews\n(39,40,42,43). What has become clear is that excessively\nhigh volumes or intensities of resistance exercise can present\nconsiderably dif", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "on the cell surface of T-lymphocytes does seem to be sensitive\nenough to distinguish between the majority of ‘‘overtrained’’\nathletes and healthy athletes. The expression of CD45RO on\nT-helper CD4+ cells (but not the circulating numbers of\nCD45RO+ T-cells) was significantly higher in athletes ex-\nperiencing OTS compared with healthy well-trained con-\ntrols. Using this indicator, ‘‘OT’’ could be classified with high\nspecificity and sensitivity. However, CD45RO is a marker of\nT-memory cells and activated T-cells. Thus, higher expres-\nsion of CD45RO on T-cells may merely be indicative of the\npresence of acute infection, which is, of course, a possible\ncause of the underperformance. Fry et al. (44) reported a\nsignificant increase in activation markers [CD25, human\nleucocyte antigen receptor test (HLA-DR)] in blood lym-\nphocytes of ‘‘overtrained’’ athletes. Unresolved viral infec-\ntions are not routinely assessed in elite athletes, but it may be\nworth investigating this in individuals experiencing fatigue\nand underperformance in training and competition. Thus,\ninfection might be one of the ‘‘triggering’’ factors that can\nlead to the induction of OTS, or in some cases, the diagnosis\nof OTS cannot be differentiated from a state of postviral\nfatigue such as that observed with episodes of glandular\nfever. In the OTS diagnostic flowchart (Fig. 3), it is rec-\nommended to evaluate for ‘‘primary’’ viral and bacterial\ninfections and systemi", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "shed light on stressful training when using heavy resis-\ntance exercise, and it is summarized in several reviews\n(39,40,42,43). What has become clear is that excessively\nhigh volumes or intensities of resistance exercise can present\nconsiderably dif"}
{"chunk_id": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining_p0012_c0016", "source_registry_id": "src_da12131b8c41", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "254b6b078e5b40ff", "text_span_hash": "254b6b078e5b40ff", "source_file": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 16, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "e can present\nconsiderably different physiological and performance pro-\nfiles when compared with OT/OR with endurance activi-\nties. When excessive volumes of maximal loads are used for\ntraining, maximal muscular strength is one of the last per-\nforma", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "on the cell surface of T-lymphocytes does seem to be sensitive\nenough to distinguish between the majority of ‘‘overtrained’’\nathletes and healthy athletes. The expression of CD45RO on\nT-helper CD4+ cells (but not the circulating numbers of\nCD45RO+ T-cells) was significantly higher in athletes ex-\nperiencing OTS compared with healthy well-trained con-\ntrols. Using this indicator, ‘‘OT’’ could be classified with high\nspecificity and sensitivity. However, CD45RO is a marker of\nT-memory cells and activated T-cells. Thus, higher expres-\nsion of CD45RO on T-cells may merely be indicative of the\npresence of acute infection, which is, of course, a possible\ncause of the underperformance. Fry et al. (44) reported a\nsignificant increase in activation markers [CD25, human\nleucocyte antigen receptor test (HLA-DR)] in blood lym-\nphocytes of ‘‘overtrained’’ athletes. Unresolved viral infec-\ntions are not routinely assessed in elite athletes, but it may be\nworth investigating this in individuals experiencing fatigue\nand underperformance in training and competition. Thus,\ninfection might be one of the ‘‘triggering’’ factors that can\nlead to the induction of OTS, or in some cases, the diagnosis\nof OTS cannot be differentiated from a state of postviral\nfatigue such as that observed with episodes of glandular\nfever. In the OTS diagnostic flowchart (Fig. 3), it is rec-\nommended to evaluate for ‘‘primary’’ viral and bacterial\ninfections and systemi", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "e can present\nconsiderably different physiological and performance pro-\nfiles when compared with OT/OR with endurance activi-\nties. When excessive volumes of maximal loads are used for\ntraining, maximal muscular strength is one of the last per-\nforma"}
{"chunk_id": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining_p0012_c0017", "source_registry_id": "src_da12131b8c41", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b3cbc727b9c813d7", "text_span_hash": "b3cbc727b9c813d7", "source_file": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 17, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "is one of the last per-\nformance measures to be adversely affected. On the other\nhand, high speed (e.g., sprinting) and power appear to be\nmore sensitive to the stressful resistance exercise training\nand are the first types of performance to decreas", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "on the cell surface of T-lymphocytes does seem to be sensitive\nenough to distinguish between the majority of ‘‘overtrained’’\nathletes and healthy athletes. The expression of CD45RO on\nT-helper CD4+ cells (but not the circulating numbers of\nCD45RO+ T-cells) was significantly higher in athletes ex-\nperiencing OTS compared with healthy well-trained con-\ntrols. Using this indicator, ‘‘OT’’ could be classified with high\nspecificity and sensitivity. However, CD45RO is a marker of\nT-memory cells and activated T-cells. Thus, higher expres-\nsion of CD45RO on T-cells may merely be indicative of the\npresence of acute infection, which is, of course, a possible\ncause of the underperformance. Fry et al. (44) reported a\nsignificant increase in activation markers [CD25, human\nleucocyte antigen receptor test (HLA-DR)] in blood lym-\nphocytes of ‘‘overtrained’’ athletes. Unresolved viral infec-\ntions are not routinely assessed in elite athletes, but it may be\nworth investigating this in individuals experiencing fatigue\nand underperformance in training and competition. Thus,\ninfection might be one of the ‘‘triggering’’ factors that can\nlead to the induction of OTS, or in some cases, the diagnosis\nof OTS cannot be differentiated from a state of postviral\nfatigue such as that observed with episodes of glandular\nfever. In the OTS diagnostic flowchart (Fig. 3), it is rec-\nommended to evaluate for ‘‘primary’’ viral and bacterial\ninfections and systemi", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "is one of the last per-\nformance measures to be adversely affected. On the other\nhand, high speed (e.g., sprinting) and power appear to be\nmore sensitive to the stressful resistance exercise training\nand are the first types of performance to decreas"}
{"chunk_id": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining_p0012_c0019", "source_registry_id": "src_da12131b8c41", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7d4bd45f0861b8c8", "text_span_hash": "7d4bd45f0861b8c8", "source_file": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 19, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "oncentrations and the testosterone/cortisol ratio may de-\ncrease because of resistance exercise OT/OR, these cannot\nbe used to define the presence of an OTS. Rather, these\nhormonal measures simply indicate the presence of stressful\ntraining. When res", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "on the cell surface of T-lymphocytes does seem to be sensitive\nenough to distinguish between the majority of ‘‘overtrained’’\nathletes and healthy athletes. The expression of CD45RO on\nT-helper CD4+ cells (but not the circulating numbers of\nCD45RO+ T-cells) was significantly higher in athletes ex-\nperiencing OTS compared with healthy well-trained con-\ntrols. Using this indicator, ‘‘OT’’ could be classified with high\nspecificity and sensitivity. However, CD45RO is a marker of\nT-memory cells and activated T-cells. Thus, higher expres-\nsion of CD45RO on T-cells may merely be indicative of the\npresence of acute infection, which is, of course, a possible\ncause of the underperformance. Fry et al. (44) reported a\nsignificant increase in activation markers [CD25, human\nleucocyte antigen receptor test (HLA-DR)] in blood lym-\nphocytes of ‘‘overtrained’’ athletes. Unresolved viral infec-\ntions are not routinely assessed in elite athletes, but it may be\nworth investigating this in individuals experiencing fatigue\nand underperformance in training and competition. Thus,\ninfection might be one of the ‘‘triggering’’ factors that can\nlead to the induction of OTS, or in some cases, the diagnosis\nof OTS cannot be differentiated from a state of postviral\nfatigue such as that observed with episodes of glandular\nfever. In the OTS diagnostic flowchart (Fig. 3), it is rec-\nommended to evaluate for ‘‘primary’’ viral and bacterial\ninfections and systemi", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "oncentrations and the testosterone/cortisol ratio may de-\ncrease because of resistance exercise OT/OR, these cannot\nbe used to define the presence of an OTS. Rather, these\nhormonal measures simply indicate the presence of stressful\ntraining. When res"}
