syndrome or burnout), the psychoneuroim-\nmunology (study of brain–behavior–immune interrelation-\nships) might shed a light on the possible mechanisms of\nOTS, but until there is no definite diagnostic tool, it is of\nutmost importance to standardize measures that are now\nthought", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "resulting in the acute\ncondition of OR and not the chronic condition of OTS. The\nmechanism of OTS could be difficult to examine in detail\nmaybe because the stress caused by excessive training load,\nin combination with other stressors, might trigger d"}
{"chunk_id": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining_p0016_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_da12131b8c41", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1325243fcbae87cb", "text_span_hash": "1325243fcbae87cb", "source_file": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "page": 16, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ms of\nOTS, but until there is no definite diagnostic tool, it is of\nutmost importance to standardize measures that are now\nthought to provide a good inventory of the training status of\nthe athlete. A primary indicator of OR or OTS is a decrease\nin sp", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Moreover, when OTS is suspected, it is also of utmost\nimportance to standardize the criteria used for diagnosis and/\nor, at least, as tools for the diagnosis of OTS are lacking, to\nstandardize the criteria of exclusion of OTS (see Fig. 1 for\nthe definition and Tables 1 and 2).\nA difficulty with recognizing and conducting research\ninto athletes with OTS is defining the point at which OTS\ndevelops. Many studies claim to have induced OTS, but it\nis more likely that they have induced a state of OR in their\nsubjects. Consequently, the majority of studies aimed at\nidentifying markers of ensuing OTS are actually reporting\nmarkers of excessive exercise stress resulting in the acute\ncondition of OR and not the chronic condition of OTS. The\nmechanism of OTS could be difficult to examine in detail\nmaybe because the stress caused by excessive training load,\nin combination with other stressors, might trigger different\n‘‘defence mechanisms’’ such as the immunological, neuro-\nendocrine, and other physiological systems that all interact\nand probably therefore cannot be pinpointed as the ‘‘sole’’\ncause of OTS. It might be that as in other syndromes (e.g.,\nchronic fatigue syndrome or burnout), the psychoneuroim-\nmunology (study of brain–behavior–immune interrelation-\nships) might shed a light on the possible mechanisms of\nOTS, but until there is no definite diagnostic tool, it is of\nutmost importance to standardize measures that are now\nthought", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ms of\nOTS, but until there is no definite diagnostic tool, it is of\nutmost importance to standardize measures that are now\nthought to provide a good inventory of the training status of\nthe athlete. A primary indicator of OR or OTS is a decrease\nin sp"}
{"chunk_id": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining_p0016_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_da12131b8c41", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "da289f48d549d66b", "text_span_hash": "da289f48d549d66b", "source_file": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "page": 16, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ment of OTS, including excessive expectations from\na coach or family members, competitive stress, personality\nstructure, social environment, relationships with family and\nfriends, monotony in training, personal or emotional prob-\nlems, and school- or", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Moreover, when OTS is suspected, it is also of utmost\nimportance to standardize the criteria used for diagnosis and/\nor, at least, as tools for the diagnosis of OTS are lacking, to\nstandardize the criteria of exclusion of OTS (see Fig. 1 for\nthe definition and Tables 1 and 2).\nA difficulty with recognizing and conducting research\ninto athletes with OTS is defining the point at which OTS\ndevelops. Many studies claim to have induced OTS, but it\nis more likely that they have induced a state of OR in their\nsubjects. Consequently, the majority of studies aimed at\nidentifying markers of ensuing OTS are actually reporting\nmarkers of excessive exercise stress resulting in the acute\ncondition of OR and not the chronic condition of OTS. The\nmechanism of OTS could be difficult to examine in detail\nmaybe because the stress caused by excessive training load,\nin combination with other stressors, might trigger different\n‘‘defence mechanisms’’ such as the immunological, neuro-\nendocrine, and other physiological systems that all interact\nand probably therefore cannot be pinpointed as the ‘‘sole’’\ncause of OTS. It might be that as in other syndromes (e.g.,\nchronic fatigue syndrome or burnout), the psychoneuroim-\nmunology (study of brain–behavior–immune interrelation-\nships) might shed a light on the possible mechanisms of\nOTS, but until there is no definite diagnostic tool, it is of\nutmost importance to standardize measures that are now\nthought", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ment of OTS, including excessive expectations from\na coach or family members, competitive stress, personality\nstructure, social environment, relationships with family and\nfriends, monotony in training, personal or emotional prob-\nlems, and school- or"}
{"chunk_id": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining_p0016_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_da12131b8c41", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4021fca433f4f449", "text_span_hash": "4021fca433f4f449", "source_file": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "page": 16, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ogical, and psy-\nchological variables would seem to be the best strategy to\nidentify athletes who are failing to cope with the stress of\ntraining. We therefore propose a ‘‘check list’’ that might help\nthe physicians to decide on the diagnosis of OTS", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Moreover, when OTS is suspected, it is also of utmost\nimportance to standardize the criteria used for diagnosis and/\nor, at least, as tools for the diagnosis of OTS are lacking, to\nstandardize the criteria of exclusion of OTS (see Fig. 1 for\nthe definition and Tables 1 and 2).\nA difficulty with recognizing and conducting research\ninto athletes with OTS is defining the point at which OTS\ndevelops. Many studies claim to have induced OTS, but it\nis more likely that they have induced a state of OR in their\nsubjects. Consequently, the majority of studies aimed at\nidentifying markers of ensuing OTS are actually reporting\nmarkers of excessive exercise stress resulting in the acute\ncondition of OR and not the chronic condition of OTS. The\nmechanism of OTS could be difficult to examine in detail\nmaybe because the stress caused by excessive training load,\nin combination with other stressors, might trigger different\n‘‘defence mechanisms’’ such as the immunological, neuro-\nendocrine, and other physiological systems that all interact\nand probably therefore cannot be pinpointed as the ‘‘sole’’\ncause of OTS. It might be that as in other syndromes (e.g.,\nchronic fatigue syndrome or burnout), the psychoneuroim-\nmunology (study of brain–behavior–immune interrelation-\nships) might shed a light on the possible mechanisms of\nOTS, but until there is no definite diagnostic tool, it is of\nutmost importance to standardize measures that are now\nthought", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ogical, and psy-\nchological variables would seem to be the best strategy to\nidentify athletes who are failing to cope with the stress of\ntraining. We therefore propose a ‘‘check list’’ that might help\nthe physicians to decide on the diagnosis of OTS"}
{"chunk_id": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining_p0016_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_da12131b8c41", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "56bb997c23dbdce9", "text_span_hash": "56bb997c23dbdce9", "source_file": "Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Meeusen_2013_Overtraining.pdf", "page": 16, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ing in-\ntensified running training results in better maintenance of per-\nformance and mood state. J Appl Physiol. 2004;96:1331–40.\n3. Achten J, Jeukendrup AE. Heart rate monitoring: applications\nand limitations. Sports Med. 2003;33:517–38.\n4. Anglem", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Moreover, when OTS is suspected, it is also of utmost\nimportance to standardize the criteria used for diagnosis and/\nor, at least, as tools for the diagnosis of OTS are lacking, to\nstandardize the criteria of exclusion of OTS (see Fig. 1 for\nthe definition and Tables 1 and 2).\nA difficulty with recognizing and conducting research\ninto athletes with OTS is defining the point at which OTS\ndevelops. Many studies claim to have induced OTS, but it\nis more likely that they have induced a state of OR in their\nsubjects. Consequently, the majority of studies aimed at\nidentifying markers of ensuing OTS are actually reporting\nmarkers of excessive exercise stress resulting in the acute\ncondition of OR and not the chronic condition of OTS. The\nmechanism of OTS could be difficult to examine in detail\nmaybe because the stress caused by excessive training load,\nin combination with other stressors, might trigger different\n‘‘defence mechanisms’’ such as the immunological, neuro-\nendocrine, and other physiological systems that all interact\nand probably therefore cannot be pinpointed as the ‘‘sole’’\ncause of OTS. It might be that as in other syndromes (e.g.,\nchronic fatigue syndrome or burnout), the psychoneuroim-\nmunology (study of brain–behavior–immune interrelation-\nships) might shed a light on the possible mechanisms of\nOTS, but until there is no definite diagnostic tool, it is of\nutmost importance to standardize measures that are now\nthought", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ing in-\ntensified running training results in better maintenance of per-\nformance and mood state. J Appl Physiol. 2004;96:1331–40.\n3. Achten J, Jeukendrup AE. Heart rate monitoring: applications\nand limitations. Sports Med. 2003;33:517–38.\n4. Anglem"}
