{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0001_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "a636da1dab44a54f", "text_span_hash": "a636da1dab44a54f", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "N, Denmark\nA R T I C L E I N F O\nA B S T R A C T\nIn this paper, we aimed to test whether we could predict sport type (badminton or running) and\nmarathon proficiency from the valence, form, and content of the athletes’ self-reported inner\nspeech. Addi", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Available online 19 February 2021\n1053-8100/© 2021 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.\nValence, form, and content of self-talk predict sport type and level\nJohanne Nedergaard a,*, Mark Schram Christensen b, Mikkel Wallentin a\na Department of Linguistics, Cognitive Science and Semiotics, Jens Chr. Skous Vej 2, Aarhus University, 8000 Aarhus C, Denmark\nb Department of Neuroscience, Blegdamsvej 3B, University of Copenhagen, 2200 Copenhagen N, Denmark\nA R T I C L E I N F O\nA B S T R A C T\nIn this paper, we aimed to test whether we could predict sport type (badminton or running) and\nmarathon proficiency from the valence, form, and content of the athletes’ self-reported inner\nspeech. Additionally, we wanted to assess the difference between self-talk during high intensity\nand low intensity exercise. The present study corroborated existing research – we were able to\npredict both sport type in Study 1 and intensity level as well as marathon proficiency in Study 2\nfrom questionnaire data using machine learning models. In Study 1, we found that badminton\nplayers engage more in worry and anxiety-control while runners are more prone to task disen­\ngagement. Interestingly, it seemed in Study 2 that the more participants engaged in condensed,\npositive, and repetitive self-talk when not pushing themselves, the slower their fastest marathons\nand half marathons were. We discuss potential explanations for these findings and make sug­\ngestions for", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "N, Denmark\nA R T I C L E I N F O\nA B S T R A C T\nIn this paper, we aimed to test whether we could predict sport type (badminton or running) and\nmarathon proficiency from the valence, form, and content of the athletes’ self-reported inner\nspeech. Addi"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0001_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "257e0580b83db532", "text_span_hash": "257e0580b83db532", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "th sport type in Study 1 and intensity level as well as marathon proficiency in Study 2\nfrom questionnaire data using machine learning models. In Study 1, we found that badminton\nplayers engage more in worry and anxiety-control while runners are more", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Available online 19 February 2021\n1053-8100/© 2021 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.\nValence, form, and content of self-talk predict sport type and level\nJohanne Nedergaard a,*, Mark Schram Christensen b, Mikkel Wallentin a\na Department of Linguistics, Cognitive Science and Semiotics, Jens Chr. Skous Vej 2, Aarhus University, 8000 Aarhus C, Denmark\nb Department of Neuroscience, Blegdamsvej 3B, University of Copenhagen, 2200 Copenhagen N, Denmark\nA R T I C L E I N F O\nA B S T R A C T\nIn this paper, we aimed to test whether we could predict sport type (badminton or running) and\nmarathon proficiency from the valence, form, and content of the athletes’ self-reported inner\nspeech. Additionally, we wanted to assess the difference between self-talk during high intensity\nand low intensity exercise. The present study corroborated existing research – we were able to\npredict both sport type in Study 1 and intensity level as well as marathon proficiency in Study 2\nfrom questionnaire data using machine learning models. In Study 1, we found that badminton\nplayers engage more in worry and anxiety-control while runners are more prone to task disen­\ngagement. Interestingly, it seemed in Study 2 that the more participants engaged in condensed,\npositive, and repetitive self-talk when not pushing themselves, the slower their fastest marathons\nand half marathons were. We discuss potential explanations for these findings and make sug­\ngestions for", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "th sport type in Study 1 and intensity level as well as marathon proficiency in Study 2\nfrom questionnaire data using machine learning models. In Study 1, we found that badminton\nplayers engage more in worry and anxiety-control while runners are more"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0001_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "314dee3a2e4d798a", "text_span_hash": "314dee3a2e4d798a", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "control while runners are more prone to task disen­\ngagement. Interestingly, it seemed in Study 2 that the more participants engaged in condensed,\npositive, and repetitive self-talk when not pushing themselves, the slower their fastest marathons\nand", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Available online 19 February 2021\n1053-8100/© 2021 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.\nValence, form, and content of self-talk predict sport type and level\nJohanne Nedergaard a,*, Mark Schram Christensen b, Mikkel Wallentin a\na Department of Linguistics, Cognitive Science and Semiotics, Jens Chr. Skous Vej 2, Aarhus University, 8000 Aarhus C, Denmark\nb Department of Neuroscience, Blegdamsvej 3B, University of Copenhagen, 2200 Copenhagen N, Denmark\nA R T I C L E I N F O\nA B S T R A C T\nIn this paper, we aimed to test whether we could predict sport type (badminton or running) and\nmarathon proficiency from the valence, form, and content of the athletes’ self-reported inner\nspeech. Additionally, we wanted to assess the difference between self-talk during high intensity\nand low intensity exercise. The present study corroborated existing research – we were able to\npredict both sport type in Study 1 and intensity level as well as marathon proficiency in Study 2\nfrom questionnaire data using machine learning models. In Study 1, we found that badminton\nplayers engage more in worry and anxiety-control while runners are more prone to task disen­\ngagement. Interestingly, it seemed in Study 2 that the more participants engaged in condensed,\npositive, and repetitive self-talk when not pushing themselves, the slower their fastest marathons\nand half marathons were. We discuss potential explanations for these findings and make sug­\ngestions for", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "control while runners are more prone to task disen­\ngagement. Interestingly, it seemed in Study 2 that the more participants engaged in condensed,\npositive, and repetitive self-talk when not pushing themselves, the slower their fastest marathons\nand"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0001_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "776495b64b1c31d8", "text_span_hash": "776495b64b1c31d8", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "man, 2018; Regier, Kay, & Khetarpal, 2007; Roberson, Davidoff, Davies, & Shapiro, 2005; Winawer et al., 2007), perception\nof space (Levinson, 2003; Wallentin, Weed, Østergaard, Mouridsen, & Roepstorff, 2008), respiration (MacLarnon & Hewitt, 1999),\np", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Available online 19 February 2021\n1053-8100/© 2021 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.\nValence, form, and content of self-talk predict sport type and level\nJohanne Nedergaard a,*, Mark Schram Christensen b, Mikkel Wallentin a\na Department of Linguistics, Cognitive Science and Semiotics, Jens Chr. Skous Vej 2, Aarhus University, 8000 Aarhus C, Denmark\nb Department of Neuroscience, Blegdamsvej 3B, University of Copenhagen, 2200 Copenhagen N, Denmark\nA R T I C L E I N F O\nA B S T R A C T\nIn this paper, we aimed to test whether we could predict sport type (badminton or running) and\nmarathon proficiency from the valence, form, and content of the athletes’ self-reported inner\nspeech. Additionally, we wanted to assess the difference between self-talk during high intensity\nand low intensity exercise. The present study corroborated existing research – we were able to\npredict both sport type in Study 1 and intensity level as well as marathon proficiency in Study 2\nfrom questionnaire data using machine learning models. In Study 1, we found that badminton\nplayers engage more in worry and anxiety-control while runners are more prone to task disen­\ngagement. Interestingly, it seemed in Study 2 that the more participants engaged in condensed,\npositive, and repetitive self-talk when not pushing themselves, the slower their fastest marathons\nand half marathons were. We discuss potential explanations for these findings and make sug­\ngestions for", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "man, 2018; Regier, Kay, & Khetarpal, 2007; Roberson, Davidoff, Davies, & Shapiro, 2005; Winawer et al., 2007), perception\nof space (Levinson, 2003; Wallentin, Weed, Østergaard, Mouridsen, & Roepstorff, 2008), respiration (MacLarnon & Hewitt, 1999),\np"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0002_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1487405ba1dd70e7", "text_span_hash": "1487405ba1dd70e7", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": ", 2008; Vargas-Tonsing, Myers, & Feltz, 2004; Galanis, Hatzigeorgiadis, Zourbanos, & Theodorakis,\n2016). It is important to study self-talk – particularly in the context of endurance and motor control, but also elsewhere – because 1)\nThere is mountin", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "dialogue (Alderson-Day & Fernyhough, 2015). Humans spend as much as a quarter of our waking hours talking to ourselves (Heavey &\nHurlburt, 2008). Self-talk is therefore gaining prominence as a topic of interest in cognitive science (e.g., Winsler, 2009; Hurlburt &\nHeavey, 2015; Morin, Uttl, & Hamper, 2011; Carruthers, 2018; Gauker, 2018; Lœvenbruck et al., 2018).\nSelf-talk already plays a very prominent role in sport psychology and is endorsed by both athletes and coaches as a fruitful tool for\nbuilding confidence, sustaining motivation, focusing, and improving technique (Shannon, Gentner, Patel, & Muccio, 2012; Thelwell,\nWeston, Greenlees, & Hutchings, 2008; Vargas-Tonsing, Myers, & Feltz, 2004; Galanis, Hatzigeorgiadis, Zourbanos, & Theodorakis,\n2016). It is important to study self-talk – particularly in the context of endurance and motor control, but also elsewhere – because 1)\nThere is mounting evidence that people report to be talking to themselves (e.g. Hurlburt, Heavey, & Kelsey, 2013; Van Raalte, Morrey,\nCornelius, & Brewer, 2015; Gammage, Hardy, & Hall, 2001), and 2) Evidence is accumulating that talking to yourself positively affects\nperformance in sport contexts (Tod, Hardy, & Oliver, 2011; Hatzigeorgiadis, Zourbanos, Galanis, & Theodorakis, 2011; Van Raalte,\nVincent, & Brewer, 2016). In the present context, we use ‘self-talk’ to refer to covert verbalizations addressed to the self, although we\nrecognize that a theo", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": ", 2008; Vargas-Tonsing, Myers, & Feltz, 2004; Galanis, Hatzigeorgiadis, Zourbanos, & Theodorakis,\n2016). It is important to study self-talk – particularly in the context of endurance and motor control, but also elsewhere – because 1)\nThere is mountin"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0003_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8afc55bff96b5a88", "text_span_hash": "8afc55bff96b5a88", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "s involving gross motor\nskills requiring strength and endurance (such as cycling). The evidence in favor of this original matching hypothesis is mixed. The\nsystematic review by Tod et al. (2011) did not find support for the matching hypothesis as the", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "and reacts to using language. Within this kind of model, System 1 and System 2 self-talk are hypothesized to serve different functions.\nSystem 1 represents the immediate, emotionally charged reaction to a situation, such as when athletes automatically swear at\nthemselves when they make a mistake. System 2 represents top-down, strategic use of self-talk whether this takes the form of moti­\nvational, instructional, or distracting self-talk. System 2 also monitors System 1 and attempts to regulate the automatic output. The\ndual-process perspective and the discursive perspective are compatible – according to Van Raalte et al. (2016) – because they together\n1 as well as our behavior).\nThe discursive aspect of Van Raalte et al.’s model is compatible with a Vygotskian model of self-regulation (see especially Larrain &\nHaye, 2012) as Vygotskian self-regulation is also hypothesized to be internalized dialogue stemming from overt dialogue with parents\nor care-givers. Similarly, the dual-process perspective is compatible if we think of System 2 as the self-regulating system, reacting to the\nautomatic inner speech emerging from System 1. As System 2 is supposedly quasi-rational and amenable to further information coming\nfrom the outside, we should also envision it as the system that actually undergoes change during self-talk interventions (this would be\ntermed ‘strategic self-talk’ in Latinjak et al.’s terminology). This happens for examp", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "s involving gross motor\nskills requiring strength and endurance (such as cycling). The evidence in favor of this original matching hypothesis is mixed. The\nsystematic review by Tod et al. (2011) did not find support for the matching hypothesis as the"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0003_c0019", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f966b1ff71b8294f", "text_span_hash": "f966b1ff71b8294f", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 19, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "iffered between\n1.3. Why should self-talk be related to skill-level in endurance sport?\nOnly a few studies to date have directly investigated self-talk in endurance sport. This type of sport is particularly interesting from a\ncognitive perspective be", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "and reacts to using language. Within this kind of model, System 1 and System 2 self-talk are hypothesized to serve different functions.\nSystem 1 represents the immediate, emotionally charged reaction to a situation, such as when athletes automatically swear at\nthemselves when they make a mistake. System 2 represents top-down, strategic use of self-talk whether this takes the form of moti­\nvational, instructional, or distracting self-talk. System 2 also monitors System 1 and attempts to regulate the automatic output. The\ndual-process perspective and the discursive perspective are compatible – according to Van Raalte et al. (2016) – because they together\n1 as well as our behavior).\nThe discursive aspect of Van Raalte et al.’s model is compatible with a Vygotskian model of self-regulation (see especially Larrain &\nHaye, 2012) as Vygotskian self-regulation is also hypothesized to be internalized dialogue stemming from overt dialogue with parents\nor care-givers. Similarly, the dual-process perspective is compatible if we think of System 2 as the self-regulating system, reacting to the\nautomatic inner speech emerging from System 1. As System 2 is supposedly quasi-rational and amenable to further information coming\nfrom the outside, we should also envision it as the system that actually undergoes change during self-talk interventions (this would be\ntermed ‘strategic self-talk’ in Latinjak et al.’s terminology). This happens for examp", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "iffered between\n1.3. Why should self-talk be related to skill-level in endurance sport?\nOnly a few studies to date have directly investigated self-talk in endurance sport. This type of sport is particularly interesting from a\ncognitive perspective be"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0003_c0020", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "216d2474268b6190", "text_span_hash": "216d2474268b6190", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 20, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "rom a\ncognitive perspective because it involves a real-world example of a challenge that requires a sustained high degree of cognitive control.\nLong-distance runners, cyclists, swimmers, rowers, etc. have to continuously inhibit the prepotent respons", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "and reacts to using language. Within this kind of model, System 1 and System 2 self-talk are hypothesized to serve different functions.\nSystem 1 represents the immediate, emotionally charged reaction to a situation, such as when athletes automatically swear at\nthemselves when they make a mistake. System 2 represents top-down, strategic use of self-talk whether this takes the form of moti­\nvational, instructional, or distracting self-talk. System 2 also monitors System 1 and attempts to regulate the automatic output. The\ndual-process perspective and the discursive perspective are compatible – according to Van Raalte et al. (2016) – because they together\n1 as well as our behavior).\nThe discursive aspect of Van Raalte et al.’s model is compatible with a Vygotskian model of self-regulation (see especially Larrain &\nHaye, 2012) as Vygotskian self-regulation is also hypothesized to be internalized dialogue stemming from overt dialogue with parents\nor care-givers. Similarly, the dual-process perspective is compatible if we think of System 2 as the self-regulating system, reacting to the\nautomatic inner speech emerging from System 1. As System 2 is supposedly quasi-rational and amenable to further information coming\nfrom the outside, we should also envision it as the system that actually undergoes change during self-talk interventions (this would be\ntermed ‘strategic self-talk’ in Latinjak et al.’s terminology). This happens for examp", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "rom a\ncognitive perspective because it involves a real-world example of a challenge that requires a sustained high degree of cognitive control.\nLong-distance runners, cyclists, swimmers, rowers, etc. have to continuously inhibit the prepotent respons"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0003_c0022", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "046f59bad0b52d5a", "text_span_hash": "046f59bad0b52d5a", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 22, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "alone with their thoughts for prolonged stretches of time during both training and competition. The self-talk\ncontent in endurance sport has previously been investigated in a questionnaire study on marathon runners by Van Raalte et al.\n(2015) and se", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "and reacts to using language. Within this kind of model, System 1 and System 2 self-talk are hypothesized to serve different functions.\nSystem 1 represents the immediate, emotionally charged reaction to a situation, such as when athletes automatically swear at\nthemselves when they make a mistake. System 2 represents top-down, strategic use of self-talk whether this takes the form of moti­\nvational, instructional, or distracting self-talk. System 2 also monitors System 1 and attempts to regulate the automatic output. The\ndual-process perspective and the discursive perspective are compatible – according to Van Raalte et al. (2016) – because they together\n1 as well as our behavior).\nThe discursive aspect of Van Raalte et al.’s model is compatible with a Vygotskian model of self-regulation (see especially Larrain &\nHaye, 2012) as Vygotskian self-regulation is also hypothesized to be internalized dialogue stemming from overt dialogue with parents\nor care-givers. Similarly, the dual-process perspective is compatible if we think of System 2 as the self-regulating system, reacting to the\nautomatic inner speech emerging from System 1. As System 2 is supposedly quasi-rational and amenable to further information coming\nfrom the outside, we should also envision it as the system that actually undergoes change during self-talk interventions (this would be\ntermed ‘strategic self-talk’ in Latinjak et al.’s terminology). This happens for examp", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "alone with their thoughts for prolonged stretches of time during both training and competition. The self-talk\ncontent in endurance sport has previously been investigated in a questionnaire study on marathon runners by Van Raalte et al.\n(2015) and se"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0003_c0024", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "41e7ddd7d3c81e34", "text_span_hash": "41e7ddd7d3c81e34", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 24, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "2007; McCormick, Meijen, & Marcora, 2018; Barwood et al.,\n2015). Van Raalte et al. (2015) asked a large number of marathon runners (N = 483) to list their thoughts in an open-ended way and\nlater coded the answers into categories. The authors found t", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "and reacts to using language. Within this kind of model, System 1 and System 2 self-talk are hypothesized to serve different functions.\nSystem 1 represents the immediate, emotionally charged reaction to a situation, such as when athletes automatically swear at\nthemselves when they make a mistake. System 2 represents top-down, strategic use of self-talk whether this takes the form of moti­\nvational, instructional, or distracting self-talk. System 2 also monitors System 1 and attempts to regulate the automatic output. The\ndual-process perspective and the discursive perspective are compatible – according to Van Raalte et al. (2016) – because they together\n1 as well as our behavior).\nThe discursive aspect of Van Raalte et al.’s model is compatible with a Vygotskian model of self-regulation (see especially Larrain &\nHaye, 2012) as Vygotskian self-regulation is also hypothesized to be internalized dialogue stemming from overt dialogue with parents\nor care-givers. Similarly, the dual-process perspective is compatible if we think of System 2 as the self-regulating system, reacting to the\nautomatic inner speech emerging from System 1. As System 2 is supposedly quasi-rational and amenable to further information coming\nfrom the outside, we should also envision it as the system that actually undergoes change during self-talk interventions (this would be\ntermed ‘strategic self-talk’ in Latinjak et al.’s terminology). This happens for examp", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "2007; McCormick, Meijen, & Marcora, 2018; Barwood et al.,\n2015). Van Raalte et al. (2015) asked a large number of marathon runners (N = 483) to list their thoughts in an open-ended way and\nlater coded the answers into categories. The authors found t"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0003_c0025", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "844793531503af3d", "text_span_hash": "844793531503af3d", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 25, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ategories. The authors found that 88% of marathon runners reported engaging in self-talk while running,\nand that this self-talk took a variety of both motivational and instructional forms. Although their study did not explicitly associate self-\ntalk", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "and reacts to using language. Within this kind of model, System 1 and System 2 self-talk are hypothesized to serve different functions.\nSystem 1 represents the immediate, emotionally charged reaction to a situation, such as when athletes automatically swear at\nthemselves when they make a mistake. System 2 represents top-down, strategic use of self-talk whether this takes the form of moti­\nvational, instructional, or distracting self-talk. System 2 also monitors System 1 and attempts to regulate the automatic output. The\ndual-process perspective and the discursive perspective are compatible – according to Van Raalte et al. (2016) – because they together\n1 as well as our behavior).\nThe discursive aspect of Van Raalte et al.’s model is compatible with a Vygotskian model of self-regulation (see especially Larrain &\nHaye, 2012) as Vygotskian self-regulation is also hypothesized to be internalized dialogue stemming from overt dialogue with parents\nor care-givers. Similarly, the dual-process perspective is compatible if we think of System 2 as the self-regulating system, reacting to the\nautomatic inner speech emerging from System 1. As System 2 is supposedly quasi-rational and amenable to further information coming\nfrom the outside, we should also envision it as the system that actually undergoes change during self-talk interventions (this would be\ntermed ‘strategic self-talk’ in Latinjak et al.’s terminology). This happens for examp", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ategories. The authors found that 88% of marathon runners reported engaging in self-talk while running,\nand that this self-talk took a variety of both motivational and instructional forms. Although their study did not explicitly associate self-\ntalk"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0003_c0026", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c6dd8b9ddf86192c", "text_span_hash": "c6dd8b9ddf86192c", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 26, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "plicitly associate self-\ntalk with marathon performance, Van Raalte and colleagues did find that elite marathon runners meeting standards set by USA Track &\nField engaged significantly more in associative self-talk (i.e. self-talk related to the body", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "and reacts to using language. Within this kind of model, System 1 and System 2 self-talk are hypothesized to serve different functions.\nSystem 1 represents the immediate, emotionally charged reaction to a situation, such as when athletes automatically swear at\nthemselves when they make a mistake. System 2 represents top-down, strategic use of self-talk whether this takes the form of moti­\nvational, instructional, or distracting self-talk. System 2 also monitors System 1 and attempts to regulate the automatic output. The\ndual-process perspective and the discursive perspective are compatible – according to Van Raalte et al. (2016) – because they together\n1 as well as our behavior).\nThe discursive aspect of Van Raalte et al.’s model is compatible with a Vygotskian model of self-regulation (see especially Larrain &\nHaye, 2012) as Vygotskian self-regulation is also hypothesized to be internalized dialogue stemming from overt dialogue with parents\nor care-givers. Similarly, the dual-process perspective is compatible if we think of System 2 as the self-regulating system, reacting to the\nautomatic inner speech emerging from System 1. As System 2 is supposedly quasi-rational and amenable to further information coming\nfrom the outside, we should also envision it as the system that actually undergoes change during self-talk interventions (this would be\ntermed ‘strategic self-talk’ in Latinjak et al.’s terminology). This happens for examp", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "plicitly associate self-\ntalk with marathon performance, Van Raalte and colleagues did find that elite marathon runners meeting standards set by USA Track &\nField engaged significantly more in associative self-talk (i.e. self-talk related to the body"}
