e\nautomatic inner speech emerging from System 1. As System 2 is supposedly quasi-rational and amenable to further information coming\nfrom the outside, we should also envision it as the system that actually undergoes change during self-talk interventions (this would be\ntermed ‘strategic self-talk’ in Latinjak et al.’s terminology). This happens for examp", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "two intervention studies to date have taken place outside the laboratory, thus limiting the ecological validity of the findings.\nIn these two “field studies”, one found an effect of self-talk on endurance sport (Schüler & Langens, 2007), while the ot"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0003_c0032", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5f7241e3242e7837", "text_span_hash": "5f7241e3242e7837", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 32, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "& Langens, 2007), while the other did not\n(McCormick et al., 2018). McCormick et al. (2018) found that runners who were trained on motivational self-talk reported finding the\nJ. Nedergaard et al.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "and reacts to using language. Within this kind of model, System 1 and System 2 self-talk are hypothesized to serve different functions.\nSystem 1 represents the immediate, emotionally charged reaction to a situation, such as when athletes automatically swear at\nthemselves when they make a mistake. System 2 represents top-down, strategic use of self-talk whether this takes the form of moti­\nvational, instructional, or distracting self-talk. System 2 also monitors System 1 and attempts to regulate the automatic output. The\ndual-process perspective and the discursive perspective are compatible – according to Van Raalte et al. (2016) – because they together\n1 as well as our behavior).\nThe discursive aspect of Van Raalte et al.’s model is compatible with a Vygotskian model of self-regulation (see especially Larrain &\nHaye, 2012) as Vygotskian self-regulation is also hypothesized to be internalized dialogue stemming from overt dialogue with parents\nor care-givers. Similarly, the dual-process perspective is compatible if we think of System 2 as the self-regulating system, reacting to the\nautomatic inner speech emerging from System 1. As System 2 is supposedly quasi-rational and amenable to further information coming\nfrom the outside, we should also envision it as the system that actually undergoes change during self-talk interventions (this would be\ntermed ‘strategic self-talk’ in Latinjak et al.’s terminology). This happens for examp", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "& Langens, 2007), while the other did not\n(McCormick et al., 2018). McCormick et al. (2018) found that runners who were trained on motivational self-talk reported finding the\nJ. Nedergaard et al."}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0004_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4bf188ea4a306ed9", "text_span_hash": "4bf188ea4a306ed9", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "intervention helpful although it did not affect their performance on a 60-mile, overnight ultramarathon. Schüler and Langens (2007)\nfound that self-talk training provided a successful buffer against the negative impact of psychological crises during", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "intervention helpful although it did not affect their performance on a 60-mile, overnight ultramarathon. Schüler and Langens (2007)\nfound that self-talk training provided a successful buffer against the negative impact of psychological crises during a marathon race.\n1.4. Present questions\nThe present study aimed to replicate existing findings on the prevalence and efficacy of self-talk in sports and relate these findings\nto cognitive science research more broadly. In addition, its goal was to explore the effects of personal and contextual factors on self-talk\n(Van Raalte et al., 2016) with Study 1 comparing self-talk across sport contexts (type of sport) and Study 2 exploring how personal\nfactors (e.g., skill-level) are related to self-talk and performance.\nIt is often assumed in the literature that different types of sports place different demands on self-talk (see for example the matching\nhypothesis; Theodorakis et al., 2000), but direct comparisons of different sports, e.g. involving different levels of fine motor control,\nhave rarely been conducted in the same study. In Study 1, we tested whether it was possible to discern the types of self-talk produced by\npractitioners of a sport involving a high degree of technical control (in casu badminton) versus a sport relying more on endurance\n(running). These two types of sport are also notably different in that badminton is a game while running is often a timed event. In Study\n2", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "intervention helpful although it did not affect their performance on a 60-mile, overnight ultramarathon. Schüler and Langens (2007)\nfound that self-talk training provided a successful buffer against the negative impact of psychological crises during"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0004_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5f1dd33dea9abb42", "text_span_hash": "5f1dd33dea9abb42", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "f psychological crises during a marathon race.\n1.4. Present questions\nThe present study aimed to replicate existing findings on the prevalence and efficacy of self-talk in sports and relate these findings\nto cognitive science research more broadly. I", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "intervention helpful although it did not affect their performance on a 60-mile, overnight ultramarathon. Schüler and Langens (2007)\nfound that self-talk training provided a successful buffer against the negative impact of psychological crises during a marathon race.\n1.4. Present questions\nThe present study aimed to replicate existing findings on the prevalence and efficacy of self-talk in sports and relate these findings\nto cognitive science research more broadly. In addition, its goal was to explore the effects of personal and contextual factors on self-talk\n(Van Raalte et al., 2016) with Study 1 comparing self-talk across sport contexts (type of sport) and Study 2 exploring how personal\nfactors (e.g., skill-level) are related to self-talk and performance.\nIt is often assumed in the literature that different types of sports place different demands on self-talk (see for example the matching\nhypothesis; Theodorakis et al., 2000), but direct comparisons of different sports, e.g. involving different levels of fine motor control,\nhave rarely been conducted in the same study. In Study 1, we tested whether it was possible to discern the types of self-talk produced by\npractitioners of a sport involving a high degree of technical control (in casu badminton) versus a sport relying more on endurance\n(running). These two types of sport are also notably different in that badminton is a game while running is often a timed event. In Study\n2", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "f psychological crises during a marathon race.\n1.4. Present questions\nThe present study aimed to replicate existing findings on the prevalence and efficacy of self-talk in sports and relate these findings\nto cognitive science research more broadly. I"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0004_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c908f370c1c75df6", "text_span_hash": "c908f370c1c75df6", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "we tested whether it was possible to discern the types of self-talk produced by\npractitioners of a sport involving a high degree of technical control (in casu badminton) versus a sport relying more on endurance\n(running). These two types of sport are", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "intervention helpful although it did not affect their performance on a 60-mile, overnight ultramarathon. Schüler and Langens (2007)\nfound that self-talk training provided a successful buffer against the negative impact of psychological crises during a marathon race.\n1.4. Present questions\nThe present study aimed to replicate existing findings on the prevalence and efficacy of self-talk in sports and relate these findings\nto cognitive science research more broadly. In addition, its goal was to explore the effects of personal and contextual factors on self-talk\n(Van Raalte et al., 2016) with Study 1 comparing self-talk across sport contexts (type of sport) and Study 2 exploring how personal\nfactors (e.g., skill-level) are related to self-talk and performance.\nIt is often assumed in the literature that different types of sports place different demands on self-talk (see for example the matching\nhypothesis; Theodorakis et al., 2000), but direct comparisons of different sports, e.g. involving different levels of fine motor control,\nhave rarely been conducted in the same study. In Study 1, we tested whether it was possible to discern the types of self-talk produced by\npractitioners of a sport involving a high degree of technical control (in casu badminton) versus a sport relying more on endurance\n(running). These two types of sport are also notably different in that badminton is a game while running is often a timed event. In Study\n2", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "we tested whether it was possible to discern the types of self-talk produced by\npractitioners of a sport involving a high degree of technical control (in casu badminton) versus a sport relying more on endurance\n(running). These two types of sport are"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0004_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "30df67dc75d58505", "text_span_hash": "30df67dc75d58505", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": ". These two types of sport are also notably different in that badminton is a game while running is often a timed event. In Study\n2, which dealt exclusively with long distance runners, we also tested whether self-talk was associated with marathon/half", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "intervention helpful although it did not affect their performance on a 60-mile, overnight ultramarathon. Schüler and Langens (2007)\nfound that self-talk training provided a successful buffer against the negative impact of psychological crises during a marathon race.\n1.4. Present questions\nThe present study aimed to replicate existing findings on the prevalence and efficacy of self-talk in sports and relate these findings\nto cognitive science research more broadly. In addition, its goal was to explore the effects of personal and contextual factors on self-talk\n(Van Raalte et al., 2016) with Study 1 comparing self-talk across sport contexts (type of sport) and Study 2 exploring how personal\nfactors (e.g., skill-level) are related to self-talk and performance.\nIt is often assumed in the literature that different types of sports place different demands on self-talk (see for example the matching\nhypothesis; Theodorakis et al., 2000), but direct comparisons of different sports, e.g. involving different levels of fine motor control,\nhave rarely been conducted in the same study. In Study 1, we tested whether it was possible to discern the types of self-talk produced by\npractitioners of a sport involving a high degree of technical control (in casu badminton) versus a sport relying more on endurance\n(running). These two types of sport are also notably different in that badminton is a game while running is often a timed event. In Study\n2", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": ". These two types of sport are also notably different in that badminton is a game while running is often a timed event. In Study\n2, which dealt exclusively with long distance runners, we also tested whether self-talk was associated with marathon/half"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0004_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7021a2d8ad293062", "text_span_hash": "7021a2d8ad293062", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "associated with marathon/half marathon\nrunning ability as well as whether participants differed in their self-talk use in high pressure versus low pressure situations. We hy­\npothesized that high effort would be associated with abbreviated, associat", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "intervention helpful although it did not affect their performance on a 60-mile, overnight ultramarathon. Schüler and Langens (2007)\nfound that self-talk training provided a successful buffer against the negative impact of psychological crises during a marathon race.\n1.4. Present questions\nThe present study aimed to replicate existing findings on the prevalence and efficacy of self-talk in sports and relate these findings\nto cognitive science research more broadly. In addition, its goal was to explore the effects of personal and contextual factors on self-talk\n(Van Raalte et al., 2016) with Study 1 comparing self-talk across sport contexts (type of sport) and Study 2 exploring how personal\nfactors (e.g., skill-level) are related to self-talk and performance.\nIt is often assumed in the literature that different types of sports place different demands on self-talk (see for example the matching\nhypothesis; Theodorakis et al., 2000), but direct comparisons of different sports, e.g. involving different levels of fine motor control,\nhave rarely been conducted in the same study. In Study 1, we tested whether it was possible to discern the types of self-talk produced by\npractitioners of a sport involving a high degree of technical control (in casu badminton) versus a sport relying more on endurance\n(running). These two types of sport are also notably different in that badminton is a game while running is often a timed event. In Study\n2", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "associated with marathon/half marathon\nrunning ability as well as whether participants differed in their self-talk use in high pressure versus low pressure situations. We hy­\npothesized that high effort would be associated with abbreviated, associat"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0004_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f04d577126085336", "text_span_hash": "f04d577126085336", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ften they exercised, as we hypothesized that people who exercise\nfrequently will be more adept at using self-talk as a tool for motor control and endurance.\nThe Automatic Self-Talk Questionnaire for Sports (ASTQS; Zourbanos, Hatzigeorgiadis, Chroni,", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "intervention helpful although it did not affect their performance on a 60-mile, overnight ultramarathon. Schüler and Langens (2007)\nfound that self-talk training provided a successful buffer against the negative impact of psychological crises during a marathon race.\n1.4. Present questions\nThe present study aimed to replicate existing findings on the prevalence and efficacy of self-talk in sports and relate these findings\nto cognitive science research more broadly. In addition, its goal was to explore the effects of personal and contextual factors on self-talk\n(Van Raalte et al., 2016) with Study 1 comparing self-talk across sport contexts (type of sport) and Study 2 exploring how personal\nfactors (e.g., skill-level) are related to self-talk and performance.\nIt is often assumed in the literature that different types of sports place different demands on self-talk (see for example the matching\nhypothesis; Theodorakis et al., 2000), but direct comparisons of different sports, e.g. involving different levels of fine motor control,\nhave rarely been conducted in the same study. In Study 1, we tested whether it was possible to discern the types of self-talk produced by\npractitioners of a sport involving a high degree of technical control (in casu badminton) versus a sport relying more on endurance\n(running). These two types of sport are also notably different in that badminton is a game while running is often a timed event. In Study\n2", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ften they exercised, as we hypothesized that people who exercise\nfrequently will be more adept at using self-talk as a tool for motor control and endurance.\nThe Automatic Self-Talk Questionnaire for Sports (ASTQS; Zourbanos, Hatzigeorgiadis, Chroni,"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0004_c0017", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "182e9c7aa38da062", "text_span_hash": "182e9c7aa38da062", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 17, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "hy­\npothesized that we would find significant differences between people who engage in a gross motor endurance sport like running and\npeople who prefer a technical sport like badminton.\nWe recruited participants from Danish Facebook groups dedicated", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "intervention helpful although it did not affect their performance on a 60-mile, overnight ultramarathon. Schüler and Langens (2007)\nfound that self-talk training provided a successful buffer against the negative impact of psychological crises during a marathon race.\n1.4. Present questions\nThe present study aimed to replicate existing findings on the prevalence and efficacy of self-talk in sports and relate these findings\nto cognitive science research more broadly. In addition, its goal was to explore the effects of personal and contextual factors on self-talk\n(Van Raalte et al., 2016) with Study 1 comparing self-talk across sport contexts (type of sport) and Study 2 exploring how personal\nfactors (e.g., skill-level) are related to self-talk and performance.\nIt is often assumed in the literature that different types of sports place different demands on self-talk (see for example the matching\nhypothesis; Theodorakis et al., 2000), but direct comparisons of different sports, e.g. involving different levels of fine motor control,\nhave rarely been conducted in the same study. In Study 1, we tested whether it was possible to discern the types of self-talk produced by\npractitioners of a sport involving a high degree of technical control (in casu badminton) versus a sport relying more on endurance\n(running). These two types of sport are also notably different in that badminton is a game while running is often a timed event. In Study\n2", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "hy­\npothesized that we would find significant differences between people who engage in a gross motor endurance sport like running and\npeople who prefer a technical sport like badminton.\nWe recruited participants from Danish Facebook groups dedicated"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0004_c0018", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ce001ef9942782b0", "text_span_hash": "ce001ef9942782b0", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 18, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "nish Facebook groups dedicated to badminton and running. In total, 270 participants completed\nthe survey (127 men and 143 women; 70 in the 18–25 age group, 55 in the 26–35 age group, 48 in the 36–45 age group, 73 in the\n46–55 age group, 19 in the 56–", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "intervention helpful although it did not affect their performance on a 60-mile, overnight ultramarathon. Schüler and Langens (2007)\nfound that self-talk training provided a successful buffer against the negative impact of psychological crises during a marathon race.\n1.4. Present questions\nThe present study aimed to replicate existing findings on the prevalence and efficacy of self-talk in sports and relate these findings\nto cognitive science research more broadly. In addition, its goal was to explore the effects of personal and contextual factors on self-talk\n(Van Raalte et al., 2016) with Study 1 comparing self-talk across sport contexts (type of sport) and Study 2 exploring how personal\nfactors (e.g., skill-level) are related to self-talk and performance.\nIt is often assumed in the literature that different types of sports place different demands on self-talk (see for example the matching\nhypothesis; Theodorakis et al., 2000), but direct comparisons of different sports, e.g. involving different levels of fine motor control,\nhave rarely been conducted in the same study. In Study 1, we tested whether it was possible to discern the types of self-talk produced by\npractitioners of a sport involving a high degree of technical control (in casu badminton) versus a sport relying more on endurance\n(running). These two types of sport are also notably different in that badminton is a game while running is often a timed event. In Study\n2", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "nish Facebook groups dedicated to badminton and running. In total, 270 participants completed\nthe survey (127 men and 143 women; 70 in the 18–25 age group, 55 in the 26–35 age group, 48 in the 36–45 age group, 73 in the\n46–55 age group, 19 in the 56–"}
