udy 1, we tested whether it was possible to discern the types of self-talk produced by\npractitioners of a sport involving a high degree of technical control (in casu badminton) versus a sport relying more on endurance\n(running). These two types of sport are also notably different in that badminton is a game while running is often a timed event. In Study\n2", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "46–55 age group, 19 in the 56–65 age group, and 5 in the 66–75 age group). Our sample included 165 runners and 105 badminton\nplayers. The project received ethical approval from the Internal Review Board at Aarhus University.\nWe deployed a Danish tran"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0004_c0027", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b29725c3501641f2", "text_span_hash": "b29725c3501641f2", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 27, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "of athletes. Base your answers on your\nlatest competition or training and use the scale to indicate which thoughts you normally experience or use on purpose while\nJ. Nedergaard et al.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "intervention helpful although it did not affect their performance on a 60-mile, overnight ultramarathon. Schüler and Langens (2007)\nfound that self-talk training provided a successful buffer against the negative impact of psychological crises during a marathon race.\n1.4. Present questions\nThe present study aimed to replicate existing findings on the prevalence and efficacy of self-talk in sports and relate these findings\nto cognitive science research more broadly. In addition, its goal was to explore the effects of personal and contextual factors on self-talk\n(Van Raalte et al., 2016) with Study 1 comparing self-talk across sport contexts (type of sport) and Study 2 exploring how personal\nfactors (e.g., skill-level) are related to self-talk and performance.\nIt is often assumed in the literature that different types of sports place different demands on self-talk (see for example the matching\nhypothesis; Theodorakis et al., 2000), but direct comparisons of different sports, e.g. involving different levels of fine motor control,\nhave rarely been conducted in the same study. In Study 1, we tested whether it was possible to discern the types of self-talk produced by\npractitioners of a sport involving a high degree of technical control (in casu badminton) versus a sport relying more on endurance\n(running). These two types of sport are also notably different in that badminton is a game while running is often a timed event. In Study\n2", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "of athletes. Base your answers on your\nlatest competition or training and use the scale to indicate which thoughts you normally experience or use on purpose while\nJ. Nedergaard et al."}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0005_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2293fe9c8aafe574", "text_span_hash": "2293fe9c8aafe574", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "r an overview of answers split into runners and badminton players and Fig. 1 for visualizations of the responses.\n3.3. Machine learning models\nWe fitted three classification models with the ‘glmnet’ algorithm on the entire data set: one model of age,", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "2.4. Machine learning models\nSince the factors that emerge from the ASTQS are heavily correlated, we could not use them for linear mixed model regression\nanalyses as predictors. Instead, we chose to use machine learning models to predict sport type and exercise frequency from all the\nquestionnaire items. For predicting sport type and exercise frequency from the questionnaire items, we fit a classification and a\nregression model respectively using the ‘glmnet’ (Friedman, Hastie, & Tibshirani, 2010) and ‘caret’ (Kuhn, 2020) packages in R version\n3.6.2 (R Core Team, 2019). The ‘glmnet’ method fits a generalized linear model via penalized maximum likelihood and thus avoids\nmulticollinearity of predictors which would be an issue for regular linear mixed models. We used repeated k-fold cross-validation in all\nthe models to get stable estimates of the models’ predictive performance.\n3.1. Questionnaire reliability\nWe calculated the internal consistency (Cronbach’s alpha) of the entire questionnaire using the following formula\nwhere p is the number of items, σ2\nx is the variance of the observed total test scores, and σ2\nyi is the variance of the ith item. The ques­\ntionnaire had a Cronbach’s alpha of 0.889, indicating a good internal consistency.\n3.2. Self-talk frequency and self-talk efficacy\nSee Table 1 for an overview of answers split into runners and badminton players and Fig. 1 for visualizations of the responses.\n3.3. Machine lea", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "r an overview of answers split into runners and badminton players and Fig. 1 for visualizations of the responses.\n3.3. Machine learning models\nWe fitted three classification models with the ‘glmnet’ algorithm on the entire data set: one model of age,"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0005_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "04c1c44aef4a9dc3", "text_span_hash": "04c1c44aef4a9dc3", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "cise frequency predicting sport type (see Table 2 for all the model fit statistics for the three\nmodels). We did this instead of training the model on a training set and testing it on a test set because the random split was too unstable\ngiven the siz", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "2.4. Machine learning models\nSince the factors that emerge from the ASTQS are heavily correlated, we could not use them for linear mixed model regression\nanalyses as predictors. Instead, we chose to use machine learning models to predict sport type and exercise frequency from all the\nquestionnaire items. For predicting sport type and exercise frequency from the questionnaire items, we fit a classification and a\nregression model respectively using the ‘glmnet’ (Friedman, Hastie, & Tibshirani, 2010) and ‘caret’ (Kuhn, 2020) packages in R version\n3.6.2 (R Core Team, 2019). The ‘glmnet’ method fits a generalized linear model via penalized maximum likelihood and thus avoids\nmulticollinearity of predictors which would be an issue for regular linear mixed models. We used repeated k-fold cross-validation in all\nthe models to get stable estimates of the models’ predictive performance.\n3.1. Questionnaire reliability\nWe calculated the internal consistency (Cronbach’s alpha) of the entire questionnaire using the following formula\nwhere p is the number of items, σ2\nx is the variance of the observed total test scores, and σ2\nyi is the variance of the ith item. The ques­\ntionnaire had a Cronbach’s alpha of 0.889, indicating a good internal consistency.\n3.2. Self-talk frequency and self-talk efficacy\nSee Table 1 for an overview of answers split into runners and badminton players and Fig. 1 for visualizations of the responses.\n3.3. Machine lea", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "cise frequency predicting sport type (see Table 2 for all the model fit statistics for the three\nmodels). We did this instead of training the model on a training set and testing it on a test set because the random split was too unstable\ngiven the siz"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0005_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b2172a851d7980a6", "text_span_hash": "b2172a851d7980a6", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "was too unstable\ngiven the size of the entire data set. As there were slightly more runners than badminton players in the current sample, we also down-\nsampled the training set to alleviate class imbalances. The model centered and scaled the data and", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "2.4. Machine learning models\nSince the factors that emerge from the ASTQS are heavily correlated, we could not use them for linear mixed model regression\nanalyses as predictors. Instead, we chose to use machine learning models to predict sport type and exercise frequency from all the\nquestionnaire items. For predicting sport type and exercise frequency from the questionnaire items, we fit a classification and a\nregression model respectively using the ‘glmnet’ (Friedman, Hastie, & Tibshirani, 2010) and ‘caret’ (Kuhn, 2020) packages in R version\n3.6.2 (R Core Team, 2019). The ‘glmnet’ method fits a generalized linear model via penalized maximum likelihood and thus avoids\nmulticollinearity of predictors which would be an issue for regular linear mixed models. We used repeated k-fold cross-validation in all\nthe models to get stable estimates of the models’ predictive performance.\n3.1. Questionnaire reliability\nWe calculated the internal consistency (Cronbach’s alpha) of the entire questionnaire using the following formula\nwhere p is the number of items, σ2\nx is the variance of the observed total test scores, and σ2\nyi is the variance of the ith item. The ques­\ntionnaire had a Cronbach’s alpha of 0.889, indicating a good internal consistency.\n3.2. Self-talk frequency and self-talk efficacy\nSee Table 1 for an overview of answers split into runners and badminton players and Fig. 1 for visualizations of the responses.\n3.3. Machine lea", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "was too unstable\ngiven the size of the entire data set. As there were slightly more runners than badminton players in the current sample, we also down-\nsampled the training set to alleviate class imbalances. The model centered and scaled the data and"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0006_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "911a2cd63ec0d55b", "text_span_hash": "911a2cd63ec0d55b", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "3.4. Interim discussion\nWe replicated the findings that self-talk is reported to be widely used in sports with approximately 70–90% of runners and\nbadminton players reporting engaging in self-talk, sometimes, often or very often (Table 1). In additio", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "3.4. Interim discussion\nWe replicated the findings that self-talk is reported to be widely used in sports with approximately 70–90% of runners and\nbadminton players reporting engaging in self-talk, sometimes, often or very often (Table 1). In addition to this, we documented\nimportant differences in the content of self-talk exhibited by practitioners of two different types of sport. According to our machine\nlearning models, runners’ self-talk was characterized by being more prone to disengagement and defeatism while badminton players’\nself-talk was more characterized by worry (e.g. ‘I’m doing it wrong again’) and anxiety-control (e.g. ‘Don’t get upset’). The results from\nthe model predicting exercise frequency provided evidence that there is a reliable connection between how people talk to themselves\nand their level of fitness. Especially the most negatively predictive item (‘My body is not in a good condition’) and the most positively\npredictive item (‘I’m very well prepared’) provide good coherence checks for the analyses (Fig. 3).\nThese results implicate that there are qualitative differences between self-talk during a sport like badminton which is a technical,\n(sometimes) team-based game and a sport like running which is more solitary, often timed, and relying on gross motor movements. The\nfinding that runners are more prone to disengagement (Fig. 2) underlines the importance of being able to control your inner voice\nwhen y", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "3.4. Interim discussion\nWe replicated the findings that self-talk is reported to be widely used in sports with approximately 70–90% of runners and\nbadminton players reporting engaging in self-talk, sometimes, often or very often (Table 1). In additio"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0006_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "6122c2070fca6fd6", "text_span_hash": "6122c2070fca6fd6", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ry often (Table 1). In addition to this, we documented\nimportant differences in the content of self-talk exhibited by practitioners of two different types of sport. According to our machine\nlearning models, runners’ self-talk was characterized by bei", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "3.4. Interim discussion\nWe replicated the findings that self-talk is reported to be widely used in sports with approximately 70–90% of runners and\nbadminton players reporting engaging in self-talk, sometimes, often or very often (Table 1). In addition to this, we documented\nimportant differences in the content of self-talk exhibited by practitioners of two different types of sport. According to our machine\nlearning models, runners’ self-talk was characterized by being more prone to disengagement and defeatism while badminton players’\nself-talk was more characterized by worry (e.g. ‘I’m doing it wrong again’) and anxiety-control (e.g. ‘Don’t get upset’). The results from\nthe model predicting exercise frequency provided evidence that there is a reliable connection between how people talk to themselves\nand their level of fitness. Especially the most negatively predictive item (‘My body is not in a good condition’) and the most positively\npredictive item (‘I’m very well prepared’) provide good coherence checks for the analyses (Fig. 3).\nThese results implicate that there are qualitative differences between self-talk during a sport like badminton which is a technical,\n(sometimes) team-based game and a sport like running which is more solitary, often timed, and relying on gross motor movements. The\nfinding that runners are more prone to disengagement (Fig. 2) underlines the importance of being able to control your inner voice\nwhen y", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ry often (Table 1). In addition to this, we documented\nimportant differences in the content of self-talk exhibited by practitioners of two different types of sport. According to our machine\nlearning models, runners’ self-talk was characterized by bei"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0006_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "10d3ac699a3a2dd7", "text_span_hash": "10d3ac699a3a2dd7", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "differences between self-talk during a sport like badminton which is a technical,\n(sometimes) team-based game and a sport like running which is more solitary, often timed, and relying on gross motor movements. The\nfinding that runners are more prone", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "3.4. Interim discussion\nWe replicated the findings that self-talk is reported to be widely used in sports with approximately 70–90% of runners and\nbadminton players reporting engaging in self-talk, sometimes, often or very often (Table 1). In addition to this, we documented\nimportant differences in the content of self-talk exhibited by practitioners of two different types of sport. According to our machine\nlearning models, runners’ self-talk was characterized by being more prone to disengagement and defeatism while badminton players’\nself-talk was more characterized by worry (e.g. ‘I’m doing it wrong again’) and anxiety-control (e.g. ‘Don’t get upset’). The results from\nthe model predicting exercise frequency provided evidence that there is a reliable connection between how people talk to themselves\nand their level of fitness. Especially the most negatively predictive item (‘My body is not in a good condition’) and the most positively\npredictive item (‘I’m very well prepared’) provide good coherence checks for the analyses (Fig. 3).\nThese results implicate that there are qualitative differences between self-talk during a sport like badminton which is a technical,\n(sometimes) team-based game and a sport like running which is more solitary, often timed, and relying on gross motor movements. The\nfinding that runners are more prone to disengagement (Fig. 2) underlines the importance of being able to control your inner voice\nwhen y", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "differences between self-talk during a sport like badminton which is a technical,\n(sometimes) team-based game and a sport like running which is more solitary, often timed, and relying on gross motor movements. The\nfinding that runners are more prone"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0006_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8e4bb2da0754665f", "text_span_hash": "8e4bb2da0754665f", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "g that runners are more prone to disengagement (Fig. 2) underlines the importance of being able to control your inner voice\nwhen you are running long distances with much opportunity for self-doubt and defeatism.\nCriticism of the ASTQS has been brough", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "3.4. Interim discussion\nWe replicated the findings that self-talk is reported to be widely used in sports with approximately 70–90% of runners and\nbadminton players reporting engaging in self-talk, sometimes, often or very often (Table 1). In addition to this, we documented\nimportant differences in the content of self-talk exhibited by practitioners of two different types of sport. According to our machine\nlearning models, runners’ self-talk was characterized by being more prone to disengagement and defeatism while badminton players’\nself-talk was more characterized by worry (e.g. ‘I’m doing it wrong again’) and anxiety-control (e.g. ‘Don’t get upset’). The results from\nthe model predicting exercise frequency provided evidence that there is a reliable connection between how people talk to themselves\nand their level of fitness. Especially the most negatively predictive item (‘My body is not in a good condition’) and the most positively\npredictive item (‘I’m very well prepared’) provide good coherence checks for the analyses (Fig. 3).\nThese results implicate that there are qualitative differences between self-talk during a sport like badminton which is a technical,\n(sometimes) team-based game and a sport like running which is more solitary, often timed, and relying on gross motor movements. The\nfinding that runners are more prone to disengagement (Fig. 2) underlines the importance of being able to control your inner voice\nwhen y", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "g that runners are more prone to disengagement (Fig. 2) underlines the importance of being able to control your inner voice\nwhen you are running long distances with much opportunity for self-doubt and defeatism.\nCriticism of the ASTQS has been brough"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0006_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "45e12d18ff659f14", "text_span_hash": "45e12d18ff659f14", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "rrespondence was observed.\nThis criticism applies less to our study for two main reasons. First, we do not see reason to believe that external and internal self-talk\nFig. 1. Self-talk frequency and self-talk efficacy in our sample of runners (1) and", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "3.4. Interim discussion\nWe replicated the findings that self-talk is reported to be widely used in sports with approximately 70–90% of runners and\nbadminton players reporting engaging in self-talk, sometimes, often or very often (Table 1). In addition to this, we documented\nimportant differences in the content of self-talk exhibited by practitioners of two different types of sport. According to our machine\nlearning models, runners’ self-talk was characterized by being more prone to disengagement and defeatism while badminton players’\nself-talk was more characterized by worry (e.g. ‘I’m doing it wrong again’) and anxiety-control (e.g. ‘Don’t get upset’). The results from\nthe model predicting exercise frequency provided evidence that there is a reliable connection between how people talk to themselves\nand their level of fitness. Especially the most negatively predictive item (‘My body is not in a good condition’) and the most positively\npredictive item (‘I’m very well prepared’) provide good coherence checks for the analyses (Fig. 3).\nThese results implicate that there are qualitative differences between self-talk during a sport like badminton which is a technical,\n(sometimes) team-based game and a sport like running which is more solitary, often timed, and relying on gross motor movements. The\nfinding that runners are more prone to disengagement (Fig. 2) underlines the importance of being able to control your inner voice\nwhen y", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "rrespondence was observed.\nThis criticism applies less to our study for two main reasons. First, we do not see reason to believe that external and internal self-talk\nFig. 1. Self-talk frequency and self-talk efficacy in our sample of runners (1) and"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0006_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0d54f3802c6156f4", "text_span_hash": "0d54f3802c6156f4", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "our sample of runners (1) and badminton players (2).\nThe best tune statistics α and λ for each of the three classification models as well as means and standard deviations of accuracy and κ on the fifth\nsubfold. κ is an adjusted measure of accuracy gi", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "3.4. Interim discussion\nWe replicated the findings that self-talk is reported to be widely used in sports with approximately 70–90% of runners and\nbadminton players reporting engaging in self-talk, sometimes, often or very often (Table 1). In addition to this, we documented\nimportant differences in the content of self-talk exhibited by practitioners of two different types of sport. According to our machine\nlearning models, runners’ self-talk was characterized by being more prone to disengagement and defeatism while badminton players’\nself-talk was more characterized by worry (e.g. ‘I’m doing it wrong again’) and anxiety-control (e.g. ‘Don’t get upset’). The results from\nthe model predicting exercise frequency provided evidence that there is a reliable connection between how people talk to themselves\nand their level of fitness. Especially the most negatively predictive item (‘My body is not in a good condition’) and the most positively\npredictive item (‘I’m very well prepared’) provide good coherence checks for the analyses (Fig. 3).\nThese results implicate that there are qualitative differences between self-talk during a sport like badminton which is a technical,\n(sometimes) team-based game and a sport like running which is more solitary, often timed, and relying on gross motor movements. The\nfinding that runners are more prone to disengagement (Fig. 2) underlines the importance of being able to control your inner voice\nwhen y", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "our sample of runners (1) and badminton players (2).\nThe best tune statistics α and λ for each of the three classification models as well as means and standard deviations of accuracy and κ on the fifth\nsubfold. κ is an adjusted measure of accuracy gi"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0007_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7b6e9817d55a1c90", "text_span_hash": "7b6e9817d55a1c90", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "queried participants’ self-talk in high versus low effort situations (in both training and competition)\nand asked about positive content, negative content, other-athletes focused content, and task-irrelevant thoughts. In terms of the form\nof self-tal", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "should be identical – for instance, previous research has shown that internal self-talk is much more frequent than external self-talk\n(Brinthaupt, Boling, & Wilson, 2000). In fact, we expect external self-talk to be mostly System 1 and internal self-talk to be mostly\nSystem 2 (Van Raalte et al., 2016). Second, Thibodeaux and Winsler (2018) reported that they were unable to code 49% of the athletes’\novert self-talk during matches, meaning that the lack of correlation between observed and reported self-talk could be due to meth­\nodological challenges. Nevertheless, in the light of these concerns with the ASTQS and our interest in topics outside its scope, we\ndecided to construct a new questionnaire for Study 2.\nFor Study 2, we wanted to explore how self-talk differed in terms of form and content in high intensity versus low intensity sit­\nuations. Our new questionnaire queried participants’ self-talk in high versus low effort situations (in both training and competition)\nand asked about positive content, negative content, other-athletes focused content, and task-irrelevant thoughts. In terms of the form\nof self-talk, the questionnaire asked about length and variety. We did not find this combination of topics in previously developed\nquestionnaires for self-talk in sport but all appear as subcomponents of separate questionnaires. For example, the Self-Talk Use\nQuestionnaire (Hardy, Hall, & Hardy, 2005) measures linguistic form (si", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "queried participants’ self-talk in high versus low effort situations (in both training and competition)\nand asked about positive content, negative content, other-athletes focused content, and task-irrelevant thoughts. In terms of the form\nof self-tal"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0007_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "fed7fc5047cf7252", "text_span_hash": "fed7fc5047cf7252", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "hts while the ASTQS measures other-athletes focus and task-irrelevant thoughts but not linguistic\nform. We also asked participants to provide their personal best marathon and/or half marathon time. See Appendix A2 for the\nquestionnaire used in Study", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "should be identical – for instance, previous research has shown that internal self-talk is much more frequent than external self-talk\n(Brinthaupt, Boling, & Wilson, 2000). In fact, we expect external self-talk to be mostly System 1 and internal self-talk to be mostly\nSystem 2 (Van Raalte et al., 2016). Second, Thibodeaux and Winsler (2018) reported that they were unable to code 49% of the athletes’\novert self-talk during matches, meaning that the lack of correlation between observed and reported self-talk could be due to meth­\nodological challenges. Nevertheless, in the light of these concerns with the ASTQS and our interest in topics outside its scope, we\ndecided to construct a new questionnaire for Study 2.\nFor Study 2, we wanted to explore how self-talk differed in terms of form and content in high intensity versus low intensity sit­\nuations. Our new questionnaire queried participants’ self-talk in high versus low effort situations (in both training and competition)\nand asked about positive content, negative content, other-athletes focused content, and task-irrelevant thoughts. In terms of the form\nof self-talk, the questionnaire asked about length and variety. We did not find this combination of topics in previously developed\nquestionnaires for self-talk in sport but all appear as subcomponents of separate questionnaires. For example, the Self-Talk Use\nQuestionnaire (Hardy, Hall, & Hardy, 2005) measures linguistic form (si", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "hts while the ASTQS measures other-athletes focus and task-irrelevant thoughts but not linguistic\nform. We also asked participants to provide their personal best marathon and/or half marathon time. See Appendix A2 for the\nquestionnaire used in Study"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0007_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c7d720d94079bc07", "text_span_hash": "c7d720d94079bc07", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "e\nquestionnaire used in Study 2.\nWe recruited participants from Danish Facebook groups dedicated to marathon running. In total, 293 participants completed the\nquestionnaire. Two of them were excluded because their marathon or half marathon time was n", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "should be identical – for instance, previous research has shown that internal self-talk is much more frequent than external self-talk\n(Brinthaupt, Boling, & Wilson, 2000). In fact, we expect external self-talk to be mostly System 1 and internal self-talk to be mostly\nSystem 2 (Van Raalte et al., 2016). Second, Thibodeaux and Winsler (2018) reported that they were unable to code 49% of the athletes’\novert self-talk during matches, meaning that the lack of correlation between observed and reported self-talk could be due to meth­\nodological challenges. Nevertheless, in the light of these concerns with the ASTQS and our interest in topics outside its scope, we\ndecided to construct a new questionnaire for Study 2.\nFor Study 2, we wanted to explore how self-talk differed in terms of form and content in high intensity versus low intensity sit­\nuations. Our new questionnaire queried participants’ self-talk in high versus low effort situations (in both training and competition)\nand asked about positive content, negative content, other-athletes focused content, and task-irrelevant thoughts. In terms of the form\nof self-talk, the questionnaire asked about length and variety. We did not find this combination of topics in previously developed\nquestionnaires for self-talk in sport but all appear as subcomponents of separate questionnaires. For example, the Self-Talk Use\nQuestionnaire (Hardy, Hall, & Hardy, 2005) measures linguistic form (si", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "e\nquestionnaire used in Study 2.\nWe recruited participants from Danish Facebook groups dedicated to marathon running. In total, 293 participants completed the\nquestionnaire. Two of them were excluded because their marathon or half marathon time was n"}
