ts. In terms of the form\nof self-talk, the questionnaire asked about length and variety. We did not find this combination of topics in previously developed\nquestionnaires for self-talk in sport but all appear as subcomponents of separate questionnaires. For example, the Self-Talk Use\nQuestionnaire (Hardy, Hall, & Hardy, 2005) measures linguistic form (si", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "on or half marathon time was not decipherable. The remaining 291\nparticipants were 111 men, 179 women, and one non-binary (12 in the 18–25 age group, 45 in the 26–35 age group, 112 in the 36–45\nFig. 2. An illustration of which individual questions co"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0007_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1438e720b14329dd", "text_span_hash": "1438e720b14329dd", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "it more likely that the individual was classified as\na badminton player while high endorsement of orange items made it more likely that the individual was classified as a runner. (For interpretation of\nthe references to color in this figure legend, t", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "should be identical – for instance, previous research has shown that internal self-talk is much more frequent than external self-talk\n(Brinthaupt, Boling, & Wilson, 2000). In fact, we expect external self-talk to be mostly System 1 and internal self-talk to be mostly\nSystem 2 (Van Raalte et al., 2016). Second, Thibodeaux and Winsler (2018) reported that they were unable to code 49% of the athletes’\novert self-talk during matches, meaning that the lack of correlation between observed and reported self-talk could be due to meth­\nodological challenges. Nevertheless, in the light of these concerns with the ASTQS and our interest in topics outside its scope, we\ndecided to construct a new questionnaire for Study 2.\nFor Study 2, we wanted to explore how self-talk differed in terms of form and content in high intensity versus low intensity sit­\nuations. Our new questionnaire queried participants’ self-talk in high versus low effort situations (in both training and competition)\nand asked about positive content, negative content, other-athletes focused content, and task-irrelevant thoughts. In terms of the form\nof self-talk, the questionnaire asked about length and variety. We did not find this combination of topics in previously developed\nquestionnaires for self-talk in sport but all appear as subcomponents of separate questionnaires. For example, the Self-Talk Use\nQuestionnaire (Hardy, Hall, & Hardy, 2005) measures linguistic form (si", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "it more likely that the individual was classified as\na badminton player while high endorsement of orange items made it more likely that the individual was classified as a runner. (For interpretation of\nthe references to color in this figure legend, t"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0008_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "023a399c470ac125", "text_span_hash": "023a399c470ac125", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ersity. All except 55 participants provided their\nbest marathon time (M = 245 min, SD = 42.7 min) while all except 15 participants provided their best half marathon time (M = 112.7\nWe deployed the questionnaire online via SurveyXact (https://www.surv", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "age group, 102 in the 46–55 age group, and 20 in the 56–66 age group). All except three reported exercising at least a few times a week.\nThe project received ethical approval from the Internal Review Board at Aarhus University. All except 55 participants provided their\nbest marathon time (M = 245 min, SD = 42.7 min) while all except 15 participants provided their best half marathon time (M = 112.7\nWe deployed the questionnaire online via SurveyXact (https://www.surveyxact.dk/). Participants were asked to indicate how\noften (‘Never’, ‘Rarely’, ‘Sometimes’, ‘Often’, ‘Very often’) they use a particular kind of self-talk (positive, negative, task-irrelevant, or\nconcerning other people) under high-pressure and low-pressure conditions. They could also answer ‘I don’t talk to myself’. High-\npressure questions were initiated with the sentence fragment ‘When I push myself while running…’ and Low-pressure questions\nwere initiated with the sentence fragment ‘When I run without pushing myself…’. Participants were also asked what form their inner\nspeech takes in terms of variation and length. Again, they had the option to answer ‘I don’t talk to myself’. In total, there were 20\nquestions about self-talk specifics. A machine learning model was not suitable to predict the difference between high and low intensity\nquestions as the high intensity/low intensity paired structure of the items would be lost. Instead, we tested this difference with", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ersity. All except 55 participants provided their\nbest marathon time (M = 245 min, SD = 42.7 min) while all except 15 participants provided their best half marathon time (M = 112.7\nWe deployed the questionnaire online via SurveyXact (https://www.surv"}
