{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0008_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "262720b354f32e37", "text_span_hash": "262720b354f32e37", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ing models\nOnce again, the questionnaire items were too heavily correlated for use in linear mixed model regression analyses as predictors.\nInstead, we chose to use machine learning models to predict marathon and half marathon proficiency from all th", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "age group, 102 in the 46–55 age group, and 20 in the 56–66 age group). All except three reported exercising at least a few times a week.\nThe project received ethical approval from the Internal Review Board at Aarhus University. All except 55 participants provided their\nbest marathon time (M = 245 min, SD = 42.7 min) while all except 15 participants provided their best half marathon time (M = 112.7\nWe deployed the questionnaire online via SurveyXact (https://www.surveyxact.dk/). Participants were asked to indicate how\noften (‘Never’, ‘Rarely’, ‘Sometimes’, ‘Often’, ‘Very often’) they use a particular kind of self-talk (positive, negative, task-irrelevant, or\nconcerning other people) under high-pressure and low-pressure conditions. They could also answer ‘I don’t talk to myself’. High-\npressure questions were initiated with the sentence fragment ‘When I push myself while running…’ and Low-pressure questions\nwere initiated with the sentence fragment ‘When I run without pushing myself…’. Participants were also asked what form their inner\nspeech takes in terms of variation and length. Again, they had the option to answer ‘I don’t talk to myself’. In total, there were 20\nquestions about self-talk specifics. A machine learning model was not suitable to predict the difference between high and low intensity\nquestions as the high intensity/low intensity paired structure of the items would be lost. Instead, we tested this difference with", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ing models\nOnce again, the questionnaire items were too heavily correlated for use in linear mixed model regression analyses as predictors.\nInstead, we chose to use machine learning models to predict marathon and half marathon proficiency from all th"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0009_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9a71cc0cb4219945", "text_span_hash": "9a71cc0cb4219945", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "participants, 91.75% (267 participants) reported talking to themselves while running ‘Sometimes’, ‘Often’, or ‘Very often’\n(compared to approx. 70% of runners in Study 1). In terms of self-talk efficacy, 63.57% (185 participants) reported that talki", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "5.1. Questionnaire reliability\nThe questionnaire we used in Study 2 only had a Cronbach’s alpha of 0.601 (see Study 1 for how it was calculated), indicating poor\ninternal consistency, i.e. that the items are unlikely to measure only one underlying concept. This is not surprising given that we\ndesigned the questionnaire to measure self-talk under high and low pressure conditions and thus the questionnaire should not be\nunidimensional. There were not enough items to calculate Cronbach’s alpha for high and low pressure separately, so in the following\nwe will conduct analyses with individual questions.\n5.2. Self-talk frequency and self-talk efficacy\nOf the participants, 91.75% (267 participants) reported talking to themselves while running ‘Sometimes’, ‘Often’, or ‘Very often’\n(compared to approx. 70% of runners in Study 1). In terms of self-talk efficacy, 63.57% (185 participants) reported that talking to\nthemselves usually had a positive effect on their performance while 25.77% (75 participants) reported that it sometimes had a positive\neffect and sometimes a negative effect. In Study 1, 43.36% of runners reported a positive effect of self-talk on performance while\n25.87% reported that it sometimes had a positive and sometimes negative effect. Only one participant in the present study reported\nself-talk usually having a negative effect on performance. In this sample, 10.31% (30 participants) reported self-talk having no effect\no", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "participants, 91.75% (267 participants) reported talking to themselves while running ‘Sometimes’, ‘Often’, or ‘Very often’\n(compared to approx. 70% of runners in Study 1). In terms of self-talk efficacy, 63.57% (185 participants) reported that talki"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0009_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "eca45c384a7eb627", "text_span_hash": "eca45c384a7eb627", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ticipants) reported that talking to\nthemselves usually had a positive effect on their performance while 25.77% (75 participants) reported that it sometimes had a positive\neffect and sometimes a negative effect. In Study 1, 43.36% of runners reported", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "5.1. Questionnaire reliability\nThe questionnaire we used in Study 2 only had a Cronbach’s alpha of 0.601 (see Study 1 for how it was calculated), indicating poor\ninternal consistency, i.e. that the items are unlikely to measure only one underlying concept. This is not surprising given that we\ndesigned the questionnaire to measure self-talk under high and low pressure conditions and thus the questionnaire should not be\nunidimensional. There were not enough items to calculate Cronbach’s alpha for high and low pressure separately, so in the following\nwe will conduct analyses with individual questions.\n5.2. Self-talk frequency and self-talk efficacy\nOf the participants, 91.75% (267 participants) reported talking to themselves while running ‘Sometimes’, ‘Often’, or ‘Very often’\n(compared to approx. 70% of runners in Study 1). In terms of self-talk efficacy, 63.57% (185 participants) reported that talking to\nthemselves usually had a positive effect on their performance while 25.77% (75 participants) reported that it sometimes had a positive\neffect and sometimes a negative effect. In Study 1, 43.36% of runners reported a positive effect of self-talk on performance while\n25.87% reported that it sometimes had a positive and sometimes negative effect. Only one participant in the present study reported\nself-talk usually having a negative effect on performance. In this sample, 10.31% (30 participants) reported self-talk having no effect\no", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ticipants) reported that talking to\nthemselves usually had a positive effect on their performance while 25.77% (75 participants) reported that it sometimes had a positive\neffect and sometimes a negative effect. In Study 1, 43.36% of runners reported"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0009_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "196cdb04bec26da5", "text_span_hash": "196cdb04bec26da5", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "1, 43.36% of runners reported a positive effect of self-talk on performance while\n25.87% reported that it sometimes had a positive and sometimes negative effect. Only one participant in the present study reported\nself-talk usually having a negative e", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "5.1. Questionnaire reliability\nThe questionnaire we used in Study 2 only had a Cronbach’s alpha of 0.601 (see Study 1 for how it was calculated), indicating poor\ninternal consistency, i.e. that the items are unlikely to measure only one underlying concept. This is not surprising given that we\ndesigned the questionnaire to measure self-talk under high and low pressure conditions and thus the questionnaire should not be\nunidimensional. There were not enough items to calculate Cronbach’s alpha for high and low pressure separately, so in the following\nwe will conduct analyses with individual questions.\n5.2. Self-talk frequency and self-talk efficacy\nOf the participants, 91.75% (267 participants) reported talking to themselves while running ‘Sometimes’, ‘Often’, or ‘Very often’\n(compared to approx. 70% of runners in Study 1). In terms of self-talk efficacy, 63.57% (185 participants) reported that talking to\nthemselves usually had a positive effect on their performance while 25.77% (75 participants) reported that it sometimes had a positive\neffect and sometimes a negative effect. In Study 1, 43.36% of runners reported a positive effect of self-talk on performance while\n25.87% reported that it sometimes had a positive and sometimes negative effect. Only one participant in the present study reported\nself-talk usually having a negative effect on performance. In this sample, 10.31% (30 participants) reported self-talk having no effect\no", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "1, 43.36% of runners reported a positive effect of self-talk on performance while\n25.87% reported that it sometimes had a positive and sometimes negative effect. Only one participant in the present study reported\nself-talk usually having a negative e"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0009_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "18f3c87e7737f7a3", "text_span_hash": "18f3c87e7737f7a3", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "lk usually having a negative effect on performance. In this sample, 10.31% (30 participants) reported self-talk having no effect\non their performance (compared to approx. 30% of runners in Study 1). See Fig. 4 for a visualization of the self-talk eff", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "5.1. Questionnaire reliability\nThe questionnaire we used in Study 2 only had a Cronbach’s alpha of 0.601 (see Study 1 for how it was calculated), indicating poor\ninternal consistency, i.e. that the items are unlikely to measure only one underlying concept. This is not surprising given that we\ndesigned the questionnaire to measure self-talk under high and low pressure conditions and thus the questionnaire should not be\nunidimensional. There were not enough items to calculate Cronbach’s alpha for high and low pressure separately, so in the following\nwe will conduct analyses with individual questions.\n5.2. Self-talk frequency and self-talk efficacy\nOf the participants, 91.75% (267 participants) reported talking to themselves while running ‘Sometimes’, ‘Often’, or ‘Very often’\n(compared to approx. 70% of runners in Study 1). In terms of self-talk efficacy, 63.57% (185 participants) reported that talking to\nthemselves usually had a positive effect on their performance while 25.77% (75 participants) reported that it sometimes had a positive\neffect and sometimes a negative effect. In Study 1, 43.36% of runners reported a positive effect of self-talk on performance while\n25.87% reported that it sometimes had a positive and sometimes negative effect. Only one participant in the present study reported\nself-talk usually having a negative effect on performance. In this sample, 10.31% (30 participants) reported self-talk having no effect\no", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "lk usually having a negative effect on performance. In this sample, 10.31% (30 participants) reported self-talk having no effect\non their performance (compared to approx. 30% of runners in Study 1). See Fig. 4 for a visualization of the self-talk eff"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0009_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3c40ed9f9df7e673", "text_span_hash": "3c40ed9f9df7e673", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ee Fig. 5 for a visual representation of the difference in answers to questions about high intensity versus low intensity.\nFig. 4. Visualisation of the marathon runners’ answer to self-talk frequency and self-talk efficacy questions.\nJ. Nedergaard et", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "5.1. Questionnaire reliability\nThe questionnaire we used in Study 2 only had a Cronbach’s alpha of 0.601 (see Study 1 for how it was calculated), indicating poor\ninternal consistency, i.e. that the items are unlikely to measure only one underlying concept. This is not surprising given that we\ndesigned the questionnaire to measure self-talk under high and low pressure conditions and thus the questionnaire should not be\nunidimensional. There were not enough items to calculate Cronbach’s alpha for high and low pressure separately, so in the following\nwe will conduct analyses with individual questions.\n5.2. Self-talk frequency and self-talk efficacy\nOf the participants, 91.75% (267 participants) reported talking to themselves while running ‘Sometimes’, ‘Often’, or ‘Very often’\n(compared to approx. 70% of runners in Study 1). In terms of self-talk efficacy, 63.57% (185 participants) reported that talking to\nthemselves usually had a positive effect on their performance while 25.77% (75 participants) reported that it sometimes had a positive\neffect and sometimes a negative effect. In Study 1, 43.36% of runners reported a positive effect of self-talk on performance while\n25.87% reported that it sometimes had a positive and sometimes negative effect. Only one participant in the present study reported\nself-talk usually having a negative effect on performance. In this sample, 10.31% (30 participants) reported self-talk having no effect\no", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ee Fig. 5 for a visual representation of the difference in answers to questions about high intensity versus low intensity.\nFig. 4. Visualisation of the marathon runners’ answer to self-talk frequency and self-talk efficacy questions.\nJ. Nedergaard et"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0010_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "6051452684596a6c", "text_span_hash": "6051452684596a6c", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "5.4. Machine learning models\n5.4.1. Variance explained by demographics and questionnaire items\nMarathon and half marathon proficiency were operationalized by using each participant’s self-reported personal record in minutes.\nGiven that running profic", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "5.4. Machine learning models\n5.4.1. Variance explained by demographics and questionnaire items\nMarathon and half marathon proficiency were operationalized by using each participant’s self-reported personal record in minutes.\nGiven that running proficiency is likely to be influenced not only by self-talk but also by a range of other factors (such as exercise\nfrequency, experience, age, and gender), we included exercise frequency, age, and gender as predictors in the models in addition to the\nquestionnaire items. To estimate the degree to which running proficiency could be predicted by self-talk, we compared the variance\nexplained by models including age, gender, exercise, and self-talk items to models only including age, gender, and exercise. In total, we\nbuilt six ‘glmnet’ predictive models: two for half marathon and marathon predicted by age, gender, and exercise frequency, two for half\nmarathon and marathon predicted by age, gender, exercise frequency, and all self-talk items, and two for half marathon and marathon\npredicted by the questionnaire items only. For all the models, we ran 5-fold cross-validation with 100 repeats. A 5-fold cross-validation\nmeans that the model splits the data into five subfolds, trains on four of these, and tests its predictions on the fifth subfold. We did this\ninstead of splitting the data into a training set and a test set because the random split would be too unstable given the size of our\ncom", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "5.4. Machine learning models\n5.4.1. Variance explained by demographics and questionnaire items\nMarathon and half marathon proficiency were operationalized by using each participant’s self-reported personal record in minutes.\nGiven that running profic"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0010_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b2eb680007ed2f4f", "text_span_hash": "b2eb680007ed2f4f", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "tes.\nGiven that running proficiency is likely to be influenced not only by self-talk but also by a range of other factors (such as exercise\nfrequency, experience, age, and gender), we included exercise frequency, age, and gender as predictors in the", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "5.4. Machine learning models\n5.4.1. Variance explained by demographics and questionnaire items\nMarathon and half marathon proficiency were operationalized by using each participant’s self-reported personal record in minutes.\nGiven that running proficiency is likely to be influenced not only by self-talk but also by a range of other factors (such as exercise\nfrequency, experience, age, and gender), we included exercise frequency, age, and gender as predictors in the models in addition to the\nquestionnaire items. To estimate the degree to which running proficiency could be predicted by self-talk, we compared the variance\nexplained by models including age, gender, exercise, and self-talk items to models only including age, gender, and exercise. In total, we\nbuilt six ‘glmnet’ predictive models: two for half marathon and marathon predicted by age, gender, and exercise frequency, two for half\nmarathon and marathon predicted by age, gender, exercise frequency, and all self-talk items, and two for half marathon and marathon\npredicted by the questionnaire items only. For all the models, we ran 5-fold cross-validation with 100 repeats. A 5-fold cross-validation\nmeans that the model splits the data into five subfolds, trains on four of these, and tests its predictions on the fifth subfold. We did this\ninstead of splitting the data into a training set and a test set because the random split would be too unstable given the size of our\ncom", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "tes.\nGiven that running proficiency is likely to be influenced not only by self-talk but also by a range of other factors (such as exercise\nfrequency, experience, age, and gender), we included exercise frequency, age, and gender as predictors in the"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0010_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f8daeaef63990913", "text_span_hash": "f8daeaef63990913", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "d gender as predictors in the models in addition to the\nquestionnaire items. To estimate the degree to which running proficiency could be predicted by self-talk, we compared the variance\nexplained by models including age, gender, exercise, and self-t", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "5.4. Machine learning models\n5.4.1. Variance explained by demographics and questionnaire items\nMarathon and half marathon proficiency were operationalized by using each participant’s self-reported personal record in minutes.\nGiven that running proficiency is likely to be influenced not only by self-talk but also by a range of other factors (such as exercise\nfrequency, experience, age, and gender), we included exercise frequency, age, and gender as predictors in the models in addition to the\nquestionnaire items. To estimate the degree to which running proficiency could be predicted by self-talk, we compared the variance\nexplained by models including age, gender, exercise, and self-talk items to models only including age, gender, and exercise. In total, we\nbuilt six ‘glmnet’ predictive models: two for half marathon and marathon predicted by age, gender, and exercise frequency, two for half\nmarathon and marathon predicted by age, gender, exercise frequency, and all self-talk items, and two for half marathon and marathon\npredicted by the questionnaire items only. For all the models, we ran 5-fold cross-validation with 100 repeats. A 5-fold cross-validation\nmeans that the model splits the data into five subfolds, trains on four of these, and tests its predictions on the fifth subfold. We did this\ninstead of splitting the data into a training set and a test set because the random split would be too unstable given the size of our\ncom", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "d gender as predictors in the models in addition to the\nquestionnaire items. To estimate the degree to which running proficiency could be predicted by self-talk, we compared the variance\nexplained by models including age, gender, exercise, and self-t"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0010_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4295b572d10c58d7", "text_span_hash": "4295b572d10c58d7", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": ", gender, exercise, and self-talk items to models only including age, gender, and exercise. In total, we\nbuilt six ‘glmnet’ predictive models: two for half marathon and marathon predicted by age, gender, and exercise frequency, two for half\nmarathon", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "5.4. Machine learning models\n5.4.1. Variance explained by demographics and questionnaire items\nMarathon and half marathon proficiency were operationalized by using each participant’s self-reported personal record in minutes.\nGiven that running proficiency is likely to be influenced not only by self-talk but also by a range of other factors (such as exercise\nfrequency, experience, age, and gender), we included exercise frequency, age, and gender as predictors in the models in addition to the\nquestionnaire items. To estimate the degree to which running proficiency could be predicted by self-talk, we compared the variance\nexplained by models including age, gender, exercise, and self-talk items to models only including age, gender, and exercise. In total, we\nbuilt six ‘glmnet’ predictive models: two for half marathon and marathon predicted by age, gender, and exercise frequency, two for half\nmarathon and marathon predicted by age, gender, exercise frequency, and all self-talk items, and two for half marathon and marathon\npredicted by the questionnaire items only. For all the models, we ran 5-fold cross-validation with 100 repeats. A 5-fold cross-validation\nmeans that the model splits the data into five subfolds, trains on four of these, and tests its predictions on the fifth subfold. We did this\ninstead of splitting the data into a training set and a test set because the random split would be too unstable given the size of our\ncom", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": ", gender, exercise, and self-talk items to models only including age, gender, and exercise. In total, we\nbuilt six ‘glmnet’ predictive models: two for half marathon and marathon predicted by age, gender, and exercise frequency, two for half\nmarathon"}
