ls, we ran 5-fold cross-validation with 100 repeats. A 5-fold cross-validation\nmeans that the model splits the data into five subfolds, trains on four of these, and tests its predictions on the fifth subfold. We did this\ninstead of splitting the data into a training set and a test set because the random split would be too unstable given the size of our\ncom", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "cts of age, gender, and exercise frequency\nIn Study 2, we replicated the finding from Study 1 that self-talk is extremely prevalent in sport – especially endurance sports like\nmarathon and half marathon running – and that most athletes experience tha"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0010_c0019", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ba49204b8333cb51", "text_span_hash": "ba49204b8333cb51", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 19, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "t most athletes experience that talking to themselves improves their performance.\nMarathon runners notably said both that they talk to themselves more and believe in the efficacy of talking to themselves more than a\nmore mixed group of runners do (co", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "5.4. Machine learning models\n5.4.1. Variance explained by demographics and questionnaire items\nMarathon and half marathon proficiency were operationalized by using each participant’s self-reported personal record in minutes.\nGiven that running proficiency is likely to be influenced not only by self-talk but also by a range of other factors (such as exercise\nfrequency, experience, age, and gender), we included exercise frequency, age, and gender as predictors in the models in addition to the\nquestionnaire items. To estimate the degree to which running proficiency could be predicted by self-talk, we compared the variance\nexplained by models including age, gender, exercise, and self-talk items to models only including age, gender, and exercise. In total, we\nbuilt six ‘glmnet’ predictive models: two for half marathon and marathon predicted by age, gender, and exercise frequency, two for half\nmarathon and marathon predicted by age, gender, exercise frequency, and all self-talk items, and two for half marathon and marathon\npredicted by the questionnaire items only. For all the models, we ran 5-fold cross-validation with 100 repeats. A 5-fold cross-validation\nmeans that the model splits the data into five subfolds, trains on four of these, and tests its predictions on the fifth subfold. We did this\ninstead of splitting the data into a training set and a test set because the random split would be too unstable given the size of our\ncom", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "t most athletes experience that talking to themselves improves their performance.\nMarathon runners notably said both that they talk to themselves more and believe in the efficacy of talking to themselves more than a\nmore mixed group of runners do (co"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0010_c0020", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "34a7463646bddff9", "text_span_hash": "34a7463646bddff9", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 20, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "mixed group of runners do (compare self-talk frequency in Study 1 and 2). Further, we found that self-talk in our sample differed\nbetween high intensity and low intensity situations with self-talk in high pressure situations being characterized as m", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "5.4. Machine learning models\n5.4.1. Variance explained by demographics and questionnaire items\nMarathon and half marathon proficiency were operationalized by using each participant’s self-reported personal record in minutes.\nGiven that running proficiency is likely to be influenced not only by self-talk but also by a range of other factors (such as exercise\nfrequency, experience, age, and gender), we included exercise frequency, age, and gender as predictors in the models in addition to the\nquestionnaire items. To estimate the degree to which running proficiency could be predicted by self-talk, we compared the variance\nexplained by models including age, gender, exercise, and self-talk items to models only including age, gender, and exercise. In total, we\nbuilt six ‘glmnet’ predictive models: two for half marathon and marathon predicted by age, gender, and exercise frequency, two for half\nmarathon and marathon predicted by age, gender, exercise frequency, and all self-talk items, and two for half marathon and marathon\npredicted by the questionnaire items only. For all the models, we ran 5-fold cross-validation with 100 repeats. A 5-fold cross-validation\nmeans that the model splits the data into five subfolds, trains on four of these, and tests its predictions on the fifth subfold. We did this\ninstead of splitting the data into a training set and a test set because the random split would be too unstable given the size of our\ncom", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "mixed group of runners do (compare self-talk frequency in Study 1 and 2). Further, we found that self-talk in our sample differed\nbetween high intensity and low intensity situations with self-talk in high pressure situations being characterized as m"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0010_c0021", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "fb4a3b67d0421e10", "text_span_hash": "fb4a3b67d0421e10", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 21, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "tions being characterized as more strongly\nvalenced (both in the positive and negative direction), shorter, and with more repetitions than self-talk in low pressure situations. Our\nmachine learning models were able to predict a large amount of varian", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "5.4. Machine learning models\n5.4.1. Variance explained by demographics and questionnaire items\nMarathon and half marathon proficiency were operationalized by using each participant’s self-reported personal record in minutes.\nGiven that running proficiency is likely to be influenced not only by self-talk but also by a range of other factors (such as exercise\nfrequency, experience, age, and gender), we included exercise frequency, age, and gender as predictors in the models in addition to the\nquestionnaire items. To estimate the degree to which running proficiency could be predicted by self-talk, we compared the variance\nexplained by models including age, gender, exercise, and self-talk items to models only including age, gender, and exercise. In total, we\nbuilt six ‘glmnet’ predictive models: two for half marathon and marathon predicted by age, gender, and exercise frequency, two for half\nmarathon and marathon predicted by age, gender, exercise frequency, and all self-talk items, and two for half marathon and marathon\npredicted by the questionnaire items only. For all the models, we ran 5-fold cross-validation with 100 repeats. A 5-fold cross-validation\nmeans that the model splits the data into five subfolds, trains on four of these, and tests its predictions on the fifth subfold. We did this\ninstead of splitting the data into a training set and a test set because the random split would be too unstable given the size of our\ncom", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "tions being characterized as more strongly\nvalenced (both in the positive and negative direction), shorter, and with more repetitions than self-talk in low pressure situations. Our\nmachine learning models were able to predict a large amount of varian"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0010_c0022", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "cacfe9d249300e1c", "text_span_hash": "cacfe9d249300e1c", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 22, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "edict a large amount of variance in half marathon and marathon performance when we\nWilcoxon paired samples tests of the difference between answers to questions about high pressure and low pressure running. Asterisks indicate\nstatistical significance", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "5.4. Machine learning models\n5.4.1. Variance explained by demographics and questionnaire items\nMarathon and half marathon proficiency were operationalized by using each participant’s self-reported personal record in minutes.\nGiven that running proficiency is likely to be influenced not only by self-talk but also by a range of other factors (such as exercise\nfrequency, experience, age, and gender), we included exercise frequency, age, and gender as predictors in the models in addition to the\nquestionnaire items. To estimate the degree to which running proficiency could be predicted by self-talk, we compared the variance\nexplained by models including age, gender, exercise, and self-talk items to models only including age, gender, and exercise. In total, we\nbuilt six ‘glmnet’ predictive models: two for half marathon and marathon predicted by age, gender, and exercise frequency, two for half\nmarathon and marathon predicted by age, gender, exercise frequency, and all self-talk items, and two for half marathon and marathon\npredicted by the questionnaire items only. For all the models, we ran 5-fold cross-validation with 100 repeats. A 5-fold cross-validation\nmeans that the model splits the data into five subfolds, trains on four of these, and tests its predictions on the fifth subfold. We did this\ninstead of splitting the data into a training set and a test set because the random split would be too unstable given the size of our\ncom", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "edict a large amount of variance in half marathon and marathon performance when we\nWilcoxon paired samples tests of the difference between answers to questions about high pressure and low pressure running. Asterisks indicate\nstatistical significance"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0012_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "67e6217d67830455", "text_span_hash": "67e6217d67830455", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "hat especially items concerning positive,\nshort, and repetitive self-talk under low pressure were associated with worse running performance for both marathon and half\nOur results from both Study 1 and Study 2 corroborated results from the existing li", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "included both the questionnaire items and age, gender, and exercise frequency. When we examined the individual questionnaire items\nmore carefully while excluding effects of age, gender, and exercise frequency, we found that especially items concerning positive,\nshort, and repetitive self-talk under low pressure were associated with worse running performance for both marathon and half\nOur results from both Study 1 and Study 2 corroborated results from the existing literature suggesting that athletes talk to\nthemselves while exercising and that they generally believe it helps them perform better. Using machine learning models, we also\nfound some indication that you can predict both people’s skill-level and type of sport from the content of their self-talk. This provides\nsupport for the idea that self-talk actually matters and is not just an epiphenomenon of mental life. Our results from Study 2 indicate\nthat self-talk differs depending on high pressure and low pressure conditions, again confirming existing findings from both sports\npsychology and cognitive psychology more broadly that the inner voice is invoked in a qualitatively different manner when we face\nchallenges (Hatzigeorgiadis et al., 2011; Hardy, Oliver, & Tod, 2009; Kray et al., 2008; Emerson & Miyake, 2003). The finding that\nmarathon runners reported talking to themselves in short sentences with much repetition under high pressure also suggests that the\ncognitive re", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "hat especially items concerning positive,\nshort, and repetitive self-talk under low pressure were associated with worse running performance for both marathon and half\nOur results from both Study 1 and Study 2 corroborated results from the existing li"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0012_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "056cbf993e68124e", "text_span_hash": "056cbf993e68124e", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "the inner voice is invoked in a qualitatively different manner when we face\nchallenges (Hatzigeorgiadis et al., 2011; Hardy, Oliver, & Tod, 2009; Kray et al., 2008; Emerson & Miyake, 2003). The finding that\nmarathon runners reported talking to themse", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "included both the questionnaire items and age, gender, and exercise frequency. When we examined the individual questionnaire items\nmore carefully while excluding effects of age, gender, and exercise frequency, we found that especially items concerning positive,\nshort, and repetitive self-talk under low pressure were associated with worse running performance for both marathon and half\nOur results from both Study 1 and Study 2 corroborated results from the existing literature suggesting that athletes talk to\nthemselves while exercising and that they generally believe it helps them perform better. Using machine learning models, we also\nfound some indication that you can predict both people’s skill-level and type of sport from the content of their self-talk. This provides\nsupport for the idea that self-talk actually matters and is not just an epiphenomenon of mental life. Our results from Study 2 indicate\nthat self-talk differs depending on high pressure and low pressure conditions, again confirming existing findings from both sports\npsychology and cognitive psychology more broadly that the inner voice is invoked in a qualitatively different manner when we face\nchallenges (Hatzigeorgiadis et al., 2011; Hardy, Oliver, & Tod, 2009; Kray et al., 2008; Emerson & Miyake, 2003). The finding that\nmarathon runners reported talking to themselves in short sentences with much repetition under high pressure also suggests that the\ncognitive re", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "the inner voice is invoked in a qualitatively different manner when we face\nchallenges (Hatzigeorgiadis et al., 2011; Hardy, Oliver, & Tod, 2009; Kray et al., 2008; Emerson & Miyake, 2003). The finding that\nmarathon runners reported talking to themse"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0012_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "895f2d963637f4e8", "text_span_hash": "895f2d963637f4e8", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ers reported talking to themselves in short sentences with much repetition under high pressure also suggests that the\ncognitive resources available for self-regulation under such conditions may be limited compared to less intense situations.\n6.1. Sel", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "included both the questionnaire items and age, gender, and exercise frequency. When we examined the individual questionnaire items\nmore carefully while excluding effects of age, gender, and exercise frequency, we found that especially items concerning positive,\nshort, and repetitive self-talk under low pressure were associated with worse running performance for both marathon and half\nOur results from both Study 1 and Study 2 corroborated results from the existing literature suggesting that athletes talk to\nthemselves while exercising and that they generally believe it helps them perform better. Using machine learning models, we also\nfound some indication that you can predict both people’s skill-level and type of sport from the content of their self-talk. This provides\nsupport for the idea that self-talk actually matters and is not just an epiphenomenon of mental life. Our results from Study 2 indicate\nthat self-talk differs depending on high pressure and low pressure conditions, again confirming existing findings from both sports\npsychology and cognitive psychology more broadly that the inner voice is invoked in a qualitatively different manner when we face\nchallenges (Hatzigeorgiadis et al., 2011; Hardy, Oliver, & Tod, 2009; Kray et al., 2008; Emerson & Miyake, 2003). The finding that\nmarathon runners reported talking to themselves in short sentences with much repetition under high pressure also suggests that the\ncognitive re", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ers reported talking to themselves in short sentences with much repetition under high pressure also suggests that the\ncognitive resources available for self-regulation under such conditions may be limited compared to less intense situations.\n6.1. Sel"}
