{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0013_c0030", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "47b40f1d0cd76dac", "text_span_hash": "47b40f1d0cd76dac", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 30, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "tent, frequency, and function of self-talk in sports. We were able to use\nanswers to a self-talk use questionnaire to predict specific sport (running versus badminton), pressure level (high versus low), and\nmarathon running proficiency. Our findings", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "situations. As in Van Raalte et al. (2015)’s study on marathon runners, we found significant differences between the self-talk of\ncompetent and of less competent marathon runners. The only type of self-talk that differed significantly (between elite and non-elite) in\nVan Raalte et al.’s study was so-called ‘associative’ self-talk with elite marathon runners engaging more in this type. Associative self-\ntalk relates to sensations of the body, pace, stride length, and posture – what one would perhaps in other sports term ‘technique’ or\n‘instructional self-talk’. The most similar item in our questionnaire (although in the opposite direction) was ‘I talk to myself about\nthings other than the race’. The low intensity answers to this item correlated negatively with half marathon time, indicating that\nparticipants who engage in dissociative self-talk while not pushing themselves have better half marathon personal records. For both\nmarathons and half marathons, participants who endorsed short, repetitive, and encouraging self-talk when not pushing themselves\nhad slower running times. We interpret this as indicating that they needed to use their self-talk strategically even during less arduous\nperiods of a marathon or half marathon, and more generally that self-talk is deployed when people face novel or challenging situations.\nThis is consistent with previous findings from both sport psychology (Hatzigeorgiadis et al., 2011; Hardy et a", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "tent, frequency, and function of self-talk in sports. We were able to use\nanswers to a self-talk use questionnaire to predict specific sport (running versus badminton), pressure level (high versus low), and\nmarathon running proficiency. Our findings"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0013_c0031", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e015ea99ae3a7ac2", "text_span_hash": "e015ea99ae3a7ac2", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 31, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ing proficiency. Our findings from Study 1 suggest that self-talk in an individual, gross motor sport like running can be\ncharacterized by more disengagement compared to a team-based, more technically demanding sport like badminton which in turn is\nJ", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "situations. As in Van Raalte et al. (2015)’s study on marathon runners, we found significant differences between the self-talk of\ncompetent and of less competent marathon runners. The only type of self-talk that differed significantly (between elite and non-elite) in\nVan Raalte et al.’s study was so-called ‘associative’ self-talk with elite marathon runners engaging more in this type. Associative self-\ntalk relates to sensations of the body, pace, stride length, and posture – what one would perhaps in other sports term ‘technique’ or\n‘instructional self-talk’. The most similar item in our questionnaire (although in the opposite direction) was ‘I talk to myself about\nthings other than the race’. The low intensity answers to this item correlated negatively with half marathon time, indicating that\nparticipants who engage in dissociative self-talk while not pushing themselves have better half marathon personal records. For both\nmarathons and half marathons, participants who endorsed short, repetitive, and encouraging self-talk when not pushing themselves\nhad slower running times. We interpret this as indicating that they needed to use their self-talk strategically even during less arduous\nperiods of a marathon or half marathon, and more generally that self-talk is deployed when people face novel or challenging situations.\nThis is consistent with previous findings from both sport psychology (Hatzigeorgiadis et al., 2011; Hardy et a", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ing proficiency. Our findings from Study 1 suggest that self-talk in an individual, gross motor sport like running can be\ncharacterized by more disengagement compared to a team-based, more technically demanding sport like badminton which in turn is\nJ"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0014_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7ff13c27d43eb4ff", "text_span_hash": "7ff13c27d43eb4ff", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 14, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "tive compared to low pressure situations.\nFurther, we found that participants engaging in condensed, encouraging, and repetitive self-talk in low intensity conditions had slower\nmarathon and half marathon times. Taken together, these results indicate", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "characterized by worry and anxiety-control. Our findings from Study 2 suggest that self-talk in high pressure situations is characterized\nby more condensed form and content, and simultaneously more positive and more negative compared to low pressure situations.\nFurther, we found that participants engaging in condensed, encouraging, and repetitive self-talk in low intensity conditions had slower\nmarathon and half marathon times. Taken together, these results indicate 1) that self-talk does serve specific, adaptive functions, and\n2) that self-talk is a cognitive tool/resource that can be strategically deployed when needed. To further investigate this, future studies\nshould explore whether tailoring self-talk to the demands of the circumstances makes a difference in terms of its efficacy, as well as test\nthe role of self-talk sport with experiments where the use of self-talk is directly interfered with during the sport or exercise situation, for\nexample via a verbal interference paradigm.\nThis work was supported by the Department of Linguistics, Cognitive Science and Semiotics at Aarhus University.\nDeclaration of Competing Interest\nThe authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to\ninfluence the work reported in this paper.\nThe authors would like to thank Nikos Comoutos (former Zourbanos) for access to the Automatic Self-Talk Questionnaire for Sports\nand", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "tive compared to low pressure situations.\nFurther, we found that participants engaging in condensed, encouraging, and repetitive self-talk in low intensity conditions had slower\nmarathon and half marathon times. Taken together, these results indicate"}
{"chunk_id": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn_p0015_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_cf4af8295fa2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "19cef8bfe20b9133", "text_span_hash": "19cef8bfe20b9133", "source_file": "Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Nedergaard_2021_SelfTalk_Predicts_Marathon_ConsciousCogn.pdf", "page": 15, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "“I believe in my abilities”\n“I’m not going to reach my goal”\n“My body doesn’t help me today”\nWhen I push myself while running… - … I say encouraging things (like ’come on!’).\nWhen I push myself while running… - … I talk down to myself.\nWhen I push my", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "“I believe in my abilities”\n“I’m not going to reach my goal”\n“My body doesn’t help me today”\nWhen I push myself while running… - … I say encouraging things (like ’come on!’).\nWhen I push myself while running… - … I talk down to myself.\nWhen I push myself while running… - … I wonder what others are thinking about me.\nWhen I push myself while running… - … I talk to myself about all sorts of other things than running.\nWhen I push myself while running… - … I don’t talk to myself.\nWhen I run without pushing myself… - … I say encouraging things (like ’come on!’).\nWhen I run without pushing myself… - … I talk down to myself.\nWhen I run without pushing myself… - … I wonder what others are thinking about me.\nWhen I run without pushing myself… - … I talk to myself about all sorts of other things than running.\nWhen I run without pushing myself… - … I don’t talk to myself.\nWhen I push myself while running… - … I talk in long sentences.\nWhen I push myself while running… - … I talk in short sentences.\nWhen I push myself while running… - … I repeated the same words again and again.\nWhen I push myself while running… - … I talk about a variety of things.\nWhen I push myself while running… - … I don’t talk to myself.\nWhen I run without pushing myself… - … I talk in long sentences.\nWhen I run without pushing myself… - … I talk in short sentences.\nWhen I run without pushing myself… - … I repeat the same words again and again.\nWhen I run without pu", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "“I believe in my abilities”\n“I’m not going to reach my goal”\n“My body doesn’t help me today”\nWhen I push myself while running… - … I say encouraging things (like ’come on!’).\nWhen I push myself while running… - … I talk down to myself.\nWhen I push my"}
