{"chunk_id": "nutrition_acsm_position_2016_p0045_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_173c5a6a63ed", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "d08f4fe419af9bfa", "text_span_hash": "d08f4fe419af9bfa", "source_file": "nutrition_acsm_position_2016.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_acsm_position_2016.pdf", "page": 45, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Hopkins WG, Gore CJ. Effects of acute\nalkalosis and acidosis on performance: a\nmeta-analysis. Sports Med. 2011;41(10):", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "NUTRITION AND ATHLETIC PERFORMANCE:\nPOSITION PAPER FEBRUARY 2016\nDIETITIANS OF CANADA I PAGE 44\n127. Jeukendrup AE. Carbohydrate and exercise\nperformance: the role of multiple transportable\ncarbohydrates. Curr Opin Clin Nutr Metab\n128. Betts JA, Williams C. Short-term recovery from\nprolonged exercise: exploring the potential for\nprotein ingestion to accentuate the benefits of\n129. Berardi JM, Noreen EE, Lemon PW. Recovery\nfrom a cycling time trial is enhanced with\ncarbohydrate-protein\nisoenergetic carbohydrate supplementation. J\nInt Soc Sports Nutr. 2008;5:24.\n130. Tipton KD, Rasmussen BB, Miller SL, et al.\nTiming of amino acid-carbohydrate ingestion\nalters anabolic response of muscle to\nresistance exercise. Am J Physiol. Endocrinol\n131. van Essen M, Gibala MJ. Failure of protein to\nimprove time trial performance when added to\na sports drink. Med Sci Sports Exerc..\n132. Ivy JL, Res PT, Sprague RC, Widzer MO. Effect of\ncarbohydrate-protein\nendurance performance during exercise of\n133. Etheridge T, Philp A, Watt PW. A single protein\nmeal increases recovery of muscle function\nfollowing an acute eccentric exercise bout.\nAppl Physiol Nutr Metab. 2008;33(3):483-\n134. Hoffman JR, Ratamess NA, Tranchina CP, Rashti\nSL, Kang J, Faigenbaum AD. Effect of a\nproprietary protein supplement on recovery\n135. Beelen M, Koopman R, Gijsen AP, et al. Protein\nsynthesis during resistance-type exercise. Am J\nPhysiol. Endocrinol Metab. 2008;295(1):E70", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Hopkins WG, Gore CJ. Effects of acute\nalkalosis and acidosis on performance: a\nmeta-analysis. Sports Med. 2011;41(10):"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_distance_runners_2019_p0001_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_a1afeb75c13f", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b17887145f09cea5", "text_span_hash": "b17887145f09cea5", "source_file": "nutrition_distance_runners_2019.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_distance_runners_2019.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "in Athletics include cross country, 10,000-m track race, half-marathon and marathon road races, and 20- and\n50-km race walking events over different terrain and environmental conditions. Race times for elite performers span ∼26 min to\n>4 hr, with ke", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Contemporary Nutrition Strategies to Optimize Performance\nin Distance Runners and Race Walkers\nAustralian Institute of Sport and Australian Catholic University\nLoughborough University\nUniversity of Exeter\nDistance events in Athletics include cross country, 10,000-m track race, half-marathon and marathon road races, and 20- and\n50-km race walking events over different terrain and environmental conditions. Race times for elite performers span ∼26 min to\n>4 hr, with key factors for success being a high aerobic power, the ability to exercise at a large fraction of this power, and high\nrunning/walking economy. Nutrition-related contributors include body mass and anthropometry, capacity to use fuels, particularly\ncarbohydrate (CHO) to produce adenosine triphosphate economically over the duration of the event, and maintenance of\nreasonable hydration status in the face of sweat losses induced by exercise intensity and the environment. Race nutrition strategies\ninclude CHO-rich eating in the hours per days prior to the event to store glycogen in amounts sufﬁcient for event fuel needs, and in\nsome cases, in-race consumption of CHO and ﬂuid to offset event losses. Beneﬁcial CHO intakes range from small amounts,\nincluding mouth rinsing, in the case of shorter events to high rates of intake (75–90 g/hr) in the longest races. A personalized and\npracticed race nutrition plan should balance the beneﬁts of ﬂuid and CHO consumed within practica", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "in Athletics include cross country, 10,000-m track race, half-marathon and marathon road races, and 20- and\n50-km race walking events over different terrain and environmental conditions. Race times for elite performers span ∼26 min to\n>4 hr, with ke"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_distance_runners_2019_p0001_c0017", "source_registry_id": "src_a1afeb75c13f", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "25135c0bf989335b", "text_span_hash": "25135c0bf989335b", "source_file": "nutrition_distance_runners_2019.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_distance_runners_2019.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 17, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "3.5 hr in the 50-km race walk) are considered “submaximal,” with\nmean energy requirements of ∼75–92% of maximal oxygen uptake\n( ˙VO2 max; Londeree, 1986). These are heavily dependent on\naerobic resynthesis of adenosine triphosphate (Coyle, 2007) and", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Contemporary Nutrition Strategies to Optimize Performance\nin Distance Runners and Race Walkers\nAustralian Institute of Sport and Australian Catholic University\nLoughborough University\nUniversity of Exeter\nDistance events in Athletics include cross country, 10,000-m track race, half-marathon and marathon road races, and 20- and\n50-km race walking events over different terrain and environmental conditions. Race times for elite performers span ∼26 min to\n>4 hr, with key factors for success being a high aerobic power, the ability to exercise at a large fraction of this power, and high\nrunning/walking economy. Nutrition-related contributors include body mass and anthropometry, capacity to use fuels, particularly\ncarbohydrate (CHO) to produce adenosine triphosphate economically over the duration of the event, and maintenance of\nreasonable hydration status in the face of sweat losses induced by exercise intensity and the environment. Race nutrition strategies\ninclude CHO-rich eating in the hours per days prior to the event to store glycogen in amounts sufﬁcient for event fuel needs, and in\nsome cases, in-race consumption of CHO and ﬂuid to offset event losses. Beneﬁcial CHO intakes range from small amounts,\nincluding mouth rinsing, in the case of shorter events to high rates of intake (75–90 g/hr) in the longest races. A personalized and\npracticed race nutrition plan should balance the beneﬁts of ﬂuid and CHO consumed within practica", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "3.5 hr in the 50-km race walk) are considered “submaximal,” with\nmean energy requirements of ∼75–92% of maximal oxygen uptake\n( ˙VO2 max; Londeree, 1986). These are heavily dependent on\naerobic resynthesis of adenosine triphosphate (Coyle, 2007) and"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_distance_runners_2019_p0001_c0019", "source_registry_id": "src_a1afeb75c13f", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8fb941feed1d5a5c", "text_span_hash": "8fb941feed1d5a5c", "source_file": "nutrition_distance_runners_2019.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_distance_runners_2019.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 19, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ormance is typically related to three key factors (Joyner &\nCoyle, 2008): ˙VO2max, the fraction of ˙VO2max that can be\nFor example, a ˙VO2max of 70 ml·kg−1·min−1 sustains at 90%\nfor 10,000 m (i.e., 63 ml·kg−1·min−1) and a running economy of\n190 ml·kg", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Contemporary Nutrition Strategies to Optimize Performance\nin Distance Runners and Race Walkers\nAustralian Institute of Sport and Australian Catholic University\nLoughborough University\nUniversity of Exeter\nDistance events in Athletics include cross country, 10,000-m track race, half-marathon and marathon road races, and 20- and\n50-km race walking events over different terrain and environmental conditions. Race times for elite performers span ∼26 min to\n>4 hr, with key factors for success being a high aerobic power, the ability to exercise at a large fraction of this power, and high\nrunning/walking economy. Nutrition-related contributors include body mass and anthropometry, capacity to use fuels, particularly\ncarbohydrate (CHO) to produce adenosine triphosphate economically over the duration of the event, and maintenance of\nreasonable hydration status in the face of sweat losses induced by exercise intensity and the environment. Race nutrition strategies\ninclude CHO-rich eating in the hours per days prior to the event to store glycogen in amounts sufﬁcient for event fuel needs, and in\nsome cases, in-race consumption of CHO and ﬂuid to offset event losses. Beneﬁcial CHO intakes range from small amounts,\nincluding mouth rinsing, in the case of shorter events to high rates of intake (75–90 g/hr) in the longest races. A personalized and\npracticed race nutrition plan should balance the beneﬁts of ﬂuid and CHO consumed within practica", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ormance is typically related to three key factors (Joyner &\nCoyle, 2008): ˙VO2max, the fraction of ˙VO2max that can be\nFor example, a ˙VO2max of 70 ml·kg−1·min−1 sustains at 90%\nfor 10,000 m (i.e., 63 ml·kg−1·min−1) and a running economy of\n190 ml·kg"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_distance_runners_2019_p0001_c0020", "source_registry_id": "src_a1afeb75c13f", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2756bf4b49edeef2", "text_span_hash": "2756bf4b49edeef2", "source_file": "nutrition_distance_runners_2019.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_distance_runners_2019.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 20, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "a running economy of\n190 ml·kg−1·km−1 translates to a sustainable speed of 19.9 km/hr\nA high ˙VO2max sets the ceiling on success in “submaximal”\ndistance events (Saltin & Astrand, 1967). Elite female distance\nathletes typically possess values of 65–8", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Contemporary Nutrition Strategies to Optimize Performance\nin Distance Runners and Race Walkers\nAustralian Institute of Sport and Australian Catholic University\nLoughborough University\nUniversity of Exeter\nDistance events in Athletics include cross country, 10,000-m track race, half-marathon and marathon road races, and 20- and\n50-km race walking events over different terrain and environmental conditions. Race times for elite performers span ∼26 min to\n>4 hr, with key factors for success being a high aerobic power, the ability to exercise at a large fraction of this power, and high\nrunning/walking economy. Nutrition-related contributors include body mass and anthropometry, capacity to use fuels, particularly\ncarbohydrate (CHO) to produce adenosine triphosphate economically over the duration of the event, and maintenance of\nreasonable hydration status in the face of sweat losses induced by exercise intensity and the environment. Race nutrition strategies\ninclude CHO-rich eating in the hours per days prior to the event to store glycogen in amounts sufﬁcient for event fuel needs, and in\nsome cases, in-race consumption of CHO and ﬂuid to offset event losses. Beneﬁcial CHO intakes range from small amounts,\nincluding mouth rinsing, in the case of shorter events to high rates of intake (75–90 g/hr) in the longest races. A personalized and\npracticed race nutrition plan should balance the beneﬁts of ﬂuid and CHO consumed within practica", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "a running economy of\n190 ml·kg−1·km−1 translates to a sustainable speed of 19.9 km/hr\nA high ˙VO2max sets the ceiling on success in “submaximal”\ndistance events (Saltin & Astrand, 1967). Elite female distance\nathletes typically possess values of 65–8"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_distance_runners_2019_p0002_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_a1afeb75c13f", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2b12b78d60e9fb81", "text_span_hash": "2b12b78d60e9fb81", "source_file": "nutrition_distance_runners_2019.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_distance_runners_2019.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ss. The fraction of ˙VO2max that can be\nsustained for a given exercise duration, which is rarely >90%\n˙VO2max in distance events, is related to muscle oxidative capacity\n(Gollnick & Saltin, 1982; Ivy et al., 1980) and, in turn, metabolic\nthresholds,", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "elite male athletes possess higher values (70–85 ml·kg−1·min−1;\nJoyner & Coyle, 2008), due to factors including higher hemoglobin\nconcentrations, which increase O2 delivery at the maximal cardiac\noutput, and lower fat mass. The fraction of ˙VO2max that can be\nsustained for a given exercise duration, which is rarely >90%\n˙VO2max in distance events, is related to muscle oxidative capacity\n(Gollnick & Saltin, 1982; Ivy et al., 1980) and, in turn, metabolic\nthresholds, such as lactate threshold (LT; the speed corresponding\nto the ﬁrst increase in blood lactate above resting levels; and critical\nspeed [CS]) and the asymptote of the hyperbolic relationship\nbetween speed and time-to-exhaustion (Hughson et al., 1984;\nPoole et al., 1988). Exercise above LT incurs a nonlinear increase\nin metabolic, respiratory, and perceptual stress and a more rapid\nfatigue development due to the effects of metabolic acidosis on\ncontractile function or an accelerated depletion of muscle glycogen\n(Sahlin, 1992). A rightward shift in the blood [lactate]–speed\nrelationship with training is a clear marker of enhanced endurance\ncapacity (Hurley et al., 1984). However, LT typically occurs\nbetween 60% and 80% ˙VO2max even in highly trained individuals;\na lower intensity than this is maintained during most distance races,\nexcept perhaps the 50-km walk. CS, representing the highest speed\nat which ˙VO2max (and blood [lactate]) can be stabilized over time,\nmay be ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ss. The fraction of ˙VO2max that can be\nsustained for a given exercise duration, which is rarely >90%\n˙VO2max in distance events, is related to muscle oxidative capacity\n(Gollnick & Saltin, 1982; Ivy et al., 1980) and, in turn, metabolic\nthresholds,"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_distance_runners_2019_p0002_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_a1afeb75c13f", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "429fe89e7ec75b94", "text_span_hash": "429fe89e7ec75b94", "source_file": "nutrition_distance_runners_2019.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_distance_runners_2019.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "n turn, metabolic\nthresholds, such as lactate threshold (LT; the speed corresponding\nto the ﬁrst increase in blood lactate above resting levels; and critical\nspeed [CS]) and the asymptote of the hyperbolic relationship\nbetween speed and time-to-exhau", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "elite male athletes possess higher values (70–85 ml·kg−1·min−1;\nJoyner & Coyle, 2008), due to factors including higher hemoglobin\nconcentrations, which increase O2 delivery at the maximal cardiac\noutput, and lower fat mass. The fraction of ˙VO2max that can be\nsustained for a given exercise duration, which is rarely >90%\n˙VO2max in distance events, is related to muscle oxidative capacity\n(Gollnick & Saltin, 1982; Ivy et al., 1980) and, in turn, metabolic\nthresholds, such as lactate threshold (LT; the speed corresponding\nto the ﬁrst increase in blood lactate above resting levels; and critical\nspeed [CS]) and the asymptote of the hyperbolic relationship\nbetween speed and time-to-exhaustion (Hughson et al., 1984;\nPoole et al., 1988). Exercise above LT incurs a nonlinear increase\nin metabolic, respiratory, and perceptual stress and a more rapid\nfatigue development due to the effects of metabolic acidosis on\ncontractile function or an accelerated depletion of muscle glycogen\n(Sahlin, 1992). A rightward shift in the blood [lactate]–speed\nrelationship with training is a clear marker of enhanced endurance\ncapacity (Hurley et al., 1984). However, LT typically occurs\nbetween 60% and 80% ˙VO2max even in highly trained individuals;\na lower intensity than this is maintained during most distance races,\nexcept perhaps the 50-km walk. CS, representing the highest speed\nat which ˙VO2max (and blood [lactate]) can be stabilized over time,\nmay be ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "n turn, metabolic\nthresholds, such as lactate threshold (LT; the speed corresponding\nto the ﬁrst increase in blood lactate above resting levels; and critical\nspeed [CS]) and the asymptote of the hyperbolic relationship\nbetween speed and time-to-exhau"}
