ortantly, one must consider that some vitamins\nmay help athletes tolerate training to a greater degree by\nreducing oxidative damage (Vitamin E, C) and/or help to\nmaintain a healthy immune system during heavy training\n(Vitamin C). Alternatively, conflicting evidence has accu-\nmulat", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "or help to\nmaintain a healthy immune system during heavy training\n(Vitamin C). Alternatively, conflicting evidence has accu-\nmulated that ingesting high doses of Vitamins C and E\nmay negatively impact intracellular adaptations seen in re-\nsponse to e"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_issn_review_2018_p0015_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_945e7b3e32e9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7b0acb9ce4f3fedf", "text_span_hash": "7b0acb9ce4f3fedf", "source_file": "nutrition_issn_review_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_issn_review_2018.pdf", "page": 15, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ations seen in re-\nsponse to exercise training [141–144], which may conse-\nFurthermore, while optimal levels of vitamin D have been\nlinked to improved muscle health [145] and strength [146]\nin general populations, research studies conducted in ath-\nl", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "vitamins consist of the entire complex of B-vitamins and\nvitamin C. Since these vitamins are water-soluble, excessive\nintake of these vitamins are eliminated in urine, with few\nexceptions (e.g. vitamin B6, which can cause peripheral\nnerve damage when consumed in excessive amounts).\nTable 1 describes the RDA, proposed ergogenic benefit,\nand summary of research findings for fat and water-soluble\nvitamins. Research has demonstrated that specific vitamins\npossess various health benefits (e.g., Vitamin E, niacin, folic\nacid, vitamin C, etc.), while few published studies have re-\nported to find an ergogenic value of vitamins for athletes\n[134–138]. Alternatively, if an athlete is deficient in a vita-\nmin, supplementation or diet modifications to improve\nvitamin status can consistently improve health and per-\nformance [139]. For example, Paschalis and colleagues\nfor 30 days and reported these individuals had significantly\nlower VO2Max levels than a group of males who were high\nin vitamin C. Further, after 30 days of supplementation,\nVO2Max significantly improved in the low vitamin C co-\nhort as did baseline levels of oxidative stress of oxidative\nstress. Importantly, one must consider that some vitamins\nmay help athletes tolerate training to a greater degree by\nreducing oxidative damage (Vitamin E, C) and/or help to\nmaintain a healthy immune system during heavy training\n(Vitamin C). Alternatively, conflicting evidence has accu-\nmulat", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ations seen in re-\nsponse to exercise training [141–144], which may conse-\nFurthermore, while optimal levels of vitamin D have been\nlinked to improved muscle health [145] and strength [146]\nin general populations, research studies conducted in ath-\nl"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_issn_review_2018_p0015_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_945e7b3e32e9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "947734636ba2644e", "text_span_hash": "947734636ba2644e", "source_file": "nutrition_issn_review_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_issn_review_2018.pdf", "page": 15, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "re for tissue, important components of enzymes and\nhormones, and regulators of metabolic and neural con-\ntrol. In athletic populations, some minerals have been\nfound to be deficient while other minerals are reduced\nsecondary to training and/or prolon", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "vitamins consist of the entire complex of B-vitamins and\nvitamin C. Since these vitamins are water-soluble, excessive\nintake of these vitamins are eliminated in urine, with few\nexceptions (e.g. vitamin B6, which can cause peripheral\nnerve damage when consumed in excessive amounts).\nTable 1 describes the RDA, proposed ergogenic benefit,\nand summary of research findings for fat and water-soluble\nvitamins. Research has demonstrated that specific vitamins\npossess various health benefits (e.g., Vitamin E, niacin, folic\nacid, vitamin C, etc.), while few published studies have re-\nported to find an ergogenic value of vitamins for athletes\n[134–138]. Alternatively, if an athlete is deficient in a vita-\nmin, supplementation or diet modifications to improve\nvitamin status can consistently improve health and per-\nformance [139]. For example, Paschalis and colleagues\nfor 30 days and reported these individuals had significantly\nlower VO2Max levels than a group of males who were high\nin vitamin C. Further, after 30 days of supplementation,\nVO2Max significantly improved in the low vitamin C co-\nhort as did baseline levels of oxidative stress of oxidative\nstress. Importantly, one must consider that some vitamins\nmay help athletes tolerate training to a greater degree by\nreducing oxidative damage (Vitamin E, C) and/or help to\nmaintain a healthy immune system during heavy training\n(Vitamin C). Alternatively, conflicting evidence has accu-\nmulat", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "re for tissue, important components of enzymes and\nhormones, and regulators of metabolic and neural con-\ntrol. In athletic populations, some minerals have been\nfound to be deficient while other minerals are reduced\nsecondary to training and/or prolon"}
