ical roles\n[74, 326, 327]. Glutamine has been reported to increase\ncell volume and stimulate protein [328–330] and glycogen\nsynthesis [331]. Despite its important role in physiological\nprocesses, there is no compelling evidence to support the\nuse of glutamine supplementation in te", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "using the leg press or bench press exercises. In\na well-designed investigation, Candow and co-workers\ntion combined with resistance training in young adults.\nThirty-one participants were randomly allocated to receive\neither glutamine (0.9 g/kg of le"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_issn_review_2018_p0027_c0018", "source_registry_id": "src_945e7b3e32e9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "231b537bf3d3909a", "text_span_hash": "231b537bf3d3909a", "source_file": "nutrition_issn_review_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_issn_review_2018.pdf", "page": 27, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 18, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "y sporting populations for their\npotential to impact growth hormone secretion, recovery\nand robustness of training [342]. Although there is clinical\nevidence that pharmaceutical grade GHRP’s and some\nnon-peptide secretagogues can increase GH and IGF-", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "extract suggests it may help improve resistance-training\nadaptations, but more research in different populations is\nneeded before any further recommendations can be made.\nGamma oryzanol (ferulic acid)\nGamma oryzanol is a mixture of a plant sterol and ferulic\nacid theorized to increase anabolic hormonal responses,\nstrength and muscle mass during training [322, 323]. Al-\nthough data are limited, one study reported no effect of\n0.5 g/d of gamma oryzanol supplementation on strength,\nmuscle mass, or anabolic hormonal profiles during 9 weeks\nof training [324]. Most recently, Eslami and colleagues\ngamma oryzanol or placebo for 9 weeks while resistance\ntraining. In this study, changes in body composition were\nnot realized, but a significant increase in strength was\nfound in the bench press and leg curl exercise. With limited\nresearch of mixed outcomes at this point, no conclu-\nsive recommendation can be made at this time as\nmore research is needed to fully determine what im-\npact, if any, gamma oryzanol supplementation may\nhave in exercising individuals.\nGlutamine is the most plentiful non-essential amino acid\nin the body and plays several important physiological roles\n[74, 326, 327]. Glutamine has been reported to increase\ncell volume and stimulate protein [328–330] and glycogen\nsynthesis [331]. Despite its important role in physiological\nprocesses, there is no compelling evidence to support the\nuse of glutamine supplementation in te", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "y sporting populations for their\npotential to impact growth hormone secretion, recovery\nand robustness of training [342]. Although there is clinical\nevidence that pharmaceutical grade GHRP’s and some\nnon-peptide secretagogues can increase GH and IGF-"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_issn_review_2018_p0028_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_945e7b3e32e9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ba22d1bb5732e55c", "text_span_hash": "ba22d1bb5732e55c", "source_file": "nutrition_issn_review_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_issn_review_2018.pdf", "page": 28, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "es with re-\nsistance training did not significantly impact strength or\nOrnithine-α-ketoglutarate (OKG)\nOKG (via enteral feeding) has been shown to significantly\nshorten wound healing time and improve nitrogen balance\nin severe burn patients [352, 353", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "isoflavone supplementation affects exercise, body compos-\nition, or training adaptations in physically active individ-\nuals. For example, Wilborn and colleagues [318] reported\nthat 8 weeks of supplementing with isoflavones with re-\nsistance training did not significantly impact strength or\nOrnithine-α-ketoglutarate (OKG)\nOKG (via enteral feeding) has been shown to significantly\nshorten wound healing time and improve nitrogen balance\nin severe burn patients [352, 353]. A 2004 review by Cyno-\nber postulated that OKG may operate as a precursor to\narginine and nitric oxide, but the overall lack of efficacy\nfor arginine and other precursors limits the potential of\nOKG. Because of its ability to improve nitrogen balance,\nOKG may provide some value for athletes engaged in\nintense training. A study by Chetlin and colleagues [354]\nreported that OKG supplementation (10 g/day) during\n6 weeks of resistance training significantly increased upper\nbody strength. However, no significant differences were\nobserved in lower body strength, training volume, gains in\nmuscle mass, or fasting insulin and growth hormone.\nSince the previously published version of this review, no\nadditional human research has been published and conse-\nquently, no further recommendations can be made regard-\ning OKG’s potential as an ergogenic aid.\nProhormones and anabolic steroids\nTestosterone and growth hormone are two primary hor-\nmones in the body that serve to promot", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "es with re-\nsistance training did not significantly impact strength or\nOrnithine-α-ketoglutarate (OKG)\nOKG (via enteral feeding) has been shown to significantly\nshorten wound healing time and improve nitrogen balance\nin severe burn patients [352, 353"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_issn_review_2018_p0028_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_945e7b3e32e9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "d7eb8580fe3bf828", "text_span_hash": "d7eb8580fe3bf828", "source_file": "nutrition_issn_review_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_issn_review_2018.pdf", "page": 28, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "uring\n6 weeks of resistance training significantly increased upper\nbody strength. However, no significant differences were\nobserved in lower body strength, training volume, gains in\nmuscle mass, or fasting insulin and growth hormone.\nSince the previo", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "isoflavone supplementation affects exercise, body compos-\nition, or training adaptations in physically active individ-\nuals. For example, Wilborn and colleagues [318] reported\nthat 8 weeks of supplementing with isoflavones with re-\nsistance training did not significantly impact strength or\nOrnithine-α-ketoglutarate (OKG)\nOKG (via enteral feeding) has been shown to significantly\nshorten wound healing time and improve nitrogen balance\nin severe burn patients [352, 353]. A 2004 review by Cyno-\nber postulated that OKG may operate as a precursor to\narginine and nitric oxide, but the overall lack of efficacy\nfor arginine and other precursors limits the potential of\nOKG. Because of its ability to improve nitrogen balance,\nOKG may provide some value for athletes engaged in\nintense training. A study by Chetlin and colleagues [354]\nreported that OKG supplementation (10 g/day) during\n6 weeks of resistance training significantly increased upper\nbody strength. However, no significant differences were\nobserved in lower body strength, training volume, gains in\nmuscle mass, or fasting insulin and growth hormone.\nSince the previously published version of this review, no\nadditional human research has been published and conse-\nquently, no further recommendations can be made regard-\ning OKG’s potential as an ergogenic aid.\nProhormones and anabolic steroids\nTestosterone and growth hormone are two primary hor-\nmones in the body that serve to promot", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "uring\n6 weeks of resistance training significantly increased upper\nbody strength. However, no significant differences were\nobserved in lower body strength, training volume, gains in\nmuscle mass, or fasting insulin and growth hormone.\nSince the previo"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_issn_review_2018_p0028_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_945e7b3e32e9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e45d6b544e10da5d", "text_span_hash": "e45d6b544e10da5d", "source_file": "nutrition_issn_review_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_issn_review_2018.pdf", "page": 28, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "nd/or promote\nrecovery during intense training [356–358, 361, 362, 375].\nResearch has generally shown that use of anabolic steroids\nand growth hormone during training can promote gains in\n376–383]. However, a number of potentially life threaten-\ning", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "isoflavone supplementation affects exercise, body compos-\nition, or training adaptations in physically active individ-\nuals. For example, Wilborn and colleagues [318] reported\nthat 8 weeks of supplementing with isoflavones with re-\nsistance training did not significantly impact strength or\nOrnithine-α-ketoglutarate (OKG)\nOKG (via enteral feeding) has been shown to significantly\nshorten wound healing time and improve nitrogen balance\nin severe burn patients [352, 353]. A 2004 review by Cyno-\nber postulated that OKG may operate as a precursor to\narginine and nitric oxide, but the overall lack of efficacy\nfor arginine and other precursors limits the potential of\nOKG. Because of its ability to improve nitrogen balance,\nOKG may provide some value for athletes engaged in\nintense training. A study by Chetlin and colleagues [354]\nreported that OKG supplementation (10 g/day) during\n6 weeks of resistance training significantly increased upper\nbody strength. However, no significant differences were\nobserved in lower body strength, training volume, gains in\nmuscle mass, or fasting insulin and growth hormone.\nSince the previously published version of this review, no\nadditional human research has been published and conse-\nquently, no further recommendations can be made regard-\ning OKG’s potential as an ergogenic aid.\nProhormones and anabolic steroids\nTestosterone and growth hormone are two primary hor-\nmones in the body that serve to promot", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "nd/or promote\nrecovery during intense training [356–358, 361, 362, 375].\nResearch has generally shown that use of anabolic steroids\nand growth hormone during training can promote gains in\n376–383]. However, a number of potentially life threaten-\ning"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_issn_review_2018_p0028_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_945e7b3e32e9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ae8c82d65d5882c2", "text_span_hash": "ae8c82d65d5882c2", "source_file": "nutrition_issn_review_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_issn_review_2018.pdf", "page": 28, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "n\ntestosterone [389, 390], virtually no evidence exists demon-\nstrating heightened training adaptations in younger men\nwith normal hormone levels. In fact, most studies indicate\nthat they do not affect testosterone and that some may ac-\ntually increa", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "isoflavone supplementation affects exercise, body compos-\nition, or training adaptations in physically active individ-\nuals. For example, Wilborn and colleagues [318] reported\nthat 8 weeks of supplementing with isoflavones with re-\nsistance training did not significantly impact strength or\nOrnithine-α-ketoglutarate (OKG)\nOKG (via enteral feeding) has been shown to significantly\nshorten wound healing time and improve nitrogen balance\nin severe burn patients [352, 353]. A 2004 review by Cyno-\nber postulated that OKG may operate as a precursor to\narginine and nitric oxide, but the overall lack of efficacy\nfor arginine and other precursors limits the potential of\nOKG. Because of its ability to improve nitrogen balance,\nOKG may provide some value for athletes engaged in\nintense training. A study by Chetlin and colleagues [354]\nreported that OKG supplementation (10 g/day) during\n6 weeks of resistance training significantly increased upper\nbody strength. However, no significant differences were\nobserved in lower body strength, training volume, gains in\nmuscle mass, or fasting insulin and growth hormone.\nSince the previously published version of this review, no\nadditional human research has been published and conse-\nquently, no further recommendations can be made regard-\ning OKG’s potential as an ergogenic aid.\nProhormones and anabolic steroids\nTestosterone and growth hormone are two primary hor-\nmones in the body that serve to promot", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "n\ntestosterone [389, 390], virtually no evidence exists demon-\nstrating heightened training adaptations in younger men\nwith normal hormone levels. In fact, most studies indicate\nthat they do not affect testosterone and that some may ac-\ntually increa"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_issn_review_2018_p0029_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_945e7b3e32e9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e222d603725ad1a8", "text_span_hash": "e222d603725ad1a8", "source_file": "nutrition_issn_review_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_issn_review_2018.pdf", "page": 29, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "mmon myostatin allele experi-\nenced greater gains in muscle mass during training and\nreduced atrophy during detraining. Interestingly, no such\nchanges were reported for men. Willoughby and colleagues\nCytoseira Canariensis (a form of sea algae), a pur", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "speed up recovery of injured muscles, and/or promote in-\ncreases in muscle mass and strength in athletes [401].\nSince 2010, no additional research has been published that\nexamined the impact of any nutritional ingredient or strat-\negy to inhibit myostatin expression. In humans, myostatin\nclearly plays a role in regulating skeletal muscle mass. For\nexample, a study by Ivey and colleagues [401] reported that\nfemale athletes with a less common myostatin allele experi-\nenced greater gains in muscle mass during training and\nreduced atrophy during detraining. Interestingly, no such\nchanges were reported for men. Willoughby and colleagues\nCytoseira Canariensis (a form of sea algae), a purported\nmyostatin inhibitor, and reported no changes for fat-free\nmass, strength and blood concentrations of myostatin.\nThese results were corroborated by Wilborn et al. [318]\nwho reported no impact of sulfo-polysaccaride supplemen-\ntation on body composition or performance changes. As it\nstands, there is currently no published data supporting the\nuse of sulfo-polysaccharides or any other ingredient touted\nto act as a myostatin inhibitor for their ability to increase\nstrength or muscle mass.\nTribulus terrestris (also known as puncture weed/vine or\ncaltrops) is a plant extract that has been suggested to\nstimulate leutinizing hormone which stimulates the natural\nproduction of testosterone [403]. Consequently, tribulus is\nmarketed as a supplement that ca", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "mmon myostatin allele experi-\nenced greater gains in muscle mass during training and\nreduced atrophy during detraining. Interestingly, no such\nchanges were reported for men. Willoughby and colleagues\nCytoseira Canariensis (a form of sea algae), a pur"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_issn_review_2018_p0031_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_945e7b3e32e9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ab06c35fde19fc63", "text_span_hash": "ab06c35fde19fc63", "source_file": "nutrition_issn_review_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_issn_review_2018.pdf", "page": 31, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "in a variety of settings [182, 460–462]. Specifically, and\nas discussed by Kreider et al. [10], studies have docu-\nmented improvements in: a) single and multiple sprints,\nb) work completed across multiple sets of maximal ef-\nfort, c) anaerobic thres", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "possible combinations of high and low pre-exercise\nintramuscular glycogen levels with and without carbo-\nhydrate provision before a standard bout of cycling\nexercise. When carbohydrate was provided, performance\nwas improved. In addition to traditional endurance exer-\ncise models, Williams and Hawley [42] summarized the\nliterature involving carbohydrate delivery and perform-\nance of team sports that are typically characterized by\nvariable intensities and intermittent periods of heavy exer-\ntion and concluded that carbohydrate intake can increase\nperformance. Pochmuller et al. [68] and Colombani et al.\nexercise bout, the intensity of exercise involved, and the\nfasting status of the individuals as key factors that may im-\npact exercise performance. Further, Burke and colleagues\n[23, 50], Hawley et al. [43] and Rodriguez et al. [54] have\nall emphasized the importance of optimal carbohydrate\ndelivery throughout various types of sport and recovery\nscenarios to support performance. Beyond ingestion, a\ngrowing body of literature has drawn attention to the po-\ntential impact of carbohydrate mouth rinsing as an ergo-\ngenic strategy. Initial work by Carter and colleagues [448]\nwhere they demonstrated an increase in time to exhaus-\ntion performance while cycling after rinsing (but not swal-\nlowing) the oral cavity with a carbohydrate solution versus\na no carbohydrate rinse revealed that receptors in the\nbrain might be linked to the mere p", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "in a variety of settings [182, 460–462]. Specifically, and\nas discussed by Kreider et al. [10], studies have docu-\nmented improvements in: a) single and multiple sprints,\nb) work completed across multiple sets of maximal ef-\nfort, c) anaerobic thres"}
