rate and carnitine\nwhile completing a 40-min ride at 65% VO2peak before\ncompleting an exhaustion ride at 85% VO2Peak. No differ-\nences in power outputs or times to exhaustion were found\nwith cycling at 85% VO2Peak. Of note, studies have sug-\ngested that co-ingesting carnitine with c", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "genic aid.\nInosine Inosine is a building block for DNA and RNA\nthat is found in muscle. Inosine possesses important roles\nthat may enhance training and/or exercise performance\n[663]. Although there is some theoretical rationale, avail-\nable studies i"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_issn_review_2018_p0038_c0019", "source_registry_id": "src_945e7b3e32e9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "d8a23bc094589cd6", "text_span_hash": "d8a23bc094589cd6", "source_file": "nutrition_issn_review_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_issn_review_2018.pdf", "page": 38, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 19, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "1]. A 2001 study\nfound that 60 g/day of MCT oil for 2 weeks did improve\nrunning performance [672]. Additionally, Van Zyl and\ncolleagues [671] reported that while MCTs negatively\ninfluenced cycling time trial performance when ingested\nalone in compari", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Similarly, Bailey and colleagues [645] concluded that acute\narginine supplementation reduced the oxygen cost of\nmoderate-intensity exercise and increased tolerance to\ndouble-blind fashion and concluded that arginine sup-\nsignificantly increased sport perform-\nance. As it stands, most of the published literature\nthat has examined the ability of arginine to operate in\nan ergogenic fashion has failed to report positive out-\ncomes. While more research is certainly indicated,\nconsumers should exercise caution when using arginine to\nenhance exercise performance.\nCarnitine Carnitine is produced endogenously by the\nliver and kidneys and plays a pivotal role in lipid metabol-\nism. Consequently, many are led to believe that carnitine\ningestion will increase the concentration of endogenous\ndecrease adipose reserves. To date, the majority of the data\ncontinues to suggest that carnitine supplementation does\nnot markedly affect muscle carnitine content [647–649],\nfat metabolism [648, 650, 651], exercise performance\n[648, 649, 652, 653], or weight loss in overweight\n[657]. For example, Burrus and investigators [658] had ten\ncyclists ingest combinations of carbohydrate and carnitine\nwhile completing a 40-min ride at 65% VO2peak before\ncompleting an exhaustion ride at 85% VO2Peak. No differ-\nences in power outputs or times to exhaustion were found\nwith cycling at 85% VO2Peak. Of note, studies have sug-\ngested that co-ingesting carnitine with c", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "1]. A 2001 study\nfound that 60 g/day of MCT oil for 2 weeks did improve\nrunning performance [672]. Additionally, Van Zyl and\ncolleagues [671] reported that while MCTs negatively\ninfluenced cycling time trial performance when ingested\nalone in compari"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_issn_review_2018_p0038_c0023", "source_registry_id": "src_945e7b3e32e9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9a144b8bd850da1f", "text_span_hash": "9a144b8bd850da1f", "source_file": "nutrition_issn_review_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_issn_review_2018.pdf", "page": 38, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 23, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "the incidence of gastrointestinal com-\nplaints increased in both studies. These findings have been\nconfirmed by others that MCT oils are not sufficient to\ninduce positive training adaptations and may cause gastric\ndistress [675, 676]. Consequently,", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Similarly, Bailey and colleagues [645] concluded that acute\narginine supplementation reduced the oxygen cost of\nmoderate-intensity exercise and increased tolerance to\ndouble-blind fashion and concluded that arginine sup-\nsignificantly increased sport perform-\nance. As it stands, most of the published literature\nthat has examined the ability of arginine to operate in\nan ergogenic fashion has failed to report positive out-\ncomes. While more research is certainly indicated,\nconsumers should exercise caution when using arginine to\nenhance exercise performance.\nCarnitine Carnitine is produced endogenously by the\nliver and kidneys and plays a pivotal role in lipid metabol-\nism. Consequently, many are led to believe that carnitine\ningestion will increase the concentration of endogenous\ndecrease adipose reserves. To date, the majority of the data\ncontinues to suggest that carnitine supplementation does\nnot markedly affect muscle carnitine content [647–649],\nfat metabolism [648, 650, 651], exercise performance\n[648, 649, 652, 653], or weight loss in overweight\n[657]. For example, Burrus and investigators [658] had ten\ncyclists ingest combinations of carbohydrate and carnitine\nwhile completing a 40-min ride at 65% VO2peak before\ncompleting an exhaustion ride at 85% VO2Peak. No differ-\nences in power outputs or times to exhaustion were found\nwith cycling at 85% VO2Peak. Of note, studies have sug-\ngested that co-ingesting carnitine with c", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "the incidence of gastrointestinal com-\nplaints increased in both studies. These findings have been\nconfirmed by others that MCT oils are not sufficient to\ninduce positive training adaptations and may cause gastric\ndistress [675, 676]. Consequently,"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_issn_review_2018_p0038_c0024", "source_registry_id": "src_945e7b3e32e9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "026bba2009c60c03", "text_span_hash": "026bba2009c60c03", "source_file": "nutrition_issn_review_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_issn_review_2018.pdf", "page": 38, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 24, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ess [675, 676]. Consequently, it does not appear likely\nthat MCT favorably impacts acute exercise performance\nand no evidence exists that training adaptations may be\npositively impacted either, while multiple studies have\nKerksick et al. Journal of t", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Similarly, Bailey and colleagues [645] concluded that acute\narginine supplementation reduced the oxygen cost of\nmoderate-intensity exercise and increased tolerance to\ndouble-blind fashion and concluded that arginine sup-\nsignificantly increased sport perform-\nance. As it stands, most of the published literature\nthat has examined the ability of arginine to operate in\nan ergogenic fashion has failed to report positive out-\ncomes. While more research is certainly indicated,\nconsumers should exercise caution when using arginine to\nenhance exercise performance.\nCarnitine Carnitine is produced endogenously by the\nliver and kidneys and plays a pivotal role in lipid metabol-\nism. Consequently, many are led to believe that carnitine\ningestion will increase the concentration of endogenous\ndecrease adipose reserves. To date, the majority of the data\ncontinues to suggest that carnitine supplementation does\nnot markedly affect muscle carnitine content [647–649],\nfat metabolism [648, 650, 651], exercise performance\n[648, 649, 652, 653], or weight loss in overweight\n[657]. For example, Burrus and investigators [658] had ten\ncyclists ingest combinations of carbohydrate and carnitine\nwhile completing a 40-min ride at 65% VO2peak before\ncompleting an exhaustion ride at 85% VO2Peak. No differ-\nences in power outputs or times to exhaustion were found\nwith cycling at 85% VO2Peak. Of note, studies have sug-\ngested that co-ingesting carnitine with c", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ess [675, 676]. Consequently, it does not appear likely\nthat MCT favorably impacts acute exercise performance\nand no evidence exists that training adaptations may be\npositively impacted either, while multiple studies have\nKerksick et al. Journal of t"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_issn_review_2018_p0039_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_945e7b3e32e9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "d648ad17271737b0", "text_span_hash": "d648ad17271737b0", "source_file": "nutrition_issn_review_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_issn_review_2018.pdf", "page": 39, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "with either 10 g of ribose\nor 10 g of dextrose for 5 days while completing a single\nbout of interval exercise and a two-minute power output\ntest. When splitting the participants into high vs. low oxy-\ngen uptake levels, the people with low peak VO2 e", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "reported that MCT ingestion may cause gastrointestinal\nupset and decrease exercise performance.\nRibose Ribose is a 5-carbon carbohydrate that is involved\nin the synthesis of adenosine triphosphate (ATP) and\nother adenine nucleotides. Clinical studies have shown\nthat ribose supplementation can increase exercise capacity\nin heart patients [677–681] leading to the development of\ntheories that it can operate as ergogenic aid for athletes.\nOf the available research, most fail to show an ergogenic\nvalue for ribose supplementation on exercise capacity in\nhealthy untrained or trained populations [682–684]. A\n2006 study [685] investigated the effects of supplementing\nwith either ribose or dextrose over 8 weeks on rowing per-\nformance and concluded that ribose was outperformed by\nthe dextrose control [685]. Kreider and associates [684]\nand Kerksick and colleagues [686] investigated ribose sup-\nplementation on measures of anaerobic capacity in trained\ncyclists and concluded ribose had no positive impact on\nperformance. In 2017, Seifert and investigators [687] had\n26 healthy subjects supplement with either 10 g of ribose\nor 10 g of dextrose for 5 days while completing a single\nbout of interval exercise and a two-minute power output\ntest. When splitting the participants into high vs. low oxy-\ngen uptake levels, the people with low peak VO2 experi-\nenced significant increases in mean and peak power\noutput along with reductions in ratings of", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "with either 10 g of ribose\nor 10 g of dextrose for 5 days while completing a single\nbout of interval exercise and a two-minute power output\ntest. When splitting the participants into high vs. low oxy-\ngen uptake levels, the people with low peak VO2 e"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_issn_review_2018_p0039_c0022", "source_registry_id": "src_945e7b3e32e9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e691f20acadf4b47", "text_span_hash": "e691f20acadf4b47", "source_file": "nutrition_issn_review_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_issn_review_2018.pdf", "page": 39, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 22, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "h intensity exercise can compromise an athlete’s\nimmune health. Infection risk and exercise workload follow\na J-Shape curve with moderate intensity exercise reducing\nthe infection risk, and high intensity exercise actually in-\ncreasing the risk of in", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "reported that MCT ingestion may cause gastrointestinal\nupset and decrease exercise performance.\nRibose Ribose is a 5-carbon carbohydrate that is involved\nin the synthesis of adenosine triphosphate (ATP) and\nother adenine nucleotides. Clinical studies have shown\nthat ribose supplementation can increase exercise capacity\nin heart patients [677–681] leading to the development of\ntheories that it can operate as ergogenic aid for athletes.\nOf the available research, most fail to show an ergogenic\nvalue for ribose supplementation on exercise capacity in\nhealthy untrained or trained populations [682–684]. A\n2006 study [685] investigated the effects of supplementing\nwith either ribose or dextrose over 8 weeks on rowing per-\nformance and concluded that ribose was outperformed by\nthe dextrose control [685]. Kreider and associates [684]\nand Kerksick and colleagues [686] investigated ribose sup-\nplementation on measures of anaerobic capacity in trained\ncyclists and concluded ribose had no positive impact on\nperformance. In 2017, Seifert and investigators [687] had\n26 healthy subjects supplement with either 10 g of ribose\nor 10 g of dextrose for 5 days while completing a single\nbout of interval exercise and a two-minute power output\ntest. When splitting the participants into high vs. low oxy-\ngen uptake levels, the people with low peak VO2 experi-\nenced significant increases in mean and peak power\noutput along with reductions in ratings of", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "h intensity exercise can compromise an athlete’s\nimmune health. Infection risk and exercise workload follow\na J-Shape curve with moderate intensity exercise reducing\nthe infection risk, and high intensity exercise actually in-\ncreasing the risk of in"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_issn_review_2018_p0040_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_945e7b3e32e9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ecf45f28f75f0521", "text_span_hash": "ecf45f28f75f0521", "source_file": "nutrition_issn_review_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_issn_review_2018.pdf", "page": 40, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ld develop scientifically based products, conduct re-\nsearch on their products, and honestly market the results\nof studies so consumers can make informed decisions.\n1RM: One-repetition maximum; ACSM: American College of Sports Medicine;\nAER: Adverse", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "health. Probiotics have been shown to reduce the number,\nduration and severity of upper-respiratory tract infections\nand gastrointestinal distress in the general population and\nin athletes, certain strains of probiotics have been shown to\nsignificantly reduce the number of upper-respiratory tract\ninfection episodes a well as their severity [705]. Health\nbenefits of probiotics are strain specific and dose\ndependent, and some strains have failed to show benefi-\ncial effects in athletes [706]. Also, consuming carbohy-\ndrate during prolonged strenuous exercise attenuates\nrises in stress hormones and appears to limit the degree\nof exercise-induced immune depression [699].\nSeveral factors operate as cornerstones to enhance athletic\nperformance and optimize training adaptations including\nthe consumption of a balanced, nutrient and energy dense\ndiet, prudent training, and obtaining adequate rest. Use of\na limited number of nutritional supplements that research\nhas supported to improve energy availability (e.g., sports\ndrinks, carbohydrate, creatine, caffeine, β-alanine, etc.)\nand/or promote recovery (carbohydrate, protein, essential\namino acids, etc.) can provide additional benefit in certain\ninstances. Dietitians and sport nutritionists should stay up\nto date on current research regarding the role of nutrition\non exercise so they can provide honest and accurate infor-\nmation to their students, clients, and/or athletes about the\nrole ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ld develop scientifically based products, conduct re-\nsearch on their products, and honestly market the results\nof studies so consumers can make informed decisions.\n1RM: One-repetition maximum; ACSM: American College of Sports Medicine;\nAER: Adverse"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_issn_review_2018_p0042_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_945e7b3e32e9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "d639b6b0b622bdfb", "text_span_hash": "d639b6b0b622bdfb", "source_file": "nutrition_issn_review_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_issn_review_2018.pdf", "page": 42, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "short-term fat adaptation on high-intensity training. Med Sci Sports\nHawley JA, Burke LM, Angus DJ, Fallon KE, Martin DT, Febbraio MA. Effect of\naltering substrate availability on metabolism and performance during intense\nVan Loon LJ, Greenhaff PL,", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Manore MM. Weight management for athletes and active individuals: a brief\nBlack KE, Skidmore PM, Brown RC. Energy intakes of ultraendurance cyclists\nduring competition, an observational study. Int J Sport Nutr Exerc Metab.\nLoucks AB. Energy balance and body composition in sports and exercise.\nBarrero A, Erola P, Bescos R. Energy balance of triathletes during an ultra-\nBrouns F, Saris WH, Stroecken J, Beckers E, Thijssen R, Rehrer NJ, Ten Hoor F.\nEating, drinking, and cycling. A controlled tour de France simulation study,\npart i. Int J Sports Med. 1989;10(Suppl 1):S32–40.\nBrouns F, Saris WH, Stroecken J, Beckers E, Thijssen R, Rehrer NJ, Ten\nHoor F. Eating, drinking, and cycling. A controlled tour de France\nsimulation study, part ii. Effect of diet manipulation. Int J Sports Med.\nHeydenreich J, Kayser B, Schutz Y, Melzer K. Total energy expenditure,\nenergy intake, and body composition in endurance athletes across the\ntraining season: a systematic review. Sports Med Open. 2017;3(1):8.\nKreider RB. Physiological considerations of ultraendurance performance. Int\nBerning JR. Energy intake, diet, and muscle wasting. In: Kreider RB, Fry AC,\nO'Toole ML, editors. Overtraining in sport. Champaign: Human Kinetics;\nViner RT, Harris M, Berning JR, Meyer NL. Energy availability and dietary patterns\nof adult male and female competitive cyclists with lower than expected bone\nmineral density. Int J Sport Nutr Exerc Metab. 2015;25(6):594–602.\nWa", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "short-term fat adaptation on high-intensity training. Med Sci Sports\nHawley JA, Burke LM, Angus DJ, Fallon KE, Martin DT, Febbraio MA. Effect of\naltering substrate availability on metabolism and performance during intense\nVan Loon LJ, Greenhaff PL,"}
