es/50 km (Blackwater Trail -\nFlorida, USA); 56 miles/90 km (Comrades Marathon -\nDurban, South Africa); 100 miles/161 km (Western States\nEndurance Run - California, USA); and 152 miles/245 km\n(Spartathlon – Athens, Greece). Moreover, such races\ntypically last between 6 and 48 h. The distances of mu", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "scle\ngrowth and/or maintenance. Thirdly, failing to adequately\nhydrate during training, and/or rehydrate following training,\ncan result in carry-over effects that may reduce perform-\nance in subsequent sessions. Chronically, this can cause\nchanges in"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019_p0003_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_6bd04c6f0cb9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "6b4dedc4455236b5", "text_span_hash": "6b4dedc4455236b5", "source_file": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "best-practice of athletes, coaches, medics, support staff,\nand race organizers. This is particularly pertinent given\nthe increased participation in ultra-marathon racing\nacross the globe, and the ever-expanding extremes of\nThis Position Stand is conc", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "extremes of temperature/altitude which are known to\ninfluence appetite [18–20]. Importantly, although ultra-\nendurance athletes have a reasonable knowledge of\nnutrition, they tend to favour the insights of other ath-\nletes over qualified nutrition experts [21]. Accordingly,\nthe aim of this paper is to provide an accessible,\nevidence-based Position Stand on the nutritional consid-\nerations of ultra-marathon training and racing to inform\nbest-practice of athletes, coaches, medics, support staff,\nand race organizers. This is particularly pertinent given\nthe increased participation in ultra-marathon racing\nacross the globe, and the ever-expanding extremes of\nThis Position Stand is concerned primarily with the nu-\ntritional considerations for single-stage ultra-marathon\ntraining and racing. Articles were searched via three\nScholar), and the main search-terms comprised various\ncombinations of the following: extreme-endurance, hy-\ndration, marathon, nutrition (various terms), pathophysi-\nology, physiology, supplements (various terms), ultra-\nmarathon, and ultra-endurance. The reference-lists of\nthose articles selected for inclusion were manually\nsearched for additional literature. The data informing\nour recommendations are incomplete, particularly rela-\ntive to other sports, for several reasons. Firstly, despite\nthe growing popularity of ultra-marathon, participant\nnumbers are still relatively low. Moreover, runners are\noften relucta", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "best-practice of athletes, coaches, medics, support staff,\nand race organizers. This is particularly pertinent given\nthe increased participation in ultra-marathon racing\nacross the globe, and the ever-expanding extremes of\nThis Position Stand is conc"}
