{"chunk_id": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019_p0006_c0024", "source_registry_id": "src_6bd04c6f0cb9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8e29630a6ea15ed3", "text_span_hash": "8e29630a6ea15ed3", "source_file": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 24, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "t oxidation, the ability to perform at higher in-\ntensities may be compromised, or even reduced, due to\ndownregulation of pyruvate dehydrogenase [54], leading\nto reduced oxygen economy [55]. Despite positive anec-\ndotal reports from ultra-marathon ru", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "requires nutrition-specific knowledge, an understanding\nof training periodization, and a degree of experience and\nself-awareness on behalf of the athlete with respect to\ntheir requirements. As such, athletes are cautioned against\ntraining in a chronically depleted state (especially during\nintensive training periods, or when repeated days of\nprolonged training are scheduled) as this may lead to low\nenergy availability and, ultimately, relative energy defi-\nciency (RED-S [46];). A further consideration is that high-\nintensity performance will likely be compromised by low\nglycogen availability, due to a relative inability to sustain a\nhigh work rate [45]. Exercising while glycogen-depleted\nincreases circulating cortisol and causes disturbances in\nseveral indices of immune function (including plasma glu-\ntamine and circulating leukocytes) [6], and post-exercise\nimmune dysfunction is most pronounced following pro-\nlonged, continuous exercise (> 1.5 h) performed without\nfood [47]. As training volume and/or intensity increase\n(e.g., an increase in running mileage or a transition to\ninterval training), relatively greater amounts of dietary\nCHO will be required to fuel performance and minimize\nthe risk of injury. Consequently, before implementing a\nnew dietary regimen, athletes and coaches must consider\neach individual’s metabolic needs, ideally having sought\nadvice from a qualified nutrition professional, with the\nprogram monitored an", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "t oxidation, the ability to perform at higher in-\ntensities may be compromised, or even reduced, due to\ndownregulation of pyruvate dehydrogenase [54], leading\nto reduced oxygen economy [55]. Despite positive anec-\ndotal reports from ultra-marathon ru"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019_p0007_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_6bd04c6f0cb9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "184f6c1372cae5f0", "text_span_hash": "184f6c1372cae5f0", "source_file": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "en balance can\nremain below baseline for several days following un-\naccustomed exercise [57]. The substantial training dis-\ntances of ultra-marathon are associated with high levels\nof mechanical stress. This is reinforced by empirical data\nshowing th", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "performance. However, caution is advised against train-\ning chronically glycogen depleted, particularly during\nperiods of repeated high-intensity exercise or prior to\nEvidence statement (category B/C)\nDespite the use of ketogenic diets to facilitate a rapid meta-\nbolic shift towards greater fat oxidation, there is insufficient\nevidence to support the use of such diets in ultra-marathon\ntraining, and further research is warranted.\nProtein and muscle damage\nProlonged or strenuous exercise, particularly that to\nwhich the individual is unaccustomed, can result in\nmuscle damage attributed to metabolic overload and/or\nmechanical strain [56]. Moreover, nitrogen balance can\nremain below baseline for several days following un-\naccustomed exercise [57]. The substantial training dis-\ntances of ultra-marathon are associated with high levels\nof mechanical stress. This is reinforced by empirical data\nshowing that whole-blood markers of muscle breakdown\n(e.g., creatine kinase, lactate dehydrogenase, and serum\ncreatine phosphokinase) were higher following ultra-\nmarathons when compared to marathons run at a rela-\ntively faster pace [58, 59]. Specifically, creatine kinase\nconcentrations of 274 ± 71 U·L−1 were observed post-\nmarathon, relative to 2983 ± 1716 U·L−1 following a 100\nkm race, and 4970 ± 2222 U·L−1 after a 308 km race [58].\nThese data suggest that race distance and/or duration\nmediate muscle damage more than race intensity, al-\nthou", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "en balance can\nremain below baseline for several days following un-\naccustomed exercise [57]. The substantial training dis-\ntances of ultra-marathon are associated with high levels\nof mechanical stress. This is reinforced by empirical data\nshowing th"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019_p0007_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_6bd04c6f0cb9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "13f7565317369c49", "text_span_hash": "13f7565317369c49", "source_file": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "d by empirical data\nshowing that whole-blood markers of muscle breakdown\n(e.g., creatine kinase, lactate dehydrogenase, and serum\ncreatine phosphokinase) were higher following ultra-\nmarathons when compared to marathons run at a rela-\ntively faster p", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "performance. However, caution is advised against train-\ning chronically glycogen depleted, particularly during\nperiods of repeated high-intensity exercise or prior to\nEvidence statement (category B/C)\nDespite the use of ketogenic diets to facilitate a rapid meta-\nbolic shift towards greater fat oxidation, there is insufficient\nevidence to support the use of such diets in ultra-marathon\ntraining, and further research is warranted.\nProtein and muscle damage\nProlonged or strenuous exercise, particularly that to\nwhich the individual is unaccustomed, can result in\nmuscle damage attributed to metabolic overload and/or\nmechanical strain [56]. Moreover, nitrogen balance can\nremain below baseline for several days following un-\naccustomed exercise [57]. The substantial training dis-\ntances of ultra-marathon are associated with high levels\nof mechanical stress. This is reinforced by empirical data\nshowing that whole-blood markers of muscle breakdown\n(e.g., creatine kinase, lactate dehydrogenase, and serum\ncreatine phosphokinase) were higher following ultra-\nmarathons when compared to marathons run at a rela-\ntively faster pace [58, 59]. Specifically, creatine kinase\nconcentrations of 274 ± 71 U·L−1 were observed post-\nmarathon, relative to 2983 ± 1716 U·L−1 following a 100\nkm race, and 4970 ± 2222 U·L−1 after a 308 km race [58].\nThese data suggest that race distance and/or duration\nmediate muscle damage more than race intensity, al-\nthou", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "d by empirical data\nshowing that whole-blood markers of muscle breakdown\n(e.g., creatine kinase, lactate dehydrogenase, and serum\ncreatine phosphokinase) were higher following ultra-\nmarathons when compared to marathons run at a rela-\ntively faster p"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019_p0007_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_6bd04c6f0cb9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7026a8bd9cfaae01", "text_span_hash": "7026a8bd9cfaae01", "source_file": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "run at a rela-\ntively faster pace [58, 59]. Specifically, creatine kinase\nconcentrations of 274 ± 71 U·L−1 were observed post-\nmarathon, relative to 2983 ± 1716 U·L−1 following a 100\nkm race, and 4970 ± 2222 U·L−1 after a 308 km race [58].\nThese data", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "performance. However, caution is advised against train-\ning chronically glycogen depleted, particularly during\nperiods of repeated high-intensity exercise or prior to\nEvidence statement (category B/C)\nDespite the use of ketogenic diets to facilitate a rapid meta-\nbolic shift towards greater fat oxidation, there is insufficient\nevidence to support the use of such diets in ultra-marathon\ntraining, and further research is warranted.\nProtein and muscle damage\nProlonged or strenuous exercise, particularly that to\nwhich the individual is unaccustomed, can result in\nmuscle damage attributed to metabolic overload and/or\nmechanical strain [56]. Moreover, nitrogen balance can\nremain below baseline for several days following un-\naccustomed exercise [57]. The substantial training dis-\ntances of ultra-marathon are associated with high levels\nof mechanical stress. This is reinforced by empirical data\nshowing that whole-blood markers of muscle breakdown\n(e.g., creatine kinase, lactate dehydrogenase, and serum\ncreatine phosphokinase) were higher following ultra-\nmarathons when compared to marathons run at a rela-\ntively faster pace [58, 59]. Specifically, creatine kinase\nconcentrations of 274 ± 71 U·L−1 were observed post-\nmarathon, relative to 2983 ± 1716 U·L−1 following a 100\nkm race, and 4970 ± 2222 U·L−1 after a 308 km race [58].\nThese data suggest that race distance and/or duration\nmediate muscle damage more than race intensity, al-\nthou", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "run at a rela-\ntively faster pace [58, 59]. Specifically, creatine kinase\nconcentrations of 274 ± 71 U·L−1 were observed post-\nmarathon, relative to 2983 ± 1716 U·L−1 following a 100\nkm race, and 4970 ± 2222 U·L−1 after a 308 km race [58].\nThese data"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019_p0007_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_6bd04c6f0cb9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f583b9fdeba83748", "text_span_hash": "f583b9fdeba83748", "source_file": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "nvironmental\nterrain typical of ultra-marathon also deserves consider-\nation in the training program. Downhill running (on\nmountainous or undulating paths) is associated with\ngreater peak flexion angles relative to level or uphill run-\nimpact-loading", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "performance. However, caution is advised against train-\ning chronically glycogen depleted, particularly during\nperiods of repeated high-intensity exercise or prior to\nEvidence statement (category B/C)\nDespite the use of ketogenic diets to facilitate a rapid meta-\nbolic shift towards greater fat oxidation, there is insufficient\nevidence to support the use of such diets in ultra-marathon\ntraining, and further research is warranted.\nProtein and muscle damage\nProlonged or strenuous exercise, particularly that to\nwhich the individual is unaccustomed, can result in\nmuscle damage attributed to metabolic overload and/or\nmechanical strain [56]. Moreover, nitrogen balance can\nremain below baseline for several days following un-\naccustomed exercise [57]. The substantial training dis-\ntances of ultra-marathon are associated with high levels\nof mechanical stress. This is reinforced by empirical data\nshowing that whole-blood markers of muscle breakdown\n(e.g., creatine kinase, lactate dehydrogenase, and serum\ncreatine phosphokinase) were higher following ultra-\nmarathons when compared to marathons run at a rela-\ntively faster pace [58, 59]. Specifically, creatine kinase\nconcentrations of 274 ± 71 U·L−1 were observed post-\nmarathon, relative to 2983 ± 1716 U·L−1 following a 100\nkm race, and 4970 ± 2222 U·L−1 after a 308 km race [58].\nThese data suggest that race distance and/or duration\nmediate muscle damage more than race intensity, al-\nthou", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "nvironmental\nterrain typical of ultra-marathon also deserves consider-\nation in the training program. Downhill running (on\nmountainous or undulating paths) is associated with\ngreater peak flexion angles relative to level or uphill run-\nimpact-loading"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019_p0007_c0021", "source_registry_id": "src_6bd04c6f0cb9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0ca767fa705b3e47", "text_span_hash": "0ca767fa705b3e47", "source_file": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 21, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "or many years. An acute bout of pro-\nlonged exercise increases the rate of BCAA oxidation in\nskeletal muscle [71], suggesting that demands in ultra-\nmarathon runners may be greater, but chronic training\nsignificantly attenuates the absolute rate of B", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "performance. However, caution is advised against train-\ning chronically glycogen depleted, particularly during\nperiods of repeated high-intensity exercise or prior to\nEvidence statement (category B/C)\nDespite the use of ketogenic diets to facilitate a rapid meta-\nbolic shift towards greater fat oxidation, there is insufficient\nevidence to support the use of such diets in ultra-marathon\ntraining, and further research is warranted.\nProtein and muscle damage\nProlonged or strenuous exercise, particularly that to\nwhich the individual is unaccustomed, can result in\nmuscle damage attributed to metabolic overload and/or\nmechanical strain [56]. Moreover, nitrogen balance can\nremain below baseline for several days following un-\naccustomed exercise [57]. The substantial training dis-\ntances of ultra-marathon are associated with high levels\nof mechanical stress. This is reinforced by empirical data\nshowing that whole-blood markers of muscle breakdown\n(e.g., creatine kinase, lactate dehydrogenase, and serum\ncreatine phosphokinase) were higher following ultra-\nmarathons when compared to marathons run at a rela-\ntively faster pace [58, 59]. Specifically, creatine kinase\nconcentrations of 274 ± 71 U·L−1 were observed post-\nmarathon, relative to 2983 ± 1716 U·L−1 following a 100\nkm race, and 4970 ± 2222 U·L−1 after a 308 km race [58].\nThese data suggest that race distance and/or duration\nmediate muscle damage more than race intensity, al-\nthou", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "or many years. An acute bout of pro-\nlonged exercise increases the rate of BCAA oxidation in\nskeletal muscle [71], suggesting that demands in ultra-\nmarathon runners may be greater, but chronic training\nsignificantly attenuates the absolute rate of B"}
