ifically, creatine kinase\nconcentrations of 274 ± 71 U·L−1 were observed post-\nmarathon, relative to 2983 ± 1716 U·L−1 following a 100\nkm race, and 4970 ± 2222 U·L−1 after a 308 km race [58].\nThese data suggest that race distance and/or duration\nmediate muscle damage more than race intensity, al-\nthou", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "tein synthesis, influencing mRNA translation\nand the mTOR cell-signalling pathway [75]. Although\nthere are no existing studies on the efficacy of L-leucine\nspecifically for ultra-marathon runners, there are reports\nthat a 3–6 g daily dose of L-leucin"}
