l runners exhib-\nited a body weight loss of 0.3 and 1.7%, in cool and\nwarm conditions, respectively, even when consuming\nfluid at a rate of 1 L·h−1 [78]. Furthermore, abstaining\nfrom fluid resulted in an average dehydration of 3.3 and\n5.3%, respectively [78]. Notwithstanding the commonly-\nreported effe", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "longer\ntraining distances (marathon), high-level runners exhib-\nited a body weight loss of 0.3 and 1.7%, in cool and\nwarm conditions, respectively, even when consuming\nfluid at a rate of 1 L·h−1 [78]. Furthermore, abstaining\nfrom fluid resulted in an"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019_p0008_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_6bd04c6f0cb9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "117283ced7fc8b23", "text_span_hash": "117283ced7fc8b23", "source_file": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "overy\nbefore a subsequent session. It stands to reason that run-\nners should replenish the fluid volume lost in training;\nthis can be estimated via pre- to post-exercise body mass\nweighing. However, even in a hypohydrated state, the\nobligatory excret", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "resistance training [75]. Furthermore, L-leucine (5g)\nconsumed with a small amount of whey protein (6g)\nmay be as effective at stimulating muscle protein synthe-\nsis as a 25 g bolus of whey protein, although the latter\nmay be more practical [76].\nEvidence statement (category B/C)\nProtein intakes of ~ 1.6–2.1 g·kg−1·d−1 are sufficient to\noptimally simulate muscle protein synthesis, which will\nlikely support recovery from training. Intakes of up to\n2.5 g·kg−1·d−1 may be warranted during demanding\ntraining periods (when caloric requirements may be sub-\nEvidence statement (category B)\nAn intermediate protein feeding strategy of ~20 g every\n3 waking hours may provide an optimal strategy to\nstimulate muscle protein synthesis for ultra-marathon\nDaily hydration guidelines\nA typical training session for the ultra-marathon runner\nappears sufficient to cause substantial dehydration. Over\nthe half-marathon distance (13.1 miles), mean sweat\nlosses of ~1.4 L were observed in male runners and,\nwhen offset against fluid ingestion during exercise, re-\nsulted in net fluid losses of ~ 0.3 L [77]. Over longer\ntraining distances (marathon), high-level runners exhib-\nited a body weight loss of 0.3 and 1.7%, in cool and\nwarm conditions, respectively, even when consuming\nfluid at a rate of 1 L·h−1 [78]. Furthermore, abstaining\nfrom fluid resulted in an average dehydration of 3.3 and\n5.3%, respectively [78]. Notwithstanding the commonly-\nreported effe", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "overy\nbefore a subsequent session. It stands to reason that run-\nners should replenish the fluid volume lost in training;\nthis can be estimated via pre- to post-exercise body mass\nweighing. However, even in a hypohydrated state, the\nobligatory excret"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019_p0008_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_6bd04c6f0cb9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4ce5e2a49c0f4d09", "text_span_hash": "4ce5e2a49c0f4d09", "source_file": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "d state, the\nobligatory excretion of metabolic waste products allows\nfor continued fluid losses [80]. Consequently, a fluid vol-\nume greater than that lost in training is necessary to\nfully restore water balance. This notion has been demon-\nstrated e", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "resistance training [75]. Furthermore, L-leucine (5g)\nconsumed with a small amount of whey protein (6g)\nmay be as effective at stimulating muscle protein synthe-\nsis as a 25 g bolus of whey protein, although the latter\nmay be more practical [76].\nEvidence statement (category B/C)\nProtein intakes of ~ 1.6–2.1 g·kg−1·d−1 are sufficient to\noptimally simulate muscle protein synthesis, which will\nlikely support recovery from training. Intakes of up to\n2.5 g·kg−1·d−1 may be warranted during demanding\ntraining periods (when caloric requirements may be sub-\nEvidence statement (category B)\nAn intermediate protein feeding strategy of ~20 g every\n3 waking hours may provide an optimal strategy to\nstimulate muscle protein synthesis for ultra-marathon\nDaily hydration guidelines\nA typical training session for the ultra-marathon runner\nappears sufficient to cause substantial dehydration. Over\nthe half-marathon distance (13.1 miles), mean sweat\nlosses of ~1.4 L were observed in male runners and,\nwhen offset against fluid ingestion during exercise, re-\nsulted in net fluid losses of ~ 0.3 L [77]. Over longer\ntraining distances (marathon), high-level runners exhib-\nited a body weight loss of 0.3 and 1.7%, in cool and\nwarm conditions, respectively, even when consuming\nfluid at a rate of 1 L·h−1 [78]. Furthermore, abstaining\nfrom fluid resulted in an average dehydration of 3.3 and\n5.3%, respectively [78]. Notwithstanding the commonly-\nreported effe", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "d state, the\nobligatory excretion of metabolic waste products allows\nfor continued fluid losses [80]. Consequently, a fluid vol-\nume greater than that lost in training is necessary to\nfully restore water balance. This notion has been demon-\nstrated e"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019_p0008_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_6bd04c6f0cb9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "63fcd0161609a7e5", "text_span_hash": "63fcd0161609a7e5", "source_file": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "s of so-\nvolumes above this are not recommended. As these data\nindicate, plain water is not likely sufficient to restore\nfluid balance following training due to the consequent\ndecrease in plasma sodium concentration and osmolality\noutput decreases as", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "resistance training [75]. Furthermore, L-leucine (5g)\nconsumed with a small amount of whey protein (6g)\nmay be as effective at stimulating muscle protein synthe-\nsis as a 25 g bolus of whey protein, although the latter\nmay be more practical [76].\nEvidence statement (category B/C)\nProtein intakes of ~ 1.6–2.1 g·kg−1·d−1 are sufficient to\noptimally simulate muscle protein synthesis, which will\nlikely support recovery from training. Intakes of up to\n2.5 g·kg−1·d−1 may be warranted during demanding\ntraining periods (when caloric requirements may be sub-\nEvidence statement (category B)\nAn intermediate protein feeding strategy of ~20 g every\n3 waking hours may provide an optimal strategy to\nstimulate muscle protein synthesis for ultra-marathon\nDaily hydration guidelines\nA typical training session for the ultra-marathon runner\nappears sufficient to cause substantial dehydration. Over\nthe half-marathon distance (13.1 miles), mean sweat\nlosses of ~1.4 L were observed in male runners and,\nwhen offset against fluid ingestion during exercise, re-\nsulted in net fluid losses of ~ 0.3 L [77]. Over longer\ntraining distances (marathon), high-level runners exhib-\nited a body weight loss of 0.3 and 1.7%, in cool and\nwarm conditions, respectively, even when consuming\nfluid at a rate of 1 L·h−1 [78]. Furthermore, abstaining\nfrom fluid resulted in an average dehydration of 3.3 and\n5.3%, respectively [78]. Notwithstanding the commonly-\nreported effe", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "s of so-\nvolumes above this are not recommended. As these data\nindicate, plain water is not likely sufficient to restore\nfluid balance following training due to the consequent\ndecrease in plasma sodium concentration and osmolality\noutput decreases as"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019_p0009_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_6bd04c6f0cb9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2742850c521bce18", "text_span_hash": "2742850c521bce18", "source_file": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "nsider that a 50 kg athlete undertaking a 50\nmile (80 km) race at 8.0 km·h−1 (~ 10 h) will expend ~\n3460 Kcal. For the same event contested at the same\npace, a 70 kg athlete would expend ~ 4845 Kcal (an ap-\nproximate Kcal range of 346–484 Kcal·h−1).", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "ultra-marathon runner, hydration monitoring strategies\nare recommended (see Hydration monitoring strategies).\nIn addition, overuse of fluids that contain insufficient\nconcentrations of electrolytes (e.g., water or hypotonic\nsports drinks) may cause overhydration, decreased elec-\ntrolyte concentrations, an increased risk of dilutional\nhyponatremia, and/or failure of the renal system [89] in\nextreme cases. Ultra-marathon runners are, therefore,\ncautioned against excessive fluid intakes to placate pseu-\ndoscientific claims that high fluid volumes are needed to\n‘flush the kidneys’ or ‘remove toxins from the blood’.\nHydration monitoring strategies\nOnly an estimated 20% of endurance runners monitor\ntheir hydration status [90]. Although direct measures\nsuch as urine osmolality are rarely practical for most in-\ndividuals, there are several simple and accessible tools\nthat can be used to estimate hydration status. The urine\ncolor chart is the most common means of estimating hy-\ndration status in runners [90]. This simple technique\nwhereby ‘pale-straw’ would indicate that the individual\nis well-hydrated (assuming this is not measured post-\ningestion of a large bolus of fluid). The Venn diagram\nproposed by Cheuvront and Sawka [91] is a more\nlow-risk populations) which estimates hydration status\nby combining measures of nude body mass, thirst\nperception, and urine color.\nEvidence statement (category B/C)\nGeneral day-to-day hydration can, ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "nsider that a 50 kg athlete undertaking a 50\nmile (80 km) race at 8.0 km·h−1 (~ 10 h) will expend ~\n3460 Kcal. For the same event contested at the same\npace, a 70 kg athlete would expend ~ 4845 Kcal (an ap-\nproximate Kcal range of 346–484 Kcal·h−1)."}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019_p0009_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_6bd04c6f0cb9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b8ac5b0d0e33a22c", "text_span_hash": "b8ac5b0d0e33a22c", "source_file": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "l range of 346–484 Kcal·h−1). Second, a 50\nmarathon at an average pace of 6.5 km·h−1 may expend ~\n6922 Kcal in ~ 25 h, whereas at the same pace, a 70 kg ath-\nlete would likely expend ~ 9891 Kcal (range of 277–395\nKcal·h−1). These values are similar t", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "ultra-marathon runner, hydration monitoring strategies\nare recommended (see Hydration monitoring strategies).\nIn addition, overuse of fluids that contain insufficient\nconcentrations of electrolytes (e.g., water or hypotonic\nsports drinks) may cause overhydration, decreased elec-\ntrolyte concentrations, an increased risk of dilutional\nhyponatremia, and/or failure of the renal system [89] in\nextreme cases. Ultra-marathon runners are, therefore,\ncautioned against excessive fluid intakes to placate pseu-\ndoscientific claims that high fluid volumes are needed to\n‘flush the kidneys’ or ‘remove toxins from the blood’.\nHydration monitoring strategies\nOnly an estimated 20% of endurance runners monitor\ntheir hydration status [90]. Although direct measures\nsuch as urine osmolality are rarely practical for most in-\ndividuals, there are several simple and accessible tools\nthat can be used to estimate hydration status. The urine\ncolor chart is the most common means of estimating hy-\ndration status in runners [90]. This simple technique\nwhereby ‘pale-straw’ would indicate that the individual\nis well-hydrated (assuming this is not measured post-\ningestion of a large bolus of fluid). The Venn diagram\nproposed by Cheuvront and Sawka [91] is a more\nlow-risk populations) which estimates hydration status\nby combining measures of nude body mass, thirst\nperception, and urine color.\nEvidence statement (category B/C)\nGeneral day-to-day hydration can, ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "l range of 346–484 Kcal·h−1). Second, a 50\nmarathon at an average pace of 6.5 km·h−1 may expend ~\n6922 Kcal in ~ 25 h, whereas at the same pace, a 70 kg ath-\nlete would likely expend ~ 9891 Kcal (range of 277–395\nKcal·h−1). These values are similar t"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019_p0009_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_6bd04c6f0cb9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "89eed39267dc747c", "text_span_hash": "89eed39267dc747c", "source_file": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "vailability for ra-\ncing can be maximized by adhering to a contemporary\nloading strategy (i.e., ~10 g·kg−1·d−1) in the 48 h leading\ninto the event [42, 44], with care taken to avoid GI dis-\ntress. On race-day, runners are advised to consume a\nfamilia", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "ultra-marathon runner, hydration monitoring strategies\nare recommended (see Hydration monitoring strategies).\nIn addition, overuse of fluids that contain insufficient\nconcentrations of electrolytes (e.g., water or hypotonic\nsports drinks) may cause overhydration, decreased elec-\ntrolyte concentrations, an increased risk of dilutional\nhyponatremia, and/or failure of the renal system [89] in\nextreme cases. Ultra-marathon runners are, therefore,\ncautioned against excessive fluid intakes to placate pseu-\ndoscientific claims that high fluid volumes are needed to\n‘flush the kidneys’ or ‘remove toxins from the blood’.\nHydration monitoring strategies\nOnly an estimated 20% of endurance runners monitor\ntheir hydration status [90]. Although direct measures\nsuch as urine osmolality are rarely practical for most in-\ndividuals, there are several simple and accessible tools\nthat can be used to estimate hydration status. The urine\ncolor chart is the most common means of estimating hy-\ndration status in runners [90]. This simple technique\nwhereby ‘pale-straw’ would indicate that the individual\nis well-hydrated (assuming this is not measured post-\ningestion of a large bolus of fluid). The Venn diagram\nproposed by Cheuvront and Sawka [91] is a more\nlow-risk populations) which estimates hydration status\nby combining measures of nude body mass, thirst\nperception, and urine color.\nEvidence statement (category B/C)\nGeneral day-to-day hydration can, ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "vailability for ra-\ncing can be maximized by adhering to a contemporary\nloading strategy (i.e., ~10 g·kg−1·d−1) in the 48 h leading\ninto the event [42, 44], with care taken to avoid GI dis-\ntress. On race-day, runners are advised to consume a\nfamilia"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019_p0010_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_6bd04c6f0cb9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f1bce0f52ad73607", "text_span_hash": "f1bce0f52ad73607", "source_file": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ciated with acute\ncognitive decline; this, in turn, can compromise athlete\nsafety on ultra-marathon courses of technical terrain or\nthose requiring navigation. Nevertheless, the absolute\nCHO requirements for ultra-marathon racing are un-\nclear. There", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Kcal·h−1 [96]. Greater caloric intakes may, therefore, be\nnecessary for longer races to enable performance.\nBased on previous estimates of energy expenditure\nduring running, and the above-mentioned research, the\nISSN recommends a caloric intake of ~ 150–300 Kcal·h−\n1 for race distances up to and including 50 miles (~ 81\nkm) during which any caloric deficits may be better tol-\nerated. By contrast, in longer races when the magnitude\nof caloric deficits is greater and less likely to be well-\ntolerated, higher intakes of ~ 200–400 Kcal·h−1 are sug-\ngested. Where GI distress is an issue, transient reduc-\ntions in energy intake to the lower-end of this range are\nreasonable, congruent with a reduction in race pace.\nHowever, persistent calorie intakes of < 200 Kcal·h−1 are\nnot recommended, and when nausea precludes this rate\nof intake, a degree of perseverance/stubbornness with\nrespect to feeding (within tolerance levels) may be re-\nquired. This may be particularly pertinent in the latter\nstages of a race in order to minimize the risk of\nhypoglycaemia which can result in race non-completion,\nand reinforces the importance of progressive gut training\nduring the preparation phase [97].\nCarbohydrate versus fat intake\nThe mechanistic link between glycogen depletion in\nskeletal muscle and liver, and a subsequent early-onset\nfatigue during prolonged exercise was made in the 1960s\n[98]. In addition to negatively impacting endurance per-\nforma", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ciated with acute\ncognitive decline; this, in turn, can compromise athlete\nsafety on ultra-marathon courses of technical terrain or\nthose requiring navigation. Nevertheless, the absolute\nCHO requirements for ultra-marathon racing are un-\nclear. There"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019_p0010_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_6bd04c6f0cb9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "24d1f8b2aa2c7c75", "text_span_hash": "24d1f8b2aa2c7c75", "source_file": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "on racing are un-\nclear. There is certainly a lower rate of CHO utilization\nduring ultra-marathon relative to marathon. Laboratory\ndata demonstrate that respiratory exchange ratio (RER)\ngradually decreases until the 8th hour of a 24 h treadmill\nrun,", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Kcal·h−1 [96]. Greater caloric intakes may, therefore, be\nnecessary for longer races to enable performance.\nBased on previous estimates of energy expenditure\nduring running, and the above-mentioned research, the\nISSN recommends a caloric intake of ~ 150–300 Kcal·h−\n1 for race distances up to and including 50 miles (~ 81\nkm) during which any caloric deficits may be better tol-\nerated. By contrast, in longer races when the magnitude\nof caloric deficits is greater and less likely to be well-\ntolerated, higher intakes of ~ 200–400 Kcal·h−1 are sug-\ngested. Where GI distress is an issue, transient reduc-\ntions in energy intake to the lower-end of this range are\nreasonable, congruent with a reduction in race pace.\nHowever, persistent calorie intakes of < 200 Kcal·h−1 are\nnot recommended, and when nausea precludes this rate\nof intake, a degree of perseverance/stubbornness with\nrespect to feeding (within tolerance levels) may be re-\nquired. This may be particularly pertinent in the latter\nstages of a race in order to minimize the risk of\nhypoglycaemia which can result in race non-completion,\nand reinforces the importance of progressive gut training\nduring the preparation phase [97].\nCarbohydrate versus fat intake\nThe mechanistic link between glycogen depletion in\nskeletal muscle and liver, and a subsequent early-onset\nfatigue during prolonged exercise was made in the 1960s\n[98]. In addition to negatively impacting endurance per-\nforma", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "on racing are un-\nclear. There is certainly a lower rate of CHO utilization\nduring ultra-marathon relative to marathon. Laboratory\ndata demonstrate that respiratory exchange ratio (RER)\ngradually decreases until the 8th hour of a 24 h treadmill\nrun,"}
