ut training\nduring the preparation phase [97].\nCarbohydrate versus fat intake\nThe mechanistic link between glycogen depletion in\nskeletal muscle and liver, and a subsequent early-onset\nfatigue during prolonged exercise was made in the 1960s\n[98]. In addition to negatively impacting endurance per-\nforma", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "compensatory increase in\nfat oxidation, with rates of 0.2–0.5 g·min−1 typically ob-\nserved during endurance exercise [100], and higher\nvalues of 1.0–1.5 g·min−1 reported in a single subject\nafter 6 h of running [101, 102]. The prolonged durations\nand"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019_p0010_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_6bd04c6f0cb9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9d7d2bece1a89e2c", "text_span_hash": "9d7d2bece1a89e2c", "source_file": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "]. The prolonged durations\nand slower relative running speeds that characterize\nultra-marathon appear, therefore, to permit increased\nrates of fat oxidation for adenosine triphosphate (ATP)\nre-synthesis [100]. However, there is still a risk of glyco-", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Kcal·h−1 [96]. Greater caloric intakes may, therefore, be\nnecessary for longer races to enable performance.\nBased on previous estimates of energy expenditure\nduring running, and the above-mentioned research, the\nISSN recommends a caloric intake of ~ 150–300 Kcal·h−\n1 for race distances up to and including 50 miles (~ 81\nkm) during which any caloric deficits may be better tol-\nerated. By contrast, in longer races when the magnitude\nof caloric deficits is greater and less likely to be well-\ntolerated, higher intakes of ~ 200–400 Kcal·h−1 are sug-\ngested. Where GI distress is an issue, transient reduc-\ntions in energy intake to the lower-end of this range are\nreasonable, congruent with a reduction in race pace.\nHowever, persistent calorie intakes of < 200 Kcal·h−1 are\nnot recommended, and when nausea precludes this rate\nof intake, a degree of perseverance/stubbornness with\nrespect to feeding (within tolerance levels) may be re-\nquired. This may be particularly pertinent in the latter\nstages of a race in order to minimize the risk of\nhypoglycaemia which can result in race non-completion,\nand reinforces the importance of progressive gut training\nduring the preparation phase [97].\nCarbohydrate versus fat intake\nThe mechanistic link between glycogen depletion in\nskeletal muscle and liver, and a subsequent early-onset\nfatigue during prolonged exercise was made in the 1960s\n[98]. In addition to negatively impacting endurance per-\nforma", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "]. The prolonged durations\nand slower relative running speeds that characterize\nultra-marathon appear, therefore, to permit increased\nrates of fat oxidation for adenosine triphosphate (ATP)\nre-synthesis [100]. However, there is still a risk of glyco-"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019_p0010_c0016", "source_registry_id": "src_6bd04c6f0cb9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0f976d912862b49a", "text_span_hash": "0f976d912862b49a", "source_file": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 16, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "may\nbe typical for slower finishers (31 ± 9 g·h−1 [103];), both\nof which were lower than faster finishers (44 ± 33 g·h−1);\nthese data reinforce the notion of broad variance in the\nstrategy used pending race pace or duration. Over the\nsame distance,", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Kcal·h−1 [96]. Greater caloric intakes may, therefore, be\nnecessary for longer races to enable performance.\nBased on previous estimates of energy expenditure\nduring running, and the above-mentioned research, the\nISSN recommends a caloric intake of ~ 150–300 Kcal·h−\n1 for race distances up to and including 50 miles (~ 81\nkm) during which any caloric deficits may be better tol-\nerated. By contrast, in longer races when the magnitude\nof caloric deficits is greater and less likely to be well-\ntolerated, higher intakes of ~ 200–400 Kcal·h−1 are sug-\ngested. Where GI distress is an issue, transient reduc-\ntions in energy intake to the lower-end of this range are\nreasonable, congruent with a reduction in race pace.\nHowever, persistent calorie intakes of < 200 Kcal·h−1 are\nnot recommended, and when nausea precludes this rate\nof intake, a degree of perseverance/stubbornness with\nrespect to feeding (within tolerance levels) may be re-\nquired. This may be particularly pertinent in the latter\nstages of a race in order to minimize the risk of\nhypoglycaemia which can result in race non-completion,\nand reinforces the importance of progressive gut training\nduring the preparation phase [97].\nCarbohydrate versus fat intake\nThe mechanistic link between glycogen depletion in\nskeletal muscle and liver, and a subsequent early-onset\nfatigue during prolonged exercise was made in the 1960s\n[98]. In addition to negatively impacting endurance per-\nforma", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "may\nbe typical for slower finishers (31 ± 9 g·h−1 [103];), both\nof which were lower than faster finishers (44 ± 33 g·h−1);\nthese data reinforce the notion of broad variance in the\nstrategy used pending race pace or duration. Over the\nsame distance,"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019_p0010_c0018", "source_registry_id": "src_6bd04c6f0cb9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1a786bb1365e327b", "text_span_hash": "1a786bb1365e327b", "source_file": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 18, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "0 g·h−1)\nduring single-stage races [96]. Although current litera-\nture advocates CHO ingestion rates up to ~ 90 g·h−1 for\nfructose [104], such high rates of ingestion may be un-\nrealistic for longer ultra-marathon races (> 6 h). More-\nmalabsorption a", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Kcal·h−1 [96]. Greater caloric intakes may, therefore, be\nnecessary for longer races to enable performance.\nBased on previous estimates of energy expenditure\nduring running, and the above-mentioned research, the\nISSN recommends a caloric intake of ~ 150–300 Kcal·h−\n1 for race distances up to and including 50 miles (~ 81\nkm) during which any caloric deficits may be better tol-\nerated. By contrast, in longer races when the magnitude\nof caloric deficits is greater and less likely to be well-\ntolerated, higher intakes of ~ 200–400 Kcal·h−1 are sug-\ngested. Where GI distress is an issue, transient reduc-\ntions in energy intake to the lower-end of this range are\nreasonable, congruent with a reduction in race pace.\nHowever, persistent calorie intakes of < 200 Kcal·h−1 are\nnot recommended, and when nausea precludes this rate\nof intake, a degree of perseverance/stubbornness with\nrespect to feeding (within tolerance levels) may be re-\nquired. This may be particularly pertinent in the latter\nstages of a race in order to minimize the risk of\nhypoglycaemia which can result in race non-completion,\nand reinforces the importance of progressive gut training\nduring the preparation phase [97].\nCarbohydrate versus fat intake\nThe mechanistic link between glycogen depletion in\nskeletal muscle and liver, and a subsequent early-onset\nfatigue during prolonged exercise was made in the 1960s\n[98]. In addition to negatively impacting endurance per-\nforma", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "0 g·h−1)\nduring single-stage races [96]. Although current litera-\nture advocates CHO ingestion rates up to ~ 90 g·h−1 for\nfructose [104], such high rates of ingestion may be un-\nrealistic for longer ultra-marathon races (> 6 h). More-\nmalabsorption a"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019_p0010_c0021", "source_registry_id": "src_6bd04c6f0cb9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0431b21d47a82749", "text_span_hash": "0431b21d47a82749", "source_file": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 21, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "finishers consumed a\ntotal of 98.1 ± 53.0 g of fat, which was approximately 5-\nfold greater than that of non-finishers (19.4 ± 21.1 g);\nmoreover, when normalized for body mass and running\nvelocity, this equated to a rate of fat ingestion that was\nthr", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Kcal·h−1 [96]. Greater caloric intakes may, therefore, be\nnecessary for longer races to enable performance.\nBased on previous estimates of energy expenditure\nduring running, and the above-mentioned research, the\nISSN recommends a caloric intake of ~ 150–300 Kcal·h−\n1 for race distances up to and including 50 miles (~ 81\nkm) during which any caloric deficits may be better tol-\nerated. By contrast, in longer races when the magnitude\nof caloric deficits is greater and less likely to be well-\ntolerated, higher intakes of ~ 200–400 Kcal·h−1 are sug-\ngested. Where GI distress is an issue, transient reduc-\ntions in energy intake to the lower-end of this range are\nreasonable, congruent with a reduction in race pace.\nHowever, persistent calorie intakes of < 200 Kcal·h−1 are\nnot recommended, and when nausea precludes this rate\nof intake, a degree of perseverance/stubbornness with\nrespect to feeding (within tolerance levels) may be re-\nquired. This may be particularly pertinent in the latter\nstages of a race in order to minimize the risk of\nhypoglycaemia which can result in race non-completion,\nand reinforces the importance of progressive gut training\nduring the preparation phase [97].\nCarbohydrate versus fat intake\nThe mechanistic link between glycogen depletion in\nskeletal muscle and liver, and a subsequent early-onset\nfatigue during prolonged exercise was made in the 1960s\n[98]. In addition to negatively impacting endurance per-\nforma", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "finishers consumed a\ntotal of 98.1 ± 53.0 g of fat, which was approximately 5-\nfold greater than that of non-finishers (19.4 ± 21.1 g);\nmoreover, when normalized for body mass and running\nvelocity, this equated to a rate of fat ingestion that was\nthr"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019_p0010_c0022", "source_registry_id": "src_6bd04c6f0cb9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "554d09280d4ca7ea", "text_span_hash": "554d09280d4ca7ea", "source_file": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 22, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "of fat ingestion that was\nthree times greater in finishers (0.06 ± 0.03 versus 0.02 ±\n0.02 g·kg−1·h−1 [15]). Collectively, these data suggest that\nsuccessful completion of ultra-marathon likely requires a\nhigher degree of tolerance to both CHO and f", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Kcal·h−1 [96]. Greater caloric intakes may, therefore, be\nnecessary for longer races to enable performance.\nBased on previous estimates of energy expenditure\nduring running, and the above-mentioned research, the\nISSN recommends a caloric intake of ~ 150–300 Kcal·h−\n1 for race distances up to and including 50 miles (~ 81\nkm) during which any caloric deficits may be better tol-\nerated. By contrast, in longer races when the magnitude\nof caloric deficits is greater and less likely to be well-\ntolerated, higher intakes of ~ 200–400 Kcal·h−1 are sug-\ngested. Where GI distress is an issue, transient reduc-\ntions in energy intake to the lower-end of this range are\nreasonable, congruent with a reduction in race pace.\nHowever, persistent calorie intakes of < 200 Kcal·h−1 are\nnot recommended, and when nausea precludes this rate\nof intake, a degree of perseverance/stubbornness with\nrespect to feeding (within tolerance levels) may be re-\nquired. This may be particularly pertinent in the latter\nstages of a race in order to minimize the risk of\nhypoglycaemia which can result in race non-completion,\nand reinforces the importance of progressive gut training\nduring the preparation phase [97].\nCarbohydrate versus fat intake\nThe mechanistic link between glycogen depletion in\nskeletal muscle and liver, and a subsequent early-onset\nfatigue during prolonged exercise was made in the 1960s\n[98]. In addition to negatively impacting endurance per-\nforma", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "of fat ingestion that was\nthree times greater in finishers (0.06 ± 0.03 versus 0.02 ±\n0.02 g·kg−1·h−1 [15]). Collectively, these data suggest that\nsuccessful completion of ultra-marathon likely requires a\nhigher degree of tolerance to both CHO and f"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019_p0011_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_6bd04c6f0cb9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "050f802be3379d05", "text_span_hash": "050f802be3379d05", "source_file": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "hew nuts (50 g)\nCheese bites (42 g / 2 portions)\nSalted potato chips (28 g / 16 chips)\nGreen olives, medium (50 g / 15 olives)\naExamples taken from a survey of recreational to elite ultra-marathon runners (n = 12). bBased on typical serving sizes. MC", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "energy metabolism under race conditions and athletes,\ntherefore, prioritize the ingestion of CHO and fat; and ii)\nstrategic ingestion of protein is difficult when runners\nrely solely on fixed checkpoints for the supply of energy/\nfluid and are, therefore, at the mercy of race organizers\nto supply foods with adequate protein. Nevertheless, it is\nplausible that protein ingested during an ultra-marathon\nwould mitigate the ill-effects of muscle damage and/or\npositively influence energy metabolism. Indeed, finishers\nof a 100-mile (161 km) race had a significantly greater\nprotein intake relative to non-finishers (131.2 ± 79.0\nversus 43.0 ± 56.7 g) and, when expressed as a relative\nratio per hour, race finishers consumed twice the quan-\ntity (0.08 versus 0.04 g·kg−1·h−1) [15]. Gastrointestinal\ndistress and a lack of appetite in non-finishers may ex-\nplain their lower overall intake.\nProtein is likely an important component for pro-\nlonged endurance exercise because of the substantial\nproteolysis and muscle damage that can manifest before\nthe conclusion of a race. In controlled studies, however,\nthere are conflicting results. Protein co-ingested with\nCHO during 6 h of running and cycling improved net\nTable 4 Example foods consumed by athletesa during single-stage ultra-marathon (35–100 miles, 56–161 km)\nFood suggestion/serveb\nSports drinks (50 g powdered serve)\nSports drinks (50 g) with added electrolytes (1 tablet)\nEnergy gels with 3", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "hew nuts (50 g)\nCheese bites (42 g / 2 portions)\nSalted potato chips (28 g / 16 chips)\nGreen olives, medium (50 g / 15 olives)\naExamples taken from a survey of recreational to elite ultra-marathon runners (n = 12). bBased on typical serving sizes. MC"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019_p0012_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_6bd04c6f0cb9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "986da88f774eb163", "text_span_hash": "986da88f774eb163", "source_file": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "nd\nfurther studies specific to ultra-marathon running are\nunderpin any beneficial effects.\nA key consideration for the ultra-marathon runner\nshould be the palatability of food (and fluid), particularly\nin longer races. Moreover, tastes and food prefe", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "protein balance to a greater extent than the ingestion of\nCHO alone [106]. By contrast, when ultra-marathon\nrunners were supplemented with 52.5 g of amino acids\nor a placebo prior to, and during, a 62-mile (100 km)\nrace, there were no significant differences in markers of\nmuscle damage or overall performance [107]. As such,\nthe equivocal findings may result from the co-ingestion\nof protein and CHO, and/or differences in the exercise\nmodality used between studies. Irrespective, nutrition\nstrategies should be implemented that mitigate the\nconsequences of prolonged protein abstinence, and a\nbalance of macronutrients should be consumed.\nA degree of self-sufficiency when racing may provide\nan opportunity for runners to follow a more bespoke nu-\ntrition strategy to better satisfy individual protein needs\n(see Table 4 for example foods). Protein-rich foods can\nbe carried in running belts and/or backpacks and con-\nsumed ad libitum, but race organizers are also encour-\naged to provide high-protein options at checkpoints.\nRunners who are concerned that consuming calories\nfrom protein might compromise energy availability (i.e.,\nby necessitating fewer calories from CHO and fat) might\nconsider BCAA supplements (as liquid or tablets) as an\nalternative, particularly when the availability of protein-\nrich foods is limited. Where possible, ultra-marathon\nrunners should strive to meet the typical dietary guide-\nlines by consuming ~ 20–30 g of p", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "nd\nfurther studies specific to ultra-marathon running are\nunderpin any beneficial effects.\nA key consideration for the ultra-marathon runner\nshould be the palatability of food (and fluid), particularly\nin longer races. Moreover, tastes and food prefe"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019_p0012_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_6bd04c6f0cb9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0958b66cbe89779b", "text_span_hash": "0958b66cbe89779b", "source_file": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "oreover, tastes and food preferences\nwill likely change throughout the course of the race\n[113]. There are several reports of runners complaining\nof the unpalatability of sweet foods, particularly energy\ngels and sports drinks, both in the heat [114]", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "protein balance to a greater extent than the ingestion of\nCHO alone [106]. By contrast, when ultra-marathon\nrunners were supplemented with 52.5 g of amino acids\nor a placebo prior to, and during, a 62-mile (100 km)\nrace, there were no significant differences in markers of\nmuscle damage or overall performance [107]. As such,\nthe equivocal findings may result from the co-ingestion\nof protein and CHO, and/or differences in the exercise\nmodality used between studies. Irrespective, nutrition\nstrategies should be implemented that mitigate the\nconsequences of prolonged protein abstinence, and a\nbalance of macronutrients should be consumed.\nA degree of self-sufficiency when racing may provide\nan opportunity for runners to follow a more bespoke nu-\ntrition strategy to better satisfy individual protein needs\n(see Table 4 for example foods). Protein-rich foods can\nbe carried in running belts and/or backpacks and con-\nsumed ad libitum, but race organizers are also encour-\naged to provide high-protein options at checkpoints.\nRunners who are concerned that consuming calories\nfrom protein might compromise energy availability (i.e.,\nby necessitating fewer calories from CHO and fat) might\nconsider BCAA supplements (as liquid or tablets) as an\nalternative, particularly when the availability of protein-\nrich foods is limited. Where possible, ultra-marathon\nrunners should strive to meet the typical dietary guide-\nlines by consuming ~ 20–30 g of p", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "oreover, tastes and food preferences\nwill likely change throughout the course of the race\n[113]. There are several reports of runners complaining\nof the unpalatability of sweet foods, particularly energy\ngels and sports drinks, both in the heat [114]"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019_p0012_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_6bd04c6f0cb9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "d9a5763b3041d365", "text_span_hash": "d9a5763b3041d365", "source_file": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "drinks, both in the heat [114] and in\nultra-marathons > 60 miles contested in thermoneutral\nenvironments [115, 116]. These data indicate that the\naversion to simple CHO is not exclusively dependent on\nambient conditions but is also influenced by race", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "protein balance to a greater extent than the ingestion of\nCHO alone [106]. By contrast, when ultra-marathon\nrunners were supplemented with 52.5 g of amino acids\nor a placebo prior to, and during, a 62-mile (100 km)\nrace, there were no significant differences in markers of\nmuscle damage or overall performance [107]. As such,\nthe equivocal findings may result from the co-ingestion\nof protein and CHO, and/or differences in the exercise\nmodality used between studies. Irrespective, nutrition\nstrategies should be implemented that mitigate the\nconsequences of prolonged protein abstinence, and a\nbalance of macronutrients should be consumed.\nA degree of self-sufficiency when racing may provide\nan opportunity for runners to follow a more bespoke nu-\ntrition strategy to better satisfy individual protein needs\n(see Table 4 for example foods). Protein-rich foods can\nbe carried in running belts and/or backpacks and con-\nsumed ad libitum, but race organizers are also encour-\naged to provide high-protein options at checkpoints.\nRunners who are concerned that consuming calories\nfrom protein might compromise energy availability (i.e.,\nby necessitating fewer calories from CHO and fat) might\nconsider BCAA supplements (as liquid or tablets) as an\nalternative, particularly when the availability of protein-\nrich foods is limited. Where possible, ultra-marathon\nrunners should strive to meet the typical dietary guide-\nlines by consuming ~ 20–30 g of p", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "drinks, both in the heat [114] and in\nultra-marathons > 60 miles contested in thermoneutral\nenvironments [115, 116]. These data indicate that the\naversion to simple CHO is not exclusively dependent on\nambient conditions but is also influenced by race"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019_p0012_c0017", "source_registry_id": "src_6bd04c6f0cb9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "95451aa1486eb206", "text_span_hash": "95451aa1486eb206", "source_file": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 17, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "consistent chemical balance in the\nbody [115]. In the aforementioned studies, runners\ntended to exhibit a penchant for savory food (i.e., fla-\nvoursome, non-sweet, and containing greater relative\namounts of fat and salt) in the latter stages of ultra", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "protein balance to a greater extent than the ingestion of\nCHO alone [106]. By contrast, when ultra-marathon\nrunners were supplemented with 52.5 g of amino acids\nor a placebo prior to, and during, a 62-mile (100 km)\nrace, there were no significant differences in markers of\nmuscle damage or overall performance [107]. As such,\nthe equivocal findings may result from the co-ingestion\nof protein and CHO, and/or differences in the exercise\nmodality used between studies. Irrespective, nutrition\nstrategies should be implemented that mitigate the\nconsequences of prolonged protein abstinence, and a\nbalance of macronutrients should be consumed.\nA degree of self-sufficiency when racing may provide\nan opportunity for runners to follow a more bespoke nu-\ntrition strategy to better satisfy individual protein needs\n(see Table 4 for example foods). Protein-rich foods can\nbe carried in running belts and/or backpacks and con-\nsumed ad libitum, but race organizers are also encour-\naged to provide high-protein options at checkpoints.\nRunners who are concerned that consuming calories\nfrom protein might compromise energy availability (i.e.,\nby necessitating fewer calories from CHO and fat) might\nconsider BCAA supplements (as liquid or tablets) as an\nalternative, particularly when the availability of protein-\nrich foods is limited. Where possible, ultra-marathon\nrunners should strive to meet the typical dietary guide-\nlines by consuming ~ 20–30 g of p", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "consistent chemical balance in the\nbody [115]. In the aforementioned studies, runners\ntended to exhibit a penchant for savory food (i.e., fla-\nvoursome, non-sweet, and containing greater relative\namounts of fat and salt) in the latter stages of ultra"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019_p0013_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_6bd04c6f0cb9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "29ec2d1179146eff", "text_span_hash": "29ec2d1179146eff", "source_file": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ing according to an in-\ndividualized schedule [122]. Moreover, finishers tend to\nconsume fluid at a greater rate than non-finishers [92].\nMean fluid ingestion rates of ~ 0.5 L·h−1 have been ob-\nserved during a road ultra-marathon of 62 miles (100\nkm)", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Evidence statement (category C)\nCalories should be consumed from a combination of\never, foods with greater fat content may be preferred in\nEvidence statement (category D)\nAs race duration increases, runners tend to favor savory\nfoods, likely reflecting energy and electrolyte insufficiencies.\nOffsetting dehydration\nThermoregulation during exercise is largely dependent\non the mammalian sweat response to evoke evaporative\nheat loss. Insufficient fluid replacement, therefore, re-\nsults in a net loss of body water, the main consequence\nof which is dehydration-induced cardiovascular drift; i.e.,\na reduction in plasma volume and a necessary increase\nin heart rate to maintain cardiac output [117]. The result\nis a diminished exercise capacity [118], and an increased\nrisk of heat illness and rhabdomyolysis [118]. Dehydra-\ntion may also diminish cognitive performance [11, 118]\nand increase perceived exertion [119]. All of the above\nmay compromise performance and exacerbate the risk\nof injury and/or illness during ultra-marathon, particu-\nlarly in arduous races, those requiring navigation, or\nthose contested on technical terrain. Although dehydra-\ntion can result from running in cold conditions due to a\nblunting of the thirst response, dehydration is more of a\nrisk during races in hot and/or humid conditions when\nsweat rates are increased [120]. Moreover, consideration\nshould be given to whether hot ambient conditions are\ndry or wet since", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ing according to an in-\ndividualized schedule [122]. Moreover, finishers tend to\nconsume fluid at a greater rate than non-finishers [92].\nMean fluid ingestion rates of ~ 0.5 L·h−1 have been ob-\nserved during a road ultra-marathon of 62 miles (100\nkm)"}
{"chunk_id": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019_p0013_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_6bd04c6f0cb9", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f77cf81b4c8a1d79", "text_span_hash": "f77cf81b4c8a1d79", "source_file": "nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\nutrition_ultramarathon_issn_2019.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "-marathon of 62 miles (100\nkm), with a broad range in the total volumes consumed\n(3.3–11.1 L) [123]. Slightly higher ingestion rates of ~\n0.75 L·h−1 have been reported in races of 100 miles (161\nkm [92]). Collectively, the available data suggest that", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Evidence statement (category C)\nCalories should be consumed from a combination of\never, foods with greater fat content may be preferred in\nEvidence statement (category D)\nAs race duration increases, runners tend to favor savory\nfoods, likely reflecting energy and electrolyte insufficiencies.\nOffsetting dehydration\nThermoregulation during exercise is largely dependent\non the mammalian sweat response to evoke evaporative\nheat loss. Insufficient fluid replacement, therefore, re-\nsults in a net loss of body water, the main consequence\nof which is dehydration-induced cardiovascular drift; i.e.,\na reduction in plasma volume and a necessary increase\nin heart rate to maintain cardiac output [117]. The result\nis a diminished exercise capacity [118], and an increased\nrisk of heat illness and rhabdomyolysis [118]. Dehydra-\ntion may also diminish cognitive performance [11, 118]\nand increase perceived exertion [119]. All of the above\nmay compromise performance and exacerbate the risk\nof injury and/or illness during ultra-marathon, particu-\nlarly in arduous races, those requiring navigation, or\nthose contested on technical terrain. Although dehydra-\ntion can result from running in cold conditions due to a\nblunting of the thirst response, dehydration is more of a\nrisk during races in hot and/or humid conditions when\nsweat rates are increased [120]. Moreover, consideration\nshould be given to whether hot ambient conditions are\ndry or wet since", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "-marathon of 62 miles (100\nkm), with a broad range in the total volumes consumed\n(3.3–11.1 L) [123]. Slightly higher ingestion rates of ~\n0.75 L·h−1 have been reported in races of 100 miles (161\nkm [92]). Collectively, the available data suggest that"}
