{"chunk_id": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024_p0005_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_8359a872ca35", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "85249b7934bce3f4", "text_span_hash": "85249b7934bce3f4", "source_file": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Descriptive results and characteristics of included studies.\n17,465 finishers in 2017 Vienna City\nAverage speed from each\nMarathon runners showed greater variability in pacing than half-\nmarathon runners.Women showed no differences in pace\nvariabilit", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Descriptive results and characteristics of included studies.\n17,465 finishers in 2017 Vienna City\nAverage speed from each\nMarathon runners showed greater variability in pacing than half-\nmarathon runners.Women showed no differences in pace\nvariability in regard to the age group.Younger half-marathon men\nand women showed the fastest end spurt compared to older age\ngroups and marathon runners.\n2 runners who broke the World Record\ntime for combined father and son in 2019\nphysiological profiles,\nAverage speed from each\nFather and son who broke the World record time for combined\nfather and son marathon had a similar level of performance, but\ntheir physiological profiles and pacing strategies during the\nmarathon were different.\n2 experienced ultra-marathon runners\nFluctuations in blood glucose levels are not related to pacing\n2 runners who create the most recent\nworld-record marathon runs\nDeviations in average\nHeadwind was a significant factor in running speed variability for\nboth runners, with Runner 2 targeting the (optimal) parallel\nvariation principle, whilst Runner 1 did not. After adjusting for\nthese responses, neither runner followed the (optimal) ‘even’\npower pacing principle.\nMu˜noz-Pe′rez et al.\n2295 runners in 2017 Berlin marathon\nAverage speed from each\nElite and trained male marathoners used similar pacing\nstrategies, whereas female athletes’ pacing strategies varied\nsignificantly across levels; athletes mainly used an ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Descriptive results and characteristics of included studies.\n17,465 finishers in 2017 Vienna City\nAverage speed from each\nMarathon runners showed greater variability in pacing than half-\nmarathon runners.Women showed no differences in pace\nvariabilit"}
{"chunk_id": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024_p0005_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_8359a872ca35", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "695cf24e7c133210", "text_span_hash": "695cf24e7c133210", "source_file": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "differences in pace\nvariability in regard to the age group.Younger half-marathon men\nand women showed the fastest end spurt compared to older age\ngroups and marathon runners.\n2 runners who broke the World Record\ntime for combined father and son in 20", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Descriptive results and characteristics of included studies.\n17,465 finishers in 2017 Vienna City\nAverage speed from each\nMarathon runners showed greater variability in pacing than half-\nmarathon runners.Women showed no differences in pace\nvariability in regard to the age group.Younger half-marathon men\nand women showed the fastest end spurt compared to older age\ngroups and marathon runners.\n2 runners who broke the World Record\ntime for combined father and son in 2019\nphysiological profiles,\nAverage speed from each\nFather and son who broke the World record time for combined\nfather and son marathon had a similar level of performance, but\ntheir physiological profiles and pacing strategies during the\nmarathon were different.\n2 experienced ultra-marathon runners\nFluctuations in blood glucose levels are not related to pacing\n2 runners who create the most recent\nworld-record marathon runs\nDeviations in average\nHeadwind was a significant factor in running speed variability for\nboth runners, with Runner 2 targeting the (optimal) parallel\nvariation principle, whilst Runner 1 did not. After adjusting for\nthese responses, neither runner followed the (optimal) ‘even’\npower pacing principle.\nMu˜noz-Pe′rez et al.\n2295 runners in 2017 Berlin marathon\nAverage speed from each\nElite and trained male marathoners used similar pacing\nstrategies, whereas female athletes’ pacing strategies varied\nsignificantly across levels; athletes mainly used an ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "differences in pace\nvariability in regard to the age group.Younger half-marathon men\nand women showed the fastest end spurt compared to older age\ngroups and marathon runners.\n2 runners who broke the World Record\ntime for combined father and son in 20"}
{"chunk_id": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024_p0005_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_8359a872ca35", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "15d98f6e729e194b", "text_span_hash": "15d98f6e729e194b", "source_file": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "combined father and son in 2019\nphysiological profiles,\nAverage speed from each\nFather and son who broke the World record time for combined\nfather and son marathon had a similar level of performance, but\ntheir physiological profiles and pacing strat", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Descriptive results and characteristics of included studies.\n17,465 finishers in 2017 Vienna City\nAverage speed from each\nMarathon runners showed greater variability in pacing than half-\nmarathon runners.Women showed no differences in pace\nvariability in regard to the age group.Younger half-marathon men\nand women showed the fastest end spurt compared to older age\ngroups and marathon runners.\n2 runners who broke the World Record\ntime for combined father and son in 2019\nphysiological profiles,\nAverage speed from each\nFather and son who broke the World record time for combined\nfather and son marathon had a similar level of performance, but\ntheir physiological profiles and pacing strategies during the\nmarathon were different.\n2 experienced ultra-marathon runners\nFluctuations in blood glucose levels are not related to pacing\n2 runners who create the most recent\nworld-record marathon runs\nDeviations in average\nHeadwind was a significant factor in running speed variability for\nboth runners, with Runner 2 targeting the (optimal) parallel\nvariation principle, whilst Runner 1 did not. After adjusting for\nthese responses, neither runner followed the (optimal) ‘even’\npower pacing principle.\nMu˜noz-Pe′rez et al.\n2295 runners in 2017 Berlin marathon\nAverage speed from each\nElite and trained male marathoners used similar pacing\nstrategies, whereas female athletes’ pacing strategies varied\nsignificantly across levels; athletes mainly used an ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "combined father and son in 2019\nphysiological profiles,\nAverage speed from each\nFather and son who broke the World record time for combined\nfather and son marathon had a similar level of performance, but\ntheir physiological profiles and pacing strat"}
