 females.\n190,228 finishers in New York City\nmarathon from 2006 to 2011\nAverage speed from each\nAlthough all runners developed a positive pace profile, a lower\nvariability of speed through the race was found in the top runners\ncompared with the less successful runners.\n31,762 masters athletes from the\n2015 TSC New York City Marathon\nAverage speed from each", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "thon records. While male athletes increased speed as the race\nprogressed (negative strategy), female athletes used a less uniform\npacing throughout the competition.\nA recreational master runner from\nthe 13th Festival Athens 48h\nUltramarathon in 2018"}
{"chunk_id": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024_p0006_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_8359a872ca35", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7ad636bf705f4289", "text_span_hash": "7ad636bf705f4289", "source_file": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ens 48h\nUltramarathon in 2018\nAverage speed from each\nIn a master runner competing in a 48 h run, running speed decreased\nnon-linearly during the race as a positive pacing. While body mass\ndecreased, body water increased.\nA 95 years old European mara", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "speed in full race from\n10 participants in two 6-h ultra-\nmarathon races with two pacing\nPercentage of the mean\nspeed in full race from\nSlow starts do not affect an athlete’s overall pacing strategy in an\n15 marathon world records for men\nduring 1967 and 2014\nAverage speed from each\nPercentage of the mean\nspeed in full race from\nThe pacing strategies of the best marathon runners in the world have\nchanged over the last 50 years. Although a negative pace distribution\nhas been proposed as the most efficient option, a pacing strategy\ncharacterised by very little speed changes across the whole race may\nbe the way to go in the future.\n196 finishers in a 100-km race\nincorporating the World Masters\nAverage speed from each\nPercentage of the mean\nspeed in full race from\n10 km-10 splits Positive\nFemales showed lower relative starting speeds and higher finishing\nspeeds than males.Although pacing remained consistent across age\ncategories, it differed with level of performance within each. Strategy\ndiffers between genders and differs depending on performance level\nachieved in males but not females.\n190,228 finishers in New York City\nmarathon from 2006 to 2011\nAverage speed from each\nAlthough all runners developed a positive pace profile, a lower\nvariability of speed through the race was found in the top runners\ncompared with the less successful runners.\n31,762 masters athletes from the\n2015 TSC New York City Marathon\nAverage speed from each", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ens 48h\nUltramarathon in 2018\nAverage speed from each\nIn a master runner competing in a 48 h run, running speed decreased\nnon-linearly during the race as a positive pacing. While body mass\ndecreased, body water increased.\nA 95 years old European mara"}
{"chunk_id": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024_p0006_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_8359a872ca35", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c531a40dbc759bc0", "text_span_hash": "c531a40dbc759bc0", "source_file": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": ".\nA 95 years old European marathon\nrecord holder in the Sri Chinmony\n12 + 24h Ultramarathon\nAverage speed from each\nA 95-year-old man was able to run during 12 h using a U-shaped\npacing and achieving a total distance of nearly 53 km. Increased\nselect", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "speed in full race from\n10 participants in two 6-h ultra-\nmarathon races with two pacing\nPercentage of the mean\nspeed in full race from\nSlow starts do not affect an athlete’s overall pacing strategy in an\n15 marathon world records for men\nduring 1967 and 2014\nAverage speed from each\nPercentage of the mean\nspeed in full race from\nThe pacing strategies of the best marathon runners in the world have\nchanged over the last 50 years. Although a negative pace distribution\nhas been proposed as the most efficient option, a pacing strategy\ncharacterised by very little speed changes across the whole race may\nbe the way to go in the future.\n196 finishers in a 100-km race\nincorporating the World Masters\nAverage speed from each\nPercentage of the mean\nspeed in full race from\n10 km-10 splits Positive\nFemales showed lower relative starting speeds and higher finishing\nspeeds than males.Although pacing remained consistent across age\ncategories, it differed with level of performance within each. Strategy\ndiffers between genders and differs depending on performance level\nachieved in males but not females.\n190,228 finishers in New York City\nmarathon from 2006 to 2011\nAverage speed from each\nAlthough all runners developed a positive pace profile, a lower\nvariability of speed through the race was found in the top runners\ncompared with the less successful runners.\n31,762 masters athletes from the\n2015 TSC New York City Marathon\nAverage speed from each", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": ".\nA 95 years old European marathon\nrecord holder in the Sri Chinmony\n12 + 24h Ultramarathon\nAverage speed from each\nA 95-year-old man was able to run during 12 h using a U-shaped\npacing and achieving a total distance of nearly 53 km. Increased\nselect"}
