all runners developed a positive pace profile, a lower\nvariability of speed through the race was found in the top runners\ncompared with the less successful runners.\n31,762 masters athletes from the\n2015 TSC New York City Marathon\nAverage speed from each", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "f all age groups reduced running speed during the\nmarathon with a final spurt in the last segment. The speed decreased\n(continued on next page)"}
{"chunk_id": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024_p0007_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_8359a872ca35", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "243a1622100d3dd1", "text_span_hash": "243a1622100d3dd1", "source_file": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "between end split and\nmore in men compared to women with a different trend in the 25–30\nkm split(speed increased in women and decreased in men).\n9111 finishers in 2017 Ljubljana\nPercentage of the mean\nspeed in full race from each\nBoth half-marathon a", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "between end split and\nmore in men compared to women with a different trend in the 25–30\nkm split(speed increased in women and decreased in men).\n9111 finishers in 2017 Ljubljana\nPercentage of the mean\nspeed in full race from each\nBoth half-marathon and marathon races presented a positive pacing.\nHalf-marathon runners did not show an end spurt, which was\nobserved in marathon runners. Women and men adopted similar\npacing pattern in half-marathon, whereas in marathon, women had\nmore even pacing than men.\n835 finishers in IAAF World Half\nMarathon Championships\nAverage speed from each\nThe best athletes displayed patterns of even pacing for some of the\nrace, while slower athletes generally slowed from a peak speed at 5\nkm until they reached 20 km. All groups increased pace considerably\nduring the final 1.1 km.\n156 finishers in 2017 Athens Authentic\nAverage speed from each\nsegments, Pace Range,\nWomen, older and faster recreational runners adopted a more even\npacing, it was related with a higher aerobic capacity and lower\nmuscle strength in men.Men with more even pacing scored higher in\npsychological coping,self-esteem, life meaning, recognition and\n298,082 finishers in the New York City\nAverage speed from each\nDeviations in average\nspeed from each segments,\nNationality may influence pacing.Ethiopians and Kenyans adopt a\nrelatively more even pace during the New York city Marathon which\nis shown by their small changes in race speed and", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "between end split and\nmore in men compared to women with a different trend in the 25–30\nkm split(speed increased in women and decreased in men).\n9111 finishers in 2017 Ljubljana\nPercentage of the mean\nspeed in full race from each\nBoth half-marathon a"}
{"chunk_id": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024_p0007_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_8359a872ca35", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "81676652b8bd8e5b", "text_span_hash": "81676652b8bd8e5b", "source_file": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "from each\nBoth half-marathon and marathon races presented a positive pacing.\nHalf-marathon runners did not show an end spurt, which was\nobserved in marathon runners. Women and men adopted similar\npacing pattern in half-marathon, whereas in marathon,", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "between end split and\nmore in men compared to women with a different trend in the 25–30\nkm split(speed increased in women and decreased in men).\n9111 finishers in 2017 Ljubljana\nPercentage of the mean\nspeed in full race from each\nBoth half-marathon and marathon races presented a positive pacing.\nHalf-marathon runners did not show an end spurt, which was\nobserved in marathon runners. Women and men adopted similar\npacing pattern in half-marathon, whereas in marathon, women had\nmore even pacing than men.\n835 finishers in IAAF World Half\nMarathon Championships\nAverage speed from each\nThe best athletes displayed patterns of even pacing for some of the\nrace, while slower athletes generally slowed from a peak speed at 5\nkm until they reached 20 km. All groups increased pace considerably\nduring the final 1.1 km.\n156 finishers in 2017 Athens Authentic\nAverage speed from each\nsegments, Pace Range,\nWomen, older and faster recreational runners adopted a more even\npacing, it was related with a higher aerobic capacity and lower\nmuscle strength in men.Men with more even pacing scored higher in\npsychological coping,self-esteem, life meaning, recognition and\n298,082 finishers in the New York City\nAverage speed from each\nDeviations in average\nspeed from each segments,\nNationality may influence pacing.Ethiopians and Kenyans adopt a\nrelatively more even pace during the New York city Marathon which\nis shown by their small changes in race speed and", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "from each\nBoth half-marathon and marathon races presented a positive pacing.\nHalf-marathon runners did not show an end spurt, which was\nobserved in marathon runners. Women and men adopted similar\npacing pattern in half-marathon, whereas in marathon,"}
{"chunk_id": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024_p0007_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_8359a872ca35", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7620e68f3cc43ad1", "text_span_hash": "7620e68f3cc43ad1", "source_file": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "arathon, whereas in marathon, women had\nmore even pacing than men.\n835 finishers in IAAF World Half\nMarathon Championships\nAverage speed from each\nThe best athletes displayed patterns of even pacing for some of the\nrace, while slower athletes general", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "between end split and\nmore in men compared to women with a different trend in the 25–30\nkm split(speed increased in women and decreased in men).\n9111 finishers in 2017 Ljubljana\nPercentage of the mean\nspeed in full race from each\nBoth half-marathon and marathon races presented a positive pacing.\nHalf-marathon runners did not show an end spurt, which was\nobserved in marathon runners. Women and men adopted similar\npacing pattern in half-marathon, whereas in marathon, women had\nmore even pacing than men.\n835 finishers in IAAF World Half\nMarathon Championships\nAverage speed from each\nThe best athletes displayed patterns of even pacing for some of the\nrace, while slower athletes generally slowed from a peak speed at 5\nkm until they reached 20 km. All groups increased pace considerably\nduring the final 1.1 km.\n156 finishers in 2017 Athens Authentic\nAverage speed from each\nsegments, Pace Range,\nWomen, older and faster recreational runners adopted a more even\npacing, it was related with a higher aerobic capacity and lower\nmuscle strength in men.Men with more even pacing scored higher in\npsychological coping,self-esteem, life meaning, recognition and\n298,082 finishers in the New York City\nAverage speed from each\nDeviations in average\nspeed from each segments,\nNationality may influence pacing.Ethiopians and Kenyans adopt a\nrelatively more even pace during the New York city Marathon which\nis shown by their small changes in race speed and", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "arathon, whereas in marathon, women had\nmore even pacing than men.\n835 finishers in IAAF World Half\nMarathon Championships\nAverage speed from each\nThe best athletes displayed patterns of even pacing for some of the\nrace, while slower athletes general"}
