decreased during the middle and late\nstages of the race. For optimal 24 h ultra-marathon\nperformance, the “big three” strategies of training,\nnutrition, and pacing are all important.\n34 experienced endurance runners in the\nStour Valley Path 100kmUltramarathon\nDeviations in average speed from\nA positive pacing pattern was observed in the ultra-\nmarathon.Pe", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "trained marathon runners\nThermal index, Heart Rate\nPhysical characteristic,\nAverage speed from each segments\nMost of the participants reduced their pace when a T-core\nof 39 ◦C was reached which occurred between 6 and 9 km\nof the half marathon race. H"}
{"chunk_id": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024_p0008_c0016", "source_registry_id": "src_8359a872ca35", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4f6fa122b001de93", "text_span_hash": "4f6fa122b001de93", "source_file": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 16, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "m\nof the half marathon race. However, participants were able\nto show a slight increase in running speed at 21 km which\nis consistent with the “end-spurt” phenomenon.\n(continued on next page)", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "the half-marathon. Most age groups in both genders exhibited a more\neven pace in the half-marathon than in the marathon.\n279 finishers in Berlin’s Marathon from\nAverage speed from each\nPositive pacing behaviors were predominantly followed by elite male\nmarathon runners. The latest stages of the race were usually run\nalone in men and women and were also characterized by an\naccentuated decrease of pace and the generation of significant pace\ndifferences between performance groups at every distance segment.\nBillat et al. [54] 4 marathon world records from Berlin\nAverage speed from each segments,\nPercentage of the mean speed in full\nrace from each segments,\n1 km-43 splits Negative\nThe best female and male marathon performances were\nrun differently, and fractional use of CS.Improvement in\nmarathon performance might depend on negative split and\nasymmetry for female runners, and on higher fractional\nutilization of CS for male runners.\nA 32 yr ultra-marathon runners in the\nHirosaki 24h Ultra-marathon\nBlood glucose level, Heart Rate,\nAverage speed from each segments,\nThe runner’s speed decreased during the middle and late\nstages of the race. For optimal 24 h ultra-marathon\nperformance, the “big three” strategies of training,\nnutrition, and pacing are all important.\n34 experienced endurance runners in the\nStour Valley Path 100kmUltramarathon\nDeviations in average speed from\nA positive pacing pattern was observed in the ultra-\nmarathon.Pe", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "m\nof the half marathon race. However, participants were able\nto show a slight increase in running speed at 21 km which\nis consistent with the “end-spurt” phenomenon.\n(continued on next page)"}
{"chunk_id": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024_p0009_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_8359a872ca35", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8cbe6e391690a6a2", "text_span_hash": "8cbe6e391690a6a2", "source_file": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "88 runners who are 35–40 yr in the 2016\nPhysiological characteristics,\nRelative-Intensity Levels of Physical\nAverage speed from each segments,\nFemales maintained a more stable pacing and ran at less\ndemanding intensity throughout the marathon, limiti", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "88 runners who are 35–40 yr in the 2016\nPhysiological characteristics,\nRelative-Intensity Levels of Physical\nAverage speed from each segments,\nFemales maintained a more stable pacing and ran at less\ndemanding intensity throughout the marathon, limiting\nthe decay of running pace in the last part of the race.\nFemales run at a more conservative intensity level in the\nfirst part of the marathon compared to males).\nNote: NR not refer, PB personal best, WR world record.", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "88 runners who are 35–40 yr in the 2016\nPhysiological characteristics,\nRelative-Intensity Levels of Physical\nAverage speed from each segments,\nFemales maintained a more stable pacing and ran at less\ndemanding intensity throughout the marathon, limiti"}
{"chunk_id": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024_p0009_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_8359a872ca35", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "15eac167ced25753", "text_span_hash": "15eac167ced25753", "source_file": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "hroughout the marathon, limiting\nthe decay of running pace in the last part of the race.\nFemales run at a more conservative intensity level in the\nfirst part of the marathon compared to males).\nNote: NR not refer, PB personal best, WR world record.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "88 runners who are 35–40 yr in the 2016\nPhysiological characteristics,\nRelative-Intensity Levels of Physical\nAverage speed from each segments,\nFemales maintained a more stable pacing and ran at less\ndemanding intensity throughout the marathon, limiting\nthe decay of running pace in the last part of the race.\nFemales run at a more conservative intensity level in the\nfirst part of the marathon compared to males).\nNote: NR not refer, PB personal best, WR world record.", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "hroughout the marathon, limiting\nthe decay of running pace in the last part of the race.\nFemales run at a more conservative intensity level in the\nfirst part of the marathon compared to males).\nNote: NR not refer, PB personal best, WR world record."}
{"chunk_id": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024_p0010_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_8359a872ca35", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "abad81b7c5190b12", "text_span_hash": "abad81b7c5190b12", "source_file": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "and parabolic pacing [17,31,36,46,52,53,56,57]. Furthermore, two studies explored alternative approaches to pacing strategies,\nsuggesting that marathon runners should either maintain a controllable pacing strategy to intentionally minimize pacing var", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "and parabolic pacing [17,31,36,46,52,53,56,57]. Furthermore, two studies explored alternative approaches to pacing strategies,\nsuggesting that marathon runners should either maintain a controllable pacing strategy to intentionally minimize pacing variability\n3.2.3. Influences on pacing strategies\nAlmost all studies concur that factors influencing pacing strategy are associated with alterations or interferences in an athlete’s split\nspeed. Consequently, when analyzing factors affecting pacing strategy, authors frequently cluster athletes to determine whether those\nwith differing characteristics exhibit variations in speed or changes in speed across each segment and the entire race (absolute or\nrelative). Key factors identified as influencing pacing strategies include gender, age, performance level, marathon category, packing,\nand physiological/psychological characteristics.\nA total of 11 studies considered gender as a subgroup to explore the similarities and differences in the pacing strategies of male and\nfemale athletes. Nine studies observed a difference in pacing between men and women, primarily in the magnitude of speed change,\nwith no significant differences in overall profiles. Only two studies reported inconsistent pacing strategies between the genders, with\none indicating that men used negative pacing and women did not; the other noted that males decreased their pacing at 25–30 km,\nRegarding age, ten studies yielded tw", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "and parabolic pacing [17,31,36,46,52,53,56,57]. Furthermore, two studies explored alternative approaches to pacing strategies,\nsuggesting that marathon runners should either maintain a controllable pacing strategy to intentionally minimize pacing var"}
