arily in the magnitude of speed change,\nwith no significant differences in overall profiles. Only two studies reported inconsistent pacing strategies between the genders, with\none indicating that men used negative pacing and women did not; the other noted that males decreased their pacing at 25–30 km,\nRegarding age, ten studies yielded tw", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ations, while\noverall pacing strategies remain relatively consistent. Exceptionally, three studies found that elite runners adopted distinct pacing\nstrategies [31,43,48]. It is generally agreed that the pacing strategies of high-level runners align m"}
