{"chunk_id": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024_p0011_c0019", "source_registry_id": "src_8359a872ca35", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "af47f647824c6c11", "text_span_hash": "af47f647824c6c11", "source_file": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 19, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "merges as a crucial metric in pacing strategy studies. It is insufficient to define pacing strategies\nsolely through pacing profiles, yet this error recurs in numerous publications. Thus, scholars have introduced the concept of pace range\nto provide", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "3.3. Methodological considerations\nRegarding study design and measured pacing variables in the systematic review, Table 3 categorizes the 39 selected studies into\ntwo primary study designs: speed and segment. Descriptive and experimental designs are the most commonly employed in pacing\nstrategy research, with descriptive studies comprising 74.4 % of the investigations. In terms of measuring segment speeds, the studies\nemploy various approaches, including the use of absolute and relative speeds, different segment types, and choices of distances, such as\nthe number of laps, time, or the distance of the race to define each segment.\nIn marathon research, 28 studies combined absolute and relative speeds to characterize pacing through absolute speeds, describe\nchanges in pacing by measuring relative speeds, and ultimately identify pacing strategies by integrating both types. Predominantly,\nstudies preferred using distance for segmentation due to the accessibility of pace data on official public marathon websites, and\nbecause the official split time is recorded by the electronic chip in the athletes’ shoes [36], which activates at set distances [33], thus\nalso provide intuitive and reliable data for longer and more duration-intensive events like ultramarathons. Segmentation by the\nnumber of laps requires that the race occurs at a circuitous location [51,61], offering its own advantages.\nMost studies (n = 33) employed analysis of vari", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "merges as a crucial metric in pacing strategy studies. It is insufficient to define pacing strategies\nsolely through pacing profiles, yet this error recurs in numerous publications. Thus, scholars have introduced the concept of pace range\nto provide"}
