{"chunk_id": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024_p0013_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_8359a872ca35", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "962cfa911ac147c3", "text_span_hash": "962cfa911ac147c3", "source_file": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "due to the shorter remaining distance, allowing for a sustained, high-intensity effort toward the end [29,78].\nConversely, early acceleration in a full marathon can lead to substantial deceleration in the latter stages [79]. Ultramarathon par-\nticip", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Female athletes tend to display a more uniform and consistent pacing strategy, potentially making it easier to identify optimal pacing\nstrategies for them [27].\n4.4. The impact of distance: the persistent influence on the pacing strategy\nMarathons, categorized into half-marathons (21.0975 km), full marathons (42.195 km), and ultramarathons (exceeding 42.195\nkm), demonstrate varied pacing strategies across distances, with consensus in the literature on this variability [26–28]. The nature of\nthe event, relying solely on running, places physical fitness as a crucial determinant of pacing [41]. In half-marathons, athletes might\ninitiate an aggressive pace due to the shorter remaining distance, allowing for a sustained, high-intensity effort toward the end [29,78].\nConversely, early acceleration in a full marathon can lead to substantial deceleration in the latter stages [79]. Ultramarathon par-\nticipants often adopt a conservative pacing strategy, maintaining minimal variation between their peak and average paces throughout\nthe race [14,34,80]. Additionally, the distance not only impacts energy distribution but also significantly influences training costs.\nSwain et al. analyzed training across various marathon events and found no substantial differences in training duration or race\nexperience among athletes, though pacing strategies varied [16]. The requirements for shorter events demand more intensive training\nrelative to longer", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "due to the shorter remaining distance, allowing for a sustained, high-intensity effort toward the end [29,78].\nConversely, early acceleration in a full marathon can lead to substantial deceleration in the latter stages [79]. Ultramarathon par-\nticip"}
{"chunk_id": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024_p0013_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_8359a872ca35", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "d359b8bf629d52b8", "text_span_hash": "d359b8bf629d52b8", "source_file": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "[79]. Ultramarathon par-\nticipants often adopt a conservative pacing strategy, maintaining minimal variation between their peak and average paces throughout\nthe race [14,34,80]. Additionally, the distance not only impacts energy distribution but also", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Female athletes tend to display a more uniform and consistent pacing strategy, potentially making it easier to identify optimal pacing\nstrategies for them [27].\n4.4. The impact of distance: the persistent influence on the pacing strategy\nMarathons, categorized into half-marathons (21.0975 km), full marathons (42.195 km), and ultramarathons (exceeding 42.195\nkm), demonstrate varied pacing strategies across distances, with consensus in the literature on this variability [26–28]. The nature of\nthe event, relying solely on running, places physical fitness as a crucial determinant of pacing [41]. In half-marathons, athletes might\ninitiate an aggressive pace due to the shorter remaining distance, allowing for a sustained, high-intensity effort toward the end [29,78].\nConversely, early acceleration in a full marathon can lead to substantial deceleration in the latter stages [79]. Ultramarathon par-\nticipants often adopt a conservative pacing strategy, maintaining minimal variation between their peak and average paces throughout\nthe race [14,34,80]. Additionally, the distance not only impacts energy distribution but also significantly influences training costs.\nSwain et al. analyzed training across various marathon events and found no substantial differences in training duration or race\nexperience among athletes, though pacing strategies varied [16]. The requirements for shorter events demand more intensive training\nrelative to longer", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "[79]. Ultramarathon par-\nticipants often adopt a conservative pacing strategy, maintaining minimal variation between their peak and average paces throughout\nthe race [14,34,80]. Additionally, the distance not only impacts energy distribution but also"}
{"chunk_id": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024_p0013_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_8359a872ca35", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "bac73c4819c53acc", "text_span_hash": "bac73c4819c53acc", "source_file": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "s energy distribution but also significantly influences training costs.\nSwain et al. analyzed training across various marathon events and found no substantial differences in training duration or race\nexperience among athletes, though pacing strategie", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Female athletes tend to display a more uniform and consistent pacing strategy, potentially making it easier to identify optimal pacing\nstrategies for them [27].\n4.4. The impact of distance: the persistent influence on the pacing strategy\nMarathons, categorized into half-marathons (21.0975 km), full marathons (42.195 km), and ultramarathons (exceeding 42.195\nkm), demonstrate varied pacing strategies across distances, with consensus in the literature on this variability [26–28]. The nature of\nthe event, relying solely on running, places physical fitness as a crucial determinant of pacing [41]. In half-marathons, athletes might\ninitiate an aggressive pace due to the shorter remaining distance, allowing for a sustained, high-intensity effort toward the end [29,78].\nConversely, early acceleration in a full marathon can lead to substantial deceleration in the latter stages [79]. Ultramarathon par-\nticipants often adopt a conservative pacing strategy, maintaining minimal variation between their peak and average paces throughout\nthe race [14,34,80]. Additionally, the distance not only impacts energy distribution but also significantly influences training costs.\nSwain et al. analyzed training across various marathon events and found no substantial differences in training duration or race\nexperience among athletes, though pacing strategies varied [16]. The requirements for shorter events demand more intensive training\nrelative to longer", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "s energy distribution but also significantly influences training costs.\nSwain et al. analyzed training across various marathon events and found no substantial differences in training duration or race\nexperience among athletes, though pacing strategie"}
{"chunk_id": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024_p0013_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_8359a872ca35", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "6e87bba46a504682", "text_span_hash": "6e87bba46a504682", "source_file": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "letes, though pacing strategies varied [16]. The requirements for shorter events demand more intensive training\nrelative to longer events, facilitating a more effective strategy discovery for athletes.\n4.5. Simplifying pacing strategies: the quest fo", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Female athletes tend to display a more uniform and consistent pacing strategy, potentially making it easier to identify optimal pacing\nstrategies for them [27].\n4.4. The impact of distance: the persistent influence on the pacing strategy\nMarathons, categorized into half-marathons (21.0975 km), full marathons (42.195 km), and ultramarathons (exceeding 42.195\nkm), demonstrate varied pacing strategies across distances, with consensus in the literature on this variability [26–28]. The nature of\nthe event, relying solely on running, places physical fitness as a crucial determinant of pacing [41]. In half-marathons, athletes might\ninitiate an aggressive pace due to the shorter remaining distance, allowing for a sustained, high-intensity effort toward the end [29,78].\nConversely, early acceleration in a full marathon can lead to substantial deceleration in the latter stages [79]. Ultramarathon par-\nticipants often adopt a conservative pacing strategy, maintaining minimal variation between their peak and average paces throughout\nthe race [14,34,80]. Additionally, the distance not only impacts energy distribution but also significantly influences training costs.\nSwain et al. analyzed training across various marathon events and found no substantial differences in training duration or race\nexperience among athletes, though pacing strategies varied [16]. The requirements for shorter events demand more intensive training\nrelative to longer", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "letes, though pacing strategies varied [16]. The requirements for shorter events demand more intensive training\nrelative to longer events, facilitating a more effective strategy discovery for athletes.\n4.5. Simplifying pacing strategies: the quest fo"}
