istribution but also significantly influences training costs.\nSwain et al. analyzed training across various marathon events and found no substantial differences in training duration or race\nexperience among athletes, though pacing strategies varied [16]. The requirements for shorter events demand more intensive training\nrelative to longer", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "athletes in achieving the requisite fitness for acceleration, deceleration, and pace maintenance at different\nstages of a race. This information could serve as a reference point for athletes to adopt a strategic pacing plan to navigate the various\nst"}
{"chunk_id": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024_p0013_c0018", "source_registry_id": "src_8359a872ca35", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f8eccaea4797894a", "text_span_hash": "f8eccaea4797894a", "source_file": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 18, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": ". Such knowledge is instrumental in developing suitable tactics and pacing\nthat enhance the likelihood of victory in a race. Additionally, an examination of pacing strategies in marathon races offers a\ncomprehensive macro- and micro-perspective on RP", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Female athletes tend to display a more uniform and consistent pacing strategy, potentially making it easier to identify optimal pacing\nstrategies for them [27].\n4.4. The impact of distance: the persistent influence on the pacing strategy\nMarathons, categorized into half-marathons (21.0975 km), full marathons (42.195 km), and ultramarathons (exceeding 42.195\nkm), demonstrate varied pacing strategies across distances, with consensus in the literature on this variability [26–28]. The nature of\nthe event, relying solely on running, places physical fitness as a crucial determinant of pacing [41]. In half-marathons, athletes might\ninitiate an aggressive pace due to the shorter remaining distance, allowing for a sustained, high-intensity effort toward the end [29,78].\nConversely, early acceleration in a full marathon can lead to substantial deceleration in the latter stages [79]. Ultramarathon par-\nticipants often adopt a conservative pacing strategy, maintaining minimal variation between their peak and average paces throughout\nthe race [14,34,80]. Additionally, the distance not only impacts energy distribution but also significantly influences training costs.\nSwain et al. analyzed training across various marathon events and found no substantial differences in training duration or race\nexperience among athletes, though pacing strategies varied [16]. The requirements for shorter events demand more intensive training\nrelative to longer", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": ". Such knowledge is instrumental in developing suitable tactics and pacing\nthat enhance the likelihood of victory in a race. Additionally, an examination of pacing strategies in marathon races offers a\ncomprehensive macro- and micro-perspective on RP"}
{"chunk_id": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024_p0013_c0020", "source_registry_id": "src_8359a872ca35", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5e8e51c7a41a2d98", "text_span_hash": "5e8e51c7a41a2d98", "source_file": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 20, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "permits only the analysis of extended subparagraphs in marathons (5 km/10 km), and it remains uncertain\nwhether a more distinct change in runners’ pacing at higher frequency segments would facilitate a clearer differentiation of pacing\nstrategies. I", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Female athletes tend to display a more uniform and consistent pacing strategy, potentially making it easier to identify optimal pacing\nstrategies for them [27].\n4.4. The impact of distance: the persistent influence on the pacing strategy\nMarathons, categorized into half-marathons (21.0975 km), full marathons (42.195 km), and ultramarathons (exceeding 42.195\nkm), demonstrate varied pacing strategies across distances, with consensus in the literature on this variability [26–28]. The nature of\nthe event, relying solely on running, places physical fitness as a crucial determinant of pacing [41]. In half-marathons, athletes might\ninitiate an aggressive pace due to the shorter remaining distance, allowing for a sustained, high-intensity effort toward the end [29,78].\nConversely, early acceleration in a full marathon can lead to substantial deceleration in the latter stages [79]. Ultramarathon par-\nticipants often adopt a conservative pacing strategy, maintaining minimal variation between their peak and average paces throughout\nthe race [14,34,80]. Additionally, the distance not only impacts energy distribution but also significantly influences training costs.\nSwain et al. analyzed training across various marathon events and found no substantial differences in training duration or race\nexperience among athletes, though pacing strategies varied [16]. The requirements for shorter events demand more intensive training\nrelative to longer", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "permits only the analysis of extended subparagraphs in marathons (5 km/10 km), and it remains uncertain\nwhether a more distinct change in runners’ pacing at higher frequency segments would facilitate a clearer differentiation of pacing\nstrategies. I"}
{"chunk_id": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024_p0013_c0022", "source_registry_id": "src_8359a872ca35", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "27486915e625d8d1", "text_span_hash": "27486915e625d8d1", "source_file": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 22, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "arily decelerate even the most skilled and experienced runners,\nbut it may also limit a runner to little more than a walking pace for the remaining race duration and could prevent some from finishing\n[71]. Therefore, “the wall”, as a specific phenome", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Female athletes tend to display a more uniform and consistent pacing strategy, potentially making it easier to identify optimal pacing\nstrategies for them [27].\n4.4. The impact of distance: the persistent influence on the pacing strategy\nMarathons, categorized into half-marathons (21.0975 km), full marathons (42.195 km), and ultramarathons (exceeding 42.195\nkm), demonstrate varied pacing strategies across distances, with consensus in the literature on this variability [26–28]. The nature of\nthe event, relying solely on running, places physical fitness as a crucial determinant of pacing [41]. In half-marathons, athletes might\ninitiate an aggressive pace due to the shorter remaining distance, allowing for a sustained, high-intensity effort toward the end [29,78].\nConversely, early acceleration in a full marathon can lead to substantial deceleration in the latter stages [79]. Ultramarathon par-\nticipants often adopt a conservative pacing strategy, maintaining minimal variation between their peak and average paces throughout\nthe race [14,34,80]. Additionally, the distance not only impacts energy distribution but also significantly influences training costs.\nSwain et al. analyzed training across various marathon events and found no substantial differences in training duration or race\nexperience among athletes, though pacing strategies varied [16]. The requirements for shorter events demand more intensive training\nrelative to longer", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "arily decelerate even the most skilled and experienced runners,\nbut it may also limit a runner to little more than a walking pace for the remaining race duration and could prevent some from finishing\n[71]. Therefore, “the wall”, as a specific phenome"}
{"chunk_id": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024_p0013_c0023", "source_registry_id": "src_8359a872ca35", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2cd1ad68b4d958d7", "text_span_hash": "2cd1ad68b4d958d7", "source_file": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 23, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "e wall”, as a specific phenomenon in marathons, has a significant impact on the speed of runners in the latter stages\nof the race, and it remains an unresolved issue how much it will alter the pacing strategy [70]. For marathon runners, additional\nMe", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Female athletes tend to display a more uniform and consistent pacing strategy, potentially making it easier to identify optimal pacing\nstrategies for them [27].\n4.4. The impact of distance: the persistent influence on the pacing strategy\nMarathons, categorized into half-marathons (21.0975 km), full marathons (42.195 km), and ultramarathons (exceeding 42.195\nkm), demonstrate varied pacing strategies across distances, with consensus in the literature on this variability [26–28]. The nature of\nthe event, relying solely on running, places physical fitness as a crucial determinant of pacing [41]. In half-marathons, athletes might\ninitiate an aggressive pace due to the shorter remaining distance, allowing for a sustained, high-intensity effort toward the end [29,78].\nConversely, early acceleration in a full marathon can lead to substantial deceleration in the latter stages [79]. Ultramarathon par-\nticipants often adopt a conservative pacing strategy, maintaining minimal variation between their peak and average paces throughout\nthe race [14,34,80]. Additionally, the distance not only impacts energy distribution but also significantly influences training costs.\nSwain et al. analyzed training across various marathon events and found no substantial differences in training duration or race\nexperience among athletes, though pacing strategies varied [16]. The requirements for shorter events demand more intensive training\nrelative to longer", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "e wall”, as a specific phenomenon in marathons, has a significant impact on the speed of runners in the latter stages\nof the race, and it remains an unresolved issue how much it will alter the pacing strategy [70]. For marathon runners, additional\nMe"}
{"chunk_id": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024_p0014_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_8359a872ca35", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c545ec3fef87d5c9", "text_span_hash": "c545ec3fef87d5c9", "source_file": "Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Pacing_Strategies_Marathons_Systematic_Review_2024.pdf", "page": 14, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "physiological and running parameters such as step length/frequency [81], heart rate [82], maximum oxygen uptake, energy cost of\nrunning, and running economy [83,84] need to be considered to optimize their performance over extended periods and distanc", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "physiological and running parameters such as step length/frequency [81], heart rate [82], maximum oxygen uptake, energy cost of\nrunning, and running economy [83,84] need to be considered to optimize their performance over extended periods and distances [85].\nTraining should also be aligned with runners to comprehend the volume and intensity of training. This finding aligns with their\nperformance pacing in races to enhance the understanding of their pacing strategies.\n4.8. Strengths and limitations\nThis study offers the initial overview of pacing strategies in marathon races, and unlike prior qualitative analyses of such pacing\nbehaviors, this paper provides a quantitative analysis from the perspective of segmented pacing data. Not only have the pacing\nstrategies currently observed in marathons been summarized and categorized, but an attempt has also been made to understand the\npacing features of marathons at both the macro and micro levels.\nThe application of the NOS, a quality assessment tool endorsed by the Cochrane Collaboration, bolsters the methodology of this\nreview. This is further supported by the involvement of a second author to validate the results of the scale, as the interrater reliability of\nthe NOS has been critically examined [86]. However, the sensitivity of the NOS might be questioned. For example, the first question of\nthe scale concerning the representativeness of the exposed cohort, and both options (a) ‘t", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "physiological and running parameters such as step length/frequency [81], heart rate [82], maximum oxygen uptake, energy cost of\nrunning, and running economy [83,84] need to be considered to optimize their performance over extended periods and distanc"}
