trength\nwas not directly related to the risk of developing PFP.\nNotably, Ramskov et al. (Ramskov et al. 2015)\nconducted a year-long prospective study of 629\nnovice runners and found no clear correlation\nbetween hip strength and the development of PFP at\nrunning distances below 50km, but stronger hip\nabduction strength at ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "concluded that hip muscle strength\nwas not directly related to the risk of developing PFP.\nNotably, Ramskov et al. (Ramskov et al. 2015)\nconducted a year-long prospective study of 629\nnovice runners and found no clear correlation\nbetween hip strength"}
{"chunk_id": "PFPS_Etiology_Rehabilitation_Review_2021_p0002_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_30e2bf260075", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "d4c0d1d5eadd2072", "text_span_hash": "d4c0d1d5eadd2072", "source_file": "PFPS_Etiology_Rehabilitation_Review_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\PFPS_Etiology_Rehabilitation_Review_2021.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "rrelation\nbetween hip strength and the development of PFP at\nrunning distances below 50km, but stronger hip\nabduction strength at running distances above 50km\nreduced the probability of PFP However, stronger hip\nabduction strength at running distance", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Advance in Etiology and Rehabilitation Treatment of Patellofemoral Pain (Review)\nhttp://www.ijSciences.com\nPFP (Karst et al. 1995). Pal et al. (Pal et al. 2019)\nused data provided by high-resolution MRI to\ndevelop a finite element model of stair walking up\nand down stairs in patients with PFP and found that\nthe interpatellofemoral joint stress is most sensitive to\nchanges in medial femoral muscle strength, but the\nexact mechanism by which medial femoral muscle\nweakness leads to PFP remains unknown.\nIn addition, muscle strength abnormalities in the hip\njoint have been implicated in the development of PFP.\nFor example, a prospective cohort study by Boil et al\n(Boling et al. 2009) of 1597 recruits showed that\ngreater external rotation strength of the hip joint was\nassociated with the development of PFP. However, a\nprospective study by Finnoff et al. (Finnoff et al.\n2011) of 98 high school runners showed that greater\nhip abduction strength and lesser external rotation\nstrength were associated with the development of\npatellofemoral joint pain. In contrast, Thijs et al\n(Thijs et al. 2011) concluded that hip muscle strength\nwas not directly related to the risk of developing PFP.\nNotably, Ramskov et al. (Ramskov et al. 2015)\nconducted a year-long prospective study of 629\nnovice runners and found no clear correlation\nbetween hip strength and the development of PFP at\nrunning distances below 50km, but stronger hip\nabduction strength at ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "rrelation\nbetween hip strength and the development of PFP at\nrunning distances below 50km, but stronger hip\nabduction strength at running distances above 50km\nreduced the probability of PFP However, stronger hip\nabduction strength at running distance"}
{"chunk_id": "PFPS_Etiology_Rehabilitation_Review_2021_p0002_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_30e2bf260075", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "55a462e5ce5a4b68", "text_span_hash": "55a462e5ce5a4b68", "source_file": "PFPS_Etiology_Rehabilitation_Review_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\PFPS_Etiology_Rehabilitation_Review_2021.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "n strength at running distances above 50km\nreduced the probability of PFP. In view of this,\nfurther high-quality studies are needed to explore the\nspecific relationship between hip muscular strength\nand PFP and the mechanisms involved.\nReduced Muscle", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Advance in Etiology and Rehabilitation Treatment of Patellofemoral Pain (Review)\nhttp://www.ijSciences.com\nPFP (Karst et al. 1995). Pal et al. (Pal et al. 2019)\nused data provided by high-resolution MRI to\ndevelop a finite element model of stair walking up\nand down stairs in patients with PFP and found that\nthe interpatellofemoral joint stress is most sensitive to\nchanges in medial femoral muscle strength, but the\nexact mechanism by which medial femoral muscle\nweakness leads to PFP remains unknown.\nIn addition, muscle strength abnormalities in the hip\njoint have been implicated in the development of PFP.\nFor example, a prospective cohort study by Boil et al\n(Boling et al. 2009) of 1597 recruits showed that\ngreater external rotation strength of the hip joint was\nassociated with the development of PFP. However, a\nprospective study by Finnoff et al. (Finnoff et al.\n2011) of 98 high school runners showed that greater\nhip abduction strength and lesser external rotation\nstrength were associated with the development of\npatellofemoral joint pain. In contrast, Thijs et al\n(Thijs et al. 2011) concluded that hip muscle strength\nwas not directly related to the risk of developing PFP.\nNotably, Ramskov et al. (Ramskov et al. 2015)\nconducted a year-long prospective study of 629\nnovice runners and found no clear correlation\nbetween hip strength and the development of PFP at\nrunning distances below 50km, but stronger hip\nabduction strength at ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "n strength at running distances above 50km\nreduced the probability of PFP. In view of this,\nfurther high-quality studies are needed to explore the\nspecific relationship between hip muscular strength\nand PFP and the mechanisms involved.\nReduced Muscle"}
{"chunk_id": "PFPS_Etiology_Rehabilitation_Review_2021_p0003_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_30e2bf260075", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5bbcae7bc77b6a3c", "text_span_hash": "5bbcae7bc77b6a3c", "source_file": "PFPS_Etiology_Rehabilitation_Review_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\PFPS_Etiology_Rehabilitation_Review_2021.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "n choices\nneed to be further validated and individualised\ndifferences fully considered.\nGait retraining is primarily indicated for patients who\nhave developed PFP as a result of prolonged running.\nThe International Association for the Study of PFP\nex", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Advance in Etiology and Rehabilitation Treatment of Patellofemoral Pain (Review)\nhttp://www.ijSciences.com\n2018). The main targets of exercise therapy include\nthe core muscles, hip muscles and knee muscles, but\nspecific exercise prescriptions remain controversial.\nFirstly, the optimal frequency of exercise remains\nunclear. Most current prescriptions instruct patients\nwith PFP to perform functional exercise for 4-6\nweeks, 2-4 times per week. chevidikunnan et al\n(Chevidikunnan et al. 2016) performed core muscle\nstrengthening exercises 3 times per week for 4 weeks\nimprovements in symptoms and function in patients\nwith PFP. Meanwhile, Hamstra et al (Hamstra-Wright\net al. 2017) instructed 157 patients with PFP in hip\nand knee strengthening training for 6 weeks, 6 times\nper week, and found significant improvements in\nsymptoms, function, and core strength before and\nafter rehabilitation, and a relapse rate of only 5%\nover 2 years. Further research is still needed to\ndetermine whether the rehabilitation effect will\nimprove further with increased exercise frequency.\nSecondly, the choice of exercise modality remains\nclearly suggest that combined hip and knee exercises\nare superior to knee exercises alone (Collins et al.\n2018). However, Hott et al (Hott et al. 2019)\ninstructed 112 young patients with PFP to perform\nhip exercise, knee exercise and free exercise\nrespectively, with a 3-month follow-up, and found\nthat symptoms and function i", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "n choices\nneed to be further validated and individualised\ndifferences fully considered.\nGait retraining is primarily indicated for patients who\nhave developed PFP as a result of prolonged running.\nThe International Association for the Study of PFP\nex"}
{"chunk_id": "PFPS_Etiology_Rehabilitation_Review_2021_p0003_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_30e2bf260075", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f2732af9b39c2b10", "text_span_hash": "f2732af9b39c2b10", "source_file": "PFPS_Etiology_Rehabilitation_Review_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\PFPS_Etiology_Rehabilitation_Review_2021.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "iation for the Study of PFP\nexpert consensus states that abnormal gait in the\ncoronal, sagittal and horizontal planes in runners\noften leads to poor patellar trajectory, increased\npatellofemoral joint pain (Powers et al. 2017).\nTherefore, correcting", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Advance in Etiology and Rehabilitation Treatment of Patellofemoral Pain (Review)\nhttp://www.ijSciences.com\n2018). The main targets of exercise therapy include\nthe core muscles, hip muscles and knee muscles, but\nspecific exercise prescriptions remain controversial.\nFirstly, the optimal frequency of exercise remains\nunclear. Most current prescriptions instruct patients\nwith PFP to perform functional exercise for 4-6\nweeks, 2-4 times per week. chevidikunnan et al\n(Chevidikunnan et al. 2016) performed core muscle\nstrengthening exercises 3 times per week for 4 weeks\nimprovements in symptoms and function in patients\nwith PFP. Meanwhile, Hamstra et al (Hamstra-Wright\net al. 2017) instructed 157 patients with PFP in hip\nand knee strengthening training for 6 weeks, 6 times\nper week, and found significant improvements in\nsymptoms, function, and core strength before and\nafter rehabilitation, and a relapse rate of only 5%\nover 2 years. Further research is still needed to\ndetermine whether the rehabilitation effect will\nimprove further with increased exercise frequency.\nSecondly, the choice of exercise modality remains\nclearly suggest that combined hip and knee exercises\nare superior to knee exercises alone (Collins et al.\n2018). However, Hott et al (Hott et al. 2019)\ninstructed 112 young patients with PFP to perform\nhip exercise, knee exercise and free exercise\nrespectively, with a 3-month follow-up, and found\nthat symptoms and function i", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "iation for the Study of PFP\nexpert consensus states that abnormal gait in the\ncoronal, sagittal and horizontal planes in runners\noften leads to poor patellar trajectory, increased\npatellofemoral joint pain (Powers et al. 2017).\nTherefore, correcting"}
{"chunk_id": "PFPS_Etiology_Rehabilitation_Review_2021_p0003_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_30e2bf260075", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "86d50943da81c8de", "text_span_hash": "86d50943da81c8de", "source_file": "PFPS_Etiology_Rehabilitation_Review_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\PFPS_Etiology_Rehabilitation_Review_2021.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "2017).\nTherefore, correcting the gait of runners is extremely\nimportant to prevent and treat PFP. For example,\nRoper et al. (Roper et al. 2016) found that instructing\nrunners with PFP to change their running posture\nfrom rearfoot to forefoot signifi", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Advance in Etiology and Rehabilitation Treatment of Patellofemoral Pain (Review)\nhttp://www.ijSciences.com\n2018). The main targets of exercise therapy include\nthe core muscles, hip muscles and knee muscles, but\nspecific exercise prescriptions remain controversial.\nFirstly, the optimal frequency of exercise remains\nunclear. Most current prescriptions instruct patients\nwith PFP to perform functional exercise for 4-6\nweeks, 2-4 times per week. chevidikunnan et al\n(Chevidikunnan et al. 2016) performed core muscle\nstrengthening exercises 3 times per week for 4 weeks\nimprovements in symptoms and function in patients\nwith PFP. Meanwhile, Hamstra et al (Hamstra-Wright\net al. 2017) instructed 157 patients with PFP in hip\nand knee strengthening training for 6 weeks, 6 times\nper week, and found significant improvements in\nsymptoms, function, and core strength before and\nafter rehabilitation, and a relapse rate of only 5%\nover 2 years. Further research is still needed to\ndetermine whether the rehabilitation effect will\nimprove further with increased exercise frequency.\nSecondly, the choice of exercise modality remains\nclearly suggest that combined hip and knee exercises\nare superior to knee exercises alone (Collins et al.\n2018). However, Hott et al (Hott et al. 2019)\ninstructed 112 young patients with PFP to perform\nhip exercise, knee exercise and free exercise\nrespectively, with a 3-month follow-up, and found\nthat symptoms and function i", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "2017).\nTherefore, correcting the gait of runners is extremely\nimportant to prevent and treat PFP. For example,\nRoper et al. (Roper et al. 2016) found that instructing\nrunners with PFP to change their running posture\nfrom rearfoot to forefoot signifi"}
