{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0001_c0018", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "46a3c46c7be4b22b", "text_span_hash": "46a3c46c7be4b22b", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 18, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "e elastic energy during the\npropulsive phase of the running cycle is determined by reactive strength and leg muscle\nstiffness [7,13]. Reactive strength is the ability of the musculoskeletal system to produce a\nhttps://www.mdpi.com/journal/sports", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Citation: Šuc, A.; Šarko, P.; Pleša, J.;\nKozinc, Ž. Resistance Exercise for\nImproving Running Economy and\nRunning Biomechanics and\nDecreasing Running-Related Injury\nRisk: A Narrative Review. Sports\nAcademic Editor: Gregory\nPublisher’s Note: MDPI stays neutral\nwith regard to jurisdictional claims in\npublished maps and institutional afﬁl-\n© 2022 by the authors.\nLicensee MDPI, Basel, Switzerland.\nThis article is an open access article\nconditions of the Creative Commons\nAttribution (CC BY) license (https://\ncreativecommons.org/licenses/by/\nResistance Exercise for Improving Running Economy and\nRunning Biomechanics and Decreasing Running-Related Injury\nRisk: A Narrative Review\nAnja Šuc 1, Pija Šarko 1, Jernej Pleša 1 and Žiga Kozinc 1,2,*\nFaculty of Health Sciences, University of Primorska, Polje 42, SI-6310 Izola, Slovenia;\nAndrej Marušiˇc Institute, University of Primorska, Muzejski trg 2, SI-6000 Koper, Slovenia\nAbstract: It is well-accepted that at least a certain amount of resistance exercise (RE) is recommended\nfor most endurance athletes. In this review, we aim to summarize the evidence regarding the effects\nof RE on running economy, running biomechanics, and running-related injury risk in endurance\nrunners. The evidence robustly shows that lower limb RE is effective for improving running economy\nand performance, with a combination of strength and plyometric training being recommended to\nimprove RE. Isometric training is also", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "e elastic energy during the\npropulsive phase of the running cycle is determined by reactive strength and leg muscle\nstiffness [7,13]. Reactive strength is the ability of the musculoskeletal system to produce a\nhttps://www.mdpi.com/journal/sports"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0002_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "aec19cf6b04467ed", "text_span_hash": "aec19cf6b04467ed", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "g\nthat stores and reuses elastic force [15]. The greater the muscle stiffness, the less muscle\nactivation is required to transfer energy during running, thus reducing energy expenditure\nand improving running economy [16]. Stiffness on the muscle leve", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "large force with concentric contraction immediately after rapid eccentric contraction, also\nknown as eccentric–concentric contraction or the stretch–shortening cycle (SSC) [14].\nMuscle stiffness is deﬁned as the ability of a system to resist deformation and is\ncalculated as the ratio of the force produced to the change in muscle length. It represents\nthe integrative action of muscles, tendons, and ligaments that work together as a spring\nthat stores and reuses elastic force [15]. The greater the muscle stiffness, the less muscle\nactivation is required to transfer energy during running, thus reducing energy expenditure\nand improving running economy [16]. Stiffness on the muscle level is difﬁcult to assess\nduring dynamic tasks, such as running. Consequently, studies have typically calculated\nquasi-stiffness, which is deﬁned as the ability of the human body to resist displacement [17].\nFor instance, vertical quasi-stiffness is deﬁned as the ratio between changes in the ground\nreaction force and vertical displacement of the center of mass, while joint quasi-stiffness\ndescribes the angular displacement within a joint in relation to joint moments [18,19]. RE\ncan increase reactive strength and quasi-stiffness (although the results on directly measured\nmuscle stiffness are inconclusive [20]). The increase in quasi-stiffness is one of the primary\ncandidates for underlying the improvement in running economy after RE [21], which is\nalso ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "g\nthat stores and reuses elastic force [15]. The greater the muscle stiffness, the less muscle\nactivation is required to transfer energy during running, thus reducing energy expenditure\nand improving running economy [16]. Stiffness on the muscle leve"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0002_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "276b189085d15206", "text_span_hash": "276b189085d15206", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": ". Stiffness on the muscle level is difﬁcult to assess\nduring dynamic tasks, such as running. Consequently, studies have typically calculated\nquasi-stiffness, which is deﬁned as the ability of the human body to resist displacement [17].\nFor instance,", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "large force with concentric contraction immediately after rapid eccentric contraction, also\nknown as eccentric–concentric contraction or the stretch–shortening cycle (SSC) [14].\nMuscle stiffness is deﬁned as the ability of a system to resist deformation and is\ncalculated as the ratio of the force produced to the change in muscle length. It represents\nthe integrative action of muscles, tendons, and ligaments that work together as a spring\nthat stores and reuses elastic force [15]. The greater the muscle stiffness, the less muscle\nactivation is required to transfer energy during running, thus reducing energy expenditure\nand improving running economy [16]. Stiffness on the muscle level is difﬁcult to assess\nduring dynamic tasks, such as running. Consequently, studies have typically calculated\nquasi-stiffness, which is deﬁned as the ability of the human body to resist displacement [17].\nFor instance, vertical quasi-stiffness is deﬁned as the ratio between changes in the ground\nreaction force and vertical displacement of the center of mass, while joint quasi-stiffness\ndescribes the angular displacement within a joint in relation to joint moments [18,19]. RE\ncan increase reactive strength and quasi-stiffness (although the results on directly measured\nmuscle stiffness are inconclusive [20]). The increase in quasi-stiffness is one of the primary\ncandidates for underlying the improvement in running economy after RE [21], which is\nalso ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": ". Stiffness on the muscle level is difﬁcult to assess\nduring dynamic tasks, such as running. Consequently, studies have typically calculated\nquasi-stiffness, which is deﬁned as the ability of the human body to resist displacement [17].\nFor instance,"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0002_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "bec00f7a2dfa913f", "text_span_hash": "bec00f7a2dfa913f", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "n quasi-stiffness is one of the primary\ncandidates for underlying the improvement in running economy after RE [21], which is\nalso corroborated by simulation studies demonstrating that a decrease in stiffness results in\nincreased energy expenditure du", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "large force with concentric contraction immediately after rapid eccentric contraction, also\nknown as eccentric–concentric contraction or the stretch–shortening cycle (SSC) [14].\nMuscle stiffness is deﬁned as the ability of a system to resist deformation and is\ncalculated as the ratio of the force produced to the change in muscle length. It represents\nthe integrative action of muscles, tendons, and ligaments that work together as a spring\nthat stores and reuses elastic force [15]. The greater the muscle stiffness, the less muscle\nactivation is required to transfer energy during running, thus reducing energy expenditure\nand improving running economy [16]. Stiffness on the muscle level is difﬁcult to assess\nduring dynamic tasks, such as running. Consequently, studies have typically calculated\nquasi-stiffness, which is deﬁned as the ability of the human body to resist displacement [17].\nFor instance, vertical quasi-stiffness is deﬁned as the ratio between changes in the ground\nreaction force and vertical displacement of the center of mass, while joint quasi-stiffness\ndescribes the angular displacement within a joint in relation to joint moments [18,19]. RE\ncan increase reactive strength and quasi-stiffness (although the results on directly measured\nmuscle stiffness are inconclusive [20]). The increase in quasi-stiffness is one of the primary\ncandidates for underlying the improvement in running economy after RE [21], which is\nalso ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "n quasi-stiffness is one of the primary\ncandidates for underlying the improvement in running economy after RE [21], which is\nalso corroborated by simulation studies demonstrating that a decrease in stiffness results in\nincreased energy expenditure du"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0002_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7aaf4c55cb094b25", "text_span_hash": "7aaf4c55cb094b25", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ncreased energy expenditure during running [22].\nEverything else being equal, stiffness is inversely related to ﬂexibility; thus, it is not\nsurprising that studies have shown an inverse relationship between ﬂexibility and running\neconomy [23,24]. On", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "large force with concentric contraction immediately after rapid eccentric contraction, also\nknown as eccentric–concentric contraction or the stretch–shortening cycle (SSC) [14].\nMuscle stiffness is deﬁned as the ability of a system to resist deformation and is\ncalculated as the ratio of the force produced to the change in muscle length. It represents\nthe integrative action of muscles, tendons, and ligaments that work together as a spring\nthat stores and reuses elastic force [15]. The greater the muscle stiffness, the less muscle\nactivation is required to transfer energy during running, thus reducing energy expenditure\nand improving running economy [16]. Stiffness on the muscle level is difﬁcult to assess\nduring dynamic tasks, such as running. Consequently, studies have typically calculated\nquasi-stiffness, which is deﬁned as the ability of the human body to resist displacement [17].\nFor instance, vertical quasi-stiffness is deﬁned as the ratio between changes in the ground\nreaction force and vertical displacement of the center of mass, while joint quasi-stiffness\ndescribes the angular displacement within a joint in relation to joint moments [18,19]. RE\ncan increase reactive strength and quasi-stiffness (although the results on directly measured\nmuscle stiffness are inconclusive [20]). The increase in quasi-stiffness is one of the primary\ncandidates for underlying the improvement in running economy after RE [21], which is\nalso ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ncreased energy expenditure during running [22].\nEverything else being equal, stiffness is inversely related to ﬂexibility; thus, it is not\nsurprising that studies have shown an inverse relationship between ﬂexibility and running\neconomy [23,24]. On"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0002_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "01822d9aff77f193", "text_span_hash": "01822d9aff77f193", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "d running\neconomy [23,24]. On the other hand, it seems that if muscle stiffness is preserved after\nstretching intervention, running economy is not affected [25]. Given that sufﬁcient range of\nmotion (particularly at the hip and ankle joints) is neede", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "large force with concentric contraction immediately after rapid eccentric contraction, also\nknown as eccentric–concentric contraction or the stretch–shortening cycle (SSC) [14].\nMuscle stiffness is deﬁned as the ability of a system to resist deformation and is\ncalculated as the ratio of the force produced to the change in muscle length. It represents\nthe integrative action of muscles, tendons, and ligaments that work together as a spring\nthat stores and reuses elastic force [15]. The greater the muscle stiffness, the less muscle\nactivation is required to transfer energy during running, thus reducing energy expenditure\nand improving running economy [16]. Stiffness on the muscle level is difﬁcult to assess\nduring dynamic tasks, such as running. Consequently, studies have typically calculated\nquasi-stiffness, which is deﬁned as the ability of the human body to resist displacement [17].\nFor instance, vertical quasi-stiffness is deﬁned as the ratio between changes in the ground\nreaction force and vertical displacement of the center of mass, while joint quasi-stiffness\ndescribes the angular displacement within a joint in relation to joint moments [18,19]. RE\ncan increase reactive strength and quasi-stiffness (although the results on directly measured\nmuscle stiffness are inconclusive [20]). The increase in quasi-stiffness is one of the primary\ncandidates for underlying the improvement in running economy after RE [21], which is\nalso ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "d running\neconomy [23,24]. On the other hand, it seems that if muscle stiffness is preserved after\nstretching intervention, running economy is not affected [25]. Given that sufﬁcient range of\nmotion (particularly at the hip and ankle joints) is neede"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0002_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3b27d081fff08354", "text_span_hash": "3b27d081fff08354", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "hip and ankle joints) is needed for optimal running technique,\nperforming some ﬂexibility exercises in addition to RE could be beneﬁcial. The interested\nreader is referred to the review paper on the acute and chronic effects of stretching on\nrunning", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "large force with concentric contraction immediately after rapid eccentric contraction, also\nknown as eccentric–concentric contraction or the stretch–shortening cycle (SSC) [14].\nMuscle stiffness is deﬁned as the ability of a system to resist deformation and is\ncalculated as the ratio of the force produced to the change in muscle length. It represents\nthe integrative action of muscles, tendons, and ligaments that work together as a spring\nthat stores and reuses elastic force [15]. The greater the muscle stiffness, the less muscle\nactivation is required to transfer energy during running, thus reducing energy expenditure\nand improving running economy [16]. Stiffness on the muscle level is difﬁcult to assess\nduring dynamic tasks, such as running. Consequently, studies have typically calculated\nquasi-stiffness, which is deﬁned as the ability of the human body to resist displacement [17].\nFor instance, vertical quasi-stiffness is deﬁned as the ratio between changes in the ground\nreaction force and vertical displacement of the center of mass, while joint quasi-stiffness\ndescribes the angular displacement within a joint in relation to joint moments [18,19]. RE\ncan increase reactive strength and quasi-stiffness (although the results on directly measured\nmuscle stiffness are inconclusive [20]). The increase in quasi-stiffness is one of the primary\ncandidates for underlying the improvement in running economy after RE [21], which is\nalso ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "hip and ankle joints) is needed for optimal running technique,\nperforming some ﬂexibility exercises in addition to RE could be beneﬁcial. The interested\nreader is referred to the review paper on the acute and chronic effects of stretching on\nrunning"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0002_c0019", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1730f9f8eaa078b5", "text_span_hash": "1730f9f8eaa078b5", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 19, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ntions or interventions other than RE [37–40]. The purpose of this\npaper is to summarize, in a narrative manner, the available evidence on effects of different\nRE modalities for different domains of application related to running. We believe that\nthi", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "large force with concentric contraction immediately after rapid eccentric contraction, also\nknown as eccentric–concentric contraction or the stretch–shortening cycle (SSC) [14].\nMuscle stiffness is deﬁned as the ability of a system to resist deformation and is\ncalculated as the ratio of the force produced to the change in muscle length. It represents\nthe integrative action of muscles, tendons, and ligaments that work together as a spring\nthat stores and reuses elastic force [15]. The greater the muscle stiffness, the less muscle\nactivation is required to transfer energy during running, thus reducing energy expenditure\nand improving running economy [16]. Stiffness on the muscle level is difﬁcult to assess\nduring dynamic tasks, such as running. Consequently, studies have typically calculated\nquasi-stiffness, which is deﬁned as the ability of the human body to resist displacement [17].\nFor instance, vertical quasi-stiffness is deﬁned as the ratio between changes in the ground\nreaction force and vertical displacement of the center of mass, while joint quasi-stiffness\ndescribes the angular displacement within a joint in relation to joint moments [18,19]. RE\ncan increase reactive strength and quasi-stiffness (although the results on directly measured\nmuscle stiffness are inconclusive [20]). The increase in quasi-stiffness is one of the primary\ncandidates for underlying the improvement in running economy after RE [21], which is\nalso ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ntions or interventions other than RE [37–40]. The purpose of this\npaper is to summarize, in a narrative manner, the available evidence on effects of different\nRE modalities for different domains of application related to running. We believe that\nthi"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0002_c0020", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "51b9d1e849c088a3", "text_span_hash": "51b9d1e849c088a3", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 20, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "o running. We believe that\nthis paper will outline speciﬁc gaps in the literature and also help coaches and clinicians\nwho work with runners to design RE programs for speciﬁc purposes and goals. A search\nof the literature was performed in January 202", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "large force with concentric contraction immediately after rapid eccentric contraction, also\nknown as eccentric–concentric contraction or the stretch–shortening cycle (SSC) [14].\nMuscle stiffness is deﬁned as the ability of a system to resist deformation and is\ncalculated as the ratio of the force produced to the change in muscle length. It represents\nthe integrative action of muscles, tendons, and ligaments that work together as a spring\nthat stores and reuses elastic force [15]. The greater the muscle stiffness, the less muscle\nactivation is required to transfer energy during running, thus reducing energy expenditure\nand improving running economy [16]. Stiffness on the muscle level is difﬁcult to assess\nduring dynamic tasks, such as running. Consequently, studies have typically calculated\nquasi-stiffness, which is deﬁned as the ability of the human body to resist displacement [17].\nFor instance, vertical quasi-stiffness is deﬁned as the ratio between changes in the ground\nreaction force and vertical displacement of the center of mass, while joint quasi-stiffness\ndescribes the angular displacement within a joint in relation to joint moments [18,19]. RE\ncan increase reactive strength and quasi-stiffness (although the results on directly measured\nmuscle stiffness are inconclusive [20]). The increase in quasi-stiffness is one of the primary\ncandidates for underlying the improvement in running economy after RE [21], which is\nalso ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "o running. We believe that\nthis paper will outline speciﬁc gaps in the literature and also help coaches and clinicians\nwho work with runners to design RE programs for speciﬁc purposes and goals. A search\nof the literature was performed in January 202"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0003_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "88e0cd8e2def5880", "text_span_hash": "88e0cd8e2def5880", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "articles. Both reviews and original research were considered. Studies were included if they\nanalyzed runners, triathletes, or military personnel. Studies were excluded if the article was\nwritten in a language other than English. Although we primarily", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "articles. Both reviews and original research were considered. Studies were included if they\nanalyzed runners, triathletes, or military personnel. Studies were excluded if the article was\nwritten in a language other than English. Although we primarily searched for randomized\ncontrolled trials and other experimental study types (i.e., assessing the effects of RE on\nrunning economy, running biomechanics, and injury-risk), we also included cross-sectional\nstudies into the narrative where appropriate.\n2. Resistance Exercise for Improving Running Economy and Performance\nRunning performance on middle and long distances depends on anthropometric,\nbiomechanical, and physiological characteristics [41]. In his endurance performance model,\nJoyner considered three key physiological parameters— ˙VO2max, blood lactate concentra-\ntion, and running economy [42]. Paavolainen et al. suggested that among well-trained\ndistance runners, running economy is a better predictor of performance than ˙VO2max [43].\nA recent systematic review showed improvements of running economy without changes in\n˙VO2max and blood lactate concentration in pre-trained athletes after RE intervention [41].\nHowever, the results between studies differed considerably, which can be attributed to\ndifferent running speeds being used for running economy assessments and other inconsis-\ntencies in the measurement protocols [41]. Di Prampero et al. demonstrated that runners\nimproved ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "articles. Both reviews and original research were considered. Studies were included if they\nanalyzed runners, triathletes, or military personnel. Studies were excluded if the article was\nwritten in a language other than English. Although we primarily"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0003_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "424b50ff7699a6a6", "text_span_hash": "424b50ff7699a6a6", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": ", and physiological characteristics [41]. In his endurance performance model,\nJoyner considered three key physiological parameters— ˙VO2max, blood lactate concentra-\ntion, and running economy [42]. Paavolainen et al. suggested that among well-trained", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "articles. Both reviews and original research were considered. Studies were included if they\nanalyzed runners, triathletes, or military personnel. Studies were excluded if the article was\nwritten in a language other than English. Although we primarily searched for randomized\ncontrolled trials and other experimental study types (i.e., assessing the effects of RE on\nrunning economy, running biomechanics, and injury-risk), we also included cross-sectional\nstudies into the narrative where appropriate.\n2. Resistance Exercise for Improving Running Economy and Performance\nRunning performance on middle and long distances depends on anthropometric,\nbiomechanical, and physiological characteristics [41]. In his endurance performance model,\nJoyner considered three key physiological parameters— ˙VO2max, blood lactate concentra-\ntion, and running economy [42]. Paavolainen et al. suggested that among well-trained\ndistance runners, running economy is a better predictor of performance than ˙VO2max [43].\nA recent systematic review showed improvements of running economy without changes in\n˙VO2max and blood lactate concentration in pre-trained athletes after RE intervention [41].\nHowever, the results between studies differed considerably, which can be attributed to\ndifferent running speeds being used for running economy assessments and other inconsis-\ntencies in the measurement protocols [41]. Di Prampero et al. demonstrated that runners\nimproved ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": ", and physiological characteristics [41]. In his endurance performance model,\nJoyner considered three key physiological parameters— ˙VO2max, blood lactate concentra-\ntion, and running economy [42]. Paavolainen et al. suggested that among well-trained"}
