dy by Beattie et al. [51] performed on endurance runners also found\nthat RE performed in addition to endurance training improved running economy and\n˙VO2max without signiﬁcant changes in body composition, which was also replicated in\nother research [41,55]. A recent study [55] found similar results in triathletes, who improved\ntheir maximal strength, cycling ec", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "et al. [51] performed on endurance runners also found\nthat RE performed in addition to endurance training improved running economy and\n˙VO2max without signiﬁcant changes in body composition, which was also replicated in\nother research [41,55]. A rec"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0004_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "be6cac819af6796d", "text_span_hash": "be6cac819af6796d", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "other research [41,55]. A recent study [55] found similar results in triathletes, who improved\ntheir maximal strength, cycling economy, and running economy in a 26-week concurrent\nendurance and RE program. Additionally, Alcaraz-Ibañez and Rodríguez-", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "maximum strength training and plyometric training, on running economy and came to the\nsame conclusion. Namely, body composition did not change with training, which indicated\nthat the adjustment to resistance training was mainly due to adaptations on the level of the\nnervous system (e.g., increased frequency of motor unit ﬁring and increased recruitment of\n2.1. Strength Training\nFive studies included in a recent systematic review [35] reported a positive effect of\nstrength training on running economy in highly trained endurance runners. The duration\nof the interventions ranged from 8 to 12 weeks, with one study using high loads (85%\n1RM) and the other four using moderate loads (40–70% 1RM). The addition of RE to\nthe endurance training of well-trained endurance runners improved running economy\n(+4%) after 10 weeks without changes in body mass and ˙VO2 [53]. In addition, an 8-week\nintervention of heavy weight training on well-trained endurance runners improved running\neconomy (+5%) and postponed the time to exhaustion, while no changes in body mass were\nreported [54]. A study by Beattie et al. [51] performed on endurance runners also found\nthat RE performed in addition to endurance training improved running economy and\n˙VO2max without signiﬁcant changes in body composition, which was also replicated in\nother research [41,55]. A recent study [55] found similar results in triathletes, who improved\ntheir maximal strength, cycling ec", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "other research [41,55]. A recent study [55] found similar results in triathletes, who improved\ntheir maximal strength, cycling economy, and running economy in a 26-week concurrent\nendurance and RE program. Additionally, Alcaraz-Ibañez and Rodríguez-"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0004_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "84a2297ab387c510", "text_span_hash": "84a2297ab387c510", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "letes, which might be a consequence of different characteristics of the applied RE.\n2.2. Plyometric Training\nSeveral studies have also investigated the effects of plyometric training on running\neconomy. A 9-week intervention of plyometric training in", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "maximum strength training and plyometric training, on running economy and came to the\nsame conclusion. Namely, body composition did not change with training, which indicated\nthat the adjustment to resistance training was mainly due to adaptations on the level of the\nnervous system (e.g., increased frequency of motor unit ﬁring and increased recruitment of\n2.1. Strength Training\nFive studies included in a recent systematic review [35] reported a positive effect of\nstrength training on running economy in highly trained endurance runners. The duration\nof the interventions ranged from 8 to 12 weeks, with one study using high loads (85%\n1RM) and the other four using moderate loads (40–70% 1RM). The addition of RE to\nthe endurance training of well-trained endurance runners improved running economy\n(+4%) after 10 weeks without changes in body mass and ˙VO2 [53]. In addition, an 8-week\nintervention of heavy weight training on well-trained endurance runners improved running\neconomy (+5%) and postponed the time to exhaustion, while no changes in body mass were\nreported [54]. A study by Beattie et al. [51] performed on endurance runners also found\nthat RE performed in addition to endurance training improved running economy and\n˙VO2max without signiﬁcant changes in body composition, which was also replicated in\nother research [41,55]. A recent study [55] found similar results in triathletes, who improved\ntheir maximal strength, cycling ec", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "letes, which might be a consequence of different characteristics of the applied RE.\n2.2. Plyometric Training\nSeveral studies have also investigated the effects of plyometric training on running\neconomy. A 9-week intervention of plyometric training in"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0004_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "a0e26ba4166f71fb", "text_span_hash": "a0e26ba4166f71fb", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "tion of plyometric training in highly trained distance runners\nimproved running economy at 18 km/h−1 (+4.1%; p = 0.02), accompanied by trends for\nimproved 5-jump plyometric test performance (+14.7%; p = 0.11) and a lower VO2max–\nspeed slope (14%; p =", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "maximum strength training and plyometric training, on running economy and came to the\nsame conclusion. Namely, body composition did not change with training, which indicated\nthat the adjustment to resistance training was mainly due to adaptations on the level of the\nnervous system (e.g., increased frequency of motor unit ﬁring and increased recruitment of\n2.1. Strength Training\nFive studies included in a recent systematic review [35] reported a positive effect of\nstrength training on running economy in highly trained endurance runners. The duration\nof the interventions ranged from 8 to 12 weeks, with one study using high loads (85%\n1RM) and the other four using moderate loads (40–70% 1RM). The addition of RE to\nthe endurance training of well-trained endurance runners improved running economy\n(+4%) after 10 weeks without changes in body mass and ˙VO2 [53]. In addition, an 8-week\nintervention of heavy weight training on well-trained endurance runners improved running\neconomy (+5%) and postponed the time to exhaustion, while no changes in body mass were\nreported [54]. A study by Beattie et al. [51] performed on endurance runners also found\nthat RE performed in addition to endurance training improved running economy and\n˙VO2max without signiﬁcant changes in body composition, which was also replicated in\nother research [41,55]. A recent study [55] found similar results in triathletes, who improved\ntheir maximal strength, cycling ec", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "tion of plyometric training in highly trained distance runners\nimproved running economy at 18 km/h−1 (+4.1%; p = 0.02), accompanied by trends for\nimproved 5-jump plyometric test performance (+14.7%; p = 0.11) and a lower VO2max–\nspeed slope (14%; p ="}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0004_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "333014ff716d2e10", "text_span_hash": "333014ff716d2e10", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "VO2max–\nspeed slope (14%; p = 0.120) [56]. A 6-week plyometric training intervention improved\nrunning economy, CMJ height, 5-jump plyometric test performance, and lower leg quasi-\nstiffness [57]. Importantly, the increase in quasi-stiffness was corr", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "maximum strength training and plyometric training, on running economy and came to the\nsame conclusion. Namely, body composition did not change with training, which indicated\nthat the adjustment to resistance training was mainly due to adaptations on the level of the\nnervous system (e.g., increased frequency of motor unit ﬁring and increased recruitment of\n2.1. Strength Training\nFive studies included in a recent systematic review [35] reported a positive effect of\nstrength training on running economy in highly trained endurance runners. The duration\nof the interventions ranged from 8 to 12 weeks, with one study using high loads (85%\n1RM) and the other four using moderate loads (40–70% 1RM). The addition of RE to\nthe endurance training of well-trained endurance runners improved running economy\n(+4%) after 10 weeks without changes in body mass and ˙VO2 [53]. In addition, an 8-week\nintervention of heavy weight training on well-trained endurance runners improved running\neconomy (+5%) and postponed the time to exhaustion, while no changes in body mass were\nreported [54]. A study by Beattie et al. [51] performed on endurance runners also found\nthat RE performed in addition to endurance training improved running economy and\n˙VO2max without signiﬁcant changes in body composition, which was also replicated in\nother research [41,55]. A recent study [55] found similar results in triathletes, who improved\ntheir maximal strength, cycling ec", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "VO2max–\nspeed slope (14%; p = 0.120) [56]. A 6-week plyometric training intervention improved\nrunning economy, CMJ height, 5-jump plyometric test performance, and lower leg quasi-\nstiffness [57]. Importantly, the increase in quasi-stiffness was corr"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0004_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0e50a29294749ac4", "text_span_hash": "0e50a29294749ac4", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "se in quasi-stiffness was correlated with improvements\nin running economy. Increasing lower leg quasi-stiffness and reactive strength through ply-\nometric training enables better utilization of the SSC [11,57]. Interestingly, Turner et al. [58]\nrepor", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "maximum strength training and plyometric training, on running economy and came to the\nsame conclusion. Namely, body composition did not change with training, which indicated\nthat the adjustment to resistance training was mainly due to adaptations on the level of the\nnervous system (e.g., increased frequency of motor unit ﬁring and increased recruitment of\n2.1. Strength Training\nFive studies included in a recent systematic review [35] reported a positive effect of\nstrength training on running economy in highly trained endurance runners. The duration\nof the interventions ranged from 8 to 12 weeks, with one study using high loads (85%\n1RM) and the other four using moderate loads (40–70% 1RM). The addition of RE to\nthe endurance training of well-trained endurance runners improved running economy\n(+4%) after 10 weeks without changes in body mass and ˙VO2 [53]. In addition, an 8-week\nintervention of heavy weight training on well-trained endurance runners improved running\neconomy (+5%) and postponed the time to exhaustion, while no changes in body mass were\nreported [54]. A study by Beattie et al. [51] performed on endurance runners also found\nthat RE performed in addition to endurance training improved running economy and\n˙VO2max without signiﬁcant changes in body composition, which was also replicated in\nother research [41,55]. A recent study [55] found similar results in triathletes, who improved\ntheir maximal strength, cycling ec", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "se in quasi-stiffness was correlated with improvements\nin running economy. Increasing lower leg quasi-stiffness and reactive strength through ply-\nometric training enables better utilization of the SSC [11,57]. Interestingly, Turner et al. [58]\nrepor"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0004_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5c9b35e8c36ee629", "text_span_hash": "5c9b35e8c36ee629", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ngly, Turner et al. [58]\nreported that moderately trained endurance runners improved running economy after\n6 weeks of plyometric training; however, there was no improvement in vertical jump\nperformance. This could have been due to the shorter duratio", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "maximum strength training and plyometric training, on running economy and came to the\nsame conclusion. Namely, body composition did not change with training, which indicated\nthat the adjustment to resistance training was mainly due to adaptations on the level of the\nnervous system (e.g., increased frequency of motor unit ﬁring and increased recruitment of\n2.1. Strength Training\nFive studies included in a recent systematic review [35] reported a positive effect of\nstrength training on running economy in highly trained endurance runners. The duration\nof the interventions ranged from 8 to 12 weeks, with one study using high loads (85%\n1RM) and the other four using moderate loads (40–70% 1RM). The addition of RE to\nthe endurance training of well-trained endurance runners improved running economy\n(+4%) after 10 weeks without changes in body mass and ˙VO2 [53]. In addition, an 8-week\nintervention of heavy weight training on well-trained endurance runners improved running\neconomy (+5%) and postponed the time to exhaustion, while no changes in body mass were\nreported [54]. A study by Beattie et al. [51] performed on endurance runners also found\nthat RE performed in addition to endurance training improved running economy and\n˙VO2max without signiﬁcant changes in body composition, which was also replicated in\nother research [41,55]. A recent study [55] found similar results in triathletes, who improved\ntheir maximal strength, cycling ec", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ngly, Turner et al. [58]\nreported that moderately trained endurance runners improved running economy after\n6 weeks of plyometric training; however, there was no improvement in vertical jump\nperformance. This could have been due to the shorter duratio"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0004_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "be5dfd7430f0ff9b", "text_span_hash": "be5dfd7430f0ff9b", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "een due to the shorter duration of the plyometric training\n(10–15 min), which was not primarily focused on improving vertical jumping performance.\nIn a systematic review of ﬁve articles [59], it was reported that both plyometric training\nand heavy we", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "maximum strength training and plyometric training, on running economy and came to the\nsame conclusion. Namely, body composition did not change with training, which indicated\nthat the adjustment to resistance training was mainly due to adaptations on the level of the\nnervous system (e.g., increased frequency of motor unit ﬁring and increased recruitment of\n2.1. Strength Training\nFive studies included in a recent systematic review [35] reported a positive effect of\nstrength training on running economy in highly trained endurance runners. The duration\nof the interventions ranged from 8 to 12 weeks, with one study using high loads (85%\n1RM) and the other four using moderate loads (40–70% 1RM). The addition of RE to\nthe endurance training of well-trained endurance runners improved running economy\n(+4%) after 10 weeks without changes in body mass and ˙VO2 [53]. In addition, an 8-week\nintervention of heavy weight training on well-trained endurance runners improved running\neconomy (+5%) and postponed the time to exhaustion, while no changes in body mass were\nreported [54]. A study by Beattie et al. [51] performed on endurance runners also found\nthat RE performed in addition to endurance training improved running economy and\n˙VO2max without signiﬁcant changes in body composition, which was also replicated in\nother research [41,55]. A recent study [55] found similar results in triathletes, who improved\ntheir maximal strength, cycling ec", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "een due to the shorter duration of the plyometric training\n(10–15 min), which was not primarily focused on improving vertical jumping performance.\nIn a systematic review of ﬁve articles [59], it was reported that both plyometric training\nand heavy we"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0004_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3fd2d6c79e0bb7ab", "text_span_hash": "3fd2d6c79e0bb7ab", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "yometric training\nand heavy weight RE improved running economy by ~5%. In some studies, a short-term\n(4-week) heavy weight training intervention improved running economy (+6.3%; 44.3 ± 4.9\nto 47.3 ± 6.8 mlkg−1min−1), but plyometric training did not [", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "maximum strength training and plyometric training, on running economy and came to the\nsame conclusion. Namely, body composition did not change with training, which indicated\nthat the adjustment to resistance training was mainly due to adaptations on the level of the\nnervous system (e.g., increased frequency of motor unit ﬁring and increased recruitment of\n2.1. Strength Training\nFive studies included in a recent systematic review [35] reported a positive effect of\nstrength training on running economy in highly trained endurance runners. The duration\nof the interventions ranged from 8 to 12 weeks, with one study using high loads (85%\n1RM) and the other four using moderate loads (40–70% 1RM). The addition of RE to\nthe endurance training of well-trained endurance runners improved running economy\n(+4%) after 10 weeks without changes in body mass and ˙VO2 [53]. In addition, an 8-week\nintervention of heavy weight training on well-trained endurance runners improved running\neconomy (+5%) and postponed the time to exhaustion, while no changes in body mass were\nreported [54]. A study by Beattie et al. [51] performed on endurance runners also found\nthat RE performed in addition to endurance training improved running economy and\n˙VO2max without signiﬁcant changes in body composition, which was also replicated in\nother research [41,55]. A recent study [55] found similar results in triathletes, who improved\ntheir maximal strength, cycling ec", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "yometric training\nand heavy weight RE improved running economy by ~5%. In some studies, a short-term\n(4-week) heavy weight training intervention improved running economy (+6.3%; 44.3 ± 4.9\nto 47.3 ± 6.8 mlkg−1min−1), but plyometric training did not ["}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0004_c0016", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0c1fcd61ba18e9d8", "text_span_hash": "0c1fcd61ba18e9d8", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 16, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "plyometric training did not [60].\n2.3. Combination of Different RE Modialities\nSkovgaard et al. [61] studied the effect of a combination of strength training and speed\nendurance training on the athletic performance of runners. They concluded that th", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "maximum strength training and plyometric training, on running economy and came to the\nsame conclusion. Namely, body composition did not change with training, which indicated\nthat the adjustment to resistance training was mainly due to adaptations on the level of the\nnervous system (e.g., increased frequency of motor unit ﬁring and increased recruitment of\n2.1. Strength Training\nFive studies included in a recent systematic review [35] reported a positive effect of\nstrength training on running economy in highly trained endurance runners. The duration\nof the interventions ranged from 8 to 12 weeks, with one study using high loads (85%\n1RM) and the other four using moderate loads (40–70% 1RM). The addition of RE to\nthe endurance training of well-trained endurance runners improved running economy\n(+4%) after 10 weeks without changes in body mass and ˙VO2 [53]. In addition, an 8-week\nintervention of heavy weight training on well-trained endurance runners improved running\neconomy (+5%) and postponed the time to exhaustion, while no changes in body mass were\nreported [54]. A study by Beattie et al. [51] performed on endurance runners also found\nthat RE performed in addition to endurance training improved running economy and\n˙VO2max without signiﬁcant changes in body composition, which was also replicated in\nother research [41,55]. A recent study [55] found similar results in triathletes, who improved\ntheir maximal strength, cycling ec", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "plyometric training did not [60].\n2.3. Combination of Different RE Modialities\nSkovgaard et al. [61] studied the effect of a combination of strength training and speed\nendurance training on the athletic performance of runners. They concluded that th"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0004_c0018", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "db95678fdc3ac530", "text_span_hash": "db95678fdc3ac530", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 18, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "x\ntraining, which combines heavy weight training and plyometric exercises with endurance\ntraining, improved maximum muscle strength and power, running economy, and velocity\nat VO2max in well-trained endurance runners, which was not the case in resist", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "maximum strength training and plyometric training, on running economy and came to the\nsame conclusion. Namely, body composition did not change with training, which indicated\nthat the adjustment to resistance training was mainly due to adaptations on the level of the\nnervous system (e.g., increased frequency of motor unit ﬁring and increased recruitment of\n2.1. Strength Training\nFive studies included in a recent systematic review [35] reported a positive effect of\nstrength training on running economy in highly trained endurance runners. The duration\nof the interventions ranged from 8 to 12 weeks, with one study using high loads (85%\n1RM) and the other four using moderate loads (40–70% 1RM). The addition of RE to\nthe endurance training of well-trained endurance runners improved running economy\n(+4%) after 10 weeks without changes in body mass and ˙VO2 [53]. In addition, an 8-week\nintervention of heavy weight training on well-trained endurance runners improved running\neconomy (+5%) and postponed the time to exhaustion, while no changes in body mass were\nreported [54]. A study by Beattie et al. [51] performed on endurance runners also found\nthat RE performed in addition to endurance training improved running economy and\n˙VO2max without signiﬁcant changes in body composition, which was also replicated in\nother research [41,55]. A recent study [55] found similar results in triathletes, who improved\ntheir maximal strength, cycling ec", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "x\ntraining, which combines heavy weight training and plyometric exercises with endurance\ntraining, improved maximum muscle strength and power, running economy, and velocity\nat VO2max in well-trained endurance runners, which was not the case in resist"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0004_c0019", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4b3cc85e7ca5bd88", "text_span_hash": "4b3cc85e7ca5bd88", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 19, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ich was not the case in resistance training,\nwhich consisted of only strength endurance exercises. A study by Barnes et al. [63] exam-\nined the impact of heavy weight training and plyometric training on endurance runners", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "maximum strength training and plyometric training, on running economy and came to the\nsame conclusion. Namely, body composition did not change with training, which indicated\nthat the adjustment to resistance training was mainly due to adaptations on the level of the\nnervous system (e.g., increased frequency of motor unit ﬁring and increased recruitment of\n2.1. Strength Training\nFive studies included in a recent systematic review [35] reported a positive effect of\nstrength training on running economy in highly trained endurance runners. The duration\nof the interventions ranged from 8 to 12 weeks, with one study using high loads (85%\n1RM) and the other four using moderate loads (40–70% 1RM). The addition of RE to\nthe endurance training of well-trained endurance runners improved running economy\n(+4%) after 10 weeks without changes in body mass and ˙VO2 [53]. In addition, an 8-week\nintervention of heavy weight training on well-trained endurance runners improved running\neconomy (+5%) and postponed the time to exhaustion, while no changes in body mass were\nreported [54]. A study by Beattie et al. [51] performed on endurance runners also found\nthat RE performed in addition to endurance training improved running economy and\n˙VO2max without signiﬁcant changes in body composition, which was also replicated in\nother research [41,55]. A recent study [55] found similar results in triathletes, who improved\ntheir maximal strength, cycling ec", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ich was not the case in resistance training,\nwhich consisted of only strength endurance exercises. A study by Barnes et al. [63] exam-\nined the impact of heavy weight training and plyometric training on endurance runners"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0005_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ab0009d9fea7f6d0", "text_span_hash": "ab0009d9fea7f6d0", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "during the competitive period. They found that, in accordance to previous research, the\nbody composition did not change, but there were some unexpected results—the effects\nof the intervention on running economy varied signiﬁcantly between male and fe", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "during the competitive period. They found that, in accordance to previous research, the\nbody composition did not change, but there were some unexpected results—the effects\nof the intervention on running economy varied signiﬁcantly between male and female\nparticipants. In particular, the addition of plyometric or heavy weight training during\nthe competition period had a negative impact on the competitive performance in males,\nwhile a positive effect was seen in female endurance runners [63]. Further research is\nurgently needed to reveal possible differences in adaptations to RE between male and\n2.4. Isometric Training\nIt has been suggested that isometric training could elicit similar or even higher adapta-\ntions in running economy than plyometric training. Two older studies reported an improve-\nment in running economy (~4–7%) after isometric plantar ﬂexor strength training [64,65].\nRecently, Lum et al. [66] directly compared the effects of isometric and plyometric train-\ning in a 6-week interventional study. Although both training types improved running\neconomy, the isometric group surprisingly improved more, which was also reﬂected in\na higher maximal aerobic speed. It could be that isometric training is superior in terms\nof increasing muscle/tendon stiffness, which was indicated in some of the previous stud-\nies [67]; however, leg quasi-stiffness during running in the study by Lum et al. [66] was not\nchanged in either group. ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "during the competitive period. They found that, in accordance to previous research, the\nbody composition did not change, but there were some unexpected results—the effects\nof the intervention on running economy varied signiﬁcantly between male and fe"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0005_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "440f2f81a85e305e", "text_span_hash": "440f2f81a85e305e", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "gniﬁcantly between male and female\nparticipants. In particular, the addition of plyometric or heavy weight training during\nthe competition period had a negative impact on the competitive performance in males,\nwhile a positive effect was seen in femal", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "during the competitive period. They found that, in accordance to previous research, the\nbody composition did not change, but there were some unexpected results—the effects\nof the intervention on running economy varied signiﬁcantly between male and female\nparticipants. In particular, the addition of plyometric or heavy weight training during\nthe competition period had a negative impact on the competitive performance in males,\nwhile a positive effect was seen in female endurance runners [63]. Further research is\nurgently needed to reveal possible differences in adaptations to RE between male and\n2.4. Isometric Training\nIt has been suggested that isometric training could elicit similar or even higher adapta-\ntions in running economy than plyometric training. Two older studies reported an improve-\nment in running economy (~4–7%) after isometric plantar ﬂexor strength training [64,65].\nRecently, Lum et al. [66] directly compared the effects of isometric and plyometric train-\ning in a 6-week interventional study. Although both training types improved running\neconomy, the isometric group surprisingly improved more, which was also reﬂected in\na higher maximal aerobic speed. It could be that isometric training is superior in terms\nof increasing muscle/tendon stiffness, which was indicated in some of the previous stud-\nies [67]; however, leg quasi-stiffness during running in the study by Lum et al. [66] was not\nchanged in either group. ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "gniﬁcantly between male and female\nparticipants. In particular, the addition of plyometric or heavy weight training during\nthe competition period had a negative impact on the competitive performance in males,\nwhile a positive effect was seen in femal"}
