etric group surprisingly improved more, which was also reﬂected in\na higher maximal aerobic speed. It could be that isometric training is superior in terms\nof increasing muscle/tendon stiffness, which was indicated in some of the previous stud-\nies [67]; however, leg quasi-stiffness during running in the study by Lum et al. [66] was not\nchanged in either group. ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "itive effect was seen in female endurance runners [63]. Further research is\nurgently needed to reveal possible differences in adaptations to RE between male and\n2.4. Isometric Training\nIt has been suggested that isometric training could elicit simila"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0005_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5039de1236f86dd7", "text_span_hash": "5039de1236f86dd7", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ning [64,65].\nRecently, Lum et al. [66] directly compared the effects of isometric and plyometric train-\ning in a 6-week interventional study. Although both training types improved running\neconomy, the isometric group surprisingly improved more, whic", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "during the competitive period. They found that, in accordance to previous research, the\nbody composition did not change, but there were some unexpected results—the effects\nof the intervention on running economy varied signiﬁcantly between male and female\nparticipants. In particular, the addition of plyometric or heavy weight training during\nthe competition period had a negative impact on the competitive performance in males,\nwhile a positive effect was seen in female endurance runners [63]. Further research is\nurgently needed to reveal possible differences in adaptations to RE between male and\n2.4. Isometric Training\nIt has been suggested that isometric training could elicit similar or even higher adapta-\ntions in running economy than plyometric training. Two older studies reported an improve-\nment in running economy (~4–7%) after isometric plantar ﬂexor strength training [64,65].\nRecently, Lum et al. [66] directly compared the effects of isometric and plyometric train-\ning in a 6-week interventional study. Although both training types improved running\neconomy, the isometric group surprisingly improved more, which was also reﬂected in\na higher maximal aerobic speed. It could be that isometric training is superior in terms\nof increasing muscle/tendon stiffness, which was indicated in some of the previous stud-\nies [67]; however, leg quasi-stiffness during running in the study by Lum et al. [66] was not\nchanged in either group. ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ning [64,65].\nRecently, Lum et al. [66] directly compared the effects of isometric and plyometric train-\ning in a 6-week interventional study. Although both training types improved running\neconomy, the isometric group surprisingly improved more, whic"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0005_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "38a0374d660d24a6", "text_span_hash": "38a0374d660d24a6", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ious stud-\nies [67]; however, leg quasi-stiffness during running in the study by Lum et al. [66] was not\nchanged in either group. While further research is clearly needed, the authors concluded\nthat isometric training could be a good alternative to p", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "during the competitive period. They found that, in accordance to previous research, the\nbody composition did not change, but there were some unexpected results—the effects\nof the intervention on running economy varied signiﬁcantly between male and female\nparticipants. In particular, the addition of plyometric or heavy weight training during\nthe competition period had a negative impact on the competitive performance in males,\nwhile a positive effect was seen in female endurance runners [63]. Further research is\nurgently needed to reveal possible differences in adaptations to RE between male and\n2.4. Isometric Training\nIt has been suggested that isometric training could elicit similar or even higher adapta-\ntions in running economy than plyometric training. Two older studies reported an improve-\nment in running economy (~4–7%) after isometric plantar ﬂexor strength training [64,65].\nRecently, Lum et al. [66] directly compared the effects of isometric and plyometric train-\ning in a 6-week interventional study. Although both training types improved running\neconomy, the isometric group surprisingly improved more, which was also reﬂected in\na higher maximal aerobic speed. It could be that isometric training is superior in terms\nof increasing muscle/tendon stiffness, which was indicated in some of the previous stud-\nies [67]; however, leg quasi-stiffness during running in the study by Lum et al. [66] was not\nchanged in either group. ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ious stud-\nies [67]; however, leg quasi-stiffness during running in the study by Lum et al. [66] was not\nchanged in either group. While further research is clearly needed, the authors concluded\nthat isometric training could be a good alternative to p"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0005_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5bd97d0d28812db8", "text_span_hash": "5bd97d0d28812db8", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "uld be a good alternative to plyometric training, especially during\ntraining cycles when runners already experience a high volume of load during their regular\n2.5. RE Coupled with Vibration Training\nAnother attractive avenue for research is the inclu", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "during the competitive period. They found that, in accordance to previous research, the\nbody composition did not change, but there were some unexpected results—the effects\nof the intervention on running economy varied signiﬁcantly between male and female\nparticipants. In particular, the addition of plyometric or heavy weight training during\nthe competition period had a negative impact on the competitive performance in males,\nwhile a positive effect was seen in female endurance runners [63]. Further research is\nurgently needed to reveal possible differences in adaptations to RE between male and\n2.4. Isometric Training\nIt has been suggested that isometric training could elicit similar or even higher adapta-\ntions in running economy than plyometric training. Two older studies reported an improve-\nment in running economy (~4–7%) after isometric plantar ﬂexor strength training [64,65].\nRecently, Lum et al. [66] directly compared the effects of isometric and plyometric train-\ning in a 6-week interventional study. Although both training types improved running\neconomy, the isometric group surprisingly improved more, which was also reﬂected in\na higher maximal aerobic speed. It could be that isometric training is superior in terms\nof increasing muscle/tendon stiffness, which was indicated in some of the previous stud-\nies [67]; however, leg quasi-stiffness during running in the study by Lum et al. [66] was not\nchanged in either group. ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "uld be a good alternative to plyometric training, especially during\ntraining cycles when runners already experience a high volume of load during their regular\n2.5. RE Coupled with Vibration Training\nAnother attractive avenue for research is the inclu"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0005_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f4606a29ff08aeb5", "text_span_hash": "f4606a29ff08aeb5", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "enue for research is the inclusion of vibration platforms into the\ntraining regimen. Vibration exposure is thought to affect the central and peripheral nerves\nand enhance neuromuscular activity, thereby increasing force production [68]. Among other\nt", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "during the competitive period. They found that, in accordance to previous research, the\nbody composition did not change, but there were some unexpected results—the effects\nof the intervention on running economy varied signiﬁcantly between male and female\nparticipants. In particular, the addition of plyometric or heavy weight training during\nthe competition period had a negative impact on the competitive performance in males,\nwhile a positive effect was seen in female endurance runners [63]. Further research is\nurgently needed to reveal possible differences in adaptations to RE between male and\n2.4. Isometric Training\nIt has been suggested that isometric training could elicit similar or even higher adapta-\ntions in running economy than plyometric training. Two older studies reported an improve-\nment in running economy (~4–7%) after isometric plantar ﬂexor strength training [64,65].\nRecently, Lum et al. [66] directly compared the effects of isometric and plyometric train-\ning in a 6-week interventional study. Although both training types improved running\neconomy, the isometric group surprisingly improved more, which was also reﬂected in\na higher maximal aerobic speed. It could be that isometric training is superior in terms\nof increasing muscle/tendon stiffness, which was indicated in some of the previous stud-\nies [67]; however, leg quasi-stiffness during running in the study by Lum et al. [66] was not\nchanged in either group. ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "enue for research is the inclusion of vibration platforms into the\ntraining regimen. Vibration exposure is thought to affect the central and peripheral nerves\nand enhance neuromuscular activity, thereby increasing force production [68]. Among other\nt"}
