etric group surprisingly improved more, which was also reﬂected in\na higher maximal aerobic speed. It could be that isometric training is superior in terms\nof increasing muscle/tendon stiffness, which was indicated in some of the previous stud-\nies [67]; however, leg quasi-stiffness during running in the study by Lum et al. [66] was not\nchanged in either group. ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "production [68]. Among other\nthings, the improvement of running economy is thought to be due to altered neuromuscular\nproperties leading to increased muscle stiffness and the increased recruitment of motor\nunits [69]. However, according to available"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0005_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "96bdf4f3668c7309", "text_span_hash": "96bdf4f3668c7309", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "wever, according to available evidence, performing RE on a vibrating platform\nhas no particular contribution to improving running economy compared to RE alone [70].\n2.6. Effects of RE Program Interruption\nInterruption of the RE training results in th", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "during the competitive period. They found that, in accordance to previous research, the\nbody composition did not change, but there were some unexpected results—the effects\nof the intervention on running economy varied signiﬁcantly between male and female\nparticipants. In particular, the addition of plyometric or heavy weight training during\nthe competition period had a negative impact on the competitive performance in males,\nwhile a positive effect was seen in female endurance runners [63]. Further research is\nurgently needed to reveal possible differences in adaptations to RE between male and\n2.4. Isometric Training\nIt has been suggested that isometric training could elicit similar or even higher adapta-\ntions in running economy than plyometric training. Two older studies reported an improve-\nment in running economy (~4–7%) after isometric plantar ﬂexor strength training [64,65].\nRecently, Lum et al. [66] directly compared the effects of isometric and plyometric train-\ning in a 6-week interventional study. Although both training types improved running\neconomy, the isometric group surprisingly improved more, which was also reﬂected in\na higher maximal aerobic speed. It could be that isometric training is superior in terms\nof increasing muscle/tendon stiffness, which was indicated in some of the previous stud-\nies [67]; however, leg quasi-stiffness during running in the study by Lum et al. [66] was not\nchanged in either group. ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "wever, according to available evidence, performing RE on a vibrating platform\nhas no particular contribution to improving running economy compared to RE alone [70].\n2.6. Effects of RE Program Interruption\nInterruption of the RE training results in th"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0005_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5c36f547999c6c1d", "text_span_hash": "5c36f547999c6c1d", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "the RE training results in the reduction of strength endurance, maxi-\nmum strength, and power [71]. Energy expenditure levels during running are maintained\neven after a 2-week period without training [72]. A recent study investigated the effects\nof", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "during the competitive period. They found that, in accordance to previous research, the\nbody composition did not change, but there were some unexpected results—the effects\nof the intervention on running economy varied signiﬁcantly between male and female\nparticipants. In particular, the addition of plyometric or heavy weight training during\nthe competition period had a negative impact on the competitive performance in males,\nwhile a positive effect was seen in female endurance runners [63]. Further research is\nurgently needed to reveal possible differences in adaptations to RE between male and\n2.4. Isometric Training\nIt has been suggested that isometric training could elicit similar or even higher adapta-\ntions in running economy than plyometric training. Two older studies reported an improve-\nment in running economy (~4–7%) after isometric plantar ﬂexor strength training [64,65].\nRecently, Lum et al. [66] directly compared the effects of isometric and plyometric train-\ning in a 6-week interventional study. Although both training types improved running\neconomy, the isometric group surprisingly improved more, which was also reﬂected in\na higher maximal aerobic speed. It could be that isometric training is superior in terms\nof increasing muscle/tendon stiffness, which was indicated in some of the previous stud-\nies [67]; however, leg quasi-stiffness during running in the study by Lum et al. [66] was not\nchanged in either group. ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "the RE training results in the reduction of strength endurance, maxi-\nmum strength, and power [71]. Energy expenditure levels during running are maintained\neven after a 2-week period without training [72]. A recent study investigated the effects\nof"}
