{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0005_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ce7ed129682b177d", "text_span_hash": "ce7ed129682b177d", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ﬁrst period\n(−2.4%), with a possible further reduction after 4 weeks without RE training (−4.4%).\nWhile this research was conducted on a very small sample and should be interpreted with\ncaution, the results indicate that the incorporation of RE shoul", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "during the competitive period. They found that, in accordance to previous research, the\nbody composition did not change, but there were some unexpected results—the effects\nof the intervention on running economy varied signiﬁcantly between male and female\nparticipants. In particular, the addition of plyometric or heavy weight training during\nthe competition period had a negative impact on the competitive performance in males,\nwhile a positive effect was seen in female endurance runners [63]. Further research is\nurgently needed to reveal possible differences in adaptations to RE between male and\n2.4. Isometric Training\nIt has been suggested that isometric training could elicit similar or even higher adapta-\ntions in running economy than plyometric training. Two older studies reported an improve-\nment in running economy (~4–7%) after isometric plantar ﬂexor strength training [64,65].\nRecently, Lum et al. [66] directly compared the effects of isometric and plyometric train-\ning in a 6-week interventional study. Although both training types improved running\neconomy, the isometric group surprisingly improved more, which was also reﬂected in\na higher maximal aerobic speed. It could be that isometric training is superior in terms\nof increasing muscle/tendon stiffness, which was indicated in some of the previous stud-\nies [67]; however, leg quasi-stiffness during running in the study by Lum et al. [66] was not\nchanged in either group. ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ﬁrst period\n(−2.4%), with a possible further reduction after 4 weeks without RE training (−4.4%).\nWhile this research was conducted on a very small sample and should be interpreted with\ncaution, the results indicate that the incorporation of RE shoul"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0005_c0016", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b614a39387e18bcb", "text_span_hash": "b614a39387e18bcb", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 16, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "the incorporation of RE should be emphasized in the\npre-competition phase, with a possible detraining or cessation of RE before the competition.\nBerryman et al. [71] also found that neuromuscular abilities were maintained after the\ninterruption peri", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "during the competitive period. They found that, in accordance to previous research, the\nbody composition did not change, but there were some unexpected results—the effects\nof the intervention on running economy varied signiﬁcantly between male and female\nparticipants. In particular, the addition of plyometric or heavy weight training during\nthe competition period had a negative impact on the competitive performance in males,\nwhile a positive effect was seen in female endurance runners [63]. Further research is\nurgently needed to reveal possible differences in adaptations to RE between male and\n2.4. Isometric Training\nIt has been suggested that isometric training could elicit similar or even higher adapta-\ntions in running economy than plyometric training. Two older studies reported an improve-\nment in running economy (~4–7%) after isometric plantar ﬂexor strength training [64,65].\nRecently, Lum et al. [66] directly compared the effects of isometric and plyometric train-\ning in a 6-week interventional study. Although both training types improved running\neconomy, the isometric group surprisingly improved more, which was also reﬂected in\na higher maximal aerobic speed. It could be that isometric training is superior in terms\nof increasing muscle/tendon stiffness, which was indicated in some of the previous stud-\nies [67]; however, leg quasi-stiffness during running in the study by Lum et al. [66] was not\nchanged in either group. ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "the incorporation of RE should be emphasized in the\npre-competition phase, with a possible detraining or cessation of RE before the competition.\nBerryman et al. [71] also found that neuromuscular abilities were maintained after the\ninterruption peri"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0005_c0017", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "79cb991263a77237", "text_span_hash": "79cb991263a77237", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 17, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ed after the\ninterruption period. A meta-analysis found that a short-term interruption of resistance\ntraining reduces muscle strength, while the magnitude of the effect varies according to the\ntype of training, age, and duration of the interruption [", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "during the competitive period. They found that, in accordance to previous research, the\nbody composition did not change, but there were some unexpected results—the effects\nof the intervention on running economy varied signiﬁcantly between male and female\nparticipants. In particular, the addition of plyometric or heavy weight training during\nthe competition period had a negative impact on the competitive performance in males,\nwhile a positive effect was seen in female endurance runners [63]. Further research is\nurgently needed to reveal possible differences in adaptations to RE between male and\n2.4. Isometric Training\nIt has been suggested that isometric training could elicit similar or even higher adapta-\ntions in running economy than plyometric training. Two older studies reported an improve-\nment in running economy (~4–7%) after isometric plantar ﬂexor strength training [64,65].\nRecently, Lum et al. [66] directly compared the effects of isometric and plyometric train-\ning in a 6-week interventional study. Although both training types improved running\neconomy, the isometric group surprisingly improved more, which was also reﬂected in\na higher maximal aerobic speed. It could be that isometric training is superior in terms\nof increasing muscle/tendon stiffness, which was indicated in some of the previous stud-\nies [67]; however, leg quasi-stiffness during running in the study by Lum et al. [66] was not\nchanged in either group. ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ed after the\ninterruption period. A meta-analysis found that a short-term interruption of resistance\ntraining reduces muscle strength, while the magnitude of the effect varies according to the\ntype of training, age, and duration of the interruption ["}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0005_c0018", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ec17724de3865edc", "text_span_hash": "ec17724de3865edc", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 18, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "duration of the interruption [73]. However, this meta-analysis\nwas not speciﬁc to RE in endurance runners. According to current ﬁndings, additional\nresearch is clearly needed in this area [71].", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "during the competitive period. They found that, in accordance to previous research, the\nbody composition did not change, but there were some unexpected results—the effects\nof the intervention on running economy varied signiﬁcantly between male and female\nparticipants. In particular, the addition of plyometric or heavy weight training during\nthe competition period had a negative impact on the competitive performance in males,\nwhile a positive effect was seen in female endurance runners [63]. Further research is\nurgently needed to reveal possible differences in adaptations to RE between male and\n2.4. Isometric Training\nIt has been suggested that isometric training could elicit similar or even higher adapta-\ntions in running economy than plyometric training. Two older studies reported an improve-\nment in running economy (~4–7%) after isometric plantar ﬂexor strength training [64,65].\nRecently, Lum et al. [66] directly compared the effects of isometric and plyometric train-\ning in a 6-week interventional study. Although both training types improved running\neconomy, the isometric group surprisingly improved more, which was also reﬂected in\na higher maximal aerobic speed. It could be that isometric training is superior in terms\nof increasing muscle/tendon stiffness, which was indicated in some of the previous stud-\nies [67]; however, leg quasi-stiffness during running in the study by Lum et al. [66] was not\nchanged in either group. ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "duration of the interruption [73]. However, this meta-analysis\nwas not speciﬁc to RE in endurance runners. According to current ﬁndings, additional\nresearch is clearly needed in this area [71]."}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0006_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "47fe3beb19b876e2", "text_span_hash": "47fe3beb19b876e2", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "2.7. Acute Effects of RE and Interference with Running Training\nIt should also be pointed out that the acute effect of RE (both strength training and\nplyometric training) is associated with increased energy expenditure during running and\nthus reduced", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "2.7. Acute Effects of RE and Interference with Running Training\nIt should also be pointed out that the acute effect of RE (both strength training and\nplyometric training) is associated with increased energy expenditure during running and\nthus reduced running economy, which could pose a problem for runners. Namely, RE could\nimpact the quality of endurance training. Marcello et al. [74] examined the acute effects\nof a combination of strength and plyometric training on running economy in endurance\nrunners. Elevated ˙VO2 and post-workout energy expenditure indicated that strength and\nplyometric training reduced running economy at moderate running intensities; however,\nthis negative effect disappeared in less than 24 h, which was also observed in a previous\nstudy [75]. Acutely, strength training leads to decreased muscle activation during running,\nleading to the reduced ability to produce force [76]. Similarly, activation inhibition occurs\nwith plyometric training. Paschalis et al. [77] analyzed the effect of eccentric exercise on\nindirect indicators of muscle damage (delayed onset of muscle soreness, range of motion,\nand isometric force) and running economy. The training consisted of 12 × 10 repetitions\nof maximal voluntary contractions by each leg. Post-exercise indirect muscle damage\nmarkers were signiﬁcantly altered after 24 to 72 h (p < 0.05), without signiﬁcant changes in\nrunning economy (p > 0.05). Later, the same research g", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "2.7. Acute Effects of RE and Interference with Running Training\nIt should also be pointed out that the acute effect of RE (both strength training and\nplyometric training) is associated with increased energy expenditure during running and\nthus reduced"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0006_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "348dac7401d293f0", "text_span_hash": "348dac7401d293f0", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "uring running and\nthus reduced running economy, which could pose a problem for runners. Namely, RE could\nimpact the quality of endurance training. Marcello et al. [74] examined the acute effects\nof a combination of strength and plyometric training on", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "2.7. Acute Effects of RE and Interference with Running Training\nIt should also be pointed out that the acute effect of RE (both strength training and\nplyometric training) is associated with increased energy expenditure during running and\nthus reduced running economy, which could pose a problem for runners. Namely, RE could\nimpact the quality of endurance training. Marcello et al. [74] examined the acute effects\nof a combination of strength and plyometric training on running economy in endurance\nrunners. Elevated ˙VO2 and post-workout energy expenditure indicated that strength and\nplyometric training reduced running economy at moderate running intensities; however,\nthis negative effect disappeared in less than 24 h, which was also observed in a previous\nstudy [75]. Acutely, strength training leads to decreased muscle activation during running,\nleading to the reduced ability to produce force [76]. Similarly, activation inhibition occurs\nwith plyometric training. Paschalis et al. [77] analyzed the effect of eccentric exercise on\nindirect indicators of muscle damage (delayed onset of muscle soreness, range of motion,\nand isometric force) and running economy. The training consisted of 12 × 10 repetitions\nof maximal voluntary contractions by each leg. Post-exercise indirect muscle damage\nmarkers were signiﬁcantly altered after 24 to 72 h (p < 0.05), without signiﬁcant changes in\nrunning economy (p > 0.05). Later, the same research g", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "uring running and\nthus reduced running economy, which could pose a problem for runners. Namely, RE could\nimpact the quality of endurance training. Marcello et al. [74] examined the acute effects\nof a combination of strength and plyometric training on"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0006_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3679b968debec63e", "text_span_hash": "3679b968debec63e", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "gth and plyometric training on running economy in endurance\nrunners. Elevated ˙VO2 and post-workout energy expenditure indicated that strength and\nplyometric training reduced running economy at moderate running intensities; however,\nthis negative eff", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "2.7. Acute Effects of RE and Interference with Running Training\nIt should also be pointed out that the acute effect of RE (both strength training and\nplyometric training) is associated with increased energy expenditure during running and\nthus reduced running economy, which could pose a problem for runners. Namely, RE could\nimpact the quality of endurance training. Marcello et al. [74] examined the acute effects\nof a combination of strength and plyometric training on running economy in endurance\nrunners. Elevated ˙VO2 and post-workout energy expenditure indicated that strength and\nplyometric training reduced running economy at moderate running intensities; however,\nthis negative effect disappeared in less than 24 h, which was also observed in a previous\nstudy [75]. Acutely, strength training leads to decreased muscle activation during running,\nleading to the reduced ability to produce force [76]. Similarly, activation inhibition occurs\nwith plyometric training. Paschalis et al. [77] analyzed the effect of eccentric exercise on\nindirect indicators of muscle damage (delayed onset of muscle soreness, range of motion,\nand isometric force) and running economy. The training consisted of 12 × 10 repetitions\nof maximal voluntary contractions by each leg. Post-exercise indirect muscle damage\nmarkers were signiﬁcantly altered after 24 to 72 h (p < 0.05), without signiﬁcant changes in\nrunning economy (p > 0.05). Later, the same research g", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "gth and plyometric training on running economy in endurance\nrunners. Elevated ˙VO2 and post-workout energy expenditure indicated that strength and\nplyometric training reduced running economy at moderate running intensities; however,\nthis negative eff"}
