oving running economy [90,91]. Although there seems to be some merit in\nattempts to optimize stride frequency, such approaches are questionable for runners who\nhave likely achieved optimal stride frequency through years of training. Stride length and\nstride frequency may also affect how far ahead of the body the foot strikes the ground. In\nrunning techniques wit", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "running\neconomy. These factors can be categorized as spatiotemporal (parameters relating to\nchanges in the phase of the gait cycle, such as ground contact time, stride length, etc.),\nlower limb kinematics (lower limb joint angles), kinetics (ground r"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0007_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "96fc822b10fb0b89", "text_span_hash": "96fc822b10fb0b89", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "nt angles), kinetics (ground reaction forces), and\nneuromuscular factors (muscle activation and coactivation). Regarding the spatiotemporal\nfactors, the literature reports that self-selected stride length and frequency is optimal or\nvery near to bein", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "were very small [87]. Further research is needed to assess the effects of uphill running on\nrunning economy. As it currently stands, it seems that this method is inferior for improving\nrunning economy in comparison to RE.\n3. Resistance Exercise for Modifying Running Technique\nRunning technique is an important aspect for movement economy and injury preven-\ntion. Several biomechanical factors were raised in the literature that may affect running\neconomy. These factors can be categorized as spatiotemporal (parameters relating to\nchanges in the phase of the gait cycle, such as ground contact time, stride length, etc.),\nlower limb kinematics (lower limb joint angles), kinetics (ground reaction forces), and\nneuromuscular factors (muscle activation and coactivation). Regarding the spatiotemporal\nfactors, the literature reports that self-selected stride length and frequency is optimal or\nvery near to being optimal for maximizing economy [88,89]. On the other hand, some of\nthe literature reports that optimal stride length could be 3% shorter than preferred stride\nlength for improving running economy [90,91]. Although there seems to be some merit in\nattempts to optimize stride frequency, such approaches are questionable for runners who\nhave likely achieved optimal stride frequency through years of training. Stride length and\nstride frequency may also affect how far ahead of the body the foot strikes the ground. In\nrunning techniques wit", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "nt angles), kinetics (ground reaction forces), and\nneuromuscular factors (muscle activation and coactivation). Regarding the spatiotemporal\nfactors, the literature reports that self-selected stride length and frequency is optimal or\nvery near to bein"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0007_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2840a6eb1955f43f", "text_span_hash": "2840a6eb1955f43f", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "s optimal or\nvery near to being optimal for maximizing economy [88,89]. On the other hand, some of\nthe literature reports that optimal stride length could be 3% shorter than preferred stride\nlength for improving running economy [90,91]. Although ther", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "were very small [87]. Further research is needed to assess the effects of uphill running on\nrunning economy. As it currently stands, it seems that this method is inferior for improving\nrunning economy in comparison to RE.\n3. Resistance Exercise for Modifying Running Technique\nRunning technique is an important aspect for movement economy and injury preven-\ntion. Several biomechanical factors were raised in the literature that may affect running\neconomy. These factors can be categorized as spatiotemporal (parameters relating to\nchanges in the phase of the gait cycle, such as ground contact time, stride length, etc.),\nlower limb kinematics (lower limb joint angles), kinetics (ground reaction forces), and\nneuromuscular factors (muscle activation and coactivation). Regarding the spatiotemporal\nfactors, the literature reports that self-selected stride length and frequency is optimal or\nvery near to being optimal for maximizing economy [88,89]. On the other hand, some of\nthe literature reports that optimal stride length could be 3% shorter than preferred stride\nlength for improving running economy [90,91]. Although there seems to be some merit in\nattempts to optimize stride frequency, such approaches are questionable for runners who\nhave likely achieved optimal stride frequency through years of training. Stride length and\nstride frequency may also affect how far ahead of the body the foot strikes the ground. In\nrunning techniques wit", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "s optimal or\nvery near to being optimal for maximizing economy [88,89]. On the other hand, some of\nthe literature reports that optimal stride length could be 3% shorter than preferred stride\nlength for improving running economy [90,91]. Although ther"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0007_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8ee5cdda2be05649", "text_span_hash": "8ee5cdda2be05649", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "economy [90,91]. Although there seems to be some merit in\nattempts to optimize stride frequency, such approaches are questionable for runners who\nhave likely achieved optimal stride frequency through years of training. Stride length and\nstride freque", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "were very small [87]. Further research is needed to assess the effects of uphill running on\nrunning economy. As it currently stands, it seems that this method is inferior for improving\nrunning economy in comparison to RE.\n3. Resistance Exercise for Modifying Running Technique\nRunning technique is an important aspect for movement economy and injury preven-\ntion. Several biomechanical factors were raised in the literature that may affect running\neconomy. These factors can be categorized as spatiotemporal (parameters relating to\nchanges in the phase of the gait cycle, such as ground contact time, stride length, etc.),\nlower limb kinematics (lower limb joint angles), kinetics (ground reaction forces), and\nneuromuscular factors (muscle activation and coactivation). Regarding the spatiotemporal\nfactors, the literature reports that self-selected stride length and frequency is optimal or\nvery near to being optimal for maximizing economy [88,89]. On the other hand, some of\nthe literature reports that optimal stride length could be 3% shorter than preferred stride\nlength for improving running economy [90,91]. Although there seems to be some merit in\nattempts to optimize stride frequency, such approaches are questionable for runners who\nhave likely achieved optimal stride frequency through years of training. Stride length and\nstride frequency may also affect how far ahead of the body the foot strikes the ground. In\nrunning techniques wit", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "economy [90,91]. Although there seems to be some merit in\nattempts to optimize stride frequency, such approaches are questionable for runners who\nhave likely achieved optimal stride frequency through years of training. Stride length and\nstride freque"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0007_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "cdfb0a02df550609", "text_span_hash": "cdfb0a02df550609", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "tride length and\nstride frequency may also affect how far ahead of the body the foot strikes the ground. In\nrunning techniques with longer stride length and lower stride frequency, the foot strikes\nthe ground far ahead of the body, with the ﬁrst grou", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "were very small [87]. Further research is needed to assess the effects of uphill running on\nrunning economy. As it currently stands, it seems that this method is inferior for improving\nrunning economy in comparison to RE.\n3. Resistance Exercise for Modifying Running Technique\nRunning technique is an important aspect for movement economy and injury preven-\ntion. Several biomechanical factors were raised in the literature that may affect running\neconomy. These factors can be categorized as spatiotemporal (parameters relating to\nchanges in the phase of the gait cycle, such as ground contact time, stride length, etc.),\nlower limb kinematics (lower limb joint angles), kinetics (ground reaction forces), and\nneuromuscular factors (muscle activation and coactivation). Regarding the spatiotemporal\nfactors, the literature reports that self-selected stride length and frequency is optimal or\nvery near to being optimal for maximizing economy [88,89]. On the other hand, some of\nthe literature reports that optimal stride length could be 3% shorter than preferred stride\nlength for improving running economy [90,91]. Although there seems to be some merit in\nattempts to optimize stride frequency, such approaches are questionable for runners who\nhave likely achieved optimal stride frequency through years of training. Stride length and\nstride frequency may also affect how far ahead of the body the foot strikes the ground. In\nrunning techniques wit", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "tride length and\nstride frequency may also affect how far ahead of the body the foot strikes the ground. In\nrunning techniques with longer stride length and lower stride frequency, the foot strikes\nthe ground far ahead of the body, with the ﬁrst grou"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0007_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4a44183afbb5b8fd", "text_span_hash": "4a44183afbb5b8fd", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ntion studies have shown that increasing vertical oscillation leads to an in-\ncrease in ˙VO2 [95,96]. It is important to mention that rearfoot striking is more prevalent\nwith increased distance within a running session/race and occurs more commonly i", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "were very small [87]. Further research is needed to assess the effects of uphill running on\nrunning economy. As it currently stands, it seems that this method is inferior for improving\nrunning economy in comparison to RE.\n3. Resistance Exercise for Modifying Running Technique\nRunning technique is an important aspect for movement economy and injury preven-\ntion. Several biomechanical factors were raised in the literature that may affect running\neconomy. These factors can be categorized as spatiotemporal (parameters relating to\nchanges in the phase of the gait cycle, such as ground contact time, stride length, etc.),\nlower limb kinematics (lower limb joint angles), kinetics (ground reaction forces), and\nneuromuscular factors (muscle activation and coactivation). Regarding the spatiotemporal\nfactors, the literature reports that self-selected stride length and frequency is optimal or\nvery near to being optimal for maximizing economy [88,89]. On the other hand, some of\nthe literature reports that optimal stride length could be 3% shorter than preferred stride\nlength for improving running economy [90,91]. Although there seems to be some merit in\nattempts to optimize stride frequency, such approaches are questionable for runners who\nhave likely achieved optimal stride frequency through years of training. Stride length and\nstride frequency may also affect how far ahead of the body the foot strikes the ground. In\nrunning techniques wit", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ntion studies have shown that increasing vertical oscillation leads to an in-\ncrease in ˙VO2 [95,96]. It is important to mention that rearfoot striking is more prevalent\nwith increased distance within a running session/race and occurs more commonly i"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0007_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1537c09798ca74d3", "text_span_hash": "1537c09798ca74d3", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "hich results in the\nuneven distribution of forces between body segments. It is suggested that one of the\ncontributing factors to the development of running-related injuries may be the suboptimal\nlower limb kinematics, especially the inability to cont", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "were very small [87]. Further research is needed to assess the effects of uphill running on\nrunning economy. As it currently stands, it seems that this method is inferior for improving\nrunning economy in comparison to RE.\n3. Resistance Exercise for Modifying Running Technique\nRunning technique is an important aspect for movement economy and injury preven-\ntion. Several biomechanical factors were raised in the literature that may affect running\neconomy. These factors can be categorized as spatiotemporal (parameters relating to\nchanges in the phase of the gait cycle, such as ground contact time, stride length, etc.),\nlower limb kinematics (lower limb joint angles), kinetics (ground reaction forces), and\nneuromuscular factors (muscle activation and coactivation). Regarding the spatiotemporal\nfactors, the literature reports that self-selected stride length and frequency is optimal or\nvery near to being optimal for maximizing economy [88,89]. On the other hand, some of\nthe literature reports that optimal stride length could be 3% shorter than preferred stride\nlength for improving running economy [90,91]. Although there seems to be some merit in\nattempts to optimize stride frequency, such approaches are questionable for runners who\nhave likely achieved optimal stride frequency through years of training. Stride length and\nstride frequency may also affect how far ahead of the body the foot strikes the ground. In\nrunning techniques wit", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "hich results in the\nuneven distribution of forces between body segments. It is suggested that one of the\ncontributing factors to the development of running-related injuries may be the suboptimal\nlower limb kinematics, especially the inability to cont"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0007_c0019", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8e445a48096c83ca", "text_span_hash": "8e445a48096c83ca", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 19, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ted from proximal to distal, suggesting that the kinematics of proximal joints affect the\nkinematics of distal joints, thus highlighting the importance of pelvis and hip musculature\nfor running and walking. Therefore, it is possible that increased hi", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "were very small [87]. Further research is needed to assess the effects of uphill running on\nrunning economy. As it currently stands, it seems that this method is inferior for improving\nrunning economy in comparison to RE.\n3. Resistance Exercise for Modifying Running Technique\nRunning technique is an important aspect for movement economy and injury preven-\ntion. Several biomechanical factors were raised in the literature that may affect running\neconomy. These factors can be categorized as spatiotemporal (parameters relating to\nchanges in the phase of the gait cycle, such as ground contact time, stride length, etc.),\nlower limb kinematics (lower limb joint angles), kinetics (ground reaction forces), and\nneuromuscular factors (muscle activation and coactivation). Regarding the spatiotemporal\nfactors, the literature reports that self-selected stride length and frequency is optimal or\nvery near to being optimal for maximizing economy [88,89]. On the other hand, some of\nthe literature reports that optimal stride length could be 3% shorter than preferred stride\nlength for improving running economy [90,91]. Although there seems to be some merit in\nattempts to optimize stride frequency, such approaches are questionable for runners who\nhave likely achieved optimal stride frequency through years of training. Stride length and\nstride frequency may also affect how far ahead of the body the foot strikes the ground. In\nrunning techniques wit", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ted from proximal to distal, suggesting that the kinematics of proximal joints affect the\nkinematics of distal joints, thus highlighting the importance of pelvis and hip musculature\nfor running and walking. Therefore, it is possible that increased hi"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0008_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "efcaab19a0cf7e63", "text_span_hash": "efcaab19a0cf7e63", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Feltner et al. reported that an 8-week intervention consisting of exercise that directly\nstrengthened the ankle and foot musculature (isokinetic eversion and inversion training)\nsigniﬁcantly reduced the inversion/eversion angle at heel strike and eve", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Feltner et al. reported that an 8-week intervention consisting of exercise that directly\nstrengthened the ankle and foot musculature (isokinetic eversion and inversion training)\nsigniﬁcantly reduced the inversion/eversion angle at heel strike and eversion range of\nmotion [102]. However, this was not true for the functional training consisting of exercises\nsuch as step-ups, balance board exercises, and one- and two-leg jumps. Furthermore,\nSnyder et al. reported that a 6-week hip-strengthening program reduced foot eversion\nrange of motion, hip internal range of motion, ankle inversion moment, and knee abduction\nmoment, while there were no changes in pelvic range of motion in the frontal plane [103].\nTogether, these ﬁndings demonstrate that strength training oriented towards strengthening\nthe hip and ankle/foot musculature reduces eversion range of motion, while strengthening\nthe hip musculature also reduces hip internal range of motion and knee abduction when\nrunning. This indicates that strength training may inﬂuence the lower extremity joint\nloading, thus improving running technique.\nMofﬁt et al. [104] found that maximal strength in squatting is associated with lower\ninternal rotation of the knee during running. Contrary to those results, Willy and Davis\nreported that strength training intervention that improved hip abduction strength, hip exter-\nnal rotation strength, and single-leg squat mechanics did not alter running techn", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Feltner et al. reported that an 8-week intervention consisting of exercise that directly\nstrengthened the ankle and foot musculature (isokinetic eversion and inversion training)\nsigniﬁcantly reduced the inversion/eversion angle at heel strike and eve"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0008_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1b33f2298d99bbac", "text_span_hash": "1b33f2298d99bbac", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "n angle at heel strike and eversion range of\nmotion [102]. However, this was not true for the functional training consisting of exercises\nsuch as step-ups, balance board exercises, and one- and two-leg jumps. Furthermore,\nSnyder et al. reported that", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Feltner et al. reported that an 8-week intervention consisting of exercise that directly\nstrengthened the ankle and foot musculature (isokinetic eversion and inversion training)\nsigniﬁcantly reduced the inversion/eversion angle at heel strike and eversion range of\nmotion [102]. However, this was not true for the functional training consisting of exercises\nsuch as step-ups, balance board exercises, and one- and two-leg jumps. Furthermore,\nSnyder et al. reported that a 6-week hip-strengthening program reduced foot eversion\nrange of motion, hip internal range of motion, ankle inversion moment, and knee abduction\nmoment, while there were no changes in pelvic range of motion in the frontal plane [103].\nTogether, these ﬁndings demonstrate that strength training oriented towards strengthening\nthe hip and ankle/foot musculature reduces eversion range of motion, while strengthening\nthe hip musculature also reduces hip internal range of motion and knee abduction when\nrunning. This indicates that strength training may inﬂuence the lower extremity joint\nloading, thus improving running technique.\nMofﬁt et al. [104] found that maximal strength in squatting is associated with lower\ninternal rotation of the knee during running. Contrary to those results, Willy and Davis\nreported that strength training intervention that improved hip abduction strength, hip exter-\nnal rotation strength, and single-leg squat mechanics did not alter running techn", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "n angle at heel strike and eversion range of\nmotion [102]. However, this was not true for the functional training consisting of exercises\nsuch as step-ups, balance board exercises, and one- and two-leg jumps. Furthermore,\nSnyder et al. reported that"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0008_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "037e23770dfa0dcc", "text_span_hash": "037e23770dfa0dcc", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ic range of motion in the frontal plane [103].\nTogether, these ﬁndings demonstrate that strength training oriented towards strengthening\nthe hip and ankle/foot musculature reduces eversion range of motion, while strengthening\nthe hip musculature also", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Feltner et al. reported that an 8-week intervention consisting of exercise that directly\nstrengthened the ankle and foot musculature (isokinetic eversion and inversion training)\nsigniﬁcantly reduced the inversion/eversion angle at heel strike and eversion range of\nmotion [102]. However, this was not true for the functional training consisting of exercises\nsuch as step-ups, balance board exercises, and one- and two-leg jumps. Furthermore,\nSnyder et al. reported that a 6-week hip-strengthening program reduced foot eversion\nrange of motion, hip internal range of motion, ankle inversion moment, and knee abduction\nmoment, while there were no changes in pelvic range of motion in the frontal plane [103].\nTogether, these ﬁndings demonstrate that strength training oriented towards strengthening\nthe hip and ankle/foot musculature reduces eversion range of motion, while strengthening\nthe hip musculature also reduces hip internal range of motion and knee abduction when\nrunning. This indicates that strength training may inﬂuence the lower extremity joint\nloading, thus improving running technique.\nMofﬁt et al. [104] found that maximal strength in squatting is associated with lower\ninternal rotation of the knee during running. Contrary to those results, Willy and Davis\nreported that strength training intervention that improved hip abduction strength, hip exter-\nnal rotation strength, and single-leg squat mechanics did not alter running techn", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ic range of motion in the frontal plane [103].\nTogether, these ﬁndings demonstrate that strength training oriented towards strengthening\nthe hip and ankle/foot musculature reduces eversion range of motion, while strengthening\nthe hip musculature also"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0008_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2fd4d6e3f0255941", "text_span_hash": "2fd4d6e3f0255941", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "that improved hip abduction strength, hip exter-\nnal rotation strength, and single-leg squat mechanics did not alter running technique [105].\nSimilar results were also reported by Snyder et al. [103], indicating that running technique\nis difﬁcult to", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Feltner et al. reported that an 8-week intervention consisting of exercise that directly\nstrengthened the ankle and foot musculature (isokinetic eversion and inversion training)\nsigniﬁcantly reduced the inversion/eversion angle at heel strike and eversion range of\nmotion [102]. However, this was not true for the functional training consisting of exercises\nsuch as step-ups, balance board exercises, and one- and two-leg jumps. Furthermore,\nSnyder et al. reported that a 6-week hip-strengthening program reduced foot eversion\nrange of motion, hip internal range of motion, ankle inversion moment, and knee abduction\nmoment, while there were no changes in pelvic range of motion in the frontal plane [103].\nTogether, these ﬁndings demonstrate that strength training oriented towards strengthening\nthe hip and ankle/foot musculature reduces eversion range of motion, while strengthening\nthe hip musculature also reduces hip internal range of motion and knee abduction when\nrunning. This indicates that strength training may inﬂuence the lower extremity joint\nloading, thus improving running technique.\nMofﬁt et al. [104] found that maximal strength in squatting is associated with lower\ninternal rotation of the knee during running. Contrary to those results, Willy and Davis\nreported that strength training intervention that improved hip abduction strength, hip exter-\nnal rotation strength, and single-leg squat mechanics did not alter running techn", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "that improved hip abduction strength, hip exter-\nnal rotation strength, and single-leg squat mechanics did not alter running technique [105].\nSimilar results were also reported by Snyder et al. [103], indicating that running technique\nis difﬁcult to"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0008_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8f02acad49b24bdd", "text_span_hash": "8f02acad49b24bdd", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ning technique\nis difﬁcult to change by strength training alone. On the other hand, the literature reports\nthat running technique could be improved with neuromuscular training with feedback\nregarding critical technique deﬁcits, such as, for example,", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Feltner et al. reported that an 8-week intervention consisting of exercise that directly\nstrengthened the ankle and foot musculature (isokinetic eversion and inversion training)\nsigniﬁcantly reduced the inversion/eversion angle at heel strike and eversion range of\nmotion [102]. However, this was not true for the functional training consisting of exercises\nsuch as step-ups, balance board exercises, and one- and two-leg jumps. Furthermore,\nSnyder et al. reported that a 6-week hip-strengthening program reduced foot eversion\nrange of motion, hip internal range of motion, ankle inversion moment, and knee abduction\nmoment, while there were no changes in pelvic range of motion in the frontal plane [103].\nTogether, these ﬁndings demonstrate that strength training oriented towards strengthening\nthe hip and ankle/foot musculature reduces eversion range of motion, while strengthening\nthe hip musculature also reduces hip internal range of motion and knee abduction when\nrunning. This indicates that strength training may inﬂuence the lower extremity joint\nloading, thus improving running technique.\nMofﬁt et al. [104] found that maximal strength in squatting is associated with lower\ninternal rotation of the knee during running. Contrary to those results, Willy and Davis\nreported that strength training intervention that improved hip abduction strength, hip exter-\nnal rotation strength, and single-leg squat mechanics did not alter running techn", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ning technique\nis difﬁcult to change by strength training alone. On the other hand, the literature reports\nthat running technique could be improved with neuromuscular training with feedback\nregarding critical technique deﬁcits, such as, for example,"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0008_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2550076e67863643", "text_span_hash": "2550076e67863643", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "eﬁcits, such as, for example, hip adduction, hip internal rota-\ntion, and contralateral pelvis drop [106]. Moreover, feedback interventions are promising\nfor reducing ground reaction forces during running [107,108].\nIn conclusion, RE for lower extrem", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Feltner et al. reported that an 8-week intervention consisting of exercise that directly\nstrengthened the ankle and foot musculature (isokinetic eversion and inversion training)\nsigniﬁcantly reduced the inversion/eversion angle at heel strike and eversion range of\nmotion [102]. However, this was not true for the functional training consisting of exercises\nsuch as step-ups, balance board exercises, and one- and two-leg jumps. Furthermore,\nSnyder et al. reported that a 6-week hip-strengthening program reduced foot eversion\nrange of motion, hip internal range of motion, ankle inversion moment, and knee abduction\nmoment, while there were no changes in pelvic range of motion in the frontal plane [103].\nTogether, these ﬁndings demonstrate that strength training oriented towards strengthening\nthe hip and ankle/foot musculature reduces eversion range of motion, while strengthening\nthe hip musculature also reduces hip internal range of motion and knee abduction when\nrunning. This indicates that strength training may inﬂuence the lower extremity joint\nloading, thus improving running technique.\nMofﬁt et al. [104] found that maximal strength in squatting is associated with lower\ninternal rotation of the knee during running. Contrary to those results, Willy and Davis\nreported that strength training intervention that improved hip abduction strength, hip exter-\nnal rotation strength, and single-leg squat mechanics did not alter running techn", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "eﬁcits, such as, for example, hip adduction, hip internal rota-\ntion, and contralateral pelvis drop [106]. Moreover, feedback interventions are promising\nfor reducing ground reaction forces during running [107,108].\nIn conclusion, RE for lower extrem"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0008_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3d154f76f5fbd2e0", "text_span_hash": "3d154f76f5fbd2e0", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "onclusion, RE for lower extremities may be beneﬁcial for improving joint kinemat-\nics during running, but the impact on overall running technique is questionable, and more\nresearch is needed. Nevertheless, the literature does not report negative effe", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Feltner et al. reported that an 8-week intervention consisting of exercise that directly\nstrengthened the ankle and foot musculature (isokinetic eversion and inversion training)\nsigniﬁcantly reduced the inversion/eversion angle at heel strike and eversion range of\nmotion [102]. However, this was not true for the functional training consisting of exercises\nsuch as step-ups, balance board exercises, and one- and two-leg jumps. Furthermore,\nSnyder et al. reported that a 6-week hip-strengthening program reduced foot eversion\nrange of motion, hip internal range of motion, ankle inversion moment, and knee abduction\nmoment, while there were no changes in pelvic range of motion in the frontal plane [103].\nTogether, these ﬁndings demonstrate that strength training oriented towards strengthening\nthe hip and ankle/foot musculature reduces eversion range of motion, while strengthening\nthe hip musculature also reduces hip internal range of motion and knee abduction when\nrunning. This indicates that strength training may inﬂuence the lower extremity joint\nloading, thus improving running technique.\nMofﬁt et al. [104] found that maximal strength in squatting is associated with lower\ninternal rotation of the knee during running. Contrary to those results, Willy and Davis\nreported that strength training intervention that improved hip abduction strength, hip exter-\nnal rotation strength, and single-leg squat mechanics did not alter running techn", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "onclusion, RE for lower extremities may be beneﬁcial for improving joint kinemat-\nics during running, but the impact on overall running technique is questionable, and more\nresearch is needed. Nevertheless, the literature does not report negative effe"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0008_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "618acc2f9252f105", "text_span_hash": "618acc2f9252f105", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "e Exercise for Reducing the Risk of Running-Related Injuries\nRunning-related injuries remain a major problem for runners [109], and the best\napproach to prevention is still largely equivocal [37,39]. The most prevalent running-related\ninjuries are me", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Feltner et al. reported that an 8-week intervention consisting of exercise that directly\nstrengthened the ankle and foot musculature (isokinetic eversion and inversion training)\nsigniﬁcantly reduced the inversion/eversion angle at heel strike and eversion range of\nmotion [102]. However, this was not true for the functional training consisting of exercises\nsuch as step-ups, balance board exercises, and one- and two-leg jumps. Furthermore,\nSnyder et al. reported that a 6-week hip-strengthening program reduced foot eversion\nrange of motion, hip internal range of motion, ankle inversion moment, and knee abduction\nmoment, while there were no changes in pelvic range of motion in the frontal plane [103].\nTogether, these ﬁndings demonstrate that strength training oriented towards strengthening\nthe hip and ankle/foot musculature reduces eversion range of motion, while strengthening\nthe hip musculature also reduces hip internal range of motion and knee abduction when\nrunning. This indicates that strength training may inﬂuence the lower extremity joint\nloading, thus improving running technique.\nMofﬁt et al. [104] found that maximal strength in squatting is associated with lower\ninternal rotation of the knee during running. Contrary to those results, Willy and Davis\nreported that strength training intervention that improved hip abduction strength, hip exter-\nnal rotation strength, and single-leg squat mechanics did not alter running techn", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "e Exercise for Reducing the Risk of Running-Related Injuries\nRunning-related injuries remain a major problem for runners [109], and the best\napproach to prevention is still largely equivocal [37,39]. The most prevalent running-related\ninjuries are me"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0008_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3fd319170e3ae1df", "text_span_hash": "3fd319170e3ae1df", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "to load progression\nand management, footwear, subjective aspects, and running-speciﬁc exercises. However,\nRE was not included. Some of the running injuries (e.g., ITBS and PFPS) are characterized\nby altered running mechanics and weakness of certain", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Feltner et al. reported that an 8-week intervention consisting of exercise that directly\nstrengthened the ankle and foot musculature (isokinetic eversion and inversion training)\nsigniﬁcantly reduced the inversion/eversion angle at heel strike and eversion range of\nmotion [102]. However, this was not true for the functional training consisting of exercises\nsuch as step-ups, balance board exercises, and one- and two-leg jumps. Furthermore,\nSnyder et al. reported that a 6-week hip-strengthening program reduced foot eversion\nrange of motion, hip internal range of motion, ankle inversion moment, and knee abduction\nmoment, while there were no changes in pelvic range of motion in the frontal plane [103].\nTogether, these ﬁndings demonstrate that strength training oriented towards strengthening\nthe hip and ankle/foot musculature reduces eversion range of motion, while strengthening\nthe hip musculature also reduces hip internal range of motion and knee abduction when\nrunning. This indicates that strength training may inﬂuence the lower extremity joint\nloading, thus improving running technique.\nMofﬁt et al. [104] found that maximal strength in squatting is associated with lower\ninternal rotation of the knee during running. Contrary to those results, Willy and Davis\nreported that strength training intervention that improved hip abduction strength, hip exter-\nnal rotation strength, and single-leg squat mechanics did not alter running techn", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "to load progression\nand management, footwear, subjective aspects, and running-speciﬁc exercises. However,\nRE was not included. Some of the running injuries (e.g., ITBS and PFPS) are characterized\nby altered running mechanics and weakness of certain"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0008_c0016", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e9aa04b64423460d", "text_span_hash": "e9aa04b64423460d", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 16, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "anics and weakness of certain muscle groups. The purpose of\nthis section is to summarize the available evidence of the effectiveness of RE for reducing\nthe risk of running-related injuries. According to Vincent et al. [112], more than 70% of\nrunning", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Feltner et al. reported that an 8-week intervention consisting of exercise that directly\nstrengthened the ankle and foot musculature (isokinetic eversion and inversion training)\nsigniﬁcantly reduced the inversion/eversion angle at heel strike and eversion range of\nmotion [102]. However, this was not true for the functional training consisting of exercises\nsuch as step-ups, balance board exercises, and one- and two-leg jumps. Furthermore,\nSnyder et al. reported that a 6-week hip-strengthening program reduced foot eversion\nrange of motion, hip internal range of motion, ankle inversion moment, and knee abduction\nmoment, while there were no changes in pelvic range of motion in the frontal plane [103].\nTogether, these ﬁndings demonstrate that strength training oriented towards strengthening\nthe hip and ankle/foot musculature reduces eversion range of motion, while strengthening\nthe hip musculature also reduces hip internal range of motion and knee abduction when\nrunning. This indicates that strength training may inﬂuence the lower extremity joint\nloading, thus improving running technique.\nMofﬁt et al. [104] found that maximal strength in squatting is associated with lower\ninternal rotation of the knee during running. Contrary to those results, Willy and Davis\nreported that strength training intervention that improved hip abduction strength, hip exter-\nnal rotation strength, and single-leg squat mechanics did not alter running techn", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "anics and weakness of certain muscle groups. The purpose of\nthis section is to summarize the available evidence of the effectiveness of RE for reducing\nthe risk of running-related injuries. According to Vincent et al. [112], more than 70% of\nrunning"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0008_c0020", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4aec275f881bdacf", "text_span_hash": "4aec275f881bdacf", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 20, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "endurance, agility, and trunk training\n(p = 0.044), and they also improved their physical ﬁtness. However, this was a very small\nstudy (n = 22), and the generalization of the effectiveness of such an exercise program", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Feltner et al. reported that an 8-week intervention consisting of exercise that directly\nstrengthened the ankle and foot musculature (isokinetic eversion and inversion training)\nsigniﬁcantly reduced the inversion/eversion angle at heel strike and eversion range of\nmotion [102]. However, this was not true for the functional training consisting of exercises\nsuch as step-ups, balance board exercises, and one- and two-leg jumps. Furthermore,\nSnyder et al. reported that a 6-week hip-strengthening program reduced foot eversion\nrange of motion, hip internal range of motion, ankle inversion moment, and knee abduction\nmoment, while there were no changes in pelvic range of motion in the frontal plane [103].\nTogether, these ﬁndings demonstrate that strength training oriented towards strengthening\nthe hip and ankle/foot musculature reduces eversion range of motion, while strengthening\nthe hip musculature also reduces hip internal range of motion and knee abduction when\nrunning. This indicates that strength training may inﬂuence the lower extremity joint\nloading, thus improving running technique.\nMofﬁt et al. [104] found that maximal strength in squatting is associated with lower\ninternal rotation of the knee during running. Contrary to those results, Willy and Davis\nreported that strength training intervention that improved hip abduction strength, hip exter-\nnal rotation strength, and single-leg squat mechanics did not alter running techn", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "endurance, agility, and trunk training\n(p = 0.044), and they also improved their physical ﬁtness. However, this was a very small\nstudy (n = 22), and the generalization of the effectiveness of such an exercise program"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0009_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "d6e13235c59fe87c", "text_span_hash": "d6e13235c59fe87c", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ing no\nor less than one unit of RE per week (p < 0.001) [116]. A 12-month combined prevention\ntraining exercise with a concomitant increase in physical exertion in 1020 soldiers did not\nresult in a statistically signiﬁcant reduction in the incidence", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "is limited. A study of 1538 soldiers found that a 12-week stretching program during\nwarm-up work did not reduce the risk of injury to soldiers; however, they found that the\nsoldiers’ physical ﬁtness itself was a statistically signiﬁcant predictor of risk of lower limb\ninjury [115]. In another cohort of soldiers, performing RE at least three times a week was\nassociated with a 54% lower risk of running-related injuries compared to performing no\nor less than one unit of RE per week (p < 0.001) [116]. A 12-month combined prevention\ntraining exercise with a concomitant increase in physical exertion in 1020 soldiers did not\nresult in a statistically signiﬁcant reduction in the incidence of injuries (p = 0.162) [117]. A\n12-week self-administered muscle strengthening program (included muscles: quadriceps,\nabductors, and trunk muscles), did not reduce the incidence of marathon injuries and the\nassociated early end of the race (p = 0.90) [118].\nStrengthening the ankle and foot muscles reduced the incidence of running-related\ninjuries [119]. This one-year intervention differed from other interventions, as it focused\nprimarily on the ankle–foot complex. The intervention strengthened the muscles of the\nankle and foot, which improved cushioning upon contact with the ground, reduced the\ncumulative load, improved the movement in the ankle, and thus reduced the occurrence of\nrunning injuries. The intervention improved tolerance for the load, a", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ing no\nor less than one unit of RE per week (p < 0.001) [116]. A 12-month combined prevention\ntraining exercise with a concomitant increase in physical exertion in 1020 soldiers did not\nresult in a statistically signiﬁcant reduction in the incidence"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0009_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "eb9418f2f76123f7", "text_span_hash": "eb9418f2f76123f7", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "nt reduction in the incidence of injuries (p = 0.162) [117]. A\n12-week self-administered muscle strengthening program (included muscles: quadriceps,\nabductors, and trunk muscles), did not reduce the incidence of marathon injuries and the\nassociated e", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "is limited. A study of 1538 soldiers found that a 12-week stretching program during\nwarm-up work did not reduce the risk of injury to soldiers; however, they found that the\nsoldiers’ physical ﬁtness itself was a statistically signiﬁcant predictor of risk of lower limb\ninjury [115]. In another cohort of soldiers, performing RE at least three times a week was\nassociated with a 54% lower risk of running-related injuries compared to performing no\nor less than one unit of RE per week (p < 0.001) [116]. A 12-month combined prevention\ntraining exercise with a concomitant increase in physical exertion in 1020 soldiers did not\nresult in a statistically signiﬁcant reduction in the incidence of injuries (p = 0.162) [117]. A\n12-week self-administered muscle strengthening program (included muscles: quadriceps,\nabductors, and trunk muscles), did not reduce the incidence of marathon injuries and the\nassociated early end of the race (p = 0.90) [118].\nStrengthening the ankle and foot muscles reduced the incidence of running-related\ninjuries [119]. This one-year intervention differed from other interventions, as it focused\nprimarily on the ankle–foot complex. The intervention strengthened the muscles of the\nankle and foot, which improved cushioning upon contact with the ground, reduced the\ncumulative load, improved the movement in the ankle, and thus reduced the occurrence of\nrunning injuries. The intervention improved tolerance for the load, a", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "nt reduction in the incidence of injuries (p = 0.162) [117]. A\n12-week self-administered muscle strengthening program (included muscles: quadriceps,\nabductors, and trunk muscles), did not reduce the incidence of marathon injuries and the\nassociated e"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0009_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "608f6329e2b13f13", "text_span_hash": "608f6329e2b13f13", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "injuries and the\nassociated early end of the race (p = 0.90) [118].\nStrengthening the ankle and foot muscles reduced the incidence of running-related\ninjuries [119]. This one-year intervention differed from other interventions, as it focused\nprimari", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "is limited. A study of 1538 soldiers found that a 12-week stretching program during\nwarm-up work did not reduce the risk of injury to soldiers; however, they found that the\nsoldiers’ physical ﬁtness itself was a statistically signiﬁcant predictor of risk of lower limb\ninjury [115]. In another cohort of soldiers, performing RE at least three times a week was\nassociated with a 54% lower risk of running-related injuries compared to performing no\nor less than one unit of RE per week (p < 0.001) [116]. A 12-month combined prevention\ntraining exercise with a concomitant increase in physical exertion in 1020 soldiers did not\nresult in a statistically signiﬁcant reduction in the incidence of injuries (p = 0.162) [117]. A\n12-week self-administered muscle strengthening program (included muscles: quadriceps,\nabductors, and trunk muscles), did not reduce the incidence of marathon injuries and the\nassociated early end of the race (p = 0.90) [118].\nStrengthening the ankle and foot muscles reduced the incidence of running-related\ninjuries [119]. This one-year intervention differed from other interventions, as it focused\nprimarily on the ankle–foot complex. The intervention strengthened the muscles of the\nankle and foot, which improved cushioning upon contact with the ground, reduced the\ncumulative load, improved the movement in the ankle, and thus reduced the occurrence of\nrunning injuries. The intervention improved tolerance for the load, a", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "injuries and the\nassociated early end of the race (p = 0.90) [118].\nStrengthening the ankle and foot muscles reduced the incidence of running-related\ninjuries [119]. This one-year intervention differed from other interventions, as it focused\nprimari"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0009_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "622a0045fb924a9a", "text_span_hash": "622a0045fb924a9a", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "incidence of running\ninjuries was 2.42 times higher in the control group, which did not undergo the intervention.\nThese ﬁndings support the previously proposed protocol to strengthen the foot muscles\nto increase the ability to cushion forces upon co", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "is limited. A study of 1538 soldiers found that a 12-week stretching program during\nwarm-up work did not reduce the risk of injury to soldiers; however, they found that the\nsoldiers’ physical ﬁtness itself was a statistically signiﬁcant predictor of risk of lower limb\ninjury [115]. In another cohort of soldiers, performing RE at least three times a week was\nassociated with a 54% lower risk of running-related injuries compared to performing no\nor less than one unit of RE per week (p < 0.001) [116]. A 12-month combined prevention\ntraining exercise with a concomitant increase in physical exertion in 1020 soldiers did not\nresult in a statistically signiﬁcant reduction in the incidence of injuries (p = 0.162) [117]. A\n12-week self-administered muscle strengthening program (included muscles: quadriceps,\nabductors, and trunk muscles), did not reduce the incidence of marathon injuries and the\nassociated early end of the race (p = 0.90) [118].\nStrengthening the ankle and foot muscles reduced the incidence of running-related\ninjuries [119]. This one-year intervention differed from other interventions, as it focused\nprimarily on the ankle–foot complex. The intervention strengthened the muscles of the\nankle and foot, which improved cushioning upon contact with the ground, reduced the\ncumulative load, improved the movement in the ankle, and thus reduced the occurrence of\nrunning injuries. The intervention improved tolerance for the load, a", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "incidence of running\ninjuries was 2.42 times higher in the control group, which did not undergo the intervention.\nThese ﬁndings support the previously proposed protocol to strengthen the foot muscles\nto increase the ability to cushion forces upon co"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0009_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f67adbf3294e85e8", "text_span_hash": "f67adbf3294e85e8", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "e runners,\nthe injuries were associated with decreased unilateral hip abductor strength and bilaterally\ndecreased hip external rotator strength [125].\n4.2. Studies Assessing Speciﬁc Running-Related Injuries\nAccording to Mucha et al. [126], decreased", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "is limited. A study of 1538 soldiers found that a 12-week stretching program during\nwarm-up work did not reduce the risk of injury to soldiers; however, they found that the\nsoldiers’ physical ﬁtness itself was a statistically signiﬁcant predictor of risk of lower limb\ninjury [115]. In another cohort of soldiers, performing RE at least three times a week was\nassociated with a 54% lower risk of running-related injuries compared to performing no\nor less than one unit of RE per week (p < 0.001) [116]. A 12-month combined prevention\ntraining exercise with a concomitant increase in physical exertion in 1020 soldiers did not\nresult in a statistically signiﬁcant reduction in the incidence of injuries (p = 0.162) [117]. A\n12-week self-administered muscle strengthening program (included muscles: quadriceps,\nabductors, and trunk muscles), did not reduce the incidence of marathon injuries and the\nassociated early end of the race (p = 0.90) [118].\nStrengthening the ankle and foot muscles reduced the incidence of running-related\ninjuries [119]. This one-year intervention differed from other interventions, as it focused\nprimarily on the ankle–foot complex. The intervention strengthened the muscles of the\nankle and foot, which improved cushioning upon contact with the ground, reduced the\ncumulative load, improved the movement in the ankle, and thus reduced the occurrence of\nrunning injuries. The intervention improved tolerance for the load, a", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "e runners,\nthe injuries were associated with decreased unilateral hip abductor strength and bilaterally\ndecreased hip external rotator strength [125].\n4.2. Studies Assessing Speciﬁc Running-Related Injuries\nAccording to Mucha et al. [126], decreased"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0009_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "efb29ce1f3acd477", "text_span_hash": "efb29ce1f3acd477", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Mucha et al. [126], decreased hip ﬂexor strength in endurance runners\nis associated with the risk of ITBS. A study of 24 endurance runners with ITBS found\nthat the strength of the hip abductors of the injured leg was lower than the strength\nof the hi", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "is limited. A study of 1538 soldiers found that a 12-week stretching program during\nwarm-up work did not reduce the risk of injury to soldiers; however, they found that the\nsoldiers’ physical ﬁtness itself was a statistically signiﬁcant predictor of risk of lower limb\ninjury [115]. In another cohort of soldiers, performing RE at least three times a week was\nassociated with a 54% lower risk of running-related injuries compared to performing no\nor less than one unit of RE per week (p < 0.001) [116]. A 12-month combined prevention\ntraining exercise with a concomitant increase in physical exertion in 1020 soldiers did not\nresult in a statistically signiﬁcant reduction in the incidence of injuries (p = 0.162) [117]. A\n12-week self-administered muscle strengthening program (included muscles: quadriceps,\nabductors, and trunk muscles), did not reduce the incidence of marathon injuries and the\nassociated early end of the race (p = 0.90) [118].\nStrengthening the ankle and foot muscles reduced the incidence of running-related\ninjuries [119]. This one-year intervention differed from other interventions, as it focused\nprimarily on the ankle–foot complex. The intervention strengthened the muscles of the\nankle and foot, which improved cushioning upon contact with the ground, reduced the\ncumulative load, improved the movement in the ankle, and thus reduced the occurrence of\nrunning injuries. The intervention improved tolerance for the load, a", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Mucha et al. [126], decreased hip ﬂexor strength in endurance runners\nis associated with the risk of ITBS. A study of 24 endurance runners with ITBS found\nthat the strength of the hip abductors of the injured leg was lower than the strength\nof the hi"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0009_c0016", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3aa286426d49c7f7", "text_span_hash": "3aa286426d49c7f7", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 16, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "er than the strength\nof the hip abductors of the intact leg as well as the strength of the hip abductors of\nrunners in the control group. In the second part of the study, a 6-week rehabilitation was\nperformed, mainly aimed at strengthening the gluteu", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "is limited. A study of 1538 soldiers found that a 12-week stretching program during\nwarm-up work did not reduce the risk of injury to soldiers; however, they found that the\nsoldiers’ physical ﬁtness itself was a statistically signiﬁcant predictor of risk of lower limb\ninjury [115]. In another cohort of soldiers, performing RE at least three times a week was\nassociated with a 54% lower risk of running-related injuries compared to performing no\nor less than one unit of RE per week (p < 0.001) [116]. A 12-month combined prevention\ntraining exercise with a concomitant increase in physical exertion in 1020 soldiers did not\nresult in a statistically signiﬁcant reduction in the incidence of injuries (p = 0.162) [117]. A\n12-week self-administered muscle strengthening program (included muscles: quadriceps,\nabductors, and trunk muscles), did not reduce the incidence of marathon injuries and the\nassociated early end of the race (p = 0.90) [118].\nStrengthening the ankle and foot muscles reduced the incidence of running-related\ninjuries [119]. This one-year intervention differed from other interventions, as it focused\nprimarily on the ankle–foot complex. The intervention strengthened the muscles of the\nankle and foot, which improved cushioning upon contact with the ground, reduced the\ncumulative load, improved the movement in the ankle, and thus reduced the occurrence of\nrunning injuries. The intervention improved tolerance for the load, a", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "er than the strength\nof the hip abductors of the intact leg as well as the strength of the hip abductors of\nrunners in the control group. In the second part of the study, a 6-week rehabilitation was\nperformed, mainly aimed at strengthening the gluteu"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0009_c0020", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "942990a265336154", "text_span_hash": "942990a265336154", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 20, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "differ between runners with and\nAn important factor in the development of PFPS is the weakness of the hip joint mus-\ncles, especially the hip abductors [130]. With an intervention that included a 3-week hip\nmuscle strengthening program, hip abductor", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "is limited. A study of 1538 soldiers found that a 12-week stretching program during\nwarm-up work did not reduce the risk of injury to soldiers; however, they found that the\nsoldiers’ physical ﬁtness itself was a statistically signiﬁcant predictor of risk of lower limb\ninjury [115]. In another cohort of soldiers, performing RE at least three times a week was\nassociated with a 54% lower risk of running-related injuries compared to performing no\nor less than one unit of RE per week (p < 0.001) [116]. A 12-month combined prevention\ntraining exercise with a concomitant increase in physical exertion in 1020 soldiers did not\nresult in a statistically signiﬁcant reduction in the incidence of injuries (p = 0.162) [117]. A\n12-week self-administered muscle strengthening program (included muscles: quadriceps,\nabductors, and trunk muscles), did not reduce the incidence of marathon injuries and the\nassociated early end of the race (p = 0.90) [118].\nStrengthening the ankle and foot muscles reduced the incidence of running-related\ninjuries [119]. This one-year intervention differed from other interventions, as it focused\nprimarily on the ankle–foot complex. The intervention strengthened the muscles of the\nankle and foot, which improved cushioning upon contact with the ground, reduced the\ncumulative load, improved the movement in the ankle, and thus reduced the occurrence of\nrunning injuries. The intervention improved tolerance for the load, a", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "differ between runners with and\nAn important factor in the development of PFPS is the weakness of the hip joint mus-\ncles, especially the hip abductors [130]. With an intervention that included a 3-week hip\nmuscle strengthening program, hip abductor"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0010_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "34c7359fc6864b1a", "text_span_hash": "34c7359fc6864b1a", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "parable to another previous study [131]. Nevertheless, the study did not show a difference\nin running mechanics between the groups, so according to the researcher’s ﬁndings, the\nweakness of the hip abductors is not associated with altered running mec", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "parable to another previous study [131]. Nevertheless, the study did not show a difference\nin running mechanics between the groups, so according to the researcher’s ﬁndings, the\nweakness of the hip abductors is not associated with altered running mechanics. A 14-week\nhip strengthening program on two subjects with increased hip adduction, internal hip\nrotation, and knee valgus, as well as decreased strength of the hip abductors, hip extensors,\nand external rotators of the hip, decreased knee pain and improved the mechanics of the\nlower limb when stepping down [131].\nAlthough more typical for team sports and related to sprinting [132,133], hamstring\ninjuries also occur in distance runners [28]. The risk of hamstring injury can be reduced\nby ﬂexibility training as well as strength training, as Wan et al. [134] found that mobility\ntraining statistically signiﬁcantly lengthened the semimembranosus and long head of the\nbiceps femoris (p ≤0.026) and reduced the maximum load on the hamstrings, while strength\ntraining increased the length and reduced maximum load on all hamstring muscles. In their\nliterature review, Raya-Gonzalez et al. [135] found that, among many other approaches,\nthe reduction of hamstring muscle injuries is thought to be largely inﬂuenced by eccentric\nand balance training. In two case reports performed on two different runners with an\ninﬂamed hamstring tendon, the eccentric exercise protocol for the posterior thigh", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "parable to another previous study [131]. Nevertheless, the study did not show a difference\nin running mechanics between the groups, so according to the researcher’s ﬁndings, the\nweakness of the hip abductors is not associated with altered running mec"}
