g muscles. In their\nliterature review, Raya-Gonzalez et al. [135] found that, among many other approaches,\nthe reduction of hamstring muscle injuries is thought to be largely inﬂuenced by eccentric\nand balance training. In two case reports performed on two different runners with an\ninﬂamed hamstring tendon, the eccentric exercise protocol for the posterior thigh", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "hened the semimembranosus and long head of the\nbiceps femoris (p ≤0.026) and reduced the maximum load on the hamstrings, while strength\ntraining increased the length and reduced maximum load on all hamstring muscles. In their\nliterature review, Raya-"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0010_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "be51a22e3cb534c9", "text_span_hash": "be51a22e3cb534c9", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "their\nliterature review, Raya-Gonzalez et al. [135] found that, among many other approaches,\nthe reduction of hamstring muscle injuries is thought to be largely inﬂuenced by eccentric\nand balance training. In two case reports performed on two differe", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "parable to another previous study [131]. Nevertheless, the study did not show a difference\nin running mechanics between the groups, so according to the researcher’s ﬁndings, the\nweakness of the hip abductors is not associated with altered running mechanics. A 14-week\nhip strengthening program on two subjects with increased hip adduction, internal hip\nrotation, and knee valgus, as well as decreased strength of the hip abductors, hip extensors,\nand external rotators of the hip, decreased knee pain and improved the mechanics of the\nlower limb when stepping down [131].\nAlthough more typical for team sports and related to sprinting [132,133], hamstring\ninjuries also occur in distance runners [28]. The risk of hamstring injury can be reduced\nby ﬂexibility training as well as strength training, as Wan et al. [134] found that mobility\ntraining statistically signiﬁcantly lengthened the semimembranosus and long head of the\nbiceps femoris (p ≤0.026) and reduced the maximum load on the hamstrings, while strength\ntraining increased the length and reduced maximum load on all hamstring muscles. In their\nliterature review, Raya-Gonzalez et al. [135] found that, among many other approaches,\nthe reduction of hamstring muscle injuries is thought to be largely inﬂuenced by eccentric\nand balance training. In two case reports performed on two different runners with an\ninﬂamed hamstring tendon, the eccentric exercise protocol for the posterior thigh", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "their\nliterature review, Raya-Gonzalez et al. [135] found that, among many other approaches,\nthe reduction of hamstring muscle injuries is thought to be largely inﬂuenced by eccentric\nand balance training. In two case reports performed on two differe"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0010_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "25100de1709396b4", "text_span_hash": "25100de1709396b4", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "c anatomical structures in the groin area, the diagnosis of pain is often\ndifﬁcult, and the terminology appears to be inconsistent [138]. In the literature, sports\nhernias and chronic groin pain can also be found as synonyms for groin pain [137,138].", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "parable to another previous study [131]. Nevertheless, the study did not show a difference\nin running mechanics between the groups, so according to the researcher’s ﬁndings, the\nweakness of the hip abductors is not associated with altered running mechanics. A 14-week\nhip strengthening program on two subjects with increased hip adduction, internal hip\nrotation, and knee valgus, as well as decreased strength of the hip abductors, hip extensors,\nand external rotators of the hip, decreased knee pain and improved the mechanics of the\nlower limb when stepping down [131].\nAlthough more typical for team sports and related to sprinting [132,133], hamstring\ninjuries also occur in distance runners [28]. The risk of hamstring injury can be reduced\nby ﬂexibility training as well as strength training, as Wan et al. [134] found that mobility\ntraining statistically signiﬁcantly lengthened the semimembranosus and long head of the\nbiceps femoris (p ≤0.026) and reduced the maximum load on the hamstrings, while strength\ntraining increased the length and reduced maximum load on all hamstring muscles. In their\nliterature review, Raya-Gonzalez et al. [135] found that, among many other approaches,\nthe reduction of hamstring muscle injuries is thought to be largely inﬂuenced by eccentric\nand balance training. In two case reports performed on two different runners with an\ninﬂamed hamstring tendon, the eccentric exercise protocol for the posterior thigh", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "c anatomical structures in the groin area, the diagnosis of pain is often\ndifﬁcult, and the terminology appears to be inconsistent [138]. In the literature, sports\nhernias and chronic groin pain can also be found as synonyms for groin pain [137,138]."}
