{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0010_c0019", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "dbde016859675ebf", "text_span_hash": "dbde016859675ebf", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 19, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "lated injuries. The evidence consistently shows that lower\nlimb RE is effective for improving running economy. A combination of strength training\nand plyometric drills is recommended to improve RE. Isometric training is also emerging\nas a possible al", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "parable to another previous study [131]. Nevertheless, the study did not show a difference\nin running mechanics between the groups, so according to the researcher’s ﬁndings, the\nweakness of the hip abductors is not associated with altered running mechanics. A 14-week\nhip strengthening program on two subjects with increased hip adduction, internal hip\nrotation, and knee valgus, as well as decreased strength of the hip abductors, hip extensors,\nand external rotators of the hip, decreased knee pain and improved the mechanics of the\nlower limb when stepping down [131].\nAlthough more typical for team sports and related to sprinting [132,133], hamstring\ninjuries also occur in distance runners [28]. The risk of hamstring injury can be reduced\nby ﬂexibility training as well as strength training, as Wan et al. [134] found that mobility\ntraining statistically signiﬁcantly lengthened the semimembranosus and long head of the\nbiceps femoris (p ≤0.026) and reduced the maximum load on the hamstrings, while strength\ntraining increased the length and reduced maximum load on all hamstring muscles. In their\nliterature review, Raya-Gonzalez et al. [135] found that, among many other approaches,\nthe reduction of hamstring muscle injuries is thought to be largely inﬂuenced by eccentric\nand balance training. In two case reports performed on two different runners with an\ninﬂamed hamstring tendon, the eccentric exercise protocol for the posterior thigh", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "lated injuries. The evidence consistently shows that lower\nlimb RE is effective for improving running economy. A combination of strength training\nand plyometric drills is recommended to improve RE. Isometric training is also emerging\nas a possible al"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0010_c0020", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0000125a0e222e09", "text_span_hash": "0000125a0e222e09", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 20, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "also emerging\nas a possible alternative and is recommend especially when the overall training load is\nalready high. Lower limb RE may be beneﬁcial for improving joint kinematics during run-", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "parable to another previous study [131]. Nevertheless, the study did not show a difference\nin running mechanics between the groups, so according to the researcher’s ﬁndings, the\nweakness of the hip abductors is not associated with altered running mechanics. A 14-week\nhip strengthening program on two subjects with increased hip adduction, internal hip\nrotation, and knee valgus, as well as decreased strength of the hip abductors, hip extensors,\nand external rotators of the hip, decreased knee pain and improved the mechanics of the\nlower limb when stepping down [131].\nAlthough more typical for team sports and related to sprinting [132,133], hamstring\ninjuries also occur in distance runners [28]. The risk of hamstring injury can be reduced\nby ﬂexibility training as well as strength training, as Wan et al. [134] found that mobility\ntraining statistically signiﬁcantly lengthened the semimembranosus and long head of the\nbiceps femoris (p ≤0.026) and reduced the maximum load on the hamstrings, while strength\ntraining increased the length and reduced maximum load on all hamstring muscles. In their\nliterature review, Raya-Gonzalez et al. [135] found that, among many other approaches,\nthe reduction of hamstring muscle injuries is thought to be largely inﬂuenced by eccentric\nand balance training. In two case reports performed on two different runners with an\ninﬂamed hamstring tendon, the eccentric exercise protocol for the posterior thigh", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "also emerging\nas a possible alternative and is recommend especially when the overall training load is\nalready high. Lower limb RE may be beneﬁcial for improving joint kinematics during run-"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0011_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0f867b3bcd587372", "text_span_hash": "0f867b3bcd587372", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": ": The authors declare no conﬂict of interest.\nNevill, A.M.; Brown, D.; Godfrey, R.; Johnson, P.J.; Romer, L.; Stewart, A.D.; Winter, E.M. Modeling Maximum Oxygen Uptake of\nElite Endurance Athletes. Med. Sci. Sports Exerc. 2003, 35, 488–494. [CrossRef", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "ning; however, biofeedback training is probably more effective for altering overall running\nbiomechanics. RE may help reduce running-related injury risk, although the evidence is\nscarce. In this regard, we recommend that all major lower limb muscle groups be targeted\nif no speciﬁc deﬁcits are present.\nAuthor Contributions: Conceptualization, A.Š., P.Š., J.P. and Ž.K.; methodology, A.Š., P.Š., J.P. and\nŽ.K.; investigation, A.Š., P.Š. and J.P.; data curation, A.Š., P.Š., J.P. and Ž.K.; writing—original draft\npreparation, A.Š., P.Š. and J.P.; writing—review and editing, Ž.K.; visualization, A.Š., P.Š., J.P. and\nŽ.K.; supervision, Ž.K. All authors have read and agreed to the published version of the manuscript.\nInstitutional Review Board Statement: Not applicable.\nInformed Consent Statement: Not applicable.\nData Availability Statement: Not applicable.\nConﬂicts of Interest: The authors declare no conﬂict of interest.\nNevill, A.M.; Brown, D.; Godfrey, R.; Johnson, P.J.; Romer, L.; Stewart, A.D.; Winter, E.M. Modeling Maximum Oxygen Uptake of\nElite Endurance Athletes. Med. Sci. Sports Exerc. 2003, 35, 488–494. [CrossRef]\nSchneider, D.A.; Lacroix, K.A.; Atkinson, G.R.; Troped, P.J.; Pollack, J. Ventilatory Threshold and Maximal Oxygen Uptake during\nCycling and Running in Triathletes. Med. Sci. Sports Exerc. 1990, 22, 257–264. [CrossRef] [PubMed]\nBeattie, K.; Kenny, I.C.; Lyons, M.; Carson, B.P. The Effect of Strength Training on Perfo", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": ": The authors declare no conﬂict of interest.\nNevill, A.M.; Brown, D.; Godfrey, R.; Johnson, P.J.; Romer, L.; Stewart, A.D.; Winter, E.M. Modeling Maximum Oxygen Uptake of\nElite Endurance Athletes. Med. Sci. Sports Exerc. 2003, 35, 488–494. [CrossRef"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0011_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "aa893eb03e92e6d0", "text_span_hash": "aa893eb03e92e6d0", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": ". 2003, 35, 488–494. [CrossRef]\nSchneider, D.A.; Lacroix, K.A.; Atkinson, G.R.; Troped, P.J.; Pollack, J. Ventilatory Threshold and Maximal Oxygen Uptake during\nCycling and Running in Triathletes. Med. Sci. Sports Exerc. 1990, 22, 257–264. [CrossRef]", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "ning; however, biofeedback training is probably more effective for altering overall running\nbiomechanics. RE may help reduce running-related injury risk, although the evidence is\nscarce. In this regard, we recommend that all major lower limb muscle groups be targeted\nif no speciﬁc deﬁcits are present.\nAuthor Contributions: Conceptualization, A.Š., P.Š., J.P. and Ž.K.; methodology, A.Š., P.Š., J.P. and\nŽ.K.; investigation, A.Š., P.Š. and J.P.; data curation, A.Š., P.Š., J.P. and Ž.K.; writing—original draft\npreparation, A.Š., P.Š. and J.P.; writing—review and editing, Ž.K.; visualization, A.Š., P.Š., J.P. and\nŽ.K.; supervision, Ž.K. All authors have read and agreed to the published version of the manuscript.\nInstitutional Review Board Statement: Not applicable.\nInformed Consent Statement: Not applicable.\nData Availability Statement: Not applicable.\nConﬂicts of Interest: The authors declare no conﬂict of interest.\nNevill, A.M.; Brown, D.; Godfrey, R.; Johnson, P.J.; Romer, L.; Stewart, A.D.; Winter, E.M. Modeling Maximum Oxygen Uptake of\nElite Endurance Athletes. Med. Sci. Sports Exerc. 2003, 35, 488–494. [CrossRef]\nSchneider, D.A.; Lacroix, K.A.; Atkinson, G.R.; Troped, P.J.; Pollack, J. Ventilatory Threshold and Maximal Oxygen Uptake during\nCycling and Running in Triathletes. Med. Sci. Sports Exerc. 1990, 22, 257–264. [CrossRef] [PubMed]\nBeattie, K.; Kenny, I.C.; Lyons, M.; Carson, B.P. The Effect of Strength Training on Perfo", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": ". 2003, 35, 488–494. [CrossRef]\nSchneider, D.A.; Lacroix, K.A.; Atkinson, G.R.; Troped, P.J.; Pollack, J. Ventilatory Threshold and Maximal Oxygen Uptake during\nCycling and Running in Triathletes. Med. Sci. Sports Exerc. 1990, 22, 257–264. [CrossRef]"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0011_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e4bde68b91bd8c26", "text_span_hash": "e4bde68b91bd8c26", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ports Med. 1996, 22, 76–89. [CrossRef] [PubMed]\nThomas, D.Q.; Fernhall, B.; Granat, H. Changes in Running Economy during a 5-Km Run in Trained Men and Women Runners. J.\nDi Prampero, P.E.; Capelli, C.; Pagliaro, P.; Antonutto, G.; Girardis, M.; Zampar", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "ning; however, biofeedback training is probably more effective for altering overall running\nbiomechanics. RE may help reduce running-related injury risk, although the evidence is\nscarce. In this regard, we recommend that all major lower limb muscle groups be targeted\nif no speciﬁc deﬁcits are present.\nAuthor Contributions: Conceptualization, A.Š., P.Š., J.P. and Ž.K.; methodology, A.Š., P.Š., J.P. and\nŽ.K.; investigation, A.Š., P.Š. and J.P.; data curation, A.Š., P.Š., J.P. and Ž.K.; writing—original draft\npreparation, A.Š., P.Š. and J.P.; writing—review and editing, Ž.K.; visualization, A.Š., P.Š., J.P. and\nŽ.K.; supervision, Ž.K. All authors have read and agreed to the published version of the manuscript.\nInstitutional Review Board Statement: Not applicable.\nInformed Consent Statement: Not applicable.\nData Availability Statement: Not applicable.\nConﬂicts of Interest: The authors declare no conﬂict of interest.\nNevill, A.M.; Brown, D.; Godfrey, R.; Johnson, P.J.; Romer, L.; Stewart, A.D.; Winter, E.M. Modeling Maximum Oxygen Uptake of\nElite Endurance Athletes. Med. Sci. Sports Exerc. 2003, 35, 488–494. [CrossRef]\nSchneider, D.A.; Lacroix, K.A.; Atkinson, G.R.; Troped, P.J.; Pollack, J. Ventilatory Threshold and Maximal Oxygen Uptake during\nCycling and Running in Triathletes. Med. Sci. Sports Exerc. 1990, 22, 257–264. [CrossRef] [PubMed]\nBeattie, K.; Kenny, I.C.; Lyons, M.; Carson, B.P. The Effect of Strength Training on Perfo", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ports Med. 1996, 22, 76–89. [CrossRef] [PubMed]\nThomas, D.Q.; Fernhall, B.; Granat, H. Changes in Running Economy during a 5-Km Run in Trained Men and Women Runners. J.\nDi Prampero, P.E.; Capelli, C.; Pagliaro, P.; Antonutto, G.; Girardis, M.; Zampar"}
{"chunk_id": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022_p0011_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_6922229c7b09", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "13e370e736d6bfaa", "text_span_hash": "13e370e736d6bfaa", "source_file": "Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Resistance_Exercise_Running_Economy_Injury_Narrative_2022.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Km\nRunning Performance. Med. Sci. Sports Exerc. 1999, 31, 124–130. [CrossRef]\nNummela, A.T.; Paavolainen, L.M.; Sharwood, K.A.; Lambert, M.I.; Noakes, T.D.; Rusko, H.K. Neuromuscular Factors De-\ntermining 5 Km Running Performance and Running Economy", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "ning; however, biofeedback training is probably more effective for altering overall running\nbiomechanics. RE may help reduce running-related injury risk, although the evidence is\nscarce. In this regard, we recommend that all major lower limb muscle groups be targeted\nif no speciﬁc deﬁcits are present.\nAuthor Contributions: Conceptualization, A.Š., P.Š., J.P. and Ž.K.; methodology, A.Š., P.Š., J.P. and\nŽ.K.; investigation, A.Š., P.Š. and J.P.; data curation, A.Š., P.Š., J.P. and Ž.K.; writing—original draft\npreparation, A.Š., P.Š. and J.P.; writing—review and editing, Ž.K.; visualization, A.Š., P.Š., J.P. and\nŽ.K.; supervision, Ž.K. All authors have read and agreed to the published version of the manuscript.\nInstitutional Review Board Statement: Not applicable.\nInformed Consent Statement: Not applicable.\nData Availability Statement: Not applicable.\nConﬂicts of Interest: The authors declare no conﬂict of interest.\nNevill, A.M.; Brown, D.; Godfrey, R.; Johnson, P.J.; Romer, L.; Stewart, A.D.; Winter, E.M. Modeling Maximum Oxygen Uptake of\nElite Endurance Athletes. Med. Sci. Sports Exerc. 2003, 35, 488–494. [CrossRef]\nSchneider, D.A.; Lacroix, K.A.; Atkinson, G.R.; Troped, P.J.; Pollack, J. Ventilatory Threshold and Maximal Oxygen Uptake during\nCycling and Running in Triathletes. Med. Sci. Sports Exerc. 1990, 22, 257–264. [CrossRef] [PubMed]\nBeattie, K.; Kenny, I.C.; Lyons, M.; Carson, B.P. The Effect of Strength Training on Perfo", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Km\nRunning Performance. Med. Sci. Sports Exerc. 1999, 31, 124–130. [CrossRef]\nNummela, A.T.; Paavolainen, L.M.; Sharwood, K.A.; Lambert, M.I.; Noakes, T.D.; Rusko, H.K. Neuromuscular Factors De-\ntermining 5 Km Running Performance and Running Economy"}
