 rear foot eversion angle\nIncreased vertical loading rates\nIncreased rearfoot striking", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "n once back to normal\nAddress lower extremity biome-\nReduce stride length/ increase\nTo reduce vertical loading rates\nModify initial foot contact\nExcessively supinated or pronated\nReduced dorsiflexion range\nIncreased peak hip adduction angle\nIncreased"}
{"chunk_id": "Return_to_Running_Tibial_BSI_2024_p0010_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_42854cfdbb45", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "fa698286888ba76c", "text_span_hash": "fa698286888ba76c", "source_file": "Return_to_Running_Tibial_BSI_2024.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Return_to_Running_Tibial_BSI_2024.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "be uti-\nlised to strengthen local and proximal muscles. Only a rela-\ntively small number of studies specified assessing lower\nextremity strength prior to introducing running-related\nloads, with one study recommending achieving 75–80%\nlower extremity", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "E. R. M. George et al.\nrunning is important. This is in line with other BSI manage-\nment recommendations discussing low-risk and high-risk\nBSIs, as there is an elevated risk of complications at high-\nrisk locations including fracture progression, refracture,\ndelayed union and non-union [6, 28].\n4.1.4  \u0007Assessment of Lower Extremity Strength\nand Functional Pain Provocation Loading Tests\nThe initial phase prior to returning to running can be uti-\nlised to strengthen local and proximal muscles. Only a rela-\ntively small number of studies specified assessing lower\nextremity strength prior to introducing running-related\nloads, with one study recommending achieving 75–80%\nlower extremity strength symmetry [41]. However, almost\nall reviewed studies recommended addressing biome-\nchanical abnormalities and muscle imbalances thought to\ncontribute to the initial injury. This is likely an important\ncomponent as reduced muscle mass and strength have been\nidentified as risk factors for tibial BSIs [11, 12, 95, 102,\n103]. However, further research is required to identify spe-\ncific strength criteria prior to returning to running following\nFunctional tests replicating some of the physical require-\nments of running may determine whether the athlete is\nready to return to running. Although only one-third of\nstudies indicated functional movements should be assessed,\nalmost all reviewed studies recommended addressing\nbiomechanical abnormalities an", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "be uti-\nlised to strengthen local and proximal muscles. Only a rela-\ntively small number of studies specified assessing lower\nextremity strength prior to introducing running-related\nloads, with one study recommending achieving 75–80%\nlower extremity"}
{"chunk_id": "Return_to_Running_Tibial_BSI_2024_p0010_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_42854cfdbb45", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e13206ffe37bfc9c", "text_span_hash": "e13206ffe37bfc9c", "source_file": "Return_to_Running_Tibial_BSI_2024.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Return_to_Running_Tibial_BSI_2024.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "port Consensus Statement recommendations,\nclinicians could consider a battery of strength, functional\nand loading tests prior to introducing running-related loads.\nAssessments could include the SLH test, lower extremity\nstrength evaluated by isometri", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "E. R. M. George et al.\nrunning is important. This is in line with other BSI manage-\nment recommendations discussing low-risk and high-risk\nBSIs, as there is an elevated risk of complications at high-\nrisk locations including fracture progression, refracture,\ndelayed union and non-union [6, 28].\n4.1.4  \u0007Assessment of Lower Extremity Strength\nand Functional Pain Provocation Loading Tests\nThe initial phase prior to returning to running can be uti-\nlised to strengthen local and proximal muscles. Only a rela-\ntively small number of studies specified assessing lower\nextremity strength prior to introducing running-related\nloads, with one study recommending achieving 75–80%\nlower extremity strength symmetry [41]. However, almost\nall reviewed studies recommended addressing biome-\nchanical abnormalities and muscle imbalances thought to\ncontribute to the initial injury. This is likely an important\ncomponent as reduced muscle mass and strength have been\nidentified as risk factors for tibial BSIs [11, 12, 95, 102,\n103]. However, further research is required to identify spe-\ncific strength criteria prior to returning to running following\nFunctional tests replicating some of the physical require-\nments of running may determine whether the athlete is\nready to return to running. Although only one-third of\nstudies indicated functional movements should be assessed,\nalmost all reviewed studies recommended addressing\nbiomechanical abnormalities an", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "port Consensus Statement recommendations,\nclinicians could consider a battery of strength, functional\nand loading tests prior to introducing running-related loads.\nAssessments could include the SLH test, lower extremity\nstrength evaluated by isometri"}
{"chunk_id": "Return_to_Running_Tibial_BSI_2024_p0010_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_42854cfdbb45", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "bb506176ca95b775", "text_span_hash": "bb506176ca95b775", "source_file": "Return_to_Running_Tibial_BSI_2024.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Return_to_Running_Tibial_BSI_2024.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "I before\nclear guidelines can be provided.\nTable 4   (continued)\nComponent of process\nInclude strengthening\nDorsiflexor/ intrinsic foot muscle\nPlyometrics/running drills\nIntroduce once athlete can squat\n1.5 times body weight\nIntroduce once able to fu", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "E. R. M. George et al.\nrunning is important. This is in line with other BSI manage-\nment recommendations discussing low-risk and high-risk\nBSIs, as there is an elevated risk of complications at high-\nrisk locations including fracture progression, refracture,\ndelayed union and non-union [6, 28].\n4.1.4  \u0007Assessment of Lower Extremity Strength\nand Functional Pain Provocation Loading Tests\nThe initial phase prior to returning to running can be uti-\nlised to strengthen local and proximal muscles. Only a rela-\ntively small number of studies specified assessing lower\nextremity strength prior to introducing running-related\nloads, with one study recommending achieving 75–80%\nlower extremity strength symmetry [41]. However, almost\nall reviewed studies recommended addressing biome-\nchanical abnormalities and muscle imbalances thought to\ncontribute to the initial injury. This is likely an important\ncomponent as reduced muscle mass and strength have been\nidentified as risk factors for tibial BSIs [11, 12, 95, 102,\n103]. However, further research is required to identify spe-\ncific strength criteria prior to returning to running following\nFunctional tests replicating some of the physical require-\nments of running may determine whether the athlete is\nready to return to running. Although only one-third of\nstudies indicated functional movements should be assessed,\nalmost all reviewed studies recommended addressing\nbiomechanical abnormalities an", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "I before\nclear guidelines can be provided.\nTable 4   (continued)\nComponent of process\nInclude strengthening\nDorsiflexor/ intrinsic foot muscle\nPlyometrics/running drills\nIntroduce once athlete can squat\n1.5 times body weight\nIntroduce once able to fu"}
{"chunk_id": "Return_to_Running_Tibial_BSI_2024_p0011_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_42854cfdbb45", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f3f61a95ce706e2f", "text_span_hash": "f3f61a95ce706e2f", "source_file": "Return_to_Running_Tibial_BSI_2024.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Return_to_Running_Tibial_BSI_2024.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ssment by a trained sports\nmedicine physician, and possible referral for mental health\ncounselling where applicable.\n4.2  \u0007Return‑to‑Run Process\n4.2.1  \u0007Consideration 1: Walk‑Run Progression\nThe initial introduction of running-related loads following", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Returning to Running Following a Tibial Bone Stress Injury\n4.1.5  \u0007Identification of Contributing Factors\nThe need to identify and address contributing factors such\nas menstrual health, REDs, nutritional factors, biome-\nchanical factors, mechanical loading issues and footwear\nwas acknowledged by reviewed studies. It is beyond the\nscope of this review to systematically review these risk\nfactors, but these recommendations are in line with the\nintrinsic and extrinsic risk factors identified by Abbott\net al. [8], as well as other reviews in this area [103, 107,\nFundamentally all overuse injuries in runners are\nlinked to mechanical loading issues [2, 73, 109, 110]. As\nsuch it is important that these are not repeated during the\nreturn to running process, or when full training eventu-\nally resumes. Alongside mechanical loading issues, it is\nparticularly important that athletes are screened for REDs\nto optimise return-to-run outcomes, and to prevent long-\nterm health consequences. REDs has a well-established\neffect on bone health and menstrual function in female\nathletes [20]. REDs and LEA should be screened for using\nthe Low Energy Availability in Females Questionnaire\n(LEAF-Q), which has been validated in female athletes\nto classify energy availability [111]. Two risk-assessment\ntools have been introduced by the Triad Coalition and the\nInternational Olympic Committee (IOC) [17, 112, 113]\nto aid the clinical decision-making process, ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ssment by a trained sports\nmedicine physician, and possible referral for mental health\ncounselling where applicable.\n4.2  \u0007Return‑to‑Run Process\n4.2.1  \u0007Consideration 1: Walk‑Run Progression\nThe initial introduction of running-related loads following"}
{"chunk_id": "Return_to_Running_Tibial_BSI_2024_p0011_c0017", "source_registry_id": "src_42854cfdbb45", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2a82ea2d89df9999", "text_span_hash": "2a82ea2d89df9999", "source_file": "Return_to_Running_Tibial_BSI_2024.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Return_to_Running_Tibial_BSI_2024.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 17, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "progression on alternate days, gradually substituting\nwalking with increasing time increments of running at a\nall provide the same walk-run progression with no further\nevidence added since the initial proposition of this idea in\nthe study by Brukner", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Returning to Running Following a Tibial Bone Stress Injury\n4.1.5  \u0007Identification of Contributing Factors\nThe need to identify and address contributing factors such\nas menstrual health, REDs, nutritional factors, biome-\nchanical factors, mechanical loading issues and footwear\nwas acknowledged by reviewed studies. It is beyond the\nscope of this review to systematically review these risk\nfactors, but these recommendations are in line with the\nintrinsic and extrinsic risk factors identified by Abbott\net al. [8], as well as other reviews in this area [103, 107,\nFundamentally all overuse injuries in runners are\nlinked to mechanical loading issues [2, 73, 109, 110]. As\nsuch it is important that these are not repeated during the\nreturn to running process, or when full training eventu-\nally resumes. Alongside mechanical loading issues, it is\nparticularly important that athletes are screened for REDs\nto optimise return-to-run outcomes, and to prevent long-\nterm health consequences. REDs has a well-established\neffect on bone health and menstrual function in female\nathletes [20]. REDs and LEA should be screened for using\nthe Low Energy Availability in Females Questionnaire\n(LEAF-Q), which has been validated in female athletes\nto classify energy availability [111]. Two risk-assessment\ntools have been introduced by the Triad Coalition and the\nInternational Olympic Committee (IOC) [17, 112, 113]\nto aid the clinical decision-making process, ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "progression on alternate days, gradually substituting\nwalking with increasing time increments of running at a\nall provide the same walk-run progression with no further\nevidence added since the initial proposition of this idea in\nthe study by Brukner"}
{"chunk_id": "Return_to_Running_Tibial_BSI_2024_p0011_c0018", "source_registry_id": "src_42854cfdbb45", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9394962646949d9b", "text_span_hash": "9394962646949d9b", "source_file": "Return_to_Running_Tibial_BSI_2024.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Return_to_Running_Tibial_BSI_2024.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 18, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "idea in\nthe study by Brukner et al. in 1997 [68]. The specific length\nof running increments varied considerably among reviewed\nstudies, however, starting with 30- to 60-s increments, and\nprogressing by 1–2 min, or equivalent distance was sug-\ngested", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Returning to Running Following a Tibial Bone Stress Injury\n4.1.5  \u0007Identification of Contributing Factors\nThe need to identify and address contributing factors such\nas menstrual health, REDs, nutritional factors, biome-\nchanical factors, mechanical loading issues and footwear\nwas acknowledged by reviewed studies. It is beyond the\nscope of this review to systematically review these risk\nfactors, but these recommendations are in line with the\nintrinsic and extrinsic risk factors identified by Abbott\net al. [8], as well as other reviews in this area [103, 107,\nFundamentally all overuse injuries in runners are\nlinked to mechanical loading issues [2, 73, 109, 110]. As\nsuch it is important that these are not repeated during the\nreturn to running process, or when full training eventu-\nally resumes. Alongside mechanical loading issues, it is\nparticularly important that athletes are screened for REDs\nto optimise return-to-run outcomes, and to prevent long-\nterm health consequences. REDs has a well-established\neffect on bone health and menstrual function in female\nathletes [20]. REDs and LEA should be screened for using\nthe Low Energy Availability in Females Questionnaire\n(LEAF-Q), which has been validated in female athletes\nto classify energy availability [111]. Two risk-assessment\ntools have been introduced by the Triad Coalition and the\nInternational Olympic Committee (IOC) [17, 112, 113]\nto aid the clinical decision-making process, ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "idea in\nthe study by Brukner et al. in 1997 [68]. The specific length\nof running increments varied considerably among reviewed\nstudies, however, starting with 30- to 60-s increments, and\nprogressing by 1–2 min, or equivalent distance was sug-\ngested"}
