{"chunk_id": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn_p0002_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_9e3c2a6c0ad2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "31a01f908532ab48", "text_span_hash": "31a01f908532ab48", "source_file": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "performance-related factors and how\napplied in practice. Therefore, the aims\nof this article are to brieﬂy review the\nliterature that has investigated the efﬁ-\ncacy of STon the determinants of endur-\nance running in adolescent runners, and\nprovide gu", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "performance-related factors and how\napplied in practice. Therefore, the aims\nof this article are to brieﬂy review the\nliterature that has investigated the efﬁ-\ncacy of STon the determinants of endur-\nance running in adolescent runners, and\nprovide guidelines for best practice to\nthe occurrence of overuse injury.\nDETERMINANTS OF ENDURANCE\ndetermined by several key physiological\nvariables, which are summarized in Fig-\nure 1. The physiological determinants of\nperformance for adolescents seem to be\nsimilar to those of adult runners (3,27).\nA number of investigations have con-\nﬁrmed that maximal oxygen uptake\n(V̇O2max) is a signiﬁcant predictor (r\n5 0.5–0.9) of performance for 1.5 km\ncross-country (24,34) in young (10–18\nyears) groups of runners. The propor-\ntion of V̇O2max that can be sustained for\na given duration (known as “fractional\nutilization”) has also been shown to sig-\nniﬁcantly correlate with endurance run-\ndeﬁned as the metabolic cost of running\na given distance (79), is related to mid-\ndle- (3,87) and long-distance running\nperformance (24,34) and, importantly,\nis inﬂuenced by neuromuscular-related\nimproved with ST activities (16,29). In\naddition, speed at V̇O2max, which is\na product of V̇O2max and RE, correlates\nwell with distance running performance\nThe contribution of anaerobic factors\nto endurance running performance in\nadults is well established (20); however,\nthe inﬂuence of anaerobic determinants\non performance i", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "performance-related factors and how\napplied in practice. Therefore, the aims\nof this article are to brieﬂy review the\nliterature that has investigated the efﬁ-\ncacy of STon the determinants of endur-\nance running in adolescent runners, and\nprovide gu"}
{"chunk_id": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn_p0002_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_9e3c2a6c0ad2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "04a4ef7ed364ff3a", "text_span_hash": "04a4ef7ed364ff3a", "source_file": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ical determinants of\nperformance for adolescents seem to be\nsimilar to those of adult runners (3,27).\nA number of investigations have con-\nﬁrmed that maximal oxygen uptake\n(V̇O2max) is a signiﬁcant predictor (r\n5 0.5–0.9) of performance for 1.5 km\ncr", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "performance-related factors and how\napplied in practice. Therefore, the aims\nof this article are to brieﬂy review the\nliterature that has investigated the efﬁ-\ncacy of STon the determinants of endur-\nance running in adolescent runners, and\nprovide guidelines for best practice to\nthe occurrence of overuse injury.\nDETERMINANTS OF ENDURANCE\ndetermined by several key physiological\nvariables, which are summarized in Fig-\nure 1. The physiological determinants of\nperformance for adolescents seem to be\nsimilar to those of adult runners (3,27).\nA number of investigations have con-\nﬁrmed that maximal oxygen uptake\n(V̇O2max) is a signiﬁcant predictor (r\n5 0.5–0.9) of performance for 1.5 km\ncross-country (24,34) in young (10–18\nyears) groups of runners. The propor-\ntion of V̇O2max that can be sustained for\na given duration (known as “fractional\nutilization”) has also been shown to sig-\nniﬁcantly correlate with endurance run-\ndeﬁned as the metabolic cost of running\na given distance (79), is related to mid-\ndle- (3,87) and long-distance running\nperformance (24,34) and, importantly,\nis inﬂuenced by neuromuscular-related\nimproved with ST activities (16,29). In\naddition, speed at V̇O2max, which is\na product of V̇O2max and RE, correlates\nwell with distance running performance\nThe contribution of anaerobic factors\nto endurance running performance in\nadults is well established (20); however,\nthe inﬂuence of anaerobic determinants\non performance i", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ical determinants of\nperformance for adolescents seem to be\nsimilar to those of adult runners (3,27).\nA number of investigations have con-\nﬁrmed that maximal oxygen uptake\n(V̇O2max) is a signiﬁcant predictor (r\n5 0.5–0.9) of performance for 1.5 km\ncr"}
