 which pro-\nvides a highly speciﬁc overload to the\nneuromuscular system in endurance\nrunners. Interestingly, participants in the\nST intervention groups in these studies\nreduced their weekly running volume to\nactivities. Total time spent training was\nhowever very similar between inter-\nvention and control groups.\nBluett et al. (18) found that 10 weeks of\nconcurrent aerobic and ST provided\nl", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ce run-\nners (mean: 17 years, V̇O2max: 62.5\nmL$kg21$min21) and after 8 weeks of\nERT, PT, and sprint training, noted\na difference (22.7%) in RE at 14\nanaerobic capabilities (speed during\nthe maximal anaerobic running test\nand 30-m sprint) compared wit"}
{"chunk_id": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn_p0003_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_9e3c2a6c0ad2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "792f57efff29f772", "text_span_hash": "792f57efff29f772", "source_file": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "d to the\nneuromuscular system in endurance\nrunners. Interestingly, participants in the\nST intervention groups in these studies\nreduced their weekly running volume to\nactivities. Total time spent training was\nhowever very similar between inter-\nventio", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "EFFICACY IN ADOLESCENT\nThree studies have investigated the\neffect of ST speciﬁcally in young (,18\nyears) middle- or long-distance runners\nand these are summarized in Table 1. A\nrecent study by Blagrove et al. (17)\nfound that 2 weekly sessions of ST\n(mainly PT and RT) added to the pro-\ngram of postpubertal adolescent dis-\ntance runners (17 years) for 10 weeks\nwas “possibly beneﬁcial” for RE (effect\nsize: 0.31–0.51) and “highly likely ben-\neﬁcial” for maximal sprint speed. How-\nimprovement reached statistical signiﬁ-\ncance (P , 0.05) compared with the\nchange observed in the control group.\nMikkola et al. (58) took a group of\ntrained male and female distance run-\nners (mean: 17 years, V̇O2max: 62.5\nmL$kg21$min21) and after 8 weeks of\nERT, PT, and sprint training, noted\na difference (22.7%) in RE at 14\nanaerobic capabilities (speed during\nthe maximal anaerobic running test\nand 30-m sprint) compared with a run-\nning-only group. It is noteworthy that\nboth of these investigations (17,58)\nincluded sprints (3–10 3 30–150 m)\nas part of the intervention, which pro-\nvides a highly speciﬁc overload to the\nneuromuscular system in endurance\nrunners. Interestingly, participants in the\nST intervention groups in these studies\nreduced their weekly running volume to\nactivities. Total time spent training was\nhowever very similar between inter-\nvention and control groups.\nBluett et al. (18) found that 10 weeks of\nconcurrent aerobic and ST provided\nl", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "d to the\nneuromuscular system in endurance\nrunners. Interestingly, participants in the\nST intervention groups in these studies\nreduced their weekly running volume to\nactivities. Total time spent training was\nhowever very similar between inter-\nventio"}
{"chunk_id": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn_p0003_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_9e3c2a6c0ad2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7c6ff789cd9890c1", "text_span_hash": "7c6ff789cd9890c1", "source_file": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "similar between inter-\nvention and control groups.\nBluett et al. (18) found that 10 weeks of\nconcurrent aerobic and ST provided\nlittle strength advantage and no change\nin 3-km time-trial performance in 10–\n13-year-old competitive runners com-\npared", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "EFFICACY IN ADOLESCENT\nThree studies have investigated the\neffect of ST speciﬁcally in young (,18\nyears) middle- or long-distance runners\nand these are summarized in Table 1. A\nrecent study by Blagrove et al. (17)\nfound that 2 weekly sessions of ST\n(mainly PT and RT) added to the pro-\ngram of postpubertal adolescent dis-\ntance runners (17 years) for 10 weeks\nwas “possibly beneﬁcial” for RE (effect\nsize: 0.31–0.51) and “highly likely ben-\neﬁcial” for maximal sprint speed. How-\nimprovement reached statistical signiﬁ-\ncance (P , 0.05) compared with the\nchange observed in the control group.\nMikkola et al. (58) took a group of\ntrained male and female distance run-\nners (mean: 17 years, V̇O2max: 62.5\nmL$kg21$min21) and after 8 weeks of\nERT, PT, and sprint training, noted\na difference (22.7%) in RE at 14\nanaerobic capabilities (speed during\nthe maximal anaerobic running test\nand 30-m sprint) compared with a run-\nning-only group. It is noteworthy that\nboth of these investigations (17,58)\nincluded sprints (3–10 3 30–150 m)\nas part of the intervention, which pro-\nvides a highly speciﬁc overload to the\nneuromuscular system in endurance\nrunners. Interestingly, participants in the\nST intervention groups in these studies\nreduced their weekly running volume to\nactivities. Total time spent training was\nhowever very similar between inter-\nvention and control groups.\nBluett et al. (18) found that 10 weeks of\nconcurrent aerobic and ST provided\nl", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "similar between inter-\nvention and control groups.\nBluett et al. (18) found that 10 weeks of\nconcurrent aerobic and ST provided\nlittle strength advantage and no change\nin 3-km time-trial performance in 10–\n13-year-old competitive runners com-\npared"}
{"chunk_id": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn_p0003_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_9e3c2a6c0ad2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "83b9885a42f989cd", "text_span_hash": "83b9885a42f989cd", "source_file": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "g from the concurrent\ntraining regimen may have compro-\nmised both strength and endurance\nadaptations (18). Interestingly, the blunt-\ning of strength adaptation, which is often\nobserved in adult performers when both\nST and endurance training are incl", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "EFFICACY IN ADOLESCENT\nThree studies have investigated the\neffect of ST speciﬁcally in young (,18\nyears) middle- or long-distance runners\nand these are summarized in Table 1. A\nrecent study by Blagrove et al. (17)\nfound that 2 weekly sessions of ST\n(mainly PT and RT) added to the pro-\ngram of postpubertal adolescent dis-\ntance runners (17 years) for 10 weeks\nwas “possibly beneﬁcial” for RE (effect\nsize: 0.31–0.51) and “highly likely ben-\neﬁcial” for maximal sprint speed. How-\nimprovement reached statistical signiﬁ-\ncance (P , 0.05) compared with the\nchange observed in the control group.\nMikkola et al. (58) took a group of\ntrained male and female distance run-\nners (mean: 17 years, V̇O2max: 62.5\nmL$kg21$min21) and after 8 weeks of\nERT, PT, and sprint training, noted\na difference (22.7%) in RE at 14\nanaerobic capabilities (speed during\nthe maximal anaerobic running test\nand 30-m sprint) compared with a run-\nning-only group. It is noteworthy that\nboth of these investigations (17,58)\nincluded sprints (3–10 3 30–150 m)\nas part of the intervention, which pro-\nvides a highly speciﬁc overload to the\nneuromuscular system in endurance\nrunners. Interestingly, participants in the\nST intervention groups in these studies\nreduced their weekly running volume to\nactivities. Total time spent training was\nhowever very similar between inter-\nvention and control groups.\nBluett et al. (18) found that 10 weeks of\nconcurrent aerobic and ST provided\nl", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "g from the concurrent\ntraining regimen may have compro-\nmised both strength and endurance\nadaptations (18). Interestingly, the blunt-\ning of strength adaptation, which is often\nobserved in adult performers when both\nST and endurance training are incl"}
{"chunk_id": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn_p0003_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_9e3c2a6c0ad2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "010b7668c8d6a2d8", "text_span_hash": "010b7668c8d6a2d8", "source_file": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "nd endurance training are included\nin the same training session (90), seems\nnot to occur in children (53) and adoles-\ncents (75,76). As the interference phe-\nvolume and recovery from sessions (5),\nit seems likely that the volumes of each\ntraining mod", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "EFFICACY IN ADOLESCENT\nThree studies have investigated the\neffect of ST speciﬁcally in young (,18\nyears) middle- or long-distance runners\nand these are summarized in Table 1. A\nrecent study by Blagrove et al. (17)\nfound that 2 weekly sessions of ST\n(mainly PT and RT) added to the pro-\ngram of postpubertal adolescent dis-\ntance runners (17 years) for 10 weeks\nwas “possibly beneﬁcial” for RE (effect\nsize: 0.31–0.51) and “highly likely ben-\neﬁcial” for maximal sprint speed. How-\nimprovement reached statistical signiﬁ-\ncance (P , 0.05) compared with the\nchange observed in the control group.\nMikkola et al. (58) took a group of\ntrained male and female distance run-\nners (mean: 17 years, V̇O2max: 62.5\nmL$kg21$min21) and after 8 weeks of\nERT, PT, and sprint training, noted\na difference (22.7%) in RE at 14\nanaerobic capabilities (speed during\nthe maximal anaerobic running test\nand 30-m sprint) compared with a run-\nning-only group. It is noteworthy that\nboth of these investigations (17,58)\nincluded sprints (3–10 3 30–150 m)\nas part of the intervention, which pro-\nvides a highly speciﬁc overload to the\nneuromuscular system in endurance\nrunners. Interestingly, participants in the\nST intervention groups in these studies\nreduced their weekly running volume to\nactivities. Total time spent training was\nhowever very similar between inter-\nvention and control groups.\nBluett et al. (18) found that 10 weeks of\nconcurrent aerobic and ST provided\nl", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "nd endurance training are included\nin the same training session (90), seems\nnot to occur in children (53) and adoles-\ncents (75,76). As the interference phe-\nvolume and recovery from sessions (5),\nit seems likely that the volumes of each\ntraining mod"}
{"chunk_id": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn_p0003_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_9e3c2a6c0ad2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3341d8b73faac473", "text_span_hash": "3341d8b73faac473", "source_file": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "e volumes of each\ntraining modality included in the afore-\nmentioned studies were insufﬁcient to\nnegatively impact on strength-related\nadaptation. Indeed, in elite youth soccer\nplayers (17 years) who use higher work-\nloads compared with younger perfo", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "EFFICACY IN ADOLESCENT\nThree studies have investigated the\neffect of ST speciﬁcally in young (,18\nyears) middle- or long-distance runners\nand these are summarized in Table 1. A\nrecent study by Blagrove et al. (17)\nfound that 2 weekly sessions of ST\n(mainly PT and RT) added to the pro-\ngram of postpubertal adolescent dis-\ntance runners (17 years) for 10 weeks\nwas “possibly beneﬁcial” for RE (effect\nsize: 0.31–0.51) and “highly likely ben-\neﬁcial” for maximal sprint speed. How-\nimprovement reached statistical signiﬁ-\ncance (P , 0.05) compared with the\nchange observed in the control group.\nMikkola et al. (58) took a group of\ntrained male and female distance run-\nners (mean: 17 years, V̇O2max: 62.5\nmL$kg21$min21) and after 8 weeks of\nERT, PT, and sprint training, noted\na difference (22.7%) in RE at 14\nanaerobic capabilities (speed during\nthe maximal anaerobic running test\nand 30-m sprint) compared with a run-\nning-only group. It is noteworthy that\nboth of these investigations (17,58)\nincluded sprints (3–10 3 30–150 m)\nas part of the intervention, which pro-\nvides a highly speciﬁc overload to the\nneuromuscular system in endurance\nrunners. Interestingly, participants in the\nST intervention groups in these studies\nreduced their weekly running volume to\nactivities. Total time spent training was\nhowever very similar between inter-\nvention and control groups.\nBluett et al. (18) found that 10 weeks of\nconcurrent aerobic and ST provided\nl", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "e volumes of each\ntraining modality included in the afore-\nmentioned studies were insufﬁcient to\nnegatively impact on strength-related\nadaptation. Indeed, in elite youth soccer\nplayers (17 years) who use higher work-\nloads compared with younger perfo"}
{"chunk_id": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn_p0003_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_9e3c2a6c0ad2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "cc7525f5918cf514", "text_span_hash": "cc7525f5918cf514", "source_file": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ds compared with younger perform-\ners, larger improvements were evident in\nstrength and sprint performance after 5\nweeks when ST was performed after\nsport-speciﬁc endurance training on 2\ndays per week, compared with a group\nwho adopted an ST followed", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "EFFICACY IN ADOLESCENT\nThree studies have investigated the\neffect of ST speciﬁcally in young (,18\nyears) middle- or long-distance runners\nand these are summarized in Table 1. A\nrecent study by Blagrove et al. (17)\nfound that 2 weekly sessions of ST\n(mainly PT and RT) added to the pro-\ngram of postpubertal adolescent dis-\ntance runners (17 years) for 10 weeks\nwas “possibly beneﬁcial” for RE (effect\nsize: 0.31–0.51) and “highly likely ben-\neﬁcial” for maximal sprint speed. How-\nimprovement reached statistical signiﬁ-\ncance (P , 0.05) compared with the\nchange observed in the control group.\nMikkola et al. (58) took a group of\ntrained male and female distance run-\nners (mean: 17 years, V̇O2max: 62.5\nmL$kg21$min21) and after 8 weeks of\nERT, PT, and sprint training, noted\na difference (22.7%) in RE at 14\nanaerobic capabilities (speed during\nthe maximal anaerobic running test\nand 30-m sprint) compared with a run-\nning-only group. It is noteworthy that\nboth of these investigations (17,58)\nincluded sprints (3–10 3 30–150 m)\nas part of the intervention, which pro-\nvides a highly speciﬁc overload to the\nneuromuscular system in endurance\nrunners. Interestingly, participants in the\nST intervention groups in these studies\nreduced their weekly running volume to\nactivities. Total time spent training was\nhowever very similar between inter-\nvention and control groups.\nBluett et al. (18) found that 10 weeks of\nconcurrent aerobic and ST provided\nl", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ds compared with younger perform-\ners, larger improvements were evident in\nstrength and sprint performance after 5\nweeks when ST was performed after\nsport-speciﬁc endurance training on 2\ndays per week, compared with a group\nwho adopted an ST followed"}
