, which pro-\nvides a highly speciﬁc overload to the\nneuromuscular system in endurance\nrunners. Interestingly, participants in the\nST intervention groups in these studies\nreduced their weekly running volume to\nactivities. Total time spent training was\nhowever very similar between inter-\nvention and control groups.\nBluett et al. (18) found that 10 weeks of\nconcurrent aerobic and ST provided\nl", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "oup\nwho adopted an ST followed by endur-\nance training sequence (33).\nResearch investigating the impact of ST\nmeasures in young athletes has tended\nto use participants from ﬁeld-based\nsports, martial arts, court sports, aquatic\nsports, gymnastics, an"}
{"chunk_id": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn_p0003_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_9e3c2a6c0ad2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "6acb8225b76385d5", "text_span_hash": "6acb8225b76385d5", "source_file": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "aquatic\nsports, gymnastics, and strength-based\nsports (40,46). A number of studies\nusing adolescent participants from other\nsports that require high levels of aerobic\nﬁtness have observed superior improve-\nments in the Yo-Yo test (44,51,72,91)\nand mi", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "EFFICACY IN ADOLESCENT\nThree studies have investigated the\neffect of ST speciﬁcally in young (,18\nyears) middle- or long-distance runners\nand these are summarized in Table 1. A\nrecent study by Blagrove et al. (17)\nfound that 2 weekly sessions of ST\n(mainly PT and RT) added to the pro-\ngram of postpubertal adolescent dis-\ntance runners (17 years) for 10 weeks\nwas “possibly beneﬁcial” for RE (effect\nsize: 0.31–0.51) and “highly likely ben-\neﬁcial” for maximal sprint speed. How-\nimprovement reached statistical signiﬁ-\ncance (P , 0.05) compared with the\nchange observed in the control group.\nMikkola et al. (58) took a group of\ntrained male and female distance run-\nners (mean: 17 years, V̇O2max: 62.5\nmL$kg21$min21) and after 8 weeks of\nERT, PT, and sprint training, noted\na difference (22.7%) in RE at 14\nanaerobic capabilities (speed during\nthe maximal anaerobic running test\nand 30-m sprint) compared with a run-\nning-only group. It is noteworthy that\nboth of these investigations (17,58)\nincluded sprints (3–10 3 30–150 m)\nas part of the intervention, which pro-\nvides a highly speciﬁc overload to the\nneuromuscular system in endurance\nrunners. Interestingly, participants in the\nST intervention groups in these studies\nreduced their weekly running volume to\nactivities. Total time spent training was\nhowever very similar between inter-\nvention and control groups.\nBluett et al. (18) found that 10 weeks of\nconcurrent aerobic and ST provided\nl", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "aquatic\nsports, gymnastics, and strength-based\nsports (40,46). A number of studies\nusing adolescent participants from other\nsports that require high levels of aerobic\nﬁtness have observed superior improve-\nments in the Yo-Yo test (44,51,72,91)\nand mi"}
{"chunk_id": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn_p0003_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_9e3c2a6c0ad2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "6c716943f2efe1c5", "text_span_hash": "6c716943f2efe1c5", "source_file": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "o-Yo test (44,51,72,91)\nand middle-distance time-trial perfor-\nmance (70,73) after various modalities\nof ST were added (6–12 weeks) to\na sports-speciﬁc training program, com-\npared with only practicing the sport.\nTaken together, it seems that the add", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "EFFICACY IN ADOLESCENT\nThree studies have investigated the\neffect of ST speciﬁcally in young (,18\nyears) middle- or long-distance runners\nand these are summarized in Table 1. A\nrecent study by Blagrove et al. (17)\nfound that 2 weekly sessions of ST\n(mainly PT and RT) added to the pro-\ngram of postpubertal adolescent dis-\ntance runners (17 years) for 10 weeks\nwas “possibly beneﬁcial” for RE (effect\nsize: 0.31–0.51) and “highly likely ben-\neﬁcial” for maximal sprint speed. How-\nimprovement reached statistical signiﬁ-\ncance (P , 0.05) compared with the\nchange observed in the control group.\nMikkola et al. (58) took a group of\ntrained male and female distance run-\nners (mean: 17 years, V̇O2max: 62.5\nmL$kg21$min21) and after 8 weeks of\nERT, PT, and sprint training, noted\na difference (22.7%) in RE at 14\nanaerobic capabilities (speed during\nthe maximal anaerobic running test\nand 30-m sprint) compared with a run-\nning-only group. It is noteworthy that\nboth of these investigations (17,58)\nincluded sprints (3–10 3 30–150 m)\nas part of the intervention, which pro-\nvides a highly speciﬁc overload to the\nneuromuscular system in endurance\nrunners. Interestingly, participants in the\nST intervention groups in these studies\nreduced their weekly running volume to\nactivities. Total time spent training was\nhowever very similar between inter-\nvention and control groups.\nBluett et al. (18) found that 10 weeks of\nconcurrent aerobic and ST provided\nl", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "o-Yo test (44,51,72,91)\nand middle-distance time-trial perfor-\nmance (70,73) after various modalities\nof ST were added (6–12 weeks) to\na sports-speciﬁc training program, com-\npared with only practicing the sport.\nTaken together, it seems that the add"}
{"chunk_id": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn_p0003_c0016", "source_registry_id": "src_9e3c2a6c0ad2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "d31664ae9d230d36", "text_span_hash": "d31664ae9d230d36", "source_file": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 16, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ogether, it seems that the addi-\ntion of 2–3 ST sessions to the weekly\nroutine of adolescent endurance run-\nners provides a small but potentially\nmeaningful beneﬁt to RE and maximal\nsprint speed after intervention over 8–\n10 weeks. Evidence for impro", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "EFFICACY IN ADOLESCENT\nThree studies have investigated the\neffect of ST speciﬁcally in young (,18\nyears) middle- or long-distance runners\nand these are summarized in Table 1. A\nrecent study by Blagrove et al. (17)\nfound that 2 weekly sessions of ST\n(mainly PT and RT) added to the pro-\ngram of postpubertal adolescent dis-\ntance runners (17 years) for 10 weeks\nwas “possibly beneﬁcial” for RE (effect\nsize: 0.31–0.51) and “highly likely ben-\neﬁcial” for maximal sprint speed. How-\nimprovement reached statistical signiﬁ-\ncance (P , 0.05) compared with the\nchange observed in the control group.\nMikkola et al. (58) took a group of\ntrained male and female distance run-\nners (mean: 17 years, V̇O2max: 62.5\nmL$kg21$min21) and after 8 weeks of\nERT, PT, and sprint training, noted\na difference (22.7%) in RE at 14\nanaerobic capabilities (speed during\nthe maximal anaerobic running test\nand 30-m sprint) compared with a run-\nning-only group. It is noteworthy that\nboth of these investigations (17,58)\nincluded sprints (3–10 3 30–150 m)\nas part of the intervention, which pro-\nvides a highly speciﬁc overload to the\nneuromuscular system in endurance\nrunners. Interestingly, participants in the\nST intervention groups in these studies\nreduced their weekly running volume to\nactivities. Total time spent training was\nhowever very similar between inter-\nvention and control groups.\nBluett et al. (18) found that 10 weeks of\nconcurrent aerobic and ST provided\nl", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ogether, it seems that the addi-\ntion of 2–3 ST sessions to the weekly\nroutine of adolescent endurance run-\nners provides a small but potentially\nmeaningful beneﬁt to RE and maximal\nsprint speed after intervention over 8–\n10 weeks. Evidence for impro"}
{"chunk_id": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn_p0003_c0017", "source_registry_id": "src_9e3c2a6c0ad2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b101315d163ec581", "text_span_hash": "b101315d163ec581", "source_file": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 17, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "–\n10 weeks. Evidence for improvements\nin performance exists for adult runners\n(16); however, there is currently a lack\nof research in younger endurance run-\nners. Beneﬁts are likely to be larger for\ninterventions of a longer duration (29)\nand for ST", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "EFFICACY IN ADOLESCENT\nThree studies have investigated the\neffect of ST speciﬁcally in young (,18\nyears) middle- or long-distance runners\nand these are summarized in Table 1. A\nrecent study by Blagrove et al. (17)\nfound that 2 weekly sessions of ST\n(mainly PT and RT) added to the pro-\ngram of postpubertal adolescent dis-\ntance runners (17 years) for 10 weeks\nwas “possibly beneﬁcial” for RE (effect\nsize: 0.31–0.51) and “highly likely ben-\neﬁcial” for maximal sprint speed. How-\nimprovement reached statistical signiﬁ-\ncance (P , 0.05) compared with the\nchange observed in the control group.\nMikkola et al. (58) took a group of\ntrained male and female distance run-\nners (mean: 17 years, V̇O2max: 62.5\nmL$kg21$min21) and after 8 weeks of\nERT, PT, and sprint training, noted\na difference (22.7%) in RE at 14\nanaerobic capabilities (speed during\nthe maximal anaerobic running test\nand 30-m sprint) compared with a run-\nning-only group. It is noteworthy that\nboth of these investigations (17,58)\nincluded sprints (3–10 3 30–150 m)\nas part of the intervention, which pro-\nvides a highly speciﬁc overload to the\nneuromuscular system in endurance\nrunners. Interestingly, participants in the\nST intervention groups in these studies\nreduced their weekly running volume to\nactivities. Total time spent training was\nhowever very similar between inter-\nvention and control groups.\nBluett et al. (18) found that 10 weeks of\nconcurrent aerobic and ST provided\nl", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "–\n10 weeks. Evidence for improvements\nin performance exists for adult runners\n(16); however, there is currently a lack\nof research in younger endurance run-\nners. Beneﬁts are likely to be larger for\ninterventions of a longer duration (29)\nand for ST"}
{"chunk_id": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn_p0003_c0020", "source_registry_id": "src_9e3c2a6c0ad2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e3347148adad5add", "text_span_hash": "e3347148adad5add", "source_file": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 20, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "signiﬁcant alterations in\nhormonal status cause rapid physical\ngrowth (52). Contemporary views of\nlong-term youth development suggest\nadolescents should avoid training rou-\ntines that focus on intensive training in\na single sport (for .8 months per y", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "EFFICACY IN ADOLESCENT\nThree studies have investigated the\neffect of ST speciﬁcally in young (,18\nyears) middle- or long-distance runners\nand these are summarized in Table 1. A\nrecent study by Blagrove et al. (17)\nfound that 2 weekly sessions of ST\n(mainly PT and RT) added to the pro-\ngram of postpubertal adolescent dis-\ntance runners (17 years) for 10 weeks\nwas “possibly beneﬁcial” for RE (effect\nsize: 0.31–0.51) and “highly likely ben-\neﬁcial” for maximal sprint speed. How-\nimprovement reached statistical signiﬁ-\ncance (P , 0.05) compared with the\nchange observed in the control group.\nMikkola et al. (58) took a group of\ntrained male and female distance run-\nners (mean: 17 years, V̇O2max: 62.5\nmL$kg21$min21) and after 8 weeks of\nERT, PT, and sprint training, noted\na difference (22.7%) in RE at 14\nanaerobic capabilities (speed during\nthe maximal anaerobic running test\nand 30-m sprint) compared with a run-\nning-only group. It is noteworthy that\nboth of these investigations (17,58)\nincluded sprints (3–10 3 30–150 m)\nas part of the intervention, which pro-\nvides a highly speciﬁc overload to the\nneuromuscular system in endurance\nrunners. Interestingly, participants in the\nST intervention groups in these studies\nreduced their weekly running volume to\nactivities. Total time spent training was\nhowever very similar between inter-\nvention and control groups.\nBluett et al. (18) found that 10 weeks of\nconcurrent aerobic and ST provided\nl", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "signiﬁcant alterations in\nhormonal status cause rapid physical\ngrowth (52). Contemporary views of\nlong-term youth development suggest\nadolescents should avoid training rou-\ntines that focus on intensive training in\na single sport (for .8 months per y"}
