 reaction force during squat jump.\nStrength and Conditioning Journal | www.nsca-scj.com\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditioning Association. Unauthorized reproduction of this article is prohibited.", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ng; RDL 5 Romanian deadlift; RE 5 running economy; RFD 5 rate of force development; RT 5 resistance\ntraining; sLTP 5 speed at lactate turnpoint; TT 5 time trial; vGRFjump 5 vertical ground reaction force during squat jump.\nStrength and Conditioning J"}
{"chunk_id": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn_p0005_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_9e3c2a6c0ad2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2ba1b07c624f7a85", "text_span_hash": "2ba1b07c624f7a85", "source_file": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "with\nuntrained adults (14). It was suggested\nthat prepubertal children avoid speciﬁc\ntraining to develop aerobic metabolic\nqualities and shift priority during post-\npubertal years once movement tech-\nhave been developed (14). Due to the\nrisks associ", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "endurance training (and metabolic con-\nditioning) is not emphasized, relative to\nother biomotor abilities, until late ado-\nlescence (49) because typically this type\nof training is associated with high vol-\numes of work, which may lead to injury\nor overtraining (54). Moreover, prepu-\nbertal children have tended to show\nsmaller changes (,10%) in aerobic\nmeasures after endurance training inter-\nventions compared with postpubertal\nadolescents and adults (54,56). A recent\nstudy also showed that prepubertal boys\n(11 years) were metabolically compara-\nble with well-trained endurance athletes\nand experienced less fatigue during\nhigh-intensity exercise compared with\nuntrained adults (14). It was suggested\nthat prepubertal children avoid speciﬁc\ntraining to develop aerobic metabolic\nqualities and shift priority during post-\npubertal years once movement tech-\nhave been developed (14). Due to the\nrisks associated with early specializa-\ntion, it is recommended that adolescent\nathletes younger than 15 years do not\nsolely specialize in endurance running,\nbut should participate in a wide range of\nsports and physical activities, includ-\nORGANIZATION OF THE TRAINING\nBefore specialization in a sport of\na young athlete’s choice, physical train-\ning should be semistructured and not\nemphasize peaking for competitions\nendurance runner will typically run\n45–55 miles weekly in preparation for\na race (80) which, when combined\nwith academic and social c", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "with\nuntrained adults (14). It was suggested\nthat prepubertal children avoid speciﬁc\ntraining to develop aerobic metabolic\nqualities and shift priority during post-\npubertal years once movement tech-\nhave been developed (14). Due to the\nrisks associ"}
{"chunk_id": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn_p0005_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_9e3c2a6c0ad2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "18036eb1c8254b53", "text_span_hash": "18036eb1c8254b53", "source_file": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "(14). Due to the\nrisks associated with early specializa-\ntion, it is recommended that adolescent\nathletes younger than 15 years do not\nsolely specialize in endurance running,\nbut should participate in a wide range of\nsports and physical activities,", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "endurance training (and metabolic con-\nditioning) is not emphasized, relative to\nother biomotor abilities, until late ado-\nlescence (49) because typically this type\nof training is associated with high vol-\numes of work, which may lead to injury\nor overtraining (54). Moreover, prepu-\nbertal children have tended to show\nsmaller changes (,10%) in aerobic\nmeasures after endurance training inter-\nventions compared with postpubertal\nadolescents and adults (54,56). A recent\nstudy also showed that prepubertal boys\n(11 years) were metabolically compara-\nble with well-trained endurance athletes\nand experienced less fatigue during\nhigh-intensity exercise compared with\nuntrained adults (14). It was suggested\nthat prepubertal children avoid speciﬁc\ntraining to develop aerobic metabolic\nqualities and shift priority during post-\npubertal years once movement tech-\nhave been developed (14). Due to the\nrisks associated with early specializa-\ntion, it is recommended that adolescent\nathletes younger than 15 years do not\nsolely specialize in endurance running,\nbut should participate in a wide range of\nsports and physical activities, includ-\nORGANIZATION OF THE TRAINING\nBefore specialization in a sport of\na young athlete’s choice, physical train-\ning should be semistructured and not\nemphasize peaking for competitions\nendurance runner will typically run\n45–55 miles weekly in preparation for\na race (80) which, when combined\nwith academic and social c", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "(14). Due to the\nrisks associated with early specializa-\ntion, it is recommended that adolescent\nathletes younger than 15 years do not\nsolely specialize in endurance running,\nbut should participate in a wide range of\nsports and physical activities,"}
{"chunk_id": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn_p0005_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_9e3c2a6c0ad2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0193525786795605", "text_span_hash": "0193525786795605", "source_file": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "orts and physical activities, includ-\nORGANIZATION OF THE TRAINING\nBefore specialization in a sport of\na young athlete’s choice, physical train-\ning should be semistructured and not\nemphasize peaking for competitions\nendurance runner will typically r", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "endurance training (and metabolic con-\nditioning) is not emphasized, relative to\nother biomotor abilities, until late ado-\nlescence (49) because typically this type\nof training is associated with high vol-\numes of work, which may lead to injury\nor overtraining (54). Moreover, prepu-\nbertal children have tended to show\nsmaller changes (,10%) in aerobic\nmeasures after endurance training inter-\nventions compared with postpubertal\nadolescents and adults (54,56). A recent\nstudy also showed that prepubertal boys\n(11 years) were metabolically compara-\nble with well-trained endurance athletes\nand experienced less fatigue during\nhigh-intensity exercise compared with\nuntrained adults (14). It was suggested\nthat prepubertal children avoid speciﬁc\ntraining to develop aerobic metabolic\nqualities and shift priority during post-\npubertal years once movement tech-\nhave been developed (14). Due to the\nrisks associated with early specializa-\ntion, it is recommended that adolescent\nathletes younger than 15 years do not\nsolely specialize in endurance running,\nbut should participate in a wide range of\nsports and physical activities, includ-\nORGANIZATION OF THE TRAINING\nBefore specialization in a sport of\na young athlete’s choice, physical train-\ning should be semistructured and not\nemphasize peaking for competitions\nendurance runner will typically run\n45–55 miles weekly in preparation for\na race (80) which, when combined\nwith academic and social c", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "orts and physical activities, includ-\nORGANIZATION OF THE TRAINING\nBefore specialization in a sport of\na young athlete’s choice, physical train-\ning should be semistructured and not\nemphasize peaking for competitions\nendurance runner will typically r"}
{"chunk_id": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn_p0005_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_9e3c2a6c0ad2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "897b07d3a857cd3a", "text_span_hash": "897b07d3a857cd3a", "source_file": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ll-organized approach to training\nthat caters to the needs of individual\nathletes and ensures sufﬁcient periods\nof recovery between bouts of training.\nTwo 7-day microcycle designs are\nshown in Tables 2 and 3 to illustrate\nhow an adolescent endurance", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "endurance training (and metabolic con-\nditioning) is not emphasized, relative to\nother biomotor abilities, until late ado-\nlescence (49) because typically this type\nof training is associated with high vol-\numes of work, which may lead to injury\nor overtraining (54). Moreover, prepu-\nbertal children have tended to show\nsmaller changes (,10%) in aerobic\nmeasures after endurance training inter-\nventions compared with postpubertal\nadolescents and adults (54,56). A recent\nstudy also showed that prepubertal boys\n(11 years) were metabolically compara-\nble with well-trained endurance athletes\nand experienced less fatigue during\nhigh-intensity exercise compared with\nuntrained adults (14). It was suggested\nthat prepubertal children avoid speciﬁc\ntraining to develop aerobic metabolic\nqualities and shift priority during post-\npubertal years once movement tech-\nhave been developed (14). Due to the\nrisks associated with early specializa-\ntion, it is recommended that adolescent\nathletes younger than 15 years do not\nsolely specialize in endurance running,\nbut should participate in a wide range of\nsports and physical activities, includ-\nORGANIZATION OF THE TRAINING\nBefore specialization in a sport of\na young athlete’s choice, physical train-\ning should be semistructured and not\nemphasize peaking for competitions\nendurance runner will typically run\n45–55 miles weekly in preparation for\na race (80) which, when combined\nwith academic and social c", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ll-organized approach to training\nthat caters to the needs of individual\nathletes and ensures sufﬁcient periods\nof recovery between bouts of training.\nTwo 7-day microcycle designs are\nshown in Tables 2 and 3 to illustrate\nhow an adolescent endurance"}
{"chunk_id": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn_p0005_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_9e3c2a6c0ad2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b830bb2fe1a4f1c0", "text_span_hash": "b830bb2fe1a4f1c0", "source_file": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "e\nhow an adolescent endurance runner\ncould incorporate S&C activities into\ntheir routine. Adolescent distance run-\nintensity running sessions per week\n(15), and these should form the priority\nsessions in the program (Tables 2 and\n3; Tuesday, Thursday", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "endurance training (and metabolic con-\nditioning) is not emphasized, relative to\nother biomotor abilities, until late ado-\nlescence (49) because typically this type\nof training is associated with high vol-\numes of work, which may lead to injury\nor overtraining (54). Moreover, prepu-\nbertal children have tended to show\nsmaller changes (,10%) in aerobic\nmeasures after endurance training inter-\nventions compared with postpubertal\nadolescents and adults (54,56). A recent\nstudy also showed that prepubertal boys\n(11 years) were metabolically compara-\nble with well-trained endurance athletes\nand experienced less fatigue during\nhigh-intensity exercise compared with\nuntrained adults (14). It was suggested\nthat prepubertal children avoid speciﬁc\ntraining to develop aerobic metabolic\nqualities and shift priority during post-\npubertal years once movement tech-\nhave been developed (14). Due to the\nrisks associated with early specializa-\ntion, it is recommended that adolescent\nathletes younger than 15 years do not\nsolely specialize in endurance running,\nbut should participate in a wide range of\nsports and physical activities, includ-\nORGANIZATION OF THE TRAINING\nBefore specialization in a sport of\na young athlete’s choice, physical train-\ning should be semistructured and not\nemphasize peaking for competitions\nendurance runner will typically run\n45–55 miles weekly in preparation for\na race (80) which, when combined\nwith academic and social c", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "e\nhow an adolescent endurance runner\ncould incorporate S&C activities into\ntheir routine. Adolescent distance run-\nintensity running sessions per week\n(15), and these should form the priority\nsessions in the program (Tables 2 and\n3; Tuesday, Thursday"}
{"chunk_id": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn_p0005_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_9e3c2a6c0ad2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1315157e3cff4566", "text_span_hash": "1315157e3cff4566", "source_file": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "les 2 and\n3; Tuesday, Thursday, and Saturday).\nSimilarly, a minimum of 2 ST sessions\nper week are suggested for adolescents\n(11,48) and endurance runners (16). RT\nsessions should ideally take place at\nleast 3 hours after a running session\n(6) and at", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "endurance training (and metabolic con-\nditioning) is not emphasized, relative to\nother biomotor abilities, until late ado-\nlescence (49) because typically this type\nof training is associated with high vol-\numes of work, which may lead to injury\nor overtraining (54). Moreover, prepu-\nbertal children have tended to show\nsmaller changes (,10%) in aerobic\nmeasures after endurance training inter-\nventions compared with postpubertal\nadolescents and adults (54,56). A recent\nstudy also showed that prepubertal boys\n(11 years) were metabolically compara-\nble with well-trained endurance athletes\nand experienced less fatigue during\nhigh-intensity exercise compared with\nuntrained adults (14). It was suggested\nthat prepubertal children avoid speciﬁc\ntraining to develop aerobic metabolic\nqualities and shift priority during post-\npubertal years once movement tech-\nhave been developed (14). Due to the\nrisks associated with early specializa-\ntion, it is recommended that adolescent\nathletes younger than 15 years do not\nsolely specialize in endurance running,\nbut should participate in a wide range of\nsports and physical activities, includ-\nORGANIZATION OF THE TRAINING\nBefore specialization in a sport of\na young athlete’s choice, physical train-\ning should be semistructured and not\nemphasize peaking for competitions\nendurance runner will typically run\n45–55 miles weekly in preparation for\na race (80) which, when combined\nwith academic and social c", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "les 2 and\n3; Tuesday, Thursday, and Saturday).\nSimilarly, a minimum of 2 ST sessions\nper week are suggested for adolescents\n(11,48) and endurance runners (16). RT\nsessions should ideally take place at\nleast 3 hours after a running session\n(6) and at"}
