{"chunk_id": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn_p0005_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_9e3c2a6c0ad2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0440d74fbcfc54f3", "text_span_hash": "0440d74fbcfc54f3", "source_file": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ore perform a largely home-\nbased routine, or are unable to commit\nto 2 full S&C sessions per week. Each\ntraining unit takes 10–20 minutes to\ncomplete, thus making it easy to inte-\ngrate some purposeful S&C before or\nafter running sessions. It is imp", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "endurance training (and metabolic con-\nditioning) is not emphasized, relative to\nother biomotor abilities, until late ado-\nlescence (49) because typically this type\nof training is associated with high vol-\numes of work, which may lead to injury\nor overtraining (54). Moreover, prepu-\nbertal children have tended to show\nsmaller changes (,10%) in aerobic\nmeasures after endurance training inter-\nventions compared with postpubertal\nadolescents and adults (54,56). A recent\nstudy also showed that prepubertal boys\n(11 years) were metabolically compara-\nble with well-trained endurance athletes\nand experienced less fatigue during\nhigh-intensity exercise compared with\nuntrained adults (14). It was suggested\nthat prepubertal children avoid speciﬁc\ntraining to develop aerobic metabolic\nqualities and shift priority during post-\npubertal years once movement tech-\nhave been developed (14). Due to the\nrisks associated with early specializa-\ntion, it is recommended that adolescent\nathletes younger than 15 years do not\nsolely specialize in endurance running,\nbut should participate in a wide range of\nsports and physical activities, includ-\nORGANIZATION OF THE TRAINING\nBefore specialization in a sport of\na young athlete’s choice, physical train-\ning should be semistructured and not\nemphasize peaking for competitions\nendurance runner will typically run\n45–55 miles weekly in preparation for\na race (80) which, when combined\nwith academic and social c", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ore perform a largely home-\nbased routine, or are unable to commit\nto 2 full S&C sessions per week. Each\ntraining unit takes 10–20 minutes to\ncomplete, thus making it easy to inte-\ngrate some purposeful S&C before or\nafter running sessions. It is imp"}
{"chunk_id": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn_p0005_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_9e3c2a6c0ad2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4ef7b45541dc59c7", "text_span_hash": "4ef7b45541dc59c7", "source_file": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "er running sessions. It is important to\nnote that studies in adolescent distance\nincluding weekly ST sessions are more\nvolume, at least in the short term\nstrength-trained status, it seems that\nExample of a 7-day microcycle for an adolescent endurance", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "endurance training (and metabolic con-\nditioning) is not emphasized, relative to\nother biomotor abilities, until late ado-\nlescence (49) because typically this type\nof training is associated with high vol-\numes of work, which may lead to injury\nor overtraining (54). Moreover, prepu-\nbertal children have tended to show\nsmaller changes (,10%) in aerobic\nmeasures after endurance training inter-\nventions compared with postpubertal\nadolescents and adults (54,56). A recent\nstudy also showed that prepubertal boys\n(11 years) were metabolically compara-\nble with well-trained endurance athletes\nand experienced less fatigue during\nhigh-intensity exercise compared with\nuntrained adults (14). It was suggested\nthat prepubertal children avoid speciﬁc\ntraining to develop aerobic metabolic\nqualities and shift priority during post-\npubertal years once movement tech-\nhave been developed (14). Due to the\nrisks associated with early specializa-\ntion, it is recommended that adolescent\nathletes younger than 15 years do not\nsolely specialize in endurance running,\nbut should participate in a wide range of\nsports and physical activities, includ-\nORGANIZATION OF THE TRAINING\nBefore specialization in a sport of\na young athlete’s choice, physical train-\ning should be semistructured and not\nemphasize peaking for competitions\nendurance runner will typically run\n45–55 miles weekly in preparation for\na race (80) which, when combined\nwith academic and social c", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "er running sessions. It is important to\nnote that studies in adolescent distance\nincluding weekly ST sessions are more\nvolume, at least in the short term\nstrength-trained status, it seems that\nExample of a 7-day microcycle for an adolescent endurance"}
{"chunk_id": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn_p0005_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_9e3c2a6c0ad2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "a006d5bb423853bd", "text_span_hash": "a006d5bb423853bd", "source_file": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "le for an adolescent endurance runner\nSession (am) Easy 30-min run;\naContinuous fast run performed at approximately 10 km race pace (speed at lactate turnpoint).\nS&C 5 strength and conditioning.\nExample of a 7-day microcycle for an adolescent enduran", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "endurance training (and metabolic con-\nditioning) is not emphasized, relative to\nother biomotor abilities, until late ado-\nlescence (49) because typically this type\nof training is associated with high vol-\numes of work, which may lead to injury\nor overtraining (54). Moreover, prepu-\nbertal children have tended to show\nsmaller changes (,10%) in aerobic\nmeasures after endurance training inter-\nventions compared with postpubertal\nadolescents and adults (54,56). A recent\nstudy also showed that prepubertal boys\n(11 years) were metabolically compara-\nble with well-trained endurance athletes\nand experienced less fatigue during\nhigh-intensity exercise compared with\nuntrained adults (14). It was suggested\nthat prepubertal children avoid speciﬁc\ntraining to develop aerobic metabolic\nqualities and shift priority during post-\npubertal years once movement tech-\nhave been developed (14). Due to the\nrisks associated with early specializa-\ntion, it is recommended that adolescent\nathletes younger than 15 years do not\nsolely specialize in endurance running,\nbut should participate in a wide range of\nsports and physical activities, includ-\nORGANIZATION OF THE TRAINING\nBefore specialization in a sport of\na young athlete’s choice, physical train-\ning should be semistructured and not\nemphasize peaking for competitions\nendurance runner will typically run\n45–55 miles weekly in preparation for\na race (80) which, when combined\nwith academic and social c", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "le for an adolescent endurance runner\nSession (am) Easy 30-min run;\naContinuous fast run performed at approximately 10 km race pace (speed at lactate turnpoint).\nS&C 5 strength and conditioning.\nExample of a 7-day microcycle for an adolescent enduran"}
{"chunk_id": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn_p0005_c0016", "source_registry_id": "src_9e3c2a6c0ad2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "85302d55a0556ce8", "text_span_hash": "85302d55a0556ce8", "source_file": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 16, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ycle for an adolescent endurance runner with strength and conditioning activities organized\nas training units before or after running sessions\nSession Easy 30-min run\n30 min + RT and\nTraining units should last 10–20 minutes with the focus on movement", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "endurance training (and metabolic con-\nditioning) is not emphasized, relative to\nother biomotor abilities, until late ado-\nlescence (49) because typically this type\nof training is associated with high vol-\numes of work, which may lead to injury\nor overtraining (54). Moreover, prepu-\nbertal children have tended to show\nsmaller changes (,10%) in aerobic\nmeasures after endurance training inter-\nventions compared with postpubertal\nadolescents and adults (54,56). A recent\nstudy also showed that prepubertal boys\n(11 years) were metabolically compara-\nble with well-trained endurance athletes\nand experienced less fatigue during\nhigh-intensity exercise compared with\nuntrained adults (14). It was suggested\nthat prepubertal children avoid speciﬁc\ntraining to develop aerobic metabolic\nqualities and shift priority during post-\npubertal years once movement tech-\nhave been developed (14). Due to the\nrisks associated with early specializa-\ntion, it is recommended that adolescent\nathletes younger than 15 years do not\nsolely specialize in endurance running,\nbut should participate in a wide range of\nsports and physical activities, includ-\nORGANIZATION OF THE TRAINING\nBefore specialization in a sport of\na young athlete’s choice, physical train-\ning should be semistructured and not\nemphasize peaking for competitions\nendurance runner will typically run\n45–55 miles weekly in preparation for\na race (80) which, when combined\nwith academic and social c", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ycle for an adolescent endurance runner with strength and conditioning activities organized\nas training units before or after running sessions\nSession Easy 30-min run\n30 min + RT and\nTraining units should last 10–20 minutes with the focus on movement"}
{"chunk_id": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn_p0005_c0018", "source_registry_id": "src_9e3c2a6c0ad2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0246f2f06bcc787c", "text_span_hash": "0246f2f06bcc787c", "source_file": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 18, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "for Adolescent Runners\nVOLUME 42 | NUMBER 1 | FEBRUARY 2020\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditioning Association. Unauthorized reproduction of this article is prohibited.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "endurance training (and metabolic con-\nditioning) is not emphasized, relative to\nother biomotor abilities, until late ado-\nlescence (49) because typically this type\nof training is associated with high vol-\numes of work, which may lead to injury\nor overtraining (54). Moreover, prepu-\nbertal children have tended to show\nsmaller changes (,10%) in aerobic\nmeasures after endurance training inter-\nventions compared with postpubertal\nadolescents and adults (54,56). A recent\nstudy also showed that prepubertal boys\n(11 years) were metabolically compara-\nble with well-trained endurance athletes\nand experienced less fatigue during\nhigh-intensity exercise compared with\nuntrained adults (14). It was suggested\nthat prepubertal children avoid speciﬁc\ntraining to develop aerobic metabolic\nqualities and shift priority during post-\npubertal years once movement tech-\nhave been developed (14). Due to the\nrisks associated with early specializa-\ntion, it is recommended that adolescent\nathletes younger than 15 years do not\nsolely specialize in endurance running,\nbut should participate in a wide range of\nsports and physical activities, includ-\nORGANIZATION OF THE TRAINING\nBefore specialization in a sport of\na young athlete’s choice, physical train-\ning should be semistructured and not\nemphasize peaking for competitions\nendurance runner will typically run\n45–55 miles weekly in preparation for\na race (80) which, when combined\nwith academic and social c", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "for Adolescent Runners\nVOLUME 42 | NUMBER 1 | FEBRUARY 2020\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditioning Association. Unauthorized reproduction of this article is prohibited."}
{"chunk_id": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn_p0006_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_9e3c2a6c0ad2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f76b5650abce9426", "text_span_hash": "f76b5650abce9426", "source_file": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "a variety of ST modalities can be used\nto achieve similar outcomes. However,\nto maximize long-term adaptations in\nyoung athletes, it is suggested that\na periodized approach is adopted with\nfundamental skills training and RT pri-\noritized initially (9", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "a variety of ST modalities can be used\nto achieve similar outcomes. However,\nto maximize long-term adaptations in\nyoung athletes, it is suggested that\na periodized approach is adopted with\nfundamental skills training and RT pri-\noritized initially (9,25,47). Figure 2 pro-\nvides an overview of the session design\nand characteristics of speciﬁc training\nunits recommended for adolescent dis-\ntance runners. A similar session design\nframework has also been used success-\nfully in other investigations that used\ndistance runners embarking on an ST\nprogram for the ﬁrst time (9,17,58).\nMOVEMENT SKILLS TRAINING\nThe inclusion of MST in the routine of\nadolescent distance runners is recom-\nmended, and is likely to reduce long-\nterm injury risk (45,62,82). This form\nof conditioning is ideal to include as\nwarm-up routine before running and\nSTsessions, or as an independent train-\ning unit (82). MST should include\nactivities to enhance both general (fun-\nrelated) movement skill and control,\nbalance and dynamic stability, and\nlow-level RT targeting speciﬁc muscle\ngroups, such as the gluteals (37).\nPLYOMETRICS AND SPRINT\nLow-intensity plyometric-based exer-\ncises that aim to develop ankle stiffness,\nsuch as skipping, low-box rebound\njumps, mini hurdle jumps, and short-\nrange hopping tasks, offer a potent\nstimulus to the neuromuscular system\nand have independently been shown\nto enhance RE and time-trial perfor-\nthat 30–60 foot contacts per session\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "a variety of ST modalities can be used\nto achieve similar outcomes. However,\nto maximize long-term adaptations in\nyoung athletes, it is suggested that\na periodized approach is adopted with\nfundamental skills training and RT pri-\noritized initially (9"}
{"chunk_id": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn_p0006_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_9e3c2a6c0ad2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5df2afa3d5279f95", "text_span_hash": "5df2afa3d5279f95", "source_file": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "RT pri-\noritized initially (9,25,47). Figure 2 pro-\nvides an overview of the session design\nand characteristics of speciﬁc training\nunits recommended for adolescent dis-\ntance runners. A similar session design\nframework has also been used success-\nf", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "a variety of ST modalities can be used\nto achieve similar outcomes. However,\nto maximize long-term adaptations in\nyoung athletes, it is suggested that\na periodized approach is adopted with\nfundamental skills training and RT pri-\noritized initially (9,25,47). Figure 2 pro-\nvides an overview of the session design\nand characteristics of speciﬁc training\nunits recommended for adolescent dis-\ntance runners. A similar session design\nframework has also been used success-\nfully in other investigations that used\ndistance runners embarking on an ST\nprogram for the ﬁrst time (9,17,58).\nMOVEMENT SKILLS TRAINING\nThe inclusion of MST in the routine of\nadolescent distance runners is recom-\nmended, and is likely to reduce long-\nterm injury risk (45,62,82). This form\nof conditioning is ideal to include as\nwarm-up routine before running and\nSTsessions, or as an independent train-\ning unit (82). MST should include\nactivities to enhance both general (fun-\nrelated) movement skill and control,\nbalance and dynamic stability, and\nlow-level RT targeting speciﬁc muscle\ngroups, such as the gluteals (37).\nPLYOMETRICS AND SPRINT\nLow-intensity plyometric-based exer-\ncises that aim to develop ankle stiffness,\nsuch as skipping, low-box rebound\njumps, mini hurdle jumps, and short-\nrange hopping tasks, offer a potent\nstimulus to the neuromuscular system\nand have independently been shown\nto enhance RE and time-trial perfor-\nthat 30–60 foot contacts per session\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "RT pri-\noritized initially (9,25,47). Figure 2 pro-\nvides an overview of the session design\nand characteristics of speciﬁc training\nunits recommended for adolescent dis-\ntance runners. A similar session design\nframework has also been used success-\nf"}
