ngroups, such as the gluteals (37).\nPLYOMETRICS AND SPRINT\nLow-intensity plyometric-based exer-\ncises that aim to develop ankle stiffness,\nsuch as skipping, low-box rebound\njumps, mini hurdle jumps, and short-\nrange hopping tasks, offer a potent\nstimulus to the neuromuscular system\nand have independently been shown\nto enhance RE and time-trial perfor-\nthat 30–60 foot contacts per session\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "has also been used success-\nfully in other investigations that used\ndistance runners embarking on an ST\nprogram for the ﬁrst time (9,17,58).\nMOVEMENT SKILLS TRAINING\nThe inclusion of MST in the routine of\nadolescent distance runners is recom-\nmended"}
{"chunk_id": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn_p0006_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_9e3c2a6c0ad2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c8ae38ac4253f272", "text_span_hash": "c8ae38ac4253f272", "source_file": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "neuromuscular system\nand have independently been shown\nto enhance RE and time-trial perfor-\nthat 30–60 foot contacts per session\nare used initially with adolescent dis-\ntance runners (17). Sprint training has\nalso been used in several investigations", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "a variety of ST modalities can be used\nto achieve similar outcomes. However,\nto maximize long-term adaptations in\nyoung athletes, it is suggested that\na periodized approach is adopted with\nfundamental skills training and RT pri-\noritized initially (9,25,47). Figure 2 pro-\nvides an overview of the session design\nand characteristics of speciﬁc training\nunits recommended for adolescent dis-\ntance runners. A similar session design\nframework has also been used success-\nfully in other investigations that used\ndistance runners embarking on an ST\nprogram for the ﬁrst time (9,17,58).\nMOVEMENT SKILLS TRAINING\nThe inclusion of MST in the routine of\nadolescent distance runners is recom-\nmended, and is likely to reduce long-\nterm injury risk (45,62,82). This form\nof conditioning is ideal to include as\nwarm-up routine before running and\nSTsessions, or as an independent train-\ning unit (82). MST should include\nactivities to enhance both general (fun-\nrelated) movement skill and control,\nbalance and dynamic stability, and\nlow-level RT targeting speciﬁc muscle\ngroups, such as the gluteals (37).\nPLYOMETRICS AND SPRINT\nLow-intensity plyometric-based exer-\ncises that aim to develop ankle stiffness,\nsuch as skipping, low-box rebound\njumps, mini hurdle jumps, and short-\nrange hopping tasks, offer a potent\nstimulus to the neuromuscular system\nand have independently been shown\nto enhance RE and time-trial perfor-\nthat 30–60 foot contacts per session\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "neuromuscular system\nand have independently been shown\nto enhance RE and time-trial perfor-\nthat 30–60 foot contacts per session\nare used initially with adolescent dis-\ntance runners (17). Sprint training has\nalso been used in several investigations"}
{"chunk_id": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn_p0006_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_9e3c2a6c0ad2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "32fa5c0eec3a8a27", "text_span_hash": "32fa5c0eec3a8a27", "source_file": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "used in several investigations\nspeed and performance-related factors\n(17,58,59,66). Three to 5 sets of short-\nmaximal sprints performed 2–3 times\nper week is likely to provide beneﬁts to\nadolescent endurance runners.\nRT, which should include both ERT", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "a variety of ST modalities can be used\nto achieve similar outcomes. However,\nto maximize long-term adaptations in\nyoung athletes, it is suggested that\na periodized approach is adopted with\nfundamental skills training and RT pri-\noritized initially (9,25,47). Figure 2 pro-\nvides an overview of the session design\nand characteristics of speciﬁc training\nunits recommended for adolescent dis-\ntance runners. A similar session design\nframework has also been used success-\nfully in other investigations that used\ndistance runners embarking on an ST\nprogram for the ﬁrst time (9,17,58).\nMOVEMENT SKILLS TRAINING\nThe inclusion of MST in the routine of\nadolescent distance runners is recom-\nmended, and is likely to reduce long-\nterm injury risk (45,62,82). This form\nof conditioning is ideal to include as\nwarm-up routine before running and\nSTsessions, or as an independent train-\ning unit (82). MST should include\nactivities to enhance both general (fun-\nrelated) movement skill and control,\nbalance and dynamic stability, and\nlow-level RT targeting speciﬁc muscle\ngroups, such as the gluteals (37).\nPLYOMETRICS AND SPRINT\nLow-intensity plyometric-based exer-\ncises that aim to develop ankle stiffness,\nsuch as skipping, low-box rebound\njumps, mini hurdle jumps, and short-\nrange hopping tasks, offer a potent\nstimulus to the neuromuscular system\nand have independently been shown\nto enhance RE and time-trial perfor-\nthat 30–60 foot contacts per session\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "used in several investigations\nspeed and performance-related factors\n(17,58,59,66). Three to 5 sets of short-\nmaximal sprints performed 2–3 times\nper week is likely to provide beneﬁts to\nadolescent endurance runners.\nRT, which should include both ERT"}
{"chunk_id": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn_p0006_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_9e3c2a6c0ad2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b718a04b81af1b71", "text_span_hash": "b718a04b81af1b71", "source_file": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "which should include both ERT\nand heavy RT, increases motor unit\nrecruitment and ﬁring frequency, and\nthus enhances a runner’s ability to\nappropriately control and express force\nbe minimal after an ST intervention in\ndistance runners (16), a targete", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "a variety of ST modalities can be used\nto achieve similar outcomes. However,\nto maximize long-term adaptations in\nyoung athletes, it is suggested that\na periodized approach is adopted with\nfundamental skills training and RT pri-\noritized initially (9,25,47). Figure 2 pro-\nvides an overview of the session design\nand characteristics of speciﬁc training\nunits recommended for adolescent dis-\ntance runners. A similar session design\nframework has also been used success-\nfully in other investigations that used\ndistance runners embarking on an ST\nprogram for the ﬁrst time (9,17,58).\nMOVEMENT SKILLS TRAINING\nThe inclusion of MST in the routine of\nadolescent distance runners is recom-\nmended, and is likely to reduce long-\nterm injury risk (45,62,82). This form\nof conditioning is ideal to include as\nwarm-up routine before running and\nSTsessions, or as an independent train-\ning unit (82). MST should include\nactivities to enhance both general (fun-\nrelated) movement skill and control,\nbalance and dynamic stability, and\nlow-level RT targeting speciﬁc muscle\ngroups, such as the gluteals (37).\nPLYOMETRICS AND SPRINT\nLow-intensity plyometric-based exer-\ncises that aim to develop ankle stiffness,\nsuch as skipping, low-box rebound\njumps, mini hurdle jumps, and short-\nrange hopping tasks, offer a potent\nstimulus to the neuromuscular system\nand have independently been shown\nto enhance RE and time-trial perfor-\nthat 30–60 foot contacts per session\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "which should include both ERT\nand heavy RT, increases motor unit\nrecruitment and ﬁring frequency, and\nthus enhances a runner’s ability to\nappropriately control and express force\nbe minimal after an ST intervention in\ndistance runners (16), a targete"}
{"chunk_id": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn_p0006_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_9e3c2a6c0ad2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "59b0d6b679fb342a", "text_span_hash": "59b0d6b679fb342a", "source_file": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "stance runners (16), a targeted RTpro-\ngram that aims to increase muscle mass\nspeciﬁcally around the proximal region\nof the lower limb may enhance biome-\nwhich positively inﬂuences RE (36). Ex-\nercises, such as squats, deadlifts, step-ups,\nand lunge", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "a variety of ST modalities can be used\nto achieve similar outcomes. However,\nto maximize long-term adaptations in\nyoung athletes, it is suggested that\na periodized approach is adopted with\nfundamental skills training and RT pri-\noritized initially (9,25,47). Figure 2 pro-\nvides an overview of the session design\nand characteristics of speciﬁc training\nunits recommended for adolescent dis-\ntance runners. A similar session design\nframework has also been used success-\nfully in other investigations that used\ndistance runners embarking on an ST\nprogram for the ﬁrst time (9,17,58).\nMOVEMENT SKILLS TRAINING\nThe inclusion of MST in the routine of\nadolescent distance runners is recom-\nmended, and is likely to reduce long-\nterm injury risk (45,62,82). This form\nof conditioning is ideal to include as\nwarm-up routine before running and\nSTsessions, or as an independent train-\ning unit (82). MST should include\nactivities to enhance both general (fun-\nrelated) movement skill and control,\nbalance and dynamic stability, and\nlow-level RT targeting speciﬁc muscle\ngroups, such as the gluteals (37).\nPLYOMETRICS AND SPRINT\nLow-intensity plyometric-based exer-\ncises that aim to develop ankle stiffness,\nsuch as skipping, low-box rebound\njumps, mini hurdle jumps, and short-\nrange hopping tasks, offer a potent\nstimulus to the neuromuscular system\nand have independently been shown\nto enhance RE and time-trial perfor-\nthat 30–60 foot contacts per session\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "stance runners (16), a targeted RTpro-\ngram that aims to increase muscle mass\nspeciﬁcally around the proximal region\nof the lower limb may enhance biome-\nwhich positively inﬂuences RE (36). Ex-\nercises, such as squats, deadlifts, step-ups,\nand lunge"}
{"chunk_id": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn_p0006_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_9e3c2a6c0ad2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b23c05c338f150ad", "text_span_hash": "b23c05c338f150ad", "source_file": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "eadlifts, step-ups,\nand lunge patterns, which possess simi-\nlar kinematic characteristics to running\ngait, are likely to provide the greatest\ntransfer (8) and have been used in several\nprevious investigations (9,17,58). Loaded\njump squats, medicine b", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "a variety of ST modalities can be used\nto achieve similar outcomes. However,\nto maximize long-term adaptations in\nyoung athletes, it is suggested that\na periodized approach is adopted with\nfundamental skills training and RT pri-\noritized initially (9,25,47). Figure 2 pro-\nvides an overview of the session design\nand characteristics of speciﬁc training\nunits recommended for adolescent dis-\ntance runners. A similar session design\nframework has also been used success-\nfully in other investigations that used\ndistance runners embarking on an ST\nprogram for the ﬁrst time (9,17,58).\nMOVEMENT SKILLS TRAINING\nThe inclusion of MST in the routine of\nadolescent distance runners is recom-\nmended, and is likely to reduce long-\nterm injury risk (45,62,82). This form\nof conditioning is ideal to include as\nwarm-up routine before running and\nSTsessions, or as an independent train-\ning unit (82). MST should include\nactivities to enhance both general (fun-\nrelated) movement skill and control,\nbalance and dynamic stability, and\nlow-level RT targeting speciﬁc muscle\ngroups, such as the gluteals (37).\nPLYOMETRICS AND SPRINT\nLow-intensity plyometric-based exer-\ncises that aim to develop ankle stiffness,\nsuch as skipping, low-box rebound\njumps, mini hurdle jumps, and short-\nrange hopping tasks, offer a potent\nstimulus to the neuromuscular system\nand have independently been shown\nto enhance RE and time-trial perfor-\nthat 30–60 foot contacts per session\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "eadlifts, step-ups,\nand lunge patterns, which possess simi-\nlar kinematic characteristics to running\ngait, are likely to provide the greatest\ntransfer (8) and have been used in several\nprevious investigations (9,17,58). Loaded\njump squats, medicine b"}
