{"chunk_id": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn_p0007_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_9e3c2a6c0ad2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "a0d363309f7dda99", "text_span_hash": "a0d363309f7dda99", "source_file": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ely because pre-\nfatiguing muscles in isolation is likely\nto be detrimental to performance in\nmultijoint tasks (4). Speciﬁcally for\ndistance runners, targeted condition-\ning exercises should focus on the spe-\nciﬁc structures that are vulnerable to\nin", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "created by the lower limbs and aid in\n(42,69,77). One to 3 sets of each exercise\nperformed in a moderate repetition\nrange (8–12 repetitions) is likely to pro-\nnon–strength-trained\nwith a stimulus sufﬁcient to drive neu-\nromuscular adaptation while developing\nskill in each exercise (9,17,59). Higher\nloads ($80% 1 repetition maximum)\nand lower repetition ranges (3–8 repe-\ntitions) are likely to be required to pro-\nvide further overload in strength-trained\nadolescents, with volume of work mod-\nerated through an increase in sets.\nSPECIFIC CONDITIONING\nMany young endurance runners are\nmotivated to include S&C activities to\nreduce injury risk more so than improve\ntheir performance (15). Youth endur-\nance athletes have been identiﬁed as\na high-risk group due to the rigorous\ntraining that they undertake during\na critical period of their physical and\nemotional development (54,80). Indeed,\ninjury incidence rate has been reported\nto be higher in adolescent elite endur-\nance runners compared with athletes\nwho participate in other endurance\nsports (88). Moreover, female adoles-\ncent runners tend to display higher\nrates of low bone mineral density\nand bone stress injuries compared\nwith young female athletes in other\nsports (78). Overuse injuries occur\nover multiple running sessions when\nstructure-speciﬁc cumulative load ex-\nceeds capacity (13). MST, PT, and\nresistance-based exercises are likely\nto contribute toward lowering risk\nof injury t", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ely because pre-\nfatiguing muscles in isolation is likely\nto be detrimental to performance in\nmultijoint tasks (4). Speciﬁcally for\ndistance runners, targeted condition-\ning exercises should focus on the spe-\nciﬁc structures that are vulnerable to\nin"}
{"chunk_id": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn_p0007_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_9e3c2a6c0ad2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "47c63af15df13100", "text_span_hash": "47c63af15df13100", "source_file": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "wever, anaerobic and\nneuromuscular factors have also been\nrecognized as being important. For the\nyoung athlete, participating in a broad\nrange of sporting and physical pursuits\nis recommended during early adoles-\nform an integral part of a well-\nroun", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "created by the lower limbs and aid in\n(42,69,77). One to 3 sets of each exercise\nperformed in a moderate repetition\nrange (8–12 repetitions) is likely to pro-\nnon–strength-trained\nwith a stimulus sufﬁcient to drive neu-\nromuscular adaptation while developing\nskill in each exercise (9,17,59). Higher\nloads ($80% 1 repetition maximum)\nand lower repetition ranges (3–8 repe-\ntitions) are likely to be required to pro-\nvide further overload in strength-trained\nadolescents, with volume of work mod-\nerated through an increase in sets.\nSPECIFIC CONDITIONING\nMany young endurance runners are\nmotivated to include S&C activities to\nreduce injury risk more so than improve\ntheir performance (15). Youth endur-\nance athletes have been identiﬁed as\na high-risk group due to the rigorous\ntraining that they undertake during\na critical period of their physical and\nemotional development (54,80). Indeed,\ninjury incidence rate has been reported\nto be higher in adolescent elite endur-\nance runners compared with athletes\nwho participate in other endurance\nsports (88). Moreover, female adoles-\ncent runners tend to display higher\nrates of low bone mineral density\nand bone stress injuries compared\nwith young female athletes in other\nsports (78). Overuse injuries occur\nover multiple running sessions when\nstructure-speciﬁc cumulative load ex-\nceeds capacity (13). MST, PT, and\nresistance-based exercises are likely\nto contribute toward lowering risk\nof injury t", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "wever, anaerobic and\nneuromuscular factors have also been\nrecognized as being important. For the\nyoung athlete, participating in a broad\nrange of sporting and physical pursuits\nis recommended during early adoles-\nform an integral part of a well-\nroun"}
{"chunk_id": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn_p0007_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_9e3c2a6c0ad2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "d5153ec1908619b0", "text_span_hash": "d5153ec1908619b0", "source_file": "Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Richard C. Blagrove, PhD, CSCS,1,2 Louis P. Howe, MSc,3 Glyn.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "integral part of a well-\nrounded approach to the long-term\nphysical development of all young\nsports performers. Participating in\nendurance running events can cer-\ntainly form part of a program of\nactivities during adolescence; how-\never, it is sugge", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "created by the lower limbs and aid in\n(42,69,77). One to 3 sets of each exercise\nperformed in a moderate repetition\nrange (8–12 repetitions) is likely to pro-\nnon–strength-trained\nwith a stimulus sufﬁcient to drive neu-\nromuscular adaptation while developing\nskill in each exercise (9,17,59). Higher\nloads ($80% 1 repetition maximum)\nand lower repetition ranges (3–8 repe-\ntitions) are likely to be required to pro-\nvide further overload in strength-trained\nadolescents, with volume of work mod-\nerated through an increase in sets.\nSPECIFIC CONDITIONING\nMany young endurance runners are\nmotivated to include S&C activities to\nreduce injury risk more so than improve\ntheir performance (15). Youth endur-\nance athletes have been identiﬁed as\na high-risk group due to the rigorous\ntraining that they undertake during\na critical period of their physical and\nemotional development (54,80). Indeed,\ninjury incidence rate has been reported\nto be higher in adolescent elite endur-\nance runners compared with athletes\nwho participate in other endurance\nsports (88). Moreover, female adoles-\ncent runners tend to display higher\nrates of low bone mineral density\nand bone stress injuries compared\nwith young female athletes in other\nsports (78). Overuse injuries occur\nover multiple running sessions when\nstructure-speciﬁc cumulative load ex-\nceeds capacity (13). MST, PT, and\nresistance-based exercises are likely\nto contribute toward lowering risk\nof injury t", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "integral part of a well-\nrounded approach to the long-term\nphysical development of all young\nsports performers. Participating in\nendurance running events can cer-\ntainly form part of a program of\nactivities during adolescence; how-\never, it is sugge"}
