]. While it has been\nsuggested that there is difficulty in developing phenomenological accounts of data due to the\ncomplexity of group dynamics (from interactional, social and contextual perspectives) [33],\nthe complexity of individual and shared contexts [33], and the influ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "(i.e., runners) as a group can elicit insightful and experiential data [32]. While it has been\nsuggested that there is difficulty in developing phenomenological accounts of data due to the\ncomplexity of group dynamics (from interactional, social and"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_injury_continuum_2023_p0003_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_5bb5d5843449", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4a89eadfd4a5ed4e", "text_span_hash": "4a89eadfd4a5ed4e", "source_file": "safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "cus group discussion can\nallow for social interaction of shared, similar or conflicting lived experiences of the phenom-\nenon of interest [31, 36].\nA purposive sample of 31 adult recreational runners were recruited. Between April and June\n2022 local", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "study was to explore recreational runners’ description of injury, and their management of the\nprocess of injury development.\nInterpretative phenomenology (IP) was deemed to be an appropriate methodological\napproach because, as a branch of phenomenology, it focuses on the lived experiences of\nhumans, eliciting insightful accounts of individuals’ subjective experiences regarding a cer-\ntain topic [24, 25]. Focus groups were deemed an appropriate method of data collection as\nthey can yield rich, in-depth data through the interaction of participants [26–28], and can\nenhance personal accounts by benefitting from the rapport built in a homogeneous sample\n[29]. Although suggested to be incompatible by some [30], executing focus groups with an\ninterpretative phenomenological approach (IPA) was congruent with the aim of the study\nand allowed for enrichment of the data regarding the phenomenon of interest through the\ninteraction between participants [31]. Despite the complex interactional environment that is\ncreated by conducting focus groups, the opportunity to engage with a homogenous sample\n(i.e., runners) as a group can elicit insightful and experiential data [32]. While it has been\nsuggested that there is difficulty in developing phenomenological accounts of data due to the\ncomplexity of group dynamics (from interactional, social and contextual perspectives) [33],\nthe complexity of individual and shared contexts [33], and the influ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "cus group discussion can\nallow for social interaction of shared, similar or conflicting lived experiences of the phenom-\nenon of interest [31, 36].\nA purposive sample of 31 adult recreational runners were recruited. Between April and June\n2022 local"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_injury_continuum_2023_p0003_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_5bb5d5843449", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "155ba3a16e1de668", "text_span_hash": "155ba3a16e1de668", "source_file": "safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "een April and June\n2022 local running clubs were contacted via email or telephone and asked to distribute\nresearch information and contact details of researchers to potential participants. Those inter-\nested then contacted the researchers. Eligible p", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "study was to explore recreational runners’ description of injury, and their management of the\nprocess of injury development.\nInterpretative phenomenology (IP) was deemed to be an appropriate methodological\napproach because, as a branch of phenomenology, it focuses on the lived experiences of\nhumans, eliciting insightful accounts of individuals’ subjective experiences regarding a cer-\ntain topic [24, 25]. Focus groups were deemed an appropriate method of data collection as\nthey can yield rich, in-depth data through the interaction of participants [26–28], and can\nenhance personal accounts by benefitting from the rapport built in a homogeneous sample\n[29]. Although suggested to be incompatible by some [30], executing focus groups with an\ninterpretative phenomenological approach (IPA) was congruent with the aim of the study\nand allowed for enrichment of the data regarding the phenomenon of interest through the\ninteraction between participants [31]. Despite the complex interactional environment that is\ncreated by conducting focus groups, the opportunity to engage with a homogenous sample\n(i.e., runners) as a group can elicit insightful and experiential data [32]. While it has been\nsuggested that there is difficulty in developing phenomenological accounts of data due to the\ncomplexity of group dynamics (from interactional, social and contextual perspectives) [33],\nthe complexity of individual and shared contexts [33], and the influ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "een April and June\n2022 local running clubs were contacted via email or telephone and asked to distribute\nresearch information and contact details of researchers to potential participants. Those inter-\nested then contacted the researchers. Eligible p"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_injury_continuum_2023_p0003_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_5bb5d5843449", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e2b48d33d012a01c", "text_span_hash": "e2b48d33d012a01c", "source_file": "safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ed the researchers. Eligible participants were recreational runners (someone\nrunning at least once per week for the previous six months [37], aged 18 years or older, and\nhad no previous education or training in musculoskeletal injuries (e.g., Athleti", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "study was to explore recreational runners’ description of injury, and their management of the\nprocess of injury development.\nInterpretative phenomenology (IP) was deemed to be an appropriate methodological\napproach because, as a branch of phenomenology, it focuses on the lived experiences of\nhumans, eliciting insightful accounts of individuals’ subjective experiences regarding a cer-\ntain topic [24, 25]. Focus groups were deemed an appropriate method of data collection as\nthey can yield rich, in-depth data through the interaction of participants [26–28], and can\nenhance personal accounts by benefitting from the rapport built in a homogeneous sample\n[29]. Although suggested to be incompatible by some [30], executing focus groups with an\ninterpretative phenomenological approach (IPA) was congruent with the aim of the study\nand allowed for enrichment of the data regarding the phenomenon of interest through the\ninteraction between participants [31]. Despite the complex interactional environment that is\ncreated by conducting focus groups, the opportunity to engage with a homogenous sample\n(i.e., runners) as a group can elicit insightful and experiential data [32]. While it has been\nsuggested that there is difficulty in developing phenomenological accounts of data due to the\ncomplexity of group dynamics (from interactional, social and contextual perspectives) [33],\nthe complexity of individual and shared contexts [33], and the influ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ed the researchers. Eligible participants were recreational runners (someone\nrunning at least once per week for the previous six months [37], aged 18 years or older, and\nhad no previous education or training in musculoskeletal injuries (e.g., Athleti"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_injury_continuum_2023_p0004_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_5bb5d5843449", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e54ff47a191b56c1", "text_span_hash": "e54ff47a191b56c1", "source_file": "safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ng their expe-\nrience of running-related pain/discomfort). Questions included were in the form of Likert\nscales and open-ended responses. Additionally on this questionnaire, participants were asked\nto draw their individual perception of how a RRI pro", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Main study procedures\nData was collected between April and June 2022. Ethical approval was granted by the local uni-\nversity’s Ethics Committee (DCUREC/2022/071), and the Standards for Reporting Qualitative\nResearch were adhered to [38] (S2 Table). Participants were organised into groups based on\ncollected prior to each focus group). Seven focus groups were moderated by two researchers\n(AL and SOK) and lasted 83.9 ± 18.1 minutes. On arrival to each focus group, participants\nwere introduced to one another by the moderators, and a casual conversation (not recorded)\ntook place prior to starting. Authors (AL and SOK) had access to participants’ identifying\ninformation during data collection. Participants provided informed consent before commenc-\ning. Each focus group began with a brief introduction and the aims of the study were outlined\n(S1 Table). Participants were encouraged to speak freely, ask each other questions, and were\ngiven the opportunity to ask the moderators questions at any point. Firstly, participants were\nasked how they would define a RRI, and then how they would describe a RRI (approx. 10 min-\nutes of discussion). Participants were prompted to elaborate on their descriptions and asked to\ndraw (as a group) their description of RRIs on a whiteboard (approx. 45 minutes of discus-\nsion). They were then asked how they manage RRIs, and asked to insert these descriptions on\ntheir drawing (approx. 30 mins). Conversation ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ng their expe-\nrience of running-related pain/discomfort). Questions included were in the form of Likert\nscales and open-ended responses. Additionally on this questionnaire, participants were asked\nto draw their individual perception of how a RRI pro"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_injury_continuum_2023_p0006_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_5bb5d5843449", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "34f04e8cf0754313", "text_span_hash": "34f04e8cf0754313", "source_file": "safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Seven focus groups were conducted with 18 (58%) female and 13 (42%) male recreational run-\nners. Participants were aged 39.7 ± 12.7 years (range 20–65 years). The majority of runners\nhad been running for more than 5 years (n = 17, 55%), fewer running", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Seven focus groups were conducted with 18 (58%) female and 13 (42%) male recreational run-\nners. Participants were aged 39.7 ± 12.7 years (range 20–65 years). The majority of runners\nhad been running for more than 5 years (n = 17, 55%), fewer running 1–3 years (n = 10, 32%),\nand the least amount running 4–5 years (n = 4, 13%). Participants trained either 2–3 times per\nweek (n = 16, 52%) or 4–6 times per week (n = 15, 48%), with the majority of runners partici-\npating in organised running events (n = 27, 87%). Most (n = 17, 55%) runners predominately\ntrained on their own, with the remainder training in small (n = 7, 23%) or large (n = 7, 23%)\ngroups. Injury prevention was ‘very’ or ‘extremely’ important to the majority of runners\n(n = 21, 68%), with fewer stating that it was ‘slightly’ or ‘moderately’ important (n = 9, 29%),\nand just one participant reporting it was ‘not at all’ important (n = 1, 3%). Further details on\ntraining practices are presented in Table 1. According to the consensus definition of injury\n[16], most runners reported having a previous RRI (n = 27, 87%), while few reported never\nhaving a RRI (n = 4, 13%). Nineteen participants had at least one RRI in the previous 12\nmonths (n = 19, 70%). All participants reported previously experiencing a lower level injury.\nFurther details on RRI history are detailed in Table 1.\nRunners’ description of injury\nRunners described RRIs and the process of injury development on ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Seven focus groups were conducted with 18 (58%) female and 13 (42%) male recreational run-\nners. Participants were aged 39.7 ± 12.7 years (range 20–65 years). The majority of runners\nhad been running for more than 5 years (n = 17, 55%), fewer running"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_injury_continuum_2023_p0006_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_5bb5d5843449", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2e471fab0f52f91c", "text_span_hash": "2e471fab0f52f91c", "source_file": "safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "s (n = 17, 55%), fewer running 1–3 years (n = 10, 32%),\nand the least amount running 4–5 years (n = 4, 13%). Participants trained either 2–3 times per\nweek (n = 16, 52%) or 4–6 times per week (n = 15, 48%), with the majority of runners partici-\npatin", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Seven focus groups were conducted with 18 (58%) female and 13 (42%) male recreational run-\nners. Participants were aged 39.7 ± 12.7 years (range 20–65 years). The majority of runners\nhad been running for more than 5 years (n = 17, 55%), fewer running 1–3 years (n = 10, 32%),\nand the least amount running 4–5 years (n = 4, 13%). Participants trained either 2–3 times per\nweek (n = 16, 52%) or 4–6 times per week (n = 15, 48%), with the majority of runners partici-\npating in organised running events (n = 27, 87%). Most (n = 17, 55%) runners predominately\ntrained on their own, with the remainder training in small (n = 7, 23%) or large (n = 7, 23%)\ngroups. Injury prevention was ‘very’ or ‘extremely’ important to the majority of runners\n(n = 21, 68%), with fewer stating that it was ‘slightly’ or ‘moderately’ important (n = 9, 29%),\nand just one participant reporting it was ‘not at all’ important (n = 1, 3%). Further details on\ntraining practices are presented in Table 1. According to the consensus definition of injury\n[16], most runners reported having a previous RRI (n = 27, 87%), while few reported never\nhaving a RRI (n = 4, 13%). Nineteen participants had at least one RRI in the previous 12\nmonths (n = 19, 70%). All participants reported previously experiencing a lower level injury.\nFurther details on RRI history are detailed in Table 1.\nRunners’ description of injury\nRunners described RRIs and the process of injury development on ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "s (n = 17, 55%), fewer running 1–3 years (n = 10, 32%),\nand the least amount running 4–5 years (n = 4, 13%). Participants trained either 2–3 times per\nweek (n = 16, 52%) or 4–6 times per week (n = 15, 48%), with the majority of runners partici-\npatin"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_injury_continuum_2023_p0006_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_5bb5d5843449", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3b74a9fd4b9ee81e", "text_span_hash": "3b74a9fd4b9ee81e", "source_file": "safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "jury prevention was ‘very’ or ‘extremely’ important to the majority of runners\n(n = 21, 68%), with fewer stating that it was ‘slightly’ or ‘moderately’ important (n = 9, 29%),\nand just one participant reporting it was ‘not at all’ important (n = 1, 3", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Seven focus groups were conducted with 18 (58%) female and 13 (42%) male recreational run-\nners. Participants were aged 39.7 ± 12.7 years (range 20–65 years). The majority of runners\nhad been running for more than 5 years (n = 17, 55%), fewer running 1–3 years (n = 10, 32%),\nand the least amount running 4–5 years (n = 4, 13%). Participants trained either 2–3 times per\nweek (n = 16, 52%) or 4–6 times per week (n = 15, 48%), with the majority of runners partici-\npating in organised running events (n = 27, 87%). Most (n = 17, 55%) runners predominately\ntrained on their own, with the remainder training in small (n = 7, 23%) or large (n = 7, 23%)\ngroups. Injury prevention was ‘very’ or ‘extremely’ important to the majority of runners\n(n = 21, 68%), with fewer stating that it was ‘slightly’ or ‘moderately’ important (n = 9, 29%),\nand just one participant reporting it was ‘not at all’ important (n = 1, 3%). Further details on\ntraining practices are presented in Table 1. According to the consensus definition of injury\n[16], most runners reported having a previous RRI (n = 27, 87%), while few reported never\nhaving a RRI (n = 4, 13%). Nineteen participants had at least one RRI in the previous 12\nmonths (n = 19, 70%). All participants reported previously experiencing a lower level injury.\nFurther details on RRI history are detailed in Table 1.\nRunners’ description of injury\nRunners described RRIs and the process of injury development on ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "jury prevention was ‘very’ or ‘extremely’ important to the majority of runners\n(n = 21, 68%), with fewer stating that it was ‘slightly’ or ‘moderately’ important (n = 9, 29%),\nand just one participant reporting it was ‘not at all’ important (n = 1, 3"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_injury_continuum_2023_p0007_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_5bb5d5843449", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "435c170d3d3e75a5", "text_span_hash": "435c170d3d3e75a5", "source_file": "safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ness” (F17),\n“tiredness” (M3), “a little bit of pain” (F6), and “stiffness” (F3). Many participants also suggested\nthat this level could be associated with previous training (i.e., delayed onset muscle soreness\n[DOMS]) and “being tired from running t", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Runners described each level of the Running Injury Continuum in terms of four key categories\nof descriptors: physical description, outcome (effect on running and effect on daily life), psy-\nchological description and management (Fig 1, Table 2).\nThe Running Injury Continuum\n‘Running smooth’ was described as “zero pain” (M4), “smooth” (M3)\nand “completely perfect” (M9). Runners did not describe a negative physical complaint, a nega-\ntive outcome, or a negative psychological reaction with this level.\nIn relation to managing this first level, some participants suggested they would complete\ninjury prevention exercises because they “don’t want injury to happen” (F11), so they “try to\nprevent it by doing [their] stretching” (F11). However, some runners also suggested that no\nmanagement strategies would be taken at this level, suggesting that they would be “complacent”\n(F10) when injury free, and they would “wait for something to happen and then treat it”(F10),\nbeing “reactive rather than proactive” (F10) to injury management.\n‘Discomfort’ was described with terms and phrases such as “tightness” (F17),\n“tiredness” (M3), “a little bit of pain” (F6), and “stiffness” (F3). Many participants also suggested\nthat this level could be associated with previous training (i.e., delayed onset muscle soreness\n[DOMS]) and “being tired from running the previous day” (M3). They described it as some-\nthing “temporary” (M8) and that they “know that [i", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ness” (F17),\n“tiredness” (M3), “a little bit of pain” (F6), and “stiffness” (F3). Many participants also suggested\nthat this level could be associated with previous training (i.e., delayed onset muscle soreness\n[DOMS]) and “being tired from running t"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_injury_continuum_2023_p0009_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_5bb5d5843449", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1edbdd2d3ec04940", "text_span_hash": "1edbdd2d3ec04940", "source_file": "safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Table 2. (Continued)\nPhysical description\nDifferent from opposite side\nPost-session complaint\nFrom an unknown cause\nPressure to continue\nStick to training plan\nDoesn’t affect training\nForced change to training\nEffect on daily life\nPsychological descr", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Table 2. (Continued)\nPhysical description\nDifferent from opposite side\nPost-session complaint\nFrom an unknown cause\nPressure to continue\nStick to training plan\nDoesn’t affect training\nForced change to training\nEffect on daily life\nPsychological description\nAffects motivation to train\nAdditional stretching\nStrength & conditioning\nThe running injury continuum: Recreational runners’ description and management of injury", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Table 2. (Continued)\nPhysical description\nDifferent from opposite side\nPost-session complaint\nFrom an unknown cause\nPressure to continue\nStick to training plan\nDoesn’t affect training\nForced change to training\nEffect on daily life\nPsychological descr"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_injury_continuum_2023_p0010_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_5bb5d5843449", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "bf1573edcab4a18f", "text_span_hash": "bf1573edcab4a18f", "source_file": "safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Table 2. (Continued)\nPhysical description\nTemporary (short-lived)\nPsychological description\nAdditional stretching\nPhysical description\nEffect on performance\nPsychological description\nAthletic Therapist/Physiotherapist\nNon-responding niggle\nPhysical d", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Table 2. (Continued)\nPhysical description\nTemporary (short-lived)\nPsychological description\nAdditional stretching\nPhysical description\nEffect on performance\nPsychological description\nAthletic Therapist/Physiotherapist\nNon-responding niggle\nPhysical description\nEffect on daily life\nConscious off-loading\nPsychological description\nAdditional stretching\nAthletic Therapist/Physiotherapist\nFailed self-management\nThe running injury continuum: Recreational runners’ description and management of injury", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Table 2. (Continued)\nPhysical description\nTemporary (short-lived)\nPsychological description\nAdditional stretching\nPhysical description\nEffect on performance\nPsychological description\nAthletic Therapist/Physiotherapist\nNon-responding niggle\nPhysical d"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_injury_continuum_2023_p0011_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_5bb5d5843449", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f9e8bea26ba5b8c1", "text_span_hash": "f9e8bea26ba5b8c1", "source_file": "safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "use a negative effect on running, with\nparticipants suggesting that “you’d definitely run” (F6) and complete full training at this level;\n“you don’t back off your mileage because you are tight” (F15). Additionally, this level did not\nhave a negative", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "but they are also confident that it will not persist into a more serious injury; “There is a good\ntightness. Like your quads after a speed session” (F18). However, some runners described this\nlevel as a “precursor to injury” (M1) and suggested that further injury can develop from this\nlevel because “you have planted the little injury seed. . . the injury is on its way” (M4). With\nregard to the outcome, this level was not suggested to cause a negative effect on running, with\nparticipants suggesting that “you’d definitely run” (F6) and complete full training at this level;\n“you don’t back off your mileage because you are tight” (F15). Additionally, this level did not\nhave a negative effect on a runner’s daily life, however, some runners associated it with ‘mental\nfatigue’ and described how they “don’t feel mentally strong” at this level (M3).\nThe majority of runners take no management strategies because “you know you will recover\nin half a day, in two days, three days”(M8) and continuing training is the best management\nTable 2. (Continued)\nPhysical description\nProgressively worsening\nContinue to run (with pain)\nEffect on daily life\nConscious off-loading\nPsychological description\nAthletic Therapist/Physiotherapist\nPhysical description\nUnable to run–long term (months)\nEffect on daily life\nConscious off-loading\nPsychological description\nAthletic Therapist/Physiotherapist\nCareer-ending injury\nPhysical description\nPain outside runnin", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "use a negative effect on running, with\nparticipants suggesting that “you’d definitely run” (F6) and complete full training at this level;\n“you don’t back off your mileage because you are tight” (F15). Additionally, this level did not\nhave a negative"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_injury_continuum_2023_p0011_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_5bb5d5843449", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "fa285ac9f90bdeb7", "text_span_hash": "fa285ac9f90bdeb7", "source_file": "safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "e no management strategies because “you know you will recover\nin half a day, in two days, three days”(M8) and continuing training is the best management\nTable 2. (Continued)\nPhysical description\nProgressively worsening\nContinue to run (with pain)\nEff", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "but they are also confident that it will not persist into a more serious injury; “There is a good\ntightness. Like your quads after a speed session” (F18). However, some runners described this\nlevel as a “precursor to injury” (M1) and suggested that further injury can develop from this\nlevel because “you have planted the little injury seed. . . the injury is on its way” (M4). With\nregard to the outcome, this level was not suggested to cause a negative effect on running, with\nparticipants suggesting that “you’d definitely run” (F6) and complete full training at this level;\n“you don’t back off your mileage because you are tight” (F15). Additionally, this level did not\nhave a negative effect on a runner’s daily life, however, some runners associated it with ‘mental\nfatigue’ and described how they “don’t feel mentally strong” at this level (M3).\nThe majority of runners take no management strategies because “you know you will recover\nin half a day, in two days, three days”(M8) and continuing training is the best management\nTable 2. (Continued)\nPhysical description\nProgressively worsening\nContinue to run (with pain)\nEffect on daily life\nConscious off-loading\nPsychological description\nAthletic Therapist/Physiotherapist\nPhysical description\nUnable to run–long term (months)\nEffect on daily life\nConscious off-loading\nPsychological description\nAthletic Therapist/Physiotherapist\nCareer-ending injury\nPhysical description\nPain outside runnin", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "e no management strategies because “you know you will recover\nin half a day, in two days, three days”(M8) and continuing training is the best management\nTable 2. (Continued)\nPhysical description\nProgressively worsening\nContinue to run (with pain)\nEff"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_injury_continuum_2023_p0012_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_5bb5d5843449", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5b243979f9bc22e4", "text_span_hash": "5b243979f9bc22e4", "source_file": "safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "remind me to do the stretching that I know I should be doing anyway”\n(M1). Additionally, some runners may look to external sources such as YouTube for appropri-\nate stretches/exercises; “I find myself YouTubing quite a lot. If I have a pain in my ca", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "strategy; “for the stiffness one, the treatment of that one probably would be to go for a run. . . to\nloosen it out” (F3). Some participants however described using self-management strategies\nsuch as stretching; “it will remind me to do the stretching that I know I should be doing anyway”\n(M1). Additionally, some runners may look to external sources such as YouTube for appropri-\nate stretches/exercises; “I find myself YouTubing quite a lot. If I have a pain in my calf, I just do\na five minute exercise that I find on YouTube and it will be gone that day” (M7), or casually chat\nto their running friends about the complaint: “but only by the by, if it came up in conversation I\nmight mention it” (F10).\n‘Niggle’ was a term used by all participants in all focus groups with different\ndescriptions presented. In its mildest form, some runners physically described a ‘niggle’ as\nbeing more “aware” (M13) or “conscious” (F8) of a certain body part, describing it as “not\n[being] the same as the other side [of the body]” (M1), or suggested it is “background noise”\n(F10). Other runners described a ‘niggle’ as something slightly more severe, with phrases such\nas “background pain” (F15), and “a pain that shouldn’t be there” (M4). The majority of partici-\npants described a ‘niggle’ as something “repeated” (F8), “persistent” (F3) and “something that\nlingers” (F11): “discomfort can come and go, whereas a niggle is sort of always there” (F10). In\nre", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "remind me to do the stretching that I know I should be doing anyway”\n(M1). Additionally, some runners may look to external sources such as YouTube for appropri-\nate stretches/exercises; “I find myself YouTubing quite a lot. If I have a pain in my ca"}
