{"chunk_id": "safety_injury_continuum_2023_p0012_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_5bb5d5843449", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "993387d91548a09a", "text_span_hash": "993387d91548a09a", "source_file": "safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "being more “aware” (M13) or “conscious” (F8) of a certain body part, describing it as “not\n[being] the same as the other side [of the body]” (M1), or suggested it is “background noise”\n(F10). Other runners described a ‘niggle’ as something slightly m", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "strategy; “for the stiffness one, the treatment of that one probably would be to go for a run. . . to\nloosen it out” (F3). Some participants however described using self-management strategies\nsuch as stretching; “it will remind me to do the stretching that I know I should be doing anyway”\n(M1). Additionally, some runners may look to external sources such as YouTube for appropri-\nate stretches/exercises; “I find myself YouTubing quite a lot. If I have a pain in my calf, I just do\na five minute exercise that I find on YouTube and it will be gone that day” (M7), or casually chat\nto their running friends about the complaint: “but only by the by, if it came up in conversation I\nmight mention it” (F10).\n‘Niggle’ was a term used by all participants in all focus groups with different\ndescriptions presented. In its mildest form, some runners physically described a ‘niggle’ as\nbeing more “aware” (M13) or “conscious” (F8) of a certain body part, describing it as “not\n[being] the same as the other side [of the body]” (M1), or suggested it is “background noise”\n(F10). Other runners described a ‘niggle’ as something slightly more severe, with phrases such\nas “background pain” (F15), and “a pain that shouldn’t be there” (M4). The majority of partici-\npants described a ‘niggle’ as something “repeated” (F8), “persistent” (F3) and “something that\nlingers” (F11): “discomfort can come and go, whereas a niggle is sort of always there” (F10). In\nre", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "being more “aware” (M13) or “conscious” (F8) of a certain body part, describing it as “not\n[being] the same as the other side [of the body]” (M1), or suggested it is “background noise”\n(F10). Other runners described a ‘niggle’ as something slightly m"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_injury_continuum_2023_p0012_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_5bb5d5843449", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8ffa4f7ea9941a3e", "text_span_hash": "8ffa4f7ea9941a3e", "source_file": "safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "tent” (F3) and “something that\nlingers” (F11): “discomfort can come and go, whereas a niggle is sort of always there” (F10). In\nrelation to the outcome, the majority of participants continue full training with a ‘niggle’, with\nthe perception that run", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "strategy; “for the stiffness one, the treatment of that one probably would be to go for a run. . . to\nloosen it out” (F3). Some participants however described using self-management strategies\nsuch as stretching; “it will remind me to do the stretching that I know I should be doing anyway”\n(M1). Additionally, some runners may look to external sources such as YouTube for appropri-\nate stretches/exercises; “I find myself YouTubing quite a lot. If I have a pain in my calf, I just do\na five minute exercise that I find on YouTube and it will be gone that day” (M7), or casually chat\nto their running friends about the complaint: “but only by the by, if it came up in conversation I\nmight mention it” (F10).\n‘Niggle’ was a term used by all participants in all focus groups with different\ndescriptions presented. In its mildest form, some runners physically described a ‘niggle’ as\nbeing more “aware” (M13) or “conscious” (F8) of a certain body part, describing it as “not\n[being] the same as the other side [of the body]” (M1), or suggested it is “background noise”\n(F10). Other runners described a ‘niggle’ as something slightly more severe, with phrases such\nas “background pain” (F15), and “a pain that shouldn’t be there” (M4). The majority of partici-\npants described a ‘niggle’ as something “repeated” (F8), “persistent” (F3) and “something that\nlingers” (F11): “discomfort can come and go, whereas a niggle is sort of always there” (F10). In\nre", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "tent” (F3) and “something that\nlingers” (F11): “discomfort can come and go, whereas a niggle is sort of always there” (F10). In\nrelation to the outcome, the majority of participants continue full training with a ‘niggle’, with\nthe perception that run"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_injury_continuum_2023_p0012_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_5bb5d5843449", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1f50f6a566c1e0c3", "text_span_hash": "1f50f6a566c1e0c3", "source_file": "safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": ", with\nthe perception that runners are “quite high functioning with a niggle” (F17). Many runners\ndescribed a ‘niggle’ as “not bad enough” (F7) to stop training, and suggested that they can “cope\nwith a niggle” (F11), “ignore it a lot of the time” (F", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "strategy; “for the stiffness one, the treatment of that one probably would be to go for a run. . . to\nloosen it out” (F3). Some participants however described using self-management strategies\nsuch as stretching; “it will remind me to do the stretching that I know I should be doing anyway”\n(M1). Additionally, some runners may look to external sources such as YouTube for appropri-\nate stretches/exercises; “I find myself YouTubing quite a lot. If I have a pain in my calf, I just do\na five minute exercise that I find on YouTube and it will be gone that day” (M7), or casually chat\nto their running friends about the complaint: “but only by the by, if it came up in conversation I\nmight mention it” (F10).\n‘Niggle’ was a term used by all participants in all focus groups with different\ndescriptions presented. In its mildest form, some runners physically described a ‘niggle’ as\nbeing more “aware” (M13) or “conscious” (F8) of a certain body part, describing it as “not\n[being] the same as the other side [of the body]” (M1), or suggested it is “background noise”\n(F10). Other runners described a ‘niggle’ as something slightly more severe, with phrases such\nas “background pain” (F15), and “a pain that shouldn’t be there” (M4). The majority of partici-\npants described a ‘niggle’ as something “repeated” (F8), “persistent” (F3) and “something that\nlingers” (F11): “discomfort can come and go, whereas a niggle is sort of always there” (F10). In\nre", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": ", with\nthe perception that runners are “quite high functioning with a niggle” (F17). Many runners\ndescribed a ‘niggle’ as “not bad enough” (F7) to stop training, and suggested that they can “cope\nwith a niggle” (F11), “ignore it a lot of the time” (F"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_injury_continuum_2023_p0012_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_5bb5d5843449", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "777d0d4b23de8829", "text_span_hash": "777d0d4b23de8829", "source_file": "safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "gnore it a lot of the time” (F13), or they can “run through it” (F18). Some\nalso described how they feel pressure to continue training with a ‘niggle’, whether it is due to\nrunning with a group: “if I am with a group I am like ‘oh I need to keep goin", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "strategy; “for the stiffness one, the treatment of that one probably would be to go for a run. . . to\nloosen it out” (F3). Some participants however described using self-management strategies\nsuch as stretching; “it will remind me to do the stretching that I know I should be doing anyway”\n(M1). Additionally, some runners may look to external sources such as YouTube for appropri-\nate stretches/exercises; “I find myself YouTubing quite a lot. If I have a pain in my calf, I just do\na five minute exercise that I find on YouTube and it will be gone that day” (M7), or casually chat\nto their running friends about the complaint: “but only by the by, if it came up in conversation I\nmight mention it” (F10).\n‘Niggle’ was a term used by all participants in all focus groups with different\ndescriptions presented. In its mildest form, some runners physically described a ‘niggle’ as\nbeing more “aware” (M13) or “conscious” (F8) of a certain body part, describing it as “not\n[being] the same as the other side [of the body]” (M1), or suggested it is “background noise”\n(F10). Other runners described a ‘niggle’ as something slightly more severe, with phrases such\nas “background pain” (F15), and “a pain that shouldn’t be there” (M4). The majority of partici-\npants described a ‘niggle’ as something “repeated” (F8), “persistent” (F3) and “something that\nlingers” (F11): “discomfort can come and go, whereas a niggle is sort of always there” (F10). In\nre", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "gnore it a lot of the time” (F13), or they can “run through it” (F18). Some\nalso described how they feel pressure to continue training with a ‘niggle’, whether it is due to\nrunning with a group: “if I am with a group I am like ‘oh I need to keep goin"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_injury_continuum_2023_p0012_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_5bb5d5843449", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0ac2843e667d996b", "text_span_hash": "0ac2843e667d996b", "source_file": "safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "o\n“start reducing [their] mileage because the niggle has hit” (F17), or to complete a more vigorous\nwarm up because they feel “I should warm up properly if I have a niggle” (F17). There was no\neffect to a runner’s daily life, but as a psychological d", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "strategy; “for the stiffness one, the treatment of that one probably would be to go for a run. . . to\nloosen it out” (F3). Some participants however described using self-management strategies\nsuch as stretching; “it will remind me to do the stretching that I know I should be doing anyway”\n(M1). Additionally, some runners may look to external sources such as YouTube for appropri-\nate stretches/exercises; “I find myself YouTubing quite a lot. If I have a pain in my calf, I just do\na five minute exercise that I find on YouTube and it will be gone that day” (M7), or casually chat\nto their running friends about the complaint: “but only by the by, if it came up in conversation I\nmight mention it” (F10).\n‘Niggle’ was a term used by all participants in all focus groups with different\ndescriptions presented. In its mildest form, some runners physically described a ‘niggle’ as\nbeing more “aware” (M13) or “conscious” (F8) of a certain body part, describing it as “not\n[being] the same as the other side [of the body]” (M1), or suggested it is “background noise”\n(F10). Other runners described a ‘niggle’ as something slightly more severe, with phrases such\nas “background pain” (F15), and “a pain that shouldn’t be there” (M4). The majority of partici-\npants described a ‘niggle’ as something “repeated” (F8), “persistent” (F3) and “something that\nlingers” (F11): “discomfort can come and go, whereas a niggle is sort of always there” (F10). In\nre", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "o\n“start reducing [their] mileage because the niggle has hit” (F17), or to complete a more vigorous\nwarm up because they feel “I should warm up properly if I have a niggle” (F17). There was no\neffect to a runner’s daily life, but as a psychological d"}
