und pain” (F15), and “a pain that shouldn’t be there” (M4). The majority of partici-\npants described a ‘niggle’ as something “repeated” (F8), “persistent” (F3) and “something that\nlingers” (F11): “discomfort can come and go, whereas a niggle is sort of always there” (F10). In\nre", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "running” (F6).\nSome runners don’t practice any management strategies because they suggested that ‘nig-\ngles’ can resolve on their own, they “get away with a lot of niggles” (M4) or they can “ignore it”\n(M11). However, some runners use self-management"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_injury_continuum_2023_p0012_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_5bb5d5843449", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1b0d916968a05d30", "text_span_hash": "1b0d916968a05d30", "source_file": "safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "me runners use self-management strategies and alter their training,\ndescribing how “niggles encourage me to stretch” (F15). Some may also “take a day or two off”\n(F15) or reduce their training load, “not run as far” (F2), or “slow down” (F5). Some ma", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "strategy; “for the stiffness one, the treatment of that one probably would be to go for a run. . . to\nloosen it out” (F3). Some participants however described using self-management strategies\nsuch as stretching; “it will remind me to do the stretching that I know I should be doing anyway”\n(M1). Additionally, some runners may look to external sources such as YouTube for appropri-\nate stretches/exercises; “I find myself YouTubing quite a lot. If I have a pain in my calf, I just do\na five minute exercise that I find on YouTube and it will be gone that day” (M7), or casually chat\nto their running friends about the complaint: “but only by the by, if it came up in conversation I\nmight mention it” (F10).\n‘Niggle’ was a term used by all participants in all focus groups with different\ndescriptions presented. In its mildest form, some runners physically described a ‘niggle’ as\nbeing more “aware” (M13) or “conscious” (F8) of a certain body part, describing it as “not\n[being] the same as the other side [of the body]” (M1), or suggested it is “background noise”\n(F10). Other runners described a ‘niggle’ as something slightly more severe, with phrases such\nas “background pain” (F15), and “a pain that shouldn’t be there” (M4). The majority of partici-\npants described a ‘niggle’ as something “repeated” (F8), “persistent” (F3) and “something that\nlingers” (F11): “discomfort can come and go, whereas a niggle is sort of always there” (F10). In\nre", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "me runners use self-management strategies and alter their training,\ndescribing how “niggles encourage me to stretch” (F15). Some may also “take a day or two off”\n(F15) or reduce their training load, “not run as far” (F2), or “slow down” (F5). Some ma"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_injury_continuum_2023_p0012_c0017", "source_registry_id": "src_5bb5d5843449", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e228742e0d981bd1", "text_span_hash": "e228742e0d981bd1", "source_file": "safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 17, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": ". Some par-\nticipants described that they would also turn towards external management strategies, such as\nonline resources or asking their running friends for advice or support; “it might come from an\nexternal source. . . somebody says, ‘maybe you sh", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "strategy; “for the stiffness one, the treatment of that one probably would be to go for a run. . . to\nloosen it out” (F3). Some participants however described using self-management strategies\nsuch as stretching; “it will remind me to do the stretching that I know I should be doing anyway”\n(M1). Additionally, some runners may look to external sources such as YouTube for appropri-\nate stretches/exercises; “I find myself YouTubing quite a lot. If I have a pain in my calf, I just do\na five minute exercise that I find on YouTube and it will be gone that day” (M7), or casually chat\nto their running friends about the complaint: “but only by the by, if it came up in conversation I\nmight mention it” (F10).\n‘Niggle’ was a term used by all participants in all focus groups with different\ndescriptions presented. In its mildest form, some runners physically described a ‘niggle’ as\nbeing more “aware” (M13) or “conscious” (F8) of a certain body part, describing it as “not\n[being] the same as the other side [of the body]” (M1), or suggested it is “background noise”\n(F10). Other runners described a ‘niggle’ as something slightly more severe, with phrases such\nas “background pain” (F15), and “a pain that shouldn’t be there” (M4). The majority of partici-\npants described a ‘niggle’ as something “repeated” (F8), “persistent” (F3) and “something that\nlingers” (F11): “discomfort can come and go, whereas a niggle is sort of always there” (F10). In\nre", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": ". Some par-\nticipants described that they would also turn towards external management strategies, such as\nonline resources or asking their running friends for advice or support; “it might come from an\nexternal source. . . somebody says, ‘maybe you sh"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_injury_continuum_2023_p0013_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_5bb5d5843449", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9cdf807343d317a8", "text_span_hash": "9cdf807343d317a8", "source_file": "safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ompletely; “I might stop if I had a darting pain” (F4). However, a twinge was\ndescribed as something temporary, short-lived, and typically a once-off; “by the time it hap-\npens, it is gone” (F3). Runners described that it would not be felt during the", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "fact of running” (F6), that “runners constantly have niggles” (F16) and they live in “Niggle\nCity” (F17). Many participants did not perceive a ‘niggle’ to be an ‘injury’, but described\nthat it can contribute to the development of an ‘injury’; F16—“it could turn into an injury,\nbut I wouldn’t consider it an injury”. Many participants reported that they perceive ‘niggles’\nto be an “early warning sign” (M11), a “potential injury” (M13), or the “root to an injury”\nA ‘twinge’ was described as a “darting pain” (M7) or an “intense, quick, sharp\npain” (F6), that is intense enough to cause a runner to “stop and walk” (F3) mid-session, or to\nstop their session completely; “I might stop if I had a darting pain” (F4). However, a twinge was\ndescribed as something temporary, short-lived, and typically a once-off; “by the time it hap-\npens, it is gone” (F3). Runners described that it would not be felt during the next session, and\nthey would “forget about it” (M7). There was no description of an effect on a runner’s daily life,\nbut participants associated a ‘twinge’ with “caution” (F7).\nIn relation to management, all runners use some sort of self-management strategy by this\nlevel; “if I had a darting pain I would do something. I wouldn’t just keep on running with that\none” (F4). Some runners described that “stretching would come in here” (M11), they would\n“stop and walk” (F3), or do “slower runs” (F7) at the onset of a ‘twinge’. Some particip", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ompletely; “I might stop if I had a darting pain” (F4). However, a twinge was\ndescribed as something temporary, short-lived, and typically a once-off; “by the time it hap-\npens, it is gone” (F3). Runners described that it would not be felt during the"}
