his\nlevel; “if I had a darting pain I would do something. I wouldn’t just keep on running with that\none” (F4). Some runners described that “stretching would come in here” (M11), they would\n“stop and walk” (F3), or do “slower runs” (F7) at the onset of a ‘twinge’. Some particip", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "t would not be felt during the next session, and\nthey would “forget about it” (M7). There was no description of an effect on a runner’s daily life,\nbut participants associated a ‘twinge’ with “caution” (F7).\nIn relation to management, all runners use"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_injury_continuum_2023_p0013_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_5bb5d5843449", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3f15003a2c0dd8a0", "text_span_hash": "3f15003a2c0dd8a0", "source_file": "safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "to management, all runners use some sort of self-management strategy by this\nlevel; “if I had a darting pain I would do something. I wouldn’t just keep on running with that\none” (F4). Some runners described that “stretching would come in here” (M11),", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "fact of running” (F6), that “runners constantly have niggles” (F16) and they live in “Niggle\nCity” (F17). Many participants did not perceive a ‘niggle’ to be an ‘injury’, but described\nthat it can contribute to the development of an ‘injury’; F16—“it could turn into an injury,\nbut I wouldn’t consider it an injury”. Many participants reported that they perceive ‘niggles’\nto be an “early warning sign” (M11), a “potential injury” (M13), or the “root to an injury”\nA ‘twinge’ was described as a “darting pain” (M7) or an “intense, quick, sharp\npain” (F6), that is intense enough to cause a runner to “stop and walk” (F3) mid-session, or to\nstop their session completely; “I might stop if I had a darting pain” (F4). However, a twinge was\ndescribed as something temporary, short-lived, and typically a once-off; “by the time it hap-\npens, it is gone” (F3). Runners described that it would not be felt during the next session, and\nthey would “forget about it” (M7). There was no description of an effect on a runner’s daily life,\nbut participants associated a ‘twinge’ with “caution” (F7).\nIn relation to management, all runners use some sort of self-management strategy by this\nlevel; “if I had a darting pain I would do something. I wouldn’t just keep on running with that\none” (F4). Some runners described that “stretching would come in here” (M11), they would\n“stop and walk” (F3), or do “slower runs” (F7) at the onset of a ‘twinge’. Some particip", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "to management, all runners use some sort of self-management strategy by this\nlevel; “if I had a darting pain I would do something. I wouldn’t just keep on running with that\none” (F4). Some runners described that “stretching would come in here” (M11),"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_injury_continuum_2023_p0013_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_5bb5d5843449", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "753b0755f72e0702", "text_span_hash": "753b0755f72e0702", "source_file": "safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "it\nhappens again the next week, and the next week, it doesn’t become a niggle anymore, it\nbecomes a problem where you know it is not going to go away”\nIn relation to the outcome, some runners will continue to train fully at this level because “it\nis", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "fact of running” (F6), that “runners constantly have niggles” (F16) and they live in “Niggle\nCity” (F17). Many participants did not perceive a ‘niggle’ to be an ‘injury’, but described\nthat it can contribute to the development of an ‘injury’; F16—“it could turn into an injury,\nbut I wouldn’t consider it an injury”. Many participants reported that they perceive ‘niggles’\nto be an “early warning sign” (M11), a “potential injury” (M13), or the “root to an injury”\nA ‘twinge’ was described as a “darting pain” (M7) or an “intense, quick, sharp\npain” (F6), that is intense enough to cause a runner to “stop and walk” (F3) mid-session, or to\nstop their session completely; “I might stop if I had a darting pain” (F4). However, a twinge was\ndescribed as something temporary, short-lived, and typically a once-off; “by the time it hap-\npens, it is gone” (F3). Runners described that it would not be felt during the next session, and\nthey would “forget about it” (M7). There was no description of an effect on a runner’s daily life,\nbut participants associated a ‘twinge’ with “caution” (F7).\nIn relation to management, all runners use some sort of self-management strategy by this\nlevel; “if I had a darting pain I would do something. I wouldn’t just keep on running with that\none” (F4). Some runners described that “stretching would come in here” (M11), they would\n“stop and walk” (F3), or do “slower runs” (F7) at the onset of a ‘twinge’. Some particip", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "it\nhappens again the next week, and the next week, it doesn’t become a niggle anymore, it\nbecomes a problem where you know it is not going to go away”\nIn relation to the outcome, some runners will continue to train fully at this level because “it\nis"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_injury_continuum_2023_p0013_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_5bb5d5843449", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "77911caf5f13986d", "text_span_hash": "77911caf5f13986d", "source_file": "safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "at this level because “it\nis tolerable to keep running” (F6) and they want to “take a chance”(M7) and hope that it will\nnot progress to a further level. However, the majority of runners will change their training at\nthis level by “decreasing [their]", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "fact of running” (F6), that “runners constantly have niggles” (F16) and they live in “Niggle\nCity” (F17). Many participants did not perceive a ‘niggle’ to be an ‘injury’, but described\nthat it can contribute to the development of an ‘injury’; F16—“it could turn into an injury,\nbut I wouldn’t consider it an injury”. Many participants reported that they perceive ‘niggles’\nto be an “early warning sign” (M11), a “potential injury” (M13), or the “root to an injury”\nA ‘twinge’ was described as a “darting pain” (M7) or an “intense, quick, sharp\npain” (F6), that is intense enough to cause a runner to “stop and walk” (F3) mid-session, or to\nstop their session completely; “I might stop if I had a darting pain” (F4). However, a twinge was\ndescribed as something temporary, short-lived, and typically a once-off; “by the time it hap-\npens, it is gone” (F3). Runners described that it would not be felt during the next session, and\nthey would “forget about it” (M7). There was no description of an effect on a runner’s daily life,\nbut participants associated a ‘twinge’ with “caution” (F7).\nIn relation to management, all runners use some sort of self-management strategy by this\nlevel; “if I had a darting pain I would do something. I wouldn’t just keep on running with that\none” (F4). Some runners described that “stretching would come in here” (M11), they would\n“stop and walk” (F3), or do “slower runs” (F7) at the onset of a ‘twinge’. Some particip", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "at this level because “it\nis tolerable to keep running” (F6) and they want to “take a chance”(M7) and hope that it will\nnot progress to a further level. However, the majority of runners will change their training at\nthis level by “decreasing [their]"}
