jury development, and ensure\npatients understand that it is normal to experience these responses (i.e. to experience anxiety\nor concern, especially with a lower level injury) [55]. Similarly, in helping athletes manage inju-\nries, coaches need to be aware of the importance of n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "emographics. Furthermore, as we only recruited recreational runners, findings\nmay differ with elite or novice runners. As the aim of this study was to explore the lived experi-\nence of runners, it was necessary for participants to have previous exper"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_injury_continuum_2023_p0021_c0018", "source_registry_id": "src_5bb5d5843449", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8d66dcad96257d48", "text_span_hash": "8d66dcad96257d48", "source_file": "safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "page": 21, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 18, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "cipants to have previous experience with lower level\ninjuries; however, it is possible that different findings would be reported from runners with\nless/no experience of lower level injuries. Given the adopted terminology of ‘niggle’, ‘persisting\nnigg", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Appropriate monitoring of RRIs.\nOur findings highlight the importance of a wider\nscope of monitoring RRIs, not just across all levels of injury, but across all categories of\ndescriptors. Firstly, HCPs should consider the potential importance of lower level injuries act-\ning as risk factors for higher level injuries (as discussed above), and appreciate that a runner\npresenting with a higher level injury may have had a preceding lower level injury, in order to\nbetter manage the whole continuum of injury. Secondly, when designing injury management\nstrategies, HCPs should understand that runners will likely have made attempts to self-manage\ntheir injury prior to presenting to them, and these attempts should be taken into consideration.\nFinally, with the emphasis placed by participants in the current study on the psychological\ndescription of each level of injury, it is crucial that HCP’s include biopsychosocial assessments\nwhen dealing with recreational runners, ensuring this psychological response is captured,\nmonitored and incorporated into management strategies. HCPs should also educate their\npatients regarding the psychological aspect of the process of injury development, and ensure\npatients understand that it is normal to experience these responses (i.e. to experience anxiety\nor concern, especially with a lower level injury) [55]. Similarly, in helping athletes manage inju-\nries, coaches need to be aware of the importance of n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "cipants to have previous experience with lower level\ninjuries; however, it is possible that different findings would be reported from runners with\nless/no experience of lower level injuries. Given the adopted terminology of ‘niggle’, ‘persisting\nnigg"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_injury_continuum_2023_p0021_c0019", "source_registry_id": "src_5bb5d5843449", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4c19c74c7f56ceb6", "text_span_hash": "4c19c74c7f56ceb6", "source_file": "safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "page": 21, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 19, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "of ‘niggle’, ‘persisting\nniggle’ and ‘non-responding niggle’, a ‘twinge’ can be viewed as a distinct level from these\nbecause of its associated sharp pain, its immediate effect on running, and its presence for only\none session. The location of ‘twin", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Appropriate monitoring of RRIs.\nOur findings highlight the importance of a wider\nscope of monitoring RRIs, not just across all levels of injury, but across all categories of\ndescriptors. Firstly, HCPs should consider the potential importance of lower level injuries act-\ning as risk factors for higher level injuries (as discussed above), and appreciate that a runner\npresenting with a higher level injury may have had a preceding lower level injury, in order to\nbetter manage the whole continuum of injury. Secondly, when designing injury management\nstrategies, HCPs should understand that runners will likely have made attempts to self-manage\ntheir injury prior to presenting to them, and these attempts should be taken into consideration.\nFinally, with the emphasis placed by participants in the current study on the psychological\ndescription of each level of injury, it is crucial that HCP’s include biopsychosocial assessments\nwhen dealing with recreational runners, ensuring this psychological response is captured,\nmonitored and incorporated into management strategies. HCPs should also educate their\npatients regarding the psychological aspect of the process of injury development, and ensure\npatients understand that it is normal to experience these responses (i.e. to experience anxiety\nor concern, especially with a lower level injury) [55]. Similarly, in helping athletes manage inju-\nries, coaches need to be aware of the importance of n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "of ‘niggle’, ‘persisting\nniggle’ and ‘non-responding niggle’, a ‘twinge’ can be viewed as a distinct level from these\nbecause of its associated sharp pain, its immediate effect on running, and its presence for only\none session. The location of ‘twin"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_injury_continuum_2023_p0022_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_5bb5d5843449", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "03120e945d7f6d38", "text_span_hash": "03120e945d7f6d38", "source_file": "safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "page": 22, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "rchers should ensure recreational runners have access to evidence-\nbased information, and can utilize this information in their running practices. HCPs, coaches\nand researchers should broaden their scope of monitoring RRIs to ensure that all levels o", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "niggle’ and ‘non-responding niggle’ was not consistent across all focus groups; however, its\nplacement on the Running Injury Continuum was arrived at because all participants described\nit as something more severe than a ‘niggle’, while the majority described it as less severe than a\n‘persisting niggle’.\nThrough capturing the lived experiences of recreational runners, we present the Running\nInjury Continuum as a representation of the development process of RRIs. Expanding on pre-\nvious research [19–23], nine distinct levels of injury were identified in the current study, with\neach level being described across four categories of descriptors: physical description, outcome,\npsychological description, and management. For research purposes, the Running Injury Con-\ntinuum is a tool that can be used in both injury surveillance research and research investigating\nrisk factors for RRIs.\nOur findings clearly highlight the importance of education and accessibility of evidence-\nbased information. HCPs need to educate their patients on appropriate self-management strat-\negies for RRIs, while researchers should ensure recreational runners have access to evidence-\nbased information, and can utilize this information in their running practices. HCPs, coaches\nand researchers should broaden their scope of monitoring RRIs to ensure that all levels of\ninjury and categories of descriptors are captured, in order to better understand the wider\nimpact ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "rchers should ensure recreational runners have access to evidence-\nbased information, and can utilize this information in their running practices. HCPs, coaches\nand researchers should broaden their scope of monitoring RRIs to ensure that all levels o"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_injury_continuum_2023_p0023_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_5bb5d5843449", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e994cbc6c6dfe993", "text_span_hash": "e994cbc6c6dfe993", "source_file": "safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_injury_continuum_2023.pdf", "page": 23, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "arnett LM, Hallal PC, Colyvas K, et al. Prev Med. 2017; 95:14–25.\nHollander K, Rahlf AL, Wilke J, Edler C, Steib S, Junge A, et al. Sex-Specific Differences in Running\nInjuries: A Systematic Review with Meta-Analysis and Meta-Regression. Sports Med.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Writing – original draft: Aisling Lacey, Enda Whyte, Kieran Moran.\nWriting – review & editing: Aisling Lacey, Enda Whyte, Sine´ad O’Keeffe, Siobha´n O’Connor,\nAoife Burke, Kieran Moran.\nHulteen RM, Smith JJ, Morgan PJ, Barnett LM, Hallal PC, Colyvas K, et al. Prev Med. 2017; 95:14–25.\nHollander K, Rahlf AL, Wilke J, Edler C, Steib S, Junge A, et al. Sex-Specific Differences in Running\nInjuries: A Systematic Review with Meta-Analysis and Meta-Regression. Sports Med. 2021; 51:1011–\nKakouris N, Yener N, Fong DTP. A systematic review of running-related musculoskeletal injuries in run-\nVidebæk S, Bueno AM, Nielsen RO, Rasmussen S. Incidence of Running-Related Injuries Per 1000 h\nof running in Different Types of Runners: A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis. Sports Med Auckl\nBertelsen ML, Hulme A, Petersen J, Brund RK, Sørensen H, Finch CF, et al. A framework for the etiol-\nogy of running-related injuries. Scandanavian J Med Sci Sport. 2017; 27:1170–80. https://doi.org/10.\nEdwards WB. Modelling overuse injuries in sport as a mechanical fatigue phenomenon. Exerc Sport Sci\nKalkhoven JT, Watsford ML, Impellizzeri FM. A conceptual model and detailed framework for stress-\nrelated, strain-related, and overuse athletic injury. J Sci Med Sport. 2020; 23(8):726–34. https://doi.org/\nMeeuwisse WH, Tyreman H, Hagel B, Emery C. A dynamic model of etiology in sport injury: the recur-\nsive nature of risk and causation. Clin J Sport Med Off J Can ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "arnett LM, Hallal PC, Colyvas K, et al. Prev Med. 2017; 95:14–25.\nHollander K, Rahlf AL, Wilke J, Edler C, Steib S, Junge A, et al. Sex-Specific Differences in Running\nInjuries: A Systematic Review with Meta-Analysis and Meta-Regression. Sports Med."}
