{"chunk_id": "safety_running_injuries_state_art_2021_p0001_c0017", "source_registry_id": "src_985dc898a0b7", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b02f093613fca10e", "text_span_hash": "b02f093613fca10e", "source_file": "safety_running_injuries_state_art_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_running_injuries_state_art_2021.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 17, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "often view added training load as a linear issue,\nfocusing more on training volume than loading magnitude.\nSince tissue is not an inert structure, consideration of the\nability of the athlete’s physiology to support tissue adapta­\ntion in response to", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "IFSPT International Perspective\nThe Prevention and Treatment of Running Injuries: A State of the\nChristopher Napier, PT, PhD\n1 a , Richard W Willy, PT, PhD\n1 Department of Physical Therapy, University of British Columbia; Schools of Mechatronic Systems Engineering and Engineering Science, Simon Fraser\nUniversity, 2 School of Physical Therapy and Rehabilitation Science, University of Montana\nKeywords: footwear, training load, treatment, injury prevention, running\nInternational Journal of Sports Physical Therapy\nDespite decades of efforts, running injury prevention\nprograms continue to fall short of achieving a reduction in\nrunning injury rates1 and most running injuries are notori­\nous for their high recurrence.2 Prevention and treatment ef­\nforts often focus on a sole factor, such as muscle strength­\nening or biomechanics, despite the multifactorial nature\nof running injuries. Additional emphasis on low-value in­\nterventions such as footwear to prevent injury often de­\ntracts from more effective prevention strategies.3 Not sur­\nprisingly, most running injury prevention programs fail to\nreduce running injury rates and, in the case of advising\non running biomechanics, may actually increase the risk of\nrunning injury.1 The purpose of this international perspec­\ntive is to describe how a causal framework approach can\nhelp to prevent and treat running injuries.\nCausal frameworks provide an important path forward\nfor running injury ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "often view added training load as a linear issue,\nfocusing more on training volume than loading magnitude.\nSince tissue is not an inert structure, consideration of the\nability of the athlete’s physiology to support tissue adapta­\ntion in response to"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_running_injuries_state_art_2021_p0001_c0022", "source_registry_id": "src_985dc898a0b7", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e1932a011b0c6cf8", "text_span_hash": "e1932a011b0c6cf8", "source_file": "safety_running_injuries_state_art_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_running_injuries_state_art_2021.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 22, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "tent,\nprogressive loading (progressive strengthening, minimizing\ntraining spikes), addressing psychological stressors, and op­\ntimizing a runner’s physiology is the first step. Second, con­\nsidering the attributes of the individual runner (i.e., mas­", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "IFSPT International Perspective\nThe Prevention and Treatment of Running Injuries: A State of the\nChristopher Napier, PT, PhD\n1 a , Richard W Willy, PT, PhD\n1 Department of Physical Therapy, University of British Columbia; Schools of Mechatronic Systems Engineering and Engineering Science, Simon Fraser\nUniversity, 2 School of Physical Therapy and Rehabilitation Science, University of Montana\nKeywords: footwear, training load, treatment, injury prevention, running\nInternational Journal of Sports Physical Therapy\nDespite decades of efforts, running injury prevention\nprograms continue to fall short of achieving a reduction in\nrunning injury rates1 and most running injuries are notori­\nous for their high recurrence.2 Prevention and treatment ef­\nforts often focus on a sole factor, such as muscle strength­\nening or biomechanics, despite the multifactorial nature\nof running injuries. Additional emphasis on low-value in­\nterventions such as footwear to prevent injury often de­\ntracts from more effective prevention strategies.3 Not sur­\nprisingly, most running injury prevention programs fail to\nreduce running injury rates and, in the case of advising\non running biomechanics, may actually increase the risk of\nrunning injury.1 The purpose of this international perspec­\ntive is to describe how a causal framework approach can\nhelp to prevent and treat running injuries.\nCausal frameworks provide an important path forward\nfor running injury ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "tent,\nprogressive loading (progressive strengthening, minimizing\ntraining spikes), addressing psychological stressors, and op­\ntimizing a runner’s physiology is the first step. Second, con­\nsidering the attributes of the individual runner (i.e., mas­"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_running_injuries_state_art_2021_p0001_c0023", "source_registry_id": "src_985dc898a0b7", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "42ff6c1a8a07fdc1", "text_span_hash": "42ff6c1a8a07fdc1", "source_file": "safety_running_injuries_state_art_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_running_injuries_state_art_2021.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 23, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "individual runner (i.e., mas­\nters male vs adolescent female) and the ability of the\nathlete’s physiology to support tissue remodeling will help\ninform physiological interventions and training load pre­\nCorresponding author:\nDr. Christopher Napier\nN", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "IFSPT International Perspective\nThe Prevention and Treatment of Running Injuries: A State of the\nChristopher Napier, PT, PhD\n1 a , Richard W Willy, PT, PhD\n1 Department of Physical Therapy, University of British Columbia; Schools of Mechatronic Systems Engineering and Engineering Science, Simon Fraser\nUniversity, 2 School of Physical Therapy and Rehabilitation Science, University of Montana\nKeywords: footwear, training load, treatment, injury prevention, running\nInternational Journal of Sports Physical Therapy\nDespite decades of efforts, running injury prevention\nprograms continue to fall short of achieving a reduction in\nrunning injury rates1 and most running injuries are notori­\nous for their high recurrence.2 Prevention and treatment ef­\nforts often focus on a sole factor, such as muscle strength­\nening or biomechanics, despite the multifactorial nature\nof running injuries. Additional emphasis on low-value in­\nterventions such as footwear to prevent injury often de­\ntracts from more effective prevention strategies.3 Not sur­\nprisingly, most running injury prevention programs fail to\nreduce running injury rates and, in the case of advising\non running biomechanics, may actually increase the risk of\nrunning injury.1 The purpose of this international perspec­\ntive is to describe how a causal framework approach can\nhelp to prevent and treat running injuries.\nCausal frameworks provide an important path forward\nfor running injury ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "individual runner (i.e., mas­\nters male vs adolescent female) and the ability of the\nathlete’s physiology to support tissue remodeling will help\ninform physiological interventions and training load pre­\nCorresponding author:\nDr. Christopher Napier\nN"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_running_injuries_state_art_2021_p0001_c0024", "source_registry_id": "src_985dc898a0b7", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1fc0c7559d9bdaed", "text_span_hash": "1fc0c7559d9bdaed", "source_file": "safety_running_injuries_state_art_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_running_injuries_state_art_2021.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 24, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "thor:\nDr. Christopher Napier\nNapier C, Willy RW. The Prevention and Treatment of Running Injuries: A State of the", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "IFSPT International Perspective\nThe Prevention and Treatment of Running Injuries: A State of the\nChristopher Napier, PT, PhD\n1 a , Richard W Willy, PT, PhD\n1 Department of Physical Therapy, University of British Columbia; Schools of Mechatronic Systems Engineering and Engineering Science, Simon Fraser\nUniversity, 2 School of Physical Therapy and Rehabilitation Science, University of Montana\nKeywords: footwear, training load, treatment, injury prevention, running\nInternational Journal of Sports Physical Therapy\nDespite decades of efforts, running injury prevention\nprograms continue to fall short of achieving a reduction in\nrunning injury rates1 and most running injuries are notori­\nous for their high recurrence.2 Prevention and treatment ef­\nforts often focus on a sole factor, such as muscle strength­\nening or biomechanics, despite the multifactorial nature\nof running injuries. Additional emphasis on low-value in­\nterventions such as footwear to prevent injury often de­\ntracts from more effective prevention strategies.3 Not sur­\nprisingly, most running injury prevention programs fail to\nreduce running injury rates and, in the case of advising\non running biomechanics, may actually increase the risk of\nrunning injury.1 The purpose of this international perspec­\ntive is to describe how a causal framework approach can\nhelp to prevent and treat running injuries.\nCausal frameworks provide an important path forward\nfor running injury ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "thor:\nDr. Christopher Napier\nNapier C, Willy RW. The Prevention and Treatment of Running Injuries: A State of the"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_running_injuries_state_art_2021_p0002_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_985dc898a0b7", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "308444d78f1554f2", "text_span_hash": "308444d78f1554f2", "source_file": "safety_running_injuries_state_art_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_running_injuries_state_art_2021.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "sub-populations.\nFor example, those recovering from, or at-risk for, Achilles\ntendinopathy should add speedwork into a training pro­\ngram judiciously, whereas downhill running should be\nadded in slowly if recovering from, or at-risk for,\npatellofemo", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "scription. Tailoring training load prescription to address\nstructure-specific cumulative microdamage should also be\nspecific to past injuries or anatomical structures that are\nmore likely to experience injury in specific sub-populations.\nFor example, those recovering from, or at-risk for, Achilles\ntendinopathy should add speedwork into a training pro­\ngram judiciously, whereas downhill running should be\nadded in slowly if recovering from, or at-risk for,\npatellofemoral pain. Wearable technologies can monitor in­\njury-specific training loads (i.e., number of steps) while per­\nforming activities known to increase loading on injury-sus­\nceptible tissues, helping inform the need for recovery days\nto restore pre-run load capacity and support tissue adap­\ntation. Lastly, clinicians should adopt routine screening for\nRED-S and other physiological conditions known to reduce\ntissue adaptability and refer out for specialized care when\nWe believe that by employing a causal framework of run­\nning injury aetiology that considers current theory in tissue\nmechanics and physiology, and by following general princi­\nples of injury risk management, the puzzle of running in­\njury prevention and treatment has potential to be solved.\nThis is an open-access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License\n(CCBY-NC-SA-4.0). View this license’s legal deed at https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "sub-populations.\nFor example, those recovering from, or at-risk for, Achilles\ntendinopathy should add speedwork into a training pro­\ngram judiciously, whereas downhill running should be\nadded in slowly if recovering from, or at-risk for,\npatellofemo"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_running_injuries_state_art_2021_p0002_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_985dc898a0b7", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "089fb65a1a092fcf", "text_span_hash": "089fb65a1a092fcf", "source_file": "safety_running_injuries_state_art_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_running_injuries_state_art_2021.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "e information.\nThe Prevention and Treatment of Running Injuries: A State of the Art\nInternational Journal of Sports Physical Therapy", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "scription. Tailoring training load prescription to address\nstructure-specific cumulative microdamage should also be\nspecific to past injuries or anatomical structures that are\nmore likely to experience injury in specific sub-populations.\nFor example, those recovering from, or at-risk for, Achilles\ntendinopathy should add speedwork into a training pro­\ngram judiciously, whereas downhill running should be\nadded in slowly if recovering from, or at-risk for,\npatellofemoral pain. Wearable technologies can monitor in­\njury-specific training loads (i.e., number of steps) while per­\nforming activities known to increase loading on injury-sus­\nceptible tissues, helping inform the need for recovery days\nto restore pre-run load capacity and support tissue adap­\ntation. Lastly, clinicians should adopt routine screening for\nRED-S and other physiological conditions known to reduce\ntissue adaptability and refer out for specialized care when\nWe believe that by employing a causal framework of run­\nning injury aetiology that considers current theory in tissue\nmechanics and physiology, and by following general princi­\nples of injury risk management, the puzzle of running in­\njury prevention and treatment has potential to be solved.\nThis is an open-access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License\n(CCBY-NC-SA-4.0). View this license’s legal deed at https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "e information.\nThe Prevention and Treatment of Running Injuries: A State of the Art\nInternational Journal of Sports Physical Therapy"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_running_injuries_state_art_2021_p0003_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_985dc898a0b7", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ae8b6cd15265b6f5", "text_span_hash": "ae8b6cd15265b6f5", "source_file": "safety_running_injuries_state_art_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_running_injuries_state_art_2021.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "1. Fokkema T, de Vos R-J, van Ochten JM, et al. Online\nmultifactorial prevention programme has no effect on\nthe number of running-related injuries: a randomised\ncontrolled trial. Br J Sports Med. 2019;53(23):1479.\n2. Blønd L, Hansen L. Patellofemoral", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "1. Fokkema T, de Vos R-J, van Ochten JM, et al. Online\nmultifactorial prevention programme has no effect on\nthe number of running-related injuries: a randomised\ncontrolled trial. Br J Sports Med. 2019;53(23):1479.\n2. Blønd L, Hansen L. Patellofemoral pain syndrome\nin athletes: A 5.7-year retrospective follow-up study\n3. Napier C, Willy RW. Logical fallacies in the running\nshoe debate: let the evidence guide prescription. Br J\n4. Bertelsen ML, Hulme A, Petersen J, et al. A\nframework for the etiology of running-related\ninjuries. Scand J Med Sci Sport.\n5. Willy RW, Paquette MR. The Physiology and\nBiomechanics of the Master Runner. Sports Med\n6. Kalkhoven JT, Watsford ML, Impellizzeri FM. A\nconceptual model and detailed framework for stress-\nrelated, strain-related, and overuse athletic injury. J\n7. Edwards WB. Modeling Overuse Injuries in Sport as\na Mechanical Fatigue Phenomenon. Exerc Sport Sci\n8. Paquette MR, Napier C, Willy RW, Stellingwerff T.\nMoving Beyond Weekly ‘Distance’ – Optimizing\nTraining Quantification in Running. J Orthop Sports\n9. Papageorgiou M, Dolan E, Elliott-Sale KJ, Sale C.\nReduced energy availability: implications for bone\nhealth in physically active populations. Eur J Nutr.\nThe Prevention and Treatment of Running Injuries: A State of the Art\nInternational Journal of Sports Physical Therapy", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "1. Fokkema T, de Vos R-J, van Ochten JM, et al. Online\nmultifactorial prevention programme has no effect on\nthe number of running-related injuries: a randomised\ncontrolled trial. Br J Sports Med. 2019;53(23):1479.\n2. Blønd L, Hansen L. Patellofemoral"}
