 bone\nhealth in physically active populations. Eur J Nutr.\nThe Prevention and Treatment of Running Injuries: A State of the Art\nInternational Journal of Sports Physical Therapy", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Sale C.\nReduced energy availability: implications for bone\nhealth in physically active populations. Eur J Nutr.\nThe Prevention and Treatment of Running Injuries: A State of the Art\nInternational Journal of Sports Physical Therapy"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012_p0001_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_f2895dc37432", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e0c3be8e4a1176b9", "text_span_hash": "e0c3be8e4a1176b9", "source_file": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "The George Institute for Global Health, Sydney, NSW, Australia\nBackground: Musculoskeletal injuries occur frequently in runners and despite\nmany studies about running injuries conducted over the past decades it is not\nclear in the literature what are", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "What are the Main Running-Related\nMusculoskeletal Injuries?\nAlexandre Dias Lopes,1,2 Luiz Carlos Hespanhol Junior,1,2 Simon S. Yeung3 and\nLeonardo Oliveira Pena Costa1,2,4\nSa˜o Paulo Running Injury Group (SPRunIG), Sa˜o Paulo, Brazil\nMasters in Physical Therapy, Universidade Cidade de Sa˜o Paulo-UNICID, Sa˜o Paulo, Brazil\nDepartment of Rehabilitation Sciences, Hong Kong Polytechnic University, Hong Kong, China\nMusculoskeletal Division, The George Institute for Global Health, Sydney, NSW, Australia\nBackground: Musculoskeletal injuries occur frequently in runners and despite\nmany studies about running injuries conducted over the past decades it is not\nclear in the literature what are the main running-related musculoskeletal in-\nObjective: The aim of this study is to systematically review studies on the\nincidence and prevalence of the main specific RRMIs.\nMethods: An electronic database search was conducted using EMBASE\n(1947 to October 2011), MEDLINE (1966 to October 2011), SPORTDiscus\u0003\n(1975 to October 2011), the Latin American and Caribbean Center on Health\nSciences Information (LILACS) [1982 to October 2011] and the Scientific\nElectronic Library Online (SciELO) [1998 to October 2011] with no limits of\ndate or language of publication. Articles that described the incidence or\nprevalence rates of RRMIs were considered eligible. Studies that reported\nonly the type of injury, anatomical region or incomplete data that precluded\nint", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "The George Institute for Global Health, Sydney, NSW, Australia\nBackground: Musculoskeletal injuries occur frequently in runners and despite\nmany studies about running injuries conducted over the past decades it is not\nclear in the literature what are"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012_p0001_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_f2895dc37432", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "eb312d82523ee99b", "text_span_hash": "eb312d82523ee99b", "source_file": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ear in the literature what are the main running-related musculoskeletal in-\nObjective: The aim of this study is to systematically review studies on the\nincidence and prevalence of the main specific RRMIs.\nMethods: An electronic database search was co", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "What are the Main Running-Related\nMusculoskeletal Injuries?\nAlexandre Dias Lopes,1,2 Luiz Carlos Hespanhol Junior,1,2 Simon S. Yeung3 and\nLeonardo Oliveira Pena Costa1,2,4\nSa˜o Paulo Running Injury Group (SPRunIG), Sa˜o Paulo, Brazil\nMasters in Physical Therapy, Universidade Cidade de Sa˜o Paulo-UNICID, Sa˜o Paulo, Brazil\nDepartment of Rehabilitation Sciences, Hong Kong Polytechnic University, Hong Kong, China\nMusculoskeletal Division, The George Institute for Global Health, Sydney, NSW, Australia\nBackground: Musculoskeletal injuries occur frequently in runners and despite\nmany studies about running injuries conducted over the past decades it is not\nclear in the literature what are the main running-related musculoskeletal in-\nObjective: The aim of this study is to systematically review studies on the\nincidence and prevalence of the main specific RRMIs.\nMethods: An electronic database search was conducted using EMBASE\n(1947 to October 2011), MEDLINE (1966 to October 2011), SPORTDiscus\u0003\n(1975 to October 2011), the Latin American and Caribbean Center on Health\nSciences Information (LILACS) [1982 to October 2011] and the Scientific\nElectronic Library Online (SciELO) [1998 to October 2011] with no limits of\ndate or language of publication. Articles that described the incidence or\nprevalence rates of RRMIs were considered eligible. Studies that reported\nonly the type of injury, anatomical region or incomplete data that precluded\nint", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ear in the literature what are the main running-related musculoskeletal in-\nObjective: The aim of this study is to systematically review studies on the\nincidence and prevalence of the main specific RRMIs.\nMethods: An electronic database search was co"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012_p0002_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_f2895dc37432", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "22ee10d13ee6b90c", "text_span_hash": "22ee10d13ee6b90c", "source_file": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "main ultra-marathon RRMIs were Achilles tendinopathy (prevalence rang-\ning from 2.0% to 18.5%) and patellofemoral syndrome (prevalence ranging\nConclusion: This systematic review provides evidence that medial tibia stress\nsyndrome, Achilles tendinopat", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "main ultra-marathon RRMIs were Achilles tendinopathy (prevalence rang-\ning from 2.0% to 18.5%) and patellofemoral syndrome (prevalence ranging\nConclusion: This systematic review provides evidence that medial tibia stress\nsyndrome, Achilles tendinopathy and plantar fasciitis were the main general\nRRMIs, while Achilles tendinopathy and patellofemoral syndrome were the\nmost common RRMIs for runners who participated in ultra-marathon races.\nRunning is one of the most popular physical\nactivities enjoyed by people around the world[1] and\nthe number of runners has grown substantially\nover the past decades. People seeking a healthier\nlifestyle through weight control and improved\nexercise capacity frequently choose running, as\nthis has been considered to be of low cost and can\nbe easily implemented.[2] More importantly,\nrunning has many beneficial effects including a\nreduction of risk factors for cardiovascular dis-\nease.[3] Despite these health benefits, running in-\njuries are common, with incidence rates ranging\nper 1000 hours of exposure to running.[7-12] This\nlarge variation in incidence rates of running in-\njury may be explained by the difference in the sub-\njects’ characteristics, as well as the definitions of\nrunning-related musculoskeletal injuries (RRMIs),\nwhich differ between studies.[5]\nStudies have been conducted to identify what are\nthe most common injuries among runners.[2,6,8,12-19]\nHowever, because of a large heterogene", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "main ultra-marathon RRMIs were Achilles tendinopathy (prevalence rang-\ning from 2.0% to 18.5%) and patellofemoral syndrome (prevalence ranging\nConclusion: This systematic review provides evidence that medial tibia stress\nsyndrome, Achilles tendinopat"}
