ence or\nadaptation is a protective factor for the onset of\ntendinopathy but from the results of the ultra-\nmarathon races, the acute onset of the tendino-\npathy is very likely to appear when the runners\nare subjected to consecutive running from 5 to\nPlantar fasciitis is considered by healthcare\nprof", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "and runners normally prefer forefoot running\nfor shorter races as this will decrease the landing\ntime and increase the running velocity. None-\ntheless, high quality, prospective cohort studies\nare needed to confirm these findings.[39]\nThe patellar t"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012_p0008_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_f2895dc37432", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9760cf458c63aa87", "text_span_hash": "9760cf458c63aa87", "source_file": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "patellar tendinopathy was frequently re-\nported in RRMIs among amateur runners who\naimed to run between 20 and 50km per week.[23,26]\nHowever, it is interesting to note that patellar\ntendinopathy is not common among marathon\nrunners.[24] It is not cle", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "physiological tolerance leading it to degenera-\ntion.[41] This excessive loading generated in the\ngastrocnemius and soleus muscles during running\nmay predispose to the development of Achilles\ntendinopathy in runners.[42] A retrospective study\nshowed that running on sand surfaces and run-\nning races from 1500 m to 5 km increased the risk\nof Achilles tendinopathy.[14] It might be because\nrunning on sand surface demands excessive push\noff, and runners normally prefer forefoot running\nfor shorter races as this will decrease the landing\ntime and increase the running velocity. None-\ntheless, high quality, prospective cohort studies\nare needed to confirm these findings.[39]\nThe patellar tendon is exposed to high and re-\npetitive eccentric loads of the quadriceps femoris\nmuscle during running, which may explain the\nhigh injury rate of patellar tendons in runners.[43]\nIndeed, patellar tendinopathy was frequently re-\nported in RRMIs among amateur runners who\naimed to run between 20 and 50km per week.[23,26]\nHowever, it is interesting to note that patellar\ntendinopathy is not common among marathon\nrunners.[24] It is not clear if running experience or\nadaptation is a protective factor for the onset of\ntendinopathy but from the results of the ultra-\nmarathon races, the acute onset of the tendino-\npathy is very likely to appear when the runners\nare subjected to consecutive running from 5 to\nPlantar fasciitis is considered by healthcare\nprof", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "patellar tendinopathy was frequently re-\nported in RRMIs among amateur runners who\naimed to run between 20 and 50km per week.[23,26]\nHowever, it is interesting to note that patellar\ntendinopathy is not common among marathon\nrunners.[24] It is not cle"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012_p0008_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_f2895dc37432", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "29b7daa4e1a2ca2c", "text_span_hash": "29b7daa4e1a2ca2c", "source_file": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "hon\nrunners.[24] It is not clear if running experience or\nadaptation is a protective factor for the onset of\ntendinopathy but from the results of the ultra-\nmarathon races, the acute onset of the tendino-\npathy is very likely to appear when the runne", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "physiological tolerance leading it to degenera-\ntion.[41] This excessive loading generated in the\ngastrocnemius and soleus muscles during running\nmay predispose to the development of Achilles\ntendinopathy in runners.[42] A retrospective study\nshowed that running on sand surfaces and run-\nning races from 1500 m to 5 km increased the risk\nof Achilles tendinopathy.[14] It might be because\nrunning on sand surface demands excessive push\noff, and runners normally prefer forefoot running\nfor shorter races as this will decrease the landing\ntime and increase the running velocity. None-\ntheless, high quality, prospective cohort studies\nare needed to confirm these findings.[39]\nThe patellar tendon is exposed to high and re-\npetitive eccentric loads of the quadriceps femoris\nmuscle during running, which may explain the\nhigh injury rate of patellar tendons in runners.[43]\nIndeed, patellar tendinopathy was frequently re-\nported in RRMIs among amateur runners who\naimed to run between 20 and 50km per week.[23,26]\nHowever, it is interesting to note that patellar\ntendinopathy is not common among marathon\nrunners.[24] It is not clear if running experience or\nadaptation is a protective factor for the onset of\ntendinopathy but from the results of the ultra-\nmarathon races, the acute onset of the tendino-\npathy is very likely to appear when the runners\nare subjected to consecutive running from 5 to\nPlantar fasciitis is considered by healthcare\nprof", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "hon\nrunners.[24] It is not clear if running experience or\nadaptation is a protective factor for the onset of\ntendinopathy but from the results of the ultra-\nmarathon races, the acute onset of the tendino-\npathy is very likely to appear when the runne"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012_p0008_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_f2895dc37432", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9f7b990aa0fc9085", "text_span_hash": "9f7b990aa0fc9085", "source_file": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "running-related musculoskeletal injuries.\nAdis ª 2012 Lopes et al., publisher and licensee Springer International Publishing AG.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "physiological tolerance leading it to degenera-\ntion.[41] This excessive loading generated in the\ngastrocnemius and soleus muscles during running\nmay predispose to the development of Achilles\ntendinopathy in runners.[42] A retrospective study\nshowed that running on sand surfaces and run-\nning races from 1500 m to 5 km increased the risk\nof Achilles tendinopathy.[14] It might be because\nrunning on sand surface demands excessive push\noff, and runners normally prefer forefoot running\nfor shorter races as this will decrease the landing\ntime and increase the running velocity. None-\ntheless, high quality, prospective cohort studies\nare needed to confirm these findings.[39]\nThe patellar tendon is exposed to high and re-\npetitive eccentric loads of the quadriceps femoris\nmuscle during running, which may explain the\nhigh injury rate of patellar tendons in runners.[43]\nIndeed, patellar tendinopathy was frequently re-\nported in RRMIs among amateur runners who\naimed to run between 20 and 50km per week.[23,26]\nHowever, it is interesting to note that patellar\ntendinopathy is not common among marathon\nrunners.[24] It is not clear if running experience or\nadaptation is a protective factor for the onset of\ntendinopathy but from the results of the ultra-\nmarathon races, the acute onset of the tendino-\npathy is very likely to appear when the runners\nare subjected to consecutive running from 5 to\nPlantar fasciitis is considered by healthcare\nprof", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "running-related musculoskeletal injuries.\nAdis ª 2012 Lopes et al., publisher and licensee Springer International Publishing AG."}
{"chunk_id": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012_p0009_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_f2895dc37432", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "93a050cada1aa3c7", "text_span_hash": "93a050cada1aa3c7", "source_file": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "; RRMIs = running-related musculoskeletal injuries.\nWhat are the Main Running-Related Injuries?\nAdis ª 2012 Lopes et al., publisher and licensee Springer International Publishing AG.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Table IV. Prevalence rates of running-related musculoskeletal injuries during ultra-marathon races\nMusculoskeletal injury\nInjuries data collection\nScheer and Murray,[29]\nrunners of a 5-day race\nInjuries were reported and\nevaluated by the medical team\nof an ultra-marathon race with\n3 general practitioners and\nPatellofemoral pain syndrome\nAnkle inversion injury\nTrochanteric bursitis\nAchilles tendinopathy\nQuadriceps muscle pain\nTibialis anterior muscle pain\nrunners of a 5- to\nInjuries were reported by the\nrunners during the race and\nevaluated by a physician\nRetropatellar pain syndrome\nAnterior compartment\nExtensor digitorum tendinitis\nMedial tibial stress syndrome\nAnterior compartment pain\nIliotibial band syndrome\nQuadriceps strain/tear\nNon-specific knee pain\nGrater trochanteric bursitis\nExtensor hallucis longus\nrunners of a 6-day race\nInjuries sufficiently\nsevere to impair their\nRecords were kept of the injuries\nsustained and of the\nconsultations with the doctor and\nFoot dorsiflexors tendinitis\nMedial tibial stress syndrome\nPatellofemoral syndrome\nGastrocnemius strain\nNR = not reported; RRMIs = running-related musculoskeletal injuries.\nWhat are the Main Running-Related Injuries?\nAdis ª 2012 Lopes et al., publisher and licensee Springer International Publishing AG.", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "; RRMIs = running-related musculoskeletal injuries.\nWhat are the Main Running-Related Injuries?\nAdis ª 2012 Lopes et al., publisher and licensee Springer International Publishing AG."}
{"chunk_id": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012_p0010_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_f2895dc37432", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "68ec03da0808f41f", "text_span_hash": "68ec03da0808f41f", "source_file": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "er, this conclusion\nwas classified as limited evidence in a previously\npublished systematic review aimed to identify\ndeterminants of lower extremity running-related\nStudies have reported that patellofemoral syn-\ndrome is the most common RRMI among ru", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "the heel strike phase of running, the heel is the\nfirst contact point that has to absorb the impact\nof up to three times the total body weight.[48] The\nability to absorb and transmit this impact de-\npends on the resilience of the plantar fascia, the\nplantar fat pad and the intrinsic muscles of the\nfoot. With ageing or prolonged repetitive over-\nuse, the absorbability of the plantar fascia and fat\npad might decrease, and this might explain why\nmaster runners are more susceptible to have\nplantar fasciitis compared with other types of\nRRMIs. A prospective study also observed that\nmore experienced runners were more prone to\nsustain foot injuries;[12] however, this conclusion\nwas classified as limited evidence in a previously\npublished systematic review aimed to identify\ndeterminants of lower extremity running-related\nStudies have reported that patellofemoral syn-\ndrome is the most common RRMI among run-\nners;[2,9,27,29] although our review revealed a\ndifferent picture. Patellofemoral syndrome did\nnot present as the highest incidence or prevalence\nrates of general RRMI, and only two articles re-\nported this injury.[10,26] We take note that one\nstudy used the term ‘runner’s knee’ to describe\ninjuries around the knee region.[24] However,\n‘runner’s knee’ has been used by some authors to\nrefer to patellofemoral syndrome[14,49,50] or ilio-\ntibial band syndrome.[51,52] Thus, we are not sure\nwhether the authors referred to patellofemoral\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "er, this conclusion\nwas classified as limited evidence in a previously\npublished systematic review aimed to identify\ndeterminants of lower extremity running-related\nStudies have reported that patellofemoral syn-\ndrome is the most common RRMI among ru"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012_p0010_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_f2895dc37432", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7abd5f78634c2bce", "text_span_hash": "7abd5f78634c2bce", "source_file": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ong the ultra-marathoners.\nThe most frequently cited aetiology for the pa-\ntellofemoral syndrome is the abnormal patellar\ntracking plus high compressive loads generated\nby the quadriceps femoris muscle contraction\nthat lead to an increased stress on", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "the heel strike phase of running, the heel is the\nfirst contact point that has to absorb the impact\nof up to three times the total body weight.[48] The\nability to absorb and transmit this impact de-\npends on the resilience of the plantar fascia, the\nplantar fat pad and the intrinsic muscles of the\nfoot. With ageing or prolonged repetitive over-\nuse, the absorbability of the plantar fascia and fat\npad might decrease, and this might explain why\nmaster runners are more susceptible to have\nplantar fasciitis compared with other types of\nRRMIs. A prospective study also observed that\nmore experienced runners were more prone to\nsustain foot injuries;[12] however, this conclusion\nwas classified as limited evidence in a previously\npublished systematic review aimed to identify\ndeterminants of lower extremity running-related\nStudies have reported that patellofemoral syn-\ndrome is the most common RRMI among run-\nners;[2,9,27,29] although our review revealed a\ndifferent picture. Patellofemoral syndrome did\nnot present as the highest incidence or prevalence\nrates of general RRMI, and only two articles re-\nported this injury.[10,26] We take note that one\nstudy used the term ‘runner’s knee’ to describe\ninjuries around the knee region.[24] However,\n‘runner’s knee’ has been used by some authors to\nrefer to patellofemoral syndrome[14,49,50] or ilio-\ntibial band syndrome.[51,52] Thus, we are not sure\nwhether the authors referred to patellofemoral\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ong the ultra-marathoners.\nThe most frequently cited aetiology for the pa-\ntellofemoral syndrome is the abnormal patellar\ntracking plus high compressive loads generated\nby the quadriceps femoris muscle contraction\nthat lead to an increased stress on"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012_p0010_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_f2895dc37432", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "af502be9fc90bc64", "text_span_hash": "af502be9fc90bc64", "source_file": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ead to an increased stress on the joint.[54,55]\nThe repetitive contractions of the quadriceps fe-\nmoris during running may contribute to the high\ncompressive loads in the patellofemoral joint\nfavouring the development of patellofemoral\nTable V. Risk", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "the heel strike phase of running, the heel is the\nfirst contact point that has to absorb the impact\nof up to three times the total body weight.[48] The\nability to absorb and transmit this impact de-\npends on the resilience of the plantar fascia, the\nplantar fat pad and the intrinsic muscles of the\nfoot. With ageing or prolonged repetitive over-\nuse, the absorbability of the plantar fascia and fat\npad might decrease, and this might explain why\nmaster runners are more susceptible to have\nplantar fasciitis compared with other types of\nRRMIs. A prospective study also observed that\nmore experienced runners were more prone to\nsustain foot injuries;[12] however, this conclusion\nwas classified as limited evidence in a previously\npublished systematic review aimed to identify\ndeterminants of lower extremity running-related\nStudies have reported that patellofemoral syn-\ndrome is the most common RRMI among run-\nners;[2,9,27,29] although our review revealed a\ndifferent picture. Patellofemoral syndrome did\nnot present as the highest incidence or prevalence\nrates of general RRMI, and only two articles re-\nported this injury.[10,26] We take note that one\nstudy used the term ‘runner’s knee’ to describe\ninjuries around the knee region.[24] However,\n‘runner’s knee’ has been used by some authors to\nrefer to patellofemoral syndrome[14,49,50] or ilio-\ntibial band syndrome.[51,52] Thus, we are not sure\nwhether the authors referred to patellofemoral\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ead to an increased stress on the joint.[54,55]\nThe repetitive contractions of the quadriceps fe-\nmoris during running may contribute to the high\ncompressive loads in the patellofemoral joint\nfavouring the development of patellofemoral\nTable V. Risk"}
