hat one\nstudy used the term ‘runner’s knee’ to describe\ninjuries around the knee region.[24] However,\n‘runner’s knee’ has been used by some authors to\nrefer to patellofemoral syndrome[14,49,50] or ilio-\ntibial band syndrome.[51,52] Thus, we are not sure\nwhether the authors referred to patellofemoral\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ucted by medical doctors. (9) Follow-up period\nfor at least 6 mo for prospective studies and retrospective studies conducted of up to 12 mo. (10) RRMIs reported by a ratio expressing the\nnumber of injuries and the exposure to running.\nStudies conduct"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012_p0010_c0017", "source_registry_id": "src_f2895dc37432", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "35407d05b2416484", "text_span_hash": "35407d05b2416484", "source_file": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 17, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "re to running.\nStudies conducted only during ultra-marathon races.\nNA = not applicable; RRMIs = running-related musculoskeletal injuries. + indicates yes: low risk of bias; - indicates no: high risk of bias.\nAdis ª 2012 Lopes et al., publisher and li", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "the heel strike phase of running, the heel is the\nfirst contact point that has to absorb the impact\nof up to three times the total body weight.[48] The\nability to absorb and transmit this impact de-\npends on the resilience of the plantar fascia, the\nplantar fat pad and the intrinsic muscles of the\nfoot. With ageing or prolonged repetitive over-\nuse, the absorbability of the plantar fascia and fat\npad might decrease, and this might explain why\nmaster runners are more susceptible to have\nplantar fasciitis compared with other types of\nRRMIs. A prospective study also observed that\nmore experienced runners were more prone to\nsustain foot injuries;[12] however, this conclusion\nwas classified as limited evidence in a previously\npublished systematic review aimed to identify\ndeterminants of lower extremity running-related\nStudies have reported that patellofemoral syn-\ndrome is the most common RRMI among run-\nners;[2,9,27,29] although our review revealed a\ndifferent picture. Patellofemoral syndrome did\nnot present as the highest incidence or prevalence\nrates of general RRMI, and only two articles re-\nported this injury.[10,26] We take note that one\nstudy used the term ‘runner’s knee’ to describe\ninjuries around the knee region.[24] However,\n‘runner’s knee’ has been used by some authors to\nrefer to patellofemoral syndrome[14,49,50] or ilio-\ntibial band syndrome.[51,52] Thus, we are not sure\nwhether the authors referred to patellofemoral\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "re to running.\nStudies conducted only during ultra-marathon races.\nNA = not applicable; RRMIs = running-related musculoskeletal injuries. + indicates yes: low risk of bias; - indicates no: high risk of bias.\nAdis ª 2012 Lopes et al., publisher and li"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012_p0011_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_f2895dc37432", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "fe8f63e8fd3980f2", "text_span_hash": "fe8f63e8fd3980f2", "source_file": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "condition is not common among non-ultra-\nmarathon runners and may indicate that this\nRRMI may be specific to ultra-marathon runners\nonly. This tendinopathy has been quoted as an\n‘ultra-marathoner’s ankle’ in one ultra-marathon\nMost of the studies in", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "syndrome. A prospective study that enrolled\nmarathon runners concluded that participating\nin a marathon race for the first time, use of\nmedication and running few kilometres per week\nwere considered to be risk factors for the devel-\nopment of patellofemoral syndrome.[4] Another\nprospective study showed that an increase of\ndistance (kilometres) and the number of hours of\ntraining per week were considered to be protec-\ntive factors against knee injuries in marathon\nrunners.[12] This was classified as strong evidence\nin a systematic review about this topic.[5]\nAnkle dorsiflexors tendinopathy is common\namong ultra-marathon runners during a race.[28,29]\nThe condition is not common among non-ultra-\nmarathon runners and may indicate that this\nRRMI may be specific to ultra-marathon runners\nonly. This tendinopathy has been quoted as an\n‘ultra-marathoner’s ankle’ in one ultra-marathon\nMost of the studies in this systematic review\npresented a moderate risk of bias. One of them\nhad not achieved a score of 4 out of possible score\nof 9.[26] Although most of the studies have a clear\ndefinition of RRMIs the definitions always differ\nbetween studies. For instance, in one study, the\ninjury was defined as ‘‘injuries sufficiently severe\nto impair their performance’’[28] while for another\nstudy, RRMIs were defined as ‘‘injuries that\nmarkedly hampered running training or compe-\ntition for at least 1 week.’’[10] It should be noted\nthat since 2007, i", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "condition is not common among non-ultra-\nmarathon runners and may indicate that this\nRRMI may be specific to ultra-marathon runners\nonly. This tendinopathy has been quoted as an\n‘ultra-marathoner’s ankle’ in one ultra-marathon\nMost of the studies in"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012_p0011_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_f2895dc37432", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "812c23e8b896c022", "text_span_hash": "812c23e8b896c022", "source_file": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "injuries that\nmarkedly hampered running training or compe-\ntition for at least 1 week.’’[10] It should be noted\nthat since 2007, it has been suggested in the sports\nTable VI. Incidence and prevalence of running-related musculoskeletal injuries sustai", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "syndrome. A prospective study that enrolled\nmarathon runners concluded that participating\nin a marathon race for the first time, use of\nmedication and running few kilometres per week\nwere considered to be risk factors for the devel-\nopment of patellofemoral syndrome.[4] Another\nprospective study showed that an increase of\ndistance (kilometres) and the number of hours of\ntraining per week were considered to be protec-\ntive factors against knee injuries in marathon\nrunners.[12] This was classified as strong evidence\nin a systematic review about this topic.[5]\nAnkle dorsiflexors tendinopathy is common\namong ultra-marathon runners during a race.[28,29]\nThe condition is not common among non-ultra-\nmarathon runners and may indicate that this\nRRMI may be specific to ultra-marathon runners\nonly. This tendinopathy has been quoted as an\n‘ultra-marathoner’s ankle’ in one ultra-marathon\nMost of the studies in this systematic review\npresented a moderate risk of bias. One of them\nhad not achieved a score of 4 out of possible score\nof 9.[26] Although most of the studies have a clear\ndefinition of RRMIs the definitions always differ\nbetween studies. For instance, in one study, the\ninjury was defined as ‘‘injuries sufficiently severe\nto impair their performance’’[28] while for another\nstudy, RRMIs were defined as ‘‘injuries that\nmarkedly hampered running training or compe-\ntition for at least 1 week.’’[10] It should be noted\nthat since 2007, i", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "injuries that\nmarkedly hampered running training or compe-\ntition for at least 1 week.’’[10] It should be noted\nthat since 2007, it has been suggested in the sports\nTable VI. Incidence and prevalence of running-related musculoskeletal injuries sustai"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012_p0011_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_f2895dc37432", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3d16fe25b0f29d4e", "text_span_hash": "3d16fe25b0f29d4e", "source_file": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "s to the number of articles that reported the incidence (total of incidence articles = 3) or\nprevalence (total of prevalence articles not conducted in ultra-marathon races = 2) of each RRMI.\nRepresents patellofemoral syndrome and patellofemoral pain.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "syndrome. A prospective study that enrolled\nmarathon runners concluded that participating\nin a marathon race for the first time, use of\nmedication and running few kilometres per week\nwere considered to be risk factors for the devel-\nopment of patellofemoral syndrome.[4] Another\nprospective study showed that an increase of\ndistance (kilometres) and the number of hours of\ntraining per week were considered to be protec-\ntive factors against knee injuries in marathon\nrunners.[12] This was classified as strong evidence\nin a systematic review about this topic.[5]\nAnkle dorsiflexors tendinopathy is common\namong ultra-marathon runners during a race.[28,29]\nThe condition is not common among non-ultra-\nmarathon runners and may indicate that this\nRRMI may be specific to ultra-marathon runners\nonly. This tendinopathy has been quoted as an\n‘ultra-marathoner’s ankle’ in one ultra-marathon\nMost of the studies in this systematic review\npresented a moderate risk of bias. One of them\nhad not achieved a score of 4 out of possible score\nof 9.[26] Although most of the studies have a clear\ndefinition of RRMIs the definitions always differ\nbetween studies. For instance, in one study, the\ninjury was defined as ‘‘injuries sufficiently severe\nto impair their performance’’[28] while for another\nstudy, RRMIs were defined as ‘‘injuries that\nmarkedly hampered running training or compe-\ntition for at least 1 week.’’[10] It should be noted\nthat since 2007, i", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "s to the number of articles that reported the incidence (total of incidence articles = 3) or\nprevalence (total of prevalence articles not conducted in ultra-marathon races = 2) of each RRMI.\nRepresents patellofemoral syndrome and patellofemoral pain."}
{"chunk_id": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012_p0011_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_f2895dc37432", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "64dc546ab40234a6", "text_span_hash": "64dc546ab40234a6", "source_file": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "drome and patellofemoral pain.\nRRMIs = running-related musculoskeletal injuries.\nWhat are the Main Running-Related Injuries?\nAdis ª 2012 Lopes et al., publisher and licensee Springer International Publishing AG.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "syndrome. A prospective study that enrolled\nmarathon runners concluded that participating\nin a marathon race for the first time, use of\nmedication and running few kilometres per week\nwere considered to be risk factors for the devel-\nopment of patellofemoral syndrome.[4] Another\nprospective study showed that an increase of\ndistance (kilometres) and the number of hours of\ntraining per week were considered to be protec-\ntive factors against knee injuries in marathon\nrunners.[12] This was classified as strong evidence\nin a systematic review about this topic.[5]\nAnkle dorsiflexors tendinopathy is common\namong ultra-marathon runners during a race.[28,29]\nThe condition is not common among non-ultra-\nmarathon runners and may indicate that this\nRRMI may be specific to ultra-marathon runners\nonly. This tendinopathy has been quoted as an\n‘ultra-marathoner’s ankle’ in one ultra-marathon\nMost of the studies in this systematic review\npresented a moderate risk of bias. One of them\nhad not achieved a score of 4 out of possible score\nof 9.[26] Although most of the studies have a clear\ndefinition of RRMIs the definitions always differ\nbetween studies. For instance, in one study, the\ninjury was defined as ‘‘injuries sufficiently severe\nto impair their performance’’[28] while for another\nstudy, RRMIs were defined as ‘‘injuries that\nmarkedly hampered running training or compe-\ntition for at least 1 week.’’[10] It should be noted\nthat since 2007, i", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "drome and patellofemoral pain.\nRRMIs = running-related musculoskeletal injuries.\nWhat are the Main Running-Related Injuries?\nAdis ª 2012 Lopes et al., publisher and licensee Springer International Publishing AG."}
{"chunk_id": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012_p0012_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_f2895dc37432", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f054648798ea1bb5", "text_span_hash": "f054648798ea1bb5", "source_file": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "medicine community that a standard RRMI de-\nfinition should be adopted[5] but, to our knowledge,\nthere is no consensus about the most appropriate\ndefinition for an RRMI. All articles included in\nthis review described the type of runners (e.g. ultra-", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "medicine community that a standard RRMI de-\nfinition should be adopted[5] but, to our knowledge,\nthere is no consensus about the most appropriate\ndefinition for an RRMI. All articles included in\nthis review described the type of runners (e.g. ultra-\nmarathoners[27-29]) or their characteristics of train-\ning (e.g. running at least five times per week[23]),\nor race participation (runners of a 10 km race[25]).\nDifferent definitions of RRMIs, as well as a dif-\nference in runners’ characteristics, hampers the\npossibility of pooling the data for a meta-analysis\nor for making a meaningful comparative analysis of\nthe incidence or prevalence rates of RRMIs.[5,21,56]\nIn terms of the participants, only one study\nperformed a random sample selection[25] and two\nstudies sampled the entire target population of\nrunners.[28,29] There are only three prospective\nstudies that could enable the assessment of the\nloss to a follow-up criterion,[10,23,24] and all of\nthem fulfilled this criterion, which indicated a\nlower risk of bias in these studies.[57] One study\ndid not collect the information of injuries directly\nfrom runners[28] and this can introduce some de-\ngree of bias.[58] Five studies used the same mode\nof injuries data collection for all runners,[10,24,25,27,29]\nand three studies utilized a mixed mode of data\ncollection (i.e. data collected while observing a\nrace can be either transmitted for assessment via\ne-mail or hard copy,[26] by telep", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "medicine community that a standard RRMI de-\nfinition should be adopted[5] but, to our knowledge,\nthere is no consensus about the most appropriate\ndefinition for an RRMI. All articles included in\nthis review described the type of runners (e.g. ultra-"}
