{"chunk_id": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012_p0013_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_f2895dc37432", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2b8116421602384d", "text_span_hash": "2b8116421602384d", "source_file": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "RRMIs in different types of runners (e.g.\nultra-marathoners, marathoners, elite, recreational,\nrace runners, etc.).\nElectronic searches were conducted in the\nmain databases related to the sports injuries field.\nHowever, it is possible that eligible", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "comparable or common unit.[5] Some studies with\nrunners as the subjects reported RRMI rates by\nusing the number of injuries per 1000 hours of\nexposure to running[7-12] and this is more appro-\npriate because the amount of exposure to the\nload (running) may influence the development of\nmusculoskeletal injuries.[63] Among the eight ar-\nticles, only two of them stated the overall incidence\nratio of RRMIs per 1000 hours of exposure to\nrunning[10,24] and no study stated the incidence or\nprevalence ratio of each RRMI. We suggest that\nfuture studies should standardize the expression\nof RRMI data by the ratio of the number of in-\njuries per hour of exposure to running. This would\nenable comparison or pooling of data for meta-\nanalysis.[64] In addition, future studies should be\nconducted to determine a consensus about the\ndefinition of RRMIs, as well as aim to identify\nthe main RRMIs in different types of runners (e.g.\nultra-marathoners, marathoners, elite, recreational,\nrace runners, etc.).\nElectronic searches were conducted in the\nmain databases related to the sports injuries field.\nHowever, it is possible that eligible articles have\nbeen published in journals that are not indexed in\nany of the searched databases.\nThe most frequent general RRMIs reported by\nstudies were medial tibial stress syndrome, Achilles\ntendinopathy and plantar fasciitis. For runners\nwho participated in ultra-marathon races that\nranged from 5 to 8.5 days, Achill", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "RRMIs in different types of runners (e.g.\nultra-marathoners, marathoners, elite, recreational,\nrace runners, etc.).\nElectronic searches were conducted in the\nmain databases related to the sports injuries field.\nHowever, it is possible that eligible"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012_p0013_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_f2895dc37432", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "42ac46b30d345508", "text_span_hash": "42ac46b30d345508", "source_file": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ors for lower extremity injuries among male marathon\nrunners. Scand J Med Sci Sports 2008 Dec; 18 (6): 691-7\n2. Taunton JE, Ryan MB, Clement DB, et al. A retrospective\ncase-control analysis of 2002 running injuries. Br J Sports\n3. Williams PT. Relati", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "comparable or common unit.[5] Some studies with\nrunners as the subjects reported RRMI rates by\nusing the number of injuries per 1000 hours of\nexposure to running[7-12] and this is more appro-\npriate because the amount of exposure to the\nload (running) may influence the development of\nmusculoskeletal injuries.[63] Among the eight ar-\nticles, only two of them stated the overall incidence\nratio of RRMIs per 1000 hours of exposure to\nrunning[10,24] and no study stated the incidence or\nprevalence ratio of each RRMI. We suggest that\nfuture studies should standardize the expression\nof RRMI data by the ratio of the number of in-\njuries per hour of exposure to running. This would\nenable comparison or pooling of data for meta-\nanalysis.[64] In addition, future studies should be\nconducted to determine a consensus about the\ndefinition of RRMIs, as well as aim to identify\nthe main RRMIs in different types of runners (e.g.\nultra-marathoners, marathoners, elite, recreational,\nrace runners, etc.).\nElectronic searches were conducted in the\nmain databases related to the sports injuries field.\nHowever, it is possible that eligible articles have\nbeen published in journals that are not indexed in\nany of the searched databases.\nThe most frequent general RRMIs reported by\nstudies were medial tibial stress syndrome, Achilles\ntendinopathy and plantar fasciitis. For runners\nwho participated in ultra-marathon races that\nranged from 5 to 8.5 days, Achill", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ors for lower extremity injuries among male marathon\nrunners. Scand J Med Sci Sports 2008 Dec; 18 (6): 691-7\n2. Taunton JE, Ryan MB, Clement DB, et al. A retrospective\ncase-control analysis of 2002 running injuries. Br J Sports\n3. Williams PT. Relati"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012_p0013_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_f2895dc37432", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7df3bed88bec65a0", "text_span_hash": "7df3bed88bec65a0", "source_file": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "y running injuries in\nlong distance runners: a systematic review. Br J Sports Med\n6. Van Middelkoop M, Kolkman J, Van Ochten J, et al. Pre-\nvalence and incidence of lower extremity injuries in male\nmarathon runners. Scand J Med Sci Sports 2008 Apr; 1", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "comparable or common unit.[5] Some studies with\nrunners as the subjects reported RRMI rates by\nusing the number of injuries per 1000 hours of\nexposure to running[7-12] and this is more appro-\npriate because the amount of exposure to the\nload (running) may influence the development of\nmusculoskeletal injuries.[63] Among the eight ar-\nticles, only two of them stated the overall incidence\nratio of RRMIs per 1000 hours of exposure to\nrunning[10,24] and no study stated the incidence or\nprevalence ratio of each RRMI. We suggest that\nfuture studies should standardize the expression\nof RRMI data by the ratio of the number of in-\njuries per hour of exposure to running. This would\nenable comparison or pooling of data for meta-\nanalysis.[64] In addition, future studies should be\nconducted to determine a consensus about the\ndefinition of RRMIs, as well as aim to identify\nthe main RRMIs in different types of runners (e.g.\nultra-marathoners, marathoners, elite, recreational,\nrace runners, etc.).\nElectronic searches were conducted in the\nmain databases related to the sports injuries field.\nHowever, it is possible that eligible articles have\nbeen published in journals that are not indexed in\nany of the searched databases.\nThe most frequent general RRMIs reported by\nstudies were medial tibial stress syndrome, Achilles\ntendinopathy and plantar fasciitis. For runners\nwho participated in ultra-marathon races that\nranged from 5 to 8.5 days, Achill", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "y running injuries in\nlong distance runners: a systematic review. Br J Sports Med\n6. Van Middelkoop M, Kolkman J, Van Ochten J, et al. Pre-\nvalence and incidence of lower extremity injuries in male\nmarathon runners. Scand J Med Sci Sports 2008 Apr; 1"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012_p0013_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_f2895dc37432", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "803d2a98609274c9", "text_span_hash": "803d2a98609274c9", "source_file": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "d J Med Sci Sports 2008 Apr; 18 (2):\n7. Bovens AM, Janssen GM, Vermeer HG, et al. Occurrence of\nrunning injuries in adults following a supervised training\nprogram. Int J Sports Med 1989 Oct; 10 (3 Suppl.): 186-90S\n8. Buist I, Bredeweg SW, Bessem B, e", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "comparable or common unit.[5] Some studies with\nrunners as the subjects reported RRMI rates by\nusing the number of injuries per 1000 hours of\nexposure to running[7-12] and this is more appro-\npriate because the amount of exposure to the\nload (running) may influence the development of\nmusculoskeletal injuries.[63] Among the eight ar-\nticles, only two of them stated the overall incidence\nratio of RRMIs per 1000 hours of exposure to\nrunning[10,24] and no study stated the incidence or\nprevalence ratio of each RRMI. We suggest that\nfuture studies should standardize the expression\nof RRMI data by the ratio of the number of in-\njuries per hour of exposure to running. This would\nenable comparison or pooling of data for meta-\nanalysis.[64] In addition, future studies should be\nconducted to determine a consensus about the\ndefinition of RRMIs, as well as aim to identify\nthe main RRMIs in different types of runners (e.g.\nultra-marathoners, marathoners, elite, recreational,\nrace runners, etc.).\nElectronic searches were conducted in the\nmain databases related to the sports injuries field.\nHowever, it is possible that eligible articles have\nbeen published in journals that are not indexed in\nany of the searched databases.\nThe most frequent general RRMIs reported by\nstudies were medial tibial stress syndrome, Achilles\ntendinopathy and plantar fasciitis. For runners\nwho participated in ultra-marathon races that\nranged from 5 to 8.5 days, Achill", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "d J Med Sci Sports 2008 Apr; 18 (2):\n7. Bovens AM, Janssen GM, Vermeer HG, et al. Occurrence of\nrunning injuries in adults following a supervised training\nprogram. Int J Sports Med 1989 Oct; 10 (3 Suppl.): 186-90S\n8. Buist I, Bredeweg SW, Bessem B, e"}
