published in journals that are not indexed in\nany of the searched databases.\nThe most frequent general RRMIs reported by\nstudies were medial tibial stress syndrome, Achilles\ntendinopathy and plantar fasciitis. For runners\nwho participated in ultra-marathon races that\nranged from 5 to 8.5 days, Achill", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "FP, et al. Epidemiology of\nmusculoskeletal injuries among high school cross-country\nrunners. Am J Epidemiol 2006 Jan; 163 (2): 151-9\n12. Wen DY, Puffer JC, Schmalzried TP. Injuries in runners:\na prospective study of alignment. Clin J Sport Med 1998"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012_p0013_c0019", "source_registry_id": "src_f2895dc37432", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "a47a923084fd1b28", "text_span_hash": "a47a923084fd1b28", "source_file": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 19, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "B, Vader JP, Minder CE, et al. On the epidemiology of\nrunning injuries: the 1984 Bern Grand-Prix study. Am J\n16. Maughan RJ, Miller JD. Incidence of training-related in-\njuries among marathon runners. Br J Sports Med 1983 Sep;\n17. Satterthwaite P, L", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "comparable or common unit.[5] Some studies with\nrunners as the subjects reported RRMI rates by\nusing the number of injuries per 1000 hours of\nexposure to running[7-12] and this is more appro-\npriate because the amount of exposure to the\nload (running) may influence the development of\nmusculoskeletal injuries.[63] Among the eight ar-\nticles, only two of them stated the overall incidence\nratio of RRMIs per 1000 hours of exposure to\nrunning[10,24] and no study stated the incidence or\nprevalence ratio of each RRMI. We suggest that\nfuture studies should standardize the expression\nof RRMI data by the ratio of the number of in-\njuries per hour of exposure to running. This would\nenable comparison or pooling of data for meta-\nanalysis.[64] In addition, future studies should be\nconducted to determine a consensus about the\ndefinition of RRMIs, as well as aim to identify\nthe main RRMIs in different types of runners (e.g.\nultra-marathoners, marathoners, elite, recreational,\nrace runners, etc.).\nElectronic searches were conducted in the\nmain databases related to the sports injuries field.\nHowever, it is possible that eligible articles have\nbeen published in journals that are not indexed in\nany of the searched databases.\nThe most frequent general RRMIs reported by\nstudies were medial tibial stress syndrome, Achilles\ntendinopathy and plantar fasciitis. For runners\nwho participated in ultra-marathon races that\nranged from 5 to 8.5 days, Achill", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "B, Vader JP, Minder CE, et al. On the epidemiology of\nrunning injuries: the 1984 Bern Grand-Prix study. Am J\n16. Maughan RJ, Miller JD. Incidence of training-related in-\njuries among marathon runners. Br J Sports Med 1983 Sep;\n17. Satterthwaite P, L"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012_p0013_c0020", "source_registry_id": "src_f2895dc37432", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3979ddf3e6e8304c", "text_span_hash": "3979ddf3e6e8304c", "source_file": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 20, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "83 Sep;\n17. Satterthwaite P, Larmer P, Gardiner J, et al. Incidence of in-\njuries and other health problems in the Auckland Citibank\nmarathon, 1993. Br J Sports Med 1996 Dec; 30 (4): 324-6\n18. Taunton JE, Ryan MB, Clement DB, et al. A prospective\nstu", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "comparable or common unit.[5] Some studies with\nrunners as the subjects reported RRMI rates by\nusing the number of injuries per 1000 hours of\nexposure to running[7-12] and this is more appro-\npriate because the amount of exposure to the\nload (running) may influence the development of\nmusculoskeletal injuries.[63] Among the eight ar-\nticles, only two of them stated the overall incidence\nratio of RRMIs per 1000 hours of exposure to\nrunning[10,24] and no study stated the incidence or\nprevalence ratio of each RRMI. We suggest that\nfuture studies should standardize the expression\nof RRMI data by the ratio of the number of in-\njuries per hour of exposure to running. This would\nenable comparison or pooling of data for meta-\nanalysis.[64] In addition, future studies should be\nconducted to determine a consensus about the\ndefinition of RRMIs, as well as aim to identify\nthe main RRMIs in different types of runners (e.g.\nultra-marathoners, marathoners, elite, recreational,\nrace runners, etc.).\nElectronic searches were conducted in the\nmain databases related to the sports injuries field.\nHowever, it is possible that eligible articles have\nbeen published in journals that are not indexed in\nany of the searched databases.\nThe most frequent general RRMIs reported by\nstudies were medial tibial stress syndrome, Achilles\ntendinopathy and plantar fasciitis. For runners\nwho participated in ultra-marathon races that\nranged from 5 to 8.5 days, Achill", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "83 Sep;\n17. Satterthwaite P, Larmer P, Gardiner J, et al. Incidence of in-\njuries and other health problems in the Auckland Citibank\nmarathon, 1993. Br J Sports Med 1996 Dec; 30 (4): 324-6\n18. Taunton JE, Ryan MB, Clement DB, et al. A prospective\nstu"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012_p0013_c0021", "source_registry_id": "src_f2895dc37432", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "a3ac2c86db61119f", "text_span_hash": "a3ac2c86db61119f", "source_file": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 21, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "t DB, et al. A prospective\nstudy of running injuries: the Vancouver sun run ‘‘in\ntraining’’ clinics. Br J Sports Med 2003 Jun; 37 (3): 239-44\n19. Walter SD, Hart LE, McIntosh JM, et al. The Ontario co-\nhort study of running-related injuries. Arch Int", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "comparable or common unit.[5] Some studies with\nrunners as the subjects reported RRMI rates by\nusing the number of injuries per 1000 hours of\nexposure to running[7-12] and this is more appro-\npriate because the amount of exposure to the\nload (running) may influence the development of\nmusculoskeletal injuries.[63] Among the eight ar-\nticles, only two of them stated the overall incidence\nratio of RRMIs per 1000 hours of exposure to\nrunning[10,24] and no study stated the incidence or\nprevalence ratio of each RRMI. We suggest that\nfuture studies should standardize the expression\nof RRMI data by the ratio of the number of in-\njuries per hour of exposure to running. This would\nenable comparison or pooling of data for meta-\nanalysis.[64] In addition, future studies should be\nconducted to determine a consensus about the\ndefinition of RRMIs, as well as aim to identify\nthe main RRMIs in different types of runners (e.g.\nultra-marathoners, marathoners, elite, recreational,\nrace runners, etc.).\nElectronic searches were conducted in the\nmain databases related to the sports injuries field.\nHowever, it is possible that eligible articles have\nbeen published in journals that are not indexed in\nany of the searched databases.\nThe most frequent general RRMIs reported by\nstudies were medial tibial stress syndrome, Achilles\ntendinopathy and plantar fasciitis. For runners\nwho participated in ultra-marathon races that\nranged from 5 to 8.5 days, Achill", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "t DB, et al. A prospective\nstudy of running injuries: the Vancouver sun run ‘‘in\ntraining’’ clinics. Br J Sports Med 2003 Jun; 37 (3): 239-44\n19. Walter SD, Hart LE, McIntosh JM, et al. The Ontario co-\nhort study of running-related injuries. Arch Int"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012_p0013_c0022", "source_registry_id": "src_f2895dc37432", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "34ce063da520007d", "text_span_hash": "34ce063da520007d", "source_file": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 22, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ing-related injuries. Arch Intern Med\nWhat are the Main Running-Related Injuries?\nAdis ª 2012 Lopes et al., publisher and licensee Springer International Publishing AG.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "comparable or common unit.[5] Some studies with\nrunners as the subjects reported RRMI rates by\nusing the number of injuries per 1000 hours of\nexposure to running[7-12] and this is more appro-\npriate because the amount of exposure to the\nload (running) may influence the development of\nmusculoskeletal injuries.[63] Among the eight ar-\nticles, only two of them stated the overall incidence\nratio of RRMIs per 1000 hours of exposure to\nrunning[10,24] and no study stated the incidence or\nprevalence ratio of each RRMI. We suggest that\nfuture studies should standardize the expression\nof RRMI data by the ratio of the number of in-\njuries per hour of exposure to running. This would\nenable comparison or pooling of data for meta-\nanalysis.[64] In addition, future studies should be\nconducted to determine a consensus about the\ndefinition of RRMIs, as well as aim to identify\nthe main RRMIs in different types of runners (e.g.\nultra-marathoners, marathoners, elite, recreational,\nrace runners, etc.).\nElectronic searches were conducted in the\nmain databases related to the sports injuries field.\nHowever, it is possible that eligible articles have\nbeen published in journals that are not indexed in\nany of the searched databases.\nThe most frequent general RRMIs reported by\nstudies were medial tibial stress syndrome, Achilles\ntendinopathy and plantar fasciitis. For runners\nwho participated in ultra-marathon races that\nranged from 5 to 8.5 days, Achill", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ing-related injuries. Arch Intern Med\nWhat are the Main Running-Related Injuries?\nAdis ª 2012 Lopes et al., publisher and licensee Springer International Publishing AG."}
