{"chunk_id": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012_p0015_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_f2895dc37432", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8ac8425d3b241e2f", "text_span_hash": "8ac8425d3b241e2f", "source_file": "safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_running_msk_injuries_2012.pdf", "page": 15, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Sa˜o Paulo,\nRua Cesa´rio Galeno, 448/475 – Sa˜o Paulo, SP – CEP: 03071-\nluca_hespanhol@hotmail.com\nWhat are the Main Running-Related Injuries?\nAdis ª 2012 Lopes et al., publisher and licensee Springer International Publishing AG.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "59. McHorney CA, Kosinski M, Ware Jr JE. Comparisons of\nthe costs and quality of norms for the SF-36 health survey\ncollected by mail versus telephone interview: results from a\nnational survey. Med Care 1994 Jun; 32 (6): 551-67\n60. Fuller CW, Ekstrand J, Junge A, et al. Consensus statement\non injury definitions and data collection procedures in\nstudies of football (soccer) injuries. Br J Sports Med 2006\n61. Twellaar M, Verstappen FT, Huson A. Is prevention of\nsports injuries a realistic goal? A four-year prospective in-\nvestigation of sports injuries among physical education\nstudents. Am J Sports Med 1996 Jul-Aug; 24 (4): 528-34\n62. Junge A, Dvorak J. Influence of definition and data collec-\ntion on the incidence of injuries in football. Am J Sports\n63. Van Mechelen W, Twisk J, Molendijk A, et al. Subject-\nrelated risk factors for sports injuries: a 1-yr prospective\nstudy in young adults. Med Sci Sports Exerc 1996 Sep;\n64. Hopkins WG, Marshall SW, Quarrie KL, et al. Risk factors\nand risk statistics for sports injuries. Clin J Sport Med 2007\nin Physical Therapy, Universidade Cidade de Sa˜o Paulo,\nRua Cesa´rio Galeno, 448/475 – Sa˜o Paulo, SP – CEP: 03071-\nluca_hespanhol@hotmail.com\nWhat are the Main Running-Related Injuries?\nAdis ª 2012 Lopes et al., publisher and licensee Springer International Publishing AG.", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Sa˜o Paulo,\nRua Cesa´rio Galeno, 448/475 – Sa˜o Paulo, SP – CEP: 03071-\nluca_hespanhol@hotmail.com\nWhat are the Main Running-Related Injuries?\nAdis ª 2012 Lopes et al., publisher and licensee Springer International Publishing AG."}
{"chunk_id": "safety_subjective_load_2018_p0001_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_a684727e89a8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8cd48440406e8440", "text_span_hash": "8cd48440406e8440", "source_file": "safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "The Current State of Subjective Training\nLoad Monitoring—a Practical Perspective\nJoseph O. C. Coyne1*, G. Gregory Haff1, Aaron J. Coutts2, Robert U. Newton1 and Sophia Nimphius1\nThis commentary delivers a practical perspective on the current state of", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "The Current State of Subjective Training\nLoad Monitoring—a Practical Perspective\nJoseph O. C. Coyne1*, G. Gregory Haff1, Aaron J. Coutts2, Robert U. Newton1 and Sophia Nimphius1\nThis commentary delivers a practical perspective on the current state of subjective training load (TL) monitoring,\nand in particular sessional ratings of perceived exertion, for performance enhancement and injury prevention.\nSubjective measures may be able to reflect mental fatigue, effort, stress, and motivation. These factors appear to be\nimportant moderators of the relationship TL has with performance and injury, and they also seem to differ between\nopen and closed skill sports. As such, mental factors may affect the interaction between TL, performance, and injury\nin different sports. Further, modeling these interactions may be limited due to the assumption that an independent\nsignal can adequately account for the performance or injury outcomes. An independent signal model does not\naccurately reflect training environments where multiple stressors (e.g., mechanical, emotional, nutritional) impact\nadaptations. Common issues with using subjective TL monitoring, including a lack of differentiation between\nbiomechanical, physiological, and cognitive load, may be overcome by considering psychometric measurement\nbest practices, finer graded scales, and differential ratings of perceived exertion. Methods of calculating TL,\nincluding different acute and chro", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "The Current State of Subjective Training\nLoad Monitoring—a Practical Perspective\nJoseph O. C. Coyne1*, G. Gregory Haff1, Aaron J. Coutts2, Robert U. Newton1 and Sophia Nimphius1\nThis commentary delivers a practical perspective on the current state of"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_subjective_load_2018_p0001_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_a684727e89a8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "91c55b10c85b6f95", "text_span_hash": "91c55b10c85b6f95", "source_file": "safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "finer graded scales, and differential ratings of perceived exertion. Methods of calculating TL,\nincluding different acute and chronic time periods, may also need to be individualized to different sports and\npotentially different individuals within th", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "The Current State of Subjective Training\nLoad Monitoring—a Practical Perspective\nJoseph O. C. Coyne1*, G. Gregory Haff1, Aaron J. Coutts2, Robert U. Newton1 and Sophia Nimphius1\nThis commentary delivers a practical perspective on the current state of subjective training load (TL) monitoring,\nand in particular sessional ratings of perceived exertion, for performance enhancement and injury prevention.\nSubjective measures may be able to reflect mental fatigue, effort, stress, and motivation. These factors appear to be\nimportant moderators of the relationship TL has with performance and injury, and they also seem to differ between\nopen and closed skill sports. As such, mental factors may affect the interaction between TL, performance, and injury\nin different sports. Further, modeling these interactions may be limited due to the assumption that an independent\nsignal can adequately account for the performance or injury outcomes. An independent signal model does not\naccurately reflect training environments where multiple stressors (e.g., mechanical, emotional, nutritional) impact\nadaptations. Common issues with using subjective TL monitoring, including a lack of differentiation between\nbiomechanical, physiological, and cognitive load, may be overcome by considering psychometric measurement\nbest practices, finer graded scales, and differential ratings of perceived exertion. Methods of calculating TL,\nincluding different acute and chro", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "finer graded scales, and differential ratings of perceived exertion. Methods of calculating TL,\nincluding different acute and chronic time periods, may also need to be individualized to different sports and\npotentially different individuals within th"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_subjective_load_2018_p0001_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_a684727e89a8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "dd18db319e14a15a", "text_span_hash": "dd18db319e14a15a", "source_file": "safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ifferent individuals within the same sport. As TL monitoring is predominately a “chronic” decision-\nmaking tool, “acute” decision-making tools, e.g., subjective wellness and autonomic nervous system measures,\nshould be combined in a bespoke multivari", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "The Current State of Subjective Training\nLoad Monitoring—a Practical Perspective\nJoseph O. C. Coyne1*, G. Gregory Haff1, Aaron J. Coutts2, Robert U. Newton1 and Sophia Nimphius1\nThis commentary delivers a practical perspective on the current state of subjective training load (TL) monitoring,\nand in particular sessional ratings of perceived exertion, for performance enhancement and injury prevention.\nSubjective measures may be able to reflect mental fatigue, effort, stress, and motivation. These factors appear to be\nimportant moderators of the relationship TL has with performance and injury, and they also seem to differ between\nopen and closed skill sports. As such, mental factors may affect the interaction between TL, performance, and injury\nin different sports. Further, modeling these interactions may be limited due to the assumption that an independent\nsignal can adequately account for the performance or injury outcomes. An independent signal model does not\naccurately reflect training environments where multiple stressors (e.g., mechanical, emotional, nutritional) impact\nadaptations. Common issues with using subjective TL monitoring, including a lack of differentiation between\nbiomechanical, physiological, and cognitive load, may be overcome by considering psychometric measurement\nbest practices, finer graded scales, and differential ratings of perceived exertion. Methods of calculating TL,\nincluding different acute and chro", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ifferent individuals within the same sport. As TL monitoring is predominately a “chronic” decision-\nmaking tool, “acute” decision-making tools, e.g., subjective wellness and autonomic nervous system measures,\nshould be combined in a bespoke multivari"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_subjective_load_2018_p0002_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_a684727e89a8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1cb349461c9219fe", "text_span_hash": "1cb349461c9219fe", "source_file": "safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "methods of monitoring TL vary considerably depending\non the type of sport or activity [3]; however, TL models\nare commonly analyzed using training impulse, which is\nnormally a product of an intensity factor and volume/\nduration factor [2, 5].\nSubject", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "methods of monitoring TL vary considerably depending\non the type of sport or activity [3]; however, TL models\nare commonly analyzed using training impulse, which is\nnormally a product of an intensity factor and volume/\nduration factor [2, 5].\nSubjective measures of TL, and in particular, sessional\nratings of perceived exertion (sRPE), are recommended\nas a primary measure of TL in systematic reviews of the\nliterature [6, 7]. Subjective measures may also be more\nsensitive and consistent than objective measures [8, 9],\nand sRPE has been reported as the most commonly\nassessed TL variable in most sports [10]. Besides sRPE,\nthere are other subjective methods of assessing an ath-\nlete’s response to training, e.g., visual analogue scales\nAlthough these measures will be highlighted later in\nthe commentary, they are not usually incorporated into\ncommon TL models using training impulse as intensity\nfactors. Due to the above factors, sRPE will be the prin-\ncipal focus of this commentary.\nA basic model of an athlete’s response to training can\nbe estimated from data collected as part of the TL\nmonitoring process [1, 5]. Specifically, the difference\nbetween “fitness” (positive) and “fatigue” (negative)\nfunctions can be quantified with internal or external TL\nvariables as training impulse [1, 5]. The genesis of this\nbasic TL model stems from the work of Bannister [5].\nA recent simplified extension of this work has been the\ndevelopment of the ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "methods of monitoring TL vary considerably depending\non the type of sport or activity [3]; however, TL models\nare commonly analyzed using training impulse, which is\nnormally a product of an intensity factor and volume/\nduration factor [2, 5].\nSubject"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_subjective_load_2018_p0002_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_a684727e89a8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0273f5021823b403", "text_span_hash": "0273f5021823b403", "source_file": "safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "of assessing an ath-\nlete’s response to training, e.g., visual analogue scales\nAlthough these measures will be highlighted later in\nthe commentary, they are not usually incorporated into\ncommon TL models using training impulse as intensity\nfactors.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "methods of monitoring TL vary considerably depending\non the type of sport or activity [3]; however, TL models\nare commonly analyzed using training impulse, which is\nnormally a product of an intensity factor and volume/\nduration factor [2, 5].\nSubjective measures of TL, and in particular, sessional\nratings of perceived exertion (sRPE), are recommended\nas a primary measure of TL in systematic reviews of the\nliterature [6, 7]. Subjective measures may also be more\nsensitive and consistent than objective measures [8, 9],\nand sRPE has been reported as the most commonly\nassessed TL variable in most sports [10]. Besides sRPE,\nthere are other subjective methods of assessing an ath-\nlete’s response to training, e.g., visual analogue scales\nAlthough these measures will be highlighted later in\nthe commentary, they are not usually incorporated into\ncommon TL models using training impulse as intensity\nfactors. Due to the above factors, sRPE will be the prin-\ncipal focus of this commentary.\nA basic model of an athlete’s response to training can\nbe estimated from data collected as part of the TL\nmonitoring process [1, 5]. Specifically, the difference\nbetween “fitness” (positive) and “fatigue” (negative)\nfunctions can be quantified with internal or external TL\nvariables as training impulse [1, 5]. The genesis of this\nbasic TL model stems from the work of Bannister [5].\nA recent simplified extension of this work has been the\ndevelopment of the ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "of assessing an ath-\nlete’s response to training, e.g., visual analogue scales\nAlthough these measures will be highlighted later in\nthe commentary, they are not usually incorporated into\ncommon TL models using training impulse as intensity\nfactors."}
{"chunk_id": "safety_subjective_load_2018_p0002_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_a684727e89a8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ef845656a76cf49b", "text_span_hash": "ef845656a76cf49b", "source_file": "safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "impulse as intensity\nfactors. Due to the above factors, sRPE will be the prin-\ncipal focus of this commentary.\nA basic model of an athlete’s response to training can\nbe estimated from data collected as part of the TL\nmonitoring process [1, 5]. Specif", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "methods of monitoring TL vary considerably depending\non the type of sport or activity [3]; however, TL models\nare commonly analyzed using training impulse, which is\nnormally a product of an intensity factor and volume/\nduration factor [2, 5].\nSubjective measures of TL, and in particular, sessional\nratings of perceived exertion (sRPE), are recommended\nas a primary measure of TL in systematic reviews of the\nliterature [6, 7]. Subjective measures may also be more\nsensitive and consistent than objective measures [8, 9],\nand sRPE has been reported as the most commonly\nassessed TL variable in most sports [10]. Besides sRPE,\nthere are other subjective methods of assessing an ath-\nlete’s response to training, e.g., visual analogue scales\nAlthough these measures will be highlighted later in\nthe commentary, they are not usually incorporated into\ncommon TL models using training impulse as intensity\nfactors. Due to the above factors, sRPE will be the prin-\ncipal focus of this commentary.\nA basic model of an athlete’s response to training can\nbe estimated from data collected as part of the TL\nmonitoring process [1, 5]. Specifically, the difference\nbetween “fitness” (positive) and “fatigue” (negative)\nfunctions can be quantified with internal or external TL\nvariables as training impulse [1, 5]. The genesis of this\nbasic TL model stems from the work of Bannister [5].\nA recent simplified extension of this work has been the\ndevelopment of the ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "impulse as intensity\nfactors. Due to the above factors, sRPE will be the prin-\ncipal focus of this commentary.\nA basic model of an athlete’s response to training can\nbe estimated from data collected as part of the TL\nmonitoring process [1, 5]. Specif"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_subjective_load_2018_p0002_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_a684727e89a8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "25ecc46f98f732a6", "text_span_hash": "25ecc46f98f732a6", "source_file": "safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "s\nbasic TL model stems from the work of Bannister [5].\nA recent simplified extension of this work has been the\ndevelopment of the acute to chronic workload ratio\n(ACWR) [12–14]. TL monitoring and in particular the\nACWR has been readily adopted (espec", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "methods of monitoring TL vary considerably depending\non the type of sport or activity [3]; however, TL models\nare commonly analyzed using training impulse, which is\nnormally a product of an intensity factor and volume/\nduration factor [2, 5].\nSubjective measures of TL, and in particular, sessional\nratings of perceived exertion (sRPE), are recommended\nas a primary measure of TL in systematic reviews of the\nliterature [6, 7]. Subjective measures may also be more\nsensitive and consistent than objective measures [8, 9],\nand sRPE has been reported as the most commonly\nassessed TL variable in most sports [10]. Besides sRPE,\nthere are other subjective methods of assessing an ath-\nlete’s response to training, e.g., visual analogue scales\nAlthough these measures will be highlighted later in\nthe commentary, they are not usually incorporated into\ncommon TL models using training impulse as intensity\nfactors. Due to the above factors, sRPE will be the prin-\ncipal focus of this commentary.\nA basic model of an athlete’s response to training can\nbe estimated from data collected as part of the TL\nmonitoring process [1, 5]. Specifically, the difference\nbetween “fitness” (positive) and “fatigue” (negative)\nfunctions can be quantified with internal or external TL\nvariables as training impulse [1, 5]. The genesis of this\nbasic TL model stems from the work of Bannister [5].\nA recent simplified extension of this work has been the\ndevelopment of the ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "s\nbasic TL model stems from the work of Bannister [5].\nA recent simplified extension of this work has been the\ndevelopment of the acute to chronic workload ratio\n(ACWR) [12–14]. TL monitoring and in particular the\nACWR has been readily adopted (espec"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_subjective_load_2018_p0003_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_a684727e89a8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5f9f2cfc62be2d81", "text_span_hash": "5f9f2cfc62be2d81", "source_file": "safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "skill sports (e.g., basketball, table tennis) require players to\nreact in an unpredictable and changing externally paced\nenvironment while closed skill sports are performed in an\nenvironment that is relatively predictable, consistent, and\ninternally", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "skill sports (e.g., basketball, table tennis) require players to\nreact in an unpredictable and changing externally paced\nenvironment while closed skill sports are performed in an\nenvironment that is relatively predictable, consistent, and\ninternally paced (e.g., shot put, swimming) [37]. Due to\nthese differences, open and closed skill sports place very\ndifferent mental demands on athletes, and these demands\ndevelop different mental qualities [38–42]. For instance,\nathletes in open skill sports may develop better visual at-\ntention, action execution, and decision-making skills com-\npared to closed skill sports athletes [40–42]. It is logical to\nsuggest that the mental fatigue/stress from sports demands\nmay moderate both TL-I and TL-P [21, 34]. Hypothetically,\nopen skill sports may have greater incidences of injury at\nthe same ACWRs and may possess a very different optimal\nTL-P relationship (i.e., require a longer taper to allow for\nextra mental load to dissipate) when compared to closed\nskill sports. This theorised relationship is detailed in Fig. 1.\nUpdates to Periodization Theory\nCoinciding with our increased knowledge of the impact\nnon-physical factors have on performance and injury, it\nis interesting to note theories underpinning training\nperiodization have recently been questioned [32, 43].\nModeling performance and injury is an associated ex-\ntension of periodization theory [43]. A major assump-\ntion and limitation related", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "skill sports (e.g., basketball, table tennis) require players to\nreact in an unpredictable and changing externally paced\nenvironment while closed skill sports are performed in an\nenvironment that is relatively predictable, consistent, and\ninternally"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_subjective_load_2018_p0003_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_a684727e89a8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5a300067a390aa8d", "text_span_hash": "5a300067a390aa8d", "source_file": "safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "e, consistent, and\ninternally paced (e.g., shot put, swimming) [37]. Due to\nthese differences, open and closed skill sports place very\ndifferent mental demands on athletes, and these demands\ndevelop different mental qualities [38–42]. For instance,\na", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "skill sports (e.g., basketball, table tennis) require players to\nreact in an unpredictable and changing externally paced\nenvironment while closed skill sports are performed in an\nenvironment that is relatively predictable, consistent, and\ninternally paced (e.g., shot put, swimming) [37]. Due to\nthese differences, open and closed skill sports place very\ndifferent mental demands on athletes, and these demands\ndevelop different mental qualities [38–42]. For instance,\nathletes in open skill sports may develop better visual at-\ntention, action execution, and decision-making skills com-\npared to closed skill sports athletes [40–42]. It is logical to\nsuggest that the mental fatigue/stress from sports demands\nmay moderate both TL-I and TL-P [21, 34]. Hypothetically,\nopen skill sports may have greater incidences of injury at\nthe same ACWRs and may possess a very different optimal\nTL-P relationship (i.e., require a longer taper to allow for\nextra mental load to dissipate) when compared to closed\nskill sports. This theorised relationship is detailed in Fig. 1.\nUpdates to Periodization Theory\nCoinciding with our increased knowledge of the impact\nnon-physical factors have on performance and injury, it\nis interesting to note theories underpinning training\nperiodization have recently been questioned [32, 43].\nModeling performance and injury is an associated ex-\ntension of periodization theory [43]. A major assump-\ntion and limitation related", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "e, consistent, and\ninternally paced (e.g., shot put, swimming) [37]. Due to\nthese differences, open and closed skill sports place very\ndifferent mental demands on athletes, and these demands\ndevelop different mental qualities [38–42]. For instance,\na"}
