ctors have on performance and injury, it\nis interesting to note theories underpinning training\nperiodization have recently been questioned [32, 43].\nModeling performance and injury is an associated ex-\ntension of periodization theory [43]. A major assump-\ntion and limitation related", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "mal\nTL-P relationship (i.e., require a longer taper to allow for\nextra mental load to dissipate) when compared to closed\nskill sports. This theorised relationship is detailed in Fig. 1.\nUpdates to Periodization Theory\nCoinciding with our increased kn"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_subjective_load_2018_p0003_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_a684727e89a8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "125e26fb693cf028", "text_span_hash": "125e26fb693cf028", "source_file": "safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "daptations and performance [26, 29, 33], nor does it\nreflect recent consensus definitions of TL [3], advances\nin the general adaptation syndrome model that con-\nsider non-mechanical training stress [43] and more\ncontemporary allostatic and cognitive", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "skill sports (e.g., basketball, table tennis) require players to\nreact in an unpredictable and changing externally paced\nenvironment while closed skill sports are performed in an\nenvironment that is relatively predictable, consistent, and\ninternally paced (e.g., shot put, swimming) [37]. Due to\nthese differences, open and closed skill sports place very\ndifferent mental demands on athletes, and these demands\ndevelop different mental qualities [38–42]. For instance,\nathletes in open skill sports may develop better visual at-\ntention, action execution, and decision-making skills com-\npared to closed skill sports athletes [40–42]. It is logical to\nsuggest that the mental fatigue/stress from sports demands\nmay moderate both TL-I and TL-P [21, 34]. Hypothetically,\nopen skill sports may have greater incidences of injury at\nthe same ACWRs and may possess a very different optimal\nTL-P relationship (i.e., require a longer taper to allow for\nextra mental load to dissipate) when compared to closed\nskill sports. This theorised relationship is detailed in Fig. 1.\nUpdates to Periodization Theory\nCoinciding with our increased knowledge of the impact\nnon-physical factors have on performance and injury, it\nis interesting to note theories underpinning training\nperiodization have recently been questioned [32, 43].\nModeling performance and injury is an associated ex-\ntension of periodization theory [43]. A major assump-\ntion and limitation related", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "daptations and performance [26, 29, 33], nor does it\nreflect recent consensus definitions of TL [3], advances\nin the general adaptation syndrome model that con-\nsider non-mechanical training stress [43] and more\ncontemporary allostatic and cognitive"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_subjective_load_2018_p0003_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_a684727e89a8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "82a9a161d52ccce6", "text_span_hash": "82a9a161d52ccce6", "source_file": "safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "rary allostatic and cognitive appraisal stress\ntheories [21, 32, 44]. These contemporary stress theories\nsuggest there is a complex and collaborative emotional,\nphysiological, immunological, and psychological response\ndriven by the brain’s perception", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "skill sports (e.g., basketball, table tennis) require players to\nreact in an unpredictable and changing externally paced\nenvironment while closed skill sports are performed in an\nenvironment that is relatively predictable, consistent, and\ninternally paced (e.g., shot put, swimming) [37]. Due to\nthese differences, open and closed skill sports place very\ndifferent mental demands on athletes, and these demands\ndevelop different mental qualities [38–42]. For instance,\nathletes in open skill sports may develop better visual at-\ntention, action execution, and decision-making skills com-\npared to closed skill sports athletes [40–42]. It is logical to\nsuggest that the mental fatigue/stress from sports demands\nmay moderate both TL-I and TL-P [21, 34]. Hypothetically,\nopen skill sports may have greater incidences of injury at\nthe same ACWRs and may possess a very different optimal\nTL-P relationship (i.e., require a longer taper to allow for\nextra mental load to dissipate) when compared to closed\nskill sports. This theorised relationship is detailed in Fig. 1.\nUpdates to Periodization Theory\nCoinciding with our increased knowledge of the impact\nnon-physical factors have on performance and injury, it\nis interesting to note theories underpinning training\nperiodization have recently been questioned [32, 43].\nModeling performance and injury is an associated ex-\ntension of periodization theory [43]. A major assump-\ntion and limitation related", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "rary allostatic and cognitive appraisal stress\ntheories [21, 32, 44]. These contemporary stress theories\nsuggest there is a complex and collaborative emotional,\nphysiological, immunological, and psychological response\ndriven by the brain’s perception"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_subjective_load_2018_p0003_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_a684727e89a8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "573e5cd27669d83b", "text_span_hash": "573e5cd27669d83b", "source_file": "safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "International Olympic Committee. CTL, chronic training load; ATL,\nacute training load; ACWR, acute to chronic workload ratio\nCoyne et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2018) 4:58", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "skill sports (e.g., basketball, table tennis) require players to\nreact in an unpredictable and changing externally paced\nenvironment while closed skill sports are performed in an\nenvironment that is relatively predictable, consistent, and\ninternally paced (e.g., shot put, swimming) [37]. Due to\nthese differences, open and closed skill sports place very\ndifferent mental demands on athletes, and these demands\ndevelop different mental qualities [38–42]. For instance,\nathletes in open skill sports may develop better visual at-\ntention, action execution, and decision-making skills com-\npared to closed skill sports athletes [40–42]. It is logical to\nsuggest that the mental fatigue/stress from sports demands\nmay moderate both TL-I and TL-P [21, 34]. Hypothetically,\nopen skill sports may have greater incidences of injury at\nthe same ACWRs and may possess a very different optimal\nTL-P relationship (i.e., require a longer taper to allow for\nextra mental load to dissipate) when compared to closed\nskill sports. This theorised relationship is detailed in Fig. 1.\nUpdates to Periodization Theory\nCoinciding with our increased knowledge of the impact\nnon-physical factors have on performance and injury, it\nis interesting to note theories underpinning training\nperiodization have recently been questioned [32, 43].\nModeling performance and injury is an associated ex-\ntension of periodization theory [43]. A major assump-\ntion and limitation related", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "International Olympic Committee. CTL, chronic training load; ATL,\nacute training load; ACWR, acute to chronic workload ratio\nCoyne et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2018) 4:58"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_subjective_load_2018_p0004_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_a684727e89a8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "390076ba2c94cb9b", "text_span_hash": "390076ba2c94cb9b", "source_file": "safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "tive\nmeasures, sRPE may be able to better account for the\nallostatic stress athlete experience in mixed training\nsessions (e.g., tactical, skill, strength, fitness) [45, 46]\nand the mental load (e.g., learning a new skill or tactical\nstrategy, compet", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "reflect both the allostatic stress and the cognitive ap-\npraisal of the stress as it theoretically encompasses both\nthe mechanical stress applied and also the (conscious)\nperception of that stress [32]. Compared to objective\nmeasures, sRPE may be able to better account for the\nallostatic stress athlete experience in mixed training\nsessions (e.g., tactical, skill, strength, fitness) [45, 46]\nand the mental load (e.g., learning a new skill or tactical\nstrategy, competing against an unfamiliar opponent)\nduring these sessions [47, 48]. However, it is currently\nunknown exactly how mental load affects sRPE, par-\nticularly in team-based open skill sports.\nAlthough subjective measures may have advantages\nas stand-alone measures, practitioners should not dis-\ncount established valid objective measures when moni-\ntoring TL. It is accepted that internal and external TL\nmeasures should be used or compared with multiple\nvariables to better understand TL-I and TL-P [31]. It is\nalso suggested that both subjective and objective in-\nternal TL measures should be viewed as interrelated\nbut different constructs [31]. For instance, previous\nstudies have reported ~ 50% unexplained variance be-\ntween objective heart rate (HR)-derived measures and\nsRPE measures [31]. This highlights that both objective\nand subjective TL measures are not interchangeable\nand can give very different information. As such, if sub-\njective measures provide a better illustr", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "tive\nmeasures, sRPE may be able to better account for the\nallostatic stress athlete experience in mixed training\nsessions (e.g., tactical, skill, strength, fitness) [45, 46]\nand the mental load (e.g., learning a new skill or tactical\nstrategy, compet"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_subjective_load_2018_p0004_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_a684727e89a8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8b960348e6f6a3b9", "text_span_hash": "8b960348e6f6a3b9", "source_file": "safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ed to be reconsidered for best prac-\ntice. Currently, it is common in sports science to com-\npare subjective perceptional measures (sRPE) to both\nobjective internal (e.g., HR) and external (e.g., GPS-de-\nrived running measures) TL measures for validi", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "reflect both the allostatic stress and the cognitive ap-\npraisal of the stress as it theoretically encompasses both\nthe mechanical stress applied and also the (conscious)\nperception of that stress [32]. Compared to objective\nmeasures, sRPE may be able to better account for the\nallostatic stress athlete experience in mixed training\nsessions (e.g., tactical, skill, strength, fitness) [45, 46]\nand the mental load (e.g., learning a new skill or tactical\nstrategy, competing against an unfamiliar opponent)\nduring these sessions [47, 48]. However, it is currently\nunknown exactly how mental load affects sRPE, par-\nticularly in team-based open skill sports.\nAlthough subjective measures may have advantages\nas stand-alone measures, practitioners should not dis-\ncount established valid objective measures when moni-\ntoring TL. It is accepted that internal and external TL\nmeasures should be used or compared with multiple\nvariables to better understand TL-I and TL-P [31]. It is\nalso suggested that both subjective and objective in-\nternal TL measures should be viewed as interrelated\nbut different constructs [31]. For instance, previous\nstudies have reported ~ 50% unexplained variance be-\ntween objective heart rate (HR)-derived measures and\nsRPE measures [31]. This highlights that both objective\nand subjective TL measures are not interchangeable\nand can give very different information. As such, if sub-\njective measures provide a better illustr", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ed to be reconsidered for best prac-\ntice. Currently, it is common in sports science to com-\npare subjective perceptional measures (sRPE) to both\nobjective internal (e.g., HR) and external (e.g., GPS-de-\nrived running measures) TL measures for validi"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_subjective_load_2018_p0004_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_a684727e89a8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8fc3f6ac66b0fd0e", "text_span_hash": "8fc3f6ac66b0fd0e", "source_file": "safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "sen on a case-by-case basis\ndepending on the nature of the sport (e.g., professional\nroad cycling may choose sRPE as a secondary measure\nbehind power output and HR response) and character-\nistics of training environment (e.g., logistics, budget). It", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "reflect both the allostatic stress and the cognitive ap-\npraisal of the stress as it theoretically encompasses both\nthe mechanical stress applied and also the (conscious)\nperception of that stress [32]. Compared to objective\nmeasures, sRPE may be able to better account for the\nallostatic stress athlete experience in mixed training\nsessions (e.g., tactical, skill, strength, fitness) [45, 46]\nand the mental load (e.g., learning a new skill or tactical\nstrategy, competing against an unfamiliar opponent)\nduring these sessions [47, 48]. However, it is currently\nunknown exactly how mental load affects sRPE, par-\nticularly in team-based open skill sports.\nAlthough subjective measures may have advantages\nas stand-alone measures, practitioners should not dis-\ncount established valid objective measures when moni-\ntoring TL. It is accepted that internal and external TL\nmeasures should be used or compared with multiple\nvariables to better understand TL-I and TL-P [31]. It is\nalso suggested that both subjective and objective in-\nternal TL measures should be viewed as interrelated\nbut different constructs [31]. For instance, previous\nstudies have reported ~ 50% unexplained variance be-\ntween objective heart rate (HR)-derived measures and\nsRPE measures [31]. This highlights that both objective\nand subjective TL measures are not interchangeable\nand can give very different information. As such, if sub-\njective measures provide a better illustr", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "sen on a case-by-case basis\ndepending on the nature of the sport (e.g., professional\nroad cycling may choose sRPE as a secondary measure\nbehind power output and HR response) and character-\nistics of training environment (e.g., logistics, budget). It"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_subjective_load_2018_p0004_c0022", "source_registry_id": "src_a684727e89a8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "06334fdbd7bfecfa", "text_span_hash": "06334fdbd7bfecfa", "source_file": "safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 22, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "r-\nformance purposes is not yet well established [52].\nSubjective Training Load Monitoring: Limitations\nand Possible Solutions\nLimitations of using sRPE to monitor TL and their pos-\nsible solutions should also be acknowledged [58]. The\nfirst potentia", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "reflect both the allostatic stress and the cognitive ap-\npraisal of the stress as it theoretically encompasses both\nthe mechanical stress applied and also the (conscious)\nperception of that stress [32]. Compared to objective\nmeasures, sRPE may be able to better account for the\nallostatic stress athlete experience in mixed training\nsessions (e.g., tactical, skill, strength, fitness) [45, 46]\nand the mental load (e.g., learning a new skill or tactical\nstrategy, competing against an unfamiliar opponent)\nduring these sessions [47, 48]. However, it is currently\nunknown exactly how mental load affects sRPE, par-\nticularly in team-based open skill sports.\nAlthough subjective measures may have advantages\nas stand-alone measures, practitioners should not dis-\ncount established valid objective measures when moni-\ntoring TL. It is accepted that internal and external TL\nmeasures should be used or compared with multiple\nvariables to better understand TL-I and TL-P [31]. It is\nalso suggested that both subjective and objective in-\nternal TL measures should be viewed as interrelated\nbut different constructs [31]. For instance, previous\nstudies have reported ~ 50% unexplained variance be-\ntween objective heart rate (HR)-derived measures and\nsRPE measures [31]. This highlights that both objective\nand subjective TL measures are not interchangeable\nand can give very different information. As such, if sub-\njective measures provide a better illustr", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "r-\nformance purposes is not yet well established [52].\nSubjective Training Load Monitoring: Limitations\nand Possible Solutions\nLimitations of using sRPE to monitor TL and their pos-\nsible solutions should also be acknowledged [58]. The\nfirst potentia"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_subjective_load_2018_p0005_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_a684727e89a8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5b317dd6eb64ed04", "text_span_hash": "5b317dd6eb64ed04", "source_file": "safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "will be used by practitioners is commonly reported. If\neducation around sRPE is not adequate, athletes may\nanswer dishonestly in an attempt to manipulate future\ntraining sessions or team selection. Using a global sRPE\nscore with the common 10-point c", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "will be used by practitioners is commonly reported. If\neducation around sRPE is not adequate, athletes may\nanswer dishonestly in an attempt to manipulate future\ntraining sessions or team selection. Using a global sRPE\nscore with the common 10-point category ratio scale\n(CR10) may also reduce the measure’s sensitivity to ac-\ncount for the range of biomechanical and physiological\nexertion demands across training [7] and fail to distin-\nguish between psychophysiological responses to train-\nrecommended that practitioners follow the fundamen-\ntal rules of psychometrics in administering sRPE or any\nsubjective measure [11]. To improve sensitivity, a\n100-point RPE category ratio scale (CR100) should be\nconsidered due to more verbal anchors and a finer\ngrading compared to the CR10 scale [59–61]. To exam-\nine different components of training stress, practi-\ntioners may also consider differential RPE. Differential\nRPE refines how athletes rate different components of\ntraining/performance and requires separate scores for\ncombinations of breathlessness (bRPE), leg muscle ex-\nertion (lRPE), upper body exertion (uRPE), and tech-\nnical/cognitive exertion (tRPE) and in some cases\nmatch exertion (mRPE) [47, 48, 62]. With these compo-\nnents, differential RPE seems to encompass perceptions\nof separate physiological (bRPE) and biomechanical\nload (lRPE) [63] while also accounting for mental load\n(tRPE). As an example, tRPE in isolation appears sens", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "will be used by practitioners is commonly reported. If\neducation around sRPE is not adequate, athletes may\nanswer dishonestly in an attempt to manipulate future\ntraining sessions or team selection. Using a global sRPE\nscore with the common 10-point c"}
