in some cases\nmatch exertion (mRPE) [47, 48, 62]. With these compo-\nnents, differential RPE seems to encompass perceptions\nof separate physiological (bRPE) and biomechanical\nload (lRPE) [63] while also accounting for mental load\n(tRPE). As an example, tRPE in isolation appears sens", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "RPE category ratio scale (CR100) should be\nconsidered due to more verbal anchors and a finer\ngrading compared to the CR10 scale [59–61]. To exam-\nine different components of training stress, practi-\ntioners may also consider differential RPE. Differ"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_subjective_load_2018_p0005_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_a684727e89a8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e223be556ed14a2c", "text_span_hash": "e223be556ed14a2c", "source_file": "safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "over the arbitrary length of acute and\nchronic periods and deciding what training components\nshould be included in the models [46, 65, 66]. Addition-\nally, there has also been criticism over appropriate cal-\nculation methods. Rolling averages (which", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "will be used by practitioners is commonly reported. If\neducation around sRPE is not adequate, athletes may\nanswer dishonestly in an attempt to manipulate future\ntraining sessions or team selection. Using a global sRPE\nscore with the common 10-point category ratio scale\n(CR10) may also reduce the measure’s sensitivity to ac-\ncount for the range of biomechanical and physiological\nexertion demands across training [7] and fail to distin-\nguish between psychophysiological responses to train-\nrecommended that practitioners follow the fundamen-\ntal rules of psychometrics in administering sRPE or any\nsubjective measure [11]. To improve sensitivity, a\n100-point RPE category ratio scale (CR100) should be\nconsidered due to more verbal anchors and a finer\ngrading compared to the CR10 scale [59–61]. To exam-\nine different components of training stress, practi-\ntioners may also consider differential RPE. Differential\nRPE refines how athletes rate different components of\ntraining/performance and requires separate scores for\ncombinations of breathlessness (bRPE), leg muscle ex-\nertion (lRPE), upper body exertion (uRPE), and tech-\nnical/cognitive exertion (tRPE) and in some cases\nmatch exertion (mRPE) [47, 48, 62]. With these compo-\nnents, differential RPE seems to encompass perceptions\nof separate physiological (bRPE) and biomechanical\nload (lRPE) [63] while also accounting for mental load\n(tRPE). As an example, tRPE in isolation appears sens", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "over the arbitrary length of acute and\nchronic periods and deciding what training components\nshould be included in the models [46, 65, 66]. Addition-\nally, there has also been criticism over appropriate cal-\nculation methods. Rolling averages (which"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_subjective_load_2018_p0005_c0017", "source_registry_id": "src_a684727e89a8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "620c49a0f01742ba", "text_span_hash": "620c49a0f01742ba", "source_file": "safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 17, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ue” decay rates being\nquestionable, the arbitrary 7- and 28-day acute and\nchronic period lengths commonly used to determine\nthe ACWR may not be appropriate for all sports or\nFig. 2 Potential measures to combine with differential RPE or sRPE in a mult", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "will be used by practitioners is commonly reported. If\neducation around sRPE is not adequate, athletes may\nanswer dishonestly in an attempt to manipulate future\ntraining sessions or team selection. Using a global sRPE\nscore with the common 10-point category ratio scale\n(CR10) may also reduce the measure’s sensitivity to ac-\ncount for the range of biomechanical and physiological\nexertion demands across training [7] and fail to distin-\nguish between psychophysiological responses to train-\nrecommended that practitioners follow the fundamen-\ntal rules of psychometrics in administering sRPE or any\nsubjective measure [11]. To improve sensitivity, a\n100-point RPE category ratio scale (CR100) should be\nconsidered due to more verbal anchors and a finer\ngrading compared to the CR10 scale [59–61]. To exam-\nine different components of training stress, practi-\ntioners may also consider differential RPE. Differential\nRPE refines how athletes rate different components of\ntraining/performance and requires separate scores for\ncombinations of breathlessness (bRPE), leg muscle ex-\nertion (lRPE), upper body exertion (uRPE), and tech-\nnical/cognitive exertion (tRPE) and in some cases\nmatch exertion (mRPE) [47, 48, 62]. With these compo-\nnents, differential RPE seems to encompass perceptions\nof separate physiological (bRPE) and biomechanical\nload (lRPE) [63] while also accounting for mental load\n(tRPE). As an example, tRPE in isolation appears sens", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ue” decay rates being\nquestionable, the arbitrary 7- and 28-day acute and\nchronic period lengths commonly used to determine\nthe ACWR may not be appropriate for all sports or\nFig. 2 Potential measures to combine with differential RPE or sRPE in a mult"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_subjective_load_2018_p0006_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_a684727e89a8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "6602065859680a7c", "text_span_hash": "6602065859680a7c", "source_file": "safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "individuals [2, 65]. Using different period lengths would\nseem relevant to athletes adapted to different micro-\nand mesocycle lengths (e.g., microcycles 3–10 days,\nmesocycles 2–6 weeks) or who are within uncommon\ntraining periods (e.g., competition p", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "individuals [2, 65]. Using different period lengths would\nseem relevant to athletes adapted to different micro-\nand mesocycle lengths (e.g., microcycles 3–10 days,\nmesocycles 2–6 weeks) or who are within uncommon\ntraining periods (e.g., competition periods or returning\nchronic time frames have resulted in varied relation-\nships with injury risk [2, 65]. For example, 3- or 6-day\nacute and 21-day chronic periods best explained injury\nrisk in one investigation involving Australian football\nplayers [65]. However, when accounting for the inter-\naction of absolute chronic TL levels with injury risk,\nanother investigation in the same sport found no im-\nproved predictive capacity above the common 7- and\n28-day periods compared with combinations in acute\nperiods of 7 and 14 days and chronic periods of 21, 28,\n35, 42, 49, and 56 days [2]. From this research, practi-\ntioners may be able to adjust the length of acute and\nchronic periods in the ACWR based on the lengths of\ntheir preferred training micro- and mesocycles [2].\nHowever, as mentioned, it is also worth considering\nacute and chronic periods may be both individual and\nidiosyncratic to different sports and may require cali-\nbration to an objective measure [5]. Further consider-\nation of which training components (e.g., technical,\nresistance, recovery) should be included in TL models\nfor association or prediction purposes is also warranted.\nA recent study on Australian football play", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "individuals [2, 65]. Using different period lengths would\nseem relevant to athletes adapted to different micro-\nand mesocycle lengths (e.g., microcycles 3–10 days,\nmesocycles 2–6 weeks) or who are within uncommon\ntraining periods (e.g., competition p"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_subjective_load_2018_p0006_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_a684727e89a8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f1cce01940927ea2", "text_span_hash": "f1cce01940927ea2", "source_file": "safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "predictive capacity above the common 7- and\n28-day periods compared with combinations in acute\nperiods of 7 and 14 days and chronic periods of 21, 28,\n35, 42, 49, and 56 days [2]. From this research, practi-\ntioners may be able to adjust the length", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "individuals [2, 65]. Using different period lengths would\nseem relevant to athletes adapted to different micro-\nand mesocycle lengths (e.g., microcycles 3–10 days,\nmesocycles 2–6 weeks) or who are within uncommon\ntraining periods (e.g., competition periods or returning\nchronic time frames have resulted in varied relation-\nships with injury risk [2, 65]. For example, 3- or 6-day\nacute and 21-day chronic periods best explained injury\nrisk in one investigation involving Australian football\nplayers [65]. However, when accounting for the inter-\naction of absolute chronic TL levels with injury risk,\nanother investigation in the same sport found no im-\nproved predictive capacity above the common 7- and\n28-day periods compared with combinations in acute\nperiods of 7 and 14 days and chronic periods of 21, 28,\n35, 42, 49, and 56 days [2]. From this research, practi-\ntioners may be able to adjust the length of acute and\nchronic periods in the ACWR based on the lengths of\ntheir preferred training micro- and mesocycles [2].\nHowever, as mentioned, it is also worth considering\nacute and chronic periods may be both individual and\nidiosyncratic to different sports and may require cali-\nbration to an objective measure [5]. Further consider-\nation of which training components (e.g., technical,\nresistance, recovery) should be included in TL models\nfor association or prediction purposes is also warranted.\nA recent study on Australian football play", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "predictive capacity above the common 7- and\n28-day periods compared with combinations in acute\nperiods of 7 and 14 days and chronic periods of 21, 28,\n35, 42, 49, and 56 days [2]. From this research, practi-\ntioners may be able to adjust the length"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_subjective_load_2018_p0006_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_a684727e89a8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "dae86a25177eeef5", "text_span_hash": "dae86a25177eeef5", "source_file": "safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "be able to adjust the length of acute and\nchronic periods in the ACWR based on the lengths of\ntheir preferred training micro- and mesocycles [2].\nHowever, as mentioned, it is also worth considering\nacute and chronic periods may be both individual an", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "individuals [2, 65]. Using different period lengths would\nseem relevant to athletes adapted to different micro-\nand mesocycle lengths (e.g., microcycles 3–10 days,\nmesocycles 2–6 weeks) or who are within uncommon\ntraining periods (e.g., competition periods or returning\nchronic time frames have resulted in varied relation-\nships with injury risk [2, 65]. For example, 3- or 6-day\nacute and 21-day chronic periods best explained injury\nrisk in one investigation involving Australian football\nplayers [65]. However, when accounting for the inter-\naction of absolute chronic TL levels with injury risk,\nanother investigation in the same sport found no im-\nproved predictive capacity above the common 7- and\n28-day periods compared with combinations in acute\nperiods of 7 and 14 days and chronic periods of 21, 28,\n35, 42, 49, and 56 days [2]. From this research, practi-\ntioners may be able to adjust the length of acute and\nchronic periods in the ACWR based on the lengths of\ntheir preferred training micro- and mesocycles [2].\nHowever, as mentioned, it is also worth considering\nacute and chronic periods may be both individual and\nidiosyncratic to different sports and may require cali-\nbration to an objective measure [5]. Further consider-\nation of which training components (e.g., technical,\nresistance, recovery) should be included in TL models\nfor association or prediction purposes is also warranted.\nA recent study on Australian football play", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "be able to adjust the length of acute and\nchronic periods in the ACWR based on the lengths of\ntheir preferred training micro- and mesocycles [2].\nHowever, as mentioned, it is also worth considering\nacute and chronic periods may be both individual an"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_subjective_load_2018_p0006_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_a684727e89a8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "dee7853ce23ea1e4", "text_span_hash": "dee7853ce23ea1e4", "source_file": "safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "iods may be both individual and\nidiosyncratic to different sports and may require cali-\nbration to an objective measure [5]. Further consider-\nation of which training components (e.g., technical,\nresistance, recovery) should be included in TL models", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "individuals [2, 65]. Using different period lengths would\nseem relevant to athletes adapted to different micro-\nand mesocycle lengths (e.g., microcycles 3–10 days,\nmesocycles 2–6 weeks) or who are within uncommon\ntraining periods (e.g., competition periods or returning\nchronic time frames have resulted in varied relation-\nships with injury risk [2, 65]. For example, 3- or 6-day\nacute and 21-day chronic periods best explained injury\nrisk in one investigation involving Australian football\nplayers [65]. However, when accounting for the inter-\naction of absolute chronic TL levels with injury risk,\nanother investigation in the same sport found no im-\nproved predictive capacity above the common 7- and\n28-day periods compared with combinations in acute\nperiods of 7 and 14 days and chronic periods of 21, 28,\n35, 42, 49, and 56 days [2]. From this research, practi-\ntioners may be able to adjust the length of acute and\nchronic periods in the ACWR based on the lengths of\ntheir preferred training micro- and mesocycles [2].\nHowever, as mentioned, it is also worth considering\nacute and chronic periods may be both individual and\nidiosyncratic to different sports and may require cali-\nbration to an objective measure [5]. Further consider-\nation of which training components (e.g., technical,\nresistance, recovery) should be included in TL models\nfor association or prediction purposes is also warranted.\nA recent study on Australian football play", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "iods may be both individual and\nidiosyncratic to different sports and may require cali-\nbration to an objective measure [5]. Further consider-\nation of which training components (e.g., technical,\nresistance, recovery) should be included in TL models"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_subjective_load_2018_p0006_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_a684727e89a8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "49398dc26323d44f", "text_span_hash": "49398dc26323d44f", "source_file": "safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ould be included in TL models\nfor association or prediction purposes is also warranted.\nA recent study on Australian football players suggested\na model using only skill training sRPE is better for pre-\ndicting performance than a model using total sRP", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "individuals [2, 65]. Using different period lengths would\nseem relevant to athletes adapted to different micro-\nand mesocycle lengths (e.g., microcycles 3–10 days,\nmesocycles 2–6 weeks) or who are within uncommon\ntraining periods (e.g., competition periods or returning\nchronic time frames have resulted in varied relation-\nships with injury risk [2, 65]. For example, 3- or 6-day\nacute and 21-day chronic periods best explained injury\nrisk in one investigation involving Australian football\nplayers [65]. However, when accounting for the inter-\naction of absolute chronic TL levels with injury risk,\nanother investigation in the same sport found no im-\nproved predictive capacity above the common 7- and\n28-day periods compared with combinations in acute\nperiods of 7 and 14 days and chronic periods of 21, 28,\n35, 42, 49, and 56 days [2]. From this research, practi-\ntioners may be able to adjust the length of acute and\nchronic periods in the ACWR based on the lengths of\ntheir preferred training micro- and mesocycles [2].\nHowever, as mentioned, it is also worth considering\nacute and chronic periods may be both individual and\nidiosyncratic to different sports and may require cali-\nbration to an objective measure [5]. Further consider-\nation of which training components (e.g., technical,\nresistance, recovery) should be included in TL models\nfor association or prediction purposes is also warranted.\nA recent study on Australian football play", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ould be included in TL models\nfor association or prediction purposes is also warranted.\nA recent study on Australian football players suggested\na model using only skill training sRPE is better for pre-\ndicting performance than a model using total sRP"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_subjective_load_2018_p0006_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_a684727e89a8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "60d3b6c9816772c5", "text_span_hash": "60d3b6c9816772c5", "source_file": "safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "for idiosyncratic acute and\nchronic periods and distinguishing between technical\nand non-technical training sRPE in TL models is not\n“Acute” and “Chronic” Decision-Making Tools\nAdditional limitations with TL monitoring using train-\ning impulse (e.g.,", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "individuals [2, 65]. Using different period lengths would\nseem relevant to athletes adapted to different micro-\nand mesocycle lengths (e.g., microcycles 3–10 days,\nmesocycles 2–6 weeks) or who are within uncommon\ntraining periods (e.g., competition periods or returning\nchronic time frames have resulted in varied relation-\nships with injury risk [2, 65]. For example, 3- or 6-day\nacute and 21-day chronic periods best explained injury\nrisk in one investigation involving Australian football\nplayers [65]. However, when accounting for the inter-\naction of absolute chronic TL levels with injury risk,\nanother investigation in the same sport found no im-\nproved predictive capacity above the common 7- and\n28-day periods compared with combinations in acute\nperiods of 7 and 14 days and chronic periods of 21, 28,\n35, 42, 49, and 56 days [2]. From this research, practi-\ntioners may be able to adjust the length of acute and\nchronic periods in the ACWR based on the lengths of\ntheir preferred training micro- and mesocycles [2].\nHowever, as mentioned, it is also worth considering\nacute and chronic periods may be both individual and\nidiosyncratic to different sports and may require cali-\nbration to an objective measure [5]. Further consider-\nation of which training components (e.g., technical,\nresistance, recovery) should be included in TL models\nfor association or prediction purposes is also warranted.\nA recent study on Australian football play", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "for idiosyncratic acute and\nchronic periods and distinguishing between technical\nand non-technical training sRPE in TL models is not\n“Acute” and “Chronic” Decision-Making Tools\nAdditional limitations with TL monitoring using train-\ning impulse (e.g.,"}
{"chunk_id": "safety_subjective_load_2018_p0006_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_a684727e89a8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2fde8446780d7733", "text_span_hash": "2fde8446780d7733", "source_file": "safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\safety_subjective_load_2018.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "sing train-\ning impulse (e.g., the ACWR) is that they are predom-\ninately “chronic” decision-making tools (e.g., how to\nstructure training from week to week). They also rely\nupon post-training analysis. It should be noted that the\nACWR can be calcula", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "individuals [2, 65]. Using different period lengths would\nseem relevant to athletes adapted to different micro-\nand mesocycle lengths (e.g., microcycles 3–10 days,\nmesocycles 2–6 weeks) or who are within uncommon\ntraining periods (e.g., competition periods or returning\nchronic time frames have resulted in varied relation-\nships with injury risk [2, 65]. For example, 3- or 6-day\nacute and 21-day chronic periods best explained injury\nrisk in one investigation involving Australian football\nplayers [65]. However, when accounting for the inter-\naction of absolute chronic TL levels with injury risk,\nanother investigation in the same sport found no im-\nproved predictive capacity above the common 7- and\n28-day periods compared with combinations in acute\nperiods of 7 and 14 days and chronic periods of 21, 28,\n35, 42, 49, and 56 days [2]. From this research, practi-\ntioners may be able to adjust the length of acute and\nchronic periods in the ACWR based on the lengths of\ntheir preferred training micro- and mesocycles [2].\nHowever, as mentioned, it is also worth considering\nacute and chronic periods may be both individual and\nidiosyncratic to different sports