{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0001_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0cddde6e8507e9e5", "text_span_hash": "0cddde6e8507e9e5", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Concurrent Resistance\nCompetitive Distance\nRunners: A Review and\nBrittney Barrie, PT, DPT\nSchool of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF Health Morsani College of Medicine, University of South\nFlorida, Tampa, Florida\nA B S T R A C T\nConcur", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Concurrent Resistance\nCompetitive Distance\nRunners: A Review and\nBrittney Barrie, PT, DPT\nSchool of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF Health Morsani College of Medicine, University of South\nFlorida, Tampa, Florida\nA B S T R A C T\nConcurrent training, deﬁned as resis-\ntance training sessions programmed\nalongside endurance training sessions\nduring the same cycle, is a tool that\nhighly trained runners can use to\naugment performance. A review of the\ncurrent literature explains how resis-\ntance training focused on increasing\nmaximal force development and power\noutput can improve running economy,\na key performance indicator for\nendurance running. This review ex-\nplains the current concepts in con-\ncurrent training for competitive\ndistance runners, discusses miscon-\nceptions related to concurrent training,\nand offers practical implementation\nexamples for coaches, athletes, and\nstrength and conditioning practitioners\nworking with this special population.\noncurrent resistance and endur-\nance training have many perfor-\nrunning athletes that are supported\nby the literature. Progressive resistance\ntraining is a tried and true method of\nincreasing maximal strength, rate of\nforce development, and muscular power\n(3,36). Each of these qualities are bene-\nﬁcial for the distance runner; however,\nmany running athletes have not imple-\nmented concurrent training into their\nprogramming due to a lack of knowledge\nor misconceptions. Con", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Concurrent Resistance\nCompetitive Distance\nRunners: A Review and\nBrittney Barrie, PT, DPT\nSchool of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF Health Morsani College of Medicine, University of South\nFlorida, Tampa, Florida\nA B S T R A C T\nConcur"}
