{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0002_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "d4eec4ae18e95441", "text_span_hash": "d4eec4ae18e95441", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ical error of measurement\nof running economy ranges from 1 to\nchange is approximately 2%. A 2%\nimprovement is believed to represent\na real improvement and is not due to\nvariability of the measure. It is likely\nthat the improvements in running\neconomy", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "to as running economy (RunE), maxi-\nmal oxygen uptake (V̇O2max), and lac-\ntate threshold (LT) (2,21). Research\ndemonstrates positive V̇O2max and LT\ntraining effects due to concurrent train-\ning in recreational and generally seden-\nis minimal research to support large\nor meaningful adaptations in either indi-\ncator with concurrent training in the\nhighly trained runner due to an insufﬁ-\ncient stimulus (2,13). Although minimal\npositive adaptations to V̇O2max or LT\nare directly suggested with concurrent\ntraining for elite runners, research dem-\nonstrates there are no negative effects\non these performance indicators in\ntrained endurance athletes (21). There-\nfore, most clinical trials experimenting\nrunners focus on RunE, the key perfor-\nmance indicator that may yield mean-\ningful positive change.\nIndeed, positive adaptations to RunE\nare consistently demonstrated in clini-\ncal trials with the prescription of con-\ncurrent resistance training (6,10,32,41).\nRunE is deﬁned as the metabolic cost\nor energy demand for a given velocity\nof submaximal running to cover a given\ndistance (5). Jung describes that a run-\nner requiring a lower V̇O2 at the given\nvelocity has better economy. Improved\nrunning economy allows a runner to\nrun faster over a given distance or to\nrun longer at a constant speed due to\nreduced consumption of oxygen (21).\nIn a recent systematic review including\nperforming concurrent high-load resis-\nmaximum [RM] or isometric 6", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ical error of measurement\nof running economy ranges from 1 to\nchange is approximately 2%. A 2%\nimprovement is believed to represent\na real improvement and is not due to\nvariability of the measure. It is likely\nthat the improvements in running\neconomy"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0002_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9844aaa1e92cc7f1", "text_span_hash": "9844aaa1e92cc7f1", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "mprovements in running\neconomy with concurrent training in\na meaningful change in running perfor-\nStoren et al. found improvements in\nRunE without changes in body weight\nor V̇O2max after 8 weeks of strength\ntraining in 17 well-trained distance\nmale a", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "to as running economy (RunE), maxi-\nmal oxygen uptake (V̇O2max), and lac-\ntate threshold (LT) (2,21). Research\ndemonstrates positive V̇O2max and LT\ntraining effects due to concurrent train-\ning in recreational and generally seden-\nis minimal research to support large\nor meaningful adaptations in either indi-\ncator with concurrent training in the\nhighly trained runner due to an insufﬁ-\ncient stimulus (2,13). Although minimal\npositive adaptations to V̇O2max or LT\nare directly suggested with concurrent\ntraining for elite runners, research dem-\nonstrates there are no negative effects\non these performance indicators in\ntrained endurance athletes (21). There-\nfore, most clinical trials experimenting\nrunners focus on RunE, the key perfor-\nmance indicator that may yield mean-\ningful positive change.\nIndeed, positive adaptations to RunE\nare consistently demonstrated in clini-\ncal trials with the prescription of con-\ncurrent resistance training (6,10,32,41).\nRunE is deﬁned as the metabolic cost\nor energy demand for a given velocity\nof submaximal running to cover a given\ndistance (5). Jung describes that a run-\nner requiring a lower V̇O2 at the given\nvelocity has better economy. Improved\nrunning economy allows a runner to\nrun faster over a given distance or to\nrun longer at a constant speed due to\nreduced consumption of oxygen (21).\nIn a recent systematic review including\nperforming concurrent high-load resis-\nmaximum [RM] or isometric 6", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "mprovements in running\neconomy with concurrent training in\na meaningful change in running perfor-\nStoren et al. found improvements in\nRunE without changes in body weight\nor V̇O2max after 8 weeks of strength\ntraining in 17 well-trained distance\nmale a"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0002_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "6c2bc138dff7958f", "text_span_hash": "6c2bc138dff7958f", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "7 well-trained distance\nmale and female runners. Running\ntraining was performed as usual, and\nthe intervention group added 4 sets\nof 4RM half-squats with 3-minute rest\nfor 3 sessions per week with progres-\nsive overload using free weights (41).\nRepet", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "to as running economy (RunE), maxi-\nmal oxygen uptake (V̇O2max), and lac-\ntate threshold (LT) (2,21). Research\ndemonstrates positive V̇O2max and LT\ntraining effects due to concurrent train-\ning in recreational and generally seden-\nis minimal research to support large\nor meaningful adaptations in either indi-\ncator with concurrent training in the\nhighly trained runner due to an insufﬁ-\ncient stimulus (2,13). Although minimal\npositive adaptations to V̇O2max or LT\nare directly suggested with concurrent\ntraining for elite runners, research dem-\nonstrates there are no negative effects\non these performance indicators in\ntrained endurance athletes (21). There-\nfore, most clinical trials experimenting\nrunners focus on RunE, the key perfor-\nmance indicator that may yield mean-\ningful positive change.\nIndeed, positive adaptations to RunE\nare consistently demonstrated in clini-\ncal trials with the prescription of con-\ncurrent resistance training (6,10,32,41).\nRunE is deﬁned as the metabolic cost\nor energy demand for a given velocity\nof submaximal running to cover a given\ndistance (5). Jung describes that a run-\nner requiring a lower V̇O2 at the given\nvelocity has better economy. Improved\nrunning economy allows a runner to\nrun faster over a given distance or to\nrun longer at a constant speed due to\nreduced consumption of oxygen (21).\nIn a recent systematic review including\nperforming concurrent high-load resis-\nmaximum [RM] or isometric 6", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "7 well-trained distance\nmale and female runners. Running\ntraining was performed as usual, and\nthe intervention group added 4 sets\nof 4RM half-squats with 3-minute rest\nfor 3 sessions per week with progres-\nsive overload using free weights (41).\nRepet"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0002_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "fee1f54eb12152b3", "text_span_hash": "fee1f54eb12152b3", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "using free weights (41).\nRepetition maximum is deﬁned as the\ngreatest amount of weight that can be\nlifted with proper technique for only\none repetition or for a speciﬁed num-\nber of repetitions (3). Another study,\nperformed by Sedano et al. on 18\nhig", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "to as running economy (RunE), maxi-\nmal oxygen uptake (V̇O2max), and lac-\ntate threshold (LT) (2,21). Research\ndemonstrates positive V̇O2max and LT\ntraining effects due to concurrent train-\ning in recreational and generally seden-\nis minimal research to support large\nor meaningful adaptations in either indi-\ncator with concurrent training in the\nhighly trained runner due to an insufﬁ-\ncient stimulus (2,13). Although minimal\npositive adaptations to V̇O2max or LT\nare directly suggested with concurrent\ntraining for elite runners, research dem-\nonstrates there are no negative effects\non these performance indicators in\ntrained endurance athletes (21). There-\nfore, most clinical trials experimenting\nrunners focus on RunE, the key perfor-\nmance indicator that may yield mean-\ningful positive change.\nIndeed, positive adaptations to RunE\nare consistently demonstrated in clini-\ncal trials with the prescription of con-\ncurrent resistance training (6,10,32,41).\nRunE is deﬁned as the metabolic cost\nor energy demand for a given velocity\nof submaximal running to cover a given\ndistance (5). Jung describes that a run-\nner requiring a lower V̇O2 at the given\nvelocity has better economy. Improved\nrunning economy allows a runner to\nrun faster over a given distance or to\nrun longer at a constant speed due to\nreduced consumption of oxygen (21).\nIn a recent systematic review including\nperforming concurrent high-load resis-\nmaximum [RM] or isometric 6", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "using free weights (41).\nRepetition maximum is deﬁned as the\ngreatest amount of weight that can be\nlifted with proper technique for only\none repetition or for a speciﬁed num-\nber of repetitions (3). Another study,\nperformed by Sedano et al. on 18\nhig"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0002_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ba312cc202d8075d", "text_span_hash": "ba312cc202d8075d", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "med by Sedano et al. on 18\nhighly trained male runners, explored\nthe effects of RunE after 12 weeks of\nstrength training at high loads (3 sets\nof 7 reps at 70% 1RM) versus resistance\ntraining at low loads (3 sets of 20 reps\nat 40% 1RM) and very low l", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "to as running economy (RunE), maxi-\nmal oxygen uptake (V̇O2max), and lac-\ntate threshold (LT) (2,21). Research\ndemonstrates positive V̇O2max and LT\ntraining effects due to concurrent train-\ning in recreational and generally seden-\nis minimal research to support large\nor meaningful adaptations in either indi-\ncator with concurrent training in the\nhighly trained runner due to an insufﬁ-\ncient stimulus (2,13). Although minimal\npositive adaptations to V̇O2max or LT\nare directly suggested with concurrent\ntraining for elite runners, research dem-\nonstrates there are no negative effects\non these performance indicators in\ntrained endurance athletes (21). There-\nfore, most clinical trials experimenting\nrunners focus on RunE, the key perfor-\nmance indicator that may yield mean-\ningful positive change.\nIndeed, positive adaptations to RunE\nare consistently demonstrated in clini-\ncal trials with the prescription of con-\ncurrent resistance training (6,10,32,41).\nRunE is deﬁned as the metabolic cost\nor energy demand for a given velocity\nof submaximal running to cover a given\ndistance (5). Jung describes that a run-\nner requiring a lower V̇O2 at the given\nvelocity has better economy. Improved\nrunning economy allows a runner to\nrun faster over a given distance or to\nrun longer at a constant speed due to\nreduced consumption of oxygen (21).\nIn a recent systematic review including\nperforming concurrent high-load resis-\nmaximum [RM] or isometric 6", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "med by Sedano et al. on 18\nhighly trained male runners, explored\nthe effects of RunE after 12 weeks of\nstrength training at high loads (3 sets\nof 7 reps at 70% 1RM) versus resistance\ntraining at low loads (3 sets of 20 reps\nat 40% 1RM) and very low l"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0002_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0c7c15c638293406", "text_span_hash": "0c7c15c638293406", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "eps\nat 40% 1RM) and very low loads (3 sets\nof 25 reps of elastic band exercises).\nThis author found consistently greater\nimprovements in RunE, peak velocity,\nand rating of perceived exertion in the\nhigh-load resistance training group and\nthe low-load", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "to as running economy (RunE), maxi-\nmal oxygen uptake (V̇O2max), and lac-\ntate threshold (LT) (2,21). Research\ndemonstrates positive V̇O2max and LT\ntraining effects due to concurrent train-\ning in recreational and generally seden-\nis minimal research to support large\nor meaningful adaptations in either indi-\ncator with concurrent training in the\nhighly trained runner due to an insufﬁ-\ncient stimulus (2,13). Although minimal\npositive adaptations to V̇O2max or LT\nare directly suggested with concurrent\ntraining for elite runners, research dem-\nonstrates there are no negative effects\non these performance indicators in\ntrained endurance athletes (21). There-\nfore, most clinical trials experimenting\nrunners focus on RunE, the key perfor-\nmance indicator that may yield mean-\ningful positive change.\nIndeed, positive adaptations to RunE\nare consistently demonstrated in clini-\ncal trials with the prescription of con-\ncurrent resistance training (6,10,32,41).\nRunE is deﬁned as the metabolic cost\nor energy demand for a given velocity\nof submaximal running to cover a given\ndistance (5). Jung describes that a run-\nner requiring a lower V̇O2 at the given\nvelocity has better economy. Improved\nrunning economy allows a runner to\nrun faster over a given distance or to\nrun longer at a constant speed due to\nreduced consumption of oxygen (21).\nIn a recent systematic review including\nperforming concurrent high-load resis-\nmaximum [RM] or isometric 6", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "eps\nat 40% 1RM) and very low loads (3 sets\nof 25 reps of elastic band exercises).\nThis author found consistently greater\nimprovements in RunE, peak velocity,\nand rating of perceived exertion in the\nhigh-load resistance training group and\nthe low-load"}
