 to cover a given\ndistance (5). Jung describes that a run-\nner requiring a lower V̇O2 at the given\nvelocity has better economy. Improved\nrunning economy allows a runner to\nrun faster over a given distance or to\nrun longer at a constant speed due to\nreduced consumption of oxygen (21).\nIn a recent systematic review including\nperforming concurrent high-load resis-\nmaximum [RM] or isometric 6", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "nd low-repetition resistance\nstrength training and power training, to\ninduce improvements in RunE. Con-\ntrary to outdated clinical practice per-\nforming muscular endurance resistance\ntraining (weight training featuring low-\nload, high-repetition, and"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0002_c0019", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "966e06f0335dc264", "text_span_hash": "966e06f0335dc264", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 19, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ow-\nload, high-repetition, and short-rest in-\ntervals), strength and power resistance\ntraining result in greater beneﬁt to the\nStrength training, traditionally deﬁned\nas resistance training greater than or\nequal to 85% 1RM load, 2–6 repetitions,\nwith", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "to as running economy (RunE), maxi-\nmal oxygen uptake (V̇O2max), and lac-\ntate threshold (LT) (2,21). Research\ndemonstrates positive V̇O2max and LT\ntraining effects due to concurrent train-\ning in recreational and generally seden-\nis minimal research to support large\nor meaningful adaptations in either indi-\ncator with concurrent training in the\nhighly trained runner due to an insufﬁ-\ncient stimulus (2,13). Although minimal\npositive adaptations to V̇O2max or LT\nare directly suggested with concurrent\ntraining for elite runners, research dem-\nonstrates there are no negative effects\non these performance indicators in\ntrained endurance athletes (21). There-\nfore, most clinical trials experimenting\nrunners focus on RunE, the key perfor-\nmance indicator that may yield mean-\ningful positive change.\nIndeed, positive adaptations to RunE\nare consistently demonstrated in clini-\ncal trials with the prescription of con-\ncurrent resistance training (6,10,32,41).\nRunE is deﬁned as the metabolic cost\nor energy demand for a given velocity\nof submaximal running to cover a given\ndistance (5). Jung describes that a run-\nner requiring a lower V̇O2 at the given\nvelocity has better economy. Improved\nrunning economy allows a runner to\nrun faster over a given distance or to\nrun longer at a constant speed due to\nreduced consumption of oxygen (21).\nIn a recent systematic review including\nperforming concurrent high-load resis-\nmaximum [RM] or isometric 6", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ow-\nload, high-repetition, and short-rest in-\ntervals), strength and power resistance\ntraining result in greater beneﬁt to the\nStrength training, traditionally deﬁned\nas resistance training greater than or\nequal to 85% 1RM load, 2–6 repetitions,\nwith"}
