{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0002_c0026", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b7740ef6d8c89ea9", "text_span_hash": "b7740ef6d8c89ea9", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 26, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "of\nexercise prescription in one training\nConcurrent Training Enhances Economy in Runners\nVOLUME 42 | NUMBER 1 | FEBRUARY 2020\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditioning Association. Unauthorized reproduction of this article is prohibited.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "to as running economy (RunE), maxi-\nmal oxygen uptake (V̇O2max), and lac-\ntate threshold (LT) (2,21). Research\ndemonstrates positive V̇O2max and LT\ntraining effects due to concurrent train-\ning in recreational and generally seden-\nis minimal research to support large\nor meaningful adaptations in either indi-\ncator with concurrent training in the\nhighly trained runner due to an insufﬁ-\ncient stimulus (2,13). Although minimal\npositive adaptations to V̇O2max or LT\nare directly suggested with concurrent\ntraining for elite runners, research dem-\nonstrates there are no negative effects\non these performance indicators in\ntrained endurance athletes (21). There-\nfore, most clinical trials experimenting\nrunners focus on RunE, the key perfor-\nmance indicator that may yield mean-\ningful positive change.\nIndeed, positive adaptations to RunE\nare consistently demonstrated in clini-\ncal trials with the prescription of con-\ncurrent resistance training (6,10,32,41).\nRunE is deﬁned as the metabolic cost\nor energy demand for a given velocity\nof submaximal running to cover a given\ndistance (5). Jung describes that a run-\nner requiring a lower V̇O2 at the given\nvelocity has better economy. Improved\nrunning economy allows a runner to\nrun faster over a given distance or to\nrun longer at a constant speed due to\nreduced consumption of oxygen (21).\nIn a recent systematic review including\nperforming concurrent high-load resis-\nmaximum [RM] or isometric 6", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "of\nexercise prescription in one training\nConcurrent Training Enhances Economy in Runners\nVOLUME 42 | NUMBER 1 | FEBRUARY 2020\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditioning Association. Unauthorized reproduction of this article is prohibited."}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0003_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7eb73b8bf070ad39", "text_span_hash": "7eb73b8bf070ad39", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "programming option for athletes. Li\net al. (26) investigates complex resis-\ntance training by alternating heavy\na given concurrent resistance training\nsession and compared results with\na heavy resistance training group and\na strength-endurance traini", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "programming option for athletes. Li\net al. (26) investigates complex resis-\ntance training by alternating heavy\na given concurrent resistance training\nsession and compared results with\na heavy resistance training group and\na strength-endurance training group.\nAll runners in each group performed\nresistance training 3 times per week\nand endurance running sessions 9 times\nper week. The complex training group\nbetween multi-joint, high-load closed-\nchain exercise (80–85% 1RM, 3 sets of\n5–6 repetitions of back squat, Bulgarian\nsquat, and Romanian deadlift) and\nmedium-to-high load plyometric train-\ning (3 sets of 5–6 repetitions of drop\njump from 40-cm box, single-leg hop,\nand a double-leg 50-cm hurdle hop)\nwith an intracomplex rest interval of\n4 minutes. The heavy resistance training\ngroup used the same resistance exer-\ncises and intensity, excluded the plyo-\nmetrics, and added 2 sets of each\nresistance exercise to balance overall\nvolume load. The strength-endurance\ngroup used the same resistance training\nexercises with a decrease in intensity to\n40% 1RM, an increase in repetitions to\n20–30, excluded the plyometrics, and\nincreased the sets by 2 to balance overall\nvolume load. Greatest improvements in\nRunE, strength and power output, plyo-\nmetric reactive strength, and timed run-\nning performance were found in the\ncomplex training group, followed by\nthe heavy resistance training group (26).\nIt should be noted that the interference\nef", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "programming option for athletes. Li\net al. (26) investigates complex resis-\ntance training by alternating heavy\na given concurrent resistance training\nsession and compared results with\na heavy resistance training group and\na strength-endurance traini"}
