0% 1RM, an increase in repetitions to\n20–30, excluded the plyometrics, and\nincreased the sets by 2 to balance overall\nvolume load. Greatest improvements in\nRunE, strength and power output, plyo-\nmetric reactive strength, and timed run-\nning performance were found in the\ncomplex training group, followed by\nthe heavy resistance training group (26).\nIt should be noted that the interference\nef", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "rammed for the\ncompetitive distance runner (between 6\nand 9 running sessions per week depend-\ning on the periodization phase), there\nmay be insufﬁcient intersession recovery\ntime between running sessions and resis-\ntance training sessions to fully re"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0003_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1468cb9be539dcac", "text_span_hash": "1468cb9be539dcac", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "training sessions to fully realize the\nanabolic stimulus from resistance train-\ning. This acute interference effect may be\navoided with appropriate intersession\nrecovery programming that will be dis-\ncussed later in this review. Because of the\nlack", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "programming option for athletes. Li\net al. (26) investigates complex resis-\ntance training by alternating heavy\na given concurrent resistance training\nsession and compared results with\na heavy resistance training group and\na strength-endurance training group.\nAll runners in each group performed\nresistance training 3 times per week\nand endurance running sessions 9 times\nper week. The complex training group\nbetween multi-joint, high-load closed-\nchain exercise (80–85% 1RM, 3 sets of\n5–6 repetitions of back squat, Bulgarian\nsquat, and Romanian deadlift) and\nmedium-to-high load plyometric train-\ning (3 sets of 5–6 repetitions of drop\njump from 40-cm box, single-leg hop,\nand a double-leg 50-cm hurdle hop)\nwith an intracomplex rest interval of\n4 minutes. The heavy resistance training\ngroup used the same resistance exer-\ncises and intensity, excluded the plyo-\nmetrics, and added 2 sets of each\nresistance exercise to balance overall\nvolume load. The strength-endurance\ngroup used the same resistance training\nexercises with a decrease in intensity to\n40% 1RM, an increase in repetitions to\n20–30, excluded the plyometrics, and\nincreased the sets by 2 to balance overall\nvolume load. Greatest improvements in\nRunE, strength and power output, plyo-\nmetric reactive strength, and timed run-\nning performance were found in the\ncomplex training group, followed by\nthe heavy resistance training group (26).\nIt should be noted that the interference\nef", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "training sessions to fully realize the\nanabolic stimulus from resistance train-\ning. This acute interference effect may be\navoided with appropriate intersession\nrecovery programming that will be dis-\ncussed later in this review. Because of the\nlack"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0003_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "47f9d514c70ccb98", "text_span_hash": "47f9d514c70ccb98", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "s review. Because of the\nlack of homogeneity of study protocols\nand the lack of protocols for longer peri-\nods, there are no scientiﬁc conclusions\nabout the mechanism of the chronic\ninterference effect of concurrent training.\nAnecdotal misconceptions", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "programming option for athletes. Li\net al. (26) investigates complex resis-\ntance training by alternating heavy\na given concurrent resistance training\nsession and compared results with\na heavy resistance training group and\na strength-endurance training group.\nAll runners in each group performed\nresistance training 3 times per week\nand endurance running sessions 9 times\nper week. The complex training group\nbetween multi-joint, high-load closed-\nchain exercise (80–85% 1RM, 3 sets of\n5–6 repetitions of back squat, Bulgarian\nsquat, and Romanian deadlift) and\nmedium-to-high load plyometric train-\ning (3 sets of 5–6 repetitions of drop\njump from 40-cm box, single-leg hop,\nand a double-leg 50-cm hurdle hop)\nwith an intracomplex rest interval of\n4 minutes. The heavy resistance training\ngroup used the same resistance exer-\ncises and intensity, excluded the plyo-\nmetrics, and added 2 sets of each\nresistance exercise to balance overall\nvolume load. The strength-endurance\ngroup used the same resistance training\nexercises with a decrease in intensity to\n40% 1RM, an increase in repetitions to\n20–30, excluded the plyometrics, and\nincreased the sets by 2 to balance overall\nvolume load. Greatest improvements in\nRunE, strength and power output, plyo-\nmetric reactive strength, and timed run-\nning performance were found in the\ncomplex training group, followed by\nthe heavy resistance training group (26).\nIt should be noted that the interference\nef", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "s review. Because of the\nlack of homogeneity of study protocols\nand the lack of protocols for longer peri-\nods, there are no scientiﬁc conclusions\nabout the mechanism of the chronic\ninterference effect of concurrent training.\nAnecdotal misconceptions"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0003_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e46787d49854142f", "text_span_hash": "e46787d49854142f", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ning.\nAnecdotal misconceptions of concurrent\ntraining that are not supported in the\nliterature often deter runners from step-\nping foot in the gym. The belief that\nnegative adaptations in body composi-\ntion will result from concurrent training\nis a c", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "programming option for athletes. Li\net al. (26) investigates complex resis-\ntance training by alternating heavy\na given concurrent resistance training\nsession and compared results with\na heavy resistance training group and\na strength-endurance training group.\nAll runners in each group performed\nresistance training 3 times per week\nand endurance running sessions 9 times\nper week. The complex training group\nbetween multi-joint, high-load closed-\nchain exercise (80–85% 1RM, 3 sets of\n5–6 repetitions of back squat, Bulgarian\nsquat, and Romanian deadlift) and\nmedium-to-high load plyometric train-\ning (3 sets of 5–6 repetitions of drop\njump from 40-cm box, single-leg hop,\nand a double-leg 50-cm hurdle hop)\nwith an intracomplex rest interval of\n4 minutes. The heavy resistance training\ngroup used the same resistance exer-\ncises and intensity, excluded the plyo-\nmetrics, and added 2 sets of each\nresistance exercise to balance overall\nvolume load. The strength-endurance\ngroup used the same resistance training\nexercises with a decrease in intensity to\n40% 1RM, an increase in repetitions to\n20–30, excluded the plyometrics, and\nincreased the sets by 2 to balance overall\nvolume load. Greatest improvements in\nRunE, strength and power output, plyo-\nmetric reactive strength, and timed run-\nning performance were found in the\ncomplex training group, followed by\nthe heavy resistance training group (26).\nIt should be noted that the interference\nef", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ning.\nAnecdotal misconceptions of concurrent\ntraining that are not supported in the\nliterature often deter runners from step-\nping foot in the gym. The belief that\nnegative adaptations in body composi-\ntion will result from concurrent training\nis a c"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0003_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "6718780690f17d53", "text_span_hash": "6718780690f17d53", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "rom concurrent training\nis a common misunderstanding that is\nnot supported by the literature at large.\nRecommended exercise selection for competitive distance runners to induce adaptations in running economy (4,10,36)\nDeadlift and Romanian deadlift v", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "programming option for athletes. Li\net al. (26) investigates complex resis-\ntance training by alternating heavy\na given concurrent resistance training\nsession and compared results with\na heavy resistance training group and\na strength-endurance training group.\nAll runners in each group performed\nresistance training 3 times per week\nand endurance running sessions 9 times\nper week. The complex training group\nbetween multi-joint, high-load closed-\nchain exercise (80–85% 1RM, 3 sets of\n5–6 repetitions of back squat, Bulgarian\nsquat, and Romanian deadlift) and\nmedium-to-high load plyometric train-\ning (3 sets of 5–6 repetitions of drop\njump from 40-cm box, single-leg hop,\nand a double-leg 50-cm hurdle hop)\nwith an intracomplex rest interval of\n4 minutes. The heavy resistance training\ngroup used the same resistance exer-\ncises and intensity, excluded the plyo-\nmetrics, and added 2 sets of each\nresistance exercise to balance overall\nvolume load. The strength-endurance\ngroup used the same resistance training\nexercises with a decrease in intensity to\n40% 1RM, an increase in repetitions to\n20–30, excluded the plyometrics, and\nincreased the sets by 2 to balance overall\nvolume load. Greatest improvements in\nRunE, strength and power output, plyo-\nmetric reactive strength, and timed run-\nning performance were found in the\ncomplex training group, followed by\nthe heavy resistance training group (26).\nIt should be noted that the interference\nef", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "rom concurrent training\nis a common misunderstanding that is\nnot supported by the literature at large.\nRecommended exercise selection for competitive distance runners to induce adaptations in running economy (4,10,36)\nDeadlift and Romanian deadlift v"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0004_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "69b8f0867681ba49", "text_span_hash": "69b8f0867681ba49", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "changes, and limb girth measurements\ndo not change signiﬁcantly in male\nand female distance runners perform-\ning concurrent training (10). The incor-\nrect belief that resistance training will\nincrease muscle mass and lead to\na slower race pace is bas", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "changes, and limb girth measurements\ndo not change signiﬁcantly in male\nand female distance runners perform-\ning concurrent training (10). The incor-\nrect belief that resistance training will\nincrease muscle mass and lead to\na slower race pace is based on unsub-\nstantiated claims and anecdotal tradi-\ntion. An observational study of 341\nmarathoners and ultramarathoners in\n2015 examined mean training distance,\nmean training pace, mean race pace,\nfrom a large cohort of marathoners\nand 100-km ultramarathoners from 3\nfemale subject included in total subject\ncount) (42). No associations between\nbody fat and race pace were found in\nrunners if their body fat measured\nbelow 15%, indicating that body com-\nposition for distance runners compet-\ning in 42- to 100-km races seem to be\nof less importance if the runners have\na relatively low level of fat mass. This\nstudy suggests that it is advantageous\nto be a runner with a high proportion\nof lean muscle mass as compared to\nbody fat (42). Logically, this trend\nmay be extrapolated to female runners\nas well; however, the speciﬁc advanta-\ngeous body composition cutoff num-\nbers for female runners are not clear\nat this time. In addition, a review of\nRunE written from a muscle energetic\nperspective provided similar conclu-\nsions, indicating that runners with\na high proportion of skeletal muscle\nmass and low fat mass may have an\nadvantage in reducing the absolute\nenergy cost of running (17). Using\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "changes, and limb girth measurements\ndo not change signiﬁcantly in male\nand female distance runners perform-\ning concurrent training (10). The incor-\nrect belief that resistance training will\nincrease muscle mass and lead to\na slower race pace is bas"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0004_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7fdff3d82b4e1181", "text_span_hash": "7fdff3d82b4e1181", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "d to\na slower race pace is based on unsub-\nstantiated claims and anecdotal tradi-\ntion. An observational study of 341\nmarathoners and ultramarathoners in\n2015 examined mean training distance,\nmean training pace, mean race pace,\nfrom a large cohort of", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "changes, and limb girth measurements\ndo not change signiﬁcantly in male\nand female distance runners perform-\ning concurrent training (10). The incor-\nrect belief that resistance training will\nincrease muscle mass and lead to\na slower race pace is based on unsub-\nstantiated claims and anecdotal tradi-\ntion. An observational study of 341\nmarathoners and ultramarathoners in\n2015 examined mean training distance,\nmean training pace, mean race pace,\nfrom a large cohort of marathoners\nand 100-km ultramarathoners from 3\nfemale subject included in total subject\ncount) (42). No associations between\nbody fat and race pace were found in\nrunners if their body fat measured\nbelow 15%, indicating that body com-\nposition for distance runners compet-\ning in 42- to 100-km races seem to be\nof less importance if the runners have\na relatively low level of fat mass. This\nstudy suggests that it is advantageous\nto be a runner with a high proportion\nof lean muscle mass as compared to\nbody fat (42). Logically, this trend\nmay be extrapolated to female runners\nas well; however, the speciﬁc advanta-\ngeous body composition cutoff num-\nbers for female runners are not clear\nat this time. In addition, a review of\nRunE written from a muscle energetic\nperspective provided similar conclu-\nsions, indicating that runners with\na high proportion of skeletal muscle\nmass and low fat mass may have an\nadvantage in reducing the absolute\nenergy cost of running (17). Using\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "d to\na slower race pace is based on unsub-\nstantiated claims and anecdotal tradi-\ntion. An observational study of 341\nmarathoners and ultramarathoners in\n2015 examined mean training distance,\nmean training pace, mean race pace,\nfrom a large cohort of"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0004_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "94c5be9b6a023f80", "text_span_hash": "94c5be9b6a023f80", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "e pace,\nfrom a large cohort of marathoners\nand 100-km ultramarathoners from 3\nfemale subject included in total subject\ncount) (42). No associations between\nbody fat and race pace were found in\nrunners if their body fat measured\nbelow 15%, indicating", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "changes, and limb girth measurements\ndo not change signiﬁcantly in male\nand female distance runners perform-\ning concurrent training (10). The incor-\nrect belief that resistance training will\nincrease muscle mass and lead to\na slower race pace is based on unsub-\nstantiated claims and anecdotal tradi-\ntion. An observational study of 341\nmarathoners and ultramarathoners in\n2015 examined mean training distance,\nmean training pace, mean race pace,\nfrom a large cohort of marathoners\nand 100-km ultramarathoners from 3\nfemale subject included in total subject\ncount) (42). No associations between\nbody fat and race pace were found in\nrunners if their body fat measured\nbelow 15%, indicating that body com-\nposition for distance runners compet-\ning in 42- to 100-km races seem to be\nof less importance if the runners have\na relatively low level of fat mass. This\nstudy suggests that it is advantageous\nto be a runner with a high proportion\nof lean muscle mass as compared to\nbody fat (42). Logically, this trend\nmay be extrapolated to female runners\nas well; however, the speciﬁc advanta-\ngeous body composition cutoff num-\nbers for female runners are not clear\nat this time. In addition, a review of\nRunE written from a muscle energetic\nperspective provided similar conclu-\nsions, indicating that runners with\na high proportion of skeletal muscle\nmass and low fat mass may have an\nadvantage in reducing the absolute\nenergy cost of running (17). Using\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "e pace,\nfrom a large cohort of marathoners\nand 100-km ultramarathoners from 3\nfemale subject included in total subject\ncount) (42). No associations between\nbody fat and race pace were found in\nrunners if their body fat measured\nbelow 15%, indicating"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0004_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "bce64b57ac072b80", "text_span_hash": "bce64b57ac072b80", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "easured\nbelow 15%, indicating that body com-\nposition for distance runners compet-\ning in 42- to 100-km races seem to be\nof less importance if the runners have\na relatively low level of fat mass. This\nstudy suggests that it is advantageous\nto be a ru", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "changes, and limb girth measurements\ndo not change signiﬁcantly in male\nand female distance runners perform-\ning concurrent training (10). The incor-\nrect belief that resistance training will\nincrease muscle mass and lead to\na slower race pace is based on unsub-\nstantiated claims and anecdotal tradi-\ntion. An observational study of 341\nmarathoners and ultramarathoners in\n2015 examined mean training distance,\nmean training pace, mean race pace,\nfrom a large cohort of marathoners\nand 100-km ultramarathoners from 3\nfemale subject included in total subject\ncount) (42). No associations between\nbody fat and race pace were found in\nrunners if their body fat measured\nbelow 15%, indicating that body com-\nposition for distance runners compet-\ning in 42- to 100-km races seem to be\nof less importance if the runners have\na relatively low level of fat mass. This\nstudy suggests that it is advantageous\nto be a runner with a high proportion\nof lean muscle mass as compared to\nbody fat (42). Logically, this trend\nmay be extrapolated to female runners\nas well; however, the speciﬁc advanta-\ngeous body composition cutoff num-\nbers for female runners are not clear\nat this time. In addition, a review of\nRunE written from a muscle energetic\nperspective provided similar conclu-\nsions, indicating that runners with\na high proportion of skeletal muscle\nmass and low fat mass may have an\nadvantage in reducing the absolute\nenergy cost of running (17). Using\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "easured\nbelow 15%, indicating that body com-\nposition for distance runners compet-\ning in 42- to 100-km races seem to be\nof less importance if the runners have\na relatively low level of fat mass. This\nstudy suggests that it is advantageous\nto be a ru"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0004_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e1c833c51a392cbf", "text_span_hash": "e1c833c51a392cbf", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "it is advantageous\nto be a runner with a high proportion\nof lean muscle mass as compared to\nbody fat (42). Logically, this trend\nmay be extrapolated to female runners\nas well; however, the speciﬁc advanta-\ngeous body composition cutoff num-\nbers for", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "changes, and limb girth measurements\ndo not change signiﬁcantly in male\nand female distance runners perform-\ning concurrent training (10). The incor-\nrect belief that resistance training will\nincrease muscle mass and lead to\na slower race pace is based on unsub-\nstantiated claims and anecdotal tradi-\ntion. An observational study of 341\nmarathoners and ultramarathoners in\n2015 examined mean training distance,\nmean training pace, mean race pace,\nfrom a large cohort of marathoners\nand 100-km ultramarathoners from 3\nfemale subject included in total subject\ncount) (42). No associations between\nbody fat and race pace were found in\nrunners if their body fat measured\nbelow 15%, indicating that body com-\nposition for distance runners compet-\ning in 42- to 100-km races seem to be\nof less importance if the runners have\na relatively low level of fat mass. This\nstudy suggests that it is advantageous\nto be a runner with a high proportion\nof lean muscle mass as compared to\nbody fat (42). Logically, this trend\nmay be extrapolated to female runners\nas well; however, the speciﬁc advanta-\ngeous body composition cutoff num-\nbers for female runners are not clear\nat this time. In addition, a review of\nRunE written from a muscle energetic\nperspective provided similar conclu-\nsions, indicating that runners with\na high proportion of skeletal muscle\nmass and low fat mass may have an\nadvantage in reducing the absolute\nenergy cost of running (17). Using\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "it is advantageous\nto be a runner with a high proportion\nof lean muscle mass as compared to\nbody fat (42). Logically, this trend\nmay be extrapolated to female runners\nas well; however, the speciﬁc advanta-\ngeous body composition cutoff num-\nbers for"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0004_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "54cf7e9cfed8a4a0", "text_span_hash": "54cf7e9cfed8a4a0", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "mposition cutoff num-\nbers for female runners are not clear\nat this time. In addition, a review of\nRunE written from a muscle energetic\nperspective provided similar conclu-\nsions, indicating that runners with\na high proportion of skeletal muscle\nmass", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "changes, and limb girth measurements\ndo not change signiﬁcantly in male\nand female distance runners perform-\ning concurrent training (10). The incor-\nrect belief that resistance training will\nincrease muscle mass and lead to\na slower race pace is based on unsub-\nstantiated claims and anecdotal tradi-\ntion. An observational study of 341\nmarathoners and ultramarathoners in\n2015 examined mean training distance,\nmean training pace, mean race pace,\nfrom a large cohort of marathoners\nand 100-km ultramarathoners from 3\nfemale subject included in total subject\ncount) (42). No associations between\nbody fat and race pace were found in\nrunners if their body fat measured\nbelow 15%, indicating that body com-\nposition for distance runners compet-\ning in 42- to 100-km races seem to be\nof less importance if the runners have\na relatively low level of fat mass. This\nstudy suggests that it is advantageous\nto be a runner with a high proportion\nof lean muscle mass as compared to\nbody fat (42). Logically, this trend\nmay be extrapolated to female runners\nas well; however, the speciﬁc advanta-\ngeous body composition cutoff num-\nbers for female runners are not clear\nat this time. In addition, a review of\nRunE written from a muscle energetic\nperspective provided similar conclu-\nsions, indicating that runners with\na high proportion of skeletal muscle\nmass and low fat mass may have an\nadvantage in reducing the absolute\nenergy cost of running (17). Using\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "mposition cutoff num-\nbers for female runners are not clear\nat this time. In addition, a review of\nRunE written from a muscle energetic\nperspective provided similar conclu-\nsions, indicating that runners with\na high proportion of skeletal muscle\nmass"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0004_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "60a0994c7540eef9", "text_span_hash": "60a0994c7540eef9", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ortion of skeletal muscle\nmass and low fat mass may have an\nadvantage in reducing the absolute\nenergy cost of running (17). Using\na lower energy cost in a race contrib-\nutes to less heat generation and a lower\nrelative muscle activation needed for\nru", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "changes, and limb girth measurements\ndo not change signiﬁcantly in male\nand female distance runners perform-\ning concurrent training (10). The incor-\nrect belief that resistance training will\nincrease muscle mass and lead to\na slower race pace is based on unsub-\nstantiated claims and anecdotal tradi-\ntion. An observational study of 341\nmarathoners and ultramarathoners in\n2015 examined mean training distance,\nmean training pace, mean race pace,\nfrom a large cohort of marathoners\nand 100-km ultramarathoners from 3\nfemale subject included in total subject\ncount) (42). No associations between\nbody fat and race pace were found in\nrunners if their body fat measured\nbelow 15%, indicating that body com-\nposition for distance runners compet-\ning in 42- to 100-km races seem to be\nof less importance if the runners have\na relatively low level of fat mass. This\nstudy suggests that it is advantageous\nto be a runner with a high proportion\nof lean muscle mass as compared to\nbody fat (42). Logically, this trend\nmay be extrapolated to female runners\nas well; however, the speciﬁc advanta-\ngeous body composition cutoff num-\nbers for female runners are not clear\nat this time. In addition, a review of\nRunE written from a muscle energetic\nperspective provided similar conclu-\nsions, indicating that runners with\na high proportion of skeletal muscle\nmass and low fat mass may have an\nadvantage in reducing the absolute\nenergy cost of running (17). Using\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ortion of skeletal muscle\nmass and low fat mass may have an\nadvantage in reducing the absolute\nenergy cost of running (17). Using\na lower energy cost in a race contrib-\nutes to less heat generation and a lower\nrelative muscle activation needed for\nru"}
