owever, the speciﬁc advanta-\ngeous body composition cutoff num-\nbers for female runners are not clear\nat this time. In addition, a review of\nRunE written from a muscle energetic\nperspective provided similar conclu-\nsions, indicating that runners with\na high proportion of skeletal muscle\nmass and low fat mass may have an\nadvantage in reducing the absolute\nenergy cost of running (17). Using\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ed between 2 and 6 sets of 3–\n10 repetitions per exercise at loads\ngreater than 70% 1RM (10). Applying\nthe principles of training prescription, it\nwould be sensible to recommend that\nthe strength and conditioning practi-\ntioner implement methods of e"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0004_c0024", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "a4839aeb9209bd8c", "text_span_hash": "a4839aeb9209bd8c", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 24, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "tioner implement methods of estimating\n1RM on a regular basis, tracking overall\nConcurrent Training Enhances Economy in Runners\nVOLUME 42 | NUMBER 1 | FEBRUARY 2020\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditioning Association. Unauthorized reproductio", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "changes, and limb girth measurements\ndo not change signiﬁcantly in male\nand female distance runners perform-\ning concurrent training (10). The incor-\nrect belief that resistance training will\nincrease muscle mass and lead to\na slower race pace is based on unsub-\nstantiated claims and anecdotal tradi-\ntion. An observational study of 341\nmarathoners and ultramarathoners in\n2015 examined mean training distance,\nmean training pace, mean race pace,\nfrom a large cohort of marathoners\nand 100-km ultramarathoners from 3\nfemale subject included in total subject\ncount) (42). No associations between\nbody fat and race pace were found in\nrunners if their body fat measured\nbelow 15%, indicating that body com-\nposition for distance runners compet-\ning in 42- to 100-km races seem to be\nof less importance if the runners have\na relatively low level of fat mass. This\nstudy suggests that it is advantageous\nto be a runner with a high proportion\nof lean muscle mass as compared to\nbody fat (42). Logically, this trend\nmay be extrapolated to female runners\nas well; however, the speciﬁc advanta-\ngeous body composition cutoff num-\nbers for female runners are not clear\nat this time. In addition, a review of\nRunE written from a muscle energetic\nperspective provided similar conclu-\nsions, indicating that runners with\na high proportion of skeletal muscle\nmass and low fat mass may have an\nadvantage in reducing the absolute\nenergy cost of running (17). Using\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "tioner implement methods of estimating\n1RM on a regular basis, tracking overall\nConcurrent Training Enhances Economy in Runners\nVOLUME 42 | NUMBER 1 | FEBRUARY 2020\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditioning Association. Unauthorized reproductio"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0005_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "bf21996878338a66", "text_span_hash": "bf21996878338a66", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "tonnage to limit the effects of overtrain-\ning, and determining the criteria for load\nprogression to reach optimal gains.\nCommon plyometric training prescrip-\ntion consisted of 1–6 exercises per-\nrepetitions, averaging 30–228 “foot con-\ntacts” per se", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "tonnage to limit the effects of overtrain-\ning, and determining the criteria for load\nprogression to reach optimal gains.\nCommon plyometric training prescrip-\ntion consisted of 1–6 exercises per-\nrepetitions, averaging 30–228 “foot con-\ntacts” per session (10). Low training vol-\nume for plyometrics (60–72 contacts\nper session) may be sufﬁcient for dis-\ntance runners who may not be accus-\ntomed to such exercises (33). It is\nsuggested to progress up to 110–228\nfoot contacts per session (10). Similarly,\nit would be sensible to track plyometric\ncontacts per week and to consider the\nresidual local and global fatigue effect of\nintensity between explosive strength\nand reactive strength plyometrics.\nThe most common prescription of resis-\ntance training frequency in clinical trials\nis between 2 and 3 sessions per week\n(10). Depending on the training cycle,\nthe ratio of running sessions to resistance\ntraining sessions per week may vary. It is\nsuggested that improvements in RunE\nare observed in running to resistance\ntraining ratios between 6:2 and 9:3, oth-\nerwise stated in percentages of resistance\ntraining to overall weekly sessions, or\n25% in this example (4). Greater fre-\nquency of resistance training sessions is\nadvocated during the general prepara-\ntory phase (GPP) as compared to the\nspeciﬁc preparatory phase (SPP) with\nperiodized concurrent training (Table 2).\nINTERSESSION RECOVERY\nAnother consideration for periodization\nis the su", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "tonnage to limit the effects of overtrain-\ning, and determining the criteria for load\nprogression to reach optimal gains.\nCommon plyometric training prescrip-\ntion consisted of 1–6 exercises per-\nrepetitions, averaging 30–228 “foot con-\ntacts” per se"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0005_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7fa32c8a008d95bb", "text_span_hash": "7fa32c8a008d95bb", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "0–228 “foot con-\ntacts” per session (10). Low training vol-\nume for plyometrics (60–72 contacts\nper session) may be sufﬁcient for dis-\ntance runners who may not be accus-\ntomed to such exercises (33). It is\nsuggested to progress up to 110–228\nfoot co", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "tonnage to limit the effects of overtrain-\ning, and determining the criteria for load\nprogression to reach optimal gains.\nCommon plyometric training prescrip-\ntion consisted of 1–6 exercises per-\nrepetitions, averaging 30–228 “foot con-\ntacts” per session (10). Low training vol-\nume for plyometrics (60–72 contacts\nper session) may be sufﬁcient for dis-\ntance runners who may not be accus-\ntomed to such exercises (33). It is\nsuggested to progress up to 110–228\nfoot contacts per session (10). Similarly,\nit would be sensible to track plyometric\ncontacts per week and to consider the\nresidual local and global fatigue effect of\nintensity between explosive strength\nand reactive strength plyometrics.\nThe most common prescription of resis-\ntance training frequency in clinical trials\nis between 2 and 3 sessions per week\n(10). Depending on the training cycle,\nthe ratio of running sessions to resistance\ntraining sessions per week may vary. It is\nsuggested that improvements in RunE\nare observed in running to resistance\ntraining ratios between 6:2 and 9:3, oth-\nerwise stated in percentages of resistance\ntraining to overall weekly sessions, or\n25% in this example (4). Greater fre-\nquency of resistance training sessions is\nadvocated during the general prepara-\ntory phase (GPP) as compared to the\nspeciﬁc preparatory phase (SPP) with\nperiodized concurrent training (Table 2).\nINTERSESSION RECOVERY\nAnother consideration for periodization\nis the su", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "0–228 “foot con-\ntacts” per session (10). Low training vol-\nume for plyometrics (60–72 contacts\nper session) may be sufﬁcient for dis-\ntance runners who may not be accus-\ntomed to such exercises (33). It is\nsuggested to progress up to 110–228\nfoot co"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0005_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "6968bbd8c4421795", "text_span_hash": "6968bbd8c4421795", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ength\nand reactive strength plyometrics.\nThe most common prescription of resis-\ntance training frequency in clinical trials\nis between 2 and 3 sessions per week\n(10). Depending on the training cycle,\nthe ratio of running sessions to resistance\ntraini", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "tonnage to limit the effects of overtrain-\ning, and determining the criteria for load\nprogression to reach optimal gains.\nCommon plyometric training prescrip-\ntion consisted of 1–6 exercises per-\nrepetitions, averaging 30–228 “foot con-\ntacts” per session (10). Low training vol-\nume for plyometrics (60–72 contacts\nper session) may be sufﬁcient for dis-\ntance runners who may not be accus-\ntomed to such exercises (33). It is\nsuggested to progress up to 110–228\nfoot contacts per session (10). Similarly,\nit would be sensible to track plyometric\ncontacts per week and to consider the\nresidual local and global fatigue effect of\nintensity between explosive strength\nand reactive strength plyometrics.\nThe most common prescription of resis-\ntance training frequency in clinical trials\nis between 2 and 3 sessions per week\n(10). Depending on the training cycle,\nthe ratio of running sessions to resistance\ntraining sessions per week may vary. It is\nsuggested that improvements in RunE\nare observed in running to resistance\ntraining ratios between 6:2 and 9:3, oth-\nerwise stated in percentages of resistance\ntraining to overall weekly sessions, or\n25% in this example (4). Greater fre-\nquency of resistance training sessions is\nadvocated during the general prepara-\ntory phase (GPP) as compared to the\nspeciﬁc preparatory phase (SPP) with\nperiodized concurrent training (Table 2).\nINTERSESSION RECOVERY\nAnother consideration for periodization\nis the su", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ength\nand reactive strength plyometrics.\nThe most common prescription of resis-\ntance training frequency in clinical trials\nis between 2 and 3 sessions per week\n(10). Depending on the training cycle,\nthe ratio of running sessions to resistance\ntraini"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0005_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "fe19d93089494a30", "text_span_hash": "fe19d93089494a30", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "sessions to resistance\ntraining sessions per week may vary. It is\nsuggested that improvements in RunE\nare observed in running to resistance\ntraining ratios between 6:2 and 9:3, oth-\nerwise stated in percentages of resistance\ntraining to overall week", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "tonnage to limit the effects of overtrain-\ning, and determining the criteria for load\nprogression to reach optimal gains.\nCommon plyometric training prescrip-\ntion consisted of 1–6 exercises per-\nrepetitions, averaging 30–228 “foot con-\ntacts” per session (10). Low training vol-\nume for plyometrics (60–72 contacts\nper session) may be sufﬁcient for dis-\ntance runners who may not be accus-\ntomed to such exercises (33). It is\nsuggested to progress up to 110–228\nfoot contacts per session (10). Similarly,\nit would be sensible to track plyometric\ncontacts per week and to consider the\nresidual local and global fatigue effect of\nintensity between explosive strength\nand reactive strength plyometrics.\nThe most common prescription of resis-\ntance training frequency in clinical trials\nis between 2 and 3 sessions per week\n(10). Depending on the training cycle,\nthe ratio of running sessions to resistance\ntraining sessions per week may vary. It is\nsuggested that improvements in RunE\nare observed in running to resistance\ntraining ratios between 6:2 and 9:3, oth-\nerwise stated in percentages of resistance\ntraining to overall weekly sessions, or\n25% in this example (4). Greater fre-\nquency of resistance training sessions is\nadvocated during the general prepara-\ntory phase (GPP) as compared to the\nspeciﬁc preparatory phase (SPP) with\nperiodized concurrent training (Table 2).\nINTERSESSION RECOVERY\nAnother consideration for periodization\nis the su", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "sessions to resistance\ntraining sessions per week may vary. It is\nsuggested that improvements in RunE\nare observed in running to resistance\ntraining ratios between 6:2 and 9:3, oth-\nerwise stated in percentages of resistance\ntraining to overall week"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0005_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "86888d10f7c3281f", "text_span_hash": "86888d10f7c3281f", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "phase (SPP) with\nperiodized concurrent training (Table 2).\nINTERSESSION RECOVERY\nAnother consideration for periodization\nis the successive timing of resistance\ntraining sessions to running sessions.\nThe acute interference effect describes\nhow the res", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "tonnage to limit the effects of overtrain-\ning, and determining the criteria for load\nprogression to reach optimal gains.\nCommon plyometric training prescrip-\ntion consisted of 1–6 exercises per-\nrepetitions, averaging 30–228 “foot con-\ntacts” per session (10). Low training vol-\nume for plyometrics (60–72 contacts\nper session) may be sufﬁcient for dis-\ntance runners who may not be accus-\ntomed to such exercises (33). It is\nsuggested to progress up to 110–228\nfoot contacts per session (10). Similarly,\nit would be sensible to track plyometric\ncontacts per week and to consider the\nresidual local and global fatigue effect of\nintensity between explosive strength\nand reactive strength plyometrics.\nThe most common prescription of resis-\ntance training frequency in clinical trials\nis between 2 and 3 sessions per week\n(10). Depending on the training cycle,\nthe ratio of running sessions to resistance\ntraining sessions per week may vary. It is\nsuggested that improvements in RunE\nare observed in running to resistance\ntraining ratios between 6:2 and 9:3, oth-\nerwise stated in percentages of resistance\ntraining to overall weekly sessions, or\n25% in this example (4). Greater fre-\nquency of resistance training sessions is\nadvocated during the general prepara-\ntory phase (GPP) as compared to the\nspeciﬁc preparatory phase (SPP) with\nperiodized concurrent training (Table 2).\nINTERSESSION RECOVERY\nAnother consideration for periodization\nis the su", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "phase (SPP) with\nperiodized concurrent training (Table 2).\nINTERSESSION RECOVERY\nAnother consideration for periodization\nis the successive timing of resistance\ntraining sessions to running sessions.\nThe acute interference effect describes\nhow the res"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0005_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0730f2f30807db24", "text_span_hash": "0730f2f30807db24", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "f subsequent sessions due to less-\nneuromuscular function and strength\nthat are evident for up to 24 hours after\na heavy training session (39).\nDamo et al. (14) determined that exer-\ncise session order in a single day is also\nan important considerati", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "tonnage to limit the effects of overtrain-\ning, and determining the criteria for load\nprogression to reach optimal gains.\nCommon plyometric training prescrip-\ntion consisted of 1–6 exercises per-\nrepetitions, averaging 30–228 “foot con-\ntacts” per session (10). Low training vol-\nume for plyometrics (60–72 contacts\nper session) may be sufﬁcient for dis-\ntance runners who may not be accus-\ntomed to such exercises (33). It is\nsuggested to progress up to 110–228\nfoot contacts per session (10). Similarly,\nit would be sensible to track plyometric\ncontacts per week and to consider the\nresidual local and global fatigue effect of\nintensity between explosive strength\nand reactive strength plyometrics.\nThe most common prescription of resis-\ntance training frequency in clinical trials\nis between 2 and 3 sessions per week\n(10). Depending on the training cycle,\nthe ratio of running sessions to resistance\ntraining sessions per week may vary. It is\nsuggested that improvements in RunE\nare observed in running to resistance\ntraining ratios between 6:2 and 9:3, oth-\nerwise stated in percentages of resistance\ntraining to overall weekly sessions, or\n25% in this example (4). Greater fre-\nquency of resistance training sessions is\nadvocated during the general prepara-\ntory phase (GPP) as compared to the\nspeciﬁc preparatory phase (SPP) with\nperiodized concurrent training (Table 2).\nINTERSESSION RECOVERY\nAnother consideration for periodization\nis the su", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "f subsequent sessions due to less-\nneuromuscular function and strength\nthat are evident for up to 24 hours after\na heavy training session (39).\nDamo et al. (14) determined that exer-\ncise session order in a single day is also\nan important considerati"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0005_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b07fb87575b843c5", "text_span_hash": "b07fb87575b843c5", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "also\nan important consideration to reap the\ngreatest beneﬁts of each training session.\nIn this study, a greater decrease in mus-\ncle voluntary contraction was noted\nwhen a resistance training session pre-\nceded a running training session by 6\nhours", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "tonnage to limit the effects of overtrain-\ning, and determining the criteria for load\nprogression to reach optimal gains.\nCommon plyometric training prescrip-\ntion consisted of 1–6 exercises per-\nrepetitions, averaging 30–228 “foot con-\ntacts” per session (10). Low training vol-\nume for plyometrics (60–72 contacts\nper session) may be sufﬁcient for dis-\ntance runners who may not be accus-\ntomed to such exercises (33). It is\nsuggested to progress up to 110–228\nfoot contacts per session (10). Similarly,\nit would be sensible to track plyometric\ncontacts per week and to consider the\nresidual local and global fatigue effect of\nintensity between explosive strength\nand reactive strength plyometrics.\nThe most common prescription of resis-\ntance training frequency in clinical trials\nis between 2 and 3 sessions per week\n(10). Depending on the training cycle,\nthe ratio of running sessions to resistance\ntraining sessions per week may vary. It is\nsuggested that improvements in RunE\nare observed in running to resistance\ntraining ratios between 6:2 and 9:3, oth-\nerwise stated in percentages of resistance\ntraining to overall weekly sessions, or\n25% in this example (4). Greater fre-\nquency of resistance training sessions is\nadvocated during the general prepara-\ntory phase (GPP) as compared to the\nspeciﬁc preparatory phase (SPP) with\nperiodized concurrent training (Table 2).\nINTERSESSION RECOVERY\nAnother consideration for periodization\nis the su", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "also\nan important consideration to reap the\ngreatest beneﬁts of each training session.\nIn this study, a greater decrease in mus-\ncle voluntary contraction was noted\nwhen a resistance training session pre-\nceded a running training session by 6\nhours"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0005_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0ba40c2bdec7c3c8", "text_span_hash": "0ba40c2bdec7c3c8", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ated\nand did not affect the performance of\nthe resistance training session. Thus, ex-\npanding the rest period between ses-\nsions and ordering the sessions to\nperform running in the ﬁrst session of\nthe day may allow for greater positive\nadaptations to", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "tonnage to limit the effects of overtrain-\ning, and determining the criteria for load\nprogression to reach optimal gains.\nCommon plyometric training prescrip-\ntion consisted of 1–6 exercises per-\nrepetitions, averaging 30–228 “foot con-\ntacts” per session (10). Low training vol-\nume for plyometrics (60–72 contacts\nper session) may be sufﬁcient for dis-\ntance runners who may not be accus-\ntomed to such exercises (33). It is\nsuggested to progress up to 110–228\nfoot contacts per session (10). Similarly,\nit would be sensible to track plyometric\ncontacts per week and to consider the\nresidual local and global fatigue effect of\nintensity between explosive strength\nand reactive strength plyometrics.\nThe most common prescription of resis-\ntance training frequency in clinical trials\nis between 2 and 3 sessions per week\n(10). Depending on the training cycle,\nthe ratio of running sessions to resistance\ntraining sessions per week may vary. It is\nsuggested that improvements in RunE\nare observed in running to resistance\ntraining ratios between 6:2 and 9:3, oth-\nerwise stated in percentages of resistance\ntraining to overall weekly sessions, or\n25% in this example (4). Greater fre-\nquency of resistance training sessions is\nadvocated during the general prepara-\ntory phase (GPP) as compared to the\nspeciﬁc preparatory phase (SPP) with\nperiodized concurrent training (Table 2).\nINTERSESSION RECOVERY\nAnother consideration for periodization\nis the su", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ated\nand did not affect the performance of\nthe resistance training session. Thus, ex-\npanding the rest period between ses-\nsions and ordering the sessions to\nperform running in the ﬁrst session of\nthe day may allow for greater positive\nadaptations to"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0005_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1283061c1ea3f528", "text_span_hash": "1283061c1ea3f528", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "reater positive\nadaptations to RunE.\nPRACTICAL PERIODIZATION\nCONCURRENT RESISTANCE\nTRAINING PERIODIZATION\nPeriodization is a concept that many\nrunning coaches and athletes use to\ndivide the year into training blocks\nand cycles to peak their performan", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "tonnage to limit the effects of overtrain-\ning, and determining the criteria for load\nprogression to reach optimal gains.\nCommon plyometric training prescrip-\ntion consisted of 1–6 exercises per-\nrepetitions, averaging 30–228 “foot con-\ntacts” per session (10). Low training vol-\nume for plyometrics (60–72 contacts\nper session) may be sufﬁcient for dis-\ntance runners who may not be accus-\ntomed to such exercises (33). It is\nsuggested to progress up to 110–228\nfoot contacts per session (10). Similarly,\nit would be sensible to track plyometric\ncontacts per week and to consider the\nresidual local and global fatigue effect of\nintensity between explosive strength\nand reactive strength plyometrics.\nThe most common prescription of resis-\ntance training frequency in clinical trials\nis between 2 and 3 sessions per week\n(10). Depending on the training cycle,\nthe ratio of running sessions to resistance\ntraining sessions per week may vary. It is\nsuggested that improvements in RunE\nare observed in running to resistance\ntraining ratios between 6:2 and 9:3, oth-\nerwise stated in percentages of resistance\ntraining to overall weekly sessions, or\n25% in this example (4). Greater fre-\nquency of resistance training sessions is\nadvocated during the general prepara-\ntory phase (GPP) as compared to the\nspeciﬁc preparatory phase (SPP) with\nperiodized concurrent training (Table 2).\nINTERSESSION RECOVERY\nAnother consideration for periodization\nis the su", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "reater positive\nadaptations to RunE.\nPRACTICAL PERIODIZATION\nCONCURRENT RESISTANCE\nTRAINING PERIODIZATION\nPeriodization is a concept that many\nrunning coaches and athletes use to\ndivide the year into training blocks\nand cycles to peak their performan"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0005_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "abd5c1993885a8c1", "text_span_hash": "abd5c1993885a8c1", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "cycles to peak their performance\non a speciﬁc date or dates (3). The\nindividual running sessions will vary\nin pace, distance, and incline to peak\nthe athlete for their most important\nraces in the year. The addition of con-\ncurrent training programmin", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "tonnage to limit the effects of overtrain-\ning, and determining the criteria for load\nprogression to reach optimal gains.\nCommon plyometric training prescrip-\ntion consisted of 1–6 exercises per-\nrepetitions, averaging 30–228 “foot con-\ntacts” per session (10). Low training vol-\nume for plyometrics (60–72 contacts\nper session) may be sufﬁcient for dis-\ntance runners who may not be accus-\ntomed to such exercises (33). It is\nsuggested to progress up to 110–228\nfoot contacts per session (10). Similarly,\nit would be sensible to track plyometric\ncontacts per week and to consider the\nresidual local and global fatigue effect of\nintensity between explosive strength\nand reactive strength plyometrics.\nThe most common prescription of resis-\ntance training frequency in clinical trials\nis between 2 and 3 sessions per week\n(10). Depending on the training cycle,\nthe ratio of running sessions to resistance\ntraining sessions per week may vary. It is\nsuggested that improvements in RunE\nare observed in running to resistance\ntraining ratios between 6:2 and 9:3, ot