h-\nerwise stated in percentages of resistance\ntraining to overall weekly sessions, or\n25% in this example (4). Greater fre-\nquency of resistance training sessions is\nadvocated during the general prepara-\ntory phase (GPP) as compared to the\nspeciﬁc preparatory phase (SPP) with\nperiodized concurrent training (Table 2).\nINTERSESSION RECOVERY\nAnother consideration for periodization\nis the su", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "s\nor substitutions and require moderate\nor high feedback to perform it indepen-\ndently. Usually, these athletes have less\nthan 1 year of quality resistance training\nexperience. Athletes with a high train-\ning age will be able to perform the\nmovement"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0005_c0026", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5a11ebca3136815d", "text_span_hash": "5a11ebca3136815d", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 26, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "able to perform the\nmovement patterns with minimal to\nno feedback, demonstrate minimal to\nno compensations, and usually have\ngreater than 1 year of quality resistance\ntraining experience. Detailed exercise\nselection based on the athlete’s training\na", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "tonnage to limit the effects of overtrain-\ning, and determining the criteria for load\nprogression to reach optimal gains.\nCommon plyometric training prescrip-\ntion consisted of 1–6 exercises per-\nrepetitions, averaging 30–228 “foot con-\ntacts” per session (10). Low training vol-\nume for plyometrics (60–72 contacts\nper session) may be sufﬁcient for dis-\ntance runners who may not be accus-\ntomed to such exercises (33). It is\nsuggested to progress up to 110–228\nfoot contacts per session (10). Similarly,\nit would be sensible to track plyometric\ncontacts per week and to consider the\nresidual local and global fatigue effect of\nintensity between explosive strength\nand reactive strength plyometrics.\nThe most common prescription of resis-\ntance training frequency in clinical trials\nis between 2 and 3 sessions per week\n(10). Depending on the training cycle,\nthe ratio of running sessions to resistance\ntraining sessions per week may vary. It is\nsuggested that improvements in RunE\nare observed in running to resistance\ntraining ratios between 6:2 and 9:3, oth-\nerwise stated in percentages of resistance\ntraining to overall weekly sessions, or\n25% in this example (4). Greater fre-\nquency of resistance training sessions is\nadvocated during the general prepara-\ntory phase (GPP) as compared to the\nspeciﬁc preparatory phase (SPP) with\nperiodized concurrent training (Table 2).\nINTERSESSION RECOVERY\nAnother consideration for periodization\nis the su", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "able to perform the\nmovement patterns with minimal to\nno feedback, demonstrate minimal to\nno compensations, and usually have\ngreater than 1 year of quality resistance\ntraining experience. Detailed exercise\nselection based on the athlete’s training\na"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0005_c0027", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "89fdc4a589b1e223", "text_span_hash": "89fdc4a589b1e223", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 27, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ed on the athlete’s training\nage and phase of periodization is rec-\nommended in Table 3.\nA competition schedule for competi-\ntive runners may include more than\n15 races per year between training\nStrength and Conditioning Journal | www.nsca-scj.com\nCo", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "tonnage to limit the effects of overtrain-\ning, and determining the criteria for load\nprogression to reach optimal gains.\nCommon plyometric training prescrip-\ntion consisted of 1–6 exercises per-\nrepetitions, averaging 30–228 “foot con-\ntacts” per session (10). Low training vol-\nume for plyometrics (60–72 contacts\nper session) may be sufﬁcient for dis-\ntance runners who may not be accus-\ntomed to such exercises (33). It is\nsuggested to progress up to 110–228\nfoot contacts per session (10). Similarly,\nit would be sensible to track plyometric\ncontacts per week and to consider the\nresidual local and global fatigue effect of\nintensity between explosive strength\nand reactive strength plyometrics.\nThe most common prescription of resis-\ntance training frequency in clinical trials\nis between 2 and 3 sessions per week\n(10). Depending on the training cycle,\nthe ratio of running sessions to resistance\ntraining sessions per week may vary. It is\nsuggested that improvements in RunE\nare observed in running to resistance\ntraining ratios between 6:2 and 9:3, oth-\nerwise stated in percentages of resistance\ntraining to overall weekly sessions, or\n25% in this example (4). Greater fre-\nquency of resistance training sessions is\nadvocated during the general prepara-\ntory phase (GPP) as compared to the\nspeciﬁc preparatory phase (SPP) with\nperiodized concurrent training (Table 2).\nINTERSESSION RECOVERY\nAnother consideration for periodization\nis the su", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ed on the athlete’s training\nage and phase of periodization is rec-\nommended in Table 3.\nA competition schedule for competi-\ntive runners may include more than\n15 races per year between training\nStrength and Conditioning Journal | www.nsca-scj.com\nCo"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0006_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "876e948d50866d57", "text_span_hash": "876e948d50866d57", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "training variables and approximately\n3–5 races per year that will require true\ntaper and peaking phases. Commonly,\nthe year may be broken down into 3–5\nmacrocycles to taper for these races,\neach composed of mesocycles consist-\ning of the GPP, the SPP", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "training variables and approximately\n3–5 races per year that will require true\ntaper and peaking phases. Commonly,\nthe year may be broken down into 3–5\nmacrocycles to taper for these races,\neach composed of mesocycles consist-\ning of the GPP, the SPP, the mainte-\nnance phase, and the tapering and\nThe GPP for distance running is similar\nto the off-season phase in team sports.\nIn this phase, the volume of resistance\ntraining is high, and the intensity is\nmoderate to high. Variability in exercise\nselection, as well as variability in run-\nning sessions, will be higher in the\nbeginning and then taper as the phase\nprogresses forward. The goal of this\nphase is to induce neuromuscular adap-\ntations to new skills and movement pat-\nmuscular strength (36). As the GPP\ntransitions to maximal strength devel-\nopment, the volume of resistance train-\nincreases. This is also the phase that\nphysical therapists rehabilitate nagging\ninjuries. Resistance training sessions are\ngenerally scheduled for 2–3 sessions per\nweek with running sessions scheduled\nfor 5–6 sessions per week. Applying ex-\nisting research and principles of training\nprescription, it is advised to program\na greater percentage of resistance train-\ning sessions to overall weekly training\nsessions in the GPP (;30–40%) and\na lower percentage of resistance training\nsessions to weekly sessions in the SPP\nNext, the athlete progresses into the\nSPP, similar to the preseason phase\nin team sp", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "training variables and approximately\n3–5 races per year that will require true\ntaper and peaking phases. Commonly,\nthe year may be broken down into 3–5\nmacrocycles to taper for these races,\neach composed of mesocycles consist-\ning of the GPP, the SPP"}
