{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0006_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b940b316bd61ecd6", "text_span_hash": "b940b316bd61ecd6", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "that\nphysical therapists rehabilitate nagging\ninjuries. Resistance training sessions are\ngenerally scheduled for 2–3 sessions per\nweek with running sessions scheduled\nfor 5–6 sessions per week. Applying ex-\nisting research and principles of training", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "training variables and approximately\n3–5 races per year that will require true\ntaper and peaking phases. Commonly,\nthe year may be broken down into 3–5\nmacrocycles to taper for these races,\neach composed of mesocycles consist-\ning of the GPP, the SPP, the mainte-\nnance phase, and the tapering and\nThe GPP for distance running is similar\nto the off-season phase in team sports.\nIn this phase, the volume of resistance\ntraining is high, and the intensity is\nmoderate to high. Variability in exercise\nselection, as well as variability in run-\nning sessions, will be higher in the\nbeginning and then taper as the phase\nprogresses forward. The goal of this\nphase is to induce neuromuscular adap-\ntations to new skills and movement pat-\nmuscular strength (36). As the GPP\ntransitions to maximal strength devel-\nopment, the volume of resistance train-\nincreases. This is also the phase that\nphysical therapists rehabilitate nagging\ninjuries. Resistance training sessions are\ngenerally scheduled for 2–3 sessions per\nweek with running sessions scheduled\nfor 5–6 sessions per week. Applying ex-\nisting research and principles of training\nprescription, it is advised to program\na greater percentage of resistance train-\ning sessions to overall weekly training\nsessions in the GPP (;30–40%) and\na lower percentage of resistance training\nsessions to weekly sessions in the SPP\nNext, the athlete progresses into the\nSPP, similar to the preseason phase\nin team sp", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "that\nphysical therapists rehabilitate nagging\ninjuries. Resistance training sessions are\ngenerally scheduled for 2–3 sessions per\nweek with running sessions scheduled\nfor 5–6 sessions per week. Applying ex-\nisting research and principles of training"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0006_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1e9c578c9cebff5d", "text_span_hash": "1e9c578c9cebff5d", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ch and principles of training\nprescription, it is advised to program\na greater percentage of resistance train-\ning sessions to overall weekly training\nsessions in the GPP (;30–40%) and\na lower percentage of resistance training\nsessions to weekly sess", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "training variables and approximately\n3–5 races per year that will require true\ntaper and peaking phases. Commonly,\nthe year may be broken down into 3–5\nmacrocycles to taper for these races,\neach composed of mesocycles consist-\ning of the GPP, the SPP, the mainte-\nnance phase, and the tapering and\nThe GPP for distance running is similar\nto the off-season phase in team sports.\nIn this phase, the volume of resistance\ntraining is high, and the intensity is\nmoderate to high. Variability in exercise\nselection, as well as variability in run-\nning sessions, will be higher in the\nbeginning and then taper as the phase\nprogresses forward. The goal of this\nphase is to induce neuromuscular adap-\ntations to new skills and movement pat-\nmuscular strength (36). As the GPP\ntransitions to maximal strength devel-\nopment, the volume of resistance train-\nincreases. This is also the phase that\nphysical therapists rehabilitate nagging\ninjuries. Resistance training sessions are\ngenerally scheduled for 2–3 sessions per\nweek with running sessions scheduled\nfor 5–6 sessions per week. Applying ex-\nisting research and principles of training\nprescription, it is advised to program\na greater percentage of resistance train-\ning sessions to overall weekly training\nsessions in the GPP (;30–40%) and\na lower percentage of resistance training\nsessions to weekly sessions in the SPP\nNext, the athlete progresses into the\nSPP, similar to the preseason phase\nin team sp", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ch and principles of training\nprescription, it is advised to program\na greater percentage of resistance train-\ning sessions to overall weekly training\nsessions in the GPP (;30–40%) and\na lower percentage of resistance training\nsessions to weekly sess"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0006_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "22e6201232726649", "text_span_hash": "22e6201232726649", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "aining\nsessions to weekly sessions in the SPP\nNext, the athlete progresses into the\nSPP, similar to the preseason phase\nin team sports. Resistance training vol-\nume progressively decreases, intensity\nprogressively increases, and the vari-\nability of", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "training variables and approximately\n3–5 races per year that will require true\ntaper and peaking phases. Commonly,\nthe year may be broken down into 3–5\nmacrocycles to taper for these races,\neach composed of mesocycles consist-\ning of the GPP, the SPP, the mainte-\nnance phase, and the tapering and\nThe GPP for distance running is similar\nto the off-season phase in team sports.\nIn this phase, the volume of resistance\ntraining is high, and the intensity is\nmoderate to high. Variability in exercise\nselection, as well as variability in run-\nning sessions, will be higher in the\nbeginning and then taper as the phase\nprogresses forward. The goal of this\nphase is to induce neuromuscular adap-\ntations to new skills and movement pat-\nmuscular strength (36). As the GPP\ntransitions to maximal strength devel-\nopment, the volume of resistance train-\nincreases. This is also the phase that\nphysical therapists rehabilitate nagging\ninjuries. Resistance training sessions are\ngenerally scheduled for 2–3 sessions per\nweek with running sessions scheduled\nfor 5–6 sessions per week. Applying ex-\nisting research and principles of training\nprescription, it is advised to program\na greater percentage of resistance train-\ning sessions to overall weekly training\nsessions in the GPP (;30–40%) and\na lower percentage of resistance training\nsessions to weekly sessions in the SPP\nNext, the athlete progresses into the\nSPP, similar to the preseason phase\nin team sp", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "aining\nsessions to weekly sessions in the SPP\nNext, the athlete progresses into the\nSPP, similar to the preseason phase\nin team sports. Resistance training vol-\nume progressively decreases, intensity\nprogressively increases, and the vari-\nability of"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0006_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b55849bada71fafc", "text_span_hash": "b55849bada71fafc", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ses, and the vari-\nability of training decreases. In this\nphase, running training distances and\npace transition to resemble the com-\npetitions ahead. For example, a mara-\nthon runner will complete a greater\nnumber of runs more than 15 miles\nand close", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "training variables and approximately\n3–5 races per year that will require true\ntaper and peaking phases. Commonly,\nthe year may be broken down into 3–5\nmacrocycles to taper for these races,\neach composed of mesocycles consist-\ning of the GPP, the SPP, the mainte-\nnance phase, and the tapering and\nThe GPP for distance running is similar\nto the off-season phase in team sports.\nIn this phase, the volume of resistance\ntraining is high, and the intensity is\nmoderate to high. Variability in exercise\nselection, as well as variability in run-\nning sessions, will be higher in the\nbeginning and then taper as the phase\nprogresses forward. The goal of this\nphase is to induce neuromuscular adap-\ntations to new skills and movement pat-\nmuscular strength (36). As the GPP\ntransitions to maximal strength devel-\nopment, the volume of resistance train-\nincreases. This is also the phase that\nphysical therapists rehabilitate nagging\ninjuries. Resistance training sessions are\ngenerally scheduled for 2–3 sessions per\nweek with running sessions scheduled\nfor 5–6 sessions per week. Applying ex-\nisting research and principles of training\nprescription, it is advised to program\na greater percentage of resistance train-\ning sessions to overall weekly training\nsessions in the GPP (;30–40%) and\na lower percentage of resistance training\nsessions to weekly sessions in the SPP\nNext, the athlete progresses into the\nSPP, similar to the preseason phase\nin team sp", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ses, and the vari-\nability of training decreases. In this\nphase, running training distances and\npace transition to resemble the com-\npetitions ahead. For example, a mara-\nthon runner will complete a greater\nnumber of runs more than 15 miles\nand close"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0006_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "782f3e9910dc95d2", "text_span_hash": "782f3e9910dc95d2", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "s more than 15 miles\nand closer to race pace. SPP is the\nphase to capitalize on the strength\ngains made in the GPP and transition\nto focus on explosive strength exer-\ncises to target changes in the rate of\nforce development. Rehabilitation of\ninjurie", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "training variables and approximately\n3–5 races per year that will require true\ntaper and peaking phases. Commonly,\nthe year may be broken down into 3–5\nmacrocycles to taper for these races,\neach composed of mesocycles consist-\ning of the GPP, the SPP, the mainte-\nnance phase, and the tapering and\nThe GPP for distance running is similar\nto the off-season phase in team sports.\nIn this phase, the volume of resistance\ntraining is high, and the intensity is\nmoderate to high. Variability in exercise\nselection, as well as variability in run-\nning sessions, will be higher in the\nbeginning and then taper as the phase\nprogresses forward. The goal of this\nphase is to induce neuromuscular adap-\ntations to new skills and movement pat-\nmuscular strength (36). As the GPP\ntransitions to maximal strength devel-\nopment, the volume of resistance train-\nincreases. This is also the phase that\nphysical therapists rehabilitate nagging\ninjuries. Resistance training sessions are\ngenerally scheduled for 2–3 sessions per\nweek with running sessions scheduled\nfor 5–6 sessions per week. Applying ex-\nisting research and principles of training\nprescription, it is advised to program\na greater percentage of resistance train-\ning sessions to overall weekly training\nsessions in the GPP (;30–40%) and\na lower percentage of resistance training\nsessions to weekly sessions in the SPP\nNext, the athlete progresses into the\nSPP, similar to the preseason phase\nin team sp", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "s more than 15 miles\nand closer to race pace. SPP is the\nphase to capitalize on the strength\ngains made in the GPP and transition\nto focus on explosive strength exer-\ncises to target changes in the rate of\nforce development. Rehabilitation of\ninjurie"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0006_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "12c8a092ace27e0a", "text_span_hash": "12c8a092ace27e0a", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "th running training sessions increas-\ning up to 9 sessions per week.\nAs the runner progresses toward the\ntapering and peaking phase of his or\nher macrocycle, resistance training will\nenter a maintenance phase in which\nthe volume will decrease, while", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "training variables and approximately\n3–5 races per year that will require true\ntaper and peaking phases. Commonly,\nthe year may be broken down into 3–5\nmacrocycles to taper for these races,\neach composed of mesocycles consist-\ning of the GPP, the SPP, the mainte-\nnance phase, and the tapering and\nThe GPP for distance running is similar\nto the off-season phase in team sports.\nIn this phase, the volume of resistance\ntraining is high, and the intensity is\nmoderate to high. Variability in exercise\nselection, as well as variability in run-\nning sessions, will be higher in the\nbeginning and then taper as the phase\nprogresses forward. The goal of this\nphase is to induce neuromuscular adap-\ntations to new skills and movement pat-\nmuscular strength (36). As the GPP\ntransitions to maximal strength devel-\nopment, the volume of resistance train-\nincreases. This is also the phase that\nphysical therapists rehabilitate nagging\ninjuries. Resistance training sessions are\ngenerally scheduled for 2–3 sessions per\nweek with running sessions scheduled\nfor 5–6 sessions per week. Applying ex-\nisting research and principles of training\nprescription, it is advised to program\na greater percentage of resistance train-\ning sessions to overall weekly training\nsessions in the GPP (;30–40%) and\na lower percentage of resistance training\nsessions to weekly sessions in the SPP\nNext, the athlete progresses into the\nSPP, similar to the preseason phase\nin team sp", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "th running training sessions increas-\ning up to 9 sessions per week.\nAs the runner progresses toward the\ntapering and peaking phase of his or\nher macrocycle, resistance training will\nenter a maintenance phase in which\nthe volume will decrease, while"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0006_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "609163ed280c494f", "text_span_hash": "609163ed280c494f", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "e volume will decrease, while inten-\nsity remains high. During the mainte-\nnance and tapering/peaking phases,\nresistance training sessions generally\ndecrease to 1–2 sessions per week with\nsigniﬁcant decrease in overall volume\nto approximately 50% of", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "training variables and approximately\n3–5 races per year that will require true\ntaper and peaking phases. Commonly,\nthe year may be broken down into 3–5\nmacrocycles to taper for these races,\neach composed of mesocycles consist-\ning of the GPP, the SPP, the mainte-\nnance phase, and the tapering and\nThe GPP for distance running is similar\nto the off-season phase in team sports.\nIn this phase, the volume of resistance\ntraining is high, and the intensity is\nmoderate to high. Variability in exercise\nselection, as well as variability in run-\nning sessions, will be higher in the\nbeginning and then taper as the phase\nprogresses forward. The goal of this\nphase is to induce neuromuscular adap-\ntations to new skills and movement pat-\nmuscular strength (36). As the GPP\ntransitions to maximal strength devel-\nopment, the volume of resistance train-\nincreases. This is also the phase that\nphysical therapists rehabilitate nagging\ninjuries. Resistance training sessions are\ngenerally scheduled for 2–3 sessions per\nweek with running sessions scheduled\nfor 5–6 sessions per week. Applying ex-\nisting research and principles of training\nprescription, it is advised to program\na greater percentage of resistance train-\ning sessions to overall weekly training\nsessions in the GPP (;30–40%) and\na lower percentage of resistance training\nsessions to weekly sessions in the SPP\nNext, the athlete progresses into the\nSPP, similar to the preseason phase\nin team sp", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "e volume will decrease, while inten-\nsity remains high. During the mainte-\nnance and tapering/peaking phases,\nresistance training sessions generally\ndecrease to 1–2 sessions per week with\nsigniﬁcant decrease in overall volume\nto approximately 50% of"}
