ions per week. Applying ex-\nisting research and principles of training\nprescription, it is advised to program\na greater percentage of resistance train-\ning sessions to overall weekly training\nsessions in the GPP (;30–40%) and\na lower percentage of resistance training\nsessions to weekly sessions in the SPP\nNext, the athlete progresses into the\nSPP, similar to the preseason phase\nin team sp", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "of an active recov-\nery phase that occurs every 4–6 weeks\nof training in the GPP or SPP phases. If\ncomplete rest from all resistance train-\ning is programmed during the active\nrecovery phase, then a week or 2 of\nintroductory resistance exercise to gr"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0006_c0017", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9f1aa67130c0acda", "text_span_hash": "9f1aa67130c0acda", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 17, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "while mindfully considering periodiza-\ntion for competitive distance runners.\nCONCURRENT ENDURANCE\nRUNNING PERIODIZATION\nFor most study protocols, running\ntraining sessions were completed as\nusual. In application, strength and con-\nditioning practit", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "training variables and approximately\n3–5 races per year that will require true\ntaper and peaking phases. Commonly,\nthe year may be broken down into 3–5\nmacrocycles to taper for these races,\neach composed of mesocycles consist-\ning of the GPP, the SPP, the mainte-\nnance phase, and the tapering and\nThe GPP for distance running is similar\nto the off-season phase in team sports.\nIn this phase, the volume of resistance\ntraining is high, and the intensity is\nmoderate to high. Variability in exercise\nselection, as well as variability in run-\nning sessions, will be higher in the\nbeginning and then taper as the phase\nprogresses forward. The goal of this\nphase is to induce neuromuscular adap-\ntations to new skills and movement pat-\nmuscular strength (36). As the GPP\ntransitions to maximal strength devel-\nopment, the volume of resistance train-\nincreases. This is also the phase that\nphysical therapists rehabilitate nagging\ninjuries. Resistance training sessions are\ngenerally scheduled for 2–3 sessions per\nweek with running sessions scheduled\nfor 5–6 sessions per week. Applying ex-\nisting research and principles of training\nprescription, it is advised to program\na greater percentage of resistance train-\ning sessions to overall weekly training\nsessions in the GPP (;30–40%) and\na lower percentage of resistance training\nsessions to weekly sessions in the SPP\nNext, the athlete progresses into the\nSPP, similar to the preseason phase\nin team sp", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "while mindfully considering periodiza-\ntion for competitive distance runners.\nCONCURRENT ENDURANCE\nRUNNING PERIODIZATION\nFor most study protocols, running\ntraining sessions were completed as\nusual. In application, strength and con-\nditioning practit"}
