sions per week. Applying ex-\nisting research and principles of training\nprescription, it is advised to program\na greater percentage of resistance train-\ning sessions to overall weekly training\nsessions in the GPP (;30–40%) and\na lower percentage of resistance training\nsessions to weekly sessions in the SPP\nNext, the athlete progresses into the\nSPP, similar to the preseason phase\nin team sp", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "gth and con-\nditioning practitioners may choose to\nnot alter endurance training intensity\nor volume with the addition of concur-\nrent training. Applying periodization\nprinciples, the intensity and volume\nof running training should undulate in\na struc"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0006_c0021", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0d16e0c8fc491fd0", "text_span_hash": "0d16e0c8fc491fd0", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 21, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ntensity distribution is represented as percentages of weekly running training volume based on race-paced zones.\naPercentages represent resistance training sessions to total training sessions per week.\nConcurrent Training Enhances Economy in Runners", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "training variables and approximately\n3–5 races per year that will require true\ntaper and peaking phases. Commonly,\nthe year may be broken down into 3–5\nmacrocycles to taper for these races,\neach composed of mesocycles consist-\ning of the GPP, the SPP, the mainte-\nnance phase, and the tapering and\nThe GPP for distance running is similar\nto the off-season phase in team sports.\nIn this phase, the volume of resistance\ntraining is high, and the intensity is\nmoderate to high. Variability in exercise\nselection, as well as variability in run-\nning sessions, will be higher in the\nbeginning and then taper as the phase\nprogresses forward. The goal of this\nphase is to induce neuromuscular adap-\ntations to new skills and movement pat-\nmuscular strength (36). As the GPP\ntransitions to maximal strength devel-\nopment, the volume of resistance train-\nincreases. This is also the phase that\nphysical therapists rehabilitate nagging\ninjuries. Resistance training sessions are\ngenerally scheduled for 2–3 sessions per\nweek with running sessions scheduled\nfor 5–6 sessions per week. Applying ex-\nisting research and principles of training\nprescription, it is advised to program\na greater percentage of resistance train-\ning sessions to overall weekly training\nsessions in the GPP (;30–40%) and\na lower percentage of resistance training\nsessions to weekly sessions in the SPP\nNext, the athlete progresses into the\nSPP, similar to the preseason phase\nin team sp", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ntensity distribution is represented as percentages of weekly running training volume based on race-paced zones.\naPercentages represent resistance training sessions to total training sessions per week.\nConcurrent Training Enhances Economy in Runners"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0006_c0022", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e161c200171b02d9", "text_span_hash": "e161c200171b02d9", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 22, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "g Enhances Economy in Runners\nVOLUME 42 | NUMBER 1 | FEBRUARY 2020\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditioning Association. Unauthorized reproduction of this article is prohibited.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "training variables and approximately\n3–5 races per year that will require true\ntaper and peaking phases. Commonly,\nthe year may be broken down into 3–5\nmacrocycles to taper for these races,\neach composed of mesocycles consist-\ning of the GPP, the SPP, the mainte-\nnance phase, and the tapering and\nThe GPP for distance running is similar\nto the off-season phase in team sports.\nIn this phase, the volume of resistance\ntraining is high, and the intensity is\nmoderate to high. Variability in exercise\nselection, as well as variability in run-\nning sessions, will be higher in the\nbeginning and then taper as the phase\nprogresses forward. The goal of this\nphase is to induce neuromuscular adap-\ntations to new skills and movement pat-\nmuscular strength (36). As the GPP\ntransitions to maximal strength devel-\nopment, the volume of resistance train-\nincreases. This is also the phase that\nphysical therapists rehabilitate nagging\ninjuries. Resistance training sessions are\ngenerally scheduled for 2–3 sessions per\nweek with running sessions scheduled\nfor 5–6 sessions per week. Applying ex-\nisting research and principles of training\nprescription, it is advised to program\na greater percentage of resistance train-\ning sessions to overall weekly training\nsessions in the GPP (;30–40%) and\na lower percentage of resistance training\nsessions to weekly sessions in the SPP\nNext, the athlete progresses into the\nSPP, similar to the preseason phase\nin team sp", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "g Enhances Economy in Runners\nVOLUME 42 | NUMBER 1 | FEBRUARY 2020\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditioning Association. Unauthorized reproduction of this article is prohibited."}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0007_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7e49183cb7b9599a", "text_span_hash": "7e49183cb7b9599a", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Exercise selection recommendations for resistance training sessions per day of the week for competitive distance\nrunners based on training age in a periodized macrocycle (4,10,36)\nBB 5 barbell; CMJ 5 countermovement jump; DB 5 dumbbell; DL 5 deadlift", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Exercise selection recommendations for resistance training sessions per day of the week for competitive distance\nrunners based on training age in a periodized macrocycle (4,10,36)\nBB 5 barbell; CMJ 5 countermovement jump; DB 5 dumbbell; DL 5 deadlift; GPP 5 general preparatory phase; PF 5 plantarﬂexion; RDL 5\nRomanian deadlift; SL 5 single leg; SLDL 5 single-leg deadlift; SPP 5 speciﬁc preparatory phase.\nStrength and Conditioning Journal | www.nsca-scj.com\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditioning Association. Unauthorized reproduction of this article is prohibited.", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Exercise selection recommendations for resistance training sessions per day of the week for competitive distance\nrunners based on training age in a periodized macrocycle (4,10,36)\nBB 5 barbell; CMJ 5 countermovement jump; DB 5 dumbbell; DL 5 deadlift"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0008_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "170105dc7574bd81", "text_span_hash": "170105dc7574bd81", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ons, drills, or\nchanges in gait pattern should be intro-\nduced in this phase. Variability in run-\nning surfaces, inclines, percentage of\nrace pace, and distances will be high\nin this phase. When runners enter the\nSPP, they should have a strong founda", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "gradually increase in volume and inten-\nsity. Moderate-distance and moderate-\neffort runs will build exercise capacity\nand conditioning while allowing the\nathlete to recover from the last peaking\nphase. Any gait alterations, drills, or\nchanges in gait pattern should be intro-\nduced in this phase. Variability in run-\nning surfaces, inclines, percentage of\nrace pace, and distances will be high\nin this phase. When runners enter the\nSPP, they should have a strong founda-\ntion of conditioning and will begin to\ntransition to greater sessions that are\nspeciﬁc to their individual race distance,\npace, and surface. The hallmarks of\na decrease in variability, increase in\nspeciﬁcity, decrease in volume, and\nincrease in intensity of runs that will\ncontinue until the peaking phase. In\nthe taper/peaking phase, individual dif-\nrates of recovery will dictate the mag-\nnitude of deload that will occur.\nThe prescription of endurance running\ntraining intensity distribution is debated\nin the literature, and variables for deter-\nmining training zones are highly diverse\nin research and in practice. Physiologi-\ncal factors such as LT, ventilatory\nthreshold, percentage of V̇O2max, and\npercentage of maximum heart rate,\nand subjective factors such as session\nrating of perceived exertion are com-\nmonly used. Recommendations for the\nintensity distribution of endurance train-\ning sessions in this review will be based\non the race-paced zones 1 through 3;\nhow", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ons, drills, or\nchanges in gait pattern should be intro-\nduced in this phase. Variability in run-\nning surfaces, inclines, percentage of\nrace pace, and distances will be high\nin this phase. When runners enter the\nSPP, they should have a strong founda"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0008_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "26c6d1249329eaec", "text_span_hash": "26c6d1249329eaec", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ey should have a strong founda-\ntion of conditioning and will begin to\ntransition to greater sessions that are\nspeciﬁc to their individual race distance,\npace, and surface. The hallmarks of\na decrease in variability, increase in\nspeciﬁcity, decrease", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "gradually increase in volume and inten-\nsity. Moderate-distance and moderate-\neffort runs will build exercise capacity\nand conditioning while allowing the\nathlete to recover from the last peaking\nphase. Any gait alterations, drills, or\nchanges in gait pattern should be intro-\nduced in this phase. Variability in run-\nning surfaces, inclines, percentage of\nrace pace, and distances will be high\nin this phase. When runners enter the\nSPP, they should have a strong founda-\ntion of conditioning and will begin to\ntransition to greater sessions that are\nspeciﬁc to their individual race distance,\npace, and surface. The hallmarks of\na decrease in variability, increase in\nspeciﬁcity, decrease in volume, and\nincrease in intensity of runs that will\ncontinue until the peaking phase. In\nthe taper/peaking phase, individual dif-\nrates of recovery will dictate the mag-\nnitude of deload that will occur.\nThe prescription of endurance running\ntraining intensity distribution is debated\nin the literature, and variables for deter-\nmining training zones are highly diverse\nin research and in practice. Physiologi-\ncal factors such as LT, ventilatory\nthreshold, percentage of V̇O2max, and\npercentage of maximum heart rate,\nand subjective factors such as session\nrating of perceived exertion are com-\nmonly used. Recommendations for the\nintensity distribution of endurance train-\ning sessions in this review will be based\non the race-paced zones 1 through 3;\nhow", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ey should have a strong founda-\ntion of conditioning and will begin to\ntransition to greater sessions that are\nspeciﬁc to their individual race distance,\npace, and surface. The hallmarks of\na decrease in variability, increase in\nspeciﬁcity, decrease"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0008_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c3d53006e65f0fe5", "text_span_hash": "c3d53006e65f0fe5", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "rease in\nspeciﬁcity, decrease in volume, and\nincrease in intensity of runs that will\ncontinue until the peaking phase. In\nthe taper/peaking phase, individual dif-\nrates of recovery will dictate the mag-\nnitude of deload that will occur.\nThe prescript", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "gradually increase in volume and inten-\nsity. Moderate-distance and moderate-\neffort runs will build exercise capacity\nand conditioning while allowing the\nathlete to recover from the last peaking\nphase. Any gait alterations, drills, or\nchanges in gait pattern should be intro-\nduced in this phase. Variability in run-\nning surfaces, inclines, percentage of\nrace pace, and distances will be high\nin this phase. When runners enter the\nSPP, they should have a strong founda-\ntion of conditioning and will begin to\ntransition to greater sessions that are\nspeciﬁc to their individual race distance,\npace, and surface. The hallmarks of\na decrease in variability, increase in\nspeciﬁcity, decrease in volume, and\nincrease in intensity of runs that will\ncontinue until the peaking phase. In\nthe taper/peaking phase, individual dif-\nrates of recovery will dictate the mag-\nnitude of deload that will occur.\nThe prescription of endurance running\ntraining intensity distribution is debated\nin the literature, and variables for deter-\nmining training zones are highly diverse\nin research and in practice. Physiologi-\ncal factors such as LT, ventilatory\nthreshold, percentage of V̇O2max, and\npercentage of maximum heart rate,\nand subjective factors such as session\nrating of perceived exertion are com-\nmonly used. Recommendations for the\nintensity distribution of endurance train-\ning sessions in this review will be based\non the race-paced zones 1 through 3;\nhow", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "rease in\nspeciﬁcity, decrease in volume, and\nincrease in intensity of runs that will\ncontinue until the peaking phase. In\nthe taper/peaking phase, individual dif-\nrates of recovery will dictate the mag-\nnitude of deload that will occur.\nThe prescript"}
