e\nin research and in practice. Physiologi-\ncal factors such as LT, ventilatory\nthreshold, percentage of V̇O2max, and\npercentage of maximum heart rate,\nand subjective factors such as session\nrating of perceived exertion are com-\nmonly used. Recommendations for the\nintensity distribution of endurance train-\ning sessions in this review will be based\non the race-paced zones 1 through 3;\nhow", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "that will occur.\nThe prescription of endurance running\ntraining intensity distribution is debated\nin the literature, and variables for deter-\nmining training zones are highly diverse\nin research and in practice. Physiologi-\ncal factors such as LT, ve"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0008_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9fee40cebe083b9f", "text_span_hash": "9fee40cebe083b9f", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "gi-\ncal factors such as LT, ventilatory\nthreshold, percentage of V̇O2max, and\npercentage of maximum heart rate,\nand subjective factors such as session\nrating of perceived exertion are com-\nmonly used. Recommendations for the\nintensity distribution of", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "gradually increase in volume and inten-\nsity. Moderate-distance and moderate-\neffort runs will build exercise capacity\nand conditioning while allowing the\nathlete to recover from the last peaking\nphase. Any gait alterations, drills, or\nchanges in gait pattern should be intro-\nduced in this phase. Variability in run-\nning surfaces, inclines, percentage of\nrace pace, and distances will be high\nin this phase. When runners enter the\nSPP, they should have a strong founda-\ntion of conditioning and will begin to\ntransition to greater sessions that are\nspeciﬁc to their individual race distance,\npace, and surface. The hallmarks of\na decrease in variability, increase in\nspeciﬁcity, decrease in volume, and\nincrease in intensity of runs that will\ncontinue until the peaking phase. In\nthe taper/peaking phase, individual dif-\nrates of recovery will dictate the mag-\nnitude of deload that will occur.\nThe prescription of endurance running\ntraining intensity distribution is debated\nin the literature, and variables for deter-\nmining training zones are highly diverse\nin research and in practice. Physiologi-\ncal factors such as LT, ventilatory\nthreshold, percentage of V̇O2max, and\npercentage of maximum heart rate,\nand subjective factors such as session\nrating of perceived exertion are com-\nmonly used. Recommendations for the\nintensity distribution of endurance train-\ning sessions in this review will be based\non the race-paced zones 1 through 3;\nhow", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "gi-\ncal factors such as LT, ventilatory\nthreshold, percentage of V̇O2max, and\npercentage of maximum heart rate,\nand subjective factors such as session\nrating of perceived exertion are com-\nmonly used. Recommendations for the\nintensity distribution of"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0008_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "da3e5fd3cd17e1ea", "text_span_hash": "da3e5fd3cd17e1ea", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "d on\nheart rate. A review examining training\nintensity distribution by Kenneally et al.\nin 2018, recommends that zones based\non race pace offer an easily understand-\nable method of programming intensity\nand a compatible way to compare\nendurance train", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "gradually increase in volume and inten-\nsity. Moderate-distance and moderate-\neffort runs will build exercise capacity\nand conditioning while allowing the\nathlete to recover from the last peaking\nphase. Any gait alterations, drills, or\nchanges in gait pattern should be intro-\nduced in this phase. Variability in run-\nning surfaces, inclines, percentage of\nrace pace, and distances will be high\nin this phase. When runners enter the\nSPP, they should have a strong founda-\ntion of conditioning and will begin to\ntransition to greater sessions that are\nspeciﬁc to their individual race distance,\npace, and surface. The hallmarks of\na decrease in variability, increase in\nspeciﬁcity, decrease in volume, and\nincrease in intensity of runs that will\ncontinue until the peaking phase. In\nthe taper/peaking phase, individual dif-\nrates of recovery will dictate the mag-\nnitude of deload that will occur.\nThe prescription of endurance running\ntraining intensity distribution is debated\nin the literature, and variables for deter-\nmining training zones are highly diverse\nin research and in practice. Physiologi-\ncal factors such as LT, ventilatory\nthreshold, percentage of V̇O2max, and\npercentage of maximum heart rate,\nand subjective factors such as session\nrating of perceived exertion are com-\nmonly used. Recommendations for the\nintensity distribution of endurance train-\ning sessions in this review will be based\non the race-paced zones 1 through 3;\nhow", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "d on\nheart rate. A review examining training\nintensity distribution by Kenneally et al.\nin 2018, recommends that zones based\non race pace offer an easily understand-\nable method of programming intensity\nand a compatible way to compare\nendurance train"}
