erse\nin research and in practice. Physiologi-\ncal factors such as LT, ventilatory\nthreshold, percentage of V̇O2max, and\npercentage of maximum heart rate,\nand subjective factors such as session\nrating of perceived exertion are com-\nmonly used. Recommendations for the\nintensity distribution of endurance train-\ning sessions in this review will be based\non the race-paced zones 1 through 3;\nhow", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ce training sessions based on the periodized phase of training for\ncompetitive endurance runners\nMesocycle 3 (4–6 wk)\nImprove rate of force\ndevelopment, maximal\nstrength development,\nand increase peak power\n3 exercises, 2–4 sets\n3 exercises, 2–4 sets"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0008_c0017", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e2181ae73c75d2ad", "text_span_hash": "e2181ae73c75d2ad", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 17, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Enhances Economy in Runners\nVOLUME 42 | NUMBER 1 | FEBRUARY 2020\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditioning Association. Unauthorized reproduction of this article is prohibited.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "gradually increase in volume and inten-\nsity. Moderate-distance and moderate-\neffort runs will build exercise capacity\nand conditioning while allowing the\nathlete to recover from the last peaking\nphase. Any gait alterations, drills, or\nchanges in gait pattern should be intro-\nduced in this phase. Variability in run-\nning surfaces, inclines, percentage of\nrace pace, and distances will be high\nin this phase. When runners enter the\nSPP, they should have a strong founda-\ntion of conditioning and will begin to\ntransition to greater sessions that are\nspeciﬁc to their individual race distance,\npace, and surface. The hallmarks of\na decrease in variability, increase in\nspeciﬁcity, decrease in volume, and\nincrease in intensity of runs that will\ncontinue until the peaking phase. In\nthe taper/peaking phase, individual dif-\nrates of recovery will dictate the mag-\nnitude of deload that will occur.\nThe prescription of endurance running\ntraining intensity distribution is debated\nin the literature, and variables for deter-\nmining training zones are highly diverse\nin research and in practice. Physiologi-\ncal factors such as LT, ventilatory\nthreshold, percentage of V̇O2max, and\npercentage of maximum heart rate,\nand subjective factors such as session\nrating of perceived exertion are com-\nmonly used. Recommendations for the\nintensity distribution of endurance train-\ning sessions in this review will be based\non the race-paced zones 1 through 3;\nhow", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Enhances Economy in Runners\nVOLUME 42 | NUMBER 1 | FEBRUARY 2020\nCopyright © National Strength and Conditioning Association. Unauthorized reproduction of this article is prohibited."}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0009_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "10878c0808ed9f7e", "text_span_hash": "10878c0808ed9f7e", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "consistently proves to be less effective in\npeer-reviewed evidence; however, there\nis anecdotal evidence promoting train-\ning in higher volumes (.20%) in the\nmoderate-intensity zone (23).\nThe zones for the race-paced approach\nwere determined in the r", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "consistently proves to be less effective in\npeer-reviewed evidence; however, there\nis anecdotal evidence promoting train-\ning in higher volumes (.20%) in the\nmoderate-intensity zone (23).\nThe zones for the race-paced approach\nwere determined in the review as fol-\nlows: zone 1, volume performed ,95%\nof goal race pace; zone 2, volume per-\nformed between 95 and 105% of goal\nrace pace; and zone 3, volume per-\nformed at .105% of goal race pace\n(23). Table 2 offers an example of how\nto implement these race-paced zones of\ntraining with resistance training sessions\nin a periodized macrocycle for endur-\nIn an observational study of marathon-\ners and ultramarathoners, Tanda states\nthat training speed (pace) was the\nmain variable predicting 42-km race\nfound to be the main factor predicting\n100-km race times (42). Kenneally’s\nreview reﬂects a similar message in\nwhich shorter races require greater vol-\nume of training to be performed at fast-\ner paces than race pace, and longer\nraces require increasing volumes in\nand around race pace (23). These con-\nclusions may suggest that strength and\na greater number of sessions in zones\n2 and 3 for 10,000 m to half-marathon\ndistance runners and a greater number\nof sessions in zone 2 for marathoners,\nand focus on greater overall volume of\nrunning training in zone 1 for ultra-\nmarathoners. Mindfully, coaches and\nathletes must take excessive running\nvolume into consideration with the\ntraining due to the", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "consistently proves to be less effective in\npeer-reviewed evidence; however, there\nis anecdotal evidence promoting train-\ning in higher volumes (.20%) in the\nmoderate-intensity zone (23).\nThe zones for the race-paced approach\nwere determined in the r"}
