ions may suggest that strength and\na greater number of sessions in zones\n2 and 3 for 10,000 m to half-marathon\ndistance runners and a greater number\nof sessions in zone 2 for marathoners,\nand focus on greater overall volume of\nrunning training in zone 1 for ultra-\nmarathoners. Mindfully, coaches and\nathletes must take excessive running\nvolume into consideration with the\ntraining due to the", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ce\n(23). Table 2 offers an example of how\nto implement these race-paced zones of\ntraining with resistance training sessions\nin a periodized macrocycle for endur-\nIn an observational study of marathon-\ners and ultramarathoners, Tanda states\nthat train"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0009_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "cb5b420db52442e5", "text_span_hash": "cb5b420db52442e5", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "oners, Tanda states\nthat training speed (pace) was the\nmain variable predicting 42-km race\nfound to be the main factor predicting\n100-km race times (42). Kenneally’s\nreview reﬂects a similar message in\nwhich shorter races require greater vol-\nume of", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "consistently proves to be less effective in\npeer-reviewed evidence; however, there\nis anecdotal evidence promoting train-\ning in higher volumes (.20%) in the\nmoderate-intensity zone (23).\nThe zones for the race-paced approach\nwere determined in the review as fol-\nlows: zone 1, volume performed ,95%\nof goal race pace; zone 2, volume per-\nformed between 95 and 105% of goal\nrace pace; and zone 3, volume per-\nformed at .105% of goal race pace\n(23). Table 2 offers an example of how\nto implement these race-paced zones of\ntraining with resistance training sessions\nin a periodized macrocycle for endur-\nIn an observational study of marathon-\ners and ultramarathoners, Tanda states\nthat training speed (pace) was the\nmain variable predicting 42-km race\nfound to be the main factor predicting\n100-km race times (42). Kenneally’s\nreview reﬂects a similar message in\nwhich shorter races require greater vol-\nume of training to be performed at fast-\ner paces than race pace, and longer\nraces require increasing volumes in\nand around race pace (23). These con-\nclusions may suggest that strength and\na greater number of sessions in zones\n2 and 3 for 10,000 m to half-marathon\ndistance runners and a greater number\nof sessions in zone 2 for marathoners,\nand focus on greater overall volume of\nrunning training in zone 1 for ultra-\nmarathoners. Mindfully, coaches and\nathletes must take excessive running\nvolume into consideration with the\ntraining due to the", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "oners, Tanda states\nthat training speed (pace) was the\nmain variable predicting 42-km race\nfound to be the main factor predicting\n100-km race times (42). Kenneally’s\nreview reﬂects a similar message in\nwhich shorter races require greater vol-\nume of"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0009_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "19a51ec8a18c96b8", "text_span_hash": "19a51ec8a18c96b8", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "s require greater vol-\nume of training to be performed at fast-\ner paces than race pace, and longer\nraces require increasing volumes in\nand around race pace (23). These con-\nclusions may suggest that strength and\na greater number of sessions in zones", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "consistently proves to be less effective in\npeer-reviewed evidence; however, there\nis anecdotal evidence promoting train-\ning in higher volumes (.20%) in the\nmoderate-intensity zone (23).\nThe zones for the race-paced approach\nwere determined in the review as fol-\nlows: zone 1, volume performed ,95%\nof goal race pace; zone 2, volume per-\nformed between 95 and 105% of goal\nrace pace; and zone 3, volume per-\nformed at .105% of goal race pace\n(23). Table 2 offers an example of how\nto implement these race-paced zones of\ntraining with resistance training sessions\nin a periodized macrocycle for endur-\nIn an observational study of marathon-\ners and ultramarathoners, Tanda states\nthat training speed (pace) was the\nmain variable predicting 42-km race\nfound to be the main factor predicting\n100-km race times (42). Kenneally’s\nreview reﬂects a similar message in\nwhich shorter races require greater vol-\nume of training to be performed at fast-\ner paces than race pace, and longer\nraces require increasing volumes in\nand around race pace (23). These con-\nclusions may suggest that strength and\na greater number of sessions in zones\n2 and 3 for 10,000 m to half-marathon\ndistance runners and a greater number\nof sessions in zone 2 for marathoners,\nand focus on greater overall volume of\nrunning training in zone 1 for ultra-\nmarathoners. Mindfully, coaches and\nathletes must take excessive running\nvolume into consideration with the\ntraining due to the", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "s require greater vol-\nume of training to be performed at fast-\ner paces than race pace, and longer\nraces require increasing volumes in\nand around race pace (23). These con-\nclusions may suggest that strength and\na greater number of sessions in zones"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0009_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "cfc33fb07694f18e", "text_span_hash": "cfc33fb07694f18e", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "er number of sessions in zones\n2 and 3 for 10,000 m to half-marathon\ndistance runners and a greater number\nof sessions in zone 2 for marathoners,\nand focus on greater overall volume of\nrunning training in zone 1 for ultra-\nmarathoners. Mindfully, coa", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "consistently proves to be less effective in\npeer-reviewed evidence; however, there\nis anecdotal evidence promoting train-\ning in higher volumes (.20%) in the\nmoderate-intensity zone (23).\nThe zones for the race-paced approach\nwere determined in the review as fol-\nlows: zone 1, volume performed ,95%\nof goal race pace; zone 2, volume per-\nformed between 95 and 105% of goal\nrace pace; and zone 3, volume per-\nformed at .105% of goal race pace\n(23). Table 2 offers an example of how\nto implement these race-paced zones of\ntraining with resistance training sessions\nin a periodized macrocycle for endur-\nIn an observational study of marathon-\ners and ultramarathoners, Tanda states\nthat training speed (pace) was the\nmain variable predicting 42-km race\nfound to be the main factor predicting\n100-km race times (42). Kenneally’s\nreview reﬂects a similar message in\nwhich shorter races require greater vol-\nume of training to be performed at fast-\ner paces than race pace, and longer\nraces require increasing volumes in\nand around race pace (23). These con-\nclusions may suggest that strength and\na greater number of sessions in zones\n2 and 3 for 10,000 m to half-marathon\ndistance runners and a greater number\nof sessions in zone 2 for marathoners,\nand focus on greater overall volume of\nrunning training in zone 1 for ultra-\nmarathoners. Mindfully, coaches and\nathletes must take excessive running\nvolume into consideration with the\ntraining due to the", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "er number of sessions in zones\n2 and 3 for 10,000 m to half-marathon\ndistance runners and a greater number\nof sessions in zone 2 for marathoners,\nand focus on greater overall volume of\nrunning training in zone 1 for ultra-\nmarathoners. Mindfully, coa"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0009_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "fc5889de49a9b35f", "text_span_hash": "fc5889de49a9b35f", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "g and\ncompetitive distance runners is rec-\nommended due to the positive perfor-\nmance effects in RunE. Negligible\nchanges in body composition will be\nobserved with the addition of resis-\nresistance training, explosive strength,\nand reactive strength", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "consistently proves to be less effective in\npeer-reviewed evidence; however, there\nis anecdotal evidence promoting train-\ning in higher volumes (.20%) in the\nmoderate-intensity zone (23).\nThe zones for the race-paced approach\nwere determined in the review as fol-\nlows: zone 1, volume performed ,95%\nof goal race pace; zone 2, volume per-\nformed between 95 and 105% of goal\nrace pace; and zone 3, volume per-\nformed at .105% of goal race pace\n(23). Table 2 offers an example of how\nto implement these race-paced zones of\ntraining with resistance training sessions\nin a periodized macrocycle for endur-\nIn an observational study of marathon-\ners and ultramarathoners, Tanda states\nthat training speed (pace) was the\nmain variable predicting 42-km race\nfound to be the main factor predicting\n100-km race times (42). Kenneally’s\nreview reﬂects a similar message in\nwhich shorter races require greater vol-\nume of training to be performed at fast-\ner paces than race pace, and longer\nraces require increasing volumes in\nand around race pace (23). These con-\nclusions may suggest that strength and\na greater number of sessions in zones\n2 and 3 for 10,000 m to half-marathon\ndistance runners and a greater number\nof sessions in zone 2 for marathoners,\nand focus on greater overall volume of\nrunning training in zone 1 for ultra-\nmarathoners. Mindfully, coaches and\nathletes must take excessive running\nvolume into consideration with the\ntraining due to the", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "g and\ncompetitive distance runners is rec-\nommended due to the positive perfor-\nmance effects in RunE. Negligible\nchanges in body composition will be\nobserved with the addition of resis-\nresistance training, explosive strength,\nand reactive strength"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0009_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "25893cdf411af963", "text_span_hash": "25893cdf411af963", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "in previously trained\nendurance runners: A systematic review.\nBaechle TR and Earle RW. Essentials of\nStrength Training and Conditioning: Hum\nKinetics Publishers, Champaign, IL, 2008.\nBalsalobre-Fernandez C, Santos-\nConcejero J, and Grivas GV. Effect", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "consistently proves to be less effective in\npeer-reviewed evidence; however, there\nis anecdotal evidence promoting train-\ning in higher volumes (.20%) in the\nmoderate-intensity zone (23).\nThe zones for the race-paced approach\nwere determined in the review as fol-\nlows: zone 1, volume performed ,95%\nof goal race pace; zone 2, volume per-\nformed between 95 and 105% of goal\nrace pace; and zone 3, volume per-\nformed at .105% of goal race pace\n(23). Table 2 offers an example of how\nto implement these race-paced zones of\ntraining with resistance training sessions\nin a periodized macrocycle for endur-\nIn an observational study of marathon-\ners and ultramarathoners, Tanda states\nthat training speed (pace) was the\nmain variable predicting 42-km race\nfound to be the main factor predicting\n100-km race times (42). Kenneally’s\nreview reﬂects a similar message in\nwhich shorter races require greater vol-\nume of training to be performed at fast-\ner paces than race pace, and longer\nraces require increasing volumes in\nand around race pace (23). These con-\nclusions may suggest that strength and\na greater number of sessions in zones\n2 and 3 for 10,000 m to half-marathon\ndistance runners and a greater number\nof sessions in zone 2 for marathoners,\nand focus on greater overall volume of\nrunning training in zone 1 for ultra-\nmarathoners. Mindfully, coaches and\nathletes must take excessive running\nvolume into consideration with the\ntraining due to the", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "in previously trained\nendurance runners: A systematic review.\nBaechle TR and Earle RW. Essentials of\nStrength Training and Conditioning: Hum\nKinetics Publishers, Champaign, IL, 2008.\nBalsalobre-Fernandez C, Santos-\nConcejero J, and Grivas GV. Effect"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0009_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0aef2cc07b0e624d", "text_span_hash": "0aef2cc07b0e624d", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ejero J, and Grivas GV. Effects of\nstrength training on running economy in\nhighly trained runners: A systematic review\nwith meta-analysis of controlled trials.\nJ Strength Cond Res 30: 2361–2368,\nBarnes KR and Kilding AE. Strategies to\nimprove running", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "consistently proves to be less effective in\npeer-reviewed evidence; however, there\nis anecdotal evidence promoting train-\ning in higher volumes (.20%) in the\nmoderate-intensity zone (23).\nThe zones for the race-paced approach\nwere determined in the review as fol-\nlows: zone 1, volume performed ,95%\nof goal race pace; zone 2, volume per-\nformed between 95 and 105% of goal\nrace pace; and zone 3, volume per-\nformed at .105% of goal race pace\n(23). Table 2 offers an example of how\nto implement these race-paced zones of\ntraining with resistance training sessions\nin a periodized macrocycle for endur-\nIn an observational study of marathon-\ners and ultramarathoners, Tanda states\nthat training speed (pace) was the\nmain variable predicting 42-km race\nfound to be the main factor predicting\n100-km race times (42). Kenneally’s\nreview reﬂects a similar message in\nwhich shorter races require greater vol-\nume of training to be performed at fast-\ner paces than race pace, and longer\nraces require increasing volumes in\nand around race pace (23). These con-\nclusions may suggest that strength and\na greater number of sessions in zones\n2 and 3 for 10,000 m to half-marathon\ndistance runners and a greater number\nof sessions in zone 2 for marathoners,\nand focus on greater overall volume of\nrunning training in zone 1 for ultra-\nmarathoners. Mindfully, coaches and\nathletes must take excessive running\nvolume into consideration with the\ntraining due to the", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ejero J, and Grivas GV. Effects of\nstrength training on running economy in\nhighly trained runners: A systematic review\nwith meta-analysis of controlled trials.\nJ Strength Cond Res 30: 2361–2368,\nBarnes KR and Kilding AE. Strategies to\nimprove running"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0009_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "77cbfad37e8165a9", "text_span_hash": "77cbfad37e8165a9", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Strategies to\nimprove running economy. Sports Med 45:\nBeattie K, Carson BP, Lyons M, Rossiter A,\nand Kenny IC. The effect of strength\ntraining on performance indicators in\ndistance runners. J Strength Cond Res 31:\nBerryman N, Maurel DB, and Bosquet", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "consistently proves to be less effective in\npeer-reviewed evidence; however, there\nis anecdotal evidence promoting train-\ning in higher volumes (.20%) in the\nmoderate-intensity zone (23).\nThe zones for the race-paced approach\nwere determined in the review as fol-\nlows: zone 1, volume performed ,95%\nof goal race pace; zone 2, volume per-\nformed between 95 and 105% of goal\nrace pace; and zone 3, volume per-\nformed at .105% of goal race pace\n(23). Table 2 offers an example of how\nto implement these race-paced zones of\ntraining with resistance training sessions\nin a periodized macrocycle for endur-\nIn an observational study of marathon-\ners and ultramarathoners, Tanda states\nthat training speed (pace) was the\nmain variable predicting 42-km race\nfound to be the main factor predicting\n100-km race times (42). Kenneally’s\nreview reﬂects a similar message in\nwhich shorter races require greater vol-\nume of training to be performed at fast-\ner paces than race pace, and longer\nraces require increasing volumes in\nand around race pace (23). These con-\nclusions may suggest that strength and\na greater number of sessions in zones\n2 and 3 for 10,000 m to half-marathon\ndistance runners and a greater number\nof sessions in zone 2 for marathoners,\nand focus on greater overall volume of\nrunning training in zone 1 for ultra-\nmarathoners. Mindfully, coaches and\nathletes must take excessive running\nvolume into consideration with the\ntraining due to the", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Strategies to\nimprove running economy. Sports Med 45:\nBeattie K, Carson BP, Lyons M, Rossiter A,\nand Kenny IC. The effect of strength\ntraining on performance indicators in\ndistance runners. J Strength Cond Res 31:\nBerryman N, Maurel DB, and Bosquet"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0009_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8bffc9977502b558", "text_span_hash": "8bffc9977502b558", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "man N, Maurel DB, and Bosquet L.\nEffect of plyometric vs. dynamic weight\ntraining on the energy cost of running.\nJ Strength Cond Res 24: 1818–1825,\nBerryman N, Mujika I, Arvisais D, et al.\nStrength training for middle- and long-\ndistance performance:", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "consistently proves to be less effective in\npeer-reviewed evidence; however, there\nis anecdotal evidence promoting train-\ning in higher volumes (.20%) in the\nmoderate-intensity zone (23).\nThe zones for the race-paced approach\nwere determined in the review as fol-\nlows: zone 1, volume performed ,95%\nof goal race pace; zone 2, volume per-\nformed between 95 and 105% of goal\nrace pace; and zone 3, volume per-\nformed at .105% of goal race pace\n(23). Table 2 offers an example of how\nto implement these race-paced zones of\ntraining with resistance training sessions\nin a periodized macrocycle for endur-\nIn an observational study of marathon-\ners and ultramarathoners, Tanda states\nthat training speed (pace) was the\nmain variable predicting 42-km race\nfound to be the main factor predicting\n100-km race times (42). Kenneally’s\nreview reﬂects a similar message in\nwhich shorter races require greater vol-\nume of training to be performed at fast-\ner paces than race pace, and longer\nraces require increasing volumes in\nand around race pace (23). These con-\nclusions may suggest that strength and\na greater number of sessions in zones\n2 and 3 for 10,000 m to half-marathon\ndistance runners and a greater number\nof sessions in zone 2 for marathoners,\nand focus on greater overall volume of\nrunning training in zone 1 for ultra-\nmarathoners. Mindfully, coaches and\nathletes must take excessive running\nvolume into consideration with the\ntraining due to the", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "man N, Maurel DB, and Bosquet L.\nEffect of plyometric vs. dynamic weight\ntraining on the energy cost of running.\nJ Strength Cond Res 24: 1818–1825,\nBerryman N, Mujika I, Arvisais D, et al.\nStrength training for middle- and long-\ndistance performance:"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0009_c0016", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "52aec9c1ee570ecb", "text_span_hash": "52aec9c1ee570ecb", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 16, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "nd long-\ndistance performance: A meta-analysis. Int\nJ Sports Physiol Perform 13: 57–63, 2018.\nBertuzzi R, Pasqua LA, Bueno S, et al.\nStrength-training with whole-body vibration\nin long-distance runners: A randomized\nBlagrove RC, Howatson G, and Hayes", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "consistently proves to be less effective in\npeer-reviewed evidence; however, there\nis anecdotal evidence promoting train-\ning in higher volumes (.20%) in the\nmoderate-intensity zone (23).\nThe zones for the race-paced approach\nwere determined in the review as fol-\nlows: zone 1, volume performed ,95%\nof goal race pace; zone 2, volume per-\nformed between 95 and 105% of goal\nrace pace; and zone 3, volume per-\nformed at .105% of goal race pace\n(23). Table 2 offers an example of how\nto implement these race-paced zones of\ntraining with resistance training sessions\nin a periodized macrocycle for endur-\nIn an observational study of marathon-\ners and ultramarathoners, Tanda states\nthat training speed (pace) was the\nmain variable predicting 42-km race\nfound to be the main factor predicting\n100-km race times (42). Kenneally’s\nreview reﬂects a similar message in\nwhich shorter races require greater vol-\nume of training to be performed at fast-\ner paces than race pace, and longer\nraces require increasing volumes in\nand around race pace (23). These con-\nclusions may suggest that strength and\na greater number of sessions in zones\n2 and 3 for 10,000 m to half-marathon\ndistance runners and a greater number\nof sessions in zone 2 for marathoners,\nand focus on greater overall volume of\nrunning training in zone 1 for ultra-\nmarathoners. Mindfully, coaches and\nathletes must take excessive running\nvolume into consideration with the\ntraining due to the", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "nd long-\ndistance performance: A meta-analysis. Int\nJ Sports Physiol Perform 13: 57–63, 2018.\nBertuzzi R, Pasqua LA, Bueno S, et al.\nStrength-training with whole-body vibration\nin long-distance runners: A randomized\nBlagrove RC, Howatson G, and Hayes"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0009_c0017", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "031e4e3edd87ea60", "text_span_hash": "031e4e3edd87ea60", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 17, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "rove RC, Howatson G, and Hayes PR.\nEffects of strength training on the physiological\ndeterminants of middle- and long-distance\nrunning performance: A systematic review.\nBonacci J, Green D, Saunders PU, et al.\nPlyometric training as an intervention to", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "consistently proves to be less effective in\npeer-reviewed evidence; however, there\nis anecdotal evidence promoting train-\ning in higher volumes (.20%) in the\nmoderate-intensity zone (23).\nThe zones for the race-paced approach\nwere determined in the review as fol-\nlows: zone 1, volume performed ,95%\nof goal race pace; zone 2, volume per-\nformed between 95 and 105% of goal\nrace pace; and zone 3, volume per-\nformed at .105% of goal race pace\n(23). Table 2 offers an example of how\nto implement these race-paced zones of\ntraining with resistance training sessions\nin a periodized macrocycle for endur-\nIn an observational study of marathon-\ners and ultramarathoners, Tanda states\nthat training speed (pace) was the\nmain variable predicting 42-km race\nfound to be the main factor predicting\n100-km race times (42). Kenneally’s\nreview reﬂects a similar message in\nwhich shorter races require greater vol-\nume of training to be performed at fast-\ner paces than race pace, and longer\nraces require increasing volumes in\nand around race pace (23). These con-\nclusions may suggest that strength and\na greater number of sessions in zones\n2 and 3 for 10,000 m to half-marathon\ndistance runners and a greater number\nof sessions in zone 2 for marathoners,\nand focus on greater overall volume of\nrunning training in zone 1 for ultra-\nmarathoners. Mindfully, coaches and\nathletes must take excessive running\nvolume into consideration with the\ntraining due to the", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "rove RC, Howatson G, and Hayes PR.\nEffects of strength training on the physiological\ndeterminants of middle- and long-distance\nrunning performance: A systematic review.\nBonacci J, Green D, Saunders PU, et al.\nPlyometric training as an intervention to"}
