ions may suggest that strength and\na greater number of sessions in zones\n2 and 3 for 10,000 m to half-marathon\ndistance runners and a greater number\nof sessions in zone 2 for marathoners,\nand focus on greater overall volume of\nrunning training in zone 1 for ultra-\nmarathoners. Mindfully, coaches and\nathletes must take excessive running\nvolume into consideration with the\ntraining due to the", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "training as an intervention to\ncorrect altered neuromotor control during\nrunning after cycling in triathletes: A\npreliminary randomised controlled trial.\nDamasceno MV, Lima-Silva AE, Pasqua\nLA, et al. Effects of resistance training on\nneuromuscular c"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0009_c0019", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "45cb5cb4ff503cd9", "text_span_hash": "45cb5cb4ff503cd9", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 19, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ce training on\nneuromuscular characteristics and pacing\nduring 10-km running time trial. Eur J Appl\nDenadai BS, de Aguiar RA, de Lima LC,\nGreco CC, and Caputo F. Explosive training\nand heavy weight training are effective for\nimproving running economy", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "consistently proves to be less effective in\npeer-reviewed evidence; however, there\nis anecdotal evidence promoting train-\ning in higher volumes (.20%) in the\nmoderate-intensity zone (23).\nThe zones for the race-paced approach\nwere determined in the review as fol-\nlows: zone 1, volume performed ,95%\nof goal race pace; zone 2, volume per-\nformed between 95 and 105% of goal\nrace pace; and zone 3, volume per-\nformed at .105% of goal race pace\n(23). Table 2 offers an example of how\nto implement these race-paced zones of\ntraining with resistance training sessions\nin a periodized macrocycle for endur-\nIn an observational study of marathon-\ners and ultramarathoners, Tanda states\nthat training speed (pace) was the\nmain variable predicting 42-km race\nfound to be the main factor predicting\n100-km race times (42). Kenneally’s\nreview reﬂects a similar message in\nwhich shorter races require greater vol-\nume of training to be performed at fast-\ner paces than race pace, and longer\nraces require increasing volumes in\nand around race pace (23). These con-\nclusions may suggest that strength and\na greater number of sessions in zones\n2 and 3 for 10,000 m to half-marathon\ndistance runners and a greater number\nof sessions in zone 2 for marathoners,\nand focus on greater overall volume of\nrunning training in zone 1 for ultra-\nmarathoners. Mindfully, coaches and\nathletes must take excessive running\nvolume into consideration with the\ntraining due to the", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ce training on\nneuromuscular characteristics and pacing\nduring 10-km running time trial. Eur J Appl\nDenadai BS, de Aguiar RA, de Lima LC,\nGreco CC, and Caputo F. Explosive training\nand heavy weight training are effective for\nimproving running economy"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0009_c0020", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "d916bb54e0e55444", "text_span_hash": "d916bb54e0e55444", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 20, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "for\nimproving running economy in endurance\nathletes: A systematic review and meta-\nDoma K and Deakin GB. The effects of\nstrength training and endurance training\norder on running economy and\nperformance. Appl Physiol Nutr Metab 38:\nFerrauti A, Berger", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "consistently proves to be less effective in\npeer-reviewed evidence; however, there\nis anecdotal evidence promoting train-\ning in higher volumes (.20%) in the\nmoderate-intensity zone (23).\nThe zones for the race-paced approach\nwere determined in the review as fol-\nlows: zone 1, volume performed ,95%\nof goal race pace; zone 2, volume per-\nformed between 95 and 105% of goal\nrace pace; and zone 3, volume per-\nformed at .105% of goal race pace\n(23). Table 2 offers an example of how\nto implement these race-paced zones of\ntraining with resistance training sessions\nin a periodized macrocycle for endur-\nIn an observational study of marathon-\ners and ultramarathoners, Tanda states\nthat training speed (pace) was the\nmain variable predicting 42-km race\nfound to be the main factor predicting\n100-km race times (42). Kenneally’s\nreview reﬂects a similar message in\nwhich shorter races require greater vol-\nume of training to be performed at fast-\ner paces than race pace, and longer\nraces require increasing volumes in\nand around race pace (23). These con-\nclusions may suggest that strength and\na greater number of sessions in zones\n2 and 3 for 10,000 m to half-marathon\ndistance runners and a greater number\nof sessions in zone 2 for marathoners,\nand focus on greater overall volume of\nrunning training in zone 1 for ultra-\nmarathoners. Mindfully, coaches and\nathletes must take excessive running\nvolume into consideration with the\ntraining due to the", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "for\nimproving running economy in endurance\nathletes: A systematic review and meta-\nDoma K and Deakin GB. The effects of\nstrength training and endurance training\norder on running economy and\nperformance. Appl Physiol Nutr Metab 38:\nFerrauti A, Berger"}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0009_c0021", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f55b75838ad4642a", "text_span_hash": "f55b75838ad4642a", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 21, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "r Metab 38:\nFerrauti A, Bergermann M, and Fernandez-\nFernandez J. Effects of a concurrent\nstrength and endurance training on running\nperformance and running economy in\nrecreational marathon runners. J Strength\nFletcher JR, Esau SP, and MacIntosh BR.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "consistently proves to be less effective in\npeer-reviewed evidence; however, there\nis anecdotal evidence promoting train-\ning in higher volumes (.20%) in the\nmoderate-intensity zone (23).\nThe zones for the race-paced approach\nwere determined in the review as fol-\nlows: zone 1, volume performed ,95%\nof goal race pace; zone 2, volume per-\nformed between 95 and 105% of goal\nrace pace; and zone 3, volume per-\nformed at .105% of goal race pace\n(23). Table 2 offers an example of how\nto implement these race-paced zones of\ntraining with resistance training sessions\nin a periodized macrocycle for endur-\nIn an observational study of marathon-\ners and ultramarathoners, Tanda states\nthat training speed (pace) was the\nmain variable predicting 42-km race\nfound to be the main factor predicting\n100-km race times (42). Kenneally’s\nreview reﬂects a similar message in\nwhich shorter races require greater vol-\nume of training to be performed at fast-\ner paces than race pace, and longer\nraces require increasing volumes in\nand around race pace (23). These con-\nclusions may suggest that strength and\na greater number of sessions in zones\n2 and 3 for 10,000 m to half-marathon\ndistance runners and a greater number\nof sessions in zone 2 for marathoners,\nand focus on greater overall volume of\nrunning training in zone 1 for ultra-\nmarathoners. Mindfully, coaches and\nathletes must take excessive running\nvolume into consideration with the\ntraining due to the", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "r Metab 38:\nFerrauti A, Bergermann M, and Fernandez-\nFernandez J. Effects of a concurrent\nstrength and endurance training on running\nperformance and running economy in\nrecreational marathon runners. J Strength\nFletcher JR, Esau SP, and MacIntosh BR."}
{"chunk_id": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He_p0009_c0023", "source_registry_id": "src_269e7fdc06e1", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "070f60fceb408d21", "text_span_hash": "070f60fceb408d21", "source_file": "School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\School of Physical Therapy & Rehabilitation Sciences, USF He.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 23, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "2017.\nGiovanelli N, Taboga P, Rejc E, and Lazzer\nS. Effects of strength, explosive and\nplyometric training on energy cost of\nrunning in ultra-endurance athletes. Eur J\nStrength and Conditioning Journal | www.nsca-scj.com\nCopyright © National Strength", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "consistently proves to be less effective in\npeer-reviewed evidence; however, there\nis anecdotal evidence promoting train-\ning in higher volumes (.20%) in the\nmoderate-intensity zone (23).\nThe zones for the race-paced approach\nwere determined in the review as fol-\nlows: zone 1, volume performed ,95%\nof goal race pace; zone 2, volume per-\nformed between 95 and 105% of goal\nrace pace; and zone 3, volume per-\nformed at .105% of goal race pace\n(23). Table 2 offers an example of how\nto implement these race-paced zones of\ntraining with resistance training sessions\nin a periodized macrocycle for endur-\nIn an observational study of marathon-\ners and ultramarathoners, Tanda states\nthat training speed (pace) was the\nmain variable predicting 42-km race\nfound to be the main factor predicting\n100-km race times (42). Kenneally’s\nreview reﬂects a similar message in\nwhich shorter races require greater vol-\nume of training to be performed at fast-\ner paces than race pace, and longer\nraces require increasing volumes in\nand around race pace (23). These con-\nclusions may suggest that strength and\na greater number of sessions in zones\n2 and 3 for 10,000 m to half-marathon\ndistance runners and a greater number\nof sessions in zone 2 for marathoners,\nand focus on greater overall volume of\nrunning training in zone 1 for ultra-\nmarathoners. Mindfully, coaches and\nathletes must take excessive running\nvolume into consideration with the\ntraining due to the", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "2017.\nGiovanelli N, Taboga P, Rejc E, and Lazzer\nS. Effects of strength, explosive and\nplyometric training on energy cost of\nrunning in ultra-endurance athletes. Eur J\nStrength and Conditioning Journal | www.nsca-scj.com\nCopyright © National Strength"}
