e. Training\nintensification studies performed on already well-trained athletes do not provide\nany convincing evidence that a greater emphasis on high-intensity interval training\nin this highly trained athlete population gives long-term performance gains. The\npredominance of low-intens", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "e for Training\nIntensity and Duration Distribution\nin Endurance Athletes?\nSuccessful endurance training involves the manipulation of training intensity,\nduration, and frequency, with the implicit goals of maximizing performance,\nminimizing risk of ne"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0001_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "fa556c7755af6215", "text_span_hash": "fa556c7755af6215", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ormance,\nminimizing risk of negative training outcomes, and timing peak fitness and per-\nformances to be achieved when they matter most. Numerous descriptive studies of\nthe training characteristics of nationally or internationally competitive enduran", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "International Journal of Sports Physiology and Performance, 2010, 5, 276-291\n© Human Kinetics, Inc.\nStephen Seiler is with the Faculty of Health and Sport Sciences, University of Agder, Kristiansand,\nWhat is Best Practice for Training\nIntensity and Duration Distribution\nin Endurance Athletes?\nSuccessful endurance training involves the manipulation of training intensity,\nduration, and frequency, with the implicit goals of maximizing performance,\nminimizing risk of negative training outcomes, and timing peak fitness and per-\nformances to be achieved when they matter most. Numerous descriptive studies of\nthe training characteristics of nationally or internationally competitive endurance\nathletes training 10 to 13 times per week seem to converge on a typical intensity\ndistribution in which about 80% of training sessions are performed at low intensity\n(2 mM blood lactate), with about 20% dominated by periods of high-intensity\nwork, such as interval training at approx. 90% VO2max. Endurance athletes appear\nto self-organize toward a high-volume training approach with careful application\nof high-intensity training incorporated throughout the training cycle. Training\nintensification studies performed on already well-trained athletes do not provide\nany convincing evidence that a greater emphasis on high-intensity interval training\nin this highly trained athlete population gives long-term performance gains. The\npredominance of low-intens", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ormance,\nminimizing risk of negative training outcomes, and timing peak fitness and per-\nformances to be achieved when they matter most. Numerous descriptive studies of\nthe training characteristics of nationally or internationally competitive enduran"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0001_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5fbc3ba6e8740670", "text_span_hash": "5fbc3ba6e8740670", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "nationally competitive endurance\nathletes training 10 to 13 times per week seem to converge on a typical intensity\ndistribution in which about 80% of training sessions are performed at low intensity\n(2 mM blood lactate), with about 20% dominated by p", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "International Journal of Sports Physiology and Performance, 2010, 5, 276-291\n© Human Kinetics, Inc.\nStephen Seiler is with the Faculty of Health and Sport Sciences, University of Agder, Kristiansand,\nWhat is Best Practice for Training\nIntensity and Duration Distribution\nin Endurance Athletes?\nSuccessful endurance training involves the manipulation of training intensity,\nduration, and frequency, with the implicit goals of maximizing performance,\nminimizing risk of negative training outcomes, and timing peak fitness and per-\nformances to be achieved when they matter most. Numerous descriptive studies of\nthe training characteristics of nationally or internationally competitive endurance\nathletes training 10 to 13 times per week seem to converge on a typical intensity\ndistribution in which about 80% of training sessions are performed at low intensity\n(2 mM blood lactate), with about 20% dominated by periods of high-intensity\nwork, such as interval training at approx. 90% VO2max. Endurance athletes appear\nto self-organize toward a high-volume training approach with careful application\nof high-intensity training incorporated throughout the training cycle.