ndividual variation in the relationship\nbetween heart rate and blood lactate concentration, or activity-specific variation,\nsuch as the tendency for maximal steady-state concentrations of blood lactate to\nbe higher in activities activating less muscle mass.3,4 In the practical perfo", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "tensity than planned on hard days, relative to coach\nprescriptions.5 It is important to point out that integrated approaches that multiply\ntraining session time by a physiological or perceptual measure of intensity (yield-\ning TRIMPS6 or LOAD7,8) hav"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0002_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "23d7e37d19232fdc", "text_span_hash": "23d7e37d19232fdc", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "d-\ning TRIMPS6 or LOAD7,8) have also been developed and used to quantify training\nTable 1  Example of a five-zone intensity scale to prescribe and\nmonitor training of endurance athletes\nTypical accumulated duration\nNote. This scale is typical of inte", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training Intensity Distribution    277\nis to discuss the roles of training duration and training intensity in the long-term\nphysiological and performance development of endurance athletes.\nMeasuring Training Intensity\nA review of training intensity and duration issues in endurance training should begin\nwith some discussion of how these variables are quantified. Measuring exercise\nduration is straightforward. Training volume can be measured in terms of distance\nreadily comparable unit across endurance sports is effective training hours. Quan-\ntifying training intensity is more complicated. Describing and comparing training\nintensity distribution requires a common intensity scale. Most national sport govern-\ning bodies employ a guiding intensity scale based on ranges of heart rate relative\nto maximum and blood lactate concentration. Often, aerobic endurance training in\nthe intensity range of approximately 50% to 100% of VO2max is divided into five\nsomewhat arbitrary intensity zones. Table 1 gives as an example a scale used by the\nNorwegian Olympic Committee. Standardizing an intensity scale can be criticized\nbecause the approach fails to account for individual variation in the relationship\nbetween heart rate and blood lactate concentration, or activity-specific variation,\nsuch as the tendency for maximal steady-state concentrations of blood lactate to\nbe higher in activities activating less muscle mass.3,4 In the practical perfo", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "d-\ning TRIMPS6 or LOAD7,8) have also been developed and used to quantify training\nTable 1  Example of a five-zone intensity scale to prescribe and\nmonitor training of endurance athletes\nTypical accumulated duration\nNote. This scale is typical of inte"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0002_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2434e799429af32f", "text_span_hash": "2434e799429af32f", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "This scale is typical of intensity zone scales used for endurance training prescription and moni-\ntoring. The scale above was developed by the Norwegian Olympic Federation as a general guideline\nbased on years of testing of cross-country skiers, row", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training Intensity Distribution    277\nis to discuss the roles of training duration and training intensity in the long-term\nphysiological and performance development of endurance athletes.\nMeasuring Training Intensity\nA review of training intensity and duration issues in endurance training should begin\nwith some discussion of how these variables are quantified. Measuring exercise\nduration is straightforward. Training volume can be measured in terms of distance\nreadily comparable unit across endurance sports is effective training hours. Quan-\ntifying training intensity is more complicated. Describing and comparing training\nintensity distribution requires a common intensity scale. Most national sport govern-\ning bodies employ a guiding intensity scale based on ranges of heart rate relative\nto maximum and blood lactate concentration. Often, aerobic endurance training in\nthe intensity range of approximately 50% to 100% of VO2max is divided into five\nsomewhat arbitrary intensity zones. Table 1 gives as an example a scale used by the\nNorwegian Olympic Committee. Standardizing an intensity scale can be criticized\nbecause the approach fails to account for individual variation in the relationship\nbetween heart rate and blood lactate concentration, or activity-specific variation,\nsuch as the tendency for maximal steady-state concentrations of blood lactate to\nbe higher in activities activating less muscle mass.3,4 In the practical perfo", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "This scale is typical of intensity zone scales used for endurance training prescription and moni-\ntoring. The scale above was developed by the Norwegian Olympic Federation as a general guideline\nbased on years of testing of cross-country skiers, row"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0003_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7ac3a52ef8b37dd0", "text_span_hash": "7ac3a52ef8b37dd0", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "exposure. However, in this review, I will focus on training intensity distribution,\nand these integrated approaches will not be presented in detail.\nSeveral recent studies examining training intensity distribution9–11 or perfor-\nmance intensity distr", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "exposure. However, in this review, I will focus on training intensity distribution,\nand these integrated approaches will not be presented in detail.\nSeveral recent studies examining training intensity distribution9–11 or perfor-\nmance intensity distribution in multiday events12,13 have employed individually\ndetermined first and second ventilatory turn points to demarcate three intensity\nzones (Zone 1, Zone 2, and Zone 3; Figure 1). Intensity distribution studies based\non ventilatory threshold–derived zones are not directly comparable with the five-\nzone model, but what is typically identified as “lactate threshold intensity,” or the\napproximately 2 to 4 mM blood lactate concentration range, corresponds well in\npractice with the intensity zone demarcated by the first and second ventilatory turn\npoints. Thus, for practical purposes, the three-zone model and five-zone model\nhave common intensity anchor points around the lactate threshold. For well-trained\nathletes, I will use the term low-intensity training (LIT) to refer to work eliciting\na stable lactate concentration of less than approximately 2 mM. High-intensity\ntraining (HIT) will refer to training above maximum lactate steady-state intensity\n(≥4 mM blood lactate). Training in the region bounded by about 2 and 4 mM blood\nlactate will be referred to as threshold training (ThT). For untrained / recreation-\nally trained subjects, we find that a 2 mM lactate turn point is diffi", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "exposure. However, in this review, I will focus on training intensity distribution,\nand these integrated approaches will not be presented in detail.\nSeveral recent studies examining training intensity distribution9–11 or perfor-\nmance intensity distr"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0003_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b92eca4108e84540", "text_span_hash": "b92eca4108e84540", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "nsity distribution studies based\non ventilatory threshold–derived zones are not directly comparable with the five-\nzone model, but what is typically identified as “lactate threshold intensity,” or the\napproximately 2 to 4 mM blood lactate concentrati", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "exposure. However, in this review, I will focus on training intensity distribution,\nand these integrated approaches will not be presented in detail.\nSeveral recent studies examining training intensity distribution9–11 or perfor-\nmance intensity distribution in multiday events12,13 have employed individually\ndetermined first and second ventilatory turn points to demarcate three intensity\nzones (Zone 1, Zone 2, and Zone 3; Figure 1). Intensity distribution studies based\non ventilatory threshold–derived zones are not directly comparable with the five-\nzone model, but what is typically identified as “lactate threshold intensity,” or the\napproximately 2 to 4 mM blood lactate concentration range, corresponds well in\npractice with the intensity zone demarcated by the first and second ventilatory turn\npoints. Thus, for practical purposes, the three-zone model and five-zone model\nhave common intensity anchor points around the lactate threshold. For well-trained\nathletes, I will use the term low-intensity training (LIT) to refer to work eliciting\na stable lactate concentration of less than approximately 2 mM. High-intensity\ntraining (HIT) will refer to training above maximum lactate steady-state intensity\n(≥4 mM blood lactate). Training in the region bounded by about 2 and 4 mM blood\nlactate will be referred to as threshold training (ThT). For untrained / recreation-\nally trained subjects, we find that a 2 mM lactate turn point is diffi", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "nsity distribution studies based\non ventilatory threshold–derived zones are not directly comparable with the five-\nzone model, but what is typically identified as “lactate threshold intensity,” or the\napproximately 2 to 4 mM blood lactate concentrati"}
