 above maximum lactate steady-state intensity\n(≥4 mM blood lactate). Training in the region bounded by about 2 and 4 mM blood\nlactate will be referred to as threshold training (ThT). For untrained / recreation-\nally trained subjects, we find that a 2 mM lactate turn point is diffi", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "del\nhave common intensity anchor points around the lactate threshold. For well-trained\nathletes, I will use the term low-intensity training (LIT) to refer to work eliciting\na stable lactate concentration of less than approximately 2 mM. High-intensit"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0003_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5696c29e65e36008", "text_span_hash": "5696c29e65e36008", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "roximately 2 mM. High-intensity\ntraining (HIT) will refer to training above maximum lactate steady-state intensity\n(≥4 mM blood lactate). Training in the region bounded by about 2 and 4 mM blood\nlactate will be referred to as threshold training (ThT)", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "exposure. However, in this review, I will focus on training intensity distribution,\nand these integrated approaches will not be presented in detail.\nSeveral recent studies examining training intensity distribution9–11 or perfor-\nmance intensity distribution in multiday events12,13 have employed individually\ndetermined first and second ventilatory turn points to demarcate three intensity\nzones (Zone 1, Zone 2, and Zone 3; Figure 1). Intensity distribution studies based\non ventilatory threshold–derived zones are not directly comparable with the five-\nzone model, but what is typically identified as “lactate threshold intensity,” or the\napproximately 2 to 4 mM blood lactate concentration range, corresponds well in\npractice with the intensity zone demarcated by the first and second ventilatory turn\npoints. Thus, for practical purposes, the three-zone model and five-zone model\nhave common intensity anchor points around the lactate threshold. For well-trained\nathletes, I will use the term low-intensity training (LIT) to refer to work eliciting\na stable lactate concentration of less than approximately 2 mM. High-intensity\ntraining (HIT) will refer to training above maximum lactate steady-state intensity\n(≥4 mM blood lactate). Training in the region bounded by about 2 and 4 mM blood\nlactate will be referred to as threshold training (ThT). For untrained / recreation-\nally trained subjects, we find that a 2 mM lactate turn point is diffi", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "roximately 2 mM. High-intensity\ntraining (HIT) will refer to training above maximum lactate steady-state intensity\n(≥4 mM blood lactate). Training in the region bounded by about 2 and 4 mM blood\nlactate will be referred to as threshold training (ThT)"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0003_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1a39005286b52135", "text_span_hash": "1a39005286b52135", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "to as threshold training (ThT). For untrained / recreation-\nally trained subjects, we find that a 2 mM lactate turn point is difficult to identify\nbecause blood lactate often approaches this concentration already at very low\nworkloads (unpublished ob", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "exposure. However, in this review, I will focus on training intensity distribution,\nand these integrated approaches will not be presented in detail.\nSeveral recent studies examining training intensity distribution9–11 or perfor-\nmance intensity distribution in multiday events12,13 have employed individually\ndetermined first and second ventilatory turn points to demarcate three intensity\nzones (Zone 1, Zone 2, and Zone 3; Figure 1). Intensity distribution studies based\non ventilatory threshold–derived zones are not directly comparable with the five-\nzone model, but what is typically identified as “lactate threshold intensity,” or the\napproximately 2 to 4 mM blood lactate concentration range, corresponds well in\npractice with the intensity zone demarcated by the first and second ventilatory turn\npoints. Thus, for practical purposes, the three-zone model and five-zone model\nhave common intensity anchor points around the lactate threshold. For well-trained\nathletes, I will use the term low-intensity training (LIT) to refer to work eliciting\na stable lactate concentration of less than approximately 2 mM. High-intensity\ntraining (HIT) will refer to training above maximum lactate steady-state intensity\n(≥4 mM blood lactate). Training in the region bounded by about 2 and 4 mM blood\nlactate will be referred to as threshold training (ThT). For untrained / recreation-\nally trained subjects, we find that a 2 mM lactate turn point is diffi", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "to as threshold training (ThT). For untrained / recreation-\nally trained subjects, we find that a 2 mM lactate turn point is difficult to identify\nbecause blood lactate often approaches this concentration already at very low\nworkloads (unpublished ob"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0003_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7749de7b5e24978e", "text_span_hash": "7749de7b5e24978e", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "low\nworkloads (unpublished observations).\nPublished studies reporting the training characteristics of endurance athletes\nhave employed several methods of quantifying intensity distribution. Self-report of\ntraining pace based on questionnaire and anc", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "exposure. However, in this review, I will focus on training intensity distribution,\nand these integrated approaches will not be presented in detail.\nSeveral recent studies examining training intensity distribution9–11 or perfor-\nmance intensity distribution in multiday events12,13 have employed individually\ndetermined first and second ventilatory turn points to demarcate three intensity\nzones (Zone 1, Zone 2, and Zone 3; Figure 1). Intensity distribution studies based\non ventilatory threshold–derived zones are not directly comparable with the five-\nzone model, but what is typically identified as “lactate threshold intensity,” or the\napproximately 2 to 4 mM blood lactate concentration range, corresponds well in\npractice with the intensity zone demarcated by the first and second ventilatory turn\npoints. Thus, for practical purposes, the three-zone model and five-zone model\nhave common intensity anchor points around the lactate threshold. For well-trained\nathletes, I will use the term low-intensity training (LIT) to refer to work eliciting\na stable lactate concentration of less than approximately 2 mM. High-intensity\ntraining (HIT) will refer to training above maximum lactate steady-state intensity\n(≥4 mM blood lactate). Training in the region bounded by about 2 and 4 mM blood\nlactate will be referred to as threshold training (ThT). For untrained / recreation-\nally trained subjects, we find that a 2 mM lactate turn point is diffi", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "low\nworkloads (unpublished observations).\nPublished studies reporting the training characteristics of endurance athletes\nhave employed several methods of quantifying intensity distribution. Self-report of\ntraining pace based on questionnaire and anc"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0003_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "35d73b15884368e5", "text_span_hash": "35d73b15884368e5", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "based on questionnaire and anchoring with different running paces (eg,\nbelow-marathon pace, 10 K pace, 3 K pace) has been used alone14 and in conjunc-\ntion with physiological testing.15 Intensity distribution based on standardized blood\nlactate range", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "exposure. However, in this review, I will focus on training intensity distribution,\nand these integrated approaches will not be presented in detail.\nSeveral recent studies examining training intensity distribution9–11 or perfor-\nmance intensity distribution in multiday events12,13 have employed individually\ndetermined first and second ventilatory turn points to demarcate three intensity\nzones (Zone 1, Zone 2, and Zone 3; Figure 1). Intensity distribution studies based\non ventilatory threshold–derived zones are not directly comparable with the five-\nzone model, but what is typically identified as “lactate threshold intensity,” or the\napproximately 2 to 4 mM blood lactate concentration range, corresponds well in\npractice with the intensity zone demarcated by the first and second ventilatory turn\npoints. Thus, for practical purposes, the three-zone model and five-zone model\nhave common intensity anchor points around the lactate threshold. For well-trained\nathletes, I will use the term low-intensity training (LIT) to refer to work eliciting\na stable lactate concentration of less than approximately 2 mM. High-intensity\ntraining (HIT) will refer to training above maximum lactate steady-state intensity\n(≥4 mM blood lactate). Training in the region bounded by about 2 and 4 mM blood\nlactate will be referred to as threshold training (ThT). For untrained / recreation-\nally trained subjects, we find that a 2 mM lactate turn point is diffi", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "based on questionnaire and anchoring with different running paces (eg,\nbelow-marathon pace, 10 K pace, 3 K pace) has been used alone14 and in conjunc-\ntion with physiological testing.15 Intensity distribution based on standardized blood\nlactate range"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0003_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "db22466a6bcb4b52", "text_span_hash": "db22466a6bcb4b52", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "andardized blood\nlactate ranges and representative sampling during workouts has been reported for\nelite swimmers16. “Time-in-zone” heart rate analysis has been employed based on\nquantification of the training time spent within different heart rate ra", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "exposure. However, in this review, I will focus on training intensity distribution,\nand these integrated approaches will not be presented in detail.\nSeveral recent studies examining training intensity distribution9–11 or perfor-\nmance intensity distribution in multiday events12,13 have employed individually\ndetermined first and second ventilatory turn points to demarcate three intensity\nzones (Zone 1, Zone 2, and Zone 3; Figure 1). Intensity distribution studies based\non ventilatory threshold–derived zones are not directly comparable with the five-\nzone model, but what is typically identified as “lactate threshold intensity,” or the\napproximately 2 to 4 mM blood lactate concentration range, corresponds well in\npractice with the intensity zone demarcated by the first and second ventilatory turn\npoints. Thus, for practical purposes, the three-zone model and five-zone model\nhave common intensity anchor points around the lactate threshold. For well-trained\nathletes, I will use the term low-intensity training (LIT) to refer to work eliciting\na stable lactate concentration of less than approximately 2 mM. High-intensity\ntraining (HIT) will refer to training above maximum lactate steady-state intensity\n(≥4 mM blood lactate). Training in the region bounded by about 2 and 4 mM blood\nlactate will be referred to as threshold training (ThT). For untrained / recreation-\nally trained subjects, we find that a 2 mM lactate turn point is diffi", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "andardized blood\nlactate ranges and representative sampling during workouts has been reported for\nelite swimmers16. “Time-in-zone” heart rate analysis has been employed based on\nquantification of the training time spent within different heart rate ra"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0003_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "aa2786b485441113", "text_span_hash": "aa2786b485441113", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "within different heart rate ranges identi-\nfied from preliminary threshold testing.9,10,17 The latter method gives total duration\nand percentage of time with heart rate within each intensity zone. This method is\nFigure 1 — A three-intensity-zone mode", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "exposure. However, in this review, I will focus on training intensity distribution,\nand these integrated approaches will not be presented in detail.\nSeveral recent studies examining training intensity distribution9–11 or perfor-\nmance intensity distribution in multiday events12,13 have employed individually\ndetermined first and second ventilatory turn points to demarcate three intensity\nzones (Zone 1, Zone 2, and Zone 3; Figure 1). Intensity distribution studies based\non ventilatory threshold–derived zones are not directly comparable with the five-\nzone model, but what is typically identified as “lactate threshold intensity,” or the\napproximately 2 to 4 mM blood lactate concentration range, corresponds well in\npractice with the intensity zone demarcated by the first and second ventilatory turn\npoints. Thus, for practical purposes, the three-zone model and five-zone model\nhave common intensity anchor points around the lactate threshold. For well-trained\nathletes, I will use the term low-intensity training (LIT) to refer to work eliciting\na stable lactate concentration of less than approximately 2 mM. High-intensity\ntraining (HIT) will refer to training above maximum lactate steady-state intensity\n(≥4 mM blood lactate). Training in the region bounded by about 2 and 4 mM blood\nlactate will be referred to as threshold training (ThT). For untrained / recreation-\nally trained subjects, we find that a 2 mM lactate turn point is diffi", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "within different heart rate ranges identi-\nfied from preliminary threshold testing.9,10,17 The latter method gives total duration\nand percentage of time with heart rate within each intensity zone. This method is\nFigure 1 — A three-intensity-zone mode"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0003_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "550f637520ac8d2d", "text_span_hash": "550f637520ac8d2d", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "— A three-intensity-zone model based on identification of ventilatory thresholds.\nDownloaded by The University of Calgary on 09/17/16, Volume 5, Article Number 3", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "exposure. However, in this review, I will focus on training intensity distribution,\nand these integrated approaches will not be presented in detail.\nSeveral recent studies examining training intensity distribution9–11 or perfor-\nmance intensity distribution in multiday events12,13 have employed individually\ndetermined first and second ventilatory turn points to demarcate three intensity\nzones (Zone 1, Zone 2, and Zone 3; Figure 1). Intensity distribution studies based\non ventilatory threshold–derived zones are not directly comparable with the five-\nzone model, but what is typically identified as “lactate threshold intensity,” or the\napproximately 2 to 4 mM blood lactate concentration range, corresponds well in\npractice with the intensity zone demarcated by the first and second ventilatory turn\npoints. Thus, for practical purposes, the three-zone model and five-zone model\nhave common intensity anchor points around the lactate threshold. For well-trained\nathletes, I will use the term low-intensity training (LIT) to refer to work eliciting\na stable lactate concentration of less than approximately 2 mM. High-intensity\ntraining (HIT) will refer to training above maximum lactate steady-state intensity\n(≥4 mM blood lactate). Training in the region bounded by about 2 and 4 mM blood\nlactate will be referred to as threshold training (ThT). For untrained / recreation-\nally trained subjects, we find that a 2 mM lactate turn point is diffi", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "— A three-intensity-zone model based on identification of ventilatory thresholds.\nDownloaded by The University of Calgary on 09/17/16, Volume 5, Article Number 3"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0004_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "78ac12fb28748797", "text_span_hash": "78ac12fb28748797", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Training Intensity Distribution    279\nappealing since it is noninvasive, individualized, and straightforward analytically.\nHowever, heart rate time-in-zone tends to underestimate the time spent working\nat high intensity (due to heart rate lag time d", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training Intensity Distribution    279\nappealing since it is noninvasive, individualized, and straightforward analytically.\nHowever, heart rate time-in-zone tends to underestimate the time spent working\nat high intensity (due to heart rate lag time during intervals). More importantly, it\ndoes not seem to correspond well with perceived effort for a given workout.10 For\nexample, applying heart rate time-in-zone analysis to an interval session such as 4\n× 4 min at a workload eliciting 95% VO2max preceded by a 20 min warm-up and\nfollowed by a 20 min cool-down will result in both average session heart rate and\ntime-in-zone distribution (dominated by time spent at low intensity) that misrepresent\nthe perceived effort and blood lactate profile of the session and probably also under-\nrepresent the autonomic stress load.18 Nominally allocating each training session to\nan intensity zone based on the intensity of the primary part of the workout, the “ses-\nsion goal approach,” yields better matching between heart rate analysis and athlete\nperception of session effort, or “session RPE,” in both cross-country skiers10 and\n1st-division Norwegian soccer players (unpublished data). Typical software-based\nheart rate analysis methods overestimate the amount of time spent training at low\nintensity and underestimate the time spent at very high workloads, compared with\nathlete perception of effort for a training bout. In training organization, the ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Training Intensity Distribution    279\nappealing since it is noninvasive, individualized, and straightforward analytically.\nHowever, heart rate time-in-zone tends to underestimate the time spent working\nat high intensity (due to heart rate lag time d"}
