blished data). Typical software-based\nheart rate analysis methods overestimate the amount of time spent training at low\nintensity and underestimate the time spent at very high workloads, compared with\nathlete perception of effort for a training bout. In training organization, the ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "sion such as 4\n× 4 min at a workload eliciting 95% VO2max preceded by a 20 min warm-up and\nfollowed by a 20 min cool-down will result in both average session heart rate and\ntime-in-zone distribution (dominated by time spent at low intensity) that mis"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0004_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "afa2b14470b5e9f0", "text_span_hash": "afa2b14470b5e9f0", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ent at low intensity) that misrepresent\nthe perceived effort and blood lactate profile of the session and probably also under-\nrepresent the autonomic stress load.18 Nominally allocating each training session to\nan intensity zone based on the intensi", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training Intensity Distribution    279\nappealing since it is noninvasive, individualized, and straightforward analytically.\nHowever, heart rate time-in-zone tends to underestimate the time spent working\nat high intensity (due to heart rate lag time during intervals). More importantly, it\ndoes not seem to correspond well with perceived effort for a given workout.10 For\nexample, applying heart rate time-in-zone analysis to an interval session such as 4\n× 4 min at a workload eliciting 95% VO2max preceded by a 20 min warm-up and\nfollowed by a 20 min cool-down will result in both average session heart rate and\ntime-in-zone distribution (dominated by time spent at low intensity) that misrepresent\nthe perceived effort and blood lactate profile of the session and probably also under-\nrepresent the autonomic stress load.18 Nominally allocating each training session to\nan intensity zone based on the intensity of the primary part of the workout, the “ses-\nsion goal approach,” yields better matching between heart rate analysis and athlete\nperception of session effort, or “session RPE,” in both cross-country skiers10 and\n1st-division Norwegian soccer players (unpublished data). Typical software-based\nheart rate analysis methods overestimate the amount of time spent training at low\nintensity and underestimate the time spent at very high workloads, compared with\nathlete perception of effort for a training bout. In training organization, the ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ent at low intensity) that misrepresent\nthe perceived effort and blood lactate profile of the session and probably also under-\nrepresent the autonomic stress load.18 Nominally allocating each training session to\nan intensity zone based on the intensi"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0004_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "eb5096fc65946e16", "text_span_hash": "eb5096fc65946e16", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "sity zone based on the intensity of the primary part of the workout, the “ses-\nsion goal approach,” yields better matching between heart rate analysis and athlete\nperception of session effort, or “session RPE,” in both cross-country skiers10 and\n1st-", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training Intensity Distribution    279\nappealing since it is noninvasive, individualized, and straightforward analytically.\nHowever, heart rate time-in-zone tends to underestimate the time spent working\nat high intensity (due to heart rate lag time during intervals). More importantly, it\ndoes not seem to correspond well with perceived effort for a given workout.10 For\nexample, applying heart rate time-in-zone analysis to an interval session such as 4\n× 4 min at a workload eliciting 95% VO2max preceded by a 20 min warm-up and\nfollowed by a 20 min cool-down will result in both average session heart rate and\ntime-in-zone distribution (dominated by time spent at low intensity) that misrepresent\nthe perceived effort and blood lactate profile of the session and probably also under-\nrepresent the autonomic stress load.18 Nominally allocating each training session to\nan intensity zone based on the intensity of the primary part of the workout, the “ses-\nsion goal approach,” yields better matching between heart rate analysis and athlete\nperception of session effort, or “session RPE,” in both cross-country skiers10 and\n1st-division Norwegian soccer players (unpublished data). Typical software-based\nheart rate analysis methods overestimate the amount of time spent training at low\nintensity and underestimate the time spent at very high workloads, compared with\nathlete perception of effort for a training bout. In training organization, the ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "sity zone based on the intensity of the primary part of the workout, the “ses-\nsion goal approach,” yields better matching between heart rate analysis and athlete\nperception of session effort, or “session RPE,” in both cross-country skiers10 and\n1st-"}
