ublished data). Typical software-based\nheart rate analysis methods overestimate the amount of time spent training at low\nintensity and underestimate the time spent at very high workloads, compared with\nathlete perception of effort for a training bout. In training organization, the ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ales and females). But the elite athletes ran more total\nkilometers and proportionally more kilometers at or above v-10K. Examination of\ndata from another descriptive study by Billat et al on elite male and female Kenyan\n5 and 10 K runners demonstrat"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0004_c0017", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "849950599c85a6ea", "text_span_hash": "849950599c85a6ea", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 17, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "5 and 10 K runners demonstrated that approximately 85% of their weekly training\nkilometers were run at below–lactate threshold velocity.20\nDownloaded by The University of Calgary on 09/17/16, Volume 5, Article Number 3", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training Intensity Distribution    279\nappealing since it is noninvasive, individualized, and straightforward analytically.\nHowever, heart rate time-in-zone tends to underestimate the time spent working\nat high intensity (due to heart rate lag time during intervals). More importantly, it\ndoes not seem to correspond well with perceived effort for a given workout.10 For\nexample, applying heart rate time-in-zone analysis to an interval session such as 4\n× 4 min at a workload eliciting 95% VO2max preceded by a 20 min warm-up and\nfollowed by a 20 min cool-down will result in both average session heart rate and\ntime-in-zone distribution (dominated by time spent at low intensity) that misrepresent\nthe perceived effort and blood lactate profile of the session and probably also under-\nrepresent the autonomic stress load.18 Nominally allocating each training session to\nan intensity zone based on the intensity of the primary part of the workout, the “ses-\nsion goal approach,” yields better matching between heart rate analysis and athlete\nperception of session effort, or “session RPE,” in both cross-country skiers10 and\n1st-division Norwegian soccer players (unpublished data). Typical software-based\nheart rate analysis methods overestimate the amount of time spent training at low\nintensity and underestimate the time spent at very high workloads, compared with\nathlete perception of effort for a training bout. In training organization, the ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "5 and 10 K runners demonstrated that approximately 85% of their weekly training\nkilometers were run at below–lactate threshold velocity.20\nDownloaded by The University of Calgary on 09/17/16, Volume 5, Article Number 3"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0005_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3548c0a0087d0bfa", "text_span_hash": "3548c0a0087d0bfa", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Esteve-Lanao et al9 analyzed over 1000 heart rate records using the time-in-\nzone approach to quantify the training of eight regional- and national-class Spanish\ndistance runners over a 6 mo period. Intensity zones were established with treadmill\ntes", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Esteve-Lanao et al9 analyzed over 1000 heart rate records using the time-in-\nzone approach to quantify the training of eight regional- and national-class Spanish\ndistance runners over a 6 mo period. Intensity zones were established with treadmill\ntesting. On average these athletes ran 70 km·wk–1 during the 6 mo period. Seventy-\none percent of running time was <VT1, 21% between VT1 and VT2, and 8% >VT2.\nMean training intensity was 64% VO2max. They also reported that performance\ntimes in both long and short races were inversely correlated with total training\ntime in zone 1. They found no correlation between the volume of HIT performed\nand race performance.\nRowers compete over a 2000 m distance requiring 6 to 7 min. Steinacker et al21\nreported that extensive endurance training (60 to 120 min sessions at <2 mM blood\nlactate) dominated the training volume of German, Danish, Dutch, and Norwegian\nelite rowers. Rowing at higher intensities was performed about 4% to 10% of the\ntotal rowed time. The data also suggested that German rowers preparing for the\nworld championships performed essentially no rowing at ThT intensity, but instead\ntrained either LIT or HIT in the 6 to 12 mM range.\nFiskerstrand and Seiler22 examined historical developments in training orga-\nnization among elite rowers. Using questionnaire data, athlete training diaries, and\nphysiological testing records, they quantified training intensity distribution in 27\nNorwegia", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Esteve-Lanao et al9 analyzed over 1000 heart rate records using the time-in-\nzone approach to quantify the training of eight regional- and national-class Spanish\ndistance runners over a 6 mo period. Intensity zones were established with treadmill\ntes"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0005_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1e5c30e84d6170d4", "text_span_hash": "1e5c30e84d6170d4", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "established with treadmill\ntesting. On average these athletes ran 70 km·wk–1 during the 6 mo period. Seventy-\none percent of running time was <VT1, 21% between VT1 and VT2, and 8% >VT2.\nMean training intensity was 64% VO2max. They also reported that", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Esteve-Lanao et al9 analyzed over 1000 heart rate records using the time-in-\nzone approach to quantify the training of eight regional- and national-class Spanish\ndistance runners over a 6 mo period. Intensity zones were established with treadmill\ntesting. On average these athletes ran 70 km·wk–1 during the 6 mo period. Seventy-\none percent of running time was <VT1, 21% between VT1 and VT2, and 8% >VT2.\nMean training intensity was 64% VO2max. They also reported that performance\ntimes in both long and short races were inversely correlated with total training\ntime in zone 1. They found no correlation between the volume of HIT performed\nand race performance.\nRowers compete over a 2000 m distance requiring 6 to 7 min. Steinacker et al21\nreported that extensive endurance training (60 to 120 min sessions at <2 mM blood\nlactate) dominated the training volume of German, Danish, Dutch, and Norwegian\nelite rowers. Rowing at higher intensities was performed about 4% to 10% of the\ntotal rowed time. The data also suggested that German rowers preparing for the\nworld championships performed essentially no rowing at ThT intensity, but instead\ntrained either LIT or HIT in the 6 to 12 mM range.\nFiskerstrand and Seiler22 examined historical developments in training orga-\nnization among elite rowers. Using questionnaire data, athlete training diaries, and\nphysiological testing records, they quantified training intensity distribution in 27\nNorwegia", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "established with treadmill\ntesting. On average these athletes ran 70 km·wk–1 during the 6 mo period. Seventy-\none percent of running time was <VT1, 21% between VT1 and VT2, and 8% >VT2.\nMean training intensity was 64% VO2max. They also reported that"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0005_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "76ec2bc5eb5ef139", "text_span_hash": "76ec2bc5eb5ef139", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "2max. They also reported that performance\ntimes in both long and short races were inversely correlated with total training\ntime in zone 1. They found no correlation between the volume of HIT performed\nand race performance.\nRowers compete over a 2000", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Esteve-Lanao et al9 analyzed over 1000 heart rate records using the time-in-\nzone approach to quantify the training of eight regional- and national-class Spanish\ndistance runners over a 6 mo period. Intensity zones were established with treadmill\ntesting. On average these athletes ran 70 km·wk–1 during the 6 mo period. Seventy-\none percent of running time was <VT1, 21% between VT1 and VT2, and 8% >VT2.\nMean training intensity was 64% VO2max. They also reported that performance\ntimes in both long and short races were inversely correlated with total training\ntime in zone 1. They found no correlation between the volume of HIT performed\nand race performance.\nRowers compete over a 2000 m distance requiring 6 to 7 min. Steinacker et al21\nreported that extensive endurance training (60 to 120 min sessions at <2 mM blood\nlactate) dominated the training volume of German, Danish, Dutch, and Norwegian\nelite rowers. Rowing at higher intensities was performed about 4% to 10% of the\ntotal rowed time. The data also suggested that German rowers preparing for the\nworld championships performed essentially no rowing at ThT intensity, but instead\ntrained either LIT or HIT in the 6 to 12 mM range.\nFiskerstrand and Seiler22 examined historical developments in training orga-\nnization among elite rowers. Using questionnaire data, athlete training diaries, and\nphysiological testing records, they quantified training intensity distribution in 27\nNorwegia", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "2max. They also reported that performance\ntimes in both long and short races were inversely correlated with total training\ntime in zone 1. They found no correlation between the volume of HIT performed\nand race performance.\nRowers compete over a 2000"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0005_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ebab4ebd516c9210", "text_span_hash": "ebab4ebd516c9210", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "e.\nRowers compete over a 2000 m distance requiring 6 to 7 min. Steinacker et al21\nreported that extensive endurance training (60 to 120 min sessions at <2 mM blood\nlactate) dominated the training volume of German, Danish, Dutch, and Norwegian\nelite r", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Esteve-Lanao et al9 analyzed over 1000 heart rate records using the time-in-\nzone approach to quantify the training of eight regional- and national-class Spanish\ndistance runners over a 6 mo period. Intensity zones were established with treadmill\ntesting. On average these athletes ran 70 km·wk–1 during the 6 mo period. Seventy-\none percent of running time was <VT1, 21% between VT1 and VT2, and 8% >VT2.\nMean training intensity was 64% VO2max. They also reported that performance\ntimes in both long and short races were inversely correlated with total training\ntime in zone 1. They found no correlation between the volume of HIT performed\nand race performance.\nRowers compete over a 2000 m distance requiring 6 to 7 min. Steinacker et al21\nreported that extensive endurance training (60 to 120 min sessions at <2 mM blood\nlactate) dominated the training volume of German, Danish, Dutch, and Norwegian\nelite rowers. Rowing at higher intensities was performed about 4% to 10% of the\ntotal rowed time. The data also suggested that German rowers preparing for the\nworld championships performed essentially no rowing at ThT intensity, but instead\ntrained either LIT or HIT in the 6 to 12 mM range.\nFiskerstrand and Seiler22 examined historical developments in training orga-\nnization among elite rowers. Using questionnaire data, athlete training diaries, and\nphysiological testing records, they quantified training intensity distribution in 27\nNorwegia", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "e.\nRowers compete over a 2000 m distance requiring 6 to 7 min. Steinacker et al21\nreported that extensive endurance training (60 to 120 min sessions at <2 mM blood\nlactate) dominated the training volume of German, Danish, Dutch, and Norwegian\nelite r"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0005_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "364e68d5d4d06fc0", "text_span_hash": "364e68d5d4d06fc0", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "lly no rowing at ThT intensity, but instead\ntrained either LIT or HIT in the 6 to 12 mM range.\nFiskerstrand and Seiler22 examined historical developments in training orga-\nnization among elite rowers. Using questionnaire data, athlete training diarie", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Esteve-Lanao et al9 analyzed over 1000 heart rate records using the time-in-\nzone approach to quantify the training of eight regional- and national-class Spanish\ndistance runners over a 6 mo period. Intensity zones were established with treadmill\ntesting. On average these athletes ran 70 km·wk–1 during the 6 mo period. Seventy-\none percent of running time was <VT1, 21% between VT1 and VT2, and 8% >VT2.\nMean training intensity was 64% VO2max. They also reported that performance\ntimes in both long and short races were inversely correlated with total training\ntime in zone 1. They found no correlation between the volume of HIT performed\nand race performance.\nRowers compete over a 2000 m distance requiring 6 to 7 min. Steinacker et al21\nreported that extensive endurance training (60 to 120 min sessions at <2 mM blood\nlactate) dominated the training volume of German, Danish, Dutch, and Norwegian\nelite rowers. Rowing at higher intensities was performed about 4% to 10% of the\ntotal rowed time. The data also suggested that German rowers preparing for the\nworld championships performed essentially no rowing at ThT intensity, but instead\ntrained either LIT or HIT in the 6 to 12 mM range.\nFiskerstrand and Seiler22 examined historical developments in training orga-\nnization among elite rowers. Using questionnaire data, athlete training diaries, and\nphysiological testing records, they quantified training intensity distribution in 27\nNorwegia", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "lly no rowing at ThT intensity, but instead\ntrained either LIT or HIT in the 6 to 12 mM range.\nFiskerstrand and Seiler22 examined historical developments in training orga-\nnization among elite rowers. Using questionnaire data, athlete training diarie"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0005_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "53e122cc12a4bc74", "text_span_hash": "53e122cc12a4bc74", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "data, athlete training diaries, and\nphysiological testing records, they quantified training intensity distribution in 27\nNorwegian athletes who had won world or Olympic medals in the 1970s, 1980s,\nor 1990s. They documented that over the three decade", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Esteve-Lanao et al9 analyzed over 1000 heart rate records using the time-in-\nzone approach to quantify the training of eight regional- and national-class Spanish\ndistance runners over a 6 mo period. Intensity zones were established with treadmill\ntesting. On average these athletes ran 70 km·wk–1 during the 6 mo period. Seventy-\none percent of running time was <VT1, 21% between VT1 and VT2, and 8% >VT2.\nMean training intensity was 64% VO2max. They also reported that performance\ntimes in both long and short races were inversely correlated with total training\ntime in zone 1. They found no correlation between the volume of HIT performed\nand race performance.\nRowers compete over a 2000 m distance requiring 6 to 7 min. Steinacker et al21\nreported that extensive endurance training (60 to 120 min sessions at <2 mM blood\nlactate) dominated the training volume of German, Danish, Dutch, and Norwegian\nelite rowers. Rowing at higher intensities was performed about 4% to 10% of the\ntotal rowed time. The data also suggested that German rowers preparing for the\nworld championships performed essentially no rowing at ThT intensity, but instead\ntrained either LIT or HIT in the 6 to 12 mM range.\nFiskerstrand and Seiler22 examined historical developments in training orga-\nnization among elite rowers. Using questionnaire data, athlete training diaries, and\nphysiological testing records, they quantified training intensity distribution in 27\nNorwegia", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "data, athlete training diaries, and\nphysiological testing records, they quantified training intensity distribution in 27\nNorwegian athletes who had won world or Olympic medals in the 1970s, 1980s,\nor 1990s. They documented that over the three decade"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0005_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b30de7d7ed6cdcc1", "text_span_hash": "b30de7d7ed6cdcc1", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ted that over the three decades (1) training volume had\nincreased about 20% and LIT volume increased relatively more, (2) the monthly\nhours of HIT had actually been reduced by one-third, (3) very high intensity over-\nspeed sprint training had decline", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Esteve-Lanao et al9 analyzed over 1000 heart rate records using the time-in-\nzone approach to quantify the training of eight regional- and national-class Spanish\ndistance runners over a 6 mo period. Intensity zones were established with treadmill\ntesting. On average these athletes ran 70 km·wk–1 during the 6 mo period. Seventy-\none percent of running time was <VT1, 21% between VT1 and VT2, and 8% >VT2.\nMean training intensity was 64% VO2max. They also reported that performance\ntimes in both long and short races were inversely correlated with total training\ntime in zone 1. They found no correlation between the volume of HIT performed\nand race performance.\nRowers compete over a 2000 m distance requiring 6 to 7 min. Steinacker et al21\nreported that extensive endurance training (60 to 120 min sessions at <2 mM blood\nlactate) dominated the training volume of German, Danish, Dutch, and Norwegian\nelite rowers. Rowing at higher intensities was performed about 4% to 10% of the\ntotal rowed time. The data also suggested that German rowers preparing for the\nworld championships performed essentially no rowing at ThT intensity, but instead\ntrained either LIT or HIT in the 6 to 12 mM range.\nFiskerstrand and Seiler22 examined historical developments in training orga-\nnization among elite rowers. Using questionnaire data, athlete training diaries, and\nphysiological testing records, they quantified training intensity distribution in 27\nNorwegia", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ted that over the three decades (1) training volume had\nincreased about 20% and LIT volume increased relatively more, (2) the monthly\nhours of HIT had actually been reduced by one-third, (3) very high intensity over-\nspeed sprint training had decline"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0005_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5575bf5fa31f2c7d", "text_span_hash": "5575bf5fa31f2c7d", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ed sprint training had declined dramatically in favor of longer interval training\nat 85% to 95% of VO2max, and (4) the number of altitude camps attended by the\nathletes increased dramatically. Over this 30 y timeline, athletes had about 12%\nhigher VO", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Esteve-Lanao et al9 analyzed over 1000 heart rate records using the time-in-\nzone approach to quantify the training of eight regional- and national-class Spanish\ndistance runners over a 6 mo period. Intensity zones were established with treadmill\ntesting. On average these athletes ran 70 km·wk–1 during the 6 mo period. Seventy-\none percent of running time was <VT1, 21% between VT1 and VT2, and 8% >VT2.\nMean training intensity was 64% VO2max. They also reported that performance\ntimes in both long and short races were inversely correlated with total training\ntime in zone 1. They found no correlation between the volume of HIT performed\nand race performance.\nRowers compete over a 2000 m distance requiring 6 to 7 min. Steinacker et al21\nreported that extensive endurance training (60 to 120 min sessions at <2 mM blood\nlactate) dominated the training volume of German, Danish, Dutch, and Norwegian\nelite rowers. Rowing at higher intensities was performed about 4% to 10% of the\ntotal rowed time. The data also suggested that German rowers preparing for the\nworld championships performed essentially no rowing at ThT intensity, but instead\ntrained either LIT or HIT in the 6 to 12 mM range.\nFiskerstrand and Seiler22 examined historical developments in training orga-\nnization among elite rowers. Using questionnaire data, athlete training diaries, and\nphysiological testing records, they quantified training intensity distribution in 27\nNorwegia", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ed sprint training had declined dramatically in favor of longer interval training\nat 85% to 95% of VO2max, and (4) the number of altitude camps attended by the\nathletes increased dramatically. Over this 30 y timeline, athletes had about 12%\nhigher VO"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0005_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "69b2993c1d09dbf4", "text_span_hash": "69b2993c1d09dbf4", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "hletes had about 12%\nhigher VO2max and a 10% improvement in rowing ergometer performance with\nno change in average height or body mass. However, most of this increase was\nseen between the 1970s and 1980s when major adjustments in training intensity\nd", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Esteve-Lanao et al9 analyzed over 1000 heart rate records using the time-in-\nzone approach to quantify the training of eight regional- and national-class Spanish\ndistance runners over a 6 mo period. Intensity zones were established with treadmill\ntesting. On average these athletes ran 70 km·wk–1 during the 6 mo period. Seventy-\none percent of running time was <VT1, 21% between VT1 and VT2, and 8% >VT2.\nMean training intensity was 64% VO2max. They also reported that performance\ntimes in both long and short races were inversely correlated with total training\ntime in zone 1. They found no correlation between the volume of HIT performed\nand race performance.\nRowers compete over a 2000 m distance requiring 6 to 7 min. Steinacker et al21\nreported that extensive endurance training (60 to 120 min sessions at <2 mM blood\nlactate) dominated the training volume of German, Danish, Dutch, and Norwegian\nelite rowers. Rowing at higher intensities was performed about 4% to 10% of the\ntotal rowed time. The data also suggested that German rowers preparing for the\nworld championships performed essentially no rowing at ThT intensity, but instead\ntrained either LIT or HIT in the 6 to 12 mM range.\nFiskerstrand and Seiler22 examined historical developments in training orga-\nnization among elite rowers. Using questionnaire data, athlete training diaries, and\nphysiological testing records, they quantified training intensity distribution in 27\nNorwegia", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "hletes had about 12%\nhigher VO2max and a 10% improvement in rowing ergometer performance with\nno change in average height or body mass. However, most of this increase was\nseen between the 1970s and 1980s when major adjustments in training intensity\nd"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0005_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9e486c8e5cc8cf95", "text_span_hash": "9e486c8e5cc8cf95", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "tments in training intensity\ndistribution were made.\nGuellich et al23 described the training of world-class junior rowers from Ger-\nmany during a 37-wk period culminating in national championships and qualifica-\ntion races for the world championships", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Esteve-Lanao et al9 analyzed over 1000 heart rate records using the time-in-\nzone approach to quantify the training of eight regional- and national-class Spanish\ndistance runners over a 6 mo period. Intensity zones were established with treadmill\ntesting. On average these athletes ran 70 km·wk–1 during the 6 mo period. Seventy-\none percent of running time was <VT1, 21% between VT1 and VT2, and 8% >VT2.\nMean training intensity was 64% VO2max. They also reported that performance\ntimes in both long and short races were inversely correlated with total training\ntime in zone 1. They found no correlation between the volume of HIT performed\nand race performance.\nRowers compete over a 2000 m distance requiring 6 to 7 min. Steinacker et al21\nreported that extensive endurance training (60 to 120 min sessions at <2 mM blood\nlactate) dominated the training volume of German, Danish, Dutch, and Norwegian\nelite rowers. Rowing at higher intensities was performed about 4% to 10% of the\ntotal rowed time. The data also suggested that German rowers preparing for the\nworld championships performed essentially no rowing at ThT intensity, but instead\ntrained either LIT or HIT in the 6 to 12 mM range.\nFiskerstrand and Seiler22 examined historical developments in training orga-\nnization among elite rowers. Using questionnaire data, athlete training diaries, and\nphysiological testing records, they quantified training intensity distribution in 27\nNorwegia", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "tments in training intensity\ndistribution were made.\nGuellich et al23 described the training of world-class junior rowers from Ger-\nmany during a 37-wk period culminating in national championships and qualifica-\ntion races for the world championships"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0005_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "91b08400e3b09b4f", "text_span_hash": "91b08400e3b09b4f", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "es for the world championships. Twenty-seven of the 36 athletes studied\nwon medals in the junior world championships that followed the training period\nanalyzed. Using the time-in-zone heart rate analysis method described above, fully\n95% of all endur", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Esteve-Lanao et al9 analyzed over 1000 heart rate records using the time-in-\nzone approach to quantify the training of eight regional- and national-class Spanish\ndistance runners over a 6 mo period. Intensity zones were established with treadmill\ntesting. On average these athletes ran 70 km·wk–1 during the 6 mo period. Seventy-\none percent of running time was <VT1, 21% between VT1 and VT2, and 8% >VT2.\nMean training intensity was 64% VO2max. They also reported that performance\ntimes in both long and short races were inversely correlated with total training\ntime in zone 1. They found no correlation between the volume of HIT performed\nand race performance.\nRowers compete over a 2000 m distance requiring 6 to 7 min. Steinacker et al21\nreported that extensive endurance training (60 to 120 min sessions at <2 mM blood\nlactate) dominated the training volume of German, Danish, Dutch, and Norwegian\nelite rowers. Rowing at higher intensities was performed about 4% to 10% of the\ntotal rowed time. The data also suggested that German rowers preparing for the\nworld championships performed essentially no rowing at ThT intensity, but instead\ntrained either LIT or HIT in the 6 to 12 mM range.\nFiskerstrand and Seiler22 examined historical developments in training orga-\nnization among elite rowers. Using questionnaire data, athlete training diaries, and\nphysiological testing records, they quantified training intensity distribution in 27\nNorwegia", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "es for the world championships. Twenty-seven of the 36 athletes studied\nwon medals in the junior world championships that followed the training period\nanalyzed. Using the time-in-zone heart rate analysis method described above, fully\n95% of all endur"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0005_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "681c77c7977d718f", "text_span_hash": "681c77c7977d718f", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "above, fully\n95% of all endurance training time was performed as LIT. This heavy dominance\nof extensive endurance training persisted throughout the 9 mo period. However,\nthe relatively small volume of ThT and HIT shifted toward higher intensities fr", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Esteve-Lanao et al9 analyzed over 1000 heart rate records using the time-in-\nzone approach to quantify the training of eight regional- and national-class Spanish\ndistance runners over a 6 mo period. Intensity zones were established with treadmill\ntesting. On average these athletes ran 70 km·wk–1 during the 6 mo period. Seventy-\none percent of running time was <VT1, 21% between VT1 and VT2, and 8% >VT2.\nMean training intensity was 64% VO2max. They also reported that performance\ntimes in both long and short races were inversely correlated with total training\ntime in zone 1. They found no correlation between the volume of HIT performed\nand race performance.\nRowers compete over a 2000 m distance requiring 6 to 7 min. Steinacker et al21\nreported that extensive endurance training (60 to 120 min sessions at <2 mM blood\nlactate) dominated the training volume of German, Danish, Dutch, and Norwegian\nelite rowers. Rowing at higher intensities was performed about 4% to 10% of the\ntotal rowed time. The data also suggested that German rowers preparing for the\nworld championships performed essentially no rowing at ThT intensity, but instead\ntrained either LIT or HIT in the 6 to 12 mM range.\nFiskerstrand and Seiler22 examined historical developments in training orga-\nnization among elite rowers. Using questionnaire data, athlete training diaries, and\nphysiological testing records, they quantified training intensity distribution in 27\nNorwegia", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "above, fully\n95% of all endurance training time was performed as LIT. This heavy dominance\nof extensive endurance training persisted throughout the 9 mo period. However,\nthe relatively small volume of ThT and HIT shifted toward higher intensities fr"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0005_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ddd35453014db592", "text_span_hash": "ddd35453014db592", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "known for performing very high training volumes,\nup to 30 to 35,000 km·yr–1. Zapico and colleagues used the three-intensity zone\nmodel to track training characteristics from November to June in a group of elite\nSpanish U23 riders.11 In addition, phys", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Esteve-Lanao et al9 analyzed over 1000 heart rate records using the time-in-\nzone approach to quantify the training of eight regional- and national-class Spanish\ndistance runners over a 6 mo period. Intensity zones were established with treadmill\ntesting. On average these athletes ran 70 km·wk–1 during the 6 mo period. Seventy-\none percent of running time was <VT1, 21% between VT1 and VT2, and 8% >VT2.\nMean training intensity was 64% VO2max. They also reported that performance\ntimes in both long and short races were inversely correlated with total training\ntime in zone 1. They found no correlation between the volume of HIT performed\nand race performance.\nRowers compete over a 2000 m distance requiring 6 to 7 min. Steinacker et al21\nreported that extensive endurance training (60 to 120 min sessions at <2 mM blood\nlactate) dominated the training volume of German, Danish, Dutch, and Norwegian\nelite rowers. Rowing at higher intensities was performed about 4% to 10% of the\ntotal rowed time. The data also suggested that German rowers preparing for the\nworld championships performed essentially no rowing at ThT intensity, but instead\ntrained either LIT or HIT in the 6 to 12 mM range.\nFiskerstrand and Seiler22 examined historical developments in training orga-\nnization among elite rowers. Using questionnaire data, athlete training diaries, and\nphysiological testing records, they quantified training intensity distribution in 27\nNorwegia", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "known for performing very high training volumes,\nup to 30 to 35,000 km·yr–1. Zapico and colleagues used the three-intensity zone\nmodel to track training characteristics from November to June in a group of elite\nSpanish U23 riders.11 In addition, phys"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0005_c0016", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e8e295a36d3dac65", "text_span_hash": "e8e295a36d3dac65", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 16, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "23 riders.11 In addition, physiological testing was performed at season\nstart and at the end of the winter and spring mesocycles to compare training changes\nand physiological test results. Figure 2 compares the training intensity distribution\nin the", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Esteve-Lanao et al9 analyzed over 1000 heart rate records using the time-in-\nzone approach to quantify the training of eight regional- and national-class Spanish\ndistance runners over a 6 mo period. Intensity zones were established with treadmill\ntesting. On average these athletes ran 70 km·wk–1 during the 6 mo period. Seventy-\none percent of running time was <VT1, 21% between VT1 and VT2, and 8% >VT2.\nMean training intensity was 64% VO2max. They also reported that performance\ntimes in both long and short races were inversely correlated with total training\ntime in zone 1. They found no correlation between the volume of HIT performed\nand race performance.\nRowers compete over a 2000 m distance requiring 6 to 7 min. Steinacker et al21\nreported that extensive endurance training (60 to 120 min sessions at <2 mM blood\nlactate) dominated the training volume of German, Danish, Dutch, and Norwegian\nelite rowers. Rowing at higher intensities was performed about 4% to 10% of the\ntotal rowed time. The data also suggested that German rowers preparing for the\nworld championships performed essentially no rowing at ThT intensity, but instead\ntrained either LIT or HIT in the 6 to 12 mM range.\nFiskerstrand and Seiler22 examined historical developments in training orga-\nnization among elite rowers. Using questionnaire data, athlete training diaries, and\nphysiological testing records, they quantified training intensity distribution in 27\nNorwegia", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "23 riders.11 In addition, physiological testing was performed at season\nstart and at the end of the winter and spring mesocycles to compare training changes\nand physiological test results. Figure 2 compares the training intensity distribution\nin the"}
