{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0006_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5132141aba72079c", "text_span_hash": "5132141aba72079c", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "te Spanish U23 cyclists training in the\nperiod November to June. Data redrawn from Zapico et al.11\nFigure 3 — Response to periodization of training intensity and volume in elite Spanish U23\ncyclists (see Figure 2). Results from tests performed before", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training Intensity Distribution    281\nintensity distributions in the two periods shows that there was both an increase in\ntotal training volume and a 4× increase in HIT training during the spring mesocycle.\nHowever, physiological testing revealed no further improvement in power at VT1,\nVT2, or at VO2max between the end of the winter and spring mesocycles, despite\na clear training intensification. Anecdotally, this is not an unusual finding. Time\nat VO2max or time at VT2 power may be more sensitive variables to evaluate the\nimpact of intensified training in highly trained athletes with stable threshold and\nFigure 2 — Cycling intensity and volume of elite Spanish U23 cyclists training in the\nperiod November to June. Data redrawn from Zapico et al.11\nFigure 3 — Response to periodization of training intensity and volume in elite Spanish U23\ncyclists (see Figure 2). Results from tests performed before starting the winter mesocycle\n(test 1), at the end of the winter mesocycle (test 2), and at the end of the spring mesocycle\n(test 3). Data redrawn from Zapico et al.11\nDownloaded by The University of Calgary on 09/17/16, Volume 5, Article Number 3", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "te Spanish U23 cyclists training in the\nperiod November to June. Data redrawn from Zapico et al.11\nFigure 3 — Response to periodization of training intensity and volume in elite Spanish U23\ncyclists (see Figure 2). Results from tests performed before"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0007_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e47b670e2487f592", "text_span_hash": "e47b670e2487f592", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Cross-country skiing has adopted spectator-friendly 1000 to 1500 m sprint\nraces in the last decade (contested as a knockout tournament). Recently, Sandbakk\net al compared the training and physiology of eight international-class and eight\nnational-cla", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Cross-country skiing has adopted spectator-friendly 1000 to 1500 m sprint\nraces in the last decade (contested as a knockout tournament). Recently, Sandbakk\net al compared the training and physiology of eight international-class and eight\nnational-class (Norway) sprint cross-country skiers.24 The internationally elite\nskiers distinguished themselves with higher VO2peak, vVO2peak, and exercise\ntime at VO2peak. Over a 6 mo registration period, the world-class skiers trained\nabout one-third greater volume (445 h vs 341), with almost all of this difference\nin training time due to greater volumes of low-intensity training (86 more hours)\nand speed training (9 more hours). The two groups performed identical volumes\nof HIT over 6 mo (19 h in both groups, or about 45 min·wk–1).\nSchumacher and Mueller25 demonstrated the validity of power balance model-\ning in predicting “gold medal standards” for physiological testing and power output\nin the 4,000 m pursuit cycling race. However, less obvious from the title was the\ndetailed description of the training program followed by the gold medal–winning\nteam monitored in the study. These athletes trained to maintain an average com-\npetition intensity of over 100% of power at VO2max with a program dominated\nby LIT (29,000–35,000 km·y–1). In the 200 d preceding the Olympics, the pursuit\nteam performed “low-intensity, high-mileage” training at 50 to 60% of VO2max\non approximately 140 d. Stage races ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Cross-country skiing has adopted spectator-friendly 1000 to 1500 m sprint\nraces in the last decade (contested as a knockout tournament). Recently, Sandbakk\net al compared the training and physiology of eight international-class and eight\nnational-cla"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0007_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e4dbfd216e14b056", "text_span_hash": "e4dbfd216e14b056", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "iod, the world-class skiers trained\nabout one-third greater volume (445 h vs 341), with almost all of this difference\nin training time due to greater volumes of low-intensity training (86 more hours)\nand speed training (9 more hours). The two groups", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Cross-country skiing has adopted spectator-friendly 1000 to 1500 m sprint\nraces in the last decade (contested as a knockout tournament). Recently, Sandbakk\net al compared the training and physiology of eight international-class and eight\nnational-class (Norway) sprint cross-country skiers.24 The internationally elite\nskiers distinguished themselves with higher VO2peak, vVO2peak, and exercise\ntime at VO2peak. Over a 6 mo registration period, the world-class skiers trained\nabout one-third greater volume (445 h vs 341), with almost all of this difference\nin training time due to greater volumes of low-intensity training (86 more hours)\nand speed training (9 more hours). The two groups performed identical volumes\nof HIT over 6 mo (19 h in both groups, or about 45 min·wk–1).\nSchumacher and Mueller25 demonstrated the validity of power balance model-\ning in predicting “gold medal standards” for physiological testing and power output\nin the 4,000 m pursuit cycling race. However, less obvious from the title was the\ndetailed description of the training program followed by the gold medal–winning\nteam monitored in the study. These athletes trained to maintain an average com-\npetition intensity of over 100% of power at VO2max with a program dominated\nby LIT (29,000–35,000 km·y–1). In the 200 d preceding the Olympics, the pursuit\nteam performed “low-intensity, high-mileage” training at 50 to 60% of VO2max\non approximately 140 d. Stage races ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "iod, the world-class skiers trained\nabout one-third greater volume (445 h vs 341), with almost all of this difference\nin training time due to greater volumes of low-intensity training (86 more hours)\nand speed training (9 more hours). The two groups"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0007_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2c8ee590383008f1", "text_span_hash": "2c8ee590383008f1", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "edal standards” for physiological testing and power output\nin the 4,000 m pursuit cycling race. However, less obvious from the title was the\ndetailed description of the training program followed by the gold medal–winning\nteam monitored in the study.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Cross-country skiing has adopted spectator-friendly 1000 to 1500 m sprint\nraces in the last decade (contested as a knockout tournament). Recently, Sandbakk\net al compared the training and physiology of eight international-class and eight\nnational-class (Norway) sprint cross-country skiers.24 The internationally elite\nskiers distinguished themselves with higher VO2peak, vVO2peak, and exercise\ntime at VO2peak. Over a 6 mo registration period, the world-class skiers trained\nabout one-third greater volume (445 h vs 341), with almost all of this difference\nin training time due to greater volumes of low-intensity training (86 more hours)\nand speed training (9 more hours). The two groups performed identical volumes\nof HIT over 6 mo (19 h in both groups, or about 45 min·wk–1).\nSchumacher and Mueller25 demonstrated the validity of power balance model-\ning in predicting “gold medal standards” for physiological testing and power output\nin the 4,000 m pursuit cycling race. However, less obvious from the title was the\ndetailed description of the training program followed by the gold medal–winning\nteam monitored in the study. These athletes trained to maintain an average com-\npetition intensity of over 100% of power at VO2max with a program dominated\nby LIT (29,000–35,000 km·y–1). In the 200 d preceding the Olympics, the pursuit\nteam performed “low-intensity, high-mileage” training at 50 to 60% of VO2max\non approximately 140 d. Stage races ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "edal standards” for physiological testing and power output\nin the 4,000 m pursuit cycling race. However, less obvious from the title was the\ndetailed description of the training program followed by the gold medal–winning\nteam monitored in the study."}
