erage com-\npetition intensity of over 100% of power at VO2max with a program dominated\nby LIT (29,000–35,000 km·y–1). In the 200 d preceding the Olympics, the pursuit\nteam performed “low-intensity, high-mileage” training at 50 to 60% of VO2max\non approximately 140 d. Stage races ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "team monitored in the study. These athletes trained to maintain an average com-\npetition intensity of over 100% of power at VO2max with a program dominated\nby LIT (29,000–35,000 km·y–1). In the 200 d preceding the Olympics, the pursuit\nteam performe"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0007_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0c6e781ffd5bc729", "text_span_hash": "0c6e781ffd5bc729", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ics, the pursuit\nteam performed “low-intensity, high-mileage” training at 50 to 60% of VO2max\non approximately 140 d. Stage races comprised approximately 40 d. Specific track\ncycling at near competition intensities was performed on fewer than 20 d be", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Cross-country skiing has adopted spectator-friendly 1000 to 1500 m sprint\nraces in the last decade (contested as a knockout tournament). Recently, Sandbakk\net al compared the training and physiology of eight international-class and eight\nnational-class (Norway) sprint cross-country skiers.24 The internationally elite\nskiers distinguished themselves with higher VO2peak, vVO2peak, and exercise\ntime at VO2peak. Over a 6 mo registration period, the world-class skiers trained\nabout one-third greater volume (445 h vs 341), with almost all of this difference\nin training time due to greater volumes of low-intensity training (86 more hours)\nand speed training (9 more hours). The two groups performed identical volumes\nof HIT over 6 mo (19 h in both groups, or about 45 min·wk–1).\nSchumacher and Mueller25 demonstrated the validity of power balance model-\ning in predicting “gold medal standards” for physiological testing and power output\nin the 4,000 m pursuit cycling race. However, less obvious from the title was the\ndetailed description of the training program followed by the gold medal–winning\nteam monitored in the study. These athletes trained to maintain an average com-\npetition intensity of over 100% of power at VO2max with a program dominated\nby LIT (29,000–35,000 km·y–1). In the 200 d preceding the Olympics, the pursuit\nteam performed “low-intensity, high-mileage” training at 50 to 60% of VO2max\non approximately 140 d. Stage races ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ics, the pursuit\nteam performed “low-intensity, high-mileage” training at 50 to 60% of VO2max\non approximately 140 d. Stage races comprised approximately 40 d. Specific track\ncycling at near competition intensities was performed on fewer than 20 d be"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0007_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f9bdef0237de799a", "text_span_hash": "f9bdef0237de799a", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "erformed on fewer than 20 d between\nMarch and September. In the approximately 110 d preceding the Olympic final,\nhigh-intensity interval track training was performed on only 6 d.\nThe descriptive studies above highlight the paradoxical finding that ev", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Cross-country skiing has adopted spectator-friendly 1000 to 1500 m sprint\nraces in the last decade (contested as a knockout tournament). Recently, Sandbakk\net al compared the training and physiology of eight international-class and eight\nnational-class (Norway) sprint cross-country skiers.24 The internationally elite\nskiers distinguished themselves with higher VO2peak, vVO2peak, and exercise\ntime at VO2peak. Over a 6 mo registration period, the world-class skiers trained\nabout one-third greater volume (445 h vs 341), with almost all of this difference\nin training time due to greater volumes of low-intensity training (86 more hours)\nand speed training (9 more hours). The two groups performed identical volumes\nof HIT over 6 mo (19 h in both groups, or about 45 min·wk–1).\nSchumacher and Mueller25 demonstrated the validity of power balance model-\ning in predicting “gold medal standards” for physiological testing and power output\nin the 4,000 m pursuit cycling race. However, less obvious from the title was the\ndetailed description of the training program followed by the gold medal–winning\nteam monitored in the study. These athletes trained to maintain an average com-\npetition intensity of over 100% of power at VO2max with a program dominated\nby LIT (29,000–35,000 km·y–1). In the 200 d preceding the Olympics, the pursuit\nteam performed “low-intensity, high-mileage” training at 50 to 60% of VO2max\non approximately 140 d. Stage races ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "erformed on fewer than 20 d between\nMarch and September. In the approximately 110 d preceding the Olympic final,\nhigh-intensity interval track training was performed on only 6 d.\nThe descriptive studies above highlight the paradoxical finding that ev"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0007_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "a56357a2885fa31b", "text_span_hash": "a56357a2885fa31b", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "he paradoxical finding that even though\nall Olympic endurance events are performed at or above the lactate threshold (or\n≥85% VO2max), the large majority of the training performed is completed below\nlactate threshold intensity. The duration of monito", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Cross-country skiing has adopted spectator-friendly 1000 to 1500 m sprint\nraces in the last decade (contested as a knockout tournament). Recently, Sandbakk\net al compared the training and physiology of eight international-class and eight\nnational-class (Norway) sprint cross-country skiers.24 The internationally elite\nskiers distinguished themselves with higher VO2peak, vVO2peak, and exercise\ntime at VO2peak. Over a 6 mo registration period, the world-class skiers trained\nabout one-third greater volume (445 h vs 341), with almost all of this difference\nin training time due to greater volumes of low-intensity training (86 more hours)\nand speed training (9 more hours). The two groups performed identical volumes\nof HIT over 6 mo (19 h in both groups, or about 45 min·wk–1).\nSchumacher and Mueller25 demonstrated the validity of power balance model-\ning in predicting “gold medal standards” for physiological testing and power output\nin the 4,000 m pursuit cycling race. However, less obvious from the title was the\ndetailed description of the training program followed by the gold medal–winning\nteam monitored in the study. These athletes trained to maintain an average com-\npetition intensity of over 100% of power at VO2max with a program dominated\nby LIT (29,000–35,000 km·y–1). In the 200 d preceding the Olympics, the pursuit\nteam performed “low-intensity, high-mileage” training at 50 to 60% of VO2max\non approximately 140 d. Stage races ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "he paradoxical finding that even though\nall Olympic endurance events are performed at or above the lactate threshold (or\n≥85% VO2max), the large majority of the training performed is completed below\nlactate threshold intensity. The duration of monito"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0007_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c4a04f82079c2804", "text_span_hash": "c4a04f82079c2804", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ensity. The duration of monitoring from published studies\nvaries from weeks to an entire season but seems to converge on a common intensity\ndistribution: about 80% of training sessions are LIT intensity and the remaining\n20% are performed as ThT or H", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Cross-country skiing has adopted spectator-friendly 1000 to 1500 m sprint\nraces in the last decade (contested as a knockout tournament). Recently, Sandbakk\net al compared the training and physiology of eight international-class and eight\nnational-class (Norway) sprint cross-country skiers.24 The internationally elite\nskiers distinguished themselves with higher VO2peak, vVO2peak, and exercise\ntime at VO2peak. Over a 6 mo registration period, the world-class skiers trained\nabout one-third greater volume (445 h vs 341), with almost all of this difference\nin training time due to greater volumes of low-intensity training (86 more hours)\nand speed training (9 more hours). The two groups performed identical volumes\nof HIT over 6 mo (19 h in both groups, or about 45 min·wk–1).\nSchumacher and Mueller25 demonstrated the validity of power balance model-\ning in predicting “gold medal standards” for physiological testing and power output\nin the 4,000 m pursuit cycling race. However, less obvious from the title was the\ndetailed description of the training program followed by the gold medal–winning\nteam monitored in the study. These athletes trained to maintain an average com-\npetition intensity of over 100% of power at VO2max with a program dominated\nby LIT (29,000–35,000 km·y–1). In the 200 d preceding the Olympics, the pursuit\nteam performed “low-intensity, high-mileage” training at 50 to 60% of VO2max\non approximately 140 d. Stage races ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ensity. The duration of monitoring from published studies\nvaries from weeks to an entire season but seems to converge on a common intensity\ndistribution: about 80% of training sessions are LIT intensity and the remaining\n20% are performed as ThT or H"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0007_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "338abf8fddb242a8", "text_span_hash": "338abf8fddb242a8", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "20% are performed as ThT or HIT. For an athlete training 10 to 14 times per week,\nthis means that two to three of these sessions would be ThT or HIT training bouts.\nThis distribution fits well with findings that adding two interval sessions per week", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Cross-country skiing has adopted spectator-friendly 1000 to 1500 m sprint\nraces in the last decade (contested as a knockout tournament). Recently, Sandbakk\net al compared the training and physiology of eight international-class and eight\nnational-class (Norway) sprint cross-country skiers.24 The internationally elite\nskiers distinguished themselves with higher VO2peak, vVO2peak, and exercise\ntime at VO2peak. Over a 6 mo registration period, the world-class skiers trained\nabout one-third greater volume (445 h vs 341), with almost all of this difference\nin training time due to greater volumes of low-intensity training (86 more hours)\nand speed training (9 more hours). The two groups performed identical volumes\nof HIT over 6 mo (19 h in both groups, or about 45 min·wk–1).\nSchumacher and Mueller25 demonstrated the validity of power balance model-\ning in predicting “gold medal standards” for physiological testing and power output\nin the 4,000 m pursuit cycling race. However, less obvious from the title was the\ndetailed description of the training program followed by the gold medal–winning\nteam monitored in the study. These athletes trained to maintain an average com-\npetition intensity of over 100% of power at VO2max with a program dominated\nby LIT (29,000–35,000 km·y–1). In the 200 d preceding the Olympics, the pursuit\nteam performed “low-intensity, high-mileage” training at 50 to 60% of VO2max\non approximately 140 d. Stage races ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "20% are performed as ThT or HIT. For an athlete training 10 to 14 times per week,\nthis means that two to three of these sessions would be ThT or HIT training bouts.\nThis distribution fits well with findings that adding two interval sessions per week"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0007_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f90776c0e06d51c7", "text_span_hash": "f90776c0e06d51c7", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "two interval sessions per week\nfor 4 to 8 wk improves performance by 2% to 4% among well-trained endurance\nathletes doing only basic endurance training.26–29 Additional increases in HIT\nfrequency do not induce further improvements and tend to induce", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Cross-country skiing has adopted spectator-friendly 1000 to 1500 m sprint\nraces in the last decade (contested as a knockout tournament). Recently, Sandbakk\net al compared the training and physiology of eight international-class and eight\nnational-class (Norway) sprint cross-country skiers.24 The internationally elite\nskiers distinguished themselves with higher VO2peak, vVO2peak, and exercise\ntime at VO2peak. Over a 6 mo registration period, the world-class skiers trained\nabout one-third greater volume (445 h vs 341), with almost all of this difference\nin training time due to greater volumes of low-intensity training (86 more hours)\nand speed training (9 more hours). The two groups performed identical volumes\nof HIT over 6 mo (19 h in both groups, or about 45 min·wk–1).\nSchumacher and Mueller25 demonstrated the validity of power balance model-\ning in predicting “gold medal standards” for physiological testing and power output\nin the 4,000 m pursuit cycling race. However, less obvious from the title was the\ndetailed description of the training program followed by the gold medal–winning\nteam monitored in the study. These athletes trained to maintain an average com-\npetition intensity of over 100% of power at VO2max with a program dominated\nby LIT (29,000–35,000 km·y–1). In the 200 d preceding the Olympics, the pursuit\nteam performed “low-intensity, high-mileage” training at 50 to 60% of VO2max\non approximately 140 d. Stage races ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "two interval sessions per week\nfor 4 to 8 wk improves performance by 2% to 4% among well-trained endurance\nathletes doing only basic endurance training.26–29 Additional increases in HIT\nfrequency do not induce further improvements and tend to induce"}
