ological test results\nand race performances during the first year were used to identify seven athletes\nwho responded well to the training and seven who showed poor VO2max and lactate\nthreshold progression, and race results. In the second year, the positive respond-\ners continued us", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "raining\nhours with 16% HIT (nominal distribution of sessions). Physiological test results\nand race performances during the first year were used to identify seven athletes\nwho responded well to the training and seven who showed poor VO2max and lactate"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0008_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c933adbe007c75b5", "text_span_hash": "c933adbe007c75b5", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "showed poor VO2max and lactate\nthreshold progression, and race results. In the second year, the positive respond-\ners continued using their established training program whereas the nonresponders\nperformed a markedly intensified training program with", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training Intensity Distribution    283\n90% of VO2max. They were then randomized to a moderate-intensity (MOD) or a\nhigh-intensity training group (HIGH). The MOD group maintained essentially the\nsame training intensity distribution, but training volume was increased from 10 to\n16 h·wk–1. The HIGH group reversed their baseline intensity distribution so that\n83% of training time was performed at 80% to 90% of VO2max, with only 17%\nperformed as low-intensity endurance training. The HIGH group trained 12 h·wk–1.\nThe training intervention period lasted 5 mo. Intensity control was achieved using\nheart rate monitoring and blood lactate sampling throughout the training period.\nDespite 60% more training volume in MOD and approximately four times more\ntraining at an intensity greater than or equal to lactate threshold in HIGH, physi-\nological and performance changes were quite modest in both groups of already\nwell-trained athletes (Table 2).\nGaskill et al reported the results of a 2 y project involving 14 cross-country\nskiers.35 During the first year, athletes trained similarly, averaging 660 training\nhours with 16% HIT (nominal distribution of sessions). Physiological test results\nand race performances during the first year were used to identify seven athletes\nwho responded well to the training and seven who showed poor VO2max and lactate\nthreshold progression, and race results. In the second year, the positive respond-\ners continued us", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "showed poor VO2max and lactate\nthreshold progression, and race results. In the second year, the positive respond-\ners continued using their established training program whereas the nonresponders\nperformed a markedly intensified training program with"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0008_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7177e8b20211766a", "text_span_hash": "7177e8b20211766a", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "nsified training program with a slight reduction in train-\ning hours. They observed that the nonresponders from year 1 showed a positive\nresponse to the intensified program in year 2 (VO2max, lactate threshold, race\nresult points). The positive respo", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training Intensity Distribution    283\n90% of VO2max. They were then randomized to a moderate-intensity (MOD) or a\nhigh-intensity training group (HIGH). The MOD group maintained essentially the\nsame training intensity distribution, but training volume was increased from 10 to\n16 h·wk–1. The HIGH group reversed their baseline intensity distribution so that\n83% of training time was performed at 80% to 90% of VO2max, with only 17%\nperformed as low-intensity endurance training. The HIGH group trained 12 h·wk–1.\nThe training intervention period lasted 5 mo. Intensity control was achieved using\nheart rate monitoring and blood lactate sampling throughout the training period.\nDespite 60% more training volume in MOD and approximately four times more\ntraining at an intensity greater than or equal to lactate threshold in HIGH, physi-\nological and performance changes were quite modest in both groups of already\nwell-trained athletes (Table 2).\nGaskill et al reported the results of a 2 y project involving 14 cross-country\nskiers.35 During the first year, athletes trained similarly, averaging 660 training\nhours with 16% HIT (nominal distribution of sessions). Physiological test results\nand race performances during the first year were used to identify seven athletes\nwho responded well to the training and seven who showed poor VO2max and lactate\nthreshold progression, and race results. In the second year, the positive respond-\ners continued us", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "nsified training program with a slight reduction in train-\ning hours. They observed that the nonresponders from year 1 showed a positive\nresponse to the intensified program in year 2 (VO2max, lactate threshold, race\nresult points). The positive respo"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0008_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "495bf735d3047894", "text_span_hash": "495bf735d3047894", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "lt points). The positive responders from year 1 showed a similar development\nin year 2 as year 1.\nEsteve-Lanao et al randomized 12 subelite distance runners to one of two\ntraining groups (Z1 and Z2) that were carefully monitored for 5 mo.36 They base", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training Intensity Distribution    283\n90% of VO2max. They were then randomized to a moderate-intensity (MOD) or a\nhigh-intensity training group (HIGH). The MOD group maintained essentially the\nsame training intensity distribution, but training volume was increased from 10 to\n16 h·wk–1. The HIGH group reversed their baseline intensity distribution so that\n83% of training time was performed at 80% to 90% of VO2max, with only 17%\nperformed as low-intensity endurance training. The HIGH group trained 12 h·wk–1.\nThe training intervention period lasted 5 mo. Intensity control was achieved using\nheart rate monitoring and blood lactate sampling throughout the training period.\nDespite 60% more training volume in MOD and approximately four times more\ntraining at an intensity greater than or equal to lactate threshold in HIGH, physi-\nological and performance changes were quite modest in both groups of already\nwell-trained athletes (Table 2).\nGaskill et al reported the results of a 2 y project involving 14 cross-country\nskiers.35 During the first year, athletes trained similarly, averaging 660 training\nhours with 16% HIT (nominal distribution of sessions). Physiological test results\nand race performances during the first year were used to identify seven athletes\nwho responded well to the training and seven who showed poor VO2max and lactate\nthreshold progression, and race results. In the second year, the positive respond-\ners continued us", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "lt points). The positive responders from year 1 showed a similar development\nin year 2 as year 1.\nEsteve-Lanao et al randomized 12 subelite distance runners to one of two\ntraining groups (Z1 and Z2) that were carefully monitored for 5 mo.36 They base"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0008_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e6d838bf66c9f120", "text_span_hash": "e6d838bf66c9f120", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "onitored for 5 mo.36 They based\nTable 2  Summary of responses to training intensification in well-\ntrained cross-country skiers32–34\nLactate-threshold speed\n20-min run at 9% grade\nSuccinate dehydrogenase\nNote. A summary of results from refs. 32–34.\nD", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training Intensity Distribution    283\n90% of VO2max. They were then randomized to a moderate-intensity (MOD) or a\nhigh-intensity training group (HIGH). The MOD group maintained essentially the\nsame training intensity distribution, but training volume was increased from 10 to\n16 h·wk–1. The HIGH group reversed their baseline intensity distribution so that\n83% of training time was performed at 80% to 90% of VO2max, with only 17%\nperformed as low-intensity endurance training. The HIGH group trained 12 h·wk–1.\nThe training intervention period lasted 5 mo. Intensity control was achieved using\nheart rate monitoring and blood lactate sampling throughout the training period.\nDespite 60% more training volume in MOD and approximately four times more\ntraining at an intensity greater than or equal to lactate threshold in HIGH, physi-\nological and performance changes were quite modest in both groups of already\nwell-trained athletes (Table 2).\nGaskill et al reported the results of a 2 y project involving 14 cross-country\nskiers.35 During the first year, athletes trained similarly, averaging 660 training\nhours with 16% HIT (nominal distribution of sessions). Physiological test results\nand race performances during the first year were used to identify seven athletes\nwho responded well to the training and seven who showed poor VO2max and lactate\nthreshold progression, and race results. In the second year, the positive respond-\ners continued us", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "onitored for 5 mo.36 They based\nTable 2  Summary of responses to training intensification in well-\ntrained cross-country skiers32–34\nLactate-threshold speed\n20-min run at 9% grade\nSuccinate dehydrogenase\nNote. A summary of results from refs. 32–34.\nD"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0009_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "897df482a063f13c", "text_span_hash": "897df482a063f13c", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "their training intensity distribution on the three-zone model described earlier. Based\non time-in-zone heart rate monitoring, Z1 performed 81, 12, and 8% of training in\nzones LIT, ThT, and HIT respectively. The Z2 group performed more ThT, with\n67, 2", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "their training intensity distribution on the three-zone model described earlier. Based\non time-in-zone heart rate monitoring, Z1 performed 81, 12, and 8% of training in\nzones LIT, ThT, and HIT respectively. The Z2 group performed more ThT, with\n67, 25, and 8% of training performed in the three respective zones. Anecdotally,\nthe authors reported that in pilot efforts, they were unable to increase the total time\nspent in intensity zone 3, as it was too hard for the athletes. Total training load was\nmatched between the groups using a modification of TRIMPS. Improvement in a\ntime trial performed before and after the 5 mo period revealed that the group that\nhad trained more zone 1 training showed significantly greater race time improve-\nIngham et al37 randomized 18 experienced U.K. national standard male rowers\ninto two training groups that were initially equivalent based on performance and\nphysiological testing. All the rowers had completed a 25-d postseason “training-\nfree” period just before baseline testing, followed by a 12 wk period of rowing\nergometer training. One group performed 98% of all training between 60 and 75%\nof peak oxygen consumption (LIT). The other group performed 70% training at\n60% to 75% VO2max, as well as 30% of training at an intensity 50% of the way\nbetween power at LT and power at VO2peak (MIX). In practice, the MIX group\nperformed HIT on 3 d·wk–1. The two groups performed virtually identical volumes\nof ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "their training intensity distribution on the three-zone model described earlier. Based\non time-in-zone heart rate monitoring, Z1 performed 81, 12, and 8% of training in\nzones LIT, ThT, and HIT respectively. The Z2 group performed more ThT, with\n67, 2"}
