{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0011_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b3b1184bda7f058b", "text_span_hash": "b3b1184bda7f058b", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ruin and colleagues50 performed a long-term\ntraining study of horses in which they manipulated the hard-easy rhythm of the\nhorses’ training in two ways. After 187 d of daily training in hard-easy fashion, hard\ntraining days were intensified by perfor", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "long-term periodization will likely require systematic athlete monitoring by govern-\ning bodies or Olympic centers in cooperation with sport scientists.\nShort-term periodization of training, involving day-to-day manipulation of\nintensity and duration over a few weeks, has been investigated more extensively.\nEndurance athletes train, rest, and repeat. Training (intensity, duration) and recovery\n(rest interval, nutrition) variables interact to induce both fitness (ie, physiological\nadaptations) and fatigue (ie, stress responses and associated negative health out-\ncomes). This practical dichotomization was introduced by Banister and colleagues\nin their modeling studies of the training process.6,47,48 The predictive value and\nstability of their mathematical approach to the relationship between training input\nand fitness outcome has been challenged.49 Conceptually, the model remains useful\nin that it predicts that day-to-day organization of training, recovery, and nutritional\nstrategies should tend to maximize the gain in fitness for a given long-term cost\n(fatigue, stress, and risk of negative health outcomes).\nOver a period of days, athletes normally perform LIT and ThT/HIT sessions.\nHorses are trained similarly, with alternating “easy days” of continuous running\nand “hard days” of interval training. Bruin and colleagues50 performed a long-term\ntraining study of horses in which they manipulated the hard-easy rhythm of the\nhorses’", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ruin and colleagues50 performed a long-term\ntraining study of horses in which they manipulated the hard-easy rhythm of the\nhorses’ training in two ways. After 187 d of daily training in hard-easy fashion, hard\ntraining days were intensified by perfor"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0011_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4c10450f699c4d49", "text_span_hash": "4c10450f699c4d49", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ays were intensified by performing more total high-intensity running, with\neasy days left unchanged. The horses exhibited improved running performance over\nthe next 75 d. After 261 d, the easy days were intensified by having the horses run\nfaster for", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "long-term periodization will likely require systematic athlete monitoring by govern-\ning bodies or Olympic centers in cooperation with sport scientists.\nShort-term periodization of training, involving day-to-day manipulation of\nintensity and duration over a few weeks, has been investigated more extensively.\nEndurance athletes train, rest, and repeat. Training (intensity, duration) and recovery\n(rest interval, nutrition) variables interact to induce both fitness (ie, physiological\nadaptations) and fatigue (ie, stress responses and associated negative health out-\ncomes). This practical dichotomization was introduced by Banister and colleagues\nin their modeling studies of the training process.6,47,48 The predictive value and\nstability of their mathematical approach to the relationship between training input\nand fitness outcome has been challenged.49 Conceptually, the model remains useful\nin that it predicts that day-to-day organization of training, recovery, and nutritional\nstrategies should tend to maximize the gain in fitness for a given long-term cost\n(fatigue, stress, and risk of negative health outcomes).\nOver a period of days, athletes normally perform LIT and ThT/HIT sessions.\nHorses are trained similarly, with alternating “easy days” of continuous running\nand “hard days” of interval training. Bruin and colleagues50 performed a long-term\ntraining study of horses in which they manipulated the hard-easy rhythm of the\nhorses’", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ays were intensified by performing more total high-intensity running, with\neasy days left unchanged. The horses exhibited improved running performance over\nthe next 75 d. After 261 d, the easy days were intensified by having the horses run\nfaster for"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0011_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "48bddec134fa520c", "text_span_hash": "48bddec134fa520c", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "thletes and conceptualized training monotony\nas increasing the risk of negative adaptations to training.51 High training stress was\nquantified as a product of large training volumes, high perceived intensity, and low\nday-to-day variation in training", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "long-term periodization will likely require systematic athlete monitoring by govern-\ning bodies or Olympic centers in cooperation with sport scientists.\nShort-term periodization of training, involving day-to-day manipulation of\nintensity and duration over a few weeks, has been investigated more extensively.\nEndurance athletes train, rest, and repeat. Training (intensity, duration) and recovery\n(rest interval, nutrition) variables interact to induce both fitness (ie, physiological\nadaptations) and fatigue (ie, stress responses and associated negative health out-\ncomes). This practical dichotomization was introduced by Banister and colleagues\nin their modeling studies of the training process.6,47,48 The predictive value and\nstability of their mathematical approach to the relationship between training input\nand fitness outcome has been challenged.49 Conceptually, the model remains useful\nin that it predicts that day-to-day organization of training, recovery, and nutritional\nstrategies should tend to maximize the gain in fitness for a given long-term cost\n(fatigue, stress, and risk of negative health outcomes).\nOver a period of days, athletes normally perform LIT and ThT/HIT sessions.\nHorses are trained similarly, with alternating “easy days” of continuous running\nand “hard days” of interval training. Bruin and colleagues50 performed a long-term\ntraining study of horses in which they manipulated the hard-easy rhythm of the\nhorses’", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "thletes and conceptualized training monotony\nas increasing the risk of negative adaptations to training.51 High training stress was\nquantified as a product of large training volumes, high perceived intensity, and low\nday-to-day variation in training"}
{"chunk_id": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice_p0011_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_6ae73701b6f8", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "07c343f33b1dc97c", "text_span_hash": "07c343f33b1dc97c", "source_file": "Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Seiler_2010_TID_Best_Practice.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "-to-day variation in training load. Elite athletes often train twice or even three\ntimes per day, making the rest interval between training sessions typically between\n4 and 12 h. Achieving this training frequency without excessive stress appears to\nr", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "long-term periodization will likely require systematic athlete monitoring by govern-\ning bodies or Olympic centers in cooperation with sport scientists.\nShort-term periodization of training, involving day-to-day manipulation of\nintensity and duration over a few weeks, has been investigated more extensively.\nEndurance athletes train, rest, and repeat. Training (intensity, duration) and recovery\n(rest interval, nutrition) variables interact to induce both fitness (ie, physiological\nadaptations) and fatigue (ie, stress responses and associated negative health out-\ncomes). This practical dichotomization was introduced by Banister and colleagues\nin their modeling studies of the training process.6,47,48 The predictive value and\nstability of their mathematical approach to the relationship between training input\nand fitness outcome has been challenged.49 Conceptually, the model remains useful\nin that it predicts that day-to-day organization of training, recovery, and nutritional\nstrategies should tend to maximize the gain in fitness for a given long-term cost\n(fatigue, stress, and risk of negative health outcomes).\nOver a period of days, athletes normally perform LIT and ThT/HIT sessions.\nHorses are trained similarly, with alternating “easy days” of continuous running\nand “hard days” of interval training. Bruin and colleagues50 performed a long-term\ntraining study of horses in which they manipulated the hard-easy rhythm of the\nhorses’", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "-to-day variation in training load. Elite athletes often train twice or even three\ntimes per day, making the rest interval between training sessions typically between\n4 and 12 h. Achieving this training frequency without excessive stress appears to\nr"}
