ults on a patient-by-patient basis. Stage of\nirritability should classify the patient’s condition as being acute or nonacute, using the diagnostic indicators outlined in component 5.\nDiagnosis of midportion Achilles tendinopathy\n• Gradual onset of pain\n• Pain 2 to 6 cm proximal to Achille", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ma (PRP)\ninjections, are included as education for patients who are\nseeking additional treatment options. Of note, the majority\nof studies include patients with chronic midportion Achilles\ntendinopathy. Therefore, treatment of a patient with acute\nAc"}
{"chunk_id": "Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG_p0012_c0020", "source_registry_id": "src_4df77e93e122", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "fc04828973e07b42", "text_span_hash": "fc04828973e07b42", "source_file": "Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 20, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ent of a patient with acute\nAchilles tendinopathy may depend more on clinical judg­\nment and expert opinion.\nJournal of Orthopaedic & Sports Physical Therapy®\nDownloaded from www.jospt.org at on May 1, 2018. For personal use only. No other uses witho", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Midportion Achilles Tendinopathy: Clinical Practice Guidelines Revision 2018\na12 | may 2018 | volume 48 | number 5 | journal of orthopaedic & sports physical therapy\nAppropriate for physical therapy\nevaluation and intervention\nAppropriate for physical therapy\nevaluation and intervention along\nwith consultation with another\nhealth care provider\nNot appropriate for physical therapy\nevaluation and intervention\nConsultation with appropriate health\nComponent 1: Medical Screening\nComponent 2: Classify Condition\nClassify condition through diﬀerential evaluation of clinical ﬁndings suggestive of musculoskeletal impairments of body functioning (ICF) and the\nassociated tissue pathology/disease (ICD)\nComponent 3: Determination of Irritability Stage\nDiagnosis of tissue irritability is important for guiding the clinical decisions regarding treatment frequency, intensity, duration, and type, with the goal of\nmatching the optimal dosage of treatment to the status of the tissue being treated. There are cases where the alignment of irritability and duration of\nsymptoms does not match, requiring clinicians to make judgments when applying time-based research results on a patient-by-patient basis. Stage of\nirritability should classify the patient’s condition as being acute or nonacute, using the diagnostic indicators outlined in component 5.\nDiagnosis of midportion Achilles tendinopathy\n• Gradual onset of pain\n• Pain 2 to 6 cm proximal to Achille", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ent of a patient with acute\nAchilles tendinopathy may depend more on clinical judg­\nment and expert opinion.\nJournal of Orthopaedic & Sports Physical Therapy®\nDownloaded from www.jospt.org at on May 1, 2018. For personal use only. No other uses witho"}
{"chunk_id": "Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG_p0013_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_4df77e93e122", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4e9327f2d180e075", "text_span_hash": "4e9327f2d180e075", "source_file": "Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Midportion Achilles Tendinopathy: Clinical Practice Guidelines Revision 2018\njournal of orthopaedic & sports physical therapy | volume 48 | number 5 | may 2018 | a13\nComponent 5: Intervention Strategies\nAcute: Diagnostic Indicators\n• Redness, warmth,", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Midportion Achilles Tendinopathy: Clinical Practice Guidelines Revision 2018\njournal of orthopaedic & sports physical therapy | volume 48 | number 5 | may 2018 | a13\nComponent 5: Intervention Strategies\nAcute: Diagnostic Indicators\n• Redness, warmth, and swelling\n• ≤3 mo in duration\n• High levels of pain limiting low-level activity (ie, walking)\nFindings/interventions\n• Pain and inﬂammation\n– Joint and/or soft tissue mobilizationF\n– Other range-of-motion protective treatment (ie, bracing)F\nInclude patient educationB and counselingE\nNonacute: Diagnostic Indicators\n• No redness, warmth, and swelling\n• >3 mo in duration\n• Pain after the onset of or after completing higher-level activity (ie,\njumping and running)\nFindings/interventions\n• Tendon pain with palpation, with or without presence of nodules\n– Mechanical loading exercises: eccentric, concentric/eccentric,\nor heavy load and slow speedA\n– Joint and/or soft tissue mobilizationF\n– Other range-of-motion protective treatment (ie, bracing)F\n• Abnormal lower-quarter musculoskeletal and biomechanical\n– Neuromuscular exercises targeting lower extremity impairments\nthat may lead to abnormal kinetics and/or kinematicsF\nInclude patient educationB and counseling\nDischarge to self-management\n• Consultation with other providers (extracorporeal\nshockwave therapy, corticosteroid injection,\nplatelet-rich plasma injection, etc)\nSuccessful recovery at 6 to 12 mo\n• Tolerable intermittent pain\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Midportion Achilles Tendinopathy: Clinical Practice Guidelines Revision 2018\njournal of orthopaedic & sports physical therapy | volume 48 | number 5 | may 2018 | a13\nComponent 5: Intervention Strategies\nAcute: Diagnostic Indicators\n• Redness, warmth,"}
{"chunk_id": "Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG_p0013_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_4df77e93e122", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "05f5fe31081a7743", "text_span_hash": "05f5fe31081a7743", "source_file": "Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "er range-of-motion protective treatment (ie, bracing)F\nInclude patient educationB and counselingE\nNonacute: Diagnostic Indicators\n• No redness, warmth, and swelling\n• >3 mo in duration\n• Pain after the onset of or after completing higher-level activi", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Midportion Achilles Tendinopathy: Clinical Practice Guidelines Revision 2018\njournal of orthopaedic & sports physical therapy | volume 48 | number 5 | may 2018 | a13\nComponent 5: Intervention Strategies\nAcute: Diagnostic Indicators\n• Redness, warmth, and swelling\n• ≤3 mo in duration\n• High levels of pain limiting low-level activity (ie, walking)\nFindings/interventions\n• Pain and inﬂammation\n– Joint and/or soft tissue mobilizationF\n– Other range-of-motion protective treatment (ie, bracing)F\nInclude patient educationB and counselingE\nNonacute: Diagnostic Indicators\n• No redness, warmth, and swelling\n• >3 mo in duration\n• Pain after the onset of or after completing higher-level activity (ie,\njumping and running)\nFindings/interventions\n• Tendon pain with palpation, with or without presence of nodules\n– Mechanical loading exercises: eccentric, concentric/eccentric,\nor heavy load and slow speedA\n– Joint and/or soft tissue mobilizationF\n– Other range-of-motion protective treatment (ie, bracing)F\n• Abnormal lower-quarter musculoskeletal and biomechanical\n– Neuromuscular exercises targeting lower extremity impairments\nthat may lead to abnormal kinetics and/or kinematicsF\nInclude patient educationB and counseling\nDischarge to self-management\n• Consultation with other providers (extracorporeal\nshockwave therapy, corticosteroid injection,\nplatelet-rich plasma injection, etc)\nSuccessful recovery at 6 to 12 mo\n• Tolerable intermittent pain\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "er range-of-motion protective treatment (ie, bracing)F\nInclude patient educationB and counselingE\nNonacute: Diagnostic Indicators\n• No redness, warmth, and swelling\n• >3 mo in duration\n• Pain after the onset of or after completing higher-level activi"}
{"chunk_id": "Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG_p0014_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_4df77e93e122", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c9bdddfc1c579741", "text_span_hash": "c9bdddfc1c579741", "source_file": "Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG.pdf", "page": 14, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "tremity tendinopathies. They\nrecommended the hop tests and the heel-raise endurance test\nin the evaluation of functional performance in patients with\nAchilles tendinopathy.\nClinicians should use physical performance mea­\nsures, including hop and heel", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Midportion Achilles Tendinopathy: Clinical Practice Guidelines Revision 2018\na14 | may 2018 | volume 48 | number 5 | journal of orthopaedic & sports physical therapy\nOUTCOME MEASURES – ACTIVITY LIMITATIONS/\nSELF-REPORTED MEASURES\nClinicians should incorporate validated functional\noutcome measures, such as the Victorian Institute\nof Sport Assessment-Achilles (VISA-A) and the\nFoot and Ankle Ability Measure (FAAM). These should be\nutilized before and after interventions intended to alleviate\nthe impairments of body function and structure, activity limi­\ntations, and participation restrictions associated with Achil­\nIversen et al97 provided evidence of validity and reli­\nability for the VISA-A questionnaire in Danish-\nspeaking individuals.\nThe VISA-A has been validated for patients with\nAchilles tendinopathy who speak Turkish57 and\nFrench.103 The validity and reliability findings in\nthese studies are consistent with the results reported in the\npreviously published Achilles tendinopathy guideline.22\nSystematic reviews have assessed the evidence to\nsupport outcome measures for those with lower-\nleg, ankle, and foot conditions.136,181 The Lower Ex­\ntremity Functional Scale (LEFS) and FAAM were found to be\nmost commonly used, with the FAAM receiving the highest\nquality assessment score for responsiveness.181 A separate sys­\ntematic review found evidence of reliability, validity, and re­\nsponsiveness to support the LEFS for individuals", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "tremity tendinopathies. They\nrecommended the hop tests and the heel-raise endurance test\nin the evaluation of functional performance in patients with\nAchilles tendinopathy.\nClinicians should use physical performance mea­\nsures, including hop and heel"}
{"chunk_id": "Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG_p0014_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_4df77e93e122", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "70cb6858469cda07", "text_span_hash": "70cb6858469cda07", "source_file": "Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG.pdf", "page": 14, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "sures, including hop and heel-raise endurance tests\nas appropriate, to assess a patient’s functional sta­\ntus and document findings.\nPHYSICAL IMPAIRMENT MEASURES\nRecommended impairment measures and their properties\nare provided in the 2010 CPG.22 Se", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Midportion Achilles Tendinopathy: Clinical Practice Guidelines Revision 2018\na14 | may 2018 | volume 48 | number 5 | journal of orthopaedic & sports physical therapy\nOUTCOME MEASURES – ACTIVITY LIMITATIONS/\nSELF-REPORTED MEASURES\nClinicians should incorporate validated functional\noutcome measures, such as the Victorian Institute\nof Sport Assessment-Achilles (VISA-A) and the\nFoot and Ankle Ability Measure (FAAM). These should be\nutilized before and after interventions intended to alleviate\nthe impairments of body function and structure, activity limi­\ntations, and participation restrictions associated with Achil­\nIversen et al97 provided evidence of validity and reli­\nability for the VISA-A questionnaire in Danish-\nspeaking individuals.\nThe VISA-A has been validated for patients with\nAchilles tendinopathy who speak Turkish57 and\nFrench.103 The validity and reliability findings in\nthese studies are consistent with the results reported in the\npreviously published Achilles tendinopathy guideline.22\nSystematic reviews have assessed the evidence to\nsupport outcome measures for those with lower-\nleg, ankle, and foot conditions.136,181 The Lower Ex­\ntremity Functional Scale (LEFS) and FAAM were found to be\nmost commonly used, with the FAAM receiving the highest\nquality assessment score for responsiveness.181 A separate sys­\ntematic review found evidence of reliability, validity, and re­\nsponsiveness to support the LEFS for individuals", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "sures, including hop and heel-raise endurance tests\nas appropriate, to assess a patient’s functional sta­\ntus and document findings.\nPHYSICAL IMPAIRMENT MEASURES\nRecommended impairment measures and their properties\nare provided in the 2010 CPG.22 Se"}
{"chunk_id": "Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG_p0015_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_4df77e93e122", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "d422d67609110d8b", "text_span_hash": "d422d67609110d8b", "source_file": "Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG.pdf", "page": 15, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "or a heavy-\nload, slow-speed exercise and an eccentric training\nprotocol. The heavy-load, slow-speed exercise pro­\ntocol included 3 bilateral full-range-of-motion heel-raise ex­\nercises performed at a speed of 6 seconds per repetition as\nfollows: (1)", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Midportion Achilles Tendinopathy: Clinical Practice Guidelines Revision 2018\njournal of orthopaedic & sports physical therapy | volume 48 | number 5 | may 2018 | a15\nA systematic search of the literature did not reveal articles to\nalter the 2010 recommendations for iontophoresis, manual\ntherapy, or heel lifts in the treatment of midportion Achilles\ntendinopathy. Updated recommendations are provided for\nexercise, which includes eccentric, eccentric/concentric, and\nheavy-load, slow-speed protocols; stretching; night splints;\nlow-level laser therapy (LLLT); orthoses; taping; neuro­\nmuscular re-education; and dry needling. Although corti­\ncosteroid injection, ESWT, and PRP injections are used as\ninterventions for those with Achilles tendinopathy, they are\noutside the scope of physical therapy practice, and therefore\nonly summaries are provided for patient education purposes.\nClinicians should consider implementing an eccen­\ntric loading program to decrease pain and improve\nfunction in patients with midportion Achilles\nIn a systematic review by Sussmilch-Leitch et al,192 9\nrandomized controlled trials, all published before\n2009, directly studied eccentric exercise. This sys­\ntematic review supported the use of eccentric exercise for\nmidportion Achilles tendinopathy.\nBeyer et al14 found similar outcomes for a heavy-\nload, slow-speed exercise and an eccentric training\nprotocol. The heavy-load, slow-speed exercise pro­\ntocol included ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "or a heavy-\nload, slow-speed exercise and an eccentric training\nprotocol. The heavy-load, slow-speed exercise pro­\ntocol included 3 bilateral full-range-of-motion heel-raise ex­\nercises performed at a speed of 6 seconds per repetition as\nfollows: (1)"}
