centric exercise. This sys­\ntematic review supported the use of eccentric exercise for\nmidportion Achilles tendinopathy.\nBeyer et al14 found similar outcomes for a heavy-\nload, slow-speed exercise and an eccentric training\nprotocol. The heavy-load, slow-speed exercise pro­\ntocol included ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "o had tried at\nleast 1 other treatment to a 12-week eccentric training pro­\ngram. Despite experiencing improved scores on the VISA-A,\npain VAS, and Tegner activity scale, only 2 participants were\nsatisfied with treatment. Compared to other studies, t"}
{"chunk_id": "Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG_p0015_c0022", "source_registry_id": "src_4df77e93e122", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "17e595dcf9e19368", "text_span_hash": "17e595dcf9e19368", "source_file": "Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG.pdf", "page": 15, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 22, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": ". Compared to other studies, the low\nsatisfaction may have to do with the fact that patients had a\nchronic condition and had tried other treatments.14\nJournal of Orthopaedic & Sports Physical Therapy®\nDownloaded from www.jospt.org at on May 1, 2018.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Midportion Achilles Tendinopathy: Clinical Practice Guidelines Revision 2018\njournal of orthopaedic & sports physical therapy | volume 48 | number 5 | may 2018 | a15\nA systematic search of the literature did not reveal articles to\nalter the 2010 recommendations for iontophoresis, manual\ntherapy, or heel lifts in the treatment of midportion Achilles\ntendinopathy. Updated recommendations are provided for\nexercise, which includes eccentric, eccentric/concentric, and\nheavy-load, slow-speed protocols; stretching; night splints;\nlow-level laser therapy (LLLT); orthoses; taping; neuro­\nmuscular re-education; and dry needling. Although corti­\ncosteroid injection, ESWT, and PRP injections are used as\ninterventions for those with Achilles tendinopathy, they are\noutside the scope of physical therapy practice, and therefore\nonly summaries are provided for patient education purposes.\nClinicians should consider implementing an eccen­\ntric loading program to decrease pain and improve\nfunction in patients with midportion Achilles\nIn a systematic review by Sussmilch-Leitch et al,192 9\nrandomized controlled trials, all published before\n2009, directly studied eccentric exercise. This sys­\ntematic review supported the use of eccentric exercise for\nmidportion Achilles tendinopathy.\nBeyer et al14 found similar outcomes for a heavy-\nload, slow-speed exercise and an eccentric training\nprotocol. The heavy-load, slow-speed exercise pro­\ntocol included ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": ". Compared to other studies, the low\nsatisfaction may have to do with the fact that patients had a\nchronic condition and had tried other treatments.14\nJournal of Orthopaedic & Sports Physical Therapy®\nDownloaded from www.jospt.org at on May 1, 2018."}
{"chunk_id": "Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG_p0016_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_4df77e93e122", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1fdf51b78b480685", "text_span_hash": "1fdf51b78b480685", "source_file": "Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG.pdf", "page": 16, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "extended to reduce\npain and improve satisfaction with outcome in pa­\ntients with midportion Achilles tendinopathy who exhibit\nlimited ankle dorsiflexion range of motion.\nNEUROMUSCULAR RE-EDUCATION\nNeuromuscular control among runners with mid­\nportio", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Midportion Achilles Tendinopathy: Clinical Practice Guidelines Revision 2018\na16 | may 2018 | volume 48 | number 5 | journal of orthopaedic & sports physical therapy\nde Vos et al48 examined changes in tendon structure\nusing a specific ultrasonic tissue characterization\napproach before and after a 16-week eccentric ex­\nercise program. The changes defined by the ultrasonic tissue\ncharacterization approach found no association between col­\nlagen type and VISA-A scores at any time point.\nSeveral randomized controlled trials compared ec­\ncentric exercise combined with other interven­\nThe observed changes in the control groups (eccentric exer­\ncise alone) provide useful information. Improvement in\nsymptom severity (VISA-A) across studies varied in these\ncontrol groups from 2.4% at 8 weeks,215 13% at 12 weeks,147\nat 52 weeks.41,197 When eccentric exercise was combined\nwith PRP,41,49,50 autologous blood injections,147 or prolother­\napy,212 the results were equivalent to eccentric exercise\nalone. However, when eccentric exercise was combined with\nLLLT,196 ESWT,165 or acupuncture,215 studies favored the\ncombined treatments.\nIt should be noted that studies have excluded participants\nwith presumed frailty of the tendon structure because of\nmetabolic or genetic diseases and drugs. Therefore, little is\nknown about risks and benefits of eccentric exercise for these\nBecause specific factors (eg, frequency, load, and speed) are\nnot standardize", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "extended to reduce\npain and improve satisfaction with outcome in pa­\ntients with midportion Achilles tendinopathy who exhibit\nlimited ankle dorsiflexion range of motion.\nNEUROMUSCULAR RE-EDUCATION\nNeuromuscular control among runners with mid­\nportio"}
{"chunk_id": "Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG_p0016_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_4df77e93e122", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "719ee6c63569211e", "text_span_hash": "719ee6c63569211e", "source_file": "Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG.pdf", "page": 16, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "among runners with mid­\nportion Achilles tendinopathy has been examined\nin several case-control studies.10,13,70 Running stud­\nies identified patterns of decreased lower extremity muscle\nactivity in participants with midportion Achilles tendinopa­\nth", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Midportion Achilles Tendinopathy: Clinical Practice Guidelines Revision 2018\na16 | may 2018 | volume 48 | number 5 | journal of orthopaedic & sports physical therapy\nde Vos et al48 examined changes in tendon structure\nusing a specific ultrasonic tissue characterization\napproach before and after a 16-week eccentric ex­\nercise program. The changes defined by the ultrasonic tissue\ncharacterization approach found no association between col­\nlagen type and VISA-A scores at any time point.\nSeveral randomized controlled trials compared ec­\ncentric exercise combined with other interven­\nThe observed changes in the control groups (eccentric exer­\ncise alone) provide useful information. Improvement in\nsymptom severity (VISA-A) across studies varied in these\ncontrol groups from 2.4% at 8 weeks,215 13% at 12 weeks,147\nat 52 weeks.41,197 When eccentric exercise was combined\nwith PRP,41,49,50 autologous blood injections,147 or prolother­\napy,212 the results were equivalent to eccentric exercise\nalone. However, when eccentric exercise was combined with\nLLLT,196 ESWT,165 or acupuncture,215 studies favored the\ncombined treatments.\nIt should be noted that studies have excluded participants\nwith presumed frailty of the tendon structure because of\nmetabolic or genetic diseases and drugs. Therefore, little is\nknown about risks and benefits of eccentric exercise for these\nBecause specific factors (eg, frequency, load, and speed) are\nnot standardize", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "among runners with mid­\nportion Achilles tendinopathy has been examined\nin several case-control studies.10,13,70 Running stud­\nies identified patterns of decreased lower extremity muscle\nactivity in participants with midportion Achilles tendinopa­\nth"}
{"chunk_id": "Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG_p0017_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_4df77e93e122", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "83f2b311e4fc1e2b", "text_span_hash": "83f2b311e4fc1e2b", "source_file": "Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG.pdf", "page": 17, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "n patients with midportion Achilles\nA foot orthosis can be used to reduce pain and alter\nankle and foot kinematics while running in patients\nwith Achilles tendinopathy.\nCheatham et al27 looked at the efficacy of soft tissue\nmobilization in a systemat", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Midportion Achilles Tendinopathy: Clinical Practice Guidelines Revision 2018\njournal of orthopaedic & sports physical therapy | volume 48 | number 5 | may 2018 | a17\nClinicians may counsel patients with midportion\nAchilles tendinopathy. Key elements of patient\ncounseling could include (1) theories supporting\nuse of physical therapy and role of mechanical loading; (2)\nmodifiable risk factors, including BMI and shoewear; and (3)\ntypical time course for recovery from symptoms.\n2010 and 2018 Recommendation\nBecause contradictory evidence exists, no recom­\nmendation can be made for the use of heel lifts in\npatients with midportion Achilles tendinopathy.\nNight splints are not beneficial in reducing pain\nwhen compared to eccentric exercise in patients\nwith Achilles tendinopathy.\nA systematic review by Sussmilch-Leitch et al192\nfound 2 studies with conflicting results on the ad­\nditional effect of night splints added to an eccentric\nexercise program. A pooled meta-analysis found that a night\nsplint provided no significant additional improvement in\npatient-reported symptoms (VISA-A) at 12 weeks.\nA 1-year follow-up randomized controlled trial\nfound no additional benefit of a night splint to ec­\ncentric exercise.40 There were no significant differ­\nences in symptom severity (VISA-A) between groups at\nbaseline or 3-month and 1-year follow-ups. There were also\nno significant differences between groups in morning stiff­\nness or patient satis", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "n patients with midportion Achilles\nA foot orthosis can be used to reduce pain and alter\nankle and foot kinematics while running in patients\nwith Achilles tendinopathy.\nCheatham et al27 looked at the efficacy of soft tissue\nmobilization in a systemat"}
{"chunk_id": "Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG_p0017_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_4df77e93e122", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "39df68f4e727dc04", "text_span_hash": "39df68f4e727dc04", "source_file": "Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG.pdf", "page": 17, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ion Achilles tendinopathy\nPATIENT EDUCATION: ACTIVITY MODIFICATION\nSilbernagel et al182 compared the effects of contin­\nued sports activity (eg, running and jumping activi­\nties below a specified pain intensity) to active rest\nwhile patients complete", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Midportion Achilles Tendinopathy: Clinical Practice Guidelines Revision 2018\njournal of orthopaedic & sports physical therapy | volume 48 | number 5 | may 2018 | a17\nClinicians may counsel patients with midportion\nAchilles tendinopathy. Key elements of patient\ncounseling could include (1) theories supporting\nuse of physical therapy and role of mechanical loading; (2)\nmodifiable risk factors, including BMI and shoewear; and (3)\ntypical time course for recovery from symptoms.\n2010 and 2018 Recommendation\nBecause contradictory evidence exists, no recom­\nmendation can be made for the use of heel lifts in\npatients with midportion Achilles tendinopathy.\nNight splints are not beneficial in reducing pain\nwhen compared to eccentric exercise in patients\nwith Achilles tendinopathy.\nA systematic review by Sussmilch-Leitch et al192\nfound 2 studies with conflicting results on the ad­\nditional effect of night splints added to an eccentric\nexercise program. A pooled meta-analysis found that a night\nsplint provided no significant additional improvement in\npatient-reported symptoms (VISA-A) at 12 weeks.\nA 1-year follow-up randomized controlled trial\nfound no additional benefit of a night splint to ec­\ncentric exercise.40 There were no significant differ­\nences in symptom severity (VISA-A) between groups at\nbaseline or 3-month and 1-year follow-ups. There were also\nno significant differences between groups in morning stiff­\nness or patient satis", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ion Achilles tendinopathy\nPATIENT EDUCATION: ACTIVITY MODIFICATION\nSilbernagel et al182 compared the effects of contin­\nued sports activity (eg, running and jumping activi­\nties below a specified pain intensity) to active rest\nwhile patients complete"}
{"chunk_id": "Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG_p0017_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_4df77e93e122", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "05058d92805c840c", "text_span_hash": "05058d92805c840c", "source_file": "Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG.pdf", "page": 17, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "with the mean VISA-A scores greater than 90 for\nFor patients with nonacute midportion Achilles\ntendinopathy, clinicians should advise that com­\nplete rest is not indicated and that they should con­\ntinue with their recreational activity within their", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Midportion Achilles Tendinopathy: Clinical Practice Guidelines Revision 2018\njournal of orthopaedic & sports physical therapy | volume 48 | number 5 | may 2018 | a17\nClinicians may counsel patients with midportion\nAchilles tendinopathy. Key elements of patient\ncounseling could include (1) theories supporting\nuse of physical therapy and role of mechanical loading; (2)\nmodifiable risk factors, including BMI and shoewear; and (3)\ntypical time course for recovery from symptoms.\n2010 and 2018 Recommendation\nBecause contradictory evidence exists, no recom­\nmendation can be made for the use of heel lifts in\npatients with midportion Achilles tendinopathy.\nNight splints are not beneficial in reducing pain\nwhen compared to eccentric exercise in patients\nwith Achilles tendinopathy.\nA systematic review by Sussmilch-Leitch et al192\nfound 2 studies with conflicting results on the ad­\nditional effect of night splints added to an eccentric\nexercise program. A pooled meta-analysis found that a night\nsplint provided no significant additional improvement in\npatient-reported symptoms (VISA-A) at 12 weeks.\nA 1-year follow-up randomized controlled trial\nfound no additional benefit of a night splint to ec­\ncentric exercise.40 There were no significant differ­\nences in symptom severity (VISA-A) between groups at\nbaseline or 3-month and 1-year follow-ups. There were also\nno significant differences between groups in morning stiff­\nness or patient satis", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "with the mean VISA-A scores greater than 90 for\nFor patients with nonacute midportion Achilles\ntendinopathy, clinicians should advise that com­\nplete rest is not indicated and that they should con­\ntinue with their recreational activity within their"}
{"chunk_id": "Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG_p0019_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_4df77e93e122", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "716b228f91d8d673", "text_span_hash": "716b228f91d8d673", "source_file": "Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG.pdf", "page": 19, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ile\nthose in the exercise-only group improved 11 points.17,26\nEXTRACORPOREAL SHOCKWAVE THERAPY\nExtracorporeal shockwave therapy, when combined with\neccentric exercise for chronic midportion Achilles tendi­\nnopathy, is supported in some systematic rev", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Midportion Achilles Tendinopathy: Clinical Practice Guidelines Revision 2018\njournal of orthopaedic & sports physical therapy | volume 48 | number 5 | may 2018 | a19\nIn a recent prospective cohort study,206 comparisons\nwere made between high-volume image-guided in­\njection (HVIGI) with and without dry needling. Par­\nticipants in the HVIGI-only group improved an average of 33.4\npoints on the VISA-A, while the participants in the HVIGI and\ndry needling group on average only improved by 6.9 points.\nIn a case series study by Yeo et al,213 participants\nreceived tendon injection of marcaine (tendon de­\ncompression) followed by dry needling in conjunc­\ntion with a 4-week eccentric exercise program. Pain VAS\nscores (0-100) during rest and activity decreased by 24% and\n39.1%, respectively, at 6 weeks post procedure. At 12 and 24\nmonths, 77% and 76% of participants, respectively, had high\nor very high satisfaction levels.\nClinicians may use combined therapy of dry nee­\ndling with injection under ultrasound guidance and\neccentric exercise to decrease pain for individuals\nwith symptoms greater than 3 months and increased tendon\nINTERVENTIONS OUTSIDE THE SCOPE\nSummaries were not provided in 2010 for corticosteroid in­\njection, ESWT, and PRP injections.\nCORTICOSTEROID INJECTION\nA systematic review of randomized controlled trials of corti­\ncosteroid injections for all types of tendinopathy concluded\nthat an initial short-term benefit is not ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ile\nthose in the exercise-only group improved 11 points.17,26\nEXTRACORPOREAL SHOCKWAVE THERAPY\nExtracorporeal shockwave therapy, when combined with\neccentric exercise for chronic midportion Achilles tendi­\nnopathy, is supported in some systematic rev"}
{"chunk_id": "Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG_p0021_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_4df77e93e122", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5b42649819734ab2", "text_span_hash": "5b42649819734ab2", "source_file": "Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Silbernagel_2018_Achilles_CPG.pdf", "page": 21, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "4:458-462. https://doi.\n13. \u0007Baur H, Müller S, Hirschmüller A, Cassel M, Weber J, Mayer F. Comparison\nin lower leg neuromuscular activity between runners with unilateral mid-\nportion Achilles tendinopathy and healthy individuals. J Electromyogr\n14. \u0007", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Midportion Achilles Tendinopathy: Clinical Practice Guidelines Revision 2018\njournal of orthopaedic & sports physical therapy | volume 48 | number 5 | may 2018 | a21\n1. \u0007Abate M, Salini V, Schiavone C. Achilles tendinopathy in elderly subjects\nwith type II diabetes: the role of sport activities. Aging Clin Exp Res.\n2. \u0007Abrahams Y, Laguette MJ, Prince S, Collins M. Polymorphisms within the\nCOL5A1 3’-UTR that alters mRNA structure and the MIR608 gene are\nassociated with Achilles tendinopathy. Ann Hum Genet. 2013;77:204-214.\n3. \u0007Albers IS, Zwerver J, Diercks RL, Dekker JH, Van den Akker-Scheek I. Inci­\ndence and prevalence of lower extremity tendinopathy in a Dutch general\npractice population: a cross sectional study. BMC Musculoskelet Disord.\n4. \u0007Alfredson H, Cook J. A treatment algorithm for managing Achilles ten­\ndinopathy: new treatment options. Br J Sports Med. 2007;41:211-216.\n5. \u0007Ames PR, Longo UG, Denaro V, Maffulli N. Achilles tendon problems: not\njust an orthopaedic issue. Disabil Rehabil. 2008;30:1646-1650. https://\n6. \u0007Amin NH, McCullough KC, Mills GL, et al. The impact and functional\noutcomes of Achilles tendon pathology in National Basketball As­\nsociation players. Clin Res Foot Ankle. 2016;4:205. https://doi.\n7. \u0007Andia I, Maffulli N. Clinical outcomes of biologic treatment for chronic ten­\n8. \u0007Arya S, Kulig K. Tendinopathy alters mechanical and material properties of\nthe Achilles tendon. J Appl Physiol (1985). 2010", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "4:458-462. https://doi.\n13. \u0007Baur H, Müller S, Hirschmüller A, Cassel M, Weber J, Mayer F. Comparison\nin lower leg neuromuscular activity between runners with unilateral mid-\nportion Achilles tendinopathy and healthy individuals. J Electromyogr\n14. \u0007"}
