{"chunk_id": "Smith_1995_Attentional_Strategy_Distance_Runners_p0005_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_be9ef5ba11d2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "047d1da797370417", "text_span_hash": "047d1da797370417", "source_file": "Smith_1995_Attentional_Strategy_Distance_Runners.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Smith_1995_Attentional_Strategy_Distance_Runners.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "the dynamical system. This is referred to as the state\nspace (Abraham, Abraham, & Shaw, 1992; Abraham &\nShaw, 1982). Attractors are formed when the system is\ndrawn to specific regions of the state space. An example\nof a phase portrait for the motion", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "tern-emerges. These patterns result from a dynamical\nsystems’ collective dynamics. We can represent these\npatterns geometrically in a phase portrait. This is done\nby creating a geometric model for all possible states of\nthe dynamical system. This is referred to as the state\nspace (Abraham, Abraham, & Shaw, 1992; Abraham &\nShaw, 1982). Attractors are formed when the system is\ndrawn to specific regions of the state space. An example\nof a phase portrait for the motion of the lower leg (i.e..\nthe shank) is depicted in Figure 1. The state space is de-\nfined by the angular inclination of the position of the\nshank on the x axis and the shank‘s angular velocity on\nthe y axis. The space defines all the possible states of the\nwalking shank. The periodic trajectories mapped in the\nstate space represent the attractor formed by this\nsystem’s dynamics. This attractor compresses the behav-\nior of multiple muscles of the leg, as well as the motor\nand sensory neurons and motor units, into one dy-\nAttractors represent stable collective states of a dy-\nnamical system. Small perturbations, such as stepping\non a pebble, would not dislodge the system from this\nregion of the state space. Rather, the system once per-\nturbed would return quickly to the original trajectory.\nStable patterns are very low in variability. When variabil-\nity increases, the system is unstable. The loss of stability\nis characteristic of a system in transition. When one\nstable", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "the dynamical system. This is referred to as the state\nspace (Abraham, Abraham, & Shaw, 1992; Abraham &\nShaw, 1982). Attractors are formed when the system is\ndrawn to specific regions of the state space. An example\nof a phase portrait for the motion"}
