{"chunk_id": "Smith_1995_Attentional_Strategy_Distance_Runners_p0005_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_be9ef5ba11d2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "a35c54c98f30077e", "text_span_hash": "a35c54c98f30077e", "source_file": "Smith_1995_Attentional_Strategy_Distance_Runners.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Smith_1995_Attentional_Strategy_Distance_Runners.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ing\non a pebble, would not dislodge the system from this\nregion of the state space. Rather, the system once per-\nturbed would return quickly to the original trajectory.\nStable patterns are very low in variability. When variabil-\nity increases, the sy", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "tern-emerges. These patterns result from a dynamical\nsystems’ collective dynamics. We can represent these\npatterns geometrically in a phase portrait. This is done\nby creating a geometric model for all possible states of\nthe dynamical system. This is referred to as the state\nspace (Abraham, Abraham, & Shaw, 1992; Abraham &\nShaw, 1982). Attractors are formed when the system is\ndrawn to specific regions of the state space. An example\nof a phase portrait for the motion of the lower leg (i.e..\nthe shank) is depicted in Figure 1. The state space is de-\nfined by the angular inclination of the position of the\nshank on the x axis and the shank‘s angular velocity on\nthe y axis. The space defines all the possible states of the\nwalking shank. The periodic trajectories mapped in the\nstate space represent the attractor formed by this\nsystem’s dynamics. This attractor compresses the behav-\nior of multiple muscles of the leg, as well as the motor\nand sensory neurons and motor units, into one dy-\nAttractors represent stable collective states of a dy-\nnamical system. Small perturbations, such as stepping\non a pebble, would not dislodge the system from this\nregion of the state space. Rather, the system once per-\nturbed would return quickly to the original trajectory.\nStable patterns are very low in variability. When variabil-\nity increases, the system is unstable. The loss of stability\nis characteristic of a system in transition. When one\nstable", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ing\non a pebble, would not dislodge the system from this\nregion of the state space. Rather, the system once per-\nturbed would return quickly to the original trajectory.\nStable patterns are very low in variability. When variabil-\nity increases, the sy"}
{"chunk_id": "Smith_1995_Attentional_Strategy_Distance_Runners_p0006_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_be9ef5ba11d2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "051380dc6709952b", "text_span_hash": "051380dc6709952b", "source_file": "Smith_1995_Attentional_Strategy_Distance_Runners.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Smith_1995_Attentional_Strategy_Distance_Runners.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "0% phasing relationship\n(Clark, Whitall, & Phillips, 1988).\nWhen first learning to walk, how will infants coordi-\nnate the two legs (i.e., their temporal phasing relation-\nship) and how long will it take them to find the adult-\nlike coordination? The", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "other, the human infant gradually, over the 1st year of\nlife, learns to roll over, scoot, crawl, pull to a stand, and\ncruise along the couch. Somewhere around the infant's\nfirst birthday, those first independent walking steps are\ntaken. It is from that point and thereafter for the next 6\nmonths of practice that we focus our analysis on learn-\nOur first research goal was to find a collective vari-\nable that would capture walking as a global pattern that\ncould then be studied for its stability and instability.\nIn gait, finding such a variable is no trivial matter. The lit-\nerature is replete with variables to describe one or more\naspects of walking. For example, Sutherland, Olshen,\n12 measures of body position, 6 force measures, 7 muscle\nactivation sequences, and 9 time/distance measures for\none cycle of walking. As walking experience increased,\nSutherland et al. found that all the measures improved.\n% how are we to find a single variable that would capture\nthe complex multidimensional system as it learns to\nwalk? First, we limit our focus to explaining the develop\nment of codination in walking. Rather than looking for\nall the possible ways in which walking changes, we sought\na narrower scope. In particular, we began with the coor-\ndination between the two legs, the interlimbcoordination.\nAfter all, to walk, the two legs must work together in sup\nporting and propelling the body to the intended loca-\ntion. We then asked the quest", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "0% phasing relationship\n(Clark, Whitall, & Phillips, 1988).\nWhen first learning to walk, how will infants coordi-\nnate the two legs (i.e., their temporal phasing relation-\nship) and how long will it take them to find the adult-\nlike coordination? The"}
