king. Rather than looking for\nall the possible ways in which walking changes, we sought\na narrower scope. In particular, we began with the coor-\ndination between the two legs, the interlimbcoordination.\nAfter all, to walk, the two legs must work together in sup\nporting and propelling the body to the intended loca-\ntion. We then asked the quest", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "walker to adopt.\nWhat is interesting and also what we would predict from\na dynamical systems perspective are the findings on the\nvariability of the temporal phasing relationship. In Fig-\nure 2, we see the mean variability within an infant across\nwal"}
