lking. Rather than looking for\nall the possible ways in which walking changes, we sought\na narrower scope. In particular, we began with the coor-\ndination between the two legs, the interlimbcoordination.\nAfter all, to walk, the two legs must work together in sup\nporting and propelling the body to the intended loca-\ntion. We then asked the quest", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ty within an infant across\nwalking experience. This average is calculated from the\nstandard deviation about each infant's mean temporal\nphasing. Clearly, the coordinative relationship is un-\nstable in the new walkers. While they attain a 50% mean\npha"}
{"chunk_id": "Smith_1995_Attentional_Strategy_Distance_Runners_p0006_c0022", "source_registry_id": "src_be9ef5ba11d2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2aec8d0ede883fe6", "text_span_hash": "2aec8d0ede883fe6", "source_file": "Smith_1995_Attentional_Strategy_Distance_Runners.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Smith_1995_Attentional_Strategy_Distance_Runners.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 22, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "hether a system is stable or not\nN-Sup 0.5Months 1 Month 3 Months 6 Months\nFigure 2. Variability of temporal phasing for interlimb coordination\nat different walking ages. N-Sup = new walkers with their hands\nheld by the experimenter. Redrawn from 'Hu", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "other, the human infant gradually, over the 1st year of\nlife, learns to roll over, scoot, crawl, pull to a stand, and\ncruise along the couch. Somewhere around the infant's\nfirst birthday, those first independent walking steps are\ntaken. It is from that point and thereafter for the next 6\nmonths of practice that we focus our analysis on learn-\nOur first research goal was to find a collective vari-\nable that would capture walking as a global pattern that\ncould then be studied for its stability and instability.\nIn gait, finding such a variable is no trivial matter. The lit-\nerature is replete with variables to describe one or more\naspects of walking. For example, Sutherland, Olshen,\n12 measures of body position, 6 force measures, 7 muscle\nactivation sequences, and 9 time/distance measures for\none cycle of walking. As walking experience increased,\nSutherland et al. found that all the measures improved.\n% how are we to find a single variable that would capture\nthe complex multidimensional system as it learns to\nwalk? First, we limit our focus to explaining the develop\nment of codination in walking. Rather than looking for\nall the possible ways in which walking changes, we sought\na narrower scope. In particular, we began with the coor-\ndination between the two legs, the interlimbcoordination.\nAfter all, to walk, the two legs must work together in sup\nporting and propelling the body to the intended loca-\ntion. We then asked the quest", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "hether a system is stable or not\nN-Sup 0.5Months 1 Month 3 Months 6 Months\nFigure 2. Variability of temporal phasing for interlimb coordination\nat different walking ages. N-Sup = new walkers with their hands\nheld by the experimenter. Redrawn from 'Hu"}
{"chunk_id": "Smith_1995_Attentional_Strategy_Distance_Runners_p0007_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_be9ef5ba11d2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "96f7a73eb2390d47", "text_span_hash": "96f7a73eb2390d47", "source_file": "Smith_1995_Attentional_Strategy_Distance_Runners.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Smith_1995_Attentional_Strategy_Distance_Runners.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "infants were willing to attempt a\nstep or two with the weights. But it was not until 1\nmonth of walking experience that the infants walked\nthe same number of steps with and without the weight.\nTemporal phasing for the l-month walker and then at 3\nan", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "is to poke at it; that is, to perturb it. If a system is stable,\nit will resist the perturbation; if not, the perturbation\nwill increase its instability and may be forced into a new\nbehavioral pattern. Because we had found that new\nwalkers immediately demonstrated a 50% phasing rela-\ntionship, although more variable than more experi-\nenced walkers, we wanted to know more about the sta-\nbility of the coordinative relationship. As a system in\ntransition, could we push the infant out of the 50%\nphasing? To test this hypothesis, we affixed a small\nweight (5% of the infant's body weight) to the ankle of\none leg. On a 20-lb infant, which was the average weight\nof infants in our study when they first began to walk, this\namounted to a l-lb weight being taped to the ankle. To\ncontrol for adding weight per se to a young walker, we\nalso had the infants walk with the same weight taped\nsnugly to their lower backs.\nOur first finding was rather robust. When we put\nthe weight on the new walkers-that is, those that could\nwalk only three or four steps before falling-our nov-\nices refused to walk. Several just stood there looking\ndown at their legs. Some just sat down and crawled away.\nOne or two tried to walk, but without success. Clearly,\nthe newly walking infant was perturbed by the weight.\nTwo weeks later, all the infants were willing to attempt a\nstep or two with the weights. But it was not until 1\nmonth of walking experience that the infan", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "infants were willing to attempt a\nstep or two with the weights. But it was not until 1\nmonth of walking experience that the infants walked\nthe same number of steps with and without the weight.\nTemporal phasing for the l-month walker and then at 3\nan"}
