just stood there looking\ndown at their legs. Some just sat down and crawled away.\nOne or two tried to walk, but without success. Clearly,\nthe newly walking infant was perturbed by the weight.\nTwo weeks later, all the infants were willing to attempt a\nstep or two with the weights. But it was not until 1\nmonth of walking experience that the infan", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "any one time. The\ninner trajectory marks the initiation of the walking\ncycle. The trajectory moves clockwise in state space and\nwithin one cycle has found the region of the space\nwhere all the other cycles are attracted. The brief\nperiod from initia"}
{"chunk_id": "Smith_1995_Attentional_Strategy_Distance_Runners_p0007_c0024", "source_registry_id": "src_be9ef5ba11d2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "698ecff785b7acf5", "text_span_hash": "698ecff785b7acf5", "source_file": "Smith_1995_Attentional_Strategy_Distance_Runners.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Smith_1995_Attentional_Strategy_Distance_Runners.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 24, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": ". The brief\nperiod from initiation until the trajectory settles into a\nperiodic orbit is called the transient and demonstrates\nwhy the picture that emerges is called an attractor.\nMapped in state space, our walking shank's behavior\nforms a periodic,", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "is to poke at it; that is, to perturb it. If a system is stable,\nit will resist the perturbation; if not, the perturbation\nwill increase its instability and may be forced into a new\nbehavioral pattern. Because we had found that new\nwalkers immediately demonstrated a 50% phasing rela-\ntionship, although more variable than more experi-\nenced walkers, we wanted to know more about the sta-\nbility of the coordinative relationship. As a system in\ntransition, could we push the infant out of the 50%\nphasing? To test this hypothesis, we affixed a small\nweight (5% of the infant's body weight) to the ankle of\none leg. On a 20-lb infant, which was the average weight\nof infants in our study when they first began to walk, this\namounted to a l-lb weight being taped to the ankle. To\ncontrol for adding weight per se to a young walker, we\nalso had the infants walk with the same weight taped\nsnugly to their lower backs.\nOur first finding was rather robust. When we put\nthe weight on the new walkers-that is, those that could\nwalk only three or four steps before falling-our nov-\nices refused to walk. Several just stood there looking\ndown at their legs. Some just sat down and crawled away.\nOne or two tried to walk, but without success. Clearly,\nthe newly walking infant was perturbed by the weight.\nTwo weeks later, all the infants were willing to attempt a\nstep or two with the weights. But it was not until 1\nmonth of walking experience that the infan", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": ". The brief\nperiod from initiation until the trajectory settles into a\nperiodic orbit is called the transient and demonstrates\nwhy the picture that emerges is called an attractor.\nMapped in state space, our walking shank's behavior\nforms a periodic,"}
{"chunk_id": "Smith_1995_Attentional_Strategy_Distance_Runners_p0008_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_be9ef5ba11d2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "a2a962f6c9038159", "text_span_hash": "a2a962f6c9038159", "source_file": "Smith_1995_Attentional_Strategy_Distance_Runners.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Smith_1995_Attentional_Strategy_Distance_Runners.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "the be-\nhavior is quite variable. Unlike the adult shank trajec-\ntory where one orbit fell upon another, the infant's tra-\njectories (except during stance) are not attracted to the\nsame region of the state space. However, after 4 weeks\nof walking exp", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "much the same way as they had done in their interlimb\ncoordination. That is, an adult-like solution would be\nfound, but it would be unstable. Indeed that was the\ncase. In Figure 3a, an exemplar for one of our newly\nwalking infants is presented (see Clark & Phillips, 1993,\nfor evidence of three infants followed longitudinally for\n1 year). Here we see that the infant's shank trajectory is\nperiodic and closed (i.e., limit cycle-like), but the be-\nhavior is quite variable. Unlike the adult shank trajec-\ntory where one orbit fell upon another, the infant's tra-\njectories (except during stance) are not attracted to the\nsame region of the state space. However, after 4 weeks\nof walking experience (see Figure 3b). the infant has\nsettled into an attractive regime. Although the shank's\ntrajectory is still not quite the same in its shape as the\nadults' trajectory during stance (the lower portion), it is\nevident that an attractive limitqcle behavior has\nformed. So in a similar fashion to the findings for\ninterlimb phasing, we see that at the onset of walking,\nthe system takes on an adult-like pattern, but one that is\nAlthough the behavior of a single segment, such as\nthe shank or the thigh, is revealing, it does not directly\naddress coordination within the leg. To examine the\nintralimb coordination, we need a measure of the rela-\ntionship between the shank's trajectory in state space and\nthe thigh's trajectory (or the foot). To calculate t", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "the be-\nhavior is quite variable. Unlike the adult shank trajec-\ntory where one orbit fell upon another, the infant's tra-\njectories (except during stance) are not attracted to the\nsame region of the state space. However, after 4 weeks\nof walking exp"}
{"chunk_id": "Smith_1995_Attentional_Strategy_Distance_Runners_p0008_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_be9ef5ba11d2", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3fc205268d07615c", "text_span_hash": "3fc205268d07615c", "source_file": "Smith_1995_Attentional_Strategy_Distance_Runners.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Smith_1995_Attentional_Strategy_Distance_Runners.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "asure of the rela-\ntionship between the shank's trajectory in state space and\nthe thigh's trajectory (or the foot). To calculate this mea-\nsure, we first transformed the Cartesian (x,x) to polar co-\nordinates, with a phase angle $ = tan.' [i/x] and r", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "much the same way as they had done in their interlimb\ncoordination. That is, an adult-like solution would be\nfound, but it would be unstable. Indeed that was the\ncase. In Figure 3a, an exemplar for one of our newly\nwalking infants is presented (see Clark & Phillips, 1993,\nfor evidence of three infants followed longitudinally for\n1 year). Here we see that the infant's shank trajectory is\nperiodic and closed (i.e., limit cycle-like), but the be-\nhavior is quite variable. Unlike the adult shank trajec-\ntory where one orbit fell upon another, the infant's tra-\njectories (except during stance) are not attracted to the\nsame region of the state space. However, after 4 weeks\nof walking experience (see Figure 3b). the infant has\nsettled into an attractive regime. Although the shank's\ntrajectory is still not quite the same in its shape as the\nadults' trajectory during stance (the lower portion), it is\nevident that an attractive limitqcle behavior has\nformed. So in a similar fashion to the findings for\ninterlimb phasing, we see that at the onset of walking,\nthe system takes on an adult-like pattern, but one that is\nAlthough the behavior of a single segment, such as\nthe shank or the thigh, is revealing, it does not directly\naddress coordination within the leg. To examine the\nintralimb coordination, we need a measure of the rela-\ntionship between the shank's trajectory in state space and\nthe thigh's trajectory (or the foot). To calculate t", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "asure of the rela-\ntionship between the shank's trajectory in state space and\nthe thigh's trajectory (or the foot). To calculate this mea-\nsure, we first transformed the Cartesian (x,x) to polar co-\nordinates, with a phase angle $ = tan.' [i/x] and r"}
