, but one that is\nAlthough the behavior of a single segment, such as\nthe shank or the thigh, is revealing, it does not directly\naddress coordination within the leg. To examine the\nintralimb coordination, we need a measure of the rela-\ntionship between the shank's trajectory in state space and\nthe thigh's trajectory (or the foot). To calculate t", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "k. (Note that in our mea-\nsurement convention, a negative phasing relationship\nmeans the thigh is leading the shank in its state space\ntrajectory.) Then in late swing (-20 to 40%), the thigh\nstops its motion and the shank rapidly overtakes it. The\nAn"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0001_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c25476d421405898", "text_span_hash": "c25476d421405898", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Comparison of Polarized Versus Other Types of Endurance Training\nIntensity Distribution on Athletes’ Endurance Performance:\nA Systematic Review with Meta‑analysis\nPedro Silva Oliveira1,2  · Giorjines Boppre1,2,3  · Hélder Fonseca1,2\n© The Author(s) 2", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Comparison of Polarized Versus Other Types of Endurance Training\nIntensity Distribution on Athletes’ Endurance Performance:\nA Systematic Review with Meta‑analysis\nPedro Silva Oliveira1,2  · Giorjines Boppre1,2,3  · Hélder Fonseca1,2\n© The Author(s) 2024\nBackground  Polarized training intensity distribution (POL) was recently suggested to be superior to other training intensity\ndistribution (TID) regimens for endurance performance improvement.\nObjective  We aimed to systematically review and meta-analyze evidence comparing POL to other TIDs on endurance\nMethods  PRISMA guidelines were followed. The protocol was registered at PROSPERO (CRD42022365117). PubMed,\nScopus, and Web of Science were searched up to 20 October 2022 for studies in adults and young adults for ≥ 4 weeks com-\nparing POL with other TID interventions regarding VO2peak, time-trial (TT), time to exhaustion (TTE) or speed or power\nat the second ventilatory or lactate threshold (V/P at ­VT2/LT2). Risk of bias was assessed with RoB-2 and ROBINS-I. Cer-\ntainty of evidence was assessed with GRADE. Results were analyzed by random effects meta-analysis using standardized\nResults  Seventeen studies met the inclusion criteria (n = 437 subjects). Pooled effect estimates suggest POL superiority\nof evidence). Superiority, however, only occurred in shorter interventions (< 12 weeks) (SMD = 0.40 [95% CI 0.08, 0.71;\nn = 125; I2 = 0%). The remaining endurance performance surroga", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Comparison of Polarized Versus Other Types of Endurance Training\nIntensity Distribution on Athletes’ Endurance Performance:\nA Systematic Review with Meta‑analysis\nPedro Silva Oliveira1,2  · Giorjines Boppre1,2,3  · Hélder Fonseca1,2\n© The Author(s) 2"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0001_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "bf99d35a69583544", "text_span_hash": "bf99d35a69583544", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "r Fonseca1,2\n© The Author(s) 2024\nBackground  Polarized training intensity distribution (POL) was recently suggested to be superior to other training intensity\ndistribution (TID) regimens for endurance performance improvement.\nObjective  We aimed to", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Comparison of Polarized Versus Other Types of Endurance Training\nIntensity Distribution on Athletes’ Endurance Performance:\nA Systematic Review with Meta‑analysis\nPedro Silva Oliveira1,2  · Giorjines Boppre1,2,3  · Hélder Fonseca1,2\n© The Author(s) 2024\nBackground  Polarized training intensity distribution (POL) was recently suggested to be superior to other training intensity\ndistribution (TID) regimens for endurance performance improvement.\nObjective  We aimed to systematically review and meta-analyze evidence comparing POL to other TIDs on endurance\nMethods  PRISMA guidelines were followed. The protocol was registered at PROSPERO (CRD42022365117). PubMed,\nScopus, and Web of Science were searched up to 20 October 2022 for studies in adults and young adults for ≥ 4 weeks com-\nparing POL with other TID interventions regarding VO2peak, time-trial (TT), time to exhaustion (TTE) or speed or power\nat the second ventilatory or lactate threshold (V/P at ­VT2/LT2). Risk of bias was assessed with RoB-2 and ROBINS-I. Cer-\ntainty of evidence was assessed with GRADE. Results were analyzed by random effects meta-analysis using standardized\nResults  Seventeen studies met the inclusion criteria (n = 437 subjects). Pooled effect estimates suggest POL superiority\nof evidence). Superiority, however, only occurred in shorter interventions (< 12 weeks) (SMD = 0.40 [95% CI 0.08, 0.71;\nn = 125; I2 = 0%). The remaining endurance performance surroga", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "r Fonseca1,2\n© The Author(s) 2024\nBackground  Polarized training intensity distribution (POL) was recently suggested to be superior to other training intensity\ndistribution (TID) regimens for endurance performance improvement.\nObjective  We aimed to"}
