alling revealed one additional\npotentially suitable article. After screening the abstract, a\nfull text analysis was performed revealing that this article\ndid not meet the eligibility criteria. Seventeen studies were\nfinally included in the systematic review. There were no dis-\ncrepancies between raters in ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "systematic review with meta-analysis was\nevaluated in terms of having adequate sample size, narrow\nconfidence intervals, and absence of heterogeneity. GRADE\nwas independently assessed by two authors (PO and GB),\nwith disagreements managed by consensu"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0005_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ba9aa8274a6410d2", "text_span_hash": "ba9aa8274a6410d2", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ics of the Studies Included\nTable 1 summarizes all the characteristics of the 17 stud-\nies included in this review that compared the effect of an\nintervention based on POL with other TIDs on improving\nendurance performance. The total number of partic", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Polarized vs. Other Training Intensity Distribution Regimens and Endurance Performance\nthe evidence by GRADE and are graded as high, moderate,\nlow, or extremely low. Certainty of evidence of the included\nstudies in this systematic review with meta-analysis was\nevaluated in terms of having adequate sample size, narrow\nconfidence intervals, and absence of heterogeneity. GRADE\nwas independently assessed by two authors (PO and GB),\nwith disagreements managed by consensus or through a third\n3.1  \u0007Study Selection\nThe flowchart details the studies included in the review. An\ninitial search returned 275 results (35 PubMed, 203 Scopus,\nand 37 Web of Science). After removing duplicates, 205\nreports remained. The screening of titles and abstracts for\neligibility criteria resulted in the exclusion of 163 reports,\nleaving 42 articles for full text analysis. Of these, 25 were\nexcluded for not meeting the eligibility criteria: seven were\noutside the research objective, 12 did not had the necessary\nintervention or comparators, three did not assess any of the\nvariables of interest, two were not written in English, and\none was an erratum. Snowballing revealed one additional\npotentially suitable article. After screening the abstract, a\nfull text analysis was performed revealing that this article\ndid not meet the eligibility criteria. Seventeen studies were\nfinally included in the systematic review. There were no dis-\ncrepancies between raters in ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ics of the Studies Included\nTable 1 summarizes all the characteristics of the 17 stud-\nies included in this review that compared the effect of an\nintervention based on POL with other TIDs on improving\nendurance performance. The total number of partic"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0005_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ccbfc98ed6c2ca1b", "text_span_hash": "ccbfc98ed6c2ca1b", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "h a median of\nn = 22 per study.\nThe studies selected for analysis included a wide vari-\nety of endurance sports, with running and cycling being\nFig. 1   PRISMA 2020 flow diagram", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Polarized vs. Other Training Intensity Distribution Regimens and Endurance Performance\nthe evidence by GRADE and are graded as high, moderate,\nlow, or extremely low. Certainty of evidence of the included\nstudies in this systematic review with meta-analysis was\nevaluated in terms of having adequate sample size, narrow\nconfidence intervals, and absence of heterogeneity. GRADE\nwas independently assessed by two authors (PO and GB),\nwith disagreements managed by consensus or through a third\n3.1  \u0007Study Selection\nThe flowchart details the studies included in the review. An\ninitial search returned 275 results (35 PubMed, 203 Scopus,\nand 37 Web of Science). After removing duplicates, 205\nreports remained. The screening of titles and abstracts for\neligibility criteria resulted in the exclusion of 163 reports,\nleaving 42 articles for full text analysis. Of these, 25 were\nexcluded for not meeting the eligibility criteria: seven were\noutside the research objective, 12 did not had the necessary\nintervention or comparators, three did not assess any of the\nvariables of interest, two were not written in English, and\none was an erratum. Snowballing revealed one additional\npotentially suitable article. After screening the abstract, a\nfull text analysis was performed revealing that this article\ndid not meet the eligibility criteria. Seventeen studies were\nfinally included in the systematic review. There were no dis-\ncrepancies between raters in ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "h a median of\nn = 22 per study.\nThe studies selected for analysis included a wide vari-\nety of endurance sports, with running and cycling being\nFig. 1   PRISMA 2020 flow diagram"}
