{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0012_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ba6faf6f04e0b249", "text_span_hash": "ba6faf6f04e0b249", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "P. Silva Oliveira et al.\npredominant. Six studies evaluated endurance runners [17,\n24, 27, 28, 56], in one case ultra-endurance runners [57].\nFour studies evaluated cyclists, of which one evaluated road\ncyclists [26], one cross-country [55], and two", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "P. Silva Oliveira et al.\npredominant. Six studies evaluated endurance runners [17,\n24, 27, 28, 56], in one case ultra-endurance runners [57].\nFour studies evaluated cyclists, of which one evaluated road\ncyclists [26], one cross-country [55], and two mountain bik-\ners [25, 58]. Six studies evaluated other sports, namely one\nstudy of swimmers [54], one of rowers [30], two of triath-\nletes [29, 32], and two included multisport participants,\nnamely cross-country skiers, cyclists, medium and long-\ndistance triathletes, and runners [8, 52]. One study included\npreviously untrained subjects [53].\nSeven studies were performed with highly trained ath-\nletes/national level athletes, of which two included moun-\ntain bikers [25, 58], one cross country cyclists [55], one\nswimmers [54], one rowers [30], and two a mixed sample of\nvarious disciplines [8, 52]. Nine studies evaluated trained/\ndevelopmental athletes, of which six included runners [17,\n24, 27, 28, 56], one of these ultra-endurance runners [57],\none road cyclists [26], and two triathletes [29, 32]. One study\nincluded previously sedentary subjects [53].\nThe duration of training interventions ranged from 4 [55]\nto 16 weeks [56], with a median of 10 weeks. Three studies\ndid not report the TID [25, 27, 58]. One study [53] reported\nthe TID only in the POL group.\nIn two studies [25, 58] the training duration and the num-\nber of sessions dedicated to high- and low-intensity training\nwere ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "P. Silva Oliveira et al.\npredominant. Six studies evaluated endurance runners [17,\n24, 27, 28, 56], in one case ultra-endurance runners [57].\nFour studies evaluated cyclists, of which one evaluated road\ncyclists [26], one cross-country [55], and two"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0012_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "54108353604249bd", "text_span_hash": "54108353604249bd", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": ",\nnamely cross-country skiers, cyclists, medium and long-\ndistance triathletes, and runners [8, 52]. One study included\npreviously untrained subjects [53].\nSeven studies were performed with highly trained ath-\nletes/national level athletes, of which", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "P. Silva Oliveira et al.\npredominant. Six studies evaluated endurance runners [17,\n24, 27, 28, 56], in one case ultra-endurance runners [57].\nFour studies evaluated cyclists, of which one evaluated road\ncyclists [26], one cross-country [55], and two mountain bik-\ners [25, 58]. Six studies evaluated other sports, namely one\nstudy of swimmers [54], one of rowers [30], two of triath-\nletes [29, 32], and two included multisport participants,\nnamely cross-country skiers, cyclists, medium and long-\ndistance triathletes, and runners [8, 52]. One study included\npreviously untrained subjects [53].\nSeven studies were performed with highly trained ath-\nletes/national level athletes, of which two included moun-\ntain bikers [25, 58], one cross country cyclists [55], one\nswimmers [54], one rowers [30], and two a mixed sample of\nvarious disciplines [8, 52]. Nine studies evaluated trained/\ndevelopmental athletes, of which six included runners [17,\n24, 27, 28, 56], one of these ultra-endurance runners [57],\none road cyclists [26], and two triathletes [29, 32]. One study\nincluded previously sedentary subjects [53].\nThe duration of training interventions ranged from 4 [55]\nto 16 weeks [56], with a median of 10 weeks. Three studies\ndid not report the TID [25, 27, 58]. One study [53] reported\nthe TID only in the POL group.\nIn two studies [25, 58] the training duration and the num-\nber of sessions dedicated to high- and low-intensity training\nwere ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": ",\nnamely cross-country skiers, cyclists, medium and long-\ndistance triathletes, and runners [8, 52]. One study included\npreviously untrained subjects [53].\nSeven studies were performed with highly trained ath-\nletes/national level athletes, of which"}
