ventions ranged from 4 [55]\nto 16 weeks [56], with a median of 10 weeks. Three studies\ndid not report the TID [25, 27, 58]. One study [53] reported\nthe TID only in the POL group.\nIn two studies [25, 58] the training duration and the num-\nber of sessions dedicated to high- and low-intensity training\nwere ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "subjects [53].\nThe duration of training interventions ranged from 4 [55]\nto 16 weeks [56], with a median of 10 weeks. Three studies\ndid not report the TID [25, 27, 58]. One study [53] reported\nthe TID only in the POL group.\nIn two studies [25, 58] th"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0012_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2eff3ac802cd5f88", "text_span_hash": "2eff3ac802cd5f88", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "up.\nIn two studies [25, 58] the training duration and the num-\nber of sessions dedicated to high- and low-intensity training\nwere reported without mentioning the percentage of the total\ntraining time distribution attributed to each intensity. Eight\ns", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "P. Silva Oliveira et al.\npredominant. Six studies evaluated endurance runners [17,\n24, 27, 28, 56], in one case ultra-endurance runners [57].\nFour studies evaluated cyclists, of which one evaluated road\ncyclists [26], one cross-country [55], and two mountain bik-\ners [25, 58]. Six studies evaluated other sports, namely one\nstudy of swimmers [54], one of rowers [30], two of triath-\nletes [29, 32], and two included multisport participants,\nnamely cross-country skiers, cyclists, medium and long-\ndistance triathletes, and runners [8, 52]. One study included\npreviously untrained subjects [53].\nSeven studies were performed with highly trained ath-\nletes/national level athletes, of which two included moun-\ntain bikers [25, 58], one cross country cyclists [55], one\nswimmers [54], one rowers [30], and two a mixed sample of\nvarious disciplines [8, 52]. Nine studies evaluated trained/\ndevelopmental athletes, of which six included runners [17,\n24, 27, 28, 56], one of these ultra-endurance runners [57],\none road cyclists [26], and two triathletes [29, 32]. One study\nincluded previously sedentary subjects [53].\nThe duration of training interventions ranged from 4 [55]\nto 16 weeks [56], with a median of 10 weeks. Three studies\ndid not report the TID [25, 27, 58]. One study [53] reported\nthe TID only in the POL group.\nIn two studies [25, 58] the training duration and the num-\nber of sessions dedicated to high- and low-intensity training\nwere ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "up.\nIn two studies [25, 58] the training duration and the num-\nber of sessions dedicated to high- and low-intensity training\nwere reported without mentioning the percentage of the total\ntraining time distribution attributed to each intensity. Eight\ns"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0012_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "48b28a97624fb264", "text_span_hash": "48b28a97624fb264", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ted to each intensity. Eight\nstudies reported the weekly TRIMP, with studies from Car-\nnes et al. [24] and Röhrken et al. [29] showing the lowest\n(POL: 389 ± 101; CrossFit ­Endurance©: 222 ± 68) and high-\nminimum weekly training frequency was three s", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "P. Silva Oliveira et al.\npredominant. Six studies evaluated endurance runners [17,\n24, 27, 28, 56], in one case ultra-endurance runners [57].\nFour studies evaluated cyclists, of which one evaluated road\ncyclists [26], one cross-country [55], and two mountain bik-\ners [25, 58]. Six studies evaluated other sports, namely one\nstudy of swimmers [54], one of rowers [30], two of triath-\nletes [29, 32], and two included multisport participants,\nnamely cross-country skiers, cyclists, medium and long-\ndistance triathletes, and runners [8, 52]. One study included\npreviously untrained subjects [53].\nSeven studies were performed with highly trained ath-\nletes/national level athletes, of which two included moun-\ntain bikers [25, 58], one cross country cyclists [55], one\nswimmers [54], one rowers [30], and two a mixed sample of\nvarious disciplines [8, 52]. Nine studies evaluated trained/\ndevelopmental athletes, of which six included runners [17,\n24, 27, 28, 56], one of these ultra-endurance runners [57],\none road cyclists [26], and two triathletes [29, 32]. One study\nincluded previously sedentary subjects [53].\nThe duration of training interventions ranged from 4 [55]\nto 16 weeks [56], with a median of 10 weeks. Three studies\ndid not report the TID [25, 27, 58]. One study [53] reported\nthe TID only in the POL group.\nIn two studies [25, 58] the training duration and the num-\nber of sessions dedicated to high- and low-intensity training\nwere ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ted to each intensity. Eight\nstudies reported the weekly TRIMP, with studies from Car-\nnes et al. [24] and Röhrken et al. [29] showing the lowest\n(POL: 389 ± 101; CrossFit ­Endurance©: 222 ± 68) and high-\nminimum weekly training frequency was three s"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0012_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "afaa25904575326c", "text_span_hash": "afaa25904575326c", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "training frequency was three sessions [24]\nand the maximum was ten sessions [55].\nVO2peak was assessed in 11 studies [8, 24, 25, 27, 28,\n54–56], TTE in four studies [8, 26, 27, 57], and V/P at ­VT2/\n3.3  \u0007Qualitative Synthesis of Findings\nFor ­VO2pea", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "P. Silva Oliveira et al.\npredominant. Six studies evaluated endurance runners [17,\n24, 27, 28, 56], in one case ultra-endurance runners [57].\nFour studies evaluated cyclists, of which one evaluated road\ncyclists [26], one cross-country [55], and two mountain bik-\ners [25, 58]. Six studies evaluated other sports, namely one\nstudy of swimmers [54], one of rowers [30], two of triath-\nletes [29, 32], and two included multisport participants,\nnamely cross-country skiers, cyclists, medium and long-\ndistance triathletes, and runners [8, 52]. One study included\npreviously untrained subjects [53].\nSeven studies were performed with highly trained ath-\nletes/national level athletes, of which two included moun-\ntain bikers [25, 58], one cross country cyclists [55], one\nswimmers [54], one rowers [30], and two a mixed sample of\nvarious disciplines [8, 52]. Nine studies evaluated trained/\ndevelopmental athletes, of which six included runners [17,\n24, 27, 28, 56], one of these ultra-endurance runners [57],\none road cyclists [26], and two triathletes [29, 32]. One study\nincluded previously sedentary subjects [53].\nThe duration of training interventions ranged from 4 [55]\nto 16 weeks [56], with a median of 10 weeks. Three studies\ndid not report the TID [25, 27, 58]. One study [53] reported\nthe TID only in the POL group.\nIn two studies [25, 58] the training duration and the num-\nber of sessions dedicated to high- and low-intensity training\nwere ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "training frequency was three sessions [24]\nand the maximum was ten sessions [55].\nVO2peak was assessed in 11 studies [8, 24, 25, 27, 28,\n54–56], TTE in four studies [8, 26, 27, 57], and V/P at ­VT2/\n3.3  \u0007Qualitative Synthesis of Findings\nFor ­VO2pea"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0012_c0018", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5a99b11dbdd532ef", "text_span_hash": "5a99b11dbdd532ef", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 18, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "O2peak\nFigure 4 shows the pooled effect estimates of POL compared\nto other TIDs on VO2peak. POL was shown to be superior to\nother TIDs in the improvement of VO2peak, although with a\nsmall effect size (SMD = 0.24; 95% confidence interval (CI)\n3.5.2  \u0007", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "P. Silva Oliveira et al.\npredominant. Six studies evaluated endurance runners [17,\n24, 27, 28, 56], in one case ultra-endurance runners [57].\nFour studies evaluated cyclists, of which one evaluated road\ncyclists [26], one cross-country [55], and two mountain bik-\ners [25, 58]. Six studies evaluated other sports, namely one\nstudy of swimmers [54], one of rowers [30], two of triath-\nletes [29, 32], and two included multisport participants,\nnamely cross-country skiers, cyclists, medium and long-\ndistance triathletes, and runners [8, 52]. One study included\npreviously untrained subjects [53].\nSeven studies were performed with highly trained ath-\nletes/national level athletes, of which two included moun-\ntain bikers [25, 58], one cross country cyclists [55], one\nswimmers [54], one rowers [30], and two a mixed sample of\nvarious disciplines [8, 52]. Nine studies evaluated trained/\ndevelopmental athletes, of which six included runners [17,\n24, 27, 28, 56], one of these ultra-endurance runners [57],\none road cyclists [26], and two triathletes [29, 32]. One study\nincluded previously sedentary subjects [53].\nThe duration of training interventions ranged from 4 [55]\nto 16 weeks [56], with a median of 10 weeks. Three studies\ndid not report the TID [25, 27, 58]. One study [53] reported\nthe TID only in the POL group.\nIn two studies [25, 58] the training duration and the num-\nber of sessions dedicated to high- and low-intensity training\nwere ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "O2peak\nFigure 4 shows the pooled effect estimates of POL compared\nto other TIDs on VO2peak. POL was shown to be superior to\nother TIDs in the improvement of VO2peak, although with a\nsmall effect size (SMD = 0.24; 95% confidence interval (CI)\n3.5.2  \u0007"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0012_c0019", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8ade864d882be588", "text_span_hash": "8ade864d882be588", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 19, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "fidence interval (CI)\n3.5.2  \u0007Effect of POL Compared to Other TIDs on TT, TTE,\nPOL in comparison to other TIDs was not shown to induce\nsignificant improvements on any of the study secondary\n3.5.3  \u0007Comparison of the Effect of POL Versus THR, PYR,\nNo", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "P. Silva Oliveira et al.\npredominant. Six studies evaluated endurance runners [17,\n24, 27, 28, 56], in one case ultra-endurance runners [57].\nFour studies evaluated cyclists, of which one evaluated road\ncyclists [26], one cross-country [55], and two mountain bik-\ners [25, 58]. Six studies evaluated other sports, namely one\nstudy of swimmers [54], one of rowers [30], two of triath-\nletes [29, 32], and two included multisport participants,\nnamely cross-country skiers, cyclists, medium and long-\ndistance triathletes, and runners [8, 52]. One study included\npreviously untrained subjects [53].\nSeven studies were performed with highly trained ath-\nletes/national level athletes, of which two included moun-\ntain bikers [25, 58], one cross country cyclists [55], one\nswimmers [54], one rowers [30], and two a mixed sample of\nvarious disciplines [8, 52]. Nine studies evaluated trained/\ndevelopmental athletes, of which six included runners [17,\n24, 27, 28, 56], one of these ultra-endurance runners [57],\none road cyclists [26], and two triathletes [29, 32]. One study\nincluded previously sedentary subjects [53].\nThe duration of training interventions ranged from 4 [55]\nto 16 weeks [56], with a median of 10 weeks. Three studies\ndid not report the TID [25, 27, 58]. One study [53] reported\nthe TID only in the POL group.\nIn two studies [25, 58] the training duration and the num-\nber of sessions dedicated to high- and low-intensity training\nwere ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "fidence interval (CI)\n3.5.2  \u0007Effect of POL Compared to Other TIDs on TT, TTE,\nPOL in comparison to other TIDs was not shown to induce\nsignificant improvements on any of the study secondary\n3.5.3  \u0007Comparison of the Effect of POL Versus THR, PYR,\nNo"}
