{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0015_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "32199864bbb74f4c", "text_span_hash": "32199864bbb74f4c", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 15, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Polarized vs. Other Training Intensity Distribution Regimens and Endurance Performance\nFig. 5   Effect of POL compared to other TIDs on TT, TTE, and V/P at ­VT2/LT2", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Polarized vs. Other Training Intensity Distribution Regimens and Endurance Performance\nFig. 5   Effect of POL compared to other TIDs on TT, TTE, and V/P at ­VT2/LT2", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Polarized vs. Other Training Intensity Distribution Regimens and Endurance Performance\nFig. 5   Effect of POL compared to other TIDs on TT, TTE, and V/P at ­VT2/LT2"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0016_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "895ceaf1ae45884a", "text_span_hash": "895ceaf1ae45884a", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 16, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "P. Silva Oliveira et al.\n3.5.5  \u0007Effects of POL Compared to Other TIDs According\nA sub-analysis comparing the effect of endurance per-\nformance starting level (highly trained/national level vs\ntrained/developmental) [44] on the selected outcomes was", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "P. Silva Oliveira et al.\n3.5.5  \u0007Effects of POL Compared to Other TIDs According\nA sub-analysis comparing the effect of endurance per-\nformance starting level (highly trained/national level vs\ntrained/developmental) [44] on the selected outcomes was\nperformed (Table 3). In highly trained/national level ath-\nletes, POL was superior to other TIDs on the improvement\n(p = 0.79). For trained/developmental athletes, there were\nno differences between POL and other TIDs for any of the\noutcomes assessed ­(VO2peak: p = 0.82; TT: p = 0.98; TTE:\n3.5.6  \u0007Effects of POL Compared to Other TIDs According\nto TT Duration (< 12 min vs. ≥ 12 min)\nA sub‐analysis according to TT duration was also performed\nand is shown in Table 3, in which interventions were divided\ninto two categories, < 12 min versus ≥ 12 min duration.\nThere were no differences in TT performance between POL\nand other TIDS, irrespective of TT test duration (< 12 min:\n3.5.7  \u0007Effect of Sex\nAlthough in the pre-registration of this work a sub-analysis\ncomparing the effect of POL versus other TIDs between\nmales and females was proposed (sex effect on the adapta-\ntion to POL), it was not possible to perform this analysis due\nto insufficient sex reporting in the included studies.\n3.6  \u0007Sensitivity Analysis\nAll the analyses performed revealed low or inexistence of\nheterogeneity. The analysis with the highest heterogeneity\nwas a secondary analysis of POL versus THR for VO2peak\n(I2 = 53%).", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "P. Silva Oliveira et al.\n3.5.5  \u0007Effects of POL Compared to Other TIDs According\nA sub-analysis comparing the effect of endurance per-\nformance starting level (highly trained/national level vs\ntrained/developmental) [44] on the selected outcomes was"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0016_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "05a97b5613272742", "text_span_hash": "05a97b5613272742", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 16, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "control group, HIIT high-intensity interval training, POL polarized training, PYR pyramidal Training, THR threshold training, TT time trial, TTE time to exhaustion, V/P at VT2/LT2 velocity\nor power at second ventilatory or lactate threshold, VO2max/p", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "P. Silva Oliveira et al.\n3.5.5  \u0007Effects of POL Compared to Other TIDs According\nA sub-analysis comparing the effect of endurance per-\nformance starting level (highly trained/national level vs\ntrained/developmental) [44] on the selected outcomes was\nperformed (Table 3). In highly trained/national level ath-\nletes, POL was superior to other TIDs on the improvement\n(p = 0.79). For trained/developmental athletes, there were\nno differences between POL and other TIDs for any of the\noutcomes assessed ­(VO2peak: p = 0.82; TT: p = 0.98; TTE:\n3.5.6  \u0007Effects of POL Compared to Other TIDs According\nto TT Duration (< 12 min vs. ≥ 12 min)\nA sub‐analysis according to TT duration was also performed\nand is shown in Table 3, in which interventions were divided\ninto two categories, < 12 min versus ≥ 12 min duration.\nThere were no differences in TT performance between POL\nand other TIDS, irrespective of TT test duration (< 12 min:\n3.5.7  \u0007Effect of Sex\nAlthough in the pre-registration of this work a sub-analysis\ncomparing the effect of POL versus other TIDs between\nmales and females was proposed (sex effect on the adapta-\ntion to POL), it was not possible to perform this analysis due\nto insufficient sex reporting in the included studies.\n3.6  \u0007Sensitivity Analysis\nAll the analyses performed revealed low or inexistence of\nheterogeneity. The analysis with the highest heterogeneity\nwas a secondary analysis of POL versus THR for VO2peak\n(I2 = 53%).", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "control group, HIIT high-intensity interval training, POL polarized training, PYR pyramidal Training, THR threshold training, TT time trial, TTE time to exhaustion, V/P at VT2/LT2 velocity\nor power at second ventilatory or lactate threshold, VO2max/p"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0016_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "03dbf0ec1145ca7a", "text_span_hash": "03dbf0ec1145ca7a", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 16, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "or lactate threshold, VO2max/peak maximal/peak oxygen uptake, SMD standardized mean difference, I2 (p) heterogeneity and p-value, Z (p) test for overall effect", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "P. Silva Oliveira et al.\n3.5.5  \u0007Effects of POL Compared to Other TIDs According\nA sub-analysis comparing the effect of endurance per-\nformance starting level (highly trained/national level vs\ntrained/developmental) [44] on the selected outcomes was\nperformed (Table 3). In highly trained/national level ath-\nletes, POL was superior to other TIDs on the improvement\n(p = 0.79). For trained/developmental athletes, there were\nno differences between POL and other TIDs for any of the\noutcomes assessed ­(VO2peak: p = 0.82; TT: p = 0.98; TTE:\n3.5.6  \u0007Effects of POL Compared to Other TIDs According\nto TT Duration (< 12 min vs. ≥ 12 min)\nA sub‐analysis according to TT duration was also performed\nand is shown in Table 3, in which interventions were divided\ninto two categories, < 12 min versus ≥ 12 min duration.\nThere were no differences in TT performance between POL\nand other TIDS, irrespective of TT test duration (< 12 min:\n3.5.7  \u0007Effect of Sex\nAlthough in the pre-registration of this work a sub-analysis\ncomparing the effect of POL versus other TIDs between\nmales and females was proposed (sex effect on the adapta-\ntion to POL), it was not possible to perform this analysis due\nto insufficient sex reporting in the included studies.\n3.6  \u0007Sensitivity Analysis\nAll the analyses performed revealed low or inexistence of\nheterogeneity. The analysis with the highest heterogeneity\nwas a secondary analysis of POL versus THR for VO2peak\n(I2 = 53%).", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "or lactate threshold, VO2max/peak maximal/peak oxygen uptake, SMD standardized mean difference, I2 (p) heterogeneity and p-value, Z (p) test for overall effect"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0017_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "68e5ee3c7d0a2bb1", "text_span_hash": "68e5ee3c7d0a2bb1", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 17, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Polarized vs. Other Training Intensity Distribution Regimens and Endurance Performance\nTable 3   Sub analysis of the effect of POL compared to other TIDs according to intervention duration and to athlete level\nALL other training intensity distributio", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Polarized vs. Other Training Intensity Distribution Regimens and Endurance Performance\nTable 3   Sub analysis of the effect of POL compared to other TIDs according to intervention duration and to athlete level\nALL other training intensity distribution, POL polarized training, TT time trial, TTE time to exhaustion, V/P at VT2/LT2 velocity or power at second ventilatory or lactate threshold, VO2max/\npeak maximal/peak oxygen uptake, SMD standardized mean difference, I2 (p) heterogeneity and p-value, Z (p) test for overall effect and p-value\n* Statistical significance: p < 0.05", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Polarized vs. Other Training Intensity Distribution Regimens and Endurance Performance\nTable 3   Sub analysis of the effect of POL compared to other TIDs according to intervention duration and to athlete level\nALL other training intensity distributio"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0017_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e356a16624537b14", "text_span_hash": "e356a16624537b14", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 17, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "training intensity distribution, POL polarized training, TT time trial, TTE time to exhaustion, V/P at VT2/LT2 velocity or power at second ventilatory or lactate threshold, VO2max/\npeak maximal/peak oxygen uptake, SMD standardized mean difference, I2", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Polarized vs. Other Training Intensity Distribution Regimens and Endurance Performance\nTable 3   Sub analysis of the effect of POL compared to other TIDs according to intervention duration and to athlete level\nALL other training intensity distribution, POL polarized training, TT time trial, TTE time to exhaustion, V/P at VT2/LT2 velocity or power at second ventilatory or lactate threshold, VO2max/\npeak maximal/peak oxygen uptake, SMD standardized mean difference, I2 (p) heterogeneity and p-value, Z (p) test for overall effect and p-value\n* Statistical significance: p < 0.05", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "training intensity distribution, POL polarized training, TT time trial, TTE time to exhaustion, V/P at VT2/LT2 velocity or power at second ventilatory or lactate threshold, VO2max/\npeak maximal/peak oxygen uptake, SMD standardized mean difference, I2"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0018_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c8f8855d8e0eb885", "text_span_hash": "c8f8855d8e0eb885", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 18, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "d to systematically review and meta-\nanalyze the evidence comparing the effect of POL with\nother TIDs on endurance performance. POL was found\nto be superior to other TIDs for VO2peak improvement,\nalthough with a small magnitude of effect (SMD = 0.24", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "P. Silva Oliveira et al.\n3.8  \u0007Publication Bias\nPublication bias was assessed for studies comparing ath-\nletes performing POL vs other TIDs on VO2peak and V/P\nat ­VT2/LT2. A non-significant publication bias was revealed\nby both the funnel plot symmetry and the Egger's test result\nadjusted for VO2peak (bias coefficient = – 0.042 (intercept);\n(intercept); p = 0.092). Detailed results are depicted in\nAppendix S5 of the ESM.\nThis study aimed to systematically review and meta-\nanalyze the evidence comparing the effect of POL with\nother TIDs on endurance performance. POL was found\nto be superior to other TIDs for VO2peak improvement,\nalthough with a small magnitude of effect (SMD = 0.24\nRegarding the secondary outcomes, there was no evidence\nof superiority of POL compared to other TIDs.\nTo date, only one systematic review with meta-analysis\nTT as a surrogate of endurance performance. In addition,\nthis meta-analysis contained only three studies due to\nthe scarcity of data available at that time. Their results\nsuggested superiority of POL compared to THR for TT\nimprovement, which is in opposition to our findings. This\ndisparity is due to the difference in the number of studies\nincluded in the analysis. The increased interest in POL\nhas led to a surge in the number of experimental studies\ninvestigating this TID. Consequently, our study included a\nsample of n = 437 participants compared to n = 112 in the\nRosenblat et al. study [33]. Ano", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "d to systematically review and meta-\nanalyze the evidence comparing the effect of POL with\nother TIDs on endurance performance. POL was found\nto be superior to other TIDs for VO2peak improvement,\nalthough with a small magnitude of effect (SMD = 0.24"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0018_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9f1baa0e39084297", "text_span_hash": "9f1baa0e39084297", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 18, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "gnitude of effect (SMD = 0.24\nRegarding the secondary outcomes, there was no evidence\nof superiority of POL compared to other TIDs.\nTo date, only one systematic review with meta-analysis\nTT as a surrogate of endurance performance. In addition,\nthis m", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "P. Silva Oliveira et al.\n3.8  \u0007Publication Bias\nPublication bias was assessed for studies comparing ath-\nletes performing POL vs other TIDs on VO2peak and V/P\nat ­VT2/LT2. A non-significant publication bias was revealed\nby both the funnel plot symmetry and the Egger's test result\nadjusted for VO2peak (bias coefficient = – 0.042 (intercept);\n(intercept); p = 0.092). Detailed results are depicted in\nAppendix S5 of the ESM.\nThis study aimed to systematically review and meta-\nanalyze the evidence comparing the effect of POL with\nother TIDs on endurance performance. POL was found\nto be superior to other TIDs for VO2peak improvement,\nalthough with a small magnitude of effect (SMD = 0.24\nRegarding the secondary outcomes, there was no evidence\nof superiority of POL compared to other TIDs.\nTo date, only one systematic review with meta-analysis\nTT as a surrogate of endurance performance. In addition,\nthis meta-analysis contained only three studies due to\nthe scarcity of data available at that time. Their results\nsuggested superiority of POL compared to THR for TT\nimprovement, which is in opposition to our findings. This\ndisparity is due to the difference in the number of studies\nincluded in the analysis. The increased interest in POL\nhas led to a surge in the number of experimental studies\ninvestigating this TID. Consequently, our study included a\nsample of n = 437 participants compared to n = 112 in the\nRosenblat et al. study [33]. Ano", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "gnitude of effect (SMD = 0.24\nRegarding the secondary outcomes, there was no evidence\nof superiority of POL compared to other TIDs.\nTo date, only one systematic review with meta-analysis\nTT as a surrogate of endurance performance. In addition,\nthis m"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0018_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b50ebe96448ffb74", "text_span_hash": "b50ebe96448ffb74", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 18, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "strongly correlated with endurance performance such\nas VO2peak, TT, TTE and V/P at ­VT2/LT2, other than TT,\nwhich allows for a much more thorough understanding of\nthe effectiveness of POL.\nFig. 6   Sub-analysis of POL vs ALL < 12 weeks\nFig. 7   Sub-a", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "P. Silva Oliveira et al.\n3.8  \u0007Publication Bias\nPublication bias was assessed for studies comparing ath-\nletes performing POL vs other TIDs on VO2peak and V/P\nat ­VT2/LT2. A non-significant publication bias was revealed\nby both the funnel plot symmetry and the Egger's test result\nadjusted for VO2peak (bias coefficient = – 0.042 (intercept);\n(intercept); p = 0.092). Detailed results are depicted in\nAppendix S5 of the ESM.\nThis study aimed to systematically review and meta-\nanalyze the evidence comparing the effect of POL with\nother TIDs on endurance performance. POL was found\nto be superior to other TIDs for VO2peak improvement,\nalthough with a small magnitude of effect (SMD = 0.24\nRegarding the secondary outcomes, there was no evidence\nof superiority of POL compared to other TIDs.\nTo date, only one systematic review with meta-analysis\nTT as a surrogate of endurance performance. In addition,\nthis meta-analysis contained only three studies due to\nthe scarcity of data available at that time. Their results\nsuggested superiority of POL compared to THR for TT\nimprovement, which is in opposition to our findings. This\ndisparity is due to the difference in the number of studies\nincluded in the analysis. The increased interest in POL\nhas led to a surge in the number of experimental studies\ninvestigating this TID. Consequently, our study included a\nsample of n = 437 participants compared to n = 112 in the\nRosenblat et al. study [33]. Ano", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "strongly correlated with endurance performance such\nas VO2peak, TT, TTE and V/P at ­VT2/LT2, other than TT,\nwhich allows for a much more thorough understanding of\nthe effectiveness of POL.\nFig. 6   Sub-analysis of POL vs ALL < 12 weeks\nFig. 7   Sub-a"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0019_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "63e68013118b7b15", "text_span_hash": "63e68013118b7b15", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 19, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Polarized vs. Other Training Intensity Distribution Regimens and Endurance Performance\nOur results showed that VO2peak was higher with POL\ncompared to other TIDs. The results displayed a low het-\nerogeneity but a small effect size. POL may have been", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Polarized vs. Other Training Intensity Distribution Regimens and Endurance Performance\nOur results showed that VO2peak was higher with POL\ncompared to other TIDs. The results displayed a low het-\nerogeneity but a small effect size. POL may have been more\neffective in promoting VO2peak adaptations as it exposes\nathletes to a combination of low- and high-intensity exer-\ncise, which appears to be particularly suited to the develop-\nment of central and peripheral aerobic adaptations [23]. The\nmain central adaptation to endurance training is the increase\nin cardiac output, which results mainly from increases in\nblood volume, left ventricle end-diastolic volume, and myo-\ncardial contractility [59, 60]. Of these adaptations, the one\nthat most likely contributes to improvements in VO2peak\nin studies with a short duration, such as those included in\nour systematic review, is an increase in blood volume. It\nhas been shown that low-intensity aerobic training is effec-\ntive in increasing plasma volume and cardiac output [61].\nHowever, high-intensity training appears to be an even more\neffective strategy for this purpose [62, 63]. Previous studies\nhave shown that intensity is a crucial variable in exercise-\ninduced hypervolemia, and that higher exercise intensities\nseem particularly effective at inducing rapid elevations in\nplasma volume [64, 65]. For instance, adding a short period\nof high intensity exercise, between 90 and 95% VO2peak,\nto", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Polarized vs. Other Training Intensity Distribution Regimens and Endurance Performance\nOur results showed that VO2peak was higher with POL\ncompared to other TIDs. The results displayed a low het-\nerogeneity but a small effect size. POL may have been"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0019_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "a15595d83933d2ad", "text_span_hash": "a15595d83933d2ad", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 19, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "evations in\nplasma volume [64, 65]. For instance, adding a short period\nof high intensity exercise, between 90 and 95% VO2peak,\nto well-trained runners, significantly increased their blood\nvolume by 4% [66]. Increases in blood volume of 10% 24\nh afte", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Polarized vs. Other Training Intensity Distribution Regimens and Endurance Performance\nOur results showed that VO2peak was higher with POL\ncompared to other TIDs. The results displayed a low het-\nerogeneity but a small effect size. POL may have been more\neffective in promoting VO2peak adaptations as it exposes\nathletes to a combination of low- and high-intensity exer-\ncise, which appears to be particularly suited to the develop-\nment of central and peripheral aerobic adaptations [23]. The\nmain central adaptation to endurance training is the increase\nin cardiac output, which results mainly from increases in\nblood volume, left ventricle end-diastolic volume, and myo-\ncardial contractility [59, 60]. Of these adaptations, the one\nthat most likely contributes to improvements in VO2peak\nin studies with a short duration, such as those included in\nour systematic review, is an increase in blood volume. It\nhas been shown that low-intensity aerobic training is effec-\ntive in increasing plasma volume and cardiac output [61].\nHowever, high-intensity training appears to be an even more\neffective strategy for this purpose [62, 63]. Previous studies\nhave shown that intensity is a crucial variable in exercise-\ninduced hypervolemia, and that higher exercise intensities\nseem particularly effective at inducing rapid elevations in\nplasma volume [64, 65]. For instance, adding a short period\nof high intensity exercise, between 90 and 95% VO2peak,\nto", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "evations in\nplasma volume [64, 65]. For instance, adding a short period\nof high intensity exercise, between 90 and 95% VO2peak,\nto well-trained runners, significantly increased their blood\nvolume by 4% [66]. Increases in blood volume of 10% 24\nh afte"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0019_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "305939bc22396999", "text_span_hash": "305939bc22396999", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 19, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "heral skeletal muscle adaptations favoring capil-\nlary oxygen extraction and use by fiber mitochondria [68].\nAlthough it is well demonstrated [69] that high-volume,\nlow-intensity training favors mitochondrial biogenesis [70],\nincreases lactate oxidat", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Polarized vs. Other Training Intensity Distribution Regimens and Endurance Performance\nOur results showed that VO2peak was higher with POL\ncompared to other TIDs. The results displayed a low het-\nerogeneity but a small effect size. POL may have been more\neffective in promoting VO2peak adaptations as it exposes\nathletes to a combination of low- and high-intensity exer-\ncise, which appears to be particularly suited to the develop-\nment of central and peripheral aerobic adaptations [23]. The\nmain central adaptation to endurance training is the increase\nin cardiac output, which results mainly from increases in\nblood volume, left ventricle end-diastolic volume, and myo-\ncardial contractility [59, 60]. Of these adaptations, the one\nthat most likely contributes to improvements in VO2peak\nin studies with a short duration, such as those included in\nour systematic review, is an increase in blood volume. It\nhas been shown that low-intensity aerobic training is effec-\ntive in increasing plasma volume and cardiac output [61].\nHowever, high-intensity training appears to be an even more\neffective strategy for this purpose [62, 63]. Previous studies\nhave shown that intensity is a crucial variable in exercise-\ninduced hypervolemia, and that higher exercise intensities\nseem particularly effective at inducing rapid elevations in\nplasma volume [64, 65]. For instance, adding a short period\nof high intensity exercise, between 90 and 95% VO2peak,\nto", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "heral skeletal muscle adaptations favoring capil-\nlary oxygen extraction and use by fiber mitochondria [68].\nAlthough it is well demonstrated [69] that high-volume,\nlow-intensity training favors mitochondrial biogenesis [70],\nincreases lactate oxidat"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0019_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4cd2a71e58ee7ce5", "text_span_hash": "4cd2a71e58ee7ce5", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 19, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "[70],\nincreases lactate oxidation rate [71] and type I muscle fib-\ners capillarization [68, 72], there is also evidence that high\nintensity is a key factor in peroxisome proliferator-activated\nreceptor-gamma coactivator-1 alpha (PGC-1α) activation\nan", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Polarized vs. Other Training Intensity Distribution Regimens and Endurance Performance\nOur results showed that VO2peak was higher with POL\ncompared to other TIDs. The results displayed a low het-\nerogeneity but a small effect size. POL may have been more\neffective in promoting VO2peak adaptations as it exposes\nathletes to a combination of low- and high-intensity exer-\ncise, which appears to be particularly suited to the develop-\nment of central and peripheral aerobic adaptations [23]. The\nmain central adaptation to endurance training is the increase\nin cardiac output, which results mainly from increases in\nblood volume, left ventricle end-diastolic volume, and myo-\ncardial contractility [59, 60]. Of these adaptations, the one\nthat most likely contributes to improvements in VO2peak\nin studies with a short duration, such as those included in\nour systematic review, is an increase in blood volume. It\nhas been shown that low-intensity aerobic training is effec-\ntive in increasing plasma volume and cardiac output [61].\nHowever, high-intensity training appears to be an even more\neffective strategy for this purpose [62, 63]. Previous studies\nhave shown that intensity is a crucial variable in exercise-\ninduced hypervolemia, and that higher exercise intensities\nseem particularly effective at inducing rapid elevations in\nplasma volume [64, 65]. For instance, adding a short period\nof high intensity exercise, between 90 and 95% VO2peak,\nto", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "[70],\nincreases lactate oxidation rate [71] and type I muscle fib-\ners capillarization [68, 72], there is also evidence that high\nintensity is a key factor in peroxisome proliferator-activated\nreceptor-gamma coactivator-1 alpha (PGC-1α) activation\nan"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0019_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c2d4133f96ee1a65", "text_span_hash": "c2d4133f96ee1a65", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 19, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "by the fact that high-intensity exer-\ncise is a major stimulus for ATP depletion and accumulation\nof ADP and AMP, which will thereby activate PGC1-α and\ntrigger mitochondrial biogenesis [75–77]. Training at higher\nintensities has also been shown to l", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Polarized vs. Other Training Intensity Distribution Regimens and Endurance Performance\nOur results showed that VO2peak was higher with POL\ncompared to other TIDs. The results displayed a low het-\nerogeneity but a small effect size. POL may have been more\neffective in promoting VO2peak adaptations as it exposes\nathletes to a combination of low- and high-intensity exer-\ncise, which appears to be particularly suited to the develop-\nment of central and peripheral aerobic adaptations [23]. The\nmain central adaptation to endurance training is the increase\nin cardiac output, which results mainly from increases in\nblood volume, left ventricle end-diastolic volume, and myo-\ncardial contractility [59, 60]. Of these adaptations, the one\nthat most likely contributes to improvements in VO2peak\nin studies with a short duration, such as those included in\nour systematic review, is an increase in blood volume. It\nhas been shown that low-intensity aerobic training is effec-\ntive in increasing plasma volume and cardiac output [61].\nHowever, high-intensity training appears to be an even more\neffective strategy for this purpose [62, 63]. Previous studies\nhave shown that intensity is a crucial variable in exercise-\ninduced hypervolemia, and that higher exercise intensities\nseem particularly effective at inducing rapid elevations in\nplasma volume [64, 65]. For instance, adding a short period\nof high intensity exercise, between 90 and 95% VO2peak,\nto", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "by the fact that high-intensity exer-\ncise is a major stimulus for ATP depletion and accumulation\nof ADP and AMP, which will thereby activate PGC1-α and\ntrigger mitochondrial biogenesis [75–77]. Training at higher\nintensities has also been shown to l"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0019_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f939d5ae74a650de", "text_span_hash": "f939d5ae74a650de", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 19, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ities has also been shown to lead to faster improve-\nments in endurance performance [78, 79] and to faster\nperipheral adaptations compared to training at moderate\nor low intensity [80]. This may also be due to the greater\nrecruitment of type II muscl", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Polarized vs. Other Training Intensity Distribution Regimens and Endurance Performance\nOur results showed that VO2peak was higher with POL\ncompared to other TIDs. The results displayed a low het-\nerogeneity but a small effect size. POL may have been more\neffective in promoting VO2peak adaptations as it exposes\nathletes to a combination of low- and high-intensity exer-\ncise, which appears to be particularly suited to the develop-\nment of central and peripheral aerobic adaptations [23]. The\nmain central adaptation to endurance training is the increase\nin cardiac output, which results mainly from increases in\nblood volume, left ventricle end-diastolic volume, and myo-\ncardial contractility [59, 60]. Of these adaptations, the one\nthat most likely contributes to improvements in VO2peak\nin studies with a short duration, such as those included in\nour systematic review, is an increase in blood volume. It\nhas been shown that low-intensity aerobic training is effec-\ntive in increasing plasma volume and cardiac output [61].\nHowever, high-intensity training appears to be an even more\neffective strategy for this purpose [62, 63]. Previous studies\nhave shown that intensity is a crucial variable in exercise-\ninduced hypervolemia, and that higher exercise intensities\nseem particularly effective at inducing rapid elevations in\nplasma volume [64, 65]. For instance, adding a short period\nof high intensity exercise, between 90 and 95% VO2peak,\nto", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ities has also been shown to lead to faster improve-\nments in endurance performance [78, 79] and to faster\nperipheral adaptations compared to training at moderate\nor low intensity [80]. This may also be due to the greater\nrecruitment of type II muscl"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0019_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8ea432aa8e3466b7", "text_span_hash": "8ea432aa8e3466b7", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 19, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "r\nrecruitment of type II muscle fibers, which positively affects\ntheir oxidative capacity [81–83]. For instance, high-intensity\nexercise seems to induce superior adaptations in type II fib-\ners’ oxidative metabolism compared to low-intensity aerobic", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Polarized vs. Other Training Intensity Distribution Regimens and Endurance Performance\nOur results showed that VO2peak was higher with POL\ncompared to other TIDs. The results displayed a low het-\nerogeneity but a small effect size. POL may have been more\neffective in promoting VO2peak adaptations as it exposes\nathletes to a combination of low- and high-intensity exer-\ncise, which appears to be particularly suited to the develop-\nment of central and peripheral aerobic adaptations [23]. The\nmain central adaptation to endurance training is the increase\nin cardiac output, which results mainly from increases in\nblood volume, left ventricle end-diastolic volume, and myo-\ncardial contractility [59, 60]. Of these adaptations, the one\nthat most likely contributes to improvements in VO2peak\nin studies with a short duration, such as those included in\nour systematic review, is an increase in blood volume. It\nhas been shown that low-intensity aerobic training is effec-\ntive in increasing plasma volume and cardiac output [61].\nHowever, high-intensity training appears to be an even more\neffective strategy for this purpose [62, 63]. Previous studies\nhave shown that intensity is a crucial variable in exercise-\ninduced hypervolemia, and that higher exercise intensities\nseem particularly effective at inducing rapid elevations in\nplasma volume [64, 65]. For instance, adding a short period\nof high intensity exercise, between 90 and 95% VO2peak,\nto", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "r\nrecruitment of type II muscle fibers, which positively affects\ntheir oxidative capacity [81–83]. For instance, high-intensity\nexercise seems to induce superior adaptations in type II fib-\ners’ oxidative metabolism compared to low-intensity aerobic"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0019_c0016", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "d23c9d7bef3f8a00", "text_span_hash": "d23c9d7bef3f8a00", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 19, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 16, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ared to low-intensity aerobic\ntraining [84]. In addition, when high-intensity exercise is\nperformed at an intensity greater than VO2peak, the oxida-\ntive capacity of type IIx muscle fibers is enhanced [85]. Col-\nlectively, this evidence suggests that", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Polarized vs. Other Training Intensity Distribution Regimens and Endurance Performance\nOur results showed that VO2peak was higher with POL\ncompared to other TIDs. The results displayed a low het-\nerogeneity but a small effect size. POL may have been more\neffective in promoting VO2peak adaptations as it exposes\nathletes to a combination of low- and high-intensity exer-\ncise, which appears to be particularly suited to the develop-\nment of central and peripheral aerobic adaptations [23]. The\nmain central adaptation to endurance training is the increase\nin cardiac output, which results mainly from increases in\nblood volume, left ventricle end-diastolic volume, and myo-\ncardial contractility [59, 60]. Of these adaptations, the one\nthat most likely contributes to improvements in VO2peak\nin studies with a short duration, such as those included in\nour systematic review, is an increase in blood volume. It\nhas been shown that low-intensity aerobic training is effec-\ntive in increasing plasma volume and cardiac output [61].\nHowever, high-intensity training appears to be an even more\neffective strategy for this purpose [62, 63]. Previous studies\nhave shown that intensity is a crucial variable in exercise-\ninduced hypervolemia, and that higher exercise intensities\nseem particularly effective at inducing rapid elevations in\nplasma volume [64, 65]. For instance, adding a short period\nof high intensity exercise, between 90 and 95% VO2peak,\nto", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ared to low-intensity aerobic\ntraining [84]. In addition, when high-intensity exercise is\nperformed at an intensity greater than VO2peak, the oxida-\ntive capacity of type IIx muscle fibers is enhanced [85]. Col-\nlectively, this evidence suggests that"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0019_c0017", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9fd5f7788cb1e1b7", "text_span_hash": "9fd5f7788cb1e1b7", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 19, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 17, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "y, this evidence suggests that adding high-intensity\nexercise to the training regimen favors a faster development\nof both central and peripheral adaptations, thereby optimally\nenhancing VO2peak. This is in agreement with our sub-\nanalysis showing tha", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Polarized vs. Other Training Intensity Distribution Regimens and Endurance Performance\nOur results showed that VO2peak was higher with POL\ncompared to other TIDs. The results displayed a low het-\nerogeneity but a small effect size. POL may have been more\neffective in promoting VO2peak adaptations as it exposes\nathletes to a combination of low- and high-intensity exer-\ncise, which appears to be particularly suited to the develop-\nment of central and peripheral aerobic adaptations [23]. The\nmain central adaptation to endurance training is the increase\nin cardiac output, which results mainly from increases in\nblood volume, left ventricle end-diastolic volume, and myo-\ncardial contractility [59, 60]. Of these adaptations, the one\nthat most likely contributes to improvements in VO2peak\nin studies with a short duration, such as those included in\nour systematic review, is an increase in blood volume. It\nhas been shown that low-intensity aerobic training is effec-\ntive in increasing plasma volume and cardiac output [61].\nHowever, high-intensity training appears to be an even more\neffective strategy for this purpose [62, 63]. Previous studies\nhave shown that intensity is a crucial variable in exercise-\ninduced hypervolemia, and that higher exercise intensities\nseem particularly effective at inducing rapid elevations in\nplasma volume [64, 65]. For instance, adding a short period\nof high intensity exercise, between 90 and 95% VO2peak,\nto", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "y, this evidence suggests that adding high-intensity\nexercise to the training regimen favors a faster development\nof both central and peripheral adaptations, thereby optimally\nenhancing VO2peak. This is in agreement with our sub-\nanalysis showing tha"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0019_c0018", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5c6cc3a5e2eebea9", "text_span_hash": "5c6cc3a5e2eebea9", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 19, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 18, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "our sub-\nanalysis showing that POL training is particularly effective\nin inducing faster increases at VO2peak. Nevertheless, the\nadvantage of POL seems to wane for interventions longer\nthan 12 weeks, suggesting that subjects who undergo other\nTIDs m", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Polarized vs. Other Training Intensity Distribution Regimens and Endurance Performance\nOur results showed that VO2peak was higher with POL\ncompared to other TIDs. The results displayed a low het-\nerogeneity but a small effect size. POL may have been more\neffective in promoting VO2peak adaptations as it exposes\nathletes to a combination of low- and high-intensity exer-\ncise, which appears to be particularly suited to the develop-\nment of central and peripheral aerobic adaptations [23]. The\nmain central adaptation to endurance training is the increase\nin cardiac output, which results mainly from increases in\nblood volume, left ventricle end-diastolic volume, and myo-\ncardial contractility [59, 60]. Of these adaptations, the one\nthat most likely contributes to improvements in VO2peak\nin studies with a short duration, such as those included in\nour systematic review, is an increase in blood volume. It\nhas been shown that low-intensity aerobic training is effec-\ntive in increasing plasma volume and cardiac output [61].\nHowever, high-intensity training appears to be an even more\neffective strategy for this purpose [62, 63]. Previous studies\nhave shown that intensity is a crucial variable in exercise-\ninduced hypervolemia, and that higher exercise intensities\nseem particularly effective at inducing rapid elevations in\nplasma volume [64, 65]. For instance, adding a short period\nof high intensity exercise, between 90 and 95% VO2peak,\nto", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "our sub-\nanalysis showing that POL training is particularly effective\nin inducing faster increases at VO2peak. Nevertheless, the\nadvantage of POL seems to wane for interventions longer\nthan 12 weeks, suggesting that subjects who undergo other\nTIDs m"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0019_c0020", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "fdae564b3b3b565c", "text_span_hash": "fdae564b3b3b565c", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 19, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 20, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "such as for instance in prehabili-\ntation exercise contexts [86].\nDespite high VO2peak being one of the determinants of\nendurance performance [87], other variables are also impor-\ntant in this context [61, 88]. Anaerobic threshold, which\ntranslates", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Polarized vs. Other Training Intensity Distribution Regimens and Endurance Performance\nOur results showed that VO2peak was higher with POL\ncompared to other TIDs. The results displayed a low het-\nerogeneity but a small effect size. POL may have been more\neffective in promoting VO2peak adaptations as it exposes\nathletes to a combination of low- and high-intensity exer-\ncise, which appears to be particularly suited to the develop-\nment of central and peripheral aerobic adaptations [23]. The\nmain central adaptation to endurance training is the increase\nin cardiac output, which results mainly from increases in\nblood volume, left ventricle end-diastolic volume, and myo-\ncardial contractility [59, 60]. Of these adaptations, the one\nthat most likely contributes to improvements in VO2peak\nin studies with a short duration, such as those included in\nour systematic review, is an increase in blood volume. It\nhas been shown that low-intensity aerobic training is effec-\ntive in increasing plasma volume and cardiac output [61].\nHowever, high-intensity training appears to be an even more\neffective strategy for this purpose [62, 63]. Previous studies\nhave shown that intensity is a crucial variable in exercise-\ninduced hypervolemia, and that higher exercise intensities\nseem particularly effective at inducing rapid elevations in\nplasma volume [64, 65]. For instance, adding a short period\nof high intensity exercise, between 90 and 95% VO2peak,\nto", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "such as for instance in prehabili-\ntation exercise contexts [86].\nDespite high VO2peak being one of the determinants of\nendurance performance [87], other variables are also impor-\ntant in this context [61, 88]. Anaerobic threshold, which\ntranslates"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0019_c0021", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "bc26cc1ee9c909f4", "text_span_hash": "bc26cc1ee9c909f4", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 19, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 21, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "c threshold, which\ntranslates into the ability to maintain high workloads without\nexponentially increasing blood lactate concentration [35], is\na variable that is more strongly correlated with endurance\nsports performance [89]. Interestingly, our res", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Polarized vs. Other Training Intensity Distribution Regimens and Endurance Performance\nOur results showed that VO2peak was higher with POL\ncompared to other TIDs. The results displayed a low het-\nerogeneity but a small effect size. POL may have been more\neffective in promoting VO2peak adaptations as it exposes\nathletes to a combination of low- and high-intensity exer-\ncise, which appears to be particularly suited to the develop-\nment of central and peripheral aerobic adaptations [23]. The\nmain central adaptation to endurance training is the increase\nin cardiac output, which results mainly from increases in\nblood volume, left ventricle end-diastolic volume, and myo-\ncardial contractility [59, 60]. Of these adaptations, the one\nthat most likely contributes to improvements in VO2peak\nin studies with a short duration, such as those included in\nour systematic review, is an increase in blood volume. It\nhas been shown that low-intensity aerobic training is effec-\ntive in increasing plasma volume and cardiac output [61].\nHowever, high-intensity training appears to be an even more\neffective strategy for this purpose [62, 63]. Previous studies\nhave shown that intensity is a crucial variable in exercise-\ninduced hypervolemia, and that higher exercise intensities\nseem particularly effective at inducing rapid elevations in\nplasma volume [64, 65]. For instance, adding a short period\nof high intensity exercise, between 90 and 95% VO2peak,\nto", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "c threshold, which\ntranslates into the ability to maintain high workloads without\nexponentially increasing blood lactate concentration [35], is\na variable that is more strongly correlated with endurance\nsports performance [89]. Interestingly, our res"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0019_c0022", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1402f08b6b4eb9e4", "text_span_hash": "1402f08b6b4eb9e4", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 19, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 22, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "e [89]. Interestingly, our results suggest\nthat, for this variable, there is no evidence of superiority of\nPOL compared to other TIDs (p = 0.75).\nExercise intensity at ­VT1 and, especially, at ­VT2 are\nmajor determinants of endurance performance sinc", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Polarized vs. Other Training Intensity Distribution Regimens and Endurance Performance\nOur results showed that VO2peak was higher with POL\ncompared to other TIDs. The results displayed a low het-\nerogeneity but a small effect size. POL may have been more\neffective in promoting VO2peak adaptations as it exposes\nathletes to a combination of low- and high-intensity exer-\ncise, which appears to be particularly suited to the develop-\nment of central and peripheral aerobic adaptations [23]. The\nmain central adaptation to endurance training is the increase\nin cardiac output, which results mainly from increases in\nblood volume, left ventricle end-diastolic volume, and myo-\ncardial contractility [59, 60]. Of these adaptations, the one\nthat most likely contributes to improvements in VO2peak\nin studies with a short duration, such as those included in\nour systematic review, is an increase in blood volume. It\nhas been shown that low-intensity aerobic training is effec-\ntive in increasing plasma volume and cardiac output [61].\nHowever, high-intensity training appears to be an even more\neffective strategy for this purpose [62, 63]. Previous studies\nhave shown that intensity is a crucial variable in exercise-\ninduced hypervolemia, and that higher exercise intensities\nseem particularly effective at inducing rapid elevations in\nplasma volume [64, 65]. For instance, adding a short period\nof high intensity exercise, between 90 and 95% VO2peak,\nto", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "e [89]. Interestingly, our results suggest\nthat, for this variable, there is no evidence of superiority of\nPOL compared to other TIDs (p = 0.75).\nExercise intensity at ­VT1 and, especially, at ­VT2 are\nmajor determinants of endurance performance sinc"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0019_c0023", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b585f0e1b7efa193", "text_span_hash": "b585f0e1b7efa193", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 19, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 23, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "of endurance performance since ­VT2\nmarks the intensity above which lactate concentration con-\nsistently rises, hindering the ability to tolerate the exercise\nintensity for a long period. Endurance training plays an\nimportant role in reducing blood", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Polarized vs. Other Training Intensity Distribution Regimens and Endurance Performance\nOur results showed that VO2peak was higher with POL\ncompared to other TIDs. The results displayed a low het-\nerogeneity but a small effect size. POL may have been more\neffective in promoting VO2peak adaptations as it exposes\nathletes to a combination of low- and high-intensity exer-\ncise, which appears to be particularly suited to the develop-\nment of central and peripheral aerobic adaptations [23]. The\nmain central adaptation to endurance training is the increase\nin cardiac output, which results mainly from increases in\nblood volume, left ventricle end-diastolic volume, and myo-\ncardial contractility [59, 60]. Of these adaptations, the one\nthat most likely contributes to improvements in VO2peak\nin studies with a short duration, such as those included in\nour systematic review, is an increase in blood volume. It\nhas been shown that low-intensity aerobic training is effec-\ntive in increasing plasma volume and cardiac output [61].\nHowever, high-intensity training appears to be an even more\neffective strategy for this purpose [62, 63]. Previous studies\nhave shown that intensity is a crucial variable in exercise-\ninduced hypervolemia, and that higher exercise intensities\nseem particularly effective at inducing rapid elevations in\nplasma volume [64, 65]. For instance, adding a short period\nof high intensity exercise, between 90 and 95% VO2peak,\nto", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "of endurance performance since ­VT2\nmarks the intensity above which lactate concentration con-\nsistently rises, hindering the ability to tolerate the exercise\nintensity for a long period. Endurance training plays an\nimportant role in reducing blood"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0019_c0025", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3439d1ebe31b85ea", "text_span_hash": "3439d1ebe31b85ea", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 19, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 25, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "s [91], and the\nability to effectively remove blood lactate [92]. The most\nplausible physiological rationale for increasing velocity at\nthe anaerobic threshold is increased lactate clearance [93].\nAfter both low-intensity [94] and high-intensity [95]", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Polarized vs. Other Training Intensity Distribution Regimens and Endurance Performance\nOur results showed that VO2peak was higher with POL\ncompared to other TIDs. The results displayed a low het-\nerogeneity but a small effect size. POL may have been more\neffective in promoting VO2peak adaptations as it exposes\nathletes to a combination of low- and high-intensity exer-\ncise, which appears to be particularly suited to the develop-\nment of central and peripheral aerobic adaptations [23]. The\nmain central adaptation to endurance training is the increase\nin cardiac output, which results mainly from increases in\nblood volume, left ventricle end-diastolic volume, and myo-\ncardial contractility [59, 60]. Of these adaptations, the one\nthat most likely contributes to improvements in VO2peak\nin studies with a short duration, such as those included in\nour systematic review, is an increase in blood volume. It\nhas been shown that low-intensity aerobic training is effec-\ntive in increasing plasma volume and cardiac output [61].\nHowever, high-intensity training appears to be an even more\neffective strategy for this purpose [62, 63]. Previous studies\nhave shown that intensity is a crucial variable in exercise-\ninduced hypervolemia, and that higher exercise intensities\nseem particularly effective at inducing rapid elevations in\nplasma volume [64, 65]. For instance, adding a short period\nof high intensity exercise, between 90 and 95% VO2peak,\nto", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "s [91], and the\nability to effectively remove blood lactate [92]. The most\nplausible physiological rationale for increasing velocity at\nthe anaerobic threshold is increased lactate clearance [93].\nAfter both low-intensity [94] and high-intensity [95]"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0019_c0027", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "287c832b492a0c74", "text_span_hash": "287c832b492a0c74", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 19, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 27, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "d hydrogen ions and increases muscle lactate\nclearance [96, 97]. In fact, this is verifiable by analyzing\nthe expression of MCT1 in well-trained subjects, which is\nmuch higher compared to less trained subjects [98]. Further-\nmore, the rate of lactate", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Polarized vs. Other Training Intensity Distribution Regimens and Endurance Performance\nOur results showed that VO2peak was higher with POL\ncompared to other TIDs. The results displayed a low het-\nerogeneity but a small effect size. POL may have been more\neffective in promoting VO2peak adaptations as it exposes\nathletes to a combination of low- and high-intensity exer-\ncise, which appears to be particularly suited to the develop-\nment of central and peripheral aerobic adaptations [23]. The\nmain central adaptation to endurance training is the increase\nin cardiac output, which results mainly from increases in\nblood volume, left ventricle end-diastolic volume, and myo-\ncardial contractility [59, 60]. Of these adaptations, the one\nthat most likely contributes to improvements in VO2peak\nin studies with a short duration, such as those included in\nour systematic review, is an increase in blood volume. It\nhas been shown that low-intensity aerobic training is effec-\ntive in increasing plasma volume and cardiac output [61].\nHowever, high-intensity training appears to be an even more\neffective strategy for this purpose [62, 63]. Previous studies\nhave shown that intensity is a crucial variable in exercise-\ninduced hypervolemia, and that higher exercise intensities\nseem particularly effective at inducing rapid elevations in\nplasma volume [64, 65]. For instance, adding a short period\nof high intensity exercise, between 90 and 95% VO2peak,\nto", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "d hydrogen ions and increases muscle lactate\nclearance [96, 97]. In fact, this is verifiable by analyzing\nthe expression of MCT1 in well-trained subjects, which is\nmuch higher compared to less trained subjects [98]. Further-\nmore, the rate of lactate"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0019_c0028", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c3aecd9498cf9cca", "text_span_hash": "c3aecd9498cf9cca", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 19, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 28, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "her-\nmore, the rate of lactate removal after maximal exertion cor-\nrelates with MCT1 expression, thereby favoring high ATP\nutilization rates without major increases in blood lactate\n[99]. Considering that both low-intensity and high-intensity", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Polarized vs. Other Training Intensity Distribution Regimens and Endurance Performance\nOur results showed that VO2peak was higher with POL\ncompared to other TIDs. The results displayed a low het-\nerogeneity but a small effect size. POL may have been more\neffective in promoting VO2peak adaptations as it exposes\nathletes to a combination of low- and high-intensity exer-\ncise, which appears to be particularly suited to the develop-\nment of central and peripheral aerobic adaptations [23]. The\nmain central adaptation to endurance training is the increase\nin cardiac output, which results mainly from increases in\nblood volume, left ventricle end-diastolic volume, and myo-\ncardial contractility [59, 60]. Of these adaptations, the one\nthat most likely contributes to improvements in VO2peak\nin studies with a short duration, such as those included in\nour systematic review, is an increase in blood volume. It\nhas been shown that low-intensity aerobic training is effec-\ntive in increasing plasma volume and cardiac output [61].\nHowever, high-intensity training appears to be an even more\neffective strategy for this purpose [62, 63]. Previous studies\nhave shown that intensity is a crucial variable in exercise-\ninduced hypervolemia, and that higher exercise intensities\nseem particularly effective at inducing rapid elevations in\nplasma volume [64, 65]. For instance, adding a short period\nof high intensity exercise, between 90 and 95% VO2peak,\nto", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "her-\nmore, the rate of lactate removal after maximal exertion cor-\nrelates with MCT1 expression, thereby favoring high ATP\nutilization rates without major increases in blood lactate\n[99]. Considering that both low-intensity and high-intensity"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0020_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ed2ee7decbbe98c5", "text_span_hash": "ed2ee7decbbe98c5", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 20, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "P. Silva Oliveira et al.\nexercise seem to induce similar adaptations in the mecha-\nnisms involved in lactate production and removal, it is not\nsurprising that several studies using different exercise train-\ning intensities, such as threshold training", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "P. Silva Oliveira et al.\nexercise seem to induce similar adaptations in the mecha-\nnisms involved in lactate production and removal, it is not\nsurprising that several studies using different exercise train-\ning intensities, such as threshold training [26, 100], train-\ning above the anaerobic threshold [101, 102], and POL [26,\n103], were all effective in improving the anaerobic threshold,\nwhich is in agreement with our findings that POL is similar\nto other TIDs regarding V/P at ­VT2/LT2.\nTT is a variable highly correlated with endurance perfor-\nmance [104]. In our study, there was no evidence of supe-\nriority of POL compared to other TIDs in the improvement\nof TT (p = 0.92). Nevertheless, the studies included in our\nmeta-analysis displayed a high variability in terms of TT\ndistances, ranging from 100 m [54] to 40 km [26], which\ncorrelate very differently with performance in aerobic activi-\nties, and therefore some of these results might not neces-\nsarily reflect adaptations of the aerobic metabolism. TT in\nendurance activities is highly dependent on VO2peak and,\nespecially for longer distances, on LT and running economy\n[87, 105–107]. Considering our findings that POL is similar\nto other TIDs regarding improvements in V/P at ­VT2/LT2\nand that POL is only able to marginally improve ­VO2peak,\nespecially for shorter duration interventions, it is not surpris-\ning that the results from our meta-analysis suggest that TT\ncan be simila", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "P. Silva Oliveira et al.\nexercise seem to induce similar adaptations in the mecha-\nnisms involved in lactate production and removal, it is not\nsurprising that several studies using different exercise train-\ning intensities, such as threshold training"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0020_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "a828db333e58d5ba", "text_span_hash": "a828db333e58d5ba", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 20, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "es, such as threshold training [26, 100], train-\ning above the anaerobic threshold [101, 102], and POL [26,\n103], were all effective in improving the anaerobic threshold,\nwhich is in agreement with our findings that POL is similar\nto other TIDs regar", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "P. Silva Oliveira et al.\nexercise seem to induce similar adaptations in the mecha-\nnisms involved in lactate production and removal, it is not\nsurprising that several studies using different exercise train-\ning intensities, such as threshold training [26, 100], train-\ning above the anaerobic threshold [101, 102], and POL [26,\n103], were all effective in improving the anaerobic threshold,\nwhich is in agreement with our findings that POL is similar\nto other TIDs regarding V/P at ­VT2/LT2.\nTT is a variable highly correlated with endurance perfor-\nmance [104]. In our study, there was no evidence of supe-\nriority of POL compared to other TIDs in the improvement\nof TT (p = 0.92). Nevertheless, the studies included in our\nmeta-analysis displayed a high variability in terms of TT\ndistances, ranging from 100 m [54] to 40 km [26], which\ncorrelate very differently with performance in aerobic activi-\nties, and therefore some of these results might not neces-\nsarily reflect adaptations of the aerobic metabolism. TT in\nendurance activities is highly dependent on VO2peak and,\nespecially for longer distances, on LT and running economy\n[87, 105–107]. Considering our findings that POL is similar\nto other TIDs regarding improvements in V/P at ­VT2/LT2\nand that POL is only able to marginally improve ­VO2peak,\nespecially for shorter duration interventions, it is not surpris-\ning that the results from our meta-analysis suggest that TT\ncan be simila", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "es, such as threshold training [26, 100], train-\ning above the anaerobic threshold [101, 102], and POL [26,\n103], were all effective in improving the anaerobic threshold,\nwhich is in agreement with our findings that POL is similar\nto other TIDs regar"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0020_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c2d3d30ff595e22c", "text_span_hash": "c2d3d30ff595e22c", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 20, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "is similar\nto other TIDs regarding V/P at ­VT2/LT2.\nTT is a variable highly correlated with endurance perfor-\nmance [104]. In our study, there was no evidence of supe-\nriority of POL compared to other TIDs in the improvement\nof TT (p = 0.92). Neverth", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "P. Silva Oliveira et al.\nexercise seem to induce similar adaptations in the mecha-\nnisms involved in lactate production and removal, it is not\nsurprising that several studies using different exercise train-\ning intensities, such as threshold training [26, 100], train-\ning above the anaerobic threshold [101, 102], and POL [26,\n103], were all effective in improving the anaerobic threshold,\nwhich is in agreement with our findings that POL is similar\nto other TIDs regarding V/P at ­VT2/LT2.\nTT is a variable highly correlated with endurance perfor-\nmance [104]. In our study, there was no evidence of supe-\nriority of POL compared to other TIDs in the improvement\nof TT (p = 0.92). Nevertheless, the studies included in our\nmeta-analysis displayed a high variability in terms of TT\ndistances, ranging from 100 m [54] to 40 km [26], which\ncorrelate very differently with performance in aerobic activi-\nties, and therefore some of these results might not neces-\nsarily reflect adaptations of the aerobic metabolism. TT in\nendurance activities is highly dependent on VO2peak and,\nespecially for longer distances, on LT and running economy\n[87, 105–107]. Considering our findings that POL is similar\nto other TIDs regarding improvements in V/P at ­VT2/LT2\nand that POL is only able to marginally improve ­VO2peak,\nespecially for shorter duration interventions, it is not surpris-\ning that the results from our meta-analysis suggest that TT\ncan be simila", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "is similar\nto other TIDs regarding V/P at ­VT2/LT2.\nTT is a variable highly correlated with endurance perfor-\nmance [104]. In our study, there was no evidence of supe-\nriority of POL compared to other TIDs in the improvement\nof TT (p = 0.92). Neverth"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0020_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "d7e2ca5401d73713", "text_span_hash": "d7e2ca5401d73713", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 20, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ment\nof TT (p = 0.92). Nevertheless, the studies included in our\nmeta-analysis displayed a high variability in terms of TT\ndistances, ranging from 100 m [54] to 40 km [26], which\ncorrelate very differently with performance in aerobic activi-\nties, an", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "P. Silva Oliveira et al.\nexercise seem to induce similar adaptations in the mecha-\nnisms involved in lactate production and removal, it is not\nsurprising that several studies using different exercise train-\ning intensities, such as threshold training [26, 100], train-\ning above the anaerobic threshold [101, 102], and POL [26,\n103], were all effective in improving the anaerobic threshold,\nwhich is in agreement with our findings that POL is similar\nto other TIDs regarding V/P at ­VT2/LT2.\nTT is a variable highly correlated with endurance perfor-\nmance [104]. In our study, there was no evidence of supe-\nriority of POL compared to other TIDs in the improvement\nof TT (p = 0.92). Nevertheless, the studies included in our\nmeta-analysis displayed a high variability in terms of TT\ndistances, ranging from 100 m [54] to 40 km [26], which\ncorrelate very differently with performance in aerobic activi-\nties, and therefore some of these results might not neces-\nsarily reflect adaptations of the aerobic metabolism. TT in\nendurance activities is highly dependent on VO2peak and,\nespecially for longer distances, on LT and running economy\n[87, 105–107]. Considering our findings that POL is similar\nto other TIDs regarding improvements in V/P at ­VT2/LT2\nand that POL is only able to marginally improve ­VO2peak,\nespecially for shorter duration interventions, it is not surpris-\ning that the results from our meta-analysis suggest that TT\ncan be simila", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ment\nof TT (p = 0.92). Nevertheless, the studies included in our\nmeta-analysis displayed a high variability in terms of TT\ndistances, ranging from 100 m [54] to 40 km [26], which\ncorrelate very differently with performance in aerobic activi-\nties, an"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0020_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "29cbe8db86921a3c", "text_span_hash": "29cbe8db86921a3c", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 20, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ce in aerobic activi-\nties, and therefore some of these results might not neces-\nsarily reflect adaptations of the aerobic metabolism. TT in\nendurance activities is highly dependent on VO2peak and,\nespecially for longer distances, on LT and running e", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "P. Silva Oliveira et al.\nexercise seem to induce similar adaptations in the mecha-\nnisms involved in lactate production and removal, it is not\nsurprising that several studies using different exercise train-\ning intensities, such as threshold training [26, 100], train-\ning above the anaerobic threshold [101, 102], and POL [26,\n103], were all effective in improving the anaerobic threshold,\nwhich is in agreement with our findings that POL is similar\nto other TIDs regarding V/P at ­VT2/LT2.\nTT is a variable highly correlated with endurance perfor-\nmance [104]. In our study, there was no evidence of supe-\nriority of POL compared to other TIDs in the improvement\nof TT (p = 0.92). Nevertheless, the studies included in our\nmeta-analysis displayed a high variability in terms of TT\ndistances, ranging from 100 m [54] to 40 km [26], which\ncorrelate very differently with performance in aerobic activi-\nties, and therefore some of these results might not neces-\nsarily reflect adaptations of the aerobic metabolism. TT in\nendurance activities is highly dependent on VO2peak and,\nespecially for longer distances, on LT and running economy\n[87, 105–107]. Considering our findings that POL is similar\nto other TIDs regarding improvements in V/P at ­VT2/LT2\nand that POL is only able to marginally improve ­VO2peak,\nespecially for shorter duration interventions, it is not surpris-\ning that the results from our meta-analysis suggest that TT\ncan be simila", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ce in aerobic activi-\nties, and therefore some of these results might not neces-\nsarily reflect adaptations of the aerobic metabolism. TT in\nendurance activities is highly dependent on VO2peak and,\nespecially for longer distances, on LT and running e"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0020_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "26dd8a2990b422cb", "text_span_hash": "26dd8a2990b422cb", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 20, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "distances, on LT and running economy\n[87, 105–107]. Considering our findings that POL is similar\nto other TIDs regarding improvements in V/P at ­VT2/LT2\nand that POL is only able to marginally improve ­VO2peak,\nespecially for shorter duration interve", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "P. Silva Oliveira et al.\nexercise seem to induce similar adaptations in the mecha-\nnisms involved in lactate production and removal, it is not\nsurprising that several studies using different exercise train-\ning intensities, such as threshold training [26, 100], train-\ning above the anaerobic threshold [101, 102], and POL [26,\n103], were all effective in improving the anaerobic threshold,\nwhich is in agreement with our findings that POL is similar\nto other TIDs regarding V/P at ­VT2/LT2.\nTT is a variable highly correlated with endurance perfor-\nmance [104]. In our study, there was no evidence of supe-\nriority of POL compared to other TIDs in the improvement\nof TT (p = 0.92). Nevertheless, the studies included in our\nmeta-analysis displayed a high variability in terms of TT\ndistances, ranging from 100 m [54] to 40 km [26], which\ncorrelate very differently with performance in aerobic activi-\nties, and therefore some of these results might not neces-\nsarily reflect adaptations of the aerobic metabolism. TT in\nendurance activities is highly dependent on VO2peak and,\nespecially for longer distances, on LT and running economy\n[87, 105–107]. Considering our findings that POL is similar\nto other TIDs regarding improvements in V/P at ­VT2/LT2\nand that POL is only able to marginally improve ­VO2peak,\nespecially for shorter duration interventions, it is not surpris-\ning that the results from our meta-analysis suggest that TT\ncan be simila", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "distances, on LT and running economy\n[87, 105–107]. Considering our findings that POL is similar\nto other TIDs regarding improvements in V/P at ­VT2/LT2\nand that POL is only able to marginally improve ­VO2peak,\nespecially for shorter duration interve"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0020_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b2ecec4ef4ba9a4a", "text_span_hash": "b2ecec4ef4ba9a4a", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 20, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ergometer to exhaustion.\nIn these types of tests, in which athletes perform exercise\nat high intensity, there is a marked production and accu-\nmulation of lactate and hydrogen ions [108]. Consequently,\nmuscle pH will drop dramatically [109] and, the", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "P. Silva Oliveira et al.\nexercise seem to induce similar adaptations in the mecha-\nnisms involved in lactate production and removal, it is not\nsurprising that several studies using different exercise train-\ning intensities, such as threshold training [26, 100], train-\ning above the anaerobic threshold [101, 102], and POL [26,\n103], were all effective in improving the anaerobic threshold,\nwhich is in agreement with our findings that POL is similar\nto other TIDs regarding V/P at ­VT2/LT2.\nTT is a variable highly correlated with endurance perfor-\nmance [104]. In our study, there was no evidence of supe-\nriority of POL compared to other TIDs in the improvement\nof TT (p = 0.92). Nevertheless, the studies included in our\nmeta-analysis displayed a high variability in terms of TT\ndistances, ranging from 100 m [54] to 40 km [26], which\ncorrelate very differently with performance in aerobic activi-\nties, and therefore some of these results might not neces-\nsarily reflect adaptations of the aerobic metabolism. TT in\nendurance activities is highly dependent on VO2peak and,\nespecially for longer distances, on LT and running economy\n[87, 105–107]. Considering our findings that POL is similar\nto other TIDs regarding improvements in V/P at ­VT2/LT2\nand that POL is only able to marginally improve ­VO2peak,\nespecially for shorter duration interventions, it is not surpris-\ning that the results from our meta-analysis suggest that TT\ncan be simila", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ergometer to exhaustion.\nIn these types of tests, in which athletes perform exercise\nat high intensity, there is a marked production and accu-\nmulation of lactate and hydrogen ions [108]. Consequently,\nmuscle pH will drop dramatically [109] and, the"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0020_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f8570e938c18b3d8", "text_span_hash": "f8570e938c18b3d8", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 20, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "nduced by different exercise\nintensities, seem to be able to effectively improve TTE in\nendurance activities, explaining the absence of differences\nbetween POL and other TIDs regarding improvements in\nTTE identified in our meta-analysis.\nKnowing a pr", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "P. Silva Oliveira et al.\nexercise seem to induce similar adaptations in the mecha-\nnisms involved in lactate production and removal, it is not\nsurprising that several studies using different exercise train-\ning intensities, such as threshold training [26, 100], train-\ning above the anaerobic threshold [101, 102], and POL [26,\n103], were all effective in improving the anaerobic threshold,\nwhich is in agreement with our findings that POL is similar\nto other TIDs regarding V/P at ­VT2/LT2.\nTT is a variable highly correlated with endurance perfor-\nmance [104]. In our study, there was no evidence of supe-\nriority of POL compared to other TIDs in the improvement\nof TT (p = 0.92). Nevertheless, the studies included in our\nmeta-analysis displayed a high variability in terms of TT\ndistances, ranging from 100 m [54] to 40 km [26], which\ncorrelate very differently with performance in aerobic activi-\nties, and therefore some of these results might not neces-\nsarily reflect adaptations of the aerobic metabolism. TT in\nendurance activities is highly dependent on VO2peak and,\nespecially for longer distances, on LT and running economy\n[87, 105–107]. Considering our findings that POL is similar\nto other TIDs regarding improvements in V/P at ­VT2/LT2\nand that POL is only able to marginally improve ­VO2peak,\nespecially for shorter duration interventions, it is not surpris-\ning that the results from our meta-analysis suggest that TT\ncan be simila", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "nduced by different exercise\nintensities, seem to be able to effectively improve TTE in\nendurance activities, explaining the absence of differences\nbetween POL and other TIDs regarding improvements in\nTTE identified in our meta-analysis.\nKnowing a pr"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0020_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f62482fb9698f4ab", "text_span_hash": "f62482fb9698f4ab", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 20, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ur meta-analysis.\nKnowing a priori that the starting endurance perfor-\nmance level of the subjects could be a differentiating factor\nin the magnitude of the response to the training stimulus,\nwe carried out a sub-analysis of our variables of interest", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "P. Silva Oliveira et al.\nexercise seem to induce similar adaptations in the mecha-\nnisms involved in lactate production and removal, it is not\nsurprising that several studies using different exercise train-\ning intensities, such as threshold training [26, 100], train-\ning above the anaerobic threshold [101, 102], and POL [26,\n103], were all effective in improving the anaerobic threshold,\nwhich is in agreement with our findings that POL is similar\nto other TIDs regarding V/P at ­VT2/LT2.\nTT is a variable highly correlated with endurance perfor-\nmance [104]. In our study, there was no evidence of supe-\nriority of POL compared to other TIDs in the improvement\nof TT (p = 0.92). Nevertheless, the studies included in our\nmeta-analysis displayed a high variability in terms of TT\ndistances, ranging from 100 m [54] to 40 km [26], which\ncorrelate very differently with performance in aerobic activi-\nties, and therefore some of these results might not neces-\nsarily reflect adaptations of the aerobic metabolism. TT in\nendurance activities is highly dependent on VO2peak and,\nespecially for longer distances, on LT and running economy\n[87, 105–107]. Considering our findings that POL is similar\nto other TIDs regarding improvements in V/P at ­VT2/LT2\nand that POL is only able to marginally improve ­VO2peak,\nespecially for shorter duration interventions, it is not surpris-\ning that the results from our meta-analysis suggest that TT\ncan be simila", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ur meta-analysis.\nKnowing a priori that the starting endurance perfor-\nmance level of the subjects could be a differentiating factor\nin the magnitude of the response to the training stimulus,\nwe carried out a sub-analysis of our variables of interest"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0020_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "80220795bbb30784", "text_span_hash": "80220795bbb30784", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 20, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "s of our variables of interest\naccording to the initial endurance performance level of the\nsubjects (highly trained/national level vs. trained/devel-\nopmental). Our results showed that baseline performance\nlevel significantly influenced the effective", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "P. Silva Oliveira et al.\nexercise seem to induce similar adaptations in the mecha-\nnisms involved in lactate production and removal, it is not\nsurprising that several studies using different exercise train-\ning intensities, such as threshold training [26, 100], train-\ning above the anaerobic threshold [101, 102], and POL [26,\n103], were all effective in improving the anaerobic threshold,\nwhich is in agreement with our findings that POL is similar\nto other TIDs regarding V/P at ­VT2/LT2.\nTT is a variable highly correlated with endurance perfor-\nmance [104]. In our study, there was no evidence of supe-\nriority of POL compared to other TIDs in the improvement\nof TT (p = 0.92). Nevertheless, the studies included in our\nmeta-analysis displayed a high variability in terms of TT\ndistances, ranging from 100 m [54] to 40 km [26], which\ncorrelate very differently with performance in aerobic activi-\nties, and therefore some of these results might not neces-\nsarily reflect adaptations of the aerobic metabolism. TT in\nendurance activities is highly dependent on VO2peak and,\nespecially for longer distances, on LT and running economy\n[87, 105–107]. Considering our findings that POL is similar\nto other TIDs regarding improvements in V/P at ­VT2/LT2\nand that POL is only able to marginally improve ­VO2peak,\nespecially for shorter duration interventions, it is not surpris-\ning that the results from our meta-analysis suggest that TT\ncan be simila", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "s of our variables of interest\naccording to the initial endurance performance level of the\nsubjects (highly trained/national level vs. trained/devel-\nopmental). Our results showed that baseline performance\nlevel significantly influenced the effective"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0020_c0016", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "52c18d37163af135", "text_span_hash": "52c18d37163af135", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 20, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 16, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "antly influenced the effectiveness of endurance\ntraining type with only highly trained/national level athletes\nshowing higher improvements in ­VO2peak in response to\nPOL compared to other TIDs. This finding is in agreement\nwith previous studies showi", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "P. Silva Oliveira et al.\nexercise seem to induce similar adaptations in the mecha-\nnisms involved in lactate production and removal, it is not\nsurprising that several studies using different exercise train-\ning intensities, such as threshold training [26, 100], train-\ning above the anaerobic threshold [101, 102], and POL [26,\n103], were all effective in improving the anaerobic threshold,\nwhich is in agreement with our findings that POL is similar\nto other TIDs regarding V/P at ­VT2/LT2.\nTT is a variable highly correlated with endurance perfor-\nmance [104]. In our study, there was no evidence of supe-\nriority of POL compared to other TIDs in the improvement\nof TT (p = 0.92). Nevertheless, the studies included in our\nmeta-analysis displayed a high variability in terms of TT\ndistances, ranging from 100 m [54] to 40 km [26], which\ncorrelate very differently with performance in aerobic activi-\nties, and therefore some of these results might not neces-\nsarily reflect adaptations of the aerobic metabolism. TT in\nendurance activities is highly dependent on VO2peak and,\nespecially for longer distances, on LT and running economy\n[87, 105–107]. Considering our findings that POL is similar\nto other TIDs regarding improvements in V/P at ­VT2/LT2\nand that POL is only able to marginally improve ­VO2peak,\nespecially for shorter duration interventions, it is not surpris-\ning that the results from our meta-analysis suggest that TT\ncan be simila", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "antly influenced the effectiveness of endurance\ntraining type with only highly trained/national level athletes\nshowing higher improvements in ­VO2peak in response to\nPOL compared to other TIDs. This finding is in agreement\nwith previous studies showi"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0020_c0018", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0cfe8cb036d5da57", "text_span_hash": "0cfe8cb036d5da57", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 20, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 18, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "eless, studies includ-\ning highly trained athletes were also those that had a shorter\nduration (< 12 weeks). Therefore, it is not possible to dis-\nentangle whether the observed effect was due to training\nlevel or to the concomitant effect of interven", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "P. Silva Oliveira et al.\nexercise seem to induce similar adaptations in the mecha-\nnisms involved in lactate production and removal, it is not\nsurprising that several studies using different exercise train-\ning intensities, such as threshold training [26, 100], train-\ning above the anaerobic threshold [101, 102], and POL [26,\n103], were all effective in improving the anaerobic threshold,\nwhich is in agreement with our findings that POL is similar\nto other TIDs regarding V/P at ­VT2/LT2.\nTT is a variable highly correlated with endurance perfor-\nmance [104]. In our study, there was no evidence of supe-\nriority of POL compared to other TIDs in the improvement\nof TT (p = 0.92). Nevertheless, the studies included in our\nmeta-analysis displayed a high variability in terms of TT\ndistances, ranging from 100 m [54] to 40 km [26], which\ncorrelate very differently with performance in aerobic activi-\nties, and therefore some of these results might not neces-\nsarily reflect adaptations of the aerobic metabolism. TT in\nendurance activities is highly dependent on VO2peak and,\nespecially for longer distances, on LT and running economy\n[87, 105–107]. Considering our findings that POL is similar\nto other TIDs regarding improvements in V/P at ­VT2/LT2\nand that POL is only able to marginally improve ­VO2peak,\nespecially for shorter duration interventions, it is not surpris-\ning that the results from our meta-analysis suggest that TT\ncan be simila", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "eless, studies includ-\ning highly trained athletes were also those that had a shorter\nduration (< 12 weeks). Therefore, it is not possible to dis-\nentangle whether the observed effect was due to training\nlevel or to the concomitant effect of interven"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0020_c0020", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "83a174096b5ce34c", "text_span_hash": "83a174096b5ce34c", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 20, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 20, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "TID.\nAlthough the authors classified the training model as POL\nor PYR, without data on the %TID performed at each zone,\nit is problematic to robustly state which model was in fact\nfollowed. Another necessary criticism is the under-reporting\nof weekl", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "P. Silva Oliveira et al.\nexercise seem to induce similar adaptations in the mecha-\nnisms involved in lactate production and removal, it is not\nsurprising that several studies using different exercise train-\ning intensities, such as threshold training [26, 100], train-\ning above the anaerobic threshold [101, 102], and POL [26,\n103], were all effective in improving the anaerobic threshold,\nwhich is in agreement with our findings that POL is similar\nto other TIDs regarding V/P at ­VT2/LT2.\nTT is a variable highly correlated with endurance perfor-\nmance [104]. In our study, there was no evidence of supe-\nriority of POL compared to other TIDs in the improvement\nof TT (p = 0.92). Nevertheless, the studies included in our\nmeta-analysis displayed a high variability in terms of TT\ndistances, ranging from 100 m [54] to 40 km [26], which\ncorrelate very differently with performance in aerobic activi-\nties, and therefore some of these results might not neces-\nsarily reflect adaptations of the aerobic metabolism. TT in\nendurance activities is highly dependent on VO2peak and,\nespecially for longer distances, on LT and running economy\n[87, 105–107]. Considering our findings that POL is similar\nto other TIDs regarding improvements in V/P at ­VT2/LT2\nand that POL is only able to marginally improve ­VO2peak,\nespecially for shorter duration interventions, it is not surpris-\ning that the results from our meta-analysis suggest that TT\ncan be simila", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "TID.\nAlthough the authors classified the training model as POL\nor PYR, without data on the %TID performed at each zone,\nit is problematic to robustly state which model was in fact\nfollowed. Another necessary criticism is the under-reporting\nof weekl"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0020_c0021", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2dbb568aa048e958", "text_span_hash": "2dbb568aa048e958", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 20, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 21, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "s the under-reporting\nof weekly TRIMPs or variables such as volume, intensity,\nand frequency of the entire training program. Therefore, it is\npossible that groups might have differed not just in intensity\nbut also in other crucial variables that were", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "P. Silva Oliveira et al.\nexercise seem to induce similar adaptations in the mecha-\nnisms involved in lactate production and removal, it is not\nsurprising that several studies using different exercise train-\ning intensities, such as threshold training [26, 100], train-\ning above the anaerobic threshold [101, 102], and POL [26,\n103], were all effective in improving the anaerobic threshold,\nwhich is in agreement with our findings that POL is similar\nto other TIDs regarding V/P at ­VT2/LT2.\nTT is a variable highly correlated with endurance perfor-\nmance [104]. In our study, there was no evidence of supe-\nriority of POL compared to other TIDs in the improvement\nof TT (p = 0.92). Nevertheless, the studies included in our\nmeta-analysis displayed a high variability in terms of TT\ndistances, ranging from 100 m [54] to 40 km [26], which\ncorrelate very differently with performance in aerobic activi-\nties, and therefore some of these results might not neces-\nsarily reflect adaptations of the aerobic metabolism. TT in\nendurance activities is highly dependent on VO2peak and,\nespecially for longer distances, on LT and running economy\n[87, 105–107]. Considering our findings that POL is similar\nto other TIDs regarding improvements in V/P at ­VT2/LT2\nand that POL is only able to marginally improve ­VO2peak,\nespecially for shorter duration interventions, it is not surpris-\ning that the results from our meta-analysis suggest that TT\ncan be simila", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "s the under-reporting\nof weekly TRIMPs or variables such as volume, intensity,\nand frequency of the entire training program. Therefore, it is\npossible that groups might have differed not just in intensity\nbut also in other crucial variables that were"}
{"chunk_id": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095_p0020_c0022", "source_registry_id": "src_875d3f5b6fef", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "24ea34f21789c71f", "text_span_hash": "24ea34f21789c71f", "source_file": "Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Sports Medicine (2024) 542071–2095.pdf", "page": 20, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 22, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "er crucial variables that were not accounted\nfor. In addition, several studies lacked adequate description\nof the training program variables, namely weekly frequency,\ntype of sessions, and robust measures of volume and inten-\nsity. It is also notewor", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "P. Silva Oliveira et al.\nexercise seem to induce similar adaptations in the mecha-\nnisms involved in lactate production and removal, it is not\nsurprising that several studies using different exercise train-\ning intensities, such as threshold training [26, 100], train-\ning above the anaerobic threshold [101, 102], and POL [26,\n103], were all effective in improving the anaerobic threshold,\nwhich is in agreement with our findings that POL is similar\nto other TIDs regarding V/P at ­VT2/LT2.\nTT is a variable highly correlated with endurance perfor-\nmance [104]. In our study, there was no evidence of supe-\nriority of POL compared to other TIDs in the improvement\nof TT (p = 0.92). Nevertheless, the studies included in our\nmeta-analysis displayed a high variability in terms of TT\ndistances, ranging from 100 m [54] to 40 km [26], which\ncorrelate very differently with performance in aerobic activi-\nties, and therefore some of these results might not neces-\nsarily reflect adaptations of the aerobic metabolism. TT in\nendurance activities is highly dependent on VO2peak and,\nespecially for longer distances, on LT and running economy\n[87, 105–107]. Considering our findings that POL is similar\nto other TIDs regarding improvements in V/P at ­VT2/LT2\nand that POL is only able to marginally improve ­VO2peak,\nespecially for shorter duration interventions, it is not surpris-\ning that the results from our meta-analysis suggest that TT\ncan be simila", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "er crucial variables that were not accounted\nfor. In addition, several studies lacked adequate description\nof the training program variables, namely weekly frequency,\ntype of sessions, and robust measures of volume and inten-\nsity. It is also notewor"}
