{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0001_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b0f8b196fd21f4a9", "text_span_hash": "b0f8b196fd21f4a9", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": ", Salzburg,\ne-mail: thomas.stoeggl@sbg.ac.at\nEndurance athletes integrate four conditioning concepts in their training programs:\nhigh-volume training (HVT), “threshold-training” (THR), high-intensity interval training\n(HIIT) and a combination of thes", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH ARTICLE\nPolarized training has greater impact on key endurance\nvariables than threshold, high intensity, or high volume\nThomas Stöggl 1,2* and Billy Sperlich 3\n1 Department of Sport Science and Kinesiology, University of Salzburg, Salzburg, Austria\n2 Department of Health Sciences, Swedish Winter Sports Research Centre, Mid Sweden University, Östersund, Sweden\n3 Institute of Sport Science, University of Würzburg, Würzburg, Germany\nNiels H. Secher, University of\nNiels H. Secher, University of\nStefanos Volianitis, Aalborg\nThomas Stöggl, Department of\nSport Science and Kinesiology,\nUniversity of Salzburg, Schlossallee\n49, 5400 Hallein/Rif, Salzburg,\ne-mail: thomas.stoeggl@sbg.ac.at\nEndurance athletes integrate four conditioning concepts in their training programs:\nhigh-volume training (HVT), “threshold-training” (THR), high-intensity interval training\n(HIIT) and a combination of these aforementioned concepts known as polarized training\n(POL). The purpose of this study was to explore which of these four training concepts\nprovides the greatest response on key components of endurance performance in\nwell-trained endurance athletes.\nMethods: Forty eight runners, cyclists, triathletes, and cross-country skiers (peak oxygen\nuptake: (VO2peak): 62.6 ± 7.1 mL·min−1·kg−1) were randomly assigned to one of four\ngroups performing over 9 weeks. An incremental test, work economy and a VO2peak tests\nwere performed. Training inten", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": ", Salzburg,\ne-mail: thomas.stoeggl@sbg.ac.at\nEndurance athletes integrate four conditioning concepts in their training programs:\nhigh-volume training (HVT), “threshold-training” (THR), high-intensity interval training\n(HIIT) and a combination of thes"}
{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0001_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f38391b1cbb3c4fd", "text_span_hash": "f38391b1cbb3c4fd", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "IIT) and a combination of these aforementioned concepts known as polarized training\n(POL). The purpose of this study was to explore which of these four training concepts\nprovides the greatest response on key components of endurance performance in\nwel", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH ARTICLE\nPolarized training has greater impact on key endurance\nvariables than threshold, high intensity, or high volume\nThomas Stöggl 1,2* and Billy Sperlich 3\n1 Department of Sport Science and Kinesiology, University of Salzburg, Salzburg, Austria\n2 Department of Health Sciences, Swedish Winter Sports Research Centre, Mid Sweden University, Östersund, Sweden\n3 Institute of Sport Science, University of Würzburg, Würzburg, Germany\nNiels H. Secher, University of\nNiels H. Secher, University of\nStefanos Volianitis, Aalborg\nThomas Stöggl, Department of\nSport Science and Kinesiology,\nUniversity of Salzburg, Schlossallee\n49, 5400 Hallein/Rif, Salzburg,\ne-mail: thomas.stoeggl@sbg.ac.at\nEndurance athletes integrate four conditioning concepts in their training programs:\nhigh-volume training (HVT), “threshold-training” (THR), high-intensity interval training\n(HIIT) and a combination of these aforementioned concepts known as polarized training\n(POL). The purpose of this study was to explore which of these four training concepts\nprovides the greatest response on key components of endurance performance in\nwell-trained endurance athletes.\nMethods: Forty eight runners, cyclists, triathletes, and cross-country skiers (peak oxygen\nuptake: (VO2peak): 62.6 ± 7.1 mL·min−1·kg−1) were randomly assigned to one of four\ngroups performing over 9 weeks. An incremental test, work economy and a VO2peak tests\nwere performed. Training inten", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "IIT) and a combination of these aforementioned concepts known as polarized training\n(POL). The purpose of this study was to explore which of these four training concepts\nprovides the greatest response on key components of endurance performance in\nwel"}
{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0001_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "6af9419173a226e5", "text_span_hash": "6af9419173a226e5", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "f endurance performance in\nwell-trained endurance athletes.\nMethods: Forty eight runners, cyclists, triathletes, and cross-country skiers (peak oxygen\nuptake: (VO2peak): 62.6 ± 7.1 mL·min−1·kg−1) were randomly assigned to one of four\ngroups performin", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH ARTICLE\nPolarized training has greater impact on key endurance\nvariables than threshold, high intensity, or high volume\nThomas Stöggl 1,2* and Billy Sperlich 3\n1 Department of Sport Science and Kinesiology, University of Salzburg, Salzburg, Austria\n2 Department of Health Sciences, Swedish Winter Sports Research Centre, Mid Sweden University, Östersund, Sweden\n3 Institute of Sport Science, University of Würzburg, Würzburg, Germany\nNiels H. Secher, University of\nNiels H. Secher, University of\nStefanos Volianitis, Aalborg\nThomas Stöggl, Department of\nSport Science and Kinesiology,\nUniversity of Salzburg, Schlossallee\n49, 5400 Hallein/Rif, Salzburg,\ne-mail: thomas.stoeggl@sbg.ac.at\nEndurance athletes integrate four conditioning concepts in their training programs:\nhigh-volume training (HVT), “threshold-training” (THR), high-intensity interval training\n(HIIT) and a combination of these aforementioned concepts known as polarized training\n(POL). The purpose of this study was to explore which of these four training concepts\nprovides the greatest response on key components of endurance performance in\nwell-trained endurance athletes.\nMethods: Forty eight runners, cyclists, triathletes, and cross-country skiers (peak oxygen\nuptake: (VO2peak): 62.6 ± 7.1 mL·min−1·kg−1) were randomly assigned to one of four\ngroups performing over 9 weeks. An incremental test, work economy and a VO2peak tests\nwere performed. Training inten", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "f endurance performance in\nwell-trained endurance athletes.\nMethods: Forty eight runners, cyclists, triathletes, and cross-country skiers (peak oxygen\nuptake: (VO2peak): 62.6 ± 7.1 mL·min−1·kg−1) were randomly assigned to one of four\ngroups performin"}
{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0001_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ba81bd3d558d2905", "text_span_hash": "ba81bd3d558d2905", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "o one of four\ngroups performing over 9 weeks. An incremental test, work economy and a VO2peak tests\nwere performed. Training intensity was heart rate controlled.\nResults: POL demonstrated the greatest increase in VO2peak (+6.8 ml·min·kg−1 or 11.7%,\nv", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH ARTICLE\nPolarized training has greater impact on key endurance\nvariables than threshold, high intensity, or high volume\nThomas Stöggl 1,2* and Billy Sperlich 3\n1 Department of Sport Science and Kinesiology, University of Salzburg, Salzburg, Austria\n2 Department of Health Sciences, Swedish Winter Sports Research Centre, Mid Sweden University, Östersund, Sweden\n3 Institute of Sport Science, University of Würzburg, Würzburg, Germany\nNiels H. Secher, University of\nNiels H. Secher, University of\nStefanos Volianitis, Aalborg\nThomas Stöggl, Department of\nSport Science and Kinesiology,\nUniversity of Salzburg, Schlossallee\n49, 5400 Hallein/Rif, Salzburg,\ne-mail: thomas.stoeggl@sbg.ac.at\nEndurance athletes integrate four conditioning concepts in their training programs:\nhigh-volume training (HVT), “threshold-training” (THR), high-intensity interval training\n(HIIT) and a combination of these aforementioned concepts known as polarized training\n(POL). The purpose of this study was to explore which of these four training concepts\nprovides the greatest response on key components of endurance performance in\nwell-trained endurance athletes.\nMethods: Forty eight runners, cyclists, triathletes, and cross-country skiers (peak oxygen\nuptake: (VO2peak): 62.6 ± 7.1 mL·min−1·kg−1) were randomly assigned to one of four\ngroups performing over 9 weeks. An incremental test, work economy and a VO2peak tests\nwere performed. Training inten", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "o one of four\ngroups performing over 9 weeks. An incremental test, work economy and a VO2peak tests\nwere performed. Training intensity was heart rate controlled.\nResults: POL demonstrated the greatest increase in VO2peak (+6.8 ml·min·kg−1 or 11.7%,\nv"}
{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0001_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9b1ef98029746138", "text_span_hash": "9b1ef98029746138", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "h no changes in the other groups. With the exception of\nslight improvements in work economy in THR, both HVT and THR had no further effects\non measured variables of endurance performance (P > 0.05).\nConclusion: POL resulted in the greatest improvemen", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH ARTICLE\nPolarized training has greater impact on key endurance\nvariables than threshold, high intensity, or high volume\nThomas Stöggl 1,2* and Billy Sperlich 3\n1 Department of Sport Science and Kinesiology, University of Salzburg, Salzburg, Austria\n2 Department of Health Sciences, Swedish Winter Sports Research Centre, Mid Sweden University, Östersund, Sweden\n3 Institute of Sport Science, University of Würzburg, Würzburg, Germany\nNiels H. Secher, University of\nNiels H. Secher, University of\nStefanos Volianitis, Aalborg\nThomas Stöggl, Department of\nSport Science and Kinesiology,\nUniversity of Salzburg, Schlossallee\n49, 5400 Hallein/Rif, Salzburg,\ne-mail: thomas.stoeggl@sbg.ac.at\nEndurance athletes integrate four conditioning concepts in their training programs:\nhigh-volume training (HVT), “threshold-training” (THR), high-intensity interval training\n(HIIT) and a combination of these aforementioned concepts known as polarized training\n(POL). The purpose of this study was to explore which of these four training concepts\nprovides the greatest response on key components of endurance performance in\nwell-trained endurance athletes.\nMethods: Forty eight runners, cyclists, triathletes, and cross-country skiers (peak oxygen\nuptake: (VO2peak): 62.6 ± 7.1 mL·min−1·kg−1) were randomly assigned to one of four\ngroups performing over 9 weeks. An incremental test, work economy and a VO2peak tests\nwere performed. Training inten", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "h no changes in the other groups. With the exception of\nslight improvements in work economy in THR, both HVT and THR had no further effects\non measured variables of endurance performance (P > 0.05).\nConclusion: POL resulted in the greatest improvemen"}
{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0001_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "208eaec2819bd0a2", "text_span_hash": "208eaec2819bd0a2", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ted in the greatest improvements in most key variables of\nendurance performance in well-trained endurance athletes. THR or HVT did not lead to\nfurther improvements in performance related variables.\nKeywords: lactate threshold, peak power, peak oxygen", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH ARTICLE\nPolarized training has greater impact on key endurance\nvariables than threshold, high intensity, or high volume\nThomas Stöggl 1,2* and Billy Sperlich 3\n1 Department of Sport Science and Kinesiology, University of Salzburg, Salzburg, Austria\n2 Department of Health Sciences, Swedish Winter Sports Research Centre, Mid Sweden University, Östersund, Sweden\n3 Institute of Sport Science, University of Würzburg, Würzburg, Germany\nNiels H. Secher, University of\nNiels H. Secher, University of\nStefanos Volianitis, Aalborg\nThomas Stöggl, Department of\nSport Science and Kinesiology,\nUniversity of Salzburg, Schlossallee\n49, 5400 Hallein/Rif, Salzburg,\ne-mail: thomas.stoeggl@sbg.ac.at\nEndurance athletes integrate four conditioning concepts in their training programs:\nhigh-volume training (HVT), “threshold-training” (THR), high-intensity interval training\n(HIIT) and a combination of these aforementioned concepts known as polarized training\n(POL). The purpose of this study was to explore which of these four training concepts\nprovides the greatest response on key components of endurance performance in\nwell-trained endurance athletes.\nMethods: Forty eight runners, cyclists, triathletes, and cross-country skiers (peak oxygen\nuptake: (VO2peak): 62.6 ± 7.1 mL·min−1·kg−1) were randomly assigned to one of four\ngroups performing over 9 weeks. An incremental test, work economy and a VO2peak tests\nwere performed. Training inten", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ted in the greatest improvements in most key variables of\nendurance performance in well-trained endurance athletes. THR or HVT did not lead to\nfurther improvements in performance related variables.\nKeywords: lactate threshold, peak power, peak oxygen"}
{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0001_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e64a2a5cdfdc4d68", "text_span_hash": "e64a2a5cdfdc4d68", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "shold, peak power, peak oxygen uptake, time to exhaustion, work economy\nAthletes participating in endurance sports such as running,\ncycling, and cross-country skiing integrate four conditioning\nconcepts into their training program to maximize athleti", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH ARTICLE\nPolarized training has greater impact on key endurance\nvariables than threshold, high intensity, or high volume\nThomas Stöggl 1,2* and Billy Sperlich 3\n1 Department of Sport Science and Kinesiology, University of Salzburg, Salzburg, Austria\n2 Department of Health Sciences, Swedish Winter Sports Research Centre, Mid Sweden University, Östersund, Sweden\n3 Institute of Sport Science, University of Würzburg, Würzburg, Germany\nNiels H. Secher, University of\nNiels H. Secher, University of\nStefanos Volianitis, Aalborg\nThomas Stöggl, Department of\nSport Science and Kinesiology,\nUniversity of Salzburg, Schlossallee\n49, 5400 Hallein/Rif, Salzburg,\ne-mail: thomas.stoeggl@sbg.ac.at\nEndurance athletes integrate four conditioning concepts in their training programs:\nhigh-volume training (HVT), “threshold-training” (THR), high-intensity interval training\n(HIIT) and a combination of these aforementioned concepts known as polarized training\n(POL). The purpose of this study was to explore which of these four training concepts\nprovides the greatest response on key components of endurance performance in\nwell-trained endurance athletes.\nMethods: Forty eight runners, cyclists, triathletes, and cross-country skiers (peak oxygen\nuptake: (VO2peak): 62.6 ± 7.1 mL·min−1·kg−1) were randomly assigned to one of four\ngroups performing over 9 weeks. An incremental test, work economy and a VO2peak tests\nwere performed. Training inten", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "shold, peak power, peak oxygen uptake, time to exhaustion, work economy\nAthletes participating in endurance sports such as running,\ncycling, and cross-country skiing integrate four conditioning\nconcepts into their training program to maximize athleti"}
{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0001_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "76240666bf36af6a", "text_span_hash": "76240666bf36af6a", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ng program to maximize athletic perfor-\nmance. The ﬁrst conditioning concept is prolonged high-volume\nlow-intensity exercise (HVT). The second is training at or near the\nlactate threshold (THR); third is low-volume high-intensity inter-\nval training", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH ARTICLE\nPolarized training has greater impact on key endurance\nvariables than threshold, high intensity, or high volume\nThomas Stöggl 1,2* and Billy Sperlich 3\n1 Department of Sport Science and Kinesiology, University of Salzburg, Salzburg, Austria\n2 Department of Health Sciences, Swedish Winter Sports Research Centre, Mid Sweden University, Östersund, Sweden\n3 Institute of Sport Science, University of Würzburg, Würzburg, Germany\nNiels H. Secher, University of\nNiels H. Secher, University of\nStefanos Volianitis, Aalborg\nThomas Stöggl, Department of\nSport Science and Kinesiology,\nUniversity of Salzburg, Schlossallee\n49, 5400 Hallein/Rif, Salzburg,\ne-mail: thomas.stoeggl@sbg.ac.at\nEndurance athletes integrate four conditioning concepts in their training programs:\nhigh-volume training (HVT), “threshold-training” (THR), high-intensity interval training\n(HIIT) and a combination of these aforementioned concepts known as polarized training\n(POL). The purpose of this study was to explore which of these four training concepts\nprovides the greatest response on key components of endurance performance in\nwell-trained endurance athletes.\nMethods: Forty eight runners, cyclists, triathletes, and cross-country skiers (peak oxygen\nuptake: (VO2peak): 62.6 ± 7.1 mL·min−1·kg−1) were randomly assigned to one of four\ngroups performing over 9 weeks. An incremental test, work economy and a VO2peak tests\nwere performed. Training inten", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ng program to maximize athletic perfor-\nmance. The ﬁrst conditioning concept is prolonged high-volume\nlow-intensity exercise (HVT). The second is training at or near the\nlactate threshold (THR); third is low-volume high-intensity inter-\nval training"}
{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0001_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b2c95d1595366e0b", "text_span_hash": "b2c95d1595366e0b", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "intensity inter-\nval training (HIIT) and the fourth concept is a combination of the\naforementioned concepts known as “polarized” training (POL).\nThere is a debate as to which of these training concepts may be\nsuperior in maximizing adaptations and pe", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH ARTICLE\nPolarized training has greater impact on key endurance\nvariables than threshold, high intensity, or high volume\nThomas Stöggl 1,2* and Billy Sperlich 3\n1 Department of Sport Science and Kinesiology, University of Salzburg, Salzburg, Austria\n2 Department of Health Sciences, Swedish Winter Sports Research Centre, Mid Sweden University, Östersund, Sweden\n3 Institute of Sport Science, University of Würzburg, Würzburg, Germany\nNiels H. Secher, University of\nNiels H. Secher, University of\nStefanos Volianitis, Aalborg\nThomas Stöggl, Department of\nSport Science and Kinesiology,\nUniversity of Salzburg, Schlossallee\n49, 5400 Hallein/Rif, Salzburg,\ne-mail: thomas.stoeggl@sbg.ac.at\nEndurance athletes integrate four conditioning concepts in their training programs:\nhigh-volume training (HVT), “threshold-training” (THR), high-intensity interval training\n(HIIT) and a combination of these aforementioned concepts known as polarized training\n(POL). The purpose of this study was to explore which of these four training concepts\nprovides the greatest response on key components of endurance performance in\nwell-trained endurance athletes.\nMethods: Forty eight runners, cyclists, triathletes, and cross-country skiers (peak oxygen\nuptake: (VO2peak): 62.6 ± 7.1 mL·min−1·kg−1) were randomly assigned to one of four\ngroups performing over 9 weeks. An incremental test, work economy and a VO2peak tests\nwere performed. Training inten", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "intensity inter-\nval training (HIIT) and the fourth concept is a combination of the\naforementioned concepts known as “polarized” training (POL).\nThere is a debate as to which of these training concepts may be\nsuperior in maximizing adaptations and pe"}
{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0001_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "55fab65d9fb62135", "text_span_hash": "55fab65d9fb62135", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "maximizing adaptations and performance.\nHVT executed with low (LOW) intensity [approximately\n65–75% of peak oxygen uptake (VO2peak) <80% of peak heart\nrate (HRpeak) or <2 mmol·L−1 blood lactate (Laursen and\nJenkins, 2002; Seiler and Kjerland, 2006)]", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH ARTICLE\nPolarized training has greater impact on key endurance\nvariables than threshold, high intensity, or high volume\nThomas Stöggl 1,2* and Billy Sperlich 3\n1 Department of Sport Science and Kinesiology, University of Salzburg, Salzburg, Austria\n2 Department of Health Sciences, Swedish Winter Sports Research Centre, Mid Sweden University, Östersund, Sweden\n3 Institute of Sport Science, University of Würzburg, Würzburg, Germany\nNiels H. Secher, University of\nNiels H. Secher, University of\nStefanos Volianitis, Aalborg\nThomas Stöggl, Department of\nSport Science and Kinesiology,\nUniversity of Salzburg, Schlossallee\n49, 5400 Hallein/Rif, Salzburg,\ne-mail: thomas.stoeggl@sbg.ac.at\nEndurance athletes integrate four conditioning concepts in their training programs:\nhigh-volume training (HVT), “threshold-training” (THR), high-intensity interval training\n(HIIT) and a combination of these aforementioned concepts known as polarized training\n(POL). The purpose of this study was to explore which of these four training concepts\nprovides the greatest response on key components of endurance performance in\nwell-trained endurance athletes.\nMethods: Forty eight runners, cyclists, triathletes, and cross-country skiers (peak oxygen\nuptake: (VO2peak): 62.6 ± 7.1 mL·min−1·kg−1) were randomly assigned to one of four\ngroups performing over 9 weeks. An incremental test, work economy and a VO2peak tests\nwere performed. Training inten", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "maximizing adaptations and performance.\nHVT executed with low (LOW) intensity [approximately\n65–75% of peak oxygen uptake (VO2peak) <80% of peak heart\nrate (HRpeak) or <2 mmol·L−1 blood lactate (Laursen and\nJenkins, 2002; Seiler and Kjerland, 2006)]"}
{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0001_c0017", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "66be21d9eb785b2e", "text_span_hash": "66be21d9eb785b2e", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 17, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "at improvements in athletic performance\nand related key variables of endurance (e.g., time to exhaustion,\ntime trial performance, VO2peak, maximal and submaximal run-\nning speed, running economy) in both trained and untrained\nindividuals (Laursen and", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH ARTICLE\nPolarized training has greater impact on key endurance\nvariables than threshold, high intensity, or high volume\nThomas Stöggl 1,2* and Billy Sperlich 3\n1 Department of Sport Science and Kinesiology, University of Salzburg, Salzburg, Austria\n2 Department of Health Sciences, Swedish Winter Sports Research Centre, Mid Sweden University, Östersund, Sweden\n3 Institute of Sport Science, University of Würzburg, Würzburg, Germany\nNiels H. Secher, University of\nNiels H. Secher, University of\nStefanos Volianitis, Aalborg\nThomas Stöggl, Department of\nSport Science and Kinesiology,\nUniversity of Salzburg, Schlossallee\n49, 5400 Hallein/Rif, Salzburg,\ne-mail: thomas.stoeggl@sbg.ac.at\nEndurance athletes integrate four conditioning concepts in their training programs:\nhigh-volume training (HVT), “threshold-training” (THR), high-intensity interval training\n(HIIT) and a combination of these aforementioned concepts known as polarized training\n(POL). The purpose of this study was to explore which of these four training concepts\nprovides the greatest response on key components of endurance performance in\nwell-trained endurance athletes.\nMethods: Forty eight runners, cyclists, triathletes, and cross-country skiers (peak oxygen\nuptake: (VO2peak): 62.6 ± 7.1 mL·min−1·kg−1) were randomly assigned to one of four\ngroups performing over 9 weeks. An incremental test, work economy and a VO2peak tests\nwere performed. Training inten", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "at improvements in athletic performance\nand related key variables of endurance (e.g., time to exhaustion,\ntime trial performance, VO2peak, maximal and submaximal run-\nning speed, running economy) in both trained and untrained\nindividuals (Laursen and"}
{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0001_c0019", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2b2443a52539ffff", "text_span_hash": "2b2443a52539ffff", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 19, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "condensed 2 week block of 10–13 ses-\nsions of HIIT led to a 7% increase in VO2peak (Stølen et al.,\nTraining at or close to the lactate threshold (LT) (Faude et al.,\n2009), referred to as “threshold training,” improves endurance\nperformance, particul", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH ARTICLE\nPolarized training has greater impact on key endurance\nvariables than threshold, high intensity, or high volume\nThomas Stöggl 1,2* and Billy Sperlich 3\n1 Department of Sport Science and Kinesiology, University of Salzburg, Salzburg, Austria\n2 Department of Health Sciences, Swedish Winter Sports Research Centre, Mid Sweden University, Östersund, Sweden\n3 Institute of Sport Science, University of Würzburg, Würzburg, Germany\nNiels H. Secher, University of\nNiels H. Secher, University of\nStefanos Volianitis, Aalborg\nThomas Stöggl, Department of\nSport Science and Kinesiology,\nUniversity of Salzburg, Schlossallee\n49, 5400 Hallein/Rif, Salzburg,\ne-mail: thomas.stoeggl@sbg.ac.at\nEndurance athletes integrate four conditioning concepts in their training programs:\nhigh-volume training (HVT), “threshold-training” (THR), high-intensity interval training\n(HIIT) and a combination of these aforementioned concepts known as polarized training\n(POL). The purpose of this study was to explore which of these four training concepts\nprovides the greatest response on key components of endurance performance in\nwell-trained endurance athletes.\nMethods: Forty eight runners, cyclists, triathletes, and cross-country skiers (peak oxygen\nuptake: (VO2peak): 62.6 ± 7.1 mL·min−1·kg−1) were randomly assigned to one of four\ngroups performing over 9 weeks. An incremental test, work economy and a VO2peak tests\nwere performed. Training inten", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "condensed 2 week block of 10–13 ses-\nsions of HIIT led to a 7% increase in VO2peak (Stølen et al.,\nTraining at or close to the lactate threshold (LT) (Faude et al.,\n2009), referred to as “threshold training,” improves endurance\nperformance, particul"}
{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0001_c0020", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2d2ba701ab891acb", "text_span_hash": "2d2ba701ab891acb", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 20, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ndurance\nperformance, particularly in untrained participants (Denis et al.,\n1984; Londeree, 1997). However, Norwegian world-class sprint\ncross-country skiers demonstrated greater training volume close\nto the LT when compared to national-level skiers", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH ARTICLE\nPolarized training has greater impact on key endurance\nvariables than threshold, high intensity, or high volume\nThomas Stöggl 1,2* and Billy Sperlich 3\n1 Department of Sport Science and Kinesiology, University of Salzburg, Salzburg, Austria\n2 Department of Health Sciences, Swedish Winter Sports Research Centre, Mid Sweden University, Östersund, Sweden\n3 Institute of Sport Science, University of Würzburg, Würzburg, Germany\nNiels H. Secher, University of\nNiels H. Secher, University of\nStefanos Volianitis, Aalborg\nThomas Stöggl, Department of\nSport Science and Kinesiology,\nUniversity of Salzburg, Schlossallee\n49, 5400 Hallein/Rif, Salzburg,\ne-mail: thomas.stoeggl@sbg.ac.at\nEndurance athletes integrate four conditioning concepts in their training programs:\nhigh-volume training (HVT), “threshold-training” (THR), high-intensity interval training\n(HIIT) and a combination of these aforementioned concepts known as polarized training\n(POL). The purpose of this study was to explore which of these four training concepts\nprovides the greatest response on key components of endurance performance in\nwell-trained endurance athletes.\nMethods: Forty eight runners, cyclists, triathletes, and cross-country skiers (peak oxygen\nuptake: (VO2peak): 62.6 ± 7.1 mL·min−1·kg−1) were randomly assigned to one of four\ngroups performing over 9 weeks. An incremental test, work economy and a VO2peak tests\nwere performed. Training inten", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ndurance\nperformance, particularly in untrained participants (Denis et al.,\n1984; Londeree, 1997). However, Norwegian world-class sprint\ncross-country skiers demonstrated greater training volume close\nto the LT when compared to national-level skiers"}
{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0002_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9cef985263234bbc", "text_span_hash": "9cef985263234bbc", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Endurance training concepts\nimprovements in running speed at lactate threshold and perfor-\nmance in a 20-min run when exercising at an intensity elicit-\ning 3–4 mmol·L−1 lactate compared with low intensity training\n(<3–4 mmol·L−1) were found (Evertse", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Endurance training concepts\nimprovements in running speed at lactate threshold and perfor-\nmance in a 20-min run when exercising at an intensity elicit-\ning 3–4 mmol·L−1 lactate compared with low intensity training\n(<3–4 mmol·L−1) were found (Evertsen et al., 2001). In contrast,\nexperimental and correlational data from well-trained athletes\nsuggest that training time close to LT may be ineffective, or even\ncounterproductive (Esteve-Lanao et al., 2007; Guellich and Seiler,\nRetrospective analysis of the intensity, duration and fre-\nquency of the training load of international-level cross-country\nskiers (Seiler and Kjerland, 2006), rowers (Steinacker et al.,\n1998), cyclists (Schumacher and Mueller, 2002), and runners\n(Billat et al., 2001; Esteve-Lanao et al., 2005) revealed that elite\nendurance athletes completed most of their yearly training ses-\nsions at either intensities below (∼75% of total training volume)\nor well above (∼15–20% of total training volume) their LT. Six\nweeks of cycling using POL resulted in greater systemic adap-\ntation in already well-trained athletes when compared to THR\n(Neal et al., 2013). However, no study has investigated the POL\nconcept in well-trained endurance athletes to determine whether\nthis concept may be superior to the aforementioned training\nIn many endurance sports, ﬁve key variables have been used\nas a benchmark to compare athletic performance in and between\nendurance athletes: (i) VO2peak ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Endurance training concepts\nimprovements in running speed at lactate threshold and perfor-\nmance in a 20-min run when exercising at an intensity elicit-\ning 3–4 mmol·L−1 lactate compared with low intensity training\n(<3–4 mmol·L−1) were found (Evertse"}
{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0002_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3513d4fa8d2b55cb", "text_span_hash": "3513d4fa8d2b55cb", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "mmol·L−1) were found (Evertsen et al., 2001). In contrast,\nexperimental and correlational data from well-trained athletes\nsuggest that training time close to LT may be ineffective, or even\ncounterproductive (Esteve-Lanao et al., 2007; Guellich and S", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Endurance training concepts\nimprovements in running speed at lactate threshold and perfor-\nmance in a 20-min run when exercising at an intensity elicit-\ning 3–4 mmol·L−1 lactate compared with low intensity training\n(<3–4 mmol·L−1) were found (Evertsen et al., 2001). In contrast,\nexperimental and correlational data from well-trained athletes\nsuggest that training time close to LT may be ineffective, or even\ncounterproductive (Esteve-Lanao et al., 2007; Guellich and Seiler,\nRetrospective analysis of the intensity, duration and fre-\nquency of the training load of international-level cross-country\nskiers (Seiler and Kjerland, 2006), rowers (Steinacker et al.,\n1998), cyclists (Schumacher and Mueller, 2002), and runners\n(Billat et al., 2001; Esteve-Lanao et al., 2005) revealed that elite\nendurance athletes completed most of their yearly training ses-\nsions at either intensities below (∼75% of total training volume)\nor well above (∼15–20% of total training volume) their LT. Six\nweeks of cycling using POL resulted in greater systemic adap-\ntation in already well-trained athletes when compared to THR\n(Neal et al., 2013). However, no study has investigated the POL\nconcept in well-trained endurance athletes to determine whether\nthis concept may be superior to the aforementioned training\nIn many endurance sports, ﬁve key variables have been used\nas a benchmark to compare athletic performance in and between\nendurance athletes: (i) VO2peak ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "mmol·L−1) were found (Evertsen et al., 2001). In contrast,\nexperimental and correlational data from well-trained athletes\nsuggest that training time close to LT may be ineffective, or even\ncounterproductive (Esteve-Lanao et al., 2007; Guellich and S"}
{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0002_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b8cc2cfdc4286066", "text_span_hash": "b8cc2cfdc4286066", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "o et al., 2007; Guellich and Seiler,\nRetrospective analysis of the intensity, duration and fre-\nquency of the training load of international-level cross-country\nskiers (Seiler and Kjerland, 2006), rowers (Steinacker et al.,\n1998), cyclists (Schumache", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Endurance training concepts\nimprovements in running speed at lactate threshold and perfor-\nmance in a 20-min run when exercising at an intensity elicit-\ning 3–4 mmol·L−1 lactate compared with low intensity training\n(<3–4 mmol·L−1) were found (Evertsen et al., 2001). In contrast,\nexperimental and correlational data from well-trained athletes\nsuggest that training time close to LT may be ineffective, or even\ncounterproductive (Esteve-Lanao et al., 2007; Guellich and Seiler,\nRetrospective analysis of the intensity, duration and fre-\nquency of the training load of international-level cross-country\nskiers (Seiler and Kjerland, 2006), rowers (Steinacker et al.,\n1998), cyclists (Schumacher and Mueller, 2002), and runners\n(Billat et al., 2001; Esteve-Lanao et al., 2005) revealed that elite\nendurance athletes completed most of their yearly training ses-\nsions at either intensities below (∼75% of total training volume)\nor well above (∼15–20% of total training volume) their LT. Six\nweeks of cycling using POL resulted in greater systemic adap-\ntation in already well-trained athletes when compared to THR\n(Neal et al., 2013). However, no study has investigated the POL\nconcept in well-trained endurance athletes to determine whether\nthis concept may be superior to the aforementioned training\nIn many endurance sports, ﬁve key variables have been used\nas a benchmark to compare athletic performance in and between\nendurance athletes: (i) VO2peak ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "o et al., 2007; Guellich and Seiler,\nRetrospective analysis of the intensity, duration and fre-\nquency of the training load of international-level cross-country\nskiers (Seiler and Kjerland, 2006), rowers (Steinacker et al.,\n1998), cyclists (Schumache"}
{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0002_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "293eaed7fbfef83d", "text_span_hash": "293eaed7fbfef83d", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "l.,\n1998), cyclists (Schumacher and Mueller, 2002), and runners\n(Billat et al., 2001; Esteve-Lanao et al., 2005) revealed that elite\nendurance athletes completed most of their yearly training ses-\nsions at either intensities below (∼75% of total trai", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Endurance training concepts\nimprovements in running speed at lactate threshold and perfor-\nmance in a 20-min run when exercising at an intensity elicit-\ning 3–4 mmol·L−1 lactate compared with low intensity training\n(<3–4 mmol·L−1) were found (Evertsen et al., 2001). In contrast,\nexperimental and correlational data from well-trained athletes\nsuggest that training time close to LT may be ineffective, or even\ncounterproductive (Esteve-Lanao et al., 2007; Guellich and Seiler,\nRetrospective analysis of the intensity, duration and fre-\nquency of the training load of international-level cross-country\nskiers (Seiler and Kjerland, 2006), rowers (Steinacker et al.,\n1998), cyclists (Schumacher and Mueller, 2002), and runners\n(Billat et al., 2001; Esteve-Lanao et al., 2005) revealed that elite\nendurance athletes completed most of their yearly training ses-\nsions at either intensities below (∼75% of total training volume)\nor well above (∼15–20% of total training volume) their LT. Six\nweeks of cycling using POL resulted in greater systemic adap-\ntation in already well-trained athletes when compared to THR\n(Neal et al., 2013). However, no study has investigated the POL\nconcept in well-trained endurance athletes to determine whether\nthis concept may be superior to the aforementioned training\nIn many endurance sports, ﬁve key variables have been used\nas a benchmark to compare athletic performance in and between\nendurance athletes: (i) VO2peak ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "l.,\n1998), cyclists (Schumacher and Mueller, 2002), and runners\n(Billat et al., 2001; Esteve-Lanao et al., 2005) revealed that elite\nendurance athletes completed most of their yearly training ses-\nsions at either intensities below (∼75% of total trai"}
{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0002_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "019532bb2d9228b2", "text_span_hash": "019532bb2d9228b2", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ties below (∼75% of total training volume)\nor well above (∼15–20% of total training volume) their LT. Six\nweeks of cycling using POL resulted in greater systemic adap-\ntation in already well-trained athletes when compared to THR\n(Neal et al., 2013).", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Endurance training concepts\nimprovements in running speed at lactate threshold and perfor-\nmance in a 20-min run when exercising at an intensity elicit-\ning 3–4 mmol·L−1 lactate compared with low intensity training\n(<3–4 mmol·L−1) were found (Evertsen et al., 2001). In contrast,\nexperimental and correlational data from well-trained athletes\nsuggest that training time close to LT may be ineffective, or even\ncounterproductive (Esteve-Lanao et al., 2007; Guellich and Seiler,\nRetrospective analysis of the intensity, duration and fre-\nquency of the training load of international-level cross-country\nskiers (Seiler and Kjerland, 2006), rowers (Steinacker et al.,\n1998), cyclists (Schumacher and Mueller, 2002), and runners\n(Billat et al., 2001; Esteve-Lanao et al., 2005) revealed that elite\nendurance athletes completed most of their yearly training ses-\nsions at either intensities below (∼75% of total training volume)\nor well above (∼15–20% of total training volume) their LT. Six\nweeks of cycling using POL resulted in greater systemic adap-\ntation in already well-trained athletes when compared to THR\n(Neal et al., 2013). However, no study has investigated the POL\nconcept in well-trained endurance athletes to determine whether\nthis concept may be superior to the aforementioned training\nIn many endurance sports, ﬁve key variables have been used\nas a benchmark to compare athletic performance in and between\nendurance athletes: (i) VO2peak ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ties below (∼75% of total training volume)\nor well above (∼15–20% of total training volume) their LT. Six\nweeks of cycling using POL resulted in greater systemic adap-\ntation in already well-trained athletes when compared to THR\n(Neal et al., 2013)."}
{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0002_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c72397413eb5e142", "text_span_hash": "c72397413eb5e142", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "d to THR\n(Neal et al., 2013). However, no study has investigated the POL\nconcept in well-trained endurance athletes to determine whether\nthis concept may be superior to the aforementioned training\nIn many endurance sports, ﬁve key variables have been", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Endurance training concepts\nimprovements in running speed at lactate threshold and perfor-\nmance in a 20-min run when exercising at an intensity elicit-\ning 3–4 mmol·L−1 lactate compared with low intensity training\n(<3–4 mmol·L−1) were found (Evertsen et al., 2001). In contrast,\nexperimental and correlational data from well-trained athletes\nsuggest that training time close to LT may be ineffective, or even\ncounterproductive (Esteve-Lanao et al., 2007; Guellich and Seiler,\nRetrospective analysis of the intensity, duration and fre-\nquency of the training load of international-level cross-country\nskiers (Seiler and Kjerland, 2006), rowers (Steinacker et al.,\n1998), cyclists (Schumacher and Mueller, 2002), and runners\n(Billat et al., 2001; Esteve-Lanao et al., 2005) revealed that elite\nendurance athletes completed most of their yearly training ses-\nsions at either intensities below (∼75% of total training volume)\nor well above (∼15–20% of total training volume) their LT. Six\nweeks of cycling using POL resulted in greater systemic adap-\ntation in already well-trained athletes when compared to THR\n(Neal et al., 2013). However, no study has investigated the POL\nconcept in well-trained endurance athletes to determine whether\nthis concept may be superior to the aforementioned training\nIn many endurance sports, ﬁve key variables have been used\nas a benchmark to compare athletic performance in and between\nendurance athletes: (i) VO2peak ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "d to THR\n(Neal et al., 2013). However, no study has investigated the POL\nconcept in well-trained endurance athletes to determine whether\nthis concept may be superior to the aforementioned training\nIn many endurance sports, ﬁve key variables have been"}
{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0002_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e847758d9aff0286", "text_span_hash": "e847758d9aff0286", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "s, ﬁve key variables have been used\nas a benchmark to compare athletic performance in and between\nendurance athletes: (i) VO2peak (Bassett and Howley, 2000); (ii)\nvelocity/power output at the lactate threshold (V/PLT) (Bassett\nand Howley, 2000; Midgl", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Endurance training concepts\nimprovements in running speed at lactate threshold and perfor-\nmance in a 20-min run when exercising at an intensity elicit-\ning 3–4 mmol·L−1 lactate compared with low intensity training\n(<3–4 mmol·L−1) were found (Evertsen et al., 2001). In contrast,\nexperimental and correlational data from well-trained athletes\nsuggest that training time close to LT may be ineffective, or even\ncounterproductive (Esteve-Lanao et al., 2007; Guellich and Seiler,\nRetrospective analysis of the intensity, duration and fre-\nquency of the training load of international-level cross-country\nskiers (Seiler and Kjerland, 2006), rowers (Steinacker et al.,\n1998), cyclists (Schumacher and Mueller, 2002), and runners\n(Billat et al., 2001; Esteve-Lanao et al., 2005) revealed that elite\nendurance athletes completed most of their yearly training ses-\nsions at either intensities below (∼75% of total training volume)\nor well above (∼15–20% of total training volume) their LT. Six\nweeks of cycling using POL resulted in greater systemic adap-\ntation in already well-trained athletes when compared to THR\n(Neal et al., 2013). However, no study has investigated the POL\nconcept in well-trained endurance athletes to determine whether\nthis concept may be superior to the aforementioned training\nIn many endurance sports, ﬁve key variables have been used\nas a benchmark to compare athletic performance in and between\nendurance athletes: (i) VO2peak ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "s, ﬁve key variables have been used\nas a benchmark to compare athletic performance in and between\nendurance athletes: (i) VO2peak (Bassett and Howley, 2000); (ii)\nvelocity/power output at the lactate threshold (V/PLT) (Bassett\nand Howley, 2000; Midgl"}
{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0002_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "24020edc6895d309", "text_span_hash": "24020edc6895d309", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "assett\nand Howley, 2000; Midgley et al., 2007; Faude et al., 2009); (iii)\nwork economy (Di Prampero et al., 1986; Helgerud et al., 2001);\n(iv) peak running velocity or power output (V/Ppeak) (Midgley\net al., 2007); and (v) time to exhaustion (TTE) (L", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Endurance training concepts\nimprovements in running speed at lactate threshold and perfor-\nmance in a 20-min run when exercising at an intensity elicit-\ning 3–4 mmol·L−1 lactate compared with low intensity training\n(<3–4 mmol·L−1) were found (Evertsen et al., 2001). In contrast,\nexperimental and correlational data from well-trained athletes\nsuggest that training time close to LT may be ineffective, or even\ncounterproductive (Esteve-Lanao et al., 2007; Guellich and Seiler,\nRetrospective analysis of the intensity, duration and fre-\nquency of the training load of international-level cross-country\nskiers (Seiler and Kjerland, 2006), rowers (Steinacker et al.,\n1998), cyclists (Schumacher and Mueller, 2002), and runners\n(Billat et al., 2001; Esteve-Lanao et al., 2005) revealed that elite\nendurance athletes completed most of their yearly training ses-\nsions at either intensities below (∼75% of total training volume)\nor well above (∼15–20% of total training volume) their LT. Six\nweeks of cycling using POL resulted in greater systemic adap-\ntation in already well-trained athletes when compared to THR\n(Neal et al., 2013). However, no study has investigated the POL\nconcept in well-trained endurance athletes to determine whether\nthis concept may be superior to the aforementioned training\nIn many endurance sports, ﬁve key variables have been used\nas a benchmark to compare athletic performance in and between\nendurance athletes: (i) VO2peak ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "assett\nand Howley, 2000; Midgley et al., 2007; Faude et al., 2009); (iii)\nwork economy (Di Prampero et al., 1986; Helgerud et al., 2001);\n(iv) peak running velocity or power output (V/Ppeak) (Midgley\net al., 2007); and (v) time to exhaustion (TTE) (L"}
{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0002_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "45a1bbb8f069f09c", "text_span_hash": "45a1bbb8f069f09c", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "v) time to exhaustion (TTE) (Laursen and\nJenkins, 2002). The aim of this study was to compare the effects\nof four training concepts (HVT vs. THR vs. HIIT vs. POL) on the\naforementioned key variables of endurance performance in well-\ntrained athletes.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Endurance training concepts\nimprovements in running speed at lactate threshold and perfor-\nmance in a 20-min run when exercising at an intensity elicit-\ning 3–4 mmol·L−1 lactate compared with low intensity training\n(<3–4 mmol·L−1) were found (Evertsen et al., 2001). In contrast,\nexperimental and correlational data from well-trained athletes\nsuggest that training time close to LT may be ineffective, or even\ncounterproductive (Esteve-Lanao et al., 2007; Guellich and Seiler,\nRetrospective analysis of the intensity, duration and fre-\nquency of the training load of international-level cross-country\nskiers (Seiler and Kjerland, 2006), rowers (Steinacker et al.,\n1998), cyclists (Schumacher and Mueller, 2002), and runners\n(Billat et al., 2001; Esteve-Lanao et al., 2005) revealed that elite\nendurance athletes completed most of their yearly training ses-\nsions at either intensities below (∼75% of total training volume)\nor well above (∼15–20% of total training volume) their LT. Six\nweeks of cycling using POL resulted in greater systemic adap-\ntation in already well-trained athletes when compared to THR\n(Neal et al., 2013). However, no study has investigated the POL\nconcept in well-trained endurance athletes to determine whether\nthis concept may be superior to the aforementioned training\nIn many endurance sports, ﬁve key variables have been used\nas a benchmark to compare athletic performance in and between\nendurance athletes: (i) VO2peak ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "v) time to exhaustion (TTE) (Laursen and\nJenkins, 2002). The aim of this study was to compare the effects\nof four training concepts (HVT vs. THR vs. HIIT vs. POL) on the\naforementioned key variables of endurance performance in well-\ntrained athletes."}
{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0002_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "07d0706d52e83ea2", "text_span_hash": "07d0706d52e83ea2", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "nce in well-\ntrained athletes. We hypothesized that the POL and HIIT group\nwould lead to superior improvements compared with HVT and\nMATERIALS AND METHODS\nForty eight healthy competitive endurance athletes who partici-\npated in either cross-country s", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Endurance training concepts\nimprovements in running speed at lactate threshold and perfor-\nmance in a 20-min run when exercising at an intensity elicit-\ning 3–4 mmol·L−1 lactate compared with low intensity training\n(<3–4 mmol·L−1) were found (Evertsen et al., 2001). In contrast,\nexperimental and correlational data from well-trained athletes\nsuggest that training time close to LT may be ineffective, or even\ncounterproductive (Esteve-Lanao et al., 2007; Guellich and Seiler,\nRetrospective analysis of the intensity, duration and fre-\nquency of the training load of international-level cross-country\nskiers (Seiler and Kjerland, 2006), rowers (Steinacker et al.,\n1998), cyclists (Schumacher and Mueller, 2002), and runners\n(Billat et al., 2001; Esteve-Lanao et al., 2005) revealed that elite\nendurance athletes completed most of their yearly training ses-\nsions at either intensities below (∼75% of total training volume)\nor well above (∼15–20% of total training volume) their LT. Six\nweeks of cycling using POL resulted in greater systemic adap-\ntation in already well-trained athletes when compared to THR\n(Neal et al., 2013). However, no study has investigated the POL\nconcept in well-trained endurance athletes to determine whether\nthis concept may be superior to the aforementioned training\nIn many endurance sports, ﬁve key variables have been used\nas a benchmark to compare athletic performance in and between\nendurance athletes: (i) VO2peak ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "nce in well-\ntrained athletes. We hypothesized that the POL and HIIT group\nwould lead to superior improvements compared with HVT and\nMATERIALS AND METHODS\nForty eight healthy competitive endurance athletes who partici-\npated in either cross-country s"}
