{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0002_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7929cdd7b1e14ee7", "text_span_hash": "7929cdd7b1e14ee7", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "strian cross-country skiing national team (n = 8), run-\nning (n = 21), triathlon (n = 4) or cycling (n = 15) teams during\nor since the year before the current study. Retrospective analysis\nof training protocols over 6 months prior to the study reveal", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Endurance training concepts\nimprovements in running speed at lactate threshold and perfor-\nmance in a 20-min run when exercising at an intensity elicit-\ning 3–4 mmol·L−1 lactate compared with low intensity training\n(<3–4 mmol·L−1) were found (Evertsen et al., 2001). In contrast,\nexperimental and correlational data from well-trained athletes\nsuggest that training time close to LT may be ineffective, or even\ncounterproductive (Esteve-Lanao et al., 2007; Guellich and Seiler,\nRetrospective analysis of the intensity, duration and fre-\nquency of the training load of international-level cross-country\nskiers (Seiler and Kjerland, 2006), rowers (Steinacker et al.,\n1998), cyclists (Schumacher and Mueller, 2002), and runners\n(Billat et al., 2001; Esteve-Lanao et al., 2005) revealed that elite\nendurance athletes completed most of their yearly training ses-\nsions at either intensities below (∼75% of total training volume)\nor well above (∼15–20% of total training volume) their LT. Six\nweeks of cycling using POL resulted in greater systemic adap-\ntation in already well-trained athletes when compared to THR\n(Neal et al., 2013). However, no study has investigated the POL\nconcept in well-trained endurance athletes to determine whether\nthis concept may be superior to the aforementioned training\nIn many endurance sports, ﬁve key variables have been used\nas a benchmark to compare athletic performance in and between\nendurance athletes: (i) VO2peak ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "strian cross-country skiing national team (n = 8), run-\nning (n = 21), triathlon (n = 4) or cycling (n = 15) teams during\nor since the year before the current study. Retrospective analysis\nof training protocols over 6 months prior to the study reveal"}
{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0002_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4df0d6fd93ac5515", "text_span_hash": "4df0d6fd93ac5515", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "nths prior to the study revealed\nthat none of the participants had regularly executed HIIT. All had\nfollowed a HVT training protocol with a maximum of two THR\ntraining sessions per week.\nBased on the participants’ baseline VO2peak and training mode\n(", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Endurance training concepts\nimprovements in running speed at lactate threshold and perfor-\nmance in a 20-min run when exercising at an intensity elicit-\ning 3–4 mmol·L−1 lactate compared with low intensity training\n(<3–4 mmol·L−1) were found (Evertsen et al., 2001). In contrast,\nexperimental and correlational data from well-trained athletes\nsuggest that training time close to LT may be ineffective, or even\ncounterproductive (Esteve-Lanao et al., 2007; Guellich and Seiler,\nRetrospective analysis of the intensity, duration and fre-\nquency of the training load of international-level cross-country\nskiers (Seiler and Kjerland, 2006), rowers (Steinacker et al.,\n1998), cyclists (Schumacher and Mueller, 2002), and runners\n(Billat et al., 2001; Esteve-Lanao et al., 2005) revealed that elite\nendurance athletes completed most of their yearly training ses-\nsions at either intensities below (∼75% of total training volume)\nor well above (∼15–20% of total training volume) their LT. Six\nweeks of cycling using POL resulted in greater systemic adap-\ntation in already well-trained athletes when compared to THR\n(Neal et al., 2013). However, no study has investigated the POL\nconcept in well-trained endurance athletes to determine whether\nthis concept may be superior to the aforementioned training\nIn many endurance sports, ﬁve key variables have been used\nas a benchmark to compare athletic performance in and between\nendurance athletes: (i) VO2peak ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "nths prior to the study revealed\nthat none of the participants had regularly executed HIIT. All had\nfollowed a HVT training protocol with a maximum of two THR\ntraining sessions per week.\nBased on the participants’ baseline VO2peak and training mode\n("}
{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0002_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "58539bb711d9be47", "text_span_hash": "58539bb711d9be47", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ne VO2peak and training mode\n(running or cycling), all athletes were randomized into HIIT,\nHVT, THR or POL. At baseline, the four groups were not sta-\ntistically different with regard to age, height, body mass or\nVO2peak. During an initial visit, stu", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Endurance training concepts\nimprovements in running speed at lactate threshold and perfor-\nmance in a 20-min run when exercising at an intensity elicit-\ning 3–4 mmol·L−1 lactate compared with low intensity training\n(<3–4 mmol·L−1) were found (Evertsen et al., 2001). In contrast,\nexperimental and correlational data from well-trained athletes\nsuggest that training time close to LT may be ineffective, or even\ncounterproductive (Esteve-Lanao et al., 2007; Guellich and Seiler,\nRetrospective analysis of the intensity, duration and fre-\nquency of the training load of international-level cross-country\nskiers (Seiler and Kjerland, 2006), rowers (Steinacker et al.,\n1998), cyclists (Schumacher and Mueller, 2002), and runners\n(Billat et al., 2001; Esteve-Lanao et al., 2005) revealed that elite\nendurance athletes completed most of their yearly training ses-\nsions at either intensities below (∼75% of total training volume)\nor well above (∼15–20% of total training volume) their LT. Six\nweeks of cycling using POL resulted in greater systemic adap-\ntation in already well-trained athletes when compared to THR\n(Neal et al., 2013). However, no study has investigated the POL\nconcept in well-trained endurance athletes to determine whether\nthis concept may be superior to the aforementioned training\nIn many endurance sports, ﬁve key variables have been used\nas a benchmark to compare athletic performance in and between\nendurance athletes: (i) VO2peak ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ne VO2peak and training mode\n(running or cycling), all athletes were randomized into HIIT,\nHVT, THR or POL. At baseline, the four groups were not sta-\ntistically different with regard to age, height, body mass or\nVO2peak. During an initial visit, stu"}
{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0002_c0017", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b96a8ee6d462f2e2", "text_span_hash": "b96a8ee6d462f2e2", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 17, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "was conducted in accor-\ndance with the Declaration of Helsinki.\nThe intervention lasted 9 weeks plus 2 days of pre- and post-\ntesting. All athletes (n = 15 cyclists; n = 3 triathletes) engaging\nin cycling training trained with their own bike and comp", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Endurance training concepts\nimprovements in running speed at lactate threshold and perfor-\nmance in a 20-min run when exercising at an intensity elicit-\ning 3–4 mmol·L−1 lactate compared with low intensity training\n(<3–4 mmol·L−1) were found (Evertsen et al., 2001). In contrast,\nexperimental and correlational data from well-trained athletes\nsuggest that training time close to LT may be ineffective, or even\ncounterproductive (Esteve-Lanao et al., 2007; Guellich and Seiler,\nRetrospective analysis of the intensity, duration and fre-\nquency of the training load of international-level cross-country\nskiers (Seiler and Kjerland, 2006), rowers (Steinacker et al.,\n1998), cyclists (Schumacher and Mueller, 2002), and runners\n(Billat et al., 2001; Esteve-Lanao et al., 2005) revealed that elite\nendurance athletes completed most of their yearly training ses-\nsions at either intensities below (∼75% of total training volume)\nor well above (∼15–20% of total training volume) their LT. Six\nweeks of cycling using POL resulted in greater systemic adap-\ntation in already well-trained athletes when compared to THR\n(Neal et al., 2013). However, no study has investigated the POL\nconcept in well-trained endurance athletes to determine whether\nthis concept may be superior to the aforementioned training\nIn many endurance sports, ﬁve key variables have been used\nas a benchmark to compare athletic performance in and between\nendurance athletes: (i) VO2peak ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "was conducted in accor-\ndance with the Declaration of Helsinki.\nThe intervention lasted 9 weeks plus 2 days of pre- and post-\ntesting. All athletes (n = 15 cyclists; n = 3 triathletes) engaging\nin cycling training trained with their own bike and comp"}
