ratory\ndata were averaged every 30 s. VO2peak was achieved if three of\nthe four following criteria were met: (1) plateau in VO2, i.e., an\nincrease < 1.0 mL·min−1·kg−1 despite an increase in velocity or\npower output; (2) respiratory exchange ratio >1.1; (3) HR ± 5%\nof age predicted HRpeak; and (4) pea", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ite an increase in velocity or\npower output; (2) respiratory exchange ratio >1.1; (3) HR ± 5%\nof age predicted HRpeak; and (4) peak blood lactate (LApeak) >\n6 mmol·L−1 after exercise. Reliability analysis of The VO2peak test\n(n = 18) revealed ICC val"}
{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0004_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "a3627bcac688c794", "text_span_hash": "a3627bcac688c794", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "SD. Two-Way 2 × 4 repeated-measures ANOVA (2 times: pre–\npost, 4 groups) to test for global differences between pre- and\npost-intervention, the four training groups and the interaction\neffect between both factors was applied. When a signiﬁcant\nmain", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Endurance training concepts\nwas calibrated before each test using a lactate standard sample\nof 12 mmol·L−1. Results within a range of ± 0.1 mmol·L−1 were\nOne day after the incremental tests, all athletes completed a\ncombined work economy and VO2peak ramp protocol to deter-\nmine their submaximal and peak VO2 (VO2submax and VO2peak)\n(TTE). First, the intensity for running was set at 8 km·h−1 (incli-\nnation: 5%) on the treadmill, and for cycling at 200 W with\na cadence of >80 rpm for 10 min to determine VO2submax and\nHRsubmax for this intensity. The mean VO2 and HR during the\nlast 5 min of these tests were used for statistical purposes. The\nintensity was then increased every 30 s by 0.5 km·h−1 (inclina-\ntion: 10%) on the treadmill or 15 W on the cycle ergometer until\nexhaustion. The overall time for the ramp test was deﬁned as time\nto exhaustion (TTE). VO2 was measured with an open circuit\nbreath-by-breath spirograph (nSpire, Zan 600 USB, Oberthulba,\nGermany), which was calibrated prior to each test using high pre-\ncision gas (15.8% O2, 5% O2 in N; Praxair, Düsseldorf, Germany)\nand a 1L syringe (nSpire, Oberthulba, Germany). All respiratory\ndata were averaged every 30 s. VO2peak was achieved if three of\nthe four following criteria were met: (1) plateau in VO2, i.e., an\nincrease < 1.0 mL·min−1·kg−1 despite an increase in velocity or\npower output; (2) respiratory exchange ratio >1.1; (3) HR ± 5%\nof age predicted HRpeak; and (4) pea", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "SD. Two-Way 2 × 4 repeated-measures ANOVA (2 times: pre–\npost, 4 groups) to test for global differences between pre- and\npost-intervention, the four training groups and the interaction\neffect between both factors was applied. When a signiﬁcant\nmain"}
{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0004_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1b72320d27bd0dc8", "text_span_hash": "1b72320d27bd0dc8", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "plied. When a signiﬁcant\nmain effect over time was observed, paired t-tests within each\ngroup were conducted. Based on the different units of peak\npower/velocity and power/velocity at 2 and 4 mmol·L−1 blood\nlactate in the incremental and VO2peak test", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Endurance training concepts\nwas calibrated before each test using a lactate standard sample\nof 12 mmol·L−1. Results within a range of ± 0.1 mmol·L−1 were\nOne day after the incremental tests, all athletes completed a\ncombined work economy and VO2peak ramp protocol to deter-\nmine their submaximal and peak VO2 (VO2submax and VO2peak)\n(TTE). First, the intensity for running was set at 8 km·h−1 (incli-\nnation: 5%) on the treadmill, and for cycling at 200 W with\na cadence of >80 rpm for 10 min to determine VO2submax and\nHRsubmax for this intensity. The mean VO2 and HR during the\nlast 5 min of these tests were used for statistical purposes. The\nintensity was then increased every 30 s by 0.5 km·h−1 (inclina-\ntion: 10%) on the treadmill or 15 W on the cycle ergometer until\nexhaustion. The overall time for the ramp test was deﬁned as time\nto exhaustion (TTE). VO2 was measured with an open circuit\nbreath-by-breath spirograph (nSpire, Zan 600 USB, Oberthulba,\nGermany), which was calibrated prior to each test using high pre-\ncision gas (15.8% O2, 5% O2 in N; Praxair, Düsseldorf, Germany)\nand a 1L syringe (nSpire, Oberthulba, Germany). All respiratory\ndata were averaged every 30 s. VO2peak was achieved if three of\nthe four following criteria were met: (1) plateau in VO2, i.e., an\nincrease < 1.0 mL·min−1·kg−1 despite an increase in velocity or\npower output; (2) respiratory exchange ratio >1.1; (3) HR ± 5%\nof age predicted HRpeak; and (4) pea", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "plied. When a signiﬁcant\nmain effect over time was observed, paired t-tests within each\ngroup were conducted. Based on the different units of peak\npower/velocity and power/velocity at 2 and 4 mmol·L−1 blood\nlactate in the incremental and VO2peak test"}
{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0004_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0e267eb342e4e8f9", "text_span_hash": "0e267eb342e4e8f9", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "t Corporation, Redmond, WA, USA) were\nused for statistical analysis.\nForty-one participants completed the 9 week training protocol,\nfulﬁlling more than 95% of the training program and staying\nwithin the given HR zones. Seven subjects (2 in HIIT, 1 in", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Endurance training concepts\nwas calibrated before each test using a lactate standard sample\nof 12 mmol·L−1. Results within a range of ± 0.1 mmol·L−1 were\nOne day after the incremental tests, all athletes completed a\ncombined work economy and VO2peak ramp protocol to deter-\nmine their submaximal and peak VO2 (VO2submax and VO2peak)\n(TTE). First, the intensity for running was set at 8 km·h−1 (incli-\nnation: 5%) on the treadmill, and for cycling at 200 W with\na cadence of >80 rpm for 10 min to determine VO2submax and\nHRsubmax for this intensity. The mean VO2 and HR during the\nlast 5 min of these tests were used for statistical purposes. The\nintensity was then increased every 30 s by 0.5 km·h−1 (inclina-\ntion: 10%) on the treadmill or 15 W on the cycle ergometer until\nexhaustion. The overall time for the ramp test was deﬁned as time\nto exhaustion (TTE). VO2 was measured with an open circuit\nbreath-by-breath spirograph (nSpire, Zan 600 USB, Oberthulba,\nGermany), which was calibrated prior to each test using high pre-\ncision gas (15.8% O2, 5% O2 in N; Praxair, Düsseldorf, Germany)\nand a 1L syringe (nSpire, Oberthulba, Germany). All respiratory\ndata were averaged every 30 s. VO2peak was achieved if three of\nthe four following criteria were met: (1) plateau in VO2, i.e., an\nincrease < 1.0 mL·min−1·kg−1 despite an increase in velocity or\npower output; (2) respiratory exchange ratio >1.1; (3) HR ± 5%\nof age predicted HRpeak; and (4) pea", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "t Corporation, Redmond, WA, USA) were\nused for statistical analysis.\nForty-one participants completed the 9 week training protocol,\nfulﬁlling more than 95% of the training program and staying\nwithin the given HR zones. Seven subjects (2 in HIIT, 1 in"}
