ratory\ndata were averaged every 30 s. VO2peak was achieved if three of\nthe four following criteria were met: (1) plateau in VO2, i.e., an\nincrease < 1.0 mL·min−1·kg−1 despite an increase in velocity or\npower output; (2) respiratory exchange ratio >1.1; (3) HR ± 5%\nof age predicted HRpeak; and (4) pea", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "± 6.8 kg, post: 70.7 ± 6.5 kg, P < 0.01) with no signiﬁcant\nchange in the HVT, THR or POL groups. The reduction in\nbody mass after HIIT was greater compared to other training\ninterventions (P < 0.001).\nPercent changes in variables from pre- to post-"}
{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0004_c0018", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "6298e1a031826c91", "text_span_hash": "6298e1a031826c91", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 18, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "n variables from pre- to post-training\nramp, work economy, and incremental tests are presented in\nTable 2. POL demonstrated the greatest increase in VO2peak\nTable 1 | The distribution of volume and training intensity within the 9 weeks training inter", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Endurance training concepts\nwas calibrated before each test using a lactate standard sample\nof 12 mmol·L−1. Results within a range of ± 0.1 mmol·L−1 were\nOne day after the incremental tests, all athletes completed a\ncombined work economy and VO2peak ramp protocol to deter-\nmine their submaximal and peak VO2 (VO2submax and VO2peak)\n(TTE). First, the intensity for running was set at 8 km·h−1 (incli-\nnation: 5%) on the treadmill, and for cycling at 200 W with\na cadence of >80 rpm for 10 min to determine VO2submax and\nHRsubmax for this intensity. The mean VO2 and HR during the\nlast 5 min of these tests were used for statistical purposes. The\nintensity was then increased every 30 s by 0.5 km·h−1 (inclina-\ntion: 10%) on the treadmill or 15 W on the cycle ergometer until\nexhaustion. The overall time for the ramp test was deﬁned as time\nto exhaustion (TTE). VO2 was measured with an open circuit\nbreath-by-breath spirograph (nSpire, Zan 600 USB, Oberthulba,\nGermany), which was calibrated prior to each test using high pre-\ncision gas (15.8% O2, 5% O2 in N; Praxair, Düsseldorf, Germany)\nand a 1L syringe (nSpire, Oberthulba, Germany). All respiratory\ndata were averaged every 30 s. VO2peak was achieved if three of\nthe four following criteria were met: (1) plateau in VO2, i.e., an\nincrease < 1.0 mL·min−1·kg−1 despite an increase in velocity or\npower output; (2) respiratory exchange ratio >1.1; (3) HR ± 5%\nof age predicted HRpeak; and (4) pea", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "n variables from pre- to post-training\nramp, work economy, and incremental tests are presented in\nTable 2. POL demonstrated the greatest increase in VO2peak\nTable 1 | The distribution of volume and training intensity within the 9 weeks training inter"}
{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0004_c0019", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "318343cbe354d2e0", "text_span_hash": "318343cbe354d2e0", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 19, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "hin the 9 weeks training intervention (excluding strength training).\nAmount of training at low\nAmount of training at lactate\nAmount of training at high\nThe values presented are means ± SD. F- and P-values were obtained by One-Way ANOVA (4 training gr", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Endurance training concepts\nwas calibrated before each test using a lactate standard sample\nof 12 mmol·L−1. Results within a range of ± 0.1 mmol·L−1 were\nOne day after the incremental tests, all athletes completed a\ncombined work economy and VO2peak ramp protocol to deter-\nmine their submaximal and peak VO2 (VO2submax and VO2peak)\n(TTE). First, the intensity for running was set at 8 km·h−1 (incli-\nnation: 5%) on the treadmill, and for cycling at 200 W with\na cadence of >80 rpm for 10 min to determine VO2submax and\nHRsubmax for this intensity. The mean VO2 and HR during the\nlast 5 min of these tests were used for statistical purposes. The\nintensity was then increased every 30 s by 0.5 km·h−1 (inclina-\ntion: 10%) on the treadmill or 15 W on the cycle ergometer until\nexhaustion. The overall time for the ramp test was deﬁned as time\nto exhaustion (TTE). VO2 was measured with an open circuit\nbreath-by-breath spirograph (nSpire, Zan 600 USB, Oberthulba,\nGermany), which was calibrated prior to each test using high pre-\ncision gas (15.8% O2, 5% O2 in N; Praxair, Düsseldorf, Germany)\nand a 1L syringe (nSpire, Oberthulba, Germany). All respiratory\ndata were averaged every 30 s. VO2peak was achieved if three of\nthe four following criteria were met: (1) plateau in VO2, i.e., an\nincrease < 1.0 mL·min−1·kg−1 despite an increase in velocity or\npower output; (2) respiratory exchange ratio >1.1; (3) HR ± 5%\nof age predicted HRpeak; and (4) pea", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "hin the 9 weeks training intervention (excluding strength training).\nAmount of training at low\nAmount of training at lactate\nAmount of training at high\nThe values presented are means ± SD. F- and P-values were obtained by One-Way ANOVA (4 training gr"}
{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0004_c0020", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "a40a85d9b7999450", "text_span_hash": "a40a85d9b7999450", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 20, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "y One-Way ANOVA (4 training groups). POL, polarized training group; HIIT, High intensity\ninterval training group; THR, threshold training group; HVT, high volume training group.\n*Different from all other groups.\n†Different from training group “POL.”", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Endurance training concepts\nwas calibrated before each test using a lactate standard sample\nof 12 mmol·L−1. Results within a range of ± 0.1 mmol·L−1 were\nOne day after the incremental tests, all athletes completed a\ncombined work economy and VO2peak ramp protocol to deter-\nmine their submaximal and peak VO2 (VO2submax and VO2peak)\n(TTE). First, the intensity for running was set at 8 km·h−1 (incli-\nnation: 5%) on the treadmill, and for cycling at 200 W with\na cadence of >80 rpm for 10 min to determine VO2submax and\nHRsubmax for this intensity. The mean VO2 and HR during the\nlast 5 min of these tests were used for statistical purposes. The\nintensity was then increased every 30 s by 0.5 km·h−1 (inclina-\ntion: 10%) on the treadmill or 15 W on the cycle ergometer until\nexhaustion. The overall time for the ramp test was deﬁned as time\nto exhaustion (TTE). VO2 was measured with an open circuit\nbreath-by-breath spirograph (nSpire, Zan 600 USB, Oberthulba,\nGermany), which was calibrated prior to each test using high pre-\ncision gas (15.8% O2, 5% O2 in N; Praxair, Düsseldorf, Germany)\nand a 1L syringe (nSpire, Oberthulba, Germany). All respiratory\ndata were averaged every 30 s. VO2peak was achieved if three of\nthe four following criteria were met: (1) plateau in VO2, i.e., an\nincrease < 1.0 mL·min−1·kg−1 despite an increase in velocity or\npower output; (2) respiratory exchange ratio >1.1; (3) HR ± 5%\nof age predicted HRpeak; and (4) pea", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "y One-Way ANOVA (4 training groups). POL, polarized training group; HIIT, High intensity\ninterval training group; THR, threshold training group; HVT, high volume training group.\n*Different from all other groups.\n†Different from training group “POL.”"}
{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0004_c0021", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "21227b2fc62fa848", "text_span_hash": "21227b2fc62fa848", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 21, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "nt from training group “POL.”\n‡Different from training group “HIIT.”\n$Different from training group “THR.”\n§Different from training group “HVT.”\naMain effect between groups.n.s., not signiﬁcant.\nFrontiers in Physiology | Exercise Physiology\nFebruary", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Endurance training concepts\nwas calibrated before each test using a lactate standard sample\nof 12 mmol·L−1. Results within a range of ± 0.1 mmol·L−1 were\nOne day after the incremental tests, all athletes completed a\ncombined work economy and VO2peak ramp protocol to deter-\nmine their submaximal and peak VO2 (VO2submax and VO2peak)\n(TTE). First, the intensity for running was set at 8 km·h−1 (incli-\nnation: 5%) on the treadmill, and for cycling at 200 W with\na cadence of >80 rpm for 10 min to determine VO2submax and\nHRsubmax for this intensity. The mean VO2 and HR during the\nlast 5 min of these tests were used for statistical purposes. The\nintensity was then increased every 30 s by 0.5 km·h−1 (inclina-\ntion: 10%) on the treadmill or 15 W on the cycle ergometer until\nexhaustion. The overall time for the ramp test was deﬁned as time\nto exhaustion (TTE). VO2 was measured with an open circuit\nbreath-by-breath spirograph (nSpire, Zan 600 USB, Oberthulba,\nGermany), which was calibrated prior to each test using high pre-\ncision gas (15.8% O2, 5% O2 in N; Praxair, Düsseldorf, Germany)\nand a 1L syringe (nSpire, Oberthulba, Germany). All respiratory\ndata were averaged every 30 s. VO2peak was achieved if three of\nthe four following criteria were met: (1) plateau in VO2, i.e., an\nincrease < 1.0 mL·min−1·kg−1 despite an increase in velocity or\npower output; (2) respiratory exchange ratio >1.1; (3) HR ± 5%\nof age predicted HRpeak; and (4) pea", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "nt from training group “POL.”\n‡Different from training group “HIIT.”\n$Different from training group “THR.”\n§Different from training group “HVT.”\naMain effect between groups.n.s., not signiﬁcant.\nFrontiers in Physiology | Exercise Physiology\nFebruary"}
{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0005_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4a97f6bc95fae52e", "text_span_hash": "4a97f6bc95fae52e", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Endurance training concepts\nTable 2 | Changes in physiological variables from pre- to post-training.\nFebruary 2014 | Volume 5 | Article 33 | 5", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Endurance training concepts\nTable 2 | Changes in physiological variables from pre- to post-training.\nFebruary 2014 | Volume 5 | Article 33 | 5", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Endurance training concepts\nTable 2 | Changes in physiological variables from pre- to post-training.\nFebruary 2014 | Volume 5 | Article 33 | 5"}
{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0006_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8397a01ee8d43cae", "text_span_hash": "8397a01ee8d43cae", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Endurance training concepts\nThe values presented are means ± SD. F and P values were obtained by Two-Way ANOVA (2 × 4: time × training group) with repeated measures. POL, polarized training group; HIIT, High intensity interval training\ngroup; THR, th", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Endurance training concepts\nThe values presented are means ± SD. F and P values were obtained by Two-Way ANOVA (2 × 4: time × training group) with repeated measures. POL, polarized training group; HIIT, High intensity interval training\ngroup; THR, threshold training group; HVT, high volume training group; VO2, oxygen uptake; HR, heart rate; LApeak, peak blood lactate concentration; *p < 0.05; **p < 0.01; ***p < 0.001 signiﬁcant difference\nwithin groups from pre- to post-training. †p < 0.05; †††p < 0.001 signiﬁcant different from POL training group.‡p < 0.05 signiﬁcant different from HIIT training group. aMain effect between pre- and post-test.\nbMain effect between training groups. ctime × training group interactive effect.\n0.05). The change in VO2peak in POL was higher compared to\nincreased in POL by 10.4 ± 7.9% (P < 0.001), which was greater\ncompared with the other training concepts (HIIT and HVT P <\n0.05, THR P < 0.001). No changes from pre to post and no dif-\nferences between training groups with respect to HRpeak, LApeak,\nand HR2 & 4 were detected (P > 0.05). Work economy increased\nfollowing HIIT (−6.7 ± 4.4% decrease in HR, P < 0.01) and\nTHR (−2.7 ± 1.0% decrease in HR, P < 0.05) with no signif-\nicant differences between the other concepts. Work economy\nexpressed as percent of VO2peak was only improved after POL\n(−4.8 ± 7.6%, P < 0.05) with no signiﬁcant differences between\nthe other training groups.\nThe changes in TTE, V", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Endurance training concepts\nThe values presented are means ± SD. F and P values were obtained by Two-Way ANOVA (2 × 4: time × training group) with repeated measures. POL, polarized training group; HIIT, High intensity interval training\ngroup; THR, th"}
