{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0007_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "07c025dca7a150a9", "text_span_hash": "07c025dca7a150a9", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "that the participants of\nthis study mainly used HVT prior to this experiment, the HVT\nmodel might not have provided an adequate stimulus for further\nThe advantage of HIIT compared to HVT lies in a shorter\nperiod of training time for similar muscular", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Endurance training concepts\nTable 3 | Per cent changes in velocity (V) and power (P) and at various lactate thresholds as well as peak velocity and power.\nThe values presented are means ± SD. F and P values were obtained by One-Way ANOVA (4 training groups) calculated over the per cent differences between pre-\nto post-training. POL, polarized training group; HIIT, High intensity interval training group; THR, threshold training group; HVT, high volume training group; TTE, time\nto exhaustion during the ramp test; V/P2, velocity or power at 2 mmol·L−1; V/P4, velocity or power at 4 mmol·L−1; V/Ppeak, peak velocity or power in the incremental\ntest; *p < 0.05; **p < 0.01; ***p < 0.001 signiﬁcant difference within groups from pre- to post-training.\n†p < 0.05; ††p < 0.01 signiﬁcant different from POL training group.\n‡p < 0.05signiﬁcant different from HIIT training group.\naMain effect between groups.\n1998; Billat et al., 2001; Schumacher and Mueller, 2002; Seiler\nand Kjerland, 2006; Esteve-Lanao et al., 2007). In these studies,\nit was demonstrated that endurance athletes perform approxi-\nmately 75% of their yearly training program either below or well\nabove (∼15–20%) the LT, but little at the LT. In the current study,\nPOL mimicked this distribution (LOW = 68%, LTP = 6%, HIGH\n= 26%). Only the study of Neal et al. (2013) demonstrated that\n6 weeks of POL resulted in greater systemic adaptation in trained\ncyclists when compared to THR, hen", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "that the participants of\nthis study mainly used HVT prior to this experiment, the HVT\nmodel might not have provided an adequate stimulus for further\nThe advantage of HIIT compared to HVT lies in a shorter\nperiod of training time for similar muscular"}
{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0007_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "a3b06b2b9b180d07", "text_span_hash": "a3b06b2b9b180d07", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "olume (Shepley et al., 1992),\nO2 extraction (Daussin et al., 2007), and improvements in aero-\nbic and anaerobic metabolism (Macdougall et al., 1998), such as\nincreased mitochondrial biogenesis and oxidative capacity, have\nbeen reported (Gibala et al.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Endurance training concepts\nTable 3 | Per cent changes in velocity (V) and power (P) and at various lactate thresholds as well as peak velocity and power.\nThe values presented are means ± SD. F and P values were obtained by One-Way ANOVA (4 training groups) calculated over the per cent differences between pre-\nto post-training. POL, polarized training group; HIIT, High intensity interval training group; THR, threshold training group; HVT, high volume training group; TTE, time\nto exhaustion during the ramp test; V/P2, velocity or power at 2 mmol·L−1; V/P4, velocity or power at 4 mmol·L−1; V/Ppeak, peak velocity or power in the incremental\ntest; *p < 0.05; **p < 0.01; ***p < 0.001 signiﬁcant difference within groups from pre- to post-training.\n†p < 0.05; ††p < 0.01 signiﬁcant different from POL training group.\n‡p < 0.05signiﬁcant different from HIIT training group.\naMain effect between groups.\n1998; Billat et al., 2001; Schumacher and Mueller, 2002; Seiler\nand Kjerland, 2006; Esteve-Lanao et al., 2007). In these studies,\nit was demonstrated that endurance athletes perform approxi-\nmately 75% of their yearly training program either below or well\nabove (∼15–20%) the LT, but little at the LT. In the current study,\nPOL mimicked this distribution (LOW = 68%, LTP = 6%, HIGH\n= 26%). Only the study of Neal et al. (2013) demonstrated that\n6 weeks of POL resulted in greater systemic adaptation in trained\ncyclists when compared to THR, hen", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "olume (Shepley et al., 1992),\nO2 extraction (Daussin et al., 2007), and improvements in aero-\nbic and anaerobic metabolism (Macdougall et al., 1998), such as\nincreased mitochondrial biogenesis and oxidative capacity, have\nbeen reported (Gibala et al."}
{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0007_c0016", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ddab856bd4c82f36", "text_span_hash": "ddab856bd4c82f36", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 16, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "e\nbeen reported (Gibala et al., 2006; Daussin et al., 2007, 2008;\nBurgomaster et al., 2008). The aforementioned adaptations in\nresponse to HIIT explain the often documented increases in TTE,\ntime trial performance (Lindsay et al., 1996), lactate and", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Endurance training concepts\nTable 3 | Per cent changes in velocity (V) and power (P) and at various lactate thresholds as well as peak velocity and power.\nThe values presented are means ± SD. F and P values were obtained by One-Way ANOVA (4 training groups) calculated over the per cent differences between pre-\nto post-training. POL, polarized training group; HIIT, High intensity interval training group; THR, threshold training group; HVT, high volume training group; TTE, time\nto exhaustion during the ramp test; V/P2, velocity or power at 2 mmol·L−1; V/P4, velocity or power at 4 mmol·L−1; V/Ppeak, peak velocity or power in the incremental\ntest; *p < 0.05; **p < 0.01; ***p < 0.001 signiﬁcant difference within groups from pre- to post-training.\n†p < 0.05; ††p < 0.01 signiﬁcant different from POL training group.\n‡p < 0.05signiﬁcant different from HIIT training group.\naMain effect between groups.\n1998; Billat et al., 2001; Schumacher and Mueller, 2002; Seiler\nand Kjerland, 2006; Esteve-Lanao et al., 2007). In these studies,\nit was demonstrated that endurance athletes perform approxi-\nmately 75% of their yearly training program either below or well\nabove (∼15–20%) the LT, but little at the LT. In the current study,\nPOL mimicked this distribution (LOW = 68%, LTP = 6%, HIGH\n= 26%). Only the study of Neal et al. (2013) demonstrated that\n6 weeks of POL resulted in greater systemic adaptation in trained\ncyclists when compared to THR, hen", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "e\nbeen reported (Gibala et al., 2006; Daussin et al., 2007, 2008;\nBurgomaster et al., 2008). The aforementioned adaptations in\nresponse to HIIT explain the often documented increases in TTE,\ntime trial performance (Lindsay et al., 1996), lactate and"}
