r well\nabove (∼15–20%) the LT, but little at the LT. In the current study,\nPOL mimicked this distribution (LOW = 68%, LTP = 6%, HIGH\n= 26%). Only the study of Neal et al. (2013) demonstrated that\n6 weeks of POL resulted in greater systemic adaptation in trained\ncyclists when compared to THR, hen", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ster et al.,\nThe present study, as well as that of Helgerud et al. (2007),\ndemonstrated that training at or near VO2peak may be more effec-\ntive in enhancing VO2peak when compared to HVT or THR.\nHowever, POL, a combination of HVT and HIIT, may be sup"}
{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0007_c0020", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "16673c2c9ed50bb8", "text_span_hash": "16673c2c9ed50bb8", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 20, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": ".\nFurthermore, in HIIT intervention studies (2–3 HIIT sessions per\nweek), VO2peak increased approximately 9% over a 10 week train-\ning intervention (McMillan et al., 2005) and 11% over a 6 week\ntraining intervention (Helgerud et al., 2001) in youth a", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Endurance training concepts\nTable 3 | Per cent changes in velocity (V) and power (P) and at various lactate thresholds as well as peak velocity and power.\nThe values presented are means ± SD. F and P values were obtained by One-Way ANOVA (4 training groups) calculated over the per cent differences between pre-\nto post-training. POL, polarized training group; HIIT, High intensity interval training group; THR, threshold training group; HVT, high volume training group; TTE, time\nto exhaustion during the ramp test; V/P2, velocity or power at 2 mmol·L−1; V/P4, velocity or power at 4 mmol·L−1; V/Ppeak, peak velocity or power in the incremental\ntest; *p < 0.05; **p < 0.01; ***p < 0.001 signiﬁcant difference within groups from pre- to post-training.\n†p < 0.05; ††p < 0.01 signiﬁcant different from POL training group.\n‡p < 0.05signiﬁcant different from HIIT training group.\naMain effect between groups.\n1998; Billat et al., 2001; Schumacher and Mueller, 2002; Seiler\nand Kjerland, 2006; Esteve-Lanao et al., 2007). In these studies,\nit was demonstrated that endurance athletes perform approxi-\nmately 75% of their yearly training program either below or well\nabove (∼15–20%) the LT, but little at the LT. In the current study,\nPOL mimicked this distribution (LOW = 68%, LTP = 6%, HIGH\n= 26%). Only the study of Neal et al. (2013) demonstrated that\n6 weeks of POL resulted in greater systemic adaptation in trained\ncyclists when compared to THR, hen", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": ".\nFurthermore, in HIIT intervention studies (2–3 HIIT sessions per\nweek), VO2peak increased approximately 9% over a 10 week train-\ning intervention (McMillan et al., 2005) and 11% over a 6 week\ntraining intervention (Helgerud et al., 2001) in youth a"}
{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0007_c0021", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2f908a2f6e2e1d54", "text_span_hash": "2f908a2f6e2e1d54", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 21, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "gerud et al., 2001) in youth and junior\nsoccer players, suggesting a 0.5% increase in VO2peak per HIIT\ntraining session. In the current study, the increase in VO2peak\nfollowing 9 weeks of HIIT (27 HIIT sessions) was 4.8% (0.18%\nincrease per training", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Endurance training concepts\nTable 3 | Per cent changes in velocity (V) and power (P) and at various lactate thresholds as well as peak velocity and power.\nThe values presented are means ± SD. F and P values were obtained by One-Way ANOVA (4 training groups) calculated over the per cent differences between pre-\nto post-training. POL, polarized training group; HIIT, High intensity interval training group; THR, threshold training group; HVT, high volume training group; TTE, time\nto exhaustion during the ramp test; V/P2, velocity or power at 2 mmol·L−1; V/P4, velocity or power at 4 mmol·L−1; V/Ppeak, peak velocity or power in the incremental\ntest; *p < 0.05; **p < 0.01; ***p < 0.001 signiﬁcant difference within groups from pre- to post-training.\n†p < 0.05; ††p < 0.01 signiﬁcant different from POL training group.\n‡p < 0.05signiﬁcant different from HIIT training group.\naMain effect between groups.\n1998; Billat et al., 2001; Schumacher and Mueller, 2002; Seiler\nand Kjerland, 2006; Esteve-Lanao et al., 2007). In these studies,\nit was demonstrated that endurance athletes perform approxi-\nmately 75% of their yearly training program either below or well\nabove (∼15–20%) the LT, but little at the LT. In the current study,\nPOL mimicked this distribution (LOW = 68%, LTP = 6%, HIGH\n= 26%). Only the study of Neal et al. (2013) demonstrated that\n6 weeks of POL resulted in greater systemic adaptation in trained\ncyclists when compared to THR, hen", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "gerud et al., 2001) in youth and junior\nsoccer players, suggesting a 0.5% increase in VO2peak per HIIT\ntraining session. In the current study, the increase in VO2peak\nfollowing 9 weeks of HIIT (27 HIIT sessions) was 4.8% (0.18%\nincrease per training"}
{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0007_c0022", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9d8b28689d36a30d", "text_span_hash": "9d8b28689d36a30d", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 22, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "(0.18%\nincrease per training session), while POL resulted in an 11.7%\nincrease in VO2peak with fewer HIIT sessions (14 HIIT) (0.84%\nincrease per training session). This result may be explained by:\n(1) peak adaptation might have been reached followin", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Endurance training concepts\nTable 3 | Per cent changes in velocity (V) and power (P) and at various lactate thresholds as well as peak velocity and power.\nThe values presented are means ± SD. F and P values were obtained by One-Way ANOVA (4 training groups) calculated over the per cent differences between pre-\nto post-training. POL, polarized training group; HIIT, High intensity interval training group; THR, threshold training group; HVT, high volume training group; TTE, time\nto exhaustion during the ramp test; V/P2, velocity or power at 2 mmol·L−1; V/P4, velocity or power at 4 mmol·L−1; V/Ppeak, peak velocity or power in the incremental\ntest; *p < 0.05; **p < 0.01; ***p < 0.001 signiﬁcant difference within groups from pre- to post-training.\n†p < 0.05; ††p < 0.01 signiﬁcant different from POL training group.\n‡p < 0.05signiﬁcant different from HIIT training group.\naMain effect between groups.\n1998; Billat et al., 2001; Schumacher and Mueller, 2002; Seiler\nand Kjerland, 2006; Esteve-Lanao et al., 2007). In these studies,\nit was demonstrated that endurance athletes perform approxi-\nmately 75% of their yearly training program either below or well\nabove (∼15–20%) the LT, but little at the LT. In the current study,\nPOL mimicked this distribution (LOW = 68%, LTP = 6%, HIGH\n= 26%). Only the study of Neal et al. (2013) demonstrated that\n6 weeks of POL resulted in greater systemic adaptation in trained\ncyclists when compared to THR, hen", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "(0.18%\nincrease per training session), while POL resulted in an 11.7%\nincrease in VO2peak with fewer HIIT sessions (14 HIIT) (0.84%\nincrease per training session). This result may be explained by:\n(1) peak adaptation might have been reached followin"}
{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0007_c0024", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "539ca93e578ac98d", "text_span_hash": "539ca93e578ac98d", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 24, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "s\nto greater long-term adaptations in endurance performance than\nwith exclusively HIIT or HVT.\nTHR improves VO2peak, lactate or ventilatory thresholds and\nendurance performance in untrained persons (Denis et al., 1984;\nLonderee, 1997; Gaskill et al.,", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Endurance training concepts\nTable 3 | Per cent changes in velocity (V) and power (P) and at various lactate thresholds as well as peak velocity and power.\nThe values presented are means ± SD. F and P values were obtained by One-Way ANOVA (4 training groups) calculated over the per cent differences between pre-\nto post-training. POL, polarized training group; HIIT, High intensity interval training group; THR, threshold training group; HVT, high volume training group; TTE, time\nto exhaustion during the ramp test; V/P2, velocity or power at 2 mmol·L−1; V/P4, velocity or power at 4 mmol·L−1; V/Ppeak, peak velocity or power in the incremental\ntest; *p < 0.05; **p < 0.01; ***p < 0.001 signiﬁcant difference within groups from pre- to post-training.\n†p < 0.05; ††p < 0.01 signiﬁcant different from POL training group.\n‡p < 0.05signiﬁcant different from HIIT training group.\naMain effect between groups.\n1998; Billat et al., 2001; Schumacher and Mueller, 2002; Seiler\nand Kjerland, 2006; Esteve-Lanao et al., 2007). In these studies,\nit was demonstrated that endurance athletes perform approxi-\nmately 75% of their yearly training program either below or well\nabove (∼15–20%) the LT, but little at the LT. In the current study,\nPOL mimicked this distribution (LOW = 68%, LTP = 6%, HIGH\n= 26%). Only the study of Neal et al. (2013) demonstrated that\n6 weeks of POL resulted in greater systemic adaptation in trained\ncyclists when compared to THR, hen", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "s\nto greater long-term adaptations in endurance performance than\nwith exclusively HIIT or HVT.\nTHR improves VO2peak, lactate or ventilatory thresholds and\nendurance performance in untrained persons (Denis et al., 1984;\nLonderee, 1997; Gaskill et al.,"}
