{"chunk_id": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold_p0009_c0027", "source_registry_id": "src_d4dd1dca785a", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "cb108f6ec8fb4383", "text_span_hash": "cb108f6ec8fb4383", "source_file": "Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Stoggl_2014_Polarized_vs_Threshold.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 27, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "olarized training has greater impact on key\nendurance variables than threshold, high intensity, or high volume training. Front.\nThis article was submitted to Exercise Physiology, a section of the journal Frontiers in\nCopyright © 2014 Stöggl and Sperl", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Endurance training concepts\nDenis, C., Dormois, D., and Lacour, J. R. (1984). Endurance training, VO2 max,\nand OBLA: a longitudinal study of two different age groups. Int. J. Sports Med.\nDi Prampero, P. E., Atchou, G., Bruckner, J. C., and Moia, C. (1986). The energetics\nof endurance running. Eur. J. Appl. Physiol. Occup. Physiol. 55, 259–266. doi:\nEdge, J., Bishop, D., Goodman, C., and Dawson, B. (2005). Effects of high- and\nmoderate-intensity training on metabolism and repeated sprints. Med. Sci.\nEsteve-Lanao, J., Foster, C., Seiler, S., and Lucia, A. (2007). Impact of training inten-\nsity distribution on performance in endurance athletes. J. Strength Cond. Res. 21,\nEsteve-Lanao, J., San Juan, A. F., Earnest, C. P., Foster, C., and Lucia, A.\n(2005). How do endurance runners actually train? Relationship with\nEvertsen, F., Medbo, J. I., and Bonen, A. (2001). Effect of training intensity on\nmuscle lactate transporters and lactate threshold of cross-country skiers. Acta\nFaude, O., Kindermann, W., and Meyer, T. (2009). Lactate threshold concepts: how\nGaskill, S. E., Walker, A. J., Serfass, R. A., Bouchard, C., Gagnon, J., Rao, D. C., et al.\n(2001). Changes in ventilatory threshold with exercise training in a sedentary\npopulation: the HERITAGE family study. Int. J. Sports Med. 22, 586–592. doi:\nGibala, M. J., Little, J. P., van Essen, M., Wilkin, G. P., Burgomaster, K. A., Safdar, A.,\net al. (2006). Short-term sprint interval vers", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "olarized training has greater impact on key\nendurance variables than threshold, high intensity, or high volume training. Front.\nThis article was submitted to Exercise Physiology, a section of the journal Frontiers in\nCopyright © 2014 Stöggl and Sperl"}
{"chunk_id": "Taberner_2019_Return_to_Sport_p0001_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_187b42bfde9e", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "31e358513c3e71ac", "text_span_hash": "31e358513c3e71ac", "source_file": "Taberner_2019_Return_to_Sport.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Taberner_2019_Return_to_Sport.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "timate this risk, then plan\nand adapt RTS accordingly.1 Quantifying\nand monitoring training load is key in\nguiding this process while managing rein-\njury risk,2–4 and global positioning systems\n(GPS) provide a valid measure of external\nrunning loads.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Taberner M, et al. Br J Sports Med Month 2019 Vol 0 No 0\nProgressing rehabilitation after injury:\nconsider the ‘control-chaos continuum’\nMatt Taberner, 1,2 Tom Allen,3 Daniel Dylan Cohen4\nmatch play following injury increases\nthe risk of reinjury. However, having\nkey players available benefits the team.1\nPractitioners must balance these two\ncomponents of the return to sport (RTS)\nprocess, combining evidence and clinical\nexperience to estimate this risk, then plan\nand adapt RTS accordingly.1 Quantifying\nand monitoring training load is key in\nguiding this process while managing rein-\njury risk,2–4 and global positioning systems\n(GPS) provide a valid measure of external\nrunning loads.5 However, as practitioners,\nwe should focus on both quantitative\naspects of running load progression and\nqualitative characteristics of movement in\ncompetition. This includes highly variable,\nspontaneous and unanticipated move-\nments (the conditions of ‘chaos’) reflecting\nthe unpredictable nature of the sport.\nIn this editorial, we present the ‘control-\nchaos continuum’ (CCC) (figure 1), inter-\nlinking GPS variables, while progressively\nincorporating greater perceptual and\nreactive neurocognitive challenges.6 7 This\nframework moves from high control to\nhigh chaos, and is based on more than a\ndecade of rehabilitation and RTS in the\ndemanding setting of English Premier\nThe control-chaos continuum\nUsing retrospective player chronic running\nloads (GPS) ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "timate this risk, then plan\nand adapt RTS accordingly.1 Quantifying\nand monitoring training load is key in\nguiding this process while managing rein-\njury risk,2–4 and global positioning systems\n(GPS) provide a valid measure of external\nrunning loads."}
