{"chunk_id": "Taberner_2019_Return_to_Sport_p0005_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_187b42bfde9e", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9739f8a679fbd26c", "text_span_hash": "9739f8a679fbd26c", "source_file": "Taberner_2019_Return_to_Sport.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Taberner_2019_Return_to_Sport.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Return to play:\nthe challenge of balancing research and practice. Br J\n3 Blanch P, Gabbett TJ. Has the athlete trained enough\nto return to play safely? The acute:chronic workload\nratio permits clinicians to quantify a player’s risk of\nsubsequent inj", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Taberner M, et al. Br J Sports Med Month 2019 Vol 0 No 0\nwith years of experience. This frame-\nwork provides the practitioner with an\nindividualised approach to RTS that\nintegrates quantitative and qualitative\nprogression criteria.\nTwitter  @MattTaberner\nContributors  MT and TA wrote the paper and\nproduced appropriate figures. Online supplementary\nvideo taken from outdoor physical preparation with MT.\nDDC provided support and feedback.\nFunding  The authors have not declared a specific\ngrant for this research from any funding agency in the\npublic, commercial or not-for-profit sectors.\nCompeting interests  DDC consults to a company\nwho sell strength and power diagnostic devices.\nPatient consent for publication  Not required.\nProvenance and peer review  Not commissioned;\nexternally peer reviewed.\nOpen access This is an open access article distributed\nin accordance with the Creative Commons Attribution\nNon Commercial (CC BY-NC 4.0) license, which permits\nothers to distribute, remix, adapt, build upon this work\nnon-commercially, and license their derivative works on\ndifferent terms, provided the original work is properly\ncited, appropriate credit is given, any changes made\nindicated, and the use is non-commercial. See: http://​\ncreativecommons.​org/​licenses/​by-​nc/​4.​0/.\n© Author(s) (or their employer(s)) 2019. Re-use\npermitted under CC BY-NC. No commercial re-use. See\nrights and permissions. Published by BMJ.\n►Additional materi", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Return to play:\nthe challenge of balancing research and practice. Br J\n3 Blanch P, Gabbett TJ. Has the athlete trained enough\nto return to play safely? The acute:chronic workload\nratio permits clinicians to quantify a player’s risk of\nsubsequent inj"}
{"chunk_id": "Teixeira_2012_SDT_Exercise_Systematic_Review_p0002_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_a082d9892fee", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f3c91875e8f197e9", "text_span_hash": "f3c91875e8f197e9", "source_file": "Teixeira_2012_SDT_Exercise_Systematic_Review.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Teixeira_2012_SDT_Exercise_Systematic_Review.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "people may exercise because of controlled motiva-\ntions, where participation in activities like going to the\ngym or running regularly is based on a feeling of “having\nto” rather than truly “wanting to” participate [7]. Con-\ntrolled forms of motivatio", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "interest me – I would rather do other things with my\nLack of motivation can broadly be explained by two\norders of factors. First, as highlighted in the previous\nstatistic, people may not be sufficiently interested in exer-\ncise, or value its outcomes enough to make it a priority\nin their lives [7]. Many individuals experience competing\ndemands on their time from educational, career, and\nfamily obligations, possibly at the expense of time and\nresources that could be invested in exercising regularly.\nSecond, some people may not feel sufficiently competent\nat physical activities, feeling either not physically fit\nenough or skilled enough to exercise, or they may have\nhealth limitations that present a barrier to activity [8].\nWhether it be low interest or low perceived competence,\nthe physical activity participation data indicate that many\npeople are either unmotivated (or amotivated), having no\nintention to be more physically active, or are insuffi-\nciently motivated in the face of other interests or\ndemands on their time.\nIn addition to those who are unmotivated, another\nsource of short-lived persistence in exercise behaviors\ncomes from people who do express personal motivation\nto exercise regularly, yet initiate exercise behaviors with\nlittle follow through. Specifically, a significant percentage\nof people may exercise because of controlled motiva-\ntions, where participation in activities like going to the\ngym or running regula", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "people may exercise because of controlled motiva-\ntions, where participation in activities like going to the\ngym or running regularly is based on a feeling of “having\nto” rather than truly “wanting to” participate [7]. Con-\ntrolled forms of motivatio"}
{"chunk_id": "Teixeira_2012_SDT_Exercise_Systematic_Review_p0017_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_a082d9892fee", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c4921b74aadba202", "text_span_hash": "c4921b74aadba202", "source_file": "Teixeira_2012_SDT_Exercise_Systematic_Review.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Teixeira_2012_SDT_Exercise_Systematic_Review.pdf", "page": 17, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "-\nteristics of participants and samples is reported in Table 2.\nSamples tended to be mixed gender and included a range\nof populations (e.g., healthy individuals, chronic disease\npatients, overweight/obese individuals, exercisers), pre-\ndominantly fro", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "analyzed data originating from more than one sample\nsamples: [31]). On the other hand, 7 studies were pub-\nlished using data from three original samples ([18,33,32];\n[35,34]; [17,36]). A summary of the demographic charac-\nteristics of participants and samples is reported in Table 2.\nSamples tended to be mixed gender and included a range\nof populations (e.g., healthy individuals, chronic disease\npatients, overweight/obese individuals, exercisers), pre-\ndominantly from Western cultures (97%), and mainly aged\nbetween 25–65 years-old.\nFrom the studies eligible for this review, 53 (K = 57)\nanalyzed associations between self-regulations and exer-\ncise behavioral outcomes, 17 studies (K = 17) investigated\nthe relations between basic psychological needs and exer-\ncise, 12 studies (K = 15) tested the associations between\nmotives and exercise, and 13 studies (K = 12) included\nmeasures of perceived need support and evaluated its pre-\ndictive effect on exercise-related outcomes (see Table 3).\nSeven intervention studies, corresponding to 6 actual\ninterventions, were identified. It should be noted that\nrelations reported in the intervention studies were also\nanalyzed in the other sections (e.g., regulations, need\nMotivational predictors of exercise-related outcomes\nExercise behavioral regulations. A total of 57 samples (53\nstudies) analyzed associations between regulations and\nexercise behavior. Of these, 37 were used in cross-\nsectional de", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "-\nteristics of participants and samples is reported in Table 2.\nSamples tended to be mixed gender and included a range\nof populations (e.g., healthy individuals, chronic disease\npatients, overweight/obese individuals, exercisers), pre-\ndominantly fro"}
{"chunk_id": "Thermal and Mountain Medicine Division, U.S. Army Research I_p0002_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_ed8cf254f065", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c388c957d1a51d0d", "text_span_hash": "c388c957d1a51d0d", "source_file": "Thermal and Mountain Medicine Division, U.S. Army Research I.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Thermal and Mountain Medicine Division, U.S. Army Research I.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "osure on aerobic exercise performance\n30-h at each temperature before\nTTE decreased 38% from\n70% V̇O2max to exhaustion\nV̇O2 was higher at 4 and\n15% reduction at 48C\n55% V̇O2max for 10 min\n95% V̇O2max for 10 min (Sulbutamol)\n85% V̇O2max for 5 min (Sal", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Runners need to experiment with dif-\nferent clothing ensembles in different\nenvironmental conditions at different\nmetabolic rates to ﬁnd the right\nExercise performance studies in cold\nair are sparse. For experiments con-\nused a time to exhaustion (TTE) test\nand compared cold air exposure to\ntemperatures (Tamb) or those deﬁned\ntypically as temperate environments\n(;208C). Table 1 lists the studies that\nhave examined aerobic performance\nduring cold air exposure. Overall, there\nis no consensus about whether exercis-\ning in cold air, compared with rela-\ntively warmer Tamb, impacts aerobic\nperformance. Some studies would sug-\ngest that cold Tamb impairs TTE\n(17,24,30,31), whereas others suggest\nan improvement (4,23) or no change\n(8,28). In the 2 laboratory studies that\nsystematically used multiple air tem-\nemerged. TTE was maximal at some\n(118C in shorts/t-shirt; 24 and 18C\nwith cross-country ski clothing), and\ndecreased or increased from this opti-\nmal temperature. However, this work\ndoes contrast with the ﬁeld study by\nCarling et al. (4) who found that run-\nning distances in soccer players, who\nprimarily engage in intermittent exer-\ncise bouts, were the same whether the\nair temperatures were in the ranges of\nmore, the data from Parkin et al. (23)\nSummary of human studies examining the effect of cold exposure on aerobic exercise performance\n30-h at each temperature before\nTTE decreased 38% from\n70% V̇O2max to exhaustion\nV̇O2 was hi", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "osure on aerobic exercise performance\n30-h at each temperature before\nTTE decreased 38% from\n70% V̇O2max to exhaustion\nV̇O2 was higher at 4 and\n15% reduction at 48C\n55% V̇O2max for 10 min\n95% V̇O2max for 10 min (Sulbutamol)\n85% V̇O2max for 5 min (Sal"}
{"chunk_id": "Thermal and Mountain Medicine Division, U.S. Army Research I_p0003_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_ed8cf254f065", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ae8bfe03e501f863", "text_span_hash": "ae8bfe03e501f863", "source_file": "Thermal and Mountain Medicine Division, U.S. Army Research I.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Thermal and Mountain Medicine Division, U.S. Army Research I.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "e was\nfound to reduce performance by 5%\ncomparisons made with 208C. By con-\ntrast, another study found that there\nwere no differences observed in V̇O2m-\nbetween 5 and 188C air (19). These\nVO2max results are similar to those re-\na marker for aerobic p", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "directly contrast those observed by\nGalloway and Maughan (17); they\nfound that TTE while cycling at 70%\nV̇O2max was 42% longer in the cold\n(85 6 8 minutes) versus temperate\nfound no difference between these 2\nCheuvront et al. (8) also did not ﬁnd\nany differences between 2 and 208C\nair during a 30-minute time trial.\nInterestingly, the studies above used\nmen as the volunteers. Sandsund and\ncolleagues repeated their study in mul-\ntiple air environments (214–208C), but\nin women (28), they found no decre-\nment in TTE as Tamb fell, in direct\ncontrast to their study in men. They\nattributed this sex difference to higher\nheat losses in men, as they exhibited\nwarmer skin temperatures and lower\nrectal temperatures. Clearly more sys-\ntematic data are needed to clarify\nwhether cold air impacts long-duration\naerobic performance. What is interest-\ning from the different studies is that\noptimal aerobic performance is likely\nnot to occur during tests conducted in\nMeasurement of V̇O2max has also been\nused as a proxy for aerobic perfor-\nmance. In the few studies that have\nused V̇O2max, cold air exposure was\nfound to reduce performance by 5%\ncomparisons made with 208C. By con-\ntrast, another study found that there\nwere no differences observed in V̇O2m-\nbetween 5 and 188C air (19). These\nVO2max results are similar to those re-\na marker for aerobic performance.\nEXERCISE PERFORMANCE IN THE\nPhysical performance is a function of\nenergy output, neuromu", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "e was\nfound to reduce performance by 5%\ncomparisons made with 208C. By con-\ntrast, another study found that there\nwere no differences observed in V̇O2m-\nbetween 5 and 188C air (19). These\nVO2max results are similar to those re-\na marker for aerobic p"}
