s that have\nused V̇O2max, cold air exposure was\nfound to reduce performance by 5%\ncomparisons made with 208C. By con-\ntrast, another study found that there\nwere no differences observed in V̇O2m-\nbetween 5 and 188C air (19). These\nVO2max results are similar to those re-\na marker for aerobic performance.\nEXERCISE PERFORMANCE IN THE\nPhysical performance is a function of\nenergy output, neuromu", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "e work\nhas been laboratory-based and used\neither cycle ergometry or running until\nexhaustion. However, several articles\nhave examined the effect of ambient\ntemperature (Tamb) on running perfor-\nmance, speciﬁcally in the marathon.\nMost studies have id"}
{"chunk_id": "Thermal and Mountain Medicine Division, U.S. Army Research I_p0003_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_ed8cf254f065", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f318c380d1401219", "text_span_hash": "f318c380d1401219", "source_file": "Thermal and Mountain Medicine Division, U.S. Army Research I.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Thermal and Mountain Medicine Division, U.S. Army Research I.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "marathon.\nMost studies have identiﬁed that mar-\nathon running performance is optimal\nat ambient temperatures of ;8–128C,\nsimilar to that observed in the labora-\ntory. Ely et al. (15) identiﬁed the top 10\nfastest, world record, and course re-\ncords fo", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "directly contrast those observed by\nGalloway and Maughan (17); they\nfound that TTE while cycling at 70%\nV̇O2max was 42% longer in the cold\n(85 6 8 minutes) versus temperate\nfound no difference between these 2\nCheuvront et al. (8) also did not ﬁnd\nany differences between 2 and 208C\nair during a 30-minute time trial.\nInterestingly, the studies above used\nmen as the volunteers. Sandsund and\ncolleagues repeated their study in mul-\ntiple air environments (214–208C), but\nin women (28), they found no decre-\nment in TTE as Tamb fell, in direct\ncontrast to their study in men. They\nattributed this sex difference to higher\nheat losses in men, as they exhibited\nwarmer skin temperatures and lower\nrectal temperatures. Clearly more sys-\ntematic data are needed to clarify\nwhether cold air impacts long-duration\naerobic performance. What is interest-\ning from the different studies is that\noptimal aerobic performance is likely\nnot to occur during tests conducted in\nMeasurement of V̇O2max has also been\nused as a proxy for aerobic perfor-\nmance. In the few studies that have\nused V̇O2max, cold air exposure was\nfound to reduce performance by 5%\ncomparisons made with 208C. By con-\ntrast, another study found that there\nwere no differences observed in V̇O2m-\nbetween 5 and 188C air (19). These\nVO2max results are similar to those re-\na marker for aerobic performance.\nEXERCISE PERFORMANCE IN THE\nPhysical performance is a function of\nenergy output, neuromu", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "marathon.\nMost studies have identiﬁed that mar-\nathon running performance is optimal\nat ambient temperatures of ;8–128C,\nsimilar to that observed in the labora-\ntory. Ely et al. (15) identiﬁed the top 10\nfastest, world record, and course re-\ncords fo"}
