-body cooling, hypother-\nmia is clinically deﬁned by a core tem-\nperature below 358C. Initial symptoms\ninclude shivering, apathy, and social\ncontinues falling below 358C, confu-\nsion, sleepiness, or slurred speech oc-\ncurs, the so-called “umbles,” that is,\nstumbles (11). Table 2 lists the core\nchanges typically associated as body\ncore temperature decreases (7).\nHypothermia can be of real ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "times, again suggesting that marathon\nperformance is optimal at low Tamb. El\nHelou et al. (13) demonstrated that run-\nning speeds were optimal in both men\nand women at ;108C. Interestingly, in\none marathon (Chicago, 2009) data set\nanalyzed in that st"}
