, the weather became progres-\nsively worse with heavy rain and high\nwinds (25 knots). Only 22 competitors\n(9.2%) completed the race that year\nHYPOTHERMIA PREVENTION\nHypothermia can occur at any ambi-\nent temperature that enables body\nheat losses to exceed the metabolic\nrate. Even exposures to temperatures\nwell above freezing can lead to hypo-\nthermia if the metabolic rate is low,\nand wet/", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "at the highest numbers of nonﬁn-\nishers (31% of runners) was in 1986,\nlikely due to low wind chill tempera-\ntures (278C) and sleeting conditions.\nAnother potential issue during long-\ndistance races is the decline in the met-\nabolic rate and heat prod"}
{"chunk_id": "Thermal and Mountain Medicine Division, U.S. Army Research I_p0005_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_ed8cf254f065", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e11ac4095d97b39d", "text_span_hash": "e11ac4095d97b39d", "source_file": "Thermal and Mountain Medicine Division, U.S. Army Research I.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Thermal and Mountain Medicine Division, U.S. Army Research I.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "numerous cases\nof hypothermia and 25 deaths. On 1\noccasion, although not a running race,\n240 men began walking at 06:00 in\na drizzle and light wind, vying to be\nthe fastest participants in the Four Inns\nWalking Competition (3), a combina-\ntion race/", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "1984 Guildford race where Tamb was\n88C, with winds up to 48 kph (30\nmph) and heavy rain. Roberts (29) re-\nported 46 cases of hypothermia over\na 12-year span of the Twin Cities (Min-\nneapolis, MN) Marathon. He reported\nthat the highest numbers of nonﬁn-\nishers (31% of runners) was in 1986,\nlikely due to low wind chill tempera-\ntures (278C) and sleeting conditions.\nAnother potential issue during long-\ndistance races is the decline in the met-\nabolic rate and heat production when\nindividuals become fatigued. Pugh ﬁrst\nsuggested that physical exertion might\nincrease the risk of hypothermia (2).\nHe analyzed reports of 23 separate oc-\ncasions that had led to numerous cases\nof hypothermia and 25 deaths. On 1\noccasion, although not a running race,\n240 men began walking at 06:00 in\na drizzle and light wind, vying to be\nthe fastest participants in the Four Inns\nWalking Competition (3), a combina-\ntion race/hike traversing 45 miles over\nthe moors of Derbyshire, England\n(time to complete usually ranged from\n9.5 to 22 hours). During this particular\ncontest, the weather became progres-\nsively worse with heavy rain and high\nwinds (25 knots). Only 22 competitors\n(9.2%) completed the race that year\nHYPOTHERMIA PREVENTION\nHypothermia can occur at any ambi-\nent temperature that enables body\nheat losses to exceed the metabolic\nrate. Even exposures to temperatures\nwell above freezing can lead to hypo-\nthermia if the metabolic rate is low,\nand wet/", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "numerous cases\nof hypothermia and 25 deaths. On 1\noccasion, although not a running race,\n240 men began walking at 06:00 in\na drizzle and light wind, vying to be\nthe fastest participants in the Four Inns\nWalking Competition (3), a combina-\ntion race/"}
{"chunk_id": "Thermal and Mountain Medicine Division, U.S. Army Research I_p0006_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_ed8cf254f065", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3886d97619e4506b", "text_span_hash": "3886d97619e4506b", "source_file": "Thermal and Mountain Medicine Division, U.S. Army Research I.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Thermal and Mountain Medicine Division, U.S. Army Research I.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "conditions. If runners can sustain rela-\ntively high metabolic rates (.50%\nV̇O2max), the risk of hypothermia is\nlowered. For example, Weller et al.\nthreshold maintained core temperature\nfor 4 hours in 48C air while wearing\nwet clothing in relatively", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "conditions. If runners can sustain rela-\ntively high metabolic rates (.50%\nV̇O2max), the risk of hypothermia is\nlowered. For example, Weller et al.\nthreshold maintained core temperature\nfor 4 hours in 48C air while wearing\nwet clothing in relatively windy condi-\ntions. However, if the exercise intensity\nis lowered (to ;35% V̇O2max), core\ntemperature falls in cold, wet, and\nwindy conditions (6,26,35). In these\nconditions, runners should be provided\nwith shelter or extra clothing before\nand after races. In addition, runners\nshould be careful during training ses-\nsions in cold, wet conditions and\nensure access to shelters and run with\nFrostbite occurs when tissue tempera-\ntures fall below 08C. The freezing point\nof skin is slightly below the freezing\npoint of water due to the electrolyte\ncontent of the cells and extracellular\nﬂuid, so dry tissue typically freezes\naround 22.28C (9). Frostbite is most\ncommon in exposed skin (nose, ears,\ncheeks, and exposed wrists) and also\noccurs in clothed hands and feet\ncan signiﬁcantly lower tissue temper-\natures. The most common initial symp-\ntom of frostbite is numbness. During\nrewarming, pain is signiﬁcant, and re-\nwarming should be clinically super-\nvised for all but the most minor injuries.\nFROSTBITE PREVENTION\nThe Wind Chill Temperature Index\n(WCTI, Figure 2) is the best indicator\nof frostbite risk. It takes into account\nthe air temperature, and wind speed\nand estimates the rate at which t", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "conditions. If runners can sustain rela-\ntively high metabolic rates (.50%\nV̇O2max), the risk of hypothermia is\nlowered. For example, Weller et al.\nthreshold maintained core temperature\nfor 4 hours in 48C air while wearing\nwet clothing in relatively"}
