ngen, Germany\nObjective: Finishing a marathon requires to prepare for a 42.2 km run. Current literature\ndescribes which training characteristics are related to marathon performance. However,\nwhich training is most effective in terms of a performance improvement remains unclear.\nMethods: W", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "were classiﬁed by the 33.3rd and 66.6th\npercentile of 1v10 and the marathon performance time, respectively.\nSubjects allocated in the faster marathon performance group showed\nsystematically higher training volume and higher shares of training at low"}
{"chunk_id": "train_app_data_marathon_2021_p0001_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_cc72f1165e30", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0e7f26a5f505caa6", "text_span_hash": "0e7f26a5f505caa6", "source_file": "train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "gher shares of training at low intensities.\nOnly subjects in the moderate and high response group increased their training velocity\ncontinuously along the 16 weeks of training.\nConclusion: We demonstrate that a combination of maximized training volum", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Frontiers in Physiology | www.frontiersin.org\nMay 2021 | Volume 12 | Article 669884\nPantelis Theodoros Nikolaidis,\nUniversity of West Attica, Greece\nSingidunum University, Serbia\nUniversity College Dublin, Ireland\nNortheastern University, United States\nmarkus.zrenner@fau.de\nThis article was submitted to\nExercise Physiology,\na section of the journal\nFrontiers in Physiology\nZrenner M, Heyde C, Duemler B,\nDykman S, Roecker K and\nEskoﬁer BM (2021) Retrospective\nAnalysis of Training and Its Response\nin Marathon Finishers Based on\nRetrospective Analysis of Training\nand Its Response in Marathon\nFinishers Based on Fitness App Data\nMarkus Zrenner 1*, Christian Heyde 2, Burkhard Duemler 3, Solms Dykman 4, Kai Roecker 5\nand Bjoern M. Eskoﬁer 1\n1 Machine Learning and Data Analytics Lab, Department Artiﬁcial Intelligence in Biomedical Engineering, University of\nErlangen-Nürnberg (FAU), Erlangen, Germany, 2 Adidas AG, Future Sport Science, Herzogenaurach, Germany, 3 Adidas AG,\nTechnology & Innovation, Herzogenaurach, Germany, 4 Runtastic GmbH, Pasching, Austria, 5 Institute for Applied Public\nHealth and Exercise Medicine, Furtwangen University (HFU), Furtwangen, Germany\nObjective: Finishing a marathon requires to prepare for a 42.2 km run. Current literature\ndescribes which training characteristics are related to marathon performance. However,\nwhich training is most effective in terms of a performance improvement remains unclear.\nMethods: W", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "gher shares of training at low intensities.\nOnly subjects in the moderate and high response group increased their training velocity\ncontinuously along the 16 weeks of training.\nConclusion: We demonstrate that a combination of maximized training volum"}
{"chunk_id": "train_app_data_marathon_2021_p0001_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_cc72f1165e30", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4c0268cd383dba08", "text_span_hash": "4c0268cd383dba08", "source_file": "train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "on of maximized training volumes at low\nintensities, a continuous increase in average running speed up to the aimed marathon\nvelocity and high intensity runs ≤5 % of the overall training volume was accompanied\nby an improved 10 km performance which l", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Frontiers in Physiology | www.frontiersin.org\nMay 2021 | Volume 12 | Article 669884\nPantelis Theodoros Nikolaidis,\nUniversity of West Attica, Greece\nSingidunum University, Serbia\nUniversity College Dublin, Ireland\nNortheastern University, United States\nmarkus.zrenner@fau.de\nThis article was submitted to\nExercise Physiology,\na section of the journal\nFrontiers in Physiology\nZrenner M, Heyde C, Duemler B,\nDykman S, Roecker K and\nEskoﬁer BM (2021) Retrospective\nAnalysis of Training and Its Response\nin Marathon Finishers Based on\nRetrospective Analysis of Training\nand Its Response in Marathon\nFinishers Based on Fitness App Data\nMarkus Zrenner 1*, Christian Heyde 2, Burkhard Duemler 3, Solms Dykman 4, Kai Roecker 5\nand Bjoern M. Eskoﬁer 1\n1 Machine Learning and Data Analytics Lab, Department Artiﬁcial Intelligence in Biomedical Engineering, University of\nErlangen-Nürnberg (FAU), Erlangen, Germany, 2 Adidas AG, Future Sport Science, Herzogenaurach, Germany, 3 Adidas AG,\nTechnology & Innovation, Herzogenaurach, Germany, 4 Runtastic GmbH, Pasching, Austria, 5 Institute for Applied Public\nHealth and Exercise Medicine, Furtwangen University (HFU), Furtwangen, Germany\nObjective: Finishing a marathon requires to prepare for a 42.2 km run. Current literature\ndescribes which training characteristics are related to marathon performance. However,\nwhich training is most effective in terms of a performance improvement remains unclear.\nMethods: W", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "on of maximized training volumes at low\nintensities, a continuous increase in average running speed up to the aimed marathon\nvelocity and high intensity runs ≤5 % of the overall training volume was accompanied\nby an improved 10 km performance which l"}
{"chunk_id": "train_app_data_marathon_2021_p0001_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_cc72f1165e30", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c10f1288911794c6", "text_span_hash": "c10f1288911794c6", "source_file": "train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "oved 10 km performance which likely beneﬁted the marathon performance as\nwell. The study at hand proves that unsupervised workouts recorded with ﬁtness apps\ncan be a valuable data source for future studies in sport science.\nKeywords: marathon trainin", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Frontiers in Physiology | www.frontiersin.org\nMay 2021 | Volume 12 | Article 669884\nPantelis Theodoros Nikolaidis,\nUniversity of West Attica, Greece\nSingidunum University, Serbia\nUniversity College Dublin, Ireland\nNortheastern University, United States\nmarkus.zrenner@fau.de\nThis article was submitted to\nExercise Physiology,\na section of the journal\nFrontiers in Physiology\nZrenner M, Heyde C, Duemler B,\nDykman S, Roecker K and\nEskoﬁer BM (2021) Retrospective\nAnalysis of Training and Its Response\nin Marathon Finishers Based on\nRetrospective Analysis of Training\nand Its Response in Marathon\nFinishers Based on Fitness App Data\nMarkus Zrenner 1*, Christian Heyde 2, Burkhard Duemler 3, Solms Dykman 4, Kai Roecker 5\nand Bjoern M. Eskoﬁer 1\n1 Machine Learning and Data Analytics Lab, Department Artiﬁcial Intelligence in Biomedical Engineering, University of\nErlangen-Nürnberg (FAU), Erlangen, Germany, 2 Adidas AG, Future Sport Science, Herzogenaurach, Germany, 3 Adidas AG,\nTechnology & Innovation, Herzogenaurach, Germany, 4 Runtastic GmbH, Pasching, Austria, 5 Institute for Applied Public\nHealth and Exercise Medicine, Furtwangen University (HFU), Furtwangen, Germany\nObjective: Finishing a marathon requires to prepare for a 42.2 km run. Current literature\ndescribes which training characteristics are related to marathon performance. However,\nwhich training is most effective in terms of a performance improvement remains unclear.\nMethods: W", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "oved 10 km performance which likely beneﬁted the marathon performance as\nwell. The study at hand proves that unsupervised workouts recorded with ﬁtness apps\ncan be a valuable data source for future studies in sport science.\nKeywords: marathon trainin"}
