 Cohen, 2015; Zinner, 2016; Rosenblat et al., 2019). Such\nconcepts suggest spending certain proportions of the total\ntraining time within a low intensity (LIT) zone, a high intensity\n(HIT) zone, and optionally a threshold zone. In practice, training\nzones are either deﬁned from a ca", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "d,\ni.e., non-uniform manner (Seiler and Tønnessen, 2009; Hydren\nand Cohen, 2015; Zinner, 2016; Rosenblat et al., 2019). Such\nconcepts suggest spending certain proportions of the total\ntraining time within a low intensity (LIT) zone, a high intensity"}
{"chunk_id": "train_app_data_marathon_2021_p0002_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_cc72f1165e30", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2e013ae2413793d5", "text_span_hash": "2e013ae2413793d5", "source_file": "train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "(LIT) zone, a high intensity\n(HIT) zone, and optionally a threshold zone. In practice, training\nzones are either deﬁned from a cardiopulmonary exercises test at\ndeﬁned percentages of the maximal oxygen uptake (V’O2max),\nat intensities related to ven", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analysis of Marathon Training\n(lose weight), and psychological (becoming less anxious) manner\n(Zach et al., 2017). Independent of the motives behind the\ndecision to participate in a marathon, all those runners are united\nin the task to prepare well by bringing their bodies in shape to run\nMarathon preparation techniques have been under scientiﬁc\ninvestigation for decades. Many researchers have evaluated\nlong distance runners’ training load by analyzing their training\nstrategies with respect to volume and intensity. A high training\nvolume has been proven to positively inﬂuence marathon\nperformance (Hagan et al., 1987; Gordon et al., 2017). Especially\nrecreational runners with lower training volumes can potentially\nincrease their performance by increasing the amount of training.\nThis was underlined by the results of Roecker et al. (1998) and\nTanda (2011), who found training volume to be one of the key\npredictors for marathon performance in recreational runners.\nIn regard to the training intensity, various overviews outline\nadvantages when training intensity is distributed in a polarized,\ni.e., non-uniform manner (Seiler and Tønnessen, 2009; Hydren\nand Cohen, 2015; Zinner, 2016; Rosenblat et al., 2019). Such\nconcepts suggest spending certain proportions of the total\ntraining time within a low intensity (LIT) zone, a high intensity\n(HIT) zone, and optionally a threshold zone. In practice, training\nzones are either deﬁned from a ca", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "(LIT) zone, a high intensity\n(HIT) zone, and optionally a threshold zone. In practice, training\nzones are either deﬁned from a cardiopulmonary exercises test at\ndeﬁned percentages of the maximal oxygen uptake (V’O2max),\nat intensities related to ven"}
{"chunk_id": "train_app_data_marathon_2021_p0002_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_cc72f1165e30", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c1a0fe3b217d1192", "text_span_hash": "c1a0fe3b217d1192", "source_file": "train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "at intensities related to ventilatory or lactate thresholds (Meyer\net al., 2005) or alternatively at percentages of maximum heart rate\n(Seiler and Tønnessen, 2009) as well as at percentages of target\nmarathon velocity (Billat et al., 2001; Kenneally", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analysis of Marathon Training\n(lose weight), and psychological (becoming less anxious) manner\n(Zach et al., 2017). Independent of the motives behind the\ndecision to participate in a marathon, all those runners are united\nin the task to prepare well by bringing their bodies in shape to run\nMarathon preparation techniques have been under scientiﬁc\ninvestigation for decades. Many researchers have evaluated\nlong distance runners’ training load by analyzing their training\nstrategies with respect to volume and intensity. A high training\nvolume has been proven to positively inﬂuence marathon\nperformance (Hagan et al., 1987; Gordon et al., 2017). Especially\nrecreational runners with lower training volumes can potentially\nincrease their performance by increasing the amount of training.\nThis was underlined by the results of Roecker et al. (1998) and\nTanda (2011), who found training volume to be one of the key\npredictors for marathon performance in recreational runners.\nIn regard to the training intensity, various overviews outline\nadvantages when training intensity is distributed in a polarized,\ni.e., non-uniform manner (Seiler and Tønnessen, 2009; Hydren\nand Cohen, 2015; Zinner, 2016; Rosenblat et al., 2019). Such\nconcepts suggest spending certain proportions of the total\ntraining time within a low intensity (LIT) zone, a high intensity\n(HIT) zone, and optionally a threshold zone. In practice, training\nzones are either deﬁned from a ca", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "at intensities related to ventilatory or lactate thresholds (Meyer\net al., 2005) or alternatively at percentages of maximum heart rate\n(Seiler and Tønnessen, 2009) as well as at percentages of target\nmarathon velocity (Billat et al., 2001; Kenneally"}
{"chunk_id": "train_app_data_marathon_2021_p0002_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_cc72f1165e30", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "6cc79420b48dba44", "text_span_hash": "6cc79420b48dba44", "source_file": "train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Billat et al., 2001; Kenneally et al., 2018).\nOverall, a signiﬁcant body of research provides evidence\nthat certain physiological factors and training characteristics\nare systematically related to marathon performance. However,\nit has yet to be shown", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analysis of Marathon Training\n(lose weight), and psychological (becoming less anxious) manner\n(Zach et al., 2017). Independent of the motives behind the\ndecision to participate in a marathon, all those runners are united\nin the task to prepare well by bringing their bodies in shape to run\nMarathon preparation techniques have been under scientiﬁc\ninvestigation for decades. Many researchers have evaluated\nlong distance runners’ training load by analyzing their training\nstrategies with respect to volume and intensity. A high training\nvolume has been proven to positively inﬂuence marathon\nperformance (Hagan et al., 1987; Gordon et al., 2017). Especially\nrecreational runners with lower training volumes can potentially\nincrease their performance by increasing the amount of training.\nThis was underlined by the results of Roecker et al. (1998) and\nTanda (2011), who found training volume to be one of the key\npredictors for marathon performance in recreational runners.\nIn regard to the training intensity, various overviews outline\nadvantages when training intensity is distributed in a polarized,\ni.e., non-uniform manner (Seiler and Tønnessen, 2009; Hydren\nand Cohen, 2015; Zinner, 2016; Rosenblat et al., 2019). Such\nconcepts suggest spending certain proportions of the total\ntraining time within a low intensity (LIT) zone, a high intensity\n(HIT) zone, and optionally a threshold zone. In practice, training\nzones are either deﬁned from a ca", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Billat et al., 2001; Kenneally et al., 2018).\nOverall, a signiﬁcant body of research provides evidence\nthat certain physiological factors and training characteristics\nare systematically related to marathon performance. However,\nit has yet to be shown"}
{"chunk_id": "train_app_data_marathon_2021_p0002_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_cc72f1165e30", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c03b7c3e68b02006", "text_span_hash": "c03b7c3e68b02006", "source_file": "train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "owever,\nit has yet to be shown which training characteristics are the\nmost eﬀective in terms of an actual ﬁtness improvement to\npositively inﬂuence an individual’s marathon performance. In\norder to demonstrate whether certain training characteristics", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analysis of Marathon Training\n(lose weight), and psychological (becoming less anxious) manner\n(Zach et al., 2017). Independent of the motives behind the\ndecision to participate in a marathon, all those runners are united\nin the task to prepare well by bringing their bodies in shape to run\nMarathon preparation techniques have been under scientiﬁc\ninvestigation for decades. Many researchers have evaluated\nlong distance runners’ training load by analyzing their training\nstrategies with respect to volume and intensity. A high training\nvolume has been proven to positively inﬂuence marathon\nperformance (Hagan et al., 1987; Gordon et al., 2017). Especially\nrecreational runners with lower training volumes can potentially\nincrease their performance by increasing the amount of training.\nThis was underlined by the results of Roecker et al. (1998) and\nTanda (2011), who found training volume to be one of the key\npredictors for marathon performance in recreational runners.\nIn regard to the training intensity, various overviews outline\nadvantages when training intensity is distributed in a polarized,\ni.e., non-uniform manner (Seiler and Tønnessen, 2009; Hydren\nand Cohen, 2015; Zinner, 2016; Rosenblat et al., 2019). Such\nconcepts suggest spending certain proportions of the total\ntraining time within a low intensity (LIT) zone, a high intensity\n(HIT) zone, and optionally a threshold zone. In practice, training\nzones are either deﬁned from a ca", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "owever,\nit has yet to be shown which training characteristics are the\nmost eﬀective in terms of an actual ﬁtness improvement to\npositively inﬂuence an individual’s marathon performance. In\norder to demonstrate whether certain training characteristics"}
{"chunk_id": "train_app_data_marathon_2021_p0002_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_cc72f1165e30", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "38de73ae2844be30", "text_span_hash": "38de73ae2844be30", "source_file": "train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "rtain training characteristics lead\nto higher ﬁtness improvements, the natural variability of the\nindividuals’ responses to training has to be considered (Bouchard\nand Rankinen, 2001; Ross et al., 2019).\nCurrent ﬁndings mainly result from studies wit", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analysis of Marathon Training\n(lose weight), and psychological (becoming less anxious) manner\n(Zach et al., 2017). Independent of the motives behind the\ndecision to participate in a marathon, all those runners are united\nin the task to prepare well by bringing their bodies in shape to run\nMarathon preparation techniques have been under scientiﬁc\ninvestigation for decades. Many researchers have evaluated\nlong distance runners’ training load by analyzing their training\nstrategies with respect to volume and intensity. A high training\nvolume has been proven to positively inﬂuence marathon\nperformance (Hagan et al., 1987; Gordon et al., 2017). Especially\nrecreational runners with lower training volumes can potentially\nincrease their performance by increasing the amount of training.\nThis was underlined by the results of Roecker et al. (1998) and\nTanda (2011), who found training volume to be one of the key\npredictors for marathon performance in recreational runners.\nIn regard to the training intensity, various overviews outline\nadvantages when training intensity is distributed in a polarized,\ni.e., non-uniform manner (Seiler and Tønnessen, 2009; Hydren\nand Cohen, 2015; Zinner, 2016; Rosenblat et al., 2019). Such\nconcepts suggest spending certain proportions of the total\ntraining time within a low intensity (LIT) zone, a high intensity\n(HIT) zone, and optionally a threshold zone. In practice, training\nzones are either deﬁned from a ca", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "rtain training characteristics lead\nto higher ﬁtness improvements, the natural variability of the\nindividuals’ responses to training has to be considered (Bouchard\nand Rankinen, 2001; Ross et al., 2019).\nCurrent ﬁndings mainly result from studies wit"}
