nd Cohen, 2015; Zinner, 2016; Rosenblat et al., 2019). Such\nconcepts suggest spending certain proportions of the total\ntraining time within a low intensity (LIT) zone, a high intensity\n(HIT) zone, and optionally a threshold zone. In practice, training\nzones are either deﬁned from a ca", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "unners. We used data recorded by\nportable sensors such as smartphones, smartwatches, or heart\nrate chest straps from anonymized users of the Runtastic ﬁtness\napp for the evaluation. The subjects were chosen based on the\n• one workout between 2017 and"}
{"chunk_id": "train_app_data_marathon_2021_p0002_c0025", "source_registry_id": "src_cc72f1165e30", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "374ec447064056f0", "text_span_hash": "374ec447064056f0", "source_file": "train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 25, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "• one workout between 2017 and 2019 with a total distance\nbetween 41 and 43 km\n• at least 16 workouts in 16 weeks leading up to the marathon\n• GPS and heart rate data for each workout\nWe deﬁned a range around the exact marathon distance of\n42.2 km in", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analysis of Marathon Training\n(lose weight), and psychological (becoming less anxious) manner\n(Zach et al., 2017). Independent of the motives behind the\ndecision to participate in a marathon, all those runners are united\nin the task to prepare well by bringing their bodies in shape to run\nMarathon preparation techniques have been under scientiﬁc\ninvestigation for decades. Many researchers have evaluated\nlong distance runners’ training load by analyzing their training\nstrategies with respect to volume and intensity. A high training\nvolume has been proven to positively inﬂuence marathon\nperformance (Hagan et al., 1987; Gordon et al., 2017). Especially\nrecreational runners with lower training volumes can potentially\nincrease their performance by increasing the amount of training.\nThis was underlined by the results of Roecker et al. (1998) and\nTanda (2011), who found training volume to be one of the key\npredictors for marathon performance in recreational runners.\nIn regard to the training intensity, various overviews outline\nadvantages when training intensity is distributed in a polarized,\ni.e., non-uniform manner (Seiler and Tønnessen, 2009; Hydren\nand Cohen, 2015; Zinner, 2016; Rosenblat et al., 2019). Such\nconcepts suggest spending certain proportions of the total\ntraining time within a low intensity (LIT) zone, a high intensity\n(HIT) zone, and optionally a threshold zone. In practice, training\nzones are either deﬁned from a ca", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "• one workout between 2017 and 2019 with a total distance\nbetween 41 and 43 km\n• at least 16 workouts in 16 weeks leading up to the marathon\n• GPS and heart rate data for each workout\nWe deﬁned a range around the exact marathon distance of\n42.2 km in"}
{"chunk_id": "train_app_data_marathon_2021_p0002_c0026", "source_registry_id": "src_cc72f1165e30", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "fa0aa53a792ad8c6", "text_span_hash": "fa0aa53a792ad8c6", "source_file": "train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 26, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "arathon distance of\n42.2 km in order to include marathons of slightly diﬀerent\ndistance and inaccuracies of GPS devices used to track the\nmarathon. Apart from distance, no additional requirements like\nproﬁle or location were set for the marathon work", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analysis of Marathon Training\n(lose weight), and psychological (becoming less anxious) manner\n(Zach et al., 2017). Independent of the motives behind the\ndecision to participate in a marathon, all those runners are united\nin the task to prepare well by bringing their bodies in shape to run\nMarathon preparation techniques have been under scientiﬁc\ninvestigation for decades. Many researchers have evaluated\nlong distance runners’ training load by analyzing their training\nstrategies with respect to volume and intensity. A high training\nvolume has been proven to positively inﬂuence marathon\nperformance (Hagan et al., 1987; Gordon et al., 2017). Especially\nrecreational runners with lower training volumes can potentially\nincrease their performance by increasing the amount of training.\nThis was underlined by the results of Roecker et al. (1998) and\nTanda (2011), who found training volume to be one of the key\npredictors for marathon performance in recreational runners.\nIn regard to the training intensity, various overviews outline\nadvantages when training intensity is distributed in a polarized,\ni.e., non-uniform manner (Seiler and Tønnessen, 2009; Hydren\nand Cohen, 2015; Zinner, 2016; Rosenblat et al., 2019). Such\nconcepts suggest spending certain proportions of the total\ntraining time within a low intensity (LIT) zone, a high intensity\n(HIT) zone, and optionally a threshold zone. In practice, training\nzones are either deﬁned from a ca", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "arathon distance of\n42.2 km in order to include marathons of slightly diﬀerent\ndistance and inaccuracies of GPS devices used to track the\nmarathon. Apart from distance, no additional requirements like\nproﬁle or location were set for the marathon work"}
{"chunk_id": "train_app_data_marathon_2021_p0002_c0027", "source_registry_id": "src_cc72f1165e30", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "00625f0b056b75c9", "text_span_hash": "00625f0b056b75c9", "source_file": "train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 27, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "were set for the marathon workout. The\nthreshold of 16 workouts in the 16 weeks leading up to the\nmarathon was empirically chosen to assure a minimum amount\nof data for evaluation. The data set included 5,288 male subjects\nunknown sex (3.4%). The sub", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analysis of Marathon Training\n(lose weight), and psychological (becoming less anxious) manner\n(Zach et al., 2017). Independent of the motives behind the\ndecision to participate in a marathon, all those runners are united\nin the task to prepare well by bringing their bodies in shape to run\nMarathon preparation techniques have been under scientiﬁc\ninvestigation for decades. Many researchers have evaluated\nlong distance runners’ training load by analyzing their training\nstrategies with respect to volume and intensity. A high training\nvolume has been proven to positively inﬂuence marathon\nperformance (Hagan et al., 1987; Gordon et al., 2017). Especially\nrecreational runners with lower training volumes can potentially\nincrease their performance by increasing the amount of training.\nThis was underlined by the results of Roecker et al. (1998) and\nTanda (2011), who found training volume to be one of the key\npredictors for marathon performance in recreational runners.\nIn regard to the training intensity, various overviews outline\nadvantages when training intensity is distributed in a polarized,\ni.e., non-uniform manner (Seiler and Tønnessen, 2009; Hydren\nand Cohen, 2015; Zinner, 2016; Rosenblat et al., 2019). Such\nconcepts suggest spending certain proportions of the total\ntraining time within a low intensity (LIT) zone, a high intensity\n(HIT) zone, and optionally a threshold zone. In practice, training\nzones are either deﬁned from a ca", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "were set for the marathon workout. The\nthreshold of 16 workouts in the 16 weeks leading up to the\nmarathon was empirically chosen to assure a minimum amount\nof data for evaluation. The data set included 5,288 male subjects\nunknown sex (3.4%). The sub"}
{"chunk_id": "train_app_data_marathon_2021_p0002_c0029", "source_registry_id": "src_cc72f1165e30", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "499008a8c609fe25", "text_span_hash": "499008a8c609fe25", "source_file": "train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 29, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "data were sampled with\ndiﬀerent sampling rates. However, data streams of each workout\nwere synchronized by global timestamps (UTC). GPS data was\nFrontiers in Physiology | www.frontiersin.org\nMay 2021 | Volume 12 | Article 669884", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analysis of Marathon Training\n(lose weight), and psychological (becoming less anxious) manner\n(Zach et al., 2017). Independent of the motives behind the\ndecision to participate in a marathon, all those runners are united\nin the task to prepare well by bringing their bodies in shape to run\nMarathon preparation techniques have been under scientiﬁc\ninvestigation for decades. Many researchers have evaluated\nlong distance runners’ training load by analyzing their training\nstrategies with respect to volume and intensity. A high training\nvolume has been proven to positively inﬂuence marathon\nperformance (Hagan et al., 1987; Gordon et al., 2017). Especially\nrecreational runners with lower training volumes can potentially\nincrease their performance by increasing the amount of training.\nThis was underlined by the results of Roecker et al. (1998) and\nTanda (2011), who found training volume to be one of the key\npredictors for marathon performance in recreational runners.\nIn regard to the training intensity, various overviews outline\nadvantages when training intensity is distributed in a polarized,\ni.e., non-uniform manner (Seiler and Tønnessen, 2009; Hydren\nand Cohen, 2015; Zinner, 2016; Rosenblat et al., 2019). Such\nconcepts suggest spending certain proportions of the total\ntraining time within a low intensity (LIT) zone, a high intensity\n(HIT) zone, and optionally a threshold zone. In practice, training\nzones are either deﬁned from a ca", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "data were sampled with\ndiﬀerent sampling rates. However, data streams of each workout\nwere synchronized by global timestamps (UTC). GPS data was\nFrontiers in Physiology | www.frontiersin.org\nMay 2021 | Volume 12 | Article 669884"}
{"chunk_id": "train_app_data_marathon_2021_p0003_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_cc72f1165e30", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "aa745d6b4be6447d", "text_span_hash": "aa745d6b4be6447d", "source_file": "train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Analysis of Marathon Training\nanonymized by adding a random oﬀset to the data stream. The\nstudy is in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki, because\nthe local ethics committee raised no objection to its conduction\ndue to the anonymized nature o", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analysis of Marathon Training\nanonymized by adding a random oﬀset to the data stream. The\nstudy is in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki, because\nthe local ethics committee raised no objection to its conduction\ndue to the anonymized nature of the data.\n2.2. Data Processing\n2.2.1. Extracting Overall Subject Features\nFor normalization purposes in later processing stages, we\nextracted the average marathon performance velocity vmp and\nthe maximum training heart rate hrmax for each subject. The\naverage marathon performance velocity vmp was determined by\nthe duration of the marathon performance time Tmp for the\ndistance between 41 and 43 km. The maximum training heart\nrate hrmax was determined to be the median of the ﬁve highest\nrecorded heart rates over the whole training process. We decided\nfor this approach to cope with short term outliers in the heart\n2.2.2. Feature Extraction of Individual Workouts\nWe computed a set of features for each of the W workouts leading\nup to a subject’s marathon. The ﬁrst feature obtained from the i-th\nworkout (i ∈{1, 2, 3, . . . W}) was the training duration Ti. Ti was\ncomputed by subtracting the ﬁrst from the last UTC timestamps\nof the GPS data. If the workout duration was longer than 90 min,\nwe saved an indicator IT90,i, which was used further on to evaluate\nhow many long workouts were performed:\nif Ti < 90 minutes\nFor all other GPS-based features, we computed the distance\nand velocity ove", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Analysis of Marathon Training\nanonymized by adding a random oﬀset to the data stream. The\nstudy is in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki, because\nthe local ethics committee raised no objection to its conduction\ndue to the anonymized nature o"}
{"chunk_id": "train_app_data_marathon_2021_p0003_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_cc72f1165e30", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "72ad0afee03edf7d", "text_span_hash": "72ad0afee03edf7d", "source_file": "train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "due to the anonymized nature of the data.\n2.2. Data Processing\n2.2.1. Extracting Overall Subject Features\nFor normalization purposes in later processing stages, we\nextracted the average marathon performance velocity vmp and\nthe maximum training heart", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analysis of Marathon Training\nanonymized by adding a random oﬀset to the data stream. The\nstudy is in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki, because\nthe local ethics committee raised no objection to its conduction\ndue to the anonymized nature of the data.\n2.2. Data Processing\n2.2.1. Extracting Overall Subject Features\nFor normalization purposes in later processing stages, we\nextracted the average marathon performance velocity vmp and\nthe maximum training heart rate hrmax for each subject. The\naverage marathon performance velocity vmp was determined by\nthe duration of the marathon performance time Tmp for the\ndistance between 41 and 43 km. The maximum training heart\nrate hrmax was determined to be the median of the ﬁve highest\nrecorded heart rates over the whole training process. We decided\nfor this approach to cope with short term outliers in the heart\n2.2.2. Feature Extraction of Individual Workouts\nWe computed a set of features for each of the W workouts leading\nup to a subject’s marathon. The ﬁrst feature obtained from the i-th\nworkout (i ∈{1, 2, 3, . . . W}) was the training duration Ti. Ti was\ncomputed by subtracting the ﬁrst from the last UTC timestamps\nof the GPS data. If the workout duration was longer than 90 min,\nwe saved an indicator IT90,i, which was used further on to evaluate\nhow many long workouts were performed:\nif Ti < 90 minutes\nFor all other GPS-based features, we computed the distance\nand velocity ove", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "due to the anonymized nature of the data.\n2.2. Data Processing\n2.2.1. Extracting Overall Subject Features\nFor normalization purposes in later processing stages, we\nextracted the average marathon performance velocity vmp and\nthe maximum training heart"}
{"chunk_id": "train_app_data_marathon_2021_p0003_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_cc72f1165e30", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0a6b60ea408d0b05", "text_span_hash": "0a6b60ea408d0b05", "source_file": "train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "and\nthe maximum training heart rate hrmax for each subject. The\naverage marathon performance velocity vmp was determined by\nthe duration of the marathon performance time Tmp for the\ndistance between 41 and 43 km. The maximum training heart\nrate hrmax", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analysis of Marathon Training\nanonymized by adding a random oﬀset to the data stream. The\nstudy is in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki, because\nthe local ethics committee raised no objection to its conduction\ndue to the anonymized nature of the data.\n2.2. Data Processing\n2.2.1. Extracting Overall Subject Features\nFor normalization purposes in later processing stages, we\nextracted the average marathon performance velocity vmp and\nthe maximum training heart rate hrmax for each subject. The\naverage marathon performance velocity vmp was determined by\nthe duration of the marathon performance time Tmp for the\ndistance between 41 and 43 km. The maximum training heart\nrate hrmax was determined to be the median of the ﬁve highest\nrecorded heart rates over the whole training process. We decided\nfor this approach to cope with short term outliers in the heart\n2.2.2. Feature Extraction of Individual Workouts\nWe computed a set of features for each of the W workouts leading\nup to a subject’s marathon. The ﬁrst feature obtained from the i-th\nworkout (i ∈{1, 2, 3, . . . W}) was the training duration Ti. Ti was\ncomputed by subtracting the ﬁrst from the last UTC timestamps\nof the GPS data. If the workout duration was longer than 90 min,\nwe saved an indicator IT90,i, which was used further on to evaluate\nhow many long workouts were performed:\nif Ti < 90 minutes\nFor all other GPS-based features, we computed the distance\nand velocity ove", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "and\nthe maximum training heart rate hrmax for each subject. The\naverage marathon performance velocity vmp was determined by\nthe duration of the marathon performance time Tmp for the\ndistance between 41 and 43 km. The maximum training heart\nrate hrmax"}
{"chunk_id": "train_app_data_marathon_2021_p0003_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_cc72f1165e30", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b235640e8b69723d", "text_span_hash": "b235640e8b69723d", "source_file": "train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "imum training heart\nrate hrmax was determined to be the median of the ﬁve highest\nrecorded heart rates over the whole training process. We decided\nfor this approach to cope with short term outliers in the heart\n2.2.2. Feature Extraction of Individual", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analysis of Marathon Training\nanonymized by adding a random oﬀset to the data stream. The\nstudy is in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki, because\nthe local ethics committee raised no objection to its conduction\ndue to the anonymized nature of the data.\n2.2. Data Processing\n2.2.1. Extracting Overall Subject Features\nFor normalization purposes in later processing stages, we\nextracted the average marathon performance velocity vmp and\nthe maximum training heart rate hrmax for each subject. The\naverage marathon performance velocity vmp was determined by\nthe duration of the marathon performance time Tmp for the\ndistance between 41 and 43 km. The maximum training heart\nrate hrmax was determined to be the median of the ﬁve highest\nrecorded heart rates over the whole training process. We decided\nfor this approach to cope with short term outliers in the heart\n2.2.2. Feature Extraction of Individual Workouts\nWe computed a set of features for each of the W workouts leading\nup to a subject’s marathon. The ﬁrst feature obtained from the i-th\nworkout (i ∈{1, 2, 3, . . . W}) was the training duration Ti. Ti was\ncomputed by subtracting the ﬁrst from the last UTC timestamps\nof the GPS data. If the workout duration was longer than 90 min,\nwe saved an indicator IT90,i, which was used further on to evaluate\nhow many long workouts were performed:\nif Ti < 90 minutes\nFor all other GPS-based features, we computed the distance\nand velocity ove", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "imum training heart\nrate hrmax was determined to be the median of the ﬁve highest\nrecorded heart rates over the whole training process. We decided\nfor this approach to cope with short term outliers in the heart\n2.2.2. Feature Extraction of Individual"}
{"chunk_id": "train_app_data_marathon_2021_p0003_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_cc72f1165e30", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "00a1b509dc276155", "text_span_hash": "00a1b509dc276155", "source_file": "train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ature Extraction of Individual Workouts\nWe computed a set of features for each of the W workouts leading\nup to a subject’s marathon. The ﬁrst feature obtained from the i-th\nworkout (i ∈{1, 2, 3, . . . W}) was the training duration Ti. Ti was\ncomputed", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analysis of Marathon Training\nanonymized by adding a random oﬀset to the data stream. The\nstudy is in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki, because\nthe local ethics committee raised no objection to its conduction\ndue to the anonymized nature of the data.\n2.2. Data Processing\n2.2.1. Extracting Overall Subject Features\nFor normalization purposes in later processing stages, we\nextracted the average marathon performance velocity vmp and\nthe maximum training heart rate hrmax for each subject. The\naverage marathon performance velocity vmp was determined by\nthe duration of the marathon performance time Tmp for the\ndistance between 41 and 43 km. The maximum training heart\nrate hrmax was determined to be the median of the ﬁve highest\nrecorded heart rates over the whole training process. We decided\nfor this approach to cope with short term outliers in the heart\n2.2.2. Feature Extraction of Individual Workouts\nWe computed a set of features for each of the W workouts leading\nup to a subject’s marathon. The ﬁrst feature obtained from the i-th\nworkout (i ∈{1, 2, 3, . . . W}) was the training duration Ti. Ti was\ncomputed by subtracting the ﬁrst from the last UTC timestamps\nof the GPS data. If the workout duration was longer than 90 min,\nwe saved an indicator IT90,i, which was used further on to evaluate\nhow many long workouts were performed:\nif Ti < 90 minutes\nFor all other GPS-based features, we computed the distance\nand velocity ove", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ature Extraction of Individual Workouts\nWe computed a set of features for each of the W workouts leading\nup to a subject’s marathon. The ﬁrst feature obtained from the i-th\nworkout (i ∈{1, 2, 3, . . . W}) was the training duration Ti. Ti was\ncomputed"}
{"chunk_id": "train_app_data_marathon_2021_p0003_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_cc72f1165e30", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7be26b342fb58cd2", "text_span_hash": "7be26b342fb58cd2", "source_file": "train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "g duration Ti. Ti was\ncomputed by subtracting the ﬁrst from the last UTC timestamps\nof the GPS data. If the workout duration was longer than 90 min,\nwe saved an indicator IT90,i, which was used further on to evaluate\nhow many long workouts were perfo", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analysis of Marathon Training\nanonymized by adding a random oﬀset to the data stream. The\nstudy is in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki, because\nthe local ethics committee raised no objection to its conduction\ndue to the anonymized nature of the data.\n2.2. Data Processing\n2.2.1. Extracting Overall Subject Features\nFor normalization purposes in later processing stages, we\nextracted the average marathon performance velocity vmp and\nthe maximum training heart rate hrmax for each subject. The\naverage marathon performance velocity vmp was determined by\nthe duration of the marathon performance time Tmp for the\ndistance between 41 and 43 km. The maximum training heart\nrate hrmax was determined to be the median of the ﬁve highest\nrecorded heart rates over the whole training process. We decided\nfor this approach to cope with short term outliers in the heart\n2.2.2. Feature Extraction of Individual Workouts\nWe computed a set of features for each of the W workouts leading\nup to a subject’s marathon. The ﬁrst feature obtained from the i-th\nworkout (i ∈{1, 2, 3, . . . W}) was the training duration Ti. Ti was\ncomputed by subtracting the ﬁrst from the last UTC timestamps\nof the GPS data. If the workout duration was longer than 90 min,\nwe saved an indicator IT90,i, which was used further on to evaluate\nhow many long workouts were performed:\nif Ti < 90 minutes\nFor all other GPS-based features, we computed the distance\nand velocity ove", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "g duration Ti. Ti was\ncomputed by subtracting the ﬁrst from the last UTC timestamps\nof the GPS data. If the workout duration was longer than 90 min,\nwe saved an indicator IT90,i, which was used further on to evaluate\nhow many long workouts were perfo"}
{"chunk_id": "train_app_data_marathon_2021_p0003_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_cc72f1165e30", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "14b0dc8c5fed4cf0", "text_span_hash": "14b0dc8c5fed4cf0", "source_file": "train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "many long workouts were performed:\nif Ti < 90 minutes\nFor all other GPS-based features, we computed the distance\nand velocity over time from the GPS data. We used the great\ncircle distance implementation of the Python package (GeoPy,\n2020) to comput", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analysis of Marathon Training\nanonymized by adding a random oﬀset to the data stream. The\nstudy is in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki, because\nthe local ethics committee raised no objection to its conduction\ndue to the anonymized nature of the data.\n2.2. Data Processing\n2.2.1. Extracting Overall Subject Features\nFor normalization purposes in later processing stages, we\nextracted the average marathon performance velocity vmp and\nthe maximum training heart rate hrmax for each subject. The\naverage marathon performance velocity vmp was determined by\nthe duration of the marathon performance time Tmp for the\ndistance between 41 and 43 km. The maximum training heart\nrate hrmax was determined to be the median of the ﬁve highest\nrecorded heart rates over the whole training process. We decided\nfor this approach to cope with short term outliers in the heart\n2.2.2. Feature Extraction of Individual Workouts\nWe computed a set of features for each of the W workouts leading\nup to a subject’s marathon. The ﬁrst feature obtained from the i-th\nworkout (i ∈{1, 2, 3, . . . W}) was the training duration Ti. Ti was\ncomputed by subtracting the ﬁrst from the last UTC timestamps\nof the GPS data. If the workout duration was longer than 90 min,\nwe saved an indicator IT90,i, which was used further on to evaluate\nhow many long workouts were performed:\nif Ti < 90 minutes\nFor all other GPS-based features, we computed the distance\nand velocity ove", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "many long workouts were performed:\nif Ti < 90 minutes\nFor all other GPS-based features, we computed the distance\nand velocity over time from the GPS data. We used the great\ncircle distance implementation of the Python package (GeoPy,\n2020) to comput"}
{"chunk_id": "train_app_data_marathon_2021_p0003_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_cc72f1165e30", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e37a37f419f62831", "text_span_hash": "e37a37f419f62831", "source_file": "train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "he total distance of the i-th workout Di by\ncomputing the sum over all samples. Similar to IT90,i (Equation\n1), we computed an indicator ID15,i for workouts with distances\nIn order to assess training progress, we extracted the best velocity\nv10,i for", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analysis of Marathon Training\nanonymized by adding a random oﬀset to the data stream. The\nstudy is in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki, because\nthe local ethics committee raised no objection to its conduction\ndue to the anonymized nature of the data.\n2.2. Data Processing\n2.2.1. Extracting Overall Subject Features\nFor normalization purposes in later processing stages, we\nextracted the average marathon performance velocity vmp and\nthe maximum training heart rate hrmax for each subject. The\naverage marathon performance velocity vmp was determined by\nthe duration of the marathon performance time Tmp for the\ndistance between 41 and 43 km. The maximum training heart\nrate hrmax was determined to be the median of the ﬁve highest\nrecorded heart rates over the whole training process. We decided\nfor this approach to cope with short term outliers in the heart\n2.2.2. Feature Extraction of Individual Workouts\nWe computed a set of features for each of the W workouts leading\nup to a subject’s marathon. The ﬁrst feature obtained from the i-th\nworkout (i ∈{1, 2, 3, . . . W}) was the training duration Ti. Ti was\ncomputed by subtracting the ﬁrst from the last UTC timestamps\nof the GPS data. If the workout duration was longer than 90 min,\nwe saved an indicator IT90,i, which was used further on to evaluate\nhow many long workouts were performed:\nif Ti < 90 minutes\nFor all other GPS-based features, we computed the distance\nand velocity ove", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "he total distance of the i-th workout Di by\ncomputing the sum over all samples. Similar to IT90,i (Equation\n1), we computed an indicator ID15,i for workouts with distances\nIn order to assess training progress, we extracted the best velocity\nv10,i for"}
{"chunk_id": "train_app_data_marathon_2021_p0003_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_cc72f1165e30", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "71db730d1f8b1b0c", "text_span_hash": "71db730d1f8b1b0c", "source_file": "train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ed the best velocity\nv10,i for a 10 km segment within each workout (if Di ≥10 km).\nFor the respective 10 km segment, we also computed the average\nheart rate ¯hr10,i during the time interval.\nAfter dividing di[n] by the corresponding duration between", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analysis of Marathon Training\nanonymized by adding a random oﬀset to the data stream. The\nstudy is in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki, because\nthe local ethics committee raised no objection to its conduction\ndue to the anonymized nature of the data.\n2.2. Data Processing\n2.2.1. Extracting Overall Subject Features\nFor normalization purposes in later processing stages, we\nextracted the average marathon performance velocity vmp and\nthe maximum training heart rate hrmax for each subject. The\naverage marathon performance velocity vmp was determined by\nthe duration of the marathon performance time Tmp for the\ndistance between 41 and 43 km. The maximum training heart\nrate hrmax was determined to be the median of the ﬁve highest\nrecorded heart rates over the whole training process. We decided\nfor this approach to cope with short term outliers in the heart\n2.2.2. Feature Extraction of Individual Workouts\nWe computed a set of features for each of the W workouts leading\nup to a subject’s marathon. The ﬁrst feature obtained from the i-th\nworkout (i ∈{1, 2, 3, . . . W}) was the training duration Ti. Ti was\ncomputed by subtracting the ﬁrst from the last UTC timestamps\nof the GPS data. If the workout duration was longer than 90 min,\nwe saved an indicator IT90,i, which was used further on to evaluate\nhow many long workouts were performed:\nif Ti < 90 minutes\nFor all other GPS-based features, we computed the distance\nand velocity ove", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ed the best velocity\nv10,i for a 10 km segment within each workout (if Di ≥10 km).\nFor the respective 10 km segment, we also computed the average\nheart rate ¯hr10,i during the time interval.\nAfter dividing di[n] by the corresponding duration between"}
{"chunk_id": "train_app_data_marathon_2021_p0003_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_cc72f1165e30", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "6ab5885a5c8e33ed", "text_span_hash": "6ab5885a5c8e33ed", "source_file": "train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "a deﬁned velocity bin ˜V during the i-th workout. To be\nable to deﬁne comparable velocity bins across all subjects,\nwe normalized the velocity data stream vi[n] by the subject’s\nmarathon performance velocity vmp:\nThe velocity bins for the distributi", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analysis of Marathon Training\nanonymized by adding a random oﬀset to the data stream. The\nstudy is in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki, because\nthe local ethics committee raised no objection to its conduction\ndue to the anonymized nature of the data.\n2.2. Data Processing\n2.2.1. Extracting Overall Subject Features\nFor normalization purposes in later processing stages, we\nextracted the average marathon performance velocity vmp and\nthe maximum training heart rate hrmax for each subject. The\naverage marathon performance velocity vmp was determined by\nthe duration of the marathon performance time Tmp for the\ndistance between 41 and 43 km. The maximum training heart\nrate hrmax was determined to be the median of the ﬁve highest\nrecorded heart rates over the whole training process. We decided\nfor this approach to cope with short term outliers in the heart\n2.2.2. Feature Extraction of Individual Workouts\nWe computed a set of features for each of the W workouts leading\nup to a subject’s marathon. The ﬁrst feature obtained from the i-th\nworkout (i ∈{1, 2, 3, . . . W}) was the training duration Ti. Ti was\ncomputed by subtracting the ﬁrst from the last UTC timestamps\nof the GPS data. If the workout duration was longer than 90 min,\nwe saved an indicator IT90,i, which was used further on to evaluate\nhow many long workouts were performed:\nif Ti < 90 minutes\nFor all other GPS-based features, we computed the distance\nand velocity ove", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "a deﬁned velocity bin ˜V during the i-th workout. To be\nable to deﬁne comparable velocity bins across all subjects,\nwe normalized the velocity data stream vi[n] by the subject’s\nmarathon performance velocity vmp:\nThe velocity bins for the distributi"}
