e\nthe share of the workout time the subjects spent in diﬀerent\nintensity zones. Similar to Kenneally et al. (2018) and Billat et al.\n(2001), we deﬁned the zones based on the marathon velocity. The\nLIT zone was deﬁned by velocities below vmp and the HIT zone\nby velocities above 1.2 · vmp (", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ent in the\nintensity zone for tby was computed as\nLITtby = Ftby[X = 1]\nthresholdtby = Ftby[X = 1.2] −Ftby[X = 1]\nHITtby = 1 −Ftby[X = 1.2]\nAll the computations for the training block analysis were also\napplied to all W workouts leading up to the mara"}
{"chunk_id": "train_app_data_marathon_2021_p0004_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_cc72f1165e30", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "83cb184d49d64d37", "text_span_hash": "83cb184d49d64d37", "source_file": "train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "orkouts leading up to the marathon in order\nto obtain each subject’s overall training statistics.\n2.2.4. Filtering Data Set\nAn interquartile range (IQR) ﬁlter was applied to exclude\nall subjects, where parameters of subjects (Tmp, D, T, hrmax)\nexceed", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analysis of Marathon Training\nThe best 10 km velocity v10,tby for training block y was chosen\nfrom all v10,i of workouts in tby:\nThe duration distribution curves for velocity Ti[ ˜V] and heart\nHR] were combined for the diﬀerent training blocks\nand converted to probability distributions Ptby[X =\ncumulative distributions Ftby[X = ˜V] (Figure A1).\nFor the y-th training block, the duration distribution curve\nTtby[ ˜V] was computed by summing the duration within the\nvelocity bin ˜V of all the workouts belonging to the training block:\nPtby[X = ˜V] by dividing the time spent in the velocity bins\nby the total training time in tby:\nThe cumulative distribution Ftby[X = ˜V] can be computed from\nthe probability distribution by\nThe same procedure was applied to the duration distribution of\nthe heart rate to obtain the probability distribution Ptby[X = ˜\nand cumulative distribution Ftby[X = ˜\nUsing the probability distribution functions, we computed the\nnormalized mean training velocity vtby and the normalized mean\nheart rate hrtby for the y-th training block:\n˜V · Ptby[X = ˜V]\nHR · Ptby[X = ˜\nWe also used the distribution function of the velocity to compute\nthe share of the workout time the subjects spent in diﬀerent\nintensity zones. Similar to Kenneally et al. (2018) and Billat et al.\n(2001), we deﬁned the zones based on the marathon velocity. The\nLIT zone was deﬁned by velocities below vmp and the HIT zone\nby velocities above 1.2 · vmp (", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "orkouts leading up to the marathon in order\nto obtain each subject’s overall training statistics.\n2.2.4. Filtering Data Set\nAn interquartile range (IQR) ﬁlter was applied to exclude\nall subjects, where parameters of subjects (Tmp, D, T, hrmax)\nexceed"}
{"chunk_id": "train_app_data_marathon_2021_p0004_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_cc72f1165e30", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "efab45483839ac29", "text_span_hash": "efab45483839ac29", "source_file": "train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ects (Tmp, D, T, hrmax)\nexceeded thresholds of 1.5·IQR below or above the lower and\nIn order to create valid response groups, we also excluded all\nsubjects who did not achieve a minimum average heart rate of 0.8·\nhrmax for the best 10 km runs in tb1", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analysis of Marathon Training\nThe best 10 km velocity v10,tby for training block y was chosen\nfrom all v10,i of workouts in tby:\nThe duration distribution curves for velocity Ti[ ˜V] and heart\nHR] were combined for the diﬀerent training blocks\nand converted to probability distributions Ptby[X =\ncumulative distributions Ftby[X = ˜V] (Figure A1).\nFor the y-th training block, the duration distribution curve\nTtby[ ˜V] was computed by summing the duration within the\nvelocity bin ˜V of all the workouts belonging to the training block:\nPtby[X = ˜V] by dividing the time spent in the velocity bins\nby the total training time in tby:\nThe cumulative distribution Ftby[X = ˜V] can be computed from\nthe probability distribution by\nThe same procedure was applied to the duration distribution of\nthe heart rate to obtain the probability distribution Ptby[X = ˜\nand cumulative distribution Ftby[X = ˜\nUsing the probability distribution functions, we computed the\nnormalized mean training velocity vtby and the normalized mean\nheart rate hrtby for the y-th training block:\n˜V · Ptby[X = ˜V]\nHR · Ptby[X = ˜\nWe also used the distribution function of the velocity to compute\nthe share of the workout time the subjects spent in diﬀerent\nintensity zones. Similar to Kenneally et al. (2018) and Billat et al.\n(2001), we deﬁned the zones based on the marathon velocity. The\nLIT zone was deﬁned by velocities below vmp and the HIT zone\nby velocities above 1.2 · vmp (", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ects (Tmp, D, T, hrmax)\nexceeded thresholds of 1.5·IQR below or above the lower and\nIn order to create valid response groups, we also excluded all\nsubjects who did not achieve a minimum average heart rate of 0.8·\nhrmax for the best 10 km runs in tb1"}
{"chunk_id": "train_app_data_marathon_2021_p0004_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_cc72f1165e30", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "42cb05cb2e210e4d", "text_span_hash": "42cb05cb2e210e4d", "source_file": "train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "or the best 10 km runs in tb1 and tb4. 0.8·hrmax was chosen\nto ensure suﬃcient cardiopulmonary eﬀort for an individual best\n10 km performance as well as a suﬃcient availability of data.\n2.2.5. Categorizing Subjects in Response and\nMarathon Performanc", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analysis of Marathon Training\nThe best 10 km velocity v10,tby for training block y was chosen\nfrom all v10,i of workouts in tby:\nThe duration distribution curves for velocity Ti[ ˜V] and heart\nHR] were combined for the diﬀerent training blocks\nand converted to probability distributions Ptby[X =\ncumulative distributions Ftby[X = ˜V] (Figure A1).\nFor the y-th training block, the duration distribution curve\nTtby[ ˜V] was computed by summing the duration within the\nvelocity bin ˜V of all the workouts belonging to the training block:\nPtby[X = ˜V] by dividing the time spent in the velocity bins\nby the total training time in tby:\nThe cumulative distribution Ftby[X = ˜V] can be computed from\nthe probability distribution by\nThe same procedure was applied to the duration distribution of\nthe heart rate to obtain the probability distribution Ptby[X = ˜\nand cumulative distribution Ftby[X = ˜\nUsing the probability distribution functions, we computed the\nnormalized mean training velocity vtby and the normalized mean\nheart rate hrtby for the y-th training block:\n˜V · Ptby[X = ˜V]\nHR · Ptby[X = ˜\nWe also used the distribution function of the velocity to compute\nthe share of the workout time the subjects spent in diﬀerent\nintensity zones. Similar to Kenneally et al. (2018) and Billat et al.\n(2001), we deﬁned the zones based on the marathon velocity. The\nLIT zone was deﬁned by velocities below vmp and the HIT zone\nby velocities above 1.2 · vmp (", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "or the best 10 km runs in tb1 and tb4. 0.8·hrmax was chosen\nto ensure suﬃcient cardiopulmonary eﬀort for an individual best\n10 km performance as well as a suﬃcient availability of data.\n2.2.5. Categorizing Subjects in Response and\nMarathon Performanc"}
{"chunk_id": "train_app_data_marathon_2021_p0004_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_cc72f1165e30", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2c1e49250f21c0e2", "text_span_hash": "2c1e49250f21c0e2", "source_file": "train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "sponse and\nMarathon Performance Groups\nConventional metrics to assess performance improvement (i.e.,\nV’O2max or lactate thresholds) were not available for the\nunsupervised data set. Therefore, we used the improvement of\nthe 10 km velocity 1v10 from t", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analysis of Marathon Training\nThe best 10 km velocity v10,tby for training block y was chosen\nfrom all v10,i of workouts in tby:\nThe duration distribution curves for velocity Ti[ ˜V] and heart\nHR] were combined for the diﬀerent training blocks\nand converted to probability distributions Ptby[X =\ncumulative distributions Ftby[X = ˜V] (Figure A1).\nFor the y-th training block, the duration distribution curve\nTtby[ ˜V] was computed by summing the duration within the\nvelocity bin ˜V of all the workouts belonging to the training block:\nPtby[X = ˜V] by dividing the time spent in the velocity bins\nby the total training time in tby:\nThe cumulative distribution Ftby[X = ˜V] can be computed from\nthe probability distribution by\nThe same procedure was applied to the duration distribution of\nthe heart rate to obtain the probability distribution Ptby[X = ˜\nand cumulative distribution Ftby[X = ˜\nUsing the probability distribution functions, we computed the\nnormalized mean training velocity vtby and the normalized mean\nheart rate hrtby for the y-th training block:\n˜V · Ptby[X = ˜V]\nHR · Ptby[X = ˜\nWe also used the distribution function of the velocity to compute\nthe share of the workout time the subjects spent in diﬀerent\nintensity zones. Similar to Kenneally et al. (2018) and Billat et al.\n(2001), we deﬁned the zones based on the marathon velocity. The\nLIT zone was deﬁned by velocities below vmp and the HIT zone\nby velocities above 1.2 · vmp (", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "sponse and\nMarathon Performance Groups\nConventional metrics to assess performance improvement (i.e.,\nV’O2max or lactate thresholds) were not available for the\nunsupervised data set. Therefore, we used the improvement of\nthe 10 km velocity 1v10 from t"}
{"chunk_id": "train_app_data_marathon_2021_p0004_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_cc72f1165e30", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "a2e6e01e46670a31", "text_span_hash": "a2e6e01e46670a31", "source_file": "train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "the 10 km velocity 1v10 from tb1 to tb4 as a surrogate to evaluate\nthe response of subjects to training throughout the 16 weeks\nbefore the marathon.\nA positive value for 1v10 indicates an improvement and in turn\na positive response to training and vi", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analysis of Marathon Training\nThe best 10 km velocity v10,tby for training block y was chosen\nfrom all v10,i of workouts in tby:\nThe duration distribution curves for velocity Ti[ ˜V] and heart\nHR] were combined for the diﬀerent training blocks\nand converted to probability distributions Ptby[X =\ncumulative distributions Ftby[X = ˜V] (Figure A1).\nFor the y-th training block, the duration distribution curve\nTtby[ ˜V] was computed by summing the duration within the\nvelocity bin ˜V of all the workouts belonging to the training block:\nPtby[X = ˜V] by dividing the time spent in the velocity bins\nby the total training time in tby:\nThe cumulative distribution Ftby[X = ˜V] can be computed from\nthe probability distribution by\nThe same procedure was applied to the duration distribution of\nthe heart rate to obtain the probability distribution Ptby[X = ˜\nand cumulative distribution Ftby[X = ˜\nUsing the probability distribution functions, we computed the\nnormalized mean training velocity vtby and the normalized mean\nheart rate hrtby for the y-th training block:\n˜V · Ptby[X = ˜V]\nHR · Ptby[X = ˜\nWe also used the distribution function of the velocity to compute\nthe share of the workout time the subjects spent in diﬀerent\nintensity zones. Similar to Kenneally et al. (2018) and Billat et al.\n(2001), we deﬁned the zones based on the marathon velocity. The\nLIT zone was deﬁned by velocities below vmp and the HIT zone\nby velocities above 1.2 · vmp (", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "the 10 km velocity 1v10 from tb1 to tb4 as a surrogate to evaluate\nthe response of subjects to training throughout the 16 weeks\nbefore the marathon.\nA positive value for 1v10 indicates an improvement and in turn\na positive response to training and vi"}
{"chunk_id": "train_app_data_marathon_2021_p0004_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_cc72f1165e30", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "05be599a32549193", "text_span_hash": "05be599a32549193", "source_file": "train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ve response to training and vice versa. The ﬁlter for the\naverage heart rate stated in the data ﬁltering section assured\nthat those assessment runs were performed with a minimum\ncardiopulmonary eﬀort. Despite the absence of conventional\nmetrics to as", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analysis of Marathon Training\nThe best 10 km velocity v10,tby for training block y was chosen\nfrom all v10,i of workouts in tby:\nThe duration distribution curves for velocity Ti[ ˜V] and heart\nHR] were combined for the diﬀerent training blocks\nand converted to probability distributions Ptby[X =\ncumulative distributions Ftby[X = ˜V] (Figure A1).\nFor the y-th training block, the duration distribution curve\nTtby[ ˜V] was computed by summing the duration within the\nvelocity bin ˜V of all the workouts belonging to the training block:\nPtby[X = ˜V] by dividing the time spent in the velocity bins\nby the total training time in tby:\nThe cumulative distribution Ftby[X = ˜V] can be computed from\nthe probability distribution by\nThe same procedure was applied to the duration distribution of\nthe heart rate to obtain the probability distribution Ptby[X = ˜\nand cumulative distribution Ftby[X = ˜\nUsing the probability distribution functions, we computed the\nnormalized mean training velocity vtby and the normalized mean\nheart rate hrtby for the y-th training block:\n˜V · Ptby[X = ˜V]\nHR · Ptby[X = ˜\nWe also used the distribution function of the velocity to compute\nthe share of the workout time the subjects spent in diﬀerent\nintensity zones. Similar to Kenneally et al. (2018) and Billat et al.\n(2001), we deﬁned the zones based on the marathon velocity. The\nLIT zone was deﬁned by velocities below vmp and the HIT zone\nby velocities above 1.2 · vmp (", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ve response to training and vice versa. The ﬁlter for the\naverage heart rate stated in the data ﬁltering section assured\nthat those assessment runs were performed with a minimum\ncardiopulmonary eﬀort. Despite the absence of conventional\nmetrics to as"}
{"chunk_id": "train_app_data_marathon_2021_p0004_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_cc72f1165e30", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7747ce679c03cd38", "text_span_hash": "7747ce679c03cd38", "source_file": "train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "of conventional\nmetrics to assess performance improvement we believe that\n1v10 is a plausible surrogate since it should reasonably reﬂect\nan improvement in endurance capacity (Roecker, 2008). Also,\nresearch has shown that the velocity of 10 km races", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analysis of Marathon Training\nThe best 10 km velocity v10,tby for training block y was chosen\nfrom all v10,i of workouts in tby:\nThe duration distribution curves for velocity Ti[ ˜V] and heart\nHR] were combined for the diﬀerent training blocks\nand converted to probability distributions Ptby[X =\ncumulative distributions Ftby[X = ˜V] (Figure A1).\nFor the y-th training block, the duration distribution curve\nTtby[ ˜V] was computed by summing the duration within the\nvelocity bin ˜V of all the workouts belonging to the training block:\nPtby[X = ˜V] by dividing the time spent in the velocity bins\nby the total training time in tby:\nThe cumulative distribution Ftby[X = ˜V] can be computed from\nthe probability distribution by\nThe same procedure was applied to the duration distribution of\nthe heart rate to obtain the probability distribution Ptby[X = ˜\nand cumulative distribution Ftby[X = ˜\nUsing the probability distribution functions, we computed the\nnormalized mean training velocity vtby and the normalized mean\nheart rate hrtby for the y-th training block:\n˜V · Ptby[X = ˜V]\nHR · Ptby[X = ˜\nWe also used the distribution function of the velocity to compute\nthe share of the workout time the subjects spent in diﬀerent\nintensity zones. Similar to Kenneally et al. (2018) and Billat et al.\n(2001), we deﬁned the zones based on the marathon velocity. The\nLIT zone was deﬁned by velocities below vmp and the HIT zone\nby velocities above 1.2 · vmp (", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "of conventional\nmetrics to assess performance improvement we believe that\n1v10 is a plausible surrogate since it should reasonably reﬂect\nan improvement in endurance capacity (Roecker, 2008). Also,\nresearch has shown that the velocity of 10 km races"}
{"chunk_id": "train_app_data_marathon_2021_p0004_c0019", "source_registry_id": "src_cc72f1165e30", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0edd0cecd06b56e0", "text_span_hash": "0edd0cecd06b56e0", "source_file": "train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 19, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "10. We decided\nfor this approach to assure equally sized response groups across\ndiﬀerent performance levels.\nIndependent of the response group, all subjects were also\ncategorized in three equally sized groups based on their marathon\nperformance times", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analysis of Marathon Training\nThe best 10 km velocity v10,tby for training block y was chosen\nfrom all v10,i of workouts in tby:\nThe duration distribution curves for velocity Ti[ ˜V] and heart\nHR] were combined for the diﬀerent training blocks\nand converted to probability distributions Ptby[X =\ncumulative distributions Ftby[X = ˜V] (Figure A1).\nFor the y-th training block, the duration distribution curve\nTtby[ ˜V] was computed by summing the duration within the\nvelocity bin ˜V of all the workouts belonging to the training block:\nPtby[X = ˜V] by dividing the time spent in the velocity bins\nby the total training time in tby:\nThe cumulative distribution Ftby[X = ˜V] can be computed from\nthe probability distribution by\nThe same procedure was applied to the duration distribution of\nthe heart rate to obtain the probability distribution Ptby[X = ˜\nand cumulative distribution Ftby[X = ˜\nUsing the probability distribution functions, we computed the\nnormalized mean training velocity vtby and the normalized mean\nheart rate hrtby for the y-th training block:\n˜V · Ptby[X = ˜V]\nHR · Ptby[X = ˜\nWe also used the distribution function of the velocity to compute\nthe share of the workout time the subjects spent in diﬀerent\nintensity zones. Similar to Kenneally et al. (2018) and Billat et al.\n(2001), we deﬁned the zones based on the marathon velocity. The\nLIT zone was deﬁned by velocities below vmp and the HIT zone\nby velocities above 1.2 · vmp (", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "10. We decided\nfor this approach to assure equally sized response groups across\ndiﬀerent performance levels.\nIndependent of the response group, all subjects were also\ncategorized in three equally sized groups based on their marathon\nperformance times"}
