{"chunk_id": "train_app_data_marathon_2021_p0009_c0022", "source_registry_id": "src_cc72f1165e30", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8186846cc832d905", "text_span_hash": "8186846cc832d905", "source_file": "train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 22, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "trol intensity well and tend to run\ntoo fast even for prescribed training plans (Foster et al., 2001).\nThe mean training parameters in Table 1 showed no diﬀerences\nbetween the response groups (all η2 < 0.012). This implicates\nthat high training volum", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analysis of Marathon Training\nis similar to the one presented by Allen et al. (2017), including\nthe peaks at the full and half hour marks. Thus, even though\nthe data query only required a workout between 41 and 43 km,\nthe marathon performance times indicate that the workouts were\nactual marathon races. This assumption is supported by the fact,\nthat 98.6% of the marathon workouts were performed on the\nweekend. The distribution of the maximum training heart rate\nhrmax shows realistic results similar to data observed by others\n(Roecker et al., 2002; Sarzynski et al., 2013), who determined\nmaximum heart rates using laboratory exercise tests. Thus, we\nbelieve that the maximum training heart rate hrmax also reﬂects\nthe actual maximum heart rate well.\nStrong correlations between average marathon velocity and\naverage 10 km velocity have been reported by others (Karp, 2007;\nTanda, 2011) and are veriﬁed by our data. The sorted values for\n1v10 show a heterogeneity in response to training. In comparison\nto the ﬁndings from Bouchard and Rankinen (2001), the portion\nof the population who showed a negative or no improvement\nin our investigation was higher.\nWe believe that the higher\nportion was due to the unsupervised nature of the data as well\nas the low threshold of > 0.8 · hrmax we set to verify the best\n10 km performances. However, increasing the threshold of hrmax\nto elevate the cardiopulmonary eﬀort for the best 10 km velocities\ndid no", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "trol intensity well and tend to run\ntoo fast even for prescribed training plans (Foster et al., 2001).\nThe mean training parameters in Table 1 showed no diﬀerences\nbetween the response groups (all η2 < 0.012). This implicates\nthat high training volum"}
{"chunk_id": "train_app_data_marathon_2021_p0009_c0023", "source_registry_id": "src_cc72f1165e30", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b4465a8138bfd35d", "text_span_hash": "b4465a8138bfd35d", "source_file": "train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 23, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "cates\nthat high training volumes do not inﬂuence the response to\ntraining in general. This should be of interest to novice runners,\nwho are at higher risk to be injured from too high training\nloads (Buist et al., 2010; Videbæk et al., 2015). Neverthe", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analysis of Marathon Training\nis similar to the one presented by Allen et al. (2017), including\nthe peaks at the full and half hour marks. Thus, even though\nthe data query only required a workout between 41 and 43 km,\nthe marathon performance times indicate that the workouts were\nactual marathon races. This assumption is supported by the fact,\nthat 98.6% of the marathon workouts were performed on the\nweekend. The distribution of the maximum training heart rate\nhrmax shows realistic results similar to data observed by others\n(Roecker et al., 2002; Sarzynski et al., 2013), who determined\nmaximum heart rates using laboratory exercise tests. Thus, we\nbelieve that the maximum training heart rate hrmax also reﬂects\nthe actual maximum heart rate well.\nStrong correlations between average marathon velocity and\naverage 10 km velocity have been reported by others (Karp, 2007;\nTanda, 2011) and are veriﬁed by our data. The sorted values for\n1v10 show a heterogeneity in response to training. In comparison\nto the ﬁndings from Bouchard and Rankinen (2001), the portion\nof the population who showed a negative or no improvement\nin our investigation was higher.\nWe believe that the higher\nportion was due to the unsupervised nature of the data as well\nas the low threshold of > 0.8 · hrmax we set to verify the best\n10 km performances. However, increasing the threshold of hrmax\nto elevate the cardiopulmonary eﬀort for the best 10 km velocities\ndid no", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "cates\nthat high training volumes do not inﬂuence the response to\ntraining in general. This should be of interest to novice runners,\nwho are at higher risk to be injured from too high training\nloads (Buist et al., 2010; Videbæk et al., 2015). Neverthe"}
