{"chunk_id": "train_app_data_marathon_2021_p0009_c0026", "source_registry_id": "src_cc72f1165e30", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9fa418eae98877c7", "text_span_hash": "9fa418eae98877c7", "source_file": "train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 26, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "he response groups, the analysis\ndemonstrates that those subjects who started to train at very low\nvelocities and continuously increased their training velocity up to\nthe actual marathon velocity throughout the 16 weeks responded\nto the highest exten", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analysis of Marathon Training\nis similar to the one presented by Allen et al. (2017), including\nthe peaks at the full and half hour marks. Thus, even though\nthe data query only required a workout between 41 and 43 km,\nthe marathon performance times indicate that the workouts were\nactual marathon races. This assumption is supported by the fact,\nthat 98.6% of the marathon workouts were performed on the\nweekend. The distribution of the maximum training heart rate\nhrmax shows realistic results similar to data observed by others\n(Roecker et al., 2002; Sarzynski et al., 2013), who determined\nmaximum heart rates using laboratory exercise tests. Thus, we\nbelieve that the maximum training heart rate hrmax also reﬂects\nthe actual maximum heart rate well.\nStrong correlations between average marathon velocity and\naverage 10 km velocity have been reported by others (Karp, 2007;\nTanda, 2011) and are veriﬁed by our data. The sorted values for\n1v10 show a heterogeneity in response to training. In comparison\nto the ﬁndings from Bouchard and Rankinen (2001), the portion\nof the population who showed a negative or no improvement\nin our investigation was higher.\nWe believe that the higher\nportion was due to the unsupervised nature of the data as well\nas the low threshold of > 0.8 · hrmax we set to verify the best\n10 km performances. However, increasing the threshold of hrmax\nto elevate the cardiopulmonary eﬀort for the best 10 km velocities\ndid no", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "he response groups, the analysis\ndemonstrates that those subjects who started to train at very low\nvelocities and continuously increased their training velocity up to\nthe actual marathon velocity throughout the 16 weeks responded\nto the highest exten"}
{"chunk_id": "train_app_data_marathon_2021_p0009_c0027", "source_registry_id": "src_cc72f1165e30", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "78771e94cf1c6130", "text_span_hash": "78771e94cf1c6130", "source_file": "train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 27, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "responded\nto the highest extent, leading up to at least an average (<4 h 14’)\nor even a fast marathon time (<3 h 44′).\nDespite all the ﬁlters applied to improve data quality, a study\nwith unsupervised data from ﬁtness apps cannot be as controlled\nas", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analysis of Marathon Training\nis similar to the one presented by Allen et al. (2017), including\nthe peaks at the full and half hour marks. Thus, even though\nthe data query only required a workout between 41 and 43 km,\nthe marathon performance times indicate that the workouts were\nactual marathon races. This assumption is supported by the fact,\nthat 98.6% of the marathon workouts were performed on the\nweekend. The distribution of the maximum training heart rate\nhrmax shows realistic results similar to data observed by others\n(Roecker et al., 2002; Sarzynski et al., 2013), who determined\nmaximum heart rates using laboratory exercise tests. Thus, we\nbelieve that the maximum training heart rate hrmax also reﬂects\nthe actual maximum heart rate well.\nStrong correlations between average marathon velocity and\naverage 10 km velocity have been reported by others (Karp, 2007;\nTanda, 2011) and are veriﬁed by our data. The sorted values for\n1v10 show a heterogeneity in response to training. In comparison\nto the ﬁndings from Bouchard and Rankinen (2001), the portion\nof the population who showed a negative or no improvement\nin our investigation was higher.\nWe believe that the higher\nportion was due to the unsupervised nature of the data as well\nas the low threshold of > 0.8 · hrmax we set to verify the best\n10 km performances. However, increasing the threshold of hrmax\nto elevate the cardiopulmonary eﬀort for the best 10 km velocities\ndid no", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "responded\nto the highest extent, leading up to at least an average (<4 h 14’)\nor even a fast marathon time (<3 h 44′).\nDespite all the ﬁlters applied to improve data quality, a study\nwith unsupervised data from ﬁtness apps cannot be as controlled\nas"}
{"chunk_id": "train_app_data_marathon_2021_p0009_c0028", "source_registry_id": "src_cc72f1165e30", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0f5dab84b1ff70dc", "text_span_hash": "0f5dab84b1ff70dc", "source_file": "train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 28, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ps cannot be as controlled\nas a supervised study. For our investigation, we are not able\nto guarantee that all subjects logged and uploaded all physical\nactivities which could have inﬂuenced their 10 km or marathon\nperformance. Contextual information", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analysis of Marathon Training\nis similar to the one presented by Allen et al. (2017), including\nthe peaks at the full and half hour marks. Thus, even though\nthe data query only required a workout between 41 and 43 km,\nthe marathon performance times indicate that the workouts were\nactual marathon races. This assumption is supported by the fact,\nthat 98.6% of the marathon workouts were performed on the\nweekend. The distribution of the maximum training heart rate\nhrmax shows realistic results similar to data observed by others\n(Roecker et al., 2002; Sarzynski et al., 2013), who determined\nmaximum heart rates using laboratory exercise tests. Thus, we\nbelieve that the maximum training heart rate hrmax also reﬂects\nthe actual maximum heart rate well.\nStrong correlations between average marathon velocity and\naverage 10 km velocity have been reported by others (Karp, 2007;\nTanda, 2011) and are veriﬁed by our data. The sorted values for\n1v10 show a heterogeneity in response to training. In comparison\nto the ﬁndings from Bouchard and Rankinen (2001), the portion\nof the population who showed a negative or no improvement\nin our investigation was higher.\nWe believe that the higher\nportion was due to the unsupervised nature of the data as well\nas the low threshold of > 0.8 · hrmax we set to verify the best\n10 km performances. However, increasing the threshold of hrmax\nto elevate the cardiopulmonary eﬀort for the best 10 km velocities\ndid no", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ps cannot be as controlled\nas a supervised study. For our investigation, we are not able\nto guarantee that all subjects logged and uploaded all physical\nactivities which could have inﬂuenced their 10 km or marathon\nperformance. Contextual information"}
{"chunk_id": "train_app_data_marathon_2021_p0009_c0030", "source_registry_id": "src_cc72f1165e30", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5f15134aa22b5fd5", "text_span_hash": "5f15134aa22b5fd5", "source_file": "train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 30, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "diﬀerent\nportable sensors recreational runners use to track their workouts.\nRunning velocity was not adjusted to the elevation proﬁle\nof the running route, which neglects the impact of inclines\nand declines to training load. Additionally, phenomenon", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analysis of Marathon Training\nis similar to the one presented by Allen et al. (2017), including\nthe peaks at the full and half hour marks. Thus, even though\nthe data query only required a workout between 41 and 43 km,\nthe marathon performance times indicate that the workouts were\nactual marathon races. This assumption is supported by the fact,\nthat 98.6% of the marathon workouts were performed on the\nweekend. The distribution of the maximum training heart rate\nhrmax shows realistic results similar to data observed by others\n(Roecker et al., 2002; Sarzynski et al., 2013), who determined\nmaximum heart rates using laboratory exercise tests. Thus, we\nbelieve that the maximum training heart rate hrmax also reﬂects\nthe actual maximum heart rate well.\nStrong correlations between average marathon velocity and\naverage 10 km velocity have been reported by others (Karp, 2007;\nTanda, 2011) and are veriﬁed by our data. The sorted values for\n1v10 show a heterogeneity in response to training. In comparison\nto the ﬁndings from Bouchard and Rankinen (2001), the portion\nof the population who showed a negative or no improvement\nin our investigation was higher.\nWe believe that the higher\nportion was due to the unsupervised nature of the data as well\nas the low threshold of > 0.8 · hrmax we set to verify the best\n10 km performances. However, increasing the threshold of hrmax\nto elevate the cardiopulmonary eﬀort for the best 10 km velocities\ndid no", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "diﬀerent\nportable sensors recreational runners use to track their workouts.\nRunning velocity was not adjusted to the elevation proﬁle\nof the running route, which neglects the impact of inclines\nand declines to training load. Additionally, phenomenon"}
{"chunk_id": "train_app_data_marathon_2021_p0009_c0031", "source_registry_id": "src_cc72f1165e30", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c0641b80b259bce2", "text_span_hash": "c0641b80b259bce2", "source_file": "train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 31, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "load. Additionally, phenomenons like\n“hitting the wall” during a marathon (Buman et al., 2008) were\nFrontiers in Physiology | www.frontiersin.org\nMay 2021 | Volume 12 | Article 669884", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analysis of Marathon Training\nis similar to the one presented by Allen et al. (2017), including\nthe peaks at the full and half hour marks. Thus, even though\nthe data query only required a workout between 41 and 43 km,\nthe marathon performance times indicate that the workouts were\nactual marathon races. This assumption is supported by the fact,\nthat 98.6% of the marathon workouts were performed on the\nweekend. The distribution of the maximum training heart rate\nhrmax shows realistic results similar to data observed by others\n(Roecker et al., 2002; Sarzynski et al., 2013), who determined\nmaximum heart rates using laboratory exercise tests. Thus, we\nbelieve that the maximum training heart rate hrmax also reﬂects\nthe actual maximum heart rate well.\nStrong correlations between average marathon velocity and\naverage 10 km velocity have been reported by others (Karp, 2007;\nTanda, 2011) and are veriﬁed by our data. The sorted values for\n1v10 show a heterogeneity in response to training. In comparison\nto the ﬁndings from Bouchard and Rankinen (2001), the portion\nof the population who showed a negative or no improvement\nin our investigation was higher.\nWe believe that the higher\nportion was due to the unsupervised nature of the data as well\nas the low threshold of > 0.8 · hrmax we set to verify the best\n10 km performances. However, increasing the threshold of hrmax\nto elevate the cardiopulmonary eﬀort for the best 10 km velocities\ndid no", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "load. Additionally, phenomenons like\n“hitting the wall” during a marathon (Buman et al., 2008) were\nFrontiers in Physiology | www.frontiersin.org\nMay 2021 | Volume 12 | Article 669884"}
{"chunk_id": "train_app_data_marathon_2021_p0010_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_cc72f1165e30", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "64132974a4ba1b4a", "text_span_hash": "64132974a4ba1b4a", "source_file": "train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Analysis of Marathon Training\nnot controlled for, which might cause subjects to be classiﬁed\nin a worse marathon performance category despite a good\ntraining process. We acknowledge that these limitations might\naﬀect the results of individuals in our", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analysis of Marathon Training\nnot controlled for, which might cause subjects to be classiﬁed\nin a worse marathon performance category despite a good\ntraining process. We acknowledge that these limitations might\naﬀect the results of individuals in our analysis. However, we\nbelieve that the number of those individuals is low compared\nto the overall number of subjects and that the eﬀect for most\nof the limitations are equally distributed over the response and\nmarathon performance groups. Thus, diﬀerences between or\nwithin groups should not be aﬀected. Nevertheless, a detailed\nanalysis of the inﬂuence of those limiting factors on the response\nto training and the marathon performance shall be conducted in\nIn this work, we retrospectively analyzed 16 weeks of training\nfor 6,771 marathon ﬁnishers. We showed that unsupervised data\nrecorded by portable sensors are suitable for performing such an\nanalysis by reproducing known trends and values from literature.\nOur analysis demonstrated that a combination of maximized\ntraining volume at velocities below an individual’s marathon\nvelocity, a continuous increase in average running velocity along\nthe complete training period up to ﬁnal average marathon\nvelocity and high velocity runs (> 1.2 · vmp) not accounting for\nmore than 5% of the overall training volume was associated with\na higher 1v10 which likely beneﬁted the marathon performance\nas well. We also demonstrated that a high training vo", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Analysis of Marathon Training\nnot controlled for, which might cause subjects to be classiﬁed\nin a worse marathon performance category despite a good\ntraining process. We acknowledge that these limitations might\naﬀect the results of individuals in our"}
