ining period up to ﬁnal average marathon\nvelocity and high velocity runs (> 1.2 · vmp) not accounting for\nmore than 5% of the overall training volume was associated with\na higher 1v10 which likely beneﬁted the marathon performance\nas well. We also demonstrated that a high training vo", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "performing such an\nanalysis by reproducing known trends and values from literature.\nOur analysis demonstrated that a combination of maximized\ntraining volume at velocities below an individual’s marathon\nvelocity, a continuous increase in average run"}
{"chunk_id": "train_app_data_marathon_2021_p0010_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_cc72f1165e30", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f957e13cfaf06e11", "text_span_hash": "f957e13cfaf06e11", "source_file": "train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_app_data_marathon_2021.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "inuous increase in average running velocity along\nthe complete training period up to ﬁnal average marathon\nvelocity and high velocity runs (> 1.2 · vmp) not accounting for\nmore than 5% of the overall training volume was associated with\na higher 1v10", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analysis of Marathon Training\nnot controlled for, which might cause subjects to be classiﬁed\nin a worse marathon performance category despite a good\ntraining process. We acknowledge that these limitations might\naﬀect the results of individuals in our analysis. However, we\nbelieve that the number of those individuals is low compared\nto the overall number of subjects and that the eﬀect for most\nof the limitations are equally distributed over the response and\nmarathon performance groups. Thus, diﬀerences between or\nwithin groups should not be aﬀected. Nevertheless, a detailed\nanalysis of the inﬂuence of those limiting factors on the response\nto training and the marathon performance shall be conducted in\nIn this work, we retrospectively analyzed 16 weeks of training\nfor 6,771 marathon ﬁnishers. We showed that unsupervised data\nrecorded by portable sensors are suitable for performing such an\nanalysis by reproducing known trends and values from literature.\nOur analysis demonstrated that a combination of maximized\ntraining volume at velocities below an individual’s marathon\nvelocity, a continuous increase in average running velocity along\nthe complete training period up to ﬁnal average marathon\nvelocity and high velocity runs (> 1.2 · vmp) not accounting for\nmore than 5% of the overall training volume was associated with\na higher 1v10 which likely beneﬁted the marathon performance\nas well. We also demonstrated that a high training vo", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "inuous increase in average running velocity along\nthe complete training period up to ﬁnal average marathon\nvelocity and high velocity runs (> 1.2 · vmp) not accounting for\nmore than 5% of the overall training volume was associated with\na higher 1v10"}
