{"chunk_id": "train_low_intensity_2025_p0001_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_d55d7dc86884", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3edb4dac1f40f4a7", "text_span_hash": "3edb4dac1f40f4a7", "source_file": "train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "European Journal of Applied Physiology (2025) 125:2401–2407\nWhy low‑intensity endurance training for athletes?\n© The Author(s) 2025\nEndurance athletes prioritize most of their training in low-intensity zone. This forms a paradox, as immediate logic w", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "European Journal of Applied Physiology (2025) 125:2401–2407\nWhy low‑intensity endurance training for athletes?\n© The Author(s) 2025\nEndurance athletes prioritize most of their training in low-intensity zone. This forms a paradox, as immediate logic would\nargue against it: Acutely low-intensity exercise does not challenge the homeostasis or cardiopulmonary system of high-\nlevel athletes sufficiently to produce performance gains comparable to those from moderate- or high-intensity exercise. In\nthis perspective study, seven possible explanations for the purpose of excessive-volume low-intensity training in endurance\nathletes are proposed. The hypotheses are not all mutually exclusive. They range from a psychological need for easy days\nand the incremental benefits of low-intensity training without accumulating stress, to the possibility that such training may\nultimately be replaceable.\nKeywords  Low-intensity training · Elite athlete · Endurance training · Endurance performance\nThere is a compelling paradox: Why do endurance athletes\nspend so much time on low-intensity training when logic\nsuggests it should no longer lead to further improvements?\nEndurance training intensities can be categorized into\nthree zones delineated by two lactate/ventilation thresholds:\nlow- (LI), moderate- (MI), and high-intensity (HI) zones.\nWhen compared to other zones, low-intensity exercises do\nnot significantly challenge the cardiopulmonary system,\nd", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "European Journal of Applied Physiology (2025) 125:2401–2407\nWhy low‑intensity endurance training for athletes?\n© The Author(s) 2025\nEndurance athletes prioritize most of their training in low-intensity zone. This forms a paradox, as immediate logic w"}
{"chunk_id": "train_low_intensity_2025_p0001_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_d55d7dc86884", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "69c5e0f6dd5a80fe", "text_span_hash": "69c5e0f6dd5a80fe", "source_file": "train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "paradox, as immediate logic would\nargue against it: Acutely low-intensity exercise does not challenge the homeostasis or cardiopulmonary system of high-\nlevel athletes sufficiently to produce performance gains comparable to those from moderate- or h", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "European Journal of Applied Physiology (2025) 125:2401–2407\nWhy low‑intensity endurance training for athletes?\n© The Author(s) 2025\nEndurance athletes prioritize most of their training in low-intensity zone. This forms a paradox, as immediate logic would\nargue against it: Acutely low-intensity exercise does not challenge the homeostasis or cardiopulmonary system of high-\nlevel athletes sufficiently to produce performance gains comparable to those from moderate- or high-intensity exercise. In\nthis perspective study, seven possible explanations for the purpose of excessive-volume low-intensity training in endurance\nathletes are proposed. The hypotheses are not all mutually exclusive. They range from a psychological need for easy days\nand the incremental benefits of low-intensity training without accumulating stress, to the possibility that such training may\nultimately be replaceable.\nKeywords  Low-intensity training · Elite athlete · Endurance training · Endurance performance\nThere is a compelling paradox: Why do endurance athletes\nspend so much time on low-intensity training when logic\nsuggests it should no longer lead to further improvements?\nEndurance training intensities can be categorized into\nthree zones delineated by two lactate/ventilation thresholds:\nlow- (LI), moderate- (MI), and high-intensity (HI) zones.\nWhen compared to other zones, low-intensity exercises do\nnot significantly challenge the cardiopulmonary system,\nd", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "paradox, as immediate logic would\nargue against it: Acutely low-intensity exercise does not challenge the homeostasis or cardiopulmonary system of high-\nlevel athletes sufficiently to produce performance gains comparable to those from moderate- or h"}
