tensities can be categorized into\nthree zones delineated by two lactate/ventilation thresholds:\nlow- (LI), moderate- (MI), and high-intensity (HI) zones.\nWhen compared to other zones, low-intensity exercises do\nnot significantly challenge the cardiopulmonary system,\nd", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ty. The hypotheses are summarized in Table 1. At the end\nof the study, the role of low-intensity training for untrained\nindividuals is briefly reflected. Athletes rarely participate in\ninvasive comparative studies, leading to small sample sizes\nand p"}
{"chunk_id": "train_low_intensity_2025_p0002_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_d55d7dc86884", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5dd5ddc289deb4f4", "text_span_hash": "5dd5ddc289deb4f4", "source_file": "train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "European Journal of Applied Physiology (2025) 125:2401–2407\nTerminological note: In this study, exercise is defined\nas a single planned endurance training session. Performing\nmultiple exercises systematically over an extended period is\ndefined as tra", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "European Journal of Applied Physiology (2025) 125:2401–2407\nTerminological note: In this study, exercise is defined\nas a single planned endurance training session. Performing\nmultiple exercises systematically over an extended period is\ndefined as training.\nHypothesis 1  LI training provides maintenance or slight\nimprovements to performance without cumulating stress.\nComplete cardiac autonomic recovery from HI exer-\ncises might take over two days (Stanley et al. 2013), limit-\ning athletes to 2–3 weekly well-rested HI sessions. This\nleaves room for weekly 4–5 days that could be filled with\nLI training. Cardiac autonomic recovery from a typical LI\nexercise occurs in < 24 h (Stanley et al. 2013), sometimes\neven within hours (Seiler et al. 2007). Thus, LI exercises\nseem to contribute only to a limited degree to cardiac\nautonomic strain accumulation. While this represents\nonly one dimension of recovery, it nevertheless suggests\nthat LI exercises may be repeated more frequently than HI\nsessions. However, LI training has been observed to ben-\nefit performance to some extent in recreationally trained\nindividuals (Nuuttila et al. 2022). This could potentially\nindicate that it has the capacity to enhance performance for\nathletes, as also suggested by some of the leading exercise\nphysiologists (Sitko et al. 2025).\nIn summary, LI training might maintain, or modestly\nimprove, performance while allowing recovery from HI\nHypothesis 2  LI trai", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "European Journal of Applied Physiology (2025) 125:2401–2407\nTerminological note: In this study, exercise is defined\nas a single planned endurance training session. Performing\nmultiple exercises systematically over an extended period is\ndefined as tra"}
{"chunk_id": "train_low_intensity_2025_p0002_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_d55d7dc86884", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e2b02276f7674b2c", "text_span_hash": "e2b02276f7674b2c", "source_file": "train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "nly one dimension of recovery, it nevertheless suggests\nthat LI exercises may be repeated more frequently than HI\nsessions. However, LI training has been observed to ben-\nefit performance to some extent in recreationally trained\nindividuals (Nuuttila", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "European Journal of Applied Physiology (2025) 125:2401–2407\nTerminological note: In this study, exercise is defined\nas a single planned endurance training session. Performing\nmultiple exercises systematically over an extended period is\ndefined as training.\nHypothesis 1  LI training provides maintenance or slight\nimprovements to performance without cumulating stress.\nComplete cardiac autonomic recovery from HI exer-\ncises might take over two days (Stanley et al. 2013), limit-\ning athletes to 2–3 weekly well-rested HI sessions. This\nleaves room for weekly 4–5 days that could be filled with\nLI training. Cardiac autonomic recovery from a typical LI\nexercise occurs in < 24 h (Stanley et al. 2013), sometimes\neven within hours (Seiler et al. 2007). Thus, LI exercises\nseem to contribute only to a limited degree to cardiac\nautonomic strain accumulation. While this represents\nonly one dimension of recovery, it nevertheless suggests\nthat LI exercises may be repeated more frequently than HI\nsessions. However, LI training has been observed to ben-\nefit performance to some extent in recreationally trained\nindividuals (Nuuttila et al. 2022). This could potentially\nindicate that it has the capacity to enhance performance for\nathletes, as also suggested by some of the leading exercise\nphysiologists (Sitko et al. 2025).\nIn summary, LI training might maintain, or modestly\nimprove, performance while allowing recovery from HI\nHypothesis 2  LI trai", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "nly one dimension of recovery, it nevertheless suggests\nthat LI exercises may be repeated more frequently than HI\nsessions. However, LI training has been observed to ben-\nefit performance to some extent in recreationally trained\nindividuals (Nuuttila"}
{"chunk_id": "train_low_intensity_2025_p0002_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_d55d7dc86884", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "393878f00aaff631", "text_span_hash": "393878f00aaff631", "source_file": "train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "trained\nindividuals (Nuuttila et al. 2022). This could potentially\nindicate that it has the capacity to enhance performance for\nathletes, as also suggested by some of the leading exercise\nphysiologists (Sitko et al. 2025).\nIn summary, LI training mi", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "European Journal of Applied Physiology (2025) 125:2401–2407\nTerminological note: In this study, exercise is defined\nas a single planned endurance training session. Performing\nmultiple exercises systematically over an extended period is\ndefined as training.\nHypothesis 1  LI training provides maintenance or slight\nimprovements to performance without cumulating stress.\nComplete cardiac autonomic recovery from HI exer-\ncises might take over two days (Stanley et al. 2013), limit-\ning athletes to 2–3 weekly well-rested HI sessions. This\nleaves room for weekly 4–5 days that could be filled with\nLI training. Cardiac autonomic recovery from a typical LI\nexercise occurs in < 24 h (Stanley et al. 2013), sometimes\neven within hours (Seiler et al. 2007). Thus, LI exercises\nseem to contribute only to a limited degree to cardiac\nautonomic strain accumulation. While this represents\nonly one dimension of recovery, it nevertheless suggests\nthat LI exercises may be repeated more frequently than HI\nsessions. However, LI training has been observed to ben-\nefit performance to some extent in recreationally trained\nindividuals (Nuuttila et al. 2022). This could potentially\nindicate that it has the capacity to enhance performance for\nathletes, as also suggested by some of the leading exercise\nphysiologists (Sitko et al. 2025).\nIn summary, LI training might maintain, or modestly\nimprove, performance while allowing recovery from HI\nHypothesis 2  LI trai", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "trained\nindividuals (Nuuttila et al. 2022). This could potentially\nindicate that it has the capacity to enhance performance for\nathletes, as also suggested by some of the leading exercise\nphysiologists (Sitko et al. 2025).\nIn summary, LI training mi"}
{"chunk_id": "train_low_intensity_2025_p0002_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_d55d7dc86884", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "11ef208ff653c621", "text_span_hash": "11ef208ff653c621", "source_file": "train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "5).\nIn summary, LI training might maintain, or modestly\nimprove, performance while allowing recovery from HI\nHypothesis 2  LI training is an alternative method for molec-\nular adaptation signals.\nAt the muscle level, some endurance adaptations, such", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "European Journal of Applied Physiology (2025) 125:2401–2407\nTerminological note: In this study, exercise is defined\nas a single planned endurance training session. Performing\nmultiple exercises systematically over an extended period is\ndefined as training.\nHypothesis 1  LI training provides maintenance or slight\nimprovements to performance without cumulating stress.\nComplete cardiac autonomic recovery from HI exer-\ncises might take over two days (Stanley et al. 2013), limit-\ning athletes to 2–3 weekly well-rested HI sessions. This\nleaves room for weekly 4–5 days that could be filled with\nLI training. Cardiac autonomic recovery from a typical LI\nexercise occurs in < 24 h (Stanley et al. 2013), sometimes\neven within hours (Seiler et al. 2007). Thus, LI exercises\nseem to contribute only to a limited degree to cardiac\nautonomic strain accumulation. While this represents\nonly one dimension of recovery, it nevertheless suggests\nthat LI exercises may be repeated more frequently than HI\nsessions. However, LI training has been observed to ben-\nefit performance to some extent in recreationally trained\nindividuals (Nuuttila et al. 2022). This could potentially\nindicate that it has the capacity to enhance performance for\nathletes, as also suggested by some of the leading exercise\nphysiologists (Sitko et al. 2025).\nIn summary, LI training might maintain, or modestly\nimprove, performance while allowing recovery from HI\nHypothesis 2  LI trai", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "5).\nIn summary, LI training might maintain, or modestly\nimprove, performance while allowing recovery from HI\nHypothesis 2  LI training is an alternative method for molec-\nular adaptation signals.\nAt the muscle level, some endurance adaptations, such"}
{"chunk_id": "train_low_intensity_2025_p0002_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_d55d7dc86884", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "922c3f75a87b4721", "text_span_hash": "922c3f75a87b4721", "source_file": "train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "e endurance adaptations, such\nas mitochondrial biogenesis, are thought to be triggered by\nperoxisome proliferator-activated receptor gamma coac-\ntivator 1 (PGC-1α). This key regulator is reported to be\nactivated through various means, including free", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "European Journal of Applied Physiology (2025) 125:2401–2407\nTerminological note: In this study, exercise is defined\nas a single planned endurance training session. Performing\nmultiple exercises systematically over an extended period is\ndefined as training.\nHypothesis 1  LI training provides maintenance or slight\nimprovements to performance without cumulating stress.\nComplete cardiac autonomic recovery from HI exer-\ncises might take over two days (Stanley et al. 2013), limit-\ning athletes to 2–3 weekly well-rested HI sessions. This\nleaves room for weekly 4–5 days that could be filled with\nLI training. Cardiac autonomic recovery from a typical LI\nexercise occurs in < 24 h (Stanley et al. 2013), sometimes\neven within hours (Seiler et al. 2007). Thus, LI exercises\nseem to contribute only to a limited degree to cardiac\nautonomic strain accumulation. While this represents\nonly one dimension of recovery, it nevertheless suggests\nthat LI exercises may be repeated more frequently than HI\nsessions. However, LI training has been observed to ben-\nefit performance to some extent in recreationally trained\nindividuals (Nuuttila et al. 2022). This could potentially\nindicate that it has the capacity to enhance performance for\nathletes, as also suggested by some of the leading exercise\nphysiologists (Sitko et al. 2025).\nIn summary, LI training might maintain, or modestly\nimprove, performance while allowing recovery from HI\nHypothesis 2  LI trai", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "e endurance adaptations, such\nas mitochondrial biogenesis, are thought to be triggered by\nperoxisome proliferator-activated receptor gamma coac-\ntivator 1 (PGC-1α). This key regulator is reported to be\nactivated through various means, including free"}
{"chunk_id": "train_low_intensity_2025_p0002_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_d55d7dc86884", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "58299de817399919", "text_span_hash": "58299de817399919", "source_file": "train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "­Ca2+\n-routes (Hoppeler 2016), although not everyone agrees\n(Gurd et al. 2023). In other words, LI training might pro-\nvide an alternative adaptation pathway and, in doing so,\nintroduce variability into the training adaptation process.\nVariability, i", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "European Journal of Applied Physiology (2025) 125:2401–2407\nTerminological note: In this study, exercise is defined\nas a single planned endurance training session. Performing\nmultiple exercises systematically over an extended period is\ndefined as training.\nHypothesis 1  LI training provides maintenance or slight\nimprovements to performance without cumulating stress.\nComplete cardiac autonomic recovery from HI exer-\ncises might take over two days (Stanley et al. 2013), limit-\ning athletes to 2–3 weekly well-rested HI sessions. This\nleaves room for weekly 4–5 days that could be filled with\nLI training. Cardiac autonomic recovery from a typical LI\nexercise occurs in < 24 h (Stanley et al. 2013), sometimes\neven within hours (Seiler et al. 2007). Thus, LI exercises\nseem to contribute only to a limited degree to cardiac\nautonomic strain accumulation. While this represents\nonly one dimension of recovery, it nevertheless suggests\nthat LI exercises may be repeated more frequently than HI\nsessions. However, LI training has been observed to ben-\nefit performance to some extent in recreationally trained\nindividuals (Nuuttila et al. 2022). This could potentially\nindicate that it has the capacity to enhance performance for\nathletes, as also suggested by some of the leading exercise\nphysiologists (Sitko et al. 2025).\nIn summary, LI training might maintain, or modestly\nimprove, performance while allowing recovery from HI\nHypothesis 2  LI trai", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "­Ca2+\n-routes (Hoppeler 2016), although not everyone agrees\n(Gurd et al. 2023). In other words, LI training might pro-\nvide an alternative adaptation pathway and, in doing so,\nintroduce variability into the training adaptation process.\nVariability, i"}
{"chunk_id": "train_low_intensity_2025_p0002_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_d55d7dc86884", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7048b9ddb557c025", "text_span_hash": "7048b9ddb557c025", "source_file": "train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "tation process.\nVariability, in turn, is one of the fundamental principles\nunderpinning functional endurance periodization (Kiely\nHypothesis 3  LI training enables structural remodeling after\nyears of consistency.\nFig. 1   Mean absolute external\ntrai", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "European Journal of Applied Physiology (2025) 125:2401–2407\nTerminological note: In this study, exercise is defined\nas a single planned endurance training session. Performing\nmultiple exercises systematically over an extended period is\ndefined as training.\nHypothesis 1  LI training provides maintenance or slight\nimprovements to performance without cumulating stress.\nComplete cardiac autonomic recovery from HI exer-\ncises might take over two days (Stanley et al. 2013), limit-\ning athletes to 2–3 weekly well-rested HI sessions. This\nleaves room for weekly 4–5 days that could be filled with\nLI training. Cardiac autonomic recovery from a typical LI\nexercise occurs in < 24 h (Stanley et al. 2013), sometimes\neven within hours (Seiler et al. 2007). Thus, LI exercises\nseem to contribute only to a limited degree to cardiac\nautonomic strain accumulation. While this represents\nonly one dimension of recovery, it nevertheless suggests\nthat LI exercises may be repeated more frequently than HI\nsessions. However, LI training has been observed to ben-\nefit performance to some extent in recreationally trained\nindividuals (Nuuttila et al. 2022). This could potentially\nindicate that it has the capacity to enhance performance for\nathletes, as also suggested by some of the leading exercise\nphysiologists (Sitko et al. 2025).\nIn summary, LI training might maintain, or modestly\nimprove, performance while allowing recovery from HI\nHypothesis 2  LI trai", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "tation process.\nVariability, in turn, is one of the fundamental principles\nunderpinning functional endurance periodization (Kiely\nHypothesis 3  LI training enables structural remodeling after\nyears of consistency.\nFig. 1   Mean absolute external\ntrai"}
{"chunk_id": "train_low_intensity_2025_p0002_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_d55d7dc86884", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "dcd960555dc3f936", "text_span_hash": "dcd960555dc3f936", "source_file": "train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Mean absolute external\ntraining time in different zones\nover the 16 weeks leading up\nto the marathon, categorized by\nmarathon finish time. Moder-\nate- and high-intensity training\ntime are not drastically different\nbetween the groups. A sample\nof 1", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "European Journal of Applied Physiology (2025) 125:2401–2407\nTerminological note: In this study, exercise is defined\nas a single planned endurance training session. Performing\nmultiple exercises systematically over an extended period is\ndefined as training.\nHypothesis 1  LI training provides maintenance or slight\nimprovements to performance without cumulating stress.\nComplete cardiac autonomic recovery from HI exer-\ncises might take over two days (Stanley et al. 2013), limit-\ning athletes to 2–3 weekly well-rested HI sessions. This\nleaves room for weekly 4–5 days that could be filled with\nLI training. Cardiac autonomic recovery from a typical LI\nexercise occurs in < 24 h (Stanley et al. 2013), sometimes\neven within hours (Seiler et al. 2007). Thus, LI exercises\nseem to contribute only to a limited degree to cardiac\nautonomic strain accumulation. While this represents\nonly one dimension of recovery, it nevertheless suggests\nthat LI exercises may be repeated more frequently than HI\nsessions. However, LI training has been observed to ben-\nefit performance to some extent in recreationally trained\nindividuals (Nuuttila et al. 2022). This could potentially\nindicate that it has the capacity to enhance performance for\nathletes, as also suggested by some of the leading exercise\nphysiologists (Sitko et al. 2025).\nIn summary, LI training might maintain, or modestly\nimprove, performance while allowing recovery from HI\nHypothesis 2  LI trai", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Mean absolute external\ntraining time in different zones\nover the 16 weeks leading up\nto the marathon, categorized by\nmarathon finish time. Moder-\nate- and high-intensity training\ntime are not drastically different\nbetween the groups. A sample\nof 1"}
{"chunk_id": "train_low_intensity_2025_p0002_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_d55d7dc86884", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ac96da58b85a7482", "text_span_hash": "ac96da58b85a7482", "source_file": "train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ween the groups. A sample\nof 100,020 male runner par-\nticipants. Data extracted from\nMuniz-Pumares et al. (2024)\nTable 1   Summary of hypotheses of why endurance athletes should\nengage in excess amount of low-intensity training\nThese hypotheses are n", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "European Journal of Applied Physiology (2025) 125:2401–2407\nTerminological note: In this study, exercise is defined\nas a single planned endurance training session. Performing\nmultiple exercises systematically over an extended period is\ndefined as training.\nHypothesis 1  LI training provides maintenance or slight\nimprovements to performance without cumulating stress.\nComplete cardiac autonomic recovery from HI exer-\ncises might take over two days (Stanley et al. 2013), limit-\ning athletes to 2–3 weekly well-rested HI sessions. This\nleaves room for weekly 4–5 days that could be filled with\nLI training. Cardiac autonomic recovery from a typical LI\nexercise occurs in < 24 h (Stanley et al. 2013), sometimes\neven within hours (Seiler et al. 2007). Thus, LI exercises\nseem to contribute only to a limited degree to cardiac\nautonomic strain accumulation. While this represents\nonly one dimension of recovery, it nevertheless suggests\nthat LI exercises may be repeated more frequently than HI\nsessions. However, LI training has been observed to ben-\nefit performance to some extent in recreationally trained\nindividuals (Nuuttila et al. 2022). This could potentially\nindicate that it has the capacity to enhance performance for\nathletes, as also suggested by some of the leading exercise\nphysiologists (Sitko et al. 2025).\nIn summary, LI training might maintain, or modestly\nimprove, performance while allowing recovery from HI\nHypothesis 2  LI trai", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ween the groups. A sample\nof 100,020 male runner par-\nticipants. Data extracted from\nMuniz-Pumares et al. (2024)\nTable 1   Summary of hypotheses of why endurance athletes should\nengage in excess amount of low-intensity training\nThese hypotheses are n"}
{"chunk_id": "train_low_intensity_2025_p0002_c0016", "source_registry_id": "src_d55d7dc86884", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1f5cbdce82b37afc", "text_span_hash": "1f5cbdce82b37afc", "source_file": "train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 16, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "bles structural remodeling after years of consistency\n4. It affects something that has not yet been measured\n5. It is needed psychologically\n6. It strengthens high-intensity training adaptations\n7. LI training is replaceable", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "European Journal of Applied Physiology (2025) 125:2401–2407\nTerminological note: In this study, exercise is defined\nas a single planned endurance training session. Performing\nmultiple exercises systematically over an extended period is\ndefined as training.\nHypothesis 1  LI training provides maintenance or slight\nimprovements to performance without cumulating stress.\nComplete cardiac autonomic recovery from HI exer-\ncises might take over two days (Stanley et al. 2013), limit-\ning athletes to 2–3 weekly well-rested HI sessions. This\nleaves room for weekly 4–5 days that could be filled with\nLI training. Cardiac autonomic recovery from a typical LI\nexercise occurs in < 24 h (Stanley et al. 2013), sometimes\neven within hours (Seiler et al. 2007). Thus, LI exercises\nseem to contribute only to a limited degree to cardiac\nautonomic strain accumulation. While this represents\nonly one dimension of recovery, it nevertheless suggests\nthat LI exercises may be repeated more frequently than HI\nsessions. However, LI training has been observed to ben-\nefit performance to some extent in recreationally trained\nindividuals (Nuuttila et al. 2022). This could potentially\nindicate that it has the capacity to enhance performance for\nathletes, as also suggested by some of the leading exercise\nphysiologists (Sitko et al. 2025).\nIn summary, LI training might maintain, or modestly\nimprove, performance while allowing recovery from HI\nHypothesis 2  LI trai", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "bles structural remodeling after years of consistency\n4. It affects something that has not yet been measured\n5. It is needed psychologically\n6. It strengthens high-intensity training adaptations\n7. LI training is replaceable"}
{"chunk_id": "train_low_intensity_2025_p0003_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_d55d7dc86884", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "206c182d934538e1", "text_span_hash": "206c182d934538e1", "source_file": "train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "European Journal of Applied Physiology (2025) 125:2401–2407\nTraining interventions typically last < 4 months. How-\never, cross-sectional studies show that over 5 years of con-\nsistent endurance training leads to greater physiological\nadaptations comp", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "European Journal of Applied Physiology (2025) 125:2401–2407\nTraining interventions typically last < 4 months. How-\never, cross-sectional studies show that over 5 years of con-\nsistent endurance training leads to greater physiological\nadaptations compared to < 2 years (Buzza 2018). These dif-\nferences include improved oxygen utilization at the muscle\nlevel and higher maximal oxygen uptake ­(VO2max) values\nDetermining whether the additional long-term adap-\ntations result specifically from high-volume LI training\nis challenging, as they may also result from a synergistic\ncombination of LI, MI, and HI training or other factors like\nnutrition and recovery or genetics. However, indirect evi-\ndence suggests LI training might play a role. An analysis of\nover 150,000 marathoners found that while weekly absolute\nMI and HI training times remained relatively stable regard-\nless of marathon performance, high-level runners had by far\nthe greatest LI training time (Muniz-Pumares et al. 2024).\nThus, it is possible that consistent high-volume LI training\nresult in structural adaptations that separate champions from\namateurs, although the effects of well-timed and sufficiently\nintense MI and HI exercises, genetics, and sufficient nutri-\ntion and rest cannot be ruled out.\nThis ‘structural hypothesis’ is not a new proposition.\nSome of the leading experts have also suggested LI training\nto predispose athletes to peripheral and even subtle central\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "European Journal of Applied Physiology (2025) 125:2401–2407\nTraining interventions typically last < 4 months. How-\never, cross-sectional studies show that over 5 years of con-\nsistent endurance training leads to greater physiological\nadaptations comp"}
{"chunk_id": "train_low_intensity_2025_p0003_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_d55d7dc86884", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "610ebaeedbfe2054", "text_span_hash": "610ebaeedbfe2054", "source_file": "train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "physiological\nadaptations compared to < 2 years (Buzza 2018). These dif-\nferences include improved oxygen utilization at the muscle\nlevel and higher maximal oxygen uptake ­(VO2max) values\nDetermining whether the additional long-term adap-\ntations res", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "European Journal of Applied Physiology (2025) 125:2401–2407\nTraining interventions typically last < 4 months. How-\never, cross-sectional studies show that over 5 years of con-\nsistent endurance training leads to greater physiological\nadaptations compared to < 2 years (Buzza 2018). These dif-\nferences include improved oxygen utilization at the muscle\nlevel and higher maximal oxygen uptake ­(VO2max) values\nDetermining whether the additional long-term adap-\ntations result specifically from high-volume LI training\nis challenging, as they may also result from a synergistic\ncombination of LI, MI, and HI training or other factors like\nnutrition and recovery or genetics. However, indirect evi-\ndence suggests LI training might play a role. An analysis of\nover 150,000 marathoners found that while weekly absolute\nMI and HI training times remained relatively stable regard-\nless of marathon performance, high-level runners had by far\nthe greatest LI training time (Muniz-Pumares et al. 2024).\nThus, it is possible that consistent high-volume LI training\nresult in structural adaptations that separate champions from\namateurs, although the effects of well-timed and sufficiently\nintense MI and HI exercises, genetics, and sufficient nutri-\ntion and rest cannot be ruled out.\nThis ‘structural hypothesis’ is not a new proposition.\nSome of the leading experts have also suggested LI training\nto predispose athletes to peripheral and even subtle central\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "physiological\nadaptations compared to < 2 years (Buzza 2018). These dif-\nferences include improved oxygen utilization at the muscle\nlevel and higher maximal oxygen uptake ­(VO2max) values\nDetermining whether the additional long-term adap-\ntations res"}
{"chunk_id": "train_low_intensity_2025_p0003_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_d55d7dc86884", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "27892f3da29d4058", "text_span_hash": "27892f3da29d4058", "source_file": "train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "r genetics. However, indirect evi-\ndence suggests LI training might play a role. An analysis of\nover 150,000 marathoners found that while weekly absolute\nMI and HI training times remained relatively stable regard-\nless of marathon performance, high-l", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "European Journal of Applied Physiology (2025) 125:2401–2407\nTraining interventions typically last < 4 months. How-\never, cross-sectional studies show that over 5 years of con-\nsistent endurance training leads to greater physiological\nadaptations compared to < 2 years (Buzza 2018). These dif-\nferences include improved oxygen utilization at the muscle\nlevel and higher maximal oxygen uptake ­(VO2max) values\nDetermining whether the additional long-term adap-\ntations result specifically from high-volume LI training\nis challenging, as they may also result from a synergistic\ncombination of LI, MI, and HI training or other factors like\nnutrition and recovery or genetics. However, indirect evi-\ndence suggests LI training might play a role. An analysis of\nover 150,000 marathoners found that while weekly absolute\nMI and HI training times remained relatively stable regard-\nless of marathon performance, high-level runners had by far\nthe greatest LI training time (Muniz-Pumares et al. 2024).\nThus, it is possible that consistent high-volume LI training\nresult in structural adaptations that separate champions from\namateurs, although the effects of well-timed and sufficiently\nintense MI and HI exercises, genetics, and sufficient nutri-\ntion and rest cannot be ruled out.\nThis ‘structural hypothesis’ is not a new proposition.\nSome of the leading experts have also suggested LI training\nto predispose athletes to peripheral and even subtle central\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "r genetics. However, indirect evi-\ndence suggests LI training might play a role. An analysis of\nover 150,000 marathoners found that while weekly absolute\nMI and HI training times remained relatively stable regard-\nless of marathon performance, high-l"}
{"chunk_id": "train_low_intensity_2025_p0003_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_d55d7dc86884", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "d781328d90f9c7ce", "text_span_hash": "d781328d90f9c7ce", "source_file": "train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "osition.\nSome of the leading experts have also suggested LI training\nto predispose athletes to peripheral and even subtle central\nadaptations (Sitko et al. 2025). LI training may be involved,\namong other factors, in the following structural changes.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "European Journal of Applied Physiology (2025) 125:2401–2407\nTraining interventions typically last < 4 months. How-\never, cross-sectional studies show that over 5 years of con-\nsistent endurance training leads to greater physiological\nadaptations compared to < 2 years (Buzza 2018). These dif-\nferences include improved oxygen utilization at the muscle\nlevel and higher maximal oxygen uptake ­(VO2max) values\nDetermining whether the additional long-term adap-\ntations result specifically from high-volume LI training\nis challenging, as they may also result from a synergistic\ncombination of LI, MI, and HI training or other factors like\nnutrition and recovery or genetics. However, indirect evi-\ndence suggests LI training might play a role. An analysis of\nover 150,000 marathoners found that while weekly absolute\nMI and HI training times remained relatively stable regard-\nless of marathon performance, high-level runners had by far\nthe greatest LI training time (Muniz-Pumares et al. 2024).\nThus, it is possible that consistent high-volume LI training\nresult in structural adaptations that separate champions from\namateurs, although the effects of well-timed and sufficiently\nintense MI and HI exercises, genetics, and sufficient nutri-\ntion and rest cannot be ruled out.\nThis ‘structural hypothesis’ is not a new proposition.\nSome of the leading experts have also suggested LI training\nto predispose athletes to peripheral and even subtle central\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "osition.\nSome of the leading experts have also suggested LI training\nto predispose athletes to peripheral and even subtle central\nadaptations (Sitko et al. 2025). LI training may be involved,\namong other factors, in the following structural changes."}
{"chunk_id": "train_low_intensity_2025_p0003_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_d55d7dc86884", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "501546f952e91490", "text_span_hash": "501546f952e91490", "source_file": "train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "following structural changes.\nStructure of the heart\nLI training might be involved to gradually remodel the struc-\nture and function of the left ventricle. To support this view,\nthe diameter of the left ventricular has increased during the\ncareers of", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "European Journal of Applied Physiology (2025) 125:2401–2407\nTraining interventions typically last < 4 months. How-\never, cross-sectional studies show that over 5 years of con-\nsistent endurance training leads to greater physiological\nadaptations compared to < 2 years (Buzza 2018). These dif-\nferences include improved oxygen utilization at the muscle\nlevel and higher maximal oxygen uptake ­(VO2max) values\nDetermining whether the additional long-term adap-\ntations result specifically from high-volume LI training\nis challenging, as they may also result from a synergistic\ncombination of LI, MI, and HI training or other factors like\nnutrition and recovery or genetics. However, indirect evi-\ndence suggests LI training might play a role. An analysis of\nover 150,000 marathoners found that while weekly absolute\nMI and HI training times remained relatively stable regard-\nless of marathon performance, high-level runners had by far\nthe greatest LI training time (Muniz-Pumares et al. 2024).\nThus, it is possible that consistent high-volume LI training\nresult in structural adaptations that separate champions from\namateurs, although the effects of well-timed and sufficiently\nintense MI and HI exercises, genetics, and sufficient nutri-\ntion and rest cannot be ruled out.\nThis ‘structural hypothesis’ is not a new proposition.\nSome of the leading experts have also suggested LI training\nto predispose athletes to peripheral and even subtle central\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "following structural changes.\nStructure of the heart\nLI training might be involved to gradually remodel the struc-\nture and function of the left ventricle. To support this view,\nthe diameter of the left ventricular has increased during the\ncareers of"}
{"chunk_id": "train_low_intensity_2025_p0003_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_d55d7dc86884", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "445ed9dd90f47df7", "text_span_hash": "445ed9dd90f47df7", "source_file": "train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ding to this hypothesis, high-volume LI\ntraining gradually helps to stretch the pericardium, allowing\nthe heart to enlarge over time.\nAngiogenesis is driven mainly by shear stress and muscle\nstretch (Hoppeler 2016), both present not only during HI\nbu", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "European Journal of Applied Physiology (2025) 125:2401–2407\nTraining interventions typically last < 4 months. How-\never, cross-sectional studies show that over 5 years of con-\nsistent endurance training leads to greater physiological\nadaptations compared to < 2 years (Buzza 2018). These dif-\nferences include improved oxygen utilization at the muscle\nlevel and higher maximal oxygen uptake ­(VO2max) values\nDetermining whether the additional long-term adap-\ntations result specifically from high-volume LI training\nis challenging, as they may also result from a synergistic\ncombination of LI, MI, and HI training or other factors like\nnutrition and recovery or genetics. However, indirect evi-\ndence suggests LI training might play a role. An analysis of\nover 150,000 marathoners found that while weekly absolute\nMI and HI training times remained relatively stable regard-\nless of marathon performance, high-level runners had by far\nthe greatest LI training time (Muniz-Pumares et al. 2024).\nThus, it is possible that consistent high-volume LI training\nresult in structural adaptations that separate champions from\namateurs, although the effects of well-timed and sufficiently\nintense MI and HI exercises, genetics, and sufficient nutri-\ntion and rest cannot be ruled out.\nThis ‘structural hypothesis’ is not a new proposition.\nSome of the leading experts have also suggested LI training\nto predispose athletes to peripheral and even subtle central\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ding to this hypothesis, high-volume LI\ntraining gradually helps to stretch the pericardium, allowing\nthe heart to enlarge over time.\nAngiogenesis is driven mainly by shear stress and muscle\nstretch (Hoppeler 2016), both present not only during HI\nbu"}
