{"chunk_id": "train_low_intensity_2025_p0003_c0020", "source_registry_id": "src_d55d7dc86884", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "cb43afcc9de1483a", "text_span_hash": "cb43afcc9de1483a", "source_file": "train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 20, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ssive LI training. Indeed, a high\nvolume of LI training has recently been associated with good\ndurability (Spragg et al. 2023).\nRecovery is another surprisingly overlooked variable.\nWhile athletes recover from submaximal exercises faster\nthan recreat", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "European Journal of Applied Physiology (2025) 125:2401–2407\nTraining interventions typically last < 4 months. How-\never, cross-sectional studies show that over 5 years of con-\nsistent endurance training leads to greater physiological\nadaptations compared to < 2 years (Buzza 2018). These dif-\nferences include improved oxygen utilization at the muscle\nlevel and higher maximal oxygen uptake ­(VO2max) values\nDetermining whether the additional long-term adap-\ntations result specifically from high-volume LI training\nis challenging, as they may also result from a synergistic\ncombination of LI, MI, and HI training or other factors like\nnutrition and recovery or genetics. However, indirect evi-\ndence suggests LI training might play a role. An analysis of\nover 150,000 marathoners found that while weekly absolute\nMI and HI training times remained relatively stable regard-\nless of marathon performance, high-level runners had by far\nthe greatest LI training time (Muniz-Pumares et al. 2024).\nThus, it is possible that consistent high-volume LI training\nresult in structural adaptations that separate champions from\namateurs, although the effects of well-timed and sufficiently\nintense MI and HI exercises, genetics, and sufficient nutri-\ntion and rest cannot be ruled out.\nThis ‘structural hypothesis’ is not a new proposition.\nSome of the leading experts have also suggested LI training\nto predispose athletes to peripheral and even subtle central\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ssive LI training. Indeed, a high\nvolume of LI training has recently been associated with good\ndurability (Spragg et al. 2023).\nRecovery is another surprisingly overlooked variable.\nWhile athletes recover from submaximal exercises faster\nthan recreat"}
{"chunk_id": "train_low_intensity_2025_p0003_c0021", "source_registry_id": "src_d55d7dc86884", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "04fe7b21950cb5e9", "text_span_hash": "04fe7b21950cb5e9", "source_file": "train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 21, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "exercises faster\nthan recreationally active individuals (Seiler et al. 2007), it\nis unclear which type of training optimally affects recovery\nability. It might be that high volume of LI training could be\ninvolved in improving the ability to recover", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "European Journal of Applied Physiology (2025) 125:2401–2407\nTraining interventions typically last < 4 months. How-\never, cross-sectional studies show that over 5 years of con-\nsistent endurance training leads to greater physiological\nadaptations compared to < 2 years (Buzza 2018). These dif-\nferences include improved oxygen utilization at the muscle\nlevel and higher maximal oxygen uptake ­(VO2max) values\nDetermining whether the additional long-term adap-\ntations result specifically from high-volume LI training\nis challenging, as they may also result from a synergistic\ncombination of LI, MI, and HI training or other factors like\nnutrition and recovery or genetics. However, indirect evi-\ndence suggests LI training might play a role. An analysis of\nover 150,000 marathoners found that while weekly absolute\nMI and HI training times remained relatively stable regard-\nless of marathon performance, high-level runners had by far\nthe greatest LI training time (Muniz-Pumares et al. 2024).\nThus, it is possible that consistent high-volume LI training\nresult in structural adaptations that separate champions from\namateurs, although the effects of well-timed and sufficiently\nintense MI and HI exercises, genetics, and sufficient nutri-\ntion and rest cannot be ruled out.\nThis ‘structural hypothesis’ is not a new proposition.\nSome of the leading experts have also suggested LI training\nto predispose athletes to peripheral and even subtle central\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "exercises faster\nthan recreationally active individuals (Seiler et al. 2007), it\nis unclear which type of training optimally affects recovery\nability. It might be that high volume of LI training could be\ninvolved in improving the ability to recover"}
{"chunk_id": "train_low_intensity_2025_p0003_c0022", "source_registry_id": "src_d55d7dc86884", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "6db358fd82e813fd", "text_span_hash": "6db358fd82e813fd", "source_file": "train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 22, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "roving the ability to recover more swiftly\nafter an exercise session and also to maintain homeostasis\nin exercise (Issurin and Dreshman 2012).\nIn summary, years of dedicated LI training may be\ninvolved in improving components that are not yet fully\nu", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "European Journal of Applied Physiology (2025) 125:2401–2407\nTraining interventions typically last < 4 months. How-\never, cross-sectional studies show that over 5 years of con-\nsistent endurance training leads to greater physiological\nadaptations compared to < 2 years (Buzza 2018). These dif-\nferences include improved oxygen utilization at the muscle\nlevel and higher maximal oxygen uptake ­(VO2max) values\nDetermining whether the additional long-term adap-\ntations result specifically from high-volume LI training\nis challenging, as they may also result from a synergistic\ncombination of LI, MI, and HI training or other factors like\nnutrition and recovery or genetics. However, indirect evi-\ndence suggests LI training might play a role. An analysis of\nover 150,000 marathoners found that while weekly absolute\nMI and HI training times remained relatively stable regard-\nless of marathon performance, high-level runners had by far\nthe greatest LI training time (Muniz-Pumares et al. 2024).\nThus, it is possible that consistent high-volume LI training\nresult in structural adaptations that separate champions from\namateurs, although the effects of well-timed and sufficiently\nintense MI and HI exercises, genetics, and sufficient nutri-\ntion and rest cannot be ruled out.\nThis ‘structural hypothesis’ is not a new proposition.\nSome of the leading experts have also suggested LI training\nto predispose athletes to peripheral and even subtle central\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "roving the ability to recover more swiftly\nafter an exercise session and also to maintain homeostasis\nin exercise (Issurin and Dreshman 2012).\nIn summary, years of dedicated LI training may be\ninvolved in improving components that are not yet fully\nu"}
{"chunk_id": "train_low_intensity_2025_p0003_c0023", "source_registry_id": "src_d55d7dc86884", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f10a5b57a73d7709", "text_span_hash": "f10a5b57a73d7709", "source_file": "train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 23, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "nents that are not yet fully\nunderstood or clearly defined.\nHypothesis 5  LI training is needed psychologically.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "European Journal of Applied Physiology (2025) 125:2401–2407\nTraining interventions typically last < 4 months. How-\never, cross-sectional studies show that over 5 years of con-\nsistent endurance training leads to greater physiological\nadaptations compared to < 2 years (Buzza 2018). These dif-\nferences include improved oxygen utilization at the muscle\nlevel and higher maximal oxygen uptake ­(VO2max) values\nDetermining whether the additional long-term adap-\ntations result specifically from high-volume LI training\nis challenging, as they may also result from a synergistic\ncombination of LI, MI, and HI training or other factors like\nnutrition and recovery or genetics. However, indirect evi-\ndence suggests LI training might play a role. An analysis of\nover 150,000 marathoners found that while weekly absolute\nMI and HI training times remained relatively stable regard-\nless of marathon performance, high-level runners had by far\nthe greatest LI training time (Muniz-Pumares et al. 2024).\nThus, it is possible that consistent high-volume LI training\nresult in structural adaptations that separate champions from\namateurs, although the effects of well-timed and sufficiently\nintense MI and HI exercises, genetics, and sufficient nutri-\ntion and rest cannot be ruled out.\nThis ‘structural hypothesis’ is not a new proposition.\nSome of the leading experts have also suggested LI training\nto predispose athletes to peripheral and even subtle central\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "nents that are not yet fully\nunderstood or clearly defined.\nHypothesis 5  LI training is needed psychologically."}
{"chunk_id": "train_low_intensity_2025_p0004_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_d55d7dc86884", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5a1b5abcfdfbaadc", "text_span_hash": "5a1b5abcfdfbaadc", "source_file": "train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "European Journal of Applied Physiology (2025) 125:2401–2407\nAcutely, LI exercise improves mood in both untrained\nand trained individuals, while HI exercise decreases it\n(Berger and Motl 2000). Moreover, in athletes, short-term\nLI training decreases m", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "European Journal of Applied Physiology (2025) 125:2401–2407\nAcutely, LI exercise improves mood in both untrained\nand trained individuals, while HI exercise decreases it\n(Berger and Motl 2000). Moreover, in athletes, short-term\nLI training decreases mental fatigue (Faude et al. 2009),\nwhile HI training block increases distress (Halson et al.\n2002). In summary, emphasized MI or HI training might\nsimply be psychologically too demanding.\nHypothesis 6  LI training strengthens HI training\nWalking and high step count alone may not be suffi-\ncient to increase ­VO2max at least in individuals with some\ntraining background (Swain and Franklin 2002). However,\ncombining a high total volume of low-intensity physical\nactivity with HI training appears to be more effective than\nHI training alone in untrained (Burton et al. 2021) and\nmoderately trained individuals (Hautala et al. 2012). The\nhypothesis in this context is that physical activity, in the\nform of LI training within athletic programming, helps the\nbody not to ‘resist’ training adaptation process (Burton\nHypothesis 7  LI training is replaceable.\nOne option is that, with appropriate programming and\ntraining dose control, the volume of LI training could be\ndramatically reduced and replaced by higher-intensity train-\ning. A concern with this approach is the increased risk of\novertraining syndrome and a higher injury rate.\nWhile overtraining syndrome may be witnessed in the\nfield, very li", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "European Journal of Applied Physiology (2025) 125:2401–2407\nAcutely, LI exercise improves mood in both untrained\nand trained individuals, while HI exercise decreases it\n(Berger and Motl 2000). Moreover, in athletes, short-term\nLI training decreases m"}
{"chunk_id": "train_low_intensity_2025_p0004_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_d55d7dc86884", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "90e52aef071c81df", "text_span_hash": "90e52aef071c81df", "source_file": "train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "pothesis 6  LI training strengthens HI training\nWalking and high step count alone may not be suffi-\ncient to increase ­VO2max at least in individuals with some\ntraining background (Swain and Franklin 2002). However,\ncombining a high total volume of l", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "European Journal of Applied Physiology (2025) 125:2401–2407\nAcutely, LI exercise improves mood in both untrained\nand trained individuals, while HI exercise decreases it\n(Berger and Motl 2000). Moreover, in athletes, short-term\nLI training decreases mental fatigue (Faude et al. 2009),\nwhile HI training block increases distress (Halson et al.\n2002). In summary, emphasized MI or HI training might\nsimply be psychologically too demanding.\nHypothesis 6  LI training strengthens HI training\nWalking and high step count alone may not be suffi-\ncient to increase ­VO2max at least in individuals with some\ntraining background (Swain and Franklin 2002). However,\ncombining a high total volume of low-intensity physical\nactivity with HI training appears to be more effective than\nHI training alone in untrained (Burton et al. 2021) and\nmoderately trained individuals (Hautala et al. 2012). The\nhypothesis in this context is that physical activity, in the\nform of LI training within athletic programming, helps the\nbody not to ‘resist’ training adaptation process (Burton\nHypothesis 7  LI training is replaceable.\nOne option is that, with appropriate programming and\ntraining dose control, the volume of LI training could be\ndramatically reduced and replaced by higher-intensity train-\ning. A concern with this approach is the increased risk of\novertraining syndrome and a higher injury rate.\nWhile overtraining syndrome may be witnessed in the\nfield, very li", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "pothesis 6  LI training strengthens HI training\nWalking and high step count alone may not be suffi-\ncient to increase ­VO2max at least in individuals with some\ntraining background (Swain and Franklin 2002). However,\ncombining a high total volume of l"}
