rol, the volume of LI training could be\ndramatically reduced and replaced by higher-intensity train-\ning. A concern with this approach is the increased risk of\novertraining syndrome and a higher injury rate.\nWhile overtraining syndrome may be witnessed in the\nfield, very li", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": ". The\nhypothesis in this context is that physical activity, in the\nform of LI training within athletic programming, helps the\nbody not to ‘resist’ training adaptation process (Burton\nHypothesis 7  LI training is replaceable.\nOne option is that, with"}
{"chunk_id": "train_low_intensity_2025_p0004_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_d55d7dc86884", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3d989ff5b3dbecba", "text_span_hash": "3d989ff5b3dbecba", "source_file": "train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "train-\ning might be considered feasible training programming.\nIn fact, there are examples where a year-long HI-focused\ntraining has not been at least counterproductive for ath-\nletes (Gaskill et al. 1999). Moreover, such training has\nbeen shown to b", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "European Journal of Applied Physiology (2025) 125:2401–2407\nAcutely, LI exercise improves mood in both untrained\nand trained individuals, while HI exercise decreases it\n(Berger and Motl 2000). Moreover, in athletes, short-term\nLI training decreases mental fatigue (Faude et al. 2009),\nwhile HI training block increases distress (Halson et al.\n2002). In summary, emphasized MI or HI training might\nsimply be psychologically too demanding.\nHypothesis 6  LI training strengthens HI training\nWalking and high step count alone may not be suffi-\ncient to increase ­VO2max at least in individuals with some\ntraining background (Swain and Franklin 2002). However,\ncombining a high total volume of low-intensity physical\nactivity with HI training appears to be more effective than\nHI training alone in untrained (Burton et al. 2021) and\nmoderately trained individuals (Hautala et al. 2012). The\nhypothesis in this context is that physical activity, in the\nform of LI training within athletic programming, helps the\nbody not to ‘resist’ training adaptation process (Burton\nHypothesis 7  LI training is replaceable.\nOne option is that, with appropriate programming and\ntraining dose control, the volume of LI training could be\ndramatically reduced and replaced by higher-intensity train-\ning. A concern with this approach is the increased risk of\novertraining syndrome and a higher injury rate.\nWhile overtraining syndrome may be witnessed in the\nfield, very li", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "train-\ning might be considered feasible training programming.\nIn fact, there are examples where a year-long HI-focused\ntraining has not been at least counterproductive for ath-\nletes (Gaskill et al. 1999). Moreover, such training has\nbeen shown to b"}
{"chunk_id": "train_low_intensity_2025_p0004_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_d55d7dc86884", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1c42d00136fb85af", "text_span_hash": "1c42d00136fb85af", "source_file": "train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "h training has\nbeen shown to be both superior (Evertsen et al. 1999) and\ninferior (Stöggl and Sperlich 2014) to traditional training\nprogramming for athletes.\nIndeed, the comparison between intensified training\nand traditional polarized/pyramidal tra", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "European Journal of Applied Physiology (2025) 125:2401–2407\nAcutely, LI exercise improves mood in both untrained\nand trained individuals, while HI exercise decreases it\n(Berger and Motl 2000). Moreover, in athletes, short-term\nLI training decreases mental fatigue (Faude et al. 2009),\nwhile HI training block increases distress (Halson et al.\n2002). In summary, emphasized MI or HI training might\nsimply be psychologically too demanding.\nHypothesis 6  LI training strengthens HI training\nWalking and high step count alone may not be suffi-\ncient to increase ­VO2max at least in individuals with some\ntraining background (Swain and Franklin 2002). However,\ncombining a high total volume of low-intensity physical\nactivity with HI training appears to be more effective than\nHI training alone in untrained (Burton et al. 2021) and\nmoderately trained individuals (Hautala et al. 2012). The\nhypothesis in this context is that physical activity, in the\nform of LI training within athletic programming, helps the\nbody not to ‘resist’ training adaptation process (Burton\nHypothesis 7  LI training is replaceable.\nOne option is that, with appropriate programming and\ntraining dose control, the volume of LI training could be\ndramatically reduced and replaced by higher-intensity train-\ning. A concern with this approach is the increased risk of\novertraining syndrome and a higher injury rate.\nWhile overtraining syndrome may be witnessed in the\nfield, very li", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "h training has\nbeen shown to be both superior (Evertsen et al. 1999) and\ninferior (Stöggl and Sperlich 2014) to traditional training\nprogramming for athletes.\nIndeed, the comparison between intensified training\nand traditional polarized/pyramidal tra"}
{"chunk_id": "train_low_intensity_2025_p0004_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_d55d7dc86884", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "d219f6d65c1a821a", "text_span_hash": "d219f6d65c1a821a", "source_file": "train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "tional polarized/pyramidal training involving a\nhigh volume of LI training reveals no clear difference in\nperformance improvements, whereas ­VO2max could be bet-\nter improved by traditional training (Silva Oliveira et al.\nAthletes may be hesitant to", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "European Journal of Applied Physiology (2025) 125:2401–2407\nAcutely, LI exercise improves mood in both untrained\nand trained individuals, while HI exercise decreases it\n(Berger and Motl 2000). Moreover, in athletes, short-term\nLI training decreases mental fatigue (Faude et al. 2009),\nwhile HI training block increases distress (Halson et al.\n2002). In summary, emphasized MI or HI training might\nsimply be psychologically too demanding.\nHypothesis 6  LI training strengthens HI training\nWalking and high step count alone may not be suffi-\ncient to increase ­VO2max at least in individuals with some\ntraining background (Swain and Franklin 2002). However,\ncombining a high total volume of low-intensity physical\nactivity with HI training appears to be more effective than\nHI training alone in untrained (Burton et al. 2021) and\nmoderately trained individuals (Hautala et al. 2012). The\nhypothesis in this context is that physical activity, in the\nform of LI training within athletic programming, helps the\nbody not to ‘resist’ training adaptation process (Burton\nHypothesis 7  LI training is replaceable.\nOne option is that, with appropriate programming and\ntraining dose control, the volume of LI training could be\ndramatically reduced and replaced by higher-intensity train-\ning. A concern with this approach is the increased risk of\novertraining syndrome and a higher injury rate.\nWhile overtraining syndrome may be witnessed in the\nfield, very li", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "tional polarized/pyramidal training involving a\nhigh volume of LI training reveals no clear difference in\nperformance improvements, whereas ­VO2max could be bet-\nter improved by traditional training (Silva Oliveira et al.\nAthletes may be hesitant to"}
{"chunk_id": "train_low_intensity_2025_p0004_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_d55d7dc86884", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3b48ba26ce416ae3", "text_span_hash": "3b48ba26ce416ae3", "source_file": "train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_low_intensity_2025.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": ".\nAthletes may be hesitant to abandon LI training, as they\nhave learned to rely on it and it has proved to be effective.\nHowever, it might be that there is not a strict necessity to\ninclude a high volume of LI training after all. Carefully\nplanned in", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "European Journal of Applied Physiology (2025) 125:2401–2407\nAcutely, LI exercise improves mood in both untrained\nand trained individuals, while HI exercise decreases it\n(Berger and Motl 2000). Moreover, in athletes, short-term\nLI training decreases mental fatigue (Faude et al. 2009),\nwhile HI training block increases distress (Halson et al.\n2002). In summary, emphasized MI or HI training might\nsimply be psychologically too demanding.\nHypothesis 6  LI training strengthens HI training\nWalking and high step count alone may not be suffi-\ncient to increase ­VO2max at least in individuals with some\ntraining background (Swain and Franklin 2002). However,\ncombining a high total volume of low-intensity physical\nactivity with HI training appears to be more effective than\nHI training alone in untrained (Burton et al. 2021) and\nmoderately trained individuals (Hautala et al. 2012). The\nhypothesis in this context is that physical activity, in the\nform of LI training within athletic programming, helps the\nbody not to ‘resist’ training adaptation process (Burton\nHypothesis 7  LI training is replaceable.\nOne option is that, with appropriate programming and\ntraining dose control, the volume of LI training could be\ndramatically reduced and replaced by higher-intensity train-\ning. A concern with this approach is the increased risk of\novertraining syndrome and a higher injury rate.\nWhile overtraining syndrome may be witnessed in the\nfield, very li", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": ".\nAthletes may be hesitant to abandon LI training, as they\nhave learned to rely on it and it has proved to be effective.\nHowever, it might be that there is not a strict necessity to\ninclude a high volume of LI training after all. Carefully\nplanned in"}
