es as well as physicians and event organizers, were\nselected for this review. Altogether, we found 1,021 arti-\ncles across the three databases. After an initial screening\nprocess (title and abstract) of the retrieved literature, 729\nwere excluded since they did not meet the inclusion cri-\nt", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "as well as the different event types\n(e.g., mountain marathons, road races, off-road races,\netc.). Other articles investigating solely cycling, triath-\nlon, stress tests, or other sports were excluded. Particu-\nlarly, articles of relevant significanc"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0003_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c1c0585b15f8d29d", "text_span_hash": "c1c0585b15f8d29d", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "well known for its many beneficial\neffects on cardiovascular health, such as lower resting\narterial blood pressure, improved endothelial function,\nand higher tolerance of myocardial ischemia [3]. Nev-\nertheless, a marathon puts immense stress on the", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\n“bone*”, “musculoskeletal system*”, “musculoskeletal dis-\neas*”, “orthopedic disord*”, “osteoarthrit*”, “central nerv-\nous system*”, “cerebrospinal axi*”, “brain*”, “spinal cord*”,\n“cognition*”, “cognitive function*”, “sleep*”, “sleep habit*”,\nand “sleeping habit*”. The full search was performed in\nNovember 2023, and all found records were imported\ninto the Zotero 6.0.30 Software (Roy Rosenzweig Center\nfor History and New Media at George Mason Univer-\nsity). Original articles, reviews, and case reports cover-\ning running, which were published in English or German,\nas well as articles exclusively with complete available\nfull text, were considered for inclusion. This incorpo-\nrates all athletes regardless of age, both female and male\nathletes, athletes of all performance levels (i.e., elite and\nrecreational runners), as well as the different event types\n(e.g., mountain marathons, road races, off-road races,\netc.). Other articles investigating solely cycling, triath-\nlon, stress tests, or other sports were excluded. Particu-\nlarly, articles of relevant significance for both athletes and\ncoaches as well as physicians and event organizers, were\nselected for this review. Altogether, we found 1,021 arti-\ncles across the three databases. After an initial screening\nprocess (title and abstract) of the retrieved literature, 729\nwere excluded since they did not meet the inclusion cri-\nt", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "well known for its many beneficial\neffects on cardiovascular health, such as lower resting\narterial blood pressure, improved endothelial function,\nand higher tolerance of myocardial ischemia [3]. Nev-\nertheless, a marathon puts immense stress on the"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0004_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0c2f9f35854fd0c1", "text_span_hash": "0c2f9f35854fd0c1", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nbeneficial effects as well as pathological changes of mara-\nthon training and running on the cardiovascular system.\nTable 2 lists the main findings and results of the said\nInfluence on Cardiovascul", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nbeneficial effects as well as pathological changes of mara-\nthon training and running on the cardiovascular system.\nTable 2 lists the main findings and results of the said\nInfluence on Cardiovascular Risk Profile and Positive\nSeveral studies showed endurance athletes to have lower\nrisk factors for CAD compared to sedentary control\ngroups [63–66]. For this reason, regular exercise, such\nas marathon training, is an important component in the\nprevention of CAD [3]. However, a recent systematic\nreview of the cardiovascular disease risk factor profile of\nexperienced male amateur marathon runners found that\nalthough marathon training can produce positive health\nbenefits, excessive training and marathon running can\nalso decrease benefits from exercise and itself does not\nnegate the long-term health effects of atherosclerosis and\ncardiovascular disease caused by past negative lifestyle\nIn general, marathon runners have lower levels of\nserum low-density lipoprotein (LDL) cholesterol and tri-\nglycerides as well as higher levels of high-density lipopro-\ntein (HDL) cholesterol than age-matched controls (see\nTables 1 and 2) [3, 64, 66, 68–70]. Furthermore, endur-\nance athletes have significantly lower values for rest-\ning blood pressure and pulse rate compared to inactive\ncontrols, which have protective effects on both the heart\nand the vasculature of various organs [66, 71–74]. Even\ncar", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nbeneficial effects as well as pathological changes of mara-\nthon training and running on the cardiovascular system.\nTable 2 lists the main findings and results of the said\nInfluence on Cardiovascul"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0004_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "90abb2239549b686", "text_span_hash": "90abb2239549b686", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "t\nalthough marathon training can produce positive health\nbenefits, excessive training and marathon running can\nalso decrease benefits from exercise and itself does not\nnegate the long-term health effects of atherosclerosis and\ncardiovascular disease", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nbeneficial effects as well as pathological changes of mara-\nthon training and running on the cardiovascular system.\nTable 2 lists the main findings and results of the said\nInfluence on Cardiovascular Risk Profile and Positive\nSeveral studies showed endurance athletes to have lower\nrisk factors for CAD compared to sedentary control\ngroups [63–66]. For this reason, regular exercise, such\nas marathon training, is an important component in the\nprevention of CAD [3]. However, a recent systematic\nreview of the cardiovascular disease risk factor profile of\nexperienced male amateur marathon runners found that\nalthough marathon training can produce positive health\nbenefits, excessive training and marathon running can\nalso decrease benefits from exercise and itself does not\nnegate the long-term health effects of atherosclerosis and\ncardiovascular disease caused by past negative lifestyle\nIn general, marathon runners have lower levels of\nserum low-density lipoprotein (LDL) cholesterol and tri-\nglycerides as well as higher levels of high-density lipopro-\ntein (HDL) cholesterol than age-matched controls (see\nTables 1 and 2) [3, 64, 66, 68–70]. Furthermore, endur-\nance athletes have significantly lower values for rest-\ning blood pressure and pulse rate compared to inactive\ncontrols, which have protective effects on both the heart\nand the vasculature of various organs [66, 71–74]. Even\ncar", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "t\nalthough marathon training can produce positive health\nbenefits, excessive training and marathon running can\nalso decrease benefits from exercise and itself does not\nnegate the long-term health effects of atherosclerosis and\ncardiovascular disease"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0004_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "213960f69c5c5e99", "text_span_hash": "213960f69c5c5e99", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ANS)\nas endurance athletes have significantly lower heart rates\nthan sedentary controls [79]. Electrocardiogram (ECG)\nstudies found sinus bradycardia in 80% of trained ath-\nletes, whereas only 19% of non-athletes exhibited a heart\nrate < 60 ­min−1 [8", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nbeneficial effects as well as pathological changes of mara-\nthon training and running on the cardiovascular system.\nTable 2 lists the main findings and results of the said\nInfluence on Cardiovascular Risk Profile and Positive\nSeveral studies showed endurance athletes to have lower\nrisk factors for CAD compared to sedentary control\ngroups [63–66]. For this reason, regular exercise, such\nas marathon training, is an important component in the\nprevention of CAD [3]. However, a recent systematic\nreview of the cardiovascular disease risk factor profile of\nexperienced male amateur marathon runners found that\nalthough marathon training can produce positive health\nbenefits, excessive training and marathon running can\nalso decrease benefits from exercise and itself does not\nnegate the long-term health effects of atherosclerosis and\ncardiovascular disease caused by past negative lifestyle\nIn general, marathon runners have lower levels of\nserum low-density lipoprotein (LDL) cholesterol and tri-\nglycerides as well as higher levels of high-density lipopro-\ntein (HDL) cholesterol than age-matched controls (see\nTables 1 and 2) [3, 64, 66, 68–70]. Furthermore, endur-\nance athletes have significantly lower values for rest-\ning blood pressure and pulse rate compared to inactive\ncontrols, which have protective effects on both the heart\nand the vasculature of various organs [66, 71–74]. Even\ncar", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ANS)\nas endurance athletes have significantly lower heart rates\nthan sedentary controls [79]. Electrocardiogram (ECG)\nstudies found sinus bradycardia in 80% of trained ath-\nletes, whereas only 19% of non-athletes exhibited a heart\nrate < 60 ­min−1 [8"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0004_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "19dbdfcb4578fe91", "text_span_hash": "19dbdfcb4578fe91", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ed a heart\nrate < 60 ­min−1 [80]. Enhanced vagal and decreased sym-\npathetic tone to the heart benefits cardiovascular health\nand reduces the risk for cardiovascular events [81]. A\nrecent study investigating QT interval (QT) variability in\nmiddle-age", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nbeneficial effects as well as pathological changes of mara-\nthon training and running on the cardiovascular system.\nTable 2 lists the main findings and results of the said\nInfluence on Cardiovascular Risk Profile and Positive\nSeveral studies showed endurance athletes to have lower\nrisk factors for CAD compared to sedentary control\ngroups [63–66]. For this reason, regular exercise, such\nas marathon training, is an important component in the\nprevention of CAD [3]. However, a recent systematic\nreview of the cardiovascular disease risk factor profile of\nexperienced male amateur marathon runners found that\nalthough marathon training can produce positive health\nbenefits, excessive training and marathon running can\nalso decrease benefits from exercise and itself does not\nnegate the long-term health effects of atherosclerosis and\ncardiovascular disease caused by past negative lifestyle\nIn general, marathon runners have lower levels of\nserum low-density lipoprotein (LDL) cholesterol and tri-\nglycerides as well as higher levels of high-density lipopro-\ntein (HDL) cholesterol than age-matched controls (see\nTables 1 and 2) [3, 64, 66, 68–70]. Furthermore, endur-\nance athletes have significantly lower values for rest-\ning blood pressure and pulse rate compared to inactive\ncontrols, which have protective effects on both the heart\nand the vasculature of various organs [66, 71–74]. Even\ncar", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ed a heart\nrate < 60 ­min−1 [80]. Enhanced vagal and decreased sym-\npathetic tone to the heart benefits cardiovascular health\nand reduces the risk for cardiovascular events [81]. A\nrecent study investigating QT interval (QT) variability in\nmiddle-age"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0005_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c6cfa0da4de1859a", "text_span_hash": "c6cfa0da4de1859a", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nTable 2  Overview of key findings from studies investigating effects of marathon training and running on the cardiovascular system\nIntervention/Observation\nAge 37 (IQR 21–69) years\nDifferences in e", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nTable 2  Overview of key findings from studies investigating effects of marathon training and running on the cardiovascular system\nIntervention/Observation\nAge 37 (IQR 21–69) years\nDifferences in exercise-induced cardiovascular remodeling in young and mid-\ndle aged healthy sedentary individuals\nPredominant cardiac remodeling such as increased LV mass and increased\nventricular cavity size in the younger group\nPredominant vascular remodeling such as reduced aortic compliance\nand reduced systemic vascular resistance and lower resting arterial blood\npressure in the older group\nAge 52.3 ± 8.0 years\nRegular exercise may improve sympathovagal balance and QT variability\nand counteract the ageing process\nRegular exercise leads to a decrease in QT variability\nDecrease in QT variability is cardioprotective and seems to counteract some\neffects of ageing on heart control\nAge 57.2 ± 5.7 years\nRisk of coronary events in marathon runners and associated risk factors\nMarathon runners had a 42% higher HDL cholesterol, an 18% lower LDL\ncholesterol, a 19% lower rate of ever smoking and a 15% lower BMI than age-\nmatched controls resulting in a 51% lower mean 10 years FRS\nA CAC Score ≥ 100 was found in 36% of runners, generally suggesting\nan underestimation of cardiovascular risk in asymptomatic runners\nAge 43 (IQR 39–48) years\nImpact of ultra-marathon and marathon on biomarkers of myocyte necrosi", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nTable 2  Overview of key findings from studies investigating effects of marathon training and running on the cardiovascular system\nIntervention/Observation\nAge 37 (IQR 21–69) years\nDifferences in e"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0005_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "774d221e06b4df3a", "text_span_hash": "774d221e06b4df3a", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "marathon, elevation in CK up to 411 U/L (± 170) and hs-cTnI up to 0.06\nhs-cTnI returned to baseline levels after 72 h\nAge 42.0 ± 9.5 years\n72-h kinetics of hs-cTnT and inflammatory markers after marathon running\nDirectly after the marathon race, hs-", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nTable 2  Overview of key findings from studies investigating effects of marathon training and running on the cardiovascular system\nIntervention/Observation\nAge 37 (IQR 21–69) years\nDifferences in exercise-induced cardiovascular remodeling in young and mid-\ndle aged healthy sedentary individuals\nPredominant cardiac remodeling such as increased LV mass and increased\nventricular cavity size in the younger group\nPredominant vascular remodeling such as reduced aortic compliance\nand reduced systemic vascular resistance and lower resting arterial blood\npressure in the older group\nAge 52.3 ± 8.0 years\nRegular exercise may improve sympathovagal balance and QT variability\nand counteract the ageing process\nRegular exercise leads to a decrease in QT variability\nDecrease in QT variability is cardioprotective and seems to counteract some\neffects of ageing on heart control\nAge 57.2 ± 5.7 years\nRisk of coronary events in marathon runners and associated risk factors\nMarathon runners had a 42% higher HDL cholesterol, an 18% lower LDL\ncholesterol, a 19% lower rate of ever smoking and a 15% lower BMI than age-\nmatched controls resulting in a 51% lower mean 10 years FRS\nA CAC Score ≥ 100 was found in 36% of runners, generally suggesting\nan underestimation of cardiovascular risk in asymptomatic runners\nAge 43 (IQR 39–48) years\nImpact of ultra-marathon and marathon on biomarkers of myocyte necrosi", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "marathon, elevation in CK up to 411 U/L (± 170) and hs-cTnI up to 0.06\nhs-cTnI returned to baseline levels after 72 h\nAge 42.0 ± 9.5 years\n72-h kinetics of hs-cTnT and inflammatory markers after marathon running\nDirectly after the marathon race, hs-"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0005_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f65d1e06795ad0f4", "text_span_hash": "f65d1e06795ad0f4", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "er a half-marathon and marathon race\nin non-elite marathon runners\nDirectly after the race in the marathon group, increase in myoglobin by 16.0-\nfold with median values at 626 mg/l (IQR 135–880), CK by 3.3-fold with median\nvalues at 432 U/L (112–705)", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nTable 2  Overview of key findings from studies investigating effects of marathon training and running on the cardiovascular system\nIntervention/Observation\nAge 37 (IQR 21–69) years\nDifferences in exercise-induced cardiovascular remodeling in young and mid-\ndle aged healthy sedentary individuals\nPredominant cardiac remodeling such as increased LV mass and increased\nventricular cavity size in the younger group\nPredominant vascular remodeling such as reduced aortic compliance\nand reduced systemic vascular resistance and lower resting arterial blood\npressure in the older group\nAge 52.3 ± 8.0 years\nRegular exercise may improve sympathovagal balance and QT variability\nand counteract the ageing process\nRegular exercise leads to a decrease in QT variability\nDecrease in QT variability is cardioprotective and seems to counteract some\neffects of ageing on heart control\nAge 57.2 ± 5.7 years\nRisk of coronary events in marathon runners and associated risk factors\nMarathon runners had a 42% higher HDL cholesterol, an 18% lower LDL\ncholesterol, a 19% lower rate of ever smoking and a 15% lower BMI than age-\nmatched controls resulting in a 51% lower mean 10 years FRS\nA CAC Score ≥ 100 was found in 36% of runners, generally suggesting\nan underestimation of cardiovascular risk in asymptomatic runners\nAge 43 (IQR 39–48) years\nImpact of ultra-marathon and marathon on biomarkers of myocyte necrosi", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "er a half-marathon and marathon race\nin non-elite marathon runners\nDirectly after the race in the marathon group, increase in myoglobin by 16.0-\nfold with median values at 626 mg/l (IQR 135–880), CK by 3.3-fold with median\nvalues at 432 U/L (112–705)"}
