largement is an established risk\nfactor for the occurrence of AF [98]. A recent cardiac\nmagnetic resonance imaging (MRI) study (see Table 2)\nidentified non-ischemic fibrotic areas in up to 50% of\nmale veteran endurance athletes [100, 101]. The preva-\nlence of myocardial fibrosis correlated w", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ic fibrotic areas in up to 50% of\nmale veteran endurance athletes [100, 101]. The preva-\nlence of myocardial fibrosis correlated with years spent\ntraining and finished competitive marathons or ultra-\nmarathons [101]. Veteran athletes with fibrosis sh"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0009_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8a43c50d5feba87b", "text_span_hash": "8a43c50d5feba87b", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "eran athletes with fibrosis showed\na greater burden of premature ventricular beats, as well\nas a higher prevalence of ventricular couplets and tri-\nplets compared to veteran runners without fibrosis [100].\nThese fibrotic areas might be the source of", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nArrhythmias in Marathon Runners\nIn addition to studies on elevations in cardiac biomark-\ners, there is evidence that marathon runners are more\nlikely to develop atrial fibrillation (AF) [95]. Male mara-\nthon runners were found to have an 8.8-fold higher risk\nof developing lone AF compared to a sedentary con-\ntrol group (see Table 2) [96]. An animal study on rats\nshowed myocardial fibrosis, especially involving the RV\nand right atria (RA) after extensive endurance training,\nsuggesting a potential source for ventricular arrhyth-\nmias and AF [97]. Lifetime training hours seem to play\na critical role in left atrial (LA) enlargement [98, 99].\nIn fact, higher lifetime training hours are associated\nwith prolonged P-wave duration indicating altered car-\ndiac conduction or LA enlargement [99]. Furthermore,\nmale marathon runners seem to be more at risk of atrial\nremodeling compared to female athletes, as prolonged\nP-wave durations and greater LA volume in male endur-\nance athletes compared to female endurance athletes\nwere observed [95]. These findings are in line with cur-\nrent knowledge, as LA enlargement is an established risk\nfactor for the occurrence of AF [98]. A recent cardiac\nmagnetic resonance imaging (MRI) study (see Table 2)\nidentified non-ischemic fibrotic areas in up to 50% of\nmale veteran endurance athletes [100, 101]. The preva-\nlence of myocardial fibrosis correlated w", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "eran athletes with fibrosis showed\na greater burden of premature ventricular beats, as well\nas a higher prevalence of ventricular couplets and tri-\nplets compared to veteran runners without fibrosis [100].\nThese fibrotic areas might be the source of"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0009_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0085011db7f4adff", "text_span_hash": "0085011db7f4adff", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "areas might be the source of ventricular\narrhythmias [100]. Whether veteran runners with fibrosis\nhave a higher risk for malignant arrhythmic events is not\nentirely clear, yet, and needs further investigation [100].\nVentricular Dysfunction\nEndurance", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nArrhythmias in Marathon Runners\nIn addition to studies on elevations in cardiac biomark-\ners, there is evidence that marathon runners are more\nlikely to develop atrial fibrillation (AF) [95]. Male mara-\nthon runners were found to have an 8.8-fold higher risk\nof developing lone AF compared to a sedentary con-\ntrol group (see Table 2) [96]. An animal study on rats\nshowed myocardial fibrosis, especially involving the RV\nand right atria (RA) after extensive endurance training,\nsuggesting a potential source for ventricular arrhyth-\nmias and AF [97]. Lifetime training hours seem to play\na critical role in left atrial (LA) enlargement [98, 99].\nIn fact, higher lifetime training hours are associated\nwith prolonged P-wave duration indicating altered car-\ndiac conduction or LA enlargement [99]. Furthermore,\nmale marathon runners seem to be more at risk of atrial\nremodeling compared to female athletes, as prolonged\nP-wave durations and greater LA volume in male endur-\nance athletes compared to female endurance athletes\nwere observed [95]. These findings are in line with cur-\nrent knowledge, as LA enlargement is an established risk\nfactor for the occurrence of AF [98]. A recent cardiac\nmagnetic resonance imaging (MRI) study (see Table 2)\nidentified non-ischemic fibrotic areas in up to 50% of\nmale veteran endurance athletes [100, 101]. The preva-\nlence of myocardial fibrosis correlated w", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "areas might be the source of ventricular\narrhythmias [100]. Whether veteran runners with fibrosis\nhave a higher risk for malignant arrhythmic events is not\nentirely clear, yet, and needs further investigation [100].\nVentricular Dysfunction\nEndurance"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0009_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5e74dc8936beb681", "text_span_hash": "5e74dc8936beb681", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "olic and diastolic ventricular dysfunc-\ntion after exercise [71, 102]. However, a recent study\nshowed negligible effects of marathon running on biven-\ntricular systolic function [71]. In contrast, impaired dias-\ntolic function, predominantly affectin", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nArrhythmias in Marathon Runners\nIn addition to studies on elevations in cardiac biomark-\ners, there is evidence that marathon runners are more\nlikely to develop atrial fibrillation (AF) [95]. Male mara-\nthon runners were found to have an 8.8-fold higher risk\nof developing lone AF compared to a sedentary con-\ntrol group (see Table 2) [96]. An animal study on rats\nshowed myocardial fibrosis, especially involving the RV\nand right atria (RA) after extensive endurance training,\nsuggesting a potential source for ventricular arrhyth-\nmias and AF [97]. Lifetime training hours seem to play\na critical role in left atrial (LA) enlargement [98, 99].\nIn fact, higher lifetime training hours are associated\nwith prolonged P-wave duration indicating altered car-\ndiac conduction or LA enlargement [99]. Furthermore,\nmale marathon runners seem to be more at risk of atrial\nremodeling compared to female athletes, as prolonged\nP-wave durations and greater LA volume in male endur-\nance athletes compared to female endurance athletes\nwere observed [95]. These findings are in line with cur-\nrent knowledge, as LA enlargement is an established risk\nfactor for the occurrence of AF [98]. A recent cardiac\nmagnetic resonance imaging (MRI) study (see Table 2)\nidentified non-ischemic fibrotic areas in up to 50% of\nmale veteran endurance athletes [100, 101]. The preva-\nlence of myocardial fibrosis correlated w", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "olic and diastolic ventricular dysfunc-\ntion after exercise [71, 102]. However, a recent study\nshowed negligible effects of marathon running on biven-\ntricular systolic function [71]. In contrast, impaired dias-\ntolic function, predominantly affectin"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0009_c0020", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "731409e2e52eb3ff", "text_span_hash": "731409e2e52eb3ff", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 20, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "by elevated\nlevels of catecholamines [106] and neurohormones dur-\ning a marathon such as aldosterone, vasopressin, and\nANP [107–109]. Elevated levels of NT-proBNP, hs-cTnT,\nCK-MB, creatinine and cystatin C [45, 85], are commonly\nobserved suggesting", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nArrhythmias in Marathon Runners\nIn addition to studies on elevations in cardiac biomark-\ners, there is evidence that marathon runners are more\nlikely to develop atrial fibrillation (AF) [95]. Male mara-\nthon runners were found to have an 8.8-fold higher risk\nof developing lone AF compared to a sedentary con-\ntrol group (see Table 2) [96]. An animal study on rats\nshowed myocardial fibrosis, especially involving the RV\nand right atria (RA) after extensive endurance training,\nsuggesting a potential source for ventricular arrhyth-\nmias and AF [97]. Lifetime training hours seem to play\na critical role in left atrial (LA) enlargement [98, 99].\nIn fact, higher lifetime training hours are associated\nwith prolonged P-wave duration indicating altered car-\ndiac conduction or LA enlargement [99]. Furthermore,\nmale marathon runners seem to be more at risk of atrial\nremodeling compared to female athletes, as prolonged\nP-wave durations and greater LA volume in male endur-\nance athletes compared to female endurance athletes\nwere observed [95]. These findings are in line with cur-\nrent knowledge, as LA enlargement is an established risk\nfactor for the occurrence of AF [98]. A recent cardiac\nmagnetic resonance imaging (MRI) study (see Table 2)\nidentified non-ischemic fibrotic areas in up to 50% of\nmale veteran endurance athletes [100, 101]. The preva-\nlence of myocardial fibrosis correlated w", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "by elevated\nlevels of catecholamines [106] and neurohormones dur-\ning a marathon such as aldosterone, vasopressin, and\nANP [107–109]. Elevated levels of NT-proBNP, hs-cTnT,\nCK-MB, creatinine and cystatin C [45, 85], are commonly\nobserved suggesting"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0010_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "a63633cb809efa06", "text_span_hash": "a63633cb809efa06", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "of coronary artery calcification (CAC) among\nhealthy male marathon runners aged ≥ 50 years with\nprior marathon experience (≥ 5 completed marathon\nraces during the previous three years). Framingham risk\nscore (FRS) was lower compared to age-matched c", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nGenerally, these effects resolve within 24–72 h post-\nexercise, with both cardiac and renal markers returning\nto baseline as fluid balance and normal blood flow are\nrestored [85, 113]. However, repetitive high-intensity\nexercise without adequate recovery may promote cumu-\nlative effects on the cardiorenal axis, potentially leading\nto long-term impact on renal and cardiovascular health\nCoronary Artery Disease and Sudden Cardiac Death\nRegular physical activity has beneficial effects in pre-\nventing CAD [3]. However, marathon runners are not\nimmune to CAD [114]. Möhlenkamp et al. assessed the\nprevalence of coronary artery calcification (CAC) among\nhealthy male marathon runners aged ≥ 50 years with\nprior marathon experience (≥ 5 completed marathon\nraces during the previous three years). Framingham risk\nscore (FRS) was lower compared to age-matched con-\ntrols; however, CAC was more frequent in marathon\nrunners. Distribution and calcification sites were similar\nbetween both groups. In total, 36% of marathon runners\nshowed a CAC score ≥ 100 [44, 69]. This is highly relevant\nsince CAD is reported to be the main cause of major car-\ndiac events during marathon competitions [9, 68]. Simi-\nlarly, a different study showed the adjusted risk of a CAC\nscore ≥ 100 in individuals with very high levels of physi-\ncal activity to be 11% greater compared to those with\nlower levels [115]. Neverthe", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "of coronary artery calcification (CAC) among\nhealthy male marathon runners aged ≥ 50 years with\nprior marathon experience (≥ 5 completed marathon\nraces during the previous three years). Framingham risk\nscore (FRS) was lower compared to age-matched c"}
