d to be the main cause of major car-\ndiac events during marathon competitions [9, 68]. Simi-\nlarly, a different study showed the adjusted risk of a CAC\nscore ≥ 100 in individuals with very high levels of physi-\ncal activity to be 11% greater compared to those with\nlower levels [115]. Neverthe", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "00 [44, 69]. This is highly relevant\nsince CAD is reported to be the main cause of major car-\ndiac events during marathon competitions [9, 68]. Simi-\nlarly, a different study showed the adjusted risk of a CAC\nscore ≥ 100 in individuals with very high"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0010_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "41f9284162077312", "text_span_hash": "41f9284162077312", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "s being more affected [116] (see Table 2). Almost\nhalf of SCD occurs in the final 1.6 km of the race, rep-\nresenting less than 5% of the whole race distance [3, 68].\nDifferent etiologies have been debated regarding SCD.\nCAD and acute myocardial infar", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nGenerally, these effects resolve within 24–72 h post-\nexercise, with both cardiac and renal markers returning\nto baseline as fluid balance and normal blood flow are\nrestored [85, 113]. However, repetitive high-intensity\nexercise without adequate recovery may promote cumu-\nlative effects on the cardiorenal axis, potentially leading\nto long-term impact on renal and cardiovascular health\nCoronary Artery Disease and Sudden Cardiac Death\nRegular physical activity has beneficial effects in pre-\nventing CAD [3]. However, marathon runners are not\nimmune to CAD [114]. Möhlenkamp et al. assessed the\nprevalence of coronary artery calcification (CAC) among\nhealthy male marathon runners aged ≥ 50 years with\nprior marathon experience (≥ 5 completed marathon\nraces during the previous three years). Framingham risk\nscore (FRS) was lower compared to age-matched con-\ntrols; however, CAC was more frequent in marathon\nrunners. Distribution and calcification sites were similar\nbetween both groups. In total, 36% of marathon runners\nshowed a CAC score ≥ 100 [44, 69]. This is highly relevant\nsince CAD is reported to be the main cause of major car-\ndiac events during marathon competitions [9, 68]. Simi-\nlarly, a different study showed the adjusted risk of a CAC\nscore ≥ 100 in individuals with very high levels of physi-\ncal activity to be 11% greater compared to those with\nlower levels [115]. Neverthe", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "s being more affected [116] (see Table 2). Almost\nhalf of SCD occurs in the final 1.6 km of the race, rep-\nresenting less than 5% of the whole race distance [3, 68].\nDifferent etiologies have been debated regarding SCD.\nCAD and acute myocardial infar"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0010_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "74c1ff0ace7bff68", "text_span_hash": "74c1ff0ace7bff68", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "CAD and acute myocardial infarction are the dominating\ncauses in over 40-year-old marathon runners, whereas\nin young athletes, mainly genetic causes such as hyper-\ntrophic or arrhythmic cardiomyopathy are more frequent\n[9, 68, 116–118]. So far, few r", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nGenerally, these effects resolve within 24–72 h post-\nexercise, with both cardiac and renal markers returning\nto baseline as fluid balance and normal blood flow are\nrestored [85, 113]. However, repetitive high-intensity\nexercise without adequate recovery may promote cumu-\nlative effects on the cardiorenal axis, potentially leading\nto long-term impact on renal and cardiovascular health\nCoronary Artery Disease and Sudden Cardiac Death\nRegular physical activity has beneficial effects in pre-\nventing CAD [3]. However, marathon runners are not\nimmune to CAD [114]. Möhlenkamp et al. assessed the\nprevalence of coronary artery calcification (CAC) among\nhealthy male marathon runners aged ≥ 50 years with\nprior marathon experience (≥ 5 completed marathon\nraces during the previous three years). Framingham risk\nscore (FRS) was lower compared to age-matched con-\ntrols; however, CAC was more frequent in marathon\nrunners. Distribution and calcification sites were similar\nbetween both groups. In total, 36% of marathon runners\nshowed a CAC score ≥ 100 [44, 69]. This is highly relevant\nsince CAD is reported to be the main cause of major car-\ndiac events during marathon competitions [9, 68]. Simi-\nlarly, a different study showed the adjusted risk of a CAC\nscore ≥ 100 in individuals with very high levels of physi-\ncal activity to be 11% greater compared to those with\nlower levels [115]. Neverthe", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "CAD and acute myocardial infarction are the dominating\ncauses in over 40-year-old marathon runners, whereas\nin young athletes, mainly genetic causes such as hyper-\ntrophic or arrhythmic cardiomyopathy are more frequent\n[9, 68, 116–118]. So far, few r"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0010_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1ce61e07c2822d90", "text_span_hash": "1ce61e07c2822d90", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "toms [9].\nModulation of Autonomic Nervous System\nSeveral studies investigated the effects of marathon\ntraining on the ANS and sympathovagal balance [32,\n71, 79, 81, 121–123]. ANS function seems to be altered\nby endurance exercise since low resting he", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nGenerally, these effects resolve within 24–72 h post-\nexercise, with both cardiac and renal markers returning\nto baseline as fluid balance and normal blood flow are\nrestored [85, 113]. However, repetitive high-intensity\nexercise without adequate recovery may promote cumu-\nlative effects on the cardiorenal axis, potentially leading\nto long-term impact on renal and cardiovascular health\nCoronary Artery Disease and Sudden Cardiac Death\nRegular physical activity has beneficial effects in pre-\nventing CAD [3]. However, marathon runners are not\nimmune to CAD [114]. Möhlenkamp et al. assessed the\nprevalence of coronary artery calcification (CAC) among\nhealthy male marathon runners aged ≥ 50 years with\nprior marathon experience (≥ 5 completed marathon\nraces during the previous three years). Framingham risk\nscore (FRS) was lower compared to age-matched con-\ntrols; however, CAC was more frequent in marathon\nrunners. Distribution and calcification sites were similar\nbetween both groups. In total, 36% of marathon runners\nshowed a CAC score ≥ 100 [44, 69]. This is highly relevant\nsince CAD is reported to be the main cause of major car-\ndiac events during marathon competitions [9, 68]. Simi-\nlarly, a different study showed the adjusted risk of a CAC\nscore ≥ 100 in individuals with very high levels of physi-\ncal activity to be 11% greater compared to those with\nlower levels [115]. Neverthe", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "toms [9].\nModulation of Autonomic Nervous System\nSeveral studies investigated the effects of marathon\ntraining on the ANS and sympathovagal balance [32,\n71, 79, 81, 121–123]. ANS function seems to be altered\nby endurance exercise since low resting he"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0010_c0016", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2e86228ada97ac0f", "text_span_hash": "2e86228ada97ac0f", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 16, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "exercise since low resting heart rate,\nfirst-degree AV block and other ECG changes are com-\nmon findings in endurance athletes [79]. Nevertheless,\nincreased vagal tone is associated with higher occurrence\nof premature atrial contractions, which may", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nGenerally, these effects resolve within 24–72 h post-\nexercise, with both cardiac and renal markers returning\nto baseline as fluid balance and normal blood flow are\nrestored [85, 113]. However, repetitive high-intensity\nexercise without adequate recovery may promote cumu-\nlative effects on the cardiorenal axis, potentially leading\nto long-term impact on renal and cardiovascular health\nCoronary Artery Disease and Sudden Cardiac Death\nRegular physical activity has beneficial effects in pre-\nventing CAD [3]. However, marathon runners are not\nimmune to CAD [114]. Möhlenkamp et al. assessed the\nprevalence of coronary artery calcification (CAC) among\nhealthy male marathon runners aged ≥ 50 years with\nprior marathon experience (≥ 5 completed marathon\nraces during the previous three years). Framingham risk\nscore (FRS) was lower compared to age-matched con-\ntrols; however, CAC was more frequent in marathon\nrunners. Distribution and calcification sites were similar\nbetween both groups. In total, 36% of marathon runners\nshowed a CAC score ≥ 100 [44, 69]. This is highly relevant\nsince CAD is reported to be the main cause of major car-\ndiac events during marathon competitions [9, 68]. Simi-\nlarly, a different study showed the adjusted risk of a CAC\nscore ≥ 100 in individuals with very high levels of physi-\ncal activity to be 11% greater compared to those with\nlower levels [115]. Neverthe", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "exercise since low resting heart rate,\nfirst-degree AV block and other ECG changes are com-\nmon findings in endurance athletes [79]. Nevertheless,\nincreased vagal tone is associated with higher occurrence\nof premature atrial contractions, which may"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0010_c0020", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f9d9721ed4eeea19", "text_span_hash": "f9d9721ed4eeea19", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 20, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "activity or a reduced parasympathetic tone, which\nreturned to pre-race values in a time-dependent manner\nThe cardiovascular system in the context of marathon\ntraining and running has been extensively studied. Reg-\nular physical exercise has a benefi", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nGenerally, these effects resolve within 24–72 h post-\nexercise, with both cardiac and renal markers returning\nto baseline as fluid balance and normal blood flow are\nrestored [85, 113]. However, repetitive high-intensity\nexercise without adequate recovery may promote cumu-\nlative effects on the cardiorenal axis, potentially leading\nto long-term impact on renal and cardiovascular health\nCoronary Artery Disease and Sudden Cardiac Death\nRegular physical activity has beneficial effects in pre-\nventing CAD [3]. However, marathon runners are not\nimmune to CAD [114]. Möhlenkamp et al. assessed the\nprevalence of coronary artery calcification (CAC) among\nhealthy male marathon runners aged ≥ 50 years with\nprior marathon experience (≥ 5 completed marathon\nraces during the previous three years). Framingham risk\nscore (FRS) was lower compared to age-matched con-\ntrols; however, CAC was more frequent in marathon\nrunners. Distribution and calcification sites were similar\nbetween both groups. In total, 36% of marathon runners\nshowed a CAC score ≥ 100 [44, 69]. This is highly relevant\nsince CAD is reported to be the main cause of major car-\ndiac events during marathon competitions [9, 68]. Simi-\nlarly, a different study showed the adjusted risk of a CAC\nscore ≥ 100 in individuals with very high levels of physi-\ncal activity to be 11% greater compared to those with\nlower levels [115]. Neverthe", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "activity or a reduced parasympathetic tone, which\nreturned to pre-race values in a time-dependent manner\nThe cardiovascular system in the context of marathon\ntraining and running has been extensively studied. Reg-\nular physical exercise has a benefi"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0011_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8ef3bc0dadac708f", "text_span_hash": "8ef3bc0dadac708f", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ount rose by 1.1-fold, aPTT decreased by 1.2-fold, D-dimers increased\nby 2.4-fold, LDH increased by 1.9-fold\nNo changes in hemoglobin levels were observed\nAge 36.8 ± 7.6 years\nChanges of inflammation in non-professional marathon running\nAfter the mar", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nTable 3  Overview of key findings from studies investigating effects of marathon training and running on the hematological system\nIntervention/Observation\nRegular sports activities decrease the risk of venous thrombosis\nRegular strenuous exercise led to a risk reduction for venous thrombosis OR 0.78\nSport activities with a higher injury risk had a less beneficial effect on thrombotic\nrisk reduction with OR 0.93 (95% CI 0.69–1.26)\nAge 38 ± 11 years\nEffects of marathon running on biochemical and hematological markers\nThe white blood cell count after a marathon rose significantly by 2.7-fold,\nplatelet count rose by 1.1-fold, aPTT decreased by 1.2-fold, D-dimers increased\nby 2.4-fold, LDH increased by 1.9-fold\nNo changes in hemoglobin levels were observed\nAge 36.8 ± 7.6 years\nChanges of inflammation in non-professional marathon running\nAfter the marathon race, leukocytes increased by 2.6-fold, erythrocytes by 1.1-\nfold, hemoglobin by 1.1-fold\nLymphocytes decreased by 2.7-fold and neutrophils increased by 1.4-fold\nNo change in Hct was observed\nAll biomarker alterations returned to baseline levels after 2–7 days of recovery\nAge 37 ± 2.1 years\nAssociation between hematological parameters and iron metabolism response\nafter a marathon race\nAfter the marathon, no changes in erythrocytes, Hb, Hct, MCV and MCH\nIron increased by 1.1-fold, ferritin by 1.3-fold immediately after the race\nE", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ount rose by 1.1-fold, aPTT decreased by 1.2-fold, D-dimers increased\nby 2.4-fold, LDH increased by 1.9-fold\nNo changes in hemoglobin levels were observed\nAge 36.8 ± 7.6 years\nChanges of inflammation in non-professional marathon running\nAfter the mar"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0011_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "35ebe40e426df57f", "text_span_hash": "35ebe40e426df57f", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "5.5-fold, lymphocytes by 1.3-fold, and platelets\nNo significant changes in Hb, MCV, MCH or Hct could be observed\nAge 41 ± 9 years\nIron homeostasis in recreational marathon runners\nIron depletion was found in 1.6% of male and in 28.0% of female athlet", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nTable 3  Overview of key findings from studies investigating effects of marathon training and running on the hematological system\nIntervention/Observation\nRegular sports activities decrease the risk of venous thrombosis\nRegular strenuous exercise led to a risk reduction for venous thrombosis OR 0.78\nSport activities with a higher injury risk had a less beneficial effect on thrombotic\nrisk reduction with OR 0.93 (95% CI 0.69–1.26)\nAge 38 ± 11 years\nEffects of marathon running on biochemical and hematological markers\nThe white blood cell count after a marathon rose significantly by 2.7-fold,\nplatelet count rose by 1.1-fold, aPTT decreased by 1.2-fold, D-dimers increased\nby 2.4-fold, LDH increased by 1.9-fold\nNo changes in hemoglobin levels were observed\nAge 36.8 ± 7.6 years\nChanges of inflammation in non-professional marathon running\nAfter the marathon race, leukocytes increased by 2.6-fold, erythrocytes by 1.1-\nfold, hemoglobin by 1.1-fold\nLymphocytes decreased by 2.7-fold and neutrophils increased by 1.4-fold\nNo change in Hct was observed\nAll biomarker alterations returned to baseline levels after 2–7 days of recovery\nAge 37 ± 2.1 years\nAssociation between hematological parameters and iron metabolism response\nafter a marathon race\nAfter the marathon, no changes in erythrocytes, Hb, Hct, MCV and MCH\nIron increased by 1.1-fold, ferritin by 1.3-fold immediately after the race\nE", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "5.5-fold, lymphocytes by 1.3-fold, and platelets\nNo significant changes in Hb, MCV, MCH or Hct could be observed\nAge 41 ± 9 years\nIron homeostasis in recreational marathon runners\nIron depletion was found in 1.6% of male and in 28.0% of female athlet"}
