aches val-\nues up to 115 l∙min−1, corresponding to a 15-fold increase\ncompared to rest [39, 126]. Lung function is significantly\nbetter in marathon runners compared to sedentary con-\ntrols [127]. Significantly higher volumes of forced expira-\ntory volume in 1 s (FEV1), forced vital capacity ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "lder runners (range 40–75 years)\ncould inhibit or decelerate the age-related decrease in\npulmonary function, thus improving general health and\nquality of life [128]. Furthermore, endurance training\nseems to mitigate the decline in lung volumes follow"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0013_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "489851709c85fe18", "text_span_hash": "489851709c85fe18", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "decline in lung volumes follow-\ning bronchoprovocation (see Table 4), as suggested by a\nsignificantly smaller reduction in lung volumes in rec-\nreational marathon runners compared to controls dur-\ning a single-dose methacholine test [127]. These resu", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nbeen well-studied in male cohorts. Nonetheless, data on\nfemale marathon runners remain sparse and points to a\nneed for studies comparing male and female marathon\nAlongside the cardiovascular system, the respiratory sys-\ntem is exposed to great stress during marathon running,\ne.g., a significant increase in minute ventilation (V̇E) [39].\nHowever, it has not received the same scientific attention\nas the cardiovascular system to date [12]. Nevertheless,\nendurance exercise such as marathon training and run-\nning affects the respiratory system in both positive and\nnegative ways [12]. In this context, we identified 20 stud-\nies investigating the positive and negative effects of mara-\nthon training and running on the respiratory system and\nits function. The key findings of said studies are summa-\nImprovement in Lung Function\nMarathon running poses a great challenge to the respira-\ntory system as adequate ventilation and blood oxygena-\ntion are crucial for athlete’s peak performance. In this\nway, V̇E in recreational athletes increases by 7–8 times\nup to 62 l∙min−1, while in elite athletes, V̇E r