nners compared to their age-matched\nsedentary controls by 1.09-fold, 1.02-fold and 1.04-fold, respectively\nEffects of marathon training and running on bone health assessed\nby calcaneal broadband ultrasound attenuation\nMen had significantly higher BMD compared to the women independ-\nent of phys", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ggesting high metabolic adaptation\nof skeletal muscle due to endurance training\nAge 16 ± 1 years\nMusculotendinous changes during a 6-month endurance training\nassessed via ultrasound measurements\nA significant increase in thickness of medial gastrocne"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0016_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "a892c86e6d8058b7", "text_span_hash": "a892c86e6d8058b7", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 16, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "nation angles significantly increased suggesting favorable muscu-\nlotendinous adaptations for endurance running", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nTable 5  Overview of key findings from studies investigating effects of marathon training and running on the musculoskeletal system and its function\nIntervention/Observation\nRole of exercise in prevention of involutional bone loss in marathon\nrunning pre-menopausal women\nIn runners, BMD was significantly greater by 1.05-fold and 1.1-fold\nat midshaft radius and the middle phalanx of the fifth digit, usual sites\nof osteoporotic fractures\nBMD at os calcis was higher by 1.07-fold in the sedentary control\nAge 40.5 ± 9.8 years\nEffects of endurance running on plasma osteoprotegerin (OPG)\nand soluble receptor activator of NF-κB ligand (sRANKL)\nAfter a marathon race, serum OPG levels increased significantly by 1.8-\nfold and sRANKL levels decreased significantly by 1.6-fold\nAge 40.5 ± 9.8 years\nComparison of calcaneus bone stiffness between endurance runners\nof different ages and age-matched controls using ultrasonographic\nWith higher age calcaneus bone stiffness decreased independent\nfrom physical activity\nCalcaneus stiffness was significantly higher in 40–44, 45–49\nand > 50-year-old marathon runners compared to their age-matched\nsedentary controls by 1.09-fold, 1.02-fold and 1.04-fold, respectively\nEffects of marathon training and running on bone health assessed\nby calcaneal broadband ultrasound attenuation\nMen had significantly higher BMD compared to the women independ-\nent of phys", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "nation angles significantly increased suggesting favorable muscu-\nlotendinous adaptations for endurance running"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0017_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "fd960823c8ba0e7b", "text_span_hash": "fd960823c8ba0e7b", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 17, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ter a marathon race\nNo negative effects were experienced in 75% of runners without major\npre-existing lesions\nIn one runner with pre-existing high-grade meniscal lesions, signs\nof progressive osteoarthritis were experienced after 2 months post-\nIn al", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nTable 5  (continued)\nIntervention/Observation\nKnee MRI before and after a marathon race\nRunners underwent an MRI of the knee before, immediately\nafter and 6–8 weeks after a marathon race\nNo negative effects were experienced in 75% of runners without major\npre-existing lesions\nIn one runner with pre-existing high-grade meniscal lesions, signs\nof progressive osteoarthritis were experienced after 2 months post-\nIn all other participants increased meniscal signals and minor bone\nmarrow signal changes were observable, yet all were transitory\nAge 47.9 ± 11.6 years (range 18 to 79)\nPrevalence of hip and knee arthritis in active marathon runners\nMarathoners which participated in ≥ 5 marathons and were running\na minimum of 10 miles per week were included in this study\nA total of 47% reported hip or knee pain and 8.8% of runners reported\nOsteoarthritis prevalence was significantly lower in marathon runners\nby twofold compared to subgroups stratified by age, sex, body mass\nindex, and physical activity level with reported osteoarthritis preva-\nMain running-related musculoskeletal injuries\nThis systematic review found most common running-related injuries\nto be medial tibial stress syndrome (with an incidence of 13.6–20.0%),\nAchilles tendinopathy (incidence ranging from 9.1–10.9%) and plantar\nfasciitis (with an incidence of 4.5–10.0%)\nOther common running-related injuries were patellar te", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ter a marathon race\nNo negative effects were experienced in 75% of runners without major\npre-existing lesions\nIn one runner with pre-existing high-grade meniscal lesions, signs\nof progressive osteoarthritis were experienced after 2 months post-\nIn al"}
