{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0018_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "fc263086ce6d69fc", "text_span_hash": "fc263086ce6d69fc", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 18, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "irst-time marathoners was assessed\nusing MRI 16 weeks before and 2 weeks after the marathon\nThe participants took part in a 16-week training program\nIn pre-race images disc degeneration was detected in 61% of partici-\npants, mostly at spinal segments", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nTable 5  (continued)\nIntervention/Observation\nAge 39.9 ± 3.8 years\nEffect of a six-month training program and a consecutive mara-\nthon on knee joint cartilage volume and thickness assessed by MRI\nin asymptomatic inexperienced runners\nLateral femur cartilage volume significantly decreased by 3.2 ± 3.0%\nand thickness decreased by 1.7 ± 1.6%\nNon-significant changes were observed at medial and lateral tibia,\nmedial femur, and the patella\nAge 30 (median with range 18–58) years\nEffects of marathon training and a consecutive race on lower lumber\nspine in first-time marathoners\nLumbar spine of asymptomatic first-time marathoners was assessed\nusing MRI 16 weeks before and 2 weeks after the marathon\nThe participants took part in a 16-week training program\nIn pre-race images disc degeneration was detected in 61% of partici-\npants, mostly at spinal segments L4-L5 and L5-S1 though asympto-\nAfter the marathon, no significant change in disc degeneration pro-\ngression or intervertebral disc height was found\nUltrasound evaluation of Achilles and patellar tendon and its associa-\ntion with future pain in marathon runners\nPrior to the marathon, tendon abnormalities were found in 24.1%\nof the Achilles and in 23.1% of the patellar tendons\nRunners with ultrasound abnormalities were significantly more likely\nto develop pain within 12 months after the race with a HR of 2.89 (95%\nlar tendon respectiv", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "irst-time marathoners was assessed\nusing MRI 16 weeks before and 2 weeks after the marathon\nThe participants took part in a 16-week training program\nIn pre-race images disc degeneration was detected in 61% of partici-\npants, mostly at spinal segments"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0018_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "dd3fc6d44e6098d4", "text_span_hash": "dd3fc6d44e6098d4", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 18, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "were measured 2 weeks before the marathon race, immedi-\nately after the race and 2 weeks post-marathon\nCK levels significantly increased immediately after marathon by 2.7-\nfold and returned to baseline values within 2 weeks\nAge 42.9 ± 8.0 years\nEffe", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nTable 5  (continued)\nIntervention/Observation\nAge 39.9 ± 3.8 years\nEffect of a six-month training program and a consecutive mara-\nthon on knee joint cartilage volume and thickness assessed by MRI\nin asymptomatic inexperienced runners\nLateral femur cartilage volume significantly decreased by 3.2 ± 3.0%\nand thickness decreased by 1.7 ± 1.6%\nNon-significant changes were observed at medial and lateral tibia,\nmedial femur, and the patella\nAge 30 (median with range 18–58) years\nEffects of marathon training and a consecutive race on lower lumber\nspine in first-time marathoners\nLumbar spine of asymptomatic first-time marathoners was assessed\nusing MRI 16 weeks before and 2 weeks after the marathon\nThe participants took part in a 16-week training program\nIn pre-race images disc degeneration was detected in 61% of partici-\npants, mostly at spinal segments L4-L5 and L5-S1 though asympto-\nAfter the marathon, no significant change in disc degeneration pro-\ngression or intervertebral disc height was found\nUltrasound evaluation of Achilles and patellar tendon and its associa-\ntion with future pain in marathon runners\nPrior to the marathon, tendon abnormalities were found in 24.1%\nof the Achilles and in 23.1% of the patellar tendons\nRunners with ultrasound abnormalities were significantly more likely\nto develop pain within 12 months after the race with a HR of 2.89 (95%\nlar tendon respectiv", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "were measured 2 weeks before the marathon race, immedi-\nately after the race and 2 weeks post-marathon\nCK levels significantly increased immediately after marathon by 2.7-\nfold and returned to baseline values within 2 weeks\nAge 42.9 ± 8.0 years\nEffe"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0018_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "116b1cbab5392e75", "text_span_hash": "116b1cbab5392e75", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 18, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "eeks\nAge 42.9 ± 8.0 years\nEffect of marathon and ultra-marathon on inflammation and iron\nImmediately after the marathon, CK values increased by 3.0-fold\nand were still increasing by 4.2-fold 5 days post-race compared\nLDH increased significantly by 1.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nTable 5  (continued)\nIntervention/Observation\nAge 39.9 ± 3.8 years\nEffect of a six-month training program and a consecutive mara-\nthon on knee joint cartilage volume and thickness assessed by MRI\nin asymptomatic inexperienced runners\nLateral femur cartilage volume significantly decreased by 3.2 ± 3.0%\nand thickness decreased by 1.7 ± 1.6%\nNon-significant changes were observed at medial and lateral tibia,\nmedial femur, and the patella\nAge 30 (median with range 18–58) years\nEffects of marathon training and a consecutive race on lower lumber\nspine in first-time marathoners\nLumbar spine of asymptomatic first-time marathoners was assessed\nusing MRI 16 weeks before and 2 weeks after the marathon\nThe participants took part in a 16-week training program\nIn pre-race images disc degeneration was detected in 61% of partici-\npants, mostly at spinal segments L4-L5 and L5-S1 though asympto-\nAfter the marathon, no significant change in disc degeneration pro-\ngression or intervertebral disc height was found\nUltrasound evaluation of Achilles and patellar tendon and its associa-\ntion with future pain in marathon runners\nPrior to the marathon, tendon abnormalities were found in 24.1%\nof the Achilles and in 23.1% of the patellar tendons\nRunners with ultrasound abnormalities were significantly more likely\nto develop pain within 12 months after the race with a HR of 2.89 (95%\nlar tendon respectiv", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "eeks\nAge 42.9 ± 8.0 years\nEffect of marathon and ultra-marathon on inflammation and iron\nImmediately after the marathon, CK values increased by 3.0-fold\nand were still increasing by 4.2-fold 5 days post-race compared\nLDH increased significantly by 1."}
