{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0020_c0019", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c390ca9c935507a9", "text_span_hash": "c390ca9c935507a9", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 20, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 19, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "s non-\ncardiogenic pulmonary edema regarding the respiratory\nsystem, have also been described after marathon running\n[141, 142]. Typically, noncardiogenic pulmonary edema\nis observed in the context of severe exercise-induced\nhyponatremia, often accom", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\ninspiratory and expiratory muscles are impaired in their\nfunction to generate force compared to baseline capacity\n[12]. Usually, respiratory muscle strength is adequately\nmeasured using mouth-pressure maneuvers [12]. Stud-\nies found contradictory results regarding respiratory\nfatigue (see Table 4). A reduction of maximum expira-\ntory mouth pressure (MEP) but no changes in peak\nexpiratory flow (PEF) was found by some studies [50], in\ncontrast to an isolated decrease in PEF [134], while oth-\ners found no changes in expiratory function [127, 130].\nSimilar results regarding inspiratory respiratory muscle\nfatigue have been observed. Some studies observed a sig-\nnificant decrease in maximum inspiratory mouth pres-\nsure (MIP) and peak inspiratory flow (PIF) [130, 134],\nwhereas others reported no changes in MIP [50]. All in\nall, ambiguous results regarding inspiratory and expira-\ntory muscle fatigue are present. Nonetheless, current lit-\nerature suggests a tendency to inspiratory muscle fatigue\nafter a marathon race and expiratory muscle fatigue after\nan ultra-marathon race [12]. Taken together, changes\nin pulmonary function after a marathon race are most\nlikely explained by a combination of airway obstruction,\nrestriction, as well as respiratory muscle fatigue [12].\nAsthma and Atopy in Marathon Runners\nDespite positive effects of endurance training on lung\nvolumes and airway respon", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "s non-\ncardiogenic pulmonary edema regarding the respiratory\nsystem, have also been described after marathon running\n[141, 142]. Typically, noncardiogenic pulmonary edema\nis observed in the context of severe exercise-induced\nhyponatremia, often accom"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0020_c0021", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "a6dbbb87addd7c12", "text_span_hash": "a6dbbb87addd7c12", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 20, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 21, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ic\npulmonary edema, although, the pathogenesis is not\nentirely understood yet [142].\nIn conclusion, marathon training and running pose\nboth benefits and risks for the respiratory tract. Particu-\nlarly, regular physical exercise such as marathon train", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\ninspiratory and expiratory muscles are impaired in their\nfunction to generate force compared to baseline capacity\n[12]. Usually, respiratory muscle strength is adequately\nmeasured using mouth-pressure maneuvers [12]. Stud-\nies found contradictory results regarding respiratory\nfatigue (see Table 4). A reduction of maximum expira-\ntory mouth pressure (MEP) but no changes in peak\nexpiratory flow (PEF) was found by some studies [50], in\ncontrast to an isolated decrease in PEF [134], while oth-\ners found no changes in expiratory function [127, 130].\nSimilar results regarding inspiratory respiratory muscle\nfatigue have been observed. Some studies observed a sig-\nnificant decrease in maximum inspiratory mouth pres-\nsure (MIP) and peak inspiratory flow (PIF) [130, 134],\nwhereas others reported no changes in MIP [50]. All in\nall, ambiguous results regarding inspiratory and expira-\ntory muscle fatigue are present. Nonetheless, current lit-\nerature suggests a tendency to inspiratory muscle fatigue\nafter a marathon race and expiratory muscle fatigue after\nan ultra-marathon race [12]. Taken together, changes\nin pulmonary function after a marathon race are most\nlikely explained by a combination of airway obstruction,\nrestriction, as well as respiratory muscle fatigue [12].\nAsthma and Atopy in Marathon Runners\nDespite positive effects of endurance training on lung\nvolumes and airway respon", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ic\npulmonary edema, although, the pathogenesis is not\nentirely understood yet [142].\nIn conclusion, marathon training and running pose\nboth benefits and risks for the respiratory tract. Particu-\nlarly, regular physical exercise such as marathon train"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0020_c0022", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "531af597ed9bc8cb", "text_span_hash": "531af597ed9bc8cb", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 20, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 22, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "xercise such as marathon train-\ning seems to improve lung function and helps to prevent\ndeterioration of age-related decline in lung function.\nOn the other hand, negative effects of marathon run-\nning on respiratory function have been observed. Espe-", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\ninspiratory and expiratory muscles are impaired in their\nfunction to generate force compared to baseline capacity\n[12]. Usually, respiratory muscle strength is adequately\nmeasured using mouth-pressure maneuvers [12]. Stud-\nies found contradictory results regarding respiratory\nfatigue (see Table 4). A reduction of maximum expira-\ntory mouth pressure (MEP) but no changes in peak\nexpiratory flow (PEF) was found by some studies [50], in\ncontrast to an isolated decrease in PEF [134], while oth-\ners found no changes in expiratory function [127, 130].\nSimilar results regarding inspiratory respiratory muscle\nfatigue have been observed. Some studies observed a sig-\nnificant decrease in maximum inspiratory mouth pres-\nsure (MIP) and peak inspiratory flow (PIF) [130, 134],\nwhereas others reported no changes in MIP [50]. All in\nall, ambiguous results regarding inspiratory and expira-\ntory muscle fatigue are present. Nonetheless, current lit-\nerature suggests a tendency to inspiratory muscle fatigue\nafter a marathon race and expiratory muscle fatigue after\nan ultra-marathon race [12]. Taken together, changes\nin pulmonary function after a marathon race are most\nlikely explained by a combination of airway obstruction,\nrestriction, as well as respiratory muscle fatigue [12].\nAsthma and Atopy in Marathon Runners\nDespite positive effects of endurance training on lung\nvolumes and airway respon", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "xercise such as marathon train-\ning seems to improve lung function and helps to prevent\ndeterioration of age-related decline in lung function.\nOn the other hand, negative effects of marathon run-\nning on respiratory function have been observed. Espe-"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0020_c0024", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "bb7d231f5a90f227", "text_span_hash": "bb7d231f5a90f227", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 20, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 24, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ased risk for asthma, allergy, and\nThe respiratory system has not received the same\nresearch interest and popularity as the cardiovascular\nsystem and many aspects of how endurance exercise\ninfluences the respiratory system are not yet entirely\nclear,", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\ninspiratory and expiratory muscles are impaired in their\nfunction to generate force compared to baseline capacity\n[12]. Usually, respiratory muscle strength is adequately\nmeasured using mouth-pressure maneuvers [12]. Stud-\nies found contradictory results regarding respiratory\nfatigue (see Table 4). A reduction of maximum expira-\ntory mouth pressure (MEP) but no changes in peak\nexpiratory flow (PEF) was found by some studies [50], in\ncontrast to an isolated decrease in PEF [134], while oth-\ners found no changes in expiratory function [127, 130].\nSimilar results regarding inspiratory respiratory muscle\nfatigue have been observed. Some studies observed a sig-\nnificant decrease in maximum inspiratory mouth pres-\nsure (MIP) and peak inspiratory flow (PIF) [130, 134],\nwhereas others reported no changes in MIP [50]. All in\nall, ambiguous results regarding inspiratory and expira-\ntory muscle fatigue are present. Nonetheless, current lit-\nerature suggests a tendency to inspiratory muscle fatigue\nafter a marathon race and expiratory muscle fatigue after\nan ultra-marathon race [12]. Taken together, changes\nin pulmonary function after a marathon race are most\nlikely explained by a combination of airway obstruction,\nrestriction, as well as respiratory muscle fatigue [12].\nAsthma and Atopy in Marathon Runners\nDespite positive effects of endurance training on lung\nvolumes and airway respon", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ased risk for asthma, allergy, and\nThe respiratory system has not received the same\nresearch interest and popularity as the cardiovascular\nsystem and many aspects of how endurance exercise\ninfluences the respiratory system are not yet entirely\nclear,"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0020_c0026", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2d4cbb081744a3ee", "text_span_hash": "2d4cbb081744a3ee", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 20, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 26, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ction after a marathon race\nare also worth mentioning. Further studies with larger\nstudy populations are required and should investigate", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\ninspiratory and expiratory muscles are impaired in their\nfunction to generate force compared to baseline capacity\n[12]. Usually, respiratory muscle strength is adequately\nmeasured using mouth-pressure maneuvers [12]. Stud-\nies found contradictory results regarding respiratory\nfatigue (see Table 4). A reduction of maximum expira-\ntory mouth pressure (MEP) but no changes in peak\nexpiratory flow (PEF) was found by some studies [50], in\ncontrast to an isolated decrease in PEF [134], while oth-\ners found no changes in expiratory function [127, 130].\nSimilar results regarding inspiratory respiratory muscle\nfatigue have been observed. Some studies observed a sig-\nnificant decrease in maximum inspiratory mouth pres-\nsure (MIP) and peak inspiratory flow (PIF) [130, 134],\nwhereas others reported no changes in MIP [50]. All in\nall, ambiguous results regarding inspiratory and expira-\ntory muscle fatigue are present. Nonetheless, current lit-\nerature suggests a tendency to inspiratory muscle fatigue\nafter a marathon race and expiratory muscle fatigue after\nan ultra-marathon race [12]. Taken together, changes\nin pulmonary function after a marathon race are most\nlikely explained by a combination of airway obstruction,\nrestriction, as well as respiratory muscle fatigue [12].\nAsthma and Atopy in Marathon Runners\nDespite positive effects of endurance training on lung\nvolumes and airway respon", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ction after a marathon race\nare also worth mentioning. Further studies with larger\nstudy populations are required and should investigate"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0021_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9f6e6d7e9082ee15", "text_span_hash": "9f6e6d7e9082ee15", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 21, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nTable 6  Overview of key findings from studies investigating effects of marathon training and running on the immune system and its\nIntervention/Observation\nReduction of plasma concentration of CRP", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nTable 6  Overview of key findings from studies investigating effects of marathon training and running on the immune system and its\nIntervention/Observation\nReduction of plasma concentration of CRP follow-\ning nine months of endurance training\nDuring nine months of marathon training there\nwas a marked decrease in baseline CRP values\nThe median fell by 1.5-fold from initially 1.19 mg/l\nto 0.82 mg/l after the training period\nAge 42.9 ± 8.0 years\nEffect of marathon and ultra-marathon on inflam-\nAfter marathon and ultra-marathon, a significant\nincrease in CRP levels could be observed by 1.2-fold\nand 6.2-fold, respectively\nIn the marathon group, CRP values were still\nincreasing, and elevated 5-days post-race 1.5-fold\ncompared to baseline levels\nUltra-Marathon CRP values after 5-days were still\nelevated but in a decreasing fashion\nAge 42 ± 9 years\n72-h kinetics of inflammatory biomarkers\nImmediately after the race, IL-6, IL-10 and TNFα\nwere significantly elevated by 15.5-fold, 5.6-fold,\nand 1.1-fold, respectively\nThe values for hs-CRP were 21.4-fold increased\nafter 24-h post-race\nAll the measured values returned to baseline levels\nafter 3 days post-race\nDifferential time responses in inflammatory stress\nmarkers after a marathon\nThe plasma concentrations of TNFα, IL-6 and IL-10\nwere increased by 45%, 43-fold and 40-fold imme-\ndiately after the race\nNo changes in CRP concentrations w", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nTable 6  Overview of key findings from studies investigating effects of marathon training and running on the immune system and its\nIntervention/Observation\nReduction of plasma concentration of CRP"}
