 measured values returned to baseline levels\nafter 3 days post-race\nDifferential time responses in inflammatory stress\nmarkers after a marathon\nThe plasma concentrations of TNFα, IL-6 and IL-10\nwere increased by 45%, 43-fold and 40-fold imme-\ndiately after the race\nNo changes in CRP concentrations w", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "normal after 48-h\nAge 36.9 ± 0.2 years\nInfectious episodes in runners before and after\nRunners with higher training mileage per week\n(≥ 97 km/week) compared to lower mileage (< 32\nkm/week) had an increased risk for infectious epi-\nsodes during the 2"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0021_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "672f967f21f1ea32", "text_span_hash": "672f967f21f1ea32", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 21, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "e first week post-race,\nwhereas only 2.2% of the control group reported\nThere is an increased risk for URTI after completion\nof a marathon, namely OR 5.9 (95% CI 1.9–18.8)", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nTable 6  Overview of key findings from studies investigating effects of marathon training and running on the immune system and its\nIntervention/Observation\nReduction of plasma concentration of CRP follow-\ning nine months of endurance training\nDuring nine months of marathon training there\nwas a marked decrease in baseline CRP values\nThe median fell by 1.5-fold from initially 1.19 mg/l\nto 0.82 mg/l after the training period\nAge 42.9 ± 8.0 years\nEffect of marathon and ultra-marathon on inflam-\nAfter marathon and ultra-marathon, a significant\nincrease in CRP levels could be observed by 1.2-fold\nand 6.2-fold, respectively\nIn the marathon group, CRP values were still\nincreasing, and elevated 5-days post-race 1.5-fold\ncompared to baseline levels\nUltra-Marathon CRP values after 5-days were still\nelevated but in a decreasing fashion\nAge 42 ± 9 years\n72-h kinetics of inflammatory biomarkers\nImmediately after the race, IL-6, IL-10 and TNFα\nwere significantly elevated by 15.5-fold, 5.6-fold,\nand 1.1-fold, respectively\nThe values for hs-CRP were 21.4-fold increased\nafter 24-h post-race\nAll the measured values returned to baseline levels\nafter 3 days post-race\nDifferential time responses in inflammatory stress\nmarkers after a marathon\nThe plasma concentrations of TNFα, IL-6 and IL-10\nwere increased by 45%, 43-fold and 40-fold imme-\ndiately after the race\nNo changes in CRP concentrations w", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "e first week post-race,\nwhereas only 2.2% of the control group reported\nThere is an increased risk for URTI after completion\nof a marathon, namely OR 5.9 (95% CI 1.9–18.8)"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0022_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "77532525c2659524", "text_span_hash": "77532525c2659524", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 22, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\npotential mechanisms leading to small airway obstruc-\ntion as well as possible mediators of allergy and asthma\nto provide athletes with the best possible support.\nAltogether, marathon training and", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\npotential mechanisms leading to small airway obstruc-\ntion as well as possible mediators of allergy and asthma\nto provide athletes with the best possible support.\nAltogether, marathon training and running are gener-\nally safe for the respiratory system particularly consider-\ning that most changes occur only transiently.\nImpairment of renal function during marathon races is\nquite often observed [45]. We identified 29 studies inves-\ntigating the impacts of marathon training and running on\nthe renal system as well as on electrolyte and fluid bal-\nance. Table 7 gives an overview of the most important\nfindings from said studies.\nSignificance of Exercise in Chronic Kidney Disease Patients\nPhysical exercise is an important factor in the therapeutic\napproach of chronic kidney disease (CKD) patients [11].\nIt seems that regular moderate physical activity, such as\nmarathon training, slows disease progression in CKD\npatients (see Table 7) as physically active CKD patients\nhave better renal function compared to inactive controls\n[143, 144]. In fact, CKD patients who exercised > 150\nmin/week had a 1.5-fold slower estimated glomeru-\nlar filtration rate (eGFR) decline per week compared to\nsedentary controls [143]. Vigorous exercise may have\nacute adverse effects on renal function. However, mod-\nerate exercise is safe for patients with CKD, and its ben-\nefits exceed possible harms [11]. In a", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\npotential mechanisms leading to small airway obstruc-\ntion as well as possible mediators of allergy and asthma\nto provide athletes with the best possible support.\nAltogether, marathon training and"}
