[45, 46, 112, 157]. All biomarker elevations were resolved\nwithin 2 weeks and no signs of persisting impaired renal\nfunction were reported [112, 157]. Recent studies showed\nelevations of new blood and urine biomarkers of tubular\ndamage, such as neutrophil gelatinase-associated lipoca-\nlin (NGAL", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": ", 141, 160, 161]. A retrospec-\ntive study found significantly elevated post-race plasma\nsodium levels compared to baseline in marathoners, with\nonly 15% of readings outside the normal range in non-\ncollapsed runners. However, no significant differenc"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0025_c0018", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b18b776dfce61b57", "text_span_hash": "b18b776dfce61b57", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 25, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 18, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ning fluids as well as an adequate drink-\ning strategy (~ 500–1000 ml/h), which must be individu-\nally adapted and implemented into each athlete’s training\nKidney function during a marathon has been diligently\nresearched. Regular strenuous exercise h", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\noccurrence of AKI during marathon competitions and\nshould be avoided [112].\nIn addition, exercise-induced release of catechola-\nmines, antidiuretic hormone (ADH), and activation of\nthe RAAS may impair kidney function and reduce GFR\nSeveral studies reported elevated kidney biomarkers\n(see Table 1, Table 7, and Fig. 2) during and after mara-\nthon races [45–47, 112, 154–157]. In general, an average\nrise in serum creatinine levels by 29 µmol/l or approxi-\nmately by 40% with an accompanying decrease in glo-\nmerular filtration rate (GFR) can be observed [112, 155].\nUsually, serum creatinine levels normalize within 24 h\npost-race [157]. Still, a post-race decline in GFR has been\nreported by several studies [46, 158].\nCreatinine plays an important role in muscle metabo-\nlism especially during exercise which might distort\nmeasurement of kidney function based on serum creati-\nnine. Nonetheless, concomitant increases up to 26% in\nserum levels of cystatin C, which is a muscle-independ-\nent marker of kidney function, have been observed (see\nTable 7 and Fig. 2), proving kidney injury to some extent\n[45, 46, 112, 157]. All biomarker elevations were resolved\nwithin 2 weeks and no signs of persisting impaired renal\nfunction were reported [112, 157]. Recent studies showed\nelevations of new blood and urine biomarkers of tubular\ndamage, such as neutrophil gelatinase-associated lipoca-\nlin (NGAL", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ning fluids as well as an adequate drink-\ning strategy (~ 500–1000 ml/h), which must be individu-\nally adapted and implemented into each athlete’s training\nKidney function during a marathon has been diligently\nresearched. Regular strenuous exercise h"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0025_c0023", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "349eff0f5b3cd87a", "text_span_hash": "349eff0f5b3cd87a", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 25, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 23, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "studying the impact of marathon\ntraining and running on the GI system.\nImprovements in Gastrointestinal Health\nModerate exercise, such as marathon training has several\nbeneficial effects on the GI system (see Table 8). Regular\nphysical activity impr", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\noccurrence of AKI during marathon competitions and\nshould be avoided [112].\nIn addition, exercise-induced release of catechola-\nmines, antidiuretic hormone (ADH), and activation of\nthe RAAS may impair kidney function and reduce GFR\nSeveral studies reported elevated kidney biomarkers\n(see Table 1, Table 7, and Fig. 2) during and after mara-\nthon races [45–47, 112, 154–157]. In general, an average\nrise in serum creatinine levels by 29 µmol/l or approxi-\nmately by 40% with an accompanying decrease in glo-\nmerular filtration rate (GFR) can be observed [112, 155].\nUsually, serum creatinine levels normalize within 24 h\npost-race [157]. Still, a post-race decline in GFR has been\nreported by several studies [46, 158].\nCreatinine plays an important role in muscle metabo-\nlism especially during exercise which might distort\nmeasurement of kidney function based on serum creati-\nnine. Nonetheless, concomitant increases up to 26% in\nserum levels of cystatin C, which is a muscle-independ-\nent marker of kidney function, have been observed (see\nTable 7 and Fig. 2), proving kidney injury to some extent\n[45, 46, 112, 157]. All biomarker elevations were resolved\nwithin 2 weeks and no signs of persisting impaired renal\nfunction were reported [112, 157]. Recent studies showed\nelevations of new blood and urine biomarkers of tubular\ndamage, such as neutrophil gelatinase-associated lipoca-\nlin (NGAL", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "studying the impact of marathon\ntraining and running on the GI system.\nImprovements in Gastrointestinal Health\nModerate exercise, such as marathon training has several\nbeneficial effects on the GI system (see Table 8). Regular\nphysical activity impr"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0027_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "84a72d4501a51308", "text_span_hash": "84a72d4501a51308", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 27, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nTable 8  Overview of key findings from studies investigating effects of marathon training and running on the GI system\nIntervention/Observation\nEffects of physical activity on intestinal gas transi", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nTable 8  Overview of key findings from studies investigating effects of marathon training and running on the GI system\nIntervention/Observation\nEffects of physical activity on intestinal gas transit\nIntestinal gas retention was lower during exercise com-\nGut transit of intraluminal gas is improved by moderate\nSedentary behavior and risk for colorectal cancer\nRisk for colorectal cancer was lower (HR = 0.84, 95% CI\n0.72–0.98) in physically active individuals with metabolic\nequivalent hours ≥ 60 per week compared to a control\ngroup with < 10 metabolic equivalent hours\nInfluence of physical activity on survival after colorectal\nCancer-specific mortality as well as overall mortality\nwere reduced in physically active women with a HR\nAge 43.4 ± 9.5 years\nPrevalence of gastrointestinal symptoms during a mara-\nthon in non-elite athletes and correlation with nutritional\n47% of runners reported symptoms 7 days prior\nto the marathon and 27% reported GI symptoms dur-\ning marathon. Most frequent symptoms were flatulence\n(16%) and nausea (8%). No statistically significant cor-\nrelation between nutritional intake and occurrence of GI\nsymptoms were observed\nFluid intake and GI problems in a 25-km race and a mara-\nGI symptoms were commonly reported. In the 25-km\nrace 25% of runners and 52% in the marathon race com-\nplained about GI symptoms, most commonly stomach\nache (25%), side ache (23%), ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nTable 8  Overview of key findings from studies investigating effects of marathon training and running on the GI system\nIntervention/Observation\nEffects of physical activity on intestinal gas transi"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0027_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f13b9e0f65fd3f9e", "text_span_hash": "f13b9e0f65fd3f9e", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 27, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "of marathon training and running on bowel func-\ntion, GI blood loss and iron status in women\nMarathon running compared to sedentary controls\nresulted in a 21% increase in gut transit time, a 27%\ndecrease in daily stool weight and 21% lower frequency", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nTable 8  Overview of key findings from studies investigating effects of marathon training and running on the GI system\nIntervention/Observation\nEffects of physical activity on intestinal gas transit\nIntestinal gas retention was lower during exercise com-\nGut transit of intraluminal gas is improved by moderate\nSedentary behavior and risk for colorectal cancer\nRisk for colorectal cancer was lower (HR = 0.84, 95% CI\n0.72–0.98) in physically active individuals with metabolic\nequivalent hours ≥ 60 per week compared to a control\ngroup with < 10 metabolic equivalent hours\nInfluence of physical activity on survival after colorectal\nCancer-specific mortality as well as overall mortality\nwere reduced in physically active women with a HR\nAge 43.4 ± 9.5 years\nPrevalence of gastrointestinal symptoms during a mara-\nthon in non-elite athletes and correlation with nutritional\n47% of runners reported symptoms 7 days prior\nto the marathon and 27% reported GI symptoms dur-\ning marathon. Most frequent symptoms were flatulence\n(16%) and nausea (8%). No statistically significant cor-\nrelation between nutritional intake and occurrence of GI\nsymptoms were observed\nFluid intake and GI problems in a 25-km race and a mara-\nGI symptoms were commonly reported. In the 25-km\nrace 25% of runners and 52% in the marathon race com-\nplained about GI symptoms, most commonly stomach\nache (25%), side ache (23%), ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "of marathon training and running on bowel func-\ntion, GI blood loss and iron status in women\nMarathon running compared to sedentary controls\nresulted in a 21% increase in gut transit time, a 27%\ndecrease in daily stool weight and 21% lower frequency"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0027_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "cb2708502b30815d", "text_span_hash": "cb2708502b30815d", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 27, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "weight and 21% lower frequency\nThis study found no significant Hb loss after a mara-\nthon as well as no differences in iron status\nFecal blood loss in response to walking or marathon\nThere was no increase in fecal hemoglobin content\nin the walking gr", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nTable 8  Overview of key findings from studies investigating effects of marathon training and running on the GI system\nIntervention/Observation\nEffects of physical activity on intestinal gas transit\nIntestinal gas retention was lower during exercise com-\nGut transit of intraluminal gas is improved by moderate\nSedentary behavior and risk for colorectal cancer\nRisk for colorectal cancer was lower (HR = 0.84, 95% CI\n0.72–0.98) in physically active individuals with metabolic\nequivalent hours ≥ 60 per week compared to a control\ngroup with < 10 metabolic equivalent hours\nInfluence of physical activity on survival after colorectal\nCancer-specific mortality as well as overall mortality\nwere reduced in physically active women with a HR\nAge 43.4 ± 9.5 years\nPrevalence of gastrointestinal symptoms during a mara-\nthon in non-elite athletes and correlation with nutritional\n47% of runners reported symptoms 7 days prior\nto the marathon and 27% reported GI symptoms dur-\ning marathon. Most frequent symptoms were flatulence\n(16%) and nausea (8%). No statistically significant cor-\nrelation between nutritional intake and occurrence of GI\nsymptoms were observed\nFluid intake and GI problems in a 25-km race and a mara-\nGI symptoms were commonly reported. In the 25-km\nrace 25% of runners and 52% in the marathon race com-\nplained about GI symptoms, most commonly stomach\nache (25%), side ache (23%), ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "weight and 21% lower frequency\nThis study found no significant Hb loss after a mara-\nthon as well as no differences in iron status\nFecal blood loss in response to walking or marathon\nThere was no increase in fecal hemoglobin content\nin the walking gr"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0028_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "75fd1e43bea7182f", "text_span_hash": "75fd1e43bea7182f", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 28, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "m of GI distress (see Table 8), younger\nrunners, as well as female athletes, are affected more\nfrequently [52, 162, 163, 170]. Typically reported symp-\ntoms include nausea, vomiting, heartburn, loss of\nappetite, abdominal cramps, flatulence, bloating", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nbowel disease (IBD) or irritable bowel syndrome (IBS)\nand reduces the risk for diverticular disease as well as\nconstipation [162, 163]. Furthermore, the risk for chole-\nlithiasis is reduced in physically active men and women\nindependently from other risk factors, such as obesity,\nby factors of up to 3.3 and 2.3, respectively [165, 166].\nRegular physical activity, such as marathon training, has\nproven positive effects on the incidence of colorectal\ncancer. Physically active individuals have a 1.2–3.6-fold\nreduced risk of developing colorectal cancer compared\nto inactive controls [167, 168]. Similarly, regular physical\nactivity reduces cancer-specific as well as overall mortal-\nity in patients suffering from colorectal cancer [169].\nGastrointestinal Distress\nHigh-intensity or long-distance exercise also seems to\nentail several negative effects on the GI system, as GI\ncomplaints are one of the most commonly reported\nsymptoms during a marathon race [51, 170]. In stud-\nies, 20–57% of marathon runners complained about at\nleast one symptom of GI distress (see Table 8), younger\nrunners, as well as female athletes, are affected more\nfrequently [52, 162, 163, 170]. Typically reported symp-\ntoms include nausea, vomiting, heartburn, loss of\nappetite, abdominal cramps, flatulence, bloating, urge\nto have a bowel movement and (bloody) diarrhea [51,\n52, 163, 171]. To date, the etiology of s", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "m of GI distress (see Table 8), younger\nrunners, as well as female athletes, are affected more\nfrequently [52, 162, 163, 170]. Typically reported symp-\ntoms include nausea, vomiting, heartburn, loss of\nappetite, abdominal cramps, flatulence, bloating"}
