ell as female athletes, are affected more\nfrequently [52, 162, 163, 170]. Typically reported symp-\ntoms include nausea, vomiting, heartburn, loss of\nappetite, abdominal cramps, flatulence, bloating, urge\nto have a bowel movement and (bloody) diarrhea [51,\n52, 163, 171]. To date, the etiology of s", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "~ 10 g/kg body\nTable 8  (continued)\nIntervention/Observation\nAge 38 ± 10 years\nIncidence of GI blood loss after running a marathon\n22.4% of marathon finishers with prior to the mara-\nthon negative Hemoccult test had positive test results\nafter the ma"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0028_c0022", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b1db6674aff2b7e4", "text_span_hash": "b1db6674aff2b7e4", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 28, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 22, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": ", NSAID non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, VIP\nvasoactive intestinal peptide, 95% CI 95% confidence interval", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nbowel disease (IBD) or irritable bowel syndrome (IBS)\nand reduces the risk for diverticular disease as well as\nconstipation [162, 163]. Furthermore, the risk for chole-\nlithiasis is reduced in physically active men and women\nindependently from other risk factors, such as obesity,\nby factors of up to 3.3 and 2.3, respectively [165, 166].\nRegular physical activity, such as marathon training, has\nproven positive effects on the incidence of colorectal\ncancer. Physically active individuals have a 1.2–3.6-fold\nreduced risk of developing colorectal cancer compared\nto inactive controls [167, 168]. Similarly, regular physical\nactivity reduces cancer-specific as well as overall mortal-\nity in patients suffering from colorectal cancer [169].\nGastrointestinal Distress\nHigh-intensity or long-distance exercise also seems to\nentail several negative effects on the GI system, as GI\ncomplaints are one of the most commonly reported\nsymptoms during a marathon race [51, 170]. In stud-\nies, 20–57% of marathon runners complained about at\nleast one symptom of GI distress (see Table 8), younger\nrunners, as well as female athletes, are affected more\nfrequently [52, 162, 163, 170]. Typically reported symp-\ntoms include nausea, vomiting, heartburn, loss of\nappetite, abdominal cramps, flatulence, bloating, urge\nto have a bowel movement and (bloody) diarrhea [51,\n52, 163, 171]. To date, the etiology of s", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": ", NSAID non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, VIP\nvasoactive intestinal peptide, 95% CI 95% confidence interval"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0029_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5d26d203452fe019", "text_span_hash": "5d26d203452fe019", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 29, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ing adequate recovery, and uti-\nlizing screening tools like the REDs Clinical Assessment\nTool for early detection [178, 179].\nGastrointestinal Bleeding\nSeveral studies observed occult GI blood loss associated\nwith long-distance running (see Table 8)", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nmass per day) 24–36 h prior to the race was shown to\nimprove the ability to maintain a steady pace for longer\nand should be encouraged for races > 30 km [175]. Other\nstudies reported dietary restrictions prior to the race to\nprevent GI distress as certain nutritional elements e.g.,\nfat and protein, have been suggested to induce exercise-\ninduced GI symptoms [176]. The most commonly omit-\nted foods were milk products (rich in fat and protein),\nhigh protein, high-fiber, chocolate, and caffeine [176].\nThe most recent study on GI cell injury after running the\nBoston Marathon found that intestinal cell injury and GI\nsymptoms decreased from the marathon finish to the day\nafter the marathon and the reduction was influenced by\nhydration status [177].\nLow energy intake and relative energy deficiency in sport\n(REDs) occur when the energy intake is insufficient to\nmeet the demands of training and basic physiological\nfunctions [178, 179]. This disproportion between caloric\nconsumption and energy expenditure is common among\nendurance athletes such as marathon runners [179]. The\nprevalence of low energy intake and REDs is particu-\nlarly high in female runners though comprehensive data\non exact prevalence rates remain limited [179]. Gen-\nerally, there is a lack of awareness regarding the energy\ndemands of high-intensity training [179]. REDs has far-\nreaching effects on metabolism, cardiova", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ing adequate recovery, and uti-\nlizing screening tools like the REDs Clinical Assessment\nTool for early detection [178, 179].\nGastrointestinal Bleeding\nSeveral studies observed occult GI blood loss associated\nwith long-distance running (see Table 8)"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0029_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f0146749fc4ee187", "text_span_hash": "f0146749fc4ee187", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 29, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "istance running (see Table 8) [182–186]. It\nis estimated that in ~ 8–30% of marathon runners occult\nbleeding is found in stool samples [187]. While the etiol-\nogy is still unclear, ischemia due to reduced splanchnic\nblood flow, as well as direct mech", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nmass per day) 24–36 h prior to the race was shown to\nimprove the ability to maintain a steady pace for longer\nand should be encouraged for races > 30 km [175]. Other\nstudies reported dietary restrictions prior to the race to\nprevent GI distress as certain nutritional elements e.g.,\nfat and protein, have been suggested to induce exercise-\ninduced GI symptoms [176]. The most commonly omit-\nted foods were milk products (rich in fat and protein),\nhigh protein, high-fiber, chocolate, and caffeine [176].\nThe most recent study on GI cell injury after running the\nBoston Marathon found that intestinal cell injury and GI\nsymptoms decreased from the marathon finish to the day\nafter the marathon and the reduction was influenced by\nhydration status [177].\nLow energy intake and relative energy deficiency in sport\n(REDs) occur when the energy intake is insufficient to\nmeet the demands of training and basic physiological\nfunctions [178, 179]. This disproportion between caloric\nconsumption and energy expenditure is common among\nendurance athletes such as marathon runners [179]. The\nprevalence of low energy intake and REDs is particu-\nlarly high in female runners though comprehensive data\non exact prevalence rates remain limited [179]. Gen-\nerally, there is a lack of awareness regarding the energy\ndemands of high-intensity training [179]. REDs has far-\nreaching effects on metabolism, cardiova", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "istance running (see Table 8) [182–186]. It\nis estimated that in ~ 8–30% of marathon runners occult\nbleeding is found in stool samples [187]. While the etiol-\nogy is still unclear, ischemia due to reduced splanchnic\nblood flow, as well as direct mech"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0029_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "58effd1e39d33015", "text_span_hash": "58effd1e39d33015", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 29, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "d flow, as well as direct mechanical trauma through\nbowel irritation, are the most plausible explanations\n[163, 184, 186]. GI bleeding is significantly associated\nwith faster race times and less experienced runners [163,\n183, 188]. In relation to GI", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nmass per day) 24–36 h prior to the race was shown to\nimprove the ability to maintain a steady pace for longer\nand should be encouraged for races > 30 km [175]. Other\nstudies reported dietary restrictions prior to the race to\nprevent GI distress as certain nutritional elements e.g.,\nfat and protein, have been suggested to induce exercise-\ninduced GI symptoms [176]. The most commonly omit-\nted foods were milk products (rich in fat and protein),\nhigh protein, high-fiber, chocolate, and caffeine [176].\nThe most recent study on GI cell injury after running the\nBoston Marathon found that intestinal cell injury and GI\nsymptoms decreased from the marathon finish to the day\nafter the marathon and the reduction was influenced by\nhydration status [177].\nLow energy intake and relative energy deficiency in sport\n(REDs) occur when the energy intake is insufficient to\nmeet the demands of training and basic physiological\nfunctions [178, 179]. This disproportion between caloric\nconsumption and energy expenditure is common among\nendurance athletes such as marathon runners [179]. The\nprevalence of low energy intake and REDs is particu-\nlarly high in female runners though comprehensive data\non exact prevalence rates remain limited [179]. Gen-\nerally, there is a lack of awareness regarding the energy\ndemands of high-intensity training [179]. REDs has far-\nreaching effects on metabolism, cardiova", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "d flow, as well as direct mechanical trauma through\nbowel irritation, are the most plausible explanations\n[163, 184, 186]. GI bleeding is significantly associated\nwith faster race times and less experienced runners [163,\n183, 188]. In relation to GI"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0029_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "eb7e531ca8fa2be1", "text_span_hash": "eb7e531ca8fa2be1", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 29, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": ". GI blood loss\nin long-distance running is usually occult and transient,\nbut there are reported cases with severe ischemic coli-\ntis, sometimes requiring surgical intervention [163, 187,\nThe GI system in relation to long-distance endurance\nsports en", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nmass per day) 24–36 h prior to the race was shown to\nimprove the ability to maintain a steady pace for longer\nand should be encouraged for races > 30 km [175]. Other\nstudies reported dietary restrictions prior to the race to\nprevent GI distress as certain nutritional elements e.g.,\nfat and protein, have been suggested to induce exercise-\ninduced GI symptoms [176]. The most commonly omit-\nted foods were milk products (rich in fat and protein),\nhigh protein, high-fiber, chocolate, and caffeine [176].\nThe most recent study on GI cell injury after running the\nBoston Marathon found that intestinal cell injury and GI\nsymptoms decreased from the marathon finish to the day\nafter the marathon and the reduction was influenced by\nhydration status [177].\nLow energy intake and relative energy deficiency in sport\n(REDs) occur when the energy intake is insufficient to\nmeet the demands of training and basic physiological\nfunctions [178, 179]. This disproportion between caloric\nconsumption and energy expenditure is common among\nendurance athletes such as marathon runners [179]. The\nprevalence of low energy intake and REDs is particu-\nlarly high in female runners though comprehensive data\non exact prevalence rates remain limited [179]. Gen-\nerally, there is a lack of awareness regarding the energy\ndemands of high-intensity training [179]. REDs has far-\nreaching effects on metabolism, cardiova", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": ". GI blood loss\nin long-distance running is usually occult and transient,\nbut there are reported cases with severe ischemic coli-\ntis, sometimes requiring surgical intervention [163, 187,\nThe GI system in relation to long-distance endurance\nsports en"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0029_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b02447252f54266c", "text_span_hash": "b02447252f54266c", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 29, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "g-distance endurance\nsports enjoys great research interest. Benefits of regu-\nlar physical exercise on the GI system include improved\nGI motility and transit times as well as reduced risk for\ndiverticulitis, cholelithiasis and colorectal cancer. How-", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nmass per day) 24–36 h prior to the race was shown to\nimprove the ability to maintain a steady pace for longer\nand should be encouraged for races > 30 km [175]. Other\nstudies reported dietary restrictions prior to the race to\nprevent GI distress as certain nutritional elements e.g.,\nfat and protein, have been suggested to induce exercise-\ninduced GI symptoms [176]. The most commonly omit-\nted foods were milk products (rich in fat and protein),\nhigh protein, high-fiber, chocolate, and caffeine [176].\nThe most recent study on GI cell injury after running the\nBoston Marathon found that intestinal cell injury and GI\nsymptoms decreased from the marathon finish to the day\nafter the marathon and the reduction was influenced by\nhydration status [177].\nLow energy intake and relative energy deficiency in sport\n(REDs) occur when the energy intake is insufficient to\nmeet the demands of training and basic physiological\nfunctions [178, 179]. This disproportion between caloric\nconsumption and energy expenditure is common among\nendurance athletes such as marathon runners [179]. The\nprevalence of low energy intake and REDs is particu-\nlarly high in female runners though comprehensive data\non exact prevalence rates remain limited [179]. Gen-\nerally, there is a lack of awareness regarding the energy\ndemands of high-intensity training [179]. REDs has far-\nreaching effects on metabolism, cardiova", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "g-distance endurance\nsports enjoys great research interest. Benefits of regu-\nlar physical exercise on the GI system include improved\nGI motility and transit times as well as reduced risk for\ndiverticulitis, cholelithiasis and colorectal cancer. How-"}
