te exercise, which makes physical activity\nsafe for cirrhotic patients, and positive effects outweigh\npossible negative consequences in the long term [14]. A\nrecent meta-analysis (see Table 9) investigating the risk\nof hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) in physically active\nsubjects found that great", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "as associated with even\nhigher risk reduction with OR 0.62 (95% CI 0.49–0.79)\nChanges of liver enzymes after completion of a mara-\nthon and a half-marathon\nAST and ALT showed significant post-race elevation\nwith peak values at 48 h after the race\nMar"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0030_c0016", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c18fe7c4f5d7d12b", "text_span_hash": "c18fe7c4f5d7d12b", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 30, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 16, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "e\nMarked increase after the marathon in ALT, AST\nand total bilirubin by 1.1-fold, 1.3-fold, and 1.1-fold,\nSignificantly higher increases in these parameters\ncould be observed after the 100-km and 308-km race,\nsuggesting intensity related mechanisms i", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nmetabolism, increase insulin sensitivity and to signifi-\ncantly reduce intrahepatic fat by 3.5%, as well as fast-\ning free fatty acids, thus improving liver function (see\nTable 9) [190]. Furthermore, physical activity reduces\noxidative stress in the liver and vascular endothelium\nand leads to generally improved cardiorespiratory fit-\nness, which decreases inflammation and fibrosis progres-\nsion in NAFLD patients [14]. Similarly, patients with a\nchronic hepatitis C infection undertaking a moderate\nexercise program showed significantly improved liver\nfunction [14]. Moderate exercise in patients with com-\npensated cirrhosis resulted in improved maximal oxy-\ngen consumption (V̇O2max) values and led to a ≥ 10%\ndecrease of hepatic venous pressure gradient (HVPG)\nin 42% of patients without changes in hepatic blood\nflow, thus improving portal hypertension and quality of\nlife [14]. Acute exercise leads to a significant increase\nin portal pressure by 16–21% and a marked decrease\nin hepatic blood flow [191]. However, pretreatment\nwith beta-blockers compensates for the HVPG increase\ncaused by acute exercise, which makes physical activity\nsafe for cirrhotic patients, and positive effects outweigh\npossible negative consequences in the long term [14]. A\nrecent meta-analysis (see Table 9) investigating the risk\nof hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) in physically active\nsubjects found that great", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "e\nMarked increase after the marathon in ALT, AST\nand total bilirubin by 1.1-fold, 1.3-fold, and 1.1-fold,\nSignificantly higher increases in these parameters\ncould be observed after the 100-km and 308-km race,\nsuggesting intensity related mechanisms i"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0030_c0018", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7a90b825d1dbd013", "text_span_hash": "7a90b825d1dbd013", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 30, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 18, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "flated up to 220 mmHg while control group occlu-\nsion cuff was set to 20 mmHg. Color flow Doppler\nultrasound was used to ensure full closure of arterial\nImmediately after the marathon. The control group\nshowed a significantly more pronounced increase", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nmetabolism, increase insulin sensitivity and to signifi-\ncantly reduce intrahepatic fat by 3.5%, as well as fast-\ning free fatty acids, thus improving liver function (see\nTable 9) [190]. Furthermore, physical activity reduces\noxidative stress in the liver and vascular endothelium\nand leads to generally improved cardiorespiratory fit-\nness, which decreases inflammation and fibrosis progres-\nsion in NAFLD patients [14]. Similarly, patients with a\nchronic hepatitis C infection undertaking a moderate\nexercise program showed significantly improved liver\nfunction [14]. Moderate exercise in patients with com-\npensated cirrhosis resulted in improved maximal oxy-\ngen consumption (V̇O2max) values and led to a ≥ 10%\ndecrease of hepatic venous pressure gradient (HVPG)\nin 42% of patients without changes in hepatic blood\nflow, thus improving portal hypertension and quality of\nlife [14]. Acute exercise leads to a significant increase\nin portal pressure by 16–21% and a marked decrease\nin hepatic blood flow [191]. However, pretreatment\nwith beta-blockers compensates for the HVPG increase\ncaused by acute exercise, which makes physical activity\nsafe for cirrhotic patients, and positive effects outweigh\npossible negative consequences in the long term [14]. A\nrecent meta-analysis (see Table 9) investigating the risk\nof hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) in physically active\nsubjects found that great", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "flated up to 220 mmHg while control group occlu-\nsion cuff was set to 20 mmHg. Color flow Doppler\nultrasound was used to ensure full closure of arterial\nImmediately after the marathon. The control group\nshowed a significantly more pronounced increase"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0032_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2b394486dc1a4d29", "text_span_hash": "2b394486dc1a4d29", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 32, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "anine transaminase (ALT)\nwith peak values at 48 h post-race (see Table 9 and Fig. 4)\n[196]. Running a marathon also leads to an increase\nin parameters of cholestasis, i.e., alkaline phosphatase\n(ALP), gamma-glutamyltransferase (GGT), and bilirubin\nwh", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nmodel comparing sorafenib with sorafenib and moderate\nexercise in mice with HCC showed a significantly higher\nreduction in tumor volume in exercising mice compared\nto sedentary controls, highlighting the positive influence\nof physical activity on tumor growth and progression\nMarkers of Hepatobiliary Damage\nNegative effects of marathon running on the liver have\nbeen described, nonetheless. Several studies reported\nelevated liver biomarkers during or after the completion\nof a marathon even in adolescent runners [59, 195–199].\nSignificant post-race changes occurred in aspartate\ntransaminase (AST) and alanine transaminase (ALT)\nwith peak values at 48 h post-race (see Table 9 and Fig. 4)\n[196]. Running a marathon also leads to an increase\nin parameters of cholestasis, i.e., alkaline phosphatase\n(ALP), gamma-glutamyltransferase (GGT), and bilirubin\nwhich normally return to baseline levels within 24 h after\nthe race (see Fig. 4) [197, 198]. These biomarker changes\nsuggest exercise-induced damage to the liver, which\nseems to depend on the exercise intensity as elevations in\nliver enzymes as well as bilirubin were markedly higher\nafter ultra-marathon events [195].\nThe etiology of these biomarker elevations is not\nentirely clear and different possible mechanisms are\ncurrently being debated. AST and GGT are not liver-\nspecific and also produced by muscle cells, which in\nturn may reflec", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "anine transaminase (ALT)\nwith peak values at 48 h post-race (see Table 9 and Fig. 4)\n[196]. Running a marathon also leads to an increase\nin parameters of cholestasis, i.e., alkaline phosphatase\n(ALP), gamma-glutamyltransferase (GGT), and bilirubin\nwh"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0033_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "950f5f45f66483ac", "text_span_hash": "950f5f45f66483ac", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 33, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "se markers of liver and heart\ndamage after a marathon (see Table 9), highlighting the\nimpact of oxidative stress on liver function during the\nlong-distance events [201].\nAcute Liver Failure in Exercise\nThere are increasing numbers of reported cases o", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nhepatocellular membrane permeability or altered met-\nabolic demands as they play key roles in glucose and\nlipid metabolism, such as fatty acid oxidation and glu-\nconeogenesis [196, 200]. Nevertheless, ALP and ALT\nare highly liver-specific parameters and underline\nhepatic damage to some extent, which might be due to\nischemic factors or increased oxidative stress induced\nby free radicals generated by the high metabolic\ndemands of muscles [195, 201]. Interestingly, remote\nischemic preconditioning (RIPC) of leg muscles using\ncycles of short-lasting leg ischemia seems to reduce\noxidative stress and decrease markers of liver and heart\ndamage after a marathon (see Table 9), highlighting the\nimpact of oxidative stress on liver function during the\nlong-distance events [201].\nAcute Liver Failure in Exercise\nThere are increasing numbers of reported cases of acute\nliver failure following a marathon [10, 202–204]. Most\nof these cases of acute liver failure were associated with\nEHS or extensive rhabdomyolysis, which in turn leads\nto a systemic inflammatory response with consecutive\nmultiple organ failure [10, 202]. These patients were criti-\ncally ill and often needed liver transplantation in ~ 30% of\ncases [202]. Some patients responded reasonably well to\nconservative treatment, but mortality in patients present-\ning with acute liver failure after a marathon must not be\nunderestimated as", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "se markers of liver and heart\ndamage after a marathon (see Table 9), highlighting the\nimpact of oxidative stress on liver function during the\nlong-distance events [201].\nAcute Liver Failure in Exercise\nThere are increasing numbers of reported cases o"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0033_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "24f3a1e7d79c4c0b", "text_span_hash": "24f3a1e7d79c4c0b", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 33, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ng numbers of reported cases of acute\nliver failure following a marathon [10, 202–204]. Most\nof these cases of acute liver failure were associated with\nEHS or extensive rhabdomyolysis, which in turn leads\nto a systemic inflammatory response with cons", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nhepatocellular membrane permeability or altered met-\nabolic demands as they play key roles in glucose and\nlipid metabolism, such as fatty acid oxidation and glu-\nconeogenesis [196, 200]. Nevertheless, ALP and ALT\nare highly liver-specific parameters and underline\nhepatic damage to some extent, which might be due to\nischemic factors or increased oxidative stress induced\nby free radicals generated by the high metabolic\ndemands of muscles [195, 201]. Interestingly, remote\nischemic preconditioning (RIPC) of leg muscles using\ncycles of short-lasting leg ischemia seems to reduce\noxidative stress and decrease markers of liver and heart\ndamage after a marathon (see Table 9), highlighting the\nimpact of oxidative stress on liver function during the\nlong-distance events [201].\nAcute Liver Failure in Exercise\nThere are increasing numbers of reported cases of acute\nliver failure following a marathon [10, 202–204]. Most\nof these cases of acute liver failure were associated with\nEHS or extensive rhabdomyolysis, which in turn leads\nto a systemic inflammatory response with consecutive\nmultiple organ failure [10, 202]. These patients were criti-\ncally ill and often needed liver transplantation in ~ 30% of\ncases [202]. Some patients responded reasonably well to\nconservative treatment, but mortality in patients present-\ning with acute liver failure after a marathon must not be\nunderestimated as", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ng numbers of reported cases of acute\nliver failure following a marathon [10, 202–204]. Most\nof these cases of acute liver failure were associated with\nEHS or extensive rhabdomyolysis, which in turn leads\nto a systemic inflammatory response with cons"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0033_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4d737e97aa27ed2f", "text_span_hash": "4d737e97aa27ed2f", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 33, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ervative treatment, but mortality in patients present-\ning with acute liver failure after a marathon must not be\nunderestimated as it approaches nearly 33% [10, 202].\nResearch interest in the effects of marathon training and\nrunning on liver function", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nhepatocellular membrane permeability or altered met-\nabolic demands as they play key roles in glucose and\nlipid metabolism, such as fatty acid oxidation and glu-\nconeogenesis [196, 200]. Nevertheless, ALP and ALT\nare highly liver-specific parameters and underline\nhepatic damage to some extent, which might be due to\nischemic factors or increased oxidative stress induced\nby free radicals generated by the high metabolic\ndemands of muscles [195, 201]. Interestingly, remote\nischemic preconditioning (RIPC) of leg muscles using\ncycles of short-lasting leg ischemia seems to reduce\noxidative stress and decrease markers of liver and heart\ndamage after a marathon (see Table 9), highlighting the\nimpact of oxidative stress on liver function during the\nlong-distance events [201].\nAcute Liver Failure in Exercise\nThere are increasing numbers of reported cases of acute\nliver failure following a marathon [10, 202–204]. Most\nof these cases of acute liver failure were associated with\nEHS or extensive rhabdomyolysis, which in turn leads\nto a systemic inflammatory response with consecutive\nmultiple organ failure [10, 202]. These patients were criti-\ncally ill and often needed liver transplantation in ~ 30% of\ncases [202]. Some patients responded reasonably well to\nconservative treatment, but mortality in patients present-\ning with acute liver failure after a marathon must not be\nunderestimated as", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ervative treatment, but mortality in patients present-\ning with acute liver failure after a marathon must not be\nunderestimated as it approaches nearly 33% [10, 202].\nResearch interest in the effects of marathon training and\nrunning on liver function"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0033_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9177ca0b44d123b2", "text_span_hash": "9177ca0b44d123b2", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 33, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "and\nrunning on liver function is quite recent. Regular physical\nactivity was found to have a positive impact on NAFLD,\nlipid and glucose metabolism, decreased portal hyperten-\nsion in cirrhotic patients and reduced the risk of HCC.\nStill, marathon r", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nhepatocellular membrane permeability or altered met-\nabolic demands as they play key roles in glucose and\nlipid metabolism, such as fatty acid oxidation and glu-\nconeogenesis [196, 200]. Nevertheless, ALP and ALT\nare highly liver-specific parameters and underline\nhepatic damage to some extent, which might be due to\nischemic factors or increased oxidative stress induced\nby free radicals generated by the high metabolic\ndemands of muscles [195, 201]. Interestingly, remote\nischemic preconditioning (RIPC) of leg muscles using\ncycles of short-lasting leg ischemia seems to reduce\noxidative stress and decrease markers of liver and heart\ndamage after a marathon (see Table 9), highlighting the\nimpact of oxidative stress on liver function during the\nlong-distance events [201].\nAcute Liver Failure in Exercise\nThere are increasing numbers of reported cases of acute\nliver failure following a marathon [10, 202–204]. Most\nof these cases of acute liver failure were associated with\nEHS or extensive rhabdomyolysis, which in turn leads\nto a systemic inflammatory response with consecutive\nmultiple organ failure [10, 202]. These patients were criti-\ncally ill and often needed liver transplantation in ~ 30% of\ncases [202]. Some patients responded reasonably well to\nconservative treatment, but mortality in patients present-\ning with acute liver failure after a marathon must not be\nunderestimated as", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "and\nrunning on liver function is quite recent. Regular physical\nactivity was found to have a positive impact on NAFLD,\nlipid and glucose metabolism, decreased portal hyperten-\nsion in cirrhotic patients and reduced the risk of HCC.\nStill, marathon r"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0033_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e2a1f2369004fadf", "text_span_hash": "e2a1f2369004fadf", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 33, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "In particular, the effects of moderate exercise\nsuch as marathon training on liver transplant recipients\nand possible beneficial metabolic adaptations are not well\nunderstood. Furthermore, acute effects of exercise on\nHCC have been investigated. How", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nhepatocellular membrane permeability or altered met-\nabolic demands as they play key roles in glucose and\nlipid metabolism, such as fatty acid oxidation and glu-\nconeogenesis [196, 200]. Nevertheless, ALP and ALT\nare highly liver-specific parameters and underline\nhepatic damage to some extent, which might be due to\nischemic factors or increased oxidative stress induced\nby free radicals generated by the high metabolic\ndemands of muscles [195, 201]. Interestingly, remote\nischemic preconditioning (RIPC) of leg muscles using\ncycles of short-lasting leg ischemia seems to reduce\noxidative stress and decrease markers of liver and heart\ndamage after a marathon (see Table 9), highlighting the\nimpact of oxidative stress on liver function during the\nlong-distance events [201].\nAcute Liver Failure in Exercise\nThere are increasing numbers of reported cases of acute\nliver failure following a marathon [10, 202–204]. Most\nof these cases of acute liver failure were associated with\nEHS or extensive rhabdomyolysis, which in turn leads\nto a systemic inflammatory response with consecutive\nmultiple organ failure [10, 202]. These patients were criti-\ncally ill and often needed liver transplantation in ~ 30% of\ncases [202]. Some patients responded reasonably well to\nconservative treatment, but mortality in patients present-\ning with acute liver failure after a marathon must not be\nunderestimated as", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "In particular, the effects of moderate exercise\nsuch as marathon training on liver transplant recipients\nand possible beneficial metabolic adaptations are not well\nunderstood. Furthermore, acute effects of exercise on\nHCC have been investigated. How"}
