ilure [10, 202]. These patients were criti-\ncally ill and often needed liver transplantation in ~ 30% of\ncases [202]. Some patients responded reasonably well to\nconservative treatment, but mortality in patients present-\ning with acute liver failure after a marathon must not be\nunderestimated as", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "205, 207, 208]. Endurance exercise probably\ninfluences the activity levels of two cytokines involved in\nbone metabolism, namely osteoprotegerin as well as sol-\nuble receptor activator of NF-κB ligand (sRANKL) (see\nTable 5) [207]. Osteoclasts are very"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0033_c0022", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8c15c2d1ff18efd8", "text_span_hash": "8c15c2d1ff18efd8", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 33, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 22, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "found ultrasonographic calcaneus bone stiffness to be\nsignificantly higher in marathon runners compared to\nsedentary controls, especially in participants older than\n40 years (see Table 5) [208, 210]. This suggests a positive\neffect of marathon traini", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nhepatocellular membrane permeability or altered met-\nabolic demands as they play key roles in glucose and\nlipid metabolism, such as fatty acid oxidation and glu-\nconeogenesis [196, 200]. Nevertheless, ALP and ALT\nare highly liver-specific parameters and underline\nhepatic damage to some extent, which might be due to\nischemic factors or increased oxidative stress induced\nby free radicals generated by the high metabolic\ndemands of muscles [195, 201]. Interestingly, remote\nischemic preconditioning (RIPC) of leg muscles using\ncycles of short-lasting leg ischemia seems to reduce\noxidative stress and decrease markers of liver and heart\ndamage after a marathon (see Table 9), highlighting the\nimpact of oxidative stress on liver function during the\nlong-distance events [201].\nAcute Liver Failure in Exercise\nThere are increasing numbers of reported cases of acute\nliver failure following a marathon [10, 202–204]. Most\nof these cases of acute liver failure were associated with\nEHS or extensive rhabdomyolysis, which in turn leads\nto a systemic inflammatory response with consecutive\nmultiple organ failure [10, 202]. These patients were criti-\ncally ill and often needed liver transplantation in ~ 30% of\ncases [202]. Some patients responded reasonably well to\nconservative treatment, but mortality in patients present-\ning with acute liver failure after a marathon must not be\nunderestimated as", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "found ultrasonographic calcaneus bone stiffness to be\nsignificantly higher in marathon runners compared to\nsedentary controls, especially in participants older than\n40 years (see Table 5) [208, 210]. This suggests a positive\neffect of marathon traini"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0033_c0023", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "a634d43df562530d", "text_span_hash": "a634d43df562530d", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 33, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 23, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "tive\neffect of marathon training on bone balance and health\n[208, 210]. There is probably a certain site-specific com-\nponent in which higher stressed bones are more modified\n[209]. Dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry (DXA) scans\nof female runners showe", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nhepatocellular membrane permeability or altered met-\nabolic demands as they play key roles in glucose and\nlipid metabolism, such as fatty acid oxidation and glu-\nconeogenesis [196, 200]. Nevertheless, ALP and ALT\nare highly liver-specific parameters and underline\nhepatic damage to some extent, which might be due to\nischemic factors or increased oxidative stress induced\nby free radicals generated by the high metabolic\ndemands of muscles [195, 201]. Interestingly, remote\nischemic preconditioning (RIPC) of leg muscles using\ncycles of short-lasting leg ischemia seems to reduce\noxidative stress and decrease markers of liver and heart\ndamage after a marathon (see Table 9), highlighting the\nimpact of oxidative stress on liver function during the\nlong-distance events [201].\nAcute Liver Failure in Exercise\nThere are increasing numbers of reported cases of acute\nliver failure following a marathon [10, 202–204]. Most\nof these cases of acute liver failure were associated with\nEHS or extensive rhabdomyolysis, which in turn leads\nto a systemic inflammatory response with consecutive\nmultiple organ failure [10, 202]. These patients were criti-\ncally ill and often needed liver transplantation in ~ 30% of\ncases [202]. Some patients responded reasonably well to\nconservative treatment, but mortality in patients present-\ning with acute liver failure after a marathon must not be\nunderestimated as", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "tive\neffect of marathon training on bone balance and health\n[208, 210]. There is probably a certain site-specific com-\nponent in which higher stressed bones are more modified\n[209]. Dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry (DXA) scans\nof female runners showe"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0033_c0024", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "eb36c261394a9ccf", "text_span_hash": "eb36c261394a9ccf", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 33, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 24, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "scans\nof female runners showed higher bone mineral densi-\nties in legs but no changes in total body bone mineral\ndensity compared to sedentary controls [209]. Similarly,\nmale runners showed higher lumbar spine bone mineral\ndensities but did not disp", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nhepatocellular membrane permeability or altered met-\nabolic demands as they play key roles in glucose and\nlipid metabolism, such as fatty acid oxidation and glu-\nconeogenesis [196, 200]. Nevertheless, ALP and ALT\nare highly liver-specific parameters and underline\nhepatic damage to some extent, which might be due to\nischemic factors or increased oxidative stress induced\nby free radicals generated by the high metabolic\ndemands of muscles [195, 201]. Interestingly, remote\nischemic preconditioning (RIPC) of leg muscles using\ncycles of short-lasting leg ischemia seems to reduce\noxidative stress and decrease markers of liver and heart\ndamage after a marathon (see Table 9), highlighting the\nimpact of oxidative stress on liver function during the\nlong-distance events [201].\nAcute Liver Failure in Exercise\nThere are increasing numbers of reported cases of acute\nliver failure following a marathon [10, 202–204]. Most\nof these cases of acute liver failure were associated with\nEHS or extensive rhabdomyolysis, which in turn leads\nto a systemic inflammatory response with consecutive\nmultiple organ failure [10, 202]. These patients were criti-\ncally ill and often needed liver transplantation in ~ 30% of\ncases [202]. Some patients responded reasonably well to\nconservative treatment, but mortality in patients present-\ning with acute liver failure after a marathon must not be\nunderestimated as", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "scans\nof female runners showed higher bone mineral densi-\nties in legs but no changes in total body bone mineral\ndensity compared to sedentary controls [209]. Similarly,\nmale runners showed higher lumbar spine bone mineral\ndensities but did not disp"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0034_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4e279fc8a9720220", "text_span_hash": "4e279fc8a9720220", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 34, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "of\nsubchondral bone marrow edema in the tibia condyles\nand femur (see Table 5) when comparing post-race to\npre-race images [211]. Generally, the condition for this is,\nof course, that the energy needs of the runners are also\nmet and that attention i", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\npositive effects on bone health [207, 208, 210]. A recent\nMRI study investigating the effects of marathon running\non the knee joint was able to demonstrate a reduction of\nsubchondral bone marrow edema in the tibia condyles\nand femur (see Table 5) when comparing post-race to\npre-race images [211]. Generally, the condition for this is,\nof course, that the energy needs of the runners are also\nmet and that attention is paid to a sufficient supply of cal-\nBenefits on Skeletal Muscle Metabolism and Tendon Strength\nThe skeletal muscle and tendon apparatus is greatly\nstressed during marathons and undergoes various posi-\ntive adaptive changes during training [36, 214–216]. In\nparticular, the metabolic function of skeletal muscle is\nbeneficially influenced by marathon training [214]. A\nstudy investigating first-time marathoners found a 48%\nincrease in muscle V̇O2 after marathon training (see\nTable 5), suggesting impressive metabolic adaptations in\nmuscles even without changes in V̇O2max values [214].\nMarathon training has been proven to lead to an adap-\ntation of muscle fiber types and metabolic enzymes\n[215, 217]. Early studies performed muscle biopsies in\nmarathon runners and found an increased mitochondria\ncount, more myoglobin, and a higher capillary density\nwhich in turn leads to an improved oxygen extraction\nfrom the blood and a more efficient energy metabolism\nsuch as an improv", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "of\nsubchondral bone marrow edema in the tibia condyles\nand femur (see Table 5) when comparing post-race to\npre-race images [211]. Generally, the condition for this is,\nof course, that the energy needs of the runners are also\nmet and that attention i"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0034_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "52efa2b5be06e406", "text_span_hash": "52efa2b5be06e406", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 34, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "arious posi-\ntive adaptive changes during training [36, 214–216]. In\nparticular, the metabolic function of skeletal muscle is\nbeneficially influenced by marathon training [214]. A\nstudy investigating first-time marathoners found a 48%\nincrease in mus", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\npositive effects on bone health [207, 208, 210]. A recent\nMRI study investigating the effects of marathon running\non the knee joint was able to demonstrate a reduction of\nsubchondral bone marrow edema in the tibia condyles\nand femur (see Table 5) when comparing post-race to\npre-race images [211]. Generally, the condition for this is,\nof course, that the energy needs of the runners are also\nmet and that attention is paid to a sufficient supply of cal-\nBenefits on Skeletal Muscle Metabolism and Tendon Strength\nThe skeletal muscle and tendon apparatus is greatly\nstressed during marathons and undergoes various posi-\ntive adaptive changes during training [36, 214–216]. In\nparticular, the metabolic function of skeletal muscle is\nbeneficially influenced by marathon training [214]. A\nstudy investigating first-time marathoners found a 48%\nincrease in muscle V̇O2 after marathon training (see\nTable 5), suggesting impressive metabolic adaptations in\nmuscles even without changes in V̇O2max values [214].\nMarathon training has been proven to lead to an adap-\ntation of muscle fiber types and metabolic enzymes\n[215, 217]. Early studies performed muscle biopsies in\nmarathon runners and found an increased mitochondria\ncount, more myoglobin, and a higher capillary density\nwhich in turn leads to an improved oxygen extraction\nfrom the blood and a more efficient energy metabolism\nsuch as an improv", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "arious posi-\ntive adaptive changes during training [36, 214–216]. In\nparticular, the metabolic function of skeletal muscle is\nbeneficially influenced by marathon training [214]. A\nstudy investigating first-time marathoners found a 48%\nincrease in mus"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0034_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b4d210d8648f440f", "text_span_hash": "b4d210d8648f440f", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 34, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "rs found a 48%\nincrease in muscle V̇O2 after marathon training (see\nTable 5), suggesting impressive metabolic adaptations in\nmuscles even without changes in V̇O2max values [214].\nMarathon training has been proven to lead to an adap-\ntation of muscle", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\npositive effects on bone health [207, 208, 210]. A recent\nMRI study investigating the effects of marathon running\non the knee joint was able to demonstrate a reduction of\nsubchondral bone marrow edema in the tibia condyles\nand femur (see Table 5) when comparing post-race to\npre-race images [211]. Generally, the condition for this is,\nof course, that the energy needs of the runners are also\nmet and that attention is paid to a sufficient supply of cal-\nBenefits on Skeletal Muscle Metabolism and Tendon Strength\nThe skeletal muscle and tendon apparatus is greatly\nstressed during marathons and undergoes various posi-\ntive adaptive changes during training [36, 214–216]. In\nparticular, the metabolic function of skeletal muscle is\nbeneficially influenced by marathon training [214]. A\nstudy investigating first-time marathoners found a 48%\nincrease in muscle V̇O2 after marathon training (see\nTable 5), suggesting impressive metabolic adaptations in\nmuscles even without changes in V̇O2max values [214].\nMarathon training has been proven to lead to an adap-\ntation of muscle fiber types and metabolic enzymes\n[215, 217]. Early studies performed muscle biopsies in\nmarathon runners and found an increased mitochondria\ncount, more myoglobin, and a higher capillary density\nwhich in turn leads to an improved oxygen extraction\nfrom the blood and a more efficient energy metabolism\nsuch as an improv", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "rs found a 48%\nincrease in muscle V̇O2 after marathon training (see\nTable 5), suggesting impressive metabolic adaptations in\nmuscles even without changes in V̇O2max values [214].\nMarathon training has been proven to lead to an adap-\ntation of muscle"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0034_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "33eef5a2ee742841", "text_span_hash": "33eef5a2ee742841", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 34, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "to an adap-\ntation of muscle fiber types and metabolic enzymes\n[215, 217]. Early studies performed muscle biopsies in\nmarathon runners and found an increased mitochondria\ncount, more myoglobin, and a higher capillary density\nwhich in turn leads to a", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\npositive effects on bone health [207, 208, 210]. A recent\nMRI study investigating the effects of marathon running\non the knee joint was able to demonstrate a reduction of\nsubchondral bone marrow edema in the tibia condyles\nand femur (see Table 5) when comparing post-race to\npre-race images [211]. Generally, the condition for this is,\nof course, that the energy needs of the runners are also\nmet and that attention is paid to a sufficient supply of cal-\nBenefits on Skeletal Muscle Metabolism and Tendon Strength\nThe skeletal muscle and tendon apparatus is greatly\nstressed during marathons and undergoes various posi-\ntive adaptive changes during training [36, 214–216]. In\nparticular, the metabolic function of skeletal muscle is\nbeneficially influenced by marathon training [214]. A\nstudy investigating first-time marathoners found a 48%\nincrease in muscle V̇O2 after marathon training (see\nTable 5), suggesting impressive metabolic adaptations in\nmuscles even without changes in V̇O2max values [214].\nMarathon training has been proven to lead to an adap-\ntation of muscle fiber types and metabolic enzymes\n[215, 217]. Early studies performed muscle biopsies in\nmarathon runners and found an increased mitochondria\ncount, more myoglobin, and a higher capillary density\nwhich in turn leads to an improved oxygen extraction\nfrom the blood and a more efficient energy metabolism\nsuch as an improv", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "to an adap-\ntation of muscle fiber types and metabolic enzymes\n[215, 217]. Early studies performed muscle biopsies in\nmarathon runners and found an increased mitochondria\ncount, more myoglobin, and a higher capillary density\nwhich in turn leads to a"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0034_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3e6d7235c467dc6b", "text_span_hash": "3e6d7235c467dc6b", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 34, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "os-\nphorylation pathway [215].\nIn addition, the muscles of endurance runners showed\na higher count of Type I (slow-twitch) fibers compared\nto Type II (fast-twitch) fibers, which apparently dem-\nonstrate a more efficient utilization of aerobic pathway", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\npositive effects on bone health [207, 208, 210]. A recent\nMRI study investigating the effects of marathon running\non the knee joint was able to demonstrate a reduction of\nsubchondral bone marrow edema in the tibia condyles\nand femur (see Table 5) when comparing post-race to\npre-race images [211]. Generally, the condition for this is,\nof course, that the energy needs of the runners are also\nmet and that attention is paid to a sufficient supply of cal-\nBenefits on Skeletal Muscle Metabolism and Tendon Strength\nThe skeletal muscle and tendon apparatus is greatly\nstressed during marathons and undergoes various posi-\ntive adaptive changes during training [36, 214–216]. In\nparticular, the metabolic function of skeletal muscle is\nbeneficially influenced by marathon training [214]. A\nstudy investigating first-time marathoners found a 48%\nincrease in muscle V̇O2 after marathon training (see\nTable 5), suggesting impressive metabolic adaptations in\nmuscles even without changes in V̇O2max values [214].\nMarathon training has been proven to lead to an adap-\ntation of muscle fiber types and metabolic enzymes\n[215, 217]. Early studies performed muscle biopsies in\nmarathon runners and found an increased mitochondria\ncount, more myoglobin, and a higher capillary density\nwhich in turn leads to an improved oxygen extraction\nfrom the blood and a more efficient energy metabolism\nsuch as an improv", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "os-\nphorylation pathway [215].\nIn addition, the muscles of endurance runners showed\na higher count of Type I (slow-twitch) fibers compared\nto Type II (fast-twitch) fibers, which apparently dem-\nonstrate a more efficient utilization of aerobic pathway"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0034_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b95d7ce1bf0d16b5", "text_span_hash": "b95d7ce1bf0d16b5", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 34, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "utilization of aerobic pathways\nand seem to give a performance advantage as endurance\nrunners with a higher Type I count had more success in\nendurance events [215]. Besides changes in muscle fiber\ntypes, endurance training seems to alter muscle archi", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\npositive effects on bone health [207, 208, 210]. A recent\nMRI study investigating the effects of marathon running\non the knee joint was able to demonstrate a reduction of\nsubchondral bone marrow edema in the tibia condyles\nand femur (see Table 5) when comparing post-race to\npre-race images [211]. Generally, the condition for this is,\nof course, that the energy needs of the runners are also\nmet and that attention is paid to a sufficient supply of cal-\nBenefits on Skeletal Muscle Metabolism and Tendon Strength\nThe skeletal muscle and tendon apparatus is greatly\nstressed during marathons and undergoes various posi-\ntive adaptive changes during training [36, 214–216]. In\nparticular, the metabolic function of skeletal muscle is\nbeneficially influenced by marathon training [214]. A\nstudy investigating first-time marathoners found a 48%\nincrease in muscle V̇O2 after marathon training (see\nTable 5), suggesting impressive metabolic adaptations in\nmuscles even without changes in V̇O2max values [214].\nMarathon training has been proven to lead to an adap-\ntation of muscle fiber types and metabolic enzymes\n[215, 217]. Early studies performed muscle biopsies in\nmarathon runners and found an increased mitochondria\ncount, more myoglobin, and a higher capillary density\nwhich in turn leads to an improved oxygen extraction\nfrom the blood and a more efficient energy metabolism\nsuch as an improv", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "utilization of aerobic pathways\nand seem to give a performance advantage as endurance\nrunners with a higher Type I count had more success in\nendurance events [215]. Besides changes in muscle fiber\ntypes, endurance training seems to alter muscle archi"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0034_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "eaf641d5f65d1b6a", "text_span_hash": "eaf641d5f65d1b6a", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 34, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "These structural changes most likely provide a bet-\nter transmission of muscular force to the ground, provid-\ning benefits during marathon running [216].\nImportance of Exercise on Joint Health and Osteoarthritis\nThe influence of marathon training an", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\npositive effects on bone health [207, 208, 210]. A recent\nMRI study investigating the effects of marathon running\non the knee joint was able to demonstrate a reduction of\nsubchondral bone marrow edema in the tibia condyles\nand femur (see Table 5) when comparing post-race to\npre-race images [211]. Generally, the condition for this is,\nof course, that the energy needs of the runners are also\nmet and that attention is paid to a sufficient supply of cal-\nBenefits on Skeletal Muscle Metabolism and Tendon Strength\nThe skeletal muscle and tendon apparatus is greatly\nstressed during marathons and undergoes various posi-\ntive adaptive changes during training [36, 214–216]. In\nparticular, the metabolic function of skeletal muscle is\nbeneficially influenced by marathon training [214]. A\nstudy investigating first-time marathoners found a 48%\nincrease in muscle V̇O2 after marathon training (see\nTable 5), suggesting impressive metabolic adaptations in\nmuscles even without changes in V̇O2max values [214].\nMarathon training has been proven to lead to an adap-\ntation of muscle fiber types and metabolic enzymes\n[215, 217]. Early studies performed muscle biopsies in\nmarathon runners and found an increased mitochondria\ncount, more myoglobin, and a higher capillary density\nwhich in turn leads to an improved oxygen extraction\nfrom the blood and a more efficient energy metabolism\nsuch as an improv", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "These structural changes most likely provide a bet-\nter transmission of muscular force to the ground, provid-\ning benefits during marathon running [216].\nImportance of Exercise on Joint Health and Osteoarthritis\nThe influence of marathon training an"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0034_c0016", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e8aacaa9e117ec0c", "text_span_hash": "e8aacaa9e117ec0c", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 34, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 16, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "d transiently decreases in thickness [224]. However, it\nis still not entirely clear how endurance exercise influ-\nences joint health on a long-term basis. Several studies\nhave been conducted investigating the effect of mara-\nthon training and running", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\npositive effects on bone health [207, 208, 210]. A recent\nMRI study investigating the effects of marathon running\non the knee joint was able to demonstrate a reduction of\nsubchondral bone marrow edema in the tibia condyles\nand femur (see Table 5) when comparing post-race to\npre-race images [211]. Generally, the condition for this is,\nof course, that the energy needs of the runners are also\nmet and that attention is paid to a sufficient supply of cal-\nBenefits on Skeletal Muscle Metabolism and Tendon Strength\nThe skeletal muscle and tendon apparatus is greatly\nstressed during marathons and undergoes various posi-\ntive adaptive changes during training [36, 214–216]. In\nparticular, the metabolic function of skeletal muscle is\nbeneficially influenced by marathon training [214]. A\nstudy investigating first-time marathoners found a 48%\nincrease in muscle V̇O2 after marathon training (see\nTable 5), suggesting impressive metabolic adaptations in\nmuscles even without changes in V̇O2max values [214].\nMarathon training has been proven to lead to an adap-\ntation of muscle fiber types and metabolic enzymes\n[215, 217]. Early studies performed muscle biopsies in\nmarathon runners and found an increased mitochondria\ncount, more myoglobin, and a higher capillary density\nwhich in turn leads to an improved oxygen extraction\nfrom the blood and a more efficient energy metabolism\nsuch as an improv", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "d transiently decreases in thickness [224]. However, it\nis still not entirely clear how endurance exercise influ-\nences joint health on a long-term basis. Several studies\nhave been conducted investigating the effect of mara-\nthon training and running"}
