es have been conducted, inves-\ntigating to what extent marathon training and running\ninfluence different joints in the human body during mar-\nathon training as well as during the race. A recent study\ninvestigating hip joints of asymptomatic first-time mara-\nthon runners during a 4-month train", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ining program (see Table 5) [233]. The MRI\nexamination after the marathon race showed in only 4.8%\nof marathon finishers new changes (both with small areas\nof bone marrow edema in the femoral heads) without any\nclinical complaints or corresponding sy"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0036_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5ef66554c7261320", "text_span_hash": "5ef66554c7261320", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 36, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "by other studies comparing pre- and post-race\nhip MRIs without any new detectable damage to the hip\njoints [234]. All in all, it seems that high-impact forces\nduring marathon training and running are well tolerated,\nlong-endurance running is safe for", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\ndays of the week compared to those running < 4 days\nper week had a higher incidence of running-related\ninjuries on the competition day [227]. Regarding sex, a\nCanadian study reported a BMI of > 26 kg/m2 in males\nand age younger than 31 years in females as protective\nfor running injuries; conversely, running once a week\nand age older than 50 years were risk injury factors in\nfemales [231]. Participating in marathons was associ-\nated with a greater risk of injury in women than men in\na systematic review on risk factors and sex differences;\nhowever, not enough studies investigated differences\nbetween male and female runners to obtain definite\nCartilage Health in Marathon Runners\nOver the past few years, the question has arisen whether\nendurance running—especially long-distance endurance\nactivity such as marathon running—leads to acute and/\nor long-term changes or even damage in cartilage of the\nlower extremities [233]. Particularly feared was the sus-\npicion of whether long-distance running could contrib-\nute to the premature onset of osteoarthritis [233]. In this\ncontext, several MRI studies have been conducted, inves-\ntigating to what extent marathon training and running\ninfluence different joints in the human body during mar-\nathon training as well as during the race. A recent study\ninvestigating hip joints of asymptomatic first-time mara-\nthon runners during a 4-month train", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "by other studies comparing pre- and post-race\nhip MRIs without any new detectable damage to the hip\njoints [234]. All in all, it seems that high-impact forces\nduring marathon training and running are well tolerated,\nlong-endurance running is safe for"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0036_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0d8d42d033f62829", "text_span_hash": "0d8d42d033f62829", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 36, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "during a 6-month intensive training program found\nsignificantly reduced cartilage volume and thickness\nat the lateral femur (see Table 5) [235]. Non-significant\nchanges were found at the medial and lateral tibia, medial\nfemur as well as patella [235]", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\ndays of the week compared to those running < 4 days\nper week had a higher incidence of running-related\ninjuries on the competition day [227]. Regarding sex, a\nCanadian study reported a BMI of > 26 kg/m2 in males\nand age younger than 31 years in females as protective\nfor running injuries; conversely, running once a week\nand age older than 50 years were risk injury factors in\nfemales [231]. Participating in marathons was associ-\nated with a greater risk of injury in women than men in\na systematic review on risk factors and sex differences;\nhowever, not enough studies investigated differences\nbetween male and female runners to obtain definite\nCartilage Health in Marathon Runners\nOver the past few years, the question has arisen whether\nendurance running—especially long-distance endurance\nactivity such as marathon running—leads to acute and/\nor long-term changes or even damage in cartilage of the\nlower extremities [233]. Particularly feared was the sus-\npicion of whether long-distance running could contrib-\nute to the premature onset of osteoarthritis [233]. In this\ncontext, several MRI studies have been conducted, inves-\ntigating to what extent marathon training and running\ninfluence different joints in the human body during mar-\nathon training as well as during the race. A recent study\ninvestigating hip joints of asymptomatic first-time mara-\nthon runners during a 4-month train", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "during a 6-month intensive training program found\nsignificantly reduced cartilage volume and thickness\nat the lateral femur (see Table 5) [235]. Non-significant\nchanges were found at the medial and lateral tibia, medial\nfemur as well as patella [235]"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0036_c0020", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7d26db7593567cb5", "text_span_hash": "7d26db7593567cb5", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 36, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 20, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "cartilage and menisci [224, 234, 235, 237].\nIn a similar fashion, there have been concerns about\nthe possible negative effects of marathon training and\nrunning on the lower lumbar spine and intervertebral\ndiscs [238]. Few studies investigating this", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\ndays of the week compared to those running < 4 days\nper week had a higher incidence of running-related\ninjuries on the competition day [227]. Regarding sex, a\nCanadian study reported a BMI of > 26 kg/m2 in males\nand age younger than 31 years in females as protective\nfor running injuries; conversely, running once a week\nand age older than 50 years were risk injury factors in\nfemales [231]. Participating in marathons was associ-\nated with a greater risk of injury in women than men in\na systematic review on risk factors and sex differences;\nhowever, not enough studies investigated differences\nbetween male and female runners to obtain definite\nCartilage Health in Marathon Runners\nOver the past few years, the question has arisen whether\nendurance running—especially long-distance endurance\nactivity such as marathon running—leads to acute and/\nor long-term changes or even damage in cartilage of the\nlower extremities [233]. Particularly feared was the sus-\npicion of whether long-distance running could contrib-\nute to the premature onset of osteoarthritis [233]. In this\ncontext, several MRI studies have been conducted, inves-\ntigating to what extent marathon training and running\ninfluence different joints in the human body during mar-\nathon training as well as during the race. A recent study\ninvestigating hip joints of asymptomatic first-time mara-\nthon runners during a 4-month train", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "cartilage and menisci [224, 234, 235, 237].\nIn a similar fashion, there have been concerns about\nthe possible negative effects of marathon training and\nrunning on the lower lumbar spine and intervertebral\ndiscs [238]. Few studies investigating this"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0036_c0021", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "be496921e38ce04c", "text_span_hash": "be496921e38ce04c", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 36, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 21, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ew studies investigating this topic have been\nconducted to date. A recent MRI study investigated first-\ntime marathon runners before and during their train-\ning period as well as after a marathon race (see Table 5)\n[238]. Before training, 61% of part", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\ndays of the week compared to those running < 4 days\nper week had a higher incidence of running-related\ninjuries on the competition day [227]. Regarding sex, a\nCanadian study reported a BMI of > 26 kg/m2 in males\nand age younger than 31 years in females as protective\nfor running injuries; conversely, running once a week\nand age older than 50 years were risk injury factors in\nfemales [231]. Participating in marathons was associ-\nated with a greater risk of injury in women than men in\na systematic review on risk factors and sex differences;\nhowever, not enough studies investigated differences\nbetween male and female runners to obtain definite\nCartilage Health in Marathon Runners\nOver the past few years, the question has arisen whether\nendurance running—especially long-distance endurance\nactivity such as marathon running—leads to acute and/\nor long-term changes or even damage in cartilage of the\nlower extremities [233]. Particularly feared was the sus-\npicion of whether long-distance running could contrib-\nute to the premature onset of osteoarthritis [233]. In this\ncontext, several MRI studies have been conducted, inves-\ntigating to what extent marathon training and running\ninfluence different joints in the human body during mar-\nathon training as well as during the race. A recent study\ninvestigating hip joints of asymptomatic first-time mara-\nthon runners during a 4-month train", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ew studies investigating this topic have been\nconducted to date. A recent MRI study investigated first-\ntime marathon runners before and during their train-\ning period as well as after a marathon race (see Table 5)\n[238]. Before training, 61% of part"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0036_c0023", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "25eb69f8b0023436", "text_span_hash": "25eb69f8b0023436", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 36, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 23, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ifference in lumbar spine appearance or differences in\nintervertebral disc height, width or intervertebral dis-\ntance could be observed using a lumbar MRI examina-\ntion [238]. These results suggest that marathon training\nand running are safe for firs", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\ndays of the week compared to those running < 4 days\nper week had a higher incidence of running-related\ninjuries on the competition day [227]. Regarding sex, a\nCanadian study reported a BMI of > 26 kg/m2 in males\nand age younger than 31 years in females as protective\nfor running injuries; conversely, running once a week\nand age older than 50 years were risk injury factors in\nfemales [231]. Participating in marathons was associ-\nated with a greater risk of injury in women than men in\na systematic review on risk factors and sex differences;\nhowever, not enough studies investigated differences\nbetween male and female runners to obtain definite\nCartilage Health in Marathon Runners\nOver the past few years, the question has arisen whether\nendurance running—especially long-distance endurance\nactivity such as marathon running—leads to acute and/\nor long-term changes or even damage in cartilage of the\nlower extremities [233]. Particularly feared was the sus-\npicion of whether long-distance running could contrib-\nute to the premature onset of osteoarthritis [233]. In this\ncontext, several MRI studies have been conducted, inves-\ntigating to what extent marathon training and running\ninfluence different joints in the human body during mar-\nathon training as well as during the race. A recent study\ninvestigating hip joints of asymptomatic first-time mara-\nthon runners during a 4-month train", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ifference in lumbar spine appearance or differences in\nintervertebral disc height, width or intervertebral dis-\ntance could be observed using a lumbar MRI examina-\ntion [238]. These results suggest that marathon training\nand running are safe for firs"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0037_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "43fc13ef3e67a403", "text_span_hash": "43fc13ef3e67a403", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 37, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "beneficial adaptive mechanism to increase mechanical\ndemands [239]. Furthermore, the volume of the retroc-\nalcaneal bursa increased significantly, which is in line\nwith the current literature and can be interpreted as an\nacute adaptive mechanism [23", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\n[239]. An MRI study investigating healthy first-time\nmarathon runners during their training and the mara-\nthon race found a positive correlation between Achil-\nles tendon thickness and body weight as well as height\n[239]. After the training and the race, a slight reduction\nin signal intensity at the insertion point of the Achil-\nles tendon could be detected, which was interpreted as\na beneficial adaptive mechanism to increase mechanical\ndemands [239]. Furthermore, the volume of the retroc-\nalcaneal bursa increased significantly, which is in line\nwith the current literature and can be interpreted as an\nacute adaptive mechanism [239]. A similar MRI study\ninvestigating the effects of marathon running on the\nAchilles tendon found signal reductions in certain parts\nof the tendon, which might be suggestive of swelling or\nedema [240]. However, most of these changes returned to\npre-race levels in a control MRI examination four weeks\npost-race [240]. Interestingly, athletes with foot pain or\nproblems had significantly more lesions in the Achil-\nles tendon compared to asymptomatic runners [239].\nOver the course of the marathon training and the follow-\ning race, adaptive changes in the Achilles tendon were\nobservable, but these did not lead to any new Achilles\ntendon lesions [239, 240].\nSimilar studies investigating tendon changes induced\nby long-distance running have been conducted us", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "beneficial adaptive mechanism to increase mechanical\ndemands [239]. Furthermore, the volume of the retroc-\nalcaneal bursa increased significantly, which is in line\nwith the current literature and can be interpreted as an\nacute adaptive mechanism [23"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0037_c0017", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f7645309e29262fc", "text_span_hash": "f7645309e29262fc", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 37, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 17, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "sis, respectively (see Table 5) [16,\n45, 46, 86, 243–251]. CK values increased rapidly after\nthe marathon and were already increased three-fold\nimmediately after the race compared to pre-race values\nFig. 6  Mean CK and myoglobin changes in studies in", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\n[239]. An MRI study investigating healthy first-time\nmarathon runners during their training and the mara-\nthon race found a positive correlation between Achil-\nles tendon thickness and body weight as well as height\n[239]. After the training and the race, a slight reduction\nin signal intensity at the insertion point of the Achil-\nles tendon could be detected, which was interpreted as\na beneficial adaptive mechanism to increase mechanical\ndemands [239]. Furthermore, the volume of the retroc-\nalcaneal bursa increased significantly, which is in line\nwith the current literature and can be interpreted as an\nacute adaptive mechanism [239]. A similar MRI study\ninvestigating the effects of marathon running on the\nAchilles tendon found signal reductions in certain parts\nof the tendon, which might be suggestive of swelling or\nedema [240]. However, most of these changes returned to\npre-race levels in a control MRI examination four weeks\npost-race [240]. Interestingly, athletes with foot pain or\nproblems had significantly more lesions in the Achil-\nles tendon compared to asymptomatic runners [239].\nOver the course of the marathon training and the follow-\ning race, adaptive changes in the Achilles tendon were\nobservable, but these did not lead to any new Achilles\ntendon lesions [239, 240].\nSimilar studies investigating tendon changes induced\nby long-distance running have been conducted us", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "sis, respectively (see Table 5) [16,\n45, 46, 86, 243–251]. CK values increased rapidly after\nthe marathon and were already increased three-fold\nimmediately after the race compared to pre-race values\nFig. 6  Mean CK and myoglobin changes in studies in"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0038_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "d2188e26615beaec", "text_span_hash": "d2188e26615beaec", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 38, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "le up to\ntwo weeks post-race and then start to normalize again\nSimilarly, elevated levels of CK-MB are also observed\nafter marathon running (see Fig. 1), similar to the bio-\nchemical alterations during a myocardial infarction [45,\n243, 244, 246, 253]", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nand reached peak values of five to tenfold compared to\nbaseline values in the first 24 h post-race (see Fig. 6) [250,\n252]. Elevated CK values are often still detectable up to\ntwo weeks post-race and then start to normalize again\nSimilarly, elevated levels of CK-MB are also observed\nafter marathon running (see Fig. 1), similar to the bio-\nchemical alterations during a myocardial infarction [45,\n243, 244, 246, 253]. CK-MB is predominantly expressed\nin cardiac muscle but is also present in smaller amounts\nin skeletal muscle [253]. However, it can be inferred that\nthe elevation of CK-MB may originate from peripheral\nsources, as muscle biopsies conducted on marathon run-\nners have demonstrated increased CK-MB levels in skel-\netal muscle, while myocardial scintigraphy has revealed\nno cardiac abnormalities [241, 242, 247].\nAdditionally, increased levels of myoglobin and lac-\ntate dehydrogenase (LDH) have been observed after the\ncompletion of a marathon (see Table 5, Fig. 5, and Fig. 6),\nwhich in combination with the other described biochem-\nical changes, indicates death of muscle cells [16, 59, 86,\n244, 246, 254]. High levels of myoglobin in the circula-\ntion may become problematic as it can obstruct kidney\ntubules and lead to pigment nephropathy with concomi-\ntant AKI. CK and CK-MB elevations are dependent\non different factors. Higher increases were observed\nin slower runners, su", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "le up to\ntwo weeks post-race and then start to normalize again\nSimilarly, elevated levels of CK-MB are also observed\nafter marathon running (see Fig. 1), similar to the bio-\nchemical alterations during a myocardial infarction [45,\n243, 244, 246, 253]"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0038_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "6ee67f1f38742454", "text_span_hash": "6ee67f1f38742454", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 38, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "oncomi-\ntant AKI. CK and CK-MB elevations are dependent\non different factors. Higher increases were observed\nin slower runners, suggestive of lower fitness levels\n[248], runners suffering from muscle cramps during the\nrace [255], as well as different", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nand reached peak values of five to tenfold compared to\nbaseline values in the first 24 h post-race (see Fig. 6) [250,\n252]. Elevated CK values are often still detectable up to\ntwo weeks post-race and then start to normalize again\nSimilarly, elevated levels of CK-MB are also observed\nafter marathon running (see Fig. 1), similar to the bio-\nchemical alterations during a myocardial infarction [45,\n243, 244, 246, 253]. CK-MB is predominantly expressed\nin cardiac muscle but is also present in smaller amounts\nin skeletal muscle [253]. However, it can be inferred that\nthe elevation of CK-MB may originate from peripheral\nsources, as muscle biopsies conducted on marathon run-\nners have demonstrated increased CK-MB levels in skel-\netal muscle, while myocardial scintigraphy has revealed\nno cardiac abnormalities [241, 242, 247].\nAdditionally, increased levels of myoglobin and lac-\ntate dehydrogenase (LDH) have been observed after the\ncompletion of a marathon (see Table 5, Fig. 5, and Fig. 6),\nwhich in combination with the other described biochem-\nical changes, indicates death of muscle cells [16, 59, 86,\n244, 246, 254]. High levels of myoglobin in the circula-\ntion may become problematic as it can obstruct kidney\ntubules and lead to pigment nephropathy with concomi-\ntant AKI. CK and CK-MB elevations are dependent\non different factors. Higher increases were observed\nin slower runners, su", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "oncomi-\ntant AKI. CK and CK-MB elevations are dependent\non different factors. Higher increases were observed\nin slower runners, suggestive of lower fitness levels\n[248], runners suffering from muscle cramps during the\nrace [255], as well as different"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0038_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f944564baaf6a4c8", "text_span_hash": "f944564baaf6a4c8", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 38, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ith reduced antioxidative capacities is the most\nprobable cause for muscle fiber necrosis [256]. A recent\nMRI study was able to detect microstructural lesions\nin skeletal muscle 24-h after a marathon race accompa-\nnied by concomitant CK elevation [25", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nand reached peak values of five to tenfold compared to\nbaseline values in the first 24 h post-race (see Fig. 6) [250,\n252]. Elevated CK values are often still detectable up to\ntwo weeks post-race and then start to normalize again\nSimilarly, elevated levels of CK-MB are also observed\nafter marathon running (see Fig. 1), similar to the bio-\nchemical alterations during a myocardial infarction [45,\n243, 244, 246, 253]. CK-MB is predominantly expressed\nin cardiac muscle but is also present in smaller amounts\nin skeletal muscle [253]. However, it can be inferred that\nthe elevation of CK-MB may originate from peripheral\nsources, as muscle biopsies conducted on marathon run-\nners have demonstrated increased CK-MB levels in skel-\netal muscle, while myocardial scintigraphy has revealed\nno cardiac abnormalities [241, 242, 247].\nAdditionally, increased levels of myoglobin and lac-\ntate dehydrogenase (LDH) have been observed after the\ncompletion of a marathon (see Table 5, Fig. 5, and Fig. 6),\nwhich in combination with the other described biochem-\nical changes, indicates death of muscle cells [16, 59, 86,\n244, 246, 254]. High levels of myoglobin in the circula-\ntion may become problematic as it can obstruct kidney\ntubules and lead to pigment nephropathy with concomi-\ntant AKI. CK and CK-MB elevations are dependent\non different factors. Higher increases were observed\nin slower runners, su", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ith reduced antioxidative capacities is the most\nprobable cause for muscle fiber necrosis [256]. A recent\nMRI study was able to detect microstructural lesions\nin skeletal muscle 24-h after a marathon race accompa-\nnied by concomitant CK elevation [25"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0038_c0017", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "db393cdaea35eca1", "text_span_hash": "db393cdaea35eca1", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 38, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 17, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "istance endurance events,\nwith estimated incidences of 18% during a marathon and\nlifetime reaching 30–50% [255, 257]. There are several\ndifferent hypotheses as to why muscle cramping occurs\nduring long-distance endurance events such as elec-\ntrolyte", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nand reached peak values of five to tenfold compared to\nbaseline values in the first 24 h post-race (see Fig. 6) [250,\n252]. Elevated CK values are often still detectable up to\ntwo weeks post-race and then start to normalize again\nSimilarly, elevated levels of CK-MB are also observed\nafter marathon running (see Fig. 1), similar to the bio-\nchemical alterations during a myocardial infarction [45,\n243, 244, 246, 253]. CK-MB is predominantly expressed\nin cardiac muscle but is also present in smaller amounts\nin skeletal muscle [253]. However, it can be inferred that\nthe elevation of CK-MB may originate from peripheral\nsources, as muscle biopsies conducted on marathon run-\nners have demonstrated increased CK-MB levels in skel-\netal muscle, while myocardial scintigraphy has revealed\nno cardiac abnormalities [241, 242, 247].\nAdditionally, increased levels of myoglobin and lac-\ntate dehydrogenase (LDH) have been observed after the\ncompletion of a marathon (see Table 5, Fig. 5, and Fig. 6),\nwhich in combination with the other described biochem-\nical changes, indicates death of muscle cells [16, 59, 86,\n244, 246, 254]. High levels of myoglobin in the circula-\ntion may become problematic as it can obstruct kidney\ntubules and lead to pigment nephropathy with concomi-\ntant AKI. CK and CK-MB elevations are dependent\non different factors. Higher increases were observed\nin slower runners, su", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "istance endurance events,\nwith estimated incidences of 18% during a marathon and\nlifetime reaching 30–50% [255, 257]. There are several\ndifferent hypotheses as to why muscle cramping occurs\nduring long-distance endurance events such as elec-\ntrolyte"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0039_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c46fe56c87c7ba17", "text_span_hash": "c46fe56c87c7ba17", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 39, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nIn addition to pathological changes, rapid muscle recov-\nery is essential for athletes [252, 260]. A recent study\nfound that the inflammatory response that occurs after\na marathon is important for", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nIn addition to pathological changes, rapid muscle recov-\nery is essential for athletes [252, 260]. A recent study\nfound that the inflammatory response that occurs after\na marathon is important for muscle recovery [252]. Par-\nticularly, some cytokines, especially IL-6, are important\nfor the activation of satellite cells i.e., precursor cells of\nmyocytes, inducing angiogenesis and myogenesis [252].\nExercise-induced muscle injury leads to a release of IL-6\nand IL-6 associated with CK in marathon runners [261].\nAdditionally, studies found that a return to moderate\nrunning 48 h post-race could speed up neuromuscu-\nlar recovery and does not lead to further muscle dam-\nage [260]. In fact, runners returning to running after 48\nh post-race have significantly improved squat jump tests\ncompared to resting runners [260]. A return to moderate\nphysical activity 48 h after a marathon race may, there-\nfore, be recommended to speed and enhance muscular\nThe musculoskeletal system is immensely stressed during\na marathon race and training. Therefore, it has enjoyed\ngreat research interest over the past years. Some relation-\nships are not yet clear or conclusive, especially the effects\nof marathons on bone mineral density and osteoporosis\nrisk, osteoarthritis risk, and long-term joint damage. In\nthis regard, further studies should be conducted to inves-\ntigate this issue in a larger patient popu", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nIn addition to pathological changes, rapid muscle recov-\nery is essential for athletes [252, 260]. A recent study\nfound that the inflammatory response that occurs after\na marathon is important for"}
