s. Some relation-\nships are not yet clear or conclusive, especially the effects\nof marathons on bone mineral density and osteoporosis\nrisk, osteoarthritis risk, and long-term joint damage. In\nthis regard, further studies should be conducted to inves-\ntigate this issue in a larger patient popu", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "uromuscu-\nlar recovery and does not lead to further muscle dam-\nage [260]. In fact, runners returning to running after 48\nh post-race have significantly improved squat jump tests\ncompared to resting runners [260]. A return to moderate\nphysical activi"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0039_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "161af10b3e0902ed", "text_span_hash": "161af10b3e0902ed", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 39, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "rn to moderate\nphysical activity 48 h after a marathon race may, there-\nfore, be recommended to speed and enhance muscular\nThe musculoskeletal system is immensely stressed during\na marathon race and training. Therefore, it has enjoyed\ngreat research", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nIn addition to pathological changes, rapid muscle recov-\nery is essential for athletes [252, 260]. A recent study\nfound that the inflammatory response that occurs after\na marathon is important for muscle recovery [252]. Par-\nticularly, some cytokines, especially IL-6, are important\nfor the activation of satellite cells i.e., precursor cells of\nmyocytes, inducing angiogenesis and myogenesis [252].\nExercise-induced muscle injury leads to a release of IL-6\nand IL-6 associated with CK in marathon runners [261].\nAdditionally, studies found that a return to moderate\nrunning 48 h post-race could speed up neuromuscu-\nlar recovery and does not lead to further muscle dam-\nage [260]. In fact, runners returning to running after 48\nh post-race have significantly improved squat jump tests\ncompared to resting runners [260]. A return to moderate\nphysical activity 48 h after a marathon race may, there-\nfore, be recommended to speed and enhance muscular\nThe musculoskeletal system is immensely stressed during\na marathon race and training. Therefore, it has enjoyed\ngreat research interest over the past years. Some relation-\nships are not yet clear or conclusive, especially the effects\nof marathons on bone mineral density and osteoporosis\nrisk, osteoarthritis risk, and long-term joint damage. In\nthis regard, further studies should be conducted to inves-\ntigate this issue in a larger patient popu", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "rn to moderate\nphysical activity 48 h after a marathon race may, there-\nfore, be recommended to speed and enhance muscular\nThe musculoskeletal system is immensely stressed during\na marathon race and training. Therefore, it has enjoyed\ngreat research"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0039_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "eeeabac166404eda", "text_span_hash": "eeeabac166404eda", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 39, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "it has enjoyed\ngreat research interest over the past years. Some relation-\nships are not yet clear or conclusive, especially the effects\nof marathons on bone mineral density and osteoporosis\nrisk, osteoarthritis risk, and long-term joint damage. In\nt", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nIn addition to pathological changes, rapid muscle recov-\nery is essential for athletes [252, 260]. A recent study\nfound that the inflammatory response that occurs after\na marathon is important for muscle recovery [252]. Par-\nticularly, some cytokines, especially IL-6, are important\nfor the activation of satellite cells i.e., precursor cells of\nmyocytes, inducing angiogenesis and myogenesis [252].\nExercise-induced muscle injury leads to a release of IL-6\nand IL-6 associated with CK in marathon runners [261].\nAdditionally, studies found that a return to moderate\nrunning 48 h post-race could speed up neuromuscu-\nlar recovery and does not lead to further muscle dam-\nage [260]. In fact, runners returning to running after 48\nh post-race have significantly improved squat jump tests\ncompared to resting runners [260]. A return to moderate\nphysical activity 48 h after a marathon race may, there-\nfore, be recommended to speed and enhance muscular\nThe musculoskeletal system is immensely stressed during\na marathon race and training. Therefore, it has enjoyed\ngreat research interest over the past years. Some relation-\nships are not yet clear or conclusive, especially the effects\nof marathons on bone mineral density and osteoporosis\nrisk, osteoarthritis risk, and long-term joint damage. In\nthis regard, further studies should be conducted to inves-\ntigate this issue in a larger patient popu", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "it has enjoyed\ngreat research interest over the past years. Some relation-\nships are not yet clear or conclusive, especially the effects\nof marathons on bone mineral density and osteoporosis\nrisk, osteoarthritis risk, and long-term joint damage. In\nt"}
