es on running performance or health,\nAlterations in Iron Metabolism and Runner’s Anemia\nIron is an important component of hemoglobin, essential\nfor oxygen transport and therefore of great importance\nfor endurance exercise [276]. A marathon race leads to\nseveral alterations in biomarkers of ir", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "[262]. It does not seem to have any nega-\ntive consequences on running performance or health,\nAlterations in Iron Metabolism and Runner’s Anemia\nIron is an important component of hemoglobin, essential\nfor oxygen transport and therefore of great impor"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0042_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1f4acc79468f71e9", "text_span_hash": "1f4acc79468f71e9", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 42, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "d then returned to baseline levels, which imply that\nthese changes are only transient [16]. More pronounced\nchanges were observed in finishers of an ultra-marathon\nrace, suggesting, to a certain extent, a duration or time\ncomponent in changes of iron", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nThese different results may be explained by different\nfluid consumption patterns as well as temperature and\nweather differences similar to those observed in EAH\nAcute Inflammatory Response\nSeveral studies reported a significant increase in leuko-\ncytes after completion of a marathon race (see Table 3),\nwhere the increase returned to baseline levels within 2 to\nThis leukocytosis is predominantly caused by neutro-\nphilia, as a marathon reportedly seems to decrease total\nYounger marathoners showed higher levels of total white\nblood cells and neutrophil counts [275]. There are dif-\nferent hypotheses as to why exercise induces immediate\nleukocytosis. However, it is assumed that leukocytes are\nincreased by an interaction of catecholamines produced\nduring exercise that provokes leukocyte demargination\nand cortisol released during exercise that causes mobi-\nlization of leukocytes from the bone marrow [262]. The\npossible advantages of running-induced leukocytosis are\nnot clear and may be associated with a general “fight or\nflight” response [262]. It does not seem to have any nega-\ntive consequences on running performance or health,\nAlterations in Iron Metabolism and Runner’s Anemia\nIron is an important component of hemoglobin, essential\nfor oxygen transport and therefore of great importance\nfor endurance exercise [276]. A marathon race leads to\nseveral alterations in biomarkers of ir", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "d then returned to baseline levels, which imply that\nthese changes are only transient [16]. More pronounced\nchanges were observed in finishers of an ultra-marathon\nrace, suggesting, to a certain extent, a duration or time\ncomponent in changes of iron"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0042_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "dd8e197d0daf1a90", "text_span_hash": "dd8e197d0daf1a90", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 42, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "e\ncomponent in changes of iron homeostasis [16].\nFurthermore, iron deficiency is a common problem in\nmarathon runners, especially in women, and may impair\nphysical performance [16, 276, 277]. In female mara-\nthon runners, iron deficiency was present", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nThese different results may be explained by different\nfluid consumption patterns as well as temperature and\nweather differences similar to those observed in EAH\nAcute Inflammatory Response\nSeveral studies reported a significant increase in leuko-\ncytes after completion of a marathon race (see Table 3),\nwhere the increase returned to baseline levels within 2 to\nThis leukocytosis is predominantly caused by neutro-\nphilia, as a marathon reportedly seems to decrease total\nYounger marathoners showed higher levels of total white\nblood cells and neutrophil counts [275]. There are dif-\nferent hypotheses as to why exercise induces immediate\nleukocytosis. However, it is assumed that leukocytes are\nincreased by an interaction of catecholamines produced\nduring exercise that provokes leukocyte demargination\nand cortisol released during exercise that causes mobi-\nlization of leukocytes from the bone marrow [262]. The\npossible advantages of running-induced leukocytosis are\nnot clear and may be associated with a general “fight or\nflight” response [262]. It does not seem to have any nega-\ntive consequences on running performance or health,\nAlterations in Iron Metabolism and Runner’s Anemia\nIron is an important component of hemoglobin, essential\nfor oxygen transport and therefore of great importance\nfor endurance exercise [276]. A marathon race leads to\nseveral alterations in biomarkers of ir", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "e\ncomponent in changes of iron homeostasis [16].\nFurthermore, iron deficiency is a common problem in\nmarathon runners, especially in women, and may impair\nphysical performance [16, 276, 277]. In female mara-\nthon runners, iron deficiency was present"}
