{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0043_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0c00e786b9864ae7", "text_span_hash": "0c00e786b9864ae7", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 43, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "should still be treated to improve running\nperformance [276]. How this should be done, remains\nunclear [278]. Usually, an initial attempt at iron supple-\nmentation with a daily oral dose of 40–60 mg is recom-\nmended [279]. The efficacy of the supple", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nalbumin content, resulting in the dilution of hemoglobin\nThe term “pseudo-anemia” is frequently used and\nthought to enhance physical performance [278]. Iron\ndeficiency should still be treated to improve running\nperformance [276]. How this should be done, remains\nunclear [278]. Usually, an initial attempt at iron supple-\nmentation with a daily oral dose of 40–60 mg is recom-\nmended [279]. The efficacy of the supplementation is\nrecommended to be monitored with blood tests 6–8\nweeks after initiation of deficiency correction [279]. If\noral supplementation fails to increase ferritin levels,\nintravenous iron supplementation may be considered\n[279]. Some studies found no improvement after cor-\nrection of low serum ferritin or folate levels in maximal\ntreadmill performance in female runners with iron or\nfolate deficiency in the absence of anemia, respectively\n[277]. A recent study, on the other hand, found an iron\noverload in 15% of male and 4.7% of female runners due\nto the use of iron supplements [276]. This can limit per-\nformance and lead to other health problems, particularly\npossible side effects of iron supplementation, such as\nnausea, abdominal pain, and constipation [276, 278, 279].\nFor this reason, the use of iron supplements is recom-\nmended only for individuals with laboratory-confirmed\niron deficiency [276].\nHypercoagulable State\nIn addition to changes in biomarkers of ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "should still be treated to improve running\nperformance [276]. How this should be done, remains\nunclear [278]. Usually, an initial attempt at iron supple-\nmentation with a daily oral dose of 40–60 mg is recom-\nmended [279]. The efficacy of the supple"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0043_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ef50b14239a9140b", "text_span_hash": "ef50b14239a9140b", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 43, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "transition the coagulation cascade into\na more coagulable state [263]. There are several pub-\nlished cases reporting DVT [280], PE [281] and other\nthromboembolic events, such as retinal vein occlusion\n[282], in otherwise healthy marathon participants", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nalbumin content, resulting in the dilution of hemoglobin\nThe term “pseudo-anemia” is frequently used and\nthought to enhance physical performance [278]. Iron\ndeficiency should still be treated to improve running\nperformance [276]. How this should be done, remains\nunclear [278]. Usually, an initial attempt at iron supple-\nmentation with a daily oral dose of 40–60 mg is recom-\nmended [279]. The efficacy of the supplementation is\nrecommended to be monitored with blood tests 6–8\nweeks after initiation of deficiency correction [279]. If\noral supplementation fails to increase ferritin levels,\nintravenous iron supplementation may be considered\n[279]. Some studies found no improvement after cor-\nrection of low serum ferritin or folate levels in maximal\ntreadmill performance in female runners with iron or\nfolate deficiency in the absence of anemia, respectively\n[277]. A recent study, on the other hand, found an iron\noverload in 15% of male and 4.7% of female runners due\nto the use of iron supplements [276]. This can limit per-\nformance and lead to other health problems, particularly\npossible side effects of iron supplementation, such as\nnausea, abdominal pain, and constipation [276, 278, 279].\nFor this reason, the use of iron supplements is recom-\nmended only for individuals with laboratory-confirmed\niron deficiency [276].\nHypercoagulable State\nIn addition to changes in biomarkers of ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "transition the coagulation cascade into\na more coagulable state [263]. There are several pub-\nlished cases reporting DVT [280], PE [281] and other\nthromboembolic events, such as retinal vein occlusion\n[282], in otherwise healthy marathon participants"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0043_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c3e95a69f92364ce", "text_span_hash": "c3e95a69f92364ce", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 43, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "healthy marathon participants, which\nimplicate the impact of strenuous exercise on a hyperco-\nagulable state [283]. Several studies identified strenuous\nexercise as an inducer of a general prothrombotic state in\nbe caused by microtrauma in skeletal", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nalbumin content, resulting in the dilution of hemoglobin\nThe term “pseudo-anemia” is frequently used and\nthought to enhance physical performance [278]. Iron\ndeficiency should still be treated to improve running\nperformance [276]. How this should be done, remains\nunclear [278]. Usually, an initial attempt at iron supple-\nmentation with a daily oral dose of 40–60 mg is recom-\nmended [279]. The efficacy of the supplementation is\nrecommended to be monitored with blood tests 6–8\nweeks after initiation of deficiency correction [279]. If\noral supplementation fails to increase ferritin levels,\nintravenous iron supplementation may be considered\n[279]. Some studies found no improvement after cor-\nrection of low serum ferritin or folate levels in maximal\ntreadmill performance in female runners with iron or\nfolate deficiency in the absence of anemia, respectively\n[277]. A recent study, on the other hand, found an iron\noverload in 15% of male and 4.7% of female runners due\nto the use of iron supplements [276]. This can limit per-\nformance and lead to other health problems, particularly\npossible side effects of iron supplementation, such as\nnausea, abdominal pain, and constipation [276, 278, 279].\nFor this reason, the use of iron supplements is recom-\nmended only for individuals with laboratory-confirmed\niron deficiency [276].\nHypercoagulable State\nIn addition to changes in biomarkers of ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "healthy marathon participants, which\nimplicate the impact of strenuous exercise on a hyperco-\nagulable state [283]. Several studies identified strenuous\nexercise as an inducer of a general prothrombotic state in\nbe caused by microtrauma in skeletal"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0043_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "409de02b9345a5b5", "text_span_hash": "409de02b9345a5b5", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 43, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ies showed a reduction in whole blood clotting time as\nwell as activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT) by\n7–38% after completion of a marathon [244, 263, 289].\nThere is evidence of increased activity of several com-\nponents of the coagulation ca", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nalbumin content, resulting in the dilution of hemoglobin\nThe term “pseudo-anemia” is frequently used and\nthought to enhance physical performance [278]. Iron\ndeficiency should still be treated to improve running\nperformance [276]. How this should be done, remains\nunclear [278]. Usually, an initial attempt at iron supple-\nmentation with a daily oral dose of 40–60 mg is recom-\nmended [279]. The efficacy of the supplementation is\nrecommended to be monitored with blood tests 6–8\nweeks after initiation of deficiency correction [279]. If\noral supplementation fails to increase ferritin levels,\nintravenous iron supplementation may be considered\n[279]. Some studies found no improvement after cor-\nrection of low serum ferritin or folate levels in maximal\ntreadmill performance in female runners with iron or\nfolate deficiency in the absence of anemia, respectively\n[277]. A recent study, on the other hand, found an iron\noverload in 15% of male and 4.7% of female runners due\nto the use of iron supplements [276]. This can limit per-\nformance and lead to other health problems, particularly\npossible side effects of iron supplementation, such as\nnausea, abdominal pain, and constipation [276, 278, 279].\nFor this reason, the use of iron supplements is recom-\nmended only for individuals with laboratory-confirmed\niron deficiency [276].\nHypercoagulable State\nIn addition to changes in biomarkers of ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ies showed a reduction in whole blood clotting time as\nwell as activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT) by\n7–38% after completion of a marathon [244, 263, 289].\nThere is evidence of increased activity of several com-\nponents of the coagulation ca"}
