y-adrenal and -thyroid axis in non-elite mara-\nAfter the race significant elevations of serum cortisol (by 3.7-fold), ACTH (by\n5.7-fold) and vasopressin (by 2.7-fold) were observable\nNo significant changes in T3, T4 or TSH\nAge 56.3 (range 45–69) years\nHormonal responses of cortisol and testos", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ge 25.4 ± 1.4 years\nHypothalamic gonadotropin-releasing hormone secretion in highly trained\nmale marathon runners\nCompared to healthy controls, frequency of LH pulses was reduced by 1.6-\nfold per eight hours as well as the amplitude of the pulses was"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0047_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f8c55e0353339e68", "text_span_hash": "f8c55e0353339e68", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 47, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "6.9 years\nNeuroendocrine alterations after a marathon run\nPlasma concentrations of ACTH (by 4.4-fold), cortisol (by 3.4-fold),\nafter the marathon and reached peak concentrations 0–1 h after the race\nLevels of ACTH and cortisol returned to baseline le", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nTable 11  Overview of key findings from studies investigating effects of marathon training and running on the endocrine system and its function\nIntervention/Observation\nAge 43.2 ± 2.6 years\nLeptin in marathon runners\nA statistically significant linear correlation between leptin and marathon\ntime could be found regardless of age and BMI\nAge 35.7 ± 8 years\nNeuro-endocrine alterations in marathon runners\nAt rest, serum levels of GH were significantly higher by 1.3-fold in athletes\ncompared to sedentary people\nAfter the race, serum levels of GH increased by 1.8-fold and cortisol by 3.1-\nfold compared to baseline values and returned to pre-race values after 72 h\nAge 29.2 ± 7.4 years\nAdrenal-sympathico function in highly trained marathon runners\nCompared to baseline levels, glucose, free fatty acids, and glycerol were\nsignificantly increased after the race by 1.1-fold, 5.8-fold, and 5.2-fold,\nSignificant elevations in norepinephrine (by 1.7-fold), epinephrine (by 1.6-\nfold), and cortisol (by 1.6-fold) were detectable immediately after the race\nAge 42.9 ± 8.5 years\nBiomarker changes of pituitary-adrenal and -thyroid axis in non-elite mara-\nAfter the race significant elevations of serum cortisol (by 3.7-fold), ACTH (by\n5.7-fold) and vasopressin (by 2.7-fold) were observable\nNo significant changes in T3, T4 or TSH\nAge 56.3 (range 45–69) years\nHormonal responses of cortisol and testos", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "6.9 years\nNeuroendocrine alterations after a marathon run\nPlasma concentrations of ACTH (by 4.4-fold), cortisol (by 3.4-fold),\nafter the marathon and reached peak concentrations 0–1 h after the race\nLevels of ACTH and cortisol returned to baseline le"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0048_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "352644dc70023255", "text_span_hash": "352644dc70023255", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 48, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "s in serum T3, T4, and TSH concentrations could be\nNo alterations in the TSH response to TRH was detectable\nSerum prolactin levels rose significantly after the marathon race by 1.3-fold\nAge 30.4 ± 1.4 years\nAlterations in gonadotropic axis in male en", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nTable 11  (continued)\nIntervention/Observation\nAge 35.2 ± 2.1 years\nBiochemical alterations of the pituitary-thyroid axis and prolactin secretion\nNo significant changes in serum T3, T4, and TSH concentrations could be\nNo alterations in the TSH response to TRH was detectable\nSerum prolactin levels rose significantly after the marathon race by 1.3-fold\nAge 30.4 ± 1.4 years\nAlterations in gonadotropic axis in male endurance athletes\nCompared to sedentary controls, mean plasma testosterone levels in endur-\nance runners were 1.5-fold lower\nAfter intense and prolonged exercise, LH concentration decreased signifi-\ncantly by 1.6-fold in the athlete group and by 1.3-fold in the control group\nPlasma testosterone levels increased by 1.8-fold in endurance athletes\nand by 1.4-fold in the control group\nAge 33.2 ± 6.1 years\nAlterations in gonadotropic axis and prolactin levels in female endurance\nImmediately after the marathon run, plasma levels of prolactin, cortisol,\ntestosterone and DHEAS were significantly elevated by 4.0-fold, 2.1-fold, 1.9-\nfold and 1.5-fold, respectively, compared to baseline levels\nNo significant changes in FSH or LH could be observed in this timeline\nAge range 22–52 years\nEffects of marathon running on the pituitary–testicular axis\nSignificantly elevated plasma levels of LH could be detected dur-\ning the three post-run days with peak values 24 h after the race (2.", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "s in serum T3, T4, and TSH concentrations could be\nNo alterations in the TSH response to TRH was detectable\nSerum prolactin levels rose significantly after the marathon race by 1.3-fold\nAge 30.4 ± 1.4 years\nAlterations in gonadotropic axis in male en"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0048_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "911f65f5f3db053e", "text_span_hash": "911f65f5f3db053e", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 48, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "n gonadotropic axis in male endurance athletes\nCompared to sedentary controls, mean plasma testosterone levels in endur-\nance runners were 1.5-fold lower\nAfter intense and prolonged exercise, LH concentration decreased signifi-\ncantly by 1.6-fold in", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nTable 11  (continued)\nIntervention/Observation\nAge 35.2 ± 2.1 years\nBiochemical alterations of the pituitary-thyroid axis and prolactin secretion\nNo significant changes in serum T3, T4, and TSH concentrations could be\nNo alterations in the TSH response to TRH was detectable\nSerum prolactin levels rose significantly after the marathon race by 1.3-fold\nAge 30.4 ± 1.4 years\nAlterations in gonadotropic axis in male endurance athletes\nCompared to sedentary controls, mean plasma testosterone levels in endur-\nance runners were 1.5-fold lower\nAfter intense and prolonged exercise, LH concentration decreased signifi-\ncantly by 1.6-fold in the athlete group and by 1.3-fold in the control group\nPlasma testosterone levels increased by 1.8-fold in endurance athletes\nand by 1.4-fold in the control group\nAge 33.2 ± 6.1 years\nAlterations in gonadotropic axis and prolactin levels in female endurance\nImmediately after the marathon run, plasma levels of prolactin, cortisol,\ntestosterone and DHEAS were significantly elevated by 4.0-fold, 2.1-fold, 1.9-\nfold and 1.5-fold, respectively, compared to baseline levels\nNo significant changes in FSH or LH could be observed in this timeline\nAge range 22–52 years\nEffects of marathon running on the pituitary–testicular axis\nSignificantly elevated plasma levels of LH could be detected dur-\ning the three post-run days with peak values 24 h after the race (2.", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "n gonadotropic axis in male endurance athletes\nCompared to sedentary controls, mean plasma testosterone levels in endur-\nance runners were 1.5-fold lower\nAfter intense and prolonged exercise, LH concentration decreased signifi-\ncantly by 1.6-fold in"}
