{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0050_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "63a9ab05896ef7c2", "text_span_hash": "63a9ab05896ef7c2", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 50, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nendurance exercise especially has positive effects on the\nimmune system. Studies have shown that regular mod-\nerate physical activity, such as marathon training, can\nreduce the risk of URTI, wherea", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nendurance exercise especially has positive effects on the\nimmune system. Studies have shown that regular mod-\nerate physical activity, such as marathon training, can\nreduce the risk of URTI, whereas intense exercise can\nincrease this risk [299, 300, 302, 309]. In this regard, an\nassociation between the risk of URTI and exercise inten-\nsity is assumed in the form of a “J-curve” (see Fig. 12)\n[302]. This underlines the immune-boosting effect of\nregular exercise. It is assumed that both cortisol and cat-\necholamines, with their immunosuppressive effects, are\nreleased in much lower doses during moderate exercise\nthan e.g., after a marathon race [299]. In addition, there is\nevidence that the cytotoxic activity of natural killer (NK)\ncells is significantly enhanced, thus strengthening the\nfirst line of defense against pathogens [299, 300, 302].\nFurthermore, studies have shown that regular moder-\nate exercise, such as marathon training, can lower base-\nline levels of C-reactive protein (CRP) (see Table 6) [16,\n243, 315]. This suggests both an immune-boosting effect\nand an anti-inflammatory effect of exercise [16, 315]. The\nreason for this is probably myokines, which are released\nmore frequently and thus promote the release of anti-\ninflammatory cytokines such as IL-10 [16, 315]. This is of\nparticular interest as high baseline CRP levels are associ-\nated with an increased risk of de", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nendurance exercise especially has positive effects on the\nimmune system. Studies have shown that regular mod-\nerate physical activity, such as marathon training, can\nreduce the risk of URTI, wherea"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0050_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f2b9837909a906a7", "text_span_hash": "f2b9837909a906a7", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 50, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "exercise\nthan e.g., after a marathon race [299]. In addition, there is\nevidence that the cytotoxic activity of natural killer (NK)\ncells is significantly enhanced, thus strengthening the\nfirst line of defense against pathogens [299, 300, 302].\nFurthe", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nendurance exercise especially has positive effects on the\nimmune system. Studies have shown that regular mod-\nerate physical activity, such as marathon training, can\nreduce the risk of URTI, whereas intense exercise can\nincrease this risk [299, 300, 302, 309]. In this regard, an\nassociation between the risk of URTI and exercise inten-\nsity is assumed in the form of a “J-curve” (see Fig. 12)\n[302]. This underlines the immune-boosting effect of\nregular exercise. It is assumed that both cortisol and cat-\necholamines, with their immunosuppressive effects, are\nreleased in much lower doses during moderate exercise\nthan e.g., after a marathon race [299]. In addition, there is\nevidence that the cytotoxic activity of natural killer (NK)\ncells is significantly enhanced, thus strengthening the\nfirst line of defense against pathogens [299, 300, 302].\nFurthermore, studies have shown that regular moder-\nate exercise, such as marathon training, can lower base-\nline levels of C-reactive protein (CRP) (see Table 6) [16,\n243, 315]. This suggests both an immune-boosting effect\nand an anti-inflammatory effect of exercise [16, 315]. The\nreason for this is probably myokines, which are released\nmore frequently and thus promote the release of anti-\ninflammatory cytokines such as IL-10 [16, 315]. This is of\nparticular interest as high baseline CRP levels are associ-\nated with an increased risk of de", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "exercise\nthan e.g., after a marathon race [299]. In addition, there is\nevidence that the cytotoxic activity of natural killer (NK)\ncells is significantly enhanced, thus strengthening the\nfirst line of defense against pathogens [299, 300, 302].\nFurthe"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0050_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e599ca1d20ea50b8", "text_span_hash": "e599ca1d20ea50b8", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 50, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "fects on baseline CRP, regular moderate endurance\nexercise leads to the induction of antioxidant enzymes\nand lower production of reactive oxygen species in\nmitochondria [316]. This reduces DNA damage as well\nas other harmful changes to cells and thus", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nendurance exercise especially has positive effects on the\nimmune system. Studies have shown that regular mod-\nerate physical activity, such as marathon training, can\nreduce the risk of URTI, whereas intense exercise can\nincrease this risk [299, 300, 302, 309]. In this regard, an\nassociation between the risk of URTI and exercise inten-\nsity is assumed in the form of a “J-curve” (see Fig. 12)\n[302]. This underlines the immune-boosting effect of\nregular exercise. It is assumed that both cortisol and cat-\necholamines, with their immunosuppressive effects, are\nreleased in much lower doses during moderate exercise\nthan e.g., after a marathon race [299]. In addition, there is\nevidence that the cytotoxic activity of natural killer (NK)\ncells is significantly enhanced, thus strengthening the\nfirst line of defense against pathogens [299, 300, 302].\nFurthermore, studies have shown that regular moder-\nate exercise, such as marathon training, can lower base-\nline levels of C-reactive protein (CRP) (see Table 6) [16,\n243, 315]. This suggests both an immune-boosting effect\nand an anti-inflammatory effect of exercise [16, 315]. The\nreason for this is probably myokines, which are released\nmore frequently and thus promote the release of anti-\ninflammatory cytokines such as IL-10 [16, 315]. This is of\nparticular interest as high baseline CRP levels are associ-\nated with an increased risk of de", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "fects on baseline CRP, regular moderate endurance\nexercise leads to the induction of antioxidant enzymes\nand lower production of reactive oxygen species in\nmitochondria [316]. This reduces DNA damage as well\nas other harmful changes to cells and thus"}
