ect of exercise [16, 315]. The\nreason for this is probably myokines, which are released\nmore frequently and thus promote the release of anti-\ninflammatory cytokines such as IL-10 [16, 315]. This is of\nparticular interest as high baseline CRP levels are associ-\nated with an increased risk of de", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "sceptibil-\nity to infections [60, 300, 305]. It is suggested that over-\ntraining syndrome is responsible to some extent at least\nfor transient immunosuppression [60]. This is explained\nby the fact that due to chronic microtrauma, an inflam-\nmatory re"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0050_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c75e61e23ca78834", "text_span_hash": "c75e61e23ca78834", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 50, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "microtrauma observed in overtrain-\ning syndrome and strongly promotes the up-regulation of\nTh2 cell response and decreases the activity of NK cells\nIntense, strenuous exercise such as marathon running\nputs immense stress on the immune system and its", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nendurance exercise especially has positive effects on the\nimmune system. Studies have shown that regular mod-\nerate physical activity, such as marathon training, can\nreduce the risk of URTI, whereas intense exercise can\nincrease this risk [299, 300, 302, 309]. In this regard, an\nassociation between the risk of URTI and exercise inten-\nsity is assumed in the form of a “J-curve” (see Fig. 12)\n[302]. This underlines the immune-boosting effect of\nregular exercise. It is assumed that both cortisol and cat-\necholamines, with their immunosuppressive effects, are\nreleased in much lower doses during moderate exercise\nthan e.g., after a marathon race [299]. In addition, there is\nevidence that the cytotoxic activity of natural killer (NK)\ncells is significantly enhanced, thus strengthening the\nfirst line of defense against pathogens [299, 300, 302].\nFurthermore, studies have shown that regular moder-\nate exercise, such as marathon training, can lower base-\nline levels of C-reactive protein (CRP) (see Table 6) [16,\n243, 315]. This suggests both an immune-boosting effect\nand an anti-inflammatory effect of exercise [16, 315]. The\nreason for this is probably myokines, which are released\nmore frequently and thus promote the release of anti-\ninflammatory cytokines such as IL-10 [16, 315]. This is of\nparticular interest as high baseline CRP levels are associ-\nated with an increased risk of de", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "microtrauma observed in overtrain-\ning syndrome and strongly promotes the up-regulation of\nTh2 cell response and decreases the activity of NK cells\nIntense, strenuous exercise such as marathon running\nputs immense stress on the immune system and its"}
