 levels, have\nbeen linked to sexual dysfunction in male athletes as well\nas amenorrhea and other menstrual cycle abnormalities\nIn addition, hypothalamic dysfunction has been\ndescribed in the context of overtraining syndrome [61].\nA study investigating hormonal responses in overtrained\nathlet", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "g hormone (LH) pulses as well as its amplitude\nwere reduced compared to controls (see Table 11) [313,\n330–332]. However, a marathon run immediately leads\nto an increase in follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH)\nlevels [331]. Regarding the effects of mara"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0052_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "fc418e0cd7b55715", "text_span_hash": "fc418e0cd7b55715", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 52, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Regarding the effects of marathon training\nand running on testosterone levels, the literature is again\ndivided. Most articles describe reduced levels of testos-\nterone immediately after a marathon run (see Table 11)\n[18, 57, 312, 332]. Nonetheless,", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nincrease of TSH after exercise and intensity-dependent\nserum change of free T4 and free T3 [327].\nInfluence of Exercise on the Hypothalamic‑Pituitary–Gonadal\nThe impact of intense exercise on the hypothalamic-pitu-\nitary–gonadal axis is probably the most discussed topic\nregarding the endocrine system [329]. Studies showed\nthat in marathon runners, both the mean frequency of\nluteinizing hormone (LH) pulses as well as its amplitude\nwere reduced compared to controls (see Table 11) [313,\n330–332]. However, a marathon run immediately leads\nto an increase in follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH)\nlevels [331]. Regarding the effects of marathon training\nand running on testosterone levels, the literature is again\ndivided. Most articles describe reduced levels of testos-\nterone immediately after a marathon run (see Table 11)\n[18, 57, 312, 332]. Nonetheless, there is literature report-\ning increased levels of testosterone, especially in female\nathletes [313, 329–331, 333]. Despite this, said hormo-\nnal alterations, i.e., increased prolactin levels, alterations\nin gonadotropins, and reduced testosterone levels, have\nbeen linked to sexual dysfunction in male athletes as well\nas amenorrhea and other menstrual cycle abnormalities\nIn addition, hypothalamic dysfunction has been\ndescribed in the context of overtraining syndrome [61].\nA study investigating hormonal responses in overtrained\nathlet", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Regarding the effects of marathon training\nand running on testosterone levels, the literature is again\ndivided. Most articles describe reduced levels of testos-\nterone immediately after a marathon run (see Table 11)\n[18, 57, 312, 332]. Nonetheless,"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0052_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1618594fd0638879", "text_span_hash": "1618594fd0638879", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 52, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ompared\nto healthy controls(see Table 11) [61]. These alterations\nresolved within 4 weeks of rest [61].\nMarathon training and running interfere with the finely\nregulated hormonal axes of the endocrine system in both\npositive and negative ways. Partic", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nincrease of TSH after exercise and intensity-dependent\nserum change of free T4 and free T3 [327].\nInfluence of Exercise on the Hypothalamic‑Pituitary–Gonadal\nThe impact of intense exercise on the hypothalamic-pitu-\nitary–gonadal axis is probably the most discussed topic\nregarding the endocrine system [329]. Studies showed\nthat in marathon runners, both the mean frequency of\nluteinizing hormone (LH) pulses as well as its amplitude\nwere reduced compared to controls (see Table 11) [313,\n330–332]. However, a marathon run immediately leads\nto an increase in follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH)\nlevels [331]. Regarding the effects of marathon training\nand running on testosterone levels, the literature is again\ndivided. Most articles describe reduced levels of testos-\nterone immediately after a marathon run (see Table 11)\n[18, 57, 312, 332]. Nonetheless, there is literature report-\ning increased levels of testosterone, especially in female\nathletes [313, 329–331, 333]. Despite this, said hormo-\nnal alterations, i.e., increased prolactin levels, alterations\nin gonadotropins, and reduced testosterone levels, have\nbeen linked to sexual dysfunction in male athletes as well\nas amenorrhea and other menstrual cycle abnormalities\nIn addition, hypothalamic dysfunction has been\ndescribed in the context of overtraining syndrome [61].\nA study investigating hormonal responses in overtrained\nathlet", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ompared\nto healthy controls(see Table 11) [61]. These alterations\nresolved within 4 weeks of rest [61].\nMarathon training and running interfere with the finely\nregulated hormonal axes of the endocrine system in both\npositive and negative ways. Partic"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0052_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c1c2490a771ef73e", "text_span_hash": "c1c2490a771ef73e", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 52, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "lly\nregarding the pituitary–gonadal axis have been observed\nin both male and female runners.\nNevertheless, there have been ambiguous results found\nin the discussed studies, and consequently, the results\nshould be interpreted with caution. In addition", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nincrease of TSH after exercise and intensity-dependent\nserum change of free T4 and free T3 [327].\nInfluence of Exercise on the Hypothalamic‑Pituitary–Gonadal\nThe impact of intense exercise on the hypothalamic-pitu-\nitary–gonadal axis is probably the most discussed topic\nregarding the endocrine system [329]. Studies showed\nthat in marathon runners, both the mean frequency of\nluteinizing hormone (LH) pulses as well as its amplitude\nwere reduced compared to controls (see Table 11) [313,\n330–332]. However, a marathon run immediately leads\nto an increase in follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH)\nlevels [331]. Regarding the effects of marathon training\nand running on testosterone levels, the literature is again\ndivided. Most articles describe reduced levels of testos-\nterone immediately after a marathon run (see Table 11)\n[18, 57, 312, 332]. Nonetheless, there is literature report-\ning increased levels of testosterone, especially in female\nathletes [313, 329–331, 333]. Despite this, said hormo-\nnal alterations, i.e., increased prolactin levels, alterations\nin gonadotropins, and reduced testosterone levels, have\nbeen linked to sexual dysfunction in male athletes as well\nas amenorrhea and other menstrual cycle abnormalities\nIn addition, hypothalamic dysfunction has been\ndescribed in the context of overtraining syndrome [61].\nA study investigating hormonal responses in overtrained\nathlet", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "lly\nregarding the pituitary–gonadal axis have been observed\nin both male and female runners.\nNevertheless, there have been ambiguous results found\nin the discussed studies, and consequently, the results\nshould be interpreted with caution. In addition"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0052_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e2e319835ae4f6f4", "text_span_hash": "e2e319835ae4f6f4", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 52, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "eted with caution. In addition, most of\nthe studies that investigated changes in the endocrine\nsystem are quite old, predominantly examined male mar-\nathon runners and differed significantly in their method-\nology, particularly the methods of analysi", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nincrease of TSH after exercise and intensity-dependent\nserum change of free T4 and free T3 [327].\nInfluence of Exercise on the Hypothalamic‑Pituitary–Gonadal\nThe impact of intense exercise on the hypothalamic-pitu-\nitary–gonadal axis is probably the most discussed topic\nregarding the endocrine system [329]. Studies showed\nthat in marathon runners, both the mean frequency of\nluteinizing hormone (LH) pulses as well as its amplitude\nwere reduced compared to controls (see Table 11) [313,\n330–332]. However, a marathon run immediately leads\nto an increase in follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH)\nlevels [331]. Regarding the effects of marathon training\nand running on testosterone levels, the literature is again\ndivided. Most articles describe reduced levels of testos-\nterone immediately after a marathon run (see Table 11)\n[18, 57, 312, 332]. Nonetheless, there is literature report-\ning increased levels of testosterone, especially in female\nathletes [313, 329–331, 333]. Despite this, said hormo-\nnal alterations, i.e., increased prolactin levels, alterations\nin gonadotropins, and reduced testosterone levels, have\nbeen linked to sexual dysfunction in male athletes as well\nas amenorrhea and other menstrual cycle abnormalities\nIn addition, hypothalamic dysfunction has been\ndescribed in the context of overtraining syndrome [61].\nA study investigating hormonal responses in overtrained\nathlet", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "eted with caution. In addition, most of\nthe studies that investigated changes in the endocrine\nsystem are quite old, predominantly examined male mar-\nathon runners and differed significantly in their method-\nology, particularly the methods of analysi"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0052_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ae457fd2ea68fffc", "text_span_hash": "ae457fd2ea68fffc", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 52, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "cularly the methods of analysis. Accordingly,\nit is difficult to generalize the results and further studies\nare needed, especially in female athletes, to investigate\nendocrine changes in connection with endurance sports.\nIn conclusion, it can be stat", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nincrease of TSH after exercise and intensity-dependent\nserum change of free T4 and free T3 [327].\nInfluence of Exercise on the Hypothalamic‑Pituitary–Gonadal\nThe impact of intense exercise on the hypothalamic-pitu-\nitary–gonadal axis is probably the most discussed topic\nregarding the endocrine system [329]. Studies showed\nthat in marathon runners, both the mean frequency of\nluteinizing hormone (LH) pulses as well as its amplitude\nwere reduced compared to controls (see Table 11) [313,\n330–332]. However, a marathon run immediately leads\nto an increase in follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH)\nlevels [331]. Regarding the effects of marathon training\nand running on testosterone levels, the literature is again\ndivided. Most articles describe reduced levels of testos-\nterone immediately after a marathon run (see Table 11)\n[18, 57, 312, 332]. Nonetheless, there is literature report-\ning increased levels of testosterone, especially in female\nathletes [313, 329–331, 333]. Despite this, said hormo-\nnal alterations, i.e., increased prolactin levels, alterations\nin gonadotropins, and reduced testosterone levels, have\nbeen linked to sexual dysfunction in male athletes as well\nas amenorrhea and other menstrual cycle abnormalities\nIn addition, hypothalamic dysfunction has been\ndescribed in the context of overtraining syndrome [61].\nA study investigating hormonal responses in overtrained\nathlet", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "cularly the methods of analysis. Accordingly,\nit is difficult to generalize the results and further studies\nare needed, especially in female athletes, to investigate\nendocrine changes in connection with endurance sports.\nIn conclusion, it can be stat"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0052_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "6c742bebad9cd61c", "text_span_hash": "6c742bebad9cd61c", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 52, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "In conclusion, it can be stated that most hormonal\nchanges in marathon training and running can be inter-\npreted as part of an exercise-induced stress reaction.\nHowever, there is evidence of disturbances in the pitui-\ntary–gonadal axis in both sexes", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nincrease of TSH after exercise and intensity-dependent\nserum change of free T4 and free T3 [327].\nInfluence of Exercise on the Hypothalamic‑Pituitary–Gonadal\nThe impact of intense exercise on the hypothalamic-pitu-\nitary–gonadal axis is probably the most discussed topic\nregarding the endocrine system [329]. Studies showed\nthat in marathon runners, both the mean frequency of\nluteinizing hormone (LH) pulses as well as its amplitude\nwere reduced compared to controls (see Table 11) [313,\n330–332]. However, a marathon run immediately leads\nto an increase in follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH)\nlevels [331]. Regarding the effects of marathon training\nand running on testosterone levels, the literature is again\ndivided. Most articles describe reduced levels of testos-\nterone immediately after a marathon run (see Table 11)\n[18, 57, 312, 332]. Nonetheless, there is literature report-\ning increased levels of testosterone, especially in female\nathletes [313, 329–331, 333]. Despite this, said hormo-\nnal alterations, i.e., increased prolactin levels, alterations\nin gonadotropins, and reduced testosterone levels, have\nbeen linked to sexual dysfunction in male athletes as well\nas amenorrhea and other menstrual cycle abnormalities\nIn addition, hypothalamic dysfunction has been\ndescribed in the context of overtraining syndrome [61].\nA study investigating hormonal responses in overtrained\nathlet", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "In conclusion, it can be stated that most hormonal\nchanges in marathon training and running can be inter-\npreted as part of an exercise-induced stress reaction.\nHowever, there is evidence of disturbances in the pitui-\ntary–gonadal axis in both sexes"}
