ne levels, have\nbeen linked to sexual dysfunction in male athletes as well\nas amenorrhea and other menstrual cycle abnormalities\nIn addition, hypothalamic dysfunction has been\ndescribed in the context of overtraining syndrome [61].\nA study investigating hormonal responses in overtrained\nathlet", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "us\nsystem. The main findings of this section are illustrated in\nPositive Effects on Cognitive Function\nThe influence of marathon training and running on the\ncentral nervous system is not well understood [335]. Pos-\nitive effects, especially on cognit"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0052_c0018", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4ecd5395bc412652", "text_span_hash": "4ecd5395bc412652", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 52, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 18, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "effects, especially on cognitive function, have been\nreported, which would be of great clinical importance,\nespecially in elderly marathoners, as dementia becomes\nan increasing problem in the ageing population [336,\n337]. Strenuous exercise such as", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nincrease of TSH after exercise and intensity-dependent\nserum change of free T4 and free T3 [327].\nInfluence of Exercise on the Hypothalamic‑Pituitary–Gonadal\nThe impact of intense exercise on the hypothalamic-pitu-\nitary–gonadal axis is probably the most discussed topic\nregarding the endocrine system [329]. Studies showed\nthat in marathon runners, both the mean frequency of\nluteinizing hormone (LH) pulses as well as its amplitude\nwere reduced compared to controls (see Table 11) [313,\n330–332]. However, a marathon run immediately leads\nto an increase in follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH)\nlevels [331]. Regarding the effects of marathon training\nand running on testosterone levels, the literature is again\ndivided. Most articles describe reduced levels of testos-\nterone immediately after a marathon run (see Table 11)\n[18, 57, 312, 332]. Nonetheless, there is literature report-\ning increased levels of testosterone, especially in female\nathletes [313, 329–331, 333]. Despite this, said hormo-\nnal alterations, i.e., increased prolactin levels, alterations\nin gonadotropins, and reduced testosterone levels, have\nbeen linked to sexual dysfunction in male athletes as well\nas amenorrhea and other menstrual cycle abnormalities\nIn addition, hypothalamic dysfunction has been\ndescribed in the context of overtraining syndrome [61].\nA study investigating hormonal responses in overtrained\nathlet", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "effects, especially on cognitive function, have been\nreported, which would be of great clinical importance,\nespecially in elderly marathoners, as dementia becomes\nan increasing problem in the ageing population [336,\n337]. Strenuous exercise such as"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0052_c0020", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ff42d5d9689ff456", "text_span_hash": "ff42d5d9689ff456", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 52, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 20, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ive\nimpairment, regular endurance exercise improved global\ncognitive ability and memory [335]. A study comparing\nelderly marathon runners to a sedentary control group\nfound significantly better performance in executive func-\ntions (see Table 12) [336", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nincrease of TSH after exercise and intensity-dependent\nserum change of free T4 and free T3 [327].\nInfluence of Exercise on the Hypothalamic‑Pituitary–Gonadal\nThe impact of intense exercise on the hypothalamic-pitu-\nitary–gonadal axis is probably the most discussed topic\nregarding the endocrine system [329]. Studies showed\nthat in marathon runners, both the mean frequency of\nluteinizing hormone (LH) pulses as well as its amplitude\nwere reduced compared to controls (see Table 11) [313,\n330–332]. However, a marathon run immediately leads\nto an increase in follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH)\nlevels [331]. Regarding the effects of marathon training\nand running on testosterone levels, the literature is again\ndivided. Most articles describe reduced levels of testos-\nterone immediately after a marathon run (see Table 11)\n[18, 57, 312, 332]. Nonetheless, there is literature report-\ning increased levels of testosterone, especially in female\nathletes [313, 329–331, 333]. Despite this, said hormo-\nnal alterations, i.e., increased prolactin levels, alterations\nin gonadotropins, and reduced testosterone levels, have\nbeen linked to sexual dysfunction in male athletes as well\nas amenorrhea and other menstrual cycle abnormalities\nIn addition, hypothalamic dysfunction has been\ndescribed in the context of overtraining syndrome [61].\nA study investigating hormonal responses in overtrained\nathlet", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ive\nimpairment, regular endurance exercise improved global\ncognitive ability and memory [335]. A study comparing\nelderly marathon runners to a sedentary control group\nfound significantly better performance in executive func-\ntions (see Table 12) [336"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0052_c0021", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3eca18a54a1562eb", "text_span_hash": "3eca18a54a1562eb", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 52, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 21, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "unc-\ntions (see Table 12) [336]. A recent study showed a posi-\ntive correlation between high-demand working memory\ntasks, other domains of higher-demand executive func-\ntion, and marathon training [19]. Other studies described\nimproved executive func", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nincrease of TSH after exercise and intensity-dependent\nserum change of free T4 and free T3 [327].\nInfluence of Exercise on the Hypothalamic‑Pituitary–Gonadal\nThe impact of intense exercise on the hypothalamic-pitu-\nitary–gonadal axis is probably the most discussed topic\nregarding the endocrine system [329]. Studies showed\nthat in marathon runners, both the mean frequency of\nluteinizing hormone (LH) pulses as well as its amplitude\nwere reduced compared to controls (see Table 11) [313,\n330–332]. However, a marathon run immediately leads\nto an increase in follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH)\nlevels [331]. Regarding the effects of marathon training\nand running on testosterone levels, the literature is again\ndivided. Most articles describe reduced levels of testos-\nterone immediately after a marathon run (see Table 11)\n[18, 57, 312, 332]. Nonetheless, there is literature report-\ning increased levels of testosterone, especially in female\nathletes [313, 329–331, 333]. Despite this, said hormo-\nnal alterations, i.e., increased prolactin levels, alterations\nin gonadotropins, and reduced testosterone levels, have\nbeen linked to sexual dysfunction in male athletes as well\nas amenorrhea and other menstrual cycle abnormalities\nIn addition, hypothalamic dysfunction has been\ndescribed in the context of overtraining syndrome [61].\nA study investigating hormonal responses in overtrained\nathlet", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "unc-\ntions (see Table 12) [336]. A recent study showed a posi-\ntive correlation between high-demand working memory\ntasks, other domains of higher-demand executive func-\ntion, and marathon training [19]. Other studies described\nimproved executive func"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0052_c0022", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5f8fb258e998ef9b", "text_span_hash": "5f8fb258e998ef9b", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 52, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 22, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "cribed\nimproved executive function, cognitive speed and work-\ning memory immediately after a marathon run [19].\nConversely, some studies found no effect of marathon\ntraining and running on cognitive function in elderly\nmarathon runners (see Table 12)", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nincrease of TSH after exercise and intensity-dependent\nserum change of free T4 and free T3 [327].\nInfluence of Exercise on the Hypothalamic‑Pituitary–Gonadal\nThe impact of intense exercise on the hypothalamic-pitu-\nitary–gonadal axis is probably the most discussed topic\nregarding the endocrine system [329]. Studies showed\nthat in marathon runners, both the mean frequency of\nluteinizing hormone (LH) pulses as well as its amplitude\nwere reduced compared to controls (see Table 11) [313,\n330–332]. However, a marathon run immediately leads\nto an increase in follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH)\nlevels [331]. Regarding the effects of marathon training\nand running on testosterone levels, the literature is again\ndivided. Most articles describe reduced levels of testos-\nterone immediately after a marathon run (see Table 11)\n[18, 57, 312, 332]. Nonetheless, there is literature report-\ning increased levels of testosterone, especially in female\nathletes [313, 329–331, 333]. Despite this, said hormo-\nnal alterations, i.e., increased prolactin levels, alterations\nin gonadotropins, and reduced testosterone levels, have\nbeen linked to sexual dysfunction in male athletes as well\nas amenorrhea and other menstrual cycle abnormalities\nIn addition, hypothalamic dysfunction has been\ndescribed in the context of overtraining syndrome [61].\nA study investigating hormonal responses in overtrained\nathlet", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "cribed\nimproved executive function, cognitive speed and work-\ning memory immediately after a marathon run [19].\nConversely, some studies found no effect of marathon\ntraining and running on cognitive function in elderly\nmarathon runners (see Table 12)"}
