o increase in BDNF after a marathon race [336]\nwhereas others found an immediate increase after the\nrace with consecutive decreases 72 h after the mara-\nthon race (see Table 12) [339].\nNeuromuscular Modifications\nNot only does cognitive function appear to be posi-\ntively influenced, but regu", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "is accentuated by increased exercise\nloads and that runners are aware of the possibility of\nbeing harmed by a deficient inner self-criticism [343].\nAnother common problem associated with aging is\nmuscle weakness and motor impairments due to reduced"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0054_c0016", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b6ccfbc4132b1519", "text_span_hash": "b6ccfbc4132b1519", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 54, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 16, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "or impairments due to reduced\nmuscle mass, decreased strength and increased reflex\nlatencies of lower limb muscles which predispose to an\nincreased probability of falls [344]. A recent study inves-\ntigating elderly half-marathon runners found signifi", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nslow down the progression of Alzheimer’s disease [336].\nFurthermore, beneficial effects on brain white matter\nhave been shown using MRI measurements [335]. A dif-\nferent study performing MRI evaluations in participants\nof a 38.6- and a 119.8-km mountain race demonstrated\nsignificant increases in cortical grey matter and total\ngrey matter volume in both groups whereas the volume\nof the thalamus, caudate, pallidus and hippocampus sig-\nnificantly increased only in the ultra-marathon groups\n(see Table 12) [338]. These results suggest that certain\nbrain regions are more sensitive to exercise [338]. As\nbasal ganglia play an essential role in cognitive function,\nendurance exercise might, therefore, improve cognitive\nprocesses due to increased grey matter volumes in these\nRecently, the positive effects of exercise on the brain\nhave been linked to brain-derived neurotrophic factor\n(BDNF), which is involved in neural plasticity, neu-\nronal growth, synaptogenesis and neuronal survival\n[339]. Regular physical exercise was shown to increase\nthe release of BDNF [339]. Nevertheless, some studies\nfound no increase in BDNF after a marathon race [336]\nwhereas others found an immediate increase after the\nrace with consecutive decreases 72 h after the mara-\nthon race (see Table 12) [339].\nNeuromuscular Modifications\nNot only does cognitive function appear to be posi-\ntively influenced, but regu", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "or impairments due to reduced\nmuscle mass, decreased strength and increased reflex\nlatencies of lower limb muscles which predispose to an\nincreased probability of falls [344]. A recent study inves-\ntigating elderly half-marathon runners found signifi"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0054_c0017", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "58359190a71e68de", "text_span_hash": "58359190a71e68de", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 54, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 17, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "marathon runners found significant\ncentral and peripheral modifications, such as an increase\nin the initial and average discharge rate of motor units,\nthus ameliorating the decline of muscle function in\nelderly individuals [344].\nLong-distance partic", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nslow down the progression of Alzheimer’s disease [336].\nFurthermore, beneficial effects on brain white matter\nhave been shown using MRI measurements [335]. A dif-\nferent study performing MRI evaluations in participants\nof a 38.6- and a 119.8-km mountain race demonstrated\nsignificant increases in cortical grey matter and total\ngrey matter volume in both groups whereas the volume\nof the thalamus, caudate, pallidus and hippocampus sig-\nnificantly increased only in the ultra-marathon groups\n(see Table 12) [338]. These results suggest that certain\nbrain regions are more sensitive to exercise [338]. As\nbasal ganglia play an essential role in cognitive function,\nendurance exercise might, therefore, improve cognitive\nprocesses due to increased grey matter volumes in these\nRecently, the positive effects of exercise on the brain\nhave been linked to brain-derived neurotrophic factor\n(BDNF), which is involved in neural plasticity, neu-\nronal growth, synaptogenesis and neuronal survival\n[339]. Regular physical exercise was shown to increase\nthe release of BDNF [339]. Nevertheless, some studies\nfound no increase in BDNF after a marathon race [336]\nwhereas others found an immediate increase after the\nrace with consecutive decreases 72 h after the mara-\nthon race (see Table 12) [339].\nNeuromuscular Modifications\nNot only does cognitive function appear to be posi-\ntively influenced, but regu", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "marathon runners found significant\ncentral and peripheral modifications, such as an increase\nin the initial and average discharge rate of motor units,\nthus ameliorating the decline of muscle function in\nelderly individuals [344].\nLong-distance partic"}
