d no increase in BDNF after a marathon race [336]\nwhereas others found an immediate increase after the\nrace with consecutive decreases 72 h after the mara-\nthon race (see Table 12) [339].\nNeuromuscular Modifications\nNot only does cognitive function appear to be posi-\ntively influenced, but regu", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ls [344].\nLong-distance participants will unavoidably be con-\nfronted with a certain amount of pain during a race.\nMarathon runners have a proven higher pain threshold\nand tolerance compared to the general population [8].\nStudies showed a temporary e"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0054_c0019", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8749db11fa4e05b6", "text_span_hash": "8749db11fa4e05b6", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 54, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 19, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": ".\nStudies showed a temporary exercise-induced hypoalge-\nsia after a marathon run [345]. This state of reduced pain\nsensation lasted up until one hour after the race, before\nthe pain threshold returned to pre-race values [345]. Dis-\ncussed mechanisms", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nslow down the progression of Alzheimer’s disease [336].\nFurthermore, beneficial effects on brain white matter\nhave been shown using MRI measurements [335]. A dif-\nferent study performing MRI evaluations in participants\nof a 38.6- and a 119.8-km mountain race demonstrated\nsignificant increases in cortical grey matter and total\ngrey matter volume in both groups whereas the volume\nof the thalamus, caudate, pallidus and hippocampus sig-\nnificantly increased only in the ultra-marathon groups\n(see Table 12) [338]. These results suggest that certain\nbrain regions are more sensitive to exercise [338]. As\nbasal ganglia play an essential role in cognitive function,\nendurance exercise might, therefore, improve cognitive\nprocesses due to increased grey matter volumes in these\nRecently, the positive effects of exercise on the brain\nhave been linked to brain-derived neurotrophic factor\n(BDNF), which is involved in neural plasticity, neu-\nronal growth, synaptogenesis and neuronal survival\n[339]. Regular physical exercise was shown to increase\nthe release of BDNF [339]. Nevertheless, some studies\nfound no increase in BDNF after a marathon race [336]\nwhereas others found an immediate increase after the\nrace with consecutive decreases 72 h after the mara-\nthon race (see Table 12) [339].\nNeuromuscular Modifications\nNot only does cognitive function appear to be posi-\ntively influenced, but regu", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": ".\nStudies showed a temporary exercise-induced hypoalge-\nsia after a marathon run [345]. This state of reduced pain\nsensation lasted up until one hour after the race, before\nthe pain threshold returned to pre-race values [345]. Dis-\ncussed mechanisms"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0055_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9d937e04807ae93b", "text_span_hash": "9d937e04807ae93b", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 55, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nNeuronal Damage and Alteration in Function after Intense\nAlthough moderate physical activity was shown to have\na beneficial impact on cognitive function, extreme bouts\nof acute exercise, such as a", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nNeuronal Damage and Alteration in Function after Intense\nAlthough moderate physical activity was shown to have\na beneficial impact on cognitive function, extreme bouts\nof acute exercise, such as a marathon, seem to decrease\ncognitive performance during the race [19, 335]. A\nrecent study investigating cognitive function directly\nafter a marathon race found a slight impairment in\nlow-demand working memory as well as reduced reac-\ntion times and accuracy [19]. In elderly marathon run-\nners, a small impairment of long-term verbal memory\nwas found probably in the context of exercise-induced\nhypothalamic-pituitary-adrenocortical\n[348]. Another study found impaired explicit memory\nbut improved implicit memory immediately after the\nmarathon race [349]. Thus, results regarding cognitive\nfunction after long-distance endurance events such as\nmarathon or ultra-marathon races are still inconclusive,\nas some studies found improvements, others found dete-\nrioration, and still others found no changes [350]. Stud-\nies assessing changes in electroencephalogram (EEG)\nand visually evoked potentials following a marathon\nrace found no evidence for central nervous system dys-\nfunction [351], whereas during marathon training, EEG\nstudies showed a decrease in theta activity (4–6 Hz) and\nincrease of the slow alpha component and subtheta activ-\nity (6–8 Hz) [335]. A recent MRI study investigating mar-", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nNeuronal Damage and Alteration in Function after Intense\nAlthough moderate physical activity was shown to have\na beneficial impact on cognitive function, extreme bouts\nof acute exercise, such as a"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0055_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "41d8a2cabe5ec8de", "text_span_hash": "41d8a2cabe5ec8de", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 55, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "of acute exercise, such as a marathon, seem to decrease\ncognitive performance during the race [19, 335]. A\nrecent study investigating cognitive function directly\nafter a marathon race found a slight impairment in\nlow-demand working memory as well as", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nNeuronal Damage and Alteration in Function after Intense\nAlthough moderate physical activity was shown to have\na beneficial impact on cognitive function, extreme bouts\nof acute exercise, such as a marathon, seem to decrease\ncognitive performance during the race [19, 335]. A\nrecent study investigating cognitive function directly\nafter a marathon race found a slight impairment in\nlow-demand working memory as well as reduced reac-\ntion times and accuracy [19]. In elderly marathon run-\nners, a small impairment of long-term verbal memory\nwas found probably in the context of exercise-induced\nhypothalamic-pituitary-adrenocortical\n[348]. Another study found impaired explicit memory\nbut improved implicit memory immediately after the\nmarathon race [349]. Thus, results regarding cognitive\nfunction after long-distance endurance events such as\nmarathon or ultra-marathon races are still inconclusive,\nas some studies found improvements, others found dete-\nrioration, and still others found no changes [350]. Stud-\nies assessing changes in electroencephalogram (EEG)\nand visually evoked potentials following a marathon\nrace found no evidence for central nervous system dys-\nfunction [351], whereas during marathon training, EEG\nstudies showed a decrease in theta activity (4–6 Hz) and\nincrease of the slow alpha component and subtheta activ-\nity (6–8 Hz) [335]. A recent MRI study investigating mar-", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "of acute exercise, such as a marathon, seem to decrease\ncognitive performance during the race [19, 335]. A\nrecent study investigating cognitive function directly\nafter a marathon race found a slight impairment in\nlow-demand working memory as well as"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0055_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "18d9dc190185f258", "text_span_hash": "18d9dc190185f258", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 55, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "mand working memory as well as reduced reac-\ntion times and accuracy [19]. In elderly marathon run-\nners, a small impairment of long-term verbal memory\nwas found probably in the context of exercise-induced\nhypothalamic-pituitary-adrenocortical\n[348].", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nNeuronal Damage and Alteration in Function after Intense\nAlthough moderate physical activity was shown to have\na beneficial impact on cognitive function, extreme bouts\nof acute exercise, such as a marathon, seem to decrease\ncognitive performance during the race [19, 335]. A\nrecent study investigating cognitive function directly\nafter a marathon race found a slight impairment in\nlow-demand working memory as well as reduced reac-\ntion times and accuracy [19]. In elderly marathon run-\nners, a small impairment of long-term verbal memory\nwas found probably in the context of exercise-induced\nhypothalamic-pituitary-adrenocortical\n[348]. Another study found impaired explicit memory\nbut improved implicit memory immediately after the\nmarathon race [349]. Thus, results regarding cognitive\nfunction after long-distance endurance events such as\nmarathon or ultra-marathon races are still inconclusive,\nas some studies found improvements, others found dete-\nrioration, and still others found no changes [350]. Stud-\nies assessing changes in electroencephalogram (EEG)\nand visually evoked potentials following a marathon\nrace found no evidence for central nervous system dys-\nfunction [351], whereas during marathon training, EEG\nstudies showed a decrease in theta activity (4–6 Hz) and\nincrease of the slow alpha component and subtheta activ-\nity (6–8 Hz) [335]. A recent MRI study investigating mar-", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "mand working memory as well as reduced reac-\ntion times and accuracy [19]. In elderly marathon run-\nners, a small impairment of long-term verbal memory\nwas found probably in the context of exercise-induced\nhypothalamic-pituitary-adrenocortical\n[348]."}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0055_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0abedd70c920b30c", "text_span_hash": "0abedd70c920b30c", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 55, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ituitary-adrenocortical\n[348]. Another study found impaired explicit memory\nbut improved implicit memory immediately after the\nmarathon race [349]. Thus, results regarding cognitive\nfunction after long-distance endurance events such as\nmarathon or ul", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nNeuronal Damage and Alteration in Function after Intense\nAlthough moderate physical activity was shown to have\na beneficial impact on cognitive function, extreme bouts\nof acute exercise, such as a marathon, seem to decrease\ncognitive performance during the race [19, 335]. A\nrecent study investigating cognitive function directly\nafter a marathon race found a slight impairment in\nlow-demand working memory as well as reduced reac-\ntion times and accuracy [19]. In elderly marathon run-\nners, a small impairment of long-term verbal memory\nwas found probably in the context of exercise-induced\nhypothalamic-pituitary-adrenocortical\n[348]. Another study found impaired explicit memory\nbut improved implicit memory immediately after the\nmarathon race [349]. Thus, results regarding cognitive\nfunction after long-distance endurance events such as\nmarathon or ultra-marathon races are still inconclusive,\nas some studies found improvements, others found dete-\nrioration, and still others found no changes [350]. Stud-\nies assessing changes in electroencephalogram (EEG)\nand visually evoked potentials following a marathon\nrace found no evidence for central nervous system dys-\nfunction [351], whereas during marathon training, EEG\nstudies showed a decrease in theta activity (4–6 Hz) and\nincrease of the slow alpha component and subtheta activ-\nity (6–8 Hz) [335]. A recent MRI study investigating mar-", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ituitary-adrenocortical\n[348]. Another study found impaired explicit memory\nbut improved implicit memory immediately after the\nmarathon race [349]. Thus, results regarding cognitive\nfunction after long-distance endurance events such as\nmarathon or ul"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0055_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2acf2c2df4f60381", "text_span_hash": "2acf2c2df4f60381", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 55, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "events such as\nmarathon or ultra-marathon races are still inconclusive,\nas some studies found improvements, others found dete-\nrioration, and still others found no changes [350]. Stud-\nies assessing changes in electroencephalogram (EEG)\nand visually", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nNeuronal Damage and Alteration in Function after Intense\nAlthough moderate physical activity was shown to have\na beneficial impact on cognitive function, extreme bouts\nof acute exercise, such as a marathon, seem to decrease\ncognitive performance during the race [19, 335]. A\nrecent study investigating cognitive function directly\nafter a marathon race found a slight impairment in\nlow-demand working memory as well as reduced reac-\ntion times and accuracy [19]. In elderly marathon run-\nners, a small impairment of long-term verbal memory\nwas found probably in the context of exercise-induced\nhypothalamic-pituitary-adrenocortical\n[348]. Another study found impaired explicit memory\nbut improved implicit memory immediately after the\nmarathon race [349]. Thus, results regarding cognitive\nfunction after long-distance endurance events such as\nmarathon or ultra-marathon races are still inconclusive,\nas some studies found improvements, others found dete-\nrioration, and still others found no changes [350]. Stud-\nies assessing changes in electroencephalogram (EEG)\nand visually evoked potentials following a marathon\nrace found no evidence for central nervous system dys-\nfunction [351], whereas during marathon training, EEG\nstudies showed a decrease in theta activity (4–6 Hz) and\nincrease of the slow alpha component and subtheta activ-\nity (6–8 Hz) [335]. A recent MRI study investigating mar-", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "events such as\nmarathon or ultra-marathon races are still inconclusive,\nas some studies found improvements, others found dete-\nrioration, and still others found no changes [350]. Stud-\nies assessing changes in electroencephalogram (EEG)\nand visually"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0055_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "03754d444d252fd6", "text_span_hash": "03754d444d252fd6", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 55, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "cephalogram (EEG)\nand visually evoked potentials following a marathon\nrace found no evidence for central nervous system dys-\nfunction [351], whereas during marathon training, EEG\nstudies showed a decrease in theta activity (4–6 Hz) and\nincrease of th", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nNeuronal Damage and Alteration in Function after Intense\nAlthough moderate physical activity was shown to have\na beneficial impact on cognitive function, extreme bouts\nof acute exercise, such as a marathon, seem to decrease\ncognitive performance during the race [19, 335]. A\nrecent study investigating cognitive function directly\nafter a marathon race found a slight impairment in\nlow-demand working memory as well as reduced reac-\ntion times and accuracy [19]. In elderly marathon run-\nners, a small impairment of long-term verbal memory\nwas found probably in the context of exercise-induced\nhypothalamic-pituitary-adrenocortical\n[348]. Another study found impaired explicit memory\nbut improved implicit memory immediately after the\nmarathon race [349]. Thus, results regarding cognitive\nfunction after long-distance endurance events such as\nmarathon or ultra-marathon races are still inconclusive,\nas some studies found improvements, others found dete-\nrioration, and still others found no changes [350]. Stud-\nies assessing changes in electroencephalogram (EEG)\nand visually evoked potentials following a marathon\nrace found no evidence for central nervous system dys-\nfunction [351], whereas during marathon training, EEG\nstudies showed a decrease in theta activity (4–6 Hz) and\nincrease of the slow alpha component and subtheta activ-\nity (6–8 Hz) [335]. A recent MRI study investigating mar-", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "cephalogram (EEG)\nand visually evoked potentials following a marathon\nrace found no evidence for central nervous system dys-\nfunction [351], whereas during marathon training, EEG\nstudies showed a decrease in theta activity (4–6 Hz) and\nincrease of th"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0055_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7b38b91a26a62013", "text_span_hash": "7b38b91a26a62013", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 55, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "p Disorders and Changes in Autonomic Nervous System\nLiterature about marathon and sleep, as well as the influ-\nence of sleep pattern on marathon performance, is very\nlimited [56]. An older study found pronounced sleep\ndisturbance after a marathon run", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nNeuronal Damage and Alteration in Function after Intense\nAlthough moderate physical activity was shown to have\na beneficial impact on cognitive function, extreme bouts\nof acute exercise, such as a marathon, seem to decrease\ncognitive performance during the race [19, 335]. A\nrecent study investigating cognitive function directly\nafter a marathon race found a slight impairment in\nlow-demand working memory as well as reduced reac-\ntion times and accuracy [19]. In elderly marathon run-\nners, a small impairment of long-term verbal memory\nwas found probably in the context of exercise-induced\nhypothalamic-pituitary-adrenocortical\n[348]. Another study found impaired explicit memory\nbut improved implicit memory immediately after the\nmarathon race [349]. Thus, results regarding cognitive\nfunction after long-distance endurance events such as\nmarathon or ultra-marathon races are still inconclusive,\nas some studies found improvements, others found dete-\nrioration, and still others found no changes [350]. Stud-\nies assessing changes in electroencephalogram (EEG)\nand visually evoked potentials following a marathon\nrace found no evidence for central nervous system dys-\nfunction [351], whereas during marathon training, EEG\nstudies showed a decrease in theta activity (4–6 Hz) and\nincrease of the slow alpha component and subtheta activ-\nity (6–8 Hz) [335]. A recent MRI study investigating mar-", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "p Disorders and Changes in Autonomic Nervous System\nLiterature about marathon and sleep, as well as the influ-\nence of sleep pattern on marathon performance, is very\nlimited [56]. An older study found pronounced sleep\ndisturbance after a marathon run"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0055_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "bb2763f7ddec0bf3", "text_span_hash": "bb2763f7ddec0bf3", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 55, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "sturbance after a marathon run [352]. REM sleep was\ninhibited, and total sleep time was decreased immedi-\nately following the marathon, whereas marathon training\ndid not have any impact on sleep characteristics [352].\nA recent review about sleep in m", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nNeuronal Damage and Alteration in Function after Intense\nAlthough moderate physical activity was shown to have\na beneficial impact on cognitive function, extreme bouts\nof acute exercise, such as a marathon, seem to decrease\ncognitive performance during the race [19, 335]. A\nrecent study investigating cognitive function directly\nafter a marathon race found a slight impairment in\nlow-demand working memory as well as reduced reac-\ntion times and accuracy [19]. In elderly marathon run-\nners, a small impairment of long-term verbal memory\nwas found probably in the context of exercise-induced\nhypothalamic-pituitary-adrenocortical\n[348]. Another study found impaired explicit memory\nbut improved implicit memory immediately after the\nmarathon race [349]. Thus, results regarding cognitive\nfunction after long-distance endurance events such as\nmarathon or ultra-marathon races are still inconclusive,\nas some studies found improvements, others found dete-\nrioration, and still others found no changes [350]. Stud-\nies assessing changes in electroencephalogram (EEG)\nand visually evoked potentials following a marathon\nrace found no evidence for central nervous system dys-\nfunction [351], whereas during marathon training, EEG\nstudies showed a decrease in theta activity (4–6 Hz) and\nincrease of the slow alpha component and subtheta activ-\nity (6–8 Hz) [335]. A recent MRI study investigating mar-", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "sturbance after a marathon run [352]. REM sleep was\ninhibited, and total sleep time was decreased immedi-\nately following the marathon, whereas marathon training\ndid not have any impact on sleep characteristics [352].\nA recent review about sleep in m"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0055_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2a628e9f2ffa1f11", "text_span_hash": "2a628e9f2ffa1f11", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 55, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "recent review about sleep in marathon runners found\nelite athletes to have poor quality and quantity of sleep\ndue to training times, competition, stress, and anxiety,\namong other things [56]. An adequate amount of sleep is\nimportant for muscle recove", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nNeuronal Damage and Alteration in Function after Intense\nAlthough moderate physical activity was shown to have\na beneficial impact on cognitive function, extreme bouts\nof acute exercise, such as a marathon, seem to decrease\ncognitive performance during the race [19, 335]. A\nrecent study investigating cognitive function directly\nafter a marathon race found a slight impairment in\nlow-demand working memory as well as reduced reac-\ntion times and accuracy [19]. In elderly marathon run-\nners, a small impairment of long-term verbal memory\nwas found probably in the context of exercise-induced\nhypothalamic-pituitary-adrenocortical\n[348]. Another study found impaired explicit memory\nbut improved implicit memory immediately after the\nmarathon race [349]. Thus, results regarding cognitive\nfunction after long-distance endurance events such as\nmarathon or ultra-marathon races are still inconclusive,\nas some studies found improvements, others found dete-\nrioration, and still others found no changes [350]. Stud-\nies assessing changes in electroencephalogram (EEG)\nand visually evoked potentials following a marathon\nrace found no evidence for central nervous system dys-\nfunction [351], whereas during marathon training, EEG\nstudies showed a decrease in theta activity (4–6 Hz) and\nincrease of the slow alpha component and subtheta activ-\nity (6–8 Hz) [335]. A recent MRI study investigating mar-", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "recent review about sleep in marathon runners found\nelite athletes to have poor quality and quantity of sleep\ndue to training times, competition, stress, and anxiety,\namong other things [56]. An adequate amount of sleep is\nimportant for muscle recove"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0055_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f8b7b3f2dd8fcc78", "text_span_hash": "f8b7b3f2dd8fcc78", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 55, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "is\nimportant for muscle recovery during training and may\nreduce infections or running-associated injuries [56].\nSome authors attributed the sleep disruption after the\nmarathon race to stress highlighted by elevated cortisol\nlevels [56] and shifted ba", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nNeuronal Damage and Alteration in Function after Intense\nAlthough moderate physical activity was shown to have\na beneficial impact on cognitive function, extreme bouts\nof acute exercise, such as a marathon, seem to decrease\ncognitive performance during the race [19, 335]. A\nrecent study investigating cognitive function directly\nafter a marathon race found a slight impairment in\nlow-demand working memory as well as reduced reac-\ntion times and accuracy [19]. In elderly marathon run-\nners, a small impairment of long-term verbal memory\nwas found probably in the context of exercise-induced\nhypothalamic-pituitary-adrenocortical\n[348]. Another study found impaired explicit memory\nbut improved implicit memory immediately after the\nmarathon race [349]. Thus, results regarding cognitive\nfunction after long-distance endurance events such as\nmarathon or ultra-marathon races are still inconclusive,\nas some studies found improvements, others found dete-\nrioration, and still others found no changes [350]. Stud-\nies assessing changes in electroencephalogram (EEG)\nand visually evoked potentials following a marathon\nrace found no evidence for central nervous system dys-\nfunction [351], whereas during marathon training, EEG\nstudies showed a decrease in theta activity (4–6 Hz) and\nincrease of the slow alpha component and subtheta activ-\nity (6–8 Hz) [335]. A recent MRI study investigating mar-", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "is\nimportant for muscle recovery during training and may\nreduce infections or running-associated injuries [56].\nSome authors attributed the sleep disruption after the\nmarathon race to stress highlighted by elevated cortisol\nlevels [56] and shifted ba"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0055_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7149e213f6a406f5", "text_span_hash": "7149e213f6a406f5", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 55, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "sol\nlevels [56] and shifted balance of the ANS from a rather\nvagal to a sympathetic predominance [353].\nThis shift in ANS balance might be related to ortho-\nstatic intolerance, which is commonly observed after\nendurance exercise [354]. Interestingly,", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nNeuronal Damage and Alteration in Function after Intense\nAlthough moderate physical activity was shown to have\na beneficial impact on cognitive function, extreme bouts\nof acute exercise, such as a marathon, seem to decrease\ncognitive performance during the race [19, 335]. A\nrecent study investigating cognitive function directly\nafter a marathon race found a slight impairment in\nlow-demand working memory as well as reduced reac-\ntion times and accuracy [19]. In elderly marathon run-\nners, a small impairment of long-term verbal memory\nwas found probably in the context of exercise-induced\nhypothalamic-pituitary-adrenocortical\n[348]. Another study found impaired explicit memory\nbut improved implicit memory immediately after the\nmarathon race [349]. Thus, results regarding cognitive\nfunction after long-distance endurance events such as\nmarathon or ultra-marathon races are still inconclusive,\nas some studies found improvements, others found dete-\nrioration, and still others found no changes [350]. Stud-\nies assessing changes in electroencephalogram (EEG)\nand visually evoked potentials following a marathon\nrace found no evidence for central nervous system dys-\nfunction [351], whereas during marathon training, EEG\nstudies showed a decrease in theta activity (4–6 Hz) and\nincrease of the slow alpha component and subtheta activ-\nity (6–8 Hz) [335]. A recent MRI study investigating mar-", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "sol\nlevels [56] and shifted balance of the ANS from a rather\nvagal to a sympathetic predominance [353].\nThis shift in ANS balance might be related to ortho-\nstatic intolerance, which is commonly observed after\nendurance exercise [354]. Interestingly,"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0055_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0b1ae4c0f6582043", "text_span_hash": "0b1ae4c0f6582043", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 55, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "exercise [354]. Interestingly, in a study com-\nparing marathon finishers with orthostatic intolerance to\na complaint-free group, only the non-orthostatic intoler-\nance group showed a rise in total peripheral resistance\nafter active standing [354]. In", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nNeuronal Damage and Alteration in Function after Intense\nAlthough moderate physical activity was shown to have\na beneficial impact on cognitive function, extreme bouts\nof acute exercise, such as a marathon, seem to decrease\ncognitive performance during the race [19, 335]. A\nrecent study investigating cognitive function directly\nafter a marathon race found a slight impairment in\nlow-demand working memory as well as reduced reac-\ntion times and accuracy [19]. In elderly marathon run-\nners, a small impairment of long-term verbal memory\nwas found probably in the context of exercise-induced\nhypothalamic-pituitary-adrenocortical\n[348]. Another study found impaired explicit memory\nbut improved implicit memory immediately after the\nmarathon race [349]. Thus, results regarding cognitive\nfunction after long-distance endurance events such as\nmarathon or ultra-marathon races are still inconclusive,\nas some studies found improvements, others found dete-\nrioration, and still others found no changes [350]. Stud-\nies assessing changes in electroencephalogram (EEG)\nand visually evoked potentials following a marathon\nrace found no evidence for central nervous system dys-\nfunction [351], whereas during marathon training, EEG\nstudies showed a decrease in theta activity (4–6 Hz) and\nincrease of the slow alpha component and subtheta activ-\nity (6–8 Hz) [335]. A recent MRI study investigating mar-", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "exercise [354]. Interestingly, in a study com-\nparing marathon finishers with orthostatic intolerance to\na complaint-free group, only the non-orthostatic intoler-\nance group showed a rise in total peripheral resistance\nafter active standing [354]. In"}
