ng a marathon\nrace found no evidence for central nervous system dys-\nfunction [351], whereas during marathon training, EEG\nstudies showed a decrease in theta activity (4–6 Hz) and\nincrease of the slow alpha component and subtheta activ-\nity (6–8 Hz) [335]. A recent MRI study investigating mar-", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "central nervous sys-\ntem following a marathon. As mentioned previously,\nEAH is a relatively common problem in marathon run-\nners (see Table 7). In rare cases, hyponatremia-induced\ncerebral edema has been reported, which often leads to\nintensive medi"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0055_c0019", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e629fed985d32ace", "text_span_hash": "e629fed985d32ace", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 55, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 19, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "often leads to\nintensive medical treatment for those affected and might\neven lead to death following brainstem herniation [141].\nOther authors reported strokes in two young men par-\nticipating in a marathon [355], a basilar artery stroke in a\n27-yea", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nNeuronal Damage and Alteration in Function after Intense\nAlthough moderate physical activity was shown to have\na beneficial impact on cognitive function, extreme bouts\nof acute exercise, such as a marathon, seem to decrease\ncognitive performance during the race [19, 335]. A\nrecent study investigating cognitive function directly\nafter a marathon race found a slight impairment in\nlow-demand working memory as well as reduced reac-\ntion times and accuracy [19]. In elderly marathon run-\nners, a small impairment of long-term verbal memory\nwas found probably in the context of exercise-induced\nhypothalamic-pituitary-adrenocortical\n[348]. Another study found impaired explicit memory\nbut improved implicit memory immediately after the\nmarathon race [349]. Thus, results regarding cognitive\nfunction after long-distance endurance events such as\nmarathon or ultra-marathon races are still inconclusive,\nas some studies found improvements, others found dete-\nrioration, and still others found no changes [350]. Stud-\nies assessing changes in electroencephalogram (EEG)\nand visually evoked potentials following a marathon\nrace found no evidence for central nervous system dys-\nfunction [351], whereas during marathon training, EEG\nstudies showed a decrease in theta activity (4–6 Hz) and\nincrease of the slow alpha component and subtheta activ-\nity (6–8 Hz) [335]. A recent MRI study investigating mar-", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "often leads to\nintensive medical treatment for those affected and might\neven lead to death following brainstem herniation [141].\nOther authors reported strokes in two young men par-\nticipating in a marathon [355], a basilar artery stroke in a\n27-yea"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0055_c0020", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "184237b72714ea2d", "text_span_hash": "184237b72714ea2d", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 55, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 20, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ilar artery stroke in a\n27-year-old female marathon runner [356] and a left mid-\ndle cerebral artery stroke in a 14-year-old runner [357].\nFortunately, these cases are rare, but nevertheless wor-\nrying. No person suffered any permanent damage [356,\n3", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nNeuronal Damage and Alteration in Function after Intense\nAlthough moderate physical activity was shown to have\na beneficial impact on cognitive function, extreme bouts\nof acute exercise, such as a marathon, seem to decrease\ncognitive performance during the race [19, 335]. A\nrecent study investigating cognitive function directly\nafter a marathon race found a slight impairment in\nlow-demand working memory as well as reduced reac-\ntion times and accuracy [19]. In elderly marathon run-\nners, a small impairment of long-term verbal memory\nwas found probably in the context of exercise-induced\nhypothalamic-pituitary-adrenocortical\n[348]. Another study found impaired explicit memory\nbut improved implicit memory immediately after the\nmarathon race [349]. Thus, results regarding cognitive\nfunction after long-distance endurance events such as\nmarathon or ultra-marathon races are still inconclusive,\nas some studies found improvements, others found dete-\nrioration, and still others found no changes [350]. Stud-\nies assessing changes in electroencephalogram (EEG)\nand visually evoked potentials following a marathon\nrace found no evidence for central nervous system dys-\nfunction [351], whereas during marathon training, EEG\nstudies showed a decrease in theta activity (4–6 Hz) and\nincrease of the slow alpha component and subtheta activ-\nity (6–8 Hz) [335]. A recent MRI study investigating mar-", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ilar artery stroke in a\n27-year-old female marathon runner [356] and a left mid-\ndle cerebral artery stroke in a 14-year-old runner [357].\nFortunately, these cases are rare, but nevertheless wor-\nrying. No person suffered any permanent damage [356,\n3"}
{"chunk_id": "train_marathon_physiology_2025_p0055_c0023", "source_registry_id": "src_2ff90fb7fcc0", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "825b3eba5c64b7e5", "text_span_hash": "825b3eba5c64b7e5", "source_file": "train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_marathon_physiology_2025.pdf", "page": 55, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 23, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ed an association between herniated\nlumbar disk and marathon running and intensive training\n[359]. However, recent studies evaluating lumbar spine\nhealth using MRI found no association with running and\nincreased risk for disk herniation (see Table 5)", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Braschler et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2025) 11:10\nNeuronal Damage and Alteration in Function after Intense\nAlthough moderate physical activity was shown to have\na beneficial impact on cognitive function, extreme bouts\nof acute exercise, such as a marathon, seem to decrease\ncognitive performance during the race [19, 335]. A\nrecent study investigating cognitive function directly\nafter a marathon race found a slight impairment in\nlow-demand working memory as well as reduced reac-\ntion times and accuracy [19]. In elderly marathon run-\nners, a small impairment of long-term verbal memory\nwas found probably in the context of exercise-induced\nhypothalamic-pituitary-adrenocortical\n[348]. Another study found impaired explicit memory\nbut improved implicit memory immediately after the\nmarathon race [349]. Thus, results regarding cognitive\nfunction after long-distance endurance events such as\nmarathon or ultra-marathon races are still inconclusive,\nas some studies found improvements, others found dete-\nrioration, and still others found no changes [350]. Stud-\nies assessing changes in electroencephalogram (EEG)\nand visually evoked potentials following a marathon\nrace found no evidence for central nervous system dys-\nfunction [351], whereas during marathon training, EEG\nstudies showed a decrease in theta activity (4–6 Hz) and\nincrease of the slow alpha component and subtheta activ-\nity (6–8 Hz) [335]. A recent MRI study investigating mar-", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ed an association between herniated\nlumbar disk and marathon running and intensive training\n[359]. However, recent studies evaluating lumbar spine\nhealth using MRI found no association with running and\nincreased risk for disk herniation (see Table 5)"}
