{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0001_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4e4b3e2dc09e2355", "text_span_hash": "4e4b3e2dc09e2355", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "nificant improvements were found\nbetween pre- and post-tapering in time–trial (TT) performance (SMD = −0.45; P < 0.05) and\ntime to exhaustion (TTE) performance (SMD = 1.28; P < 0.05). However, There were no\nimprovements in maximal oxygen consumption", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Effects of tapering on performance in\nendurance athletes: A systematic review and\nZhiqiang Wang1,2☯, Yong “Tai” Wang3☯, Weifeng Gao1☯, Yaping ZhongID1,2☯*\n1 Sports Big-data Research Center, Wuhan Sports University, Wuhan, HuBei, China, 2 Hubei Sports and\nHealth Research Center, Wuhan, HuBei, China, 3 Rochester Institute of Technology, College of Health\nSciences and Technology, New York, Rochester, United States of America\n☯These authors contributed equally to this work.\n* zhongyaping@whsu.edu.cn\nTo assess the responses to taper in endurance athletes using meta-analysis.\nSystematic searches were conducted in China National Knowledge Infrastructure, PubMed,\nWeb of Science, SPORTDiscus, and EMBASE databases. Standardized mean difference\n(SMD) and 95% confidence interval (CI) of outcome measures were calculated as effect\n14 studies were included in this meta-analysis. Significant improvements were found\nbetween pre- and post-tapering in time–trial (TT) performance (SMD = −0.45; P < 0.05) and\ntime to exhaustion (TTE) performance (SMD = 1.28; P < 0.05). However, There were no\nimprovements in maximal oxygen consumption ( _VO2max) and economy of movement (EM)\n(P > 0.05) between pre- and post-tapering. Further subgroup analysis showed that tapering\ncombined with pre-taper overload training had a more significant effect on TT performance\nthan conventional tapering (P < 0.05). A tapering strategy that reduced training volume by\n41–60%, m", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "nificant improvements were found\nbetween pre- and post-tapering in time–trial (TT) performance (SMD = −0.45; P < 0.05) and\ntime to exhaustion (TTE) performance (SMD = 1.28; P < 0.05). However, There were no\nimprovements in maximal oxygen consumption"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0001_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e5d3759d0cf2e59e", "text_span_hash": "e5d3759d0cf2e59e", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "in maximal oxygen consumption ( _VO2max) and economy of movement (EM)\n(P > 0.05) between pre- and post-tapering. Further subgroup analysis showed that tapering\ncombined with pre-taper overload training had a more significant effect on TT performance", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Effects of tapering on performance in\nendurance athletes: A systematic review and\nZhiqiang Wang1,2☯, Yong “Tai” Wang3☯, Weifeng Gao1☯, Yaping ZhongID1,2☯*\n1 Sports Big-data Research Center, Wuhan Sports University, Wuhan, HuBei, China, 2 Hubei Sports and\nHealth Research Center, Wuhan, HuBei, China, 3 Rochester Institute of Technology, College of Health\nSciences and Technology, New York, Rochester, United States of America\n☯These authors contributed equally to this work.\n* zhongyaping@whsu.edu.cn\nTo assess the responses to taper in endurance athletes using meta-analysis.\nSystematic searches were conducted in China National Knowledge Infrastructure, PubMed,\nWeb of Science, SPORTDiscus, and EMBASE databases. Standardized mean difference\n(SMD) and 95% confidence interval (CI) of outcome measures were calculated as effect\n14 studies were included in this meta-analysis. Significant improvements were found\nbetween pre- and post-tapering in time–trial (TT) performance (SMD = −0.45; P < 0.05) and\ntime to exhaustion (TTE) performance (SMD = 1.28; P < 0.05). However, There were no\nimprovements in maximal oxygen consumption ( _VO2max) and economy of movement (EM)\n(P > 0.05) between pre- and post-tapering. Further subgroup analysis showed that tapering\ncombined with pre-taper overload training had a more significant effect on TT performance\nthan conventional tapering (P < 0.05). A tapering strategy that reduced training volume by\n41–60%, m", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "in maximal oxygen consumption ( _VO2max) and economy of movement (EM)\n(P > 0.05) between pre- and post-tapering. Further subgroup analysis showed that tapering\ncombined with pre-taper overload training had a more significant effect on TT performance"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0001_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5568fbb1d66fd8a9", "text_span_hash": "5568fbb1d66fd8a9", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "cant effect on TT performance\nthan conventional tapering (P < 0.05). A tapering strategy that reduced training volume by\n41–60%, maintained training intensity and frequency, lasted \u00147 days, 8–14 days, or 15–21\ndays, used a progressive or step taper c", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Effects of tapering on performance in\nendurance athletes: A systematic review and\nZhiqiang Wang1,2☯, Yong “Tai” Wang3☯, Weifeng Gao1☯, Yaping ZhongID1,2☯*\n1 Sports Big-data Research Center, Wuhan Sports University, Wuhan, HuBei, China, 2 Hubei Sports and\nHealth Research Center, Wuhan, HuBei, China, 3 Rochester Institute of Technology, College of Health\nSciences and Technology, New York, Rochester, United States of America\n☯These authors contributed equally to this work.\n* zhongyaping@whsu.edu.cn\nTo assess the responses to taper in endurance athletes using meta-analysis.\nSystematic searches were conducted in China National Knowledge Infrastructure, PubMed,\nWeb of Science, SPORTDiscus, and EMBASE databases. Standardized mean difference\n(SMD) and 95% confidence interval (CI) of outcome measures were calculated as effect\n14 studies were included in this meta-analysis. Significant improvements were found\nbetween pre- and post-tapering in time–trial (TT) performance (SMD = −0.45; P < 0.05) and\ntime to exhaustion (TTE) performance (SMD = 1.28; P < 0.05). However, There were no\nimprovements in maximal oxygen consumption ( _VO2max) and economy of movement (EM)\n(P > 0.05) between pre- and post-tapering. Further subgroup analysis showed that tapering\ncombined with pre-taper overload training had a more significant effect on TT performance\nthan conventional tapering (P < 0.05). A tapering strategy that reduced training volume by\n41–60%, m", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "cant effect on TT performance\nthan conventional tapering (P < 0.05). A tapering strategy that reduced training volume by\n41–60%, maintained training intensity and frequency, lasted \u00147 days, 8–14 days, or 15–21\ndays, used a progressive or step taper c"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0001_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "a3ca76543ae95dcf", "text_span_hash": "a3ca76543ae95dcf", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "a progressive or step taper could significantly improve TT performance (P <\nThe tapering applied in conjunction with pre-taper overload training seems to be more con-\nducive to maximize performance gains. Current evidence suggests that a \u001421-day tap", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Effects of tapering on performance in\nendurance athletes: A systematic review and\nZhiqiang Wang1,2☯, Yong “Tai” Wang3☯, Weifeng Gao1☯, Yaping ZhongID1,2☯*\n1 Sports Big-data Research Center, Wuhan Sports University, Wuhan, HuBei, China, 2 Hubei Sports and\nHealth Research Center, Wuhan, HuBei, China, 3 Rochester Institute of Technology, College of Health\nSciences and Technology, New York, Rochester, United States of America\n☯These authors contributed equally to this work.\n* zhongyaping@whsu.edu.cn\nTo assess the responses to taper in endurance athletes using meta-analysis.\nSystematic searches were conducted in China National Knowledge Infrastructure, PubMed,\nWeb of Science, SPORTDiscus, and EMBASE databases. Standardized mean difference\n(SMD) and 95% confidence interval (CI) of outcome measures were calculated as effect\n14 studies were included in this meta-analysis. Significant improvements were found\nbetween pre- and post-tapering in time–trial (TT) performance (SMD = −0.45; P < 0.05) and\ntime to exhaustion (TTE) performance (SMD = 1.28; P < 0.05). However, There were no\nimprovements in maximal oxygen consumption ( _VO2max) and economy of movement (EM)\n(P > 0.05) between pre- and post-tapering. Further subgroup analysis showed that tapering\ncombined with pre-taper overload training had a more significant effect on TT performance\nthan conventional tapering (P < 0.05). A tapering strategy that reduced training volume by\n41–60%, m", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "a progressive or step taper could significantly improve TT performance (P <\nThe tapering applied in conjunction with pre-taper overload training seems to be more con-\nducive to maximize performance gains. Current evidence suggests that a \u001421-day tap"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0001_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "fa125ef658b62e8d", "text_span_hash": "fa125ef658b62e8d", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ce suggests that a \u001421-day taper, in\nwhich training volume is progressively reduced by 41–60% without changing training inten-\nsity or frequency, is an effective tapering strategy.\nCitation: Wang Z, Wang Y“, Gao W, Zhong Y\n(2023) Effects of tapering", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Effects of tapering on performance in\nendurance athletes: A systematic review and\nZhiqiang Wang1,2☯, Yong “Tai” Wang3☯, Weifeng Gao1☯, Yaping ZhongID1,2☯*\n1 Sports Big-data Research Center, Wuhan Sports University, Wuhan, HuBei, China, 2 Hubei Sports and\nHealth Research Center, Wuhan, HuBei, China, 3 Rochester Institute of Technology, College of Health\nSciences and Technology, New York, Rochester, United States of America\n☯These authors contributed equally to this work.\n* zhongyaping@whsu.edu.cn\nTo assess the responses to taper in endurance athletes using meta-analysis.\nSystematic searches were conducted in China National Knowledge Infrastructure, PubMed,\nWeb of Science, SPORTDiscus, and EMBASE databases. Standardized mean difference\n(SMD) and 95% confidence interval (CI) of outcome measures were calculated as effect\n14 studies were included in this meta-analysis. Significant improvements were found\nbetween pre- and post-tapering in time–trial (TT) performance (SMD = −0.45; P < 0.05) and\ntime to exhaustion (TTE) performance (SMD = 1.28; P < 0.05). However, There were no\nimprovements in maximal oxygen consumption ( _VO2max) and economy of movement (EM)\n(P > 0.05) between pre- and post-tapering. Further subgroup analysis showed that tapering\ncombined with pre-taper overload training had a more significant effect on TT performance\nthan conventional tapering (P < 0.05). A tapering strategy that reduced training volume by\n41–60%, m", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ce suggests that a \u001421-day taper, in\nwhich training volume is progressively reduced by 41–60% without changing training inten-\nsity or frequency, is an effective tapering strategy.\nCitation: Wang Z, Wang Y“, Gao W, Zhong Y\n(2023) Effects of tapering"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0001_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4e2f4981bf58b90c", "text_span_hash": "4e2f4981bf58b90c", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Y\n(2023) Effects of tapering on performance in\nendurance athletes: A systematic review and meta-\nanalysis. PLoS ONE 18(5): e0282838. https://doi.\nEditor: Laurent Mourot, University of Bourgogne\nFrance Comte´, FRANCE\nCopyright: © 2023 Wang et al. Thi", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Effects of tapering on performance in\nendurance athletes: A systematic review and\nZhiqiang Wang1,2☯, Yong “Tai” Wang3☯, Weifeng Gao1☯, Yaping ZhongID1,2☯*\n1 Sports Big-data Research Center, Wuhan Sports University, Wuhan, HuBei, China, 2 Hubei Sports and\nHealth Research Center, Wuhan, HuBei, China, 3 Rochester Institute of Technology, College of Health\nSciences and Technology, New York, Rochester, United States of America\n☯These authors contributed equally to this work.\n* zhongyaping@whsu.edu.cn\nTo assess the responses to taper in endurance athletes using meta-analysis.\nSystematic searches were conducted in China National Knowledge Infrastructure, PubMed,\nWeb of Science, SPORTDiscus, and EMBASE databases. Standardized mean difference\n(SMD) and 95% confidence interval (CI) of outcome measures were calculated as effect\n14 studies were included in this meta-analysis. Significant improvements were found\nbetween pre- and post-tapering in time–trial (TT) performance (SMD = −0.45; P < 0.05) and\ntime to exhaustion (TTE) performance (SMD = 1.28; P < 0.05). However, There were no\nimprovements in maximal oxygen consumption ( _VO2max) and economy of movement (EM)\n(P > 0.05) between pre- and post-tapering. Further subgroup analysis showed that tapering\ncombined with pre-taper overload training had a more significant effect on TT performance\nthan conventional tapering (P < 0.05). A tapering strategy that reduced training volume by\n41–60%, m", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Y\n(2023) Effects of tapering on performance in\nendurance athletes: A systematic review and meta-\nanalysis. PLoS ONE 18(5): e0282838. https://doi.\nEditor: Laurent Mourot, University of Bourgogne\nFrance Comte´, FRANCE\nCopyright: © 2023 Wang et al. Thi"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0002_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "6794f99c73505df5", "text_span_hash": "6794f99c73505df5", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ators have conducted numerous experimental studies on swimmers [7–9], cyclists\n[10,11], and runners [12,13] to verify the effect of pre-competition taper on the athletic perfor-\nmance based on physiological and psychological factors affecting such pe", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "The taper is a pre-competition training strategy that reduces training load in a progressive,\nnon-linear manner to induce optimal athletic performance at the right moment of the season\n[1]. Based on positive (adaptations) and negative (fatigue) effects of training load on the\nhuman body [2], the taper aims to reduce negative impact and increase positive training-\ninduced adaptations [3,4], by reducing training load to balance the antagonistic effects of accu-\nmulated fatigue and training-induced adaptations [5]. Since 1985, when Costill et al. [6] first\nexperimentally evaluated the impact of pre-competition training load reduction in swimmers,\ninvestigators have conducted numerous experimental studies on swimmers [7–9], cyclists\n[10,11], and runners [12,13] to verify the effect of pre-competition taper on the athletic perfor-\nmance based on physiological and psychological factors affecting such performance, and to\nexplore tapering strategies for different sports.\nThe key to the pre-competition taper as a typical strategy for improving the performance of\nendurance athletes is to integrate a scientific arrangement of training variables such as training\nintensity, volume, frequency, duration of taper, and type of taper [1,14]. Most of the studies\nconfirm that their designed pre-competition tapering strategies could improve sports perfor-\nmance [15–18]. However, researchers disagree on which variables and how much to change.\nJafer", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ators have conducted numerous experimental studies on swimmers [7–9], cyclists\n[10,11], and runners [12,13] to verify the effect of pre-competition taper on the athletic perfor-\nmance based on physiological and psychological factors affecting such pe"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0002_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9d3ba849456e4df2", "text_span_hash": "9d3ba849456e4df2", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ical factors affecting such performance, and to\nexplore tapering strategies for different sports.\nThe key to the pre-competition taper as a typical strategy for improving the performance of\nendurance athletes is to integrate a scientific arrangement", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "The taper is a pre-competition training strategy that reduces training load in a progressive,\nnon-linear manner to induce optimal athletic performance at the right moment of the season\n[1]. Based on positive (adaptations) and negative (fatigue) effects of training load on the\nhuman body [2], the taper aims to reduce negative impact and increase positive training-\ninduced adaptations [3,4], by reducing training load to balance the antagonistic effects of accu-\nmulated fatigue and training-induced adaptations [5]. Since 1985, when Costill et al. [6] first\nexperimentally evaluated the impact of pre-competition training load reduction in swimmers,\ninvestigators have conducted numerous experimental studies on swimmers [7–9], cyclists\n[10,11], and runners [12,13] to verify the effect of pre-competition taper on the athletic perfor-\nmance based on physiological and psychological factors affecting such performance, and to\nexplore tapering strategies for different sports.\nThe key to the pre-competition taper as a typical strategy for improving the performance of\nendurance athletes is to integrate a scientific arrangement of training variables such as training\nintensity, volume, frequency, duration of taper, and type of taper [1,14]. Most of the studies\nconfirm that their designed pre-competition tapering strategies could improve sports perfor-\nmance [15–18]. However, researchers disagree on which variables and how much to change.\nJafer", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ical factors affecting such performance, and to\nexplore tapering strategies for different sports.\nThe key to the pre-competition taper as a typical strategy for improving the performance of\nendurance athletes is to integrate a scientific arrangement"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0002_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "41ce9394b46ae326", "text_span_hash": "41ce9394b46ae326", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "rate a scientific arrangement of training variables such as training\nintensity, volume, frequency, duration of taper, and type of taper [1,14]. Most of the studies\nconfirm that their designed pre-competition tapering strategies could improve sports p", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "The taper is a pre-competition training strategy that reduces training load in a progressive,\nnon-linear manner to induce optimal athletic performance at the right moment of the season\n[1]. Based on positive (adaptations) and negative (fatigue) effects of training load on the\nhuman body [2], the taper aims to reduce negative impact and increase positive training-\ninduced adaptations [3,4], by reducing training load to balance the antagonistic effects of accu-\nmulated fatigue and training-induced adaptations [5]. Since 1985, when Costill et al. [6] first\nexperimentally evaluated the impact of pre-competition training load reduction in swimmers,\ninvestigators have conducted numerous experimental studies on swimmers [7–9], cyclists\n[10,11], and runners [12,13] to verify the effect of pre-competition taper on the athletic perfor-\nmance based on physiological and psychological factors affecting such performance, and to\nexplore tapering strategies for different sports.\nThe key to the pre-competition taper as a typical strategy for improving the performance of\nendurance athletes is to integrate a scientific arrangement of training variables such as training\nintensity, volume, frequency, duration of taper, and type of taper [1,14]. Most of the studies\nconfirm that their designed pre-competition tapering strategies could improve sports perfor-\nmance [15–18]. However, researchers disagree on which variables and how much to change.\nJafer", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "rate a scientific arrangement of training variables such as training\nintensity, volume, frequency, duration of taper, and type of taper [1,14]. Most of the studies\nconfirm that their designed pre-competition tapering strategies could improve sports p"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0002_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "a76768c7171a07e6", "text_span_hash": "a76768c7171a07e6", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ategies could improve sports perfor-\nmance [15–18]. However, researchers disagree on which variables and how much to change.\nJafer et al. [19] found that a 50% reduction in training volume significantly improved long-dis-\ntance runners’ performance.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "The taper is a pre-competition training strategy that reduces training load in a progressive,\nnon-linear manner to induce optimal athletic performance at the right moment of the season\n[1]. Based on positive (adaptations) and negative (fatigue) effects of training load on the\nhuman body [2], the taper aims to reduce negative impact and increase positive training-\ninduced adaptations [3,4], by reducing training load to balance the antagonistic effects of accu-\nmulated fatigue and training-induced adaptations [5]. Since 1985, when Costill et al. [6] first\nexperimentally evaluated the impact of pre-competition training load reduction in swimmers,\ninvestigators have conducted numerous experimental studies on swimmers [7–9], cyclists\n[10,11], and runners [12,13] to verify the effect of pre-competition taper on the athletic perfor-\nmance based on physiological and psychological factors affecting such performance, and to\nexplore tapering strategies for different sports.\nThe key to the pre-competition taper as a typical strategy for improving the performance of\nendurance athletes is to integrate a scientific arrangement of training variables such as training\nintensity, volume, frequency, duration of taper, and type of taper [1,14]. Most of the studies\nconfirm that their designed pre-competition tapering strategies could improve sports perfor-\nmance [15–18]. However, researchers disagree on which variables and how much to change.\nJafer", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ategies could improve sports perfor-\nmance [15–18]. However, researchers disagree on which variables and how much to change.\nJafer et al. [19] found that a 50% reduction in training volume significantly improved long-dis-\ntance runners’ performance."}
