me, frequency, duration of taper, and type of taper [1,14]. Most of the studies\nconfirm that their designed pre-competition tapering strategies could improve sports perfor-\nmance [15–18]. However, researchers disagree on which variables and how much to change.\nJafer", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ormance and suggested that\na duration of 14-day, with no change in training intensity nor frequency, but with training vol-\nume exponentially reduced by 41–60%, appears to be the most effective pre-competition taper-\ning strategy [27]. Despite this m"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0002_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "dee742d76705ae3f", "text_span_hash": "dee742d76705ae3f", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "strategy [27]. Despite this meta-analysis evaluating the effects of taper component changes\non performance in competitive athletes, the limitation is that this study focused not only on\nendurance athletes but also sprinters. We did not perform a com", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "The taper is a pre-competition training strategy that reduces training load in a progressive,\nnon-linear manner to induce optimal athletic performance at the right moment of the season\n[1]. Based on positive (adaptations) and negative (fatigue) effects of training load on the\nhuman body [2], the taper aims to reduce negative impact and increase positive training-\ninduced adaptations [3,4], by reducing training load to balance the antagonistic effects of accu-\nmulated fatigue and training-induced adaptations [5]. Since 1985, when Costill et al. [6] first\nexperimentally evaluated the impact of pre-competition training load reduction in swimmers,\ninvestigators have conducted numerous experimental studies on swimmers [7–9], cyclists\n[10,11], and runners [12,13] to verify the effect of pre-competition taper on the athletic perfor-\nmance based on physiological and psychological factors affecting such performance, and to\nexplore tapering strategies for different sports.\nThe key to the pre-competition taper as a typical strategy for improving the performance of\nendurance athletes is to integrate a scientific arrangement of training variables such as training\nintensity, volume, frequency, duration of taper, and type of taper [1,14]. Most of the studies\nconfirm that their designed pre-competition tapering strategies could improve sports perfor-\nmance [15–18]. However, researchers disagree on which variables and how much to change.\nJafer", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "strategy [27]. Despite this meta-analysis evaluating the effects of taper component changes\non performance in competitive athletes, the limitation is that this study focused not only on\nendurance athletes but also sprinters. We did not perform a com"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0002_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7c3b77102706223f", "text_span_hash": "7c3b77102706223f", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ters. We did not perform a comparative analysis of the\nreported type of sports event, and therefore the authors could not provide more specific rec-\nommendations on endurance sports. Given the differences sport-to-sport and athlete-to-ath-\nlete, thes", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "The taper is a pre-competition training strategy that reduces training load in a progressive,\nnon-linear manner to induce optimal athletic performance at the right moment of the season\n[1]. Based on positive (adaptations) and negative (fatigue) effects of training load on the\nhuman body [2], the taper aims to reduce negative impact and increase positive training-\ninduced adaptations [3,4], by reducing training load to balance the antagonistic effects of accu-\nmulated fatigue and training-induced adaptations [5]. Since 1985, when Costill et al. [6] first\nexperimentally evaluated the impact of pre-competition training load reduction in swimmers,\ninvestigators have conducted numerous experimental studies on swimmers [7–9], cyclists\n[10,11], and runners [12,13] to verify the effect of pre-competition taper on the athletic perfor-\nmance based on physiological and psychological factors affecting such performance, and to\nexplore tapering strategies for different sports.\nThe key to the pre-competition taper as a typical strategy for improving the performance of\nendurance athletes is to integrate a scientific arrangement of training variables such as training\nintensity, volume, frequency, duration of taper, and type of taper [1,14]. Most of the studies\nconfirm that their designed pre-competition tapering strategies could improve sports perfor-\nmance [15–18]. However, researchers disagree on which variables and how much to change.\nJafer", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ters. We did not perform a comparative analysis of the\nreported type of sports event, and therefore the authors could not provide more specific rec-\nommendations on endurance sports. Given the differences sport-to-sport and athlete-to-ath-\nlete, thes"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0002_c0016", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2dfe9d5bcf92dcaa", "text_span_hash": "2dfe9d5bcf92dcaa", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 16, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "and athlete-to-ath-\nlete, these findings might not accurately reflect the characteristics of tapering for endurance\nsports. Although the application effects of tapering in endurance events have received much\nattention in the last decade [13,22,28–31]", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "The taper is a pre-competition training strategy that reduces training load in a progressive,\nnon-linear manner to induce optimal athletic performance at the right moment of the season\n[1]. Based on positive (adaptations) and negative (fatigue) effects of training load on the\nhuman body [2], the taper aims to reduce negative impact and increase positive training-\ninduced adaptations [3,4], by reducing training load to balance the antagonistic effects of accu-\nmulated fatigue and training-induced adaptations [5]. Since 1985, when Costill et al. [6] first\nexperimentally evaluated the impact of pre-competition training load reduction in swimmers,\ninvestigators have conducted numerous experimental studies on swimmers [7–9], cyclists\n[10,11], and runners [12,13] to verify the effect of pre-competition taper on the athletic perfor-\nmance based on physiological and psychological factors affecting such performance, and to\nexplore tapering strategies for different sports.\nThe key to the pre-competition taper as a typical strategy for improving the performance of\nendurance athletes is to integrate a scientific arrangement of training variables such as training\nintensity, volume, frequency, duration of taper, and type of taper [1,14]. Most of the studies\nconfirm that their designed pre-competition tapering strategies could improve sports perfor-\nmance [15–18]. However, researchers disagree on which variables and how much to change.\nJafer", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "and athlete-to-ath-\nlete, these findings might not accurately reflect the characteristics of tapering for endurance\nsports. Although the application effects of tapering in endurance events have received much\nattention in the last decade [13,22,28–31]"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0002_c0017", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "cd514d3511619be2", "text_span_hash": "cd514d3511619be2", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 17, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "the last decade [13,22,28–31], it is very difficult for athletes, coaches, and research-\ners to find tapering strategies to increase performance in endurance athletes. The previous sys-\ntematic reviews have helped to shed light on the effects of tap", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "The taper is a pre-competition training strategy that reduces training load in a progressive,\nnon-linear manner to induce optimal athletic performance at the right moment of the season\n[1]. Based on positive (adaptations) and negative (fatigue) effects of training load on the\nhuman body [2], the taper aims to reduce negative impact and increase positive training-\ninduced adaptations [3,4], by reducing training load to balance the antagonistic effects of accu-\nmulated fatigue and training-induced adaptations [5]. Since 1985, when Costill et al. [6] first\nexperimentally evaluated the impact of pre-competition training load reduction in swimmers,\ninvestigators have conducted numerous experimental studies on swimmers [7–9], cyclists\n[10,11], and runners [12,13] to verify the effect of pre-competition taper on the athletic perfor-\nmance based on physiological and psychological factors affecting such performance, and to\nexplore tapering strategies for different sports.\nThe key to the pre-competition taper as a typical strategy for improving the performance of\nendurance athletes is to integrate a scientific arrangement of training variables such as training\nintensity, volume, frequency, duration of taper, and type of taper [1,14]. Most of the studies\nconfirm that their designed pre-competition tapering strategies could improve sports perfor-\nmance [15–18]. However, researchers disagree on which variables and how much to change.\nJafer", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "the last decade [13,22,28–31], it is very difficult for athletes, coaches, and research-\ners to find tapering strategies to increase performance in endurance athletes. The previous sys-\ntematic reviews have helped to shed light on the effects of tap"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0002_c0019", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "07906afe5ecade8e", "text_span_hash": "07906afe5ecade8e", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 19, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "indicators used to evaluate spe-\ncific sports and aerobic capacities, such as maximal oxygen consumption ( _VO2max), time to\nexhaustion (TTE), and economy of movement (EM), which determine endurance\nEffects of tapering on performance in endurance ath", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "The taper is a pre-competition training strategy that reduces training load in a progressive,\nnon-linear manner to induce optimal athletic performance at the right moment of the season\n[1]. Based on positive (adaptations) and negative (fatigue) effects of training load on the\nhuman body [2], the taper aims to reduce negative impact and increase positive training-\ninduced adaptations [3,4], by reducing training load to balance the antagonistic effects of accu-\nmulated fatigue and training-induced adaptations [5]. Since 1985, when Costill et al. [6] first\nexperimentally evaluated the impact of pre-competition training load reduction in swimmers,\ninvestigators have conducted numerous experimental studies on swimmers [7–9], cyclists\n[10,11], and runners [12,13] to verify the effect of pre-competition taper on the athletic perfor-\nmance based on physiological and psychological factors affecting such performance, and to\nexplore tapering strategies for different sports.\nThe key to the pre-competition taper as a typical strategy for improving the performance of\nendurance athletes is to integrate a scientific arrangement of training variables such as training\nintensity, volume, frequency, duration of taper, and type of taper [1,14]. Most of the studies\nconfirm that their designed pre-competition tapering strategies could improve sports perfor-\nmance [15–18]. However, researchers disagree on which variables and how much to change.\nJafer", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "indicators used to evaluate spe-\ncific sports and aerobic capacities, such as maximal oxygen consumption ( _VO2max), time to\nexhaustion (TTE), and economy of movement (EM), which determine endurance\nEffects of tapering on performance in endurance ath"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0002_c0020", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "cfa25f1144918ee2", "text_span_hash": "cfa25f1144918ee2", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 20, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "n performance in endurance athletes", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "The taper is a pre-competition training strategy that reduces training load in a progressive,\nnon-linear manner to induce optimal athletic performance at the right moment of the season\n[1]. Based on positive (adaptations) and negative (fatigue) effects of training load on the\nhuman body [2], the taper aims to reduce negative impact and increase positive training-\ninduced adaptations [3,4], by reducing training load to balance the antagonistic effects of accu-\nmulated fatigue and training-induced adaptations [5]. Since 1985, when Costill et al. [6] first\nexperimentally evaluated the impact of pre-competition training load reduction in swimmers,\ninvestigators have conducted numerous experimental studies on swimmers [7–9], cyclists\n[10,11], and runners [12,13] to verify the effect of pre-competition taper on the athletic perfor-\nmance based on physiological and psychological factors affecting such performance, and to\nexplore tapering strategies for different sports.\nThe key to the pre-competition taper as a typical strategy for improving the performance of\nendurance athletes is to integrate a scientific arrangement of training variables such as training\nintensity, volume, frequency, duration of taper, and type of taper [1,14]. Most of the studies\nconfirm that their designed pre-competition tapering strategies could improve sports perfor-\nmance [15–18]. However, researchers disagree on which variables and how much to change.\nJafer", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "n performance in endurance athletes"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0003_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b5d82ea897abc738", "text_span_hash": "b5d82ea897abc738", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "performance [33–38]. These limitations have a significant impact on how tapering measures\nare interpreted to utilize effective evidence-based practices for endurance athletes. Therefore,\nit is very difficult for athletes, coaches, and researchers to", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "performance [33–38]. These limitations have a significant impact on how tapering measures\nare interpreted to utilize effective evidence-based practices for endurance athletes. Therefore,\nit is very difficult for athletes, coaches, and researchers to identify appropriate tapering strate-\ngies to enhance performance in endurance athletes. It seems necessary to perform a systematic\nreview and meta-analysis of the literature on the effect of tapering on endurance performance.\nA TT test is defined as an endurance performance test with a known endpoint. In this type\nof test, subjects are required to complete a set distance in as fast a time as possible [37]. TT is\nthe measurement of the time taken to complete a given distance (e.g., a race or time trial) [36].\nIt can effectively simulate the physiological responses of athletes during competition, is often\nused to evaluate the specific performance of athletes, and is closely related to endurance perfor-\nmance [36,39]. TTE, _VO2max, and EM are typical indicators that objectively and directly\nreflect specific exercise and aerobic capabilities [34,37,39,40]. The TTE test requires subjects to\nperform submaximal exercise intensity to exhaustion until they no longer maintain the\nrequired work rate (e.g., speed or power output) [33,37]. _VO2max is defined as the maximal\noxygen consumption rate measured during incremental exercise [41]. EM is defined as the\noxygen consumption during exercise", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "performance [33–38]. These limitations have a significant impact on how tapering measures\nare interpreted to utilize effective evidence-based practices for endurance athletes. Therefore,\nit is very difficult for athletes, coaches, and researchers to"}
