 exhaustion until they no longer maintain the\nrequired work rate (e.g., speed or power output) [33,37]. _VO2max is defined as the maximal\noxygen consumption rate measured during incremental exercise [41]. EM is defined as the\noxygen consumption during exercise", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "detailed pre-competition tapering data, including training intensity, volume, frequency,\ntype, and duration. A taper is a short-term (\u00156 days [46,47]) reduction in training load (e.g.,\nEffects of tapering on performance in endurance athletes"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0004_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f2d73f52b19c3a02", "text_span_hash": "f2d73f52b19c3a02", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "the alteration of training volume, intensity, and frequency); 3) Study design: randomized con-\ntrolled trial (RCT) or controlled trial [48,49]; 4) Outcome indicators: TT tests that required\nparticipants to complete a set distance, TTE, _VO2max, and E", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "the alteration of training volume, intensity, and frequency); 3) Study design: randomized con-\ntrolled trial (RCT) or controlled trial [48,49]; 4) Outcome indicators: TT tests that required\nparticipants to complete a set distance, TTE, _VO2max, and EM. Measurements of TT and TTE\nrefer to the total time desired to achieve the whole goal quantity, including direct measure-\nments such as performance during either a constant distance (at least middle distance) or a\nconstant intensity test. We chose TT as the primary-outcome index for examining athletic per-\nformance, and the endurance performance–related indices TTE, _VO2max, and EM as second-\nary-outcome indices, to assess the effects of pre-competition taper on sports performance in\nendurance events; and 5) Quality assessment: at least moderate study quality on the Physio-\ntherapy Evidence Database (PEDro) scale or better (> 4 points) [50–52].\nExclusion criteria were as follows: 1) experimental data could not be extracted and\nremained unavailable after we contacted the authors; 2) the full text of the study was unavail-\nable; 3) taper duration was less than 6 days; 4) published in a non-English language; or 5) TT\nnot based on distance. Time-based TT tests (e.g., a 60-minute time trial or a 5-minute TT), in\nwhich an athlete attempts to travel the furthest distance or to maintain the highest average\npower or velocity within a set duration, are common. However, relative to time-bas", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "the alteration of training volume, intensity, and frequency); 3) Study design: randomized con-\ntrolled trial (RCT) or controlled trial [48,49]; 4) Outcome indicators: TT tests that required\nparticipants to complete a set distance, TTE, _VO2max, and E"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0004_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "4f651ff925949e43", "text_span_hash": "4f651ff925949e43", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "distance, TTE, _VO2max, and EM. Measurements of TT and TTE\nrefer to the total time desired to achieve the whole goal quantity, including direct measure-\nments such as performance during either a constant distance (at least middle distance) or a\ncons", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "the alteration of training volume, intensity, and frequency); 3) Study design: randomized con-\ntrolled trial (RCT) or controlled trial [48,49]; 4) Outcome indicators: TT tests that required\nparticipants to complete a set distance, TTE, _VO2max, and EM. Measurements of TT and TTE\nrefer to the total time desired to achieve the whole goal quantity, including direct measure-\nments such as performance during either a constant distance (at least middle distance) or a\nconstant intensity test. We chose TT as the primary-outcome index for examining athletic per-\nformance, and the endurance performance–related indices TTE, _VO2max, and EM as second-\nary-outcome indices, to assess the effects of pre-competition taper on sports performance in\nendurance events; and 5) Quality assessment: at least moderate study quality on the Physio-\ntherapy Evidence Database (PEDro) scale or better (> 4 points) [50–52].\nExclusion criteria were as follows: 1) experimental data could not be extracted and\nremained unavailable after we contacted the authors; 2) the full text of the study was unavail-\nable; 3) taper duration was less than 6 days; 4) published in a non-English language; or 5) TT\nnot based on distance. Time-based TT tests (e.g., a 60-minute time trial or a 5-minute TT), in\nwhich an athlete attempts to travel the furthest distance or to maintain the highest average\npower or velocity within a set duration, are common. However, relative to time-bas", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "distance, TTE, _VO2max, and EM. Measurements of TT and TTE\nrefer to the total time desired to achieve the whole goal quantity, including direct measure-\nments such as performance during either a constant distance (at least middle distance) or a\ncons"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0004_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c986b1fd5218f4a2", "text_span_hash": "c986b1fd5218f4a2", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ast middle distance) or a\nconstant intensity test. We chose TT as the primary-outcome index for examining athletic per-\nformance, and the endurance performance–related indices TTE, _VO2max, and EM as second-\nary-outcome indices, to assess the effects", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "the alteration of training volume, intensity, and frequency); 3) Study design: randomized con-\ntrolled trial (RCT) or controlled trial [48,49]; 4) Outcome indicators: TT tests that required\nparticipants to complete a set distance, TTE, _VO2max, and EM. Measurements of TT and TTE\nrefer to the total time desired to achieve the whole goal quantity, including direct measure-\nments such as performance during either a constant distance (at least middle distance) or a\nconstant intensity test. We chose TT as the primary-outcome index for examining athletic per-\nformance, and the endurance performance–related indices TTE, _VO2max, and EM as second-\nary-outcome indices, to assess the effects of pre-competition taper on sports performance in\nendurance events; and 5) Quality assessment: at least moderate study quality on the Physio-\ntherapy Evidence Database (PEDro) scale or better (> 4 points) [50–52].\nExclusion criteria were as follows: 1) experimental data could not be extracted and\nremained unavailable after we contacted the authors; 2) the full text of the study was unavail-\nable; 3) taper duration was less than 6 days; 4) published in a non-English language; or 5) TT\nnot based on distance. Time-based TT tests (e.g., a 60-minute time trial or a 5-minute TT), in\nwhich an athlete attempts to travel the furthest distance or to maintain the highest average\npower or velocity within a set duration, are common. However, relative to time-bas", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ast middle distance) or a\nconstant intensity test. We chose TT as the primary-outcome index for examining athletic per-\nformance, and the endurance performance–related indices TTE, _VO2max, and EM as second-\nary-outcome indices, to assess the effects"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0004_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "79808d4b416b7101", "text_span_hash": "79808d4b416b7101", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "indices, to assess the effects of pre-competition taper on sports performance in\nendurance events; and 5) Quality assessment: at least moderate study quality on the Physio-\ntherapy Evidence Database (PEDro) scale or better (> 4 points) [50–52].\nExclu", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "the alteration of training volume, intensity, and frequency); 3) Study design: randomized con-\ntrolled trial (RCT) or controlled trial [48,49]; 4) Outcome indicators: TT tests that required\nparticipants to complete a set distance, TTE, _VO2max, and EM. Measurements of TT and TTE\nrefer to the total time desired to achieve the whole goal quantity, including direct measure-\nments such as performance during either a constant distance (at least middle distance) or a\nconstant intensity test. We chose TT as the primary-outcome index for examining athletic per-\nformance, and the endurance performance–related indices TTE, _VO2max, and EM as second-\nary-outcome indices, to assess the effects of pre-competition taper on sports performance in\nendurance events; and 5) Quality assessment: at least moderate study quality on the Physio-\ntherapy Evidence Database (PEDro) scale or better (> 4 points) [50–52].\nExclusion criteria were as follows: 1) experimental data could not be extracted and\nremained unavailable after we contacted the authors; 2) the full text of the study was unavail-\nable; 3) taper duration was less than 6 days; 4) published in a non-English language; or 5) TT\nnot based on distance. Time-based TT tests (e.g., a 60-minute time trial or a 5-minute TT), in\nwhich an athlete attempts to travel the furthest distance or to maintain the highest average\npower or velocity within a set duration, are common. However, relative to time-bas", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "indices, to assess the effects of pre-competition taper on sports performance in\nendurance events; and 5) Quality assessment: at least moderate study quality on the Physio-\ntherapy Evidence Database (PEDro) scale or better (> 4 points) [50–52].\nExclu"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0004_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "bc8400a0f063b01c", "text_span_hash": "bc8400a0f063b01c", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "er (> 4 points) [50–52].\nExclusion criteria were as follows: 1) experimental data could not be extracted and\nremained unavailable after we contacted the authors; 2) the full text of the study was unavail-\nable; 3) taper duration was less than 6 days;", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "the alteration of training volume, intensity, and frequency); 3) Study design: randomized con-\ntrolled trial (RCT) or controlled trial [48,49]; 4) Outcome indicators: TT tests that required\nparticipants to complete a set distance, TTE, _VO2max, and EM. Measurements of TT and TTE\nrefer to the total time desired to achieve the whole goal quantity, including direct measure-\nments such as performance during either a constant distance (at least middle distance) or a\nconstant intensity test. We chose TT as the primary-outcome index for examining athletic per-\nformance, and the endurance performance–related indices TTE, _VO2max, and EM as second-\nary-outcome indices, to assess the effects of pre-competition taper on sports performance in\nendurance events; and 5) Quality assessment: at least moderate study quality on the Physio-\ntherapy Evidence Database (PEDro) scale or better (> 4 points) [50–52].\nExclusion criteria were as follows: 1) experimental data could not be extracted and\nremained unavailable after we contacted the authors; 2) the full text of the study was unavail-\nable; 3) taper duration was less than 6 days; 4) published in a non-English language; or 5) TT\nnot based on distance. Time-based TT tests (e.g., a 60-minute time trial or a 5-minute TT), in\nwhich an athlete attempts to travel the furthest distance or to maintain the highest average\npower or velocity within a set duration, are common. However, relative to time-bas", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "er (> 4 points) [50–52].\nExclusion criteria were as follows: 1) experimental data could not be extracted and\nremained unavailable after we contacted the authors; 2) the full text of the study was unavail-\nable; 3) taper duration was less than 6 days;"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0004_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "bf9ee09b270aea52", "text_span_hash": "bf9ee09b270aea52", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "mmon type of\nTT test, which most closely represent a true race environment. They may be the most appro-\npriate for endurance performance assessment [53].\nStudy selection and methodological quality assessment\nThe lead author removed duplicates of arti", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "the alteration of training volume, intensity, and frequency); 3) Study design: randomized con-\ntrolled trial (RCT) or controlled trial [48,49]; 4) Outcome indicators: TT tests that required\nparticipants to complete a set distance, TTE, _VO2max, and EM. Measurements of TT and TTE\nrefer to the total time desired to achieve the whole goal quantity, including direct measure-\nments such as performance during either a constant distance (at least middle distance) or a\nconstant intensity test. We chose TT as the primary-outcome index for examining athletic per-\nformance, and the endurance performance–related indices TTE, _VO2max, and EM as second-\nary-outcome indices, to assess the effects of pre-competition taper on sports performance in\nendurance events; and 5) Quality assessment: at least moderate study quality on the Physio-\ntherapy Evidence Database (PEDro) scale or better (> 4 points) [50–52].\nExclusion criteria were as follows: 1) experimental data could not be extracted and\nremained unavailable after we contacted the authors; 2) the full text of the study was unavail-\nable; 3) taper duration was less than 6 days; 4) published in a non-English language; or 5) TT\nnot based on distance. Time-based TT tests (e.g., a 60-minute time trial or a 5-minute TT), in\nwhich an athlete attempts to travel the furthest distance or to maintain the highest average\npower or velocity within a set duration, are common. However, relative to time-bas", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "mmon type of\nTT test, which most closely represent a true race environment. They may be the most appro-\npriate for endurance performance assessment [53].\nStudy selection and methodological quality assessment\nThe lead author removed duplicates of arti"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0004_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5edd446b884afb94", "text_span_hash": "5edd446b884afb94", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "luded [50,51].\nThe two reviewers (WZQ and GWF) independently extracted information from each study,\nincluding the characteristics of participants, tapering strategies, and outcome data (Table 1):\n1. Participants: age, sex, sample size, training statu", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "the alteration of training volume, intensity, and frequency); 3) Study design: randomized con-\ntrolled trial (RCT) or controlled trial [48,49]; 4) Outcome indicators: TT tests that required\nparticipants to complete a set distance, TTE, _VO2max, and EM. Measurements of TT and TTE\nrefer to the total time desired to achieve the whole goal quantity, including direct measure-\nments such as performance during either a constant distance (at least middle distance) or a\nconstant intensity test. We chose TT as the primary-outcome index for examining athletic per-\nformance, and the endurance performance–related indices TTE, _VO2max, and EM as second-\nary-outcome indices, to assess the effects of pre-competition taper on sports performance in\nendurance events; and 5) Quality assessment: at least moderate study quality on the Physio-\ntherapy Evidence Database (PEDro) scale or better (> 4 points) [50–52].\nExclusion criteria were as follows: 1) experimental data could not be extracted and\nremained unavailable after we contacted the authors; 2) the full text of the study was unavail-\nable; 3) taper duration was less than 6 days; 4) published in a non-English language; or 5) TT\nnot based on distance. Time-based TT tests (e.g., a 60-minute time trial or a 5-minute TT), in\nwhich an athlete attempts to travel the furthest distance or to maintain the highest average\npower or velocity within a set duration, are common. However, relative to time-bas", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "luded [50,51].\nThe two reviewers (WZQ and GWF) independently extracted information from each study,\nincluding the characteristics of participants, tapering strategies, and outcome data (Table 1):\n1. Participants: age, sex, sample size, training statu"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0004_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ff5cfbfacb8e0590", "text_span_hash": "ff5cfbfacb8e0590", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "x, sample size, training status, and TT distance.\n2. Interventions: training intensity, volume, frequency, duration, type of tapering. In accor-\ndance with the meta-analysis conducted by Bosquet et al. [27], we coded training intensity\n(decreased or", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "the alteration of training volume, intensity, and frequency); 3) Study design: randomized con-\ntrolled trial (RCT) or controlled trial [48,49]; 4) Outcome indicators: TT tests that required\nparticipants to complete a set distance, TTE, _VO2max, and EM. Measurements of TT and TTE\nrefer to the total time desired to achieve the whole goal quantity, including direct measure-\nments such as performance during either a constant distance (at least middle distance) or a\nconstant intensity test. We chose TT as the primary-outcome index for examining athletic per-\nformance, and the endurance performance–related indices TTE, _VO2max, and EM as second-\nary-outcome indices, to assess the effects of pre-competition taper on sports performance in\nendurance events; and 5) Quality assessment: at least moderate study quality on the Physio-\ntherapy Evidence Database (PEDro) scale or better (> 4 points) [50–52].\nExclusion criteria were as follows: 1) experimental data could not be extracted and\nremained unavailable after we contacted the authors; 2) the full text of the study was unavail-\nable; 3) taper duration was less than 6 days; 4) published in a non-English language; or 5) TT\nnot based on distance. Time-based TT tests (e.g., a 60-minute time trial or a 5-minute TT), in\nwhich an athlete attempts to travel the furthest distance or to maintain the highest average\npower or velocity within a set duration, are common. However, relative to time-bas", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "x, sample size, training status, and TT distance.\n2. Interventions: training intensity, volume, frequency, duration, type of tapering. In accor-\ndance with the meta-analysis conducted by Bosquet et al. [27], we coded training intensity\n(decreased or"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0004_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0aa17653d855e6ec", "text_span_hash": "0aa17653d855e6ec", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ining intensity\n(decreased or unchanged), volume (\u0014 20%, 21–40%, 41–60%, or \u0015 60%), frequency\n(decreased or unchanged), duration of taper (\u0014 7 days, 8–14 days, 15–21 days, or \u0015 22\ndays), and type of tapering (progressive or step).\n3. Outcome data: pr", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "the alteration of training volume, intensity, and frequency); 3) Study design: randomized con-\ntrolled trial (RCT) or controlled trial [48,49]; 4) Outcome indicators: TT tests that required\nparticipants to complete a set distance, TTE, _VO2max, and EM. Measurements of TT and TTE\nrefer to the total time desired to achieve the whole goal quantity, including direct measure-\nments such as performance during either a constant distance (at least middle distance) or a\nconstant intensity test. We chose TT as the primary-outcome index for examining athletic per-\nformance, and the endurance performance–related indices TTE, _VO2max, and EM as second-\nary-outcome indices, to assess the effects of pre-competition taper on sports performance in\nendurance events; and 5) Quality assessment: at least moderate study quality on the Physio-\ntherapy Evidence Database (PEDro) scale or better (> 4 points) [50–52].\nExclusion criteria were as follows: 1) experimental data could not be extracted and\nremained unavailable after we contacted the authors; 2) the full text of the study was unavail-\nable; 3) taper duration was less than 6 days; 4) published in a non-English language; or 5) TT\nnot based on distance. Time-based TT tests (e.g., a 60-minute time trial or a 5-minute TT), in\nwhich an athlete attempts to travel the furthest distance or to maintain the highest average\npower or velocity within a set duration, are common. However, relative to time-bas", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ining intensity\n(decreased or unchanged), volume (\u0014 20%, 21–40%, 41–60%, or \u0015 60%), frequency\n(decreased or unchanged), duration of taper (\u0014 7 days, 8–14 days, 15–21 days, or \u0015 22\ndays), and type of tapering (progressive or step).\n3. Outcome data: pr"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0004_c0016", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "114b0c7b34896866", "text_span_hash": "114b0c7b34896866", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 16, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "or step).\n3. Outcome data: pre- and post-tapering mean ± standard deviation (SD) of TT, TTE, and\n_VO2max. If only standard error (SE) was reported in the study, we used the formula SD = SE\nEffects of tapering on performance in endurance athletes", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "the alteration of training volume, intensity, and frequency); 3) Study design: randomized con-\ntrolled trial (RCT) or controlled trial [48,49]; 4) Outcome indicators: TT tests that required\nparticipants to complete a set distance, TTE, _VO2max, and EM. Measurements of TT and TTE\nrefer to the total time desired to achieve the whole goal quantity, including direct measure-\nments such as performance during either a constant distance (at least middle distance) or a\nconstant intensity test. We chose TT as the primary-outcome index for examining athletic per-\nformance, and the endurance performance–related indices TTE, _VO2max, and EM as second-\nary-outcome indices, to assess the effects of pre-competition taper on sports performance in\nendurance events; and 5) Quality assessment: at least moderate study quality on the Physio-\ntherapy Evidence Database (PEDro) scale or better (> 4 points) [50–52].\nExclusion criteria were as follows: 1) experimental data could not be extracted and\nremained unavailable after we contacted the authors; 2) the full text of the study was unavail-\nable; 3) taper duration was less than 6 days; 4) published in a non-English language; or 5) TT\nnot based on distance. Time-based TT tests (e.g., a 60-minute time trial or a 5-minute TT), in\nwhich an athlete attempts to travel the furthest distance or to maintain the highest average\npower or velocity within a set duration, are common. However, relative to time-bas", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "or step).\n3. Outcome data: pre- and post-tapering mean ± standard deviation (SD) of TT, TTE, and\n_VO2max. If only standard error (SE) was reported in the study, we used the formula SD = SE\nEffects of tapering on performance in endurance athletes"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0005_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "353de906e60eb8d4", "text_span_hash": "353de906e60eb8d4", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Because this study included both RCTs and controlled trials, and also included\ndifferent types of endurance events, we used the inverse-variance method for meta-analysis\nbased on type of experimental design, in accordance with the Cochrane Handbook f", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Statistical analysis\nWe used Review Manager (RevMan) version 5.4 (Cochrane Collaboration, Oxford, UK) for\nheterogeneity assessment, sensitivity analysis, synthesis of results, subgroup analysis, and forest\nmap creation. Because this study included both RCTs and controlled trials, and also included\ndifferent types of endurance events, we used the inverse-variance method for meta-analysis\nbased on type of experimental design, in accordance with the Cochrane Handbook for System-\natic Reviews of Interventions [61]. Standardized mean difference (SMD) and 95% confidence\ninterval (CI) of TT, TTE, _VO2max, and EM outcomes were calculated as effect sizes. Heteroge-\nneity was quantitatively evaluated by I2 values: 25% (low), 50% (moderate), and 75% (high)\n[62]. We used the Q statistic to test heterogeneity. The P-value for χ2 was < 0.1, indicating\nwhether a study showed statistically significant levels of heterogeneity [63].\nTwo investigators (WZQ and GWF) independently assessed the studies’ risk of bias by\ninterpreting the funnel in the Begg and Egger tests using STATA software version 16.0 (Stata\nCorp., College Station, TX, USA).\nTable 1. Characteristics and outcomes of included studies.\nMcConnell et al. [55]\nSkovgaard et al. [13]\nSpilsbury et al. [30]\nSpilsbury et al. [60]\nU = unchanged; S = step; P = progressive; TT = time trial; TTE = time to exhaustion; _V_O2max = maximum rate of oxygen consumption measured during incremental\nexer", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Because this study included both RCTs and controlled trials, and also included\ndifferent types of endurance events, we used the inverse-variance method for meta-analysis\nbased on type of experimental design, in accordance with the Cochrane Handbook f"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0005_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1b31f16538ad24d5", "text_span_hash": "1b31f16538ad24d5", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "e with the Cochrane Handbook for System-\natic Reviews of Interventions [61]. Standardized mean difference (SMD) and 95% confidence\ninterval (CI) of TT, TTE, _VO2max, and EM outcomes were calculated as effect sizes. Heteroge-\nneity was quantitatively", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Statistical analysis\nWe used Review Manager (RevMan) version 5.4 (Cochrane Collaboration, Oxford, UK) for\nheterogeneity assessment, sensitivity analysis, synthesis of results, subgroup analysis, and forest\nmap creation. Because this study included both RCTs and controlled trials, and also included\ndifferent types of endurance events, we used the inverse-variance method for meta-analysis\nbased on type of experimental design, in accordance with the Cochrane Handbook for System-\natic Reviews of Interventions [61]. Standardized mean difference (SMD) and 95% confidence\ninterval (CI) of TT, TTE, _VO2max, and EM outcomes were calculated as effect sizes. Heteroge-\nneity was quantitatively evaluated by I2 values: 25% (low), 50% (moderate), and 75% (high)\n[62]. We used the Q statistic to test heterogeneity. The P-value for χ2 was < 0.1, indicating\nwhether a study showed statistically significant levels of heterogeneity [63].\nTwo investigators (WZQ and GWF) independently assessed the studies’ risk of bias by\ninterpreting the funnel in the Begg and Egger tests using STATA software version 16.0 (Stata\nCorp., College Station, TX, USA).\nTable 1. Characteristics and outcomes of included studies.\nMcConnell et al. [55]\nSkovgaard et al. [13]\nSpilsbury et al. [30]\nSpilsbury et al. [60]\nU = unchanged; S = step; P = progressive; TT = time trial; TTE = time to exhaustion; _V_O2max = maximum rate of oxygen consumption measured during incremental\nexer", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "e with the Cochrane Handbook for System-\natic Reviews of Interventions [61]. Standardized mean difference (SMD) and 95% confidence\ninterval (CI) of TT, TTE, _VO2max, and EM outcomes were calculated as effect sizes. Heteroge-\nneity was quantitatively"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0005_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "38b96a148c2d2306", "text_span_hash": "38b96a148c2d2306", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ressive; TT = time trial; TTE = time to exhaustion; _V_O2max = maximum rate of oxygen consumption measured during incremental\nexercise; EM = economy of movement.\nEffects of tapering on performance in endurance athletes", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Statistical analysis\nWe used Review Manager (RevMan) version 5.4 (Cochrane Collaboration, Oxford, UK) for\nheterogeneity assessment, sensitivity analysis, synthesis of results, subgroup analysis, and forest\nmap creation. Because this study included both RCTs and controlled trials, and also included\ndifferent types of endurance events, we used the inverse-variance method for meta-analysis\nbased on type of experimental design, in accordance with the Cochrane Handbook for System-\natic Reviews of Interventions [61]. Standardized mean difference (SMD) and 95% confidence\ninterval (CI) of TT, TTE, _VO2max, and EM outcomes were calculated as effect sizes. Heteroge-\nneity was quantitatively evaluated by I2 values: 25% (low), 50% (moderate), and 75% (high)\n[62]. We used the Q statistic to test heterogeneity. The P-value for χ2 was < 0.1, indicating\nwhether a study showed statistically significant levels of heterogeneity [63].\nTwo investigators (WZQ and GWF) independently assessed the studies’ risk of bias by\ninterpreting the funnel in the Begg and Egger tests using STATA software version 16.0 (Stata\nCorp., College Station, TX, USA).\nTable 1. Characteristics and outcomes of included studies.\nMcConnell et al. [55]\nSkovgaard et al. [13]\nSpilsbury et al. [30]\nSpilsbury et al. [60]\nU = unchanged; S = step; P = progressive; TT = time trial; TTE = time to exhaustion; _V_O2max = maximum rate of oxygen consumption measured during incremental\nexer", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ressive; TT = time trial; TTE = time to exhaustion; _V_O2max = maximum rate of oxygen consumption measured during incremental\nexercise; EM = economy of movement.\nEffects of tapering on performance in endurance athletes"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0006_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ea2e5e9117d710f1", "text_span_hash": "ea2e5e9117d710f1", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "We extracted TT data from all 14 studies, 4 studies provided TTE data,\n8 studies provided _VO2max data, and 6 studies provided EM data (Table 1). In the included\nstudy, TT was measured at different distances, ranging from 800 m to 40 km. Performance", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Characteristics of included studies\nA total of 2309 studies were identified with an additional 6 studies included from other\nsources. After excluding duplicates and screening the title, abstract, or full text, we ultimately\nincluded 14 studies in this meta-analysis (Fig 1). These 14 studies included 174 athletes, aged\n17–32 years. Their sports were mainly middle-distance, long-distance, and ultra distance run-\nning, as well as cycling. We extracted TT data from all 14 studies, 4 studies provided TTE data,\n8 studies provided _VO2max data, and 6 studies provided EM data (Table 1). In the included\nstudy, TT was measured at different distances, ranging from 800 m to 40 km. Performance\nwas assessed on the track, treadmill, and cyclcle ergometer in the selected studies.\nTable 1 shows the details of different tapering strategies included in this meta-analysis. Mul-\ntiple information from the same eligible study needs to be collated. Twenty-one data sets main-\nwhile two decreased it [16,55]. The range of training volume reduction was \u0014 85%. One data\nset reduced volume by \u0014 20% [16], four data sets by 21–40% [10,17,30,60], nine data sets by\n61, 62]. Regarding the reduction of volume, different training methods were usually applied,\nsuch as a progressive reduction in training distance, a step reduction in both training duration\nand frequency, and a step reduction in training hours during tapering days. Training fre-\ndecreased in seven. ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "We extracted TT data from all 14 studies, 4 studies provided TTE data,\n8 studies provided _VO2max data, and 6 studies provided EM data (Table 1). In the included\nstudy, TT was measured at different distances, ranging from 800 m to 40 km. Performance"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0006_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "edc061d52c72bf24", "text_span_hash": "edc061d52c72bf24", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "m 800 m to 40 km. Performance\nwas assessed on the track, treadmill, and cyclcle ergometer in the selected studies.\nTable 1 shows the details of different tapering strategies included in this meta-analysis. Mul-\ntiple information from the same eligibl", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Characteristics of included studies\nA total of 2309 studies were identified with an additional 6 studies included from other\nsources. After excluding duplicates and screening the title, abstract, or full text, we ultimately\nincluded 14 studies in this meta-analysis (Fig 1). These 14 studies included 174 athletes, aged\n17–32 years. Their sports were mainly middle-distance, long-distance, and ultra distance run-\nning, as well as cycling. We extracted TT data from all 14 studies, 4 studies provided TTE data,\n8 studies provided _VO2max data, and 6 studies provided EM data (Table 1). In the included\nstudy, TT was measured at different distances, ranging from 800 m to 40 km. Performance\nwas assessed on the track, treadmill, and cyclcle ergometer in the selected studies.\nTable 1 shows the details of different tapering strategies included in this meta-analysis. Mul-\ntiple information from the same eligible study needs to be collated. Twenty-one data sets main-\nwhile two decreased it [16,55]. The range of training volume reduction was \u0014 85%. One data\nset reduced volume by \u0014 20% [16], four data sets by 21–40% [10,17,30,60], nine data sets by\n61, 62]. Regarding the reduction of volume, different training methods were usually applied,\nsuch as a progressive reduction in training distance, a step reduction in both training duration\nand frequency, and a step reduction in training hours during tapering days. Training fre-\ndecreased in seven. ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "m 800 m to 40 km. Performance\nwas assessed on the track, treadmill, and cyclcle ergometer in the selected studies.\nTable 1 shows the details of different tapering strategies included in this meta-analysis. Mul-\ntiple information from the same eligibl"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0006_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "11b98ff042650bdf", "text_span_hash": "11b98ff042650bdf", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ormation from the same eligible study needs to be collated. Twenty-one data sets main-\nwhile two decreased it [16,55]. The range of training volume reduction was \u0014 85%. One data\nset reduced volume by \u0014 20% [16], four data sets by 21–40% [10,17,30,60]", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Characteristics of included studies\nA total of 2309 studies were identified with an additional 6 studies included from other\nsources. After excluding duplicates and screening the title, abstract, or full text, we ultimately\nincluded 14 studies in this meta-analysis (Fig 1). These 14 studies included 174 athletes, aged\n17–32 years. Their sports were mainly middle-distance, long-distance, and ultra distance run-\nning, as well as cycling. We extracted TT data from all 14 studies, 4 studies provided TTE data,\n8 studies provided _VO2max data, and 6 studies provided EM data (Table 1). In the included\nstudy, TT was measured at different distances, ranging from 800 m to 40 km. Performance\nwas assessed on the track, treadmill, and cyclcle ergometer in the selected studies.\nTable 1 shows the details of different tapering strategies included in this meta-analysis. Mul-\ntiple information from the same eligible study needs to be collated. Twenty-one data sets main-\nwhile two decreased it [16,55]. The range of training volume reduction was \u0014 85%. One data\nset reduced volume by \u0014 20% [16], four data sets by 21–40% [10,17,30,60], nine data sets by\n61, 62]. Regarding the reduction of volume, different training methods were usually applied,\nsuch as a progressive reduction in training distance, a step reduction in both training duration\nand frequency, and a step reduction in training hours during tapering days. Training fre-\ndecreased in seven. ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ormation from the same eligible study needs to be collated. Twenty-one data sets main-\nwhile two decreased it [16,55]. The range of training volume reduction was \u0014 85%. One data\nset reduced volume by \u0014 20% [16], four data sets by 21–40% [10,17,30,60]"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0006_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "6dfe21901cddc94b", "text_span_hash": "6dfe21901cddc94b", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "a sets by 21–40% [10,17,30,60], nine data sets by\n61, 62]. Regarding the reduction of volume, different training methods were usually applied,\nsuch as a progressive reduction in training distance, a step reduction in both training duration\nand freque", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Characteristics of included studies\nA total of 2309 studies were identified with an additional 6 studies included from other\nsources. After excluding duplicates and screening the title, abstract, or full text, we ultimately\nincluded 14 studies in this meta-analysis (Fig 1). These 14 studies included 174 athletes, aged\n17–32 years. Their sports were mainly middle-distance, long-distance, and ultra distance run-\nning, as well as cycling. We extracted TT data from all 14 studies, 4 studies provided TTE data,\n8 studies provided _VO2max data, and 6 studies provided EM data (Table 1). In the included\nstudy, TT was measured at different distances, ranging from 800 m to 40 km. Performance\nwas assessed on the track, treadmill, and cyclcle ergometer in the selected studies.\nTable 1 shows the details of different tapering strategies included in this meta-analysis. Mul-\ntiple information from the same eligible study needs to be collated. Twenty-one data sets main-\nwhile two decreased it [16,55]. The range of training volume reduction was \u0014 85%. One data\nset reduced volume by \u0014 20% [16], four data sets by 21–40% [10,17,30,60], nine data sets by\n61, 62]. Regarding the reduction of volume, different training methods were usually applied,\nsuch as a progressive reduction in training distance, a step reduction in both training duration\nand frequency, and a step reduction in training hours during tapering days. Training fre-\ndecreased in seven. ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "a sets by 21–40% [10,17,30,60], nine data sets by\n61, 62]. Regarding the reduction of volume, different training methods were usually applied,\nsuch as a progressive reduction in training distance, a step reduction in both training duration\nand freque"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0006_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "65ebd9ec20edd16f", "text_span_hash": "65ebd9ec20edd16f", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "h training duration\nand frequency, and a step reduction in training hours during tapering days. Training fre-\ndecreased in seven. The shortest duration of taper was 6 days, the longest 28 days; seventeen\n8–14 days [58(2)], three data sets used 15–21", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Characteristics of included studies\nA total of 2309 studies were identified with an additional 6 studies included from other\nsources. After excluding duplicates and screening the title, abstract, or full text, we ultimately\nincluded 14 studies in this meta-analysis (Fig 1). These 14 studies included 174 athletes, aged\n17–32 years. Their sports were mainly middle-distance, long-distance, and ultra distance run-\nning, as well as cycling. We extracted TT data from all 14 studies, 4 studies provided TTE data,\n8 studies provided _VO2max data, and 6 studies provided EM data (Table 1). In the included\nstudy, TT was measured at different distances, ranging from 800 m to 40 km. Performance\nwas assessed on the track, treadmill, and cyclcle ergometer in the selected studies.\nTable 1 shows the details of different tapering strategies included in this meta-analysis. Mul-\ntiple information from the same eligible study needs to be collated. Twenty-one data sets main-\nwhile two decreased it [16,55]. The range of training volume reduction was \u0014 85%. One data\nset reduced volume by \u0014 20% [16], four data sets by 21–40% [10,17,30,60], nine data sets by\n61, 62]. Regarding the reduction of volume, different training methods were usually applied,\nsuch as a progressive reduction in training distance, a step reduction in both training duration\nand frequency, and a step reduction in training hours during tapering days. Training fre-\ndecreased in seven. ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "h training duration\nand frequency, and a step reduction in training hours during tapering days. Training fre-\ndecreased in seven. The shortest duration of taper was 6 days, the longest 28 days; seventeen\n8–14 days [58(2)], three data sets used 15–21"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0006_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "da346fc6d023de2b", "text_span_hash": "da346fc6d023de2b", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "], three data sets used 15–21 days [15, 58(2)], and one data set used \u0015 22 days\n[64]. Ten data sets used a step taper [10(3), 13, 14, 15, 17(3), 63], and thirteen data sets used a\nStudy quality assessment\nTable 1 shows the results of the quality asse", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Characteristics of included studies\nA total of 2309 studies were identified with an additional 6 studies included from other\nsources. After excluding duplicates and screening the title, abstract, or full text, we ultimately\nincluded 14 studies in this meta-analysis (Fig 1). These 14 studies included 174 athletes, aged\n17–32 years. Their sports were mainly middle-distance, long-distance, and ultra distance run-\nning, as well as cycling. We extracted TT data from all 14 studies, 4 studies provided TTE data,\n8 studies provided _VO2max data, and 6 studies provided EM data (Table 1). In the included\nstudy, TT was measured at different distances, ranging from 800 m to 40 km. Performance\nwas assessed on the track, treadmill, and cyclcle ergometer in the selected studies.\nTable 1 shows the details of different tapering strategies included in this meta-analysis. Mul-\ntiple information from the same eligible study needs to be collated. Twenty-one data sets main-\nwhile two decreased it [16,55]. The range of training volume reduction was \u0014 85%. One data\nset reduced volume by \u0014 20% [16], four data sets by 21–40% [10,17,30,60], nine data sets by\n61, 62]. Regarding the reduction of volume, different training methods were usually applied,\nsuch as a progressive reduction in training distance, a step reduction in both training duration\nand frequency, and a step reduction in training hours during tapering days. Training fre-\ndecreased in seven. ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "], three data sets used 15–21 days [15, 58(2)], and one data set used \u0015 22 days\n[64]. Ten data sets used a step taper [10(3), 13, 14, 15, 17(3), 63], and thirteen data sets used a\nStudy quality assessment\nTable 1 shows the results of the quality asse"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0006_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "66625cb0771421d3", "text_span_hash": "66625cb0771421d3", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "e trial performance.\nFourteen studies reported data for TT. Levels of heterogeneity\nwere low among these studies (I2 = 8%, P > 0.1). Results for overall effects on TT performance\nshowed a significant improvement between pre- and post-tapering (SMD, −", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Characteristics of included studies\nA total of 2309 studies were identified with an additional 6 studies included from other\nsources. After excluding duplicates and screening the title, abstract, or full text, we ultimately\nincluded 14 studies in this meta-analysis (Fig 1). These 14 studies included 174 athletes, aged\n17–32 years. Their sports were mainly middle-distance, long-distance, and ultra distance run-\nning, as well as cycling. We extracted TT data from all 14 studies, 4 studies provided TTE data,\n8 studies provided _VO2max data, and 6 studies provided EM data (Table 1). In the included\nstudy, TT was measured at different distances, ranging from 800 m to 40 km. Performance\nwas assessed on the track, treadmill, and cyclcle ergometer in the selected studies.\nTable 1 shows the details of different tapering strategies included in this meta-analysis. Mul-\ntiple information from the same eligible study needs to be collated. Twenty-one data sets main-\nwhile two decreased it [16,55]. The range of training volume reduction was \u0014 85%. One data\nset reduced volume by \u0014 20% [16], four data sets by 21–40% [10,17,30,60], nine data sets by\n61, 62]. Regarding the reduction of volume, different training methods were usually applied,\nsuch as a progressive reduction in training distance, a step reduction in both training duration\nand frequency, and a step reduction in training hours during tapering days. Training fre-\ndecreased in seven. ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "e trial performance.\nFourteen studies reported data for TT. Levels of heterogeneity\nwere low among these studies (I2 = 8%, P > 0.1). Results for overall effects on TT performance\nshowed a significant improvement between pre- and post-tapering (SMD, −"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0006_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "95fb90ad01b55a29", "text_span_hash": "95fb90ad01b55a29", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "pre- and post-tapering (SMD, −0.45; 95% CI,\n−0.68 to −0.23; P < 0.05; Fig 2). Significant improvement was also observed in experimental\ngroups compared with control groups (SMD, −0.73; 95% CI, −1.25 to −0.21; P < 0.05; Fig 3),\nwith a low level of het", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Characteristics of included studies\nA total of 2309 studies were identified with an additional 6 studies included from other\nsources. After excluding duplicates and screening the title, abstract, or full text, we ultimately\nincluded 14 studies in this meta-analysis (Fig 1). These 14 studies included 174 athletes, aged\n17–32 years. Their sports were mainly middle-distance, long-distance, and ultra distance run-\nning, as well as cycling. We extracted TT data from all 14 studies, 4 studies provided TTE data,\n8 studies provided _VO2max data, and 6 studies provided EM data (Table 1). In the included\nstudy, TT was measured at different distances, ranging from 800 m to 40 km. Performance\nwas assessed on the track, treadmill, and cyclcle ergometer in the selected studies.\nTable 1 shows the details of different tapering strategies included in this meta-analysis. Mul-\ntiple information from the same eligible study needs to be collated. Twenty-one data sets main-\nwhile two decreased it [16,55]. The range of training volume reduction was \u0014 85%. One data\nset reduced volume by \u0014 20% [16], four data sets by 21–40% [10,17,30,60], nine data sets by\n61, 62]. Regarding the reduction of volume, different training methods were usually applied,\nsuch as a progressive reduction in training distance, a step reduction in both training duration\nand frequency, and a step reduction in training hours during tapering days. Training fre-\ndecreased in seven. ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "pre- and post-tapering (SMD, −0.45; 95% CI,\n−0.68 to −0.23; P < 0.05; Fig 2). Significant improvement was also observed in experimental\ngroups compared with control groups (SMD, −0.73; 95% CI, −1.25 to −0.21; P < 0.05; Fig 3),\nwith a low level of het"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0006_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2c3d8a8a1bdece64", "text_span_hash": "2c3d8a8a1bdece64", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ig 3),\nwith a low level of heterogeneity (I2 = 31%, P > 0.1). Begg and Egger tests results showed no\nsignificant publication bias in the experimental group for TT (P > 0.1).\nSubgroup analysis with or without overload training before taper showed sign", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Characteristics of included studies\nA total of 2309 studies were identified with an additional 6 studies included from other\nsources. After excluding duplicates and screening the title, abstract, or full text, we ultimately\nincluded 14 studies in this meta-analysis (Fig 1). These 14 studies included 174 athletes, aged\n17–32 years. Their sports were mainly middle-distance, long-distance, and ultra distance run-\nning, as well as cycling. We extracted TT data from all 14 studies, 4 studies provided TTE data,\n8 studies provided _VO2max data, and 6 studies provided EM data (Table 1). In the included\nstudy, TT was measured at different distances, ranging from 800 m to 40 km. Performance\nwas assessed on the track, treadmill, and cyclcle ergometer in the selected studies.\nTable 1 shows the details of different tapering strategies included in this meta-analysis. Mul-\ntiple information from the same eligible study needs to be collated. Twenty-one data sets main-\nwhile two decreased it [16,55]. The range of training volume reduction was \u0014 85%. One data\nset reduced volume by \u0014 20% [16], four data sets by 21–40% [10,17,30,60], nine data sets by\n61, 62]. Regarding the reduction of volume, different training methods were usually applied,\nsuch as a progressive reduction in training distance, a step reduction in both training duration\nand frequency, and a step reduction in training hours during tapering days. Training fre-\ndecreased in seven. ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ig 3),\nwith a low level of heterogeneity (I2 = 31%, P > 0.1). Begg and Egger tests results showed no\nsignificant publication bias in the experimental group for TT (P > 0.1).\nSubgroup analysis with or without overload training before taper showed sign"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0006_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "6800aef3c9424728", "text_span_hash": "6800aef3c9424728", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ining before taper showed significantly\nlower heterogeneity in both subgroups, with I2 = 0% (P > 0.1) in both the overload training\ngroup and conventional taper group. This indicated that pre-taper overload training might\nhave been the primary source", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Characteristics of included studies\nA total of 2309 studies were identified with an additional 6 studies included from other\nsources. After excluding duplicates and screening the title, abstract, or full text, we ultimately\nincluded 14 studies in this meta-analysis (Fig 1). These 14 studies included 174 athletes, aged\n17–32 years. Their sports were mainly middle-distance, long-distance, and ultra distance run-\nning, as well as cycling. We extracted TT data from all 14 studies, 4 studies provided TTE data,\n8 studies provided _VO2max data, and 6 studies provided EM data (Table 1). In the included\nstudy, TT was measured at different distances, ranging from 800 m to 40 km. Performance\nwas assessed on the track, treadmill, and cyclcle ergometer in the selected studies.\nTable 1 shows the details of different tapering strategies included in this meta-analysis. Mul-\ntiple information from the same eligible study needs to be collated. Twenty-one data sets main-\nwhile two decreased it [16,55]. The range of training volume reduction was \u0014 85%. One data\nset reduced volume by \u0014 20% [16], four data sets by 21–40% [10,17,30,60], nine data sets by\n61, 62]. Regarding the reduction of volume, different training methods were usually applied,\nsuch as a progressive reduction in training distance, a step reduction in both training duration\nand frequency, and a step reduction in training hours during tapering days. Training fre-\ndecreased in seven. ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ining before taper showed significantly\nlower heterogeneity in both subgroups, with I2 = 0% (P > 0.1) in both the overload training\ngroup and conventional taper group. This indicated that pre-taper overload training might\nhave been the primary source"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0006_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8f7ec6c69be8463d", "text_span_hash": "8f7ec6c69be8463d", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "t\nhave been the primary source of heterogeneity. We observed a significant difference between\ngroups when we compared the overload training group with the conventional taper group\nTo further explore potential variables affecting TT improvement in end", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Characteristics of included studies\nA total of 2309 studies were identified with an additional 6 studies included from other\nsources. After excluding duplicates and screening the title, abstract, or full text, we ultimately\nincluded 14 studies in this meta-analysis (Fig 1). These 14 studies included 174 athletes, aged\n17–32 years. Their sports were mainly middle-distance, long-distance, and ultra distance run-\nning, as well as cycling. We extracted TT data from all 14 studies, 4 studies provided TTE data,\n8 studies provided _VO2max data, and 6 studies provided EM data (Table 1). In the included\nstudy, TT was measured at different distances, ranging from 800 m to 40 km. Performance\nwas assessed on the track, treadmill, and cyclcle ergometer in the selected studies.\nTable 1 shows the details of different tapering strategies included in this meta-analysis. Mul-\ntiple information from the same eligible study needs to be collated. Twenty-one data sets main-\nwhile two decreased it [16,55]. The range of training volume reduction was \u0014 85%. One data\nset reduced volume by \u0014 20% [16], four data sets by 21–40% [10,17,30,60], nine data sets by\n61, 62]. Regarding the reduction of volume, different training methods were usually applied,\nsuch as a progressive reduction in training distance, a step reduction in both training duration\nand frequency, and a step reduction in training hours during tapering days. Training fre-\ndecreased in seven. ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "t\nhave been the primary source of heterogeneity. We observed a significant difference between\ngroups when we compared the overload training group with the conventional taper group\nTo further explore potential variables affecting TT improvement in end"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0006_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "506d037de830626b", "text_span_hash": "506d037de830626b", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ffecting TT improvement in endurance athletes, we\nconducted a subgroup analysis of training intensity, volume, frequency, duration of taper, and\nEffects of tapering on performance in endurance athletes", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Characteristics of included studies\nA total of 2309 studies were identified with an additional 6 studies included from other\nsources. After excluding duplicates and screening the title, abstract, or full text, we ultimately\nincluded 14 studies in this meta-analysis (Fig 1). These 14 studies included 174 athletes, aged\n17–32 years. Their sports were mainly middle-distance, long-distance, and ultra distance run-\nning, as well as cycling. We extracted TT data from all 14 studies, 4 studies provided TTE data,\n8 studies provided _VO2max data, and 6 studies provided EM data (Table 1). In the included\nstudy, TT was measured at different distances, ranging from 800 m to 40 km. Performance\nwas assessed on the track, treadmill, and cyclcle ergometer in the selected studies.\nTable 1 shows the details of different tapering strategies included in this meta-analysis. Mul-\ntiple information from the same eligible study needs to be collated. Twenty-one data sets main-\nwhile two decreased it [16,55]. The range of training volume reduction was \u0014 85%. One data\nset reduced volume by \u0014 20% [16], four data sets by 21–40% [10,17,30,60], nine data sets by\n61, 62]. Regarding the reduction of volume, different training methods were usually applied,\nsuch as a progressive reduction in training distance, a step reduction in both training duration\nand frequency, and a step reduction in training hours during tapering days. Training fre-\ndecreased in seven. ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ffecting TT improvement in endurance athletes, we\nconducted a subgroup analysis of training intensity, volume, frequency, duration of taper, and\nEffects of tapering on performance in endurance athletes"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0007_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f21947cb28eecb38", "text_span_hash": "f21947cb28eecb38", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Fig 1. Flow chart of study selection.\nFig 2. Forest plot of effect size between pre- and post-tapering for TT.\nEffects of tapering on performance in endurance athletes", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Fig 1. Flow chart of study selection.\nFig 2. Forest plot of effect size between pre- and post-tapering for TT.\nEffects of tapering on performance in endurance athletes", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Fig 1. Flow chart of study selection.\nFig 2. Forest plot of effect size between pre- and post-tapering for TT.\nEffects of tapering on performance in endurance athletes"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0008_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e7776e190f2df5b8", "text_span_hash": "e7776e190f2df5b8", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "type of taper pattern. The results showed that a tapering strategy with a reduction in training\nvolume of 41–60% that maintained training intensity and frequency, lasted \u0014 21 days, and\nused progressive or step tapering could significantly improve the", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "type of taper pattern. The results showed that a tapering strategy with a reduction in training\nvolume of 41–60% that maintained training intensity and frequency, lasted \u0014 21 days, and\nused progressive or step tapering could significantly improve the TT of endurance athletes\n1. Training intensity subgroup analysis showed that maintaining training intensity had a\nlarger and significant overall effect on TT improvement (SMD, −0.55; 95% CI, −0.79 to\n−0.31; P < 0.05), while decreasing training intensity did not improve TT (SMD, 0.25; 95%\n2. Training volume subgroup analysis showed that a reduction in training volume of 41–60%\nachieved the largest and most significant improvement in TT (SMD, −0.77; 95% CI, −1.23\nto −0.30; P < 0.05). The overall effects for the \u0014 20%, 21–40%, and \u0015 60% groups were\nsmaller and did not reach significance levels (P > 0.05).\n3. Training frequency subgroup analysis showed a significant improvement in TT in the sub-\ngroups in which training frequency was unchanged (SMD, −0.53; 95% CI, −0.82 to −0.25;\n4. Subgroup analysis of taper duration showed that the subgroup with duration of 8–14-days\nexperienced the largest overall effect (SMD, −1.47; 95% CI, −2.75 to −0.19; P < 0.05), signif-\nicantly better than the other subgroups. The 15–21-day group experienced the next-largest\neffect, and the smallest was seen in the \u0015 22-day group. All three subgroups with durations\nof \u0014 21 days significantly improved their TT", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "type of taper pattern. The results showed that a tapering strategy with a reduction in training\nvolume of 41–60% that maintained training intensity and frequency, lasted \u0014 21 days, and\nused progressive or step tapering could significantly improve the"}
