{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0008_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ea2cbc38471c9b45", "text_span_hash": "ea2cbc38471c9b45", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "of effect size between experimental and control athletes for TT.\nFig 4. Forest plot of effect size between overload training group and conventional tapering group athletes for TT.\nEffects of tapering on performance in endurance athletes", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "type of taper pattern. The results showed that a tapering strategy with a reduction in training\nvolume of 41–60% that maintained training intensity and frequency, lasted \u0014 21 days, and\nused progressive or step tapering could significantly improve the TT of endurance athletes\n1. Training intensity subgroup analysis showed that maintaining training intensity had a\nlarger and significant overall effect on TT improvement (SMD, −0.55; 95% CI, −0.79 to\n−0.31; P < 0.05), while decreasing training intensity did not improve TT (SMD, 0.25; 95%\n2. Training volume subgroup analysis showed that a reduction in training volume of 41–60%\nachieved the largest and most significant improvement in TT (SMD, −0.77; 95% CI, −1.23\nto −0.30; P < 0.05). The overall effects for the \u0014 20%, 21–40%, and \u0015 60% groups were\nsmaller and did not reach significance levels (P > 0.05).\n3. Training frequency subgroup analysis showed a significant improvement in TT in the sub-\ngroups in which training frequency was unchanged (SMD, −0.53; 95% CI, −0.82 to −0.25;\n4. Subgroup analysis of taper duration showed that the subgroup with duration of 8–14-days\nexperienced the largest overall effect (SMD, −1.47; 95% CI, −2.75 to −0.19; P < 0.05), signif-\nicantly better than the other subgroups. The 15–21-day group experienced the next-largest\neffect, and the smallest was seen in the \u0015 22-day group. All three subgroups with durations\nof \u0014 21 days significantly improved their TT", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "of effect size between experimental and control athletes for TT.\nFig 4. Forest plot of effect size between overload training group and conventional tapering group athletes for TT.\nEffects of tapering on performance in endurance athletes"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0009_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8fcdc15a2f470dda", "text_span_hash": "8fcdc15a2f470dda", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "5. Subgroup analysis of tapering type showed that the progressive-taper subgroup saw a larger\nand more significant improvement in TT (SMD, −0.51; 95% CI, −0.81 to −0.20; P < 0.05).\nThere was also significant effect of the step taper on change in TT p", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "5. Subgroup analysis of tapering type showed that the progressive-taper subgroup saw a larger\nand more significant improvement in TT (SMD, −0.51; 95% CI, −0.81 to −0.20; P < 0.05).\nThere was also significant effect of the step taper on change in TT performance(P < 0.05).\nFour studies reported data for TTE. Moderate levels of heterogeneity\nexisted among these studies (I2 = 64%, P < 0.1). Results for overall effects on TTE showed a\nsignificant improvement between pre- and post-tapering (SMD, 1.28; 95% CI, 0.43 to 2.12;\nMaximal oxygen consumption.\nSix studies reported data for _VO2max. We observed high\nlevels of heterogeneity among these studies (I2 = 87%, P < 0.1). Results showed that there were\nno improvement on _VO2max between pre- and post-tapering (SMD, 0.20; 95% CI, −0.93 to\n1.33; P > 0.05; Fig 6). However, the studies with both groups showed a significant improve-\nment in the experimental groups compared with the control groups (SMD, 0.77; 95% CI, 0.31–\n1.23; P < 0.05; Fig 6), with a low level of heterogeneity (I2 = 0%, P > 0.1).\nEconomy of movement.\nFour studies reported data for EM, showing moderate levels het-\nerogeneity among them (I2 = 57%, P < 0.1). Results showed that there were no improvement\non EM between pre- and post-tapering (SMD, −0.47; 95% CI, −1.06 to 0.12; P > 0.05; Fig 7).\nTable 2. Subgroup analysis of the effects of tapering on TT.\nFig 5. Forest plot of effect size between pre- and post-tapering for TTE.\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "5. Subgroup analysis of tapering type showed that the progressive-taper subgroup saw a larger\nand more significant improvement in TT (SMD, −0.51; 95% CI, −0.81 to −0.20; P < 0.05).\nThere was also significant effect of the step taper on change in TT p"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0009_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "98384ee50a96cab7", "text_span_hash": "98384ee50a96cab7", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "e step taper on change in TT performance(P < 0.05).\nFour studies reported data for TTE. Moderate levels of heterogeneity\nexisted among these studies (I2 = 64%, P < 0.1). Results for overall effects on TTE showed a\nsignificant improvement between pre-", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "5. Subgroup analysis of tapering type showed that the progressive-taper subgroup saw a larger\nand more significant improvement in TT (SMD, −0.51; 95% CI, −0.81 to −0.20; P < 0.05).\nThere was also significant effect of the step taper on change in TT performance(P < 0.05).\nFour studies reported data for TTE. Moderate levels of heterogeneity\nexisted among these studies (I2 = 64%, P < 0.1). Results for overall effects on TTE showed a\nsignificant improvement between pre- and post-tapering (SMD, 1.28; 95% CI, 0.43 to 2.12;\nMaximal oxygen consumption.\nSix studies reported data for _VO2max. We observed high\nlevels of heterogeneity among these studies (I2 = 87%, P < 0.1). Results showed that there were\nno improvement on _VO2max between pre- and post-tapering (SMD, 0.20; 95% CI, −0.93 to\n1.33; P > 0.05; Fig 6). However, the studies with both groups showed a significant improve-\nment in the experimental groups compared with the control groups (SMD, 0.77; 95% CI, 0.31–\n1.23; P < 0.05; Fig 6), with a low level of heterogeneity (I2 = 0%, P > 0.1).\nEconomy of movement.\nFour studies reported data for EM, showing moderate levels het-\nerogeneity among them (I2 = 57%, P < 0.1). Results showed that there were no improvement\non EM between pre- and post-tapering (SMD, −0.47; 95% CI, −1.06 to 0.12; P > 0.05; Fig 7).\nTable 2. Subgroup analysis of the effects of tapering on TT.\nFig 5. Forest plot of effect size between pre- and post-tapering for TTE.\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "e step taper on change in TT performance(P < 0.05).\nFour studies reported data for TTE. Moderate levels of heterogeneity\nexisted among these studies (I2 = 64%, P < 0.1). Results for overall effects on TTE showed a\nsignificant improvement between pre-"}
