ts showed that there were no improvement\non EM between pre- and post-tapering (SMD, −0.47; 95% CI, −1.06 to 0.12; P > 0.05; Fig 7).\nTable 2. Subgroup analysis of the effects of tapering on TT.\nFig 5. Forest plot of effect size between pre- and post-tapering for TTE.\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": ", P < 0.1). Results showed that there were\nno improvement on _VO2max between pre- and post-tapering (SMD, 0.20; 95% CI, −0.93 to\n1.33; P > 0.05; Fig 6). However, the studies with both groups showed a significant improve-\nment in the experimental grou"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0009_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "bb6cb74649ae4063", "text_span_hash": "bb6cb74649ae4063", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "r EM, showing moderate levels het-\nerogeneity among them (I2 = 57%, P < 0.1). Results showed that there were no improvement\non EM between pre- and post-tapering (SMD, −0.47; 95% CI, −1.06 to 0.12; P > 0.05; Fig 7).\nTable 2. Subgroup analysis of the e", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "5. Subgroup analysis of tapering type showed that the progressive-taper subgroup saw a larger\nand more significant improvement in TT (SMD, −0.51; 95% CI, −0.81 to −0.20; P < 0.05).\nThere was also significant effect of the step taper on change in TT performance(P < 0.05).\nFour studies reported data for TTE. Moderate levels of heterogeneity\nexisted among these studies (I2 = 64%, P < 0.1). Results for overall effects on TTE showed a\nsignificant improvement between pre- and post-tapering (SMD, 1.28; 95% CI, 0.43 to 2.12;\nMaximal oxygen consumption.\nSix studies reported data for _VO2max. We observed high\nlevels of heterogeneity among these studies (I2 = 87%, P < 0.1). Results showed that there were\nno improvement on _VO2max between pre- and post-tapering (SMD, 0.20; 95% CI, −0.93 to\n1.33; P > 0.05; Fig 6). However, the studies with both groups showed a significant improve-\nment in the experimental groups compared with the control groups (SMD, 0.77; 95% CI, 0.31–\n1.23; P < 0.05; Fig 6), with a low level of heterogeneity (I2 = 0%, P > 0.1).\nEconomy of movement.\nFour studies reported data for EM, showing moderate levels het-\nerogeneity among them (I2 = 57%, P < 0.1). Results showed that there were no improvement\non EM between pre- and post-tapering (SMD, −0.47; 95% CI, −1.06 to 0.12; P > 0.05; Fig 7).\nTable 2. Subgroup analysis of the effects of tapering on TT.\nFig 5. Forest plot of effect size between pre- and post-tapering for TTE.\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "r EM, showing moderate levels het-\nerogeneity among them (I2 = 57%, P < 0.1). Results showed that there were no improvement\non EM between pre- and post-tapering (SMD, −0.47; 95% CI, −1.06 to 0.12; P > 0.05; Fig 7).\nTable 2. Subgroup analysis of the e"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0009_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "d032abc82826880a", "text_span_hash": "d032abc82826880a", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "2. Subgroup analysis of the effects of tapering on TT.\nFig 5. Forest plot of effect size between pre- and post-tapering for TTE.\nEffects of tapering on performance in endurance athletes", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "5. Subgroup analysis of tapering type showed that the progressive-taper subgroup saw a larger\nand more significant improvement in TT (SMD, −0.51; 95% CI, −0.81 to −0.20; P < 0.05).\nThere was also significant effect of the step taper on change in TT performance(P < 0.05).\nFour studies reported data for TTE. Moderate levels of heterogeneity\nexisted among these studies (I2 = 64%, P < 0.1). Results for overall effects on TTE showed a\nsignificant improvement between pre- and post-tapering (SMD, 1.28; 95% CI, 0.43 to 2.12;\nMaximal oxygen consumption.\nSix studies reported data for _VO2max. We observed high\nlevels of heterogeneity among these studies (I2 = 87%, P < 0.1). Results showed that there were\nno improvement on _VO2max between pre- and post-tapering (SMD, 0.20; 95% CI, −0.93 to\n1.33; P > 0.05; Fig 6). However, the studies with both groups showed a significant improve-\nment in the experimental groups compared with the control groups (SMD, 0.77; 95% CI, 0.31–\n1.23; P < 0.05; Fig 6), with a low level of heterogeneity (I2 = 0%, P > 0.1).\nEconomy of movement.\nFour studies reported data for EM, showing moderate levels het-\nerogeneity among them (I2 = 57%, P < 0.1). Results showed that there were no improvement\non EM between pre- and post-tapering (SMD, −0.47; 95% CI, −1.06 to 0.12; P > 0.05; Fig 7).\nTable 2. Subgroup analysis of the effects of tapering on TT.\nFig 5. Forest plot of effect size between pre- and post-tapering for TTE.\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "2. Subgroup analysis of the effects of tapering on TT.\nFig 5. Forest plot of effect size between pre- and post-tapering for TTE.\nEffects of tapering on performance in endurance athletes"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0010_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f0c137824f9faf9b", "text_span_hash": "f0c137824f9faf9b", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Summary of main result\nThe main finding of this meta-analysis was that pre-competition tapering significantly\nsubgroup analysis results showed that pre-competition tapering combined with pre-taper\noverload training had a significant effect on TT impr", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Summary of main result\nThe main finding of this meta-analysis was that pre-competition tapering significantly\nsubgroup analysis results showed that pre-competition tapering combined with pre-taper\noverload training had a significant effect on TT improvement (P < 0.05), which was superior\nto that of conventional tapering (P < 0.05 for the difference between groups). A tapering strat-\negy that reduced training volume progressively by 41–60%, maintained training intensity and\nfrequency, lasted \u001421 days could significantly improve TT. In endurance athletes, the primary\nmechanism for tapering to improve endurance sports performance is to maintain or further\nincrease aerobic exercise capacity while eliminating the psychological and physiological stress\naccumulated from training [23,27]. For example, cardiac pump function and work efficiency\nimproved [10,65]; blood testosterone and blood testosterone/cortisol ratio improved\nFig 6. Forest plot of effect size between pre- and post-tapering for _V_O2max (A), and between experimental and control\nathletes for _V_O2max (B).\nFig 7. Forest plot of effect size between post and pre-tapering for EM.\nEffects of tapering on performance in endurance athletes", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Summary of main result\nThe main finding of this meta-analysis was that pre-competition tapering significantly\nsubgroup analysis results showed that pre-competition tapering combined with pre-taper\noverload training had a significant effect on TT impr"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0010_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5db23f4e12ede224", "text_span_hash": "5db23f4e12ede224", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "significant effect on TT improvement (P < 0.05), which was superior\nto that of conventional tapering (P < 0.05 for the difference between groups). A tapering strat-\negy that reduced training volume progressively by 41–60%, maintained training intens", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Summary of main result\nThe main finding of this meta-analysis was that pre-competition tapering significantly\nsubgroup analysis results showed that pre-competition tapering combined with pre-taper\noverload training had a significant effect on TT improvement (P < 0.05), which was superior\nto that of conventional tapering (P < 0.05 for the difference between groups). A tapering strat-\negy that reduced training volume progressively by 41–60%, maintained training intensity and\nfrequency, lasted \u001421 days could significantly improve TT. In endurance athletes, the primary\nmechanism for tapering to improve endurance sports performance is to maintain or further\nincrease aerobic exercise capacity while eliminating the psychological and physiological stress\naccumulated from training [23,27]. For example, cardiac pump function and work efficiency\nimproved [10,65]; blood testosterone and blood testosterone/cortisol ratio improved\nFig 6. Forest plot of effect size between pre- and post-tapering for _V_O2max (A), and between experimental and control\nathletes for _V_O2max (B).\nFig 7. Forest plot of effect size between post and pre-tapering for EM.\nEffects of tapering on performance in endurance athletes", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "significant effect on TT improvement (P < 0.05), which was superior\nto that of conventional tapering (P < 0.05 for the difference between groups). A tapering strat-\negy that reduced training volume progressively by 41–60%, maintained training intens"}
