e accu-\nmulation. TTE reflects the fatigue tolerance of these athletes [33], which is closely related to\nperformance [37]. The increase in TTE implied that the taper played a significant role in pro-\nmoting the regulation and recovery of fatigue, improving the body’", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "he previous overload,\ntraining could be performed at a higher intensity in the later taper phase [4]. Therefore, some\nstudies have aeeerted that when training volume is dramatically decreased, high-intensity\nEffects of tapering on performance in endu"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0011_c0018", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "41043bb3f356a770", "text_span_hash": "41043bb3f356a770", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 11, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 18, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "apering on performance in endurance athletes", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "significantly, whereas blood creatine kinase concentration and cortisol concentration\ndecreased significantly [57,58,66]; heart rate to rating of perceived exertion (RPE) ratio\ndecreased [10], and emotional state [67,68] improved. In addition, studies have shown that\ntapering in endurance athletes significantly increases blood volume, hemoglobin, and erythro-\ncyte pressure volume [1], and improves mitochondrial respiratory function [68]. The improve-\nment of these indicators enables the improvement of the oxygen-carrying and oxygen-\ntransport capacity, maintaining a higher percentage of oxygen utilization capacity, which ulti-\nmately leads to an increase in endurance performance. The results of the present meta-analysis\nshowed that there was no effect of pre-competition taper on _VO2max (SMD, 0.20; 95% CI,\nimprovement was found for TTE (SMD, 1.28; 95% CI, 0.43–0.12; P < 0.05). The present study\nconfirms previous findings. Busso et al. [2] indicated that long-term high-intensity sports\ntraining leads to a continuous reduction in endurance athletes’ training-induced adaptability,\nwhich is often caused by a decline in aerobic exercise fatigue tolerance caused by fatigue accu-\nmulation. TTE reflects the fatigue tolerance of these athletes [33], which is closely related to\nperformance [37]. The increase in TTE implied that the taper played a significant role in pro-\nmoting the regulation and recovery of fatigue, improving the body’", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "apering on performance in endurance athletes"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0012_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b1d9c46771f0c8c7", "text_span_hash": "b1d9c46771f0c8c7", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "training (HIT) is crucial for maintaining and improving physical fitness and performance dur-\ning tapering [27,73–75]. Physiological responses to this strategy may include increases in buff-\nering capacity [56], oxidase activity, red blood cell volum", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "training (HIT) is crucial for maintaining and improving physical fitness and performance dur-\ning tapering [27,73–75]. Physiological responses to this strategy may include increases in buff-\nering capacity [56], oxidase activity, red blood cell volume, and muscle glycogen concentration\n[47], all of which may contribute to improved performance during a subsequent events. This is\nsupported by training data from the most successful female cross-country skier globally. Solli\net al. [64] reported that the proportion of HIT progressive increased in the the 3 weeks before\nthe major competition, with three HIT sessions completed in the last 7 days. From the perspec-\ntive of the intensity distribution characteristics included in this meta-analysis, we found that\nmost pre-competition tapering strategies in the research used HIT. The distribution of training\nintensity in the included studies was characterized by alternation among HIT, moderate-inten-\nsity continuous training (MICT), and low-intensity continuous training (LICT). However, the\nincluded studies did not detail the intensity distribution characteristics. Generally speaking, as\nthe competition day was approaching, endurance athletes tended to reduce the duration of\nexercise, and increase the number of exercises of a specific intensity to further increase HIT\nintensity to that of race pace [19], 90% _VO2max [17,76], or > 90% HRmax [59,77]. In general,\nintensity distribution grew", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "training (HIT) is crucial for maintaining and improving physical fitness and performance dur-\ning tapering [27,73–75]. Physiological responses to this strategy may include increases in buff-\nering capacity [56], oxidase activity, red blood cell volum"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0012_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f487f00a13655446", "text_span_hash": "f487f00a13655446", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "rspec-\ntive of the intensity distribution characteristics included in this meta-analysis, we found that\nmost pre-competition tapering strategies in the research used HIT. The distribution of training\nintensity in the included studies was characterize", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "training (HIT) is crucial for maintaining and improving physical fitness and performance dur-\ning tapering [27,73–75]. Physiological responses to this strategy may include increases in buff-\nering capacity [56], oxidase activity, red blood cell volume, and muscle glycogen concentration\n[47], all of which may contribute to improved performance during a subsequent events. This is\nsupported by training data from the most successful female cross-country skier globally. Solli\net al. [64] reported that the proportion of HIT progressive increased in the the 3 weeks before\nthe major competition, with three HIT sessions completed in the last 7 days. From the perspec-\ntive of the intensity distribution characteristics included in this meta-analysis, we found that\nmost pre-competition tapering strategies in the research used HIT. The distribution of training\nintensity in the included studies was characterized by alternation among HIT, moderate-inten-\nsity continuous training (MICT), and low-intensity continuous training (LICT). However, the\nincluded studies did not detail the intensity distribution characteristics. Generally speaking, as\nthe competition day was approaching, endurance athletes tended to reduce the duration of\nexercise, and increase the number of exercises of a specific intensity to further increase HIT\nintensity to that of race pace [19], 90% _VO2max [17,76], or > 90% HRmax [59,77]. In general,\nintensity distribution grew", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "rspec-\ntive of the intensity distribution characteristics included in this meta-analysis, we found that\nmost pre-competition tapering strategies in the research used HIT. The distribution of training\nintensity in the included studies was characterize"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0012_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "602f7a02d8a3225d", "text_span_hash": "602f7a02d8a3225d", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "luded studies was characterized by alternation among HIT, moderate-inten-\nsity continuous training (MICT), and low-intensity continuous training (LICT). However, the\nincluded studies did not detail the intensity distribution characteristics. Generall", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "training (HIT) is crucial for maintaining and improving physical fitness and performance dur-\ning tapering [27,73–75]. Physiological responses to this strategy may include increases in buff-\nering capacity [56], oxidase activity, red blood cell volume, and muscle glycogen concentration\n[47], all of which may contribute to improved performance during a subsequent events. This is\nsupported by training data from the most successful female cross-country skier globally. Solli\net al. [64] reported that the proportion of HIT progressive increased in the the 3 weeks before\nthe major competition, with three HIT sessions completed in the last 7 days. From the perspec-\ntive of the intensity distribution characteristics included in this meta-analysis, we found that\nmost pre-competition tapering strategies in the research used HIT. The distribution of training\nintensity in the included studies was characterized by alternation among HIT, moderate-inten-\nsity continuous training (MICT), and low-intensity continuous training (LICT). However, the\nincluded studies did not detail the intensity distribution characteristics. Generally speaking, as\nthe competition day was approaching, endurance athletes tended to reduce the duration of\nexercise, and increase the number of exercises of a specific intensity to further increase HIT\nintensity to that of race pace [19], 90% _VO2max [17,76], or > 90% HRmax [59,77]. In general,\nintensity distribution grew", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "luded studies was characterized by alternation among HIT, moderate-inten-\nsity continuous training (MICT), and low-intensity continuous training (LICT). However, the\nincluded studies did not detail the intensity distribution characteristics. Generall"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0012_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8ef7c0512c63b16e", "text_span_hash": "8ef7c0512c63b16e", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "tion characteristics. Generally speaking, as\nthe competition day was approaching, endurance athletes tended to reduce the duration of\nexercise, and increase the number of exercises of a specific intensity to further increase HIT\nintensity to that of", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "training (HIT) is crucial for maintaining and improving physical fitness and performance dur-\ning tapering [27,73–75]. Physiological responses to this strategy may include increases in buff-\nering capacity [56], oxidase activity, red blood cell volume, and muscle glycogen concentration\n[47], all of which may contribute to improved performance during a subsequent events. This is\nsupported by training data from the most successful female cross-country skier globally. Solli\net al. [64] reported that the proportion of HIT progressive increased in the the 3 weeks before\nthe major competition, with three HIT sessions completed in the last 7 days. From the perspec-\ntive of the intensity distribution characteristics included in this meta-analysis, we found that\nmost pre-competition tapering strategies in the research used HIT. The distribution of training\nintensity in the included studies was characterized by alternation among HIT, moderate-inten-\nsity continuous training (MICT), and low-intensity continuous training (LICT). However, the\nincluded studies did not detail the intensity distribution characteristics. Generally speaking, as\nthe competition day was approaching, endurance athletes tended to reduce the duration of\nexercise, and increase the number of exercises of a specific intensity to further increase HIT\nintensity to that of race pace [19], 90% _VO2max [17,76], or > 90% HRmax [59,77]. In general,\nintensity distribution grew", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "tion characteristics. Generally speaking, as\nthe competition day was approaching, endurance athletes tended to reduce the duration of\nexercise, and increase the number of exercises of a specific intensity to further increase HIT\nintensity to that of"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0012_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "6e27aa0d2906a567", "text_span_hash": "6e27aa0d2906a567", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ease HIT\nintensity to that of race pace [19], 90% _VO2max [17,76], or > 90% HRmax [59,77]. In general,\nintensity distribution grew more polarized as the major competition approached [76], espe-\ncially for endurance events such as long- and ultra-long", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "training (HIT) is crucial for maintaining and improving physical fitness and performance dur-\ning tapering [27,73–75]. Physiological responses to this strategy may include increases in buff-\nering capacity [56], oxidase activity, red blood cell volume, and muscle glycogen concentration\n[47], all of which may contribute to improved performance during a subsequent events. This is\nsupported by training data from the most successful female cross-country skier globally. Solli\net al. [64] reported that the proportion of HIT progressive increased in the the 3 weeks before\nthe major competition, with three HIT sessions completed in the last 7 days. From the perspec-\ntive of the intensity distribution characteristics included in this meta-analysis, we found that\nmost pre-competition tapering strategies in the research used HIT. The distribution of training\nintensity in the included studies was characterized by alternation among HIT, moderate-inten-\nsity continuous training (MICT), and low-intensity continuous training (LICT). However, the\nincluded studies did not detail the intensity distribution characteristics. Generally speaking, as\nthe competition day was approaching, endurance athletes tended to reduce the duration of\nexercise, and increase the number of exercises of a specific intensity to further increase HIT\nintensity to that of race pace [19], 90% _VO2max [17,76], or > 90% HRmax [59,77]. In general,\nintensity distribution grew", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ease HIT\nintensity to that of race pace [19], 90% _VO2max [17,76], or > 90% HRmax [59,77]. In general,\nintensity distribution grew more polarized as the major competition approached [76], espe-\ncially for endurance events such as long- and ultra-long"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0012_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2a693b85cdc83f2b", "text_span_hash": "2a693b85cdc83f2b", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "s such as long- and ultra-long-distance running. Some middle-dis-\ntance events used HIT with a higher intensity than race pace (115%) for training during the\ntapering [29]. However, HIT at a higher intensity than race pace was less effective in impro", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "training (HIT) is crucial for maintaining and improving physical fitness and performance dur-\ning tapering [27,73–75]. Physiological responses to this strategy may include increases in buff-\nering capacity [56], oxidase activity, red blood cell volume, and muscle glycogen concentration\n[47], all of which may contribute to improved performance during a subsequent events. This is\nsupported by training data from the most successful female cross-country skier globally. Solli\net al. [64] reported that the proportion of HIT progressive increased in the the 3 weeks before\nthe major competition, with three HIT sessions completed in the last 7 days. From the perspec-\ntive of the intensity distribution characteristics included in this meta-analysis, we found that\nmost pre-competition tapering strategies in the research used HIT. The distribution of training\nintensity in the included studies was characterized by alternation among HIT, moderate-inten-\nsity continuous training (MICT), and low-intensity continuous training (LICT). However, the\nincluded studies did not detail the intensity distribution characteristics. Generally speaking, as\nthe competition day was approaching, endurance athletes tended to reduce the duration of\nexercise, and increase the number of exercises of a specific intensity to further increase HIT\nintensity to that of race pace [19], 90% _VO2max [17,76], or > 90% HRmax [59,77]. In general,\nintensity distribution grew", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "s such as long- and ultra-long-distance running. Some middle-dis-\ntance events used HIT with a higher intensity than race pace (115%) for training during the\ntapering [29]. However, HIT at a higher intensity than race pace was less effective in impro"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0012_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "6b23b6ce89556f42", "text_span_hash": "6b23b6ce89556f42", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ce was less effective in improving\nTT than HIT at race pace [29]. For middle-distance events, race pace is already relatively high\nin intensity, using a continuous HIT that exceeds race pace for pre-competition training can\nincrease the risk of injur", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "training (HIT) is crucial for maintaining and improving physical fitness and performance dur-\ning tapering [27,73–75]. Physiological responses to this strategy may include increases in buff-\nering capacity [56], oxidase activity, red blood cell volume, and muscle glycogen concentration\n[47], all of which may contribute to improved performance during a subsequent events. This is\nsupported by training data from the most successful female cross-country skier globally. Solli\net al. [64] reported that the proportion of HIT progressive increased in the the 3 weeks before\nthe major competition, with three HIT sessions completed in the last 7 days. From the perspec-\ntive of the intensity distribution characteristics included in this meta-analysis, we found that\nmost pre-competition tapering strategies in the research used HIT. The distribution of training\nintensity in the included studies was characterized by alternation among HIT, moderate-inten-\nsity continuous training (MICT), and low-intensity continuous training (LICT). However, the\nincluded studies did not detail the intensity distribution characteristics. Generally speaking, as\nthe competition day was approaching, endurance athletes tended to reduce the duration of\nexercise, and increase the number of exercises of a specific intensity to further increase HIT\nintensity to that of race pace [19], 90% _VO2max [17,76], or > 90% HRmax [59,77]. In general,\nintensity distribution grew", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ce was less effective in improving\nTT than HIT at race pace [29]. For middle-distance events, race pace is already relatively high\nin intensity, using a continuous HIT that exceeds race pace for pre-competition training can\nincrease the risk of injur"}
