g, endurance athletes tended to reduce the duration of\nexercise, and increase the number of exercises of a specific intensity to further increase HIT\nintensity to that of race pace [19], 90% _VO2max [17,76], or > 90% HRmax [59,77]. In general,\nintensity distribution grew", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ue in a timely and rapid manner [80]. In\ncomparison, a reduction in volume of \u0015 60% had no significant effect on TT improvement,\nwhich was related to the lack of quantity and quality of endurance training due to a larger vol-\nume reduction [13]. Acco"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0012_c0019", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "558b714138cf43c5", "text_span_hash": "558b714138cf43c5", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 19, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ound by reducing volume up to 50% before a competition. Neary\net al. [10] performed a comparative study with three reductions in training volume—30%,\n50%, and 80%—in cyclists and concluded that VO2 and O2 pulse were optimized only with a\n50% reductio", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "training (HIT) is crucial for maintaining and improving physical fitness and performance dur-\ning tapering [27,73–75]. Physiological responses to this strategy may include increases in buff-\nering capacity [56], oxidase activity, red blood cell volume, and muscle glycogen concentration\n[47], all of which may contribute to improved performance during a subsequent events. This is\nsupported by training data from the most successful female cross-country skier globally. Solli\net al. [64] reported that the proportion of HIT progressive increased in the the 3 weeks before\nthe major competition, with three HIT sessions completed in the last 7 days. From the perspec-\ntive of the intensity distribution characteristics included in this meta-analysis, we found that\nmost pre-competition tapering strategies in the research used HIT. The distribution of training\nintensity in the included studies was characterized by alternation among HIT, moderate-inten-\nsity continuous training (MICT), and low-intensity continuous training (LICT). However, the\nincluded studies did not detail the intensity distribution characteristics. Generally speaking, as\nthe competition day was approaching, endurance athletes tended to reduce the duration of\nexercise, and increase the number of exercises of a specific intensity to further increase HIT\nintensity to that of race pace [19], 90% _VO2max [17,76], or > 90% HRmax [59,77]. In general,\nintensity distribution grew", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ound by reducing volume up to 50% before a competition. Neary\net al. [10] performed a comparative study with three reductions in training volume—30%,\n50%, and 80%—in cyclists and concluded that VO2 and O2 pulse were optimized only with a\n50% reductio"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0012_c0020", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "fbb5714c265b1cfb", "text_span_hash": "fbb5714c265b1cfb", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 20, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "mized only with a\n50% reduction in training volume. A meta-analysis by Bosquet et al. [27] also found that a 21–\n40% reduction in volume also significantly affected TT improvement (P < 0.05). This might\nEffects of tapering on performance in endurance", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "training (HIT) is crucial for maintaining and improving physical fitness and performance dur-\ning tapering [27,73–75]. Physiological responses to this strategy may include increases in buff-\nering capacity [56], oxidase activity, red blood cell volume, and muscle glycogen concentration\n[47], all of which may contribute to improved performance during a subsequent events. This is\nsupported by training data from the most successful female cross-country skier globally. Solli\net al. [64] reported that the proportion of HIT progressive increased in the the 3 weeks before\nthe major competition, with three HIT sessions completed in the last 7 days. From the perspec-\ntive of the intensity distribution characteristics included in this meta-analysis, we found that\nmost pre-competition tapering strategies in the research used HIT. The distribution of training\nintensity in the included studies was characterized by alternation among HIT, moderate-inten-\nsity continuous training (MICT), and low-intensity continuous training (LICT). However, the\nincluded studies did not detail the intensity distribution characteristics. Generally speaking, as\nthe competition day was approaching, endurance athletes tended to reduce the duration of\nexercise, and increase the number of exercises of a specific intensity to further increase HIT\nintensity to that of race pace [19], 90% _VO2max [17,76], or > 90% HRmax [59,77]. In general,\nintensity distribution grew", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "mized only with a\n50% reduction in training volume. A meta-analysis by Bosquet et al. [27] also found that a 21–\n40% reduction in volume also significantly affected TT improvement (P < 0.05). This might\nEffects of tapering on performance in endurance"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0012_c0021", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "d72a54a03e1c28a8", "text_span_hash": "d72a54a03e1c28a8", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 12, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 21, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ng on performance in endurance athletes", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "training (HIT) is crucial for maintaining and improving physical fitness and performance dur-\ning tapering [27,73–75]. Physiological responses to this strategy may include increases in buff-\nering capacity [56], oxidase activity, red blood cell volume, and muscle glycogen concentration\n[47], all of which may contribute to improved performance during a subsequent events. This is\nsupported by training data from the most successful female cross-country skier globally. Solli\net al. [64] reported that the proportion of HIT progressive increased in the the 3 weeks before\nthe major competition, with three HIT sessions completed in the last 7 days. From the perspec-\ntive of the intensity distribution characteristics included in this meta-analysis, we found that\nmost pre-competition tapering strategies in the research used HIT. The distribution of training\nintensity in the included studies was characterized by alternation among HIT, moderate-inten-\nsity continuous training (MICT), and low-intensity continuous training (LICT). However, the\nincluded studies did not detail the intensity distribution characteristics. Generally speaking, as\nthe competition day was approaching, endurance athletes tended to reduce the duration of\nexercise, and increase the number of exercises of a specific intensity to further increase HIT\nintensity to that of race pace [19], 90% _VO2max [17,76], or > 90% HRmax [59,77]. In general,\nintensity distribution grew", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ng on performance in endurance athletes"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0013_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "71101121ea38c700", "text_span_hash": "71101121ea38c700", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "be because our meta-analysis only included endurance events, whereas the meta-analysis\ndeveloped by Bosquet et al. [27] included both speed events (249 athletes total) and endurance\nevents (190 athletes total). Li et al. [79] pointed out that pre-com", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "be because our meta-analysis only included endurance events, whereas the meta-analysis\ndeveloped by Bosquet et al. [27] included both speed events (249 athletes total) and endurance\nevents (190 athletes total). Li et al. [79] pointed out that pre-competition volume was reduced\nmore significantly for endurance events than for speed events. This showed that the range of\nvolume reduction could vary by sport events. Whether the volume reduction range differed by\ntype of endurance event (middle-distance, long-distance, ultra-long-distance) is unclear. To\nmaximize the effect of pre-competition volume reduction on sports performance and to make\nthe implementation of pre-competition taper strategies more pertinent and maneuverable,\nfuture research should further investigate differences in volume reduction between middle-\ndistance endurance and long-distance/ultra-long distance endurance events.\nTraining load can also be adjusted by changing training frequency. However,\nthe results of studies on the effect of such change on sports performance are inconsistent, and\nwhether a clear relationship exists between them is still unclear [79]. The pooled results of the\npresent meta-analysis showed that a decrease in training frequency had no significant effect on\nTT (SMD, −0.32; 95% CI, −0.76 to 0.13; P > 0.05) and that maintenance of training frequency\ncould significantly improve TT (SMD, −0.53; 95% CI, −0.83 to −0.25; P < 0.05). The reason\nmi", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "be because our meta-analysis only included endurance events, whereas the meta-analysis\ndeveloped by Bosquet et al. [27] included both speed events (249 athletes total) and endurance\nevents (190 athletes total). Li et al. [79] pointed out that pre-com"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0013_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "087687890673b246", "text_span_hash": "087687890673b246", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "[79] pointed out that pre-competition volume was reduced\nmore significantly for endurance events than for speed events. This showed that the range of\nvolume reduction could vary by sport events. Whether the volume reduction range differed by\ntype of", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "be because our meta-analysis only included endurance events, whereas the meta-analysis\ndeveloped by Bosquet et al. [27] included both speed events (249 athletes total) and endurance\nevents (190 athletes total). Li et al. [79] pointed out that pre-competition volume was reduced\nmore significantly for endurance events than for speed events. This showed that the range of\nvolume reduction could vary by sport events. Whether the volume reduction range differed by\ntype of endurance event (middle-distance, long-distance, ultra-long-distance) is unclear. To\nmaximize the effect of pre-competition volume reduction on sports performance and to make\nthe implementation of pre-competition taper strategies more pertinent and maneuverable,\nfuture research should further investigate differences in volume reduction between middle-\ndistance endurance and long-distance/ultra-long distance endurance events.\nTraining load can also be adjusted by changing training frequency. However,\nthe results of studies on the effect of such change on sports performance are inconsistent, and\nwhether a clear relationship exists between them is still unclear [79]. The pooled results of the\npresent meta-analysis showed that a decrease in training frequency had no significant effect on\nTT (SMD, −0.32; 95% CI, −0.76 to 0.13; P > 0.05) and that maintenance of training frequency\ncould significantly improve TT (SMD, −0.53; 95% CI, −0.83 to −0.25; P < 0.05). The reason\nmi", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "[79] pointed out that pre-competition volume was reduced\nmore significantly for endurance events than for speed events. This showed that the range of\nvolume reduction could vary by sport events. Whether the volume reduction range differed by\ntype of"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0013_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "eb6a634add8c933b", "text_span_hash": "eb6a634add8c933b", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "tion range differed by\ntype of endurance event (middle-distance, long-distance, ultra-long-distance) is unclear. To\nmaximize the effect of pre-competition volume reduction on sports performance and to make\nthe implementation of pre-competition taper", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "be because our meta-analysis only included endurance events, whereas the meta-analysis\ndeveloped by Bosquet et al. [27] included both speed events (249 athletes total) and endurance\nevents (190 athletes total). Li et al. [79] pointed out that pre-competition volume was reduced\nmore significantly for endurance events than for speed events. This showed that the range of\nvolume reduction could vary by sport events. Whether the volume reduction range differed by\ntype of endurance event (middle-distance, long-distance, ultra-long-distance) is unclear. To\nmaximize the effect of pre-competition volume reduction on sports performance and to make\nthe implementation of pre-competition taper strategies more pertinent and maneuverable,\nfuture research should further investigate differences in volume reduction between middle-\ndistance endurance and long-distance/ultra-long distance endurance events.\nTraining load can also be adjusted by changing training frequency. However,\nthe results of studies on the effect of such change on sports performance are inconsistent, and\nwhether a clear relationship exists between them is still unclear [79]. The pooled results of the\npresent meta-analysis showed that a decrease in training frequency had no significant effect on\nTT (SMD, −0.32; 95% CI, −0.76 to 0.13; P > 0.05) and that maintenance of training frequency\ncould significantly improve TT (SMD, −0.53; 95% CI, −0.83 to −0.25; P < 0.05). The reason\nmi", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "tion range differed by\ntype of endurance event (middle-distance, long-distance, ultra-long-distance) is unclear. To\nmaximize the effect of pre-competition volume reduction on sports performance and to make\nthe implementation of pre-competition taper"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0013_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "01387b3948aceace", "text_span_hash": "01387b3948aceace", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "tion of pre-competition taper strategies more pertinent and maneuverable,\nfuture research should further investigate differences in volume reduction between middle-\ndistance endurance and long-distance/ultra-long distance endurance events.\nTraining l", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "be because our meta-analysis only included endurance events, whereas the meta-analysis\ndeveloped by Bosquet et al. [27] included both speed events (249 athletes total) and endurance\nevents (190 athletes total). Li et al. [79] pointed out that pre-competition volume was reduced\nmore significantly for endurance events than for speed events. This showed that the range of\nvolume reduction could vary by sport events. Whether the volume reduction range differed by\ntype of endurance event (middle-distance, long-distance, ultra-long-distance) is unclear. To\nmaximize the effect of pre-competition volume reduction on sports performance and to make\nthe implementation of pre-competition taper strategies more pertinent and maneuverable,\nfuture research should further investigate differences in volume reduction between middle-\ndistance endurance and long-distance/ultra-long distance endurance events.\nTraining load can also be adjusted by changing training frequency. However,\nthe results of studies on the effect of such change on sports performance are inconsistent, and\nwhether a clear relationship exists between them is still unclear [79]. The pooled results of the\npresent meta-analysis showed that a decrease in training frequency had no significant effect on\nTT (SMD, −0.32; 95% CI, −0.76 to 0.13; P > 0.05) and that maintenance of training frequency\ncould significantly improve TT (SMD, −0.53; 95% CI, −0.83 to −0.25; P < 0.05). The reason\nmi", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "tion of pre-competition taper strategies more pertinent and maneuverable,\nfuture research should further investigate differences in volume reduction between middle-\ndistance endurance and long-distance/ultra-long distance endurance events.\nTraining l"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0013_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2971cd9b2764e3e0", "text_span_hash": "2971cd9b2764e3e0", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "e endurance events.\nTraining load can also be adjusted by changing training frequency. However,\nthe results of studies on the effect of such change on sports performance are inconsistent, and\nwhether a clear relationship exists between them is still", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "be because our meta-analysis only included endurance events, whereas the meta-analysis\ndeveloped by Bosquet et al. [27] included both speed events (249 athletes total) and endurance\nevents (190 athletes total). Li et al. [79] pointed out that pre-competition volume was reduced\nmore significantly for endurance events than for speed events. This showed that the range of\nvolume reduction could vary by sport events. Whether the volume reduction range differed by\ntype of endurance event (middle-distance, long-distance, ultra-long-distance) is unclear. To\nmaximize the effect of pre-competition volume reduction on sports performance and to make\nthe implementation of pre-competition taper strategies more pertinent and maneuverable,\nfuture research should further investigate differences in volume reduction between middle-\ndistance endurance and long-distance/ultra-long distance endurance events.\nTraining load can also be adjusted by changing training frequency. However,\nthe results of studies on the effect of such change on sports performance are inconsistent, and\nwhether a clear relationship exists between them is still unclear [79]. The pooled results of the\npresent meta-analysis showed that a decrease in training frequency had no significant effect on\nTT (SMD, −0.32; 95% CI, −0.76 to 0.13; P > 0.05) and that maintenance of training frequency\ncould significantly improve TT (SMD, −0.53; 95% CI, −0.83 to −0.25; P < 0.05). The reason\nmi", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "e endurance events.\nTraining load can also be adjusted by changing training frequency. However,\nthe results of studies on the effect of such change on sports performance are inconsistent, and\nwhether a clear relationship exists between them is still"}
