eta-analysis showed that a decrease in training frequency had no significant effect on\nTT (SMD, −0.32; 95% CI, −0.76 to 0.13; P > 0.05) and that maintenance of training frequency\ncould significantly improve TT (SMD, −0.53; 95% CI, −0.83 to −0.25; P < 0.05). The reason\nmi", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "rations of \u0014 7 and 15–21 days also produced positive training effects\n(P < 0.05). The reason might be related to the different types of athletes included. According\nto Skorski et al. [83], in addition to _VO2max and EM, anaerobic power and capacity a"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0013_c0020", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "62881cc672998c76", "text_span_hash": "62881cc672998c76", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 20, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "anaerobic power and capacity are impor-\ntant factors that determine performance in road cycling. This is because in many road cycling\nraces, first place can be decided in the final sprint, and cyclists with high anaerobic capacity\nEffects of tapering", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "be because our meta-analysis only included endurance events, whereas the meta-analysis\ndeveloped by Bosquet et al. [27] included both speed events (249 athletes total) and endurance\nevents (190 athletes total). Li et al. [79] pointed out that pre-competition volume was reduced\nmore significantly for endurance events than for speed events. This showed that the range of\nvolume reduction could vary by sport events. Whether the volume reduction range differed by\ntype of endurance event (middle-distance, long-distance, ultra-long-distance) is unclear. To\nmaximize the effect of pre-competition volume reduction on sports performance and to make\nthe implementation of pre-competition taper strategies more pertinent and maneuverable,\nfuture research should further investigate differences in volume reduction between middle-\ndistance endurance and long-distance/ultra-long distance endurance events.\nTraining load can also be adjusted by changing training frequency. However,\nthe results of studies on the effect of such change on sports performance are inconsistent, and\nwhether a clear relationship exists between them is still unclear [79]. The pooled results of the\npresent meta-analysis showed that a decrease in training frequency had no significant effect on\nTT (SMD, −0.32; 95% CI, −0.76 to 0.13; P > 0.05) and that maintenance of training frequency\ncould significantly improve TT (SMD, −0.53; 95% CI, −0.83 to −0.25; P < 0.05). The reason\nmi", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "anaerobic power and capacity are impor-\ntant factors that determine performance in road cycling. This is because in many road cycling\nraces, first place can be decided in the final sprint, and cyclists with high anaerobic capacity\nEffects of tapering"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0013_c0021", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3d25e08c7dda094f", "text_span_hash": "3d25e08c7dda094f", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 21, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "c capacity\nEffects of tapering on performance in endurance athletes", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "be because our meta-analysis only included endurance events, whereas the meta-analysis\ndeveloped by Bosquet et al. [27] included both speed events (249 athletes total) and endurance\nevents (190 athletes total). Li et al. [79] pointed out that pre-competition volume was reduced\nmore significantly for endurance events than for speed events. This showed that the range of\nvolume reduction could vary by sport events. Whether the volume reduction range differed by\ntype of endurance event (middle-distance, long-distance, ultra-long-distance) is unclear. To\nmaximize the effect of pre-competition volume reduction on sports performance and to make\nthe implementation of pre-competition taper strategies more pertinent and maneuverable,\nfuture research should further investigate differences in volume reduction between middle-\ndistance endurance and long-distance/ultra-long distance endurance events.\nTraining load can also be adjusted by changing training frequency. However,\nthe results of studies on the effect of such change on sports performance are inconsistent, and\nwhether a clear relationship exists between them is still unclear [79]. The pooled results of the\npresent meta-analysis showed that a decrease in training frequency had no significant effect on\nTT (SMD, −0.32; 95% CI, −0.76 to 0.13; P > 0.05) and that maintenance of training frequency\ncould significantly improve TT (SMD, −0.53; 95% CI, −0.83 to −0.25; P < 0.05). The reason\nmi", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "c capacity\nEffects of tapering on performance in endurance athletes"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0014_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ce21e1b248506b67", "text_span_hash": "ce21e1b248506b67", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 14, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "and power can achieve better results [31,84]. Therefore, it may be important to determine the\nappropriate reduction time to optimize the power and anaerobic capacity of cyclists. Studies\nhave indicated that 1–2 weeks of reduction is the time needed t", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "and power can achieve better results [31,84]. Therefore, it may be important to determine the\nappropriate reduction time to optimize the power and anaerobic capacity of cyclists. Studies\nhave indicated that 1–2 weeks of reduction is the time needed to optimize power and/or anaer-\nobic capacity in cyclists [84,85]. When training stress decreases, there is a period of rapid adap-\ntation and fiber hypertrophy, as evidenced by a 14.2% increase in the cross-sectional area of\ntype IIa fiber in cyclists [86]. However, athletes who participate in cyclic sports, such as run-\nning, may require more time (2–3 weeks) to reach peak performance [27,87]. Running perfor-\nmance has also been found to improve after 1 or 4 weeks of tapering, but some athletes may\nexperience negative results [27]. The increase in sports-specific muscle power during tapering\ntends to be greater than the improvement in aerobic fitness [88], which may be the main rea-\nson for the different durations of taper in cycling and running. The results of the present meta-\nanalysis indicated that a period of 8–14 days appears to be the optimal taper duration for\ncycling and running. However, the effect of taper is also influenced by intensity, volume, fre-\nquency, and individual differences [1,89]. Recovery from a reduction in training load varies\nbetween individuals, with no clear timeline, implying that the corresponding optimal taper\nduration also varies [90]. Unfortunate", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "and power can achieve better results [31,84]. Therefore, it may be important to determine the\nappropriate reduction time to optimize the power and anaerobic capacity of cyclists. Studies\nhave indicated that 1–2 weeks of reduction is the time needed t"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0014_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c2ec9be1c71e96f4", "text_span_hash": "c2ec9be1c71e96f4", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 14, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "reduction is the time needed to optimize power and/or anaer-\nobic capacity in cyclists [84,85]. When training stress decreases, there is a period of rapid adap-\ntation and fiber hypertrophy, as evidenced by a 14.2% increase in the cross-sectional are", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "and power can achieve better results [31,84]. Therefore, it may be important to determine the\nappropriate reduction time to optimize the power and anaerobic capacity of cyclists. Studies\nhave indicated that 1–2 weeks of reduction is the time needed to optimize power and/or anaer-\nobic capacity in cyclists [84,85]. When training stress decreases, there is a period of rapid adap-\ntation and fiber hypertrophy, as evidenced by a 14.2% increase in the cross-sectional area of\ntype IIa fiber in cyclists [86]. However, athletes who participate in cyclic sports, such as run-\nning, may require more time (2–3 weeks) to reach peak performance [27,87]. Running perfor-\nmance has also been found to improve after 1 or 4 weeks of tapering, but some athletes may\nexperience negative results [27]. The increase in sports-specific muscle power during tapering\ntends to be greater than the improvement in aerobic fitness [88], which may be the main rea-\nson for the different durations of taper in cycling and running. The results of the present meta-\nanalysis indicated that a period of 8–14 days appears to be the optimal taper duration for\ncycling and running. However, the effect of taper is also influenced by intensity, volume, fre-\nquency, and individual differences [1,89]. Recovery from a reduction in training load varies\nbetween individuals, with no clear timeline, implying that the corresponding optimal taper\nduration also varies [90]. Unfortunate", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "reduction is the time needed to optimize power and/or anaer-\nobic capacity in cyclists [84,85]. When training stress decreases, there is a period of rapid adap-\ntation and fiber hypertrophy, as evidenced by a 14.2% increase in the cross-sectional are"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0014_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "42dbb1335073dd56", "text_span_hash": "42dbb1335073dd56", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 14, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ase in the cross-sectional area of\ntype IIa fiber in cyclists [86]. However, athletes who participate in cyclic sports, such as run-\nning, may require more time (2–3 weeks) to reach peak performance [27,87]. Running perfor-\nmance has also been found", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "and power can achieve better results [31,84]. Therefore, it may be important to determine the\nappropriate reduction time to optimize the power and anaerobic capacity of cyclists. Studies\nhave indicated that 1–2 weeks of reduction is the time needed to optimize power and/or anaer-\nobic capacity in cyclists [84,85]. When training stress decreases, there is a period of rapid adap-\ntation and fiber hypertrophy, as evidenced by a 14.2% increase in the cross-sectional area of\ntype IIa fiber in cyclists [86]. However, athletes who participate in cyclic sports, such as run-\nning, may require more time (2–3 weeks) to reach peak performance [27,87]. Running perfor-\nmance has also been found to improve after 1 or 4 weeks of tapering, but some athletes may\nexperience negative results [27]. The increase in sports-specific muscle power during tapering\ntends to be greater than the improvement in aerobic fitness [88], which may be the main rea-\nson for the different durations of taper in cycling and running. The results of the present meta-\nanalysis indicated that a period of 8–14 days appears to be the optimal taper duration for\ncycling and running. However, the effect of taper is also influenced by intensity, volume, fre-\nquency, and individual differences [1,89]. Recovery from a reduction in training load varies\nbetween individuals, with no clear timeline, implying that the corresponding optimal taper\nduration also varies [90]. Unfortunate", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ase in the cross-sectional area of\ntype IIa fiber in cyclists [86]. However, athletes who participate in cyclic sports, such as run-\nning, may require more time (2–3 weeks) to reach peak performance [27,87]. Running perfor-\nmance has also been found"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0014_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5e2ebd4f409d5472", "text_span_hash": "5e2ebd4f409d5472", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 14, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "or-\nmance has also been found to improve after 1 or 4 weeks of tapering, but some athletes may\nexperience negative results [27]. The increase in sports-specific muscle power during tapering\ntends to be greater than the improvement in aerobic fitness", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "and power can achieve better results [31,84]. Therefore, it may be important to determine the\nappropriate reduction time to optimize the power and anaerobic capacity of cyclists. Studies\nhave indicated that 1–2 weeks of reduction is the time needed to optimize power and/or anaer-\nobic capacity in cyclists [84,85]. When training stress decreases, there is a period of rapid adap-\ntation and fiber hypertrophy, as evidenced by a 14.2% increase in the cross-sectional area of\ntype IIa fiber in cyclists [86]. However, athletes who participate in cyclic sports, such as run-\nning, may require more time (2–3 weeks) to reach peak performance [27,87]. Running perfor-\nmance has also been found to improve after 1 or 4 weeks of tapering, but some athletes may\nexperience negative results [27]. The increase in sports-specific muscle power during tapering\ntends to be greater than the improvement in aerobic fitness [88], which may be the main rea-\nson for the different durations of taper in cycling and running. The results of the present meta-\nanalysis indicated that a period of 8–14 days appears to be the optimal taper duration for\ncycling and running. However, the effect of taper is also influenced by intensity, volume, fre-\nquency, and individual differences [1,89]. Recovery from a reduction in training load varies\nbetween individuals, with no clear timeline, implying that the corresponding optimal taper\nduration also varies [90]. Unfortunate", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "or-\nmance has also been found to improve after 1 or 4 weeks of tapering, but some athletes may\nexperience negative results [27]. The increase in sports-specific muscle power during tapering\ntends to be greater than the improvement in aerobic fitness"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tapering_meta_2023_p0014_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_87c6ca7e6cb5", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "83c0f35b8a86ded9", "text_span_hash": "83c0f35b8a86ded9", "source_file": "train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tapering_meta_2023.pdf", "page": 14, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "mprovement in aerobic fitness [88], which may be the main rea-\nson for the different durations of taper in cycling and running. The results of the present meta-\nanalysis indicated that a period of 8–14 days appears to be the optimal taper duration fo", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "and power can achieve better results [31,84]. Therefore, it may be important to determine the\nappropriate reduction time to optimize the power and anaerobic capacity of cyclists. Studies\nhave indicated that 1–2 weeks of reduction is the time needed to optimize power and/or anaer-\nobic capacity in cyclists [84,85]. When training stress decreases, there is a period of rapid adap-\ntation and fiber hypertrophy, as evidenced by a 14.2% increase in the cross-sectional area of\ntype IIa fiber in cyclists [86]. However, athletes who participate in cyclic sports, such as run-\nning, may require more time (2–3 weeks) to reach peak performance [27,87]. Running perfor-\nmance has also been found to improve after 1 or 4 weeks of tapering, but some athletes may\nexperience negative results [27]. The increase in sports-specific muscle power during tapering\ntends to be greater than the improvement in aerobic fitness [88], which may be the main rea-\nson for the different durations of taper in cycling and running. The results of the present meta-\nanalysis indicated that a period of 8–14 days appears to be the optimal taper duration for\ncycling and running. However, the effect of taper is also influenced by intensity, volume, fre-\nquency, and individual differences [1,89]. Recovery from a reduction in training load varies\nbetween individuals, with no clear timeline, implying that the corresponding optimal taper\nduration also varies [90]. Unfortunate", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "mprovement in aerobic fitness [88], which may be the main rea-\nson for the different durations of taper in cycling and running. The results of the present meta-\nanalysis indicated that a period of 8–14 days appears to be the optimal taper duration fo"}
