4). Recent years have\nwitnessed a deepening examination of this topic, resulting in more\ndetailed classification schemes. The Norwegian Olympic Committee\n(NOC) adopted a six-zone intensity model for elite endurance\nathletes (Tønnessen et al., 2024) and clas", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "mixed aerobic-anaerobic\ncapacity, L5 and L6 to foster anaerobic endurance, and L7 and\nL8 to prioritize speed capacity (Table 4). These advancements\ncollectively indicate that a more scientific and nuanced classification\nof training intensity aims to"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tid_theory_2025_p0004_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_7d8a7374e23c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1b7a466f83935e6f", "text_span_hash": "1b7a466f83935e6f", "source_file": "train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "of training intensity aims to better address athletes’ unique\nrequirements and enhance training results.\nFrontiers in Physiology", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Hemodynamic representation of typical changes in blood lactate, heart rate, and selected gas exchange parameters during progressive exercise from\nrest to maximal oxygen consumption. PHASE I - PHASE III refers to low to maximal intensity exercise. ̇V O2, oxygen uptake per minute; ̇ V CO2, carbon\ndioxide expired per minute; R, respiratory quotient; ̇ V E, pulmonary ventilation per minute; FEO2, expired oxygen concentration; FECO2, expired\ncarbon dioxide concentration; HR, heart rate; LA, blood lactate concentration.\nof aerobic and anaerobic thresholds. Endurance training intensity is\ncommonly categorized into three principal zones: low-intensity\ntraining (LIT), moderate-intensity training (MIT), and high-\nintensity training (HIT). Among these, LIT generally refers to\nexercise performed below the first ventilatory threshold (VT1),\nMIT spans the range between VT1 and the second ventilatory\nthreshold (VT2), and HIT involves efforts exceeding VT2\nResearchers proposed more sophisticated classification systems\nas a result of recent study developments, including four-zone and\nfive-zone models predicated on the correlation between training\nduration and load intensity (Kong et al., 2024). Recent years have\nwitnessed a deepening examination of this topic, resulting in more\ndetailed classification schemes. The Norwegian Olympic Committee\n(NOC) adopted a six-zone intensity model for elite endurance\nathletes (Tønnessen et al., 2024) and clas", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "of training intensity aims to better address athletes’ unique\nrequirements and enhance training results.\nFrontiers in Physiology"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tid_theory_2025_p0005_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_7d8a7374e23c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c68dfabeecee9523", "text_span_hash": "c68dfabeecee9523", "source_file": "train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "TABLE 2 Hypothetical model of various thresholds and phases selected characteristics during progressive exercise from rest to maximal oxygen\nVentilation Volume-Intensity Curve. VT1, first ventilation threshold; VT2, second ventilation threshold.\n2.3", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "TABLE 2 Hypothetical model of various thresholds and phases selected characteristics during progressive exercise from rest to maximal oxygen\nVentilation Volume-Intensity Curve. VT1, first ventilation threshold; VT2, second ventilation threshold.\n2.3 The emergence of training intensity\nIn conclusion, all models, regardless of the number of zones\nincluded in categorization, are essentially based on intrinsic\nphysiological responses to dynamic variations in exercise intensity,\nparticularly in terms of energy metabolism and gas exchange. The\nsubsequent models additionally integrate psychological indicators.\nEssential physiological indicators are the principal criterion for\nestablishing zone boundaries, including heart rate (HR), blood\nlactate concentration, ventilatory thresholds (VT), lactate thresholds\n(LT), and maximal lactate steady state (MLSS).\nFrontiers in Physiology", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "TABLE 2 Hypothetical model of various thresholds and phases selected characteristics during progressive exercise from rest to maximal oxygen\nVentilation Volume-Intensity Curve. VT1, first ventilation threshold; VT2, second ventilation threshold.\n2.3"}
