entially an extension and refinement\nof the latter. Both are relevant for the meticulous regulation of\ntraining programs and the precise management of training load\nThe three-zone concept has been successfully improved by the\nmany training zone classification t", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "itional phases in metabolic reactions by\nclassifying training intensity into low, moderate, and high zones\nbased on the aerobic threshold (VT1/LT1) and the anaerobic\nthreshold (VT2/LT2). This is enhanced by the five-zone model\nthrough the further spl"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tid_theory_2025_p0006_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_7d8a7374e23c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "329312e48898b3b8", "text_span_hash": "329312e48898b3b8", "source_file": "train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ive-zone model and the three-zone model exhibit significant\nconsistency—the former is essentially an extension and refinement\nof the latter. Both are relevant for the meticulous regulation of\ntraining programs and the precise management of training l", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "TABLE 3 Intensity scale for elite endurance athletes.\nVO2, oxygen uptake; RPE, rating of perceived exertion (based on Borg’s 6–20 scale); HIT, high intensity training; MIT, moderate intensity training; LIT, low intensity training.\nTABLE 4 Intensity level and standard of Chinese kayak team water training load.\nPaddle Frequency (times/minute)\nK1 is a single, K2 is a double and K4 is a quad.\nFor instance, a comparison of the initial three-zone model\n(Z1–Z3) with the more current five-zone model (z0–z5) reveals\nthat both are based on analogous physiological concepts\n(Faude et al., 2009; Rivera Köfler et al., 2025). The three-zone\nmodel highlights the transitional phases in metabolic reactions by\nclassifying training intensity into low, moderate, and high zones\nbased on the aerobic threshold (VT1/LT1) and the anaerobic\nthreshold (VT2/LT2). This is enhanced by the five-zone model\nthrough the further splitting of the low- and high-intensity domains,\nyielding a more operationally accurate zoning framework. Although\nthere are variations in granularity, the physiological underpinnings\nof the five-zone model and the three-zone model exhibit significant\nconsistency—the former is essentially an extension and refinement\nof the latter. Both are relevant for the meticulous regulation of\ntraining programs and the precise management of training load\nThe three-zone concept has been successfully improved by the\nmany training zone classification t", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ive-zone model and the three-zone model exhibit significant\nconsistency—the former is essentially an extension and refinement\nof the latter. Both are relevant for the meticulous regulation of\ntraining programs and the precise management of training l"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tid_theory_2025_p0006_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_7d8a7374e23c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2d9e32bc0eb688dd", "text_span_hash": "2d9e32bc0eb688dd", "source_file": "train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ecise management of training load\nThe three-zone concept has been successfully improved by the\nmany training zone classification techniques described above. A\nthree-zone classification was built through the unique “inflection\npoints” identified in ga", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "TABLE 3 Intensity scale for elite endurance athletes.\nVO2, oxygen uptake; RPE, rating of perceived exertion (based on Borg’s 6–20 scale); HIT, high intensity training; MIT, moderate intensity training; LIT, low intensity training.\nTABLE 4 Intensity level and standard of Chinese kayak team water training load.\nPaddle Frequency (times/minute)\nK1 is a single, K2 is a double and K4 is a quad.\nFor instance, a comparison of the initial three-zone model\n(Z1–Z3) with the more current five-zone model (z0–z5) reveals\nthat both are based on analogous physiological concepts\n(Faude et al., 2009; Rivera Köfler et al., 2025). The three-zone\nmodel highlights the transitional phases in metabolic reactions by\nclassifying training intensity into low, moderate, and high zones\nbased on the aerobic threshold (VT1/LT1) and the anaerobic\nthreshold (VT2/LT2). This is enhanced by the five-zone model\nthrough the further splitting of the low- and high-intensity domains,\nyielding a more operationally accurate zoning framework. Although\nthere are variations in granularity, the physiological underpinnings\nof the five-zone model and the three-zone model exhibit significant\nconsistency—the former is essentially an extension and refinement\nof the latter. Both are relevant for the meticulous regulation of\ntraining programs and the precise management of training load\nThe three-zone concept has been successfully improved by the\nmany training zone classification t", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ecise management of training load\nThe three-zone concept has been successfully improved by the\nmany training zone classification techniques described above. A\nthree-zone classification was built through the unique “inflection\npoints” identified in ga"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tid_theory_2025_p0006_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_7d8a7374e23c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e3fef99d392c3a1a", "text_span_hash": "e3fef99d392c3a1a", "source_file": "train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ction\npoints” identified in gas exchange and energy metabolism at\nthe two ventilatory or lactate thresholds and is considered the\nmost typical and commonly utilized framework for delineating\nResearchers, based on the framework, have progressively\ndev", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "TABLE 3 Intensity scale for elite endurance athletes.\nVO2, oxygen uptake; RPE, rating of perceived exertion (based on Borg’s 6–20 scale); HIT, high intensity training; MIT, moderate intensity training; LIT, low intensity training.\nTABLE 4 Intensity level and standard of Chinese kayak team water training load.\nPaddle Frequency (times/minute)\nK1 is a single, K2 is a double and K4 is a quad.\nFor instance, a comparison of the initial three-zone model\n(Z1–Z3) with the more current five-zone model (z0–z5) reveals\nthat both are based on analogous physiological concepts\n(Faude et al., 2009; Rivera Köfler et al., 2025). The three-zone\nmodel highlights the transitional phases in metabolic reactions by\nclassifying training intensity into low, moderate, and high zones\nbased on the aerobic threshold (VT1/LT1) and the anaerobic\nthreshold (VT2/LT2). This is enhanced by the five-zone model\nthrough the further splitting of the low- and high-intensity domains,\nyielding a more operationally accurate zoning framework. Although\nthere are variations in granularity, the physiological underpinnings\nof the five-zone model and the three-zone model exhibit significant\nconsistency—the former is essentially an extension and refinement\nof the latter. Both are relevant for the meticulous regulation of\ntraining programs and the precise management of training load\nThe three-zone concept has been successfully improved by the\nmany training zone classification t", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ction\npoints” identified in gas exchange and energy metabolism at\nthe two ventilatory or lactate thresholds and is considered the\nmost typical and commonly utilized framework for delineating\nResearchers, based on the framework, have progressively\ndev"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tid_theory_2025_p0006_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_7d8a7374e23c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "72bac3334637dc32", "text_span_hash": "72bac3334637dc32", "source_file": "train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "mework, have progressively\ndeveloped various specific Training Intensity Distribution (TID)\nmodels by measuring and allocating the training volume\nacross intensity zones, combined with physiological parameters\n(Kong et al., 2024; Seiler and Kjerland,", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "TABLE 3 Intensity scale for elite endurance athletes.\nVO2, oxygen uptake; RPE, rating of perceived exertion (based on Borg’s 6–20 scale); HIT, high intensity training; MIT, moderate intensity training; LIT, low intensity training.\nTABLE 4 Intensity level and standard of Chinese kayak team water training load.\nPaddle Frequency (times/minute)\nK1 is a single, K2 is a double and K4 is a quad.\nFor instance, a comparison of the initial three-zone model\n(Z1–Z3) with the more current five-zone model (z0–z5) reveals\nthat both are based on analogous physiological concepts\n(Faude et al., 2009; Rivera Köfler et al., 2025). The three-zone\nmodel highlights the transitional phases in metabolic reactions by\nclassifying training intensity into low, moderate, and high zones\nbased on the aerobic threshold (VT1/LT1) and the anaerobic\nthreshold (VT2/LT2). This is enhanced by the five-zone model\nthrough the further splitting of the low- and high-intensity domains,\nyielding a more operationally accurate zoning framework. Although\nthere are variations in granularity, the physiological underpinnings\nof the five-zone model and the three-zone model exhibit significant\nconsistency—the former is essentially an extension and refinement\nof the latter. Both are relevant for the meticulous regulation of\ntraining programs and the precise management of training load\nThe three-zone concept has been successfully improved by the\nmany training zone classification t", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "mework, have progressively\ndeveloped various specific Training Intensity Distribution (TID)\nmodels by measuring and allocating the training volume\nacross intensity zones, combined with physiological parameters\n(Kong et al., 2024; Seiler and Kjerland,"}
