{"chunk_id": "train_tid_theory_2025_p0007_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_7d8a7374e23c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7a7ec3db9cca4af8", "text_span_hash": "7a7ec3db9cca4af8", "source_file": "train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "insights that later informed\nTID concepts were provided (Hartmann et al., 1990), and intensity\ndistribution was quantified using heart rate and lactate markers\n(Billat et al., 2001). Systematic characterization of TID patterns\nin elite athletes was r", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Comparative schematic of TID models and intensity zones. In this figure, threshold training is defined as the intensity between the first and second\nlactate turn points (LT1 and LT2) or near the maximal lactate steady state (MLSS). This corresponds to Zone 2 in the 3-zone model and Zone 3 in the\n5-zone model. Zone1, training intensity zone 1 in the triphasic model; Zone2, training intensity zone 2 in the triphasic model;Zone3, Z3 in the triphasic\nmodel; z0,training intensity zone 0 in the 5-zone model; z1, training intensity zone 1 in the 5-zone model; z2,training intensity zone 2 in the 5-zone\nmodel; z3,training intensity zone 3 in the 5-zone model; z4,training intensity zone 4 in the 5-zone model;z5,training intensity zone 5 in the 5-zone\nmodel; POL, polarized training; PYR, pyramidal training; THR, threshold training;BLa,blood lactate;VT1,first ventilatory threshold; OPLA, onset of plasma\nlactate accumulation; LT1, first lactate threshold; VT2,second ventilatory threshold; OBLA, onset of blood lactate accumulation onset; IAT, Individual\nAnaerobic Threshold; MLSS, maximum lactate steady state; LT2, second lactate threshold; VO2max, maximum oxygen uptake; HRmax, maximum heart\nrate; mas/map, maximum aerobic speed/maximum aerobic power; vVO2max, velocity associated with the maximum oxygen consumption; CP/CS,\ncritical power/speed; FTP, functional threshold power; RPE,rating of perceived exertion in the Borg 6–20 scale.\nfollowed ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "insights that later informed\nTID concepts were provided (Hartmann et al., 1990), and intensity\ndistribution was quantified using heart rate and lactate markers\n(Billat et al., 2001). Systematic characterization of TID patterns\nin elite athletes was r"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tid_theory_2025_p0007_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_7d8a7374e23c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "a7f25f615d710fb9", "text_span_hash": "a7f25f615d710fb9", "source_file": "train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "), and comparisons of polarized and other\nTID models demonstrated their effects on physiological outcomes\nand endurance performance (Rivera-Köfler et al., 2025). Figure 4\ncontextualizes their historical progression.\nFrontiers in Physiology", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Comparative schematic of TID models and intensity zones. In this figure, threshold training is defined as the intensity between the first and second\nlactate turn points (LT1 and LT2) or near the maximal lactate steady state (MLSS). This corresponds to Zone 2 in the 3-zone model and Zone 3 in the\n5-zone model. Zone1, training intensity zone 1 in the triphasic model; Zone2, training intensity zone 2 in the triphasic model;Zone3, Z3 in the triphasic\nmodel; z0,training intensity zone 0 in the 5-zone model; z1, training intensity zone 1 in the 5-zone model; z2,training intensity zone 2 in the 5-zone\nmodel; z3,training intensity zone 3 in the 5-zone model; z4,training intensity zone 4 in the 5-zone model;z5,training intensity zone 5 in the 5-zone\nmodel; POL, polarized training; PYR, pyramidal training; THR, threshold training;BLa,blood lactate;VT1,first ventilatory threshold; OPLA, onset of plasma\nlactate accumulation; LT1, first lactate threshold; VT2,second ventilatory threshold; OBLA, onset of blood lactate accumulation onset; IAT, Individual\nAnaerobic Threshold; MLSS, maximum lactate steady state; LT2, second lactate threshold; VO2max, maximum oxygen uptake; HRmax, maximum heart\nrate; mas/map, maximum aerobic speed/maximum aerobic power; vVO2max, velocity associated with the maximum oxygen consumption; CP/CS,\ncritical power/speed; FTP, functional threshold power; RPE,rating of perceived exertion in the Borg 6–20 scale.\nfollowed ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "), and comparisons of polarized and other\nTID models demonstrated their effects on physiological outcomes\nand endurance performance (Rivera-Köfler et al., 2025). Figure 4\ncontextualizes their historical progression.\nFrontiers in Physiology"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tid_theory_2025_p0008_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_7d8a7374e23c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2dcb605c7ae3d85e", "text_span_hash": "2dcb605c7ae3d85e", "source_file": "train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Evolution of Training Intensity Distribution (TID) Theory in Endurance Sports (1979–2025). POL, polarized training; PYR, pyramidal training; THR,\n3 Analysis of training characteristics,\nbenefits, and mechanisms of the three\n3.1 Pyramidal model (PYR)", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Evolution of Training Intensity Distribution (TID) Theory in Endurance Sports (1979–2025). POL, polarized training; PYR, pyramidal training; THR,\n3 Analysis of training characteristics,\nbenefits, and mechanisms of the three\n3.1 Pyramidal model (PYR)\n3.1.1 Analysis of training characteristics\nCoaches and researchers have been interested in the correlation\nbetween training intensity and volume to enhance the training\noutcomes of endurance events. The “pyramid” structural paradigm\nhas been initially implemented in training practice since the\n1950s The training load distribution across all levels of the\npyramid model exhibits a negative correlation with exercise\nintensity. Specifically, low-intensity training should make up around\n80% of the overall training volume, primarily focusing on low-\nand moderate-intensity intervals below the aerobic-anaerobic\nthresholds. Moderate-intensity training should not exceed 10%\nof the total training volume, while high-intensity anaerobic training\nshould be confined to 5%–10% of the annual training volume\n(Sleamaker and Browning, 1996). Z1>Z2>Z3 (Figure 5) delineate\nthe training distribution, with Z1 of relatively high proportion\nand accompanied by extended low-intensity training (Treff et al.,\n2019); Z1 and Z2 primarily utilize the aerobic oxidative system,\nwhereas Z3 partly relies on the ATP-CP and anaerobic glycolysis\nsystems. This model was based on aerobic metabolism and\nsynchronized well wi", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Evolution of Training Intensity Distribution (TID) Theory in Endurance Sports (1979–2025). POL, polarized training; PYR, pyramidal training; THR,\n3 Analysis of training characteristics,\nbenefits, and mechanisms of the three\n3.1 Pyramidal model (PYR)"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tid_theory_2025_p0008_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_7d8a7374e23c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e09e49ff618fc484", "text_span_hash": "e09e49ff618fc484", "source_file": "train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ree\n3.1 Pyramidal model (PYR)\n3.1.1 Analysis of training characteristics\nCoaches and researchers have been interested in the correlation\nbetween training intensity and volume to enhance the training\noutcomes of endurance events. The “pyramid” structu", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Evolution of Training Intensity Distribution (TID) Theory in Endurance Sports (1979–2025). POL, polarized training; PYR, pyramidal training; THR,\n3 Analysis of training characteristics,\nbenefits, and mechanisms of the three\n3.1 Pyramidal model (PYR)\n3.1.1 Analysis of training characteristics\nCoaches and researchers have been interested in the correlation\nbetween training intensity and volume to enhance the training\noutcomes of endurance events. The “pyramid” structural paradigm\nhas been initially implemented in training practice since the\n1950s The training load distribution across all levels of the\npyramid model exhibits a negative correlation with exercise\nintensity. Specifically, low-intensity training should make up around\n80% of the overall training volume, primarily focusing on low-\nand moderate-intensity intervals below the aerobic-anaerobic\nthresholds. Moderate-intensity training should not exceed 10%\nof the total training volume, while high-intensity anaerobic training\nshould be confined to 5%–10% of the annual training volume\n(Sleamaker and Browning, 1996). Z1>Z2>Z3 (Figure 5) delineate\nthe training distribution, with Z1 of relatively high proportion\nand accompanied by extended low-intensity training (Treff et al.,\n2019); Z1 and Z2 primarily utilize the aerobic oxidative system,\nwhereas Z3 partly relies on the ATP-CP and anaerobic glycolysis\nsystems. This model was based on aerobic metabolism and\nsynchronized well wi", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ree\n3.1 Pyramidal model (PYR)\n3.1.1 Analysis of training characteristics\nCoaches and researchers have been interested in the correlation\nbetween training intensity and volume to enhance the training\noutcomes of endurance events. The “pyramid” structu"}
