mpanied by extended low-intensity training (Treff et al.,\n2019); Z1 and Z2 primarily utilize the aerobic oxidative system,\nwhereas Z3 partly relies on the ATP-CP and anaerobic glycolysis\nsystems. This model was based on aerobic metabolism and\nsynchronized well wi", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ercise\nintensity. Specifically, low-intensity training should make up around\n80% of the overall training volume, primarily focusing on low-\nand moderate-intensity intervals below the aerobic-anaerobic\nthresholds. Moderate-intensity training should no"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tid_theory_2025_p0008_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_7d8a7374e23c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0125d0f33782ca3f", "text_span_hash": "0125d0f33782ca3f", "source_file": "train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "e-intensity training should not exceed 10%\nof the total training volume, while high-intensity anaerobic training\nshould be confined to 5%–10% of the annual training volume\n(Sleamaker and Browning, 1996). Z1>Z2>Z3 (Figure 5) delineate\nthe training dis", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Evolution of Training Intensity Distribution (TID) Theory in Endurance Sports (1979–2025). POL, polarized training; PYR, pyramidal training; THR,\n3 Analysis of training characteristics,\nbenefits, and mechanisms of the three\n3.1 Pyramidal model (PYR)\n3.1.1 Analysis of training characteristics\nCoaches and researchers have been interested in the correlation\nbetween training intensity and volume to enhance the training\noutcomes of endurance events. The “pyramid” structural paradigm\nhas been initially implemented in training practice since the\n1950s The training load distribution across all levels of the\npyramid model exhibits a negative correlation with exercise\nintensity. Specifically, low-intensity training should make up around\n80% of the overall training volume, primarily focusing on low-\nand moderate-intensity intervals below the aerobic-anaerobic\nthresholds. Moderate-intensity training should not exceed 10%\nof the total training volume, while high-intensity anaerobic training\nshould be confined to 5%–10% of the annual training volume\n(Sleamaker and Browning, 1996). Z1>Z2>Z3 (Figure 5) delineate\nthe training distribution, with Z1 of relatively high proportion\nand accompanied by extended low-intensity training (Treff et al.,\n2019); Z1 and Z2 primarily utilize the aerobic oxidative system,\nwhereas Z3 partly relies on the ATP-CP and anaerobic glycolysis\nsystems. This model was based on aerobic metabolism and\nsynchronized well wi", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "e-intensity training should not exceed 10%\nof the total training volume, while high-intensity anaerobic training\nshould be confined to 5%–10% of the annual training volume\n(Sleamaker and Browning, 1996). Z1>Z2>Z3 (Figure 5) delineate\nthe training dis"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tid_theory_2025_p0008_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_7d8a7374e23c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "00ac516637e0bdea", "text_span_hash": "00ac516637e0bdea", "source_file": "train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "5) delineate\nthe training distribution, with Z1 of relatively high proportion\nand accompanied by extended low-intensity training (Treff et al.,\n2019); Z1 and Z2 primarily utilize the aerobic oxidative system,\nwhereas Z3 partly relies on the ATP-CP a", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Evolution of Training Intensity Distribution (TID) Theory in Endurance Sports (1979–2025). POL, polarized training; PYR, pyramidal training; THR,\n3 Analysis of training characteristics,\nbenefits, and mechanisms of the three\n3.1 Pyramidal model (PYR)\n3.1.1 Analysis of training characteristics\nCoaches and researchers have been interested in the correlation\nbetween training intensity and volume to enhance the training\noutcomes of endurance events. The “pyramid” structural paradigm\nhas been initially implemented in training practice since the\n1950s The training load distribution across all levels of the\npyramid model exhibits a negative correlation with exercise\nintensity. Specifically, low-intensity training should make up around\n80% of the overall training volume, primarily focusing on low-\nand moderate-intensity intervals below the aerobic-anaerobic\nthresholds. Moderate-intensity training should not exceed 10%\nof the total training volume, while high-intensity anaerobic training\nshould be confined to 5%–10% of the annual training volume\n(Sleamaker and Browning, 1996). Z1>Z2>Z3 (Figure 5) delineate\nthe training distribution, with Z1 of relatively high proportion\nand accompanied by extended low-intensity training (Treff et al.,\n2019); Z1 and Z2 primarily utilize the aerobic oxidative system,\nwhereas Z3 partly relies on the ATP-CP and anaerobic glycolysis\nsystems. This model was based on aerobic metabolism and\nsynchronized well wi", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "5) delineate\nthe training distribution, with Z1 of relatively high proportion\nand accompanied by extended low-intensity training (Treff et al.,\n2019); Z1 and Z2 primarily utilize the aerobic oxidative system,\nwhereas Z3 partly relies on the ATP-CP a"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tid_theory_2025_p0008_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_7d8a7374e23c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "d20413c9d0feaf2a", "text_span_hash": "d20413c9d0feaf2a", "source_file": "train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "partly relies on the ATP-CP and anaerobic glycolysis\nsystems. This model was based on aerobic metabolism and\nsynchronized well with medium- and high-intensity training.\nIt follows the fundamental principle of “negative correlation”\nbetween loading i", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Evolution of Training Intensity Distribution (TID) Theory in Endurance Sports (1979–2025). POL, polarized training; PYR, pyramidal training; THR,\n3 Analysis of training characteristics,\nbenefits, and mechanisms of the three\n3.1 Pyramidal model (PYR)\n3.1.1 Analysis of training characteristics\nCoaches and researchers have been interested in the correlation\nbetween training intensity and volume to enhance the training\noutcomes of endurance events. The “pyramid” structural paradigm\nhas been initially implemented in training practice since the\n1950s The training load distribution across all levels of the\npyramid model exhibits a negative correlation with exercise\nintensity. Specifically, low-intensity training should make up around\n80% of the overall training volume, primarily focusing on low-\nand moderate-intensity intervals below the aerobic-anaerobic\nthresholds. Moderate-intensity training should not exceed 10%\nof the total training volume, while high-intensity anaerobic training\nshould be confined to 5%–10% of the annual training volume\n(Sleamaker and Browning, 1996). Z1>Z2>Z3 (Figure 5) delineate\nthe training distribution, with Z1 of relatively high proportion\nand accompanied by extended low-intensity training (Treff et al.,\n2019); Z1 and Z2 primarily utilize the aerobic oxidative system,\nwhereas Z3 partly relies on the ATP-CP and anaerobic glycolysis\nsystems. This model was based on aerobic metabolism and\nsynchronized well wi", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "partly relies on the ATP-CP and anaerobic glycolysis\nsystems. This model was based on aerobic metabolism and\nsynchronized well with medium- and high-intensity training.\nIt follows the fundamental principle of “negative correlation”\nbetween loading i"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tid_theory_2025_p0008_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_7d8a7374e23c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "43bbd85934bbecb6", "text_span_hash": "43bbd85934bbecb6", "source_file": "train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "page": 8, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "correlation”\nbetween loading intensity and volume (i.e., training volume\ndiminishes as intensity escalates). Consequently, it has received\nstrong support from both theoretical and practical spheres,\nparticularly in long-term endurance training regime", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Evolution of Training Intensity Distribution (TID) Theory in Endurance Sports (1979–2025). POL, polarized training; PYR, pyramidal training; THR,\n3 Analysis of training characteristics,\nbenefits, and mechanisms of the three\n3.1 Pyramidal model (PYR)\n3.1.1 Analysis of training characteristics\nCoaches and researchers have been interested in the correlation\nbetween training intensity and volume to enhance the training\noutcomes of endurance events. The “pyramid” structural paradigm\nhas been initially implemented in training practice since the\n1950s The training load distribution across all levels of the\npyramid model exhibits a negative correlation with exercise\nintensity. Specifically, low-intensity training should make up around\n80% of the overall training volume, primarily focusing on low-\nand moderate-intensity intervals below the aerobic-anaerobic\nthresholds. Moderate-intensity training should not exceed 10%\nof the total training volume, while high-intensity anaerobic training\nshould be confined to 5%–10% of the annual training volume\n(Sleamaker and Browning, 1996). Z1>Z2>Z3 (Figure 5) delineate\nthe training distribution, with Z1 of relatively high proportion\nand accompanied by extended low-intensity training (Treff et al.,\n2019); Z1 and Z2 primarily utilize the aerobic oxidative system,\nwhereas Z3 partly relies on the ATP-CP and anaerobic glycolysis\nsystems. This model was based on aerobic metabolism and\nsynchronized well wi", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "correlation”\nbetween loading intensity and volume (i.e., training volume\ndiminishes as intensity escalates). Consequently, it has received\nstrong support from both theoretical and practical spheres,\nparticularly in long-term endurance training regime"}
