its implementation in\nparticular endurance disciplines or high-intensity, interval-centric\nsports remains comparatively constrained. This training approach\nmay pr", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "e THR model primarily featured a substantial increase in\ntraining duration within the lactate threshold zone (Z2). This zone\nFrontiers in Physiology"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tid_theory_2025_p0010_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_7d8a7374e23c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "7469a5c810984de1", "text_span_hash": "7469a5c810984de1", "source_file": "train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ss.\nEvidence supports THR effectiveness in several contexts,\nincluding swimming, triathlon, and middle-distance running,\nwhere technical efficiency is critical (Billat et al., 2001;\nGonzález Ravé et al., 2021; Neal et al., 2013). These findings indic", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "is sometimes referred to as “sweet spot training” in international\nliterature, highlighting a balance between optimal metabolic\nefficiency and tolerable fatigue (Neal et al., 2013). The intensity\ndistribution characteristic of THR training typically exhibits either\na Z1 > Z2 > Z3 or Z2 > Z1 > Z3 pattern (Treff et al., 2019).\nProlonged training in this intensity zone can create\na considerable physiological burden and possibly lead to\nsevere fatigue (Seiler and Tønnessen, 2009). Therefore, it is necessary\nto arrange recuperation days or low-intensity sessions reasonably\nin the THR training to mitigate fatigue accumulation and improve\ntraining effectiveness.\nEvidence supports THR effectiveness in several contexts,\nincluding swimming, triathlon, and middle-distance running,\nwhere technical efficiency is critical (Billat et al., 2001;\nGonzález Ravé et al., 2021; Neal et al., 2013). These findings indicate\nthat THR may be especially valuable when refining technique under\nsustained submaximal intensities.\n3.2.2 Physiological benefits and underlying\nmechanisms of training\nThe lactate threshold (THR) paradigm, which has long been\nregarded as a traditional and important strategy for improving\nendurance, places a strong emphasis on training inside the\ndesignated “golden intensity zone” of endurance training. Its\ntraining advantages and the physiological adaptation have garnered\ncontinuous scrutiny and have been fully corroborated for the", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ss.\nEvidence supports THR effectiveness in several contexts,\nincluding swimming, triathlon, and middle-distance running,\nwhere technical efficiency is critical (Billat et al., 2001;\nGonzález Ravé et al., 2021; Neal et al., 2013). These findings indic"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tid_theory_2025_p0010_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_7d8a7374e23c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "d27a37d0ef72bdea", "text_span_hash": "d27a37d0ef72bdea", "source_file": "train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "., 2013). These findings indicate\nthat THR may be especially valuable when refining technique under\nsustained submaximal intensities.\n3.2.2 Physiological benefits and underlying\nmechanisms of training\nThe lactate threshold (THR) paradigm, which has l", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "is sometimes referred to as “sweet spot training” in international\nliterature, highlighting a balance between optimal metabolic\nefficiency and tolerable fatigue (Neal et al., 2013). The intensity\ndistribution characteristic of THR training typically exhibits either\na Z1 > Z2 > Z3 or Z2 > Z1 > Z3 pattern (Treff et al., 2019).\nProlonged training in this intensity zone can create\na considerable physiological burden and possibly lead to\nsevere fatigue (Seiler and Tønnessen, 2009). Therefore, it is necessary\nto arrange recuperation days or low-intensity sessions reasonably\nin the THR training to mitigate fatigue accumulation and improve\ntraining effectiveness.\nEvidence supports THR effectiveness in several contexts,\nincluding swimming, triathlon, and middle-distance running,\nwhere technical efficiency is critical (Billat et al., 2001;\nGonzález Ravé et al., 2021; Neal et al., 2013). These findings indicate\nthat THR may be especially valuable when refining technique under\nsustained submaximal intensities.\n3.2.2 Physiological benefits and underlying\nmechanisms of training\nThe lactate threshold (THR) paradigm, which has long been\nregarded as a traditional and important strategy for improving\nendurance, places a strong emphasis on training inside the\ndesignated “golden intensity zone” of endurance training. Its\ntraining advantages and the physiological adaptation have garnered\ncontinuous scrutiny and have been fully corroborated for the", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "., 2013). These findings indicate\nthat THR may be especially valuable when refining technique under\nsustained submaximal intensities.\n3.2.2 Physiological benefits and underlying\nmechanisms of training\nThe lactate threshold (THR) paradigm, which has l"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tid_theory_2025_p0010_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_7d8a7374e23c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "16c488cc7f86bd07", "text_span_hash": "16c488cc7f86bd07", "source_file": "train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ld (THR) paradigm, which has long been\nregarded as a traditional and important strategy for improving\nendurance, places a strong emphasis on training inside the\ndesignated “golden intensity zone” of endurance training. Its\ntraining advantages and the", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "is sometimes referred to as “sweet spot training” in international\nliterature, highlighting a balance between optimal metabolic\nefficiency and tolerable fatigue (Neal et al., 2013). The intensity\ndistribution characteristic of THR training typically exhibits either\na Z1 > Z2 > Z3 or Z2 > Z1 > Z3 pattern (Treff et al., 2019).\nProlonged training in this intensity zone can create\na considerable physiological burden and possibly lead to\nsevere fatigue (Seiler and Tønnessen, 2009). Therefore, it is necessary\nto arrange recuperation days or low-intensity sessions reasonably\nin the THR training to mitigate fatigue accumulation and improve\ntraining effectiveness.\nEvidence supports THR effectiveness in several contexts,\nincluding swimming, triathlon, and middle-distance running,\nwhere technical efficiency is critical (Billat et al., 2001;\nGonzález Ravé et al., 2021; Neal et al., 2013). These findings indicate\nthat THR may be especially valuable when refining technique under\nsustained submaximal intensities.\n3.2.2 Physiological benefits and underlying\nmechanisms of training\nThe lactate threshold (THR) paradigm, which has long been\nregarded as a traditional and important strategy for improving\nendurance, places a strong emphasis on training inside the\ndesignated “golden intensity zone” of endurance training. Its\ntraining advantages and the physiological adaptation have garnered\ncontinuous scrutiny and have been fully corroborated for the", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ld (THR) paradigm, which has long been\nregarded as a traditional and important strategy for improving\nendurance, places a strong emphasis on training inside the\ndesignated “golden intensity zone” of endurance training. Its\ntraining advantages and the"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tid_theory_2025_p0010_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_7d8a7374e23c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "986db6dac22ce91a", "text_span_hash": "986db6dac22ce91a", "source_file": "train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ts\ntraining advantages and the physiological adaptation have garnered\ncontinuous scrutiny and have been fully corroborated for their\nimpact on cardiovascular control and metabolic efficiency through\nextensive empirical research.\nA 9-week continuous l", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "is sometimes referred to as “sweet spot training” in international\nliterature, highlighting a balance between optimal metabolic\nefficiency and tolerable fatigue (Neal et al., 2013). The intensity\ndistribution characteristic of THR training typically exhibits either\na Z1 > Z2 > Z3 or Z2 > Z1 > Z3 pattern (Treff et al., 2019).\nProlonged training in this intensity zone can create\na considerable physiological burden and possibly lead to\nsevere fatigue (Seiler and Tønnessen, 2009). Therefore, it is necessary\nto arrange recuperation days or low-intensity sessions reasonably\nin the THR training to mitigate fatigue accumulation and improve\ntraining effectiveness.\nEvidence supports THR effectiveness in several contexts,\nincluding swimming, triathlon, and middle-distance running,\nwhere technical efficiency is critical (Billat et al., 2001;\nGonzález Ravé et al., 2021; Neal et al., 2013). These findings indicate\nthat THR may be especially valuable when refining technique under\nsustained submaximal intensities.\n3.2.2 Physiological benefits and underlying\nmechanisms of training\nThe lactate threshold (THR) paradigm, which has long been\nregarded as a traditional and important strategy for improving\nendurance, places a strong emphasis on training inside the\ndesignated “golden intensity zone” of endurance training. Its\ntraining advantages and the physiological adaptation have garnered\ncontinuous scrutiny and have been fully corroborated for the", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ts\ntraining advantages and the physiological adaptation have garnered\ncontinuous scrutiny and have been fully corroborated for their\nimpact on cardiovascular control and metabolic efficiency through\nextensive empirical research.\nA 9-week continuous l"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tid_theory_2025_p0010_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_7d8a7374e23c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c5c762540673a65f", "text_span_hash": "c5c762540673a65f", "source_file": "train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "esearch.\nA 9-week continuous lactate threshold training can significantly\nimprove heart rate economy, and a reported 2.7% ± 1.0% reduction\nin heart rate (P < 0.05) indicates favorable regulatory effects on the\ncardiovascular and autonomic nervous sys", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "is sometimes referred to as “sweet spot training” in international\nliterature, highlighting a balance between optimal metabolic\nefficiency and tolerable fatigue (Neal et al., 2013). The intensity\ndistribution characteristic of THR training typically exhibits either\na Z1 > Z2 > Z3 or Z2 > Z1 > Z3 pattern (Treff et al., 2019).\nProlonged training in this intensity zone can create\na considerable physiological burden and possibly lead to\nsevere fatigue (Seiler and Tønnessen, 2009). Therefore, it is necessary\nto arrange recuperation days or low-intensity sessions reasonably\nin the THR training to mitigate fatigue accumulation and improve\ntraining effectiveness.\nEvidence supports THR effectiveness in several contexts,\nincluding swimming, triathlon, and middle-distance running,\nwhere technical efficiency is critical (Billat et al., 2001;\nGonzález Ravé et al., 2021; Neal et al., 2013). These findings indicate\nthat THR may be especially valuable when refining technique under\nsustained submaximal intensities.\n3.2.2 Physiological benefits and underlying\nmechanisms of training\nThe lactate threshold (THR) paradigm, which has long been\nregarded as a traditional and important strategy for improving\nendurance, places a strong emphasis on training inside the\ndesignated “golden intensity zone” of endurance training. Its\ntraining advantages and the physiological adaptation have garnered\ncontinuous scrutiny and have been fully corroborated for the", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "esearch.\nA 9-week continuous lactate threshold training can significantly\nimprove heart rate economy, and a reported 2.7% ± 1.0% reduction\nin heart rate (P < 0.05) indicates favorable regulatory effects on the\ncardiovascular and autonomic nervous sys"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tid_theory_2025_p0010_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_7d8a7374e23c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "b5aaf6c8c67a7462", "text_span_hash": "b5aaf6c8c67a7462", "source_file": "train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ular and autonomic nervous systems (Stöggl and Sperlich,\n2014). Such a moderate-intensity continuous load is especially\neffective in increasing myocardial function and parasympathetic\nactivity, which improves energy efficiency and running economy.\nTH", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "is sometimes referred to as “sweet spot training” in international\nliterature, highlighting a balance between optimal metabolic\nefficiency and tolerable fatigue (Neal et al., 2013). The intensity\ndistribution characteristic of THR training typically exhibits either\na Z1 > Z2 > Z3 or Z2 > Z1 > Z3 pattern (Treff et al., 2019).\nProlonged training in this intensity zone can create\na considerable physiological burden and possibly lead to\nsevere fatigue (Seiler and Tønnessen, 2009). Therefore, it is necessary\nto arrange recuperation days or low-intensity sessions reasonably\nin the THR training to mitigate fatigue accumulation and improve\ntraining effectiveness.\nEvidence supports THR effectiveness in several contexts,\nincluding swimming, triathlon, and middle-distance running,\nwhere technical efficiency is critical (Billat et al., 2001;\nGonzález Ravé et al., 2021; Neal et al., 2013). These findings indicate\nthat THR may be especially valuable when refining technique under\nsustained submaximal intensities.\n3.2.2 Physiological benefits and underlying\nmechanisms of training\nThe lactate threshold (THR) paradigm, which has long been\nregarded as a traditional and important strategy for improving\nendurance, places a strong emphasis on training inside the\ndesignated “golden intensity zone” of endurance training. Its\ntraining advantages and the physiological adaptation have garnered\ncontinuous scrutiny and have been fully corroborated for the", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ular and autonomic nervous systems (Stöggl and Sperlich,\n2014). Such a moderate-intensity continuous load is especially\neffective in increasing myocardial function and parasympathetic\nactivity, which improves energy efficiency and running economy.\nTH"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tid_theory_2025_p0010_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_7d8a7374e23c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5e5147f6f4183339", "text_span_hash": "5e5147f6f4183339", "source_file": "train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ciency and running economy.\nTHR in elite endurance athletes is characterized by intensity\ndistributions that emphasize low to moderate intensities, with\nhigh-intensity efforts making up only around 25% of the whole\ntraining duration (Rosenblat et al.", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "is sometimes referred to as “sweet spot training” in international\nliterature, highlighting a balance between optimal metabolic\nefficiency and tolerable fatigue (Neal et al., 2013). The intensity\ndistribution characteristic of THR training typically exhibits either\na Z1 > Z2 > Z3 or Z2 > Z1 > Z3 pattern (Treff et al., 2019).\nProlonged training in this intensity zone can create\na considerable physiological burden and possibly lead to\nsevere fatigue (Seiler and Tønnessen, 2009). Therefore, it is necessary\nto arrange recuperation days or low-intensity sessions reasonably\nin the THR training to mitigate fatigue accumulation and improve\ntraining effectiveness.\nEvidence supports THR effectiveness in several contexts,\nincluding swimming, triathlon, and middle-distance running,\nwhere technical efficiency is critical (Billat et al., 2001;\nGonzález Ravé et al., 2021; Neal et al., 2013). These findings indicate\nthat THR may be especially valuable when refining technique under\nsustained submaximal intensities.\n3.2.2 Physiological benefits and underlying\nmechanisms of training\nThe lactate threshold (THR) paradigm, which has long been\nregarded as a traditional and important strategy for improving\nendurance, places a strong emphasis on training inside the\ndesignated “golden intensity zone” of endurance training. Its\ntraining advantages and the physiological adaptation have garnered\ncontinuous scrutiny and have been fully corroborated for the", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ciency and running economy.\nTHR in elite endurance athletes is characterized by intensity\ndistributions that emphasize low to moderate intensities, with\nhigh-intensity efforts making up only around 25% of the whole\ntraining duration (Rosenblat et al."}
{"chunk_id": "train_tid_theory_2025_p0010_c0015", "source_registry_id": "src_7d8a7374e23c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ae8709bbf03a5b05", "text_span_hash": "ae8709bbf03a5b05", "source_file": "train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "page": 10, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 15, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ignificant metabolic\nadaptations (Seiler and Kjerland, 2006).\nProfessional cyclists often display a pyramidal distribution when\ntraining load is measured by distance (Zapico et al., 2007). Similarly,\ndistance runners and cross-country skiers frequent", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "is sometimes referred to as “sweet spot training” in international\nliterature, highlighting a balance between optimal metabolic\nefficiency and tolerable fatigue (Neal et al., 2013). The intensity\ndistribution characteristic of THR training typically exhibits either\na Z1 > Z2 > Z3 or Z2 > Z1 > Z3 pattern (Treff et al., 2019).\nProlonged training in this intensity zone can create\na considerable physiological burden and possibly lead to\nsevere fatigue (Seiler and Tønnessen, 2009). Therefore, it is necessary\nto arrange recuperation days or low-intensity sessions reasonably\nin the THR training to mitigate fatigue accumulation and improve\ntraining effectiveness.\nEvidence supports THR effectiveness in several contexts,\nincluding swimming, triathlon, and middle-distance running,\nwhere technical efficiency is critical (Billat et al., 2001;\nGonzález Ravé et al., 2021; Neal et al., 2013). These findings indicate\nthat THR may be especially valuable when refining technique under\nsustained submaximal intensities.\n3.2.2 Physiological benefits and underlying\nmechanisms of training\nThe lactate threshold (THR) paradigm, which has long been\nregarded as a traditional and important strategy for improving\nendurance, places a strong emphasis on training inside the\ndesignated “golden intensity zone” of endurance training. Its\ntraining advantages and the physiological adaptation have garnered\ncontinuous scrutiny and have been fully corroborated for the", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ignificant metabolic\nadaptations (Seiler and Kjerland, 2006).\nProfessional cyclists often display a pyramidal distribution when\ntraining load is measured by distance (Zapico et al., 2007). Similarly,\ndistance runners and cross-country skiers frequent"}
