{"chunk_id": "train_tid_theory_2025_p0016_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_7d8a7374e23c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "65ba33a245cb86d1", "text_span_hash": "65ba33a245cb86d1", "source_file": "train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "page": 16, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "gh Volume Low Intensity Training (HVLIT) comprising up to\n88% of the training regimen (Torvik et al., 2021). This suggests\nthat some athletes depend heavily on low-intensity training to\nfacilitate recovery and physiological adaptation even amid repea", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analogous TID patterns are also seen in cross-country skiers\nand biathletes during competition, with augmented high-volume,\nlow-intensity, and high-intensity training enabling athletes to\nsustain performance levels without inducing extreme fatigue.\nThe latest research indicates that the Training Intensity\nDistribution (TID) of top cross-country skiers may still maintain\na pyramidal structure even during peak competition periods, with\nHigh Volume Low Intensity Training (HVLIT) comprising up to\n88% of the training regimen (Torvik et al., 2021). This suggests\nthat some athletes depend heavily on low-intensity training to\nfacilitate recovery and physiological adaptation even amid repeated\nhigh-intensity racing.\nDespite sport and individual variations, Zone 3 training\ngenerally increases slightly during competition periods. This\nunderscores how crucial high-intensity capacity is in competition,\nand athletes need to make a significant modification in their training\nregimen as the season enters its final stage.\n5 Investigation of the optimal training\nintensity distribution model:\nConsensus and controversies\nThe introduction of Training Intensity Distribution (TID)\nstrategies, including the pyramidal model, lactate threshold model,\nand polarized model, in the middle of the 20th century has\nsparked continuous debate about which model improves endurance\nperformance more universally and effectively in both academic and\npractical contexts", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "gh Volume Low Intensity Training (HVLIT) comprising up to\n88% of the training regimen (Torvik et al., 2021). This suggests\nthat some athletes depend heavily on low-intensity training to\nfacilitate recovery and physiological adaptation even amid repea"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tid_theory_2025_p0016_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_7d8a7374e23c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c554b4dcda34dd50", "text_span_hash": "c554b4dcda34dd50", "source_file": "train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "page": 16, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "cal adaptation even amid repeated\nhigh-intensity racing.\nDespite sport and individual variations, Zone 3 training\ngenerally increases slightly during competition periods. This\nunderscores how crucial high-intensity capacity is in competition,\nand ath", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analogous TID patterns are also seen in cross-country skiers\nand biathletes during competition, with augmented high-volume,\nlow-intensity, and high-intensity training enabling athletes to\nsustain performance levels without inducing extreme fatigue.\nThe latest research indicates that the Training Intensity\nDistribution (TID) of top cross-country skiers may still maintain\na pyramidal structure even during peak competition periods, with\nHigh Volume Low Intensity Training (HVLIT) comprising up to\n88% of the training regimen (Torvik et al., 2021). This suggests\nthat some athletes depend heavily on low-intensity training to\nfacilitate recovery and physiological adaptation even amid repeated\nhigh-intensity racing.\nDespite sport and individual variations, Zone 3 training\ngenerally increases slightly during competition periods. This\nunderscores how crucial high-intensity capacity is in competition,\nand athletes need to make a significant modification in their training\nregimen as the season enters its final stage.\n5 Investigation of the optimal training\nintensity distribution model:\nConsensus and controversies\nThe introduction of Training Intensity Distribution (TID)\nstrategies, including the pyramidal model, lactate threshold model,\nand polarized model, in the middle of the 20th century has\nsparked continuous debate about which model improves endurance\nperformance more universally and effectively in both academic and\npractical contexts", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "cal adaptation even amid repeated\nhigh-intensity racing.\nDespite sport and individual variations, Zone 3 training\ngenerally increases slightly during competition periods. This\nunderscores how crucial high-intensity capacity is in competition,\nand ath"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tid_theory_2025_p0016_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_7d8a7374e23c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "aed790d19698cfd8", "text_span_hash": "aed790d19698cfd8", "source_file": "train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "page": 16, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ity is in competition,\nand athletes need to make a significant modification in their training\nregimen as the season enters its final stage.\n5 Investigation of the optimal training\nintensity distribution model:\nConsensus and controversies\nThe introduc", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analogous TID patterns are also seen in cross-country skiers\nand biathletes during competition, with augmented high-volume,\nlow-intensity, and high-intensity training enabling athletes to\nsustain performance levels without inducing extreme fatigue.\nThe latest research indicates that the Training Intensity\nDistribution (TID) of top cross-country skiers may still maintain\na pyramidal structure even during peak competition periods, with\nHigh Volume Low Intensity Training (HVLIT) comprising up to\n88% of the training regimen (Torvik et al., 2021). This suggests\nthat some athletes depend heavily on low-intensity training to\nfacilitate recovery and physiological adaptation even amid repeated\nhigh-intensity racing.\nDespite sport and individual variations, Zone 3 training\ngenerally increases slightly during competition periods. This\nunderscores how crucial high-intensity capacity is in competition,\nand athletes need to make a significant modification in their training\nregimen as the season enters its final stage.\n5 Investigation of the optimal training\nintensity distribution model:\nConsensus and controversies\nThe introduction of Training Intensity Distribution (TID)\nstrategies, including the pyramidal model, lactate threshold model,\nand polarized model, in the middle of the 20th century has\nsparked continuous debate about which model improves endurance\nperformance more universally and effectively in both academic and\npractical contexts", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ity is in competition,\nand athletes need to make a significant modification in their training\nregimen as the season enters its final stage.\n5 Investigation of the optimal training\nintensity distribution model:\nConsensus and controversies\nThe introduc"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tid_theory_2025_p0016_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_7d8a7374e23c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "49672768ae12ff2c", "text_span_hash": "49672768ae12ff2c", "source_file": "train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "page": 16, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "and controversies\nThe introduction of Training Intensity Distribution (TID)\nstrategies, including the pyramidal model, lactate threshold model,\nand polarized model, in the middle of the 20th century has\nsparked continuous debate about which model imp", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analogous TID patterns are also seen in cross-country skiers\nand biathletes during competition, with augmented high-volume,\nlow-intensity, and high-intensity training enabling athletes to\nsustain performance levels without inducing extreme fatigue.\nThe latest research indicates that the Training Intensity\nDistribution (TID) of top cross-country skiers may still maintain\na pyramidal structure even during peak competition periods, with\nHigh Volume Low Intensity Training (HVLIT) comprising up to\n88% of the training regimen (Torvik et al., 2021). This suggests\nthat some athletes depend heavily on low-intensity training to\nfacilitate recovery and physiological adaptation even amid repeated\nhigh-intensity racing.\nDespite sport and individual variations, Zone 3 training\ngenerally increases slightly during competition periods. This\nunderscores how crucial high-intensity capacity is in competition,\nand athletes need to make a significant modification in their training\nregimen as the season enters its final stage.\n5 Investigation of the optimal training\nintensity distribution model:\nConsensus and controversies\nThe introduction of Training Intensity Distribution (TID)\nstrategies, including the pyramidal model, lactate threshold model,\nand polarized model, in the middle of the 20th century has\nsparked continuous debate about which model improves endurance\nperformance more universally and effectively in both academic and\npractical contexts", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "and controversies\nThe introduction of Training Intensity Distribution (TID)\nstrategies, including the pyramidal model, lactate threshold model,\nand polarized model, in the middle of the 20th century has\nsparked continuous debate about which model imp"}
