es, including the pyramidal model, lactate threshold model,\nand polarized model, in the middle of the 20th century has\nsparked continuous debate about which model improves endurance\nperformance more universally and effectively in both academic and\npractical contexts", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "s debate about which model improves endurance\nperformance more universally and effectively in both academic and\npractical contexts. Researchers have come to recognize since the 21st\ncentury that the lactate threshold model, allocating most training\nt"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tid_theory_2025_p0016_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_7d8a7374e23c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "01802ddd5a026238", "text_span_hash": "01802ddd5a026238", "source_file": "train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "page": 16, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "end (Li,\n2015). Therefore, the polarized and pyramidal models, both of which\nprioritize low-intensity training complemented with minor amounts\nof high-intensity training, have increasingly acquired traction and\nshown favorable training results in emp", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analogous TID patterns are also seen in cross-country skiers\nand biathletes during competition, with augmented high-volume,\nlow-intensity, and high-intensity training enabling athletes to\nsustain performance levels without inducing extreme fatigue.\nThe latest research indicates that the Training Intensity\nDistribution (TID) of top cross-country skiers may still maintain\na pyramidal structure even during peak competition periods, with\nHigh Volume Low Intensity Training (HVLIT) comprising up to\n88% of the training regimen (Torvik et al., 2021). This suggests\nthat some athletes depend heavily on low-intensity training to\nfacilitate recovery and physiological adaptation even amid repeated\nhigh-intensity racing.\nDespite sport and individual variations, Zone 3 training\ngenerally increases slightly during competition periods. This\nunderscores how crucial high-intensity capacity is in competition,\nand athletes need to make a significant modification in their training\nregimen as the season enters its final stage.\n5 Investigation of the optimal training\nintensity distribution model:\nConsensus and controversies\nThe introduction of Training Intensity Distribution (TID)\nstrategies, including the pyramidal model, lactate threshold model,\nand polarized model, in the middle of the 20th century has\nsparked continuous debate about which model improves endurance\nperformance more universally and effectively in both academic and\npractical contexts", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "end (Li,\n2015). Therefore, the polarized and pyramidal models, both of which\nprioritize low-intensity training complemented with minor amounts\nof high-intensity training, have increasingly acquired traction and\nshown favorable training results in emp"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tid_theory_2025_p0016_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_7d8a7374e23c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "74354eb2f8788c2b", "text_span_hash": "74354eb2f8788c2b", "source_file": "train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "page": 16, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "orable training results in empirical research (Stöggl and\nSperlich, 2014; Kenneally et al., 2021; Sperlich et al., 2023).\n5.1 Polarized training vs. lactate threshold\nThe lactate threshold (THR) training concept is well known\nas a fundamental approac", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analogous TID patterns are also seen in cross-country skiers\nand biathletes during competition, with augmented high-volume,\nlow-intensity, and high-intensity training enabling athletes to\nsustain performance levels without inducing extreme fatigue.\nThe latest research indicates that the Training Intensity\nDistribution (TID) of top cross-country skiers may still maintain\na pyramidal structure even during peak competition periods, with\nHigh Volume Low Intensity Training (HVLIT) comprising up to\n88% of the training regimen (Torvik et al., 2021). This suggests\nthat some athletes depend heavily on low-intensity training to\nfacilitate recovery and physiological adaptation even amid repeated\nhigh-intensity racing.\nDespite sport and individual variations, Zone 3 training\ngenerally increases slightly during competition periods. This\nunderscores how crucial high-intensity capacity is in competition,\nand athletes need to make a significant modification in their training\nregimen as the season enters its final stage.\n5 Investigation of the optimal training\nintensity distribution model:\nConsensus and controversies\nThe introduction of Training Intensity Distribution (TID)\nstrategies, including the pyramidal model, lactate threshold model,\nand polarized model, in the middle of the 20th century has\nsparked continuous debate about which model improves endurance\nperformance more universally and effectively in both academic and\npractical contexts", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "orable training results in empirical research (Stöggl and\nSperlich, 2014; Kenneally et al., 2021; Sperlich et al., 2023).\n5.1 Polarized training vs. lactate threshold\nThe lactate threshold (THR) training concept is well known\nas a fundamental approac"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tid_theory_2025_p0016_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_7d8a7374e23c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "fd1f44088a977b9d", "text_span_hash": "fd1f44088a977b9d", "source_file": "train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "page": 16, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "known\nas a fundamental approach for improving endurance performance.\nIts fundamental idea is to enhance athletes’ performance at\nthe lactate threshold via a substantial amount of moderate-to-\nhigh-intensity training. Subsequent research has revealed", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analogous TID patterns are also seen in cross-country skiers\nand biathletes during competition, with augmented high-volume,\nlow-intensity, and high-intensity training enabling athletes to\nsustain performance levels without inducing extreme fatigue.\nThe latest research indicates that the Training Intensity\nDistribution (TID) of top cross-country skiers may still maintain\na pyramidal structure even during peak competition periods, with\nHigh Volume Low Intensity Training (HVLIT) comprising up to\n88% of the training regimen (Torvik et al., 2021). This suggests\nthat some athletes depend heavily on low-intensity training to\nfacilitate recovery and physiological adaptation even amid repeated\nhigh-intensity racing.\nDespite sport and individual variations, Zone 3 training\ngenerally increases slightly during competition periods. This\nunderscores how crucial high-intensity capacity is in competition,\nand athletes need to make a significant modification in their training\nregimen as the season enters its final stage.\n5 Investigation of the optimal training\nintensity distribution model:\nConsensus and controversies\nThe introduction of Training Intensity Distribution (TID)\nstrategies, including the pyramidal model, lactate threshold model,\nand polarized model, in the middle of the 20th century has\nsparked continuous debate about which model improves endurance\nperformance more universally and effectively in both academic and\npractical contexts", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "known\nas a fundamental approach for improving endurance performance.\nIts fundamental idea is to enhance athletes’ performance at\nthe lactate threshold via a substantial amount of moderate-to-\nhigh-intensity training. Subsequent research has revealed"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tid_theory_2025_p0016_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_7d8a7374e23c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8960a4d9bcae1d05", "text_span_hash": "8960a4d9bcae1d05", "source_file": "train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "page": 16, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "volume is high (Seiler, 2010).\nThe polarized training approach, with a significant proportion\nof low-intensity training (about 75%–80%) and a small quantity\nof high-intensity activity (around 10%–15%), provides a more\nequitable load distribution. It", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analogous TID patterns are also seen in cross-country skiers\nand biathletes during competition, with augmented high-volume,\nlow-intensity, and high-intensity training enabling athletes to\nsustain performance levels without inducing extreme fatigue.\nThe latest research indicates that the Training Intensity\nDistribution (TID) of top cross-country skiers may still maintain\na pyramidal structure even during peak competition periods, with\nHigh Volume Low Intensity Training (HVLIT) comprising up to\n88% of the training regimen (Torvik et al., 2021). This suggests\nthat some athletes depend heavily on low-intensity training to\nfacilitate recovery and physiological adaptation even amid repeated\nhigh-intensity racing.\nDespite sport and individual variations, Zone 3 training\ngenerally increases slightly during competition periods. This\nunderscores how crucial high-intensity capacity is in competition,\nand athletes need to make a significant modification in their training\nregimen as the season enters its final stage.\n5 Investigation of the optimal training\nintensity distribution model:\nConsensus and controversies\nThe introduction of Training Intensity Distribution (TID)\nstrategies, including the pyramidal model, lactate threshold model,\nand polarized model, in the middle of the 20th century has\nsparked continuous debate about which model improves endurance\nperformance more universally and effectively in both academic and\npractical contexts", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "volume is high (Seiler, 2010).\nThe polarized training approach, with a significant proportion\nof low-intensity training (about 75%–80%) and a small quantity\nof high-intensity activity (around 10%–15%), provides a more\nequitable load distribution. It"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tid_theory_2025_p0016_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_7d8a7374e23c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f608c1056c196959", "text_span_hash": "f608c1056c196959", "source_file": "train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "page": 16, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "quitable load distribution. It efficiently mitigates recovery stress\nand the restricted adaptation efficiency usually associated with\nprolonged moderate-intensity training, thereby offering a more\nrational framework for balancing training outcomes an", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analogous TID patterns are also seen in cross-country skiers\nand biathletes during competition, with augmented high-volume,\nlow-intensity, and high-intensity training enabling athletes to\nsustain performance levels without inducing extreme fatigue.\nThe latest research indicates that the Training Intensity\nDistribution (TID) of top cross-country skiers may still maintain\na pyramidal structure even during peak competition periods, with\nHigh Volume Low Intensity Training (HVLIT) comprising up to\n88% of the training regimen (Torvik et al., 2021). This suggests\nthat some athletes depend heavily on low-intensity training to\nfacilitate recovery and physiological adaptation even amid repeated\nhigh-intensity racing.\nDespite sport and individual variations, Zone 3 training\ngenerally increases slightly during competition periods. This\nunderscores how crucial high-intensity capacity is in competition,\nand athletes need to make a significant modification in their training\nregimen as the season enters its final stage.\n5 Investigation of the optimal training\nintensity distribution model:\nConsensus and controversies\nThe introduction of Training Intensity Distribution (TID)\nstrategies, including the pyramidal model, lactate threshold model,\nand polarized model, in the middle of the 20th century has\nsparked continuous debate about which model improves endurance\nperformance more universally and effectively in both academic and\npractical contexts", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "quitable load distribution. It efficiently mitigates recovery stress\nand the restricted adaptation efficiency usually associated with\nprolonged moderate-intensity training, thereby offering a more\nrational framework for balancing training outcomes an"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tid_theory_2025_p0016_c0018", "source_registry_id": "src_7d8a7374e23c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9689366ffebaadaf", "text_span_hash": "9689366ffebaadaf", "source_file": "train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "page": 16, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 18, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "sive development of elite endurance athletes because it offers\ngreater flexibility and long-term sustainability for coaches.\nIn conclusion, the polarized training model has gained wide\nattention in modern endurance training for its physiological\nand", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analogous TID patterns are also seen in cross-country skiers\nand biathletes during competition, with augmented high-volume,\nlow-intensity, and high-intensity training enabling athletes to\nsustain performance levels without inducing extreme fatigue.\nThe latest research indicates that the Training Intensity\nDistribution (TID) of top cross-country skiers may still maintain\na pyramidal structure even during peak competition periods, with\nHigh Volume Low Intensity Training (HVLIT) comprising up to\n88% of the training regimen (Torvik et al., 2021). This suggests\nthat some athletes depend heavily on low-intensity training to\nfacilitate recovery and physiological adaptation even amid repeated\nhigh-intensity racing.\nDespite sport and individual variations, Zone 3 training\ngenerally increases slightly during competition periods. This\nunderscores how crucial high-intensity capacity is in competition,\nand athletes need to make a significant modification in their training\nregimen as the season enters its final stage.\n5 Investigation of the optimal training\nintensity distribution model:\nConsensus and controversies\nThe introduction of Training Intensity Distribution (TID)\nstrategies, including the pyramidal model, lactate threshold model,\nand polarized model, in the middle of the 20th century has\nsparked continuous debate about which model improves endurance\nperformance more universally and effectively in both academic and\npractical contexts", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "sive development of elite endurance athletes because it offers\ngreater flexibility and long-term sustainability for coaches.\nIn conclusion, the polarized training model has gained wide\nattention in modern endurance training for its physiological\nand"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tid_theory_2025_p0016_c0019", "source_registry_id": "src_7d8a7374e23c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "724c6f04da52bcd8", "text_span_hash": "724c6f04da52bcd8", "source_file": "train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "page": 16, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 19, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ing for its physiological\nand performance benefits. However, evidence also highlights\nthe effectiveness of pyramidal and threshold models in certain\ncontexts, underscoring that no single model is universally\n5.2 Polarized training vs. pyramidal train", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Analogous TID patterns are also seen in cross-country skiers\nand biathletes during competition, with augmented high-volume,\nlow-intensity, and high-intensity training enabling athletes to\nsustain performance levels without inducing extreme fatigue.\nThe latest research indicates that the Training Intensity\nDistribution (TID) of top cross-country skiers may still maintain\na pyramidal structure even during peak competition periods, with\nHigh Volume Low Intensity Training (HVLIT) comprising up to\n88% of the training regimen (Torvik et al., 2021). This suggests\nthat some athletes depend heavily on low-intensity training to\nfacilitate recovery and physiological adaptation even amid repeated\nhigh-intensity racing.\nDespite sport and individual variations, Zone 3 training\ngenerally increases slightly during competition periods. This\nunderscores how crucial high-intensity capacity is in competition,\nand athletes need to make a significant modification in their training\nregimen as the season enters its final stage.\n5 Investigation of the optimal training\nintensity distribution model:\nConsensus and controversies\nThe introduction of Training Intensity Distribution (TID)\nstrategies, including the pyramidal model, lactate threshold model,\nand polarized model, in the middle of the 20th century has\nsparked continuous debate about which model improves endurance\nperformance more universally and effectively in both academic and\npractical contexts", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ing for its physiological\nand performance benefits. However, evidence also highlights\nthe effectiveness of pyramidal and threshold models in certain\ncontexts, underscoring that no single model is universally\n5.2 Polarized training vs. pyramidal train"}
