e resulting training intensity\ndistribution (TID) often exhibits a pyramidal structure when training\nload is assessed using the subjective rating of perceived exertion (RPE)\nscale. However, a more distinctly polarized distribution is typically seen\nin classifica", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "marathon running.\nIn contrast, activities like cycling, swimming, and rowing\ntypically show a pyramidal distribution (Selles Pérez et al., 2019;\nTreff et al., 2017; Sperlich et al., 2023).\nTraining volume and periodization methodologies are\nsignific"}
