rther shapes model choice. During general\npreparation, PYR is advantageous for accumulating large volumes\nof low-intensity work and building aerobic capacity. In the specific\npreparation phase, coaches often introduce more Z2 and Z3 load,\nwhich may resemble a POL ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "wing, threshold (THR) or PYR\nstructures are common, reflecting the importance of technical\nefficiency and sustained submaximal workloads. In triathlon, mixed\ndistributions are frequently applied, with model selection varying\naccording to the relative"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tid_theory_2025_p0020_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_7d8a7374e23c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ba49ec6f9e4ec5bb", "text_span_hash": "ba49ec6f9e4ec5bb", "source_file": "train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "page": 20, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "align TID selection with the sport’s demands, training phase,\nathlete level, and physiological profile, rather than adhering to a\nTraining Intensity Distribution (TID) has emerged as a critical\nelement in sports training science, holding significant", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "This would enhance the clarity and comparability of future studies.\nSecond, there is a need for longitudinal research to examine how\nTID evolves over an athlete’s career and in response to different\nFinally, bridging the gap between theory and practice is\nparamount. The practical application of TID models must be\nadaptable and individualized, not prescriptive.\n7 Practical considerations for coaches\nEffective application of Training Intensity Distribution (TID)\nmodels requires consideration of multiple contextual factors rather\nthan reliance on a single framework. Sport type plays an important\nrole: running and cross-country skiing athletes often benefit\nfrom polarized training (POL) to maximize VO2max and lactate\nclearance, whereas professional cycling more frequently shows a\npyramidal (PYR) distribution when training volume is measured\nby distance. In swimming and rowing, threshold (THR) or PYR\nstructures are common, reflecting the importance of technical\nefficiency and sustained submaximal workloads. In triathlon, mixed\ndistributions are frequently applied, with model selection varying\naccording to the relative demands of each discipline and phase-\nTraining phase further shapes model choice. During general\npreparation, PYR is advantageous for accumulating large volumes\nof low-intensity work and building aerobic capacity. In the specific\npreparation phase, coaches often introduce more Z2 and Z3 load,\nwhich may resemble a POL ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "align TID selection with the sport’s demands, training phase,\nathlete level, and physiological profile, rather than adhering to a\nTraining Intensity Distribution (TID) has emerged as a critical\nelement in sports training science, holding significant"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tid_theory_2025_p0020_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_7d8a7374e23c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "374ad770a3bee458", "text_span_hash": "374ad770a3bee458", "source_file": "train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "page": 20, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "science, holding significant theoretical\nimportance and practical applicability in cyclic endurance sports.\nWhile models such as Polarized (POL), Pyramidal (PYR), and\nThreshold (THR) each demonstrate value, the central finding of\nthis review is that", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "This would enhance the clarity and comparability of future studies.\nSecond, there is a need for longitudinal research to examine how\nTID evolves over an athlete’s career and in response to different\nFinally, bridging the gap between theory and practice is\nparamount. The practical application of TID models must be\nadaptable and individualized, not prescriptive.\n7 Practical considerations for coaches\nEffective application of Training Intensity Distribution (TID)\nmodels requires consideration of multiple contextual factors rather\nthan reliance on a single framework. Sport type plays an important\nrole: running and cross-country skiing athletes often benefit\nfrom polarized training (POL) to maximize VO2max and lactate\nclearance, whereas professional cycling more frequently shows a\npyramidal (PYR) distribution when training volume is measured\nby distance. In swimming and rowing, threshold (THR) or PYR\nstructures are common, reflecting the importance of technical\nefficiency and sustained submaximal workloads. In triathlon, mixed\ndistributions are frequently applied, with model selection varying\naccording to the relative demands of each discipline and phase-\nTraining phase further shapes model choice. During general\npreparation, PYR is advantageous for accumulating large volumes\nof low-intensity work and building aerobic capacity. In the specific\npreparation phase, coaches often introduce more Z2 and Z3 load,\nwhich may resemble a POL ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "science, holding significant theoretical\nimportance and practical applicability in cyclic endurance sports.\nWhile models such as Polarized (POL), Pyramidal (PYR), and\nThreshold (THR) each demonstrate value, the central finding of\nthis review is that"}
{"chunk_id": "train_tid_theory_2025_p0020_c0016", "source_registry_id": "src_7d8a7374e23c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "32cbda92601b4f02", "text_span_hash": "32cbda92601b4f02", "source_file": "train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_tid_theory_2025.pdf", "page": 20, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 16, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "sequently, ideal TID selection should not be a\nrigid formula but rather a dynamic process. Model choice must\nbe flexibly adjusted according to the sport’s nature, training phase,\nand individual athlete profile. For instance, POL often benefits\nendura", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "This would enhance the clarity and comparability of future studies.\nSecond, there is a need for longitudinal research to examine how\nTID evolves over an athlete’s career and in response to different\nFinally, bridging the gap between theory and practice is\nparamount. The practical application of TID models must be\nadaptable and individualized, not prescriptive.\n7 Practical considerations for coaches\nEffective application of Training Intensity Distribution (TID)\nmodels requires consideration of multiple contextual factors rather\nthan reliance on a single framework. Sport type plays an important\nrole: running and cross-country skiing athletes often benefit\nfrom polarized training (POL) to maximize VO2max and lactate\nclearance, whereas professional cycling more frequently shows a\npyramidal (PYR) distribution when training volume is measured\nby distance. In swimming and rowing, threshold (THR) or PYR\nstructures are common, reflecting the importance of technical\nefficiency and sustained submaximal workloads. In triathlon, mixed\ndistributions are frequently applied, with model selection varying\naccording to the relative demands of each discipline and phase-\nTraining phase further shapes model choice. During general\npreparation, PYR is advantageous for accumulating large volumes\nof low-intensity work and building aerobic capacity. In the specific\npreparation phase, coaches often introduce more Z2 and Z3 load,\nwhich may resemble a POL ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "sequently, ideal TID selection should not be a\nrigid formula but rather a dynamic process. Model choice must\nbe flexibly adjusted according to the sport’s nature, training phase,\nand individual athlete profile. For instance, POL often benefits\nendura"}
