0–170 vs. 50–120 km·week−1 during the prepara-\ntion period) and higher aerobic/anaerobic training distribution (90/10 vs. 60/40% of the annual running sessions below vs.\nat or above anaerobic threshold) distinguish 1500- and 800-m runners. Lactate tolerance and lactate production training are\nregularly i", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "1\nDespite an increasing amount of research devoted to middle-distance training (herein the 800 and 1500 m events), informa-\ntion regarding the training methodologies of world-class runners is limited. Therefore, the objective of this review was to\nin"}
{"chunk_id": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021_p0001_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_13b96c46afc6", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "577b05b3ce91dd98", "text_span_hash": "577b05b3ce91dd98", "source_file": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ctive of this review was to\nintegrate scientific and best practice literature and outline a novel framework for understanding the training and development\nof elite middle-distance performance. Herein, we describe how well-known training principles an", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Crossing the Golden Training Divide: The Science and Practice\nof Training World‑Class 800‑ and 1500‑m Runners\nThomas Haugen1  · Øyvind Sandbakk2 · Eystein Enoksen3 · Stephen Seiler4 · Espen Tønnessen1\n© The Author(s) 2021\nDespite an increasing amount of research devoted to middle-distance training (herein the 800 and 1500 m events), informa-\ntion regarding the training methodologies of world-class runners is limited. Therefore, the objective of this review was to\nintegrate scientific and best practice literature and outline a novel framework for understanding the training and development\nof elite middle-distance performance. Herein, we describe how well-known training principles and fundamental training\ncharacteristics are applied by world-leading middle-distance coaches and athletes to meet the physiological and neuromus-\ncular demands of 800 and 1500 m. Large diversities in physiological profiles and training emerge among middle-distance\nrunners, justifying a categorization into types across a continuum (400–800 m types, 800 m specialists, 800–1500 m types,\n1500 m specialists and 1500–5000 m types). Larger running volumes (120–170 vs. 50–120 km·week−1 during the prepara-\ntion period) and higher aerobic/anaerobic training distribution (90/10 vs. 60/40% of the annual running sessions below vs.\nat or above anaerobic threshold) distinguish 1500- and 800-m runners. Lactate tolerance and lactate production training are\nregularly i", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ctive of this review was to\nintegrate scientific and best practice literature and outline a novel framework for understanding the training and development\nof elite middle-distance performance. Herein, we describe how well-known training principles an"}
{"chunk_id": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021_p0001_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_13b96c46afc6", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "660d93223a3e506f", "text_span_hash": "660d93223a3e506f", "source_file": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "page": 1, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "l-known training principles and fundamental training\ncharacteristics are applied by world-leading middle-distance coaches and athletes to meet the physiological and neuromus-\ncular demands of 800 and 1500 m. Large diversities in physiological profile", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Crossing the Golden Training Divide: The Science and Practice\nof Training World‑Class 800‑ and 1500‑m Runners\nThomas Haugen1  · Øyvind Sandbakk2 · Eystein Enoksen3 · Stephen Seiler4 · Espen Tønnessen1\n© The Author(s) 2021\nDespite an increasing amount of research devoted to middle-distance training (herein the 800 and 1500 m events), informa-\ntion regarding the training methodologies of world-class runners is limited. Therefore, the objective of this review was to\nintegrate scientific and best practice literature and outline a novel framework for understanding the training and development\nof elite middle-distance performance. Herein, we describe how well-known training principles and fundamental training\ncharacteristics are applied by world-leading middle-distance coaches and athletes to meet the physiological and neuromus-\ncular demands of 800 and 1500 m. Large diversities in physiological profiles and training emerge among middle-distance\nrunners, justifying a categorization into types across a continuum (400–800 m types, 800 m specialists, 800–1500 m types,\n1500 m specialists and 1500–5000 m types). Larger running volumes (120–170 vs. 50–120 km·week−1 during the prepara-\ntion period) and higher aerobic/anaerobic training distribution (90/10 vs. 60/40% of the annual running sessions below vs.\nat or above anaerobic threshold) distinguish 1500- and 800-m runners. Lactate tolerance and lactate production training are\nregularly i", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "l-known training principles and fundamental training\ncharacteristics are applied by world-leading middle-distance coaches and athletes to meet the physiological and neuromus-\ncular demands of 800 and 1500 m. Large diversities in physiological profile"}
