{"chunk_id": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021_p0002_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_13b96c46afc6", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "32ba8a8447730928", "text_span_hash": "32ba8a8447730928", "source_file": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Middle-distance running was a central part of the Olym-\npic program for men already at the first modern Games\nin 1896. Over the last century, quantum leaps in men’s\nperformance have been achieved by barrier breaking ath-\nletes such as Paavo Nurmi, Gu", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Middle-distance running was a central part of the Olym-\npic program for men already at the first modern Games\nin 1896. Over the last century, quantum leaps in men’s\nperformance have been achieved by barrier breaking ath-\nletes such as Paavo Nurmi, Gunder Hägg, Rudolf Harbig\nand Roger Bannister. The progression of female middle-\ndistance running performances was initially slower than\nthat observed for men [1], but this was due to social, not\nbiological constraints. By the 1928 Olympic Games,\nwomen competed in 2 of the 13 running events contested\nby men, the 100 and 800 m. Unfortunately, even this small\nprogress was halted when the International Olympic Com-\nmittee (IOC) received erroneous reports of female athletes\ncollapsing after running the 800 m and decided to ban\nwomen from competing over distances longer than 200 m.\nThe middle-distance events were not added to the Olympic\nprogram for women until 1960, after which the sex-gap in\nmiddle-distance performance declined gradually until the\n1980s. Since then, male and female sex-specific perfor-\nmance differences have stabilized around ~ 10% [2].\nDespite an increasing amount of research devoted to\nmiddle-distance training [e.g., 3–17], it is reasonable to\nargue that the developments in these disciplines have not\nbeen driven by sport scientists [18]. Publicly available\n“recipe books” and training diaries based upon the practi-\ncal experience and intuition of world-leading athlete", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Middle-distance running was a central part of the Olym-\npic program for men already at the first modern Games\nin 1896. Over the last century, quantum leaps in men’s\nperformance have been achieved by barrier breaking ath-\nletes such as Paavo Nurmi, Gu"}
{"chunk_id": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021_p0002_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_13b96c46afc6", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "36af46db27027ffb", "text_span_hash": "36af46db27027ffb", "source_file": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "~ 10% [2].\nDespite an increasing amount of research devoted to\nmiddle-distance training [e.g., 3–17], it is reasonable to\nargue that the developments in these disciplines have not\nbeen driven by sport scientists [18]. Publicly available\n“recipe book", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Middle-distance running was a central part of the Olym-\npic program for men already at the first modern Games\nin 1896. Over the last century, quantum leaps in men’s\nperformance have been achieved by barrier breaking ath-\nletes such as Paavo Nurmi, Gunder Hägg, Rudolf Harbig\nand Roger Bannister. The progression of female middle-\ndistance running performances was initially slower than\nthat observed for men [1], but this was due to social, not\nbiological constraints. By the 1928 Olympic Games,\nwomen competed in 2 of the 13 running events contested\nby men, the 100 and 800 m. Unfortunately, even this small\nprogress was halted when the International Olympic Com-\nmittee (IOC) received erroneous reports of female athletes\ncollapsing after running the 800 m and decided to ban\nwomen from competing over distances longer than 200 m.\nThe middle-distance events were not added to the Olympic\nprogram for women until 1960, after which the sex-gap in\nmiddle-distance performance declined gradually until the\n1980s. Since then, male and female sex-specific perfor-\nmance differences have stabilized around ~ 10% [2].\nDespite an increasing amount of research devoted to\nmiddle-distance training [e.g., 3–17], it is reasonable to\nargue that the developments in these disciplines have not\nbeen driven by sport scientists [18]. Publicly available\n“recipe books” and training diaries based upon the practi-\ncal experience and intuition of world-leading athlete", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "~ 10% [2].\nDespite an increasing amount of research devoted to\nmiddle-distance training [e.g., 3–17], it is reasonable to\nargue that the developments in these disciplines have not\nbeen driven by sport scientists [18]. Publicly available\n“recipe book"}
{"chunk_id": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021_p0002_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_13b96c46afc6", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "2828bbca63e4b5dc", "text_span_hash": "2828bbca63e4b5dc", "source_file": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ublicly available\n“recipe books” and training diaries based upon the practi-\ncal experience and intuition of world-leading athletes and\ncoaches have become important and popular sources of\nbest practice training information and framework devel-\nopmen", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Middle-distance running was a central part of the Olym-\npic program for men already at the first modern Games\nin 1896. Over the last century, quantum leaps in men’s\nperformance have been achieved by barrier breaking ath-\nletes such as Paavo Nurmi, Gunder Hägg, Rudolf Harbig\nand Roger Bannister. The progression of female middle-\ndistance running performances was initially slower than\nthat observed for men [1], but this was due to social, not\nbiological constraints. By the 1928 Olympic Games,\nwomen competed in 2 of the 13 running events contested\nby men, the 100 and 800 m. Unfortunately, even this small\nprogress was halted when the International Olympic Com-\nmittee (IOC) received erroneous reports of female athletes\ncollapsing after running the 800 m and decided to ban\nwomen from competing over distances longer than 200 m.\nThe middle-distance events were not added to the Olympic\nprogram for women until 1960, after which the sex-gap in\nmiddle-distance performance declined gradually until the\n1980s. Since then, male and female sex-specific perfor-\nmance differences have stabilized around ~ 10% [2].\nDespite an increasing amount of research devoted to\nmiddle-distance training [e.g., 3–17], it is reasonable to\nargue that the developments in these disciplines have not\nbeen driven by sport scientists [18]. Publicly available\n“recipe books” and training diaries based upon the practi-\ncal experience and intuition of world-leading athlete", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ublicly available\n“recipe books” and training diaries based upon the practi-\ncal experience and intuition of world-leading athletes and\ncoaches have become important and popular sources of\nbest practice training information and framework devel-\nopmen"}
{"chunk_id": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021_p0002_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_13b96c46afc6", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "006c7fac168a4a1a", "text_span_hash": "006c7fac168a4a1a", "source_file": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ion and framework devel-\nopment for the international middle-distance community\nsprinting [60] and long-distance running [61–65] has been\nscientifically reported, information regarding the varying\ntraining components across the annual cycle of world-", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Middle-distance running was a central part of the Olym-\npic program for men already at the first modern Games\nin 1896. Over the last century, quantum leaps in men’s\nperformance have been achieved by barrier breaking ath-\nletes such as Paavo Nurmi, Gunder Hägg, Rudolf Harbig\nand Roger Bannister. The progression of female middle-\ndistance running performances was initially slower than\nthat observed for men [1], but this was due to social, not\nbiological constraints. By the 1928 Olympic Games,\nwomen competed in 2 of the 13 running events contested\nby men, the 100 and 800 m. Unfortunately, even this small\nprogress was halted when the International Olympic Com-\nmittee (IOC) received erroneous reports of female athletes\ncollapsing after running the 800 m and decided to ban\nwomen from competing over distances longer than 200 m.\nThe middle-distance events were not added to the Olympic\nprogram for women until 1960, after which the sex-gap in\nmiddle-distance performance declined gradually until the\n1980s. Since then, male and female sex-specific perfor-\nmance differences have stabilized around ~ 10% [2].\nDespite an increasing amount of research devoted to\nmiddle-distance training [e.g., 3–17], it is reasonable to\nargue that the developments in these disciplines have not\nbeen driven by sport scientists [18]. Publicly available\n“recipe books” and training diaries based upon the practi-\ncal experience and intuition of world-leading athlete", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ion and framework devel-\nopment for the international middle-distance community\nsprinting [60] and long-distance running [61–65] has been\nscientifically reported, information regarding the varying\ntraining components across the annual cycle of world-"}
{"chunk_id": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021_p0002_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_13b96c46afc6", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e6718f225b7766a7", "text_span_hash": "e6718f225b7766a7", "source_file": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "oss the annual cycle of world-class\nmiddle-distance runners is limited. Furthermore, the train-\ning characteristics of 800- and 1500-m runners have not\nyet been systematically compared. Such a comparison is\nwarranted because of the marked shift towar", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Middle-distance running was a central part of the Olym-\npic program for men already at the first modern Games\nin 1896. Over the last century, quantum leaps in men’s\nperformance have been achieved by barrier breaking ath-\nletes such as Paavo Nurmi, Gunder Hägg, Rudolf Harbig\nand Roger Bannister. The progression of female middle-\ndistance running performances was initially slower than\nthat observed for men [1], but this was due to social, not\nbiological constraints. By the 1928 Olympic Games,\nwomen competed in 2 of the 13 running events contested\nby men, the 100 and 800 m. Unfortunately, even this small\nprogress was halted when the International Olympic Com-\nmittee (IOC) received erroneous reports of female athletes\ncollapsing after running the 800 m and decided to ban\nwomen from competing over distances longer than 200 m.\nThe middle-distance events were not added to the Olympic\nprogram for women until 1960, after which the sex-gap in\nmiddle-distance performance declined gradually until the\n1980s. Since then, male and female sex-specific perfor-\nmance differences have stabilized around ~ 10% [2].\nDespite an increasing amount of research devoted to\nmiddle-distance training [e.g., 3–17], it is reasonable to\nargue that the developments in these disciplines have not\nbeen driven by sport scientists [18]. Publicly available\n“recipe books” and training diaries based upon the practi-\ncal experience and intuition of world-leading athlete", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "oss the annual cycle of world-class\nmiddle-distance runners is limited. Furthermore, the train-\ning characteristics of 800- and 1500-m runners have not\nyet been systematically compared. Such a comparison is\nwarranted because of the marked shift towar"}
{"chunk_id": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021_p0002_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_13b96c46afc6", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "626a13cbfa0ff189", "text_span_hash": "626a13cbfa0ff189", "source_file": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "page": 2, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ause of the marked shift towards a more dis-\ntinct emphasis on aerobic energy provision from 800 to\n1500 m as well as the interactions between mechanical\neffectiveness and metabolic efficiency in this transition.\nTherefore, the objective of this revi", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Middle-distance running was a central part of the Olym-\npic program for men already at the first modern Games\nin 1896. Over the last century, quantum leaps in men’s\nperformance have been achieved by barrier breaking ath-\nletes such as Paavo Nurmi, Gunder Hägg, Rudolf Harbig\nand Roger Bannister. The progression of female middle-\ndistance running performances was initially slower than\nthat observed for men [1], but this was due to social, not\nbiological constraints. By the 1928 Olympic Games,\nwomen competed in 2 of the 13 running events contested\nby men, the 100 and 800 m. Unfortunately, even this small\nprogress was halted when the International Olympic Com-\nmittee (IOC) received erroneous reports of female athletes\ncollapsing after running the 800 m and decided to ban\nwomen from competing over distances longer than 200 m.\nThe middle-distance events were not added to the Olympic\nprogram for women until 1960, after which the sex-gap in\nmiddle-distance performance declined gradually until the\n1980s. Since then, male and female sex-specific perfor-\nmance differences have stabilized around ~ 10% [2].\nDespite an increasing amount of research devoted to\nmiddle-distance training [e.g., 3–17], it is reasonable to\nargue that the developments in these disciplines have not\nbeen driven by sport scientists [18]. Publicly available\n“recipe books” and training diaries based upon the practi-\ncal experience and intuition of world-leading athlete", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ause of the marked shift towards a more dis-\ntinct emphasis on aerobic energy provision from 800 to\n1500 m as well as the interactions between mechanical\neffectiveness and metabolic efficiency in this transition.\nTherefore, the objective of this revi"}
