{"chunk_id": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021_p0003_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_13b96c46afc6", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "415d8c288908d358", "text_span_hash": "415d8c288908d358", "source_file": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "training log\nChronicle and training log\nOlympic gold 1500 m 1976\nMagazine article/interview\nPan American champion 1971\nChronicle and training log\nChronicle and training log\nNatalia Rodriguez [43]\nWC and EC gold 2010–2011\nOlympic medals 2008 and 2016", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training and development of world-class middle-distance athletes\nenergy contribution estimates overlap reasonably well with the\nreported type I muscle fiber distribution ranges in middle-dis-\ntance runners [13]. Just as has been well established for long-\ndistance running, maximal oxygen uptake (VO2max), fractional\nutilization of VO2max, running economy (RE), velocity at the\nanaerobic threshold (vAT), and velocity at VO2max (vVO2max)\nare all positively correlated with middle-distance perfor-\nmance [5, 8, 67]. However, to optimize energy mobilization\nand utilization, ­O2 kinetics as well as anaerobic power and\ncapacity play decisive roles in middle distance performance.\nAs Olympic gold medalist 800-m runner Vebjørn Rodal suc-\ncinctly summarized the importance of ­O2 kinetics to one of\nthe authors (ØS): “It does not matter if I can reach a higher\nVO2max in five minutes when I have to cross the finish line\nin 102 s.” In addition, both energy expenditure capacity and\neconomy/efficiency likely deteriorate during middle-distance\nTable 1   Sources of best practice training information\nIn addition, we have had personal communications with Vebjørn Rodal (Olympic 800-m champion in 1996) and Arturo Casado (European\n1500-m champion in 2010). Novel training data from these athletes are presented in Table 6\nWC world championships, EC European championships, WR former or current world-record holder\na Honore Hoedt coached Sifan Hassan during ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "training log\nChronicle and training log\nOlympic gold 1500 m 1976\nMagazine article/interview\nPan American champion 1971\nChronicle and training log\nChronicle and training log\nNatalia Rodriguez [43]\nWC and EC gold 2010–2011\nOlympic medals 2008 and 2016"}
{"chunk_id": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021_p0003_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_13b96c46afc6", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f1a3be1335858c10", "text_span_hash": "f1a3be1335858c10", "source_file": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "1\nOlympic medals 2008 and 2016\nChronicle and training log\nOlympic gold 1984, WC gold 1987\nTaoufik Makloufi [46]\nSuccessful middle-distance athletes\nArthur Lydiard [19–21]\nPeter Snell (WR), Murray Halberg, Barry Magee\nOlympic gold 1960 and 1964\nSteve", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training and development of world-class middle-distance athletes\nenergy contribution estimates overlap reasonably well with the\nreported type I muscle fiber distribution ranges in middle-dis-\ntance runners [13]. Just as has been well established for long-\ndistance running, maximal oxygen uptake (VO2max), fractional\nutilization of VO2max, running economy (RE), velocity at the\nanaerobic threshold (vAT), and velocity at VO2max (vVO2max)\nare all positively correlated with middle-distance perfor-\nmance [5, 8, 67]. However, to optimize energy mobilization\nand utilization, ­O2 kinetics as well as anaerobic power and\ncapacity play decisive roles in middle distance performance.\nAs Olympic gold medalist 800-m runner Vebjørn Rodal suc-\ncinctly summarized the importance of ­O2 kinetics to one of\nthe authors (ØS): “It does not matter if I can reach a higher\nVO2max in five minutes when I have to cross the finish line\nin 102 s.” In addition, both energy expenditure capacity and\neconomy/efficiency likely deteriorate during middle-distance\nTable 1   Sources of best practice training information\nIn addition, we have had personal communications with Vebjørn Rodal (Olympic 800-m champion in 1996) and Arturo Casado (European\n1500-m champion in 2010). Novel training data from these athletes are presented in Table 6\nWC world championships, EC European championships, WR former or current world-record holder\na Honore Hoedt coached Sifan Hassan during ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "1\nOlympic medals 2008 and 2016\nChronicle and training log\nOlympic gold 1984, WC gold 1987\nTaoufik Makloufi [46]\nSuccessful middle-distance athletes\nArthur Lydiard [19–21]\nPeter Snell (WR), Murray Halberg, Barry Magee\nOlympic gold 1960 and 1964\nSteve"}
{"chunk_id": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021_p0003_c0012", "source_registry_id": "src_13b96c46afc6", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "136b739f43181f6c", "text_span_hash": "136b739f43181f6c", "source_file": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 12, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "als since 2001\nHarry Wilson [34, 57]\nOlympic gold 1980, EC gold 1978\nChronicle/training log\nSifan Hassan (WR)a, Brad Som, Amoud Okken\nWC & EC medals since 2006\nCoached seven athletes to the U.S. Olympic team Olympic finalists\nGenzebe Dibaba (WR), Abd", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training and development of world-class middle-distance athletes\nenergy contribution estimates overlap reasonably well with the\nreported type I muscle fiber distribution ranges in middle-dis-\ntance runners [13]. Just as has been well established for long-\ndistance running, maximal oxygen uptake (VO2max), fractional\nutilization of VO2max, running economy (RE), velocity at the\nanaerobic threshold (vAT), and velocity at VO2max (vVO2max)\nare all positively correlated with middle-distance perfor-\nmance [5, 8, 67]. However, to optimize energy mobilization\nand utilization, ­O2 kinetics as well as anaerobic power and\ncapacity play decisive roles in middle distance performance.\nAs Olympic gold medalist 800-m runner Vebjørn Rodal suc-\ncinctly summarized the importance of ­O2 kinetics to one of\nthe authors (ØS): “It does not matter if I can reach a higher\nVO2max in five minutes when I have to cross the finish line\nin 102 s.” In addition, both energy expenditure capacity and\neconomy/efficiency likely deteriorate during middle-distance\nTable 1   Sources of best practice training information\nIn addition, we have had personal communications with Vebjørn Rodal (Olympic 800-m champion in 1996) and Arturo Casado (European\n1500-m champion in 2010). Novel training data from these athletes are presented in Table 6\nWC world championships, EC European championships, WR former or current world-record holder\na Honore Hoedt coached Sifan Hassan during ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "als since 2001\nHarry Wilson [34, 57]\nOlympic gold 1980, EC gold 1978\nChronicle/training log\nSifan Hassan (WR)a, Brad Som, Amoud Okken\nWC & EC medals since 2006\nCoached seven athletes to the U.S. Olympic team Olympic finalists\nGenzebe Dibaba (WR), Abd"}
{"chunk_id": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021_p0003_c0013", "source_registry_id": "src_13b96c46afc6", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8f64d8e1e9f704c0", "text_span_hash": "8f64d8e1e9f704c0", "source_file": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 13, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "lists\nGenzebe Dibaba (WR), Abdi Bile, Taoufik\nOlympic & WC medals since 1987\nMagazine article/interview\nCoach for the US Olympic team in 1998\nDaniel Komen (WR), Noah Ngeny, Laban Rotich Multiple WC medals in the 1990s\nChronicles/training logs\nMaria M", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training and development of world-class middle-distance athletes\nenergy contribution estimates overlap reasonably well with the\nreported type I muscle fiber distribution ranges in middle-dis-\ntance runners [13]. Just as has been well established for long-\ndistance running, maximal oxygen uptake (VO2max), fractional\nutilization of VO2max, running economy (RE), velocity at the\nanaerobic threshold (vAT), and velocity at VO2max (vVO2max)\nare all positively correlated with middle-distance perfor-\nmance [5, 8, 67]. However, to optimize energy mobilization\nand utilization, ­O2 kinetics as well as anaerobic power and\ncapacity play decisive roles in middle distance performance.\nAs Olympic gold medalist 800-m runner Vebjørn Rodal suc-\ncinctly summarized the importance of ­O2 kinetics to one of\nthe authors (ØS): “It does not matter if I can reach a higher\nVO2max in five minutes when I have to cross the finish line\nin 102 s.” In addition, both energy expenditure capacity and\neconomy/efficiency likely deteriorate during middle-distance\nTable 1   Sources of best practice training information\nIn addition, we have had personal communications with Vebjørn Rodal (Olympic 800-m champion in 1996) and Arturo Casado (European\n1500-m champion in 2010). Novel training data from these athletes are presented in Table 6\nWC world championships, EC European championships, WR former or current world-record holder\na Honore Hoedt coached Sifan Hassan during ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "lists\nGenzebe Dibaba (WR), Abdi Bile, Taoufik\nOlympic & WC medals since 1987\nMagazine article/interview\nCoach for the US Olympic team in 1998\nDaniel Komen (WR), Noah Ngeny, Laban Rotich Multiple WC medals in the 1990s\nChronicles/training logs\nMaria M"}
{"chunk_id": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021_p0003_c0014", "source_registry_id": "src_13b96c46afc6", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "54f73a4a4b0628bf", "text_span_hash": "54f73a4a4b0628bf", "source_file": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "page": 3, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 14, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ronicles/training logs\nMaria Mutola, Kelly Holmes\nOlympic & WC medals 1993–2004\nOlympic gold 1980 and 1984\nAssistant coach and scientific advisor for elite\nOlympic & WC medals 2011–2012\nE-book and presentation\nTomasz Lewandowski [25]\nEC gold 2010, WC", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training and development of world-class middle-distance athletes\nenergy contribution estimates overlap reasonably well with the\nreported type I muscle fiber distribution ranges in middle-dis-\ntance runners [13]. Just as has been well established for long-\ndistance running, maximal oxygen uptake (VO2max), fractional\nutilization of VO2max, running economy (RE), velocity at the\nanaerobic threshold (vAT), and velocity at VO2max (vVO2max)\nare all positively correlated with middle-distance perfor-\nmance [5, 8, 67]. However, to optimize energy mobilization\nand utilization, ­O2 kinetics as well as anaerobic power and\ncapacity play decisive roles in middle distance performance.\nAs Olympic gold medalist 800-m runner Vebjørn Rodal suc-\ncinctly summarized the importance of ­O2 kinetics to one of\nthe authors (ØS): “It does not matter if I can reach a higher\nVO2max in five minutes when I have to cross the finish line\nin 102 s.” In addition, both energy expenditure capacity and\neconomy/efficiency likely deteriorate during middle-distance\nTable 1   Sources of best practice training information\nIn addition, we have had personal communications with Vebjørn Rodal (Olympic 800-m champion in 1996) and Arturo Casado (European\n1500-m champion in 2010). Novel training data from these athletes are presented in Table 6\nWC world championships, EC European championships, WR former or current world-record holder\na Honore Hoedt coached Sifan Hassan during ", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ronicles/training logs\nMaria Mutola, Kelly Holmes\nOlympic & WC medals 1993–2004\nOlympic gold 1980 and 1984\nAssistant coach and scientific advisor for elite\nOlympic & WC medals 2011–2012\nE-book and presentation\nTomasz Lewandowski [25]\nEC gold 2010, WC"}
{"chunk_id": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021_p0004_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_13b96c46afc6", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8580f451dad961bd", "text_span_hash": "8580f451dad961bd", "source_file": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "page": 4, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "–240 s duration cycling time tri-\nals to result in a ~ 30% underestimation of the anaerobic energy\ncontribution to total energy expenditure. Unfortunately, com-\nprehensive quantification of running economy (total external\nwork performed/total energy", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "events, indicating that fatigue-resistance/resilience might\nhave a decisive performance-impact. To this point, de Koning\nand colleagues have directly challenged the assumption of a\nstable gross efficiency during short maximal cycling efforts\nwithin the middle-distance time window [68, 69]. Using a\nsequence of sub-maximal-maximal-sub-maximal trials and\nback-extrapolation, they estimate that metabolic efficiency\ndeclines enough during 100–240 s duration cycling time tri-\nals to result in a ~ 30% underestimation of the anaerobic energy\ncontribution to total energy expenditure. Unfortunately, com-\nprehensive quantification of running economy (total external\nwork performed/total energy expenditure) at speeds above the\nlactate threshold remains elusive [12].\nWhile traditional endurance disciplines can be described\nas maximization challenges (i.e., training that enhances\nVO2max or fractional utilization is “always positive” for per-\nformance), we propose that the 800-m event in particular\nrequires an energy release optimization strategy that respects\nthe interactions and trade-offs between anaerobic and aero-\nbic metabolism emerging in both training and performance.\nThis complexity allows internationally successful middle-\ndistance runners to present a variety of physiological pro-\nfiles [12–15]. For example, VO2max ranges from ~ 65 to 85\nml·kg·min−1 in elite men [16, 29, 70, 71]. Similar variation\nis seen among elite women, albeit a", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "–240 s duration cycling time tri-\nals to result in a ~ 30% underestimation of the anaerobic energy\ncontribution to total energy expenditure. Unfortunately, com-\nprehensive quantification of running economy (total external\nwork performed/total energy"}
