urther developed\nby Sandford and associates [12–15] to provide a “first layer\ninsight” of athlete profiling. ASR is defined as the speed\nzone ranging from vVO2max to MSS. MSS can be accurately\nmeasured using radar technology or timing gates [82, 83],\nwhile vVO2max (also known as maximal aerobic speed;\nMAS) tradit", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "tative and reflects actual\nperformance [13]. A brief review of the World Athletics\nall-time top lists (https://​www.​world​athle​tics.​org/​recor​ds/​\nall-​time-​topli​sts) clearly shows that 1500-m runners pos-\nsess a broader distance performance ra"}
{"chunk_id": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021_p0005_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_13b96c46afc6", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "be7a8e3e1352465e", "text_span_hash": "be7a8e3e1352465e", "source_file": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "roader distance performance range, while a larger\nproportion of world-class 800-m runners appears to be “spe-\ncialists”. These observations are in accordance with Dan-\niels [58], who argued that a strong performance relationship\nexists among distance", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training and development of world-class middle-distance athletes\nindividual strengths and weaknesses can be based on per-\nformance across a spread of distances below and above the\nmain discipline (e.g., using IAAF points or percent time\nbehind current world record). For example, 400, 800 and\n1500-m performance can form the basis for analyzing an\n800-m runner, presupposing that the performance level\nacross all these distances is representative and reflects actual\nperformance [13]. A brief review of the World Athletics\nall-time top lists (https://​www.​world​athle​tics.​org/​recor​ds/​\nall-​time-​topli​sts) clearly shows that 1500-m runners pos-\nsess a broader distance performance range, while a larger\nproportion of world-class 800-m runners appears to be “spe-\ncialists”. These observations are in accordance with Dan-\niels [58], who argued that a strong performance relationship\nexists among distances ranging from 1500 m to marathon in\nheterogeneous subsets, while 800 and 1500 m performances\nare considerably less related.\nThe concept of anaerobic speed reserve (ASR) was origi-\nnally introduced by Blondel et al. [81] and further developed\nby Sandford and associates [12–15] to provide a “first layer\ninsight” of athlete profiling. ASR is defined as the speed\nzone ranging from vVO2max to MSS. MSS can be accurately\nmeasured using radar technology or timing gates [82, 83],\nwhile vVO2max (also known as maximal aerobic speed;\nMAS) tradit", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "roader distance performance range, while a larger\nproportion of world-class 800-m runners appears to be “spe-\ncialists”. These observations are in accordance with Dan-\niels [58], who argued that a strong performance relationship\nexists among distance"}
{"chunk_id": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021_p0005_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_13b96c46afc6", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "56a19654558da4aa", "text_span_hash": "56a19654558da4aa", "source_file": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "tionship\nexists among distances ranging from 1500 m to marathon in\nheterogeneous subsets, while 800 and 1500 m performances\nare considerably less related.\nThe concept of anaerobic speed reserve (ASR) was origi-\nnally introduced by Blondel et al. [81]", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training and development of world-class middle-distance athletes\nindividual strengths and weaknesses can be based on per-\nformance across a spread of distances below and above the\nmain discipline (e.g., using IAAF points or percent time\nbehind current world record). For example, 400, 800 and\n1500-m performance can form the basis for analyzing an\n800-m runner, presupposing that the performance level\nacross all these distances is representative and reflects actual\nperformance [13]. A brief review of the World Athletics\nall-time top lists (https://​www.​world​athle​tics.​org/​recor​ds/​\nall-​time-​topli​sts) clearly shows that 1500-m runners pos-\nsess a broader distance performance range, while a larger\nproportion of world-class 800-m runners appears to be “spe-\ncialists”. These observations are in accordance with Dan-\niels [58], who argued that a strong performance relationship\nexists among distances ranging from 1500 m to marathon in\nheterogeneous subsets, while 800 and 1500 m performances\nare considerably less related.\nThe concept of anaerobic speed reserve (ASR) was origi-\nnally introduced by Blondel et al. [81] and further developed\nby Sandford and associates [12–15] to provide a “first layer\ninsight” of athlete profiling. ASR is defined as the speed\nzone ranging from vVO2max to MSS. MSS can be accurately\nmeasured using radar technology or timing gates [82, 83],\nwhile vVO2max (also known as maximal aerobic speed;\nMAS) tradit", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "tionship\nexists among distances ranging from 1500 m to marathon in\nheterogeneous subsets, while 800 and 1500 m performances\nare considerably less related.\nThe concept of anaerobic speed reserve (ASR) was origi-\nnally introduced by Blondel et al. [81]"}
