{"chunk_id": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021_p0005_c0019", "source_registry_id": "src_13b96c46afc6", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0ff9dc59ef8b9f11", "text_span_hash": "0ff9dc59ef8b9f11", "source_file": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 19, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "formance age [87]. These differences may be\nexplained by differences in training status, responsiveness\nto training, coaching quality, doping, etc. Although there is\nconsiderable variation among athletes and numerous routes\nto expertise under optimal", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training and development of world-class middle-distance athletes\nindividual strengths and weaknesses can be based on per-\nformance across a spread of distances below and above the\nmain discipline (e.g., using IAAF points or percent time\nbehind current world record). For example, 400, 800 and\n1500-m performance can form the basis for analyzing an\n800-m runner, presupposing that the performance level\nacross all these distances is representative and reflects actual\nperformance [13]. A brief review of the World Athletics\nall-time top lists (https://​www.​world​athle​tics.​org/​recor​ds/​\nall-​time-​topli​sts) clearly shows that 1500-m runners pos-\nsess a broader distance performance range, while a larger\nproportion of world-class 800-m runners appears to be “spe-\ncialists”. These observations are in accordance with Dan-\niels [58], who argued that a strong performance relationship\nexists among distances ranging from 1500 m to marathon in\nheterogeneous subsets, while 800 and 1500 m performances\nare considerably less related.\nThe concept of anaerobic speed reserve (ASR) was origi-\nnally introduced by Blondel et al. [81] and further developed\nby Sandford and associates [12–15] to provide a “first layer\ninsight” of athlete profiling. ASR is defined as the speed\nzone ranging from vVO2max to MSS. MSS can be accurately\nmeasured using radar technology or timing gates [82, 83],\nwhile vVO2max (also known as maximal aerobic speed;\nMAS) tradit", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "formance age [87]. These differences may be\nexplained by differences in training status, responsiveness\nto training, coaching quality, doping, etc. Although there is\nconsiderable variation among athletes and numerous routes\nto expertise under optimal"}
{"chunk_id": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021_p0005_c0020", "source_registry_id": "src_13b96c46afc6", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "3e72f9206fbd021a", "text_span_hash": "3e72f9206fbd021a", "source_file": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 20, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "tes\nto expertise under optimal conditions, a review of the best\npractice literature listed in Table 1 indicates that the majority\nof world-class 800- and 1500-m runners have specialized in\nthe middle-distances already as juniors.\n5  \u0007Training Princip", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training and development of world-class middle-distance athletes\nindividual strengths and weaknesses can be based on per-\nformance across a spread of distances below and above the\nmain discipline (e.g., using IAAF points or percent time\nbehind current world record). For example, 400, 800 and\n1500-m performance can form the basis for analyzing an\n800-m runner, presupposing that the performance level\nacross all these distances is representative and reflects actual\nperformance [13]. A brief review of the World Athletics\nall-time top lists (https://​www.​world​athle​tics.​org/​recor​ds/​\nall-​time-​topli​sts) clearly shows that 1500-m runners pos-\nsess a broader distance performance range, while a larger\nproportion of world-class 800-m runners appears to be “spe-\ncialists”. These observations are in accordance with Dan-\niels [58], who argued that a strong performance relationship\nexists among distances ranging from 1500 m to marathon in\nheterogeneous subsets, while 800 and 1500 m performances\nare considerably less related.\nThe concept of anaerobic speed reserve (ASR) was origi-\nnally introduced by Blondel et al. [81] and further developed\nby Sandford and associates [12–15] to provide a “first layer\ninsight” of athlete profiling. ASR is defined as the speed\nzone ranging from vVO2max to MSS. MSS can be accurately\nmeasured using radar technology or timing gates [82, 83],\nwhile vVO2max (also known as maximal aerobic speed;\nMAS) tradit", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "tes\nto expertise under optimal conditions, a review of the best\npractice literature listed in Table 1 indicates that the majority\nof world-class 800- and 1500-m runners have specialized in\nthe middle-distances already as juniors.\n5  \u0007Training Princip"}
{"chunk_id": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021_p0005_c0021", "source_registry_id": "src_13b96c46afc6", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e6c4566edb588d34", "text_span_hash": "e6c4566edb588d34", "source_file": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "page": 5, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 21, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "juniors.\n5  \u0007Training Principles\n5.1  \u0007Progressive Overload\nThe process of training adaptation is an interplay between\nloading and recovery, and the principle of progressive\noverload refers to the gradual increase of stress placed\nupon the body duri", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training and development of world-class middle-distance athletes\nindividual strengths and weaknesses can be based on per-\nformance across a spread of distances below and above the\nmain discipline (e.g., using IAAF points or percent time\nbehind current world record). For example, 400, 800 and\n1500-m performance can form the basis for analyzing an\n800-m runner, presupposing that the performance level\nacross all these distances is representative and reflects actual\nperformance [13]. A brief review of the World Athletics\nall-time top lists (https://​www.​world​athle​tics.​org/​recor​ds/​\nall-​time-​topli​sts) clearly shows that 1500-m runners pos-\nsess a broader distance performance range, while a larger\nproportion of world-class 800-m runners appears to be “spe-\ncialists”. These observations are in accordance with Dan-\niels [58], who argued that a strong performance relationship\nexists among distances ranging from 1500 m to marathon in\nheterogeneous subsets, while 800 and 1500 m performances\nare considerably less related.\nThe concept of anaerobic speed reserve (ASR) was origi-\nnally introduced by Blondel et al. [81] and further developed\nby Sandford and associates [12–15] to provide a “first layer\ninsight” of athlete profiling. ASR is defined as the speed\nzone ranging from vVO2max to MSS. MSS can be accurately\nmeasured using radar technology or timing gates [82, 83],\nwhile vVO2max (also known as maximal aerobic speed;\nMAS) tradit", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "juniors.\n5  \u0007Training Principles\n5.1  \u0007Progressive Overload\nThe process of training adaptation is an interplay between\nloading and recovery, and the principle of progressive\noverload refers to the gradual increase of stress placed\nupon the body duri"}
