y of intensive training. Long-term progression\nrates depend on training experience and individual pre-\ndispositions, but total training volume and peak weekly\nmileage may increase up to ~ 10% per year during the late\nteens in well-trained athletes [17, 42, 55, 56].\nA common “periodization” approach observed within\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "the specific\ntraining methods for middle-distance running are described\nin Table 2. We refer to previously published review papers\nregarding physiological adaptations and responses associ-\nated with such training forms [6, 7, 107–109].\nMany successf"}
{"chunk_id": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021_p0006_c0016", "source_registry_id": "src_13b96c46afc6", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "9b7cbbd2819317bf", "text_span_hash": "9b7cbbd2819317bf", "source_file": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 16, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "[6, 7, 107–109].\nMany successful athletes in typical endurance sports sup-\nplement their sport-specific training with alternative activity\nforms, so called cross-training [110–113]. Arguments sup-\nporting the inclusion of such non-specific training i", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "biomechanical external load metrics such as tibial shock,\nfoot-strike angle, ground contact time and leg stiffness\nto enable a more precise quantification of training stress\nThe principle of progressive overload is envisioned\nto enhance performance over time and reduce the risk\nof injury and overtraining [96–98]. Indeed, a large pro-\nportion of injuries are attributed to rapid and excessive\nincreases in training load [101, 102]. During the initial\n8–12 weeks of the training year, it is therefore widely\naccepted in the middle-distance community that running\nvolume must be increased gradually. In elite athletes, the\ninitial training week is performed with ~ 40–60% of peak\nweekly running volume, increasing by ~ 5–15 km each\nweek until maximal volume is reached [19–26, 28–32,\n34, 36–46, 52, 54–59]. This increase is mainly achieved\nby increasing training frequency in the initial phase, then\nsubsequently extended by lengthening individual training\nsessions. When peak running volume is achieved, the fur-\nther progression in training load among middle-distance\nrunners is normally achieved by increasing the amount\nor intensity of intensive training. Long-term progression\nrates depend on training experience and individual pre-\ndispositions, but total training volume and peak weekly\nmileage may increase up to ~ 10% per year during the late\nteens in well-trained athletes [17, 42, 55, 56].\nA common “periodization” approach observed within\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "[6, 7, 107–109].\nMany successful athletes in typical endurance sports sup-\nplement their sport-specific training with alternative activity\nforms, so called cross-training [110–113]. Arguments sup-\nporting the inclusion of such non-specific training i"}
{"chunk_id": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021_p0006_c0019", "source_registry_id": "src_13b96c46afc6", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c48796777c752fa3", "text_span_hash": "c48796777c752fa3", "source_file": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 19, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "is is a\npart of the regular plan in certain training groups. Other\n“less specific” training forms such as strength, power and\nplyometric training are more commonly performed to tar-\nget the underlying anaerobic performance components (see\nSect. 6.4).", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "biomechanical external load metrics such as tibial shock,\nfoot-strike angle, ground contact time and leg stiffness\nto enable a more precise quantification of training stress\nThe principle of progressive overload is envisioned\nto enhance performance over time and reduce the risk\nof injury and overtraining [96–98]. Indeed, a large pro-\nportion of injuries are attributed to rapid and excessive\nincreases in training load [101, 102]. During the initial\n8–12 weeks of the training year, it is therefore widely\naccepted in the middle-distance community that running\nvolume must be increased gradually. In elite athletes, the\ninitial training week is performed with ~ 40–60% of peak\nweekly running volume, increasing by ~ 5–15 km each\nweek until maximal volume is reached [19–26, 28–32,\n34, 36–46, 52, 54–59]. This increase is mainly achieved\nby increasing training frequency in the initial phase, then\nsubsequently extended by lengthening individual training\nsessions. When peak running volume is achieved, the fur-\nther progression in training load among middle-distance\nrunners is normally achieved by increasing the amount\nor intensity of intensive training. Long-term progression\nrates depend on training experience and individual pre-\ndispositions, but total training volume and peak weekly\nmileage may increase up to ~ 10% per year during the late\nteens in well-trained athletes [17, 42, 55, 56].\nA common “periodization” approach observed within\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "is is a\npart of the regular plan in certain training groups. Other\n“less specific” training forms such as strength, power and\nplyometric training are more commonly performed to tar-\nget the underlying anaerobic performance components (see\nSect. 6.4)."}
{"chunk_id": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021_p0006_c0020", "source_registry_id": "src_13b96c46afc6", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "629ae6696e894c03", "text_span_hash": "629ae6696e894c03", "source_file": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 20, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ce components (see\nSect. 6.4). Although these training forms do not duplicate\nthe holistic running movement, they may target specific\ncomponents that limit performance.\n5.3  \u0007Individualization\nThe majority of training intervention studies demonstrate", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "biomechanical external load metrics such as tibial shock,\nfoot-strike angle, ground contact time and leg stiffness\nto enable a more precise quantification of training stress\nThe principle of progressive overload is envisioned\nto enhance performance over time and reduce the risk\nof injury and overtraining [96–98]. Indeed, a large pro-\nportion of injuries are attributed to rapid and excessive\nincreases in training load [101, 102]. During the initial\n8–12 weeks of the training year, it is therefore widely\naccepted in the middle-distance community that running\nvolume must be increased gradually. In elite athletes, the\ninitial training week is performed with ~ 40–60% of peak\nweekly running volume, increasing by ~ 5–15 km each\nweek until maximal volume is reached [19–26, 28–32,\n34, 36–46, 52, 54–59]. This increase is mainly achieved\nby increasing training frequency in the initial phase, then\nsubsequently extended by lengthening individual training\nsessions. When peak running volume is achieved, the fur-\nther progression in training load among middle-distance\nrunners is normally achieved by increasing the amount\nor intensity of intensive training. Long-term progression\nrates depend on training experience and individual pre-\ndispositions, but total training volume and peak weekly\nmileage may increase up to ~ 10% per year during the late\nteens in well-trained athletes [17, 42, 55, 56].\nA common “periodization” approach observed within\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ce components (see\nSect. 6.4). Although these training forms do not duplicate\nthe holistic running movement, they may target specific\ncomponents that limit performance.\n5.3  \u0007Individualization\nThe majority of training intervention studies demonstrate"}
{"chunk_id": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021_p0006_c0021", "source_registry_id": "src_13b96c46afc6", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "1ac77a6a6fe2a9cb", "text_span_hash": "1ac77a6a6fe2a9cb", "source_file": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "page": 6, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 21, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "tervention studies demonstrate\nthat considerable variability in adaptation to a given exer-\ncise stimulus is the norm [e.g., 115–117]. The principle of\nindividualization refers to the notion that training prescrip-\ntion must be adapted and optimized", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "biomechanical external load metrics such as tibial shock,\nfoot-strike angle, ground contact time and leg stiffness\nto enable a more precise quantification of training stress\nThe principle of progressive overload is envisioned\nto enhance performance over time and reduce the risk\nof injury and overtraining [96–98]. Indeed, a large pro-\nportion of injuries are attributed to rapid and excessive\nincreases in training load [101, 102]. During the initial\n8–12 weeks of the training year, it is therefore widely\naccepted in the middle-distance community that running\nvolume must be increased gradually. In elite athletes, the\ninitial training week is performed with ~ 40–60% of peak\nweekly running volume, increasing by ~ 5–15 km each\nweek until maximal volume is reached [19–26, 28–32,\n34, 36–46, 52, 54–59]. This increase is mainly achieved\nby increasing training frequency in the initial phase, then\nsubsequently extended by lengthening individual training\nsessions. When peak running volume is achieved, the fur-\nther progression in training load among middle-distance\nrunners is normally achieved by increasing the amount\nor intensity of intensive training. Long-term progression\nrates depend on training experience and individual pre-\ndispositions, but total training volume and peak weekly\nmileage may increase up to ~ 10% per year during the late\nteens in well-trained athletes [17, 42, 55, 56].\nA common “periodization” approach observed within\n", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "tervention studies demonstrate\nthat considerable variability in adaptation to a given exer-\ncise stimulus is the norm [e.g., 115–117]. The principle of\nindividualization refers to the notion that training prescrip-\ntion must be adapted and optimized"}
