ixed with periods of slower running. The pacing variations\nare determined by the athlete’s feelings and rhythms and terrain\nProgressive long runs\nA commonly used training form used by African runners. The first part of the session\nis identical to an easy long run. After about half the distance, the pace grad", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "aths, etc.). Typical duration is 10–30 min\nLow-intensive steady-state running (marathon pace or 1–2 km·h−1 slower, i.e., 3:30–\n4:00 and 4:00–4:30 min·km−1 for men and women) performed on forgiving surfaces\nsuch as forest trails where possible. Typica"}
{"chunk_id": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021_p0007_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_13b96c46afc6", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "bd3a1a42d483bf6f", "text_span_hash": "bd3a1a42d483bf6f", "source_file": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": ":10–\n3:30 min·km−1 for world-class male and female middle-distance runners). Typical\nduration 15–40 min. The session should not be extremely fatiguing\nAn unstructured long-distance run in various terrains over 30–60 min. where periods\nof fast running", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training and development of world-class middle-distance athletes\nTable 2   Specific training methods for middle-distance running\nWarm up/recovery run/cool down Low-intensive running (typically 3–5 km·h−1 slower than marathon pace, i.e.,\n4:00–4:45 and 4:30–5:15 min·km−1 for men and women, however, the last part of the\nwarm-up may approach marathon pace or slightly above), predominantly performed\non soft surface (grass, woodland, forest paths, etc.). Typical duration is 10–30 min\nLow-intensive steady-state running (marathon pace or 1–2 km·h−1 slower, i.e., 3:30–\n4:00 and 4:00–4:30 min·km−1 for men and women) performed on forgiving surfaces\nsuch as forest trails where possible. Typical duration is 60–90 min, but 2-h runs are\nalso performed during the preparation period\nAnaerobic threshold run\nA sustained run at moderate intensity/half-marathon pace (i.e., 2:55–3:15 and 3:10–\n3:30 min·km−1 for world-class male and female middle-distance runners). Typical\nduration 15–40 min. The session should not be extremely fatiguing\nAn unstructured long-distance run in various terrains over 30–60 min. where periods\nof fast running are intermixed with periods of slower running. The pacing variations\nare determined by the athlete’s feelings and rhythms and terrain\nProgressive long runs\nA commonly used training form used by African runners. The first part of the session\nis identical to an easy long run. After about half the distance, the pace grad", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": ":10–\n3:30 min·km−1 for world-class male and female middle-distance runners). Typical\nduration 15–40 min. The session should not be extremely fatiguing\nAn unstructured long-distance run in various terrains over 30–60 min. where periods\nof fast running"}
{"chunk_id": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021_p0007_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_13b96c46afc6", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "a0caf2f23f159b8c", "text_span_hash": "a0caf2f23f159b8c", "source_file": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "where periods\nof fast running are intermixed with periods of slower running. The pacing variations\nare determined by the athlete’s feelings and rhythms and terrain\nProgressive long runs\nA commonly used training form used by African runners. The firs", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training and development of world-class middle-distance athletes\nTable 2   Specific training methods for middle-distance running\nWarm up/recovery run/cool down Low-intensive running (typically 3–5 km·h−1 slower than marathon pace, i.e.,\n4:00–4:45 and 4:30–5:15 min·km−1 for men and women, however, the last part of the\nwarm-up may approach marathon pace or slightly above), predominantly performed\non soft surface (grass, woodland, forest paths, etc.). Typical duration is 10–30 min\nLow-intensive steady-state running (marathon pace or 1–2 km·h−1 slower, i.e., 3:30–\n4:00 and 4:00–4:30 min·km−1 for men and women) performed on forgiving surfaces\nsuch as forest trails where possible. Typical duration is 60–90 min, but 2-h runs are\nalso performed during the preparation period\nAnaerobic threshold run\nA sustained run at moderate intensity/half-marathon pace (i.e., 2:55–3:15 and 3:10–\n3:30 min·km−1 for world-class male and female middle-distance runners). Typical\nduration 15–40 min. The session should not be extremely fatiguing\nAn unstructured long-distance run in various terrains over 30–60 min. where periods\nof fast running are intermixed with periods of slower running. The pacing variations\nare determined by the athlete’s feelings and rhythms and terrain\nProgressive long runs\nA commonly used training form used by African runners. The first part of the session\nis identical to an easy long run. After about half the distance, the pace grad", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "where periods\nof fast running are intermixed with periods of slower running. The pacing variations\nare determined by the athlete’s feelings and rhythms and terrain\nProgressive long runs\nA commonly used training form used by African runners. The firs"}
{"chunk_id": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021_p0007_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_13b96c46afc6", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "631000bd072c9bc0", "text_span_hash": "631000bd072c9bc0", "source_file": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "d by African runners. The first part of the session\nis identical to an easy long run. After about half the distance, the pace gradually\nquickens. In the final portion, the pace increases to the anaerobic threshold (half-\nmarathon pace) or slightly pa", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training and development of world-class middle-distance athletes\nTable 2   Specific training methods for middle-distance running\nWarm up/recovery run/cool down Low-intensive running (typically 3–5 km·h−1 slower than marathon pace, i.e.,\n4:00–4:45 and 4:30–5:15 min·km−1 for men and women, however, the last part of the\nwarm-up may approach marathon pace or slightly above), predominantly performed\non soft surface (grass, woodland, forest paths, etc.). Typical duration is 10–30 min\nLow-intensive steady-state running (marathon pace or 1–2 km·h−1 slower, i.e., 3:30–\n4:00 and 4:00–4:30 min·km−1 for men and women) performed on forgiving surfaces\nsuch as forest trails where possible. Typical duration is 60–90 min, but 2-h runs are\nalso performed during the preparation period\nAnaerobic threshold run\nA sustained run at moderate intensity/half-marathon pace (i.e., 2:55–3:15 and 3:10–\n3:30 min·km−1 for world-class male and female middle-distance runners). Typical\nduration 15–40 min. The session should not be extremely fatiguing\nAn unstructured long-distance run in various terrains over 30–60 min. where periods\nof fast running are intermixed with periods of slower running. The pacing variations\nare determined by the athlete’s feelings and rhythms and terrain\nProgressive long runs\nA commonly used training form used by African runners. The first part of the session\nis identical to an easy long run. After about half the distance, the pace grad", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "d by African runners. The first part of the session\nis identical to an easy long run. After about half the distance, the pace gradually\nquickens. In the final portion, the pace increases to the anaerobic threshold (half-\nmarathon pace) or slightly pa"}
{"chunk_id": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021_p0007_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_13b96c46afc6", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "de6db41bd17925bb", "text_span_hash": "de6db41bd17925bb", "source_file": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "marathon pace) or slightly past it. Athletes are advised to slow down when the pace\nbecomes too strenuous\nAnaerobic threshold intervals\nIntervals of 3–10 min. duration at an intensity around anaerobic threshold (half-\nmarathon pace) or slightly fast", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training and development of world-class middle-distance athletes\nTable 2   Specific training methods for middle-distance running\nWarm up/recovery run/cool down Low-intensive running (typically 3–5 km·h−1 slower than marathon pace, i.e.,\n4:00–4:45 and 4:30–5:15 min·km−1 for men and women, however, the last part of the\nwarm-up may approach marathon pace or slightly above), predominantly performed\non soft surface (grass, woodland, forest paths, etc.). Typical duration is 10–30 min\nLow-intensive steady-state running (marathon pace or 1–2 km·h−1 slower, i.e., 3:30–\n4:00 and 4:00–4:30 min·km−1 for men and women) performed on forgiving surfaces\nsuch as forest trails where possible. Typical duration is 60–90 min, but 2-h runs are\nalso performed during the preparation period\nAnaerobic threshold run\nA sustained run at moderate intensity/half-marathon pace (i.e., 2:55–3:15 and 3:10–\n3:30 min·km−1 for world-class male and female middle-distance runners). Typical\nduration 15–40 min. The session should not be extremely fatiguing\nAn unstructured long-distance run in various terrains over 30–60 min. where periods\nof fast running are intermixed with periods of slower running. The pacing variations\nare determined by the athlete’s feelings and rhythms and terrain\nProgressive long runs\nA commonly used training form used by African runners. The first part of the session\nis identical to an easy long run. After about half the distance, the pace grad", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "marathon pace) or slightly past it. Athletes are advised to slow down when the pace\nbecomes too strenuous\nAnaerobic threshold intervals\nIntervals of 3–10 min. duration at an intensity around anaerobic threshold (half-\nmarathon pace) or slightly fast"}
{"chunk_id": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021_p0007_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_13b96c46afc6", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "12693771b62cbb2d", "text_span_hash": "12693771b62cbb2d", "source_file": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "arathon pace) or slightly faster. Typical sessions: 8–12 × 800–1000-m with 1 min.\nrecovery between intervals, 4–8 × 1500–2000 m with 1–2 min. recovery between\nintervals, or 2–4 × 10-min. with 2–3 min. recovery between intervals. As a rule of\nthumb, t", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training and development of world-class middle-distance athletes\nTable 2   Specific training methods for middle-distance running\nWarm up/recovery run/cool down Low-intensive running (typically 3–5 km·h−1 slower than marathon pace, i.e.,\n4:00–4:45 and 4:30–5:15 min·km−1 for men and women, however, the last part of the\nwarm-up may approach marathon pace or slightly above), predominantly performed\non soft surface (grass, woodland, forest paths, etc.). Typical duration is 10–30 min\nLow-intensive steady-state running (marathon pace or 1–2 km·h−1 slower, i.e., 3:30–\n4:00 and 4:00–4:30 min·km−1 for men and women) performed on forgiving surfaces\nsuch as forest trails where possible. Typical duration is 60–90 min, but 2-h runs are\nalso performed during the preparation period\nAnaerobic threshold run\nA sustained run at moderate intensity/half-marathon pace (i.e., 2:55–3:15 and 3:10–\n3:30 min·km−1 for world-class male and female middle-distance runners). Typical\nduration 15–40 min. The session should not be extremely fatiguing\nAn unstructured long-distance run in various terrains over 30–60 min. where periods\nof fast running are intermixed with periods of slower running. The pacing variations\nare determined by the athlete’s feelings and rhythms and terrain\nProgressive long runs\nA commonly used training form used by African runners. The first part of the session\nis identical to an easy long run. After about half the distance, the pace grad", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "arathon pace) or slightly faster. Typical sessions: 8–12 × 800–1000-m with 1 min.\nrecovery between intervals, 4–8 × 1500–2000 m with 1–2 min. recovery between\nintervals, or 2–4 × 10-min. with 2–3 min. recovery between intervals. As a rule of\nthumb, t"}
{"chunk_id": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021_p0007_c0010", "source_registry_id": "src_13b96c46afc6", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "809f2a1247d5b1a2", "text_span_hash": "809f2a1247d5b1a2", "source_file": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 10, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "tervals. As a rule of\nthumb, the recovery periods are ~ 1 min. of easy jogging per 5 min of running. Rec-\nommended total time for elite runners is 25–40 min. Such intervals are advantageous\nbecause they allow the athlete to accumulate more total time", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training and development of world-class middle-distance athletes\nTable 2   Specific training methods for middle-distance running\nWarm up/recovery run/cool down Low-intensive running (typically 3–5 km·h−1 slower than marathon pace, i.e.,\n4:00–4:45 and 4:30–5:15 min·km−1 for men and women, however, the last part of the\nwarm-up may approach marathon pace or slightly above), predominantly performed\non soft surface (grass, woodland, forest paths, etc.). Typical duration is 10–30 min\nLow-intensive steady-state running (marathon pace or 1–2 km·h−1 slower, i.e., 3:30–\n4:00 and 4:00–4:30 min·km−1 for men and women) performed on forgiving surfaces\nsuch as forest trails where possible. Typical duration is 60–90 min, but 2-h runs are\nalso performed during the preparation period\nAnaerobic threshold run\nA sustained run at moderate intensity/half-marathon pace (i.e., 2:55–3:15 and 3:10–\n3:30 min·km−1 for world-class male and female middle-distance runners). Typical\nduration 15–40 min. The session should not be extremely fatiguing\nAn unstructured long-distance run in various terrains over 30–60 min. where periods\nof fast running are intermixed with periods of slower running. The pacing variations\nare determined by the athlete’s feelings and rhythms and terrain\nProgressive long runs\nA commonly used training form used by African runners. The first part of the session\nis identical to an easy long run. After about half the distance, the pace grad", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "tervals. As a rule of\nthumb, the recovery periods are ~ 1 min. of easy jogging per 5 min of running. Rec-\nommended total time for elite runners is 25–40 min. Such intervals are advantageous\nbecause they allow the athlete to accumulate more total time"}
{"chunk_id": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021_p0007_c0011", "source_registry_id": "src_13b96c46afc6", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "c25e9be517f604c1", "text_span_hash": "c25e9be517f604c1", "source_file": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "page": 7, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 11, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "to accumulate more total time than during a continu-\nous anaerobic threshold run\nIntervals of 2–4 min. duration at 3–10 K pace, with 2–3 min. recovery periods between\nintervals. Typical sessions: 4–7 × 800–1000 m or 2 × (6 × 400 m) with 30–60 s and", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training and development of world-class middle-distance athletes\nTable 2   Specific training methods for middle-distance running\nWarm up/recovery run/cool down Low-intensive running (typically 3–5 km·h−1 slower than marathon pace, i.e.,\n4:00–4:45 and 4:30–5:15 min·km−1 for men and women, however, the last part of the\nwarm-up may approach marathon pace or slightly above), predominantly performed\non soft surface (grass, woodland, forest paths, etc.). Typical duration is 10–30 min\nLow-intensive steady-state running (marathon pace or 1–2 km·h−1 slower, i.e., 3:30–\n4:00 and 4:00–4:30 min·km−1 for men and women) performed on forgiving surfaces\nsuch as forest trails where possible. Typical duration is 60–90 min, but 2-h runs are\nalso performed during the preparation period\nAnaerobic threshold run\nA sustained run at moderate intensity/half-marathon pace (i.e., 2:55–3:15 and 3:10–\n3:30 min·km−1 for world-class male and female middle-distance runners). Typical\nduration 15–40 min. The session should not be extremely fatiguing\nAn unstructured long-distance run in various terrains over 30–60 min. where periods\nof fast running are intermixed with periods of slower running. The pacing variations\nare determined by the athlete’s feelings and rhythms and terrain\nProgressive long runs\nA commonly used training form used by African runners. The first part of the session\nis identical to an easy long run. After about half the distance, the pace grad", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "to accumulate more total time than during a continu-\nous anaerobic threshold run\nIntervals of 2–4 min. duration at 3–10 K pace, with 2–3 min. recovery periods between\nintervals. Typical sessions: 4–7 × 800–1000 m or 2 × (6 × 400 m) with 30–60 s and"}
