eparation. Some athletes apply dou-\nble periodization (i.e., two peaking phases), consisting of\na preparation phase, an indoor season, a new preparation\nphase and finally an outdoor competition season [24, 32,\n43]. However, most world-class middle-distance runners\napply single periodization. Although they may participa", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ltivate an ability\nto “feel” the proper intensity, as intensity integrates three\nforms of feedback: running pace, physiological responses\nand perception of effort [55]. Intensity scales are imperfect\ntools, but the above-mentioned potential sources o"}
{"chunk_id": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021_p0009_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_13b96c46afc6", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "254749c26685260d", "text_span_hash": "254749c26685260d", "source_file": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "Training and development of world-class middle-distance athletes\nTable 3   An historical overview of middle-distance training organization\nKey coaches and athletes driving the development\nUse of systematic methodologies targeting middle-distance runn", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training and development of world-class middle-distance athletes\nTable 3   An historical overview of middle-distance training organization\nKey coaches and athletes driving the development\nUse of systematic methodologies targeting middle-distance running\nPaavo Nurmi was the pioneer of interval training and introduced the\n“even pace” strategy to running, using a stopwatch to control his speed\n[120]. He also developed systematic all-year-round training programs\nthat included both long-distance work and high-intensive running [1],\nbringing middle- and long-distance training to a new and modern level\nwith intelligent application of effort\nIntroduction of interval concepts and use of heart rate for intensity\nGerman Waldemar Gerschler (coach of e.g. Harbig and Moens) together\nwith the physiologist Herbert Reindell refined the interval training\nconcept [1]. The intensity in each interval was carefully controlled by\nheart rate and typically higher than competition pace interspersed by\nIntroduction of “fartlek” as a training method\nSwedish Gösta Holmer (coach of e.g. Hägg and Anderson) developed\n“fartlek” as a training method [1], an unstructured long-distance run\nin various terrains where periods of fast running are intermixed with\nperiods of slower running\nUse of high-volume low intensity running as a basis of middle-dis-\nGradually reduced volume and more competition-specific speed/inten-\nsity towards the competition period\nNew Zealan", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "Training and development of world-class middle-distance athletes\nTable 3   An historical overview of middle-distance training organization\nKey coaches and athletes driving the development\nUse of systematic methodologies targeting middle-distance runn"}
{"chunk_id": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021_p0009_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_13b96c46afc6", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "be68cc4eb5742ccf", "text_span_hash": "be68cc4eb5742ccf", "source_file": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "targeting middle-distance running\nPaavo Nurmi was the pioneer of interval training and introduced the\n“even pace” strategy to running, using a stopwatch to control his speed\n[120]. He also developed systematic all-year-round training programs\nthat in", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training and development of world-class middle-distance athletes\nTable 3   An historical overview of middle-distance training organization\nKey coaches and athletes driving the development\nUse of systematic methodologies targeting middle-distance running\nPaavo Nurmi was the pioneer of interval training and introduced the\n“even pace” strategy to running, using a stopwatch to control his speed\n[120]. He also developed systematic all-year-round training programs\nthat included both long-distance work and high-intensive running [1],\nbringing middle- and long-distance training to a new and modern level\nwith intelligent application of effort\nIntroduction of interval concepts and use of heart rate for intensity\nGerman Waldemar Gerschler (coach of e.g. Harbig and Moens) together\nwith the physiologist Herbert Reindell refined the interval training\nconcept [1]. The intensity in each interval was carefully controlled by\nheart rate and typically higher than competition pace interspersed by\nIntroduction of “fartlek” as a training method\nSwedish Gösta Holmer (coach of e.g. Hägg and Anderson) developed\n“fartlek” as a training method [1], an unstructured long-distance run\nin various terrains where periods of fast running are intermixed with\nperiods of slower running\nUse of high-volume low intensity running as a basis of middle-dis-\nGradually reduced volume and more competition-specific speed/inten-\nsity towards the competition period\nNew Zealan", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "targeting middle-distance running\nPaavo Nurmi was the pioneer of interval training and introduced the\n“even pace” strategy to running, using a stopwatch to control his speed\n[120]. He also developed systematic all-year-round training programs\nthat in"}
