{"chunk_id": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021_p0009_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_13b96c46afc6", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f2ba4fa0bb22a044", "text_span_hash": "f2ba4fa0bb22a044", "source_file": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "of middle-dis-\nGradually reduced volume and more competition-specific speed/inten-\nsity towards the competition period\nNew Zealander Arthur Lydiard (coach of e.g. Snell and Halberg)\nbroke with contemporary practice by prescribing a large volume of\nl", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training and development of world-class middle-distance athletes\nTable 3   An historical overview of middle-distance training organization\nKey coaches and athletes driving the development\nUse of systematic methodologies targeting middle-distance running\nPaavo Nurmi was the pioneer of interval training and introduced the\n“even pace” strategy to running, using a stopwatch to control his speed\n[120]. He also developed systematic all-year-round training programs\nthat included both long-distance work and high-intensive running [1],\nbringing middle- and long-distance training to a new and modern level\nwith intelligent application of effort\nIntroduction of interval concepts and use of heart rate for intensity\nGerman Waldemar Gerschler (coach of e.g. Harbig and Moens) together\nwith the physiologist Herbert Reindell refined the interval training\nconcept [1]. The intensity in each interval was carefully controlled by\nheart rate and typically higher than competition pace interspersed by\nIntroduction of “fartlek” as a training method\nSwedish Gösta Holmer (coach of e.g. Hägg and Anderson) developed\n“fartlek” as a training method [1], an unstructured long-distance run\nin various terrains where periods of fast running are intermixed with\nperiods of slower running\nUse of high-volume low intensity running as a basis of middle-dis-\nGradually reduced volume and more competition-specific speed/inten-\nsity towards the competition period\nNew Zealan", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "of middle-dis-\nGradually reduced volume and more competition-specific speed/inten-\nsity towards the competition period\nNew Zealander Arthur Lydiard (coach of e.g. Snell and Halberg)\nbroke with contemporary practice by prescribing a large volume of\nl"}
{"chunk_id": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021_p0009_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_13b96c46afc6", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "5722b8ebb994abcf", "text_span_hash": "5722b8ebb994abcf", "source_file": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "rescribing a large volume of\nlow intensity running to his middle-distance athletes, peppered with\nspecific high-intensity training, hill bounding and plyometric training\nThe emphasis on high-volume aerobic training shifted towards less vol-\nume and m", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training and development of world-class middle-distance athletes\nTable 3   An historical overview of middle-distance training organization\nKey coaches and athletes driving the development\nUse of systematic methodologies targeting middle-distance running\nPaavo Nurmi was the pioneer of interval training and introduced the\n“even pace” strategy to running, using a stopwatch to control his speed\n[120]. He also developed systematic all-year-round training programs\nthat included both long-distance work and high-intensive running [1],\nbringing middle- and long-distance training to a new and modern level\nwith intelligent application of effort\nIntroduction of interval concepts and use of heart rate for intensity\nGerman Waldemar Gerschler (coach of e.g. Harbig and Moens) together\nwith the physiologist Herbert Reindell refined the interval training\nconcept [1]. The intensity in each interval was carefully controlled by\nheart rate and typically higher than competition pace interspersed by\nIntroduction of “fartlek” as a training method\nSwedish Gösta Holmer (coach of e.g. Hägg and Anderson) developed\n“fartlek” as a training method [1], an unstructured long-distance run\nin various terrains where periods of fast running are intermixed with\nperiods of slower running\nUse of high-volume low intensity running as a basis of middle-dis-\nGradually reduced volume and more competition-specific speed/inten-\nsity towards the competition period\nNew Zealan", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "rescribing a large volume of\nlow intensity running to his middle-distance athletes, peppered with\nspecific high-intensity training, hill bounding and plyometric training\nThe emphasis on high-volume aerobic training shifted towards less vol-\nume and m"}
{"chunk_id": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021_p0009_c0009", "source_registry_id": "src_13b96c46afc6", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "0372561d1a04b098", "text_span_hash": "0372561d1a04b098", "source_file": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "page": 9, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 9, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ed towards less vol-\nume and more specific anaerobic and race-specific workouts towards\nthe competitive season, which remains the foundation for most mod-\nern training programs. This training model bears great resemblance to\nMatveyev’s traditional tr", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training and development of world-class middle-distance athletes\nTable 3   An historical overview of middle-distance training organization\nKey coaches and athletes driving the development\nUse of systematic methodologies targeting middle-distance running\nPaavo Nurmi was the pioneer of interval training and introduced the\n“even pace” strategy to running, using a stopwatch to control his speed\n[120]. He also developed systematic all-year-round training programs\nthat included both long-distance work and high-intensive running [1],\nbringing middle- and long-distance training to a new and modern level\nwith intelligent application of effort\nIntroduction of interval concepts and use of heart rate for intensity\nGerman Waldemar Gerschler (coach of e.g. Harbig and Moens) together\nwith the physiologist Herbert Reindell refined the interval training\nconcept [1]. The intensity in each interval was carefully controlled by\nheart rate and typically higher than competition pace interspersed by\nIntroduction of “fartlek” as a training method\nSwedish Gösta Holmer (coach of e.g. Hägg and Anderson) developed\n“fartlek” as a training method [1], an unstructured long-distance run\nin various terrains where periods of fast running are intermixed with\nperiods of slower running\nUse of high-volume low intensity running as a basis of middle-dis-\nGradually reduced volume and more competition-specific speed/inten-\nsity towards the competition period\nNew Zealan", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ed towards less vol-\nume and more specific anaerobic and race-specific workouts towards\nthe competitive season, which remains the foundation for most mod-\nern training programs. This training model bears great resemblance to\nMatveyev’s traditional tr"}
