c features for middle-distance athletes (800-m runners\nin particular), as such training rarely occurs among world-\nleading sprinters [60] or long-distance runners [61–65]. In\ncontrast, 1500-m runners maintain their zone", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "running economy, time trial performance and\nMSS in middle- and long-distance runners across a broad\nperformance range [4, 147–149]. In contrast, a causal"}
{"chunk_id": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021_p0013_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_13b96c46afc6", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "00453170ebe24036", "text_span_hash": "00453170ebe24036", "source_file": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "ycle of\nworld-class middle-distance runners are described con-\nsiderably more in detail in best practice versus research\nliterature, tapering represents an area where more infor-\nmation can be obtained from scientific studies. Although\npotential diff", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training and development of world-class middle-distance athletes\nrelationship between core stability, athletic performance\nand injury risk has not been established [150].\nWhile the training components across the annual cycle of\nworld-class middle-distance runners are described con-\nsiderably more in detail in best practice versus research\nliterature, tapering represents an area where more infor-\nmation can be obtained from scientific studies. Although\npotential differences in tapering strategies between 800-\nand 1500-m runners cannot be identified based on current\navailable information, it is reasonable to assume that the\ntraining volume is lower and the key workouts are shorter\nand more intensive for 800-m runners during this period.\nThe general scientific guidelines for a likely effective\ntaper in endurance-related sports are a 2- to 3-week period\nincorporating 40–60% reduction in training volume fol-\nlowing a progressive non-linear format, while training\nintensity and frequency are maintained or only slightly\nreduced [151–155]. However, although individual differ-\nences are clearly present, tapering length increases with\ncompetition distance, and approximately 1 week seems\nsufficient for middle-distance athletes [33, 56, 156, 157].\nSpilsbury and associates reported that elite middle-dis-\ntance runners perform three interval training sessions\non average during the last tapering week [157]. Each of\nthese interval sessions are", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "ycle of\nworld-class middle-distance runners are described con-\nsiderably more in detail in best practice versus research\nliterature, tapering represents an area where more infor-\nmation can be obtained from scientific studies. Although\npotential diff"}
{"chunk_id": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021_p0013_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_13b96c46afc6", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "8e4c540c0177b387", "text_span_hash": "8e4c540c0177b387", "source_file": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "udies. Although\npotential differences in tapering strategies between 800-\nand 1500-m runners cannot be identified based on current\navailable information, it is reasonable to assume that the\ntraining volume is lower and the key workouts are shorter\nan", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training and development of world-class middle-distance athletes\nrelationship between core stability, athletic performance\nand injury risk has not been established [150].\nWhile the training components across the annual cycle of\nworld-class middle-distance runners are described con-\nsiderably more in detail in best practice versus research\nliterature, tapering represents an area where more infor-\nmation can be obtained from scientific studies. Although\npotential differences in tapering strategies between 800-\nand 1500-m runners cannot be identified based on current\navailable information, it is reasonable to assume that the\ntraining volume is lower and the key workouts are shorter\nand more intensive for 800-m runners during this period.\nThe general scientific guidelines for a likely effective\ntaper in endurance-related sports are a 2- to 3-week period\nincorporating 40–60% reduction in training volume fol-\nlowing a progressive non-linear format, while training\nintensity and frequency are maintained or only slightly\nreduced [151–155]. However, although individual differ-\nences are clearly present, tapering length increases with\ncompetition distance, and approximately 1 week seems\nsufficient for middle-distance athletes [33, 56, 156, 157].\nSpilsbury and associates reported that elite middle-dis-\ntance runners perform three interval training sessions\non average during the last tapering week [157]. Each of\nthese interval sessions are", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "udies. Although\npotential differences in tapering strategies between 800-\nand 1500-m runners cannot be identified based on current\navailable information, it is reasonable to assume that the\ntraining volume is lower and the key workouts are shorter\nan"}
{"chunk_id": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021_p0013_c0004", "source_registry_id": "src_13b96c46afc6", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e08f2964d85729a5", "text_span_hash": "e08f2964d85729a5", "source_file": "train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\train_world_class_middle_distance_2021.pdf", "page": 13, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 4, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "he key workouts are shorter\nand more intensive for 800-m runners during this period.\nThe general scientific guidelines for a likely effective\ntaper in endurance-related sports are a 2- to 3-week period\nincorporating 40–60% reduction in training volum", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "Training and development of world-class middle-distance athletes\nrelationship between core stability, athletic performance\nand injury risk has not been established [150].\nWhile the training components across the annual cycle of\nworld-class middle-distance runners are described con-\nsiderably more in detail in best practice versus research\nliterature, tapering represents an area where more infor-\nmation can be obtained from scientific studies. Although\npotential differences in tapering strategies between 800-\nand 1500-m runners cannot be identified based on current\navailable information, it is reasonable to assume that the\ntraining volume is lower and the key workouts are shorter\nand more intensive for 800-m runners during this period.\nThe general scientific guidelines for a likely effective\ntaper in endurance-related sports are a 2- to 3-week period\nincorporating 40–60% reduction in training volume fol-\nlowing a progressive non-linear format, while training\nintensity and frequency are maintained or only slightly\nreduced [151–155]. However, although individual differ-\nences are clearly present, tapering length increases with\ncompetition distance, and approximately 1 week seems\nsufficient for middle-distance athletes [33, 56, 156, 157].\nSpilsbury and associates reported that elite middle-dis-\ntance runners perform three interval training sessions\non average during the last tapering week [157]. Each of\nthese interval sessions are", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "he key workouts are shorter\nand more intensive for 800-m runners during this period.\nThe general scientific guidelines for a likely effective\ntaper in endurance-related sports are a 2- to 3-week period\nincorporating 40–60% reduction in training volum"}
