y\ncompared to one another in the short, medium, and long\nterm,132,164,250,289 it is not yet known whether either is superior\nto control in the medium and long term.\nHigh Versus Low Volume of Knee-Targeted\nExercise Therapy While Avoiding Pain\nA single moderate-q", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "nutes of aerobic cycling.217,218,289\nThe cited evidence to support high-volume exercise is from\na single cohort and lacked a control group of wait and see.\nThus, additional research is needed to make a definitive\nrecommendation regarding high- versus"}
{"chunk_id": "Willy_2019_PFPS_CPG_p0039_c0001", "source_registry_id": "src_c91b8bca258c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "6787ac6f368d1737", "text_span_hash": "6787ac6f368d1737", "source_file": "Willy_2019_PFPS_CPG.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Willy_2019_PFPS_CPG.pdf", "page": 39, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 1, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "journal of orthopaedic & sports physical therapy | volume 49 | number 9 | september 2019 | cpg39\nPatellofemoral Pain: Clinical Practice Guidelines\nRunning Gait Retraining\nOne high-quality RCT studied patient education\nfocused on load management (avoi", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "journal of orthopaedic & sports physical therapy | volume 49 | number 9 | september 2019 | cpg39\nPatellofemoral Pain: Clinical Practice Guidelines\nRunning Gait Retraining\nOne high-quality RCT studied patient education\nfocused on load management (avoid running hills\nand reduce run-session volume while increasing\nsession frequency) combined with five 10-minute sessions of\ninstruction on running modification. The running modifica­\ntion education consisted of instruction to increase running\ncadence, reduce audible sound of foot strike, and/or alter foot\nstrike from rearfoot to forefoot. However, combining patient\neducation on load management with running modification\nwas no more effective in the medium term than patient edu­\ncation on load management alone for runners with PFP, and\nwas no more effective than hip- and knee-targeted exercise\ntherapy plus patient education.100 There was no control group\nincluded in this study. Therefore, it is unknown whether the\nimprovements noted with the 3 interventions exceeded “wait\nIn a moderate-quality RCT, 8 sessions of gait re­\ntraining over 2 weeks in rearfoot-strike runners,\nconsisting of cuing to alter foot strike from rearfoot\nto forefoot using a faded-feedback design, were more effec­\ntive at reducing pain immediately following the conclusion of\nthe retraining period and at short-term follow-up (4 weeks)\ncompared with a control group that received a graded in­\ncrease in running volume m", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "journal of orthopaedic & sports physical therapy | volume 49 | number 9 | september 2019 | cpg39\nPatellofemoral Pain: Clinical Practice Guidelines\nRunning Gait Retraining\nOne high-quality RCT studied patient education\nfocused on load management (avoi"}
{"chunk_id": "Willy_2019_PFPS_CPG_p0039_c0002", "source_registry_id": "src_c91b8bca258c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "e7c3c1b85ffe1bf2", "text_span_hash": "e7c3c1b85ffe1bf2", "source_file": "Willy_2019_PFPS_CPG.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Willy_2019_PFPS_CPG.pdf", "page": 39, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 2, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "cused on load management (avoid running hills\nand reduce run-session volume while increasing\nsession frequency) combined with five 10-minute sessions of\ninstruction on running modification. The running modifica­\ntion education consisted of instructio", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "journal of orthopaedic & sports physical therapy | volume 49 | number 9 | september 2019 | cpg39\nPatellofemoral Pain: Clinical Practice Guidelines\nRunning Gait Retraining\nOne high-quality RCT studied patient education\nfocused on load management (avoid running hills\nand reduce run-session volume while increasing\nsession frequency) combined with five 10-minute sessions of\ninstruction on running modification. The running modifica­\ntion education consisted of instruction to increase running\ncadence, reduce audible sound of foot strike, and/or alter foot\nstrike from rearfoot to forefoot. However, combining patient\neducation on load management with running modification\nwas no more effective in the medium term than patient edu­\ncation on load management alone for runners with PFP, and\nwas no more effective than hip- and knee-targeted exercise\ntherapy plus patient education.100 There was no control group\nincluded in this study. Therefore, it is unknown whether the\nimprovements noted with the 3 interventions exceeded “wait\nIn a moderate-quality RCT, 8 sessions of gait re­\ntraining over 2 weeks in rearfoot-strike runners,\nconsisting of cuing to alter foot strike from rearfoot\nto forefoot using a faded-feedback design, were more effec­\ntive at reducing pain immediately following the conclusion of\nthe retraining period and at short-term follow-up (4 weeks)\ncompared with a control group that received a graded in­\ncrease in running volume m", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "cused on load management (avoid running hills\nand reduce run-session volume while increasing\nsession frequency) combined with five 10-minute sessions of\ninstruction on running modification. The running modifica­\ntion education consisted of instructio"}
{"chunk_id": "Willy_2019_PFPS_CPG_p0039_c0003", "source_registry_id": "src_c91b8bca258c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "6c444fe1be2cff21", "text_span_hash": "6c444fe1be2cff21", "source_file": "Willy_2019_PFPS_CPG.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Willy_2019_PFPS_CPG.pdf", "page": 39, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 3, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "cation consisted of instruction to increase running\ncadence, reduce audible sound of foot strike, and/or alter foot\nstrike from rearfoot to forefoot. However, combining patient\neducation on load management with running modification\nwas no more effect", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "journal of orthopaedic & sports physical therapy | volume 49 | number 9 | september 2019 | cpg39\nPatellofemoral Pain: Clinical Practice Guidelines\nRunning Gait Retraining\nOne high-quality RCT studied patient education\nfocused on load management (avoid running hills\nand reduce run-session volume while increasing\nsession frequency) combined with five 10-minute sessions of\ninstruction on running modification. The running modifica­\ntion education consisted of instruction to increase running\ncadence, reduce audible sound of foot strike, and/or alter foot\nstrike from rearfoot to forefoot. However, combining patient\neducation on load management with running modification\nwas no more effective in the medium term than patient edu­\ncation on load management alone for runners with PFP, and\nwas no more effective than hip- and knee-targeted exercise\ntherapy plus patient education.100 There was no control group\nincluded in this study. Therefore, it is unknown whether the\nimprovements noted with the 3 interventions exceeded “wait\nIn a moderate-quality RCT, 8 sessions of gait re­\ntraining over 2 weeks in rearfoot-strike runners,\nconsisting of cuing to alter foot strike from rearfoot\nto forefoot using a faded-feedback design, were more effec­\ntive at reducing pain immediately following the conclusion of\nthe retraining period and at short-term follow-up (4 weeks)\ncompared with a control group that received a graded in­\ncrease in running volume m", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "cation consisted of instruction to increase running\ncadence, reduce audible sound of foot strike, and/or alter foot\nstrike from rearfoot to forefoot. However, combining patient\neducation on load management with running modification\nwas no more effect"}
{"chunk_id": "Willy_2019_PFPS_CPG_p0039_c0005", "source_registry_id": "src_c91b8bca258c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "f5fcad5272655d26", "text_span_hash": "f5fcad5272655d26", "source_file": "Willy_2019_PFPS_CPG.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Willy_2019_PFPS_CPG.pdf", "page": 39, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 5, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "00 There was no control group\nincluded in this study. Therefore, it is unknown whether the\nimprovements noted with the 3 interventions exceeded “wait\nIn a moderate-quality RCT, 8 sessions of gait re­\ntraining over 2 weeks in rearfoot-strike runners,", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "journal of orthopaedic & sports physical therapy | volume 49 | number 9 | september 2019 | cpg39\nPatellofemoral Pain: Clinical Practice Guidelines\nRunning Gait Retraining\nOne high-quality RCT studied patient education\nfocused on load management (avoid running hills\nand reduce run-session volume while increasing\nsession frequency) combined with five 10-minute sessions of\ninstruction on running modification. The running modifica­\ntion education consisted of instruction to increase running\ncadence, reduce audible sound of foot strike, and/or alter foot\nstrike from rearfoot to forefoot. However, combining patient\neducation on load management with running modification\nwas no more effective in the medium term than patient edu­\ncation on load management alone for runners with PFP, and\nwas no more effective than hip- and knee-targeted exercise\ntherapy plus patient education.100 There was no control group\nincluded in this study. Therefore, it is unknown whether the\nimprovements noted with the 3 interventions exceeded “wait\nIn a moderate-quality RCT, 8 sessions of gait re­\ntraining over 2 weeks in rearfoot-strike runners,\nconsisting of cuing to alter foot strike from rearfoot\nto forefoot using a faded-feedback design, were more effec­\ntive at reducing pain immediately following the conclusion of\nthe retraining period and at short-term follow-up (4 weeks)\ncompared with a control group that received a graded in­\ncrease in running volume m", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "00 There was no control group\nincluded in this study. Therefore, it is unknown whether the\nimprovements noted with the 3 interventions exceeded “wait\nIn a moderate-quality RCT, 8 sessions of gait re­\ntraining over 2 weeks in rearfoot-strike runners,"}
{"chunk_id": "Willy_2019_PFPS_CPG_p0039_c0006", "source_registry_id": "src_c91b8bca258c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "cabf6b41492afcac", "text_span_hash": "cabf6b41492afcac", "source_file": "Willy_2019_PFPS_CPG.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Willy_2019_PFPS_CPG.pdf", "page": 39, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 6, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "s in rearfoot-strike runners,\nconsisting of cuing to alter foot strike from rearfoot\nto forefoot using a faded-feedback design, were more effec­\ntive at reducing pain immediately following the conclusion of\nthe retraining period and at short-term fol", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "journal of orthopaedic & sports physical therapy | volume 49 | number 9 | september 2019 | cpg39\nPatellofemoral Pain: Clinical Practice Guidelines\nRunning Gait Retraining\nOne high-quality RCT studied patient education\nfocused on load management (avoid running hills\nand reduce run-session volume while increasing\nsession frequency) combined with five 10-minute sessions of\ninstruction on running modification. The running modifica­\ntion education consisted of instruction to increase running\ncadence, reduce audible sound of foot strike, and/or alter foot\nstrike from rearfoot to forefoot. However, combining patient\neducation on load management with running modification\nwas no more effective in the medium term than patient edu­\ncation on load management alone for runners with PFP, and\nwas no more effective than hip- and knee-targeted exercise\ntherapy plus patient education.100 There was no control group\nincluded in this study. Therefore, it is unknown whether the\nimprovements noted with the 3 interventions exceeded “wait\nIn a moderate-quality RCT, 8 sessions of gait re­\ntraining over 2 weeks in rearfoot-strike runners,\nconsisting of cuing to alter foot strike from rearfoot\nto forefoot using a faded-feedback design, were more effec­\ntive at reducing pain immediately following the conclusion of\nthe retraining period and at short-term follow-up (4 weeks)\ncompared with a control group that received a graded in­\ncrease in running volume m", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "s in rearfoot-strike runners,\nconsisting of cuing to alter foot strike from rearfoot\nto forefoot using a faded-feedback design, were more effec­\ntive at reducing pain immediately following the conclusion of\nthe retraining period and at short-term fol"}
{"chunk_id": "Willy_2019_PFPS_CPG_p0039_c0007", "source_registry_id": "src_c91b8bca258c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "ad8f75656aa68714", "text_span_hash": "ad8f75656aa68714", "source_file": "Willy_2019_PFPS_CPG.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Willy_2019_PFPS_CPG.pdf", "page": 39, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 7, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "g period and at short-term follow-up (4 weeks)\ncompared with a control group that received a graded in­\ncrease in running volume matching the gait retraining\nA moderate-quality RCT reported that runners\ntreated with 10 sessions of gait retraining to", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "journal of orthopaedic & sports physical therapy | volume 49 | number 9 | september 2019 | cpg39\nPatellofemoral Pain: Clinical Practice Guidelines\nRunning Gait Retraining\nOne high-quality RCT studied patient education\nfocused on load management (avoid running hills\nand reduce run-session volume while increasing\nsession frequency) combined with five 10-minute sessions of\ninstruction on running modification. The running modifica­\ntion education consisted of instruction to increase running\ncadence, reduce audible sound of foot strike, and/or alter foot\nstrike from rearfoot to forefoot. However, combining patient\neducation on load management with running modification\nwas no more effective in the medium term than patient edu­\ncation on load management alone for runners with PFP, and\nwas no more effective than hip- and knee-targeted exercise\ntherapy plus patient education.100 There was no control group\nincluded in this study. Therefore, it is unknown whether the\nimprovements noted with the 3 interventions exceeded “wait\nIn a moderate-quality RCT, 8 sessions of gait re­\ntraining over 2 weeks in rearfoot-strike runners,\nconsisting of cuing to alter foot strike from rearfoot\nto forefoot using a faded-feedback design, were more effec­\ntive at reducing pain immediately following the conclusion of\nthe retraining period and at short-term follow-up (4 weeks)\ncompared with a control group that received a graded in­\ncrease in running volume m", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "g period and at short-term follow-up (4 weeks)\ncompared with a control group that received a graded in­\ncrease in running volume matching the gait retraining\nA moderate-quality RCT reported that runners\ntreated with 10 sessions of gait retraining to"}
{"chunk_id": "Willy_2019_PFPS_CPG_p0039_c0008", "source_registry_id": "src_c91b8bca258c", "section_anchor": "d54f4dae1d15", "paragraph_hash": "28aad79c3f03f7e7", "text_span_hash": "28aad79c3f03f7e7", "source_file": "Willy_2019_PFPS_CPG.pdf", "source_path": "C:\\Users\\26318\\Documents\\trae_projects\\ollama_pro\\马拉松助手\\data\\domain_docs\\Willy_2019_PFPS_CPG.pdf", "page": 39, "section": "fixed_window", "paragraph_index": 8, "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "text_span": "essions of gait retraining to in­\ncrease running cadence by 10%, combined with the\nuse of minimalist, barefoot-mimicking footwear for 20% of\nrunning volume, had greater pain reductions compared with\nrunners treated with foot orthoses prescribed to op", "chunking_strategy": "fixed_window", "parent_text": "journal of orthopaedic & sports physical therapy | volume 49 | number 9 | september 2019 | cpg39\nPatellofemoral Pain: Clinical Practice Guidelines\nRunning Gait Retraining\nOne high-quality RCT studied patient education\nfocused on load management (avoid running hills\nand reduce run-session volume while increasing\nsession frequency) combined with five 10-minute sessions of\ninstruction on running modification. The running modifica­\ntion education consisted of instruction to increase running\ncadence, reduce audible sound of foot strike, and/or alter foot\nstrike from rearfoot to forefoot. However, combining patient\neducation on load management with running modification\nwas no more effective in the medium term than patient edu­\ncation on load management alone for runners with PFP, and\nwas no more effective than hip- and knee-targeted exercise\ntherapy plus patient education.100 There was no control group\nincluded in this study. Therefore, it is unknown whether the\nimprovements noted with the 3 interventions exceeded “wait\nIn a moderate-quality RCT, 8 sessions of gait re­\ntraining over 2 weeks in rearfoot-strike runners,\nconsisting of cuing to alter foot strike from rearfoot\nto forefoot using a faded-feedback design, were more effec­\ntive at reducing pain immediately following the conclusion of\nthe retraining period and at short-term follow-up (4 weeks)\ncompared with a control group that received a graded in­\ncrease in running volume m", "keywords_zh": [], "keywords_en": [], "synonyms": [], "domain_terms": ["training_protocol"], "text": "essions of gait retraining to in­\ncrease running cadence by 10%, combined with the\nuse of minimalist, barefoot-mimicking footwear for 20% of\nrunning volume, had greater pain reductions compared with\nrunners treated with foot orthoses prescribed to op"}
